Lat pulldowns can be performed with a variety of hand grips, including different grip widths and overhand, underhand, neutral, or hybrid (a combination of neutral and underhand or overhand) grips. Additionally, the shoulder motion occurring during a lat pulldown can be performed predominantly in the sagittal (front to back) plane, frontal (side to side) plane, or in a combination of the sagittal and frontal planes. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the sagittal plane, as would occur when lifting your arms straight out in front of you, the upper arms remain parallel to each other and do not deviate away from the mid-line of your body. Lifting your arms straight out to your sides, such as with a snow angle motion, would be an example of moving your shoulders strictly in the frontal plane. When raising your arms in the frontal plane, the upper arms move away from the mid-line of your body until they are parallel to the ground, and then they move closer to the mid-line of your body with further elevation. During the downward pulling phase of a lat pulldown, the shoulders may extend in the sagittal plane, adduct in the frontal plane, or exhibit a combination of extension and adduction in a hybrid plane between the sagittal and frontal planes. Rather than moving the shoulders exclusively through one plane, lat pulldowns are commonly performed with a technique that allows the shoulders to move in between these two planes, but one plane of shoulder motion often makes a greater contribution than the other.

Muscles acting on the shoulder joint have different leverage to generate extension torque in the sagittal plane versus adduction torque in the frontal plane. During any active movement, the nervous system is most likely to recruit the muscles best suited to produce the joint actions required for efficient movement. Consequently, determining which muscles have meaningful leverage to extend or adduct the shoulder can inform how well these muscles will be trained by a pulldown targeting a specific shoulder motion. A muscle’s leverage to contribute to a particular joint action can be quantified as its moment arm, which is the perpendicular distance between a joint’s axis of rotation and the muscle’s line of pull. When multiplied by how much force its contraction produces, the moment arm determines how much torque a muscle can generate for a particular joint action.

With any particular grip for a lat pulldown, you can intentionally rotate your shoulders in a manner that allows you to move your shoulder relatively more in the sagittal or frontal plane. However, most people find that a particular path of shoulder motion feels most comfortable and stable for their shoulders, elbows, and wrists when using a specific grip. For instance, people typically perform a wide overhand grip lat pulldown with shoulder motion predominantly in the frontal plane, whereas they typically perform a close underhand grip lat pulldown with shoulder motion predominantly in the sagittal plane. Performing either variation in the alternative plane of motion would require greater-than-normal rotational range of motion in the shoulders and forearms, and attempting to do so could feel very awkward and uncomfortable. People typically feel most comfortable when positioning themselves so that, at the portion of the lat pulldown when their upper arms are parallel to the ground, their elbows and wrists are vertically aligned or their elbows are slightly lateral (outside) of their wrists.

In general, widening a grip facilitates relatively more frontal plane shoulder motion, while narrowing a grip facilitates relatively more sagittal plane shoulder motion. An overhand, underhand, or neutral grip may be used with any grip width, but the combination of these grip variables will influence comfort and which plane of shoulder motion feels most natural. When performing a pulldown predominantly in the sagittal plane with a relatively close grip, an underhand or neutral grip is typically more comfortable than an overhand grip, but an overhand grip can still be used. Similarly, when performing a pulldown predominantly in the frontal plane with a rather wide grip, an overhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable and enable more frontal plane motion than an underhand grip. With a moderate width grip, people may commonly find that pulling mostly in the sagittal plane feels natural with an underhand grip, but pulling relatively more in the frontal plane feels natural with an overhand grip. Fundamentally, shoulder motion is what matters to the back muscles, and grip is relevant only insofar as it influences which shoulder motion is performed.

The lat and teres major in the back function as both primary adductors and extensors and are therefore highly likely to be trained effectively by lat pulldowns, regardless of grip or shoulder plane. However, the preponderance of available evidence indicates that the rear delt lacks meaningful leverage to contribute to shoulder adduction, but it clearly has favorable leverage to function as a shoulder extensor and, correspondingly, is likely to be trained better with a closer grip lat pulldown emphasizing shoulder extension. Alternatively, the middle and lower regions of the chest’s pectoralis major that constitute the sternoclavicular head have large moment arms to function as shoulder adductors. However, only the lower portion of the pec major exhibits a shoulder extension moment arm, and this moment arm is quite small and only present in flexed shoulder positions, like at the top of a lat pulldown. Consequently, wider grip pulldowns involving greater frontal plane shoulder motion are likely to train the pec major more effectively than closer grip pulldowns. Which regions of the lat are preferentially biased by a pulldown may also be influenced by the extent of shoulder motion in the frontal or sagittal planes. The middle and lower regions have relatively greater leverage to function as shoulder adductors, while the upper region of the lat has relatively greater leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. Plausibly, these biomechanical differences may facilitate the middle and lower regions of the lat being preferentially trained by wider grip pulldowns, while the upper region may be preferentially trained by closer grip pulldowns. That said, the extent to which any preferential biasing may occur across different regions of the lat when comparing different pulling techniques remains speculative and has not been tested in any longitudinal training interventions. While certain regions may have greater leverage than others for a particular technique, all lat regions may be recruited near their maximal capacity during a challenging set of lat pulldowns, so it is unclear whether regional biasing would occur.*Key takeaway: Deep squats can train your glutes and inner thigh muscles meaningfully better than shallower squats.*

To lift yourself out of the bottom of a squat, your knees and hips must simultaneously extend against meaningful resistance. Like the gluteus maximus, the hamstrings have excellent leverage for hip extension, so contraction of these muscles generates hip extension torque that is conducive to squatting. However, no study measuring changes in hamstring muscle size following a squatting intervention has reported significant increases. This lack of meaningful hamstring hypertrophy may be due to the fact that the hamstrings cross the knee joint and function as primary knee flexors, and thus can be trained effectively with hamstring curls, in addition to acting as hip extensors.

When a muscle contracts, it generates a force that pulls the bones to which it is attached closer together. This pulling force cannot be exerted on one end of the muscle without simultaneously acting on the other end. As a result, contraction of a muscle that crosses two joints, known as a biarticular muscle, inevitably generates torque at both joints it crosses. Consequently, it is physically impossible for hamstring contraction to produce hip extension torque without concurrently generating knee flexion torque. During a squat, the knee flexion torque produced by hamstring contraction directly opposes the quadriceps as they contract to generate the knee extension torque required to stand upright during the lifting phase and to control the descent during the lowering phase.

To maximize efficiency, the nervous system appears to limit hamstring recruitment during squatting, favoring activation of the gluteus maximus and adductor magnus to generate the desired hip extension torque without producing knee flexion torque. This likely explains why squatting interventions do not yield significant hamstring growth but do induce significant growth of the gluteus maximus and hip adductors. A similar phenomenon almost certainly exists with other compound exercises that meaningfully resist both knee extension and hip extension, such as leg press, lunge, and split squat variations. However, changes in hamstring muscle size following these exercises have not yet been studied, in contrast to squats.

The hamstrings remain somewhat active in a stabilizing role during squatting, but not to a degree sufficient to train them robustly. Squats can effectively train a substantial portion of the body’s musculature, but for specifically developing the hamstrings, they cannot match hamstring curl or hip hinge variations such as Romanian deadlifts.
Nonetheless, many lifters report feeling soreness in their inner hamstrings after squatting. This common occurrence can be attributed to adductor magnus tenderness, which lies adjacent to the semimembranosus on the medial aspect of the thigh. The adductor magnus functions as a powerful hip extensor, particularly at high degrees of hip flexion, such as at the bottom of a squat, and therefore serves as a prime mover during squatting. As a result, it can become sore after a squat session. The adductor magnus can also play a prominent role during leg press, lunge, and split squat variations, all of which can elicit soreness that is often mistaken for hamstring soreness.During a horizontal cable fly, the shoulders move in the same plane of motion that they do during a flat dumbbell fly, so these variations will train the same muscles. The primary difference between them is that cables provide a more even level of resistance throughout the rep’s range of motion, in contrast to the dumbbell variation, which imparts maximal resistance when the arms are fully spread but essentially no resistance to the pec major and front delt at the end of the lifting phase. The available body of evidence currently indicates that many muscles likely experience the greatest growth when trained at relatively long lengths, which is achieved by both dumbbell and cable flies. However, it is currently unclear whether concentrating resistance at the position where the working muscles are most lengthened has any effect on muscle growth compared with more evenly applied resistance throughout the range of motion.In contrast to squat variations, where you do not keep your back against a wall, wall squat variations allow you to keep a completely vertical torso position that shifts your center of mass closer toward your hips and farther away from your knees. This, correspondingly, results in wall squats with a greater lever arm of resistance acting on the knees and a shorter lever arm acting on the hips than in a free squat, where your torso naturally inclines to maintain your balance. As a result, wall squats can be more quad-dominant than free squats, which may train hip extensor muscles such as the gluteus maximus more effectively.

However, the way in which you perform a wall squat can influence how much load is shifted onto your quads or onto your hip extensor muscles. During a wall squat, you can transmit pressure to the wall through your upper back and pelvis in an even manner, or you can predominantly transmit pressure through one of these sites while still keeping both of them in contact with the wall. Biscarini et al. (2020) found that chiefly applying pressure to the wall through the pelvis shifted almost all the resistive torque onto the knees (which would train the quads more effectively) relative to the hips, which were minimally loaded. In contrast, applying all pressure to the wall through the upper back minimized knee loading while increasing hip loading (which would train the gluteus maximus more effectively), especially when participants focused on bearing weight through the forefoot (ball of the foot and toes) rather than the rearfoot. It is worth noting that even when participants used the technique that maximized hip loading, the magnitude of peak hip loading was approximately half the magnitude of peak knee loading during the condition that maximized knee loading. Consequently, you can modify your wall squat technique intentionally to make it as hip-dominant as possible, but you will likely be better served by simply performing a different exercise if you wish to train your hip extensor muscles most effectively.As a fun (or morbid) fact, upright row maximal power has been reported by Araujo et al. (2019) to correlate significantly with all-cause mortality risk in a study of 3,827 participants aged 41-85 years old. The quartile of participants with the highest upright row power had only a slightly higher risk of death than the quartile immediately below it throughout the follow-up period (median 6.5 years). However, half of the participants with the lowest upright row power had a considerably greater risk of dying, especially the least powerful quartile of participants, who had a 10-13 times greater risk of dying from any cause compared to the two quartiles with greater than average upright row power. These findings do not mean that improving your upright row performance will directly reduce your risk of mortality, but they indicate that upright row power is associated with overall health. Such a relationship is not unique to the upright row, as other studies have found bench press, leg press, hip flexor, and grip (which is especially well researched) strength to correlate significantly with mortality risk. It should not be surprising that people who have above-average strength or power in various movements tend to be physically healthier than weaker people. Upright rows may not be the fountain of youth, but including some form of resistance training (which may or may not involve upright rows) as part of your lifestyle has the potential to make a meaningful difference to your overall health.As with many other exercises with bodyweight variations, you can naturally increase the intensity of your crunch by performing a variation that uses an external load (e.g., holding a weight plate) as your abdominal strength improves over time. However, you can also modify the intensity to which your abdominal muscles must contract during each rep by adjusting your arm position. The difficulty of a crunch is lowest when you rest your arms by your sides, and it progressively increases as you shift the center of mass of your arms closer toward your head. Correspondingly, crossing your arms to rest your hands on your shoulders can be more challenging than keeping your arms by your sides, and keeping your arms up and behind your head can further increase difficulty. In light of this relationship, the “bodyweight crunch with hands on floor” is an easier variation than the “bodyweight crunch with hands on shoulders,” which is an easier variation than the “bodyweight crunch with hands behind head.”You can use one of three arm positions when performing wrist roller wrist curls, each of which is described as its own variation in this database. In addition to holding your arms out in front of you with straight elbows, which places the shoulders at about 90 degrees of flexion, you can keep your upper arms by your sides with your elbows bent to about 90 degrees, or you can keep your arms slightly in front of you and directed toward the ground with straight elbows. None of the wrist flexor muscles cross the shoulder joint, so shoulder position does not directly affect them. Some wrist flexor muscles slightly cross the elbow joint and may be marginally more lengthened with straight elbows than with bent elbows, but elbow position also does not meaningfully affect wrist flexor function.

However, holding your arms outstretched parallel to the floor requires your shoulder flexor muscles, such as the front deltoid and upper pectoralis major, to act isometrically to prevent your arms from lowering. Similarly, keeping your elbows bent requires your elbow flexor muscles, such as the biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, to act isometrically to prevent elbow extension during the exercise. In contrast, keeping your arms directed down toward the ground places minimal demand on the shoulder or elbow flexor muscles due to the position of the weight relative to those joints.

When using one of the two variations in which the weight is held farther in front of your body, more muscle groups are recruited than when it is kept close to your body, which has potential advantages and disadvantages. If your shoulder or elbow flexor muscles must act isometrically to maintain arm position, they will receive some stimulus for strength and size adaptations alongside the wrist flexors. However, there is limited evidence on the effectiveness of mid-range isometric training for developing these muscles compared with dynamic training through a full range of motion. Rasch and Morehouse (1957) reported that six weeks of dynamic curls led to greater increases in upper arm circumference than mid-range isometric curls. That said, arm circumference is not a direct measure of muscle size, and these findings should be interpreted cautiously, especially given the study's age.

The added isometric demands placed on the shoulder or elbow flexors when holding the wrist roller farther from the body may also come at the expense of wrist flexor training. If your sets are limited by shoulder or elbow fatigue rather than by the ability of your wrist flexors to lift the weight, the wrist flexors may receive a suboptimal stimulus. Conversely, if your wrist flexors remain the limiting factor when using the bent-elbow or outstretched-arm variations, the additional isometric work for the shoulder or elbow flexors is unlikely to impair wrist flexor training.
Of the three positions, the outstretched-arm variation is the most likely to shift the limiting factor away from the wrist flexors. In this position, the weight has a much longer lever arm acting on the shoulder than on the wrist, and this shoulder lever arm is substantially longer than the elbow lever arm involved in the bent-elbow variation. As a result, the shoulder flexors must generate considerably more torque to maintain position. Although Bober et al. (2002) found average maximal shoulder flexion torque at 90 degrees to be slightly lower than maximal elbow flexion torque at 90 degrees, individual strength ratios vary widely. Consequently, the likelihood that shoulder flexion strength limits performance is generally higher with outstretched arms than the likelihood that elbow flexion strength limits performance with bent elbows, though this will differ between individuals.

A simple way to assess whether your shoulder or elbow muscles are limiting your wrist roller performance is to immediately perform additional reps with your arms directed down toward the ground after completing a set with one of the other positions. If you can perform more reps with the weight closer to your body, it suggests that your forearm muscles were not the limiting factor during the previous variation. Overall, the key takeaway is that arm position during wrist roller wrist curls influences which muscle groups are trained most effectively. If your goal is to maximize forearm development, it is best to use a variation in which the wrist flexors, rather than other muscle groups, limit the set.*Key takeaway: Squats are ineffective for training the hamstrings because knee flexion opposes the quadriceps.*

To lift yourself out of the bottom of a squat, your knees and hips must simultaneously extend against meaningful resistance. Like the gluteus maximus, the hamstrings have excellent leverage for hip extension, so contraction of these muscles generates hip extension torque that is conducive to squatting. However, no study measuring changes in hamstring muscle size after a squatting intervention has found significant increases in hamstring size. This lack of meaningful hamstring hypertrophy may be due to the fact that the hamstrings cross the knee joint and function as primary knee flexors, and thus can be trained effectively with hamstring curls, in addition to acting as hip extensors.

When a muscle contracts, it fundamentally generates a force that pulls the bones to which it attaches closer together. This pulling force cannot be exerted on one end of the muscle without simultaneously acting on the other end. As a result, contraction of a muscle that crosses two joints, known as a biarticular muscle, inevitably generates torque at both joints it crosses. Consequently, it is physically impossible for hamstring contraction to produce hip extension torque without concurrently generating knee flexion torque. During a squat, the knee flexion torque produced by hamstring contraction directly opposes the quadriceps as they contract to generate the knee extension torque required to stand upright during the lifting phase and to control the descent during the lowering phase.

To maximize efficiency, the nervous system appears to limit hamstring recruitment during squatting, favoring activation of the gluteus maximus and adductor magnus to generate the desired hip extension torque without producing knee flexion torque. This likely explains why squatting interventions do not yield significant hamstring growth but do induce significant growth of the gluteus maximus and hip adductors. A similar phenomenon almost certainly exists with other compound exercises that meaningfully resist both knee extension and hip extension, such as leg press, lunge, and split squat variations. However, changes in hamstring muscle size following these exercises have not yet been studied, in contrast to squats.

The hamstrings remain somewhat active in a stabilizing role during squatting, but not to a degree sufficient to train them robustly. Squats can effectively train a substantial portion of the body’s musculature, but for specifically developing the hamstrings, they cannot match hamstring curl or hip hinge variations such as Romanian deadlifts.
Nonetheless, many lifters report feeling soreness in their inner hamstrings after squatting. This common occurrence can be attributed to soreness of the adductor magnus, which lies directly next to the semimembranosus on the inner portion of the back of the thigh. The adductor magnus functions as a powerful hip extensor, particularly at high degrees of hip flexion, such as at the bottom of a squat, and therefore serves as a prime mover during squatting. As a result, it can become sore after a squat session. The adductor magnus can also play a prominent role during leg press, lunge, and split squat variations, all of which can elicit soreness that is often mistaken for hamstring soreness.As with many other exercises, you can naturally progress the intensity of this weighted crunch by using a heavier weight. However, you can also modify the intensity to which your abdominal muscles must contract during each rep by adjusting your arm position. As the horizontal distance between the weight and your abdomen increases, the lever arm of resistance that your abdominal muscles must overcome increases in turn. Increasing the load you use increases the resistive torque you experience, because torque is the product of force and the lever arm through which the force acts at a particular joint (in this case, the joints in the lower and middle back). Relative to keeping arms directed straight upward, the resistive torque from the weight will progressively increase as you shift your arms farther backward (which would move your elbows closer toward the top of your head, like when raising your arms overhead while standing upright). When you can simply select an incrementally heavier load to use as a progression for your weighted crunch, there is no particular advantage in modifying the difficulty of the exercise by adjusting the arm position. However, shifting your arms farther back can be useful if you have equipment limitations during your training session. Even if you train at a well-equipped gym on certain days, it can be beneficial to know how to adjust the difficulty of various exercises at home with minimal equipment on days when it may not be feasible to go to a gym.Shrimp squats and skater squats are similar unilateral squatting movements that both dynamically train the quads through knee extension, along with the gluteus maximus and adductor magnus through hip extension. Additionally, both exercises can be challenging for many people without adding external load, making them useful when training in environments with limited equipment. The key difference is that shrimp squats use one hand to hold the foot of the nonworking leg, keeping that knee fully bent, whereas skater squats allow you to keep both arms outstretched or hold a weight if you wish.

Compared with the shrimp squat, holding both arms out in front of you during a skater squat shifts your center of mass forward and provides a counterbalance that helps maintain balance throughout the exercise. With only one arm outstretched during a shrimp squat, it can be noticeably more difficult to descend to the same depth that can be achieved during a skater squat while maintaining balance. Relative to a skater squat performed to the same depth, shrimp squats may also require greater ankle dorsiflexion (the opposite motion of the lifting phase of a calf raise) to allow greater forward knee tracking during the descent. These increased balance and mobility demands can make shrimp squats a more difficult variation than skater squats.As the name implies, hamstring curl variations primarily train the hamstrings, including the semitendinosus, semimembranosus, biceps femoris long head, and biceps femoris short head. However, exercises that resist knee flexion can effectively train other muscles, including the gracilis (a hip adductor) and the sartorius (which crosses the quadriceps on the front of the thigh). These two less commonly discussed muscles share an insertion on the medial (inner) tibia with the hamstring’s semitendinosus and correspondingly have large knee flexion moment arms that have been measured to even exceed the knee flexion moment arm of the hamstring’s biceps femoris. Although they are thin, the gracilis and, especially, the sartorius (which has the longest muscle fibers in the human body) are very long muscles because they originate from the pelvis and descend to the tibia. Together, they have been measured to have a combined muscle volume of 36% of the total hamstring volume, so developing them can allow hamstring curl variations to contribute to a greater increase in total thigh muscle size than if only the hamstrings were trained with resisted knee flexion exercises.

Given their anatomy, hamstring curls are expected to elicit meaningful hypertrophy of the gracilis and sartorius; however, well-designed training intervention studies provide further robust evidence that this is indeed the case. Maeo et al. (2020) found both the gracilis and sartorius to increase in size after participants completed a 12-week training intervention exclusively involving seated hamstring curls or lying hamstring curls. In a later study, Maeo et al. (2024) also found these two muscles to increase in size after participants completed a 12-week training intervention exclusively involving Nordic hamstring curls or seated accentuated eccentric hamstring curls. When averaging the increases in volume measured after each of the four interventions, the gracilis and semitendinosus responded remarkably well to the resisted knee flexion exercises, with increases of 24% and 22%, respectively. The specific variations that stimulated the greatest or least hypertrophy differed among the muscles, but the sartorius, biceps femoris long head, and biceps femoris short head also grew similarly overall by averages of 11-12% after the interventions. Of the six muscles (when considering the two heads of the biceps femoris to be distinct muscles) measured, the semimembranosus tended to grow the least, as indicated by its volume increasing by an average of 7% when pooling the results of the four different hamstring curl interventions together.### **What to do if you are in between step heights for your step-down**

Depending on the equipment that is available to you, you may find that you are in between step heights relative to the rep range in which you wish to train for this exercise. In some situations, the height of the step you are currently using may not be sufficiently challenging, but the smallest increment by which you can increase the height results in the step being excessively tall. If you find yourself in this scenario, you can use the taller step and simply perform your step-down through the range of motion you can appropriately control without tapping the ground with your heel, or you can tap the ground with your toes pointed down (which would reduce the range of motion compared to if you tapped the ground with your heel). If you do not have access to a taller step, you can alternatively reach your free leg forward to increase the range of motion before your heel taps the ground, or you can progress the intensity by performing a weighted step-down variation.Some people have labeled the upright row as an exercise that is dangerous to shoulder health, but the same can be said of other compound shoulder movements, such as the bench press and overhead press. As an exercise that resists a type of shoulder motion and engages multiple shoulder muscles, the upright row certainly loads the shoulder. Loading any region of the body is neither inherently beneficial nor deleterious because the dosage and rate of loading can considerably affect the body's response. Individual differences among factors such as prior injury history and training experience can also dramatically affect how two different people respond to the same exercise.

Upright rows may provoke shoulder pain in individuals with pre-existing shoulder conditions, such as massive rotator cuff tears. However, that does not mean that upright rows are necessarily dangerous to the shoulder in the same way that walking up stairs is not necessarily dangerous to the knee, because people with severe knee osteoarthritis may feel notable knee pain doing so. When performed with excessively high volumes, a wide variety of exercises and physical activities can cause pain or injury, as they can load regions of the body beyond their load tolerances. Regardless of whether you have had a history of any shoulder issues, performing an absurdly high volume of upright rows to which you are accustomed may prompt your shoulders to feel achy, just like your knees may be hurting if you run 10 miles (16km) without progressively building up running distance over time. In either instance, the physical activity is not the root issue; rather, the issue lies in its application.

Sometimes, particular exercises may simply not feel right to your body with the technique you use, even if you start with conservative loads and training volumes while lacking any notable prior injuries. If that is the case, trying out different technique modifications may be warranted to determine if performing the exercise in a somewhat different manner feels better to you as an individual. While plenty of people may find their shoulders feel comfortable when upright rowing until their upper arms are near parallel to the ground, perhaps your shoulders will feel better when using a moderately lower range of motion. Alternatively, you, as an individual, may find that grasping the bar with a well outside shoulder width grip feels better to you than using a closer to shoulder width grip. At times, performing the same exercise with a different modality can allow the movement to feel more comfortable for you as an individual. Perhaps barbell upright rows simply do not feel right to you, regardless of trying out different techniques, but dumbbell upright rows feel fine. When you have a wide variety of exercises and different ways to perform each exercise, there is no need to simply push through any joint pain or discomfort.

Exercise variations are fundamentally tools available in your toolbox, and the utility of each exercise is determined by how you use that exercise and the overall context in which you use it, as is the case with other tools. No single exercise is required to achieve excellent results from weight training, and upright rows are not the optimal option for every person. Nonetheless, there is little reason to think that upright rows are an inherently dangerous exercise, especially when focusing on high-quality technique individualized to what feels right to the lifter and performing appropriately dosed training volumes.Compared to flat variations, incline chest fly and bench press variations most likely train the upper pec major’s clavicular head to a preferentially greater degree than the sternocostal head that constitutes the middle and lower pec major. However, flat variations can still train the upper pec effectively, and it is currently unclear whether incline variations meaningfully train the upper pec more effectively than flat variations in an absolute sense. At first glance, these statements may appear contradictory, but there is an important distinction between training a muscle to a greater degree than another and training a muscle more effectively overall.

Some studies have found the upper pec to exhibit significantly greater EMG activity during flat pressing compared to incline pressing, while other studies using EMG and related proxies have reported similar upper pec activity during flat and incline variations. In contrast, the lower pec has been more consistently shown to exhibit lower activity during incline variations relative to flat variations. As a result, incline variations may bias the upper pec to a relatively greater degree primarily by reducing lower pec engagement rather than by increasing upper pec engagement. If so, the upper pec may experience similar development from incline and flat variations, while the lower pec may experience less development during incline variations. That said, it remains possible that incline variations induce greater upper pec hypertrophy than flat variations, given the mixed findings.

To date, only one study, conducted by Chaves et al. (2020), has directly measured changes in the size of different regions of the pectoralis major following a training intervention using flat or incline chest exercises. In that study, untrained participants were assigned to perform the flat Smith machine bench press, the incline Smith machine bench press, or a combination of both. After eight weeks of training with one weekly session consisting of 4–6 sets of the assigned exercise, the incline group increased upper pec thickness by 62% and middle and lower pec thickness by 55–58%. The flat and mixed groups both increased upper pec thickness by 32%, which was significantly lower than the incline group. Middle pec thickness increased by 46% and 24% in the flat and mixed groups, respectively, while lower pec thickness increased by 43–42%. Differences in middle and lower pec growth were not statistically significant, but average changes favored the incline group.
Based on these results, incline training appeared superior to flat variations for chest muscle growth, as the incline group experienced substantially greater upper pec hypertrophy without sacrificing middle or lower pec growth. However, these findings warrant skepticism. The magnitude of hypertrophy observed after only eight sessions and approximately 40 total sets was unusually large, raising concerns about potential measurement error. Additionally, it is unclear why the group performing both flat and incline pressing experienced the same upper pec growth as the flat-only group, or why middle pec growth was markedly lower in the mixed group.

Despite these concerns, the findings from Chaves et al. (2020) provide some evidence supporting incline variations for upper chest development. Their observation that flat pressing still produced meaningful upper pec growth aligns with findings from Ogasawara et al. (2013) and Davies et al. (2021), who reported similar increases in upper and lower pec size following flat bench press training. Pinto et al. (2025) likewise found that machine pec fly training resulted in similar hypertrophy of the clavicular and sternocostal heads. These results are consistent with EMG evidence and the anatomy of the pectoralis major, which allows all regions to contribute to shoulder horizontal flexion.
If upper chest development is a priority, including incline press and/or incline fly variations is reasonable, as there is sound rationale for their potential to bias the upper pec more than horizontal variations. However, horizontal variations clearly train the entire pectoral major effectively, and meaningful upper pectoral development can still occur without incline exercises. It is also worth noting that the clavicular head comprises roughly one-fifth of total pectoralis major muscle volume, with the sternocostal head accounting for the remainder. As a result, increasing upper pec size by a given percentage will have a smaller effect on total chest mass than an equivalent percentage increase in the sternocostal head.If the total load is equated, holding a weight on one side of the body or on both sides will not directly affect how the gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus function as hip extensors to lift the torso and control descent during the lowering phase of a hip hinge exercise. That is because hip extension occurs in the sagittal plane of motion, which runs front to back and divides the body into right and left halves. However, holding a single weight on the side opposite your working leg can increase the demands placed on the hip abductor muscles on the side of the working leg.

When holding weights on both sides, the weight opposite the working leg imparts a torque toward hip adduction of the working leg, while the weight on the same side imparts a torque toward hip abduction. The weight opposite the working leg is farther from the working hip joint, so it has a greater lever arm to produce hip adduction torque than the other weight has to produce hip abduction torque. This results in a net resistive hip adduction torque acting on the working leg, but the weight on the same side partially counteracts that torque. When holding a weight only on the side opposite the working leg, no such counterbalance exists, requiring the hip abductors of the working leg to generate greater hip abduction torque to resist hip adduction and stabilize the pelvis.
As a result, holding one 20 lb (9 kg) weight on the side opposite the working leg may require greater hip abduction torque than holding two 20 lb (9 kg) weights symmetrically. In practice, the prime movers that extend the hip in the sagittal plane to lift the body may be challenged to a similar degree whether you hold a single 40 lb (18 kg) weight or two 20 lb (9 kg) weights. Consequently, if you have sufficient hip extension strength to use two 20 lb (9 kg) weights, you may be able to challenge the hip abductor muscles of the working leg more effectively by holding a single 40 lb (18 kg) weight on the opposite side. This offset loading approach can enable greater hip abductor training without reducing the stimulus to the hip extensors, provided that the total load is matched. However, if offset loading limits the total weight you can use because hip abduction strength becomes the limiting factor, the stimulus to the hip extensor muscles may be reduced.

Mo et al. (2023) provided further insight by assessing EMG activity during single-leg Romanian deadlifts performed with a weight held on the same side or the opposite side of the working leg. The upper region of the gluteus maximus and the gluteus medius both showed significantly greater EMG activity when the weight was held opposite the working leg. Because these muscles function as hip abductors, this finding aligns with the greater hip abductor demands created by offset loading. Similarly, Neumann and Cook (1985) found that carrying a load in an offset position elicited the greatest gluteus medius EMG activity when compared with other loading configurations.The sissy squat exercise performed on a sissy squat machine involves comparable knee and hip motion, so it looks more like a standard bodyweight squat or a back squat than a machine leg extension. However, the training effect of the sissy squat machine is likely more similar to a machine leg extension than to a standard squat, given how it distributes resistance to the knees relative to the hips. With many squat variations, such as bodyweight squats and barbell back squats, you would fall backward if you tried to maintain a completely vertical torso, so you must lean your torso forward by flexing your hips to maintain balance. When the load is situated in front of your body, such as during a front squat or a goblet squat, you can adopt a relatively more upright torso position, but some degree of forward trunk lean will still be normally required to maintain your balance. On the other hand, machine sissy squats allow you to maintain a completely vertical torso, shifting your center of mass closer to your hips and farther from your knees. This correspondingly results in machine sissy squats involving a much greater lever arm of resistance acting on the knees than the hips that should allow them to train all four quad muscles effectively. When the hip is minimally loaded, the hip flexion torque that the contraction of the rectus femoris generates is not deleterious to performance in contrast to free squats, which can involve a much closer to even balance between knee loading and hip loading.
In addition to biasing the quads more than the hip extensors, the longer lever arm at the knee during a machine sissy squat makes this exercise notably more challenging per rep than a standard bodyweight squat. If you opt to perform a weighted variation, you will similarly find that not nearly as much additional weight is needed to match the difficulty of a weighted free squat.As with many other abdominal exercises, this variation trains the abdominal muscles concurrently with the hip flexors because these muscles generate opposing forces at the lumbopelvic region (i.e., the pelvis and lower back). For the sake of having clearer directions when referring to “up” or “down” during this explanation, we can imagine that you are standing upright rather than lying down, as you would when performing this exercise. To lift the thigh via hip flexion, the hip flexor muscles contract, thereby creating a pulling force from the front of the pelvis toward the thigh. Muscle contraction generates a force at both ends to which a muscle attaches, and the hip flexor muscles either originate on the front of the pelvis or cross the front of the pelvis as they course toward their insertion on the femur (thigh bone) or tibia (shinbone). If left unopposed, the force from the hip flexor muscles will rotate the front of the pelvis downward toward the thigh, while the back of the pelvis rotates upward toward the lower back, a motion known as anterior (forward) pelvic tilting. When the pelvis tilts in this manner, the lumbar spine correspondingly extends as the natural arch of the lower back becomes exaggerated into a sway back posture.

Contraction of the abdominal muscles can produce the opposite motions, given that they arise from the ribs and attach to the top surface of the front aspect of the pelvis. To pull the ribs closer to the pelvis, the lumbar spine flexes, initially flattening the natural curvature of the lower back and then rounding it with further flexion. Contraction of the abdominal muscles can also pull the front of the pelvis up toward the ribs as the back of the pelvis rotates down toward the thigh via a motion known as posterior (backward) pelvic tilt. Because the abdominals function to posteriorly tilt the pelvis and the hip flexors function to anteriorly tilt the pelvis, these two muscle groups can operate in tandem. Whenever the legs lift off the ground via hip flexion, the abdominal muscles stabilize the lumbopelvic region by preventing the lower back from arching beyond its natural curvature through the combination of anterior pelvic tilt and lumbar spine extension that would otherwise occur with hip flexor contraction.In theory, you can perform your partial range of motion skater squat with the identical technique regardless of whether you tap your knee to an elevated object or descend to the same depth without tapping any object. However, when an exercise has an ambiguous endpoint for its end range, it can be difficult to use the same exact range of motion during every rep. This is especially relevant to an exercise that is most difficult at the end of its range, such as a skater squat, which becomes increasingly challenging the further you descend. Even when intending to achieve the same depth for every rep, people may have a tendency to shorten a rep’s range of motion as fatigue accumulates during a set with high relative intensity when the end range lacks an external stopping point or is determined by their available joint range of motion. If this unintentionally occurs, the set can still effectively stimulate muscle hypertrophy and strength improvements, but it can be more difficult to track progress over time. If you can perform more reps per set than in the prior session but do so through a reduced range of motion, it is unclear whether your performance actually improved. Accurately assessing your rate of progress can help enhance your training by revealing how you respond to different training methods. When you do not know if you are actually progressing, you can shortchange yourself of valuable information that can otherwise help you accomplish your goals.

In addition to standardizing your range of motion and aiding you in accurately tracking your progress, using a fixed endpoint for your skater squat can reinforce a technique that involves a well-controlled descent. If you do not control the descent during your skater squat, you would receive immediate tactile feedback that you are doing so by banging your knee onto the object underneath you. While you do not need to use a super slow lowering phase for exercises to be effective, using a sufficiently controlled descent can be beneficial when learning and refining the technique for an exercise such as a skater squat.If you have minimal experience training hip hinge exercises such as Romanian deadlift and good morning variations, it is normal to have difficulty performing this movement pattern. Like riding a bicycle or shooting a basketball, hip hinging is a motor skill that may require repeated practice before you feel comfortable consistently using it. Broadly speaking, there are two types of movement substitution patterns that people commonly perform when learning how to hip hinge.

One of these is a stoop lift, which involves dynamically rounding the back through flexion of the lumbar and thoracic spine to bend over before uncoiling the spine into an upright position during the ascent. This is often accompanied by the buttocks not moving particularly far backward and the arms drifting out in front of the legs. In contrast, a strict hip hinge uses a visibly flat back position with a near neutral lumbar spine and involves the buttocks moving noticeably backward during the descent. In terms of similarity, a stoop lift and a hip hinge both involve only a mild degree of knee bend during the descent while keeping the hips high. A clue that you may be performing more of a stoop lift than a hip hinge is feeling a stretching sensation in your back rather than feeling your back muscles remain statically tight throughout the set as they work to maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about the hips. With a properly performed hip hinge, you should feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the bottom of the lift. You may also feel this stretching sensation during a stoop lift. However, a hip hinge often allows you to feel the hamstring stretch earlier during the descent because the hamstrings are shortened by the posterior pelvic tilt that accompanies lumbar spine flexion.

In addition to unintentionally performing a stoop lift, people commonly use a more squatting motion when attempting to hip hinge. This pattern is characterized by greater knee bend and forward tracking of the knees during the descent. Compared to a hip hinge, a squat pattern causes the buttocks to move downward rather than backward, and the shins to incline forward rather than remain vertical. Because the hamstrings shorten as the knees bend, someone using a squat pattern during a hip hinge exercise is unlikely to feel much of a stretching sensation in the hamstrings at the bottom of the lift. Not feeling a hamstring stretch is the most straightforward indicator that you may be squatting your hip hinge exercise. Another clue is perceiving the quads to be working more intensely than the hamstrings, such as feeling a burning sensation or substantial tension.

Hip hinge, stoop lift, and squat patterns exist along a spectrum. You can perform a hip hinge exercise that is mostly a hinge while still incorporating elements of a stoop lift or squat that may not be ideal for your goal. Likewise, your technique can gradually progress from being stoop lift dominant or squat dominant to becoming progressively more hinge dominant as your body learns the movement through repeated practice. If your hip hinge technique has significant room for improvement, it can be helpful to start with lighter loads that are not especially challenging until you develop a reasonable level of competency with the movement. You do not need to wait for immaculate technique before loading the movement meaningfully, but it would also be inadvisable to push your body to its limits without first learning the fundamentals.

Depending on which aspect of hip hinging you currently struggle with, certain external cues can help accelerate learning. If you unintentionally squat or stoop lift, using a wall can provide tactile feedback to cue sitting the hips backward. Perform the hip hinge with your back facing a nearby wall and aim to make contact with the wall using your buttocks at the end of the descent. As an initial starting point, you can set up in a heel to toe tandem stance with the back heel touching the wall, then bring your rear foot forward to align with your front foot in the stance you plan to use for the exercise. This provides a reasonable approximation of how far most people sit their hips back, and you can adjust your distance from the wall as needed.

If you tend to perform a squatty hip hinge, practicing with your calves in contact with a flat exercise bench, couch, or chair can be effective. As you descend, you will receive immediate tactile feedback if your knees track forward because your calves will lose contact. This helps reinforce keeping your shins vertical. When using this method, maintain full contact between your feet and the ground and avoid leaning your bodyweight into the bench. If you keep your calves in contact and feel a pronounced hamstring stretch at the bottom, you are likely performing a high quality hip hinge.

As an additional learning tool, you can place a long object such as a dowel rod, PVC pipe, or yardstick along your back while hip hinging if you tend to use a stoop lift technique with excessive back rounding. Either the calf-to-bench technique or the buttock-to-wall technique can be incorporated into a Romanian deadlift performed with weights, but using a rod as a cue without a partner requires you to hold the rod in place, which means you cannot load the drill independently.

To perform this drill, hold the rod against your back with one hand higher near your head and the other hand lower near your buttocks. While standing fully upright and looking straight ahead, you should feel the rod contacting your sacrum (the bone between your upper buttock cheeks and just above your tailbone), your upper back between your shoulder blades, and the back of your head. You will then hip hinge while maintaining all three points of contact throughout the movement. If you begin rounding your back or moving your neck beyond the neutral position you adopt while standing upright, the rod will lose contact with one of these points.

This provides immediate feedback and can help develop your ability to move primarily through your lower body while keeping your trunk rigid, which can transfer to improved technique across many exercises. When maintaining these three contact points, it is still possible to perform a squatting motion rather than a true hip hinge, since minimizing back motion is also a component of squatting technique. However, if you maintain all three contact points and also feel a pronounced stretch in your hamstrings at the bottom of the descent, those two factors together strongly indicate that you have successfully hip hinged. If the rod cue shifts your movement from a stoop lift toward a squat, you can combine the rod cue with the calf-to-bench cue to address multiple aspects of the hip hinge at the same time.

In the context of performing a hip hinge exercise, using a technique that more closely resembles a squat or a stoop lift can be considered a technique error. That said, squatting and stoop lifting are not inherently faulty movement patterns. They simply differ from a hip hinge and can be suboptimal ways to perform an exercise when your goal is to train specifically for the benefits that hip hinging provides. The hip hinge can take time to learn, but investing the effort to develop this motor skill can meaningfully expand how you move both inside and outside the gym.If you have access to hanging ab straps, you can use them for this exercise by hanging them from your pull-up bar about shoulder width apart and resting your upper arms on the straps while otherwise using the same form described in the exercise technique section. Using straps ensures that grip strength does not function as the limiting factor during your set without otherwise affecting how your abdominal and hip flexor muscles are trained. Correspondingly, using straps removes the stimulus that this exercise can provide to the forearm and hand muscles involved in gripping. Performing the exercise with straps also keeps the shoulders moderately flexed, as the upper arms remain roughly parallel to the ground, in contrast to the fully flexed position adopted when hanging directly from the bar. This can remove the stimulus to improve or maintain overhead shoulder mobility that may be experienced when hanging from the bar. On the other hand, the shoulder extensor muscles (for example, the lats, teres major, and rear delts) must act isometrically to maintain the moderately flexed shoulder position when using straps. While these muscles are likely more engaged with straps than without, they are still unlikely to be loaded enough to provide a meaningful training stimulus for most lifters.

If you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance rather than abdominal strength, a simple approach is to compare one set performed without straps to one set performed with straps. To make this comparison meaningful, ensure that you use the same technique in both conditions, including the same range of motion and tempo. You should also perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate, enabling a useful comparison. For a more robust self-assessment, you can repeat the comparison on a separate day while reversing the order of the conditions. If you perform the set without straps before the set with straps on the first day, perform the set with straps first on another day. This helps control for fatigue from the first set, influencing performance on the second. If you consistently complete more reps meaningfully when using straps, grip strength is likely limiting your performance during this exercise, which can reduce the stimulus provided to your abdominal muscles.*Key takeaway: Squats can be excellent for developing the gluteus maximus, but they are unlikely to train the gluteus medius or gluteus minimus meaningfully.*

Squats have the reputation of being a high-quality glute exercise, and that certainly is the case for the gluteus maximus when squatting sufficiently deep. Hip extension strength is vital for squat performance, and the prominent contribution of the gluteus maximus to hip extension explains why multiple studies have found that this muscle grows meaningfully with back squat training interventions. However, the gluteus maximus is not your only gluteal muscle. The gluteus medius and gluteus minimus constitute a notable proportion of the musculature on the side of your hip, and they together reflect a third of total glute muscle volume based on data from Handsfield et al. (2014).

The gluteus medius and gluteus minimus primarily function as hip abductors (bringing the thigh out to the side) when you are in an upright position or in a low angle of hip flexion, like during the initial part of a squat’s descent. In these positions, contraction of the two gluteal muscles can help prevent the knees from caving inward, which results from hip adduction and internal rotation. While these glutes act in a stabilizing role to prevent other muscles from adducting the hip, squats do not provide external resistance that pulls the hip toward adduction unless a band is looped around your thighs. The resistance from squatting is directed downward, which pulls the hip toward a flexed position, and only a portion of the gluteus medius can assist in extending the hips at certain joint angles. Once the hip is flexed by about 90 degrees of greater (like at the bottom of a half squat), the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus lose most of their leverage to function as hip abductors, while they develop the greatest leverage to act as hip internal rotators. Forceful contraction of these muscles can consequently make it more difficult to prevent the knees from caving inward, so highly activating them may not be particularly beneficial at the bottom of a squat.

Based on biomechanical modeling research on the functions of the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, it is reasonable to anticipate that squats would not effectively train these muscles. That is indeed what Plotkin et al. (2023) found after participants trained deep back squats for nine weeks, which prompted the gluteus maximus to grow significantly, but the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus essentially remained the same size on average. In light of these findings, you can be confident in the ability of sufficiently deep squats to induce notable glute growth since the gluteus maximus is the largest part of the glutes. However, it is worth recognizing that other exercises directly training hip abduction will likely be required if you want to train your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus effectively.When prioritizing high-quality technique that emphasizes a very controlled descent, level hip bones, and the working leg’s knee stacked over the foot, a fairly low step can be sufficiently challenging for most people. As you become more proficient with this exercise over time, you can incrementally increase the height of your step to increase the intensity of the exercise, but refrain from selecting a height that prompts your technique to degrade. There are a few common signs that may appear at the end of the step-down’s lowering phase, indicating that a particular step may be too high for someone’s current ability. These signs include the hip bone of the free leg dropping down or the knee of the working leg caving inward shortly before the heel reaches the ground. Additionally, people may begin descending slowly and in a controlled manner, then abruptly accelerate at the end of the descent due to an inability to control the most difficult end range. This technique fault is commonly accompanied by forcefully contacting the ground, followed by pushing off the ground with the free leg to make the ascent easier, rather than lifting the body exclusively with the elevated leg.During a dual cable fly, the concentric phase (when the muscles are shortening) of the rep finishes when your palms are facing each other as your hands are completely level with each other in front of your chest. In comparison, the hands slightly cross the midline of the body during a cable crossover, resulting in the forearms intersecting. Consequently, a cable crossover allows you to reach a mildly greater peak angle of shoulder horizontal flexion, which is the motion you perform as you sweep your arm forward and inward while your arm is held parallel to the ground. Because your pec major and front delt shorten as the angle of shoulder horizontal flexion progressively increases, the end range of the cable crossover can facilitate a mildly greater sensation of these muscles contracting compared to the end range of a dual cable fly. While this may lead some people to perceive their muscles as working more effectively during a cable crossover, this difference is unlikely to affect how well any muscles are trained, and the dual cable fly and cable crossover can essentially be considered interchangeable exercises.The considerable back arch used during a sternum chin-up renders the exercise a hybrid of a vertical pull and a horizontal row. The initial portion of the lifting phase more closely resembles a vertical pull, while the end of the lifting phase more closely resembles a row. While standard chin-ups can be performed with a slight back arch, they are clearly vertical pulling movements. When considering potential differences in how effectively each exercise may train particular muscle groups, comparing rows to vertical pulls in general can provide useful insight. Naturally, the difference between a pure row and a pure vertical pull should be greater than the difference between a sternum chin-up and a standard chin-up, since the sternum chin-up is a hybrid movement. Nonetheless, the direction of these differences will likely mirror the differences observed between pure vertical pulls and pure rows performed in the same shoulder plane of motion, though to a lesser degree.

Because both variations resist shoulder extension and elbow flexion, both can train the primary shoulder extensors (lat, teres major, and rear delt) and elbow flexors (biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis). However, the standard chin-up involves a greater elbow range of motion than the sternum chin-up and therefore may have greater potential to train the elbow flexor muscles. Vertical pulling exercises have not yet been directly compared with rowing exercises in a single longitudinal study that measures muscle growth; however, other research has shown that lat pulldowns can produce elbow flexor hypertrophy comparable to curls, whereas dumbbell rows have produced less elbow flexor growth than curls. Whether these findings generalize across vertical pull and row variations remains unclear, but it is plausible that vertical pulls, on average, train the elbow flexors more robustly than rows.

In contrast, row variations are likely to train the traps and rhomboids more effectively than vertical pull variations due to differences in the direction of the resistive force. During a row, the horizontally directed resistance (relative to the shoulder girdle) creates a protraction force that is opposed by the traps and rhomboids as they retract the shoulder girdle during the lifting phase. During a vertical pull, the vertically directed resistance creates a force toward shoulder girdle elevation, which is opposed primarily by the lower trapezius, lats, and pectoralis minor, which contribute to shoulder girdle depression. Because only part of the trapezius contributes to depression, whereas all regions of the trapezius contribute to retraction, rows are likely better suited than vertical pulls for training the trapezius and rhomboids. Correspondingly, sternum chin-ups likely train the traps and rhomboids more effectively than standard chin-ups, while standard chin-ups may train the elbow flexor muscles more effectively.*Key takeaway: Not everyone has the mobility to pull from the floor with ideal technique, but slightly elevating the bar can accommodate mobility limitations while effectively training most of the same muscles.*

Plenty of lifters can comfortably train conventional deadlifts from the floor, but the arbitrary starting height may not be ideal for everyone. While deadlifting from the floor, your body proportions determine the magnitude of hip and knee flexion required to grasp the bar. When a barbell is loaded with at least one pair of full-diameter plates (such as 45lb (20kg) plates or bumper plates), the center of the barbell will be elevated slightly, under 9in (23cm) above the ground when the plates rest directly on the ground. Based on differences in body proportions, such as arm length relative to height, the joint angles required to deadlift from the floor can vary from person to person, and some lifters will have to adopt a noticeably more bent-over position than others to reach the bar. For some people, this can result in a deadlift from the floor requiring joint angles similar to those of a deficit deadlift, whereas for others it may require joint angles similar to those of a block pull or rack pull. Certain individuals may lack the hip flexion range required to deadlift from the floor without adopting a highly flexed lumbar spine to assume the starting position. Lumbar spine flexion occurs along a spectrum, and deadlifting with a controlled degree of lumbar spine flexion (which can be present even with a visibly flat back, since the lumbar spine is naturally arched in an upright resting position) is normal and not inherently injurious. However, repeatedly lifting heavy loads with excessive lumbar spine flexion may increase the risk of certain lower back injuries and may tax the lower back during recovery, potentially interfering with the performance of other exercises. When moderately elevating the bar for a block pull or rack pull, you can modify the exercise to align with your mobility and individual body proportions. With a somewhat higher starting height, you can train a very similar movement pattern to deadlifting from the floor without requiring as much hip flexion range of motion to reach the bar while keeping your lower back near a visibly flat position.

Reduced range of motion exercises like block pulls and rack pulls are often perceived as “overloading” lifts with the purpose of being able to utilize heavier loads. With a high elevation that situates the bar at or above knee level, most people can lift meaningfully heavier loads than they can from the floor, but pulling from mid-shin often has a small effect on how much weight someone can lift. When regularly training mid-shin pulls, people can often pull slightly more weight than from the ground, but some may not be able to lift a heavier load if they rely on momentum from leg drive to overcome a mid-shin sticking point. In terms of muscle contributions, the primary difference between a mid-shin elevated deadlift variation and a deadlift from the floor is that the elevated pull involves lower quad involvement. When deadlifting from the floor, the quads are most strongly utilized during the initial portion of the pull to help lift the bar off the floor, so a mid-shin pull omitting this portion is unlikely to train the quads as effectively. However, deadlifting from the floor is still unlikely to be the ideal exercise for quad development, and mid-shin pulls are likely to train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and back muscles to a similarly effective degree as deadlifts from the floor. Consequently, if you utilize other exercises to train your quads (like squat, lunge, leg press, or leg extension variations), the reduced quad contribution from a mid-shin pull compared to a deadlift from the floor should not be problematic.The equipment that you use for this exercise is normally called a sissy squat machine or a sissy squat bench, but the term “sissy squat” commonly refers to an exercise that differs from the exercise performed with a sissy squat machine. A sissy squat performed without a machine involves keeping your hips extended (so that you can draw a straight line from your trunk to your thighs) as you push your knees, lift your heels off the ground, and lean your torso back. Such a movement, which is described in detail under the “sissy squat” page from this database, can concentrate the vast majority of loading onto the quads and knees, which is also the case for the exercise that uses the sissy squat machine and a vertical torso position. Although they may use different methods, these two variations can produce a similar effect on quadriceps training while not meaningfully training other muscle groups.

When comparing them, the exercise using the sissy squat machine may be more stable since the lower legs can be locked in place while the soles of the feet remain in full contact with the foot plate. On the other hand, the sissy squat performed without a machine may be able to train the quad’s rectus femoris at longer muscle lengths because this muscle can be most lengthened when the hips remain extended as the knees fully flexed. This may amplify the stimulus to the rectus femoris, as indicated by Larsen et al. (2025), who reported greater growth in the rectus femoris when machine leg extensions were performed at 40 degrees of hip flexion compared with 90 degrees. Although it has not been studied, the sissy squat machine is likely to effectively train the rectus femoris at moderate muscle lengths because hip extensor demands are minimal with the vertical torso position.Most of the work performed during any hamstring curl exercise can be attributed to the thigh muscles discussed in the “hamstring curls develop more than just your hamstrings” passage. However, the medial and lateral gastrocnemius muscles of the calf also assist with knee flexion. These muscles have favorable knee flexion moment arms of a similar magnitude to that of the hamstring’s biceps femoris and contain substantial contractile tissue that allows them to generate high forces. A muscle’s physiological cross-sectional area (PCSA) is a key determinant of its force-producing capacity and is calculated by dividing muscle volume by fiber length. Holzbaur et al. (2007) reported that the combined PCSA of the gastrocnemii is about 90% of the hamstrings’ PCSA, indicating a substantial capacity for force production. When paired with their meaningful knee flexion moment arms, the gastrocnemii contribute notably to total knee flexion torque.

While several studies have examined changes in gastrocnemius size following resisted ankle plantarflexion exercises, no studies to date have directly assessed how hamstring curl training affects gastrocnemius size. As a result, it remains unclear how much hypertrophic stimulus the gastrocnemii receive from resisted knee flexion. However, other lines of evidence provide insight into their role during hamstring curls. Li et al. (2002) demonstrated that electrically stimulated gastrocnemius contractions generate knee flexion torque, and Cadeo et al. (2023) reported moderately high gastrocnemius EMG activity during resisted knee flexion. Li et al. (2002) also showed that both knee and ankle position influence the magnitude of knee flexion torque produced by the gastrocnemii. Torque was greatest with the knee fully extended and declined progressively as the knee flexed, reaching near zero at 115 degrees of knee flexion. These findings suggest that the gastrocnemii function primarily as knee flexors when the knee is minimally flexed, such as at the start of a leg curl rep.

Li et al. (2002) further reported that knee flexion torque was significantly greater when the ankle was dorsiflexed than when it was plantarflexed. This is likely because plantarflexion shortens the gastrocnemii, placing them at a less favorable length for force production, whereas dorsiflexion lengthens them. Knee position has a similar influence on gastrocnemius hypertrophy during calf training, with extended-knee calf raises producing markedly greater growth than bent-knee variations. More broadly, several biarticular muscles exhibit greater hypertrophy when trained at longer muscle lengths. Accordingly, it is reasonable to hypothesize that hamstring curls may train the gastrocnemii more effectively when performed with a dorsiflexed ankle.

The influence of ankle position on gastrocnemius contribution helps explain why multiple studies have found maximal knee flexion torque to be significantly greater in dorsiflexion than plantarflexion. This effect allows people to use heavier loads during hamstring curls when the ankle is dorsiflexed. Some individuals may naturally adopt dorsiflexion during hamstring curls because it is their strongest position. Cadeo et al. (2023) examined whether this strategy compromises hamstring training by assigning trained participants to perform unilateral lying hamstring curls for 10 weeks, with one leg trained in dorsiflexion and the other in plantarflexion. Both conditions produced similar increases in biceps femoris size, the only muscle measured. Both also increased knee flexion strength, but the dorsiflexed condition showed a larger average increase, about 12.2% versus 4.6%, despite the difference not reaching statistical significance. Given the trained status of the participants and the within-subject design, this difference is still noteworthy.

These findings suggest that using a dorsiflexed ankle during hamstring curls does not detract from the hamstring stimulus and may enhance training of other knee flexors, likely the gastrocnemii. Kim et al. (2015) reported similar results in untrained participants who performed hamstring curls and leg extensions for three weeks with either a dorsiflexed or plantarflexed ankle. The dorsiflexed group experienced a 45% greater increase in knee flexion strength. Given the short duration of the intervention, these gains were likely driven by neural adaptations, but the most plausible explanation is greater gastrocnemius involvement in the dorsiflexed condition.

Taken together, the available evidence suggests that performing hamstring curls with a dorsiflexed ankle likely enhances gastrocnemius involvement without reducing the stimulus to the hamstrings. While future research may clarify the hypertrophic effects on the gastrocnemii, current evidence indicates no meaningful adverse effects on dorsiflexion. That said, hamstring curls should not be considered a replacement for extended-knee calf raise variations if calf development is a priority, as the gastrocnemii remain better suited to function as ankle plantarflexors than as knee flexors.To help you use a standardized foot placement you can reliably replicate from set to set, you can use an alternative approach instead of the stepping method described in the “setting up” section. For this method, sit on the edge of your elevated surface and extend one leg in front of you by straightening your knee. Wherever that foot reaches determines how far from your elevated surface you position your front foot. Regardless of the setup approach you select, it can be beneficial to use a method that promotes a consistent technique in an appropriate posture, reducing the likelihood that you unintentionally end up in a less-than-ideal position.As you increase your grip strength over time, you can progress this dead hang variation by incrementally supporting more of your bodyweight with your hands rather than through your legs until you are ready to perform the standard dead hang with your feet fully off the ground.Forward and backward lunges are similar to split squats, as they target the same muscle groups and can appear identical at the bottom of the lowering phase. The distinction between them is that your feet remain stationary throughout a split squat set, except for the initial step you take into the split stance position and the step you take to reposition your feet near each other to conclude the set. With lunges, you start each rep with your feet in a symmetrical stance, step backward or forward (depending on the variation) with one foot during a rep, and then reverse the step to bring your feet back together at the end of each rep. This difference contributes to a lunge set, in which your balance is most challenged for a greater proportion of the time, since the least stable portion of either exercise occurs when one foot is briefly off the ground during a step, which occurs much less frequently during split squats. However, a lunge set also involves a greater proportion of time in which your balance can be least challenged since you are in a highly stable position whenever you are standing in a symmetrical hip-width stance, which you adopt between each lunge rep but not between each split squat rep. Correspondingly, lunges can exhibit greater fluctuations in balance demands throughout a set, with both more frequent moments of maximal balance difficulty and more frequent moments of minimal balance difficulty. In comparison, split squats typically involve a relatively constant level of moderate instability throughout most of the set, which contributes to their reputation as less challenging from a balance perspective than lunges. Nonetheless, certain individuals may find it more difficult to maintain balance during a split-squat set because they do not receive the brief “balance break” after each rep that lunges provide, when the feet are returned to a highly stable, symmetrical stance.While maintaining approximately 90 degrees of hip flexion throughout the exercise, L-sit pull-ups naturally engage the hip flexor muscles to a greater extent than standard pull-ups. Even without external load added to the legs, L-sit pull-ups position the legs in a way that maximizes their lever arm, which increases the challenge to the hip flexor muscles as they act isometrically to prevent the legs from lowering. The hip flexors work in tandem with the abdominal muscles, which are more strongly engaged during L-sit pull-ups than during standard pull-ups.

Hip flexion can occur either by the thigh moving while the pelvis remains stationary or by the pelvis rotating forward (anteriorly) while the thigh remains relatively stationary. Anterior pelvic rotation is coupled with lumbar spine extension, resulting in a sway-back posture with an exaggerated lower-back arch. When the hip flexor muscles contract, they exert force on both the pelvis and the thigh, and which segment moves depends on how well other muscles stabilize the pelvis or thigh. Contraction of the abdominal muscles can rotate the pelvis backward (posteriorly), allowing the abdominals to stabilize the pelvis and provide a stable base for the hip flexors to lift the legs. As hip flexor force increases, the abdominal muscles must contract more forcefully to lift the thighs rather than simply tilting the pelvis forward.

The three hamstring muscles that originate from the pelvis are also influenced by pelvic position. They are shortened by posterior pelvic rotation and lengthened by anterior pelvic rotation. These hamstrings are additionally lengthened by hip flexion and knee extension, which means they may not be able to elongate sufficiently to achieve an L-sit position unless the pelvis simultaneously rotates backward. As a result, posterior pelvic rotation can naturally occur when maintaining the L-sit position. Just as anterior pelvic rotation is coupled with lumbar spine extension, posterior pelvic rotation is coupled with lumbar spine flexion.

Part of the latissimus dorsi originates from the back of the pelvis. The lats are shortened with lumbar spine extension and lengthened with lumbar spine flexion. Because the L-sit position involves posterior pelvic rotation and lumbar spine flexion, the lats operate at longer muscle lengths during L-sit pull-ups than during standard pull-ups, which are typically performed with a more extended lumbar spine. Muscles have an optimal length for active force production, and when they are lengthened beyond that range, their ability to generate force can decline. In an overhead position, the lats are already lengthened by the shoulder position, and additional lengthening from lumbar flexion may further reduce their force-producing capacity.

In addition to placing greater demands on the hip flexor and abdominal muscles, L-sit pull-ups can therefore be more challenging for the lats compared to standard pull-ups. Due to these combined factors, some people may be able to perform L-sit holds and standard pull-ups separately but lack the strength to perform L-sit pull-ups.*Key takeaway: Not everybody can perform a very deep squat, but you likely can squat deeper over time.*

While recognizing that squatting through a long range of motion is likely ideal for muscle growth and general lower body strength development, it is worth noting that not everyone will be able to perform a deep squat right away with quality technique. Some people may reach the end range of hip and ankle mobility before their thighs are parallel to the ground during squatting, and going any deeper would require them to begin rounding their backs or lifting their heels off the ground. There is no need to force yourself to squat so deeply that your technique degrades, and certain individuals have body structures that can make it difficult to perform a very deep squat, regardless of their technique. However, being limited with your squat depth when you start squatting does not mean that a reasonably deep squat will always be unattainable.

If your squat depth is initially limited, it is worth trying different stance widths and degrees of toe flare, as you may not yet have found the foot placement that best suits your body. Over time, squatting through your full range of motion with proper technique may gradually improve the mobility required to squat deeper. Stretches (e.g., spending time at the bottom of a bodyweight squat while holding onto something in front of you with your hands) and exercises (e.g., seated calf raises) that train restricted joints to their end ranges of motion can also improve squat mobility for many people. Without improving mobility, lifters often can reach greater squat depths simply by wearing squat shoes with elevated heels or by squatting with their heels elevated on top of weight plates or squat wedges, thereby reducing the ankle mobility demands required for a deep squat. Additionally, certain squat variations commonly allow people to achieve greater squat depths than others. Goblet squats and front squats, which provide a counterbalance from holding the weight in front of the body, frequently enable individuals to squat more deeply than other variations, so struggling with your depth during one type of squat does not mean that other forms of squatting will be the same.In addition to strengthening and increasing the size of your hip adductor muscles, performing seated machine hip adduction through a full range of motion can be a viable means of increasing the flexibility of these muscles. The flexibility of these muscles may limit hip abduction range of motion, which refers to how far from the midline of the body you can spread your legs. As their flexibility increases over time, you may be able to position the machine’s pads farther apart to continue training your hip adductors from a stretched position. In addition to benefiting certain physical activities such as dancing and yoga, increasing hip adductor flexibility may help you achieve more mechanically advantageous positions during wide-stance squat variations and sumo deadlifts.

Weight training may not have the same reputation for improving flexibility as static stretching, but training through a full range of motion that loads the working muscles at long lengths can improve flexibility just as effectively while also improving strength in those positions to a much greater degree. When pooling the results of relevant studies into a systematic review and meta-analysis, Alizadeh et al. (2023) found that resistance training and static stretching interventions produced similar increases in available range of motion.In this database and in common usage, standing exercises, including “overhead press,” are strict variations in which the legs remain stationary throughout the rep, requiring only the upper body to lift the weight upward. The push press is also a standing overhead press variation, but it differs from strict variations by utilizing leg drive to assist the upper body in propelling the weight upward. Because of the leg-drive contribution, which is especially impactful during the first half of the pressing phase, push presses allow lifters to utilize heavier loads than strict overhead presses. A push press is also a more technically complicated exercise than a strict overhead press, so it would be helpful for someone to develop competent technique with a strict overhead press before starting to train push presses.Lunge variations are commonly considered to be unilateral exercises, which have been defined by Fountaine (2018) to be exercises where “each limb works independently of the other to create the desired movement.” Unilateral exercises differ from bilateral exercises, where “both limbs are used in unison to contract the muscles, which create forces, and subsequently move a given load.” Exercises can clearly be bilateral when both limbs are symmetrically loaded as they simultaneously move in the same manner (such as during a barbell back squat) or unilateral when one limb is loaded as the other limb remains suspended in space (such as during a pistol squat). However, both legs function simultaneously against resistance in an asymmetrical manner to move your body (and any weights you may add) during lunge variations.
The front leg may be loaded to a greater extent than the rear leg, but the rear leg can still be meaningfully loaded during lunging, as demonstrated by Hefzy et al. (1997), who found the rear leg receiving about one quarter of the total load during a forward lunge. Hofmann et al. (2017) provided further evidence supporting the notable involvement of the rear leg when assessing forward lunges performed with a vertical trunk and vertical shin, a vertical trunk and inclined shin, or an inclined trunk and inclined shin. The researchers found that knee loading (measured as knee extensor impulse) was similar between the front and rear legs when lunging with the trunk and front shin both inclined forward, but the rear knee experienced meaningfully greater loading when using either technique with a vertical trunk position. In light of these data, Hofmann et al. (2017) considered lunges to be bilateral exercises, along with split squats, which Schultz et al. (2014) also reported imparted greater loading to the rear knee than to the front knee while simultaneously transferring more weight to the front leg. The specific technique used to perform a lunge can affect the distribution of load between the two sides. Besides modifying the trunk angle, people may intentionally be able to shift almost all of their weight into the lead leg while using the rear leg lightly for balance assistance, or they may be able to focus on pushing off forcibly through the rear leg to perform much of the ascent through work from the rear leg. The ability to lunge with variable techniques can further complicate the classification of lunges as unilateral or bilateral. What someone considers a unilateral or bilateral exercise can also be notably influenced by how the terms are operationally defined, and there can be legitimate reasons to classify lunges as either unilateral or bilateral. Given this lack of clarity, this database seeks a middle ground by classifying lunge variations simply as asymmetrical exercises, since both legs are loaded asymmetrically.While exceptions exist, people often find overhand grip pull-ups to be more difficult on a per-rep basis than chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. This discrepancy is most likely influenced by chin-ups and neutral grip pull-ups typically being performed with a technique that emphasizes shoulder extension in the sagittal plane, whereas overhand grip pull-ups are typically performed with relatively more shoulder adduction in the frontal plane. As demonstrated by studies such as those from Mayer et al. (1994) along with Shklar and Dvir (1995), on average, maximal torque for shoulder extension exceeds maximal torque for shoulder adduction. The sample of participants, velocity, and type of muscle action tested can all influence the degree to which torque differs between these two actions, but maximal shoulder adduction torque has been measured to be 63-90% of maximal shoulder extension depending on the specific test conditions. In addition to the effect from how the shoulder moves, the forearm position can also affect how much elbow flexion torque you can generate during your pull. Guenzkofer et al. (2012) found that up to 15% greater elbow flexion torque can be generated when the forearm is in a supinated (used for an underhand grip) or neutral position compared to a pronated (used with an overhand grip) position.

Together, these relationships suggest that most people can generate greater torque at the shoulders and elbows when performing chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups compared to overhand grip pull-ups, which are therefore likely to be more challenging when performing the same number of reps. Consequently, it is common to be unable to complete as many full range of motion reps of overhand grip pull-ups in a set as with chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. Nonetheless, individual differences in factors such as training history and the relative strength of different muscle groups can lead some individuals not to notice differences in difficulty between these variations, or even to find overhand grip pull-ups somewhat easier per repetition.While performing a low handle trap bar deadlift from the floor, your body proportions will determine the magnitude of hip flexion and knee flexion that is required in order to grasp the handles. When loaded with at least one pair of full diameter plates (such as 45lb (20kg) plates or bumper plates), a trap bar’s low handles will be elevated slightly under 9in (23cm) above the ground. Based on differences in body proportions such as arm length relative to height, certain individuals may find that the handles at this level of elevation require deeper joint angles than they wish to train. In light of a particular person’s mobility, training goals, and injury history, the ideal starting height of any deadlift variation will vary among different individuals. For instance, someone with short arms relative to their height and limited hip mobility may find pulling from the floor awkward, but elevating the handles slightly allows for a more comfortable starting position that feels better to that individual. Many trap models have a pair of high handles that allow for a lifter to pull from a height akin to a block pull, but some models lack such handles, so you can perform a trap bar block pull to replicate the high handle trap bar deadlift if you do not have access to a trap bar with high handles. Even when using a trap bar with high handles, a trap bar block pull can still serve a purpose because the height of the handles is fixed. The high handles of trap bars are commonly elevated 4in (10cm) above the low handles, but some models have slightly lower high handles, while others have high handles that are up to about 8in (20cm) above the low handles. Based on your specific trap bar, body dimensions, and training goals, high handle trap deadlifting from the floor may not provide quite enough handle elevation, or it may provide excessive elevation. Consequently, you can adjust the starting position to your preference by adjusting the block height and the handles you use.This terminal knee extension (commonly called a TKE) is a short-muscle-length, partial-range-of-motion exercise, since the quads operate at short lengths when the knee is only mildly flexed. While this variation may not have been directly studied, the available evidence on performing machine leg extensions through a similarly short range of motion indicates that training the quads at rather short muscle lengths is not as productive for stimulating hypertrophy compared to training at least at moderately long lengths (which may involve reaching at least 90 degrees of knee flexion). Compared with training at long muscle lengths, loading muscles at short lengths is also likely to produce less muscle damage, which may help explain why terminal knee extensions are not particularly fatiguing. Correspondingly, terminal knee extensions performed at the start of a training session are less likely to interfere with the performance of subsequent exercises relative to other exercises that load the quads through deeper angles of knee flexion.

When beginning a training session, some people may prefer to proceed directly into a light set of their initial primary exercise of the day as a warmup, while others may find it better to perform a more general warmup that involves different exercises (often called “activation” exercises). If you consider yourself to be a part of the latter group, banded terminal knee extensions can be incorporated into your warmup during your lower body training sessions. While there may not be any research on the matter, some people who have had a history of knee discomfort will anecdotally report that their knees feel better if they start a session with an exercise like terminal knee extensions as a warmup before higher intensity exercises like squats, as opposed to starting off with light squats. This does not mean that terminal knee extensions are a special knee exercise, but if certain individuals find that their knees feel better prepared for more strenuous exercises after performing them, that can certainly be a legitimate reason to include them in a broader training program.When lunging, you can use a range of step lengths while keeping your rear knee in contact with the ground, maintaining the entire sole of your front foot on the ground. At the short end of this step range, you will be able to drive your front knee farther forward relative to your foot, which will result in your shin being inclined forward. With a long step, you can keep your front shin vertical while keeping the knee stacked above the ankle, which requires less knee flexion (bending) range of motion. A longer step may also allow the thigh of the rear leg to be angled further backward (via greater hip extension) than with a shorter step, which keeps the thigh more vertical. Because the rear knee can initially be positioned higher off the floor when the thigh is angled backward, a long step allows the hip to flex slightly more during the descent until the rear knee contacts the floor.

These differences can enable a lifter to train the quads in a slightly more extended position during lunges with a short step, since the quads are lengthened at increasing angles of knee flexion. On the other hand, a longer step may allow someone to train the gluteus maximus and adductor magnus at somewhat longer muscle lengths, since these primary hip extensor muscles are lengthened as hip flexion increases. This is potentially relevant because the available evidence indicates that the peak length at which a muscle is trained during a particular exercise likely influences the extent of growth the exercise may stimulate. Plenty of muscles have yet to be studied in terms of how their growth may be affected by the lengths in which they are loaded during exercise, but several studies have investigated this topic for the quads. The findings of five of these studies align with each other in the sense that training the quads at moderately long peak lengths (involving up to 90-120 degrees of knee flexion) stimulates greater quad growth than training the quads at moderately short peak lengths (involving up to 50-65 degrees of knee flexion). With at least four separate studies (involving squat interventions, machine leg extension interventions, and a multi-exercise intervention that included lunges) reporting the same outcome, there is fairly strong justification to perform lunges until the rear knee gently taps the ground rather than training through a meaningfully more shallow range of motion. However, the quads can already be trained at moderately long lengths when lunging with a long step, and using a short step changes that only by a fairly small amount. This was demonstrated by Escamalla et al. (2025), who reported peak knee flexion angles of approximately 90-100 degrees when participants lunged with a long step until their rear knee contacted the ground, whereas they reached about 100-110 degrees with a short step.

Up to a point, training the quads with longer peak lengths than shorter ones is likely to promote greater hypertrophy, but this effect may have a ceiling effect. Kubo et al. (2019) found similar quadriceps growth after participants performed a half-squat intervention with 90 degrees of peak knee flexion or a full-squat intervention with 140 degrees of knee flexion. Larsen et al. (2025) also found similar quadriceps growth whether leg pressing was performed to 100 degrees of knee flexion or to the end range of knee flexion for each participant (154 degrees on average). While new evidence may emerge in the future indicating otherwise, these findings suggest that training to very high angles of knee flexion to lengthen the quadriceps is not necessarily a superior means of developing the quadriceps than training to approximately 90-100 degrees of knee flexion. Furthermore, the previously mentioned study by Escamalla et al. (2025) found that EMG activity in the quadriceps was generally greater when using a long step than when using a short step. This does not mean that lunging with a long step is necessarily more effective for training the quads than lunging with a short step, but the preponderance of evidence suggests that using a short step is unlikely to train the quads better than lunging with a long step.

On the other hand, Kubo et al. (2019) found that the aforementioned full-squat group experienced approximately three times as much gluteus maximus growth and slightly more than twice the growth of the hip adductor muscle (the primary hip extensor, adductor magnus) relative to the half-squat group. With a compound exercise that effectively trains knee and hip extension simultaneously (e.g., lunge and squat variations), training at high angles of hip flexion may increase the hypertrophic stimulus to the hip extensors relative to reaching only a moderately high degree of hip flexion. The findings from one squat study do not guarantee that the same relationship will hold for lunges, but it is certainly plausible that lunges will show a similar relationship. While there is no legitimate reason to think that lunging with a long step would train the hip extensor muscles less effectively than lunging with a short step, the difference in hip range of motion between step lengths may be of a magnitude low enough to be inconsequential for many people. The directionality may be similar, but lunging with a long step rather than a short step increases hip range of motion to a lesser degree than full squatting rather than half squatting. In isolation, the small effect that lunge step length has on the length of hip extensor muscles may limit the degree to which step length influences hip extensor muscle growth. Still, step length is not the only variable you can modify in an effort to amplify the stimulus you provide to these muscles.

Just as moderately different step lengths can be used when lunging, so too can somewhat different trunk positions be utilized when performing lunge variations. On one end of the spectrum, you can maintain a fully upright trunk as your torso faces straight ahead throughout your lunge, as it would when normally standing upright. Alternatively, you can incline your trunk forward by hinging at the hip of your front leg, which results in your torso facing at an angle between forward and downward. It should be noted that this forward trunk lean occurs from hip motion as you maintain the natural curvature of your lower back, rather than spinal motion that can occur from simply rounding your lower back. Relative to lunging with an upright trunk position, lunging with an inclined trunk can allow you to reach a greater angle of hip flexion for the front leg, which will allow you to train your hip extensor muscles (e.g., gluteus maximus and adductor magnus) at longer muscle lengths. When you use this forward trunk lean simultaneously with a long step, you may be able to achieve a significantly greater angle of hip flexion (e.g., approximately 40 degrees more) than would otherwise be possible with a short step and an upright trunk position. The discrepancy between these two lunge approaches could correspondingly be comparable to the difference between half squats and full squats in terms of the peak lengths at which the gluteus maximus and adductor magnus are trained. In addition to training duration, trunk position during lunging can affect the resistance imposed on the hip extensor muscles. Relative to an upright trunk, an inclined trunk shifts your upper body’s center of mass forward along with any weights you may be using. Doing so increases the lever arm through which resistance acts on your front leg’s hip joint while simultaneously decreasing the lever arm through which resistance acts on your front leg’s knee joint. Consequently, your front leg’s hip extensor muscles will be loaded to a relatively greater degree when lunging with an inclined trunk, while your front leg’s quads will receive comparatively greater loading when lunging with an upright trunk. While not directly proven, the combination of subjecting your hip extensor muscles to relatively greater resistance while they are trained at longer muscle lengths can likely contribute to lunges with a long step and forward trunk lean being more effective for developing your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus than lunging with a short step and upright trunk. On the other hand, keeping your trunk upright while using whichever step length feels most comfortable may be somewhat more effective if you prioritize quadriceps development.This exercise is referred to as a “back extension” as an alternative name in this database because it is most commonly called that, or a “hyperextension.” However, this variation is performed as a hip hinge, dynamically training hip extension while keeping the lumbar (lower back) and thoracic (middle and upper back) spine rigidly static throughout the set. Accordingly, it would be more precise to call this exercise a “Roman chair hip hinge” or “Roman chair hip extension,” especially to differentiate it from another Roman chair exercise. In contrast to hinging at the hips, you can alternatively keep your hips stationary and intentionally move through dynamic trunk flexion (as your back rounds) during the lowering phase and dynamic trunk extension (as your back straightens) during the lifting phase. This exercise is also commonly referred to as a “back extension” or “hyperextension,” but it produces a distinctly different training effect.

When performing the hip hinge variation on the Roman chair, the training effect should be similar to other hip hinge exercises such as Romanian deadlifts. During these movements, the primary hip extensor muscles (gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and the three hamstrings that cross the hip) function dynamically as the prime movers, while the trunk extensor muscles (such as the erectors spinae) act isometrically to resist trunk flexion and maintain a relatively fixed trunk position. Although studies directly comparing these two groups have not yet been conducted, other lines of evidence suggest that hip hinge exercises provide a greater hypertrophic stimulus to the hip extensor muscles than to the trunk extensor muscles. In contrast, the Roman chair spinal extension variation, which involves dynamic trunk motion with a fixed hip position, is likely more effective for stimulating increases in trunk extensor muscle size while not meaningfully training the hip extensor muscles. With this variation, the trunk extensors can be loaded eccentrically and concentrically at long muscle lengths, making trunk extension strength the primary limiting factor. While the trunk extensor muscles are still active during hip hinge exercises, performance is often limited by hip extension strength, and the trunk extensors may not be loaded at long lengths, which may be suboptimal for hypertrophy. Neither variation is inherently superior. Understanding their differences can help you choose the option that best aligns with your specific training goals.People commonly perform pause squats with such a short pause that it defeats the purpose of using a pause squat rather than a standard squat. Your perception of time can easily become distorted at the bottom of a squat, where half a second can feel like two seconds due to the difficulty of maintaining that position under load. Recording some of your sets and watching the video of your performance can help determine the quality of your technique for any exercise, but this is especially the case for exercises that involve a pause during a challenging portion of the rep. If you have to analyze a video frame by frame to determine whether you paused for at least a second, it is reasonable to conclude that you did not actually perform a true pause squat.

In addition to rushing the pause, lifters commonly squat slightly lower after the pause before initiating the ascent. This can provide a mild stretch reflex from your muscles and tendons that makes it easier to squat up compared to if you started ascending immediately after the pause. In addition to extending the duration of muscle activity during each rep, pause squats can be more challenging than standard squats because they do not rely on the stretch reflex to the same extent. Quickly lengthening your tendons and muscles right before you reverse the movement can provide some elastic recoil that helps with the ascent, so your body may naturally try to take advantage of this stretch reflex unless you consciously focus on squatting straight up right after your pause.Forward and backward lunges are much more similar than different, as they both involve similar movement patterns that resist knee extension to train the quads, while resisting hip extension to train gluteus maximus and adductor magnus. While you can consciously strive to reach the same knee and hip positions during each variation, people may naturally tend to perform these two exercises with somewhat different knee and hip motions. Comfort et al. (2015) and Goulette et al. (2021) compared bodyweight forward and backward lunges and found that participants achieved a slightly greater angle of peak knee flexion and peak ankle dorsiflexion (resulting in a greater forward lean of the shin) during the forward lunge. The differences were significant for Goulette et al. (2021) but not for Comfort et al. (2015). While not significantly different, Comfort et al. (2015) also found reverse lunges to involve slightly greater peak hip flexion, and Goulette et al. (2021) found reverse lunges to involve significantly greater forward trunk lean despite participants being instructed to maintain an upright trunk position. When taking a step backward, people may naturally adopt a forward trunk lean to maintain balance during a reverse lunge, which may contribute to a slightly greater hip flexion angle during the descent, even without consciously striving to do so. This difference in average trunk position may help explain why Song and Woo (2021) found that the lumbar erector spinae exhibited significantly higher EMG activity during bodyweight backward lunges than during bodyweight forward lunges.

The available evidence also demonstrates that loading parameters may differ between the forward and backward lunge. Comfort et al. (2015) calculated that the resistive torque acting on the knee and hip was greater during forward lunges. Because resistive torque acting on the knee and hip is what your lower body muscles must oppose while lunging, this suggests that forward lunges may tend to be more challenging than backward lunges when performed for the same number of reps with the same resistance. If a similar relationship applies to weighted lunges, heavier loads may be required during reverse lunges to achieve the same level of resistive torque as during forward lunges. Goulette et al. (2021) additionally reported that the patellofemoral joint (of the knee) experienced significantly greater forces, stress, and loading rates during forward lunges, which also resulted in higher quad force. Along with the difference in resistive torque that Comfort et al. (2015) identified, these differences in knee loading may arise in part from greater momentum being present when the lead leg begins being loaded during the forward lunge compared to the backward lunge. With a backward lunge, you can start loading your lead leg with your entire sole flat on the ground the moment you lift your other foot up off the ground. In contrast, your center of mass has forward momentum by the time the heel of your lead leg contacts the ground during a forward lunge, which may increase the demands imposed on your lead leg. Of all the differences identified, the discrepancy in patellofemoral joint loading rate was the greatest, given that the loading rate of the forward lunge was 2.2 times that of the backward lunge. A higher loading rate and magnitude of forces applied to the knee do not necessarily indicate that forward lunges are problematic for the knee, but these factors may be relevant for lifters with pre-existing knee issues. While there is no reason to fear forward lunges, certain individuals may find them provoke knee discomfort. In contrast, backward lunges may feel better, particularly with a slower descent, and are more readily performed than forward lunges. Because they are quite similar to one another, if one lunge variation simply feels better to your body than the other, that can be a completely legitimate justification to train that variation.Based on the available evidence, ten different muscles have meaningful moment arms, which quantify leverage for generating torque, that allow them to function as hip flexors. Seven of these muscles have sufficiently large moment arms to warrant classification as primary hip flexors in at least certain positions. Three of the muscles have smaller moment arms that allow them to assist in a secondary hip flexion role, and they are likely to be trained more effectively by exercises that resist their primary functions rather than hip flexion.

Except for the psoas major, iliacus (which together constitute the iliopsoas muscle group), and the pectineus, each of the other hip flexor muscles has primary functions beyond hip flexion. Four of the hip flexor muscles (adductor longus, adductor brevis, gracilis, and pectineus) are part of the hip adductor muscle group and can all be trained with resisted hip adduction exercises, such as seated machine hip adduction; however, the pectineus contributes less to hip adduction than the other three muscles. A combination of leg curls and single-joint knee extension exercises, such as machine leg extensions, can train the primary functions of two additional hip flexor muscles, the rectus femoris and sartorius, along with one hip flexor that is also a hip adductor, the gracilis. If you wish to train two additional hip flexor muscles, you can perform hip abduction and hip external rotation exercises to train the tensor fasciae latae (TFL) and obturator externus, respectively, although these are the smallest hip flexors and contribute the least to total lower body muscle size.

Given the multiple functions of most hip flexor muscles, it is not strictly necessary to perform resisted hip flexion exercises to train the majority of the hip flexor musculature. Nonetheless, the psoas major and iliacus are relatively large muscles, and together they have been measured to constitute close to one third of the total volume of all ten hip flexor muscles combined. Whether resisted hip flexion exercises are worth including ultimately depends on your individual goals, but incorporating at least one such exercise is sensible if you wish to maximize overall muscularity. In addition to training the iliopsoas, which is largely neglected by other lower body exercises, resisted hip flexion exercises may also help more fully develop hip flexor muscles that are otherwise trained indirectly. The combined volume of the seven primary hip flexor muscles has been measured to be approximately 60% greater than the total volume of the hamstrings, and total hip flexor muscle volume, including secondary hip flexors, has been measured to be roughly twice that of the hamstrings. Consequently, resisted hip flexion exercises are capable of training a substantial proportion of the lower body musculature.As you progressively increase your abdominal strength, you can progress from the six-inch leg raise with the head on the ground to the six-inch leg raise with the head and upper back lifted up off the ground. When the upper back is off the ground, the abdominals must work to prevent the thoracic spine from extending, as they are already functioning to prevent the lumbar spine from extending, thereby increasing the exercise intensity.The adage “use it or lose it” can readily apply to the available range of motion you have in your body. If you chronically do not perform any activities where you raise your arms up fully overhead, your shoulders can gradually lose the range of motion required to do so, especially as you age beyond young adulthood. Alternatively, performing overhead activities can help you improve your shoulder range of motion if it is limited or allow you to maintain your range of motion if you already have a full range of motion. Vertical pulling exercises like lat pulldown and pull-up variations may assist with improving your shoulder range of motion in an overhead position, but dead hangs can be even more efficient for doing so since you spend the entire duration of each set in a fully overhead position.After completing a set of dumbbell presses, it is not uncommon for people to drop the dumbbells forward while still lying on the bench. If one end of the dumbbell contacts the ground before the other end, the dumbbell can ricochet off the ground and potentially injure someone nearby or damage equipment. While you can proceed as you wish if you are training alone with your own equipment in a home gym, dropping the dumbbells this way is inappropriate and strongly discouraged. While lying on the bench after the last rep, some individuals may set the dumbbells on the ground by straightening their elbows and allowing the dumbbells to pull their arms down below the bench until they contact the ground. Doing so can rapidly pull the shoulder into a position of end-range shoulder extension that can be quite stressful to the shoulder when using loads that are sufficiently challenging for a press, so this maneuver should also be avoided.Relative to when both feet are symmetrically in contact with the ground, the hip abductor muscles (e.g., gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and the upper region of the gluteus maximus) serve a notably greater role in stabilizing the pelvis whenever the body bears weight predominantly through only one foot. When distributing load evenly through both feet, no resistive torque acts on the hips in the frontal (side-to-side) plane unless the load is asymmetric (e.g., holding a weight on only one side). If you perform movements such as a squat, the hip abductor muscles still resist the hip adduction torque generated by contraction of the hip adductors (e.g., the adductor magnus, which serves as a primary hip extensor). However, in this case, they only need to counteract the torque produced by your own muscles. During exercises such as lunges and step-up variations, the hip abductor muscles also help prevent the hip adductors from adducting the thigh, which would result in knee cave, but they serve an additional key function whenever most of your weight is borne through one leg.

In a single-leg stance position, gravity acting on your bodyweight (and any additional load) produces a meaningful magnitude of resistive torque at the stance hip toward adduction. During an open-chain hip action, when the foot is free to move, the thigh moves toward the body’s midline during hip adduction and away from the midline during hip abduction. With a closed-chain hip action, such as when the foot remains in contact with the ground, hip adduction would result in the top of the pelvis on the side of the free limb dropping. In contrast, the top of the pelvis on the side of the free limb hikes upward if closed-chain hip abduction is performed dynamically. During exercises such as single-leg squats, split squats, lunges, and step-up variations, the hip abductor muscles function isometrically, producing torque that maintains a level pelvis. If they fail to generate sufficient torque, pelvic drop will occur. With all other factors held constant, the degree to which the hip abductor muscles are challenged during a set increases as a progressively larger proportion of total weight is shifted onto one leg. Correspondingly, hip abductor demands are generally greater during single-leg squat variations (e.g., pistol or skater squats) than during lunges or split squats, which are themselves more demanding for the hip abductors than symmetrical squats. This relationship is supported by EMG studies showing greater gluteus medius activity during single-leg squats than during forward lunges or forward step-ups, which are often performed with assistance from the non-working leg. Rear-foot elevated split squats have also been shown to elicit greater gluteus medius EMG activity than back squats.When performed with a sufficiently high relative intensity and appropriate load, machine leg extensions can increase rectus femoris size regardless of seat position; however, seat position can influence the magnitude of muscle growth. Larsen et al. (2025) assessed changes in the thickness of the rectus femoris and vastus lateralis after untrained participants trained single leg machine leg extensions for 10 weeks. For each participant, one leg was trained with an upright torso, positioning the hip into 90 degrees of flexion, while the other leg was trained with a recline torso, positioning the hip into only 40 degrees of flexion. The two interventions resulted in similar growth of the vastus lateralis, which is unsurprising since that muscle does not cross the hip joint. However, the rectus femoris experienced significantly greater growth from the leg extensions using a reclined torso position compared to the upright torso position. Because the rectus femoris functions as a hip flexor, it shortens in progressively greater angles of hip flexion, while it lengthens as the hip extends. When you are able to lean your torso back when seated, your hip is in a lower angle of flexion compared to when you are seated upright or leaned forward, so your rectus femoris can operate at a longer muscle length when you lean back. The available evidence indicates that biarticular muscles (which cross two joints) are likely to experience greater hypertrophy when trained at longer than shorter lengths, and the rectus femoris, hamstrings, calves, and triceps fit this trend.

If you wish to train your rectus femoris at longer lengths during machine leg extensions, you can intentionally position the back pad as far back as you can to allow you to lean further back at your hips when performing the exercise. With this approach, you would still want to align your knee joint with the machine’s pivot point, so you would position your thighs at the same part of the seat as you would if you had the back pad further forward to maintain an upright torso. When using the reclined technique, your upper back would still rest against the back pad, but your lower back would no longer contact the back pad, in contrast to if you positioned the seat further forward. While Larsen et al. (2025) specifically utilized a machine that allowed participants to be meaningfully reclined, many leg extensions are not constructed in a manner that would allow users to set up appropriately, with only 40 degrees of hip flexion. Consequently, the degree to which you practically can modify your hip position during a machine leg extension will likely be smaller than what was studied by Larsen et al. (2025). This may potentially result in using a reclined torso position on your leg extension machine, having a smaller effect on rectus femoris hypertrophy compared to what was reported by Larsen et al. (2025).As an alternative to positioning the safety pins just under the lowest point in which the bar would descend during your good morning, you can intentionally position them directly at the lowest point in which the bar reaches when you have finished fully sitting your buttock back and reaching your full hamstring stretch. With this approach, you can lightly tap the safety pins with the bar during each rep, which will provide confirmation that you reached the same depth every rep. Doing so can help you standardize your technique and know that any progressions in your reps per set or load do not come at the expense of reducing your range of motion, which can help with tracking your improvement over time. During exercises that lack an external stopping point such as barbell good mornings, people may have a tendency to reduce depth unintentionally during challenging reps, so you can know that will not be occurring if you lightly tap the pins at the bottom of each rep. It is worth noting using pins in this manner is distinct from the barbell pin good morning, which involves letting the bar come to a dead stop and fully resting on the pins at the bottom of each rep.Performing the six-inch leg raise with the head and upper back lifted off the ground constitutes a more advanced variation of the six-inch leg raise with the head on the ground. When the upper back is off the ground, the abdominals must work to prevent the thoracic spine from extending, as they are already functioning to prevent the lumbar spine from extending, thereby increasing the exercise intensity.Cossack squats and lateral lunges (also known as side lunges) are very similar and can target the same muscle groups, much like split squats and forward or backward lunges. However, each rep of the Cossack squat begins with the feet spread apart in a wide stance, whereas each rep of a lateral lunge begins with the feet together in an approximately hip-width stance. To initiate a lateral lunge rep, you step out to the side with one foot and finish the rep by stepping back to bring your feet together. In contrast, you only step out to the side when setting up for a set of Cossack squats and only bring your feet back together at the end of the set.

When stepping into the descent of a lateral lunge, you can build greater momentum, which may increase the demands on the lower body muscles to control the descent compared with a Cossack squat. Similarly, stepping back to bring the feet together at the end of the lifting phase of a lateral lunge may facilitate a more explosive push-off than a Cossack squat, which is typically performed with a slower tempo. This difference has not been directly tested in research, but it could plausibly contribute to lateral lunges having greater transfer to change-of-direction ability in athletes who need to decelerate laterally and accelerate explosively in the opposite direction.

While lateral lunges and Cossack squats can be performed to the same depth, people commonly perform Cossack squats to a greater depth than lateral lunges. This difference in range of motion can be influenced by foot position relative to the straight knee. During lateral lunges, this foot remains flat on the floor. In contrast, Cossack squats can be performed with the foot flat or with the heel on the ground, with the rest of the foot lifted during part of the rep. Although some individuals can reach maximal depth with the entire foot flat on the ground, most can achieve greater depth by allowing increased knee and hip flexion and letting part of the foot on the straight-leg side lift off the floor. Compared with lateral lunges, people may be more likely to use the Cossack squat to improve hip mobility, and it is also common to pause at the bottom of a Cossack squat to increase stretch duration.

If the Cossack squat allows you to reach greater depth than the lateral lunge, it may also train certain lower body muscles more effectively. Kubo et al. (2019) examined changes in lower body muscle size after participants completed either a half squat intervention, reaching 90 degrees of knee flexion at end range, or a full squat intervention, reaching 140 degrees of knee flexion. The researchers found that the full squat group experienced about three times as much gluteus maximus growth and a little over twice as much hip adductor growth (including the primary hip extensor, the adductor magnus) compared with the half squat group, while quadriceps growth was similar between groups. If you can reach a meaningfully greater depth during a Cossack squat, it may similarly train the gluteus maximus and hip adductors more effectively than a lateral lunge. That said, Kubo et al. (2019) specifically studied back squats, so it remains unclear whether the same effect would apply when comparing Cossack squats and lateral lunges. Moreover, when both exercises are performed to the same depth, they are likely to elicit similar magnitudes of muscle growth.With a collective volume exceeding that of the hamstrings, the six hip adductor muscles constitute a meaningful proportion of your total lower body musculature. Except for seated machine hip adduction, resisted hip adduction exercises are not commonly performed by most lifters. However, most lifters will still train their hip adductor muscles if they perform exercises for their glutes, hamstrings, and or abs because the hip adductor muscles contribute to much more than hip adduction.

The adductor magnus constitutes over half of the total volume of the six hip adductor muscles combined, and this muscle is a powerful hip extensor, especially at high degrees of hip flexion, such as at the bottom of a squat, where it has greater hip extension leverage than any other muscle in the body. At high degrees of hip flexion, three other hip adductor muscles, namely the adductor longus, adductor brevis, and gracilis, also function as hip extensors and can assist the larger adductor magnus. While the adductor longus and adductor brevis can be trained by deep squats, lunges, and leg press variations, the gracilis is unique in that it also has excellent leverage to function as a knee flexor. Like the hamstrings, the gracilis is correspondingly unlikely to be trained effectively as a hip extensor during compound exercises that notably load the quads, since its contraction opposes the quads at the knee, but it can be trained effectively by leg curls and, to a lesser degree, hip hinges such as Romanian deadlifts and good mornings. When the hip is in a neutral position, such as when standing upright, or at very low angles of hip flexion, the adductor brevis and especially the adductor longus also function as hip flexors, while the pectineus functions as a hip flexor in any hip position. They can therefore be trained by exercises that involve hip flexion from near a neutral hip position, such as the V-up, also known as the jackknife, or the dragon flag, which are often performed with the primary intent of training the abs, since the abdominal muscles and hip flexors function together during these exercises.

Given their multiple functions, you can train your hip adductor muscles quite well overall without using resisted hip adduction exercises. However, you will most likely experience greater development of these muscles if you include at least one direct hip adduction exercise in your program. Seated machine hip adduction is an ideal option because it allows you to train these muscles at very long lengths in a stable manner, limited only by the strength of your hip adductors. In contrast to squats, hip hinge variations, and hip flexion exercises such as V-ups, no other muscles are likely to fatigue before the hip adductor muscles during seated machine hip adduction, so these muscles will be the limiting factor for each set.

Beyond likely contributing to the development of the aforementioned muscles, resisted hip adduction may be required to train the adductor minimus effectively, since it does not meaningfully contribute to hip extension or flexion, unlike the other hip adductors. As its name suggests, the adductor minimus is a small muscle, but training a muscle that is otherwise largely neglected by other exercises is an additional benefit of including direct hip adduction exercises.*Key takeaway: Squats are unlikely to train one of the quad muscles as effectively as the others, but that muscle can be robustly trained with leg extensions.*

For the same reason that squats do not train the hamstrings effectively, squats also neglect to train one of the quad muscles as effectively as the others. Three of the four quad muscles originate from the femur and exclusively function as knee extensors. These monoarticular (crossing only one joint) quad muscles have been consistently measured to experience significant growth following squat intervention studies that tracked changes in their size. However, the rectus femoris is the one quad muscle that originates from the pelvis and crosses the hip to function as a hip flexor, in addition to acting as a knee extensor. Notably, the rectus femoris does not just weakly assist in flexing the hip, but rather functions as a primary hip flexor that has greater leverage for producing hip flexion torque than any other muscle in the human body at certain joint angles. Because contraction of the rectus femoris can produce a substantial amount of hip flexion torque, its recruitment during a movement that requires both hip and knee extension is less biomechanically efficient than the recruitment of the other muscle bellies of the quadriceps. As the rectus femoris contracts more forcefully during movements like squat, leg press, and lunge variations, the hip extensors must work harder to resist the hip flexion torque it produces, so it is unlikely to be engaged to the same degree as the other three quad muscles that only generate knee extension torque. This would explain why the available body of evidence indicates that the rectus femoris is unlikely to grow as effectively as the other quad muscles after squatting interventions.

While recognizing that squats have the potential to induce some rectus femoris growth (particularly in individuals with limited training experience), you can be much more confident in your ability to grow the rectus femoris with a quad exercise that does not simultaneously train hip extension, such as a machine leg extension. In contrast to squats, every study involving a machine leg extension training intervention has found meaningful rectus femoris hypertrophy, with a magnitude similar to or even greater than that of the three monoarticular quadriceps muscles. Exercises like leg extensions (which include reverse Nordic curls and sissy squats) may be able to help you maximize your quad growth, but squats can still allow you to train the vast majority of your quad musculature in a very effective manner. The rectus femoris has been measured to contribute only 15% to total quad muscle volume, so you will not be missing out on a ton of quad growth if you rely on compound exercises like squats to develop your quads while simultaneously training other muscles very effectively.When performing a horizontal pressing exercise like bench press variations, your shoulders can move through one of two planes of motion, or a combination of the two, which most often occurs. One of these planes of motion is the transverse plane, which divides your body into upper and lower halves and allows shoulder horizontal flexion and extension, with the elbows level with the shoulders and fully flared out away from the sides. With an overhand grip dumbbell bench press, using fully flared elbows that result in your upper arms forming a “T-shape” with your torso at the bottom, your shoulders can flex horizontally during the lifting phase and extend horizontally during the lowering phase. Shoulder horizontal flexion can also be trained with chest fly variations, such as the pec deck machine fly and the flat dumbbell fly. The other plane of motion is the sagittal plane, which divides your body into right and left halves and allows for shoulder flexion and extension with the elbows tucked close to your sides. With a neutral grip dumbbell bench press, use fully tucked elbows so your upper arms are parallel to your torso at the bottom; this allows your shoulders to flex during the lifting phase and extend during the lowering phase. Sagittal shoulder flexion can similarly be trained with dumbbell front raises, while sagittal shoulder extension can be trained with pullover variations.

Certain horizontal pressing variations can allow you to train your shoulders exclusively through one plane of motion, but most presses involve a hybrid shoulder motion that occurs in between these two planes. If your upper arm forms a 45-degree angle with your torso when your elbow is level with your shoulder toward the end of a press’s lowering phase, the shoulder can be moving symmetrically in between the two planes, resulting in shoulder horizontal flexion and sagittal flexion being trained in equal proportions. As this angle progressively decreases from 45 degrees, the press becomes more sagittal flexion-dominant, while it becomes more horizontal flexion-dominant with angles greater than 45 degrees. Dumbbells allow you full freedom to move in whichever plane you wish, but the grip you use for a barbell bench press will constrain you to a particular type of shoulder motion while keeping the wrists stacked over the elbows for stability. The elbows naturally flare further out as the grip widens, so wide grip bench presses are typically shoulder horizontal flexion-dominant, while close grip presses can be comparatively more shoulder sagittal flexion-dominant, with the medium grip press lying in between them.

The muscles that act on the shoulder have different leverages to function as sagittal flexors or horizontal flexors, so the manner in which lifters press may influence how much of a contribution to the press different muscles have. Multiple muscles can contribute to the shoulder motion that occurs when performing a horizontal press, but the pectoralis major and anterior deltoid serve the most prominent role. The anterior deltoid has meaningful leverage to function as a primary sagittal flexor or horizontal flexor, so it will serve a notable role in any pressing exercise. However, the available evidence indicates that its leverage to generate shoulder horizontal flexion torque steadily declines as the angle of shoulder horizontal flexion progressively increases, as it would throughout the lifting phase of a wide grip bench press. In contrast, the anterior deltoid retains excellent leverage to function as a sagittal flexor throughout the shoulder angles that could be present in horizontal pressing exercises. The anterior deltoid has greater leverage to serve as a sagittal flexor than any other muscle in the body, but the pec major has greater leverage to serve as a horizontal flexor throughout most of a horizontal press’s range of motion. Consequently, the shoulder torque produced by the anterior deltoid may be more impactful during a sagittal flexion-dominant press compared to a horizontal flexion-dominant press.

The reverse can be true for the pectoralis major, which functions as the most potent shoulder horizontal flexor in the body. In terms of its contribution to shoulder sagittal flexion, the different regions of the pectoralis major can have a pronounced impact. The clavicular region that constitutes the upper pec major has very favorable leverage to function as a shoulder sagittal flexor, so it can meaningfully contribute to sagittal flexion-dominant or horizontal flexion-dominant presses. However, the sternocostal head that constitutes its middle and lower regions and forms about four-fifths of the total pectoralis major muscle volume, lacks any leverage to function as a shoulder sagittal flexor. Correspondingly, this large portion of the pec major may serve a more prominent role during a horizontal flexion-dominant press compared to a sagittal flexion-dominant press.

In light of the aforementioned differences in leverage these muscles have to contribute to the two shoulder actions, there is reason to think that a horizontal flexion-dominant press will likely develop the middle and lower chest more effectively than a sagittal flexion-dominant press. On the other hand, a sagittal flexion-dominant press may be somewhat more effective for developing the anterior deltoid. Nonetheless, these potential differences are speculative in nature rather than being supported by any conclusive evidence. No study has yet measured changes in muscle size after participants completed bench pressing interventions with either a wide grip or a close grip that would facilitate firmer conclusions. The EMG muscle activity results from different studies are also inconclusive, given that certain studies have reported significant differences in EMG values when comparing different grip widths, while others have found similar EMG activity to be present. Consequently, it is unclear how much the shoulder plane of motion affects muscular development during horizontal press variations. With that said, if you wish to develop your chest from pressing, keeping your elbows completely tucked by your sides to move your shoulders exclusively in the sagittal plane is highly unlikely to train your middle and lower pec major as effectively compared to if you utilize at least some elbow flare. When comparing other ways in which you can press besides that extreme end of the spectrum, they are far more similar to each other than they are different, so you should be best served selecting an option that you feel comfortable progressively overloading over time.*Key takeaway: To train your lower body and back muscles most effectively, use a grip that does not limit grip strength during this deadlift variation.*

The double overhand grip described in the setup for this deadlift variation can feel natural, and it is intuitive for people who are fairly new to the exercise. However, as lifters become increasingly proficient with deadlift variations, grip strength often becomes the limiting factor with the double-overhand approach. With any grip, using a towel to ensure your hands are dry before the set and applying chalk can meaningfully improve your ability to hold the bar. Nonetheless, it can be very challenging to maintain a double-overhand grip once you reach a particular weight, and your lower-body and back muscles will not be trained as effectively by a grip-limited deadlift variation. As an alternative to the double overhand, you can use a mixed grip (also known as an alternate grip), a hook grip, or lifting straps, while otherwise using the same technique described on this exercise page.

With a mixed grip, you use an overhand grip (with the palm facing backward) with one hand and an underhand grip (with the palm facing forward) with the other hand, which makes it more difficult for the bar to roll out of your hands. The mixed grip, widely used by powerlifters and recreational lifters, can feel somewhat awkward at first, but it typically does not take long to adapt and feel comfortable pulling with it. While the probability is low, the primary con of a mixed grip is that the distal (near the elbow) biceps tendon on the side of the underhand grip may be at increased risk of injury during deadlifts compared with symmetrical grips. Empirical data on the matter is unavailable, but there is reason to think that the risk of this injury would be elevated if a deadlift is initiated with a slight elbow bend on the underhand side in a “grip and rip” style. Doing so could result in that elbow rapidly extending under a very heavy load, imparting considerable eccentric loading into a stretched distal biceps tendon. With any grip, you should initiate the pull with fully straightened elbows, but this is especially important with the mixed grip. Besides prioritizing this technical component, sensible programming is also likely to reduce the risk of a biceps tendon further when pulling with a mixed grip. If you are new to using a mixed grip or have taken a fairly long break from it, it may not be prudent to jump right into a maximal pull instead of progressively increasing the intensity over time to give your biceps tendon time to adapt. While recognizing that the risk of biceps tendon injury cannot be completely eliminated when pulling very heavy loads with a mixed grip, the risk can be extremely low for the vast majority of lifters who use high-quality technique and reasonable progressions, so there is no need to fear using a mixed grip. This is especially the case for females, given that 95% of reported distal biceps tendon injuries have occurred in males, with middle-aged males who use tobacco appearing to have the greatest injury risk based on retrospective data.

Some people have also expressed concern about the risk of developing side-to-side muscle asymmetry with a mixed grip, but there is little reason to think that any problematic muscle imbalance is likely to develop. If your training exclusively involves mixed-grip pulls, without any other back exercises such as rows or pulldowns, mixed-grip pulls may contribute to a slight muscle imbalance, but this should not be an issue if you perform additional exercises. While assessing the upper trap and upper lat, Beggs (2011) found these back muscles to exhibit similar EMG activity between sides when participants deadlifted with a mixed-grip, as was true when using a double overhand grip. Beggs found the biceps on the underhand side to exhibit greater EMG activity than the biceps on the overhand side with the mixed grip, but deadlift variations will not meaningfully develop your biceps regardless, so this asymmetrical EMG activity should be inconsequential. If you are still concerned about the potential to develop a minuscule muscle imbalance, you also have the option of switching which side you use for the underhand grip from set to set or from session to session, but such an approach is quite unlikely to make any noticeable difference.

An alternative to the mixed grip is the hook grip, which involves sandwiching your thumbs between the bar and your other fingers instead of placing your thumbs over your other fingers (further away from the bar) as you would do with a mixed grip or standard double overhand grip. The hook grip allows you to use a symmetrical double overhand grip, so it likely imparts a lower risk of distal biceps tendon injury than the mixed grip while allowing you to train both sides in the exact same positions. Especially for people with relatively long fingers, the hook grip can provide a very stable grip once acclimated, making it advantageous for competitive strength athletes. For weightlifters who perform the snatch and clean and jerk (which cannot be performed with a mixed grip), the hook grip is the sole grip of choice when competing, and a proportion of powerlifters also deadlift with the hook grip due to how secure it can be. However, if you have no plans of competing in a weightlifting or powerlifting meet, the key tradeoff of the hook grip will most likely be greater than the benefits it provides compared to using a mixed grip or straps. That trade-off is the pronounced thumb pain caused by crushing your thumbs against the bar. Over time, your thumbs can become desensitized to some degree (likely due to nerve endings being destroyed), but the hook grip can remain intensely uncomfortable for several months of training. For this reason, most recreational lifters do not find the hook grip to be worth using over the mixed grip or straps when performing deadlift variations.

While not permitted in powerlifting competitions, straps can be valuable training tools, as they help ensure grip strength does not limit your pulling performance when used appropriately. When utilizing straps, both of your palms will be facing backward, like with a double overhand grip, which should likely result in a lower risk of biceps tendon injury than a mixed grip. Utilizing straps also limits your hand skin from restricting your pulling volume. When a bar has aggressive knurling that pulls on your hand skin, you can only grip it directly for a limited volume before you risk tearing a callus, so straps can be a useful way to circumvent that issue. In part due to this advantage, strength athletes who compete with mixed or hook grips commonly use straps for a portion of their total training volume. Accompanying the pro of ensuring grip strength does not limit your pulling performance is the con of not training your forearm and hand gripping muscles as effectively when pulling with straps. However, most people do not perform deadlift variations specifically to target these gripping muscles, and you can likely increase forearm size more effectively with isolation exercises such as wrist curls. Additionally, you can still train your static grip strength with horizontal and vertical pulling exercises like rows and pull-ups, which will train your grip strength with lighter loads than many deadlift variations that reduce the likelihood of grip strength limiting the stimulus provided to other muscles. If you wish to train your grip strength further, you can also perform exercises like dead hangs, farmer’s carries, or suitcase carries for additional grip strengthening. When using straps for work sets of a deadlift variation, you can also use a double overhand grip for your warm-up sets before incorporating straps. Once you have reached a weight you cannot firmly hold with a double overhand grip, you can incorporate straps.

Sometimes it is clear that grip strength limits your performance on deadlift variations without straps, which suggests your lower-body and spinal extensor muscles are not being trained as effectively as they could be. However, it is not always evident what precisely limits your performance, which may warrant a quick personal experiment. As a simple approach, if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps, then compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, ensure you use the same technique across conditions, with the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting for at least five minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, thereby enabling a useful comparison of how the performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day when you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps on the first day, perform the set with straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for any fatigue from the first set that may influence the second. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well-informed as to how grip strength may be affecting the performance of this exercise for you as an individual.Some people experience foot or ankle discomfort when performing rear-foot elevated split squats with the rear foot on a flat surface, such as an exercise bench, but do not feel any discomfort when using a cylindrical object, such as a barbell with a pad, in a power rack's J-cups. This may be due to the way the object you use affects your foot and ankle position. With a flat surface like a bench, your ankle is positioned into end-range plantarflexion, which is the position in which you are at the top of a calf raise rep. Such a position is by no means inherently problematic. Still, certain individuals can find it quite uncomfortable when loaded into this end range, which may also be accompanied by discomfort from pressure transmitted through the top of the elevated foot. For some people, this position can be painful due to a pre-existing condition such as posterior ankle impingement (which can cause pain at the back of the ankle), whereas others may experience discomfort without any particular injuries or disorders. When elevating your foot on a cylindrical object, you can maintain a closer-to-neutral ankle position while distributing the force over a wider area of your body, including the front of your ankle, which can reduce the pressure transmitted through the top of your foot. While this commonly results in a greater comfort to the elevated foot and ankle, it otherwise does not meaningfully affect the exercise. Consequently, if your feet and ankles feel fine when using a flat surface like a bench, you are not missing out on any benefits by continuing to perform your rear foot elevated split squats on that surface instead of a cylindrical object.

###### **How safety squat bar squats may differ from straight-barbell back squats**

*Key takeaway: Compared to back squatting with a straight barbell, safety squat bar squats can minimize shoulder, elbow, and wrist stress while training the lower body similarly and the upper back somewhat more.*

When comparing safety squat bar (commonly known as SSB) squats to back squats with a traditional straight barbell, the most visible difference is arm position. Straight bar squats require a meaningful amount of shoulder external rotation to hold the bar, whereas the safety squat bar allows the shoulders to remain closer to a neutral position, similar to when standing with your arms at your sides. Due to mobility limitations or prior injuries, some people experience pain or discomfort in their shoulders, elbows, or wrists when squatting with a straight bar, but feel comfortable using a safety squat bar. In some cases, discomfort with straight bar squats can be reduced by adjusting hand placement or by gradually improving shoulder mobility while managing training volume and intensity. However, if straight bar squats consistently aggravate the upper body joints, using a safety squat bar may be a sensible alternative. While a straight barbell is an extremely versatile training tool, it is not the ideal option for every individual when squatting.

Beyond reducing stress on the shoulders, elbows, and wrists, the safety squat bar positions the load farther forward than a straight bar back squat, though not as far forward as a front squat. Load placement affects leverage and can influence the technique people naturally adopt. Although individual studies differ somewhat in EMG findings, the available research consistently shows that safety squat bar squats involve a more upright average trunk position than straight bar back squats. With identical bar placement, a more upright trunk increases the resistance moment arm at the knee while reducing it at the hip and lumbar spine. However, positioning the load farther forward has the opposite effect, reducing the knee moment arm while increasing the hip and lumbar spine moment arms. Because these factors counterbalance one another, safety squat bar squats tend to be fairly similar to straight bar back squats in overall load distribution across the knee, hip, and lower back muscles, though straight bar back squats appear to involve slightly greater hip loading.

When comparing safety squat bar squats to straight barbell back squats, bar placement during the back squat is also relevant. High bar squats more closely resemble the load position of a safety squat bar than low bar squats. With a forward inclined torso, a higher bar position places the load farther in front of the hips and lower back, making safety squat bar squats more similar to high bar squats than to low bar squats. Kristiansen et al. (2021) examined high bar back squats, low bar back squats, and safety squat bar squats and found that safety squat bar squats exhibited a slightly higher knee to hip loading ratio than low bar squats, whereas they were similar to high bar squats. Although these differences exist, they do not necessarily mean that safety squat bar squats train the quadriceps meaningfully more or the glutes meaningfully less than low bar squats. Determining whether meaningful differences in muscle hypertrophy exist would require longitudinal training studies, but it is reasonable to expect broadly similar lower body muscle growth across these squat variations.

Nonetheless, the thoracic extensor muscles, which function to prevent the chest from caving through thoracic flexion while squatting, are likely trained more effectively during safety squat bar squats compared to straight bar back squats. Positioning the weight farther forward increases the lever arm of resistance acting on the thoracic spine to a relatively greater degree than on the lumbar spine, and maintaining a mildly upright trunk position is unlikely to offset this effect for the thoracic spine in the same way it may for the lumbar spine. While no studies have yet directly investigated this specific question, there is widespread agreement among lifters that safety squat bar squats impose a greater upper back demand than straight bar back squats, which aligns with expectations based on load placement. Consequently, safety squat bar squats may be a more potent means of developing thoracic extension strength than straight bar squats.

Forward positioning of the load can also make safety squat bar squats more difficult than straight bar squats when lifting the same load for the same number of reps. To date, every study that has measured one rep max or three rep max performance with both a safety squat bar and a straight barbell has found that straight bar back squats allow heavier loads to be lifted. When pooling findings from four studies, straight bar back squat one rep max or three rep max performance has been approximately 5 to 10% higher, on average, than with the safety squat bar. Kristiansen et al. (2021) reported only a 2.6% difference for the high bar back squat but a 7% difference for the low bar back squat, which is logical given that high bar positioning more closely resembles the safety squat bar. The magnitude of this difference is almost certainly influenced by training familiarity. If you have limited experience with a safety squat bar but are proficient with straight bar squatting, the gap will likely be larger than if you regularly train with a safety squat bar. Thoracic extension strength may also influence the difference. If your thoracic extension strength is high relative to your overall squat strength, the disparity between safety squat bar and straight bar back squats may be smaller than average.

Ultimately, safety squat bar squats and straight bar back squats are far more similar than different. Unless you are competing in powerlifting, where straight bar back squats are required, squatting exclusively with a safety squat bar can be a viable option if you prefer it. Conversely, if your shoulders and elbows tolerate straight bar back squats well, there is little reason to worry about missing meaningful strength or hypertrophy adaptations by sticking with the straight bar. If you do not have access to a safety squat bar and want to squat with a more upright torso while placing greater demandLateral lunges and forward lunges both primarily train hip extension and knee extension, since gravity exerts a downward force on the hips and knees regardless of the direction in which the lunge is performed. However, data from Riemann et al. (2013) and Flanagan et al. (2004) indicate that forward lunges involve a greater ratio of hip loading to knee loading compared with lateral lunges. On average, lateral lunges were found to impart mildly greater hip loading than knee loading, but the discrepancy between hip and knee loading was smaller than that observed during the more hip-dominant forward lunge. The researchers also reported that lateral lunges involve greater peak ankle dorsiflexion, which would manifest as greater forward knee tracking during lateral lunges compared with forward lunges. That said, individuals can intentionally perform forward lunges with greater forward knee tracking if they wish.

Compared with maintaining a more vertical shin angle during lunging, allowing the knee to track further forward can shift additional loading onto the quadriceps. Consequently, people may naturally perform forward lunges in a more hip-dominant manner than lateral lunges, but lunge technique can be modified to achieve similar relative contributions from the knee and hip muscles in both variations.*Key takeaway: Overhead pressing with your elbows flared out may potentially bias the lateral deltoid to a greater extent than pressing with your elbows tucked in, which may involve a greater contribution from your upper pec major.*

When performing an overhead pressing exercise, your shoulders can move through one of two planes of motion or a combination of the two planes, which often occurs. One of these planes of motion is the frontal plane, which divides your body into front and back halves and allows for shoulder abduction and adduction with the elbows fully flared out away from the sides. With a dumbbell overhead press using fully flared elbows that results in your upper arms forming a “T-shape” with your torso near the bottom of the rep, your shoulders can abduct during the lifting phase and adduct during the lowering phase. Shoulder flexion can also be trained with single-joint exercises such as front raise variations. Shoulder abduction can also be trained with single-joint exercises such as lateral raise variations. The other plane of motion is the sagittal plane, which divides your body into right and left halves and allows for shoulder flexion and extension with the elbows tucked close to your sides. With a neutral grip dumbbell overhead press using fully tucked elbows that result in your elbows pointing straight ahead when your upper arms are parallel to the ground near the bottom of the rep, your shoulders can flex during the lifting phase and extend during the lowering phase. Shoulder flexion can similarly be trained with single-joint exercises like front raise variations.

Depending on the variation you are training, you can intentionally move your shoulders exclusively through the frontal plane or the sagittal plane, but people commonly utilize a hybrid shoulder motion that occurs in between these two planes. If your elbows are directed at an angle equally between either of the pure planes of motion, such that they are pointing diagonally outward and forward, you can train symmetrically between the frontal and sagittal planes. As your elbows flare further out to your sides, the press involves a greater proportion of frontal plane motion, while it involves a greater proportion of sagittal plane movement as your elbows tuck in closer to pointing straight ahead.

Dumbbells allow you full freedom to move in whichever plane you wish, but the grip you use for a barbell or machine overhead press variation will constrain you to a particular type of shoulder motion while keeping the wrists stacked over the elbows for stability. For instance, using a neutral shoulder width grip for a machine overhead press will typically result in you emphasizing sagittal plane shoulder motion to a greater degree than a wider overhand grip, which facilitates a more frontal plane dominant shoulder motion. Similarly, a behind-the-neck overhead press with a barbell compels you to move your shoulders almost entirely through the frontal plane, while pressing to the front of the body during a standard barbell overhead press allows you to move your shoulders in between the two planes.

The muscles that act on the shoulder have different leverages to function as flexors in the sagittal plane or abductors in the frontal plane, so the manner in which lifters press overhead may influence how much of a contribution to the press different muscles have. Currently, no study has yet measured changes in the size of any shoulder muscles after participants completed an overhead pressing intervention. Consequently, we must draw on other forms of indirect evidence to determine how the overhead pressing technique influences hypertrophy across different muscles.

When performing a particular joint action, the nervous system is most likely to recruit the muscles that are best leveraged to produce that joint action. Nearly every muscle studied to increase in size significantly after a resistance training intervention has been found to have meaningful moment arms (which quantify leverage) for the joint actions, notably those loaded by the exercises in the training interventions. Correspondingly, overhead pressing predominantly in the frontal plane will likely develop muscles with large moment arms to function as shoulder abductors. On the other hand, overhead pressing predominantly in the sagittal plane will likely develop the muscles that have large moment arms to function as shoulder flexors.

The front deltoid and lateral deltoid can function both as shoulder flexors and abductors, but the front deltoid is relatively better suited to function as a shoulder flexor, while the lateral deltoid is relatively better suited to function as an abductor. Similarly, the rotator cuff’s supraspinatus can also contribute to both shoulder actions, but it has greater leverage to function as an abductor than a flexor. On the other hand, the clavicular head of the pectoralis major that constitutes the upper chest has notable leverage to flex the shoulder, but its leverage to function as a shoulder abductor is quite small in magnitude. In light of these differences, overhead predominantly in the frontal plane with your elbows flared out may train the lateral deltoid and supraspinatus more effectively compared to if you primarily moved your shoulders through the sagittal plane. However, overhead pressing predominantly in the sagittal plane with your elbows tucked has the potential to train your front deltoid and upper pec major to a preferentially greater degree based on the moment arms of the shoulder muscles.

In addition to assessing the extent to which the muscles of the shoulder lever arm contribute to different shoulder actions, we can draw on studies that measure EMG activity in these muscles during shoulder exercises. Coratella et al. (2022) assessed participants' EMG activity during overhead presses behind the neck (exclusively involving frontal-plane shoulder motion) and during presses to the front of the body with a barbell (involving a hybrid shoulder motion). The participants also performed machine overhead pressing with a wide overhead grip, emphasizing shoulder abduction, along with a closer neutral grip involving relatively more shoulder flexion. The researchers reported that the lateral deltoid exhibited greater EMG activity with variations involving greater frontal-plane shoulder motion. On the other hand, the upper pec major exhibited greater EMG activity during the variations involving relatively greater sagittal plane shoulder motion. In contrast, the front deltoid, upper trapezius, and triceps exhibited similar EMG activity regardless of the shoulder motion. The differences in EMG activity of the lateral deltoid and upper pec major are congruent with what could be predicted based on the aforementioned moment arms of the muscles. The absence of an effect for the front deltoid was accompanied by very high activity in every variation, so it may be close to being fully active regardless of the plane of motion in which someone overhead presses. Throughout the joint angles present when overhead pressing, the front deltoid may have a greater contribution to shoulder abduction than the lateral deltoid has for shoulder flexion, so the lateral deltoid may be more sensitive to changes in the shoulder’s plane of motion.

Muscle moment arm and EMG activity data have their limitations, so the degree to which your overhead pressing technique can influence which muscles are most likely to grow is still speculative in nature. While not definitively proven, the front deltoid is likely to be effectively trained when performing overhead pressing with the shoulders in the frontal plane, the sagittal plane, or between these two planes. However, it would be sensible for you to emphasize frontal plane movement if you wish to develop the lateral deltoid and supraspinatus through overhead pressing, while emphasizing sagittal plane motion may be preferable if you wish to develop your upper pec major to a greater degree. With that said, moderate or low incline bench press variations will likely train the upper pec major more effectively than any overhead press, and lateral raise variations may still be the gold standard for training the lateral deltoid and supraspinatus.If the total load is equated, holding a weight on only one side of your body or holding weights on both sides will not directly affect how the quads function as knee extensors or how the adductor magnus and gluteus maximus function as hip extensors. That is because these are actions that occur in the sagittal plane of motion, which runs from front to back and divides the body into right and left halves. However, holding only one weight on the side opposite your lead leg can increase the demands on your hip abductor muscles on the lead-leg side during split squat, lunge, and step-up variations. When holding weights on both sides, the weight opposite to your lead leg imparts a torque toward hip adduction of your lead leg, while the weight on the same side of your lead leg imparts a torque toward hip abduction of the lead leg. The weight opposite to your lead leg is situated farther away from your lead leg’s hip than the weight on the same side, so the weight opposite to your lead leg has a greater lever arm to produce hip adduction torque than the other weight’s lever arm to produce hip abduction torque. This results in net resistive hip-adduction torque acting on the lead leg, but the weight on the lead leg counteracts a portion of that torque. However, when you hold a weight only on the side opposite to your lead leg, no counterbalance force exists, requiring the hip abductors on the side of your lead leg to generate greater hip abduction torque to resist hip adduction and stabilize your pelvis.

This may result in holding one 20lb (9kg) weight on the side opposite to your lead leg, requiring your lead leg’s muscles to generate greater hip abduction torque than if you symmetrically held a 20lb (9kg) weight in each hand. In reality, though, the prime movers of the exercise that extend your knees and hips in the sagittal plane to lift your body could be similarly challenged whether you hold a 40lb (18kg) weight on one side or a 20lb (9kg) weight on each side. Consequently, if you have the knee extension and hip extension strength to use two 20lb (9kg) weights for your exercise, you may be able to challenge the hip abductor muscles of your lead leg meaningfully more when holding a single 40lb (18kg) weight on the side opposite to your lead leg. Using this offset-loading approach could allow you to train your hip abductor muscles more effectively without training your quads or hip extensor muscles any less effectively, even with the same total load. However, if the use of the offset loading limits how much total weight you can utilize due to hip abduction strength being the limiting factor, the stimulus provided to your quads and hip extensor muscles may be blunted.
When investigating EMG activity of the gluteus medius as participants carried a load in different positions, Neumann and Cook (1985) found that this primary hip abductor's activity aligned with expectations based on the aforementioned biomechanics. Holding a load on the same side of the studied gluteus medius yielded less EMG activity than when no load was held, while holding a weight on the opposite side yielded the greatest activity of any condition. Holding a weight symmetrically to the chest or on the back also induced greater gluteus medius EMG activity than the unloaded condition, but lesser activity than holding the weight on the opposite side.

As with the hip abductor muscles, the same principles discussed apply: the muscles that produce trunk side bending on the side of the lead leg will be trained more effectively when holding one weight on the opposite side. This means that muscles like the obliques, quadratus lumborum, and various intrinsic back muscles will have to work harder to prevent your trunk from bending to the side of the weight. When holding two equal weights symmetrically, the right and left weights completely counteract each other in the trunk’s frontal plane. However, the trunk side bending muscles will have to function isometrically to maintain an upright trunk when you hold a weight on only one side of your body.Aside from using a straighter knee position, the technique for the straight knee leg raise is essentially the same as for the bent knee leg raise, so both variations primarily train the same muscle groups. In terms of training effect, the main difference is that the straight knee leg raise is a higher-intensity variation because it shifts the center of mass of the legs farther forward relative to the trunk and hips. This increases the lever arm of resistance acting on the trunk and hips, which requires the abdominal muscles and hip flexor muscles to generate greater trunk flexion and hip flexion torque, respectively. Resistive torque can be increased either by increasing the load or by increasing the lever arm through which that load acts on a joint, so the straight knee leg raise functions similarly to a weighted knee raise without requiring additional external weight. This greater resistance likely explains why McGill et al. (2014) found noticeably higher abdominal EMG activity during straight knee leg raises compared to bent knee leg raises when participants performed three reps of each exercise.

However, this does not mean that the straight knee leg raise is inherently superior to the bent knee leg raise for developing the abdominal muscles, just as squatting 200 lb (91 kg) is not necessarily better than squatting 150 lb (68 kg) for inducing quadriceps hypertrophy. If you have the strength to perform both variations with sound technique, you simply need to perform more reps per set with the bent knee variation to achieve a similar hypertrophic stimulus. When an equal number of sets is performed to a similar level of fatigue (for example, performing as many reps as possible in each condition), comparable muscle growth can occur across a wide range of rep counts. Accordingly, the bent knee variation can be just as effective for abdominal development as the straight knee variation for most people when sets are performed with a similar number of reps in reserve. A potential exception may be individuals with especially strong abdominals who can perform very high numbers of bent knee leg raise reps before substantial fatigue, indicating that the resistance is very low relative to their strength. At some point, resistance may be too low to optimally stimulate muscle growth, even when a set is taken to failure; however, the exact threshold is unclear. In practice, this is unlikely to be a concern unless you can perform more than about 30 controlled reps per set, which will not apply to most people.You do not need to tap the ground with your foot for your step-down to be effective, but it is recommended if you have access to a step at an appropriate height. In theory, you can perform a step-down with an identical technique regardless of whether you tap your foot on the ground or use a taller step and descend to the same depth without tapping. However, when an exercise has an ambiguous endpoint at the end range, it can be difficult to use the exact same range of motion for every rep. This is especially relevant for exercises that are most difficult at the end range, such as step-downs, which become increasingly more challenging the further you descend. Even when intending to achieve the same depth for every rep, people may shorten a rep’s range of motion as fatigue accumulates during a set performed at a high relative intensity when the end range lacks an external stopping point or is determined by available joint range of motion. If this occurs unintentionally, the set can still stimulate muscle hypertrophy and strength improvements, but it becomes more difficult to track progress over time. If you perform more reps per set than in a prior session but complete them through a reduced range of motion, it is unclear whether performance actually improved. Accurately assessing your rate of progress can enhance your training by helping you understand how you respond to different training methods. When you cannot determine whether you are progressing, you may forgo valuable information that could otherwise help you achieve your goals.

In addition to standardizing your range of motion and helping you accurately track progress, using a fixed endpoint during a step-down can reinforce a technique that involves a well-controlled descent. If you do not control the descent, you will receive immediate tactile feedback when your foot contacts the ground with excessive force. While you do not need to use an extremely slow lowering phase for an exercise to be effective, maintaining sufficient control during the descent can be beneficial when learning and refining the technique for a step-down.As you increase your grip strength over time, you can progress your dead hangs in a few ways. Just like you could increase the number of reps you perform with a particular load during a dynamic exercise, you naturally can increase the duration in which you perform your dead hang sets. Rather than simply performing progressively longer sets, you can alternatively perform a more intense variation, such as a single arm dead hang. However, switching from a standard dead hang to a one arm dead hang can provide a considerable increase in difficulty that may be a more dramatic change in challenge than you wish. As an intermediate option, you can perform a dead hang with both arms, but you grip lightly with one side as the other side supports more than half of your bodyweight. Over time, you can progressively shift more of your bodyweight into one arm until you feel ready to dead hang with only one arm. You can also perform a weighted dead hang with both hands as you decide specifically how much more challenging you wish to make the movement based on how much weight you add to your pull-up/dip belt. As another way to intensify the standard dead hang, you can additionally add fat grip attachments to the bar and perform a dead hang with both arms, or you can wrap a thick towel around the bar and hang while gripping the towel.Squatting to a box and box squatting appear similar and can share identical setups, but they are different exercises. With a box squat, you sit onto the box and briefly pause before squatting back up. When squatting to a box, you begin squatting up immediately after lightly tapping the box, without any pause between the descent and ascent.

Squatting to a box allows you to use essentially the same back squat technique you would use without a box. In contrast, sitting on the box during a box squat can alter technique because it allows you to partially deload at the bottom. This partial deloading can reduce the stretch reflex contribution at the start of the lifting phase, thereby increasing the difficulty of the ascent if you avoid rocking on the box. As with squatting to a box, box squats can be performed with the same stance, knee angles, and hip angles you would use during a standard back squat. However, box squats also allow you to emphasize sitting back farther if you choose.

Emphasizing sitting back may contribute to a more hip-dominant squat with more vertical shins and less forward knee travel. This can be desirable for lifters who experience knee discomfort when their knees track farther forward during squats. Squatting with a high degree of forward knee travel is not inherently harmful to knee health, but some individuals may have pre-existing issues that make this technique uncomfortable. In those cases, box squats with reduced forward knee travel may feel more tolerable. Sitting back farther during box squats may also benefit people who struggle to sit their hips back sufficiently to optimize performance during back squats. There is a sweet spot for how much the hips should travel backward to achieve the most mechanically advantageous squat, and that position does not always come naturally. Using a variation that allows the back to sit farther back can help teach this aspect of squat technique and potentially improve back squat form.

Both box squats and squats to a box allow you to standardize depth across reps, which can be helpful for refining technique and tracking progress over time. Even when aiming for consistent depth, people may unintentionally shorten a rep’s range of motion as fatigue accumulates, especially during high-intensity sets without an external depth reference. While such reps can still stimulate strength and hypertrophy, they make progress harder to assess. If you perform more reps than in a prior session but do so through a reduced range of motion, it becomes unclear whether performance truly improved. Accurately assessing progress can enhance training decisions by helping you better understand how you respond to different methods. When progress is unclear, you may miss valuable information that could otherwise help you achieve your goals.No study has yet assessed whether hip abduction exercise interventions induce hypertrophy of any particular muscles, so we must rely on other forms of evidence to determine which muscles are most likely to be effectively developed. Naturally, determining which muscles can contribute meaningfully to hip abduction in a seated position is a logical starting point. With only one exception, every muscle that has been studied to increase in size significantly after a resistance training intervention has been found to have meaningful moment arms (which quantify leverage) for the joint actions notably loaded by the exercises in the training interventions. This applies to the calves, quads, hip adductors, gluteus maximus, sartorius, triceps, elbow flexors, lateral deltoid, neck extensors, pec major, trapezius, sternocleidomastoid, and masseter. When accounting for muscles and specific muscle heads that have been studied independently rather than being collectively assessed as a group, more than 30 different muscle groups, muscles, and muscle heads have been found to increase in size significantly after a resistance training intervention. Except for the neck’s scalene muscles (which grew after a neck extension exercise intervention) every single one has evidence of having a meaningful moment arm to contribute to a joint action that was loaded by the exercise intervention prompting the hypertrophy.

The available evidence indicates that the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus have very large hip abduction moment arms in a neutral sagittal hip position (like when you are standing upright), and they undoubtedly function as primary hip abductors in such a position. However, biomechanical modeling research demonstrates that these moment arms steadily decrease as the hip flexes, becoming essentially non-existent near 90 degrees of hip flexion, at which point they have large hip internal rotation moment arms. When the hip abducts from 90 degrees of flexion, motion occurs in the transverse plane (which divides the body into upper and lower halves) rather than the frontal plane (which divides the body into front and back halves), which is where motion occurs when the hip abducts at 0 degrees of hip flexion. As a comparison to this change in a muscle’s leverage with varying joint positions and different planes of motion, we can think about how the lateral deltoid functions at the shoulder. When abducting your shoulder with your arm starting at your side (such as during a lateral raise), your lateral deltoid has excellent leverage to perform this action as you move in the frontal plane. However, if you raise your arms up in front of you to be parallel with the ground and then spread your arms apart to horizontally abduct (AKA horizontally extend) your shoulders, your lateral deltoid has lower leverage to perform this action in the transverse plane. For this reason, you would be better served performing lateral raises rather than machine reverse flies (AKA rear delt flies) if you wish to train your lateral deltoid most effectively. Similarly, the absence of any meaningful hip abduction moment in a rather flexed hip position indicates that hip abduction exercises performed while seated upright are unlikely to train the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus effectively, but other forms of evidence suggest otherwise.

When examining muscle EMG, muscles that function as prime movers during exercise typically exhibit high EMG activity when the exercise is performed at sufficiently high intensity. If a muscle lacks meaningful leverage to contribute to a particular exercise, it would be expected not to exhibit high EMG activity. For instance, the quad’s vastus lateralis does not have any hip joint moment arms, and it exhibits low EMG activity during seated abduction exercise. Similarly, the hamstring’s semitendinosus has a very small hip adduction moment arm, and it exhibits moderately low EMG activity during seated hip adduction exercise in contrast to the adductor longus, which has a very large hip adduction moment arm and exhibits high EMG activity during seated hip adduction exercise.

In contrast to this trend, Brandt et al. (2013) reported the gluteus medius to exhibit high EMG activity during high-intensity seated machine hip abduction exercise (involving 80 degrees of hip flexion) and very high EMG activity during standing banded hip abduction exercise, which was performed with a low angle of hip flexion. Paz et al. (2022) also found the gluteus medius to exhibit moderate EMG activity during a 10-rep set with 10-rep max loads of seated machine hip abduction exercise (involving 90 degrees of hip flexion) and somewhat greater EMG activity that did not significantly differ during side-lying hip abduction with the hip fully extended. Muscles sometimes exhibit meaningful EMG activity during exercises in which they do not function as agonists if their contraction can serve a stabilizing role. However, the external stability that is present when performing the seated machine hip abduction exercise reduces the likelihood that the gluteus medius would be notably engaged in this manner. While not specifically assessing the seated machine variation, Fujisawa et al. (2014) also measured EMG activity of the gluteus medius, upper gluteus maximus, lower gluteus maximus, and tensor fasciae latae during resisted isometric hip abduction performed in 0, 20, 40, 60, or 80 degrees of hip flexion. They found the gluteus medius to exhibit similar EMG activity regardless of position, the tensor fasciae latae to exhibit the greatest activity in 0-20 degrees of hip flexion, and the gluteus maximus to exhibit the greatest activity in 80 degrees of hip flexion. While the lower gluteus maximus exhibited fairly low activity even in the most flexed position, the upper gluteus maximus was markedly affected by the hip angle, as its mean activity exceeded that of any other muscle at 80 degrees of hip flexion. Like biomechanical modeling research that is used to estimate muscle moment arms, EMG research has its limitations, but the aforementioned EMG data is noteworthy nonetheless, as it suggests that the gluteus medius can still be trained by seated hip abduction exercise.

If the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus do not meaningfully contribute to hip abduction in a seated position, hip abduction torque should be significantly less when tested in 90 degrees of hip flexion compared to a neutral hip position, unless other muscles are able to substitute as abductors. When transitioning from 0-90 degrees of hip flexion, the obturator internus and superior gemellus develop meaningful hip abductor moment arms, and the piriformis has even greater leverage to function as an abductor. These deep hip muscles are most likely to be heavily engaged during seated hip abduction exercise, but they are dwarfed by the gluteal muscles. The combined physiological cross-sectional area (a key determinant of how much force a muscle can generate) of the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus is over five times greater than the combined physiological cross-sectional area of the piriformis, obturator internus, and superior gemellus. Besides those three small muscles, no other muscles have been reported to experience notable increases in hip abduction moment arms as the hip flexes, but older biomechanical models potentially may not have accurately estimated the contribution the upper gluteus maximus may have. Although they did not investigate how hip flexion angle may influence abduction moment arms, Blemker and Delp (2005) demonstrated that prior research methods likely failed to capture the hip-abduction contribution of the upper gluteus maximus. Quantitative data is lacking on how much leverage the upper gluteus maximus has to function as a hip abductor in a flexed hip position, but it is plausible that it is able to compensate for reduced abduction contributions from the gluteus medius and minimus in a seated position. Its very large cross-sectional area certainly permits it to have a high force-producing capacity, and its potentially amplified role as an abductor in a flexed hip position could explain the previously reported high EMG activity recorded by Fujisawa et al. (2014).

Hoch et al. (2025) provided further insight into this topic by testing maximal hip abduction torque with the hip in 30 degrees of flexion, neutral, or 30 degrees of hyperextension. To determine the contributions of different muscles, they utilized nerve blocks that selectively paralyzed the gluteus maximus, tensor fasciae latae, or the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus together. With the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus paralyzed, hip abduction torque was reduced by 62-63% in each position. Paralysis of the gluteus maximus decreased hip abduction torque by 44-46% in neutral or hyperextension, while torque declined by 56% in 30 degrees of flexion, indicating a greater relative contribution of the gluteus maximus in a flexed hip position. Most likely due to its small cross-sectional area, paralysis of the tensor fasciae latae reduced abduction torque by only 6-15%, with the greatest reduction occurring in 30 degrees of hip flexion. When examining the reported data, it is worth noting that part of the reduction in hip abduction torque resulting from paralysis of each muscle may be attributable to reduced hip joint stabilization, which may restrict the functional hip abductors from fully contributing. If we simply sum the reductions in hip abduction torque when comparing each of the three paralysis conditions with the pre-nerve block baseline, we obtain a negative value; therefore, the observed torque reductions do not imply that those muscles directly generate those percentages of total torque. Besides directly producing hip abduction torque, contraction of these muscles together can assist in stabilizing the hip in the sagittal and transverse planes, allowing for a more efficient expression of frontal plane torque. As an example, contraction of the gluteus maximus in 30 degrees of hip flexion generates hip external rotation torque, while contraction of the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus generates net internal rotation torque. If the gluteus maximus is inactive, the internal rotation torque from the other gluteal muscles may no longer be sufficiently counteracted to direct as much of the force from their contraction into abduction. Correspondingly, the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus may not be able to reach their level of activation during a hip abduction effort without inadvertently internally rotating the hip with the gluteus maximus completely inactive. While the gluteus maximus may not directly produce half of the total hip abduction torque, the findings from Hoch et al. (2025) nonetheless provide strong evidence that the gluteus maximus makes a notable contribution to abducting the hip.

Questions remain unanswered about how well each of the gluteal muscles will be trained by seated hip abduction exercise, but it is reasonable to predict that the upper gluteus maximus may be able to contribute most of the gluteal muscles. In addition to the aforementioned piriformis, obturator internus, and superior gemellus, the tensor fasciae latae, sartorius, and rectus femoris will likely assist the upper gluteus maximus during seated hip abduction exercise. However, the tensor fasciae latae and sartorius will be working at rather short muscle lengths in the seated position, so their contributions may be lesser compared to if the hip abduction exercise were performed at a lower angle of hip flexion. This may explain why the tensor fasciae latae has been measured to exhibit significantly lower EMG activity during machine seated hip abduction compared to side-lying hip abduction exercise. Because it functions as a knee extensor and hip flexor, the rectus femoris will be working at moderate muscle lengths when seated with flexed knees. Providing evidence of its contribution, Brandt et al. (2013) found the rectus femoris to exhibit moderate EMG activity during seated machine hip abduction. Nonetheless, this muscle will most likely be more effectively trained with hip flexion or single-joint knee extension exercises.The technique instructions do not specify where any particular part of your body should be positioned in relation to the bar at the end of the lifting phase because your full range of motion can differ from someone else’s. Pulling until your chin is above the bar is a common standard used by the United States Marine Corps for testing and by the World Pull-Up Organization for competitions, and you can certainly use this standard if you wish. However, when focusing on chin clearance, some people tend to extend their necks and lift their chins to clear the bar, reducing the range of motion through the pull compared with maintaining a neutral neck position. In this case, focusing on a proxy for the full range of motion may result in a different movement than focusing on the individual’s true full range of motion. When competing or completing a standardized test that affects professional prospects, it is sensible to perform the minimum work required to complete each rep according to the rules, but training to build muscle and strength is a different endeavor.Relative to other exercises that train the same muscle groups, the single-leg bodyweight Romanian deadlift imparts a low level of resistance to the gluteus maximus and other hip extensor muscles. If training with the objective of increasing muscle size and or strength, Romanian deadlifts that use additional load, such as single-leg dumbbell Romanian deadlifts, will likely be more efficient for most able-bodied individuals. Nonetheless, if you are new to single-leg Romanian deadlifts, starting with the bodyweight variation can be quite helpful for learning the appropriate technique before progressing to using weights. Even if your hip and thigh muscles are not meaningfully challenged during this bodyweight exercise, it can still serve as a dynamic exercise that trains balance and hamstring flexibility, and it can be performed for these purposes or as part of a warm-up before higher-intensity exercises.Because the rectus femoris functions as a hip flexor, it can operate at greater muscle lengths in a neutral hip position than in a flexed hip position. No study has yet compared how reverse Nordic curls and machine leg extensions differ in the muscle hypertrophy they may stimulate; however, the available evidence indicates that training the rectus femoris at longer lengths may be particularly effective. For instance, Larsen et al. (2025) reported that the rectus femoris experienced greater growth when machine leg extensions were performed with 40 degrees of hip flexion compared to 90 degrees of hip flexion. It is not practical to keep the hip flexed at approximately 40 degrees on most leg extension machines, whereas reverse Nordic curls can readily be performed with 0 degrees of hip flexion.

The three other quad muscles can also be trained at longer lengths during reverse Nordic curls due to the greater knee flexion angles they allow compared to machine leg extensions. With reverse Nordic curls, you can start the rep near 90 degrees of knee flexion and reach up to your maximal achievable knee flexion angle if you perform the full range of motion. With machine leg extensions, you are often restricted from reaching greater than approximately 90-100 degrees of knee flexion due to the design of the machine, which limits how much you can lengthen each of the quad muscles. Multiple studies have found that training the quads at moderately long peak lengths (involving up to 90-120 degrees of knee flexion) stimulates greater quad growth than training the quads at moderately short peak lengths (involving up to 50-65 degrees of knee flexion). However, data from squat and leg press training interventions conducted by Kubo et al. (2019) and Larsen et al. (2025) suggest that training up to 90-100 degrees of knee flexion may be just as effective in stimulating quad hypertrophy as training into end-range knee flexion or close to it (i.e., 140 degrees of knee flexion or greater). Correspondingly, reverse Nordic curls and machine leg extensions are likely able to grow the three monoarticular quad muscles similarly well despite the differences in knee angles. While reverse Nordic curls have the potential to stimulate somewhat more rectus femoris hypertrophy than machine leg extensions performed when sitting upright, this is speculative rather than clearly proven, so both exercises can be quality options for training each of your quad muscles.The grip used for the dumbbell pullover has been successfully used by many people and helps you hold the dumbbell securely throughout the exercise. However, some people may not feel comfortable holding a weight above their head with this grip and may prefer to perform a pullover with the weight held differently. If you would personally prefer to do so, you can select from this database the plate pullover or barbell pullover (which can be performed with fixed weight bars and EZ curls bars if you wish to use a weight that is lighter than a standard unloaded barbell) to use a different grip to train the same muscles targeted by a dumbbell pullover.In theory, you can perform your partial range of motion shrimp squat with the identical technique regardless of whether you tap your knee to an elevated object or descend to the same depth without tapping any object. However, when an exercise has an ambiguous endpoint for its end range, it can be difficult to use the same exact range of motion during every rep. This can be especially relevant to an exercise that is most difficult at the end range of its rep, such as a shrimp squat, which becomes increasingly more challenging the further you descend. Even when intending to achieve the same depth for every rep, people may have a tendency to shorten a rep’s range of motion as fatigue accumulates during a set with high relative intensity when the end range lacks an external stopping point or is determined by their available joint range of motion. If this unintentionally occurs, the set can still effectively stimulate muscle hypertrophy and strength improvements, but it can be more difficult to track progress over time. If you can perform more reps per set than in the prior session but do so through a reduced range of motion, it is unclear whether your performance actually improved. Accurately assessing your rate of progress can help enhance your training by revealing how you respond to different training methods. When you do not know if you are actually progressing, you can shortchange yourself of valuable information that can otherwise help you accomplish your goals.

In addition to standardizing your range of motion and helping you accurately track your progress, using a fixed endpoint for your shrimp squat can reinforce a technique with a well-controlled descent. If you do not control the descent during your shrimp squat, you will receive immediate tactile feedback that you are doing so by banging your knee onto the object underneath you. While you do not need to use a super slow lowering phase for exercises to be effective, using a sufficiently controlled descent can be beneficial when learning and refining the technique for an exercise such as a shrimp squat.This dumbbell skater squat resembles a counterbalanced skater squat performed with dumbbells; both variations target the same muscle groups but can serve somewhat different roles in a program. The key difference is that the counterbalanced variation involves keeping the arms outstretched parallel to the ground, whereas this dumbbell skater squat uses only a mild degree of shoulder flexion through a small forward arm movement. Holding a light weight in the counterbalanced position requires the lower body muscles to generate greater torque throughout the exercise, but it can also make balance easier compared with performing the movement without any weight. The counterbalance can also allow a slightly more upright torso than would otherwise be possible during the bodyweight variation without causing the body to fall backward. To a lesser degree, the small forward reach used during this dumbbell skater squat can also help with balance compared with keeping the arms directed straight down throughout the rep.

However, the primary reason to perform a dumbbell skater squat with this technique rather than in the counterbalanced position is to enable heavier loads for training the lower body. When holding a weight with the arms outstretched parallel to the ground, the long lever arm creates substantial resistive torque at the shoulder. As a result, the amount of weight that can be used may be limited by fatigue of the shoulder flexor muscles, such as the front deltoid and upper pectoralis major, rather than by lower body strength. If shoulder strength becomes the limiting factor, the lower body muscles receive a suboptimal training stimulus. For individuals who are newer to skater squats or who do not yet have advanced lower body strength, counterbalanced skater squats with light weights may still provide a high-quality stimulus for increasing lower body muscle size and strength. As lower body strength improves over time, however, the dumbbell skater squat performed with the weights held closer to the body may be a more appropriate option for continuing to train the lower body effectively.Even when using chalk, the load you can use or the distance you can complete with a given load will often be limited by grip strength during this loaded carry, so this exercise can be a viable means of training static grip strength. However, if you wish to increase the stimulus provided to muscles besides the forearm and hand muscles, you have the option of performing this carry with lifting straps. With the heavier loads or longer distances that straps typically facilitate, the exercise can be more challenging to your core and upper back muscles as they support your trunk and shoulder girdle in addition to your lower body muscles that stabilize your pelvis and propel you forward.Relative to conventional deadlifts or block pulls with a straight barbell, trap bar deadlifts can allow you greater liberty in how you perform them with respect to biasing more of a hip hinge or squat movement pattern. A conventional deadlift keeps the load in front of you and constrains forward knee travel, while a trap bar deadlift positions the load closer to your center of mass and permits pushing your knees farther forward than you would during a conventional deadlift. Trap bar deadlifting with greater forward knee travel makes the movement more squat-like, whereas maintaining a more vertical shin position results in a more hip-hinge-dominant movement.

Squat and deadlift variations can be analyzed as existing along a “squat-hinge continuum” based on the demands they impart to the hip extensor muscles (e.g., gluteus maximus, hamstrings, adductor magnus) relative to the quads, which extend the knees. Compared with trap bar deadlifting with the shins vertical, the lever arm of the resistance acting on the knees is greater when the knees are positioned farther forward, resulting in a greater ratio of loading imparted to the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. In contrast, the lever arm of the resistance acting on the hips is greater when deadlifting with vertical shins, which prompts you to sit your hips farther back and experience a lower ratio of loading imparted to the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. While people tend to perform trap bar deadlifts with their knees situated farther forward than when they conventional deadlift, you certainly have the ability to trap bar deadlift with the same knee position you would use for conventional deadlifts. Correspondingly, you can trap bar deadlift with whichever technique you prefer in congruence with your training goals.

Based on research assessing participants' self-selected technique for trap bar deadlifting (likely the technique in which they were most efficient at pulling), trap bar deadlifts have been found to be more hip-dominant than knee-dominant. Swinton et al. (2011) specifically assessed powerlifters performing low handle trap bar deadlifts and conventional deadlifts. Both exercises imparted meaningfully greater loading to the hip extensors and lumbar spinal extensors than the quads, but conventional deadlifts were relatively more hip dominant. When lifting 80% of the participants’ conventional deadlift one-rep maxes, the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee was 3.7 for conventional deadlifts and 1.8 for trap bar deadlifts. This difference was primarily attributable to the trap bar deadlift, which produced a peak resistive torque at the knee that was 190% of that in the conventional deadlift. A smaller gap was present for hip extensor loading, as the peak resistive torque acting on the hip for the trap bar deadlift was 92% that of the conventional deadlift. These findings indicate that the degree to which trap bar deadlifts train the quads more than conventional deadlifts most likely exceeds the degree to which conventional deadlifts train the hip extensor muscles more than trap bar deadlifts. In terms of which hip extensor muscles may be less involved during trap bar deadlifts, the biarticular hamstrings in particular will likely receive a lesser stimulus. Camara et al. (2016) and Andersen et al. (2018) both found the hamstring’s biceps femoris to exhibit lower EMG activity during the low handle trap bar deadlift compared to the conventional deadlift, which is congruent with what would be anticipated in light of the mechanical differences between these two exercises. Since the trap bar deadlift imparts greater loading to the knee, the knee flexion torque that results from hamstring contraction will have more of a deleterious effect on performance than during the conventional deadlift. While the hip extension torque that the hamstrings produce is beneficial, their knee flexion torque will oppose the quads as they extend the knee, so the hamstrings are not the most efficient hip extensor muscles for the nervous system to recruit during an exercise that imparts meaningful resistance to the quads. To a lesser extent, this is the same principle that results in the hamstrings experiencing minimal hypertrophy after interventions of squatting, which imparts even greater loading of the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. Accompanying the lower hamstring EMG activity, Camara et al. (2016) found the quad’s vastus lateralis (which was not assessed by Andersen et al.) to exhibit greater activity with the trap bar deadlift compared to the conventional deadlift. In contrast to the difference in hamstring EMG activity, Andersen et al. (2018) also found the gluteus maximus (which was not assessed by Camara et al.) to exhibit similar EMG activity during the two deadlift variations, which makes sense given that the gluteus maximus is able to generate hip extension without simultaneously generating knee flexion torque.Unlike seated wrist curls with a barbell or dumbbells, the standing barbell wrist curl only permits you to train wrist flexion through a partial range of motion. During the standing variation, the wrist can transition from a neutral position to a fully flexed position, in contrast to the seated variations, which allow you to reach a full extended wrist position at the start of each rep. Given the vertical vector of gravitational force, resistive torque is also absent on the wrist when the wrist is in a neutral position during a standing barbell wrist curl, since the weight is situated directly under the wrist in this starting position. With the standing wrist curl, resistive torque peaks at full wrist flexion, when the weight is horizontally farthest from the wrist, resulting in the greatest lever arm of resistance acting on the wrist. Because the wrist flexor muscles are shortened in a flexed wrist position, standing barbell wrist curls predominantly load them at short muscle lengths in contrast to seated wrist curls with free weights, which can train them at longer lengths.

Currently, no study has yet investigated how hypertrophy of the forearm muscles may be influenced by the peak muscle length reached while resistance training. However, research on a variety of other muscle groups has indicated that training muscles at longer peak lengths up to a certain point may promote greater magnitudes of hypertrophy than training at shorter peak lengths. The effect may vary among different muscle groups, and training at very long peak muscle lengths does not necessarily induce greater hypertrophy than training at moderately long peak muscle lengths, as data from Larsen et al. (2025) suggests. However, with one study by Goto et al. (2019) being an exception, the available body of evidence indicates that training at longer peak muscle lengths is likely to promote greater or at least equal magnitudes of hypertrophy relative to training at shorter peak lengths. This does not mean that standing barbell wrist curls are ineffective for training the forearm muscles, but variations (like seated barbell wrist curls) that allow you to load them at longer muscle lengths have the potential to be somewhat more effective to induce increases in the size of these muscles.Two studies by Lockie et al. (2018) compared one-repetition maximums without straps between conventional deadlifts and trap-bar deadlifts with 4 in (10cm) higher handles. Together, they assessed a total of 54 mixed sex participants with at least two years of lifting experience that included conventional and trap bar deadlifts in addition to hitting maxes. Pooling the data from the two studies together, the average high handle trap bar max was 12% greater than the conventional deadlift max, resulting in a 39lb (17.7kg) mean difference. When comparing 1-rep maxes of the conventional deadlift to the low handle trap bar deadlift, Camara et al. (2016) found the maxes to be nearly identical to each other for the two lifts, but Swinton et al. (2011) and Lake et al. (2017) found their participants to lift an average of 6-8% greater loads during the low handle trap bar deadlift.McGill et al. (2009) measured EMG activity of several core and lower body muscles as competitive strongmen performed a variety of movements that appear in strongman competitions, including the farmer’s walk, which was performed for several steps with a 165lb (75kg) weight in each hand. During the farmer’s walk, peak EMG activity was high for the internal abdominal obliques, lats, thoracic erector spinae, lumbar erector spinae, gluteus medius, gluteus maximus, and rectus femoris. The external abdominal obliques and biceps femoris also exhibited moderate peak EMG activity, while the rectus abdominis exhibited low EMG activity. The abdominal muscles and rectus femoris showed peak activity during the walking portion of the carry, whereas the lats, erector spinae, and biceps femoris showed peak activity during the initial lifting phase. The gluteal muscles exhibited peak activity during the initial lifting phase and during the first step of the carry. As indicated by these findings, the farmer’s carry can recruit a large amount of musculature simultaneously, making the exercise metabolically demanding when performed at a high relative intensity, particularly for longer-duration sets. Correspondingly, challenging sets of farmer’s carries may be able to train your cardiovascular system more robustly than most resistance training exercises that localize the workload to a smaller amount of total muscle mass.People sometimes perform a dumbbell exercise that is quite similar to this cable shoulder external rotation exercise in terms of body position and joint motion. While holding a dumbbell with a 90-degree elbow bend and keeping the upper arm by the side, dynamic shoulder external and internal rotation can be performed. However, the dumbbell variation provides no meaningful resistance to shoulder rotation in either direction because the dumbbell's resistance is directed vertically downward, as gravitational force acts on it. The elbow flexor muscles (i.e., biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis) must function isometrically to maintain the 90-degree elbow joint angle when holding the dumbbell, but the dumbbell does not provide any additional resistance to shoulder rotation. In contrast, the horizontally directed line of resistance from this cable variation directly resists rotational shoulder motion and allows for effective training of the shoulder external rotator muscles. You can still utilize a dumbbell to train shoulder external rotation effectively with your upper arm by your side, but instead of standing, you would need to adopt a side-lying position as you lie on your resting shoulder to experience the appropriate line of resistance.Glute bridge, hamstring bridge, and hip thrust variations are similar in that they primarily train hip extension using similar techniques, but they employ different body positions. Glute bridges and hamstring bridges are both performed while lying down with your upper back contacting the ground, but hamstring bridges involve an extended or slightly flexed knee position, while glute bridges are performed with the heel closer to the buttock, resulting in a moderately high to high degree of knee flexion. Like glute bridges, hip thrusts are conventionally performed with a relatively flexed knee, but they differ in that the upper back rests on an elevated surface, such as a bench, which allows greater hip range of motion during the exercise.As you pull the cable toward you, you can consciously think about pinching your shoulder blades back together in an effort to increase potential engagement of the trapezius and rhomboids, since these muscles function to retract (pull back and together) the shoulder blades. While focusing on doing so may have an effect, shoulder blade retraction is naturally coupled with end range shoulder horizontal extension, so your shoulder blade retractor muscles will be active during this exercise even if you do not focus on shoulder blade motion while performing it.The overhand grip machine reverse fly has been found to yield lower mean EMG activity of the rear delt and the rotator cuff’s infraspinatus compared to the neutral grip machine reverse fly. The difference for EMG activity of the rear delt was found to be of a small effect size, while the difference for EMG activity of the infraspinatus was found to be of a moderate effect size. When comparing these two machine reverse fly variations, the mean EMG activity of the lateral deltoid also trended toward being greater with the neutral grip, but the difference was not statistically significant.Compared to the underhand grip band pull, the neutral grip band apart has been found to yield lower mean EMG activity of the lower trapezius and the infraspinatus of the rotator cuff. Additionally, the neutral grip band pull apart has been found to yield lower mean EMG activity of the posterior deltoid than the overhand grip band pull apart.Compared to the underhand grip band pull and the neutral grip band apart, the overhand grip band pull apart has been found to yield greater mean EMG activity of the posterior deltoid. However, the overhand grip band pull apart has also been found to yield lower mean EMG activity of the lower trapezius and the rotator cuff’s infraspinatus than the underhand grip band pull apart.Compared to using a barbell for biceps curls, some people find that performing biceps curls with an EZ bar feels more comfortable on their wrists and/or elbows. This is most likely influenced by an underhand grip with a straight barbell, requiring a high degree of forearm supination range of motion that may approach or even exceed the maximal range of motion for forearm supination before certain individuals experience discomfort when curling. To grasp a bar with a pure underhand grip while keeping the shoulders in a neutral position, 90 degrees of supination range of motion will be needed, which is approximately the end range of the normal supination range of motion. Naturally, the available forearm supination range of motion varies from person to person, so some people reach the end range when supinating less than 90 degrees (which is more likely in males and older individuals). Someone with limited supination may compensate by externally rotating their shoulders to grasp a straight bar with a pure underhand grip. Curling in this manner is not inherently injurious, but it can transmit forces through the elbows that some people find uncomfortable when using a straight bar. In contrast, the EZ bar biceps curl requires only a moderate amount of forearm supination range of motion because it uses a semi-underhand grip rather than a fully underhand grip. This means that almost everybody with remotely close to normal supination range of motion will be able to curl with an EZ bar without reaching their end range of supination motion, which can consequently allow them to keep their shoulders in a position of neutral shoulder rotation. If you find straight bar biceps curls to feel comfortable, there is no reason to avoid them, but if they just do not feel right to you as an individual, curling with an EZ bar can be a high quality alternative.Even experienced lifters who are rather strong can often find fairly light ankle weights to be sufficiently challenging when performing side-lying hip abduction straight leg raises with appropriate technique due to the physics of this exercise. What determines the difficulty for your hip abductor muscles is the magnitude of resistive torque acting on the hip joint in the direction of adduction (i.e., bringing your thigh toward the midline of your body). When you are in the side-lying position with your top leg situated parallel to the ground, the force produced by gravity pulling downward on the ankle weight is multiplied by the distance from the weight to your hip joint to yield the resistive torque, which opposes the hip abductor muscles. When located at your ankle, the weight has a long lever arm, so it can produce meaningful torque at the hip even if the load is light overall.While hammer curls have a reputation for training the brachioradialis more effectively than biceps curls with an underhand grip, multiple studies have found the brachioradialis to exhibit similar EMG activity whether curls are performed with a neutral grip or with an underhand grip. Correspondingly, it is presently unclear whether hammer curls and biceps curls meaningfully differ in their ability to train the brachioradialis.Based on muscle size data reported by Holzbaur et al. (2007), the wrist flexor muscles trained by this exercise constitute approximately half of the total forearm muscle volume. Nearly two-thirds of the total wrist flexor muscle volume is situated within the muscle bellies of the flexor digitorum superficialis and the flexor digitorum profundus, which together have a volume exceeding the volume of the upper arm’s biceps brachii. Besides flexing the wrist, these two flexor digitorum muscles flex the joints of the four long fingers and are the two strongest gripping muscles. Correspondingly, increasing the size and strength of these muscles through wrist curl variations may improve grip strength potential.You can naturally increase the intensity of an inverted row exercise by adding an external load, such as a weighted vest, but you can also adjust the intensity by modifying your body position. One noteworthy factor affecting the difficulty of this exercise is the degree to which your torso is horizontally angled. Compared with the standard positioning described in the “setting up” section, positioning the bar higher will allow you to row with a more upright torso, thereby reducing intensity. On the other hand, situating your heels on an elevated surface (such as a bench, plyometric box, or bumper plate) allows you to row with a more nearly horizontal torso position, thereby increasing the intensity of this exercise. In addition to the angle of torso inclination, your lower body positioning also affects the difficulty of an inverted row. Bending your knees and positioning your feet closer to your torso with your soles flat on the floor will reduce the intensity of your row compared to if you keep your knees straightened and have only the backs of your heels in contact with the floor. By adjusting the position of your torso and/or your lower body, you can tailor the difficulty of inverted rows to you as an individual.Because greater elbow flexion torque can be generated with a supinated forearm position than a pronated forearm position, the dumbbell Zottman curl can function as an eccentric overload variation of the typical reverse curl, where a reverse grip is used for both the concentric and eccentric phases. For the same number of reps, a greater weight can most likely be used for a Zottman curl than a reverse curl due to a Zottman curl facilitating greater elbow flexion torque during its concentric phase using a supinated forearm position.Compared with other exercises, barbell exercises using a power rack may require more time initially to set them up properly, as evidenced by the fairly lengthy “setting up” section. However, subsequent sessions in which you perform the same exercise may require a shorter setup time than the first time you use it. To save time in future sessions, you can record the height you determined to be appropriate for positioning the J-hooks and safety pins. If your power rack has numbered pinholes, you can simply note the positions you used. Without numbered holes, you can note how many holes were above your J-hook position and how many holes were between your safety pin position and your J-hook position. Consequently, you will not need to take a trial-and-error approach when setting up your power rack each session.When performing a cable lateral raise, you can move your shoulders in essentially the same manner as you would during a dumbbell lateral raise. Correspondingly, these exercises train the same muscle groups and have been shown to elicit similar EMG activity in the lateral deltoid, but their resistance profiles differ. With a dumbbell lateral raise, the shoulder does not experience any resistive torque in a neutral frontal plane position (when your arm is at your side), and the resistive torque steadily increases as the shoulder abducts up to 90 degrees. This occurs because the resistance is provided by a vertically directed gravitational force acting on the dumbbell, and the perpendicular distance between the shoulder joint and the dumbbell in the frontal plane progressively increases as the arm becomes closer to being parallel with the ground.

With a cable lateral raise, resistive torque acts on the shoulder throughout the entire rep, because it is absent only when the arm is completely parallel to the cable, which never occurs during a cable lateral raise. Resistive torque in the frontal plane peaks at the position where the angle between the cable and arm reaches 90 degrees, typically in the middle range of a cable lateral raise when the cable is at its lowest setting. At the start of each rep, where the shoulder abductor muscles are at their longest lengths, these muscles will correspondingly experience meaningful (albeit submaximal when the cable is positioned to its lowest setting) tension during a cable lateral raise and minimal tension during a dumbbell lateral raise.

While exceptions exist, the available evidence indicates that training a variety of muscles at longer peak lengths, up to a certain point, is likely to promote greater hypertrophy than training at shorter peak lengths, using the same equipment. Consequently, it is rational to hypothesize that selecting a modality that maximizes resistance at the portion of an exercise where the working muscles operate at their longest lengths may induce greater hypertrophy than selecting a modality that minimizes resistance at this point. If this occurs, a cable lateral raise should be able to induce greater growth of the lateral delt than a dumbbell lateral raise. Larsen et al. (2024) investigated this matter by assigning 24 well-trained participants (with an average of seven years of lifting experience) to an eight-week intervention in which one shoulder was trained with a unilateral cable lateral raise and the other with a dumbbell lateral raise. Twice per week, they performed 5 sets of 12-16 reps until failure through the same 90-degree range of motion for each side while refraining from any other shoulder exercises that would meaningfully train the lateral delt. Following the intervention, the thickness of the lateral delt increased similarly between groups, indicating that lateral raises are likely to train this muscle just as well whether cables or dumbbells are utilized. New evidence may potentially emerge in the future suggesting otherwise, but it is quite reasonable to use whichever lateral raise variation you personally prefer in light of the data supporting their similar effectiveness for lateral delt development.Based on muscle size data reported by Holzbaur et al. (2007), the wrist extensor muscles trained by this exercise constitute approximately one fifth of total forearm muscle volume. These muscles are noticeably smaller than the wrist flexor muscles on the opposite side of the forearm, which constitute approximately half of total forearm muscle volume. Primarily due to this discrepancy in muscle size, wrist extension strength is typically lower than wrist flexion strength; therefore, it is common to use lighter loads for wrist extension exercises than for wrist flexion exercises.Because both types of exercises involve resisted shoulder horizontal extension, the muscles trained by reverse fly variations (including band pull-aparts) will also be trained by face pulls. However, face pulls most likely train the rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor to a comparatively greater degree. Like the posterior deltoid, these two rotator cuff muscles have meaningful shoulder horizontal extension moment arms, but they also have the two greatest shoulder external rotation moment arms of any muscles in the body (regardless of shoulder position) and therefore function as primary shoulder external rotators. In contrast, the posterior deltoid does not exhibit a meaningful shoulder external rotation moment arm. Correspondingly, the inclusion of resisted shoulder external rotation with a face pull will likely bias training of the infraspinatus and teres minor relative to the posterior deltoid when comparing this exercise to reverse fly variations.During a side bend, you are predominantly training the core muscles opposite to the side in which you are holding the weight. When holding a weight with your right side, the resistive torque will bend your trunk toward your right side if unopposed by your muscles. The core muscles on your left side can bend your trunk toward your left when they contract. They lengthen and slow down the descent as you bend your trunk toward your right side during the lowering phase. After doing so, these muscles on your left side shorten as they bring your trunk toward your left side into an upright position during the lifting phase of a side bend.

The core muscles on your right side work in the opposite direction, bending your trunk toward the right, and are consequently trained when holding a weight on your left side. People sometimes perform this exercise while holding a weight in each hand, which counteracts the resistance to trunk side bending that can be experienced when holding the weight on only one side. Due to the weights opposing each other in the side-to-side plane of motion (i.e., frontal plane), holding the same weight on each side of the body is correspondingly not an effective means of training trunk side bending.While reverse curls are often cited as training the brachialis more effectively than biceps curls with an underhand grip, evidence supporting this claim is lacking. The limited research that has measured EMG activity of the brachialis during resisted elbow flexion with different grips has not found the pronated grip to elicit greater brachialis EMG activity than a supinated or neutral grip. The biceps have been found to exhibit lower EMG activity when curling with a pronated grip than with a supinated grip, whereas a corresponding increase in brachialis EMG activity with a pronated grip has not yet been measured.When using techniques involving the same upper body motions (which would be facilitated by using similar grips), the back muscles most effectively trained by bent over row variations can be the same back muscles most effectively trained by chest-supported rows. However, bent over rows require additional isometric engagement of the lumbar and thoracic spinal extensor muscles to maintain the trunk position throughout the set.

For most people, these trunk extensor muscles will likely be working submaximally throughout a set of bent over rows and are unlikely to be trained to the extent that they meaningfully fatigue and limit set performance, in contrast to the back muscles functioning to row the weight dynamically. How much of a stimulus bent over rows can provide the trunk extensor muscles will likely vary among different individuals based on their relative strength ratios. For instance, lifters who consistently train deadlifts and back squats but do not regularly perform rows or vertical pulls (e.g., lat pulldown or pull-up variations) may have isometric trunk extension strength that is comparatively much higher than their upper body pulling strength. Their primary shoulder extensor muscles (i.e., lat, teres major, and rear delt) may be challenged considerably more and fatigue well before their trunk extensor muscles during a set of a bent over rows, resulting in their trunk extensor muscles being trained not nearly as well as their upper body pulling muscles. In comparison to those lifters, people who regularly perform upper body pulling exercises but neglect exercises that impart high trunk extensor demands (which may be the case for some individuals who have focused on calisthenics or avoid hip hinge and squat variations) can exhibit a lower ratio of trunk extension strength to upper body pulling strength. During a set of bent over rows, their trunk extensor muscles may be challenged relatively more and correspondingly be trained more effectively in comparison to lifters who have greater trunk extension strength in proportion to their upper body pulling strength.

While recognizing that the degree to which bent over rows train the trunk extensor muscles can vary from person to person, it is nonetheless worth reiterating that the trunk extensor muscles do not function as the prime movers but rather serve a stabilizing role during bent over rows. Consequently, bent over row variations can contribute to the total workload for your trunk extensor muscles, but these muscles are likely to be better trained by other exercises during which trunk extensor strength directly functions as the limiting factor in set performance.If you have a difficult time limiting extraneous movement of your upper arm while performing this exercise, you can pin a cylindrical object (e.g., rolled up hand towel, water bottle) between your upper arm and your torso as a tactile cue during this exercise. Doing so can help you use a technique that strictly involves shoulder rotation, as indicated by your forearm remaining parallel to the ground throughout your set.The gluteus maximus can be effectively trained with a variety of commonly performed exercise groups that resist hip extension, including a host of different squat, lunge, step up, hip hinge (e.g., deadlift, good morning, pull through), glute bridge, and hip thrust variations. To variable degrees, the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus will be engaged during those types of movements, particularly during single leg exercises. However, these two gluteal muscles typically function to maintain a stable hip position (especially in the frontal plane) during the aforementioned exercises, rather than as prime movers. Along with the tensor fascia latae, the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus primarily function as hip abductors when the hip is in an extended or minimally flexed position. Correspondingly, they are most likely to be effectively trained by exercises limited by hip abduction strength rather than by exercises limited by hip extension strength, which are better suited to training the gluteus maximus.

While no study has yet measured changes in the size of any gluteal muscles after an intervention involving only hip abduction exercise, Plotkin et al. (2023) measured changes in the size of each glute after participants trained with deep back squats or hip thrusts for nine weeks. After the intervention, the two training groups significantly increased gluteus maximus size by similar amounts, whereas gluteus medius and gluteus minimus essentially remained the same size on average. These results are consistent with predictions based on the anatomy of the three gluteal muscles. With a single joint hip abduction exercise (like from this exercise page), the gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia latae have excellent leverage to function as the prime movers that work directly against the resistance provided by the exercise. The strength of these muscles will, in turn, be the limiting factor in set performance; therefore, the primary hip abductor muscles can receive a greater stimulus to increase their size and strength than hip extension exercises.With the obvious exception of sitting as opposed to standing, seated dumbbell curls may be performed with a technique that is essentially identical to that of standing dumbbell curls. However, some people may be naturally inclined to perform standing dumbbell curls with a relatively loose technique that uses the lower body to generate momentum, thereby facilitating dumbbell movement. Using momentum from other muscle groups to assist the elbow flexor muscles in moving the dumbbells will not necessarily reduce the hypertrophy stimulus provided to the elbow flexor muscles relative to a stricter technique. Such an outcome was demonstrated by Augustin et al. (2025), who found curling with a “cheating” technique or a strict technique induced similar increases in biceps thickness. Nonetheless, if you happen to use a somewhat looser technique when curling while standing compared to sitting, it is worth recognizing that a difference in technique will affect how much weight you can use or reps you can perform with each variation.When performing a bridge or hip thrust variation, you can modify your knee angle based on how far out in front of you you position your feet. Because the hamstrings function both as hip extensors and knee flexors, the knee position can affect the degree to which the hamstrings can be trained by a resisted hip extension exercise like a bridge. The hamstrings operate at short lengths when the hip is near an extended position with the knee in a rather flexed position, like during the end of the lifting phase of a glute bridge or hip thrust. When shortened to this extent, biarticular (crossing two joints) muscles, such as the hamstrings, are in a relatively disadvantaged position that can limit force production and reduce their contribution to an exercise.

While hip extension is the primary joint action, bridging with a rather flexed knee position or hip thrusting also requires the knee to extend during the lifting phase. Contraction of the biarticular hamstrings facilitates hip extension, but their activation also generates knee flexion torque that is not conducive to bridging with a meaningfully flexed knee. Consequently, the gluteus maximus may be preferentially recruited over the hamstrings to function as a hip extensor during bridge variations or hip thrusts performed with a notable degree of knee flexion.

Norikazu and Masaaki (2018) investigated how EMG activity of three biarticular hamstrings and the gluteus maximus may be affected by performing unilateral and bilateral bridges with the knee flexed to 30, 60, 90, or 120 degrees. The gluteus maximus exhibited similar EMG activity regardless of the knee position, but EMG activity for each of the biarticular hamstrings was considerably affected by the knee angle. The biceps femoris long head, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus all exhibited their highest EMG activity when bridging with 30 degrees of knee flexion, and their activity progressively decreased with each increment of greater knee flexion. A longitudinal training study comparing hypertrophy induced by hamstring bridges (with minimal knee flexion) and glute bridges (with substantial knee flexion) has not yet been conducted. However, Plotkin et al. (2023) assessed changes in the cross-sectional area of the hamstrings, quad, and gluteus maximus after untrained participants completed a nine-week intervention of barbell hip thrusts (which use a rather flexed knee angle like glute bridges but have the upper back elevated) or barbell back squats. While both exercises resulted in meaningful gluteus maximus hypertrophy, the cross-sectional area of the hamstrings did not significantly increase after hip thrusts or back squats. Squats resulted in greater quad growth than hip thrusts, but the quads still exhibited greater average growth than the hamstrings after the hip thrust intervention. While hip thrusts are not identical to glute bridges, these findings, alongside the aforementioned EMG research and mechanistic reasoning, suggest that bridge variations performed with a meaningfully flexed knee position are unlikely to train the hamstrings effectively. Alternatively, bridges performed with an extended or minimally flexed knee (i.e., hamstring bridges) are more promising variations for training the biarticular hamstrings.Compared to the overhand grip band pull and the neutral grip band apart, the underhand grip band pull apart has been found to yield greater mean EMG activity of the lower trapezius and the rotator cuff’s infraspinatus. However, the underhand grip band pull apart has also been found to yield lower mean EMG activity of the posterior deltoid than the overhand grip band pull apart.It is by no means rare for people to perform exercises with a bar on their back without setting up safety pins, but using safety pins when appropriate is strongly recommended. Positioning safety pins for an exercise such as the barbell good morning is the equivalent of wearing a seat belt when driving. In either situation, you may not actually intend to have to use them, but both safety mechanisms can reduce the risk of injury if an accident occurs. While you can try to refrain from performing a set of barbell good mornings with a load or number of reps that is beyond your capacity, sometimes lifters can overestimate what is left in the tank and unintentionally fail a rep. Furthermore, a non-zero risk of injury exists during any reasonably challenging physical activity, even when using high-quality technique and an intelligently designed program that utilizes appropriate volumes, intensities, and rates of progression. For instance, the likelihood may be very low on a per rep basis, but it is certainly possible to sustain a sudden strain injury to a hamstring or a spinal extensor muscle during the descent of a good morning. If such an acute injury occurs, you can simply set the bar down onto the safety pins when the safety pins are positioned appropriately. However, if you did not previously set up the safety pins, you face a risk of sustaining further injury if an unforeseen injury acutely occurs while your torso is in an inclined position with a bar on your back. That unfortunate predicament can easily be avoided when investing a moment of time into just positioning the safety pins beforehand. As demonstrated by competitive powerlifters, weightlifters, and bodybuilders having lower rates of reported injuries than athletes in many other sports, strenuous resistance training is not uniquely dangerous, and there is no need to be fearful about lifting weights. Nonetheless, the potential for an injury to occur acutely while training can never be eliminated, and it is prudent to take the simple precautionary actions that promote your safety in the weight room.Sensations associated with training a muscle effectively (e.g., sensations of high tension, a burn, and/or a pump in a muscle) during a particular exercise are also not a precise means of determining how well an exercise trains a muscle. However, if you prioritize training your back muscles and do not feel that your back muscles are working as prominently as you believe they should during your lat pulldown, you can cue to pull through your back. Specifically, you can consciously focus on lowering your elbows toward your sides as a cue while performing the exercise, rather than on pulling the cable attachment you are gripping. To pull the cable downward, your elbows and shoulders will operate in a coordinated manner. Your back muscles act on your shoulders to pull the upper arms downward, and your arm muscles (i.e., biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis) act on your elbows to pull your forearms toward your upper arms. The elbows are pulled down toward the sides through shoulder adduction and/or extension performed by the back muscles, such as the lat and teres major, so consciously focusing on this aspect of the movement rather than simply thinking about pulling the cable downward may facilitate engagement of these back muscles.

Compared with receiving only basic lat pulldown technique instructions, Snyder and Leech (2009) found that untrained women demonstrated significantly greater EMG activity in the lat during a lat pulldown after receiving cuing intended to emphasize back muscle engagement. The researchers also found that the mean EMG activity of the biceps was similar across cuing conditions, whereas the mean EMG activity of the teres major was slightly higher with cuing; however, the difference relative to the no-cuing condition was not statistically significant. It is worth noting that Snyder and Leech (2009) specifically studied very low intensity lat pulldowns that were performed for 3-rep sets with loads corresponding to 30% of the participants’ max, and greater EMG activity does not necessarily mean greater muscle hypertrophy will occur after long-term training. Consequently, using certain cues may influence muscle activity during resistance training, but it is unclear how much effect cuing has on the effectiveness of muscle training during challenging sets. It remains unclear whether differences in muscle activity induced by cuing would be meaningful enough to influence long-term muscle growth. Regardless of cueing, the back and arm muscles work together during pulling movements, but different cues may alter the emphasis on how well certain muscles are trained.People sometimes perform a dumbbell exercise that is quite similar to this cable shoulder internal rotation exercise in terms of body position and joint motion. While holding a dumbbell with a 90-degree elbow bend and keeping the upper arm by the side, dynamic shoulder external and internal rotation can be performed. However, the dumbbell variation provides no meaningful resistance to shoulder rotation in either direction because the dumbbell's resistance is directed vertically downward, as gravitational force acts on it. The elbow flexor muscles must function isometrically to maintain a 90-degree elbow joint angle while holding the dumbbell, but the dumbbell provides no additional resistance to shoulder rotation. In contrast, the horizontally oriented line of resistance from this cable variation directly resists rotational shoulder motion and enables effective training of the shoulder internal rotator muscles.Compared to the standing dumbbell biceps curl (specifically using a technique involving dynamic forearm supination during the lifting phase and a return to a neutral forearm position during the lowering phase), greater EMG activity of the brachioradialis and the biceps has been measured throughout the range of motion of an EZ curl bar biceps curl, which was not significantly from a standing barbell biceps curl.Even experienced lifters who are rather strong can often find fairly light ankle weights to be sufficiently challenging when performing hip flexion straight leg raises with appropriate technique due to the physics of this exercise. What determines the difficulty of your hip flexor muscles is the magnitude of resistive torque acting on the hip joint in the direction of extension (i.e., bringing your thigh toward the backside of your body). When you are lying on your back with your knee straightened, the force produced by gravity pulling downward on the ankle weight is multiplied by the distance from the weight to your hip joint to yield the resistive torque, which opposes the hip flexor muscles. When located at your ankle, the weight has a long lever arm, so it can produce meaningful torque at the hip even if the load is light overall.During the lifting phase of a row, your traps and rhomboids will function to retract (pull backward and together) your scapulae (shoulder blades). Because scapular motion is naturally coupled with motion of the shoulder’s glenohumeral joint, your traps and rhomboids will be trained while rowing, regardless of whether you focus on any particular scapular motion. However, you may be able to amplify the stimulus that the rows provide to these muscles through two approaches; the first of which was included in the technique described above. Allowing your scapulae to fully protract (move forward and apart from each other) at the end of the lowering phase of a row will subject the traps and rhomboids to tension in a more lengthened position compared to if you only returned your scapulae to their resting position (in between full protraction and retraction). Currently, no study has yet investigated how hypertrophy of the traps or rhomboids may be influenced by the peak muscle length reached while resistance training. However, research on other muscle groups indicates that training at longer peak lengths, up to a certain point, may promote greater hypertrophy than training at shorter peak lengths. The effect may vary among different muscle groups, and training at very long peak muscle lengths does not necessarily induce greater hypertrophy than training at moderately long peak muscle lengths, as data from Larsen et al. (2025) suggests. However, with the exception of one study by Goto et al. (2019), the available evidence indicates that training at longer peak muscle lengths is likely to promote greater, or at least equal, hypertrophy relative to training at shorter peak lengths. Even if the effect of training at longer muscle lengths is uncertain for the traps and rhomboids, it is sensible to use a technique that may provide additional benefits if it does not impose any cost.

It is worth noting that scapular protraction is naturally coupled with flexion of the thoracic spine, while scapular retraction is naturally coupled with extension of the thoracic spine. With a row variation that has external stabilization provided to the trunk, such as a chest-supported machine row, trunk flexion can be limited, but the trunk can be free to flex during free weight row variations, such as a bent over barbell row. Consequently, full scapular protraction can readily be reached with minimal trunk flexion during a chest-supported row, but it can be very difficult for most people to protract their scapulae fully during a bent over barbell row without also exhibiting noticeable trunk flexion. As a result, intentionally moving into full scapular protraction at the end of a bent over barbell row’s lowering phase changes its technique elsewhere in the body to a greater degree than with a chest-supported row. Performing a bent over barbell row with notable trunk flexion as a consequence of reaching full scapular protraction is not necessarily problematic, but it deviates enough from the standard technique for this exercise to be its own variation in contrast to how full scapular protraction can be incorporated into a chest-supported row without meaningfully affecting spinal motion.

Along with emphasizing the achievement of full scapular protraction at the end of each rep or this row, you can likely further amplify the stimulus provided to your traps and rhomboids by immediately following up a set of rows with pure scapular retraction reps. When a row can no longer be performed due to fatigue, the strength of other back muscles, rather than the trapezius and rhomboids, may be the limiting factor. If your scapular retractor muscles are not the limiting factor during a multi-joint row, you can perform isolated scapular retraction reps directly after a set of rows to ensure that these muscles are further stimulated. To do so, you would keep your elbows extended after your last rep of standard rows and alternate between fully retracting and fully protracting your scapulae. Performing multiple reps of these pure scapular retraction after a set of rows to fatigue suggests that the rows may have initially trained other back muscles more effectively than your traps and rhomboids, but the pure scapular retraction can readily rectify that discrepancy in a time-efficient manner.You can naturally increase the intensity of an inverted row exercise by adding an external load, such as a weighted vest, but you can also adjust the intensity by modifying your body position. One noteworthy factor affecting the difficulty of this exercise is the degree to which your torso is horizontally angled. Compared to the standard positioning described in the “setting up” section, you can reduce the intensity by rowing with a more upright torso. To do so, you could initially grasp the handles while standing and walk backward to angle the straps backward (rather than being directed straight down) before performing the exercise with otherwise the same technique. If the inverted row is sufficiently challenging to you with a moderately upright torso position when you start performing this exercise, you can gradually progress its intensity over time as you gain strength by incrementally situating your feet farther forward to change your torso’s angle of inclination. To increase the intensity of your inverted row beyond the standard variation, you can also modify your torso position by placing your heels on an elevated surface, such as a bench, plyometric box, or bumper plate. Along with your angle of torso inclination, your lower body positioning also affects the difficulty of an inverted row. To reduce the intensity of your row, keep your knees fully extended and have only the backs of your heels in contact with the floor, then bend your knees and position your feet closer to your torso with your soles flat on the floor. By adjusting the position of your torso and/or your lower body, you can tailor the difficulty of inverted rows to you as an individual.Compared to the standard biceps curl, where you use an underhand grip, you will most likely not be able to perform as many reps with the same weight when performing the reverse curl, since your forearm position influences how much elbow flexion torque can be generated. Relative to using an underhand grip or a neutral grip, less elbow flexion torque can be generated with an overhand grip, likely in part due to the biceps having poorer leverage to flex the elbow with an overhand grip. Multiple studies have also found that the biceps exhibit lower EMG activity during curling (or resisted isometric elbow flexion) with an overhand grip than with an underhand grip, suggesting that overhand grip curls may not be the optimal means of developing the biceps.Even experienced lifters who are rather strong can often find a fairly light band to be sufficiently challenging when performing side-lying hip abduction straight leg raises with the band situated near the ankles due to the physics of this exercise. What determines the difficulty for your hip abductor muscles is the magnitude of resistive torque acting on the hip joint in the direction of adduction (i.e., bringing your thigh toward the midline of your body). When you are in the side-lying position with your top leg situated parallel to the ground, the force produced by the band pulling downward is multiplied by the distance from the band to your hip joint to yield the resistive torque, which opposes the hip abductor muscles. When situated near your ankle, the band has a very long lever arm to produce torque at the hip, so a meaningful magnitude of torque can be generated even if you are using a fairly low tension band.

In light of how the position of the band influences the torque your hip abductor muscles must resist, you can modify the position of the band as a form of progression as you increase your strength over time. For instance, a particular band may initially be appropriately challenging for the rep range you wish to perform for this exercise when you situate your band just above your knees. Over multiple training sessions, you can position the band lower along your legs to increase resistance, much like increasing the weight you use during free-weight or machine exercises. Once you have reached near ankle level with the band and wish to progress the resistance further, you can switch to a higher tension band. If the higher tension band provides more resistance than you want when positioned near your ankles, you can situate that band higher up on your legs to a position that results in mildly greater resistance than the lighter band near your ankles before incrementally lowering that higher tension band as you continue increasing your hip abduction strength. This dual-factor progression model, where you adjust band placement and tension, enables a gradual increase in resistance rather than the abrupt increase that can result from increasing band tension without adjusting band placement.As you spread your arms apart, you can consciously think about pinching your shoulder blades back together in an effort to increase potential engagement of the trapezius and rhomboids, since these muscles function to retract (pull back and together) the shoulder blades. While focusing on doing so may have an effect, shoulder blade retraction is naturally coupled with end range shoulder horizontal extension, so your shoulder blade retractor muscles will be active during this exercise even if you do not focus on shoulder blade motion while performing it. Some lifters will alternatively restrict shoulder blade movement during this exercise, with the expressed intent of enhancing the effectiveness of training the rear delts. This approach could involve keeping the shoulder blades in a protracted (pushed forward and spread apart) position throughout the set while only moving through a range that could be performed without shoulder blade motion, which would limit how far the arms spread apart. Such a technique would likely reduce the effectiveness with which the traps and rhomboids are trained by this exercise, but it is unclear whether the rear delts would be trained any more effectively. When multiple muscles can contribute to a particular movement, research on other types of exercise (such as calf raises and hamstring curls) indicates that disadvantaging certain contributing muscles does not necessarily increase the stimulus to the other working muscles. However, it remains unclear whether a similar relationship would occur in the context of reverse flies.The foot-elevated single leg bodyweight hamstring bridge is a straightforward progression from the feet-elevated double leg bodyweight hamstring bridge. Unsurprisingly, EMG activity of the hamstrings can be notably greater when performing a single leg hamstring bridge compared to the double leg hamstring bridge as demonstrated by Norikazu and Masaaki (2018). The foot-elevated single hamstring bridge may be preferable to the single-leg hamstring bridge performed with the heel on the ground, as it trains the hamstrings through longer muscle lengths and a greater range of motion. When the knee is held in a mildly flexed position without elevating the heel, the ground permits only a mild degree of hip flexion, and hip range of motion will be constrained to a rather low amplitude. When elevating the heel, greater hip flexion can be achieved at the start of each rep, allowing you to train the biarticular hamstrings (which cross the hip and knee joints) at longer peak lengths, which may facilitate greater hamstring hypertrophy relative to training at shorter peak lengths.When performed as a maximal isometric action against manual resistance, the prone Y raise has demonstrated high EMG activity in all three regions of the trapezius (particularly the lower region) and in the rhomboids. Moderately high EMG activity of the serratus anterior and levator scapulae has also been measured to occur during this exercise. The levator scapulae functions as an elevator and downward rotator of the scapula, so its high EMG activity would not necessarily be expected during this exercise, which resists upward rotation of the scapula.While performing a single arm dead hang, your torso may have a tendency to start rotating to one side. This rotation will not detract from the grip training stimulus the exercise provides, but if you intentionally keep your chest facing the same direction as when you start the exercise, you may be able to resist the rotation and maintain a static body position. Alternatively, you may be able to use your free hand to help stabilize yourself and limit any inadvertent rotation. To do so, you could place your hand on a nearby surface, such as a beam from a power rack or a wall, and use that hand to counteract any rotation of your torso. This approach would not be viable if you do not have an elevated surface nearby. However, you can use a similar technique by contacting with one leg a box, step, or exercise bench that you place underneath you. When using either your free hand or a leg to limit torso rotation, press horizontally with that limb to stabilize yourself, rather than pressing downward into the nearby surface to support your bodyweight, which would reduce the intensity of the hang.This variation combines asymmetrical weight forearm pronation and asymmetrical weight forearm supination exercises into one, which can readily be performed in part because the two exercises share the same starting position. As you transition from the neutral forearm position (with your palm facing in toward your opposite side) to your palm facing down, you are eccentrically training your supinators as your forearm pronates. When moving from the fully pronated position (palm down) to the neutral position, you are performing concentric supination. As you transition from the neutral forearm position to your palm facing up, you are eccentrically training your pronators as your forearm supinates. When moving from the fully supinated position (palm up) to the neutral position, you are performing concentric pronation.
When combining two movements that train different muscle groups together into one exercise, it is worth noting that whichever muscle group is most fatigued by the time you finish the set will likely be the muscle group that receives the greatest stimulus. Depending on which movements are paired together, you can risk one of the movements being considerably underloaded to the extent that it provides a magnitude of stimulus that is simply insufficient to promote increases in strength or muscle size. For instance, if you combine a dumbbell squat and dumbbell biceps curl into one exercise where you perform a squatting movement followed by a curling movement, you would be noticeably limited by your curling strength in all likelihood. People can normally squat markedly more weight than they curl, and the lower body muscles would not experience remotely close to the same stimulus that they could receive from a squat using loads that are not constrained by curling strength.
However, average forearm pronation and supination strength have been found to be fairly similar in the general population. When pooling the results together from nine studies that measured both pronation and supination strength, Kotte et al. (2018) found average pronation strength to be 93% of supination strength for the dominant arm and 98% of supination strength for the non-dominant arm among males. Females exhibited a mildly greater discrepancy, as their average pronation strength was 90% of their supination strength for the dominant arm and 89% of supination strength for the non-dominant arm. Correspondingly, forearm pronation strength will more likely serve as the limiting factor than supination strength during an exercise training both functions, but the difference may be fairly small. Compared with the general population, the discrepancy between supination and pronation strength is likely to be greater among lifters who commonly perform curling, vertical pulling (e.g., lat pulldown, pull-up), or rowing variations, since these exercises can strengthen the biceps. Except for the brachioradialis, which can contribute to both pronation and supination and is effectively trained along with the biceps by exercises that resist elbow flexion, the other muscles that function as forearm pronators or supinators are unlikely to be meaningfully trained by the exercises most commonly performed. Compared to the general population, lifters will consequently likely have proportionally greater forearm supination strength relative to pronation strength. If your pronation strength limits the performance of this combined exercise before you feel your supinators are meaningfully fatigued, you simply can finish the set by performing reps through the half of the movement that resists supination (i.e., alternating between the neutral start position and the palm down position). With that approach, you can ensure that both your supinators and your pronators are effectively trained by the time you finish the exercise.While it serves as a dynamic stretching exercise, the absence of external loads (e.g., dumbbells, kettlebells, barbells) precludes bodyweight Jefferson curls from meaningfully increasing strength and muscle size in able-bodied individuals. Nonetheless, trying out bodyweight Jefferson curls before loaded Jefferson curls can provide insight into how your back feels with this type of movement before considering whether higher intensity variations are appropriate for you at this time. Based on personal factors such as prior injuries, certain individuals may find the Jefferson curl movement to be quite uncomfortable with bodyweight alone, so it is sensible to find out how you, as an individual, feel while performing the bodyweight version before moving on to any loaded variations.Low-to-high and high-to-low overhead triceps extensions both allow you to train your triceps from a greater angle of shoulder flexion than triceps pushdowns, which involve a close to neutral shoulder position. However, low-to-high variations position you in a higher angle of shoulder flexion and consequently lengthen the long head of the triceps to a greater degree than high-to-low variations.If you notice that your weight contacts the ground before you have reached what is your body’s full available range of motion, you can perform the Jefferson curl while standing on top of a sturdy elevated surface, such as an aerobic step or bumper plate, to facilitate a greater range of motion than could be reached on the ground. Alternatively, you may be able to select smaller diameter weight plates to extend the range of motion if you initially used larger diameter weight plates that restrict how far you can descend.You can use one of three arm positions for wrist roller wrist extension, each described on its own exercise page as a variation in this database. In addition to holding your arms out in front of you with straight elbows (which flexes the shoulders by about 90 degrees), you can keep your upper arms by your sides with your elbows bent by about 90 degrees, or you can keep your arms just in front of you, directed toward the ground with straight elbows. Keeping your arms outstretched parallel to the floor requires your shoulder flexor muscles (e.g., front delt, upper pec major) to act isometrically (statically) to prevent your arms from lowering. Similarly, keeping your elbows bent requires your elbow flexor muscles (e.g., biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis) to act isometrically to prevent your elbows from straightening during wrist roller wrist extension. In contrast to the other two positions, minimal resistance will be applied to your shoulder or elbow flexor muscles when keeping your arms directed down toward the ground, due to the weight's position relative to your elbows and shoulders.
When performing one of the two variations with the weight held farther in front of you, you train more muscles than when you keep the weight close to you, which has potential advantages and disadvantages. If your shoulder or elbow flexor muscles must act isometrically to maintain your arm position, they will also receive a stimulus to increase in size and strength, along with the wrist flexor muscles that lift the weight. However, we lack data to conclude with any degree of confidence how effective isometric mid-range exercise is for developing the shoulder or elbow flexor muscles compared with dynamic full range of motion exercise training these muscle groups. A study from Rasch and Morehouse (1957) found that a six-week intervention of dynamic curls yielded greater increases in upper arm circumference than an intervention of isometric mid-range curls. While arm circumference may increase from increased elbow flexor muscle thickness, circumference measurements are not necessarily the same as muscle size, so these findings should be taken with a grain (or shaker) of salt, particularly in light of the study being published more than a decade before a man landed on the moon.
Isometric training targeting the shoulder or elbow flexor muscles from holding the wrist roller out in front of you may come at the expense of wrist extensor training. If your sets are limited by the ability of your shoulder or elbow flexor muscles to maintain arm position rather than by your wrist extensor muscles' ability to dynamically lift the weight, your wrist extensor muscles will receive a suboptimal stimulus compared with the case where their strength is the limiting factor. On the other hand, if your wrist extensor muscles still serve as the limiting factor in set performance when keeping your elbows bent or arms outstretched, the additional isometric stimulus provided to the elbow or shoulder flexor muscles should not be accompanied by an impaired stimulus to the wrist flexor muscles.
Of the different variations, the likelihood of your wrist extensor muscles not functioning as the limiting factor (and therefore receiving the least stimulus) will likely be greatest when keeping your arms parallel to the floor with straight elbows. In this position, the weight has a dramatically longer lever arm acting on the shoulder than the wrist, and the lever arm acting on the shoulder is much longer than the lever arm (via the length of your upper arm) acting on the elbow when performing the bent elbow variation. Consequently, the shoulder flexor muscles will have to generate considerably greater torque to maintain the outstretched arm position than the elbow flexor muscles will have to generate to maintain the bent elbow position. However, data from Bober et al. (2002) indicate that average maximal shoulder flexion torque at 90 degrees of shoulder flexion is mildly less than average maximal elbow flexion torque at 90 degrees of elbow flexion. As a result, the probability that the shoulder flexor muscles are the limiting factor when using the wrist roller with the outstretched arm position meaningfully exceeds the probability that the elbow flexor muscles limit set performance when using the wrist roller with bent elbows. Nonetheless, strength ratios across muscle groups can vary substantially among individuals.
Due to their larger cross-sectional area (a key determinant of force production) and longer internal lever arms, the shoulder flexor muscles can generate much more torque than the wrist extensor muscles. However, the advantage in sheer torque production that the shoulder flexor muscles have can be offset by the markedly greater resistive lever arm they face compared with the wrist extensor muscles. While shoulder flexion strength may limit set performance when some people train wrist roller wrist extension with outstretched arms, others with proportionally greater shoulder flexion strength and lower wrist extension strength may have different outcomes. As a straightforward way to gauge whether your shoulder or elbow muscles are limiting your wrist roller exercise sets while your arms are parallel to the ground or your elbows bent, you can attempt to perform additional reps with your arms directed down toward the ground immediately after completing your initial set. If you can indeed perform more reps with the weight closer to you after completing as many reps as you could with either of the other two variations, your forearm muscles may not be the limiting factor, or receive the optimal stimulus from the other variations with the weight held farther in front of your body. Overall, the key takeaway is that the arm position you use for wrist roller exercises can influence which muscle groups are trained most effectively. When your goal is to maximize your forearm muscle development, it will be wise to perform a variation that you find to be limited by the strength of these muscles rather than by the strength of other muscle groups in the body.Because the base of support is narrow when the heels are off the ground, some people may lose their balance at certain points in a set, which can increase difficulty during the controlled lowering phase. Other calf raise variations that use a machine or allow you to place a hand on a nearby surface for balance support can be viable alternatives if you find your balance interferes with your ability to perform this exercise with controlled technique.If you notice your chest lifting off the bench during this movement, it likely indicates that you are dynamically extending your lumbar spine, which can serve as a compensatory pattern to assist your scapular retractor muscles in lifting the weight.As you increase your shoulder girdle depression strength over time, you can progress this exercise by incrementally supporting more of your bodyweight with your hands rather than through your legs. Once you have reached the point at which you are only lightly contacting the ground with your feet, you can advance onto the standard version of the scapular pull-up from this exercise library, which involves lifting your feet completely off the ground to increase the magnitude of resistance.Some evidence indicates that overhead triceps extensions can induce a greater magnitude of triceps muscle growth than triceps pushdowns. At the time of writing this passage, two studies conducted by Maeo et al. (2022) and Stasinaki et al. (2018) have measured changes in triceps size after interventions involving an overhead triceps extension or a triceps pushdown. Both studies utilized a within-subject unilateral approach where each participant trained overhead extensions with one side and pushdowns with the other side, which is a rather effective method to control for extraneous factors that could affect results if each participant only performed one exercise. The training interventions by Stasinaki et al. (2018), which lasted 6 weeks, included exercises performed through 80 degrees of elbow range of motion; however, the pushdown was trained through 10-90 degrees of elbow flexion, whereas the overhead extension was trained through 70-150 degrees of elbow flexion.

The training interventions by Maeo et al. (2022), which lasted 12 weeks, had both exercises performed through 0-90 degrees of elbow flexion. Stasinaki et al. (2018) used ultrasound to measure size changes in only the long head of the triceps and found similar muscle growth during overhead extensions and pushdowns. Maeo et al. (2022) used MRI to measure size changes in the long, lateral, and medial heads of the triceps and found each head to grow meaningfully more in the arms training overhead extensions compared to pushdowns.

Because the long head of the triceps originates on the scapula posterior to the shoulder joint’s axis of rotation, it has a shoulder extension moment arm that results in this muscle lengthening as the angle of shoulder flexion increases. Correspondingly, the long head of the triceps will work at longer lengths during overhead triceps extension variations compared to pushdown variations, where the shoulder is in or near a neutral sagittal position. Other muscles that cross multiple joints (such as the rectus femoris of the quadriceps, medial and lateral gastrocnemii of the calf, along with the semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and biceps femoris long head of the hamstrings) have been found to experience greater hypertrophy when trained with variations that load them at longer lengths compared to variations loading them at short lengths. With this in mind, the greater hypertrophy Maeo et al. (2022) measured in the long head of the triceps from the overhead extensions is not particularly surprising. However, the superior increases in size of the lateral and medial heads was unexpected by the researchers since these two heads do not cross the shoulder joint and are consequently not directly lengthened by increasing the angle of shoulder flexion.

Without delving too much into the nuances of the studies by Maeo et al. (2022) and Stasinaki et al. (2018) and potential reasons for their different findings, it is reasonable to conclude that overhead extension variations are likely to be particularly effective for lifters who wish to increase triceps muscle size.If you notice that your weight contacts the ground before you have reached what is your body’s full available range of motion, you can perform the Jefferson curl while standing on top of a sturdy elevated surface, such as an aerobic step or bumper plate, to facilitate a greater range of motion than could be reached on the ground. Additionally, you can perform this exercise with a single dumbbell, holding it by the ends with both hands.Consistently performing the lowering phase of each rep through your full ankle dorsiflexion range may help this exercise function as a form of loaded stretching and increase your available ankle dorsiflexion in a flexed knee position. Weight training may not have the same reputation for flexibility as static stretching, but training through a full range of motion that loads the working muscles at long lengths can improve flexibility just as well while improving your strength in those positions to a much greater degree. When pooling the results of all relevant studies in a systematic review and meta-analysis, Alizadeh et al. (2023) found that resistance training and static stretching interventions yielded similar increases in available range of motion. In addition to performing each rep of your seated calf raise through a full range of motion, you also can finish a set with a bout of sustained static stretching (such as for about 30-45 seconds) in the bottom position to amplify the ankle mobility stimulus even further. If you opt to do so, be aware that adding this sustained stretch may reduce how many reps you can complete on subsequent calf raise sets when using the same load.

Of any ankle motion, ankle dorsiflexion range of motion is generally the most relevant for lifters, and lifters reporting to have “tight ankles” are normally referring to having limited ankle dorsiflexion range of motion. This is because ankle dorsiflexion range of motion can meaningfully affect someone’s technique when performing squatting variations, along with exercises involving similar lower body movement like leg pressing. Ankle dorsiflexion range of motion, which is measured as the angle between the tibia and foot, determines how far someone’s knees can track forward when squatting down while keeping the heels in contact with the ground. If someone reaches the end of their available ankle dorsiflexion range of motion while squatting, descending further may require a more horizontal trunk position and greater hip flexion range of motion. This is not necessarily problematic, but the lifter may also reach the end range of hip flexion before achieving the desired depth. Consequently, squat depth can be limited, and a lifter may be more likely to experience “butt wink,” where the pelvis rotates posteriorly (backward) in a manner that is accompanied by dynamic lumbar spine flexion (lower back rounding). While not inherently injurious, repeatedly experiencing “butt wink” under conditions of high loading can be provocative of low back pain for some individuals.

Ankle dorsiflexion range of motion is not the only variable that can affect squat depth, the degree of horizontal trunk lean, and the presence of “butt wink,” but a limitation in this ankle motion commonly serves as a contributing factor. Ankle dorsiflexion range of motion differs depending on the degree to which the knee is flexed because the calf’s gastrocnemii muscles cross the back of the knee joint and are lengthened as the knee straightens and/or as the ankle dorsiflexes. When the knee is fully straight, ankle dorsiflexion range of motion will normally be limited by the flexibility of the gastrocnemii, which can be indicated by feeling a stretching sensation in the back of the knee. Performing straight-knee calf raise variations into end range ankle dorsiflexion at the bottom of each rep can increase the flexibility of the gastrocnemii and enhance ankle dorsiflexion range of motion in this knee position. However, flexibility of the gastrocnemii does not limit ankle dorsiflexion range of motion in higher angles of knee flexion since the gastrocnemii shorten as the angle of knee flexion increases. Alternatively, ankle dorsiflexion range of motion from a flexed knee position can be limited by the extensibility of the ankle’s joint capsule or the calf’s soleus, which exclusively crosses the ankle joint. At the bottom of a straight-knee calf raise, these two structures are unlikely to be stretched effectively since the gastrocnemii reach their maximal lengths beforehand. However, a seated calf raise allows the ankle’s joint capsule and soleus to be elongated to greater degrees since a greater angle of dorsiflexion can be reached when the flexed knee position slackens the gastrocnemii. Ankle dorsiflexion range of motion from a flexed-knee position is what matters for movements like squats, since the knees are flexed at the bottom of a squat. Therefore, a full range of motion calf raises, specifically from a seated position, can train ankle mobility relevant to squatting.As their names suggest, the primary function of the pronator teres and pronator quadratus is forearm pronation, so they can be most effectively developed through a resisted pronation exercise. While the pronator teres has a small elbow flexion that may allow curls to train it to some degree, the pronator teres and pronator quadratus are unlikely to be trained particularly well by any other type of exercise besides pronation exercises. Consequently, including at least one pronation exercise in your program will be important if you prioritize increasing the size and strength of these two muscles. Training the two pronator muscles directly may be invaluable for maximizing arm wrestling performance and beneficial when rehabbing golfer’s elbow (a type of tendinopathy often involving the pronator teres), but they are otherwise not especially noteworthy for typical physique or strength goals. Together, the pronator teres and pronator quadratus have been measured by Holzbaur et al. (2007) to have a combined volume that is only about 9% of the total forearm muscle volume. Consequently, they are noticeably smaller than the wrist flexor muscles that constitute close to half of the forearm’s muscle volume or even the wrist extensor muscles that reflect about one fifth of the forearm musculature. Nonetheless, pronation exercise will likely be required to maximize forearm muscle size since the two pronator muscles will otherwise not be robustly trained by other movements.

While the pronator teres and pronator quadratus may be trained the most effectively by pronation exercise, three other forearms also have leverage to generate pronation torque with the flexed elbow position and forearm joint angles that are utilized during this exercise. These additional muscles are the brachioradialis, flexor carpi radialis, and palmaris longus, which have a combined volume equivalent to about one fifth of the total forearm muscle volume. How effectively they can be developed by a pronation exercise is unclear, but data from Bremer et al. (2006) indicates that they can have leverage to pronate the forearm of magnitudes that are similar to the pronator teres and pronator quadratus over certain joint angle ranges. When tested in neutral or supinated forearm positions, Bader et al. (2017) also found the flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, and brachioradialis to exhibit significantly greater EMG activity during maximal isometric (static) forearm pronation compared to isometric supination, further supporting the pronation function these muscles may have. While these three muscles should be engaged during this pronation exercise, each has better leverage when trained through different movements. In particular, the brachioradialis has much better leverage to function as a primary elbow flexor, allowing it to be trained effectively by curl variations. On the other hand, the flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus have better leverage to function as wrist flexors, allowing them to be trained effectively by wrist curl variations, which will train a greater volume of forearm musculature than pronation exercise.Researchers have investigated EMG activity of the long head of the triceps, lateral head of the triceps, flexor carpi radialis, and extensor carpi radialis muscles (brevis and longus not differentiated) as participants performed overhand and underhand grip triceps pushdowns. Both variations were performed as unilateral variations with a D-handle attachment using the same loads. Compared to the overhand grip variation, the underhand grip pushdown yielded similar mean EMG activity of the lateral head of the triceps but greater mean EMG activity of the long head of the triceps (with a medium effect size). While median EMG activity of the long head was fairly similar for each grip, the difference was more pronounced in terms of their upper quartiles. These findings suggest that the two pushdown variations may engage the long head of the triceps similarly in many individuals, but a proportion of individuals (large enough to contribute to significantly different means) exhibit greater activity during underhand pushdowns. However, it remains to be seen whether these differences in mean EMG activity of the long head of the triceps are accompanied by differences in muscle hypertrophy across longitudinal training interventions that compare these variations.

It is worth noting that each variation was assessed during one set of as many reps as possible with 80% of the underhand version’s one-rep max, and the reported EMG activity was the mean of all reps, excluding the first and last reps. On average, participants completed approximately 8 reps with the underhand grip and approximately 10 reps with the overhand grip; thus, the relative load differed between the variations despite the absolute load being equal. Correspondingly, the underhand grip pushdown utilizing a greater relative load in comparison to the overhand grip variation may have influenced the difference in mean EMG activity of the long head of the triceps.

Unsurprisingly, the researchers also found that the underhand grip variation elicited significantly greater EMG activity in the extensor carpi radialis muscles but significantly less EMG activity in the flexor carpi radialis than the overhand grip pushdown. With the underhand grip, the wrist extensors will have to act isometrically to resist wrist flexion, while the wrist flexors will have to act isometrically to resist wrist extension during overhand grip pushdowns due to the cable’s line of pull during these exercises.While assessing a shrug variation (that was performed unilaterally in a standing position for 3 reps with free weight resistance loaded to a 5-RM), Ekstrom et al. (2003) found EMG activity to be very high in the upper trapezius, moderate in the middle trapezius, and low in the lower trapezius. This very high upper trapezius activity during a shrug is congruent with the upper trapezius elevating the shoulder girdle by drawing the lateral aspect of the clavicle superomedially. Jakobsen et al. (2012) similarly found that the upper trap exhibited the greatest EMG activity of any trap region during standing dumbbell shrugs (performed for 15 reps with a 15-RM load), while the lower trap exhibited the least EMG activity of the three regions.

Moseley et al. (1992) also found the levator scapulae to exhibit very high peak EMG activity during a shrug variation (that was performed bilaterally for 10 reps in a seated position with “light weight” dumbbells using loads self-selected by participants), which is congruent with the ability of this muscle to elevate the shoulder girdle through scapular elevation. Moseley et al. (1992) also assessed EMG activity across all three trapezius regions during the shrug variation, along with 15 other exercises. For each muscle, they reported EMG findings from exercises they considered “optimal” based on the duration and magnitude of EMG activity, but they did not identify the shrug as “optimal” for any trapezius region. Correspondingly, Moseley et al. (1992) did not report specific EMG values of any trapezius region while performing shrugs, indicating that no trapezius region exhibited at least moderately high EMG activity for what was considered a meaningful duration of each shrug rep. The absence of high EMG activity in the upper trapezius may have been due to participants performing a set of shrugs at a relative intensity insufficient to optimize agonist muscle peak activity, in contrast to Ekstrom et al. (2003), who explicitly assessed a set of shrugs with only 2 reps in reserve. If the shrug was performed with a load and/or rep-scheme that brought the set closer to volitional failure, Moseley et al. (1992) may have measured higher upper trapezius EMG activity.

While the anatomy of the upper trapezius and levator scapulae warrants their functional classification as shoulder girdle elevators, the limited research assessing changes in the size of these muscles after a training intervention does not indicate that shrugs are effective for inducing hypertrophy of these muscles. Conley et al. (1997) used MRI to measure the cross-sectional area of the neck musculature at nine different sites (involving 13 total muscles, including the trapezius and levator scapulae) before and after recreationally active participants completed a 12-week resistance training intervention. Each week, the intervention involved four full body training sessions, with each exercise performed for 3-5 sets of 3-10 reps. The exercises included squats, push presses, bench presses, mid-thigh pulls (i.e., above the knee rack pulls), Romanian deadlifts, crunches, bent over rows, and shrugs. Following the intervention, the cross sectional area did not change at the sites corresponding to the upper trapezius and levator scapulae. Especially since the participants reportedly were not well-trained lifters (who would most likely have lower rates of muscle growth than recreationally active individuals), one would expect the upper trapezius and levator scapulae to grow if they functioned as prime movers while shrugging. While not as directly as shrugs, mid-thigh pulls, and Romanian deadlifts would also be expected to train muscles that function as shoulder girdle elevators in an isometric fashion.

When our anatomical understanding of muscles indicates that they are well suited to perform a function meaningfully resisted by an exercise, we cannot necessarily conclude from a single training study that that exercise is incapable of effectively training those muscles. This is especially the case when EMG research supports high muscle activity during that exercise. While anecdotes are not at the level of empirical evidence derived from well controlled experiments, they still can function as a form of evidence while being recognized for their clear limitations. In isolation, countless lifters use shrug variations to train their upper trapezius and report that this muscle is meaningfully working, while shrugging does not provide irrefutable evidence that it trains the upper trapezius very effectively; however, this phenomenon is not meaningless either. Ultimately, the findings from Conley et al. (1997) provide notable evidence against the claim that shrugs effectively train the upper trapezius and levator scapulae, but this single study does not preclude classifying these muscles as prime movers during shrug variations.If you notice that your weight contacts the ground before you have reached what is your body’s full available range of motion, you can perform the Jefferson curl while standing on top of a sturdy elevated surface, such as an aerobic step or bumper plate, to facilitate a greater range of motion than could be reached on the ground. Additionally, you have the option of performing this exercise while holding one kettlebell in each hand if you prefer to do so.Resistive torque is what fundamentally determines the challenge any particular muscle group experiences during an exercise. To increase resistive torque, you can increase the weight of the resistance you use, or you can increase the lever arm on which the resistance acts on the joints (and the muscles acting on those joints) you are training. Either variable can be adjusted as a form of progressive overload to increase the torque that your muscles must overcome as they become stronger (i.e., are able to generate greater torque) over time. Whether you modify the load or the lever arm of the resistance is inconsequential to your muscles since the product of those two variables (torque) is what directly matters to the muscles you train. With a forearm exercise such as this, you can progress by using a heavier load or by incrementally grasping the implement closer to one end as your strength increases. If you have reached the end of your resistance progression and wish to continue increasing the resistance, you will need to add load to your implement (which is easiest to do when using an adjustable dumbbell) or switch to an implement with a longer handle or greater weight.While the scapular wall slide does not utilize any external resistance, most individuals will find this exercise challenging when performed with the strict technique described here. It may not be the most effective method for stimulating muscle growth, but the scapular wall slide can be a beneficial component of an active warmup prior to exercises such as overhead presses and back squats. Additionally, it is an exercise that can be performed at home to improve shoulder external rotation mobility.While lifting your arms up, you can consciously think about pinching your shoulder blades back together in an effort to increase potential engagement of the trapezius and rhomboids, since these muscles function to retract (pull back and together) the scapulae (shoulder blades). However, scapular (shoulder blade) retraction is naturally coupled with shoulder horizontal extension, so your scapular retractor muscles will be active during this exercise even if you do not focus on shoulder blade motion while performing it. The relative contribution of scapulothoracic motion to total shoulder girdle motion is particularly pronounced when nearing the end range of shoulder horizontal extension motion, so you physically would not be able to reach a position of full shoulder horizontal extension during this exercise without fully retracting your scapula.If you are not accustomed to performing shoulder girdle depression exercises, it may be difficult to learn the appropriate movement pattern at the intensity required when you are fully hanging from the bar. Even if you have developed a proficient technique, you may simply wish to perform a similar variation with a lower absolute intensity that allows you to perform more reps per set. If either scenario applies to you, you can perform the regressed scapular pull-up from this exercise library, which involves keeping the balls of your feet and your toes on the ground.Most lifters do not perform any exercises that effectively train the ankle dorsiflexor muscles, so including an ankle dorsiflexion exercise in your program can allow you to train muscles that would otherwise most likely be neglected. However, the ankle dorsiflexor muscles are fairly small as they have been measured to have a combined muscle volume that is about one fifth of the ankle plantar flexor muscles, which are trained by calf raise variations. Correspondingly, the ankle dorsiflexor muscles have a limited contribution to your lower body muscularity, and increasing their size by a particular percentage will have a considerably lower effect than if you increased the size of your calf muscles by the same percentage. Nonetheless, the tibialis anterior (which has the fourth largest volume among the lower leg muscles) is one of the most visible muscles because it lies superficial to other muscles on the front of the lower leg, making its definition quite apparent when you wear shorts.While simple from a technique standpoint, the single arm dead hang can be a challenging exercise that many people cannot perform. If this variation is too intense for your current level of strength, you can readily select the standard dead hang from this database, where you hang with both arms. Progressively increasing the time you perform for your sets of standard dead hangs can develop your grip strength and transfer to better single arm dead performance, but you can also perform variations that are more intense than the standard dead hang but less intense than the single arm dead hang. As an intermediate option, you can perform a dead hang with both arms, gripping lightly with one arm while the other supports more than half your bodyweight. Over time, you can progressively shift more of your bodyweight into one arm until you feel ready to dead hang with only one arm. You can also perform a weighted dead hang with both hands as you decide specifically how much more challenging you wish to make the movement based on how much weight you add to your pull-up/dip belt. As another way to intensify the standard dead hang, you can additionally add fat grip attachments to the bar and perform a dead hang with both arms, or you can wrap a thick towel around the bar and hang while gripping the towel. If you have very advanced levels of grip strength, you can apply the aforementioned strategies to progress beyond the conventional single arm dead hang by using a weight belt, fat grip attachments, or a towel while performing single arm dead hangs.Compared to the cable straight bar reverse curl (using an overhand grip) and the cable rope hammer curl (using a neutral grip), greater EMG activity of the biceps and the brachioradialis has been measured to occur during the lifting phase of the cable straight bar biceps curl (using an underhand grip). The size of this effect was greater for the biceps than the brachioradialis, and EMG activity of these muscles during the eccentric phase of the curls was similar regardless of which grip was used. During the lifting phase, the cable rope hammer curl has also been reported to yield greater EMG activity of the biceps than the cable straight bar reverse curl, but these two variations have been found to result in similar brachioradialis EMG activity.Studies such as those by Aasa et al. (2019) and Edington (2017) have demonstrated that it is normal for the lumbar spine to flex and extend during deadlift and squat variations with at least moderately heavy loads. However, lifters typically perform deadlift and squat variations with the intention of minimizing lumbar spine motion while striving to move their hips and knees. Correspondingly, the lumbar spinal extensor muscles are not necessarily trained by deadlifts and squats in a truly isometric (static muscle contraction) manner, which would only occur if no lumbar spine motion occurred during these exercises. However, they are normally performed with the intent to train the spinal extensor muscles isometrically, as lifters strive to keep a rigid trunk position, in contrast to Jefferson curls, which are intentionally performed through dynamic flexion and extension of the lumbar and thoracic spine. As a result, the trunk extensor muscles function as prime movers during Jefferson curls, thereby training the hip extensor muscles secondarily, as the hips dynamically flex and extend under load during this movement.

When consciously aiming to flex the lumbar and thoracic spine into end range, the muscles and ligaments on the back of the spine experience greater strain than when maintaining a rigid trunk position during exercise. While it is not inherently injurious to load the trunk in a highly flexed position, caution is advised during this exercise to not allow the weight to pull you forcibly beyond what is your end range. If you use a weight that is excessively heavy relative to your body’s capacity and exceed your normal available range of motion under load, you can face an elevated risk of experiencing sprain injuries of certain spinal ligaments or strain injuries of the spinal extensor muscles or hamstrings (since the hamstrings can also be fully lengthened with the straight knee and flexed hip position). It can be wise to be conservative with the loads and volumes you utilize when starting to train a variety of movements, but this is especially the case if you opt to perform Jefferson curls.When setting up, people often set the cable to its lowest height rather than at waist level, which is a viable approach but can make it more awkward to pivot into position with the cable behind you, especially with heavier loads.Especially for individuals experienced with training shrug variations, fatigue commonly develops more rapidly in the gripping muscles of the forearm and hand than in the back muscles that move the shoulder girdle. If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your back muscle strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your back muscles. As a simple approach, if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps, and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, ensure that you use the same technique across conditions, with the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day, where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps on the first day, perform the set with straps on a different day before the set without straps to control for any fatigue from the first set that may influence the second. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.

While assessing a shrug variation (that was performed unilaterally in a standing position for 3 reps with free weight resistance loaded to a 5-RM), Ekstrom et al. (2003) found EMG activity to be very high in the upper trapezius, moderate in the middle trapezius, and low in the lower trapezius. This very high upper trapezius activity during a shrug is congruent with the upper trapezius elevating the shoulder girdle by drawing the lateral aspect of the clavicle superomedially.

Moseley et al. (1992) also found the levator scapulae to exhibit very high peak EMG activity during a shrug variation (that was performed bilaterally for 10 reps in a seated position with “light weight” dumbbells using loads self-selected by participants), which is congruent with the ability of this muscle to elevate the shoulder girdle through scapular elevation. Moseley et al. (1992) additionally assessed EMG activity of all three trapezius regions during that shrug variation, along with 15 other exercises. For each muscle, they reported EMG findings from exercises they considered “optimal” based on the duration and magnitude of EMG activity, but they did not identify the shrug as “optimal” for any trapezius region. Correspondingly, Moseley et al. (1992) did not report specific EMG values of any trapezius region while performing shrugs, indicating that no trapezius region exhibited at least moderately high EMG activity for what was considered a meaningful duration of each shrug rep. The absence of high EMG activity in the upper trapezius may have been influenced by the participants performing a set of shrugs at a relative intensity insufficient to optimize agonist muscle peak activity, in contrast to Ekstrom et al. (2003), who explicitly assessed a set of shrugs with only 2 reps in reserve. If the shrug was performed with a load and/or rep-scheme that brought the set closer to volitional failure, Moseley et al. (1992) may have measured higher upper trapezius EMG activity.

While the anatomy of the upper trapezius and levator scapulae warrants their functional classification as shoulder girdle elevators, the limited research that has assessed changes in the size of these muscles after a training intervention does not indicate that shrugs are effective for inducing hypertrophy of these muscles. Conley et al. (1997) used MRI to measure the cross sectional area of the neck musculature at nine different sites (involving 13 total muscles, including the trapezius and levator scapulae) before and after recreationally active participants completed a 12-week resistance training intervention. Each week, the intervention involved four full body training sessions, with each exercise performed for 3-5 sets of 3-10 reps. The exercises included squats, push presses, bench presses, midthigh pulls (i.e., above the knee rack pulls), Romanian deadlifts, crunches, bent over rows, and shrugs. Following the intervention, the cross sectional area did not change at the sites corresponding to the upper trapezius and levator scapulae. Especially since the participants reportedly were not well trained lifters (who would most likely have lower rates of muscle growth than recreationally active individuals), one would expect the upper trapezius and levator scapulae to grow if they functioned as prime movers while shrugging. While not as directly as shrugs, midthigh pulls and Romanian deadlifts would also be expected to train muscles that function as shoulder girdle elevators in an isometric fashion.

When our anatomical understanding of muscles indicates that they are well suited to perform a function meaningfully resisted by an exercise, we cannot necessarily conclude from a single training study that that exercise is incapable of effectively training those muscles. This is especially the case when EMG research demonstrates high muscle activity during that exercise. While anecdotes are not at the level of empirical evidence derived from well controlled experiments, they still can function as a form of evidence while being recognized for their clear limitations. In isolation, countless lifters use shrug variations with the intent to train their upper trapezius and report feeling this muscle meaningfully working, while shrugging does not provide irrefutable evidence that shrugs indeed train the upper trapezius very effectively, but this phenomenon is not meaningless either. Ultimately, the findings from Conley et al. (1997) provide notable evidence against the claim that shrugs effectively train the upper trapezius and levator scapulae, but this single study does not preclude classifying these muscles as prime movers during shrug variations.Compared with prone Y, T, or I raises performed on the ground, lying down on a low incline bench allows you to train these movements through a greater range of motion and load the working muscles at longer lengths. While there is a difference in degree, neither variation of these exercises will load the prime movers into remotely lengthened positions.If you wish to do so, you can alternatively perform this wrist roller wrist curl with an underhand grip if you set up with the uncoiled part of the rope on the side of the roller farther away from you. This approach is similarly viable to the overhand technique described above, so you can feel free to try out both positions and utilize whichever feels most comfortable to you.While a full range of motion seated calf raises can serve as a useful means of improving ankle mobility that may transfer to squatting, they are noticeably less effective than straight knee calf raises (like standing calf raises) for inducing total calf muscle growth. There is reason to believe that seated calf raises may train the soleus (which only crosses the ankle joint and exclusively functions as an ankle plantarflexor) to a slightly greater degree than straight knee calf raises. However, two studies (one, two) have found that seated and straight knee calf raises elicit statistically similar magnitudes of soleus hypertrophy. On the other hand, the gastrocnemii (which cross both the ankle and knee joints and function both as ankle plantarflexors and knee flexors) have been reported to grow considerably more from straight knee calf raises compared to seated calf raises.

Because they function as knee flexors, a straight knee position allows the gastrocnemii to be loaded at longer lengths, which is conducive to muscle development. While the soleus is the largest calf muscle, the two gastrocnemii have been measured to have a combined muscle volume that is only slightly lower than the volume of the soleus. Furthermore, the soleus appears to have a lower capacity for muscle growth than the gastrocnemii, which is likely influenced by the soleus having a very high proportion of slow twitch muscle fibers in comparison to the gastrocnemii having a more even balance between slow and fast twitch fibers. Consequently, straight knee calf raises can be meaningfully more effective for increasing total calf muscle size relative to seated calf raises.When a step is available, selecting the calf raise variation in which you stand on top of a step may be preferable for most people looking to increase calf muscle size. Using a step allows the calf muscles to be trained in a lengthened position, which may promote greater muscle growth, according to the current body of evidence.By itself, the prone blackburn may not be an especially effective exercise to stimulate increases in the size of any particular shoulder girdle muscles. However, it can be a viable way to improve your shoulders’ active range of motion through actions such as elevation (in the starting position with your arms overhead), horizontal extension (in the “T-position”), and internal rotation (with your hands behind your back). While it may not load any particular muscle in a manner that optimizes muscle hypertrophy, the prone blackburn can engage much of your shoulder girdle musculature in a fashion that may allow it to serve as a useful active warmup prior to heavier shoulder exercises such as overhead presses.

It is worth noting that the effectiveness of this exercise can be meaningfully influenced by the intent with which you perform it. Keeping your arms slightly off the ground is not the same as keeping them as high off the ground as possible, which is what stimulates a greater range of motion and increases muscle engagement more than a half-hearted approach.Compared to the shoulder width grip that is used for the standard barbell Jefferson curl, using a snatch grip for this variation can allow you to descend through a greater range of motion before the weight contacts the ground, since the snatch grip results in the weight being held higher off the floor. However, if you have a rather high degree of mobility and notice that your weight contacts the ground before you have reached what is your body’s full available range of motion, you can perform this exercise while standing on top of a sturdy elevated surface such as an aerobic step or bumper plate. Doing so can facilitate a greater range of motion than could be reached on the ground, but you may alternatively be able to select smaller diameter weight plates to extend the range of motion if you initially used larger diameter weight plates that restrict how far you can descend.When performed as a maximal isometric action against manual resistance, the prone T raise has demonstrated very high EMG activity in the middle trapezius, high EMG activity in the lower trapezius, rhomboids, and levator scapulae, and moderately high EMG activity in the upper trapezius. While the other muscles can contribute to retraction of the shoulder girdle, the levator scapulae functions as an elevator and downward rotator of the scapula, so its high EMG activity would not necessarily be expected during this exercise.The end range of the lifting phase is typically the most challenging portion of chin-ups, largely because the torque our pulling muscles can generate varies meaningfully across joint angles. When the elbow is flexed to 120 degrees, Bober et al. (2002) found maximal elbow flexion torque to be approximately 35% of the peak torque that can be generated in a moderately flexed elbow position (i.e., near 60-75 degrees of elbow flexion). With your elbows approaching full flexion toward the end range of a chin-up, your elbow flexor muscles are correspondingly operating from a rather disadvantaged position to help you complete the rep. On average, maximal shoulder extension torque is also lower when the shoulder is mildly elevated (like at the end of a chin-up’s lifting phase) compared to when it is moderately elevated (like during the middle range of a chin-up). The difference can be of a notable magnitude, as indicated by Bober et al. (2002), who found maximal shoulder extension in a neutral shoulder position to be only 60% of its peak value that could be generated in 90 degrees of shoulder flexion. Together, these strength curves of the muscle groups acting on the elbow and shoulder can contribute to your chin-ups consistently feeling the most difficult during the last quarter of the lifting phase.The end range of the lifting phase is typically the most challenging portion of pull-up variations, largely because the torque our pulling muscles can generate varies meaningfully across joint angles. When the elbow is flexed to 120 degrees, Bober et al. (2002) found maximal elbow flexion torque to be approximately 35% of the peak torque that can be generated in a moderately flexed elbow position (i.e., near 60-75 degrees of elbow flexion). With your elbows approaching full flexion toward the end range of a pull-up, your elbow flexor muscles are correspondingly operating from a rather disadvantaged position to help you complete the rep. On average, maximal shoulder extension and adduction torques are also lower when the shoulder is mildly elevated (like at the end of a pull-up’s lifting phase) compared to when it is moderately elevated (like during the middle range of a pull-up)(one, two). Together, these strength curves of the muscle groups acting on the elbow and shoulder can contribute to your pull-ups consistently feeling the most difficult during the last quarter of the lifting phase.When performing a step-up variation, you will simultaneously train your quads as they extend your knee, along with your gluteus maximus as they extend your hip. Depending on how you perform your step-up, you may be able to shift a greater proportion of the load onto your quads or your gluteus maximus. At one end of the spectrum, keeping your torso completely upright as you drive your knee forward over your toes increases the lever arm of the resistance acting on the knee while decreasing the lever arm acting on the hip, thereby biasing the quadriceps relative to the gluteus maximus. Alternatively, you can focus on sitting your buttocks back as much as possible while inclining your torso and keeping your shin vertical, thereby increasing the lever arm acting on the hip and decreasing the lever arm acting on the knee, thereby biasing the gluteus maximus relative to the quads. With either approach, the quads and the gluteus maximus will be working, but the technique you use determines which muscles are proportionally emphasized to a greater degree.

If you use a relatively tall step to train through a long range of motion, you may be limited in how much you can modify your technique to shift load from the knee to the hip and vice versa while maintaining your balance in a deep position. While you may be able to adjust your technique to some extent, both your trunk and your shin will likely need to lean forward to maintain balance over your lead foot during a high step-up. However, you have greater freedom in how you perform your step-up when using a shorter step, which reduces your range of motion.With many squat variations, such as bodyweight squats and barbell back squats, you would fall backward if you tried to maintain a completely vertical torso, so you must lean your torso forward by flexing your hips to maintain balance. When the load is situated in front of your body, such as during a front squat or a goblet squat, you can adopt a relatively more upright torso position, but some degree of forward trunk lean will still be normally required to maintain your balance. On the other hand, Spanish squats allow you to maintain a completely vertical torso, shifting your center of mass closer to your hips and farther from your knees. This correspondingly results in Spanish squats involving a greater lever arm of resistance acting on the knees and a lesser lever arm acting on the hips compared to a free squat where your torso naturally inclines to maintain your balance. Consequently, Spanish squats can be significantly more quad-dominant than free squats, which may train hip extensor muscles such as the gluteus maximus more effectively than Spanish squats. In addition to biasing the quads to a greater degree than the hip extensor muscles, the longer lever arm acting on the knee during a Spanish squat makes this exercise notably more challenging than a standard bodyweight squat on a per-rep basis. If you opt to perform a weighted variation, you will similarly find that not nearly as much additional weight is needed to match the difficulty of a weighted free squat.It is by no means uncommon for people to perform exercises with a bar on their back without using safety pins, but using them when appropriate is strongly recommended. Positioning safety pins for an exercise, such as a barbell lunge, is the equivalent of wearing a seat belt when driving. In either situation, you may not intend to use them, but both safety mechanisms can reduce the risk of injury if an accident occurs. While you can try to refrain from performing a set of barbell lunges with a load or number of reps that is beyond your capacity, sometimes lifters can overestimate what is left in the tank and unintentionally fail a rep. Furthermore, a non-zero risk of injury exists during any reasonably challenging physical activity, even when using high quality technique and an intelligently designed program that utilizes appropriate volumes, intensities, and rates of progression. For instance, the likelihood may be low on a per-rep basis, but it is certainly possible to lose your balance or sustain a sudden muscle strain injury during the descent of a lunge. If such an event occurs, appropriately positioned safety pins can reduce the likelihood that an incident worsens when you have a bar on your back. As demonstrated by competitive powerlifters, weightlifters, and bodybuilders, who have lower rates of reported injuries than athletes in many other sports, strenuous resistance training is not inherently dangerous, and there is no need to fear lifting weights. Nonetheless, the risk of an acute injury or accident during training cannot be eliminated, and it is prudent to take simple precautionary measures to protect your safety in the weight room.While much remains to be learned, the barbell hip thrust has been one of the most well studied exercises throughout the past decade. Thousands of exercise variations exist, and only a small fraction have been assessed in studies designed to determine which muscles increase in size after a resistance training intervention, including specific variations. At least three longitudinal training studies have investigated the ability of the hip thrust to induce hypertrophy of the gluteus maximus; two compared a 9-12-week intervention exclusively involving barbell hip thrusts with an intervention exclusively involving barbell back squats. Plotkin et al. (2023) and Barbalho et al. (2020) both found that the gluteus maximus increased in size after the hip thrust and back squat interventions, whereas the quadriceps increased significantly more with the back squat intervention. Plotkin et al. (2023), who assessed untrained participants with MRI, found the gluteus maximus to grow similarly after either intervention, but Barbalho et al. (2020), who assessed women with at least three years of resistance training experience, found the gluteus maximus to grow significantly more after the back squat intervention compared to the hip thrust intervention.

Plotkin et al. (2023) measured additional muscles and found that the hamstrings, gluteus medius, and gluteus minimus showed negligible changes in size after either intervention. Furthermore, Plotkin et al. (2023) assessed the hip adductors (the adductor magnus, adductor longus, and adductor brevis combined) and found that they increased in size to a greater extent after back squats than after hip thrusts. As part of a rather detailed study, Plotkin et al. (2023) measured changes in the upper, middle, and lower subregions of the gluteus maximus, each of which grew similarly in the hip thrust and back squat groups. Based on mean increases in cross-sectional area, the lower region of the gluteus maximus experienced the greatest hypertrophy of any region, which is congruent with this region having the greatest hip extension moment arm.

Plotkin et al. (2023) and Barbalho et al. (2020) also tracked changes in hip thrust and back squat strength (as a 1-RM or a 3-RM), and they found that the squat group increased squat strength to a greater degree than hip thrust strength, while the hip thrust group increased hip thrust strength to a greater degree than back squat strength in accordance with the principle of training specificity. With respect to transfer to deadlift performance, Plotkin et al. (2023) reported that the hip thrust and back squat interventions yielded similar increases in deadlift performance.Compared with other exercises that train the same muscle groups, the bodyweight glute bridge provides low resistance to the gluteus maximus and other hip extensor muscles. If training with the objective of increasing muscle size and/or strength, other glute bridge variations, such as unilateral bodyweight glute bridges or glute bridges with added external resistance, will likely be more efficient for most able-bodied individuals.As with many other bodyweight exercises, you can naturally increase the progress of your sit-up by performing a variation that uses an external load (e.g., holding a weight plate) as your abdominal strength improves over time. However, you can also modify the intensity to which your abdominal muscles must contract during each rep by adjusting your arm position. The difficulty of a sit-up is lowest when you rest your arms by your sides, and it progressively increases as you shift the center of mass of your arms closer toward your head. Correspondingly, crossing your arms to rest your hands on your shoulders can be a more challenging variation than keeping your arms on the floor by your sides, and keeping your arms up and behind your head can further increase the difficulty. In light of this relationship, the “bodyweight sit-up with hands on floor” is an easier variation than the “bodyweight sit-up with hands on shoulders,” which is an easier variation than the “bodyweight sit-up with hands behind head.”Depending on the path in which your upper arm moves, you can row with different techniques that influence which back muscles are preferentially trained. On one of the spectrums, you can row predominantly through shoulder extension in the sagittal plane, which occurs when keeping your elbow tucked close to your side. When moving your shoulder exclusively through extension in the sagittal plane (which can also occur during exercises like pullovers and underhand grip lat pulldowns), your upper arm will be parallel with your torso toward the end of the row’s lifting phase. Alternatively, you can row predominantly through shoulder horizontal extension (AKA horizontal abduction) in the horizontal plane (AKA transverse plane), which occurs when flaring your elbow out to your side and away from the mid-line of your body. When moving your shoulder exclusively through horizontal extension (which can also occur during exercises like the reverse fly on a pec deck machine), your upper arm will form a right angle with your torso toward the end of the row’s lifting phase. Naturally, you can also row in a manner that involves a hybrid between sagittal extension and horizontal extension, and if your shoulder moves equally between these two planes, your upper arm will form a 45-degree angle with your torso toward the end of the row’s lifting phase.

Without consciously intending to move in a particular plane of motion, people will commonly move their shoulders through a plane that feels comfortable for their shoulders, elbows, and wrists. Which plane of motion feels most comfortable can be influenced by which modality (e.g., barbell, dumbbell, machine) is used in addition to the specific grip used. As grip width progressively increases, rowing with a technique emphasizing greater shoulder horizontal extension (resulting in greater elbow flare) tends to feel more comfortable. However, rowing with a technique that emphasizes greater shoulder sagittal extension (resulting in greater elbow tuck) tends to feel more comfortable as grip width progressively decreases. Whether you utilize an overhand, underhand, neutral, or hybrid (i.e., a combination between neutral and underhand or overhand) grip during any particular row can also affect which grip width and plane of shoulder motion feels comfortable. For instance, when performing a barbell row with an underhand grip, using a shoulder width grip and keeping your elbows tucked while rowing predominantly through shoulder sagittal extension will typically feel most comfortable and natural. However, with an overhand grip, a wider range of grip widths and shoulder planes of motion can be more comfortable. For example, a wide overhand grip barbell row can be performed predominantly through horizontal shoulder extension, resulting in the elbows flaring outward. On the other hand, a shoulder width overhand grip barbell row can be comfortable to perform in a manner that emphasizes more sagittal shoulder extension or exhibits a fairly even balance between horizontal extension and sagittal extension.

Fundamentally, the plane of shoulder motion is what matters to the back muscles, and the grip is only relevant to the back muscles based on how it influences which shoulder motion is performed. Back muscles acting on the shoulder have different leverage to generate sagittal extension torque versus horizontal extension torque, so their ability to contribute to a row will be affected by the specific plane in which shoulder motion occurs during the row. A muscle’s leverage to contribute to a particular joint action can be quantified as its moment arm, which is the perpendicular distance between a joint’s axis of rotation and the muscle’s line of pull. When multiplied by how much force its contraction produces, the moment arm determines how much torque a muscle can generate for a particular joint action. The rear delt has meaningful moment arms to function as both a primary sagittal extensor and a primary horizontal extensor, while the rotator cuff’s teres minor also has leverage to function as both a secondary sagittal extensor and a secondary horizontal extensor. While they cannot meaningfully contribute to sagittal extension, the rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and supraspinatus can also assist as secondary horizontal extensors, but like the teres minor, their horizontal extension moment arms are not particularly large. Across the joint angles used in a row, the lat and teres major have negligibly small horizontal extension moment arms and are therefore incapable of generating any meaningful magnitude of shoulder horizontal extension torque. However, both of these back muscles are well situated to function as primary shoulder extensors.

During any active movement, the nervous system is most likely to recruit the muscles best suited to produce the joint actions required for efficient performance. Consequently, the biomechanical differences among the aforementioned back muscles can contribute to different row techniques, influencing how likely certain muscles are to be effectively trained. Rowing with a technique that emphasizes sagittal extension of the shoulder will most likely be preferable if you prioritize development of your lats and teres major, but using a technique emphasizing horizontal extension will most likely be better if you prioritize development of your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and supraspinatus. Primarily due to the lats being the most voluminous back muscle, a shoulder extension-dominant row can effectively train more overall muscle mass than a shoulder horizontal extension-dominant row. Nevertheless, the rotator cuff muscles are larger than many people may realize, so a shoulder horizontal extension row still preferentially targets a sizable amount of muscle. According to two studies, the combined volume of the teres minor, infraspinatus, and supraspinatus has been measured to be 55-67% of the volume of the lats and teres major together. The teres minor and supraspinatus are slightly smaller and larger, respectively, than the fairly small teres major, but the infraspinatus is a sizable muscle along with the rear delt. In fact, the combined volume of these two shoulder horizontal extensor muscles has been measured to be essentially equivalent (ranging from slightly lower to mildly higher) to the volume of the lat. When considering their locations, these muscles are all part of the upper back, except for the lat, which is predominantly located in the middle back. Consequently, a row that emphasizes sagittal extension of the shoulder is likely to be relatively better for developing the middle back in comparison to a row that emphasizes the shoulder horizontal extension, which is likely to be relatively better for developing the upper back.Rather than alternating between steps 1-4 described under the exercise technique (which may be considered the standard version of the reverse crunch), some people perform a reverse crunch by exclusively alternating between steps 2 and 3. Neither technique is inherently right nor wrong, but each variation could have a somewhat different training effect. Using an abbreviated range of motion would reduce the stimulus to the hip flexor muscles and result in a variation that predominantly trains the abdominal muscles dynamically, rather than training them both statically (during steps 1 and 4) and dynamically (during steps 2 and 3) per rep.Just as the terms “straight-leg deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” are commonly used as synonyms of each other, so too are the terms “Romanian deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” often used interchangeably to describe the same exercise by scholarly articles and lifters in general. However, some people consider these names to refer to different hip variations, and this database includes three different exercises to allow you to select whichever specific variation you wish to utilize. Under this definition, the straight-leg deadlift employs a straight knee position, in contrast to the mild knee bend used in the Romanian or stiff-leg deadlift from the floor. This conception of the straight-leg deadlift, as demonstrated by Hegyi et al. (2018) and Piper and Waller (2001), may be used less frequently than the other two variations.

Both the straight-leg deadlift and the Romanian deadlift begin in an upright position and use a range of motion tailored to individual flexibility, as the plates often do not contact the floor. If someone has sufficient hamstring flexibility for the plates to contact the floor during either variation (which is much rarer with the straight-leg deadlift), a light tap to the ground is used. In contrast, the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor begins with the plates fully on the floor, as in a conventional deadlift, and results in the full weight of the bar being transferred to the ground at the end of each rep. All three variations are hip-hinge exercises that train the same muscle groups, but the relative emphasis on different muscles may vary slightly across variations. Compared with the other two variations, the straight-leg deadlift results in a lower range of motion for most people, as the hamstrings are lengthened to a greater extent with fully straight knees than with mildly bent knees. Consequently, someone can achieve maximal hamstring elongation earlier during the descent of a straight-leg deadlift, thereby limiting the degree to which the hips can flex before the lifting phase begins. Compared to using a mild knee bend, a straight-leg deadlift also constrains how far you can sit your buttocks backward and results in the bar drifting farther out in front of your legs during the descent.The terms “Romanian deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” are often used interchangeably to describe the same exercise by scholarly articles and lifters in general. The nomenclature of the Romanian deadlift has been attributed to this exercise being performed by Nicu Vlad, a Romanian weightlifter who won gold, silver, and bronze medals respectively in the 1984, 1988, and 1996 Olympic Games in addition to earning medals in seven World Weightlifting Championships (with three of them being gold). American weightlifting coach Jim Schmitz reportedly saw Vlad performing the barbell hip hinge exercise and subsequently called it the Romanian deadlift, since Vlad did not have a particular name for the variation he was performing.

Although they are often used interchangeably, some people use the terms “Romanian deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” to denote different variations. They consider the stiff-leg deadlift to be what this database describes as the “barbell stiff-leg deadlift from floor,” in which each rep begins with the plates fully on the floor, as in a conventional deadlift. This variation differs from the Romanian deadlift, which starts from an upright position and uses a range of motion individualized to one’s flexibility, as the plates often do not contact the floor. If someone has sufficient hamstring flexibility for the plates to contact the floor during a Romanian deadlift, a light tap to the ground is used, but the full weight of the bar is not transferred to the ground, as in a stiff-leg deadlift performed from the floor. In contrast to the Romanian deadlift, which is typically performed with a controlled lowering phase, this conception of the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor often involves a quicker lowering phase with a tempo akin to conventional deadlifts: a controlled drop. Both variations can involve a mild degree of knee flexion at the bottom of the hinge, but the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor may require adopting a more flexed lumbar position to pull from the floor with the knee in position while maintaining high hips. This differs from the Romanian deadlift, which is performed only within the range of motion in which the lumbar spine can be maintained in close to neutral or flat back position.

As demonstrated by sources such as Hegyi et al. (2018) along with Piper and Waller (2001), another interpretation of the stiff-leg deadlift also exists as a truly straight leg deadlift. Under this conception of the exercise, the knees are kept extended to the point of complete straightness, but not fully locked (which could otherwise allow knee hyperextension in individuals with sufficient mobility). Such a technique would result in a movement that is more akin to “bending over” rather than “sitting back,” and the bar would correspondingly be likely to drift further in front of the legs compared to a Romanian deadlift, which utilizes a mild knee bend that allows you to “sit back.” In light of the different ways in which these names may be interpreted, hopefully, this note provides some clarification if you have ever been confused by how different individuals may view Romanian deadlifts and stiff-leg deadlifts exercises as the same exercise or distinct variations.Pull-ups can be performed with a variety of hand grips, including different grip widths and whether an overhand, underhand (resulting in the exercise being called a chin-up), neutral, or hybrid (i.e., a combination between neutral and underhand or overhand) grip is utilized. Additionally, the shoulder motion occurring during a pull-up can be performed predominantly in the sagittal (front to back) plane, frontal (side to side) plane, or in a combination between the sagittal and frontal planes. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the sagittal plane, as would occur when lifting your arms straight out in front of you, the upper arms remain parallel to each other and do not deviate away from the midline of your body. Lifting your arms straight out to your sides, such as with a snow angel motion, would be an example of moving your shoulders strictly in the frontal plane. When raising your arms in the frontal plane, the upper arms move away from the midline of your body until they are parallel to the ground, and then they move closer to the midline of your body with further elevation. During the lifting phase of a pull-up, the shoulders may extend in the sagittal plane, adduct in the frontal plane, or exhibit a combination of extension and adduction in a hybrid plane between the sagittal and frontal planes. Rather than moving the shoulders exclusively through a single plane, pull-up variations are commonly performed with a technique that allows the shoulders to move between these two planes, but one plane of shoulder motion often contributes more than the other.

Muscles acting on the shoulder joint have different leverage to generate extension torque in the sagittal plane versus adduction torque in the frontal plane. During any active movement, the nervous system is most likely to recruit the muscles best suited to produce the joint actions required for efficient performance. Consequently, determining which muscles have meaningful leverage to extend or adduct the shoulder can inform how effectively these muscles will be trained by a pull-up variation that utilizes a specific shoulder motion. A muscle’s leverage to contribute to a particular joint action can be quantified as its moment arm, which is the perpendicular distance between a joint’s axis of rotation and the muscle’s line of pull. When multiplied by how much force its contraction produces, the moment arm determines how much torque a muscle can generate for a particular joint action.

With any particular grip for a pull-up, you can intentionally rotate your shoulders in a manner that allows you to move your shoulder relatively more in the sagittal or frontal plane. However, most people find that a particular path of shoulder motion is most comfortable and stable for their shoulders, elbows, and wrists when using a specific grip. For instance, people typically perform a wide overhand grip pull-up with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the frontal plane, whereas they typically perform a close grip chin-up (underhand) with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the sagittal plane. Performing either variation in the alternative plane of motion would require greater than normal rotational range of motion in the shoulders and forearms, and attempting to do so could feel very awkward and uncomfortable. People typically feel most comfortable when positioning themselves so that, at the portion of the pull-up when their upper arms are parallel to the ground, their elbows and wrists are vertically aligned, or their elbows lie slightly lateral (outside) to their wrists.

In general, widening a grip facilitates relatively more frontal plane shoulder motion, while narrowing a grip facilitates relatively more sagittal plane shoulder motion. An overhand, underhand, or neutral grip may be used with any grip width, but the combination of these grip variables will influence comfort and which plane of shoulder motion feels most natural. When pulling predominantly in the sagittal plane with a relatively close grip, an underhand or neutral grip is typically more comfortable than an overhand grip. Similarly, when pulling predominantly in the frontal plane with a relatively wide grip, an overhand or neutral grip is typically more comfortable and allows greater frontal plane motion than an underhand grip. With a moderate grip width, people may commonly find that pulling mostly in the sagittal plane feels natural with an underhand grip, but pulling relatively more in the frontal plane feels natural with an overhand grip. Fundamentally, shoulder motion is what matters to the back muscles, and grip is relevant only insofar as it influences which shoulder motion is performed.

The lat and teres major in the back function both as primary adductors and extensors and are therefore highly likely to be trained effectively by pull-up variations regardless of the grip or plane of shoulder motion. However, the preponderance of available evidence indicates that the rear delt lacks meaningful leverage to contribute to shoulder adduction, but it clearly has favorable leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. The rear delt is correspondingly more likely to be trained effectively with a closer grip that emphasizes shoulder extension, which is most comfortable when using an underhand or neutral grip. Alternatively, the middle and lower regions of the chest’s pec major that constitute the sternocostal head have large moment arms to function as shoulder adductors. However, only the lower portion of the pec major exhibits a shoulder extension moment arm, and this moment arm is quite small and only present in flexed shoulder positions, like at the start of a pull-up. Consequently, wider grip pull-ups (that will be most comfortable with an overhand or neutral grip) involving greater frontal plane shoulder motion are likely to train the pec major more effectively than closer grip pulldowns. Which regions of the lat are preferentially biased by a pull-up variation may also be influenced by the extent of shoulder motion in the frontal or sagittal planes. The middle and lower regions have relatively greater leverage to function as shoulder adductors, while the upper region of the lat has relatively greater leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. Plausibly, these biomechanical differences may facilitate the middle and lower regions of the lats being preferentially trained by wider grip pull-ups, while the upper region potentially may be preferentially trained by closer grip pulling variations. That said, the extent to which any preferential biasing may occur across different regions of the lat when comparing different vertical pulling variations remains speculative and has not been tested by any longitudinal training interventions. While certain regions may have better leverage than others to contribute to a particular technique, all regions of the lat may potentially be recruited near their maximal capacity during a challenging set of a vertical pulling exercise, so it is unclear if regional biasing would actually occur.As with many other exercises that have bodyweight variations, you naturally can progress the intensity of this hip hinge variation by performing a variation that utilizes an external load (e.g., holding a weight plate) as your strength improves over time. However, you can also modify the intensity to which your hip extensor and trunk extensor muscles must contract during each rep by adjusting your arm position. If you cross your arms to keep your hands on your shoulders, holding your hands behind your head shifts the center of your upper body’s mass farther away from your hips and lower back. By doing so, the force from the weight of your upper body has a greater lever arm to resist your hip extensor and trunk extensor muscles. Consequently, performing the bodyweight back extension with your hands behind your head can function as a progression from the variation with your hands on your shoulders.Some lifters prefer to train barefoot, but this type of ankle dorsiflexion exercise can be fairly uncomfortable without shoes. With the force from your bodyweight concentrated on the bottom of your heels, where the plantar fascia (AKA plantar aponeurosis) attaches, you may experience an unpleasant pinching sensation as this portion of the plantar fascia is compressively stressed against the heel bone without wearing a cushioned heel. This can especially be the case for lean individuals who have lower than typical fat reserves that can otherwise help cushion the heel, so wearing shoes can make this exercise more comfortable.Exercises that resist knee extension exclusively, such as machine leg extensions, or that primarily load knee extension while imparting minimal hip extensor loading (e.g., reverse Nordic curls and sissy squats), can serve a distinct role in a training program. A wide array of exercises can train knee and hip extension simultaneously, including squats, lunges, step-ups, and leg presses. These multi-joint exercises can certainly stimulate meaningful increases in the size and strength of the quads, but they are not ideal for training the rectus femoris, the quad muscle that crosses the hip and functions as a hip flexor and a knee extensor. Notably, the rectus femoris does not just weakly assist in flexing the hip, but rather functions as a primary hip flexor that has greater leverage for producing hip flexion torque than any other muscle in the human body at certain joint angles. Because contraction of the rectus femoris can produce a substantial amount of hip flexion torque, its recruitment during a movement that requires both hip and knee extension is less biomechanically efficient than recruitment of the other muscle bellies of the quadriceps. As the rectus femoris contracts more forcefully during a movement such as the squat, the hip extensors must work harder to resist the hip flexion torque it generates. Striving to maximize efficiency, the nervous system preferentially recruits the three monoarticular (crossing only the knee joint) muscles of the quads to a greater extent than the rectus femoris during compound exercises in which extension occurs simultaneously at the hip and knee joints.

Although some research indicates that the rectus femoris does not increase significantly with multi-joint exercises such as squats, other studies have reported statistically significant increases in this muscle following such training among participants new to lifting. When a relatively small magnitude of growth occurs (as is the case even in studies that achieve statistical significance), it is unsurprising that the change is statistically significant in some studies but not in others. While the rectus femoris will be trained to a degree during these types of multi-joint exercises, the available body of evidence indicates that the monoarticular quad muscles are much more likely to experience a robust hypertrophy response. Correspondingly, your rectus femoris will almost certainly lag behind your other quad muscles if all of your quad training is performed through multi-joint exercises like squats.

However, during a single joint knee extension exercise where no movement occurs at the hip joint, the rectus femoris is just as active as the monoarticular muscles of the quad, if not more so. This is logical, since the hip flexion torque generated by rectus femoris contraction is essentially inconsequential during an exercise that does not simultaneously resist the hip extensor muscles. Congruent with what would be expected, machine seated leg extensions have been demonstrated to produce a considerable degree of hypertrophy of the rectus femoris that is similar to or even more pronounced than that of the three monoarticular quad muscles. While less studied than machine leg extensions, reverse Nordic curls have been shown to produce significant increases in rectus femoris size, and the biomechanically similar sissy squat would likely yield a similar result, although it has not been directly studied.If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your back muscle strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your back muscles. As a simple approach, if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps, then compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, ensure you use the same technique across conditions, with the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day when you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps on the first day, perform the set with straps on a different day before the set without straps to control for any fatigue from the first set that may influence the second. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well-informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.*Key takeaway: Setting up the safety pins can help promote safety in the weight room.*

It is by no means uncommon for people to perform exercises with a bar on their backs without using safety pins, but using them when appropriate is strongly recommended. Positioning safety pins for exercises such as barbell squat variations is the equivalent of wearing a seat belt when driving. In either situation, you may not intend to use them, but both safety mechanisms can reduce the risk of injury in the event of an accident. While you can try to refrain from performing a set of barbell squats with a load or number of reps that is beyond your capacity, sometimes lifters can overestimate what is left in the tank and unintentionally fail a rep. Furthermore, a non-zero risk of injury exists during any reasonably challenging physical activity, even when using high quality technique and an intelligently designed program that utilizes appropriate volumes, intensities, and rates of progression. For instance, the likelihood may be very low on a per-rep basis, but it is certainly possible to sustain a sudden muscle strain injury during a challenging set of squats. If such an acute injury occurs, set the bar down onto the safety pins when they are positioned appropriately. However, if you did not previously set up the safety pins, you face a risk of sustaining further injury or damaging equipment if an unforeseen injury acutely occurs while your torso is in an inclined position with a bar on your back.

Many experienced lifters have learned how to bail out of a squat that they can no longer complete by dumping the bar off their back and rapidly moving forward away from the dropping bar. This approach may be a viable alternative to using safety pins for individuals proficient in the bailing technique who are using bumper plates on a floor that can tolerate dropping a loaded barbell from a notable height. However, safely bailing out of a squat requires greater coordination than simply using the safety pins, and it can be difficult for lifters who squat with a substantial forward torso lean. Although not suitable for every environment, an individual’s ability to bail out of a squat may be impaired if an injury is the reason the person failed the rep, so using safety pins may be preferable in many circumstances.

As demonstrated by competitive powerlifters, weightlifters, and bodybuilders, who have lower rates of reported injuries than athletes in many other sports, strenuous resistance training is not inherently dangerous, and there is no need to fear lifting weights. Nonetheless, the potential for acute injury during training cannot be eliminated, and it is prudent to take simple precautionary measures to promote your safety in the weight room.If you find that grip strength is the limiting factor, rather than your lower body or lower back strength, when performing this exercise, you can use chalk or straps to increase the stimulus for your lower-body and back muscles. When performing deadlift variations, the hip extensor muscles (e.g., gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus) have the capacity to move very heavy loads that can commonly exceed someone’s grip strength. If your grip strength limits you, your lower body and back muscles are unlikely to be trained as effectively as they would otherwise be if you trained with lifting straps. Sometimes it is clear that grip strength limits your performance on a deadlift variation without straps, but it is not always clear what precisely limits your performance, which may warrant a quick personal experiment.

As a simple approach, if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps, then compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, ensure you use the same technique across conditions, with the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least a few minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day when you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps on the first day, perform the set with straps on a different day before the set without straps to control for any fatigue from the first set that may influence the second. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well-informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.If you find that grip strength is the limiting factor rather than core strength when performing this exercise, you can use chalk or straps to increase the stimulus to your core muscles with each set. As a simple approach, if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps, then compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, ensure you use the same technique across conditions, with the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day when you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps on the first day, perform the set with straps on a different day before the set without straps to control for any fatigue from the first set that may influence the second. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well-informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.When prioritizing a technique that emphasizes performing all the work with the elevated leg, controlling the descent, maintaining level hip bones, and keeping the lead leg knee stacked over the foot, a moderately low step can be sufficiently challenging for most people. As you become more proficient with this exercise over time, you can incrementally increase the height of your step to increase the intensity of the exercise, but refrain from selecting a height that prompts your technique to degrade. Signs that a particular step may be excessively high for someone’s current ability commonly involve pushing off with the rear foot and rapidly dropping onto the ground during the descent, which makes the exercise considerably easier than using the technique described above. Additional common technical errors include allowing the hip bone of the free leg to drop significantly or allowing the knee of the working leg to cave inward.A low-to-high cable fly can train the pec major and front delt in a manner that is quite similar to incline dumbbell fly and incline press variations. In terms of shifting the emphasis to different muscle regions, lowering the cables also increases the angle for an incline fly or press. When positioned near hip level, the low-to-high cable fly likely results in a training effect that is similar to a low incline (e.g., near 30 degrees) fly for many individuals, while situating the cables near knee height likely results in a training effect that is more similar to a moderate incline (e.g., near 45 degrees) fly.To save time when you set up to perform this exercise again during future sessions, you can make a note about which safety bar setting positioned the bar at the optimal starting height for you and which safety bar setting placed the safety stops at the ideal height under the bar. If your Smith machine does not have numbered safety bars, you can note how many safety bars were situated above the bar’s starting position. Similarly, you can note how many safety bars were situated below the level where you set the safety stops or how many safety bars were between where you positioned the safety stops and the bar. Subsequently, you can use these numbers to help you set up the Smith machine efficiently the same way every time, rather than visually having to estimate where you situated the bar and safety stops in a less reliable manner.To save time when setting up during future sessions, it can be useful to record which number handle position you determined to place you in the proper starting position for this exercise if you will repeatedly use the same machine. Besides reducing the time required to re-adjust the handles, ensuring you use the same handle position from session to session helps track progress over time. Since changing the handle position can affect the range of motion you use for this exercise, it can correspondingly influence the difficulty of each rep. In turn, this can affect how many reps you can perform with a given weight or how much weight you can use for a given number of reps.If you wish to optimize your biceps development, curls cannot be beat, but chest fly variations will still train the biceps to a degree, so you are not alone if you feel your biceps working when performing a chest fly. While the lever arm of resistance acting on the shoulder is greater, resistance will also act on the elbow, and your biceps generate elbow flexion torque to maintain the mild degree of elbow bend and prevent your elbow from fully straightening. Through this elbow action, the biceps function statically, but the biceps also have leverage to aid the pec major and front delt in generating shoulder horizontal flexion torque to perform the chest fly motion. Biceps contraction can assist in bringing the hands inward during the lifting phase and control the reversal of this movement during a chest fly. The elbow flexion and shoulder horizontal flexion functions of the biceps explain why Solstad et al. (2020) found the biceps to exhibit high EMG activity when participants performed a flat dumbbell fly, which should engage the biceps similarly to other chest fly variations.

The perception of the biceps being involved during a chest fly can be further amplified by the contribution of another upper arm muscle, namely the coracobrachialis. The coracobrachialis is situated directly deep to the upper half of the short head of the biceps, so contraction of the coracobrachialis can easily be perceived as contraction of the adjacent biceps. This thin upper arm muscle has meaningful leverage to function as a shoulder horizontal flexor, so it should be trained effectively by any pec fly variation that resists shoulder horizontal flexion, along with pressing variations that resist this shoulder motion.People commonly perform the high-to-low dual cable fly with a meaningful degree of forward torso lean, rather than a slight lean or a fully upright torso. While the cables can move through the same path, a notable amount of forward lean can make the shoulder motion essentially the same as the horizontal dual cable fly. High-to-low flies and horizontal flies can both train the middle and lower regions of the pectoralis major in a very effective manner, and it is currently unclear if the high-to-low variation is necessarily any more effective in developing the lower chest. However, if you are using the high-to-low dual cable fly with the intent of training the lower chest in a way that differs from the horizontal variation, you should avoid meaningfully leaning forward when performing this exercise.After completing a set of dumbbell presses, it is not uncommon for people to drop the dumbbells forward while still lying on the bench. If one end of the dumbbell contacts the ground before the other end, the dumbbell can ricochet off the ground and potentially injure someone nearby or damage equipment. While you can proceed as you wish if you are training alone with your own equipment in a home gym, dropping the dumbbells this way is inappropriate and strongly discouraged.With the obvious difference of holding a weight on only one side of the body instead of on both sides, a suitcase carry otherwise looks quite similar to a farmer’s carry. If the total load is equated, holding a weight on one side of the body or on both sides will not directly affect how muscles function in the sagittal (front to back) plane, the plane in which actions such as trunk and hip extension occur. However, it will affect how muscles function in the frontal (side to side) plane, which is the plane in which actions like trunk side bending and hip abduction occur.

When holding the same weight on each side, the resistances from the weights are able to counteract each other in the frontal plane for the trunk, so your core muscles do not have to resist bending to either side. However, if you hold a weight only on one side, the core muscles (like the obliques, quadratus lumborum, and various intrinsic back muscles) on the opposite side will have to contract to prevent your trunk from bending to the side of the weight. If they contract dynamically, the core muscles can bend the trunk toward the same side, so the right core muscles can bend the trunk to the right and the left core muscles can bend the trunk to the left. During a suitcase carry, these muscles function isometrically (statically) as they resist trunk side bending; therefore, holding a weight on your right side predominantly trains your left core muscles, and vice versa. Compared to a farmer’s carry, using twice as much load, a suitcase carry will impart a greater challenge to those core muscles opposite to the weight, which is demonstrated by greater core muscle EMG activity occurring during a suitcase carry.Throughout the exercise technique section, it advises avoiding letting your hips sag or hike up, as these are two of the most common push-up technique errors. A key sign of letting your hips sag is if your hips contact the ground before your chest. On the other hand, key signs of excessive hip hiking include your face touching the ground before your chest or needing to extend your neck to look ahead to avoid hitting the ground with your face. Hip hiking can result from inappropriate overcorrection to avoid hip sag, while hip sag can result from insufficient ab muscle activity as gravity exerts a downward force on the hips. Keeping your glutes squeezed and your abdominals tight throughout your set, as you would during a plank, is the most straightforward way to prevent these errors.

Another common error is starting to push up before the chest reaches the ground, which limits range of motion and reduces the extent to which the working muscles are lengthened. This can occur to complete more reps in a set, since the bottom of the push-up is the most challenging part of the rep. Plenty of people lack the strength to perform a full range of motion standard push-up with quality technique, but if you cannot currently do so, it would be more productive to select a regressed variation that allows you to train through a full range of motion. For instance, you can perform full range of motion push-ups with your knees on the ground to reduce the difficulty and allow you to train at the same shoulder and elbow angles that are present with the standard version of the push-up. Strength gains exhibit a meaningful degree of specificity to the joint angles that are trained, so a partial range of motion push-up is not the most efficient means of developing strength at the bottom of a push-up. While push-ups have not been directly tested, training through a full range of motion during other exercises, such as squats, has been shown to be more effective at inducing muscle hypertrophy than training through an abbreviated range that loads the working muscles at shorter lengths. Consequently, selecting a push-up variation that allows you to train through a full range of motion may be better for muscle growth than one that limits your range.Relative to most biceps curl variations, this exercise allows you to train your biceps in a more lengthened position by positioning your upper arm behind your torso, which situates your shoulder in an extended position. Both the long head and the short head of the biceps originate from the scapula and have shoulder flexion moment arms, resulting in greater lengthening in an extended shoulder position compared to a neutral or flexed shoulder position. However, the brachialis and brachioradialis, which are the two other primary elbow flexor muscles (respectively present in the lower half of the upper arm and the forearm), originate from the humerus and are not lengthened or shortened by modifying the shoulder position.

The available evidence indicates that training a variety of muscles at longer peak lengths, up to a certain point, is likely to promote greater hypertrophy than training at shorter peak lengths. Consequently, it is rational to hypothesize that curling with an extended shoulder position may train the biceps somewhat more effectively than curling with a neutral or flexed shoulder position. Attarieh et al. (2025) provided direct insight into this matter by assigning 15 untrained men to a 10-week single-arm underhand-grip cable curl intervention, performed twice per week for 3-5 sets of 8-12 reps to failure. For each participant, one arm performed a preacher curl facing toward the cable station with 50 degrees of shoulder flexion, while the other arm performed a Bayesian curl through the same elbow range of motion, facing away from the cable station with 50 degrees of shoulder extension. Assigning one arm of each participant to a different curl variation helps control for many individual factors (e.g., genetics, diet, lifestyle, prior training history) that can confound results when each person is assigned to only one exercise. Following the intervention, the thickness of both the biceps and brachialis similarly increased for the two curl groups. The absence of any difference for the brachialis is unsurprising given that it does not cross the shoulder joint. However, similar biceps growth across the two exercises suggests that the biceps may respond differently to lengthened training than other biarticular (i.e., crossing two joints) muscles. Other biarticular muscles like three of the hamstrings, the calf’s gastrocnemii, the quad’s rectus femoris, and the long head of the triceps have been reported to experience significantly greater growth when trained with a variation that loads them at longer lengths compared to a variation that loads them at shorter lengths. While the difference was not statistically significant, it is worth noting that the average increase in biceps size still slightly favored the Bayesian curl. The preacher curl group increased mean thickness at the proximal (closest to the shoulder), middle, and distal (closest to the elbow) sites of the biceps, respectively, by 6%, 7%, and 7%, while the Bayesian curl group increased thickness by 9% at all three sites. On the other hand, the brachialis increased thickness by an average of 8% in the Bayesian curl group and by 10% in the preacher curl group. These small differences may be due to chance and measurement error, but additional evidence indicates that curling from an extended shoulder position may train the biceps more effectively than curling from a flexed shoulder position.

Through a study presented as a conference abstract that did not undergo peer review, Kobayashi et al. (2024) described an experiment quite similar to the one conducted by Attarieh et al. (2025). Kobayashi et al. (2024) assigned 21 untrained participants to a 12-week intervention in which they trained single-arm dumbbell curls twice per week for 5 sets of 10 reps each session. For each participant, one arm performed a preacher curl with 50 degrees of shoulder flexion, while the other arm performed an incline curl with 50 degrees of shoulder extension. After the intervention, the authors reported that the incline and preacher curl groups, respectively, increased biceps volume by 18% and 14.9%, which was a statistically significant difference between groups. However, the preacher curl group showed a 21% increase in brachialis size, exceeding the 17% increase observed in the incline curl group. Similarly, the brachioradialis increased its volume by a greater degree for the preacher curl group (13%) than the incline curl group (10%). Whenever a study is presented only as an abstract, not submitted for peer review in a journal, the validity of its results should be appraised with a healthy dose of skepticism. Nonetheless, Kobayashi et al. (2024) included renowned researchers in this field who have produced high quality peer reviewed publications, so their findings are worth mentioning despite the limitations from this form of evidence.

Kassiano et al. (2025) also compared the effects of training dumbbell preacher curls at approximately 45 degrees of shoulder flexion with dumbbell incline curls at approximately 30 degrees of shoulder extension. They assigned 63 untrained women to either group, which performed 3 sets of 8-12 reps until failure twice per week for eight weeks. In contrast to Attarieh et al. (2025) and Kobayashi et al. (2024), Kassiano et al. (2025) measured elbow flexor thickness of the biceps and brachialis combined at three different upper arm sites, so we cannot cleanly differentiate whether these two muscles may have individually responded. Following the intervention, thickness of the proximal site increased by 11.2% for the incline curl group, exceeding the 8.3% increase for the preacher curl group. While the two groups experienced similar increases in middle site muscle thickness, the preacher curl group's increase in thickness of 10.3% at the distal site was greater than the incline curl group’s 6.4% increase at this distal site. While speculative in nature, these regional differences may be influenced by the biceps reflecting a greater proportion of total elbow flexor muscle thickness at the proximal site than the distal site, which is where the brachialis may be thicker.

If the biceps grew more from incline curls, it would be sensible that the total elbow flexor muscle thickness would favor the incline curl group at the proximal site, most reflective of biceps size relative to brachialis size. Similarly, it would be sensible that the total elbow flexor muscle thickness would favor the preacher curl group at the distal site, most reflective of brachialis size relative to biceps size, if the brachialis grew more from preacher curls. More likely than the shoulder position itself, the resistance profile of free-weight preacher curls may contribute to the brachialis being trained somewhat more effectively when the shoulder is flexed. When the resistance is provided by gravity acting on a free weight, resistive torque on the elbow will be greatest when the forearm is parallel to the ground and least when the forearm is perpendicular to the ground. The elbow flexor muscles are most lengthened at the starting position of any curl when the elbow is fully straight. At this point during an incline dumbbell curl, the arm is perpendicular to the ground, so resistive torque does not act on the elbow in this position. However, the flexed shoulder position present during a dumbbell preacher curl prompts the arm not to be perpendicular to the ground in the starting position, so resistive torque acts on the elbow flexor muscles when they reach their longest lengths during each rep. Even though the brachialis reaches the same lengths during each variation, the ability to be meaningfully loaded at its longest length during a preacher curl potentially may amplify the stimulus provided to this muscle when using free weights. This may also explain why Kobayashi et al. (2024) found that the brachioradialis experienced greater growth during the dumbbell preacher curl.

While the resistance profile may have an effect, its role remains speculative at this time, and we cannot draw strong conclusions about the differential effects of training curls from a flexed versus an extended shoulder position. There is reason to think that curls with an extended shoulder position may train the biceps more effectively than curls with a flexed shoulder position, and that curls with tension at the start of each rep (e.g., preacher curls) may be ideal for training the brachialis and brachioradialis. However, the evidence is insufficient to support either possibility with a high degree of confidence. Nonetheless, you can be confident that curls can effectively train your primary elbow flexor muscles, regardless of whether they involve an extended or flexed shoulder position and whether they use high resistance at the start of each rep. These variables may have some effect, but biceps curl variations are far more similar than different, and you have many viable options from which to select to develop your arms.A push-up and a bench press can be performed with essentially the same shoulder and elbow motion, thereby training the pressing muscles in a very similar manner. During either type of exercise with hands spaced apart by the same distance, the pec major and front delt can perform the same shoulder functions, while the triceps can perform the same elbow function. Two studies have compared changes in muscle size after participants completed a push-up intervention or a flat barbell bench intervention, and both found that the two exercises induced similar magnitudes of pec major growth. One of these studies measured triceps growth and found it was similar between the two groups. The lack of any difference in muscle growth aligns with other studies’ findings that push-ups and bench presses yield similar degrees of EMG activity in the pec major and triceps when resistance is equated between the exercises, but some research has found greater front delt EMG activity to occur when bench pressing.

Studies have equated resistance in various ways, such as by adding elastic bands or a weighted vest to push-ups, up to a rep max that the bench press also uses. Alternatively, research has attempted to equate resistance by loading the bench press with the weight measured by scales under someone’s hands during push-ups (as the average of the top and bottom positions). However, this approach results in each bench press rep having a higher intensity than each push-up rep, with a particularly profound difference for females, so it does not provide the ideal comparison for EMG research. For instance, the available evidence indicates that individuals support about 70-75% of their bodyweight through their hands at the bottom of a standard push-up. When loading a barbell with this proportion of body weight, people, on average, can complete significantly more push-up reps than bench press reps to fatigue. Consequently, EMG research using this approach may be comparing a high intensity bench press to a moderate intensity push-up. This could lead to higher EMG values during the bench press than if a study used a bench press load that allowed participants to perform the same number of reps to fatigue as on a push-up.

Although less studied than the upper body muscles, the rectus abdominis of the core has been shown to exhibit significantly greater EMG activity during push-ups than during bench presses. Other abdominal muscles are likely to be more active during a push-up, as they function to resist lumbar extension and thereby prevent the hips from sagging downward. When resistance is properly equated, a push-up can likely train the upper body’s pressing muscles similarly to a bench press while also training the core muscles similarly to a tall plank, an advantage the bench press lacks. Kohiruimaki et al. (2019) examined how well push-up variations can develop the abdominals by assigning eight previously untrained men to an 8-week intervention in which they performed suspended push-ups in three weekly sessions, with three sets each. When performing this push-up variation, participants held handles attached to suspension straps with their feet elevated to the height of their hands. In addition to the thickness of the triceps increasing by 16%, the thickness of the rectus abdominis and external abdominal oblique, respectively, increased by 27% and 14% after the intervention. This is an impressive magnitude of abdominal muscle growth for an exercise training the abs in a stabilizing manner rather than as prime movers, and it provides notable evidence supporting the ability of suspended push-ups to train the abdominal muscles effectively. However, we cannot necessarily generalize these findings to other push-up variations, as suspended push-ups have been shown to elicit significantly greater EMG activity in the abdominal muscles than more stable traditional push-ups.

Another notable difference between the two exercises is that push-ups allow your scapulae (shoulder blades) to move freely throughout each rep. During a push-up, you can fluidly retract your scapulae during the descent and protract your scapulae during the ascent since you do not have your scapulae pressed into a bench. While you can try to do so when bench pressing, the bench contacting your scapulae can make it more difficult to move them fluidly and with sound technique. To ensure your scapulae are retracted at the bottom of a bench press rep (when scapular retraction can particularly enhance shoulder stability and performance), standard bench press technique involves setting up in a position of scapular retraction and maintaining it throughout the set.

In a push-up position, gravity exerts a force that will cause your scapulae to retract if unopposed, so the scapular protractor muscles are directly resisted as you push up and transition from a retracted scapulae position to near a neutral position at the top. They similarly act eccentrically to control the dynamic scapular retraction that occurs during the lowering phase. Even when attempting to keep their scapulae fully retracted, some scapular protraction and retraction motion likely occurs during bench press, whereas push-ups likely train the scapular protractor muscles to a greater extent. If this occurs, the serratus anterior and pec minor, which are the two primary scapular protractor muscles, should be more effectively trained by push-ups than bench presses. However, only one study has measured changes in primary scapular protractor muscle size after a horizontal press training intervention. That study found the cross-sectional area of the pec minor to increase by 11.5% after 26 untrained males completed a 10-week intervention of flat wide grip Smith machine bench pressing. While this finding provides evidence that bench pressing can meaningfully develop the pec minor (which experienced the same magnitude of relative growth as the triceps), it did not include a push-up training group for comparison. Consequently, the potential for push-ups to train the serratus anterior and pec minor more effectively than bench pressing is speculative in nature.Push-up variations with the standard open-hand position place the wrist in a high degree of extension, which is not inherently injurious to the wrist. However, some individuals have a maximal pain-free wrist extension range of motion that is less than that required to perform a push-up in this position. Other people may have the wrist range of motion needed to reach this position without pain when minimally loaded, but experience notable discomfort when bearing a notable magnitude of force through their wrists in a highly extended position. Importantly, a neutral wrist position can also be used for push-ups, and a substantial proportion of individuals who experience wrist pain or discomfort with extended-wrist push-up variations may feel completely fine if they use a neutral wrist position instead. This database includes knuckle push-ups and neutral grip deficit push-ups as push-up variations that use a neutral wrist position, but the knuckle position or neutral grip handles can be readily substituted for the standard open hand position used for the vast majority of other push-up variations.The diamond push-up is a more advanced variation than the standard push-up, and it has been measured to produce greater average EMG activity in both the triceps and pec major than the standard push-up or wide grip push-up. Given the greater difficulty per rep, it is strongly recommended that you become proficient with standard push-ups, performed through a full range of motion with quality technique, before attempting diamond push-ups.The purpose of the incline push-up is to strengthen and develop the same muscles used in a standard push-up while using a lower resistance magnitude that can be scaled to an individual’s current pressing strength. This differs from other incline pressing exercises, such as incline bench press variations, which involve a different plane of shoulder motion than flat bench press variations. People typically use an incline bench press to shift training emphasis from the lower pec major to the upper pec major and the front delt, compared with a flat bench press. When setting up with the arms forming approximately 90-degree angles with the torso in the starting position, an incline push-up allows you to train through nearly the same plane of shoulder motion as a standard push-up. Consequently, these two push-up variations involve similar ratios of contributions from the pressing muscles. In contrast, a decline push-up performed with your feet elevated would allow you to train through the same shoulder plane of motion as an incline bench press and correspondingly provide a similar stimulus to the chest and shoulder muscles.A kettlebell deadlift can train the same muscles as a conventional deadlift with a barbell in a very similar manner. However, the range of motion may somewhat differ depending on the type of kettlebell you are using. The handles of competition kettlebells, which all have the same standardized size regardless of weight and are often color-coded, will be about 2in (5cm) higher off the ground than a barbell loaded with full diameter plates (such as bumper plates or standard 45lb (20kg) plates). Naturally, the difference will be greater if a barbell is loaded with smaller diameter plates. Regular cast-iron kettlebells increase in height as their weights increase, so deadlifting one of these kettlebells may require a somewhat greater or lesser range of motion than with a conventional deadlift, depending on the size of the kettlebell. Nonetheless, the range of motion can be the same if you elevate yourself (like during a deficit deadlift) or the kettlebell (like during a block pull). When using the same weight, a kettlebell deadlift will also be more challenging for your grip strength because a kettlebell lacks the textured knurling that a barbell provides to help you hold the weight.

However, the primary meaningful difference between kettlebell deadlifts and barbell deadlifts is that kettlebell deadlifts can provide a limited level of resistance. The lower body muscles that lift the weight and the back muscles that support the trunk during any deadlift variation can become quite strong when consistently training them over time. It is very common for healthy, able-bodied individuals to deadlift more than their bodyweight after a reasonably short period of appropriate deadlift training. The heaviest kettlebell available at most gyms weighs less than 100lb (45kg), so the heaviest kettlebell accessible to most people may not be sufficiently challenging for deadlifts, requiring many reps per set, which is inefficient for strength training. That said, the kettlebell deadlift can be an excellent variation for individuals new to deadlifting and those looking to use kettlebell loads.Compared to using a shoulder width grip for a conventional deadlift, the snatch grip deadlift situates the bar further below your hands, like during a deficit deadlift. Consequently, you will require a greater degree of knee flexion and/or hip flexion in order to reach the bar during a snatch grip deadlift while keeping your lower back in a similar position to a conventional deadlift. A proportion of people will already reach the end range of hip flexion at the bottom of a conventional deadlift, so they will require greater knee flexion to achieve the starting position for a snatch grip deadlift. With the deeper joint angles, you will need to have a greater degree of mobility to perform a snatch grip deadlift with appropriate positioning compared to a conventional deadlift. Additionally, a snatch grip deadlift will also be more challenging than a conventional deadlift, so you will not be able to use as much weight when performing the same number of reps as a conventional deadlift. Currently, no study has yet compared the snatch grip deadlift to the conventional deadlift, but the deeper knee angles with the snatch grip deadlift are most likely accompanied by a greater contribution from the quads during the initial portion of the lifting phase. The closer to horizontal trunk position that is typically present in a snatch grip deadlift will also most likely tax various back muscles to a greater degree as they function to resist trunk flexion and support the shoulder girdle.Performing the flutter kick with the head and upper back lifted off the ground constitutes a more advanced variation of the flutter kick with the head on the ground. When the upper back is off the ground, the abdominals have to work to prevent the thoracic spine from extending, as they are already functioning to prevent the lumbar spine from extending, which increases the intensity of the exercise.When the feet are held off the ground during this variation, you naturally will not be able to utilize any leg drive like you could when performing a standard bench press. Driving your feet into the ground immediately as you press the bar off your chest can help you push the bar horizontally from your lower chest toward your shoulders, facilitating a more mechanically advantageous bar path than if you moved the bar exclusively in a vertical line. For someone (such as a powerlifter) aiming to bench press as much weight as possible, effective use of leg drive can increase the maximum weight that can be bench pressed. However, whether you effectively incorporate leg drive into your bench is largely irrelevant for training muscle growth, and you can still develop high pressing strength without focusing on leg drive.

Besides removing leg drive, bench pressing with your feet off the ground can also reduce stability since you are removing two points of contact that could otherwise assist in stabilizing your body. This effect can be more pronounced if the bench you use is narrow relative to the width of your body, which reduces stability compared to if you used a wider bench. A rather unstable exercise can be suboptimal for training to increase muscle size and strength, but lifters accustomed to the feet-up bench press often find that this variation is sufficiently stable when using a bench of appropriate size and width.

Another effect of keeping your feet off the ground is that it limits the extent to which you can arch your back. You can intentionally perform a bench press with your feet on the ground while maintaining a mild arch, as in the feet-up variation, but lifters often use a greater arch when pressing with their feet on the ground. Compared with a mild arch, using a more pronounced back arch can lift the lower chest higher off the bench, which shortens the distance the bar must travel to reach the lower chest. Setting up with a greater arch can often allow someone to lift greater loads compared to if someone set up with a mild arch, but shortening the range of motion in this manner is likely suboptimal when training for muscle growth. As with leg drive, employing an aggressive arch can be advantageous for demonstrating bench press strength, but it is no more beneficial than a mild arch when training to increase muscle size and improve overall pressing strength.Tempo variations are normally characterized by a meaningfully slower lowering phase than the standard variation for a particular movement, and they often include a pause at the bottom of the rep, but the pause can be optional. The combination of a slow descent and a pause at the bottom can be quite effective for refining technique and allows you to train the working muscles effectively with lighter loads than you would otherwise use with the standard variation. When training at the same proximity to failure, a slow lowering phase (lasting a few to several seconds) can elicit the same amount of muscle growth as a quicker descent that is still reasonably controlled. While a slow descent is not necessarily superior for training your muscles to generate greater force, performing a tempo exercise may augment your ability to express your strength with a particular lift if the tempo variation helps you find a more efficient technique. However, intentionally slowing the lifting phase is likely suboptimal for strength development and does not benefit muscle hypertrophy, as it may reduce the number of muscle fibers you activate during the lifting phase compared with a more explosive intent. Consequently, tempo variations typically include a lifting phase that can be performed as quickly as possible with proper technique, as with other variations.

Different lifters will naturally use different speeds for the phases of a tempo lift, and it can be useful to track specifically how long you are aiming each phase to last, since that will affect how much you can use for a particular number of reps. There is no single way to perform a tempo lift effectively, so you try different lowering phases and pause durations. When performing a 3-second lowering phase, a 2-second pause, and an explosive lifting phase, lifters often log this as 3-2-X tempo. The first two values indicate the time of the lowering phase and the pause in seconds, while the “X” indicates the intent to perform the lifting phase explosively. Alternatively, someone performing a tempo lift without a pause may record it as a 3-0-X tempo.Pause variations can be performed with the pause at any point in the lift’s range of motion, so where the pause is utilized can be individualized to address someone’s specific weakness or technical deficiencies. You can certainly try pausing at different points in the rep; pausing near the start of the lifting phase, when the working muscles are at their longest length, can generally be an effective approach. During the pause portion, muscles act isometrically (statically), without changing their length, in contrast to concentric shortening during the lifting phase or eccentric lengthening during the lowering phase. Compared with performing isometric exercise at shorter muscle lengths, training isometrically at longer lengths may contribute to greater muscle hypertrophy and improved strength across a wider range of the lift. Isometrically training at long muscle lengths can transfer to better performance at short lengths, while isometrically training at short lengths is comparatively more limited in its ability to improve strength at long muscle lengths.

Along with having greater direct transfer to strength throughout the range of motion, pausing near the start of the lifting phase can often improve your positioning during subsequent parts of the lifting phase. If you begin lifting in a mechanically advantageous position, it may be easier to complete the rep than if you start with suboptimal positioning.

Increasing strength at the start of the lifting phase can make it easier to complete the initial portion of the rep, which may increase speed and reduce difficulty in subsequent parts of the lift. If you have greater momentum entering into a later portion of the rep, you can be less likely to fail than compared to if the bar was moving quite slowly into that point from having to grind to begin the lifting phase. For instance, lifters who consistently fail their bench press at halfway up may be able to increase the weight they can handle before reaching their limit at that point. When the bar already has meaningful upward momentum that was generated by an explosive start off the chest, you will not have to generate as much force midway through the lift compared to if the bar moved with a lesser velocity from the start. To effectively transfer improved starting strength to increased bar speed with a touch-and-go bench press, include both the touch-and-go and pause variations in your training. Exclusively training the pause variation may fail to optimize your technique when you're not pausing, so keep that in mind if you prioritize bench press volume without pauses.Pause variations can be performed with the pause at any point in the lift’s range of motion, so where the pause is utilized can be individualized to address someone’s specific weakness or technical deficiencies. While you can certainly try pausing at different points in the rep, pausing near the start of the lifting phase (when the working muscles are at their longest lengths) can generally be an effective approach. During the pause portion, muscles act isometrically (statically), without changing their length, in contrast to concentrically shortening during the lifting phase or eccentrically lengthening during the lowering phase. Compared to performing isometric exercise at shorter muscle lengths, training isometrically at longer lengths may contribute to greater muscle hypertrophy and improved strength across a wider portion of the lift. Isometrically training at long muscle lengths can transfer to better performance at short lengths, while isometrically training at short lengths is comparatively more limited in its ability to improve strength at long muscle lengths.

Along with having greater direct transfer to strength throughout the range of motion, pausing near the start of the lifting phase can often improve your positioning during subsequent parts of the lifting phase. If you begin lifting in a mechanically advantageous position, it may be easier to complete the rep than if you start with suboptimal positioning. For instance, if your hips shoot up prematurely when squatting out of the hole, you can be pitched forward excessively and have a greater difficulty with the rest of your squat. Even if you consistently fail midway through the lifting phase, starting your ascent with optimal positioning can increase the load you can handle before reaching your limit at that point.

Increasing strength near the start of the lifting phase can also make it easier to complete the initial portion of the rep, which may build greater speed and reduce difficulty in subsequent parts of the lift. If you have greater momentum entering into a later portion of the rep, you can be less likely to fail compared to if the bar was moving quite slowly into that point from having to grind to begin the lifting phase. To have improved starting strength effectively transfer to increased bar speed, you just want to make sure that you train the exercise without the pause as well, because exclusively training the pause variation can neglect to optimize the technique you use when not pausing.Pause variations can be performed with the pause at any point in the lift’s range of motion, so where the pause is utilized can be individualized to address someone’s specific weakness or technical deficiencies. You can certainly try pausing at different points in the rep; pausing near the start of the lifting phase, when the working muscles are at their longest length, can generally be an effective approach. During the pause phase, muscles act isometrically (statically), without changing their length, in contrast to concentric shortening during the lifting phase or eccentric lengthening during the lowering phase. Compared with performing isometric exercise at shorter muscle lengths, training isometrically at longer lengths may contribute to greater muscle hypertrophy and improved strength across a wider portion of the lift. Isometric training at long muscle lengths can transfer to improved performance at short lengths, whereas isometric training at short lengths is comparatively less effective at improving strength at long muscle lengths.

Along with having greater direct transfer to strength throughout the range of motion, pausing near the start of the lifting phase, you can often improve positioning during subsequent parts of the lifting phase. If you begin lifting in a mechanically advantageous position, it may be easier to complete the rep than if you start with suboptimal positioning. Failing a deadlift near the top of the lift often results from suboptimal positioning during the first quarter of the lifting phase. In many instances, refining technique and improving strength during the first quarter of the lifting phase can allow someone to approach the end of the lift with more advantageous positioning, making it easier to complete successfully.

Increasing strength near the start of the lifting phase can also make it easier to complete the initial portion of the rep, which may build greater speed and reduce difficulty in subsequent parts of the lift. If you have greater momentum entering into a later portion of the rep, you can be less likely to fail than compared to if the bar was moving quite slowly into that point from having to grind to begin the lifting phase. To effectively transfer improved starting strength to increased bar speed, make sure you train the exercise without the pause as well, because training only the pause variation can neglect to optimize your technique when you're not pausing.The available evidence clearly indicates that horizontal pressing exercises can induce significant triceps growth, consistent with biomechanical and EMG data predictions. Across studies that measured changes in triceps and pecs major size, it is also clear that bench pressing typically results in greater pecs major growth than triceps growth on average. At least seven different studies have measured changes in the size of the pec major and the whole triceps after a bench press intervention, and each one found the pec major to grow by a greater percentage than the triceps. When pooling all data, the pec major showed approximately twice the magnitude of average growth relative to the triceps after bench press training interventions. Most studies measuring changes in triceps size have examined the triceps as a single muscle group rather than the three individual muscles. However, the specific response of the long head of the triceps almost certainly contributes to why the triceps as a whole does not grow as much from bench pressing as the pec major.

All three heads of the triceps function as primary elbow extensors, and elbow extension is the only function of the medial and lateral heads, which originate from the humerus and exclusively cross the elbow joint. In contrast, the long head originates from the scapula (shoulder blade) and crosses the shoulder joint. Although its shoulder function is less well studied than that of other muscles, the available evidence indicates that it contributes to shoulder extension and adduction, owing to its origin on the scapula. During the lifting phase of most bench press variations, the shoulder moves through a combination of flexion in the sagittal plane and horizontal flexion in the transverse plane. When the long head of the triceps contracts, it cannot selectively function at the elbow without simultaneously imparting a pulling force on the shoulder joint. Consequently, when bench pressing, the desirable elbow extension torque produced by the long head is coupled with undesirable shoulder extension torque that directly opposes the front delt and upper pec major. On the other hand, contraction of the medial and lateral heads of the triceps is exclusively beneficial to bench press performance. It can correspondingly be more efficient for the nervous system to recruit these two heads to a preferentially greater degree than the long head during an exercise that resists both shoulder flexion and elbow extension. When performing a challenging set of bench pressing, the long head of the triceps may still be effectively trained if it is highly activated due to its contraction producing greater extension torque at the elbow than the shoulder.

Brandão et al. (2020) provided insight into whether this would occur by measuring changes in the size of each head of the triceps and the pec major after 43 untrained participants completed a 10-week training intervention with two sessions per week. One group exclusively performed a flat wide grip barbell bench press, another group exclusively performed a barbell skullcrusher, while two other groups performed a combination of the two exercises in different orders. After the intervention, the bench press-only group experienced an insignificant 2% average increase in long head size, whereas each of the three other groups experienced significant increases in long head size ranging from 14-18%. The medial head also increased in size by 14-16% for the three groups, including the skullcrusher, but it only grew by an average of 7% in the bench press-only group, which was not a statistically significant change. However, the lateral head grew similarly well by about 7% in the three groups, including the bench press, but it failed to grow at all in the skull crusher-only group. For comparison, the pec major increased in size by 9% in the bench press-only group, which exceeded the 4.8% increase in total triceps muscle size.

Wanttaja (2020) also measured changes in the size of the long head and lateral head of the triceps after 17 men with at least one year of upper body lifting experience completed a 10-week flat Smith machine bench press intervention. Using specialized equipment, one group exclusively trained the concentric (lifting) phase of the bench press, while the other exclusively trained the eccentric (lowering) phase. After the intervention, the long head increased in size by 2% and 5%, respectively, in the concentric and eccentric training groups. The lateral head additionally increased in size by 4% and 14%, respectively, in the concentric and eccentric training groups. Outside a laboratory, you will be training both the concentric and eccentric phases of a bench press, and this conventional style of training has been reported by other studies to produce similar magnitudes of muscle growth as eccentric only training. Wakahara et al. (2013) also found that a 12-week flat dumbbell bench press training intervention increased total triceps muscle volume without significantly increasing the thickness of the long head.

Although they trained different bench press variations, the results from the three aforementioned studies indicate that the long head of the triceps is unlikely to be trained as effectively as the other triceps heads during bench pressing. Other biarticular muscles have been found not to grow significantly from multi-joint exercises that oppose one of their functions at a joint while training another function at a different joint.

For instance, the rectus femoris is the one biarticular quad muscle that functions as a hip flexor in addition to a knee extensor, whereas the three other quad muscles function only as knee extensors. Squats, which resist both knee extension and hip extension, effectively develop the three quad muscles that exclusively function as knee extensors. However, most studies that have measured rectus femoris size before and after a back squat intervention have found no increase in size; these studies include all studies of participants with resistance training experience. Just as the elbow extension torque from the long head of the triceps is beneficial to bench press performance, so too is the knee extension torque from the rectus femoris beneficial to squat performance. However, just as the shoulder extension torque from the long head of the triceps is deleterious to bench press performance, so too is the hip flexion torque from the rectus femoris deleterious to squat performance. Correspondingly, engagement of the monoarticular heads of the triceps is more efficient than the long head when bench pressing, just like engagement of the monoarticular quads is more efficient than the rectus femoris. Both of these biarticular muscles can be developed more effectively with a single joint exercise, such as a machine leg extension for the rectus femoris and a lying skullcrusher for the long head of the triceps.

In addition to Brandão et al. (2020), two other studies have measured changes in the size of the long head of the triceps after participants performed an intervention consisting exclusively of a single joint elbow extension exercise. Both of those studies assessed one group of participants training cable triceps pushdowns and the other group training overhead cable triceps extensions. The two different groups experienced significant growth in the long head of the triceps in both studies, providing further evidence that the long head is highly likely to be trained most effectively by single joint elbow extension exercises. During this type of exercise, the shoulder extension torque that contraction of the long head generates is inconsequential to performance, in contrast to bench press variations.

While bench pressing can contribute to meaningful triceps development, it simply cannot replace single joint elbow extension exercises if you wish to optimize total triceps development. The poor stimulus of bench pressing may be particularly noteworthy for the long head of the triceps, which is the largest of the three triceps heads, accounting for 44% of total triceps volume. Consequently, you cannot optimize your total arm muscularity without including a triceps “isolation” exercise that can robustly train the long head to a considerably greater degree than what bench pressing provides.*Key takeaway: Research has found seated hamstring curls to stimulate significantly greater hamstring growth than lying hamstring curls, which are like to train the hamstrings similarly to standing hamstring curls.* 

All four of the hamstring muscles function as knee flexors and will consequently be trained by any hamstring curl variation. However, the hip position you adopt for a variation can affect the degree of hamstring growth that exercise is likely to stimulate. The hip position is relevant because three of the four hamstring muscles originate from the pelvis (on the ischial tuberosity, which is also known as the “sit bone”) and cross the back of the hip joint. This allows them to function as hip extensors, shortening as the hip extends and lengthening as the hip flexes. Since they cross two joints, the semitendinosus, semimembranous, and biceps femoris long head are known as the biarticular (crossing two joints) hamstrings. In contrast, the biceps femoris short head originates from the femur (thigh bone) and exclusively crosses the knee joint, so it is unaffected by the hip position.

A seated hamstring curl places the hip into a meaningfully flexed hip position and allows the biarticular hamstrings to be trained at longer lengths than a lying hamstring curl, which situates the hip in only a mildly flexed position. Standing hamstring curl machines also commonly place the hip in a mildly flexed position, resulting in the hamstring being trained in a manner similar to that of lying hamstring curls. However, certain standing models permit you to lean forward meaningfully, which can place the hip into a higher degree of hip flexion than lying hamstring curl machines.

Like with other biarticular muscles (such as the gastrocnemii, rectus femoris, and long head of the triceps), the available body evidence indicates that the biarticular hamstrings are likely to experience greater hypertrophy when trained at long lengths compared to shorter lengths. Maeo et al. (2020) assigned 20 participants without resistance training experience to train single leg hamstring curls for 12 weeks. For each participant, one leg trained seated hamstring curls with the hip flexed by approximately 90 degrees, while the other leg trained lying hamstring curls with the hip flexed by approximately 30 degrees. Each exercise was performed through the same 90-degree knee range of motion for 5 sets of 10 reps twice per week. Assigning one leg of each participant to a different training group can help control for many individual factors (e.g., genetics, diet, lifestyle) that could confound results when each person is assigned to only one group. Consequently, this study design is a particularly useful means of comparing the effects of two hamstring curl variations. Following the intervention, the biceps femoris short head grew similarly across the two variations, whereas each of the three biarticular hamstrings showed significantly greater growth with the seated hamstring curls.

This is quite meaningful to total hamstring growth because the biarticular hamstring constitutes the overwhelming majority (90% according to some data) of total hamstring muscle volume. Correspondingly, the seated group experienced a 14% increase in total hamstring muscle volume, which exceeded the 9% increase observed in the lying group. While not directly tested, standing hamstring curls would most likely induce hamstring growth, as with lying hamstring curls, if they place the hip in a mildly flexed position. When comparing different hamstring exercises, other studies (one, two) have also found that exercises loading the biarticular hamstring at long lengths (i.e., in a greater angle of hip flexion) resulted in greater total hamstring growth than exercises training them at shorter lengths (i.e., in a lower angle of hip flexion). Consequently, the outcomes reported by Maeo et al. (2020) align with the broader body of evidence available on this topic.

The aforementioned findings indicate that seated hamstring curls are likely the more effective variation for developing your hamstrings, but this does not mean that the other variations are ineffective for training your hamstrings. Sometimes, your gym may have a lying or standing hamstring curl machine that you feel suits your body well, whereas a seated machine simply feels awkward, regardless of how you adjust it. Certain machines do not accommodate all body types equally, and some are designed better than others. If you have the option between a low-quality seated machine and a high-quality lying or standing machine, you should not necessarily ignore the hamstring curl machine that feels like it suits you better just because it trains the hamstrings at short muscle lengths. Furthermore, it is unclear how much effect the hamstring curl variation has on muscle growth when included in a program that also includes other exercises that train the hamstrings in a lengthened position. For instance, if you perform Romanian deadlifts and seated hamstring curls, we lack evidence to determine whether seated hamstring curls still elicit greater long-term hamstring growth than lying hamstring curls. Additionally, incorporating variation into your program can help prevent training from becoming stale for many people. If you have been training seated hamstring curls for a long time and feel that it has become monotonous, there is nothing wrong with switching to a lying or standing variation for a period as a means to enjoy training more. While seated hamstring curls can be an excellent variation to develop the hamstrings, lying or standing hamstring curls may still serve a useful function in your overall training program.The Pallof press is named after John Pallof, a renowned American physical therapist who popularized this exercise for sports injury rehabilitation.If you have limited shoulder mobility, it may be prudent to address your mobility limitations prior to performing challenging behind the neck press sets. This exercise is not inherently injurious to the shoulders, but it requires more shoulder mobility to perform effectively than most pressing variations. Exercises like the scapular wall slide may be of assistance in helping to improve the shoulder mobility that is required to perform behind the neck press comfortably.The JM press is named for JM (John Mark) Blakely, who is an acclaimed American bench press specialist who has bench pressed 710lb (322kg) in the 308lb (140kg) weight class of multi-ply powerlifting. Blakely popularized this exercise as a hybrid between a lying triceps extension and a close grip bench press. He reported using this exercise as a way to develop triceps strength for the bench press with lower shoulder loading than a close grip bench press provides. When putting his bench press strength into perspective, it is worth noting that he primarily competed from 1992-2001. Multi-ply bench press shirts have evolved considerably since then, so the equipment Blakely used did not provide nearly as much assistance for bench pressing as modern multi-ply lifters use. For reference, the equipped bench press world record was 802lb (364kg) in 2001 when Blakely completed his remarkable 710lb (322kg) bench press, but it now exceeds 1,400lb (635kg) as the bench press shirts have evolved.Flat bench pressing is not inherently injurious to the shoulders, but some lifters with pre-existing shoulder issues report that their shoulders feel more comfortable when pressing from a slight decline. Performing a flat bench press with greater back arching can place the shoulders in a position similar to that of a slight decline press with less back arch. However, some people with limited mobility or pre-existing lower back issues cannot comfortably arch to that degree. Compared to using a flat bench, a slight decline is unlikely to detract from the stimulus provided to the upper pec major to any meaningful extent. Consequently, it is reasonable for lifters to use the slight decline bench press as a substitute for flat bench pressing if they are more comfortable with the slight decline.The available evidence clearly supports the conclusion that leg pressing and squatting can effectively increase the size of the quads and gluteus maximus. This is unsurprising, given that a combination of hip and knee extension moves the load during both exercises. To date, no study has compared how a squat intervention compares with a leg press intervention in inducing increases in the size of specific muscles. However, Rossi et al. (2018) assigned untrained participants to one of the three groups performing a 10-week training intervention involving only back squats, only leg presses, or an equal combination of squats and leg presses.

After the intervention, no significant differences existed among the groups with respect to their increases in whole body fat-free mass. Because the entire body was assessed as a single measure, this finding does not necessarily indicate whether the groups experienced similar degrees of growth in any particular muscles. Before and after the intervention, the participants were also tested for their one-rep maxes in the back squat and leg press. The average back squat max increased by approximately 8%, 32%, and 20%, respectively, in the leg press group, squat group, and combination group. On the other hand, the average leg press max increased similarly for each group, with the leg press group and squat group both experiencing 34% increases, while the combination group experienced a 31% increase. It should be unsurprising that squat strength improvements were the greatest in the group training the highest volume of squats and the least in the group not training squats at all. Back squat performance can be strongly influenced by the magnitude of knee and hip extension torque a person can generate, but efficiently expressing that torque output during squatting requires practice of the motor skill of squatting. Especially in untrained individuals, improvements in a maximal squat after training squats can largely result from neural adaptations as individuals learn to squat in a coordinated, efficient manner. Exclusively leg pressing does not allow someone to practice the back squat, and it also does not meaningfully train the spinal extensor muscles that maintain a rigid trunk position during back squatting. Compared with back squatting, leg pressing is less technically demanding; therefore, practicing leg pressing has a smaller effect on leg press performance than practicing squatting has on squat performance.

The finding that the squat only group improved leg press strength to the same degree as the leg press only group indicates that the trunk extension demands present while back squatting did not impair the stimulus provided to lower body muscles. During leg pressing, the combination of knee and hip extension strength will likely be the limiting factor in performance. While lower body strength may also limit back squat performance, lifters may also be limited by isometric (static) trunk extension strength if they are unable to prevent their lumbar or thoracic spine from excessively flexing into a rounded position. If trunk extension strength is the limiting factor during back squatting, the lower body muscles may not be trained as effectively as when lower body strength is the limiting factor. However, this did not seem to be an issue for the squat only group, as demonstrated by its improvement in leg press strength equalling that of the leg press only group. While it may not be a focus of many lifters, it is also worth noting that the back squat group experienced a significantly greater increase in vertical jump performance than the leg press group, which is a finding in agreement with another study comparing these two exercises. Whether such an outcome would occur if the participants were also performing jumps as part of their training programs remains to be seen, but the joint angles and movement pattern of jumping more closely resemble that of back squatting than leg pressing.

In isolation, neither exercise is inherently superior to the other; however, the back squat and leg press are not completely interchangeable. If someone wishes to improve isometric trunk extension strength along with lower body strength and muscle size, training the back squat by itself would be more sensible than training only the leg press. However, training programs typically include a variety of exercises, so the additional spinal erector training that back squats provide may not be as notable in a program that already includes deadlifts and other hip hinge variations that meaningfully engage these muscles. With a certain volume of hip hinge exercises at a high relative intensity in one session, lifters may actually find that they are approaching the current limit to which their lower back can recover, but they may still benefit from further training the quads and gluteus maximus. In this instance, a leg press may be a more suitable exercise to incorporate into the session than the back squat, which may contribute to excessive lower back loading for someone whose lumbar spinal erectors are already fatigued from deadlifting.

Squats and leg presses are ultimately different tools available in your toolbox, and the value that either one provides will be dependent on what you specifically wish to accomplish from using it as part of your border training program.With a certain amount of weight, getting into and out of position for a decline dumbbell press without a spotter may be more challenging than performing the exercise itself. Performing higher rep sets with a lighter load can reduce this challenge to a degree, but you can alternatively select a different decline variation, such as the decline single arm dumbbell bench press, which will naturally be easier to get into and out of position with the lighter load. The slight decline dumbbell bench press can additionally allow you to train decline pressing with a meaningfully lower difficulty for getting into and out of position.With a rather heavy load, getting into the starting position and returning the weight to the ground can be awkward and difficult when setting up a landmine goblet squat with the plates on the ground. This bottoms-up landmine goblet squat effectively circumvents that issue and ensures that the most difficult part of the exercise is your actual set rather than the set-up. The elevated object can also function as safety pins in a power rack, absorbing the load if you fail a rep. When utilizing the same squat depth, load, and volume, the two methods of performing the landmine goblet squat can provide the same stimulus to your muscles. However, they are presented as two distinct exercise pages in this database because the set-up instructions differ for each approach, and the sets begin from different positions. While quite useful for heavy loads, the cons of the bottoms-up method are that it requires additional equipment and takes more time to set up.With a rather heavy load, getting into the starting position and returning the weight to the ground can be awkward and difficult when setting up a landmine goblet squat with the plates on the ground. To circumvent this challenge, you have the option of performing the bottoms-up landmine goblet squat, where you set up with the weight on an elevated object like a plyometric box, aerobic step with risers, or a flat exercise bench. Doing so lets you start from the bottom with the weight in the goblet position and set it by squatting until the plates rest on the object, rather than switching grips before lowering the bar. The elevated object can also function as safety pins in a power rack, absorbing the load if you fail a rep. When utilizing the same squat depth, load, and volume, the two methods of performing the landmine goblet squat can provide the same stimulus to your muscles. However, they are presented as two distinct exercise pages in this database because the set-up instructions differ for each approach, and the sets begin from different positions. While quite useful for heavy loads, the cons of the bottoms-up method are that it requires additional equipment and takes more time to set up.If you lack a V-bar attachment, you can still perform a neutral grip landmine T-bar row with a very similar technique if you grasp the barbell directly at the end of the shaft where the V-bar would otherwise be situated. With this modification, one hand would grasp the barbell, and the other would wrap around the hand holding it. Rowing can feel awkward when your hands are on top of each other, but you still use this approach in the absence of a V-bar attachment. Alternatively, certain landmine attachments have a pair of parallel handles that can be used to perform a neutral grip landmine T-bar row. If you have access to one of these attachments, you can slide it onto the barbell’s sleeve before adding the weight plates and row with the same technique described above. These landmine attachments can be quite comfortable to use, but V-bar attachments are much more commonly available.*Key takeaway: High step-ups likely elicit greater muscle growth and strength gains than low step-ups across a wider range of motion; therefore, it is recommended to progress to high step-ups before moving on to weighted variations.*

Step-up exercises (including step-down variations) can be performed at various step heights, which can affect the joint range of motion used and the stimulus provided to the lower body muscles. Currently, no study has yet investigated how training step-ups through different ranges of motion may influence muscle hypertrophy. However, research on squatting can provide insight into how step-up range of motion most likely affects muscle growth because both types of movements primarily train hip and knee extension.

Kubo et al. (2019) assessed changes in lower body muscle size after participants completed a half squat intervention that involved reaching 90 degrees of knee flexion at end range or a full squat intervention that involved reaching 140 degrees of knee flexion at end range. The participants trained barbell back squats twice per week over the span of 10 weeks, and each session involved 3 sets of 8 reps, except for the initial two sessions. Both groups experienced significant increases in quad muscle volume of essentially the same magnitude, and neither group significantly increased the size of their hamstrings (which aligns with other studies finding that squats are ineffective for developing the hamstrings). However, the researchers found that the full-squat group experienced approximately three times the gluteus maximus growth and slightly more than twice the growth of the hip adductor muscles (which can function as hip extensors like the gluteus maximus) on the inner thigh relative to the half squat group. The hip adductor muscles that Kubo and colleagues specifically assessed were the adductor magnus, adductor longus, and adductor brevis, and the volume of these muscles added to the volume of the gluteus maximus is similar to the volume of the quads based on data from Handsfield et al. (2014). Correspondingly, full squats stimulated a meaningfully greater increase in total lower body muscle size than half squats.

Although not directly tested, the range of motion for step-up variations is likely similar to that of other muscle development tasks. Consequently, high step-ups that position you into a highly flexed hip and knee position are likely superior to low step-ups involving shallower hip and knee angles if your objective is to optimize lower body muscle growth. Furthermore, high step-ups are almost certainly preferable to low step-ups if you wish to increase strength from a position of high hip and knee flexion, since strength gains exhibit joint angle specificity to a notable degree. Kubo et al. (2019) unsurprisingly found that training full squats transferred to increases in half squat 1-RM to a greater degree than training half squats transferred to increases in full squat 1-RM. If you wish your step-ups to transfer to enhanced lower body strength when squatting, your step-ups ideally should include the deepest hip and knee angles present when you squat.

In light of the available evidence, most people who train step-up variations would benefit from progressing toward being able to perform high step-ups with quality technique. Nonetheless, training lower height step-ups with an emphasis on proper technique and controlling the descent can be appropriate for many people who are new to step-ups. This can be a more prudent approach than simply starting with a high step-up and using sloppy technique to move from point A to point B through whatever means necessary. Over time, you can incrementally increase the height of your step-ups until you can perform high step-ups with quality technique. Since training through deep ranges of motion can confer distinct benefits, progressing the range of motion with bodyweight before increasing the load via weighted variations can be a prudent strategy for many lifters. Like with half squats, adding heavier weights to lower step-ups can serve a sport-specific role among athletes (such as track athletes) who wish to focus on increasing force production in shallower joint angles. However, training to maximize performance in specific athletic endeavors is distinct from training to enhance muscularity and overall strength.

Given the benefits that training high step-ups can provide, the “muscles trained at long lengths” classification for most step-up variations specifically refers to high step-ups that begin with hip and knee flexion similar to that achieved at the bottom of a full squat. With a low step-up variation, you naturally will not be training any particular muscles at long lengths.Exercises like bench press, machine chest press, and fly variations can train the pec major primarily through the shoulder horizontal flexion function that each of its regions exhibits. Shoulder horizontal flexion occurs in its purest form when the arms move inward, forming 90-degree angles with the torso throughout a movement such as a flat dumbbell fly. On the other hand, dip variations performed with the “chest dip” technique, using moderately flared elbows, train a combination of shoulder flexion and adduction. In addition to occurring when raising the arm straight out in front of the body, like during a front raise, shoulder flexion occurs when moving your elbows forward if they start behind your body, like during a dip. If your arms are positioned straight out away from your sides, shoulder adduction is the motion that pulls them down toward your sides. When performing a “chest dip” with your elbows flared out, shoulder adduction pulls your elbows in toward the midline of your body.

The sternocostal portion of the pec major that constitutes your middle and lower chest functions as a shoulder adductor, while the clavicular portion that forms your upper chest functions as a shoulder flexor. These functions allow “chest dip” variations to train the pec major meaningfully even though they look very different from other types of chest exercises.

In contrast to “chest dips,” “triceps dips” are performed with the elbows tucked closely to the sides, thereby effectively training shoulder flexion (back-to-front motion) while minimizing shoulder adduction (outward-to-inward motion) demands. Currently, no research has investigated how different dip techniques may affect activity among the different pec major regions. However, the upper pec major will most likely serve a greater role during a “triceps dips” than the middle and lower pec major since the middle and lower regions do not meaningfully contribute to shoulder flexion. While “triceps dips” with fully tucked elbows may train the upper pec major, they most likely largely neglect the bulk of the chest musculature since the middle and lower regions have been measured to constitute about four fifths of total pec major muscle volume.Positioning the seated hip abduction machine’s footplates to the closest setting does not permit many lifters to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion that is possible for this movement. Unless you have a relatively wide frame, the machine often resists when there is still space between your knees, rather than when your knees are in contact.

You can slightly increase the range of motion of your seated machine hip abduction by making a fist with each hand and placing your hands between knee pads and the outer aspect of your lower thighs. With this approach, you would point your knuckles forward with your palms facing down. Alternatively, you may be able to fit certain yoga blocks between your outer thighs and the thigh pads. Either option will allow your thighs to be slightly closer together at the start of each rep, which adducts your hips to a greater degree compared to if your lower thighs/knees directly contact the pads. Consequently, this mild variation in technique allows you to train your hip abductors at longer peak muscle lengths, as hip adduction lengthens them.

Currently, no study has yet investigated how muscle hypertrophy may be influenced by the peak muscle length reached while performing any type of hip abduction exercise. However, research on other muscle groups indicates that training at longer peak lengths, up to a certain point, may promote greater hypertrophy than training at shorter peak lengths. The effect may vary among different muscle groups, and training at very long peak muscle lengths does not necessarily induce greater hypertrophy than training at moderately long peak muscle lengths as data from Larsen et al. (2025) suggests. However, with the exception of one study by Goto et al. (2019), the available evidence indicates that training at longer peak muscle lengths is likely to promote greater, or at least equal, hypertrophy relative to training at shorter peak lengths. Consequently, training your hip abductors at slightly longer muscle lengths with the aforementioned technique may be more effective.Two intervertebral joints (namely L4-L5 and L5-S1) are normally situated below the height of the iliac crests, which form the top of your hip bones. If you set up with the top of your bones contacting the top of the pad, the motion of these two intervertebral joints will be restricted. Some of the muscles (such as the lowest portions of the multifidus and the interspinales lumborum) that function to extend these joints will likely not be trained as effectively as they otherwise could be if the pad were positioned lower. However, in some situations, it may still be beneficial to set the hip pad so its top contacts the tops of your hip bones.

Placing the pad at this high position helps the user perform the exercise as a pure spinal extension while restricting hip motion. When the hips are not fully supported, moving purely at the lumbar and thoracic spine rather than moving the spine simultaneously with the hips is a coordinated motor skill that people may have a difficult time executing initially. Especially when performing a reasonably challenging set, someone may unconsciously be meaningfully using the hip muscles to perform the exercise due to previously learned motor patterns. Essentially locking the hips in place with a high pad placement can teach someone what nearly isolated spinal motion feels like and make it easier to replicate this motor task when lowering the pad in the future. If you personally find it difficult to perform your back extension purely as a spinal movement when setting the top slightly below the tops of your hip bones, training the exercise with a higher pad placement for some time may be warranted.As with many other exercises that have bodyweight variations, you naturally can progress the intensity of your back extension by performing a variation that utilizes an external load (e.g., holding a weight plate) as your strength improves over time. However, you also can modify the intensity to which your trunk extensor muscles must contract during each rep by adjusting your arm position. Relative to if you cross your arms to keep your hands on your shoulders, holding your hands behind your head shifts the center of your upper body’s mass farther away from your back’s intervertebral joints. This results in the force from the weight of your upper body having a greater lever arm to resist your trunk extensor muscles. Consequently, performing the bodyweight back extension with your hands behind your head can function as a progression from the variation with your hands on your shoulders.Reverse hyperextension machines fall into two categories: center-pendulum and side-loaded models. Center-pendulum machines let you add weights to a pair of sleeves positioned in front of your lower legs. Side-loaded machines position the mobile lever arm on the outer side of the machine and let you add weight to a single sleeve or, in some cases, to two sleeves. While all center-pendulum machines are plate-loaded, some side-loaded models are pin-loaded.

A key difference between the two machine types is that side-loaded machines have a stopper under the mobile lever arm that prevents your legs from swinging forward beyond the starting position. Center-pendulum machines lack a stopper and therefore allow a greater possible range of motion, allowing the legs to move forward during the lowering phase. This allows someone to transfer momentum from the lowering phase to the lifting phase in a swinging motion, as the weight plates function like a pendulum’s bob. If you were to swing the weight down with a side-loaded model, the lever arm would simply crash into the stopper, so side-loaded machines naturally facilitate a stricter, more controlled technique.What distinguishes the Yates row from a traditional bent-over barbell row is the torso angle relative to the ground. Relative to the moderately inclined torso position that allows the bar to start near knee level during a Yates row, a traditional bent-over row involves a deeper hip hinge that positions the torso in a closer to horizontal position, enabling the bar to start below knee height. The degree to which these two variations differ will be affected by the position in which someone can perform a traditional bent-over row. While some lifters have the mobility to achieve a nearly horizontal torso position from hinging at their hips during a traditional bent-over row, others will be meaningfully more limited with respect to how deeply they can hip hinge. This can result in the torso being only mildly more inclined during a traditional bent-over row compared to a Yates row for individuals who have mobility limitations, but the difference can be more pronounced for rather mobile lifters.The Yates row is named after Dorian Yates, widely recognized as one of the greatest bodybuilders of all time, with his crowning achievement being six consecutive Mr. Olympia titles from 1992 to 1997. Dorian’s total number of Mr. Olympia wins has only been exceeded by four bodybuilders in history, namely Arnold Schwarzenegger, Phil Heath, Lee Haney, and Ronnie Coleman. Yates received international acclaim for the remarkable size of his back, which he developed by using rows as a foundational component of his training routine. Dorian reported that he previously performed his barbell rows with an underhand grip but switched to an overhand grip after sustaining a biceps tendon rupture. While an underhand grip can promote greater biceps engagement, note that this does not mean that rowing with an underhand grip is inherently dangerous to the biceps.You can perform dead bug variations with your lower back flattened against the ground or in a neutral position that maintains its natural curvature. Neither approach is inherently superior to the other, and your abs must function to resist the extension (arching) of your lumbar spine with both techniques. The flat-back technique may be easier to learn because you can feel your lower back contacting the ground, and you can receive immediate feedback if your lower back begins to arch. In comparison, it can be more challenging to determine whether you are effectively resisting lumbar spine motion when you lack ground feedback with the neutral technique. However, most activities in which you move your extremities while striving to resist lumbar spine motion do not provide immediate tactile feedback on whether you are actually keeping your lumbar spine rigid. Given that motor learning exhibits joint angle specificity, performing a dead bug with a neutral lumbar spine may potentially better prepare you to brace your core effectively during other movements in which you strive to maintain a neutral lumbar spine. Nonetheless, any difference in the extent to which neutral dead bugs and flat-back dead bugs may confer improved performance in other movements remains speculative and has not been empirically tested.A marching dead bug serves as a regressed version of a standard dead bug, which involves the simultaneous motion of one leg and the opposite arm. As an alternative to the technique described on this page, you have the option of keeping both arms directed straight up to the ceiling, which is the starting position for a standard dead bug. However, the hand placement instructed on this page can provide immediate tactile feedback on whether you are appropriately bracing your core and resisting lumbar spine motion. When you are new to this type of movement, receiving this feedback can help you learn what it feels like to move your extremities independently of your trunk. Once you have learned how to do so, you can be better prepared to replicate this bracing ability during other exercises that lack this feedback.While overhead slams can be used to enhance your shoulder extension (the motion of pulling your arms down and back from an overhead position), they are not well-suited to stimulate meaningful increases in the size of any particular muscle. Someone with no experience lifting weights may still see some growth in the lats, teres major, and rear delts by consistently training a sufficiently high volume of overhead slams. However, overhead slams train these muscles quite differently compared to rows, vertical pulls, and pullovers, which are much better suited to stimulate hypertrophy. When tracking volume through the lens of its impact on muscle growth, overhead slams are excluded from that total. In the same way that you would not utilize a hammer to tighten a screw, you should not rely on overhead slams when striving to increase muscle size. The overhead slam is by no means an inherently bad exercise, but it simply is not the proper tool to use for the specific objective of stimulating muscle hypertrophy.In terms of the side of the body in which the working muscles are situated, spine rotation differs from any other action. Right trunk rotation torque is produced by contraction of your right internal abdominal oblique, left external abdominal oblique, and left rotatores muscles (which are small deep muscles that attach to the thoracic spine). On the other hand, left trunk rotation torque is produced by the contraction of your left internal abdominal oblique, right external abdominal oblique, and right rotatores muscles. Neck rotation to a particular side is similarly performed by several muscles located on the side to which the neck rotates, along with the side away from which the neck rotates.

In contrast to how spine rotation to one side is simultaneously performed by muscles on both sides of the spine, side bending (also known as lateral flexion) of the spine is exclusively performed by muscles situated on the side to which the spine bends. Joint actions of any extremity are also always performed by muscles on the same side of that joint action. For instance, any right hip action is only performed by muscles on your right, while any left shoulder action is only performed by muscles on your left.

When a muscle rotates spinal joints to the side in which the muscle lies, that muscle is considered to have an ipsilateral rotation function, but a muscle rotating spinal joints to the opposite side is considered to have a contralateral rotation function. The root “-lateral” is derived from the Latin word for “side,” while the roots “ipsi-” and “contra-” respectively are derived from the Latin words for “same/self” and “opposite/against.” A muscle’s line of pull between its anatomical attachments (origins and insertions) determines whether that muscle can have an ipsilateral or contralateral rotation function.This exercise is known as a hip thrust rather than a glute bridge because your upper back is elevated rather than resting on the ground. However, a Smith machine hip thrust can functionally be closer to a typical glute bridge than a typical hip thrust if your Smith machine prevents you from descending until the weight plates or your buttocks contact the ground. The purpose of elevating the upper back during a free weight or bodyweight hip thrust is to allow you to achieve a greater angle of hip flexion at the bottom of each compared to when your upper back rests on the ground during a glute bridge. If a Smith machine prevents you from reaching a greater angle of hip flexion than you could reach during a typical glute bridge, a Smith machine hip thrust can train the gluteus maximus in essentially the same way that a typical glute bridge does.While not as challenging to learn as Olympic lift variations such as the snatch and split jerk, the barbell push press is not easy to develop technical proficiency in. Prior to attempting the barbell push press, it can be quite beneficial to accrue some experience with the clean grip front squat and the strict version of the standing barbell overhead press. Once you are reasonably competent in performing those two exercises, you can more readily learn how to execute the barbell push press.

If you are unable to achieve a stable front rack position with a clean grip, you likely have mobility restrictions in your wrists and/or shoulders that will limit the degree to which your legs can assist in driving the bar up. In addition to statically stretching your wrists and/or shoulders, repeatedly working into as much of the front rack position as you can without pain can improve your mobility over time. It is common for people to lack the mobility to assume a stable front rack position with a clean grip when attempting it for the first time, so there is no need to become discouraged if you cannot initially do so. If your mobility currently limits your ability to perform the barbell push press, you also have the option of training the dumbbell push press in the meantime.When performing a vertical pulling exercise with a fixed bar, the movement is known as a chin-up if your palms face backward, while it is known as a neutral grip pull-up if your palms face in toward each other. Because gymnastic rings permit your palms to rotate freely, a consensus does not exist in terms of what specifically constitutes a ring chin-up or a ring pull-up. This database describes a ring chin-up as finishing the lifting phase with an underhand grip and starting the pull with a neutral grip, while it describes a ring pull-up as starting the lifting phase with an overhand grip and typically finishing the pull with a neutral grip. While it is rational to view a ring chin-up as requiring you to have an underhand grip at some point during the rep, other valid interpretations of ring chin-ups and ring pull-ups exist beyond what is discussed in this database.Compared to strict pull-ups, kipping pull-ups have been measured to involve significantly lower EMG activity of the lats and biceps when five of each variation were performed by individuals with at least one year of CrossFit training experience. On the other hand, kipping pull-ups were found to produce significantly greater EMG activity of the rectus abdominis, gluteus maximus, and rectus femoris. These results should be unsurprising, since the mechanics of the kipping motion indicate that it should be largely driven by the core and hip musculature, and the momentum generated by these muscles can reduce the demands imparted onto the upper body’s pulling muscles to perform each rep.

It is worth noting that a notable difference in the intensity of a 5-rep set will exist when comparing strict pull-ups to kipping pull-ups in participants who have developed competent technique for both variations. We lack the data to know the magnitude to which the discrepancy existed for the participants of that study, but any person who is skilled at performing kipping pull-ups should be able to complete more kipping pull-ups meaningfully until they become fatigued, compared to strict pull-ups. This would result in a 5-rep set of kipping pull-ups being notably more submaximal than a 5-rep set of strict pull-ups, which could affect the reported EMG values. In practice, people typically perform higher-rep sets of kipping pull-ups than strict pull-ups, so comparing muscle EMG activity between the two variations at the same number of reps is not equivalent to comparing EMG activity during the last 5 reps of sets performed for as many reps as possible.Kipping proficiently involves transitioning efficiently from an arched body position to a hollow body position. Learning how to perform kipping pull-ups can be a meaningfully easier task if you have initially developed competency in achieving these two positions independently, which training the hollow body hold and the superman exercise can provide. If you have a difficult time performing either exercise by itself in a controlled manner, it can be rather challenging to integrate them together into a coordinated, higher velocity movement.During a lateral walk, placing the band just above your ankles can serve as a progression relative to placing it just above your knees, because positioning the band farther from your hips increases the lever arm over which the band acts on your hip. At the same band tension, the longer lever arm results in greater resistive torque toward hip adduction (bringing the thigh toward the body's midline), which requires greater torque production by the hip abductor muscles to overcome. This increased demand on the hip abductor muscles was reflected in the findings of Cambridge et al. (2012) and Lewis et al. (2019), who compared EMG activity during banded lateral walks with the band positioned around the lower thighs or just above the ankles. Both studies found EMG activity of the gluteus medius, gluteus maximus, and tensor fasciae latae (all of which function as hip abductors) to be significantly greater when performing a thigh-banded lateral walk compared to an ankle-banded lateral walk.Any cable chop variation will train the abdominal obliques because they are the strongest trunk rotators in the human body. However, the low-to-high variation potentially may not train the obliques quite as effectively as high-to-low or horizontal cable chops. In addition to primary trunk rotation and side bending, the obliques secondarily function as trunk flexors. During a low-to-high cable chop, the downward line of pull requires you to generate trunk extension torque in addition to trunk rotation torque to move your trunk diagonally away from the pulley. This means that the trunk flexion torque generated by contraction of the obliques may be counterproductive to performance, in contrast to the high-to-low chop (which trains trunk rotation and trunk flexion together) or the horizontal chop (which exclusively trains trunk rotation). Engaging the spinal extensor muscles can counteract the undesirable trunk flexion torque produced by the obliques during a low-to-high chop; however, it is unclear whether the nervous system still somewhat limits oblique engagement relative to a variation that does not train trunk extension with trunk rotation.A seated back extension machine provides resistance to the spinal joints below the roller pad and thus trains the spinal extensor muscles that act on those levels. However, the lever arm of the resistance increases with the distance between the roller pad and the spinal joint, resulting in the greatest resistive torque acting on the lowest lumbar joints. This can prompt the exercise to train lumbar extensor musculature to a preferentially greater degree than the thoracic extensor musculature.
The erector spinae have long muscle fibers that cross many spinal levels since they originate from the sacrum and pelvis and insert onto the thoracic region's ribs. Since their muscle bellies lie in both the lumbar and thoracic regions, a lumbar extension exercise can increase the size of the thoracic extensor musculature even if thoracic extension is not directly resisted. However, deeper intrinsic back muscles (such as the multifidus) have shorter muscle fibers that cross fewer spinal segments. A trunk extension exercise emphasizing thoracic extension rather than lumbar extension may be required to optimize development of the middle and upper thoracic portions of these deeper back muscles, since their muscle fibers are not long enough to span the lumbar region and therefore function as lumbar extensors. Nonetheless, the deep intrinsic back muscles have their own muscle fibers within the lumbar region that will be effectively developed by a lumbar extension focused exercise. The lumbar and thoracic portions of the deep intrinsic back muscles can just function in a more independent manner compared to the long erector spinae.When performing this exercise, you can move your shoulders in essentially the same manner as you would during a dumbbell lateral raise, and these exercises train the same muscle groups. However, the resistance profiles of these variations differ meaningfully. With a dumbbell lateral raise, the shoulder does not experience any resistive torque in a neutral frontal plane position (when your arm is at your side), and the resistive torque steadily increases as the shoulder abducts up to 90 degrees. This occurs because the resistance is provided by a vertically directed gravitational force acting on the dumbbell, and the perpendicular distance between the shoulder joint and the dumbbell in the frontal plane progressively increases as the arm approaches parallelism with the ground.

With a machine lateral raise, resistive torque acts on the shoulder throughout the rep. The specific resistance profile can vary across machines, but many machines provide a flat resistance profile, such that resistive torque is essentially constant throughout the range of motion once the machine’s weight plates separate at the start of a rep. Other machines can use a cam mechanism that contributes to greater resistance torque being present toward the start or end of the exercise’s range of motion. At the start of each rep, where the shoulder abductor muscles are at their longest lengths, these muscles will correspondingly experience meaningful tension during a typical machine lateral raise and minimal tension during a dumbbell lateral raise.You can optionally place a full diameter weight plate (like a 45lb (20kg) plate or thick bumper plate) or an aerobic step underneath, where you will keep your non-working leg’s foot. While not required, standing on a slightly elevated, sturdy surface can provide clearance for your working leg, so you do not have to lean forward to avoid brushing your foot against the ground. What you use as a step should be far enough in front of your working leg that your working leg’s foot does not contact it.Instead of using a moderately staggered stance, you have the option of performing the single arm overhand grip landmine row with a symmetrical approximately shoulder width stance if you prefer. As an alternative to grasping the barbell’s sleeve directly, you can also perform this exercise while grasping a landmine attachment that has a single handle. If you have access to this attachment and wish to use it, slide it onto the barbell’s sleeve before adding the weight plates, then row using the same technique described above.Kelso shrugs are named after the American Paul Kelso (1937-2016), who authored numerous magazine articles and several books on resistance training, including *Kelso’s Shrug Book*. Paul Kelso advocated training direct scapular exercises from a variety of angles rather than relying solely on typical vertical shrugging variations and compound exercises such as rows to train the upper back. One of the scapular exercises about which he wrote is the horizontal shrug that directly trains scapular retraction and has come to be known as the Kelso shrug.As with many other exercises that have bodyweight variations, you naturally can progress the intensity of your glute ham raise by performing a variation that adds an external load (e.g., holding a weight plate) as your strength improves over time. However, you can also modify the intensity to which your hamstrings and lower body's other muscles must contract during each rep by adjusting your arm position. Among arm positions, keeping your arms by your sides with straight elbows keeps your upper body’s center of mass closest to your knees throughout the rep and minimizes difficulty. In comparison, crossing your arms across your chest shifts the center of mass of your upper body further from your knees and increases the intensity. Lifting your arms to hold your hands above your head will further increase the torque from your body weight acting on your knees and further increase the difficulty. Consequently, you can determine which arm position you will use based on the intensity you wish to have for each of your glute ham raise reps.Compared to free weight squats like back squats, front squats, and goblet squats, belt squats will involve a greater ratio of knee loading to hip loading and consequently be more quad-dominant. In a belt squat, the resistance pulls downward from the belt near the hips, resulting in a lever arm acting on the knees that is notably larger than that acting on the hips. This imposes higher demands meaningfully on the quads as the function to extend the knee than the gluteus maximus and adductor magnus, as they function to extend the hip. Free weight squats involve relatively higher resistive lever arms acting on the hip, while still having notable resistive lever arms acting on the knee. Provided that they are performed through a sufficiently long range of motion, free weight squats can effectively train the quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus simultaneously.

When assessing the moment arms, the gluteus maximus and adductor magnus are unlikely to be trained as effectively by belt squats as by free weight squats; however, no study has yet measured muscle growth after a belt-squat training intervention. Consequently, we cannot confidently determine how much difference may exist. When looking at other forms of evidence, it is worth noting that EMG activity of the gluteus maximus has been measured to be lower during a belt squat compared to a back squat, but quad EMG activity has been found to be similar. While belt squats most likely have a higher ratio of quad engagement to gluteus maximus engagement, that does not necessarily mean that belt squats are likely to train the quads any more effectively than back squats. Belt squats may provide a similar stimulus to the quads as free weights squats while just training the hip musculature to a lesser degree.

If your goal is to train the greatest amount of muscle mass, free weight squats are likely preferable to belt squats, but if you want to target the quads while minimizing fatigue elsewhere in the body, belt squats can be quite useful. More evident than the likely reduced hip muscle utilization is the lower spinal extensor muscle demand with belt squats compared with free weight squats. Unsurprisingly, EMG activity of the lumbar extensor muscles has been measured to be significantly lower when belt squatting than back squatting. If you feel that you are approaching the limit from which your lower back can recover, belt squats can help you train your quads like free weight squats without imposing the same recovery cost to your lower back.If you notice that the belt squat machine bottoms out when squatting down before you have reached the full range of motion you wish to perform regardless of how you set up, you have the option of elevating your feet on the platform. To do so, you can place a pair of sturdy elevated objects that will not slide, like deadlift blocks or bumper plates, onto the platform where you will stand. This approach may be more relevant for highly mobile lifters, along with shorter lifters, since machines are often designed for individuals within the average range for height.More than most muscles in the human body, the hip extensors trained by this exercise can become very strong with consistent training over time. Up to a certain point, you can increase the weight you use for this exercise to progressively overload your hip extensor muscles and increase the force they produce. However, this hip hinge variation is only feasible up to a particular weight due to the horizontally directed component of the cable’s resistance. Regardless of how strong you are, you will not be able to maintain your balance and keep your feet planted on the ground once you reach a certain weight threshold. Where this threshold lies will be affected by your bodyweight, the design of the cable machine, and the flooring upon which you are standing. If you reach the maximum weight you use for this exercise in a stable manner, you can still progress by adding more reps per set. However, if you are striving to stay within a particular rep range, it may be more practical to switch to a different hip hinge variation without the same limitations, such as a barbell Romanian deadlift.As an alternative to deadlifting the bar from the floor into this exercise’s starting position, you can initially set up with the bar elevated just above your knees in a half-rack or on top of deadlift blocks (or a viable substitute like bumper plates or aerobic steps with risers). To assume the starting position with the elevated setup, perform an above-the-knee rack pull or above-the-knee block pull, then walk out with the bar until its plates no longer lie above the rack or blocks. Some lifters may opt to use the elevated setup to minimize fatigue when deadlifting the bar off the ground. However, setting up from an elevated surface requires additional equipment and may take more time if the rack’s safety arms are not already at the appropriate height. The walkout may also feel awkward to individuals not accustomed to it, and there is little reason to think that fatigue from performing one deadlift rep before a set of Romanian deadlifts meaningfully impairs the exercise's ability to stimulate increases in muscle size and strength. Ultimately, you should use whichever approach you prefer, since neither is inherently superior.Compared to most curl variations, preacher curls provide greater stability from the arm support that can essentially isolate elbow motion by restricting motion from other joints, such as the shoulders and hips. For instance, when performing a standing curl with free weights (such as dumbbells, barbells, or EZ bars), someone can swing their elbows forward through shoulder motion or generate momentum from their hips. Seated dumbbell curls do not use lower body momentum, but still do not prevent someone from using shoulder motion during the curl, since the upper arm is not externally supported. Using a strict technique in which only elbow motion occurs during a curl does not necessarily result in greater muscle growth, but it can make it easier to track progress over time and reduce fatigue.

In addition to the enhanced stability support that reinforces strict technique, preacher curls exhibit a different resistance profile than most other curl variations. During any curl using free weights, resistance from gravity acting on the primary elbow flexor muscles (i.e., biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis) peaks when the forearm is parallel to the ground. At this point, the horizontal distance between the weight and elbow is maximized, which results in the greatest lever arm of resistance acting on the elbow. On the other hand, resistance to the elbow flexor muscles is absent when the forearm is perpendicular to the ground. When you keep your upper arms by your sides during a free weight curl, resistance is correspondingly the greatest when the elbow is flexed by 90 degrees and the least when the elbow is fully extended in the starting position of each rep. With a preacher curl, the flexed shoulder position from the arm pad means that the forearm is not directed straight down toward the ground in the starting position when the elbow is fully extended. This results in the elbow flexor muscles experiencing greater resistance during the initial portion of the lifting phase compared to free weight curls performed with the arms situated by the sides, where the shoulders are in a neutral position.

While far from conclusive, the greater resistance preacher curls impart in an extended elbow position may allow them to train the brachialis and brachioradialis somewhat more effectively than other curls that lack resistance in this position. The brachialis and brachioradialis do not cross the shoulder joint, so they experience greater resistance at long muscle lengths during preacher curls compared to curls that position the elbow in a neutral position (like a seated curl) or extended position (like an incline curl or Bayesian curl). However, both the long head and the short head of the biceps originate from the scapula and have shoulder flexion moment arms that result in them being shortened in a flexed shoulder position compared to a neutral or extended shoulder position. The available evidence indicates that training a variety of muscles at longer peak lengths, up to a certain point, is likely to promote greater hypertrophy than training at shorter peak lengths. Consequently, curls performed with an extended shoulder position have the potential to train the biceps mildly more effectively than preacher curls.

Attarieh et al. (2025) provided direct insight into this matter by assigning 15 untrained men to a 10-week intervention training single arm underhand grip cable curls twice per week for 3-5 sets of 8-12 reps to failure. For each participant, one arm performed a preacher curl facing toward the cable station with 50 degrees of shoulder flexion, while the other arm performed a Bayesian curl through the same elbow range of motion, facing away from the cable station with 50 degrees of shoulder extension. Assigning one arm of each participant to a different curl variation helps control for many individual factors (e.g., genetics, diet, lifestyle, prior training history) that can confound results when each person is assigned to only one exercise. Following the intervention, the thickness of both the biceps and brachialis similarly increased for the two curl groups. The absence of any difference for the brachialis is not particularly surprising, given that it does not cross the shoulder joint. However, similar biceps growth across the two exercises suggests that the biceps may respond differently to lengthened training than other biarticular (i.e., crossing two joints) muscles. Other biarticular muscles like three of the hamstrings, the calf’s gastrocnemii, the quad’s rectus femoris, and the long head of the triceps have been reported to experience significantly greater growth when trained with a variation that loads them at longer lengths compared to a variation that loads them at shorter lengths. While the difference was not statistically significant, it is worth noting that the average increase in biceps size still slightly favored the Bayesian curl. The preacher curl group increased mean thickness at the proximal (closest to the shoulder), middle, and distal (closest to the elbow) sites of the biceps, respectively, by 6%, 7%, and 7%, while the preacher curl group increased thickness by 9% at all three sites. On the other hand, the brachialis increased thickness by an average of 8% in the Bayesian curl group and by 10% in the preacher curl group. These small differences may reflect a combination of chance and measurement error; however, additional evidence indicates that curling from an extended shoulder position may train the biceps more effectively than curling from a flexed shoulder position.

In a study presented as a conference abstract that did not undergo peer review, Kobayashi et al. (2024) described an experiment similar to that conducted by Attarieh et al. (2025). Kobayashi et al. (2024) assigned 21 untrained participants to a 12-week intervention training single arm dumbbell curls twice per week for 5 sets of 10 reps each session. For each participant, one arm performed a preacher curl with 50 degrees of shoulder flexion, while the other arm performed an incline curl with 50 degrees of shoulder extension. After the intervention, the authors reported that the incline and preacher curl groups, respectively, increased biceps volume by 18% and 14.9%, which was a statistically significant difference between groups. However, the preacher curl group experienced a 21% increase in brachialis size, which exceeded the 17% increase for the incline curl group. Similarly, the brachioradialis increased its volume by a greater degree for the preacher curl group (13%) than the incline curl group (10%). Whenever a study is presented only as an abstract not submitted for peer review in a journal, the validity of its results should be appraised with a healthy dose of skepticism. Nonetheless, Kobayashi et al. (2024) included renowned researchers in this field who have produced high quality peer-reviewed publications, so their findings are worth mentioning despite the limitations from this form of evidence.

Kassiano et al. (2025) also compared the effects of training dumbbell preacher curls at approximately 45 degrees of shoulder flexion with dumbbell incline curls at approximately 30 degrees of shoulder extension. They assigned 63 untrained women to either group, which performed 3 sets of 8-12 reps until failure twice per week for eight weeks. In contrast to Attarieh et al. (2025) and Kobayashi et al. (2024), Kassiano et al. (2025) measured elbow flexor thickness of the biceps and brachialis combined at three different upper arm sites, so we cannot cleanly differentiate whether these two muscles may have individually responded. Following the intervention, the thickness of the proximal site increased by 11.2% for the preacher curl group, exceeding the 8.3% increase for the preacher curl group. While the two groups experienced similar increases in middle site muscle thickness, the preacher curl group's increase in thickness of 10.3% at the distal site was greater than the incline curl group’s 6.4% increase at this distal site. While speculative in nature, these regional differences potentially may be influenced by the biceps reflecting a greater proportion of total elbow flexor muscle thickness at the proximal site than the distal site, which is where the brachialis may be thicker.

If the biceps grew more from incline curls, it would be sensible that the total elbow flexor muscle thickness would favor the incline curl group at the proximal site, most reflective of biceps size relative to brachialis size. Similarly, it would be sensible that the total elbow flexor muscle thickness would favor the preacher curl group at the distal site, most reflective of brachialis size relative to biceps size, if the brachialis grew more from preacher curls. More likely than the shoulder position itself, the resistance profile from free weight preacher curls may contribute to the brachialis potentially being trained somewhat more effectively by these curls involving a flexed shoulder position. Even though the brachialis reaches the same lengths during each variation, the ability to be meaningfully loaded at its longest length during a preacher curl potentially may amplify the stimulus provided to this muscle when using free weights. This may also explain why Kobayashi et al. (2024) found that the brachioradialis experienced greater growth during the dumbbell preacher curl.

While the resistance profile may have an effect, its role remains speculative at this time, and we cannot draw strong conclusions about the differential effects of training curls from a flexed versus an extended shoulder position. There is reason to think that curls with an extended shoulder position may train the biceps mildly more effectively than curls with a flexed shoulder position, while curls that have tension at the start of each rep (like preacher curls) may be ideal for training the brachialis and brachioradialis. However, the evidence is insufficient to support either possibility with a high degree of confidence. Nonetheless, you can be confident that curls effectively train your primary elbow flexor muscles, regardless of whether they involve an extended or flexed shoulder position and whether they use high resistance at the start of each rep. These variables may have some effect, but curl variations are far more similar than different, and you have many viable options from which to select to develop your arms.The Pendlay row is named in reference to Glenn Pendlay, who was a renowned United States weightlifting coach. The technique for the Pendlay row is quite similar to the bent over overhand grip barbell row, but in contrast to Pendlay row, the plates do not touch the ground in between reps of the bent over overhand grip barbell row. Coupled with this difference, the Pendlay row typically uses a torso position closer to horizontal relative to the ground.While explosive intent during the lifting phase can be employed for either variation, an explosive tempo is typically emphasized in the Pendlay row. This explosive emphasis commonly manifests as the Pendlay row’s lowering phase resembling more of a controlled free fall of the bar (like is often the case with deadlifts), which results in its performance biasing the concentric (lifting) phase of the movement more than the eccentric (lowering) phase.

Muscles can certainly act eccentrically at high velocities, but if the lowering phase of an exercise is akin to the weight falling while simply maintaining a grip on it, the exercise’s prime movers may not be receiving a meaningful eccentric stimulus. When comparing concentric only, eccentric only, and concentric-eccentric resistance training for inducing muscle hypertrophy, the current body of evidence is divided in terms of the influence of muscle action type. Certain studies, such as those conducted by Sato et al. (2022) and Vikne et al. (2006), have found that concentric only resistance training yields inferior muscle growth compared to eccentric only or concentric-eccentric resistance training. Other studies, such as those conducted by Blazevich et al. (2007) and Gürcan et al. (2020), found concentric only resistance training to stimulate similar magnitudes of muscle growth as eccentric only resistance training. When pooling the results of 26 studies comparing concentric only resistance training to eccentric only resistance, da Silva et al. (2025) found the two muscle action types to be similarly effective on average for stimulating muscle hypertrophy. However, when analyzing studies that specifically assessed upper body muscles, the data favored eccentric only training over concentric only training. Although rows have not been specifically studied, this evidence suggests that rowing in a manner that minimizes eccentric action may not be optimal for inducing muscle hypertrophy.Dumbbell biceps curls are often performed with the lifting phase of each rep beginning with a neutral grip and ending with an underhand grip, but it is also common to maintain an underhand grip throughout the rep. To transition from a neutral grip to an underhand grip, the forearm supinates, and the biceps function as one of the two primary supinators in the body (along with the aptly named supinator muscle). The fact that the biceps function both as an elbow flexor and a forearm supinator may prompt someone to believe that combining both actions into one exercise can train the biceps more effectively than if elbow flexion were the only motion occurring during the lifting phase of a curl. However, when you hold the middle of a dumbbell’s handle, the evenly distributed load fails to resist forearm supination, so the supination function is essentially not trained any more than if you performed the same movement without a weight. Because the two techniques most likely provide the same stimulus to the biceps, it is sensible to use whichever approach you personally prefer.

With a symmetrically loaded dumbbell weight, you can resist forearm supination during a biceps curl if you hold the handle where your index finger and thumb are pressed up against one end of the dumbbell. This results in an asymmetric distribution of force that simultaneously loads the biceps' supination and elbow flexion functions. Potentially, this technique may provide a different stimulus to certain regions of the biceps compared to if you curled in a way that only trained its elbow flexion function. However, this matter has not been directly studied, so any possible differences these curl techniques may have upon biceps development are completely speculative.If you fully arched your back, using a less arched back position during bench pressing positions the lower chest closer to the bench, thereby increasing the distance the bar must travel to reach the lower chest. This increases the amount of shoulder and elbow motion required during each rep with a flatter back bench press compared with a meaningfully arched bench press. Correspondingly, utilizing a lower degree of arching can allow you to train your pecs, front delts, and triceps at longer muscle lengths, similar to how a cambered bar bench press enables you to train these muscles at longer lengths than a standard barbell bench press. While this specific comparison has not yet been studied, the available evidence indicates that training muscles at long muscle lengths tends to promote greater hypertrophy than training them at short lengths. Consequently, bench pressing with a lower degree of arching may potentially be more effective for stimulating muscle growth than bench pressing with a very high degree of arching. However, this is speculative rather than proven, and the likelihood of any difference existing is very low if someone’s standard bench press involves only a mildly more arched position than its natural curvature.Individuals may have different conceptions of what specifically defines low and high rows, which can cause confusion when using these terms. In one sense, the plane of motion through which the shoulders move as a consequence of how much the elbows flare outward can be considered the distinguishing characteristic.

With respect to the shoulder plane of motion, low rows are predominantly performed through shoulder extension in the sagittal plane as the elbows and upper arms remain tucked close to the side, like during a shoulder-width underhand or neutral grip seated cable row. On the other hand, high rows are predominantly performed through shoulder horizontal extension in the transverse plane as the elbows and upper arms notably flare out away from the sides, like during a wide overhand grip seated cable row. In this regard, the low or high component can apply to the elbows relative to the shoulders at the end of the row’s lifting phase, where the elbows are lower during a low row compared to a high row.

Others alternatively view the vertical component of a row’s directionality as what differentiates a low row from a high row. Certain machines, called low row machines by their manufacturers, are designed to ensure that the handles travel diagonally upward and backward during the lifting phase before traveling diagonally downward and forward during the lowering phase. These machines, which involve a low-to-high rowing motion, typically have handles close enough together that they naturally prompt users to keep their elbows tucked inward during the lifting phase when using an underhand or neutral grip. Consequently, they enable lifters to perform a low row under either definition. In contrast, other machines, called high-row machines by their manufacturers, are designed to ensure that the handles travel diagonally downward and backward during the pulling phase, then diagonally upward and forward during the reversal. These machines involve a high-to-low rowing motion that functions as a hybrid between a vertical pulldown and a standard horizontal row. Depending on the grip used, a high row machine may allow the user to keep their elbows tucked inward or flared outward.The Helms row is named after Dr. Eric Helms, an exercise science researcher, author, bodybuilder, and powerlifter, and a renowned coach for bodybuilders and strength athletes. Helms popularized this rowing variation, which provides chest support during a bent-over row, resulting in an exercise with greater external stability and lower demands on trunk extension than a standard bent-over row.A feet-up bench press is also called a Larsen press in reference to Adrian Larsen, who utilized this technique as one of the strongest raw bench pressers of all time. In 2013, Larsen ascended to rarified air when he broke the world record in the 220lb (100kg) weight class with a monstrous 585lb (265kg) raw bench press without his feet touching the ground.When crossing and uncrossing your arms over each other during each rep, your arms will naturally be slightly uneven, resulting in a very small difference in how your right and left sides move during a set. There is little reason to think that such a small difference is likely to result in the development of a meaningful muscle size or strength asymmetry. Still, you have the option of alternating which arm crosses in the higher position from set to set if you wish to do so. If you wish to have every rep train each side with the identical technique, you can also train the single arm cable rear delt fly.When using a bent-over position, the overhand grip dumbbell reverse fly has been found to elicit significantly less EMG activity in the posterior deltoid than the bent-over neutral grip dumbbell reverse fly during the eccentric phase of these exercises, but no difference was found during the concentric phase.When your arms are at your sides during this exercise, your elbow flexor muscles act isometrically to maintain elbow position. When your arms are fully elevated in a position of shoulder abduction, your shoulder external rotator muscles act isometrically to prevent internal rotation of the shoulder.With the obvious exception of sitting as opposed to standing, the seated dumbbell lateral raise may be performed with a technique that is essentially identical to that of the standing dumbbell lateral raise. However, some people may be naturally inclined to perform the standing dumbbell lateral raise with a less strict technique that uses lower body muscles to generate momentum, assisting with dumbbell movement. Using momentum from other muscle groups to assist the shoulder muscles in moving the dumbbells will not necessarily reduce the hypertrophy stimulus to the shoulders compared with a stricter technique. However, if someone uses a stricter technique that does not involve generating momentum with additional muscles during seated dumbbell lateral raises, less weight will be required to complete the same number of reps as with a looser technique during standing dumbbell lateral raises.As an alternative to setting the bar at its lowest height in the Smith machine, you can start with the bar elevated to the height of your knees/lower thighs. Doing so would reduce the depth at which you have to hinge to grasp the bar before picking it up, which can make it somewhat easier to assume the starting position for this exercise. If you opt to start with the bar racked in an elevated position, you would have to rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion while you start standing up with the bar, so that the safety bars do not catch the safety hooks.The barbell deficit Romanian deadlift is identical to the standard form of the barbell Romanian deadlift, except that it allows you to hip hinge through a greater range of motion before the plates contact the ground. Correspondingly, using the deficit variation does not provide any advantage over the standard Romanian deadlift if your hinging range of motion is not constrained by the plates contacting the ground at the end of the descent during a standard barbell Romanian deadlift.If you notice that your hinge range of motion is constrained by the bar contacting the bottom of the machine before you feel that you have finished sitting your hips fully backward, you can select the Smith machine deficit Romanian deadlift from this database. Compared with the barbell Romanian deadlift, the deficit variation may be appropriate for a greater proportion of lifters when using the Smith machine. This is due to the lowest height to which a bar can descend in a Smith machine, resulting in the bar being situated higher up than a barbell would be on the ground, even when loaded with large-diameter weight plates.As an alternative to performing this exercise with the front of your body facing the machine’s weight stack, you can also set up facing the opposite direction, with your back directed toward the machine’s weight stack. With this approach, you would not be able to keep your thighs pressed under the machine’s thigh pad, which could reduce the stability of this variation. However, as long as you are not using a weight heavy enough to lift your whole body from the seat, the exercise remains sufficiently stable to effectively train the same muscles without the thigh pad. This modification may be appropriate if your head continues to obstruct the bar's path when facing the cable station, even when you are fully scooted forward on the seat with your chin tucked to your chest. Because a less flexed neck position may be required for bar clearance, the oppositely directed sitting position can also be preferable for people who experience symptom aggravation of pre-existing conditions when meaningfully flexing their neck forward.Specifically with behind-the-neck lat pulldowns, people often find that a wider grip feels more comfortable on their shoulders than a moderate grip. Regardless of grip width, some people lack the mobility required to perform behind-the-neck pulldowns without shoulder or other joint discomfort. If you find that to be the case for you, you can readily perform a different lat pulldown variation, such as the standard overhand wide grip lat pulldown that is performed to the chest rather than behind the neck.Studies such as those by Aasa et al. (2019) and Edington (2017) have demonstrated that it is normal for the lumbar spine to flex and extend during deadlift and squat variations with at least moderately heavy loads. However, lifters typically perform deadlift and squat variations with the intention of minimizing lumbar spine motion while striving to move their hips and knees. Correspondingly, the lumbar spinal extensor muscles are not necessarily trained by deadlifts and squats in a truly isometric (static muscle contraction) manner, which would only occur if no lumbar spine motion occurred during these exercises. However, they are normally performed with the intent to train the spinal extensor muscles isometrically, as lifters strive to keep a rigid trunk position, in contrast to Jefferson curl variations, which are intentionally performed through dynamic flexion and extension of the lumbar and thoracic spine. As a result, the trunk extensor muscles function as prime movers during Jefferson curl variations.

When consciously aiming to flex the lumbar and thoracic spine into end range, the muscles and ligaments on the back of the spine experience greater strain than when maintaining a rigid trunk position during exercise. While it is not inherently injurious to load the trunk in a highly flexed position, caution is advised during this exercise to not allow the weight to pull you forcibly beyond what is your end range. If you use a weight that is excessively heavy relative to your body’s capacity and exceed your normal available range of motion under load, you can face an elevated risk of experiencing sprain injuries of certain spinal ligaments or strain injuries of the spinal extensor muscles. It can be wise to be conservative with the loads and volumes you utilize when starting to train a variety of movements, but this is especially the case if you opt to perform Jefferson curl variations.During a standard Jefferson curl, the hips flex with the spine during the lowering phase, and the hips extend with the spine during the lifting phase. In contrast, the hips statically maintain a flexed hip position (like at the bottom of a Romanian deadlift) throughout a fixed-hip Jefferson curl. While the hip extensor muscles will still serve a stabilizing role, this results in the fixed-hip Jefferson curl functioning more like a spinal erector isolation exercise compared to a standard Jefferson curl, where the hip extensor muscles (e.g., gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus) dynamically function with the spinal erectors.

The hip motion and hip extensor muscle contribution that occurs during a standard Jefferson will not necessarily mean that it will train the spinal erectors any less effectively than the fixed-hip Jefferson curl. However, a fixed-hip Jefferson curl does not require as heavy weights to train the spinal erectors compared to a standard Jefferson curl. When looking to train the spinal erectors effectively in a targeted manner, the fixed-hip Jefferson curl may correspondingly impart lower magnitudes of shear forces and compressive forces to the lumbar spine, compared to the standard Jefferson curl. Shear and compressive forces are by no means inherently deleterious to the lumbar spine, but an excessive volume of these stressors may increase the likelihood of injury occurring. If you are already heavily loading your lumbar spine through deadlift and squat variations, the lighter loads with a fixed-hip Jefferson curl may contribute to it being easier from which to recover compared to a standard Jefferson curl.Lunges and split squats can naturally pose a challenge to a lifter’s balance, and the intensity of this challenge can increase as the load increases. When holding dumbbells or kettlebells for lunges and split squats, people can quickly set the weights down onto the ground if they start losing their balance during a rep, which can help reduce the risk of an accident occurring. Even if lunges and split squats are performed within a rack with the safety pins appropriately set up, losing balance with a barbell on the back during a rep can impart a greater risk of injury and equipment damage compared to using dumbbells or kettlebells.

On the other hand, a barbell can enable smaller increments of load modifications compared to dumbbells or kettlebells, which can more readily help you select a specific weight that appropriately challenges the rep range in which you wish to train. Progressing from holding 25lb in each hand to 30lb in each hand (or from holding 12.5kg in each hand to 15kg in each hand) can be more difficult than adding 2.5lb (1kg) plates to each side of a barbell. At a certain level of strength, some lifters may also reach the heaviest dumbbells or kettlebells available to them when lunging and split squatting, precluding them from progressing their weights any further. In contrast, barbells have functionally unlimited loading potential.

If you have access to one to use for lunges and split squats, an open trap bar provides the safety benefits of dumbbells and kettlebells in case you lose your balance, while simultaneously offering the same loading benefits of a barbell. This combination of characteristics can make the open trap bar a particularly useful tool for very strong lifters who have maxed out their dumbbells or kettlebells.The D.Y. row machine is named after Dorian Yates, widely recognized as one of the greatest bodybuilders of all time, with his crowning achievement being six consecutive Mr. Olympia titles from 1992 to 1997. Dorian’s total number of Mr. Olympia wins has only been exceeded by four bodybuilders in history, namely Arnold Schwarzenegger, Phil Heath, Lee Haney, and Ronnie Coleman.

Gary Jones, the founder of Hammer Strength, said he initially designed a prototype of what later became the D.Y. row machine as a custom machine for Yates after he sustained a wrist injury that interfered with his ability to perform the barbell rows, which served as a staple of his back training. Demand subsequently grew for the machine, and Jones refined the machine into its commercially available version. While Hammer Strength developed the first D.Y row machine, other manufacturers have since released their own similarly designed models.

In contrast to most other plate-loaded row machines, D.Y. row machines have handles designed to enable an underhand grip while also having their pivot points positioned overhead. Typical plate-loaded row machines have handles designed to be grasped with an overhand and/or neutral grip while having their pivot points situated closer to the ground.Lying dumbbell biceps curls and incline dumbbell biceps curls both can train the biceps at longer lengths than seated or standing biceps curls because the biceps reaches a longer length in an extended shoulder position (when the elbow is behind the torso) compared to a neutral shoulder position (when the elbow is by the side). While lying dumbbell biceps curls can be performed with variable shoulder angles, they are commonly performed with a shoulder position fairly similar to an incline curl.

With an incline dumbbell curl, the upper arms can hang straight down perpendicular to the ground, which can position the shoulders in about 45 degrees of extension beyond the neutral position when using a 45-degree incline bench. When performing a lying curl, the shoulders would be meaningfully more extended if the upper arms were also directed straight down perpendicular to the ground, but the conventional technique for this variation involves keeping the upper arms angled diagonally rather than vertically. Lifters with short arms can maintain a more extended shoulder position without the dumbbells contacting the ground compared to lifters with longer arms, but people can generally maintain a position of moderate shoulder hyperextension that is comparable to a 45-degree incline curl. If the same shoulder and elbow angles are used for both variations, the biceps will function at the same lengths during each type of curl.

However, the resistance profile differs between these two exercises. In the starting position, the dumbbell is situated vertically under the elbow with a vertical forearm during an incline curl, but the dumbbell is positioned horizontally in front of the elbow with a diagonally angled forearm during a lying curl. Because gravity exerts its force straight downward, the lever arm of resistance provided by a dumbbell is equivalent to the horizontal distance between the dumbbell and the center of the elbow joint, and this lever arm is greatest when the forearm is parallel to the ground. This means that the lever arm of resistance will be meaningfully greater during the initial range of lying curl compared to an incline curl.

Because the resistive torque acting on a joint is the product of the lever arm of the resistive force acting on a joint and the magnitude of the resistive force (which is determined by how heavy the weight is in this context), resistive torque will correspondingly be greater earlier in the range of a lying curl relative to an incline curl. This results in the biceps experiencing greater resistance in its most lengthened position when performing a lying curl, despite the biceps being able to work at similar lengths during the two different curls.

Thus far, no study has yet assessed muscle hypertrophy after a lying curl training intervention, let alone compared it to a different biceps curl training intervention, so insufficient evidence is available to draw any firm conclusions about whether lying curls may stimulate distinct adaptations relative to other curls. Due to their ability to provide notable resistance to the elbow flexor muscles at the position in which the biceps are meaningfully lengthened, it is theoretically plausible that lying curls may stimulate a mildly greater degree of elbow flexor muscle hypertrophy than other curl variations. However, any potential difference is speculative at this moment.To move into the starting position for this exercise, you are essentially performing the lowering phase of a rep with some shoulder motion. If you end your set by curling the dumbbells up before sitting up from the bench (instead of just setting the dumbbells down onto the ground), you are also finishing with half of a rep. While each full rep of the lying dumbbell curl starts with the elbows extended and finishes with the elbows flexed, you can consider the two half reps you perform to get into and out of position for the exercise to together add up to a whole rep that you can include for how many reps you log per set. Whether you do so is completely up to your preference, since what matters is that you track your reps in a consistent manner from set to set to help you reliably measure your progress.You can perform a single arm dumbbell row with your free hand and knee supported on a bench, or you can perform it in the tripod (AKA three-point stance) with both feet on the ground and one hand supported on a bench (or other object). A strict or “cheating” technique can be used with either setup, but Kroc has personally used the standing tripod approach whenever demonstrating the technique for what is now known as the Kroc row. When striving to maximize how much weight can be lifted while using momentum to achieve that objective, the tripod stance may be more efficient than the kneeling position, which likely influenced Kroc’s preferred way to row.The single arm dumbbell cheat row is commonly called the Kroc row, in reference to Matt Kroc (with Kroczaleski being the full last name), the elite powerlifter who now goes by the name Janae. Kroc’s greatest strength feats were achieved in the multi-ply division of a 2009 powerlifting meet, where Kroc squatted 1003lb (455kg), bench pressed 739lb (335kg), and deadlifted 810lb (367.5kg) at a bodyweight of 220lb (100kg).

Having a brutally strong back is a key component of being able to complete these awe-inspiring lifts, and any picture of Kroc clearly shows that the champion powerlifter (who also competed in bodybuilding) took back training very seriously. One tool Kroc used in pursuance of building a massive, powerful back was the single arm dumbbell row performed with the objective of consistently lifting heavier weights. With the focus on maximizing how much weight could be lifted, Kroc would row with a “cheating” style that used torso rotation throughout the rep to build momentum, rather than keeping the torso locked in a static position with very strict technique.

Kroc reported that a personal best for these single arm rows involved a set of 13 reps with 310lb, which seems like a very credible claim, particularly in light of footage showing Kroc rowing 310lb for 6 reps years after Kroc was at peak strength for powerlifting.

Besides building back strength and size, Kroc would also perform this style of rowing to develop elite grip strength. As a mortal human being, Kroc used straps when rowing more than 300lb (which would still require extreme grip strength), but Kroc would also regularly refrain from straps for comparatively lighter sets performed for at least 20 reps and sometimes up to 40 reps. Kroc has described that consistently training these rows (both with and without straps) successfully resolved a grip weakness for the deadlift and enhanced deadlift lockout performance.If your goal is to stimulate increases in your strength and/or muscle size, moving from point A to point B in whatever manner minimizes muscle demand is not the most efficient means of doing so. However, if you were competing in a competition with the goal of maximizing how many times you can touch the bar with your toes, the strict form described by this exercise’s technique section would not be the best means of achieving that particular objective. In a competition like the CrossFit Games, optimizing the likelihood of success involves expending the least amount of energy and effort to complete each rep within the specific parameters dictated by the rules. When performing max toes-to-bar reps in a competitive environment, that will involve meaningfully using momentum in a swinging manner and explosively kicking the feet up to finish each rep while minimizing the muscular challenge from each rep.While the rear delt may be the most effectively trained muscle during a rear delt fly (AKA reverse fly), rear delt flies can still train multiple other muscles and, correspondingly, do not actually isolate the rear delt. When seeking to determine how effectively a particular muscle may be trained by an exercise, it is ideal to measure the size of that muscle before and after a training intervention involving that muscle without any other exercises that may plausibly train it. At the time of writing this passage, no applicable training studies have yet tested the degree to which different muscles may grow following an intervention exclusively using a rear delt fly variation.

In the absence of that data, we can look at research modeling the moment arms (AKA lever arms) muscles have to contribute to shoulder horizontal extension, which is the shoulder action that is resisted during a rear delt fly, as you sweep your arm out away from the midline of your body. A moment arm quantifies how much leverage a muscle has for producing a particular type of joint torque, so a muscle with a sufficiently large moment arm for generating the type of joint torque strongly resisted by an exercise is well-suited to be trained by that exercise. Essentially every muscle that is widely recognized to be effectively trained by a particular exercise has a moment arm that allows it to produce torque for a joint action meaningfully resisted by that exercise. When studies measure significant growth to occur in a muscle after a resistance training intervention, that muscle almost always has been found to have a notable moment arm that lets it generate a type of joint torque resisted by an exercise from the training intervention. Consequently, muscle moment arms can help us determine which muscles may most likely be trained effectively by an exercise, even if changes in the size of potentially involved muscles have not been assessed following an intervention using that exercise.

Multiple studies have unsurprisingly found the rear delt to have a prominent shoulder horizontal extension moment arm. While more widely known for its shoulder abduction function, which is trained during lateral raises, the lateral delt has also been reported to have a meaningful shoulder horizontal extension moment arm. Similarly, the rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, which are situated on the back of the shoulder blade, additionally have notable leverage to function as shoulder horizontal extensors. Another rotator cuff muscle, namely the supraspinatus (which lies on the top of the shoulder blade deep to the upper trap), also has a favorable shoulder horizontal extension moment arm, specifically with its posterior (closer to the backside) portion, but its anterior (closer to the front) portion lacks this moment arm.

Further evidence is provided by a variety of studies measuring EMG activity of the aforementioned muscles with shoulder horizontal extension moment arms. During resisted shoulder horizontal extension exercises like rear delt flies and prone T-raises, these muscles have been reported to demonstrate levels of EMG activity indicative of meaningfully contributing to these exercises. In isolation, a single EMG study does not conclusively demonstrate that a muscle will be effectively trained by a particular type of exercise. However, when the findings of a variety of EMG studies align with what would be predicted from the muscle moment arm data, their findings can serve as additional evidence supporting the contribution that the lateral delt and three rotator cuff muscles have during rear delt flies.

With all of that said, if you want to maximize the development of your side delts and rotator cuff muscles, it would be unwise to rely exclusively on rear delt flies. Rear delt flies are not necessarily a replacement for lateral raises (to target the lateral delt and supraspinatus) and shoulder external rotation exercises (to target the infraspinatus and teres minor) when striving to optimally train these muscles. However, the available evidence indicates that rear delt flies likely can stimulate a non-negligible increase in size and strength for these muscles, so they can provide more bang for your buck than just training the rear delts very effectively.Optimizing squat efficiency involves keeping your whole center of mass with the combined load of your bodyweight and the bar weight over your midfoot to maximize your stability. As you squat down, a greater proportion of your body’s mass shifts behind your midfoot due to the mass that is concentrated in your torso, hips, and upper thighs. To keep your whole center of mass over your midfoot when a bar is on your back, the bar may have to track forward to a degree as you descend before tracking back to a degree as you ascend. When the bar’s weight is relatively light relative to your body’s weight, a visible amount of forward and backward bar motion may occur (when viewed from the side) to keep the whole center of mass aligned with your midfoot. As the bar weight progressively increases, the optimal bar path becomes correspondingly closer to fully vertical.Optimizing squat efficiency involves keeping your whole center of mass with the combined load of your bodyweight and the bar weight over your midfoot to maximize your stability. As you squat down, a greater proportion of your body’s mass shifts behind your midfoot due to the mass that is concentrated in your torso, hips, and upper thighs. To keep your whole center of mass over your midfoot when a bar is on your back, the bar may have to track forward to a degree as you descend before tracking back to a degree as you ascend. When the bar’s weight is relatively light relative to your body’s weight, a visible amount of forward and backward bar motion may occur (when viewed from the side) to keep the whole center of mass aligned with your midfoot. As the bar weight progressively increases, the optimal bar path becomes correspondingly closer to fully vertical.

Recognizing that a non-zero degree of forward bar motion can be appropriate when descending, some people squat with an excessive degree of horizontal motion, which may negatively affect squat performance. Due to its ability to oscillate forward and backward, a cambered bar provides immediate feedback about whether your bar path is likely deviating beyond vertical to an excessive degree. If the bar starts swinging, the rep can become more challenging to complete, so it incentivizes you to use a closer to vertical bar path.Equipment manufacturers most often use the name “linear hack squat” to refer to the machine that uses the primary name “seated hack squat” in this app to differentiate it from the more commonly available traditional hack squat, which has a back pad supporting most of the back, along with shoulder pads. In contrast, a seated hack squat lacks shoulder pads, has a much shorter backrest to support only the lower back, and a seat that is distinct from the angled backrest. Both types of machines have sleds that move linearly along rails, and certain equipment manufacturers will label their traditional hack squats as “linear hack squats.” Consequently, the term “linear” does not actually differentiate one machine from the other, in contrast to using “seated” as a more intuitive distinction. Given its meaningfully greater prevalence, the traditional hack squat simply uses the primary name “hack squat” in this app, but calling it a “standing hack squat” (which is assigned as an alternative name) is a logical way to distinguish it from a seated hack squat when discussing both machines.

The design differences result in the hips being moderately flexed in the starting position of a seated hack squat, while they are near a neutral position (like when normally standing upright) in the starting position with a standing hack squat. When performing a squatting motion, the end range of the lowering phase can be dictated by when you have reached the end range of motion of hip flexion (when your thighs are close to your torso), knee flexion (when your thighs are close to your shins), and ankle dorsiflexion (with your shins angling forward over your toes).

With either type of hack squat machine, you can avoid having your ankle dorsiflexion range of motion limit your depth if you position your feet higher up on the foot platform. Because your hips start moderately flexed, your hips will normally reach the end range to which they can flex before reaching your end range knee flexion with a seated hack squat. In contrast, you may be able to approach the end range of knee flexion and hip flexion simultaneously with a standing hack squat if you set your feet appropriately on the platform.Seated hack squat machines (AKA linear hack squat machines) look very similar to the less commonly available incline plate-loaded calf press machines, but they can differ in their usage. Compared to seated hack squat machines, incline plate-loaded calf press machines are typically designed with smaller foot platforms, may lack a safety lock mechanism, and can have a sled that reaches the bottom of its rails before most users would achieve a moderately high degree of knee bend when performing a squat motion. If people look to perform a squatting movement on an incline plate-loaded calf press machine, their depth can be meaningfully restricted, and they may not be able to set up with their preferred foot position for that type of exercise.

As mentioned in the setup details, some seated hack squat machines are designed in a way that lets them be comfortably used for both seated hack squats and full range of motion calf raises. However, other seated hack squat machines have foot platforms that prevent you from hanging your heels off the bottom edge, so they cannot serve the same role as a dedicated incline plate-loaded calf press machine.Pin squats can be performed with the pins set to any point in the lift’s range of motion, so where the pins are positioned can be individualized to address someone’s specific weakness or technical deficiencies. With that said, setting the pins close to where the bar would be at the bottom of your back squat can generally be an effective approach if you are using pin squats as a means to improve back squat performance or are generally looking to enhance your lower body strength through a long range of motion.

Along with directly improving your strength at the joint angles present when starting the lifting phase, setting the pins low enough to train through close to your back squat’s full range of motion can often improve your positioning during subsequent parts of the lifting phase. If you begin lifting in a mechanically advantageous position, it may be easier to complete the rep than if you start with suboptimal positioning. For instance, if your hips shoot up prematurely when squatting out of the hole, you can be pitched forward excessively and have a greater difficulty with the rest of your squat. Even if you consistently fail midway through the lifting phase, starting your ascent with optimal positioning can increase the load you can handle before reaching your limit at that point.

Increasing strength near the start of the lifting phase can also make it easier to complete the initial portion of the rep, which may build greater speed and reduce difficulty in subsequent parts of the lift. If you have greater momentum entering into a later portion of the rep, you can be less likely to fail compared to if the bar was moving quite slowly into that point from having to grind to begin the lifting phase. To have improved starting strength effectively transfer to increased bar speed, you just want to make sure that you train standard back squats at some point as well, because exclusively training the pin variation can neglect to optimize the technique you use without the pins.Rather than keeping the elbow bent with the forearms parallel, some lifters perform standing cable wrist curls with the elbows straight as the forearms are directed down toward  the pulley. The wrist flexor muscles can be trained with either approach, but keeping the forearms parallel to the ground lets the cable provide resistance throughout your wrist’s full range of motion, including when the wrist is in an extended position. In contrast, keeping the elbows straight with the forearms angled toward the pulley prevents the cable from providing any resistance to the wrist flexors in an extended wrist position when the wrist flexor muscles are lengthened. While no study has yet assessed the wrist flexor muscles directly, other muscles have been indicated to experience greater growth when trained with exercises that load them in lengthened positions compared to variations that train them at short lengths.If you have very strong ankle dorsiflexor muscles, you may be able to reach the limit of small-diameter plates that you can add to the machine’s loading sleeves. If you still wish to progress the load over time, switching from the double leg variation to the single leg machine tib raise may be appropriate. Alternatively, you may be able to elevate the machine onto an object like an aerobic step to provide clearance above the ground for you to be able to load larger diameter plates without the plates touching the ground and limiting your range of motion. On a per-pound (or per-kilogram) basis, typical 10lb (or 5kg) weight plates can be meaningfully thicker than heavier plates, so using heavier plates will not require as much loading sleeve length to provide the same load.The saying that “abs are made in the kitchen” expresses the legitimate point that your body fat percentage is the most prominent factor influencing how visibly defined your ab muscles are. At a sufficiently low level of body fat, essentially anyone can have visible ab muscle definition, and at some point, a certain level of body fat accumulation can inevitably obscure ab muscle definition.

The ability to lose or gain body fat is most strongly driven by the difference between your caloric intake and your caloric expenditure. Naturally, increasing your volume of physical activity can increase your caloric expenditure and make it easier to achieve a caloric deficit that facilitates fat loss. However, it is typically impractical for the clear majority of people to experience meaningful reductions in body fat levels without also including dietary modifications. With a six-pack of Oreos containing more calories than a one-hour walk expends for most people, dietary choices are unmatched in their impact they have on body fat percentage.

Nonetheless, developing your abs through resistance training can reduce how lean you may need to be to start showing visible ab definition. As a hypothetical example, a man with minimal ab development may need to reach 12% body fat to start showing noticeable ab definition, but ab definition may be visibly apparent at 14% body fat if that person has well-trained abs. Note that these values are used to illustrate the principle rather than indicating that you should expect to start seeing ab definition at those particular body fat percentages. Two individuals with the same exact percentage of body fat can have that fat distributed in meaningfully different ways throughout their bodies, so one person’s midsection may look quite different from another person’s midsection if their abs are equally developed, and they share the same body fat percentage. Even when comparing individuals of the same sex (since men and women have different average body fat distributions), some people will naturally store a much higher proportion of their total body fat in their abdominal region than others.Front raise variations are widely considered front delt isolation exercises, and while they can certainly train the front delts very effectively, the front delts will not act in isolation during front raises. Shoulder flexion occurs during the lifting phase of a front raise, and the front delt can function as the strongest shoulder flexor in the body, but the side delt, the upper portion of the chest’s pec major, and the rotator cuff’s supraspinatus are also able to contribute to shoulder flexion. These three other muscles are unlikely to be trained as effectively as the front delt during a front raise, and other movements are better suited to target them optimally (such as incline presses for the upper pec major and lateral raises for the side delt and supraspinatus), but they can still receive a non-negligible training stimulus from front raises.In addition to its padding, the handles of a safety squat bar (often abbreviated as an SSB) visibly distinguish it from a straight barbell. For lifters with pre-existing injuries or mobility limitations who may experience discomfort in their shoulders, elbows, or wrists when back squatting with a straight bar, the ability to grasp a safety squat bar’s handles can help squatting with a safety squat bar feel meaningfully more comfortable than back squatting with a straight bar. If you are one of those lifters and do not have to back squat specifically with a straight bar for a competition like a powerlifting meet, there is little reason to push through pain in your shoulders, elbows, or wrists if you can comfortably squat with a safety squat bar.

While no study has yet compared back squat and safety squat bar squat training interventions in terms of how much muscle growth they may induce, the two variations train the same muscles in similar ways, so they can most likely stimulate comparable degrees of muscle hypertrophy on average when using similar volumes and proximities to failure. With either squat variation, your quads will be heavily loaded to extend your knees while your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus will be heavily loaded to extend your hips. Simultaneously, your spinal extensors must function to keep your spine from flexing under the weight of the bar regardless of which variation you perform. Nonetheless, they do exhibit certain biomechanical differences.

The safety squat bar’s camber pitches the load further forward than a straight bar does with a back squat but to a lesser degree than a front squat does, resulting in a safety squat bar squat mechanically functioning like a hybrid between a back squat and a front squat. Situating the load further forward than a back squat particularly increases the demands on the thoracic erectors to maintain a sufficiently extended thoracic spine and prevent the chest from collapsing through thoracic spine flexion. For this reason, safety squat bar squats and, to a greater degree, front squats will preferentially train the upper back musculature compared to back squats, where the difference is more pronounced with a low bar back squat than a high bar back squat.

This difference in thoracic erector loading is accompanied by a difference in the torso angle when comparing the squat variations, as evidenced by studies reporting that, on average, people squat with a more upright torso when safety squat bar squatting or front squatting compared to back squatting.  Balance is optimized when squatting by keeping the center of total mass (accounting for the mass of the bar and the body) over the midfoot. Relative to a back squat, positioning the bar further forward means a lesser degree of forward torso lean will be required to keep the center of total mass over your midfoot when squatting down. Research has also found that the greater degree of forward torso lean when back squatting is also accompanied by a greater degree of hip flexion. As torso lean increases, the weight’s moment arm acting on the hips increases, which may slightly increase the demands on the hip extensor muscles (e.g., gluteus maximus and adductor magnus) when back squatting compared to safety squat bar squatting. While they are detectable, the differences in hip angles and hip extensor loading are of small enough magnitudes that they are unlikely to contribute to a significant difference in how much hip extensor muscle growth may be stimulated by safety squat bar squats and back squats.

When testing lifters’ 1-RM or 3-RM with the two bars, research has consistently shown that people, on average, can squat more weight when back squatting compared to safety squat bar squatting. As of writing this passage, five different studies have investigated this topic, and they all reported that their participants lifted greater max loads when back squatting. If we average their findings together, they collectively indicated that the difference in max load was about 9%.  

Ultimately, if you prefer squatting with one bar compared to the other, it is fully viable to select whichever squat variation feels best to you when training for muscle hypertrophy and/or general squatting strength. Back squats and safety squat bar squats do not have identical mechanics, but they are far more similar than different, and both can be high-quality exercises in your programs.While the biceps is widely recognized for its elbow flexion function, it similarly serves a primary role as a forearm supinator and will correspondingly be meaningfully engaged during a resisted forearm supination exercise. As its name suggests, the supinator (a deep muscle in the upper forearm) also functions as a primary forearm supinator (which is its only joint function), but its cross-sectional area is less than that of the biceps, so the biceps is able to generate greater force. In an extended elbow position, the biceps and supinator have comparable forearm supination moment arms, but the moment arm for the biceps increases as the elbow flexes, in contrast to the moment arm of the supinator, which is not affected by elbow position. This means that the biceps will contribute the greater proportion of total supination torque in a flexed elbow position.

Research measuring maximal elbow flexion and forearm supination torque actually indicates that the biceps can generate a greater proportion of total forearm supination torque than total elbow flexion torque. In large part, this is due to the existence of the brachialis, which has favorable leverage to function as a primary elbow flexor while having a considerably greater cross-sectional area than the supinator. Correspondingly, the biceps receives more help in flexing the elbow than supinating the forearm, so its relative contribution to generating forearm supination torque can be greater.

Like the biceps, the brachioradialis can also function as an elbow flexor and a forearm supinator, but the brachioradialis has worse peak leverage for supinating the forearm, and it can actually switch to functioning as a pronator when the forearm is in a supinated position. While the brachioradialis can mildly assist in forearm supination from a pronated position, its contribution is modest, and it is meaningfully better suited to function as an elbow flexor during any curling or upper body pulling exercise.The standard thickness for a single bench press board corresponds to the thickness of a 2x4 piece of wood, which is 1.5in (3.8cm). A 2x4 piece of wood receives its name from its dimensions before the wood is further processed from having an initial thickness of 2in (5.1cm) and width of 4in (10.2cm) to its final thickness of 1.5in (3.8cm) and width of 3.5in (8.9cm). While wooden boards were exclusively used when board presses were first performed, bench block attachments are now commonly used as alternatives to boards. While they do not enhance the training response of board pressing, bench block attachments can be more convenient, since they do not require a training partner to use and can also be readily transported in a gym bag due to their more compact size.A cable triceps press functions as a hybrid between a triceps pushdown, where the shoulders are in a neutral position with the elbows by your sides, and an overhead triceps extension, where the shoulders are fully flexed with the elbows raised fully upward. Since the long head of the triceps lengthens as the angle of shoulder flexion increases, a cable triceps press will correspondingly train the long head of the triceps at longer lengths than a triceps pushdown but at shorter lengths than an overhead triceps extension.The vast majority of triceps machines can be categorized into one of four types, each of which has its own pin-loaded and plate-loaded variants.

Seated dip machines (which are also called triceps press machines) are very commonly available at commercial gyms. These machines are characterized by having a pair of handles that users can grasp with their elbows behind their torsos before pressing down through a combination of shoulder motion and elbow motion. The exercise using this style of machine looks similar to a bodyweight dip, but the hands and handles move while the torso remains stationary, in contrast to a bodyweight dip where the inverse occurs. The shoulder contribution involved with using this machine makes it distinct from any of the other triceps machines, which conventionally use techniques where the shoulders remain stationary as all of the movement occurs from elbow motion. While the triceps still can function as the primary muscle group when using a seated dip machine, the front delt and pec major assist during the exercise by moving the shoulder, but they serve negligible roles when using other types of triceps machines that do not provide resistance to the shoulder.

Another very common type of triceps machine has a sloped arm pad that resembles a preacher curl pad, onto which you can rest your triceps with your upper arms elevated in front of you. This type of machine (which is often just called a triceps extension machine) sometimes functions as a combo machine with a lever arm that can be set forward and downward to train the biceps with a preacher curl. In contrast to a seated dip machine, which situates the shoulders in an extended (i.e., elbows behind the torso) to neutral (i.e., elbows aligned with the torso) position, the preacher-style machine situates your shoulders in a moderately flexed position (i.e., elbows in front of the torso). The long head of the triceps lengthens as the shoulder flexes, so a preacher-style machine lets you train the long head at longer lengths than a seated dip machine.

Overhead triceps extension machines constitute another triceps machine category, and they are characterized by having a bar that can be grasped with the upper arms raised fully overhead. These machines may let you train the long head of the triceps at longer lengths than any other triceps machine type.

The fourth type of triceps machine lets you position your upper arms by your sides with your shoulders near a neutral position to train your triceps in a similar position as you would with a cable triceps pushdown. Manufacturers often label these machines simply as triceps extensions, arm extensions, or triceps kickbacks, but it can be appropriate to call them triceps pushdown machines to differentiate them from the other types of triceps machines. Compared to seated dip machines, pushdown machines let you train the long head of the triceps at somewhat longer lengths, while preacher-style and overhead triceps extension machines train the long head at longer lengths.Prior to starting your single leg leg press set, you have the option of initially setting up with both feet on the foot plate to press the sled off the safety stoppers, or you can situate only your working side’s foot on the foot plate from the start. After finishing your set, you similarly have the option to lower the sled onto the safety stoppers with both legs, or you can lower it only with the leg you just trained. Both approaches are viable, so you can proceed whichever one you personally prefer.

Using both legs to get into the starting position will naturally be easier for what will be the working leg during the upcoming set, and returning the sled onto the safety stoppers will also be less taxing to the already fatigued working leg after the set. Some people may find that initially placing both feet on the foot plate can provide a reference point that helps achieve the ideal foot position as well.Typical incline fly machines position the torso similarly to a dumbbell incline fly, but they train the pec major and front delt through the same plane of shoulder motion that a flat dumbbell fly could. Incline fly machines have handles that provide resistance that is directed backward and downward at an angle, in contrast to dumbbells that provide resistance directed purely downward due to gravity acting on them. The angle of the resistance that an incline fly machine provides aligns with the angle to which the bench is inclined, so the arm motion relative to the torso winds up being the same as what could be performed with a flat dumbbell fly.Leg presses are among the most popular gym machines, and they come in a variety of forms. The two most widely available subtypes are seated pin-loaded leg presses and plate-loaded 45° linear leg presses. Seated pin-loaded leg presses, which can also be called pin-loaded horizontal leg presses, can have a seat that moves when pressing with a stationary foot plate or a foot plate that moves when pressing with a stationary seat. The mobile part of the machine chiefly has a horizontal trajectory during the leg press set, and the reps start in the bottom position with the knees in their peak degree of bend.

In contrast, the foot plate always moves throughout a set with a plate-loaded 45° linear leg press, while the seat remains stationary. With this type of machine, the set begins with the knees in an extended position, and a user must disengage safety locks to achieve full depth. While all of these machines do not necessarily have sleds that move precisely at 45° angles, their sled trajectory is clearly diagonal in nature with similar proportions of vertical and horizontal movement.

Plate-loaded leverage leg presses, which are also called pivot leg presses, look reasonably comparable to plate-loaded 45° linear leg presses, as they both have similarly angled backrests and can position the legs in a similar starting position angle relative to the ground. However, a leverage model lacks sliding rails on which the sled moves, but rather has rotational motion occurring about a pivot point at the base of the machine that is connected to the foot plate and the loading sleeves. This design can cause the weight plates to move horizontally closer toward the machine’s pivot point as the knees and hips progressively bend during the lowering phase of a rep. As this lever arm decreases throughout the lowering phase, the weight plates provide a lower magnitude of resistive torque, while they provide a greater magnitude of resistive torque as their lever arm increases when the knees and hips extend during the lifting phase. In contrast to this variable resistance profile with a leverage leg press, a 45° linear leg press provides a constant level of resistance at the start and end of each rep.

Plate-loaded horizontal leg presses, which are also called hip presses, additionally involve machine motion that occurs about a pivot point. This design can prompt the resistance provided by the weight plates to vary throughout a rep’s range of motion when using a plate-loaded horizontal leg press in a similar way to a leverage leg press. Plate-loaded horizontal leg presses are visually characterized by having backrests that are mostly upright, in contrast to the comparatively more horizontal backrests that leverage leg presses or 45° linear leg presses have. Additionally, horizontal leg presses have foot plates that predominantly move in horizontal trajectories.

As the name suggests, plate-loaded vertical leg presses differ from the other types of leg presses by having a sled that moves in a fully vertical or close to fully vertical trajectory while having a horizontal backrest. These machines have linear slide rails like their 45° counterparts and will correspondingly provide the same degree of resistive torque throughout each rep.A Marrs bar looks similar to a safety squat bar in the sense that both bars have padding and handles that can easily be held in front of the body. For lifters who may experience discomfort in their shoulders or elbows when holding a straight bar on the back due to pre-existing injuries or mobility limitations, using a specialty bar with handles can let them comfortably train squats or good mornings. However, a Marrs bar situates the load lower on the back than a low bar back squat would, in contrast to a safety squat bar, which situates the load like a hybrid between a high bar back squat and front squat. For a given degree of forward torso lean, the low positioning of a Marrs bar situates its load horizontally closer to the lumbar spine and hips than a straight bar would, and the difference is further magnified with a safety squat bar. To keep the total center of mass of your body weight and the bar weight over your midfoot (which is needed to maintain balance), squatting with a Marrs bar encourages a greater degree of forward torso lean compared to a straight bar and even more so compared to a safety squat bar.If you want to maximize your ratio of pec major engagement to front delt engagement, a standing machine chest fly may be difficult to beat. Both the pec major and front delt have large moment arms for horizontally flexing the shoulder, which is the movement that occurs when bringing the arms toward midline while they are elevated to where they form 90-degree angles with the torso (like during a pec deck fly or flat dumbbell fly). Shoulder horizontal flexion occurs in the transverse plane, which is the plane of motion that divides the body into upper and lower halves.

As with other chest flies, a standing machine chest fly trains shoulder horizontal flexion, but it more so trains shoulder adduction in the frontal plane, which is the plane of motion that divides the body into front and back halves. If your arms are elevated and form a “T-position” with your torso without your elbows being in front of your shoulders (like during part of a snow angel), shoulder adduction is the movement that brings your arms down and in toward your sides, while shoulder abduction is the motion that sweeps your arms out away from your sides. The sternocostal region of your pec major, constituting your middle and lower chest, has meaningful leverage to function as a shoulder adductor, so it is very well suited to function as the prime mover during a standing machine chest fly.

In contrast, the front delt has notable leverage for functioning as a shoulder abductor in the frontal plane, which is why it can assist the side delt during a lateral raise. While its ability to generate shoulder horizontal flexion torque would be beneficial during a standing machine chest fly, the shoulder abduction torque its contraction simultaneously generates is counterproductive during this exercise. Since a greater degree of shoulder motion occurs in the transverse plane than the frontal plane during a standing machine chest fly (so long as you do not excessively lean forward), it would be particularly inefficient for the front delt to be heavily engaged throughout this variation. The front delt will correspondingly provide minimal assistance to the pec major during a standing machine chest fly, in contrast to most fly chest fly variations that meaningfully train both muscles.Like a standard seated hip abductor machine, a 3D hip abductor machine trains hip abduction as you push the thighs out away from the midline of your body. In contrast to a typical seated hip abductor, a 3D hip abductor simultaneously trains hip extension, as the thighs move backward relative to the pelvis. The upper region of the gluteus maximus functions as both a hip abductor and a hip extensor, so it is particularly well suited as the prime mover when using a 3D hip abductor. On the other hand, the tensor fascia latae functions as both a hip abductor and a hip flexor, so it most likely is trained less effectively with a 3D hip abductor compared to a standard hip abductor, where the hip flexion torque its contraction generates is not counterproductive to exercise performance.A buffalo bar (which is also known as a curved bar or bow bar) and a cambered bench press bar can serve similar purposes, as they let you descend through a greater range of motion when bench pressing than a straight barbell would permit. Compared to a straight bar, these specialty bars can correspondingly allow you to train your pec major, front delt, and triceps at longer muscle lengths. Dumbbells can similarly let you train through a longer range of motion than a straight bar, but buffalo bars and cambered bench press bars provide greater stability. Since dumbbell size scales with weight, dumbbells of a certain weight may also be bulky enough that they restrict pressing range of motion, in contrast to the specialty bars. Dumbbell pressing additionally trains the triceps less effectively than any of the bar alternatives, since maximal engagement of the triceps could prompt you to dump the dumbbells out to the sides, but a bar lets you more efficiently direct triceps force into the press.

A buffalo bar has a shaft that is continuously curved except for its ends that meet the collars, while a cambered bench press bar has a straight shaft except for the small portions where the camber is incorporated. This design can let you grasp a cambered bench press bar with a grip that is identical to a straight bar, but a buffalo bar requires a slightly different grip to account for its curvature. While variation exists among different models, some cambered bench press bars will also have a steeper camber than a buffalo bar to let lifters with a high degree of shoulder mobility train through an even greater range of motion.

Cambered squat bars and buffalo bars can both be performed for presses and rowing variations, but a buffalo bar is more versatile because it can also be comfortably positioned on your back for exercises such as squats, good mornings, and lunges. The same technique can be used for those lower body variations whether a buffalo bar or straight bar is used, but the curved shaft reduces the upper body mobility that is required to grasp the bar, which can be useful for lifters with mobility limitations. A cambered squat bar can also be used for those exercises, but a cambered squat bar is distinct from a cambered bench press bar, which is not designed to rest on your back.When performing lunges, you can alternate lead legs every rep within one set, or you can continuously perform each rep with one lead leg as a set before performing another set where you use your other side as the lead leg for all of its reps. Either approach can be used to train your lower body effectively, but one strategy may be preferable for you based on the context.

The main advantage of the alternating approach is that you can train each side of your body with a similar intensity in a time-efficient manner. If you continuously lunge with one leg in the lead position, your rear leg will experience fatigue by the end of the set because it is still contributing to a non-negligible degree. For your rear leg to be close to fully recovered before lunging continuously with it as the lead leg, you will have to rest after the set that just trained your other side in the lead position. If you do not rest between these sets, the leg that you train second in the lead position will be unlikely to match the first set’s reps with the same proximity to failure if your legs are similarly strong. Resting between sets with the continuous approach will let your two legs be similarly fresh for their sets as the lead leg, but the additional rest time can contribute to a longer duration being required to complete the same number of reps per lead leg compared to the alternating approach. With greater time-efficiency, the alternating strategy can help you complete your workout more quickly or have more time to complete additional training within the workout window you have.

The alternating approach can also pose a greater peak cardiovascular demand with its longer set duration than the continuous approach, which may be viewed as a pro or a con based on the individual. If you find that, rather than the strength/strength endurance of your lower body muscles, your cardiovascular fitness limits your lunge performance with the alternating strategy, it may not be stimulating increases in lower body strength or size as effectively as the continuous technique. Whether this may happen will be influenced by an individual’s cardiovascular fitness and the rep range used for lunges. Performing a greater number of reps per lead leg in a set will naturally be more cardiovascularly demanding, so some people may prefer to avoid high-rep sets when lunging with the alternating approach to prevent the cardiovascular demand from limiting performance. Sets of lunges performed close to failure with 20-25 reps per lead leg can be exhausting with the alternating strategy, but performing 8-10 reps per lead leg may feel much more manageable.Plate-loaded squat machines with shoulder pads come in a variety of subtypes, including standing hack squat, pendulum squat, V-squat, leverage squat, and power squat pro machines. Standing hack squat machines are characterized by their carriages (AKA sleds) linearly moving along rails that are typically about 45 degrees from vertical. In contrast to the other squat machines, a standing hack squat lacks lever arms that arc about a pivot point, which means that a standing hack squat will provide a constant level of resistance torque throughout a rep’s range of motion. Resistive torque from the other machines will vary during a rep depending on how close their lever arms are to being parallel to the ground, which is when the torque from gravitational force can be maximized.

Like standing hack squats, pendulum squats and V-squats also have long back pads that can extend along the length of the entire back. The designs of these three machines let users avoid any forward torso lean when using them, which minimizes resistive torque acting on the spinal extensor muscles. A pendulum squat has a long lever arm connecting the carriage (with the shoulder and back pads) to the machine’s pivot point, resulting in the carriage moving in a prominent pendulum motion during the exercise. When resting your back against the back pad of a pendulum squat, you face toward the base of the machine with the machine’s pivot point in front of you. In contrast, when resting your back against the back pad of a V-squat, you face away from the base of the machine with the machine’s pivot point behind you. This design divergence contributes to pendulum squats and V-squats having different paths of motion.

Leverage squat and power squat pro machines most visually differ from the other three aforementioned machine types by lacking back pads, which gives you the freedom to vary the degree to which you lean your torso forward at your hips or keep it upright. The ability to  lean the torso forward relative to the line of resistance provided by the machine can let you load your spinal extensor muscles to a greater degree with either of these machines compared to the machines that have long back pads. While leverage squats exclusively have shoulder pads, power squat pros additionally have an upper back pad positioned between the shoulder pads, which can distribute the machine’s loading through a wider surface area. Like V-squats and pendulum squats, leverage squats also have angled footplates, but power squat pros have completely flat footplates. Pro power squats also differ from any of the other squat machines by normally having multiple pairs of loading sleeves that shift the resistance profile based on where you load the weight plates, which can let you adjust how difficult the bottom of the rep is compared to its start.

When differentiating these squat machine types, it is worth noting that manufacturers do not follow a universal nomenclature. In particular, “power squat pro” is not a standardized name for this fairly uncommon design, but manufacturers most often incorporate some variant of “power squat pro” into their model names for this style of machine.

While manufacturers may not consistently use any names that differentiate them, V-squat machines can also be subdivided into one of two subtypes that can affect their usage. Some V-squats have a pair of short shoulder pads that are completely perpendicular to the back pad, designed to be used with a user’s back against the back pad. Other V-squats (which some manufacturers call V-front squat or front squat machines) have a second pair of shoulder pads extending from those pads further away from the back pad, and these pads are typically angled with corners for the shoulders to rest snugly under while facing toward the back pad. The machines with these extended shoulder pads are intentionally designed for users to train standard back-supported V-squats along with reverse V-squats (squatting like you would with a leverage squat) and good mornings while facing toward the back pad. Some people still perform reverse V-squats with machines that only have the short shoulder pads, but not everyone can do so comfortably while feeling stable. When squatting while facing toward the back pad with one of these machines, the neck may have cock back into an extended position with the face pressed against the pad to keep the shoulder pads sufficiently on the shoulders. This is not inherently problematic, but some people find the head positioning to feel awkward and uncomfortable, in contrast to when using a machine with the additional shoulder pads that are designed with this variation in mind.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of bench press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins/spotter arms.
    2. Certain power rack and bench press station models have fairly wide spaces between safety pin/spotter arm holes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your rack, it is not ideal, but the safety pins/spotter arms can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning to bench press, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you are specifically training to bench press as much weight as possible, you can optionally exaggerate your lower back arch, which may allow you to shorten the range of motion a bit and potentially lift heavier weights.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending until you are able to tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    2. When aiming to touch the bar to your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. To touch your lower chest, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Once the bar contacts your chest, pause in that bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds) while staying tight.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This time-tested exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of bench press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins/spotter arms.
    2. Certain power rack and bench press station models have fairly wide spaces between safety pin/spotter arm holes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your rack, it is not ideal, but the safety pins/spotter arms can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning to bench press, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you are specifically training to bench press as much weight as possible, you can optionally exaggerate your lower back arch, which may allow you to shorten the range of motion a bit and potentially lift heavier weights.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner. Progressively bend your elbows and allow them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending until you can tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. Strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set when viewed from the side.
    2. When aiming to touch the bar to your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. To touch your lower chest, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This time-tested exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. If you are using an adjustable incline bench, position it in the fully upright setting. Otherwise, proceed to the next step.
    1. You can interchangeably use an adjustable bench that is positioned fully upright or a seated bench that is permanently fixed in an upright position.
    2. Alternatively, you can use a bench that is permanently fixed in a flat position. This will preclude you from resting your back against the bench, but otherwise allow you to use the same technique that the exercise involves when using back support.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and hold them with your palms facing in toward your sides.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
3. Sit down on a seated bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your lower thighs without leaning back onto the back rest.
    1. To move the dumbbells into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
    2. When seated, your elbows should be tucked by your sides with your palms facing in toward each other.
4. March your thighs up one at a time as you lift the dumbbells up near your shoulders with your elbows bent. Simultaneously lean back to situate your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench.
    1. After doing so, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your shoulders with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
5. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling, and maintain this posture throughout your set.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
6. In the starting position, your palms should be facing in toward each other as your upper arms are close to being parallel to each other in front of your body (as opposed to being flared out away from your sides).
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping your palms facing in toward each other, press the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward as you progressively straighten them until your elbows are fully straightened with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your forearms should be in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your feet flat on the floor along with your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. Since they will naturally arc back toward your shoulders as you press up, your elbows and dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders at the end of the lifting phase.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively bend while simultaneously moving downward until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbells in front of your shoulders.
    1. Lower the dumbbells as far as you can while keeping your wrists in a straight position stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical, which should result in your elbows being lower than your shoulders.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearms vertical.
    3. If you are using heavy, bulky dumbbells, the dumbbells contacting your shoulders may also determine your end range of the lowering phase.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your shoulders following the lowering phase of the last rep, march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to situate the dumbbells on your thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, upper pec major, triceps, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Alternatively, you can also use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline. 
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on the incline bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your lower thighs near your abdomen with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    2. To move the dumbbells into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up one at a time in quick succession to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench.
    1. Once you are inclined, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
5. Flare your elbows moderately out to your sides such that your upper arms form approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    1. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable on your shoulders, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbows out within this range.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the dumbbells up by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten them until your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press, as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner toward the sides of your chest through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Let your elbows moderately flare out to your sides as they descend and bend enough to keep your wrists stacked above them with vertical forearms. Lifters often find that at least a mild degree of elbow tuck makes this exercise feel most comfortable for the shoulders compared to fully flaring the elbows straight out to the sides.
    2. How far you can lower the dumbbells with vertical forearms will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility, but most people have the mobility to descend until their hands are at least level with their chest.
    3. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    4. There is no need to lower the dumbbells extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Once you have fully lowered the dumbbells, pause in this bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., about two seconds).
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your chest following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arms in line with your sides to tuck your elbows and position the dumbbells closer to your thighs.
    2. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with your middle-to-upper chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is excessively low or high, the exercise may feel awkward and uncomfortable.
2. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the stoppers (that are positioned under the arms) to a height that allows you to press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with proper positioning, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
    3. Various machine models do not allow you to adjust the stoppers.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground under your bent knees.
    2. If the back pad is sufficiently tall, rest your head against it. Otherwise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. Firmly grasp the horizontally directed handles with an overhand grip. Stack your elbows with your wrists such that your forearms align with the incline angle at which you will press.
    1. In contrast to a purely horizontal seated chest press, where you would position your elbows at the height of your wrists, your elbows should be lower than your wrists during this incline variation. Specifically how much lower you should position your elbows will be affected by the machine model you are using, but machines commonly allow you to press at an angle that is 30-45 degrees beyond horizontal. Consequently, you can initially position your elbows so that your forearms form approximately 30-45 degree angles with the ground. After starting your set, you can allow this forearm angle based on what feels most natural and comfortable with the machine you are using.
    2. Aligning your forearms with the incline angle at which you will press will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    3. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    4. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping your forearms aligned with the angle at which you are pressing, press the handles forward and upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows diagonally forward and upward while they progressively straighten.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handles to move downward and backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your forearms aligned with the angle of your press.
    2. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers behind them or stop just before that point.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arms abruptly back in an uncontrolled fashion.
    4. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
