    2. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable for your shoulders, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbows out within this range.
    3. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are completely tucked by your sides, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your upper arms would form a “T-shape” with your torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbells up by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten them until your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your wrists stacked over your elbows, which will keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press.
    2. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner. Move through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits while keeping your wrists stacked over your elbows.
    1. Allow your elbows to flare out moderately away from your sides so that your upper arms form close to 45-75-degree angles with your torso at the bottom.
    2. People commonly can lower the weights until their elbows are somewhat lower than their shoulders while keeping their forearms vertical, but your specific end range will be determined by your individual shoulder mobility. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulders at your end range.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the entire movement.
4. Once you have fully lowered the dumbbells, pause in this bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
6. After finishing the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arms in line with your sides to tuck your elbows and position the dumbbells closer to your torso.
7. If you are able to do so, rock forward in a sit-up motion until you are sitting upright and can place the dumbbells on top of your thighs before unhooking your feet and stepping down with the dumbbells by your sides.
    1. This maneuver is only feasible if you have the core strength to perform a sit-up with the weights you selected.
    2. If you cannot sit up with the weights, you can have a spotter take the dumbbells from your hands and set them down one at a time while you are lying down on the bench. This option can be ideal if you are using heavy weights, but it naturally requires another person. When using this approach, make sure to communicate what you want your spotter to do before starting the set.
    3. Alternatively, you can set the dumbbells directly onto the floor while still lying down with the weights by your chest. Compared to the other options, this approach potentially may involve a higher risk of injury if you move your shoulders into a disadvantaged position with heavy weights. Relative to setting the weights under control, dumping the weights forward may be less demanding on your shoulders, but it involves the risk of having a dumbbell ricochet and potentially injuring someone nearby or damaging equipment. For these reasons, it is recommended to use a spotter or sit up with the dumbbells when possible.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions  while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and place them on the ground far enough apart that you can sit in between them while being able to reach them.
2. Sit down with your buttocks and the back of your heels on the ground in between the dumbbells with your knees straight and your torso upright.
    1. Based on your preference, you can situate your feet hip-width apart from each other, or you can spread your legs further apart from each other to widen your base of support.
3. Firmly grasp the dumbbells and lift them up near your shoulders with your elbows bent, forearms in a vertical position, and wrists stacked over your elbows while keeping your torso upright.
    1. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
    2. If the dumbbells are light enough to do so, you can lift the dumbbells toward your shoulders by curling them up as you swing your elbows forward.
    3. With heavier dumbbells, you can initially place their handles across your lower thighs near your knees with your palms facing your thighs. Then you can bend your knees and march your thighs up one at a time to help lift the dumbbells up toward your shoulders.
    4. At a certain weight, moving the dumbbells into the starting position can be the limiting factor rather than pressing them up during this variation, so you are not alone if you find it awkward and difficult to use at least moderately heavy loads for this exercise. If you wish to train a dumbbell Z press with heavier loads than you can move into position with this variation, you can select the single arm dumbbell Z press, which allows you to use your free arm to assist with setting up.
4. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, pull your shoulder blades back together to adopt a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
5. Flare your elbows at least moderately out to your sides to a degree that feels comfortable for your shoulders.
    1. When 0 degrees of elbow flare means that your wrists are directly in front of your shoulders with your palms facing each other, and 90 degrees of elbow flare means that your wrists are fully out to your sides with your palms facing straight forward, using about 60-75 degrees of elbow flare is commonly appropriate. In this position, your elbows will point more out to the side than forward, but they will not be fully flared out.
    2. If your shoulders feel comfortable when pressing with a greater degree of elbow flare, you can certainly flare your elbows beyond that point, but some people find that utilizing a closer to moderate degree of elbow flare feels most comfortable.
    3. The handles of the dumbbells should be parallel to your upper arms.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping your torso upright, press the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten your elbows until they are fully straightened with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your forearms should be in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
    3. While keeping your heels on the ground, be careful to avoid letting your torso tip backward.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively bend while simultaneously moving outward and downward until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbells by the sides of your shoulders.
    1. Lower the dumbbells as far as you can while keeping your wrists in a straight position stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical, which should result in your elbows being lower than your shoulders.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearms vertical.
    3. Continue keeping your torso upright and chest up as you avoid slouching forward or rocking back.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Position the J-hooks of a rack to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are lying on the ground with your mouth under the bar.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
2. Load the bar with your intended weight, and have your spotter stand behind the bar if you are using a spotter (which is recommended).
    1. For comfort, you can optionally place a yoga mat on the floor where you will lie.
    2. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, it will be safer and easier to bail if you have at least one pair of full-diameter weight plates (e.g., 45lb (20kg) plates or bumper plates) loaded on the bar.
3. Lie down with your back on the ground, with your legs out in front of you and your knees straight. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell
    1. The back of your heels should be on the ground, about hip-width to shoulder-width apart.
    2. The back of your head and buttocks should be resting on the ground.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
5. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your middle chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists (when viewed from the side). This will keep your forearms close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set.
    2. When bringing the bar toward your middle, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. Avoid fully flaring your elbows out away from your sides or completely tucking them. The appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar toward your middle chest.
4. Continue lowering the bar toward your middle chest until the back of your upper arms and elbows contact the ground in a controlled manner.
    1. While you do not have to finish the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you certainly should avoid slamming your arms onto the ground.
    2. You will not be bouncing your arms off the ground.
5. Pause with your upper arms on the ground for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds) while staying tight.
    1. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back, glutes squeezed, and core braced.
    2. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability since this is not a touch-and-go press.
6. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up with explosive intent. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your chest.
    3. Continue keeping your buttocks and the back of your heels firmly planted on the ground.
7. Alternate between steps 3-6 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
8. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
9. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
While training your pec major, front delts, and triceps, this exercise can effectively transfer to improved bench press strength.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Lift the dumbbells up near the front of your shoulders with your elbows bent by curling the weights and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbells into position if they are too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbells mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbows to curl the weights upward while swinging your elbows forward. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your shoulders with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, pull your shoulder blades back together, and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back along with your neck, like when you are normally standing upright.
4. Flare your elbows at least moderately out to your sides to a degree that feels comfortable for your shoulders.
    1. When 0 degrees of elbow flare means that your wrists are directly in front of your shoulders with your palms facing each other, and 90 degrees of elbow flare means that your wrists are fully out to your sides with your palms facing straight forward, using about 60-75 degrees of elbow flare is commonly appropriate. In this position, your elbows will point more out to the side than forward, but they will not be fully flared out.
    2. If your shoulders feel comfortable when pressing with a greater degree of elbow flare, you can certainly flare your elbows beyond that point, but some people find that utilizing a closer to moderate degree of elbow flare feels most comfortable.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes like you are trying to crush a walnut, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core and tightening your glutes throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten your elbows until they are fully straightened with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your forearms should be in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your knees and hips straightened as you avoid using your legs to help lift the weights or leaning your torso back via lower back arching.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively bend while simultaneously moving outward and downward until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbells by the sides of your shoulders.
    1. Lower the dumbbells as far as you can while keeping your wrists in a straight position stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical, which should result in your elbows being lower than your shoulders.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearms vertical.
    3. If you are using heavy, bulky dumbbells, the dumbbells contacting your shoulders may also determine your end range of the lowering phase.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your shoulders following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arms in line with your sides to point the backside of your elbows forward, which will result in your palms facing in toward each other.
    2. Then lower the dumbbells to your sides as you straighten your elbows and swing your elbows back by your sides.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
As an alternative to setting up with the bar starting from a rack, you may begin with the bar on the floor and perform a clean or power clean to move the bar into the starting position for your push press. Either option is viable, but cleans and power cleans are distinct exercises described in detail by their own exercise pages.

1. Adjust the J-hooks of a power rack or squat stand to a height that allows you to position the bar on the front of your shoulders and walk out the weight comfortably.
    1. As an initial approximation, you can position the J-hooks a couple of finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
    2. If you find that the J-hook increments available to you are further apart than ideal, it is better to place the J-hooks lower than what is optimal, compared to higher than optimal.
    3. If you are unsure whether you initially positioned the J-hooks to an appropriate height, you can finish setting up without adding plates to the bar, unrack the empty bar, walk it out, and then rerack it. This can allow you to confirm that the J-hooks are appropriate before adding plates to the bar.
    4. If you need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks excessively high.
    5. If you find that a greater than a mild knee bend (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) is required to position the bar on your shoulders, you initially positioned the J-hooks excessively low.
2. Place the barbell on top of the J-hooks, and add weight plates of your desired load to it.
3. Face the center of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance, and grasp the bar with your palms facing down as your hands are mildly outside shoulder width.
    1. For the full clean grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your 2nd-5th fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. Starting with a mildly outside shoulder-width grip is appropriate for many lifters, but you can try out different grip widths over time to determine where you personally feel the best. A proportion of lifters feel strongest with a moderately outside shoulder-width grip.
4. While simultaneously bending your elbows, shallowly bend at your knees and hips with an upright torso to walk under the bar and situate the bar on the front of your shoulders just in front of the base of your neck.
    1. The bar should be resting on your front delts (which are the muscles on the front of your shoulders that attach to the outer aspects of your collarbones) and the outer portions of your collarbones, which together form a shelf with the base of your neck to cradle the bar.
    2. While the bar should be directly in front of your neck, it should not be pressing into your neck to the extent that it feels like it is choking you.
    3. With the appropriate bar positioning, you should feel that the bar is stable on the front of your shoulders if you were to un-rack the bar and walk out without even having your hands around the bar.
    4. If you are not accustomed to push presses or front squats, it is normal to experience discomfort from the pressure the bar places on the front of your shoulders (which may cause some form of skin bruising), but it should not be painful with the correct bar placement.
5. With the bar now on the front of your shoulders, lift your elbows up until your upper arms are parallel to the ground while keeping your fingers wrapped around the bar.
    1. You can perform steps 3 and 4 simultaneously with each other.
    2. The bony prominence on the back of each elbow should now be pointing straight forward as your palms are facing backward.
    3. Your hands can assist in keeping the bar balanced, but the bar should first and foremost be resting on the front of your shoulders without necessarily needing your hands to hold it there.
    4. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your wrists and/or forearm muscles in this position. However, if you find this position to be painful or lack the mobility to achieve it, you can lift your elbows as high as is comfortable to you rather than trying to force yourself into an aggravating position.
    5. You will maintain this arm position and bar position until you start driving the bar up during your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Stand up in a quarter squat-like manner by extending (straightening) your knees and hips.
3. Take a couple of steps backward, and position your feet near hip width apart from each other as your toes are pointing slightly out to the sides (rather than pointing straight ahead).
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to push press effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on hip structure.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling strong and stable when push pressing, which may involve trying out mildly different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
4. Tuck your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
    2. You can perform this step simultaneously when dipping during the next step.
5. Mildly bend your knees to dip down while maintaining an upright torso position and keeping your chest and elbows up.
    1. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground during this dip phase.
    2. Your knees should track straight forward toward your toes rather than caving inward.
    3. The dip should resemble a quarter squat but without any of the forward trunk lean that occurs when squatting. Your shoulders should remain vertically stacked above your hips by keeping your torso fully upright.
6. Directly after reaching the bottom of your dip, forcibly push through your feet as you rapidly extend (straighten) your knees and hips to generate upward momentum.
    1. The intent of this leg drive phase should be akin to a jump without lifting your feet fully off the ground.
    2. At the end of this drive, you can allow your heels to lift up slightly off the ground while keeping the front of your feet firmly in contact with the ground if a slight heel lift feels natural to you. However, you do not need to think about lifting your heels up intentionally, and you can keep your heels down when learning how to push press if doing so allows you to feel more stable and balanced.
7. Directly after you have finished extending your legs, explosively press the bar upward by forcibly driving your elbows upward. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
    1. To the best of your ability, avoid pushing the bar forward out in front of your shoulders. This can be challenging if you are new to this exercise, since you want to avoid hitting your face with the bar, but initially tucking your chin back appropriately can provide sufficient clearance.
    2. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward during this step, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
8. Continue explosively pressing the bar upward until you have fully straightened your elbows with the bar overhead.
    1. At the top, you ideally should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    2. Regardless of whether you lifted your heels up off the ground after extending your legs during the leg drive phase, your heels should be flat on the ground with the rest of your feet at the end of the pressing phase.
    3. Throughout the exercise, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back. Your torso should remain vertical rather than leaning back.
    4. While they are presented as different steps for clarity, you should fluidly perform steps 6-8 as a continuous movement.
9. Reverse the movement by tucking your chin back and lowering the bar toward your shoulders in a controlled manner.
    1. If you are using a load that meaningfully exceeds what you can overhead press without leg drive, you may have to lower the bar fairly quickly, even when controlling it to the best of your ability, since your legs cannot assist you until the end.
10. Shallowly bend your knees as you did during step 5 (the dip phase) to help control the weight once the bar reaches your shoulders at the end of the lowering phase.
11. Stand upright with the bar on your shoulders by fully straightening your knees to return to the starting position.
12. Alternate between steps 4-11 until you have completed your set.
13. Following completion of your final rep, walk forward until the bar contacts the back of both J-hooks before bending your knees to lower the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While developing explosive overhead pressing strength and engaging the quads to generate leg drive, this exercise can train  your front delts, triceps, traps, lateral delts,  and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a long looped elastic band with both hands inside its ends and your thumbs outside.
    1. The band should be situated between the bases of your palms and the bases of your fingers, with one side of the band contacting your palms and the other side of the band contacting the backside of your hands.
    2. Inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
2. Loop the band around the lower aspect of your upper back (i.e., near the bottom of your shoulder blades) by lifting your arms up and back before making sure the band is resting flat on your back without being twisted.
    1. After doing so, your palms should be facing forward, and the band should be taut in a stable position against your back, where it will not excessively roll up or down.
3. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
    1. In this “on all fours” position, your elbows should be stacked underneath your shoulders with your arms pointing straight down toward the ground.
    2. As you are setting up, it is common for the band to slide a bit up or down, but the band should remain on your upper back or just below your upper back. At no point should the band slide up to your neck.
4. With your fingers spread somewhat apart from each other, situate your hands mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other.
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
    2. You will now be feeling peak resistance from the band.
5. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner until it lightly touches the ground.
    1. Let your elbows flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you. This commonly results in the upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with the torso (when viewed from above) at the bottom of the push-up. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are fully tucked to your sides, remaining shoulder-width the whole rep, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows point straight out to your sides, letting your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact the ground instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    5. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
    6. Resistance from the band will progressively decrease as you descend.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
    4. Resistance from the band will progressively increase as you push up.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This classic exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your front delts, triceps, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Alternatively, you can also use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline. 
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on the incline bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your lower thighs near your abdomen with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    2. To move the dumbbells into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up one at a time in quick succession to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench.
    1. Once you are inclined, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
5. In the starting position, your palms should face each other, and your elbows should be tucked fairly close to your sides.
    1. Keeping your elbows tucked to the extent that your upper arms form near 30-degree angles with your torso (when viewed from above) is generally appropriate.
    2. If you completely tuck your elbows, you will likely reduce how effectively this exercise can train your chest compared to using a mild degree of elbow flare.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward as you progressively straighten them until your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your palms facing each other as you avoid notably flaring your elbows out away from your sides, like you otherwise do with a standard dumbbell bench press.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press, as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    3. Keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench throughout the exercise. 
    4. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
3. While keeping your elbows tucked fairly close to your sides, reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. While they descend, let your elbows bend enough to keep your wrists stacked above them with vertical forearms.
    2. Strive to finish with your upper arms forming close to 30-degree angles with your torso.
    3. How far you can lower the dumbbells with vertical forearms will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility, but most people have the mobility to descend until their hands are at least level with their chest.
    4. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders at the bottom.
    5. There is no need to lower the dumbbells extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your chest following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arms in line with your sides to tuck your elbows and position the dumbbells closer to your thighs.
    2. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on a flat exercise bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your upper thighs near your abdomen, with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Position yourself so that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    2. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    3. To move heavy dumbbells efficiently into the proper position, you can initially situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up. Then you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
2. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
4. Flare your elbows moderately out to your sides such that your upper arms form approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    1. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable for your shoulders, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbows out within this range.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the dumbbells up by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten them until your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press, as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner toward the sides of your chest through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Let your elbows moderately flare out to your sides as they descend and bend enough to keep your wrists stacked above them with vertical forearms. Lifters often find that at least a mild degree of elbow tuck makes this exercise feel most comfortable for the shoulders compared to fully flaring the elbows straight out to the sides.
    2. How far you can lower the dumbbells with vertical forearms will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility, but most people have the mobility to descend until their hands are at least level with their chest.
    3. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    4. There is no need to lower the dumbbells extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your chest following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arms in line with your sides to tuck your elbows and position the dumbbells closer to your thighs.
    2. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
While not loading your triceps as effectively as a barbell bench press, this exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delts.## Setting up
In addition to situating a mobile seated or adjustable bench within a power rack or squat stand, you have the option of setting up this exercise within a seated overhead press station in which a seated bench is fused to a rack. The exercise technique is the same for either option, but the set-up details differ depending on which equipment you use, so each set-up is described separately.

### **Setting up a power rack or squat stand**

1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a 90-degree angle, or approach a mobile exercise bench that is permanently fixed with an upright backrest.
    1. If you have access to one, a seated bench with a backrest that does not reach your head is ideal because it can allow you to move your head back away from the bar during the exercise.
2. Place the bench centered within a power rack or squat stand with the backrest in between the uprights, where you will adjust the J-hooks.
    1. The proper placement of the bench should allow you to unrack the bar comfortably without bumping the J-hooks with the bar during your set. This may require some trial and error.
    2. If the backrest is situated in front of where the bar will start, you will have to reach back behind your head to unrack the bar, which requires greater than typical shoulder mobility.
    3. If the backrest is situated too far behind where the bar will start, you may bump the J-hooks with the bar when pressing the bar up.
3. Position the J-hooks at a height that will allow you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are seated on the bench.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks without having to shrug your shoulders up.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the top of the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If neither of the two J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little lower than optimal, since it may not be feasible to unrack the bar with proper positioning if the J-hooks are excessively high.
    4. You may have to try out different J-hook heights to determine which setting is best for you.
4. Place the bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks.
    1. Initially leave the bar unloaded to check that the bench and J-hooks are positioned correctly.
5. Sit down with your buttocks and back in contact with the bench. Firmly place your feet flat on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart with your knees bent.
    1. Your hips should be fully back on the seat.
    2. If your bench’s backrest reaches your head, rest your head against it as well.
6. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
7. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width as your palms are facing forward.
    1. Situating your index fingers directly outside the outer aspects of your shoulders is generally an effective approach, but what is personally your ideal grip may be a bit wider or closer. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
8. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of overhead press reps with the form described under the exercise technique section prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. If you cannot comfortably unrack the bar and avoid bumping the J-hooks during these reps, adjust your bench placement and/or J-hook height and repeat this step until you can do so.
9. If you have access to adjustable safety pins, position the pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase.
    1. This will entail placing the pins just below the height of your upper chest (when seated) if you can comfortably lower the bar to your upper chest.
10. Add plates of your desired load to the bar, and repeat steps 5-7 before commencing your working set.

### **Setting up an overhead press station**

1. Place the bar on top of the J-hooks behind the seat that will allow you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are seated on the bench.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks without having to shrug your shoulders up.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the top of the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. You may have to try out different J-hook heights to determine which setting is best for you, so you can initially leave the bar unloaded to check if you can comfortably unrack the bar.
2. Sit down with your buttocks and back in contact with the bench. Firmly place your feet flat on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart with your knees bent.
    1. Your hips should be fully back on the seat.
    2. If your bench’s backrest reaches your head, rest your head against it as well.
3. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
4. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width as your palms are facing forward.
    1. Situating your index fingers directly outside the outer aspects of your shoulders is generally an effective approach, but what is personally your ideal grip may be a bit wider or closer. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of overhead press reps with the form described under the exercise technique section prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. If you cannot comfortably unrack the bar, you can situate the bar on a different pair of J-hooks and try again.
    2. If you lack the shoulder mobility to unrack the bar from the J-hooks behind the bench, you can situate the bar on top of the J-hooks in front of you that are slightly above shoulder height (when seated). To unrack the bar from these J-hooks, you could initially scoot forward on the seat until your shoulders are close to being under the bar, lift the bar up off the J-hooks, and scoot back to the back rest. Note that this approach to unrack the bar from the front J-hooks differs from the standard unrack method described in the exercise technique section.
6. Add plates of your desired load to the bar, and repeat steps 2-4 before commencing your working set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position and throughout the exercise, your feet should be firmly planted on the ground, while your buttocks and upper back are snugly contacting the bench.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward until it is just in front of the front edge of the J-hooks. Hold the bar in this position for a moment.
    1. Maintain your “proud chest” posture to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar in a controlled manner until you are able to tap near your upper chest lightly with the bar, or until you have fully moved through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. If your bench permits you to do so, tuck your chin back to move your face away from the bar and allow you to lower the bar in a close to fully vertical path. If your bench restricts your head, you will have to move the bar slightly forward to avoid hitting your head. Try not to move the bar any further in front of you than is required to clear your head.
    2. Keep your elbows mostly stacked under the bar rather than meaningfully tracking in front of the bar or behind. However, you can allow your elbows to track slightly forward in front of the bar at the bottom of the rep if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    3. Your elbows should neither be fully flared, such that they are pointing completely out to your sides, nor should they be fully tucked, such that they are pointing directly forward. Aligning your elbows close to directly under the bar will naturally promote the appropriate balance between elbow flare and elbow tuck.
    4. Most lifters can comfortably lower the bar to their upper chest, but certain individuals with limited shoulder mobility may not be able to touch their chest with the bar without discomfort. If this applies to you, lower the bar as far as you can without discomfort.
    5. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up overhead until your elbows are fully straightened.
    1. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    2. Keep your hip bones snugly against the bench as you avoid scooting your hips forward or excessively arching your lower back.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows mostly stacked under the bar.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a high incline with an angle that is at least 60 degrees but less than 90 degrees.
    1. If your bench lacks a high incline setting, you can alternatively select the 45° incline barbell press or seated barbell overhead press as a substitution that you can perform with your bench.
2. Place the bench centered within a power rack with the head of the bench in between the posts, where you will adjust the J-hooks.
    1. You should position the bench in a spot that allows your shoulders to be a little in front of the J-hooks after setting up the rack and lying down on the bench.
3. Position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the incline bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. Then place the bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the top of the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater than ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of incline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your incline press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins.
    2. Certain power rack models have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes that may result in the highest setting not restricting your incline press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. It is not ideal if this applies to your rack, but the safety pins can still enhance safety if they are high enough to receive the bar if you dump it forward, in case you are completely pinned without anyone to assist you.
6. After loading the bar with your intended weight, lie down with your buttocks, back, and head on the incline bench. Firmly place your feet flat on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart with your knees bent.
    1. With the appropriate bench placement, your shoulders should be a little in front of the front edge of the J-hooks. If your shoulders are under the J-hooks from the bench being situated too far back, you may bump the J-hooks with the bar when pressing the bar up. If the J-hooks are excessively behind your shoulders from the bench being situated too far forward, it can be difficult to unrack the bar.
    2. Based on what feels most stable, you can position your heels directly under your knees with vertical shins, or you can bend your knees a bit beyond this point to position your heels slightly behind your knees with slightly angled shins.
    3. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
    4. If you are not training with safety pins that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for bench press variations is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground, squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your upper chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other. Descend until you are able to tap your upper chest or collarbone lightly with the bar or until you have fully moved through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom, but certain individuals with limited shoulder mobility may not be able to touch their upper chest or collarbone with the bar without discomfort. If this applies to you, lower the bar as far as you can without discomfort.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists (when viewed from the side). This will keep your forearms close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set and naturally promote the appropriate balance between elbow flare and elbow tuck.
    3. Avoid fully flaring your elbows out away from your sides or completely tucking them. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
While not training your pecs as effectively as lower incline presses, this exercise can develop your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each cable to be somewhat above the top of your head, connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, set the arms so that their ends are about the length of your wingspan apart from each other.
2. While standing to the side of one cable, grasp its handle, and then walk over to grasp the handle from the other cable with your palms facing forward and your elbows slightly bent.
    1. You will keep your elbows slightly bent throughout the exercise.
3. Center yourself between the cables, and step forward while allowing the cables to spread your arms apart and pull your arms up and back through a range that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. You want to be sufficiently positioned in front of the cables that the cables exert resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise. This will involve your hands being in front of the cable pulleys with tension still present from the cables when you are fully spread apart in the starting position of each rep.
4. While mildly bending your knees, adopt a staggered stance with one foot in front of the other as your feet are approximately hip width apart from each other.
    1. In this position, you should feel stable and balanced.
5. As you are standing with an upright torso or a slight forward torso lean, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows mildly bent, pull your hands in toward each other as they move diagonally forward and downward until they are close to touching with your arms out in front of your torso.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, your hands should be below the height of your chest.
    2. Keep your chest up throughout the set as you avoid hunching over with your shoulders slouched forward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your hands to spread apart from each other in a controlled manner as they move diagonally backward and upward in line with the resistance from the cables until you have returned to the starting position with your arms fully spread apart.
    1. At the end of this movement, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulders, but the end range should not be painful.
    2. People commonly have the shoulder mobility to allow their arms to pull back until their elbows are slightly behind the torso, but your shoulder mobility will determine what your individual end range is.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows mildly bent as the movement comes from shoulder motion rather than elbow motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your front delt, this exercise can effectively develop your pec major with an emphasis on the middle and lower pec.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with the lower half of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is too low (placing the handles near shoulder level or above), some lifters may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort from having to flare their elbows outward excessively.
    4. If the seat is excessively high (placing the handles below the lowest part of the chest), the exercise may be suboptimal for developing the chest.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust either aspect, position its arms or back pad to a setting that enables you to press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Some machines allow you to increase the range of motion by placing the arms further back toward the back pad, while others allow you to move the back pad forward, closer toward the arms. However, certain machine models may not permit you to adjust either part.
    2. If you have normal shoulder mobility, you can initially position the arms or the seat to a setting that results in the handles being near the front of your chest (when viewed from the side) once you are seated. This commonly involves setting the arms as far backward or the seat as far forward as the machine enables.
    3. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles with proper positioning during step 5, you can adjust the machine to increase the distance between the back pad and the handles until you can do so.
    4. The ideal setting should allow your elbows to start as far behind your torso as is comfortable for your shoulders while keeping your elbows level with the handles.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. With your palms facing down and elbows bent, firmly grasp the handles that are directed out to the sides. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles and position your elbows level with them, you may have to position the machine’s arms further forward or the back pad further back before resuming your setup.
    3. Aligning your elbows directly behind (rather than being above or below) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you select a proper seat height.
    4. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles forward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows forward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain level with your wrists and the handles when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handles to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers behind them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows and wrists aligned as they remain level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell, and sit down on a flat exercise bench while lifting the dumbbell onto your upper thigh near your abdomen with your palm facing inward.
    1. Position yourself so that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    2. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    3. To move the dumbbell into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbell on the front of your thigh while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest it on your thighs while sitting down.
2. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbell back toward your chest with your elbow bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. Even though only one thigh will be initially against the weight, marching both thighs up can help you rock your torso back.
    2. Throughout the exercise, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting on your thigh or chest.
    3. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
    4. Once you are lying down, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    5. You should now be holding the dumbbell right by your chest with your forearm in a vertical position, and your wrist stacked over your elbow as the handle of the dumbbell is parallel to your upper arm.
    6. You should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
4. Flare your elbow moderately out to your side such that your upper arm forms an approximately 45-75-degree angle with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arm is tucked by your side, and 90 degrees means your upper arm forms half of a “T-shape” with your torso).
    1. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable for your shoulders, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbow out within this range.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the dumbbell up by driving your elbow upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten it until your arm is directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbow, keep your forearm in a vertical position throughout the press, as your wrist and dumbbell remain stacked over your elbow.
    2. Throughout your set, keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbell should be situated vertically over your shoulder.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner toward the side of your chest through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Let your elbow moderately flare out to your side as it descends and bends enough to keep your wrist stacked above it with a vertical forearm. Lifters often find that at least a mild degree of elbow tuck makes this exercise feel most comfortable for the shoulder compared to fully flaring the elbow straight out to the side.
    2. How far you can lower the dumbbell with a vertical forearm will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility, but most people have the mobility to descend until their hand is at least level with their chest.
    3. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulder at the bottom.
    4. There is no need to lower the dumbbell extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. It can be awkward to transfer the weight to your other hand with proper positioning while lying down if you are using a weight that is at least fairly heavy to you, so you can initially return to an upright position to transfer the weight before resetting to train your other side.
5. After the dumbbell is by your chest following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arm in line with your side to tuck your elbow and position the dumbbell closer to your thigh.
    1. Once you are in this position, you can optionally reach across your body with your free hand to place it around your hand holding the dumbbell for extra stability.
6. March your thighs up until they are close to being vertical, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbell on your upper thigh before standing up with the dumbbell.
7. Place the dumbbell onto the bench, and pick it up with your other side before repeating the exercise with that side.
## Description
While not loading your triceps as effectively as a barbell bench press, this exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delts.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbells with straight elbows.
2. Position your hands by the front of your outer thighs and direct your palms so that they are facing backward.
3. Slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain the slight forward lean throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward and out to your sides through a range of motion that feels comfortable to your shoulders while bending your elbows.
    1. Your mobility will affect how high you are comfortably able to lift the dumbbells, and there is no need to force your shoulders into a range that provokes any discomfort.
    2. While different individuals may be able to lift higher up and others not quite as high, people often can lift their upper arms until they are close to parallel to the ground for a comfortable and effective range of motion.
    3. The dumbbells should remain close to your torso throughout the exercise as they move vertically.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to descend in toward your sides as you lower the dumbbells in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with straight elbows.
    1. At the end of the lowering phase, the dumbbells should be situated in front of your thighs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Position the safety pins of a power rack slightly below the height of your shoulders when you are seated on the ground with an upright torso.
    1. Ideally, the pins should be set to a height that is high enough to pick up the bar (once it is on the pins) comfortably, but low enough so that the pins do not restrict your pressing range of motion.
    2. Certain power racks have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes that can make it difficult to perform this exercise. For some racks, the lowest pin height that would not meaningfully restrict your pressing range of motion may be too low to pick up the bar comfortably. If this is the case for you, you can set the pins to the higher setting and perform this exercise through a somewhat reduced range of motion. Alternatively, you can position the pins to the lower setting and place J-hooks at the higher setting, where you would set the barbell and sit down just far enough away that the J-hooks will not interfere with your bar path during the press. With this approach, it could be challenging to unrack the bar independently since you will have to reach out in front of you. Consequently, you can have a partner assist you in lifting the bar off the J-hooks and positioning it over your upper chest if you are using a load that you cannot unrack by yourself.
2. Symmetrically place a barbell onto the middle of the safety pins and load it with your desired weight.
3. Sit down on the ground with an upright torso as the center of the barbell is directly in front of your chest. Your knees should be straight with the back of your heels on the ground.
    1. You can determine how wide apart you position your legs based on your preference. Spreading your legs fairly wide apart may assist with maintaining your balance, but you can alternatively position your legs closer together if you wish to do so.
    2. Rather than being slouched forward, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width as your palms are facing away from you.
    1. Situating your index fingers directly outside the outer aspects of your shoulders is generally an effective approach, but what is personally your ideal grip may be a bit wider or closer. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. This step can be performed simultaneously with step 4.
    2. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    3. Your forearms should now be close to a vertical position when viewed from the front.
    4. From this position, your elbows should be reasonably close to being flared 45 degrees out from your sides. 0 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the front. 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the side.
6. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension that provides a stable platform from which to press.
2. Tuck or tilt your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
3. While keeping your torso upright, press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. To the best of your ability, avoid pushing the bar forward out in front of your shoulders. This can be challenging if you are new to this exercise, since you want to avoid hitting your face with the bar. You can still meaningfully increase your strength and grow muscle if the bar drifts a bit forward, but strive to minimize any forward bar motion as well as you can for an efficient press.
    2. Keep the back of your heels firmly planted on the ground throughout your set.
    3. Avoid leaning back at any point during this exercise. If you start doing so, it can be difficult to stop your torso from tipping backward and falling to the ground.
4. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
5. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders and hips.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe after locking out the weight overhead. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to lowering the bar.
6. Reverse the movement by tucking your chin back and lowering the bar toward your collarbone in a controlled manner.
    1. Continue keeping your torso fully upright.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
    3. If the safety pins are at the height of your collarbone or slightly higher, lower the bar as close as you can to your collarbone without bumping the pins.
7. Alternate between steps 3-6 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the safety pins.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Lift the dumbbells up near the front of your shoulders with your elbows bent by curling the weights and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbells into position if they are too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbells mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbows to curl the weights upward while swinging your elbows forward. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your shoulders with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, pull your shoulder blades back together, and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back along with your neck, like when you are normally standing upright.
4. In the starting position, your palms should be facing in toward each other as your upper arms are close to being parallel to each other in front of your body (as opposed to being flared out away from your sides).
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes like you are trying to crush a walnut, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core and tightening your glutes throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping your palms facing in toward each other, press the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward as you progressively straighten them until your elbows are fully straightened with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your forearms should be in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your knees and hips straightened as you avoid using your legs to help lift the weights or leaning your torso back via lower back arching.
    3. Since they will naturally arc back toward your shoulders as you press up, your elbows and dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders at the end of the lifting phase.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively bend while simultaneously moving downward until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbells in front of your shoulders.
    1. Lower the dumbbells as far as you can while keeping your wrists in a straight position stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical, which should result in your elbows being lower than your shoulders.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearms vertical.
    3. If you are using heavy, bulky dumbbells, the dumbbells contacting your shoulders may also determine your end range of the lowering phase.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your shoulders following the lowering phase of the last rep, lower the dumbbells to your sides as you straighten your elbows and swing your elbows back by your sides.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, upper pec major, triceps, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Position the J-hooks of a power rack (or squat stand) near the height of your upper chest.
2. Place a barbell onto the J-hooks and load it with your desired weight.
    1. It is recommended to use an empty barbell if you are new to this exercise.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands at least moderately outside shoulder width as your palms are facing away from you.
    1. Relative to a standard overhead press performed to the front of your body, a wider grip will normally be more comfortable for the part of this exercise where you press behind your head.
    2. If you raise your arms straight out to your sides until they are parallel to the ground, note the midway points between your elbows and the outer aspect of your shoulders. Gripping the bar with your hands near these two points can be an appropriate starting grip width for many people. However, the specific grip width that is best to use for this exercise will vary among different individuals. To personally find your ideal placement, feel free to try out different hand widths over time while noting how your shoulders feel. The hand placement that is best for you should feel comfortable on your shoulders while enabling you to train through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility permits.
    3. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Slightly bend your knees, and step forward to situate your collarbone or upper chest directly under the bar. Simultaneously, position your elbows under the bar. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Relative to when you overhead press to the front with a closer grip, which normally involves a greater degree of elbow tuck (pointing forward), your elbows should naturally be flared out (pointing out to the side) to a greater extent during this variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Fully stand upright while holding the bar.
    1. With your knees straightened, the bar should now be above the tops of the J-hooks.
3. Take a couple of steps backward and set your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
4. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
5. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
6. Press the bar upward just in front of your face without hitting your face with the bar.
    1. In contrast to a standard overhead press, you will not be meaningfully moving your head during the Bradford press. Since you are not tucking your chin back, you will have to arc the bar slightly forward to clear your face instead of pressing straight up. This is an inefficient bar path for pressing maximal weights, but pressing maximal weights is not the goal for this variation.
    2. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position.
7. Once the bar clears the top of your head, drive your elbows back to move the bar back behind your head.
    1. Your elbows should remain stacked under the bar.
    2. Avoid tilting your forearms back to move the bar instead of driving your elbows. If you tilt your forearms back, the bar will be behind your elbows when viewed from the side.
    3. In contrast to a standard overhead press, you will not be pressing the bar until your elbows are straightened. However, you can still be pressing the bar mildly upward as you move it back, resulting in a smooth arcing motion as opposed to the bar path forming a 90-degree angle from completely stopping any vertical motion during this step.
8. Lower the bar behind your head through the range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. If you have the mobility to do so comfortably, you can lower the bar until it lightly contacts your upper back muscles just under the base of your neck. However, what constitutes your end range will be determined by your individual shoulder mobility.
    2. If you start experiencing shoulder pain or joint discomfort at the bottom of the lowering phase, you likely are moving beyond the current limit of your shoulder mobility. Training this exercise over time may progressively improve your shoulder mobility and gradually permit you to descend through a greater range, but there is no need to force yourself into a position that currently does not feel right for your shoulders.
    3. With sufficient shoulder mobility, you should not have to push your head meaningfully forward to avoid scraping your head or neck with the bar. However, you can slightly tuck your chin toward your chest if you wish to do so for additional clearance. If you opt to tuck your chin slightly, you will want to untuck it after completing the next step.
    4. Continue keeping your elbows stacked under the bar when viewed from the side. To do so, your elbows should be pointing out away from your sides.
    5. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
    6. Once you are familiar with the technique, strive to perform steps 6-8 as one fluid motion.
9. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up until you have cleared your head.
10. Drive your elbows forward to move the bar in front of your head.
11. Lower the bar toward your upper chest.
    1. You can similarly aim to perform steps 9-11 as one fluid movement.
12. Alternate between steps 6-11 (which results in the completion of one rep) until you have finished your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
13. With the bar now in front of your body, walk forward until the bar contacts the back of both J-hooks
14. Bend your knees slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts and lateral delts while also training your triceps, supraspinatus, serratus anterior, and traps.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, you will find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of bench press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins/spotter arms.
    2. Certain power rack and bench press station models have fairly wide spaces between safety pin/spotter arm holes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your rack, it is not ideal, but the safety pins/spotter arms can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench as your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor approximately shoulder width apart from each other.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. You can similarly decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) as your hands are well outside shoulder width, with the bar on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A wide grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be near twice your shoulder width, and this involves somewhat of a range of grip widths, so you can feel free to try out mildly different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. What is personally considered a wide grip to you can also be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard bench press, which you can somewhat widen to individualize your wide grip. For instance, for your wide grip, you can place your index finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing your standard bench press.
    3. For stability, your thumbs should be wrapped around the bar from the opposite side of your other fingers, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands in contrast to if you positioned your thumbs on the same side of your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip that is also called a “suicide grip”).
    4. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists meaningfully cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort.
    5. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together, which is a position you will maintain throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades, which should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you are specifically training to bench press as much weight as possible, you can optionally exaggerate your lower back arch, which may allow you to shorten the range of motion a bit and potentially lift heavier weights.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together as you avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them naturally to flare out away from each other while descending until you are able to tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists rather than letting your elbows track in front of your wrists (closer to your hips) or behind them. Maintaining this alignment as you touch the bar to your lower chest will promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare.
    2. Near the bottom of the rep, the appropriate amount of elbow flare should result in your upper arms forming reasonably close to 75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are touching your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    3. Compared to a closer grip bench press, your elbows should flare out further away from your sides during a wide grip bench press.
    4. When aiming to touch the bar to your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    5. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up as you progressively drive your elbows upward and inward while straightening them until you have returned to the starting position with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is situated above your shoulders.
    1. To finish with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest, the bar will now arc slightly backward as you lift it up.
    2. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows mildly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your entire pec major, which is assisted by your front delt and triceps.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a low incline with an angle up to 30 degrees.
    1. If your bench can be adjusted to a moderate incline near 45 degrees but does not have a low incline setting, you can alternatively select the moderate incline barbell bench press from this database.
2. Place the bench centered within a power rack with the head of the bench between the posts, where you will adjust the J-hooks.
    1. You should position the bench in a spot that allows your mouth to be directly under the barbell after setting up the rack and lying down on the bench.
3. Position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the incline bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. Then place the bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of incline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your incline press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins.
    2. Certain power rack models have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your incline press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. It is not ideal if this applies to your rack, but the safety pins can still enhance safety if they are high enough to receive the bar if you dump it forward, in case you are completely pinned without anyone to assist you.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and lie down with your back and head on the incline bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Firmly situate the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    2. Based on what feels most stable, you can position your heels directly under your knees with vertical shins, or you can bend your knees a bit beyond this point to position your heels slightly behind your knees with slightly angled shins.
    3. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
    4. If you are not training with safety pins that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for bench press variations is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground, squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your middle chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending until you are able to tap your middle chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    2. When aiming to touch the bar to your middle chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. To touch your middle chest, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your middle chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your middle chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts and pec major with a slight emphasis on the upper pec while also training the middle and lower pec major, along with the triceps.## Setting up
1. Position a flat exercise bench centered underneath a Smith machine’s bar in a spot that will situate the bar over your chest when lying down on the bench.
    1. Specifically how you should set up can be influenced by whether your Smith machine has sliding rails that are purely vertical or angled. With either type, you want to set up in a manner that results in the bar being able to touch your lower chest at the bottom of the press.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, place the side of the bench where you will place your feet on the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends. This will result in the top of the rails being horizontally closer to your head than the bottom of the rails when lying down on the bench. An angled Smith machine will allow you to use a bar path that most closely resembles that of a standard barbell bench press.
    3. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which side of the bar you place your feet.
2. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to grasp it with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently.
    1. The ideal height allows you to unrack the bar while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together without requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    2. If the bar is situated too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart to lift the bar off the safety bars (on which the safety hooks that are attached to the bar rest). This can place you in a less stable position to start your set since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    3. If the bar is situated excessively low, you will have to grasp the bar initially with a greater degree of elbow bend, and unracking it will involve a greater than ideal pressing range of motion. This will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    4. If neither of the two closest bar heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    5. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best starting bar height for you.
3. After positioning the bar to the appropriate height, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of bench press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section. Use these reps to determine the appropriate height of the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails under the bar) prior to re-racking the bar.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety stops to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could untuck your shoulder blades and flatten out your back to lose your arch. This will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety stops.
    2. Certain Smith machine models have fairly wide spaces between where you could position safety stops. This may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your Smith machine, it is not ideal, and you have the option of using a spotter. However, the safety stops can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck if you are pinned under a bar and try to scoot out.
    3. If you cannot press the bar fully up and lack a spotter or safety stops that are able to receive the bar when flattening your back, you may be able to re-rack the bar on the closest safety bars above your chest if you are able to complete part of the press. If you happen to become pinned in this situation and are initially unable to reach the nearest safety bars above you, you can let the bar rest on your chest until you feel recovered enough to press the bar up high enough to re-rack the bar on the closest safety bars.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your chest is underneath the bar, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor approximately shoulder width apart.
    1. If your Smith machine has vertical slide rails, situate your lower chest under the bar.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, situate your upper chest under the bar to allow the bar to reach your lower chest at the bottom of the rep.
    3. With the fixed bar path from the Smith machine, it may be worthwhile to try scooting slightly forward or backward on the bench if the exercise feels a bit awkward with your initial positioning. 
    4. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    5. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning to bench press, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you are specifically training to bench press as much weight as possible, you can optionally exaggerate your lower back arch, which may allow you to shorten the range of motion a bit and potentially lift heavier weights.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the safety bars by straightening your elbows before rotating the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion. After unracking it, hold the bar stationary for a moment before starting to lower it.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending until you are able to tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    2. To touch your lower chest, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    3. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up until you have fully straightened your elbows. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
    1. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second.
6. Rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars by flicking your wrists before bending your elbows to lower the safety hooks securely onto the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. At a cable station, adjust the cable to be near the height of your lower chest, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at the weight stack.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you will also position the arm furthest back such that it is directed out to the side of the machine.
    2. Dual cable stations that have their two weight stacks facing each other (often with a pull-up in between them) normally lack adjustable arms.
2. While standing with the side of one shoulder facing the cable station, reach to the side and grasp the handle firmly with the hand closer to the cable station as your palm is facing forward.
    1. Throughout the exercise, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting by your side or on top of your hip bone.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the handle since that is the side you will be training first.
3. Step out to the side away from the cable station until you feel tension from the cable (as the weight stack separates) while keeping your arm aligned with the cable.
    1. In this position, your arm should be outstretched away from your side such that your arm and torso resemble half of a “T-shape.”
4. Pivot your feet about 45 degrees away from the cable station as you keep the center of your hips and chest facing the same direction, while allowing the cable’s tension to pull your arm back behind your torso through a range that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. You will now be facing near a 135-degree angle away from the cable station, which will allow you to train through a full range of motion.
    2. How far you can position your arm behind your torso will be influenced by your individual shoulder mobility.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulder in this position, but it should not be painful.
    4. Throughout the exercise, avoid letting the cable’s resistance twist your trunk toward the cable, which would result in your chest facing a different direction than the center of your hips.
5. While mildly bending your knees and your elbow, adopt a staggered stance with one foot in front of the other as your feet are approximately hip width apart from each other.
    1. In this position, you should feel stable and balanced.
6. As you are standing with an upright torso, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow mildly bent and your torso stationary, sweep your arm forward and inward in an arcing motion until your arm is outstretched in front of your chest.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, your hand should be close to chest height, and your arm should be close to facing the same direction as your chest.
    2. Keep your chest up throughout the set as you avoid hunching over with your shoulders slouched forward.
    3. As a cue to avoid letting the cable twist your torso, you can think about imaginary laser beams shooting out from your breastbone and where you would wear a belt buckle above your hips. Throughout the set, the lasers from your breastbone and belt buckle should be facing the same direction as they normally would when standing upright without being twisted toward either side.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arm to sweep out to the side and back toward the cable station in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your elbow behind your torso through the range of motion your shoulder mobility allows.
    1. People commonly have the shoulder mobility to allow their arm to pull back until the elbow is at least slightly behind the torso, but your shoulder mobility will determine what your individual end range is.
    2. Continue keeping your torso stationary and your elbow mildly bent, as the movement comes from shoulder motion rather than elbow motion or trunk twisting motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Alternatively, you can also use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
2. Pick up one dumbbell, and sit down on the incline bench while lifting the dumbbell onto your lower thigh with your palm facing inward.
    1. Initially pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    2. To move the dumbbell into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbell on the front of your thigh while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest it on your thighs while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbell back toward your chest with your elbow bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench.
    1. Even though only one thigh will be initially against the weight, marching both thighs up can help you rock your torso back.
    2. Throughout the exercise, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting on your thigh or chest.
    3. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
    4. Once you are lying down, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    5. You should now be holding the dumbbell right by your chest with your forearm in a vertical position, and your wrist stacked over your elbow as the handle of the dumbbell is parallel to your upper arm.
    6. You should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
5. Flare your elbow moderately out to your side such that your upper arm forms an approximately 45-75-degree angle with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arm is tucked by your side, and 90 degrees means your upper arm forms half of a “T-shape” with your torso).
    1. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable for your shoulders, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbow out within this range.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the dumbbell up by driving your elbow upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten it until your arm is directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbow, keep your forearm in a vertical position throughout the press, as your wrist and dumbbell remain stacked over your elbow.
    2. Throughout your set, keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbell should be situated vertically over your shoulder.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner toward the side of your chest through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Let your elbow moderately flare out to your side as it descends and bends enough to keep your wrist stacked above it with a vertical forearm. Lifters often find that at least a mild degree of elbow tuck makes this exercise feel most comfortable for the shoulder compared to fully flaring the elbow straight out to the side.
    2. How far you can lower the dumbbell with a vertical forearm will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility, but most people have the mobility to descend until their hand is at least level with their chest.
    3. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulder at the bottom.
    4. There is no need to lower the dumbbell extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. It can be awkward to transfer the weight to your other hand with proper positioning while lying down if you are using a weight that is at least fairly heavy to you, so you can initially return to an upright position to transfer the weight before resetting to train your other side.
5. After the dumbbell is by your chest following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arm in line with your side to tuck your elbow and position the dumbbell closer to your thigh.
    1. Once you are in this position, you can optionally reach across your body with your free hand to place it around your hand holding the dumbbell for extra stability.
6. March your thighs up, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbell on your upper thigh before standing up with the dumbbell.
7. Place the dumbbell onto the seat of the bench, and pick it up with your other side before repeating the exercise with that side.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
1. Follow the set-up instructions for the single arm kettlebell clean, and perform the lifting phase of the single arm kettlebell clean described in steps 1-5 of its exercise technique instructions.
    1. After doing so, you should now be holding the kettlebell in the front rack position. This involves situating it in front of your shoulder with your elbow bent slightly in front of your ribcage, palm facing in toward your other side, and the weight resting on your forearm, which is mostly vertically with your wrist slightly closer to the midline of your body than your elbow. The handle should also be diagonally contacting your palm, such that the side of the handle by your thumb is angled mildly higher than the side of the handle by your little finger.
    2. As an alternative to cleaning the kettlebell, you can curl it up while using your free hand to help lift the weight up under its base and guide the kettlebell into the same front rack position described above. Initially grasp the handle with your little finger closer to one of the corner horns than your thumb to help the handle rest diagonally across your palm in the front rack position. Feel free to perform a cheat curl-like motion where you initially generate some momentum by swinging the weight mildly backward with a straight elbow before swinging your elbow forward while curling it and assisting with your free hand. This is a less technically complicated means of moving the kettlebell into the front rack, but it is not as efficient when using a heavy weight for lifters who are proficient with cleaning the kettlebell.
    3. Once the kettlebell is in the front rack position, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the kettlebell.
2. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, keep your chest up in a “proud chest” posture, rather than letting your shoulders and upper back round forward into a hunched position.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain its natural curvature like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes like you are trying to crush a walnut. Simultaneously, brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your glutes tightened and core braced throughout your set.
2. While simultaneously flaring your elbow out away from your side and directing your palm forward, press the kettlebell upward.
    1. Strive to finish with your arm directed straight up toward the ceiling with your elbow fully straightened, and your biceps by your ear.
    2. While your forearm starts slightly angled in the front rack position, it should otherwise remain essentially in a vertical position with your elbow stacked under your wrist throughout the press.
    3. Rather than turning the exercise into a segmented movement where you flare your elbow fully outward and then drive it upward, aim to have the outward elbow flare fluidly occur while pressing up. 
    4. Continue keeping your knees and wrist straight as the kettlebell contacts your forearm throughout the set.
    5. Avoid leaning your torso back via lower back arching, which may be more likely to occur if you relax your abs or glutes.
    6. Allow your shoulder blade to move naturally.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the kettlebell in a controlled manner as you let your elbow bend while descending and arcing forward toward the front rack position. 
    1. Finish with your wrist in front of your shoulder, palm facing in toward your side, and elbow bent slightly in front of your ribcage.
    2. While keeping it vertical throughout most of this step, allow your forearm to angle slightly inward at the end, resulting in your straight wrist being slightly closer to your midline than your elbow.
    3. There is no need to lower the kettlebell very slowly, but you should avoid letting it drop down abruptly in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. After the kettlebell is in the front rack position after your final rep, set it down in the opposite manner to how you initially lifted it into position.
    2. If you initially cleaned the kettlebell, you can let the kettlebell fall forward as you swing your arm down while straightening your elbow and hinging at your hips to absorb the force of the lowering phase.
    3. If you initially curled the kettlebell up with assistance from your free hand, you can similarly use your free hand to help guide the kettlebell down while letting your working arm’s elbow straighten in a controlled manner.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. If you are using an adjustable incline bench, position it in the fully upright setting. Otherwise, proceed to the next step.
    1. You can interchangeably use an adjustable bench that is positioned fully upright or a seated bench that is permanently fixed in an upright position.
    2. Alternatively, you can use a bench that is permanently fixed in a flat position. This will preclude you from resting your back against the bench, but otherwise allow you to use the same technique that the exercise involves when using back support.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and hold them with your palms facing in toward your sides.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
3. Sit down on a seated bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your lower thighs without leaning back onto the back rest.
    1. To move the dumbbells into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
    2. When seated, your elbows should be tucked by your sides with your palms facing in toward each other.
4. March your thighs up one at a time as you lift the dumbbells up near your shoulders with your elbows bent. Simultaneously lean back to situate your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench.
    1. After doing so, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your shoulders with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
5. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling, and maintain this posture throughout your set.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
6. Flare your elbows at least moderately out to your sides to a degree that feels comfortable for your shoulders.
    1. When 0 degrees of elbow flare means that your wrists are directly in front of your shoulders with your palms facing each other, and 90 degrees of elbow flare means that your wrists are fully out to your sides with your palms facing straight forward, using about 60-75 degrees of elbow flare is commonly appropriate. In this position, your elbows will point more out to the side than forward, but they will not be fully flared out.
    2. If your shoulders feel comfortable when pressing with a greater degree of elbow flare, you can certainly flare your elbows beyond that point, but some people find that utilizing a closer to moderate degree of elbow flare feels most comfortable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten your elbows until they are fully straightened with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your forearms should be in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your feet flat on the floor along with your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively bend while simultaneously moving outward and downward until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbells by the sides of your shoulders.
    1. Lower the dumbbells as far as you can while keeping your wrists in a straight position stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical, which should result in your elbows being lower than your shoulders.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearms vertical.
    3. If you are using heavy, bulky dumbbells, the dumbbells contacting your shoulders may also determine your end range of the lowering phase.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your shoulders following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arms in line with your sides to tuck your elbows and position the dumbbells closer to your thighs.
    2. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to situate the dumbbells on your thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable decline bench at a decline angle of 15-30 degrees.
    1. As the decline angle becomes more pronounced, transitioning into the starting position can become more awkward, so a relatively mild decline is typically appropriate.
    2. Utilizing an approximately 15-degree decline angle is recommended if you are new to this exercise.
    3. Some adjustable benches have a mild decline setting along with incline settings, but lack a pad to secure your feet. Using a decline bench with a foot/ankle pad is recommended for stability during this exercise.
2. Pick up one dumbbell on the side you will be training, and sit down on the higher end of the bench while lifting the dumbbell onto your upper thigh.
    1. The directions describe how to assume the starting position for this exercise independently, but you can alternatively have a spotter transfer the dumbbell to you after you lie down on the bench, which can be ideal if you are using a heavy dumbbell.
3. Snugly position the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
4. On the side firmly holding the dumbbell, tuck your elbow by your side. Tightly wrap your free hand around the hand holding the dumbbell.
5. Lean back in a controlled manner to lie down while keeping your elbow tucked by your side and the dumbbell close to your torso.
    1. While using both arms to help control weight, move your torso and arms together as one unit to minimize any shoulder stress when lying down.
    2. Your head and upper back should now be in contact with the bench, along with your buttocks and feet (which remain hooked under the pad). Maintain this body position until you have finished your set.
    3. Strive to have the dumbbell be vertically stacked over your elbow once you are lying down, resulting in your forearm being in a vertical position.
6. Unwrap your outer hand, not directly holding the dumbbell, and move it into a comfortable position away from the weight, such as resting on your torso.
    1. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training.
    2. You should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
7. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
8. Flare your elbow moderately out to your side such that your upper arm forms near a 45-75-degree angle with your torso.
    1. Keep your forearm vertical while doing so.
    2. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable for your shoulder, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbow out within this range.
    3. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbow is completely tucked by your side, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your upper arm would form half of a “T-shape” with your torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbell upward until your elbow is fully straightened with your arm directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. It may help to focus on driving your elbow upward and inward.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, your forearm should be in a vertical position throughout the press. This will result in your wrist and dumbbell remaining stacked over your elbow.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbell should be situated vertically over your shoulder.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner. Move through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits while keeping your wrist stacked over your elbow.
    1. Let your elbow flare out moderately away from your side so that your upper arm forms an approximately 45-75-degree angle with your torso at the bottom. Lifters often find that at least a mild degree of elbow tuck makes this exercise feel most comfortable for the shoulder compared to fully flaring the elbow straight out to the side.
    2. People commonly can lower the weight until their elbows are somewhat lower than their shoulders while keeping their forearms vertical, but your specific end range will be determined by your individual shoulder mobility. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulder at your end range.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the entire movement.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. It can be awkward to transfer the weight to your other hand with proper positioning while lying down if you are using a weight that is at least fairly heavy to you, so you can initially return to an upright position to transfer the weight before resetting to train your other side.
5. After finishing the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arm in line with your side to tuck your elbow and position the dumbbell closer to your torso.
6. Wrap your free hand around the hand holding the dumbbell for additional stability.
7. If you are able to do so, rock forward in a sit-up motion until you are sitting upright and can place the dumbbell on top of your thigh before grasping the dumbbell with your opposite side and repeating the exercise.
    1. This maneuver is only feasible if you have the core strength to perform a sit-up with the weight you selected. Compared to if you decline pressed two dumbbells simultaneously, repositioning yourself will be easier with this single arm variation due to the lighter total load.
    2. If you cannot sit up with the weight, you can have a spotter take the dumbbell from your hand while you are lying down on the bench. This option can be ideal if you are using a heavy weight, but it naturally requires another person. When using this approach, make sure to communicate what you want your spotter to do before starting the set.
    3. Alternatively, you can set the dumbbell directly onto the floor while still lying down before picking up the weight with your other side and resetting. Compared to the other options, this approach potentially may involve a higher risk of injury if you move your shoulder into a disadvantaged position with a heavy weight, but you can assist with your free hand. Relative to setting the weight down under control, dumping the weight forward may be less demanding on your shoulder, but it involves the risk of having a dumbbell ricochet and potentially injuring someone nearby or damaging equipment. For these reasons, it is recommended to use a spotter or to sit up with the dumbbell when possible.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. With a cable station positioned to its lowest height, connect an attachment with a straight bar body, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with extended elbows.
3. Grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder-width grip and an overhand grip (AKA reverse grip) such that your palms are directed back toward you.
4. While keeping your elbows straight, stand upright while gripping the bar.
5. Make sure tension is present from the cable station while your elbows are straight, rather than slack being present in the cable.
    1. Depending on your arm length relative to your height and which specific cable station you are using, you may have to take a small step backward to take out the slack of the cable.
    2. When taking a small step backwards, the cable will now have a mildly diagonal line of pull rather than being completely vertical.
    3. To maintain a straight elbow position with a diagonal line of pull, you will position your upper arms slightly in front of your body rather than keeping them directly at your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by driving your elbows upward and out to your sides through a range of motion that feels comfortable to your shoulders while bending your elbows.
    1. Your mobility will affect how high you are comfortably able to lift the bar, and there is no need to force your shoulders into a range that provokes any discomfort.
    2. While different individuals may be able to lift higher up and others not quite as high, people often can lift their upper arms until they are close to parallel to the ground for a comfortable and effective range of motion.
    3. The bar should move predominantly vertically, but it will also move slightly back toward you if you are positioned a step back from the cable station, resulting in a slight diagonal line of pull from the cable.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to descend in toward your sides as you lower the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with straight elbows.
    1. At the end of the lowering phase, the bar should be situated in front of your thighs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt, upper trap, and supraspinatus while also training your front delt to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. You can initially set the bar up on the floor or from an elevated position, such as from a power rack.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with straight elbows.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with a mildly outside shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are directed toward you.
    1. A mildly outside shoulder-width grip works well for many lifters, but feel free to try out somewhat different grip widths to determine if any particular hand positions feel best to you as an individual.
4. While keeping your elbows straight, stand upright with the bar and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain the slight forward lean throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by driving your elbows upward and out to your sides through a range of motion that feels comfortable to your shoulders while bending your elbows.
    1. Your mobility will affect how high you are comfortably able to lift the bar, and there is no need to force your shoulders into a range that provokes any discomfort.
    2. While different individuals may be able to lift higher up and others not quite as high, people often can lift their upper arms until they are close to parallel to the ground for a comfortable and effective range of motion.
    3. The bar should remain close to your torso throughout the exercise as it moves vertically.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to descend in toward your sides as you lower the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with straight elbows.
    1. At the end of the lowering phase, the bar should be situated in front of your thighs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt, upper trap, and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, you will find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your rep.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could relax your upper back and flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins/spotter arms.
    2. Certain power rack and bench press station models have fairly wide spaces between safety pin/spotter arm holes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your rack, it is not ideal, but the safety pins/spotter arms can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench as your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet flat on the floor, approximately shoulder width apart from each other.
    1. Starting with your feet on the floor, as you would with a standard bench press, can help you set up optimally and feel more stable when unracking the weight compared to if you lifted your feet off the ground before unracking the bar.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) as your hands are moderately outside shoulder width apart from each other, with the bar on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be near 1.5 times your shoulder width, and this involves somewhat of a range of grip widths, so you can feel free to try out mildly different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, your thumbs should be wrapped around the bar from the opposite side of your other fingers, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands in contrast to if you positioned your thumbs on the same side of your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip that is also called a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists meaningfully cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together, which is a position you will maintain throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades, which should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together as you avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. One at a time, lift your feet off the ground and straighten your knees with your legs close together while keeping the bar stationary above your shoulders.
    1. Keep your legs in this position until you finish your set.
    2. While your thighs are on the bench, your calves may or may not be resting on the bench as well, depending on your height and the length of your bench.
4. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending until you are able to tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. Except for keeping your knees straight with your feet off the ground, you will otherwise perform your set like you would with a standard bench press.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    3. When aiming to touch the bar to your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    4. To touch your lower chest, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    5. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up as you progressively drive your elbows upward and inward while straightening them until you have returned to the starting position with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is situated above your shoulders.
    1. To finish with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest, the bar will now arc slightly backward as you lift it up.
    2. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together and your feet off the ground as your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows mildly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on a flat exercise bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your upper thighs near your abdomen with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Position yourself so that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    2. If you can curl the weight of the dumbbells, you can sit down on the bench with the dumbbells by your sides before curling them up onto your lap
    3. With heavier dumbbells, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs with your palms facing each other while standing over the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
2. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent, and you will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest, as you would be in the starting position for a dumbbell bench press.
3. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
4. Press the dumbbells up until your elbows are straight, and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. In this starting position, your wrists should be straight, and you will keep them straight throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your elbows to bend mildly, spread your arms apart from each other as you lower the dumbbells out away from your sides in a slow and controlled arcing manner through the available range of motion of your shoulders.
    1. People commonly have the shoulder mobility to lower the dumbbells at least until their elbows are level with their shoulders and often a little beyond this point, but what is specifically your full range of motion will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulders at the bottom, but the end range should not be painful.
    3. After fully spreading your arms apart, your upper arms should form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    4. While your elbows should not be locked into a fully straightened position, they also should be notably less bent than when you are bench pressing, so the dumbbells should be clearly further out to the sides than your elbows (as opposed to being stacked over your elbows like at the bottom of a bench press).
2. Reverse the movement by pulling your arms in toward each other in a hug-like motion to lift the dumbbells upward until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbells being approximately over your shoulders.
    1. Near the end of the lifting phase, the resistance from the dumbbells is minimal, and there is no purpose of touching the dumbbells to each other.
    2. Toward the end of the lifting phase, you can straighten your elbows, or you can maintain the same degree of mild elbow bend throughout the set.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your last rep, lower the dumbbells toward the sides of your chest and abdomen as you allow your elbows to bend like you would during the lowering phase of a dumbbell bench press.
    2. Once the dumbbells are by your chest, you can rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before placing them down onto the ground one at a time or standing up with them.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Position a pair of kettlebells in front of the base of a flat exercise bench.
2. Complete one of the following methods to lift the kettlebells into the front rack position in front of your shoulders. Your elbows should finish bent slightly in front of your ribcage with your palms facing in toward each other and the weights resting on your forearms.
    1. Option 1: After following its set-up instructions, perform the lifting phase of the dual kettlebell clean described in steps 1-5 of its exercise technique instructions.
    2. Option 2: Curl the kettlebells into the front rack position described above. With this technique, initially grasp the handles with your little fingers directly next to one of the horns (i.e., side handles) on each kettlebell to help the handles rest diagonally across your palms in the front rack position. Feel free to perform a cheat curl-like motion where you initially generate some momentum by swinging the weights mildly backward with straight elbows before swinging your elbows forward while curling them and turning your palms to face each other at the top. If you wish to do so, you can curl the kettlebells while sitting upright on the bench.
    3. Curling the kettlebells is a less technically complicated means of moving them into the front rack, but it is not as efficient when using heavy weights for lifters who are proficient with cleaning kettlebells.
3. With your back facing the head of the position, sit down on a flat exercise bench while keeping the kettlebells close to you in the front rack position.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
4. Lean back to lie down on the bench while marching your thighs up to help transition. Keep your forearms in a mostly vertical position, slightly angled inward, with the kettlebells contacting them as your elbows remain bent and close to your torso.
    1. To maintain a mostly vertical forearm position, you should let your elbows lower toward the ground when leaning back. When sitting upright, this would be the same motion as moving your elbows back slightly behind your ribcage.
    2. Once you are lying down, return your feet to the ground in a stable position with your knees bent.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
6. Flare your elbows moderately out to your sides to your preferred degree that feels comfortable to your shoulders.
    1. Flaring out such that your upper arms form approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso is appropriate for many lifters, but feel free to try out different angles to determine what feels best for you. In this context, 0 degrees of elbow flare means your upper arms are fully tucked by your sides, and 90 degrees means your upper arms would form a “T-shape” with your torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. Press the kettlebells upward until your arms are directed up toward the ceiling with straight elbows.
    1. Drive your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner while keeping your wrists stacked over your elbows when viewed from the side.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your feet flat on the floor along with your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, your wrists and elbows should be vertically aligned above your shoulders.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the kettlebells in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively bend while simultaneously moving outward and downward.
    1. Descend through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility permits, which typically results in a stretching sensation in the chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    2. People commonly can lower the weights until their elbows are somewhat lower than their shoulders, but your specific end range will be determined by your individual shoulder mobility.
    3. Allow your elbows to flare out moderately away from each other through a range comfortable to your shoulders.
    4. Continue keeping your wrists straightened and aligned with your elbows.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, tuck your elbows fully in toward your sides to position the kettlebells close to your torso.
    2. March your thighs up before rocking forward while keeping your forearms mostly vertical to sit up with the kettlebells close to your shoulders in the front rack position.
    3. Set the kettlebells down in the opposite manner to how you initially lifted them into the front rack position.
    4. If you initially cleaned the kettlebells, stand upright and take a step or two forward away from the bench to have clearance behind you. Then let the kettlebells fall forward as you swing your arms down while straightening your elbows and hinging at your hips to absorb the force of the lowering phase.
    5. If you initially curled the kettlebells up, turn your palms back toward your shoulders, and guide them down by letting your elbows straighten and swinging your elbows mildly back to help control the weights.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major while also training your front delt and triceps.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Alternatively, you can also use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline. 
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on the incline bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your lower thighs near your abdomen with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    2. To move the dumbbells into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up one at a time in quick succession to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench.
    1. Once you are inclined, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
5. Flare your elbows moderately out to your sides such that your upper arms form approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    1. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable on your shoulders, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbows out within this range.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the dumbbells up by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten them until your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press, as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner toward the sides of your chest through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Let your elbows moderately flare out to your sides as they descend and bend enough to keep your wrists stacked above them with vertical forearms. Lifters often find that at least a mild degree of elbow tuck makes this exercise feel most comfortable for the shoulders compared to fully flaring the elbows straight out to the sides.
    2. How far you can lower the dumbbells with vertical forearms will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility, but most people have the mobility to descend until their hands are at least level with their chest.
    3. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    4. There is no need to lower the dumbbells extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your chest following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arms in line with your sides to tuck your elbows and position the dumbbells closer to your thighs.
    2. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
1. Position the J-hooks of a power rack (or squat stand) near the height of your upper chest.
    1. As an alternative to starting from a rack, you can begin with the barbell on the floor and power clean it into position. However, this page will describe how to set up from a rack.
2. Place a barbell onto the J-hooks and load it with your desired weight.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands moderately outside shoulder width as your palms are facing away from you.
    1. What is personally considered a wide grip to you can be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard overhead press, which you can somewhat widen to individualize your wide grip. For instance, for your wide grip, you can place your index finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing your standard overhead press. This approach provides many lifters with an appropriately wide grip, but there is a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine specifically where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Slightly bend your knees, and step forward to situate your collarbone or upper chest directly under the bar. Simultaneously, position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Relative to when you overhead press with a closer grip, which normally involves a greater degree of elbow tuck (pointing forward), your elbows should naturally be flared out (pointing out to the side) to a greater extent during this wide grip variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Fully stand upright while holding the bar.
    1. With your knees straightened, the bar should now be above the tops of the J-hooks.
3. Take a couple of steps backward and set your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
4. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
5. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
6. Tuck or tilt your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
7. While keeping your legs straight, press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. To the best of your ability, avoid pushing the bar forward out in front of your shoulders. This can be challenging if you are new to this exercise, since you want to avoid hitting your face with the bar. You can still meaningfully increase your strength and grow muscle if the bar drifts a bit forward, but strive to minimize any forward bar motion as well as you can for an efficient overhead press.
    2. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position throughout your rep.
    3. Avoid using your legs to help with driving the bar up. This is not a push press.
8. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
9. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe after locking out the weight overhead. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to lowering the bar.
10. Reverse the movement by tucking your chin back and lowering the bar toward your collarbone in a controlled manner.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
11. Alternate between steps 7-10 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
12. After completing the lowering phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second before walking forward.
13. After the bar contacts the back of both J-hooks, bend your knees slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, serratus anterior, and traps.## Setting up
1. Place a pair of kettlebells on the ground hip-width apart with their handles facing up.
    1. You can optionally place an exercise mat on the ground behind the kettlebells for comfort.
    2. Note that getting into the starting position of this exercise can be challenging when using weights that are heavy relative to your strength.
2. Sit down on the ground with your hips between the kettlebells and your torso upright.
3. Rotate the kettlebells’ handles so that the horns (i.e., side handles) closer to you are angled diagonally in toward you and backward.
4. Firmly grasp the handles deep in your palms with your bent elbows pointing down toward the floor.
5. March your knees up and rock back to situate your elbows, back, and head on the ground, which will correspondingly lift the kettlebells. Keep your elbows statically bent and wrist straight.
    1. Strive to rock back with torso and arms moving as one unit to help position the kettlebells rather than just curling the weights up once you are lying down.
6. Rotate your palms to face in toward each other with your wrists stacked over your elbows and your forearms vertical.
7. Position your legs straight out in front of you with straight knees if you have yet to do so.
    1. The back of your heels should now be on the ground along with your buttocks, upper back, and head.
8. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. Drive your elbows upward to press the kettlebells upward until your arms are directed up toward the ceiling with straight elbows.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your straight wrists stacked over your elbows throughout the press. This will result in your forearms remaining in a vertical position.
    2. At the end of the lifting phase, your wrists and elbows should be vertically aligned above your shoulders.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your heels, buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the kettlebells in a controlled manner until your elbows and upper arms softly contact the ground.
    1. Based on your preference, you can allow your elbows to flare out moderately away from each other through a range comfortable to your shoulders, or you can keep your elbows tucked fairly close to your sides.
    2. Continue keeping your wrists stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical.
    3. While you do not have to finish the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you certainly should avoid slamming your arms onto the ground. You will not be bouncing your arms off the ground.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebells down in the reverse manner to how you initially set up.
    1. Rotate your palms to face backward and march your knees up before rocking forward to set the kettlebells down onto the ground.
## Description
This exercise can develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
You have the option of performing this exercise within a specific incline press station (in which the bench is fused to the rack) or within an adjustable power rack using a mobile incline bench. Specifically how you will set up will differ depending on which equipment you use, so the setup for each approach is described separately. However, the exercise technique is the same for either option. A power rack normally takes longer to prepare than an incline press station, but power racks typically have safety pins in contrast to incline press stations. It is recommended to have a spotter behind you if you will incline press without safety pins.

### Setting up in a power rack

1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
2. Center the bench within a power rack with the head of the bench in between the posts, where you will adjust the J-hooks.
    1. You should position the bench in a spot that allows your shoulders to be a little in front of the J-hooks’ front edges after setting up the rack and lying down on the bench. After initially estimating which bench placement allows this to occur, you may have to move the bench a bit forward or backward during later steps if you notice that you are not positioned appropriately.
3. Position the J-hooks at a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the incline bench.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking. This can place you in a less stable position to start your set since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
4. After placing the bar on top of the J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of incline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section. Use these reps to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your incline press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins.
    2. Certain power rack models have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your incline press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. It is not ideal if this applies to your rack, but the safety pins can still enhance safety if they are high enough to receive the bar if you dump it forward in the event that you become completely pinned without anyone to assist you.
6. After loading the bar with your intended weight, lie down with your back and head on the incline bench. Firmly place your feet flat on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart with your knees bent and buttocks resting on the seat of the bench.
    1. With the appropriate bench placement, your shoulders should now be a little in front of the front edge of the J-hooks. If your shoulders are under the J-hooks from the bench being situated too far back, you may bump the J-hooks with the bar when pressing the bar up. If the J-hooks are excessively behind your shoulders from the bench being situated too far forward, it can be difficult to unrack the bar.
    2. Based on what feels most stable, you can position your heels directly under your knees with vertical shins, or you can bend your knees a bit beyond this point to position your heels slightly behind your knees with slightly angled shins.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for bench press variations is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.

### Setting up on an incline press station

1. Adjust the seat to a height that allows your shoulders to be a little in front of the J-hooks when lying down on the bench with your buttocks on the seat and feet flat on the ground.
    1. If the seat is too high, you may bump the J-hooks with the bar when pressing the bar up.
    2. If the seat is too low, it can be difficult to unrack the bar.
    3. After estimating what looks like an appropriate seat height, you have to adjust it later if you notice that you are not positioned appropriately.
2. Place the bar in the J-hooks that initially allow you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the incline bench.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After loading the bar with your intended weight, lie down with your buttocks, back, and head on the incline bench. Firmly place your feet flat on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart with your knees bent.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position your heels directly under your knees with vertical shins, or you can bend your knees a bit beyond this point to position your heels slightly behind your knees with slightly angled shins.
    2. If you are not training with a spotter, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for bench press variations is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
5. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform for pressing.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your upper chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other. Descend until you are able to tap your upper chest lightly with the bar or until you have fully moved through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom, but certain individuals with limited shoulder mobility may not be able to touch their upper chest with the bar without discomfort. If this applies to you, lower the bar as far as you can without discomfort.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath (when viewed from the side) your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    3. Avoid fully flaring your elbows out away from your sides or completely tucking them. The appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Once you have fully lowered the bar, pause in that bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., 1-2 seconds) while staying tight.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts and pec major with an emphasis on the upper pec while also training the middle and lower pec major, along with the triceps.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to pin press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Whatever you select must have safety pins or spotter arms to perform this variation.
    2. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    3. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a viable J-hook height, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of reps of a standard bench press to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be at the lowest point that still situates the bar above your chest when the bar rests on them.
    1. The safety pins/spotter arms ideally should receive the bar within 2in (5cm) of your lower chest for this variation.
    2. Certain power rack and bench press station models have fairly wide spaces between safety pin/spotter arm holes that may result in the lowest point above your chest stopping the bar higher above your chest. This will result in a higher pin press with a reduced range of motion that may likely decrease how effectively the exercise can stimulate muscle growth. A pin press will be most effective in strengthening the range of the bench press it includes, so a higher pin is unlikely to be an efficient means of developing strength during the initial range of a bench press’s lifting phase.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning to bench press, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you prefer to perform your standard bench press with a greater arch, you can also adopt a more prominent arch for this variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    2. When aiming to touch the bar to the pins above your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. To touch that point, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out. The appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar toward your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
4. Continue lowering the bar toward your lower chest until it contacts the pins in a controlled manner and comes to a dead stop.
    1. While you do not have to finish the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you certainly should avoid crashing the bar into the pins in a jarring manner.
5. Allow the weight from the bar to transmit fully into the pins, and remain completely stationary with the bar resting on the pins for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back, glutes squeezed, and core braced rather than losing tightness in this position.
6. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up with explosive intent. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
7. Alternate between steps 3-6 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could lower the bar onto the pins before relaxing your upper back to untuck your shoulder blades and flatten out your back to lose your arch. This will lower your chest and provide you with greater room to scoot your way forward until you are no longer underneath the bar.
8. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
9. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
While effectively training your pec major, front delts, and triceps, this exercise can improve bench press strength, especially when starting the press.## Setting up
1. Adjust a pair of securely anchored gymnastic rings to be slightly outside shoulder width apart.
2. Set the rings to a height that allows your elbows to be aligned with the center of the rings or to whatever is your preferred starting height. Set up a step (like a plyometric box, aerobic step on risers, or a couple of bumper plates stacked on each other) slightly behind the rings so that you can grasp the rings with straight elbows once you are standing on it while mildly leaning your torso forward.
    1. The appropriate ring height should allow you to train dips through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits, while also allowing you to get into the proper starting position with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. The right ring height should also enable you to set your feet onto the ground or step while holding onto the rings if you fail a rep.
    3. While setting up the rings so that their centers are near elbow height is an appropriate starting point for many people, the ideal ring height can vary among different individuals. Someone’s shoulder mobility and preferred knee position during dips can affect where it is best to set the rings.
    4. While not required since you can jump up into the starting position, setting up a step is highly recommended if you are not already accustomed to performing ring dips, where you jump up from the ground. Correspondingly, the directions will describe the usage of the step.
3. Step up onto the step while facing the rings.
4. Firmly grasp the rings with your palms facing each other. Hold the rings directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the rings tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
5. Step forward off the step, and hold yourself up with straight elbows.
    1. Keep your knees as straight as you can without your feet restricting your range of motion from contacting the ground before you reach the bottom of your rep. Initially, you can maintain a slight knee bend and then adjust your knee position during your set if warranted.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
7. Rather than keeping your torso fully upright, mildly lean your torso forward and maintain this mild lean throughout your set.
    1. The appropriate degree of lean should result in your chest being slightly in front of your hips (when viewed from the side) while helping your shoulders feel comfortable throughout the set.
    2. Feel free to try out different degrees of torso lean over time to determine what personally feels best to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up. If you notice that either motion starts occurring, strive to pull your shoulder blades further backward and/or downward.
    2. Your elbows should be predominantly pointing backward rather than out to your sides from excessive elbow flare. However, you do not have to keep your elbows completely tucked by your sides, and you can allow them to flare mildly outward if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    3. Rather than letting them swing, keep your legs stationary relative to your hips until you finish your set.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point in which their upper arms are at least parallel to the ground, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by pushing through the rings to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your hands and elbows downward to lift your body upward.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step back onto the step before releasing the rings.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your pec major, front delt, and triceps while being able to improve both your horizontal and vertical pressing strength.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that places the handles near your lower chest or slightly lower once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, but the ideal seat height specifically should allow you to train through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably allows.
    2. Individuals with limited mobility may have to position the seat higher to reach the handles, while those with greater mobility may benefit from positioning the seat lower to train through a full range of motion.
    3. If the seat is excessively high, your range of motion for this exercise will be limited.
    4. If the seat is excessively low, you may not be able to reach the handles with proper positioning. Forcing your elbows back beyond what your mobility permits in an effort to reach the handles may increase the risk of experiencing shoulder discomfort.
    5. Some trial and error may be needed to find the best height for you as an individual. When continuing to set up, you can always adjust the seat height if you determine the initial height is suboptimal for you.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your feet flat on the ground.
    1. If your machine has a back pad, position your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against it.
    2. If your machine lacks a back pad, sit with a fully upright torso.
4. If your machine has a thigh pad, snugly position it on top of your thighs. If your machine has a seat belt, securely fasten it around your waist.
    1. Either option will help you anchor your body.
    2. Certain machines lack a thigh pad or seat belt, which can contribute to the exercise being less stable.
5. If your machine allows you to rotate its handles inward or outward, position them inward unless your thighs restrict the range of motion for the closer setting or if you simply find the wider setting to feel more comfortable.
    1. The closer setting facilitates a greater degree of elbow tuck, which is typically desirable when striving to emphasize the triceps and/or the front delts to a greater degree than the pec major during this exercise.
    2. However, the closer setting may position the handles excessively close for individuals with rather wide body types.
    3. Even if your body does not restrict your range of motion with the closer handle setting, you can also select the wider setting if it just feels more comfortable to you.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back, and move your elbows fully back behind your torso while bending them by at least 90 degrees.
    1. Position your elbows close to being directly behind your wrists and the handles. Keeping your elbows tucked close to your sides rather than flaring them out away from your sides will limit the degree to which this exercise trains the pec major.
    2. If the handles are now just within your reach, the seat height you initially selected is appropriate.
    3. If you cannot reach the handles without shrugging your shoulders up or tipping them forward, raise the seat before resuming your setup.
    4. If the handles are lower than what you can comfortably reach from this position, you can lower the seat to facilitate a greater degree of elbow bend before resuming your setup.
7. Firmly grasp the handles with the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) against them.
    1. While almost all machines allow you to use a neutral grip with your palms facing in toward each other, some machines also have handles that allow you to use an overhand grip with your palms facing backward. If your machine enables you to use both grips, select whichever feels most comfortable to you.
    2. Hold the handles with mostly straight wrists. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Push the handles down by driving your elbows down until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Throughout your set, continue keeping your torso fully upright and feet planted on the ground.
    2. You should finish with your elbows close to your sides and your arms directed down toward the ground.
    3. While driving downward and straightening, your elbows will simultaneously move forward.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing the handles to lift up to the starting position. Bend your elbows while arcing them upward and backward.
    1. Keep your elbows tucked so that they remain behind your wrists rather than flaring them out away from your sides.
    2. Avoid letting your shoulders shrug up or roll forward.
    3. With the appropriate seat height, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders at the end range, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain. If you feel joint discomfort at the end range, increase the seat height before resuming your set.
    4. At the end range, you can allow the weights you selected to tap the rest of the weight stack. A light tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the lateral and medial heads of your triceps while also training your front delts and pec major to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, you will find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you, and the optimal J-hook height for a close grip bench press may be slightly higher compared to when you use a wider grip.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of bench press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins/spotter arms.
    2. Certain power rack and bench press station models have fairly wide spaces between safety pin/spotter arm holes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your rack, it is not ideal, but the safety pins/spotter arms can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench as your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor approximately shoulder width apart from each other.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. You can similarly decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) as your hands are near shoulder width apart from each other, with the bar on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. Positioning your hands near shoulder-width apart results in somewhat of a range, but you can initially start with a shoulder-width grip and try out mildly different hand widths over time to determine where specifically you feel best.
    2. What is personally considered a close grip to you can also be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard bench press, which you can somewhat narrow to individualize your close grip. For instance, for your close grip, you can place your little finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your index finger when performing your standard bench press.  
    3. For stability, your thumbs should be wrapped around the bar from the opposite side of your other fingers, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands in contrast to if you positioned your thumbs on the same side of your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip that is also called a “suicide grip”).
    4. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists meaningfully cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort.
    5. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together, which is a position you will maintain throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades, which should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you are specifically training to bench press as much weight as possible, you can optionally exaggerate your lower back arch, which may allow you to shorten the range of motion a bit and potentially lift heavier weights.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together as you avoid losing your upper back tightness.
    2. In this position, your wrist should be stacked above your elbows, which should be stacked above your shoulders as your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and keeping them reasonably tucked in toward your sides while descending until you are able to tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set, which will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow tuck as you touch the bar to your lower chest.
    2. Near the bottom of the rep, the appropriate amount of elbow tuck should result in your upper arms forming close to 30-45-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are touching your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    3. Compared to a wider grip bench press, your elbows should not flare out away from your sides as much during a close grip bench press.
    4. When aiming to touch the bar to your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    5. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up as you progressively drive your elbows upward and inward while straightening them until you have returned to the starting position with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is situated above your shoulders.
    1. To finish with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest, the bar will now arc slightly backward as you lift it up.
    2. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows mildly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
While still training the chest’s pec major and front delts effectively, this exercise can preferentially train the triceps to a greater degree than wider grip bench press variations.## Setting up
Certain chest press machines have a foot pedal that you can press to move the machine’s arms while setting up. Other machines lack a foot pedal but commonly allow you to modify the starting position with adjustable arms or a back pad that can be moved forward and backward. Some of the specific setup details differ depending on which type of machine you are using, so the setup for each approach is described separately. However, the exercise technique can be very similar for either option, so one technique section is provided with a couple of details that depend on which type of machine you utilize.

### Setting up a machine without a foot pedal

1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with the lower half of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is too low (placing the handles near shoulder level or above), some lifters may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort from having to flare their elbows outward excessively.
    4. If the seat is excessively high (placing the handles below the lowest part of the chest), the exercise may be suboptimal for developing the chest.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust either aspect, position its arms or back pad to a setting that enables you to press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Some machines allow you to increase the range of motion by placing the arms further back toward the back pad, while others allow you to move the back pad forward, closer toward the arms. However, certain machine models may not permit you to adjust either part.
    2. If you have normal shoulder mobility, you can initially position the arms or the seat to a setting that results in the handles being near the front of your chest (when viewed from the side) once you are seated. This commonly involves setting the arms as far backward or the seat as far forward as the machine enables.
    3. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles with proper positioning during step 5, you can adjust the machine to increase the distance between the back pad and the handles until you can do so.
    4. The ideal setting should allow your elbows to start as far behind your torso as is comfortable for your shoulders while keeping your elbows level with the handles.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. With your palms facing down and elbows bent, firmly grasp the handles that are directed out to the sides. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles and position your elbows level with them, you may have to position the machine’s arms further forward or the back pad further back before resuming your setup.
    3. Aligning your elbows directly behind (rather than being above or below) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    4. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.

### Setting up a machine with a foot pedal

1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with the lower half of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is too low (placing the handles near shoulder level or above), some lifters may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort from having to flare their elbows outward excessively.
    4. If the seat is excessively high (placing the handles below the lowest part of the chest), the exercise may be suboptimal for developing the chest.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
4. Place one foot against the machine’s foot pedal, and push the pedal forward with your leg.
    1. This brings the handles forward to a position you can comfortably reach.
    2. Keep the pedal pushed away during the next two steps, while keeping your other foot on the ground.
5. With your palms facing down and elbows bent, firmly grasp the horizontally directed handles. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbows directly behind (rather than being above or below) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    3. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
7. Bring the foot pedal back to its starting position by bending your knee, which will shift the machine’s resistance to your upper body. Simultaneously, allow your elbows to move backward through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Keep your elbows level with the handles, not lifting above them or dropping below them when viewed from the side.
    2. How far your elbows can comfortably move backward while keeping them aligned with the handles will be dependent on your mobility.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range, but you should not allow the machine to pull you into a position that causes shoulder discomfort.
8. Lift your foot off the foot pedal and place it flat on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles forward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows forward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain level with your wrists and the handles when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handles to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and wrists aligned as they remain level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    2. Move through the full range of motion that your mobility comfortably permits. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
    3. If your machine lacks a foot pedal, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack at the end range to confirm that you have moved through the full range in a controlled manner. If your machine has a foot pedal, you can finish the lowering phase once you can no longer comfortably move your elbows backward while keeping your elbows at the height of the handles.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. If your machine has a foot pedal, push the pedal forward with your leg until you are no longer supporting any of the machine’s resistance with your upper body. Then let go of the handles and bend your knee to bring the foot pedal back to its initial position.
    2. If your machine lacks a foot pedal, simply rest the machine’s arms on the stoppers after the lowering phase of your last rep before letting go of the handles.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Place a pair of sturdy, flat, mildly elevated objects that you will use for your deficit onto the ground mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other.
    1. So long as they can support your bodyweight with your hands on top of them and will not slide, you have multiple options that you can use based on your equipment availability.
    2. The height of the objects should allow you to descend through your full available shoulder range of motion without the ground restricting your push-up range of motion. The minimum height required to achieve this full range will vary from person to person, depending on someone's shoulder mobility, but a height of about 4in (10cm) should permit a full range for most people.
    3. Yoga blocks, deadlift blocks, aerobic steps, and thick bumper plates can all be similarly used with your palms flat on top of them.
    4. Alternatively, you can stack standard metal weight plates on top of each other to provide a similar elevation. If you do so, it is recommended to use fairly heavy weights for stability.
2. Position yourself with your palms mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other on the elevated objects with straight elbows as your knees and toes are on the ground with bent knees.
    1. In this position, your elbows should be a little outside and underneath your shoulders rather than being in front of your shoulders (i.e., horizontally closer to your head) or behind your shoulders (i.e., horizontally closer to your hips).
3. Spread your fingers somewhat apart from each other, and shift your bodyweight into your hands.
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
4. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. How far you can descend depends on your individual shoulder mobility, but you should be able to reach the point where your chest is at least slightly lower than your hands. Otherwise, the deficit serves no function compared to a standard push-up.
    2. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    3. Let your elbows flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you. This commonly results in the upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with the torso (when viewed from above) at the bottom of the push-up. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are fully tucked to your sides, remaining shoulder-width the whole rep, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows point straight out to your sides, letting your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    4. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” at the bottom instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    5. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    6. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    7. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your front delts, triceps, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. In an approximately hip-width stance, face the stable object with a flat, elevated surface that you will use for this push-up variation.
    1. So long as the object can support your bodyweight and will not slide, you can use a variety of objects based on the degree of difficulty you wish to have and what is available to you.
    2. As the height of the surface decreases, the difficulty of the exercise will increase from being able to adopt a closer to horizontal torso position, where gravity can provide greater resistance during a push-up.
    3. A flat exercise bench (which typically has a surface near 18in (46cm) above the ground) can be appropriate to use in a gym as an initial starting point for many people who struggle to perform more than a couple of reps of standard push-ups through a full range of motion.
    4. In addition to a plyometric box, an aerobic step elevated on top of risers can also be a quality object to use, since you can adjust the height (typically in 2in (5cm) increments) to match the specific level of difficulty you wish to have.
    5. For a lower intensity variation at home, furniture like a bed can alternatively be used to elevate your hands.
2. After leaning forward at your hips, place your palms on top of the elevated object with the base of your palms on the corner as your elbows are straight, and your hands are mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other.
    1. In this position, your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
    2. Based on your preference, you can spread your fingers somewhat apart from each other or keep them close together.
3. While bearing weight through your hands and allowing your heels to lift off the ground, walk your feet back until your hips and knees are straightened as your arms form approximately 90-degree angles with your torso.
    1. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    3. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner until it lightly touches the elevated surface.
    1. Let your elbows flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you. This commonly results in the upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with the torso (when viewed from above) at the bottom of the push-up. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are fully tucked to your sides, remaining shoulder-width the whole rep, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows point straight out to your sides, letting your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact the ground instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    5. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the surface to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your pec major, front delts, and triceps like a standard push-up but with a lower intensity.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
    1. In this position, your knees should be approximately hip-width apart, with your elbows stacked under your shoulders and your arms pointing straight down toward the ground. 
    2. For comfort, you can optionally place a fitness mat, cushion, or pillow under your knees.
2. Situate your hands mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other, with your fingers spread somewhat apart from each other.
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
3. Walk your hands forward while allowing your hips to straighten until you can draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso, like when you are normally standing upright.
    1. After doing so, your hands should still be underneath and a little outside your shoulders rather than being in front of your shoulders (i.e., horizontally closer to your head) or behind your shoulders (i.e., horizontally closer to your hips).
    2. Rather than being directly above your knees like they were in step 1, your hips should now be horizontally in front of your knees.
    3. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
4. Lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees further, and keep them off the ground throughout your set.
    1. How much you bend your knees is up to preference, and whether you cross your ankles or leave them uncrossed is also up to your preference.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag downward, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag toward the ground, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid, lower your chest in a controlled manner by bending your elbows until you can lightly touch the ground with your chest.
    1. Let your elbows flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you. This commonly results in the upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with the torso (when viewed from above) at the bottom of the push-up. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are fully tucked to your sides, remaining shoulder-width the whole rep, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows point straight out to your sides, letting your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact the ground instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    5. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the starting position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your thighs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still allow you to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your front delts, triceps, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Place one dumbbell on the ground.
    1. Both of its weighted ends should be on the ground.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground, with the dumbbell by the side of your body you are about to train. When your upper arm (closer to the dumbbell) is by your side, the dumbbell’s handle should be parallel with your upper arm and out to the side of your elbow.
    1. Specifically, the dumbbell should be slightly greater than the length of your forearm away from your elbow to help you pick it up.
3. On the side closer to the dumbbell, bend your elbow by about 90 degrees with your upper arm right by your side.
4. Roll onto your side toward the dumbbell.
5. Firmly grasp the dumbbell with your palm on the side of your lower arm facing up toward the ceiling. Tightly wrap your other hand over your lower hand, so that your palms are facing each other.
6. While pulling the dumbbell up with your top arm, roll over onto your back. Move your torso and arms together as one unit, such that the dumbbell remains directly in front of your elbow (on the side holding the dumbbell directly) throughout the roll.
    1. When appropriately transitioning to lying on your back, you should not feel that you are stressing your shoulder on the side directly holding the dumbbell. You are primarily using the rest of your body to reposition yourself and lift the dumbbell rather than using your shoulder muscles (on the side directly holding the dumbbell) to rotate the forearm.
    2. Once you are on your back, you should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. On the side holding the dumbbell, your upper arm should be resting on the ground, and your forearm should be vertical.
7. Move your arm that is not directly holding the dumbbell into a comfortable position, such as resting by your side.
    1. Some lifters may prefer to use the free hand to hold onto a sturdy object (like the beam of a power rack) for additional stability, but you can position that hand wherever you like.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
8. If you have not already, position your legs straight out in front of you with straight knees.
    1. The back of your heels should now be on the ground along with your buttocks, upper back, and head.
9. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
10. Flare your elbow moderately out to your side such that your upper arm forms an approximately 45-75-degree angle with your torso.
    1. Keep your forearm vertical while doing so.
    2. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable for your shoulder, you can decide how far you flare your elbow within this range.
    3. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbow is completely tucked by your side, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your upper arm would form half of a “T-shape” with your torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbell upward until your elbow is fully straightened with your arm directed straight up toward the ceiling. It may help to focus on driving your elbow upward and inward.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, your forearm should be in a vertical position throughout the press. This will result in your wrist and dumbbell remaining stacked over your elbow.
    2. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbell should be situated vertically over your shoulder.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your heels, buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner. Move your elbow outward and downward while bending it.
    1. Continue keeping your wrist stacked over your elbow as your forearm remains vertical.
    2. Allow your elbow to flare out moderately away from your side so that your upper arm forms an approximately 45-75-degree angle with your torso at the bottom.
    3. You should finish with the back of your upper arm and elbow contacting the ground.
    4. While you do not have to finish the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you certainly should avoid slamming your arm onto the ground. You will not be bouncing your arm off the ground.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. It can be awkward to transfer the weight to your other hand with proper positioning while lying down if you are using a weight that is at least fairly heavy to you.
    2. Consequently, you can set the dumbbell down in an opposite manner to how you initially set up before switching to your other side.
5. After finishing the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arm in line with your side to tuck your elbow and position the dumbbell closer to your torso.
6. Wrap your free hand around your hand holding the dumbbell.
7. Roll onto your side (in which you are holding the dumbbell) while using your outer hand’s arm to help control the weight onto the ground. Move your torso and arms together as one unit, such that the dumbbell remains directly in front of your elbow (on the side holding the dumbbell directly) throughout the roll.
8. Let go of the dumbbell before setting up on the opposite side of the dumbbell to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Position the safety pins of a power rack at the height of your collarbone or slightly above this point.
    1. For this variation, the goal is to position the pins in a manner that results in the bar (once it is on the pins) being as close to your starting position for the standard overhead press as possible. Ideally, the pins should allow the bar to start below your chin.
    2. While the pins can be slightly above your collarbone, it is not feasible to situate the pins slightly below your collarbone.
    3. Certain power racks have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes. This may result in the lowest pin height that is at least at collarbone height, placing the bar closer toward the midpoint of the press rather than the start of the press. If this is the case for you, it is not feasible for you to train the overhead low pin press with your current rack. However, you may be able to perform the overhead midpoint pin press.
2. Symmetrically place a barbell in the middle of the safety pins and load it with your desired weight.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width as your palms are facing away from you.
    1. Situating your index fingers directly outside the outer aspects of your shoulders is generally an effective approach, but what is personally your ideal grip may be a bit wider or closer. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Step forward to situate your collarbone directly under the bar with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart. The middle of your feet should be under the bar, and your knees should be straight.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
    2. You can perform this step simultaneously with step 6.
6. Position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Your forearms should now be close to a vertical position when viewed from the front.
    3. From this position, your elbows should be reasonably close to being flared 45 degrees out from your sides. 0 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the front. 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the side.
7. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
3. Tuck or tilt your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
4. While keeping your legs straight, press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. To the best of your ability, avoid pushing the bar forward out in front of your shoulders. This can be challenging if you are new to this exercise, since you want to avoid hitting your face with the bar. You can still meaningfully increase your strength and grow muscle if the bar drifts a bit forward, but strive to minimize any forward bar motion as well as you can for an efficient overhead press.
    2. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position throughout your rep.
5. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
6. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
7. Reverse the movement by tucking your chin back and lowering the bar toward your collarbone in a controlled manner.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down. Do not let the bar crash into the pins.
8. Once the bar contacts the pins, allow the weight to transmit fully into the pins. Remain completely stationary with the bar resting on the pins for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe when the bar is resting on the pins. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating your next rep.
9. Alternate between steps 4-8 until you have completed your set.
    1. In contrast to a standard overhead press, you will finish your set with the bar resting on the pins rather than racking it onto J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also meaningfully training your lateral delts, triceps, traps, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of standard bench press reps to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
4. Load the bar with your intended plate weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins/spotter arms.
    2. Certain power rack and bench press station models have fairly wide spaces between safety pin/spotter arm holes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your rack, it is not ideal, but the safety pins/spotter arms can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Based on the type of equipment you have, securely attach a sufficiently anchored pair of long looped elastic bands or a single band to the bar through your preferred set-up method.
    1. Ultimately, you want to make sure that whatever you use as your anchor point will not move during your set. Your anchor point should have a smooth surface that will not tear the band.
    2. The appropriate set-up should result in the bands remaining taut at the bottom of each rep. If the bands become slack at any point during your reps, you did not stretch them sufficiently in the starting position.
    3. Once positioned, the bands should be flat rather than having any twists.
    4. If you have access to band pegs and a pair of bands with equal tensions, anchoring the bands to the pegs and looping them around each side of the bar will be the ideal method of setting up. The bands should be set up where they are pulling vertically downward rather than being angled to one side. Other methods are less stable and not recommended if you can use band pegs.
    5. Without band pegs, you can anchor the bands to heavy dumbbells on each end of the bar. To reduce the risk that they move, the dumbbells should ideally be as heavy as possible. It can help to place a pair of weight plates on the sides of each dumbbell to reduce the likelihood that they will roll. If you are using bands with a high enough tension that they move the heaviest dumbbells you have, this method is simply not practical to use.
    6. Without band pegs or a pair of equal bands, you can loop each end of one band through the bar and anchor it under the bench pad. With this approach, it is generally recommended to anchor the band at a point on the bench that will result in the band being slightly further away horizontally from the head of the bench compared to your shoulders. After anchoring the band, you should pull the bottom of the band down under the bench to check whether it is applying the same resistance on each side of the bar.
    7. Prior to using them, it is recommended that you inspect the bands to confirm they do not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
6. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning to bench press, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you are specifically training to bench press as much weight as possible, you can optionally exaggerate your lower back arch, which may allow you to shorten the range of motion a bit and potentially lift heavier weights.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending until you are able to tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    2. When aiming to touch the bar to your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. To touch your lower chest, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
    4. As you lift the bar higher up, the band tension will progressively increase.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Use the machine’s loading pin to select your desired level of assistance, and ensure that the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is positioned down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you select heavier loads from the assisted dip machine, since the weight determines the extent to which the machine will support your bodyweight.
    2. Certain machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but essentially the same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates.
3. If your machine allows you to rotate its parallel handles inward or outward, position them inward unless your body’s width restricts the range of motion for the closer setting.
    1. The closer setting facilitates a greater degree of elbow tuck, which is typically desirable when striving to emphasize the triceps and/or the front delts to a greater degree than the chest during this exercise.
    2. However, the closer setting may position the handles excessively close for individuals with rather wide body types.
    3. Even if your body does not restrict your range of motion with the closer handle setting, you have the option of selecting the wider setting if it just feels more comfortable to you. However, that may contribute to the exercise being more chest-dominant.
    4. Note that a variety of machines do not allow you to adjust the handle position.
4. Firmly grasp the parallel handles with your palms facing each other. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
5. One leg at a time, kneel onto the machine’s knee pad (if your machine has a knee pad) or step onto the machine’s foot bar (if your machine has a foot bar) while continuing to grasp the handles firmly. Maintain straight elbows when doing so.
    1. If using a machine with a knee pad, you will keep your knees bent on the pad throughout your set, but if you are using a machine with a foot bar, you will keep your knees straight while standing on the bar throughout your set.
6. Assume a “proud chest” posture as you pull your shoulder blades back with an upright torso.
    1. Avoid letting your shoulders shrug up throughout the exercise.
    2. In contrast to a “chest dip,” you want to avoid leaning forward at your hips during this “triceps dip.”
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While keeping them tucked close to your sides, bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, continue keeping your torso upright and avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up.
    2. Avoid flaring your elbows out away from your sides. When viewed from behind, your elbows should remain stacked over your wrists and the handles rather than having your elbows move outside your wrists.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their elbows are bent by at least 90 degrees, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by pushing through the handles to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows straight down.
    2. You should finish with your elbows close to your sides and your arms directed down toward the ground.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down onto the foot plate.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the lateral and medial heads of your triceps while also training the long head to a lesser degree, along with your front delts and pec major.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
    1. In this position, your elbows should be stacked underneath your shoulders with your arms pointing straight down toward the ground. 
2. Situate your hands clearly outside shoulder width apart from each other (i.e., about twice shoulder width distance apart), with your fingers spread somewhat apart from each other.
    1. So long as your hands are wider apart than during your standard push-up with a moderate grip width, you can use somewhat of a range of hand placements for your wide grip, but you should place your hands in a position that feels right to your shoulders.
    2. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner until it lightly touches the ground.
    1. Rather than trying to keep your elbows tucked in by your sides, allow your elbows to flare out away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you.  This commonly results in the upper arms forming at least 60-degree angles with the torso (when viewed from above) at the bottom of a wide grip push-up. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are fully tucked to your sides, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows point straight out to your sides, letting your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact the ground instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    5. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This classic exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your front delts, triceps, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Approach the pair of bars/handles that you will use for your dip.
    1. Ideally select a pair of bars that enables you to use a near shoulder-width grip for this variation.
    2. A variety of pieces of equipment can be used to perform this exercise.
    3. You can use bars that are completely parallel to each other or angled “V-grip” bars that converge to be closer together near the base and wider apart at the free ends. In contrast to stationary parallel bars, angled bars allow you to adjust your grip width based on specifically where you grasp them. While gymnastics rings can also be used to train dips, this variation specifically uses bars.
    4. Gyms commonly have a power tower (which has dip bars protruding from its vertical frame and often has a pull-up bar and/or forearm pads to use for leg raises) or a dip stand/station (which is specifically designed for dips).
    5. You can alternatively use a dip attachment that is connected to a power rack.
    6. Some assisted dip/pull-up machines also permit you to flip the knee pad or foot bar up, therefore enabling you to train dips without machine assistance. Certain machine models allow you to adjust the distance between the bars and position them near shoulder width apart.
    7. Other options include portable parallettes (which can be positioned closer or wider apart to adjust your grip width) or parallel bars located at a playground.
2. Firmly grasp the bars with your palms facing each other and your elbows straight. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
    2. If you are using angled bars, you can grasp the bars at the point where they are near shoulder width apart for you.
    3. Depending on the equipment you use, you initially may have to step up onto the equipment or a step you position nearby before you can grasp the bars with straight elbows.
3. While keeping your elbows straightened and torso upright, lift your feet off the ground.
    1. Depending on the equipment you use, you may have to bend your knees in order for your feet to clear the ground, or you may be able to keep your knees straight.
    2. With straight knees, you can keep the inner aspect of your feet against each other. If you bend your knees, you can cross your ankles.
    3. Compared to positioning your legs further backward or forward, keeping your thighs close to vertical can generally promote an upright torso.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. As you keep them tucked close to your sides, bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. While you do not have to maintain a completely upright torso, keep your torso mostly upright for this triceps dip rather than more prominently leaning your torso forward like you would during a chest dip. A mild degree of torso lean can naturally occur without striving to lean forward and often contributes to the exercise feeling more comfortable than if you focus on being fully upright.
    2. Throughout your set, keep your legs stationary relative to your hips (rather than swinging them) and avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up.
    3. Avoid flaring your elbows out away from your sides. When viewed from behind, your elbows should remain stacked over your wrists and the bars rather than having your elbows move noticeably outside your wrists.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their elbows are bent by at least 90 degrees, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Once you have finished lowering to your end range, pause in that bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Continue to stay braced with your shoulder blades pulled back and down.
    2. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Reverse the movement by pushing through the bars to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows straight down.
    2. You should finish with your elbows close to your sides and your arms directed down toward the ground.
6. Alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down from the bars.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the lateral and medial heads of your triceps while also training its long head to a lesser degree, along with your front delts and pec major.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each cable to be near the height of your lower chest.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you can position the arms so that their ends are moderately outside shoulder width apart.
    2. Dual cable stations that have their two weight stacks facing each other (often with a pull-up in between them) normally lack adjustable arms.
2. Connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
3. With your elbows bent and raised out to your sides to the height of the pulleys, firmly grasp each handle as your palms are facing down.
    1. Keep your wrists mostly straight while grasping the handles.
    2. If your pulleys are fairly far apart from each other, you may have to grasp one handle initially before stepping to the side until the other handle is within your reach.
4. As you are centered between the pulleys, step forward while allowing the cables to pull your arms back behind your torso through a range that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. You want to be sufficiently positioned in front of the cables that the cables exert resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise. This will involve your hands being in front of the pulleys with tension still present from the cables when you are fully spread apart in the starting position of each rep.
    2. If your shoulder mobility comfortably allows your arms to move further behind your torso than they initially are, you can take another step forward to be able to train through a full range of motion.
    3. Your elbows should be level with your chest as your forearms are aligned with the cables.
5. While mildly bending your knees, adopt a staggered stance with one foot in front of the other as your feet are approximately hip-width apart from each other.
    1. In this position, you should feel stable and balanced.
6. As you are standing with an upright torso, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles forward until your arms are outstretched in front of you with straight elbows.
    1. Drive your elbows forward and inward in an arcing manner while straightening them.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
    3. Keep your chest up as you avoid hunching over with your shoulders slouched forward.
    4. Keep your mostly straight wrists stacked in front of your elbows throughout the rep.
    5. Your forearms should remain aligned with the cables, which will naturally occur when you keep your elbows level with the cables.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move out to your sides and backward in an arcing manner while bending them.
    1. Allow the cables to pull your elbows back through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    2. It is common to have the mobility to finish with the elbows at least slightly behind the torso, but your shoulder mobility will determine what your individual end range is.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders and/or chest when your arms are fully spread apart, but the appropriate end range should not prompt you to feel shoulder discomfort.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major while also  training your front delt and triceps.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to use a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position an incline bench within the rack, or within a specific incline press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. For this variation, you will use a bench with a moderate incline near 45 degrees. Any bench angle (which includes most incline press stations) that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
    2. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    3. Incline bench press stations typically lack spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to incline press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it more difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    4. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and an incline press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it normally takes longer to prepare compared to an incline press station. It is recommended to have a spotter behind you if you will incline press without safety pins.
2. If you are using a power rack, place the moderate incline bench centered within the power rack. The head of the bench should be between the posts, where you will adjust the J-hooks.
    1. You should position the bench in a spot that allows your nose to be directly under the barbell after setting up the rack and lying down on the bench.
3. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the incline bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With an incline press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will require a greater-than-needed pressing range of motion, which will increase effort and fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
4. Place the bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks. If you are training in a power rack with safety pins, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of incline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. If you are training in an incline press station without spotter arms, proceed to step 6.
5. If you are training in a power rack, position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your incline press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins.
    2. Certain power rack models have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your incline press range of motion, being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. It is not ideal if this applies to your rack, but the safety pins can still enhance safety if they are high enough to receive the bar if you dump it forward in the event that you become completely pinned without anyone to assist you.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and lie down with your back and head on the incline bench. Position yourself so that your nose is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Firmly situate the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    2. Based on what feels most stable, you can position your heels directly under your knees with vertical shins, or you can bend your knees a bit beyond this point to position your heels slightly behind your knees with slightly angled shins.
    3. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
    4. If you are not training with safety pins that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands near shoulder width apart. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. You can initially start with a shoulder-width grip, but positioning your hands near shoulder-width apart results in a bit of a range of possible hand placements. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. What is personally considered a close grip to you can also be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard incline press, which you can narrow somewhat to individualize your close grip. For instance, for your close grip, you can place your little finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your index finger when performing your standard incline press.
    3. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    4. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    5. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground, squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your upper chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and keeping them reasonably tucked in toward your sides. Descend until you are able to tap your upper chest lightly with the bar or until you have fully moved through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom, but certain individuals with limited shoulder mobility may not be able to touch their upper chest with the bar without discomfort. If this applies to you, lower the bar as far as you can without discomfort.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists (when viewed from the side). This will keep your forearms close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set and naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow tuck.
    3. You should not completely tuck your elbows, but your elbows should not flare out away from your sides as much during a close grip incline press compared to a wider grip incline press. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming close to 30-45-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts and pec major with an emphasis on the upper pec while also training the middle and lower pec major, along with the triceps.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
    1. Your elbows should be stacked under your shoulders with your arms pointing straight down toward the ground.
    2. For comfort, you optionally can set up on an exercise mat.
2. Situate your hands mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other, with your fingers spread somewhat apart from each other.
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon which hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner until it lightly touches the ground.
    1. Let your elbows flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you. This commonly results in the upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with the torso (when viewed from above) at the bottom of the push-up. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are fully tucked to your sides, remaining shoulder-width the whole rep, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows point straight out to your sides, letting your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact the ground instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    5. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This classic exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your front delts, triceps, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on a flat exercise bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your upper thighs near your abdomen with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Position yourself so that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    2. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    3. To move the dumbbells into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
2. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
4. In the starting position, your palms should face each other, and your elbows should be tucked fairly close to your sides.
    1. Keeping your elbows tucked to the extent that your upper arms form near 30-degree angles with your torso (when viewed from above) is generally appropriate.
    2. If you completely tuck your elbows, you will likely reduce how effectively this exercise can train your chest compared to using a mild degree of elbow flare.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward as you progressively straighten them until your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your palms facing each other as you avoid notably flaring your elbows out away from your sides, like you otherwise do with a standard dumbbell bench press.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press, as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    3. Keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench throughout the exercise. 
    4. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
3. While keeping your elbows tucked fairly close to your sides, reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. While they descend, let your elbows bend enough to keep your wrists stacked above them with vertical forearms.
    2. Strive to finish with your upper arms forming close to 30-degree angles with your torso.
    3. How far you can lower the dumbbells with vertical forearms will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility, but most people have the mobility to descend until their hands are at least level with their chest.
    4. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders at the bottom.
    5. There is no need to lower the dumbbells extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After the lowering phase of the last rep, fully tuck your elbows by your sides to position the dumbbells closer to your thighs.
    2. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
This exercise predominantly trains your pec major, front delts, and triceps with a technique that some people find feels more comfortable for their shoulders compared to other presses.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, you will find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of bench press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. In addition to setting up safety pins/spotter arms, it is strongly recommended that you use a spotter, since the reverse grip bench press does not typically enable you to have as secure a grip on the bar compared to a standard overhand grip bench press.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins/spotter arms.
    2. Certain power rack and bench press station models have fairly wide spaces between safety pin/spotter arm holes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your rack, it is not ideal, but the safety pins/spotter arms can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench as your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor approximately shoulder width apart from each other.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. You can similarly decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing backward (resulting in an underhand grip) as your hands are mildly wider apart than your grip for a standard bench press. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands).
    1. As a general rule of thumb for your reverse grip, you can place your index finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing your standard bench press. Based on how that feels, you can try out mildly different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best. 
    2. Securely holding the bar with an underhand grip requires that you somewhat cock your wrists back rather than keeping them fully straight. However, you should avoid excessively cocking them back into a fully extended position, which may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort.
    3. For stability, your thumbs should be wrapped around the bar from the opposite side of your other fingers, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands in contrast to if you positioned your thumbs on the same side of your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip that is also called a “suicide grip”).
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together, which is a position you will maintain throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades, which should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you are specifically training to bench press as much weight as possible, you can optionally exaggerate your lower back arch, which may allow you to shorten the range of motion a bit and potentially lift heavier weights.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground, squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together as you avoid losing your upper back tightness.
    2. In this position, your wrist should be stacked above your elbows, which should be stacked above your shoulders as your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest or upper abdomen in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and keeping them tucked in toward your sides. Descend until you are able to tap a little below your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. A reverse grip bench press will normally allow you to contact the bar lower on your body than a standard bench press. You could decide specifically where you touch the bar based on what feels more comfortable to you. 
    2. The bar should track mildly forward away from your shoulders, rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. Compared to a standard bench press, which involves a greater degree of elbow flare, your elbows should be tucked closer toward your sides during a reverse grip bench press.
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your body with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your body.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up as you progressively drive your elbows upward while straightening them until you have returned to the starting position with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is situated above your shoulders.
    1. To finish with the bar over your shoulders instead of your upper abdomen, the bar will now arc mildly backward as you lift it up.
    2. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows mildly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbell at your side with a straight elbow as your palm faces in toward your thigh.
    1. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    2. Throughout the exercise, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting by your side.
    3. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
2. Lift the dumbbell up near the front of your shoulder with your elbow bent by curling the weight and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbell into position if it is too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbell mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbow to curl the weight upward while swinging your elbow forward. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbell right by your shoulder with your forearm in a vertical position, and your wrist stacked over your elbow as the handle of the dumbbell is parallel to your upper arm.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, pull your shoulder blades back together, and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back along with your neck, like when you are normally standing upright.
4. Flare your elbow at least moderately out to your side to a degree that feels comfortable for your shoulder.
    1. When 0 degrees of elbow flare means your wrist is directly in front of your shoulder, and 90 degrees of elbow flare means your wrist is fully out to your side with your palm facing straight forward, about 60-75 degrees of elbow flare is commonly appropriate. In this position, your elbow will point more out to the side than forward, but it will not be fully flared out.
    2. If your shoulder feels comfortable when pressing with a greater degree of elbow flare, you can certainly flare your elbow beyond that point, but some people find that utilizing a closer to moderate degree of elbow flare feels most comfortable.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes like you are trying to crush a walnut, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core and tightening your glutes throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbell upward by driving your elbow upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten your elbow until it is fully straightened with your arm directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, your forearm should be in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrist and dumbbell remain stacked over your elbow.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your knees and hips straightened as you avoid using your legs to help lift the weights or leaning your torso back via lower back arching.
    3. Keep your shoulders level with each other throughout the exercise.
    4. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbell should be situated vertically over your shoulder.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner as your elbow progressively bends while simultaneously moving outward and downward until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbell by the side of your shoulder.
    1. Lower the dumbbell as far as you can while keeping your wrist in a straight position stacked over your elbow as your forearm remains vertical, which should result in your elbow being lower than your shoulder.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearm vertical.
    3. If you are using a heavy, bulky dumbbell, the dumbbell contacting your shoulder may also determine your end range of the lowering phase.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
5. After your dumbbell is by your shoulder following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arm in line with your side to point the backside of your elbow forward, which will result in your palm facing inward.
6. Lower the dumbbell to your side as you straighten your elbow and swing your elbow back by your side.
7. Set the dumbbell down and pick it up with your other side before repeating the exercise with that side.
## Description
This exercise can train your shoulder’s front and lateral deltoids along with the triceps on the back of your upper arm and back’s trapezius.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to use a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position an incline bench within the rack, or within a specific incline press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. For this variation, you will use a bench with a moderate incline near 45 degrees. Any bench angle (which includes most incline press stations) that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
    2. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    3. Incline bench press stations typically lack spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to incline press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it more difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    4. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and an incline press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it normally takes longer to prepare compared to an incline press station. It is recommended to have a spotter behind you if you will be incline pressing without safety pins.
2. If you are using a power rack, place the moderate incline bench centered within the power rack. The head of the bench should be between the posts, where you will adjust the J-hooks.
    1. You should position the bench in a spot that allows your nose to be directly under the barbell after setting up the rack and lying down on the bench.
3. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the incline bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With an incline press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
4. Place the bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks. If you are training in a power rack with safety pins, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of incline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. If you are training in an incline press station without spotter arms, proceed to step 6.
5. If you are training in a power rack, position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your incline press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins.
    2. Certain power rack models have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your incline press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. It is not ideal if this applies to your rack, but the safety pins can still enhance safety if they are high enough to receive the bar if you dump it forward in the event that you become completely pinned without anyone to assist you.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and lie down with your back and head on the incline bench. Position yourself so that your nose is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Firmly situate the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    2. Based on what feels most stable, you can position your heels directly under your knees with vertical shins, or you can bend your knees a bit beyond this point to position your heels slightly behind your knees with slightly angled shins.
    3. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
    4. If you are not training with safety pins that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands well outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A wide grip width for bench press variations is commonly considered to be around two times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. What is personally considered a wide grip to you can also be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard incline press, which you can somewhat widen to individualize your wide grip. For instance, for your wide grip, you can place your index finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing your standard incline press.
    3. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    4. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    5. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground, squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your upper chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them naturally to flare out away from each other. Descend until you are able to tap your upper chest lightly with the bar or until you have fully moved through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom, but certain individuals with limited shoulder mobility may not be able to touch their upper chest with the bar without discomfort. If this applies to you, lower the bar as far as you can without discomfort.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath (when viewed from the side) your mostly straight wrists. Avoid letting your elbows track meaningfully in front of your wrists (closer to your hips) or behind them.
    3. Compared to a closer grip incline press, your elbows should flare out further away from your sides during a wide grip incline press, but you should not fully flare them out.
    4. The appropriate degree of elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists. Near the bottom of the rep, the appropriate amount of elbow flare should result in your upper arms forming reasonably close to 75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are touching your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    5. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts and pec major with an emphasis on the upper pec while also training the middle and lower pec major, along with the triceps.## Setting up
1. If your decline press station allows you to adjust them, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench. If your decline press station has fixed J-hook heights, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
2. After situating a bar on top of the J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of decline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to confirm they are the appropriate height prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
3. Load the bar with your intended weight.
    1. It is recommended to avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would permit you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
4. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
    2. Most decline press stations do not allow you to adjust this pad.
5. Lie down with your back and head on the bench.
    1. Ideally, the bar should now be reasonably close to being above your eyes. However, decline press stations do not allow you to adjust where you position your body on the bench as you could do on a flat bench or an incline press station (which typically has an adjustable seat).
    2. When locking your feet and ankles under the pad, you cannot meaningfully adjust where your body will be in relation to the bar. Consequently, a decline bench press station may not be well suited to accommodate every body type. Rather short people may find that the bar is positioned behind the back of their head, which can make it quite difficult to unrack the bar. In this instance, a spotter may be required to provide meaningful assistance when unracking and racking the bar. Alternatively, if you happen to be tall enough that the bar is positioned above your shoulders when lying down, it may simply not be practical to use a decline press station since the J-hooks will interfere with your bar path.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for a bench press variation is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, but avoid exaggerating this arch any further.
## Exercise technique
1. While squeezing your glutes, brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform for pressing.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be securely locked in place, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other. Descend until you are able to tap your lower chest or lower breastbone lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set.
    2. When aiming to touch the bar near your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. To touch that point, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out. The appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar near your lower chest. At the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Once the bar contacts your lower chest or lower breastbone, pause in that bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds) while staying tight.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Determine the pair of bars/handles that you will use for your dip.
    1. IIdeally select a pair of bars that enables you to use a near shoulder-width grip for this variation.
    2. A variety of pieces of equipment can be used to perform this exercise.
    3. You can use bars that are completely parallel to each other or angled “V-grip” bars that converge to be closer together near the base and wider apart at the free ends. In contrast to stationary parallel bars, angled bars allow you to adjust your grip width based on specifically where you grasp them. While gymnastics rings can also be used to train dips, this variation specifically uses bars.
    4. Gyms commonly have a power tower (which has dip bars protruding from its vertical frame and often has a pull-up bar and/or forearm pads to use for leg raises) or a dip stand/station (which is specifically designed for dips).
    5. You can alternatively use a dip attachment that is connected to a power rack.
    6. Some assisted dip/pull-up machines also permit you to flip the knee pad or foot bar up, therefore enabling you to train dips without machine assistance. Certain machine models allow you to adjust the distance between the bars and position them near shoulder width apart.
    7. Other options include portable parallettes (which can be positioned closer or wider apart to adjust your grip width) or parallel bars located at a playground.
2. While standing in front of the bars, add your desired load (in the form of weight plates or a kettlebell) to a dip/pull-up belt you have around your waist.
    1. The back of your belt should be securely resting on top of your buttocks. You can loop the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) through the center hole of a weight plate or under the handle of a kettlebell. If you have a loading pin for your belt, you would alternatively loop the chain or strap through the loading pin after adding weight plates to it.
    2. It will be easiest if you carry the weight over to the dip bars and add the weight to your belt while you are under the bar. Adding the weight to your belt farther away from the bar before walking over to the bar can be awkward.
    3. As an alternative to using a pull-up/dip belt, you can wear a weighted vest.
    4. While less stable and more awkward than using a belt or weighted vest, you also have the option of holding a dumbbell between your thighs or feet. This approach is suboptimal and will limit how much weight you can use.
3. Firmly grasp the bars with your palms facing each other and your elbows straight. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
    2. If you are using angled bars, you can grasp the bars at the point where they are near shoulder width apart for you.
    3. Depending on the equipment you use, you initially may have to step up onto the equipment or a step you position nearby before you can grasp the bars with straight elbows.
4. While keeping your elbows straightened and torso upright, lift your feet off the ground, and position your legs against the weight (if you are using a dip belt).
    1. Depending on the equipment you use, you may have to bend your knees in order for your feet to clear the ground, or you may be able to keep your knees straight.
    2. Based on your preference, you can spread your legs just enough to position the weight between them, or you can position your legs together with the weight just in front of them. Which option is most comfortable will be influenced by how you set up your belt and the shape of your weight(s). Contacting the weight with your legs can help you prevent the weight from excessively swinging during your set.
    3. If you opt to bend your knees, you can cross your ankles for additional stability
    4. Compared to positioning your legs further backward or forward, keeping your thighs close to vertical can generally promote an upright torso.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. As you keep them tucked close to your sides, bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. While you do not have to maintain a completely upright torso, keep your torso mostly upright for this triceps dip rather than more prominently leaning your torso forward like you would during a chest dip. A mild degree of torso lean can naturally occur without striving to lean forward and often contributes to the exercise feeling more comfortable than if you focus on being fully upright.
    2. Throughout your set, avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up.
    3. Avoid flaring your elbows out away from your sides. When viewed from behind, your elbows should remain stacked over your wrists and the bars rather than having your elbows move noticeably outside your wrists.
    4. Rather than swinging them, continue keeping your legs stationary relative to your hips until you finish your set. If you are using a dip belt, keeping your legs in contact with the weight can help you stabilize it. If the weight is between your legs, squeezing your thighs together can stop the weight from swinging.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their elbows are bent by at least 90 degrees, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by pushing through the bars to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows straight down.
    2. You should finish with your elbows close to your sides and your arms directed down toward the ground.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down from the bars.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the lateral and medial heads of your triceps while also training its long head to a lesser degree, along with your front delts and pec major.## Setting up
1. If your decline press station allows you to adjust them, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench. If your decline press station has fixed J-hook heights, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
2. After situating a bar on top of the J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of decline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to confirm they are the appropriate height prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
3. Load the bar with your intended weight.
    1. It is recommended to avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would permit you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
4. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
    2. Most decline press stations do not allow you to adjust this pad.
5. Lie down with your back and head on the bench.
    1. Ideally, the bar should now be reasonably close to being above your eyes. However, decline press stations do not allow you to adjust where you position your body on the bench as you could do on a flat bench or an incline press station (which typically has an adjustable seat).
    2. When locking your feet and ankles under the pad, you cannot meaningfully adjust where your body will be in relation to the bar. Consequently, a decline bench press station may not be well suited to accommodate every body type. Rather short people may find that the bar is positioned behind the back of their head, which can make it quite difficult to unrack the bar. In this instance, a spotter may be required to provide meaningful assistance when unracking and racking the bar. Alternatively, if you happen to be tall enough that the bar is positioned above your shoulders when lying down, it may simply not be practical to use a decline press station since the J-hooks will interfere with your bar path.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for a bench press variation is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, but avoid exaggerating this arch any further.
