## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the lateral and medial heads of your triceps while also training its long head to a lesser degree, along with your front delts and pec major.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a multi-grip Swiss bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of bench press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. If your Swiss bar has angled handles instead of purely parallel handles, you can position the bar so that the end of the handles closer toward your feet are wider apart than the end of the handles closer toward your head. This will allow you to grasp the handles with your hands angled in toward each other and somewhat forward.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins/spotter arms.
    2. Certain power rack and bench press station models have fairly wide spaces between safety pin/spotter arm holes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your rack, it is not ideal, but the safety pins/spotter arms can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the center of the Swiss bar, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor approximately shoulder width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. With your palms facing each other, firmly grasp the handles that are near shoulder width apart or mildly wider. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. Depending on the specific Swiss bar you are using and your body proportions, sometimes you may feel that your ideal grip width is between two handle pairs that are available to you. Feel free to try out different grips over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the handles, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands.
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you are specifically training to bench press as much weight as possible, you can optionally exaggerate your lower back arch, which may allow you to shorten the range of motion a bit and potentially lift heavier weights.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground, squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and keeping them reasonably tucked in toward your sides. Lower it until you are able to tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set. This will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow tuck as you touch the bar to your lower chest.
    2. Near the bottom of the rep, the appropriate amount of elbow tuck should result in your upper arms forming close to 30-45-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are touching your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    3. When aiming to touch the bar to your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
While still training the chest’s pec major and front delts effectively, this exercise can likely preferentially train the triceps to a greater degree than a standard barbell bench press.## Setting up
1. Position the J-hooks of a rack near the height of your upper chest.
    1. As an alternative to starting from a rack, you can begin with the barbell on the floor and power clean it into position. However, this page will describe how to set up from a rack.
2. Place a barbell onto the J-hooks and load it with your desired weight.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width as your palms are facing away from you.
    1. Situating your index fingers directly outside the outer aspects of your shoulders is generally an effective approach, but what is personally your ideal grip may be a bit wider or closer. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Slightly bend your knees, and step forward to situate your collarbone or upper chest directly under the bar. Simultaneously, position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Your forearms should now be close to a vertical position when viewed from the front.
    3. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    4. From this position, your elbows should be reasonably close to being flared 45 degrees out from your sides. 0 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the front. 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the side.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bra
    3. cing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Fully stand upright while holding the bar.
    1. With your knees straightened, the bar should now be above the tops of the J-hooks.
3. Take a couple of steps backward and set your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
4. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
5. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
6. Tuck or tilt your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
7. While keeping your legs straight, press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. To the best of your ability, avoid pushing the bar forward out in front of your shoulders. This can be challenging if you are new to this exercise, since you want to avoid hitting your face with the bar. You can still meaningfully increase your strength and grow muscle if the bar drifts a bit forward, but strive to minimize any forward bar motion as well as you can for an efficient overhead press.
    2. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position throughout your rep.
    3. Avoid using your legs to help with driving the bar up. This is not a push press.
8. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
9. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe after locking out the weight overhead. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to lowering the bar.
10. Reverse the movement by tucking your chin back and lowering the bar toward your collarbone in a controlled manner.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
11. Alternate between steps 7-10 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
12. After completing the lowering phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second before walking forward.
13. After the bar contacts the back of both J-hooks, bend your knees slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your upper pec major, lateral delts, triceps, traps, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. At a cable station, adjust the cable to be near the height of your lower chest, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at the weight stack.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you will also position the arm furthest back, such that it is directed out to the side of the machine.
    2. Dual cable stations that have their two weight stacks facing each other (often with a pull-up in between them) normally lack adjustable arms.
2. Stand with the handle just in front of the arm you are going to train as you are facing away from the cable station.
3. With the hand closer to the cable station, firmly grasp the handle and direct your palm to face downward and inward at an angle.
    1. Your elbow should be bent with your forearm pointing straight ahead.
4. Walk forward a little beyond the point where the weight stack separates and starts providing you resistance from the cable.
    1. The goal is to be far enough from the cable station that you will experience resistance throughout the full range of motion of this exercise.
    2. Keep your chest and imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead rather than letting the cable twist your body when walking out.
    3. You can optionally use your free arm to help you stabilize the arm holding the cable when walking out. To do so, you could place your free hand firmly around your other hand until you finish setting your feet (which is when you will be more stable than walking out).
5. With the leg opposite to the side in which you are holding the cable, step forward to adopt a staggered stance while mildly bending your knees.
    1. In this position, you should feel stable and balanced.
    2. You can keep your free arm wherever you prefer, such as resting by your side or held out to the side for balance assistance.
    3. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the handle since that is the side you will be training first.
6. As you are standing with an upright torso, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
7. If you did not do so already, allow the cable’s tension to pull your elbow back behind your torso through a range that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. This will be the starting position of your arm, and you can assume it at any point from step 4 onward.
    2. How far you can position your elbow behind your torso will be influenced by your individual shoulder mobility.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulder in this position, but it should not be painful.
    4. Throughout the exercise, avoid letting the cable’s resistance twist your trunk toward the cable, which would result in your chest facing a different direction than the center of your hips.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handle forward until your arm is outstretched in front of you with a straight elbow.
    1. Drive your elbow forward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, strive to keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
    3. Continue keeping your chest and imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead in the same direction as you avoid twisting your body.
    4. Keep your mostly straight wrist stacked in front of your elbow throughout the rep.
    5. Keep your chest up as you avoid hunching over with your shoulders slouched forward.
3. Reverse the movement by bending your elbow and moving it outward and backward in a controlled arcing manner.
    1. Allow the cable to pull your elbow back through your shoulder’s full range of motion to return to the starting position.
    2. People commonly have the shoulder mobility to allow the arm to pull back until the elbow is at least slightly behind the torso, but your shoulder mobility will determine what your individual end range is.
    3. Do not let your chest twist toward the cable station.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While statically training your obliques, this exercise will develop your pec major, which is assisted by your front delt and triceps.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
2. Center the incline bench underneath a Smith machine’s bar in a spot that will allow you to touch your upper chest with the bar at the bottom of the press.
    1. After initially estimating which bench placement allows this to occur, you may have to move the bench a bit forward or backward during later steps if you notice the bar does not reach your upper chest.
    2. Specifically how you should set up can be influenced by whether your Smith machine has sliding rails that are purely vertical or angled.
    3. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, place the side of the bench where you will place your feet on the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends. This will result in the top of the rails being horizontally closer to your head than the bottom of the rails when lying down on the bench. An angled Smith machine will prompt the bar to track mildly back toward your head when pressing the bar up.
    4. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which side of the bar you place your feet.
3. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to grasp it with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently.
    1. The ideal height allows you to unrack the bar while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together without requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    2. If the bar is situated too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart to lift the bar off the safety bars (on which the safety hooks that are attached to the bar rest). This can place you in a less stable position to start your set since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    3. If the bar is situated excessively low, you will have to grasp the bar initially with a greater degree of elbow bend, and unracking it will involve a greater than ideal pressing range of motion. This will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    4. If neither of the two closest bar heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    5. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best starting bar height for you.
4. After positioning the bar to the appropriate height, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of incline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section. Use these reps to determine the appropriate height of the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails under the bar) prior to re-racking the bar.
5. Position the safety stops slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your incline press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could untuck your shoulder blades and flatten out your back to lose your arch. This will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety stops.
    2. Certain Smith machine models have fairly wide spaces between where you could position safety stops. This may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your Smith machine, it is not ideal, and you have the option of using a spotter.
    3. If you cannot press the bar fully up and lack a spotter or safety stops that are able to receive the bar when flattening your back, you may be able to re-rack the bar on the closest safety bars above your chest if you are able to complete part of the press. If you happen to become pinned in this situation and are initially unable to reach the nearest safety bars above you, you can let the bar rest on your chest until you feel recovered enough to press the bar up high enough to re-rack the bar on the closest safety bars.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and lie down with your buttocks, back, and head on the incline bench. Firmly place your feet flat on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart with your knees bent.
    1. With the appropriate bench placement, your upper chest should be under the bar if your Smith machine has vertical slide rails. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, your upper chest should be mildly horizontally in front of the bar.
    2. Based on what feels most stable, you can position your heels directly under your knees with vertical shins, or you can bend your knees a bit beyond this point to position your heels slightly behind your knees with slightly angled shins.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for bench press variations is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform for pressing.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the safety bars by straightening your elbows before rotating the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion. After unracking it, hold the bar stationary for a moment before starting to lower it.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your upper chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other. Descend until you are able to tap your upper chest lightly with the bar or until you have fully moved through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom, but certain individuals with limited shoulder mobility may not be able to touch their upper chest or collarbone with the bar without discomfort. If this applies to you, lower the bar as far as you can without discomfort.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists (when viewed from the side). This will keep your forearms close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set and naturally promote the appropriate balance between elbow flare and elbow tuck.
    3. Avoid fully flaring your elbows out away from your sides or completely tucking them. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up until you have fully straightened your elbows. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together as your feet and buttocks remain firmly planted respectively on the ground and bench.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
    1. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second.
6. Rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars by flicking your wrists before bending your elbows to lower the safety hooks securely onto the safety bars.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
1. Stand with your back facing the stable object with a flat elevated surface that you will use for this push-up variation.
    1. So long as the object can support your bodyweight and will not slide, you can use a variety of objects based on the degree of decline you wish to use and what is available to you.
    2. As the height of the surface increases, the exercise will function closer to a vertical press (like a shoulder press) than a horizontal press (like a flat bench press), which will increase its difficulty.
    3. Initially selecting an object near mid-shin in height or lower is recommended if you are new to decline push-ups.
    4. For a steeper decline that will be more challenging, you can select an object near knee height.
    5. An aerobic step that can be elevated on top of risers can be a quality object to use, since you can adjust the height (typically in 2in (5cm) increments) to match the specific decline and level of difficulty you wish to have.
    6. Thick bumper plates, which can be stacked on top of each other, can be useful to provide a low to moderately low height that can be adjusted.
    7. A flat exercise bench (which typically has a surface near 18in (46cm) above the ground) or plyometric box can additionally be used.
    8. At home, you can also use sturdy furniture like a bed or couch.
2. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
    1. In this “on all fours” position, your elbows should be stacked underneath your shoulders with your arms pointing straight down toward the ground.
    2. You should be situated close enough to the elevated surface that you will be able to place your feet on top of it after you straighten your knees.
3. Situate your hands mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other, with your fingers spread somewhat apart from each other.
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
4. One at a time, lift your legs off the ground and place the balls of your feet and toes on top of the elevated surface.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest toward the ground in a controlled manner until.
    1. A low to moderate decline can often allow you to touch your chest lightly to the ground, but your head may obstruct how far you can descend if you use a steeper decline. Consequently, you can aim to move through the full range of motion you can while keeping your neck in a neutral position.
    2. Let your elbows flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you. This commonly results in the upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with the torso (when viewed from above) at the bottom of the push-up. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are fully tucked to your sides, remaining shoulder-width the whole rep, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows point straight out to your sides, letting your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    3. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” at the bottom instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    4. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    5. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    6. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet to the ground.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
In addition to situating a mobile bench within a power rack or squat stand, you have the option of setting up this exercise within a seated overhead press station in which a seated bench is fused to a rack. The exercise technique is the same for either option, but the set-up details differ depending on which equipment you use, so each set-up is described separately.

### **Setting up a power rack or squat stand**

1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a 90-degree angle, or approach a mobile exercise bench that is permanently fixed with an upright backrest.
    1. If you have access to one, a seated bench with a backrest that does not reach your head is ideal, because a taller backrest can interfere with your bar path.
2. Place the bench centered within a power rack or squat stand with the backrest in between the uprights, where you will adjust the J-hooks.
    1. The proper placement of the bench should allow you to unrack the bar comfortably without bumping the J-hooks with the bar during your set. This may require some trial and error.
    2. If the backrest is situated in front of where the bar will start, you will have to reach back behind your head to unrack the bar, which requires greater than typical shoulder mobility.
    3. If the backrest is situated too far behind where the bar will start, you may bump the J-hooks with the bar when pressing the bar up.
3. Position the J-hooks at a height that will allow you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are seated on the bench.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks without having to shrug your shoulders up.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the top of the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If neither of the two J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little lower than optimal, since it may not be feasible to unrack the bar with proper positioning if the J-hooks are excessively high.
    4. You may have to try out different J-hook heights to determine which setting is best for you.
4. Place the bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks.
    1. Initially leave the bar unloaded to check that the bench and J-hooks are positioned correctly.
5. Sit down with your buttocks and back in contact with the bench. Firmly place your feet flat on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart with your knees bent.
    1. Your hips should be fully back on the seat.
    2. If your bench’s backrest reaches your head, rest your head against it as well.
6. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
7. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the bar with your hands at least moderately outside shoulder width as your palms are facing forward.
    1. Relative to a standard overhead press performed to the front of your body, a wider grip will normally be more comfortable when pressing behind your head.
    2. If you raise your arms straight out to your sides until they are parallel to the ground, note the midway points between your elbows and the outer aspect of your shoulders. Gripping the bar with your hands near these two points can be an appropriate starting grip width for many people. However, the specific grip width that is best to use for this exercise will vary among different individuals. To personally find your ideal placement, feel free to try out different hand widths over time while noting how your shoulders feel. The hand placement that is best for you should feel comfortable on your shoulders while enabling you to train through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility permits.
    3. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
8. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of reps with the form described under the exercise technique section prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. If you cannot comfortably unrack the bar and avoid bumping the J-hooks during these reps, adjust your bench placement and/or J-hook height and repeat this step until you can do so.
    2. If you are using an adjustable bench with a tall backrest and notice that the top of the backrest restricts your range of motion, you can position the bench to its flat setting and simply perform the exercise without any back support. This is a modification to the standard technique, so you will ignore the directions to keep your back against the backrest if you use this approach and otherwise use the same pressing technique.
9. If you have access to adjustable safety pins, position the pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase.
    1. This will entail placing the pins slightly below shoulder height (when seated) if you can comfortably lower the bar to your upper back.
10. Add plates of your desired load to the bar, and repeat steps 5-7 before commencing your working set.

### **Setting up an overhead press station**

1. Place the bar on top of the J-hooks behind the seat that will allow you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are seated on the bench.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks without having to shrug your shoulders up.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the top of the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. You may have to try out different J-hook heights to determine which setting is best for you, so you can initially leave the bar unloaded to check if you can comfortably unrack the bar.
2. Sit down with your buttocks and back in contact with the bench. Firmly place your feet flat on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart with your knees bent.
    1. Your hips should be fully back against the seat.
3. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
4. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the bar with your hands at least moderately outside shoulder width as your palms are facing forward.
    1. Relative to a standard overhead press performed to the front of your body, a wider grip will normally be more comfortable when pressing behind your head.
    2. If you raise your arms straight out to your sides until they are parallel to the ground, note the midway points between your elbows and the outer aspect of your shoulders. Gripping the bar with your hands near these two points can be an appropriate starting grip width for many people. However, the specific grip width that is best to use for this exercise will vary among different individuals. To personally find your ideal placement, feel free to try out different hand widths over time while noting how your shoulders feel. The hand placement that is best for you should feel comfortable on your shoulders while enabling you to train through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility permits.
    3. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of overhead press reps with the form described under the exercise technique section prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. If you cannot comfortably unrack the bar, you can situate the bar on a different pair of J-hooks and try again.
    2. If you lack the shoulder mobility to unrack the bar from the J-hooks behind the bench, you can situate the bar on top of the J-hooks in front of you that are slightly above shoulder height (when seated). To unrack the bar from these J-hooks, you could initially scoot forward on the seat until your shoulders are close to being under the bar, lift the bar up off the J-hooks, and scoot back to the back rest. Then you would have to press the bar overhead before lowering it behind your head. Note that this approach to unrack the bar from the front J-hooks differs from the standard unrack method described in the exercise technique section.
6. Add plates of your desired load to the bar, and repeat steps 2-4 before commencing your working set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position and throughout the exercise, your feet should be firmly planted on the ground.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward until it is just in front of the front edge of the J-hooks. Hold the bar in this position for a moment.
    1. Maintain your “proud chest” posture to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. In a slow and controlled manner, lower the bar behind your head through the range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. If you have the mobility to do so comfortably, you can lower the bar until it lightly contacts your upper back muscles just under the base of your neck. However, what constitutes your end range will be determined by your individual shoulder mobility.
    2. If you start experiencing shoulder pain or joint discomfort at the bottom of the lowering phase, you likely are moving beyond the current limit of your shoulder mobility. Training this exercise over time may progressively improve your shoulder mobility and gradually permit you to descend through a greater range, but there is no need to force yourself into a position that currently does not feel right for your shoulders.
    3. With sufficient shoulder mobility, you should not have to push your head meaningfully forward to avoid scraping your head or neck with the bar. However, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest if you wish to do so for additional clearance.
    4. Strive to keep your elbows stacked under the bar when viewed from the side. To do so, your elbows should be pointing out away from your sides.
    5. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up overhead until your elbows are fully straightened.
    1. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows stacked under the bar.
    3. Maintain an upright torso as you avoid arching your lower back excessively.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts and lateral delts while also training your triceps, supraspinatus, serratus anterior, and traps.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the seat so that your upper arms can be parallel with the floor or a little lower while grasping the handles.
    1. After initially adjusting the seat height, spread your arms outward so that your hands are level with the handles.
    2. You may have to re-adjust the seat if your upper arms are not parallel with the floor or a little lower.
    3. Some people will find their shoulders feel more comfortable when the seat is set to a height that allows their hands to be a bit lower than their shoulders, compared to when their hands are completely level with their shoulders.
2. While sitting with your back against the backrest, spread your arms backward into a “T-position” through your available range of motion.
3. Adjust the pins above the handles to a position that allows you to grasp the horizontal handles with an overhand grip (i.e., palms facing down) when your arms are in this “T-position.”
    1. You may have to readjust the handles and recheck to see that the handles are not positioned too far behind, such that you cannot grab the handles, or positioned too far in front, such that your arms are not as far back into the “T-position” as your mobility allows.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your shoulder blades in this tucked back position throughout the set.
2. Spread your arms apart into the “T-position” with a slight bend in your elbows before grasping the handles with an overhand grip (i.e., palms facing down).
3. Pull your arms together as the handles move toward each other until they are just about to contact each other or until they lightly contact each other.
4. Reverse the motion by spreading your arms in a controlled manner until you have returned to your starting position (i.e., the “T-position”).
5. Alternate between pulling your arms together and spreading them apart until you have completed the set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each cable to be near the height of your lower chest, connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you will also position the arms furthest back, such that they are directed out to the sides of the machine.
    2. Dual cable stations that have their two weight stacks facing each other (often with a pull-up in between them) normally lack adjustable arms.
2. While standing to the side of one cable, grasp its handle, and then walk over to grasp the handle of the other cable.
3. With your palms facing forward and your elbows slightly bent, center yourself between the cables, and step forward while allowing the cables to spread your arms apart and pull your arms back behind your torso through a range that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. You want to be sufficiently positioned in front of the cables that the cables exert resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise. This will involve your hands being in front of the cable pulleys with tension still present from the cables when you are fully spread apart in the starting position of each rep.
    2. If your shoulder mobility comfortably allows your arms to move further behind your torso than they initially are, you can take another step forward to be able to train through a full range of motion.
    3. In line with the resistance from the cables, your upper arms should look like they form a “T-shape” with your torso as your elbows are close to shoulder height.
4. While mildly bending your knees, adopt a staggered stance with one foot in front of the other as your feet are approximately hip width apart from each other.
    1. In this position, you should feel stable and balanced.
5. As you are standing with an upright torso, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows mildly bent, sweep your arms forward and in toward each other in an arcing motion before mildly crossing your forearms over each other in front of your chest.
    1. To cross your forearms, you will naturally need to lift one arm slightly higher than the other arm, and you can decide which side you place in each position.
    2. At the end of the lifting phase, your forearms should form an “X-shape” with each other as your right hand is in front of your left shoulder, and your left hand is in front of your right shoulder.
    3. Keep your chest up throughout the set as you avoid hunching over with your shoulders slouched forward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your hands to spread apart from each other in a controlled manner as they move backward and out away from your sides in line with the resistance from the cables until you have returned to the starting position with your arms fully spread apart.
    1. At the end of this movement, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulders, but the end range should not be painful.
    2. People commonly have the shoulder mobility to allow their arms to pull back until their elbows are slightly behind the torso, but your shoulder mobility will determine what your individual end range is.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows mildly bent as the movement comes from shoulder motion rather than elbow motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Based on your preference, you can alternate which arm crosses over the other during each rep, or you can use the same position throughout the set. If you prefer to use the same position throughout the set, you can alternate which arm crosses over the other from set to set, or you can use the same position during each set. Each side is being trained in an extremely similar manner, so there is little reason to be concerned about developing any meaningful side-to-side imbalance, regardless of whether you decide to alternate which arm crosses over the other.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. If your decline press station allows you to adjust them, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench. If your decline press station has fixed J-hook heights, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
2. After situating a bar on top of the J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of decline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to confirm they are the appropriate height prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
3. Load the bar with your intended weight.
    1. It is recommended to avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would permit you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
4. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
    2. Most decline press stations do not allow you to adjust this pad.
5. Lie down with your back and head on the bench.
    1. Ideally, the bar should now be reasonably close to being above your eyes. However, decline press stations do not allow you to adjust where you position your body on the bench as you could do on a flat bench or an incline press station (which typically has an adjustable seat).
    2. When locking your feet and ankles under the pad, you cannot meaningfully adjust where your body will be in relation to the bar. Consequently, a decline bench press station may not be well suited to accommodate every body type. Rather short people may find that the bar is positioned behind the back of their head, which can make it quite difficult to unrack the bar. In this instance, a spotter may be required to provide meaningful assistance when unracking and racking the bar. Alternatively, if you happen to be tall enough that the bar is positioned above your shoulders when lying down, it may simply not be practical to use a decline press station since the J-hooks will interfere with your bar path.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands near shoulder width apart. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. Positioning your hands near shoulder-width apart results in somewhat of a range, but you can initially start with a shoulder-width grip and try out mildly different hand widths over time to determine where specifically you feel best.
    2. What is personally considered a close grip to you can also be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard decline press, which you can mildly narrow to individualize your close grip. For instance, for your close grip, you can place your little finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your index finger when performing your standard decline press.
    3. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    4. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    5. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, but avoid exaggerating this arch any further.
## Exercise technique
1. While squeezing your glutes, brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be securely locked in place, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and keeping them reasonably tucked in toward your sides. Descend until you are able to tap your lower chest or lower breastbone lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists (when viewed from the side) rather than letting your elbows track in front of your wrists (closer to your hips) or behind them. This should result in your forearms remaining close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set.
    2. Keeping your wrists stacked over your elbows while touching near your lower chest will naturally promote the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare. At the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming reasonably close to 45-30-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso). This should involve a lesser degree of elbow flare compared to if you used a wider grip.
    3. When aiming to touch the bar near your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up until it is situated above your shoulders with your elbows fully straightened.
    1. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and in toward each other.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions  while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable decline bench at a decline angle near 15-30 degrees.
    1. As the decline angle becomes more pronounced, the range of motion for this exercise decreases, so a relatively mild decline is typically appropriate.
    2. Some adjustable benches have a mild decline setting along with incline settings, but lack a pad to secure your feet. Using a decline bench with a foot/ankle pad is recommended for stability during this exercise.
2. Center the decline bench underneath a Smith machine’s bar in a spot that will allow you to touch near your lower chest with the bar at the bottom of the press.
    1. After initially estimating which bench placement allows this to occur, you may have to move the bench a bit forward or backward during later steps if you notice the bar does not reach near your lower chest.
    2. Specifically how you should set up can be influenced by whether your Smith machine has sliding rails that are purely vertical or angled.
    3. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, place the side of the bench where you will place your feet on the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends. This will result in the top of the rails being horizontally closer to your head than the bottom of the rails when lying down on the bench. An angled Smith machine will prompt the bar to track mildly back toward your head when pressing the bar up.
    4. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which side of the bar you place your feet.
3. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to grasp it with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench.
    1. The ideal height allows you to unrack the bar while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together without requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    2. If the bar is situated too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart to lift the bar off the safety bars (on which the safety hooks that are attached to the bar rest). This can place you in a less stable position to start your set since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    3. If the bar is situated excessively low, you will have to grasp the bar initially with a greater degree of elbow bend, and unracking it will involve a greater than ideal pressing range of motion. This will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    4. If neither of the two closest bar heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    5. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best starting bar height for you.
4. After positioning the bar to the appropriate height, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of decline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section. Use these reps to determine the appropriate height of the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails under the bar) prior to re-racking the bar.
5. Position the safety stops slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your decline press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could untuck your shoulder blades. This will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety stops if the safety stops are just under your lower chest.
    2. Certain Smith machine models have fairly wide spaces between where you could position safety stops. This may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your decline press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you untuck your shoulder blades. If this applies to your Smith machine, you have the option of using a spotter.
    3. If you cannot press the bar fully up and lack a spotter or safety stops that are able to receive the bar, you may be able to re-rack the bar on the closest safety bars above your chest if you are able to complete part of the press. If you happen to become pinned in this situation and are initially unable to reach the nearest safety bars above you, you can let the bar rest on your chest until you feel recovered enough to press the bar up high enough to re-rack the bar on the closest safety bars.
6. After loading the bar with your intended weight, sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
7. Lie down with your back and head on the bench.
    1. With the appropriate bench placement, your lower chest should now be under the bar if your Smith machine has vertical slide rails. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, your lower chest should be mildly horizontally in front of the bar.
8. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for a bench press variation is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
9. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, but avoid exaggerating this arch any further.
## Exercise technique
1. While squeezing your glutes, brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be securely locked in place, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the safety bars by straightening your elbows before rotating the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion. After unracking it, hold the bar stationary for a moment before starting to lower it.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other. Descend until you are able to tap near your lower chest lightly with the bar
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set.
    2. Avoid fully flaring your elbows out away from your sides or completely tucking them. The appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar near your lower chest. At the bottom of the rep, your upper arms should be reasonably close to forming 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    3. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up until you have fully straightened your elbows. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
    1. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second.
6. Rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars by flicking your wrists before bending your elbows to lower the safety hooks securely onto the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions  while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Follow the set-up instructions for the dual kettlebell clean, and perform the lifting phase of the dual kettlebell clean described in steps 1-5 of its exercise technique instructions.
    1. After doing so, you should now be holding the kettlebell in the front rack position. This involves situating them in front of your shoulders with your elbows bent slightly in front of your ribcage, palms facing in toward each other, and the weights resting on your forearms, which are mostly vertically with your wrists slightly closer to the midline of your body than your elbows. The handle should also be diagonally contacting your palms, such that the sides of the handles by your thumbs are angled mildly higher than the sides of the handles by your little fingers.
    2. As an alternative to cleaning the kettlebells, you can curl them up into the same front rack position described above if you are using loads that are light enough to do so. With this technique, initially grasp the handles with your little fingers directly next to one of the horns (i.e., side handles) on each kettlebell to help the handles rest diagonally across your palms in the front rack position. Feel free to perform a cheat curl-like motion where you initially generate some momentum by swinging the weights mildly backward with straight elbows before swinging your elbows forward while curling them. This is a less technically complicated means of moving the kettlebells into the front rack, but it is not as efficient when using heavy weights for lifters who are proficient with cleaning kettlebells.
2. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, keep your chest up in a “proud chest” posture, rather than letting your shoulders and upper back round forward into a hunched position.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain its natural curvature like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes like you are trying to crush a walnut. Simultaneously, brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your glutes tightened and core braced throughout your set.
2. While simultaneously flaring your elbows out away from your sides and directing your palms forward, press the kettlebells upward.
    1. Strive to finish with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling with your elbows fully straightened, and your biceps by your ears.
    2. Rather than turning the exercise into a segmented movement where you flare your elbows fully outward and then drive them upward, aim to have the outward elbow flare fluidly occur while pressing up.
    3. While your forearms start slightly angled in the front rack position, they should otherwise remain essentially in a vertical position with your elbows stacked under your wrists throughout the press.
    4. Continue keeping your knees and wrists straight as the kettlebells contact your forearms throughout the set.
    5. Avoid leaning your torso back via lower back arching, which may be more likely to occur if you relax your abs or glutes.
    6. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the kettlebells in a controlled manner as you let your elbows bend while descending and arcing forward toward the front rack position.
    1. Finish with your wrists in front of your shoulders, palms facing in toward each other, and elbows bent slightly in front of your ribcage.
    2. While keeping them vertical throughout most of this step, allow your forearms to angle slightly inward at the end, resulting in your straight wrists being slightly closer to your midline than your elbows.
    3. There is no need to lower the kettlebells very slowly, but you should avoid letting them drop down abruptly in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebells down in the opposite manner to how you initially lifted them into the front rack position.
    1. If you initially cleaned the kettlebells, you can let the kettlebells fall forward as you swing your arms down while straightening your elbows and hinging at your hips to absorb the force of the lowering phase.
    2. If you initially curled the kettlebells up, you can similarly guide them down by letting your elbows straighten and swinging your elbows mildly back to help control the weights.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Position the J-hooks of a power rack (or squat stand) near the height of your upper chest.
    1. As an alternative to starting from a rack, you can begin with the barbell on the floor and power clean it into position. However, this page will describe how to set up from a rack.
2. Place a barbell onto the J-hooks and load it with your desired weight.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands near shoulder width apart, as your palms are facing away from you.
    1. What is personally considered a close grip to you can be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard overhead press, which you can narrow somewhat to individualize your close grip. For instance, for your close grip, you can place your little finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your index finger when performing your standard overhead press. However, if you already use a shoulder-width grip for your standard overhead press rather than a slightly outside shoulder-width grip, overhead pressing may feel awkward with your hands any closer than shoulder-width apart.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Slightly bend your knees, and step forward to situate your collarbone or upper chest directly under the bar. Simultaneously, position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Your forearms should now be close to a vertical position when viewed from the front.
    3. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    4. Relative to when you overhead press with a wider grip, which normally involves a greater degree of elbow flare (pointing out to the side), your elbows should naturally be tucked in (pointing forward) to a greater extent during this close-grip variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Fully stand upright while holding the bar.
    1. With your knees straightened, the bar should now be above the tops of the J-hooks.
3. Take a couple of steps backward and set your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
4. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
5. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
6. Tuck or tilt your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
7. While keeping your legs straight, press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. To the best of your ability, avoid pushing the bar forward out in front of your shoulders. This can be challenging if you are new to this exercise, since you want to avoid hitting your face with the bar. You can still meaningfully increase your strength and grow muscle if the bar drifts a bit forward, but strive to minimize any forward bar motion as well as you can for an efficient overhead press.
    2. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position throughout your rep.
    3. Avoid using your legs to help with driving the bar up. This is not a push press.
8. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
9. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe after locking out the weight overhead. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to lowering the bar.
10. Reverse the movement by tucking your chin back and lowering the bar toward your collarbone in a controlled manner.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
    2. Compared to if you used a moderate grip width, it may be more difficult to touch your collarbone with the bar when using a close grip. If it feels awkward to reach your collarbone with this variation, there is no need to force yourself into that position.
11. Alternate between steps 7-10 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
12. After completing the lowering phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second before walking forward.
13. After the bar contacts the back of both J-hooks, bend your knees slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your upper pec major, lateral delts, triceps, traps, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each cable to be near the height of your lower chest, connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, set the arms so that their ends are about the length of your wingspan apart from each other.
2. While standing to the side of one cable, grasp its handle, and then walk over to grasp the handle of the other cable.
3. With your palms facing forward and your elbows slightly bent, center yourself between the cables, and step forward while allowing the cables to spread your arms apart and pull your arms back behind your torso through a range that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. You want to be sufficiently positioned in front of the cables that the cables exert resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise. This will involve your hands being in front of the cable pulleys with tension still present from the cables when you are fully spread apart in the starting position of each rep.
    2. If your shoulder mobility comfortably allows your arms to move further behind your torso than they initially are, you can take another step forward to be able to train through a full range of motion.
    3. In line with the resistance from the cables, your upper arms should look like they form a “T-shape” with your torso as your elbows are close to shoulder height.
4. While mildly bending your knees, adopt a staggered stance with one foot in front of the other as your feet are approximately hip width apart from each other.
    1. In this position, you should feel stable and balanced.
5. As you are standing with an upright torso, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows mildly bent, pull your hands in toward each and forward in an arcing motion until they are close to touching with your arms out in front of your torso.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, your hands should be close to shoulder height, and your arms should look like you are hugging a barrel.
    2. Keep your chest up throughout the set as you avoid hunching over with your shoulders slouched forward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your hands to spread apart from each other in a controlled manner as they move backward and out away from your sides in line with the resistance from the cables until you have returned to the starting position with your arms fully spread apart.
    1. At the end of this movement, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulders, but the end range should not be painful.
    2. People commonly have the shoulder mobility to allow their arms to pull back until their elbows are slightly behind the torso, but your shoulder mobility will determine what your individual end range is.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows mildly bent as the movement comes from shoulder motion rather than elbow motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can set up on an exercise mat.
2. Spread your fingers somewhat apart from each other as your hands are situated directly underneath your shoulders (i.e., shoulder-width apart).
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner until it lightly touches the ground.
    1. Rather than excessively flaring your elbows out away from your sides, keep your elbows reasonably tucked by your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you as you descend.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact the ground instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    5. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your front delts, triceps, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Approach the pair of bars/handles that you will use for your dip.
    1. While you do not have to maintain a completely upright torso, keep your torso mostly upright for this triceps dip rather than more prominently leaning your torso forward like you would during a chest dip. A mild degree of torso lean can naturally occur without striving to lean forward and often contributes to the exercise feeling more comfortable than if you focus on being fully upright.
2. Firmly grasp the bars with your palms facing each other and your elbows straight. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
    2. If you are using angled bars, you can grasp the bars at the point where they are near shoulder width apart for you.
    3. Depending on the equipment you use, you initially may have to step up onto the equipment or a step you position nearby before you can grasp the bars with straight elbows.
3. While keeping your elbows straightened and torso upright, lift your feet off the ground.
    1. Depending on the equipment you use, you may have to bend your knees in order for your feet to clear the ground, or you may be able to keep your knees straight.
    2. With straight knees, you can keep the inner aspect of your feet against each other. If you bend your knees, you can cross your ankles.
    3. Compared to positioning your legs further backward or forward, keeping your thighs close to vertical can generally promote an upright torso.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. As you keep them tucked close to your sides, bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. While you do not have to maintain a completely upright torso, keep your torso mostly upright for this triceps dip rather than more prominently leaning your torso forward like you would during a chest dip. A mild degree of torso lean can naturally occur without striving to lean forward and often contribute to the exercise feeling more comfortable than if you focused on being fully upright.
    2. Throughout your set, keep your legs stationary relative to your hips (rather than swinging them) and avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up.
    3. Avoid flaring your elbows out away from your sides. When viewed from behind, your elbows should remain stacked over your wrists and the bars rather than having your elbows move noticeably outside your wrists.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their elbows are bent by at least 90 degrees, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by pushing through the bars to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows straight down.
    2. You should finish with your elbows close to your sides and your arms directed down toward the ground.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down from the bars.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the lateral and medial heads of your triceps while also training its long head to a lesser degree, along with your front delts and pec major.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbell at your side with a straight elbow as your palm faces in toward your thigh.
    1. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    2. Throughout the exercise, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting by your side or held out to the side for balance assistance.
    3. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
2. Lift the dumbbell up near the front of your shoulder with your elbow bent by curling the weight and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbell into position if it is too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbell mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbow to curl the weight upward while swinging your elbow forward. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbell right by your shoulder with your forearm in a vertical position, and your wrist stacked over your elbow as the handle of the dumbbell is parallel to your upper arm.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, pull your shoulder blades back together to adopt a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back along with your neck, like when you are normally standing upright.
4. In the starting position, your palm should be facing inward (with a neutral grip) as your upper arm is in front of your body with the back of your elbow pointing downward and forward (as opposed to being flared out away from your side).
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Without moving your arm, mildly bend your knees in a quarter squat-like manner to dip down while maintaining an upright torso position with your chest up.
    1. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground during this dip phase.
    2. Your knees should track straight forward toward your toes rather than caving inward.
3. Directly after reaching the bottom of your dip, forcibly push through your feet as you rapidly extend (straighten) your knees and hips to generate upward momentum.
    1. The intent of this leg drive phase should be akin to a jump without lifting your feet fully off the ground.
    2. At the end of this drive, you can either keep the soles of your feet fully on the ground, or you can allow your heels to lift up slightly off the ground while keeping the front of your feet firmly in contact with the ground if a slight heel lift feels natural to you.
4. Immediately after you have finished extending your legs, explosively push the dumbbell upward by forcibly driving your elbow upward as you swiftly straighten your elbow.
    1. At the end of the pressing phase, your knees, hips, and elbow should be fully straightened, and your arm should be directed straight up toward the ceiling with your elbow and dumbbell situated vertically over your shoulder.
    2. To transfer your leg drive into pushing the dumbbell upward in an explosive manner, you should perform steps 3 and 4 as a continuous movement.
    3. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, your forearm should be in a vertical position throughout the rep as your wrist and dumbbell remain stacked over your elbow.
    4. Throughout the exercise, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back.
    5. Regardless of whether you lifted your heels up off the ground after extending your legs during the leg drive phase, your heels should be flat on the ground with the rest of your feet at the end of the pressing phase.
5. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner as your elbow progressively bends while simultaneously moving downward.
6. As you approach the end of the lowering phase, shallowly bend your knees and hips as you did during step 2 (the dip phase) to help control the weight until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbell in front of your shoulder.
    1. Lower the dumbbell as far as you can while keeping your wrist in a straight position stacked over your elbow as your forearm remains vertical, which should result in your elbow being lower than your shoulder.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearm vertical.
    3. If you are using a heavy, bulky dumbbell, the dumbbell contacting your shoulder may also determine your end range of the lowering phase.
7. After you have fully lowered the weight, extend your knees and hips to stand upright before initiating your next rep.
8. Alternate between steps 2-7 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
9. After your dumbbell is by your shoulder following the lowering phase of the last rep, lower the dumbbell to your side as you straighten your elbow and swing your elbow back by your side.
10. Set the dumbbell down and pick it up with your other side before repeating the exercise with that side.
## Description
While developing explosive overhead pressing strength and engaging the quads to generate leg drive, this exercise can train  your front delts, triceps, traps, lateral delts,  and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with slightly bent elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
2. Slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the dumbbells out and away from your sides until your upper arms are close to parallel with the ground.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner back to your sides.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus while also training your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Place a pair of dumbbells on the ground next to each other with their handles vertical, if possible for the dumbbells you are using.
    1. Positioning them in this manner will result in one weighted end of each dumbbell being off the ground.
    2. However, you will have to situate both ends of each dumbbell on the ground (with their handles parallel to the ground) if you are using dumbbells that do not have flat ends (such as with a plate-loaded adjustable dumbbell). You can still floor press these dumbbells, but it can be more difficult to move them into the starting position, particularly if you are using heavy loads.
    3. You can optionally place an exercise mat on the ground behind the dumbbells for comfort.
2. Sit down on the ground with your thighs spread apart just outside the dumbbells.
    1. Your knees should be straight as you are sitting with an upright torso and the back of your heels on the ground.
3. One at a time, firmly grasp the dumbbells and position them on top of your upper thighs with their handles vertical and your elbows tucked close to your sides.
    1. When using dumbbells with flat ends, place them on the middle part of your thighs.
    2. If you are using plate-loaded adjustable dumbbells with protruding sleeves, you can rest the outer part of the dumbbells on the inner aspect of the front of your thighs. This will result in the lower sleeve contacting the inner aspect of your thighs
4. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back in a controlled manner to lie down.
    1. Move your torso and arms together as one unit. This will result in your upper arms remaining by your sides throughout the transition and contacting the ground at the same time as your back.
    2. When appropriately moving into position, you should not feel that you are meaningfully stressing your shoulders.
    3. When marching your thighs up, your thighs should be close to vertical with your knees bent once your back contacts the ground.
    4. Strive to have the dumbbells vertically stacked over your elbows when lying on the ground, resulting in your forearms being in a vertical position.
    5. Once you are on your back, you should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists. This should promote a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
5. Position your legs straight out in front of you with straight knees.
    1. The back of your heels should now be on the ground along with your buttocks, upper back, and head.
6. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
7. Flare your elbows moderately out to your sides such that your upper arms form approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso.
    1. Keep your forearms vertical while doing so.
    2. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable for your shoulders, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbows out within this range.
    3. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are completely tucked by your sides, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your upper arms would form a “T-shape” with your torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbells upward until your elbows are fully straightened with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your wrists mostly straight and stacked under your elbows throughout the press. This will result in your forearms remaining in a vertical position.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
    4. Throughout your set, continue keeping your heels, buttock, upper back, and head in contact with the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows and upper arms softly contact the ground.
    1. Allow your elbows to flare out moderately away from your sides so that your upper arms form close to 45-75-degree angles with your torso at the bottom.
    2. Continue keeping your wrists stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical.
    3. While you do not have to finish the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you certainly should avoid slamming your arms onto the ground. You will not be bouncing your arms off the ground.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the reverse manner to how you initially set up.
5. After finishing the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arms in line with your sides to tuck your elbows and position the dumbbells closer to your torso.
6. March your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before setting them down.
## Description
This exercise can develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Position the J-hooks of a power rack (or squat stand) near the height of your upper chest.
2. Place a barbell onto the J-hooks and load it with your desired weight.
    1. It is recommended to use an empty barbell if you are new to this exercise.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands at least moderately outside shoulder width as your palms are facing away from you.
    1. Relative to a standard overhead press performed to the front of your body, a wider grip will normally be more comfortable when pressing behind your head.
    2. If you raise your arms straight out to your sides until they are parallel to the ground, note the midway points between your elbows and the outer aspect of your shoulders. Gripping the bar with your hands near these two points can be an appropriate starting grip width for many people. However, the specific grip width that is best to use for this exercise will vary among different individuals. To personally find your ideal placement, feel free to try out different hand widths over time while noting how your shoulders feel. The hand placement that is best for you should feel comfortable on your shoulders while enabling you to train through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility permits.
    3. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Slightly bend your knees, and step forward to situate your collarbone or upper chest directly under the bar. Simultaneously, position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Relative to when you overhead press to the front with a closer grip, which normally involves a greater degree of elbow tuck (pointing forward), your elbows should naturally be flared out (pointing out to the side) to a greater extent during this variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Fully stand upright while holding the bar.
    1. With your knees straightened, the bar should now be above the tops of the J-hooks.
3. Take a couple of steps backward and set your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
4. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
5. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
6. Tuck your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
7. Press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position.
8. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar. Simultaneously, arc the bar slightly backward while pressing it up.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
9. Finish the initial press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe after locking out the weight overhead. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to lowering the bar.
    3. You are now in the starting position for your behind-the-neck press.
10. In a slow and controlled manner, lower the bar behind your head through the range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. If you have the mobility to do so comfortably, you can lower the bar until it lightly contacts your upper back muscles just under the base of your neck. However, what constitutes your end range will be determined by your individual shoulder mobility.
    2. If you start experiencing shoulder pain or joint discomfort at the bottom of the lowering phase, you likely are moving beyond the current limit of your shoulder mobility. Training this exercise over time may progressively improve your shoulder mobility and gradually permit you to descend through a greater range, but there is no need to force yourself into a position that currently does not feel right for your shoulders.
    3. With sufficient shoulder mobility, you should not have to push your head meaningfully forward to avoid scraping your head or neck with the bar. However, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest if you wish to do so for additional clearance.
    4. Strive to keep your elbows stacked under the bar when viewed from the side. To do so, your elbows should be pointing out away from your sides.
    5. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
11. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up until you have fully straightened your elbows with the bar overhead.
12. Alternate between steps 10 and 11 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
13. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, tuck your chin back and lower the bar to your collarbone.
14. With the bar now in front of your body, walk forward until the bar contacts the back of both J-hooks
15. Bend your knees slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts and lateral delts while also training your triceps, supraspinatus, serratus anterior, and traps.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width to hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your toes point straight ahead.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Lift the dumbbells up near the front of your shoulders with your elbows bent by curling the weights and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbells into position if they are too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbells mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbows to curl the weights upward while swinging your elbows forward. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your shoulders with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, pull your shoulder blades back together to adopt a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back along with your neck, like when you are normally standing upright.
4. In the starting position, your palms should be facing in toward each other as your upper arms are close to being parallel to each other in front of your body (as opposed to being flared out away from your sides).
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Without moving your arms, mildly bend your knees in a quarter squat-like manner to dip down while maintaining an upright torso position with your chest up.
    1. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground during this dip phase.
    2. Your knees should track straight forward toward your toes rather than caving inward.
3. Directly after reaching the bottom of your dip, forcibly push through your feet as you rapidly extend (straighten) your knees and hips to generate upward momentum.
    1. The intent of this leg drive phase should be akin to a jump without lifting your feet fully off the ground.
    2. At the end of this drive, you can either keep the soles of your feet fully on the ground, or you can allow your heels to lift up slightly off the ground while keeping the front of your feet firmly in contact with the ground if a slight heel lift feels natural to you.
4. Immediately after you have finished extending your legs, explosively push the dumbbells upward by forcibly driving your elbows upward as you swiftly straighten your elbows.
    1. At the end of the pressing phase, your knees, hips, and elbows should be fully straightened, and your arms should be directed straight up toward the ceiling with your elbows and dumbbells situated vertically over your shoulders.
    2. To transfer your leg drive into pushing the dumbbells upward in an explosive manner, you should perform steps 3 and 4 as a continuous movement.
    3. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your forearms should be in a vertical position throughout the rep as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    4. Throughout the exercise, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back.
    5. Regardless of whether you lifted your heels up off the ground after extending your legs during the leg drive phase, your heels should be flat on the ground with the rest of your feet at the end of the pressing phase.
5. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively bend while simultaneously moving downward.
6. As you approach the end of the lowering phase, shallowly bend your knees and hips like you did during step 2 (the dip phase) to help control the weight until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbells in front of your shoulders.
    1. Lower the dumbbells as far as you can while keeping your wrists in a straight position stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical, which should result in your elbows being lower than your shoulders.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearms vertical.
    3. If you are using heavy, bulky dumbbells, the dumbbells contacting your shoulders may also determine your end range of the lowering phase.
7. After you have fully lowered the weights, extend your knees and hips to stand upright before initiating your next rep.
8. Alternate between steps 2-7 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your shoulders following the lowering phase of the last rep, lower the dumbbells to your sides as you straighten your elbows and swing your elbows back by your sides.
## Description
While developing explosive overhead pressing strength and engaging the quads to generate leg drive, this exercise can train  your front delts, triceps, traps, lateral delts,  and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. If your decline press station allows you to adjust them, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench. If your decline press station has fixed J-hook heights, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
2. After situating a bar on top of the J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of decline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to confirm they are the appropriate height prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
3. Load the bar with your intended weight.
    1. It is recommended to avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would permit you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
4. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
    2. Most decline press stations do not allow you to adjust this pad.
5. Lie down with your back and head on the bench.
    1. Ideally, the bar should now be reasonably close to being above your eyes. However, decline press stations do not allow you to adjust where you position your body on the bench as you could do on a flat bench or an incline press station (which typically has an adjustable seat).
    2. When locking your feet and ankles under the pad, you cannot meaningfully adjust where your body will be in relation to the bar. Consequently, a decline bench press station may not be well suited to accommodate every body type. Rather short people may find that the bar is positioned behind the back of their head, which can make it quite difficult to unrack the bar. In this instance, a spotter may be required to provide meaningful assistance when unracking and racking the bar. Alternatively, if you happen to be tall enough that the bar is positioned above your shoulders when lying down, it may simply not be practical to use a decline press station since the J-hooks will interfere with your bar path.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands well outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A wide grip width for a bench press variation is commonly considered to be near twice your shoulder width, and this involves somewhat of a range of grip widths, so you can feel free to try out mildly different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. What is personally considered a wide grip to you can also be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard decline press, which you can somewhat widen to individualize your wide grip. For instance, for your wide grip, you can place your index finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing your standard decline press.
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, but avoid exaggerating this arch any further.
## Exercise technique
1. While squeezing your glutes, brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be securely locked in place, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them naturally to flare out moderately away from each other. Descend until you are able to tap your lower chest or lower breastbone lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists (when viewed from the side) rather than letting your elbows track in front of your wrists (closer to your hips) or behind them. This should result in your forearms remaining close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set.
    2. When aiming to touch the bar near your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. To touch that point, you will not fully flare your elbows out away from your side to the extent that your upper arms form 90-degree angles with your torso. However, keeping your wrists stacked over your elbows will naturally promote a greater degree of elbow flare during this wide grip variation compared to when pressing with a closer grip. Near the bottom of the rep, the appropriate amount of elbow flare should result in your upper arms forming reasonably close to 75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are touching your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Adjust a pair of securely anchored gymnastic rings to be near shoulder width apart with the bottom of the rings at hand height.
    1. For a more advanced fly variation, you can alternatively situate the rings closer to the ground, but this page will describe how to set up for an intermediate difficulty.
2. Grasp the rings with your palms facing in toward each other and your elbows straight. Hold the rings directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
3. How you subsequently position your body will be affected by the degree of difficulty you wish each to have. For a lower intensity fly, raise your arms so that they are directed diagonally forward and downward. For higher intensity fly, keep your arms directed down toward the ground.
    1. The difficulty of each rep will directly increase as your torso becomes closer to horizontal, which is facilitated by keeping your arms directed closer to straight down.
    2. As you angle your arms forward, you will be able to set up with a more vertical torso, which makes the exercise easier.
    3. The appropriate position should allow you to perform your fly through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits for your target number of reps.
    4. If you are new to this exercise, it is recommended to angle your arms diagonally forward to let you use a more vertical torso position during your set.
4. While bearing weight through the rings, step backward while allowing your torso to lean forward as one unit until your arms form approximately 90-degree angles with your torso when viewed from the side.
    1. Finish with your knee straight and your legs fully aligned with your torso, allowing you to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulder when viewed from the side.
    2. You should now look like you are in a tall plank position.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abdominal muscles to brace your core and squeeze your glutes to maintain a flat back position while keeping your elbows and knees straight.
    1. Continue keeping your abs and glutes tensed throughout the set, like you would during a plank.
    2. Throughout the set, avoid letting your lower back arch excessively, which could occur if your hips start sagging.
2. Spread your arms apart from each other in a controlled manner while letting the rest of your body lean forward as one unit. Simultaneously, let your shoulder blades pull back together and your elbows mildly bend.
    1. Maintain the approximately 90-degree angles between your arms and torso as you avoid moving your arms closer toward your sides or further away from your sides.
    2. While motion chiefly occurs at your shoulders and mildly at your elbows, you should keep the rest of your body in a plank position as you avoid moving your legs or torso, besides allowing them to lean forward together.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their elbows are at least level with their shoulders when viewed from the side, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders and chest, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility or have adopted an excessively horizontal torso relative to your current strength. Training this exercise over time may improve your mobility and strength, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by driving your arms in toward each other to lift your body up toward a more vertical position.
    1. Allow your elbows to straighten naturally, and finish this lifting phase once the rings are shoulder width apart.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep, step forward to return to an upright torso position.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. For the guillotine press, you will set up just like you would with a wide grip bench press.
    1. While not inherently injurious, the guillotine press is not suitable for everyone’s shoulders, and it is strongly encouraged to use a spotter, particularly if you perform it without safety pins/spotter arms that are able to protect your neck in case you fail a rep.
2. Determine whether you will set up within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
3. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, you will find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
4. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of guillotine press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your guillotine press.
    1. If you have the shoulder mobility to touch the bar to your collarbone, it can be quite difficult to position the safety pins/spotter arms at a height that allows you to train through your full range of motion while simultaneously protecting your neck, since your neck is right next to your collarbone. If you are in this situation, you can decide whether you will accept the risk of performing this exercise through a full range of motion without safety pins/spotter arms while using a competent spotter. Alternatively, you can position the safety pins/spotter arms to the lowest point that is above your neck and lower the bar until it lightly contacts the safety mechanisms, which will slightly reduce the range of motion.
    2. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could relax your upper back by allowing your shoulder blades to spread apart, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins/spotter arms.
    3. You can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar to allow you to dump the weight to one side and have the weights slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
6. Lie down with your back on the bench as your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor approximately shoulder width apart from each other.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. You can similarly decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) as your hands are well outside shoulder width apart from each other, with the bar on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. Like with a wide grip bench press, you can aim to situate your hands about twice the distance of your shoulder width apart from each other.
    2. For stability, your thumbs should be wrapped around the bar from the opposite side of your other fingers, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands in contrast to if you positioned your thumbs on the same side of your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip that is also called a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists meaningfully cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together, which is a position you will maintain throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades, which should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform for pressing.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together as you avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your collarbone in a slow and controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them naturally to flare out away from each other while descending. 
    1. While motion occurs simultaneously at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked under your mostly straight wrists, rather than letting them track in front of your wrists (closer to your hips) or behind them (closer to your head). Maintaining this alignment will promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare.
    2. The bar should move straight down without any forward movement toward your lower chest, unlike a typical bench press.
    3. An intentionally slow descent is especially beneficial for the guillotine press. Prioritize fully controlling every moment of the lowering phase.
4. Continue lowering the bar slowly until you can tap your collarbone lightly with it, or until you reach the end of the comfortable range of motion permitted by your shoulder mobility.
    1. Not everyone has the shoulder mobility to touch the bar to the collarbone, and you should not force your shoulders into a position exceeding what your mobility safely permits.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom of the rep, but you should not experience pain or joint discomfort.
    3. Near the bottom of the rep, your upper arms should be close to forming a “T-shape” (i.e., 90-degree angles) with your torso due to your elbows being flared out further away from your sides compared to a standard bench press.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up as you progressively drive your elbows upward and in toward each other while straightening them.
    1. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. Like during the descent, the bar should move in a vertical path as you press it up until it has returned to being situated above your shoulders.
    3. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly in contact with the bench, feet planted on the ground, and shoulder blades pulled back together.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows mildly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your entire pec major, which is assisted by your front delt and triceps.## Setting up
1. If you are using an adjustable incline bench, position it in the fully upright setting. Otherwise, proceed to the next step.
    1. You can interchangeably use an adjustable bench that is positioned fully upright or a seated bench that is permanently fixed in an upright position.
    2. Alternatively, you can use a bench that is permanently fixed in a flat position. This will preclude you from resting your back against the bench, but otherwise allow you to use the same technique that the exercise involves when using back support.
2. After picking up one dumbbell, sit down on a seated bench while lifting the dumbbell onto your lower thigh without leaning back onto the back rest.
    1. To move the dumbbell into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbell on the front of your thigh while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest it on your thigh while sitting down.
3. On the side firmly holding the dumbbell, tuck your elbow by your side. Tightly wrap your free hand around the hand holding the dumbbell.
4. March your thigh up as you lift the dumbbell up near your shoulder with your elbow bent. Simultaneously lean back to situate your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench.
    1. You can use both arms to help you lift the weight.
    2. After doing so, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    3. You should now be holding the dumbbell right by your shoulder with your forearm in a vertical position, and your wrist stacked over your elbow as the handle of the dumbbell is parallel to your upper arm.
5. Unwrap your outer hand, not directly holding the dumbbell, and move it into a comfortable position away from the weight, such as resting by your side.
    1. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training.
    2. You should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
6. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling, and maintain this posture throughout your set.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
7. Flare your elbow at least moderately out to your side to a degree that feels comfortable for your shoulder.
    1. When 0 degrees of elbow flare means your wrist is directly in front of your shoulder, and 90 degrees of elbow flare means your wrist is fully out to your side with your palm facing straight forward, about 60-75 degrees of elbow flare is commonly appropriate. In this position, your elbow will point more out to the side than forward, but it will not be fully flared out.
    2. If your shoulder feels comfortable when pressing with a greater degree of elbow flare, you can certainly flare your elbow beyond that point, but some people find that utilizing a closer to moderate degree of elbow flare feels most comfortable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbell upward by driving your elbow upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten your elbow until it is fully straightened with your arm directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, your forearm should be in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrist and dumbbell remain stacked over your elbow.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your feet flat on the floor along with your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbell should be situated vertically over your shoulder.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner as your elbow progressively bends while simultaneously moving outward and downward until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbell by the side of your shoulder.
    1. Lower the dumbbell as far as you can while keeping your wrist in a straight position stacked over your elbow as your forearm remains vertical, which should result in your elbow being lower than your shoulder.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearm vertical.
    3. If you are using a heavy, bulky dumbbell, the dumbbell contacting your shoulder may also determine your end range of the lowering phase.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
5. After the dumbbell is by your shoulder following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arm in line with your side to tuck your elbow and position the dumbbell closer to your thigh.
6. Wrap your free hand around the hand holding the dumbbell for additional stability.
7. March your thigh up, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbell on your upper thigh before standing up with the dumbbell.
8. Place the dumbbell onto the seat of the bench, and pick it up with your other side before repeating the exercise with that side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Alternatively, you can also use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline. 
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on the incline bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your lower thighs near your abdomen with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. If you can curl the weight of the dumbbells, you can sit down on the bench with the dumbbells by your sides before curling them up onto your lap
    2. With heavier dumbbells, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs with your palms facing each other while standing over the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up one at a time to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench.
    1. Once you are inclined, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent, and you will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest, as you would be in the starting position for a dumbbell incline press.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
5. Press the dumbbells up until your elbows are straight, and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. In this starting position, your wrists should be straight, and you will keep them straight throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your elbows to bend mildly, spread your arms apart from each other as you lower the dumbbells out away from your sides in a slow and controlled arcing manner through the available range of motion of your shoulders.
    1. People commonly have the shoulder mobility to lower the dumbbells at least until their elbows are level with their shoulders and often a little beyond this point, but what is specifically your full range of motion will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulder at the bottom, but the end range should not be painful.
    3. After fully spreading your arms apart, your upper arms should form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    4. While your elbows should not be locked into a fully straightened position, they also should be notably less bent than when you are bench pressing, so the dumbbells should be clearly further out to the sides than your elbows (as opposed to being stacked over your elbows like at the bottom of a bench press).
2. Reverse the movement by pulling your arms in toward each other in a hug-like motion to lift the dumbbells upward until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbells being approximately over your shoulders.
    1. Near the end of the lifting phase, the resistance from the dumbbells is minimal, and there is no purpose of touching the dumbbells to each other.
    2. Toward the end of the lifting phase, you can straighten your elbows, or you can maintain the same degree of mild elbow bend throughout the set.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After your last rep, lower the dumbbells toward the sides of your chest as you allow your elbows to bend like you would during the lowering phase of a dumbbell incline press.
    2. Once the dumbbells are by your chest, you can rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your thighs before placing them down onto the ground one at a time or standing up with them.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt and pec major with an emphasis on the upper pec while still training the middle and lower pec.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to a barbell situated in a landmine attachment (or a viable substitute), stand in a shoulder-width stance near the end of the barbell’s sleeve that is loaded with plates, with one side facing the barbell.
    1. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    2. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel in between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
    3. The diameter of the plates you select can influence the range of motion of this exercise, so you can use smaller diameter plates if you wish to row through a longer range of motion.
    4. Plates weighing 45lb or 20kg (which differ slightly) along with bumper plates of various weights typically have a diameter near 45cm (slightly under 18 inches) that can physically constrain the range of motion for your row by contacting the ground at the end of the lowering phase.
2. Adopt a moderately staggered stance with your foot on the side closer to the bar positioned behind you and the inner aspect of your opposite foot in line with the barbell.
3. Simultaneously bend your hips and knees in a lunging motion to descend until you are low enough to grasp the end of the barbell’s sleeve with an extended elbow and an overhand grip, resulting in your palm facing backward.
    1. You will naturally grasp the bar with your hand on the side closer to the bar.
4. Brace your core and stand upright with the bar by extending your hips and knees while keeping your elbow straight.
    1. Throughout this movement, keep your lower back reasonably close to a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
    2. To brace effectively, tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
5. Hinge at your hips through your available range of motion while you mildly bend your knees to position your torso at an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow straight, core braced, and lower back near a neutral position while doing so.
    2. Your buttock should move backward while you hinge at your hips, as if you were closing a car door with your buttock while your hands are carrying something.
    3. How close to horizontal you are able to position your torso without meaningfully rounding your lower back will be dependent on your mobility.
    4. In the hinged position, it is natural to feel a stretching sensation in the hamstrings of your forward leg.
    5. Maintain this hinged position throughout your set.
    6. If using large diameter plates, the plates have the potential to contact the ground before you have finished hinging through your available range of motion, therefore requiring a relatively more upright trunk position.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward by driving your elbow upward and out to your side while progressively bending your elbow and pulling your shoulder blade backward .
    1. Allow your elbow naturally to flare out away from your side in a manner that feels comfortable to you.
    2. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your upper arm being close to parallel with the ground at the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbow to move downward and inward until your elbow has finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blade will naturally move forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar to its initial position by bending your hips and knees to lower the bar before repeating the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can train much of the musculature constituting your upper back including your traps, rhomboids, rear deltoid, and three rotator cuff muscles situated on the back of your shoulder blade.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up and firmly grasp it with your palms facing backward (i.e., an underhand grip) and your hands shoulder-width apart.
    1. Specifically, how closely you position your hands is up to your preference. Still, they should be clearly closer than for a standard chin-up with a moderate grip width (i.e., approximately shoulder width).
    2. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    3. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up onto a sturdy box, an aerobic step, or a bumper plate to place the bar within reach.
    4. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar than if you jumped up from the ground to reach it.
    5. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    6. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will place you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back puts you in a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps; however, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.

### **How Your Grip and Pull-Up Technique Influence Back Muscle Development**

Somewhat of a range of grip widths can fall within what is closer than shoulder width, but whether you opt to position your hands as close together as possible or only mildly within shoulder width is unlikely to alter to any meaningful degree which muscles are trained from this chin-up variation. Nonetheless, when repeatedly performing this exercise over time, you can feel free to try out different grip widths to determine if any particular position feels most comfortable to you and/or allows you to perceive your target muscles working particularly well. Pull-ups can be performed with a variety of hand grips including different grip widths and whether an overhand, underhand (resulting in the exercise being called a chin-up), neutral, or hybrid (i.e., a combination between neutral and underhand or overhand) grip is utilized. Additionally, the shoulder motion occurring during a pull-up can be performed predominantly in the sagittal (front to back) plane, frontal (side to side) plane, or in a combination between the sagittal and frontal planes. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the sagittal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out in front of you), the upper arms remain parallel to each other and do not deviate away from the mid-line of your body. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the frontal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out to your sides such as with a snow angle motion), the upper arms move away from the mid-line of your body until they are parallel to the ground, and then they move closer to the mid-line of your body with further elevation. During the lifting phase of a pull-up, the shoulders could extend when moving in the sagittal plane, adduct when moving in the frontal plane, or exhibit a combination of extension and adduction when moving at a hybrid between the sagittal and frontal planes. Rather than moving the shoulders exclusively through one plane, pull-up variations are commonly performed with a technique that allows for the shoulders to move in between these two planes, but one plane of shoulder motion often makes a greater contribution than the other.

Muscles acting on the shoulder joint have different leverage to generate extension torque in the sagittal plane versus adduction torque in the frontal plane, and the nervous system is most likely to recruit the particular muscles that are best leveraged to produce the intended joint action during any active movement. Correspondingly, the plane of motion in which the shoulders move during a pull-up can affect how well different muscles are likely to be trained. With any particular grip for a pull-up, you can intentionally rotate your shoulders in a manner that allows you to move your shoulder relatively more in the sagittal or frontal plane. However, most people will typically find that a particular path of shoulder motion feels most comfortable and stable to their shoulders, elbows, and wrists when using a specific grip. For instance, people will normally perform a wide overhand grip pull-up with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the frontal plane, while they will normally perform a close grip chin-up (using an underhand grip) with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the sagittal plane. To perform either variation in the alternative plane of motion would require greater than normal rotational range of motion in the shoulders and forearms, and attempting to do so could feel very awkward and uncomfortable. People will typically feel most comfortable when positioning themselves in a manner that, at the portion of the pull-up when their upper arms are situated parallel with the ground, results in their elbows and wrists being vertically aligned or their elbows lying somewhat lateral (outside) to their wrists.

In general, widening a grip facilitates relatively more frontal plane shoulder motion, while narrowing a grip facilitates relatively more sagittal plane shoulder motion. An overhand, underhand, or neutral grip may be used with any grip width, but the combination of these grip variables will influence comfort and which plane of shoulder motion feels most natural. When pulling predominantly in the sagittal plane with a rather close grip, an underhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable than an overhand grip. Similarly, when pulling predominantly in the frontal plane with a rather wide grip, an overhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable and enable more frontal plane motion than an underhand grip. With a moderate width grip, people may commonly find that pulling mostly in the sagittal plane feels natural with an underhand or neutral grip, but pulling relatively more in the frontal plane feels natural with an overhand grip. Fundamentally, the shoulder motion is what matters to the back muscles, and the grip is only relevant to the back muscles based on how it influences which shoulder motion is performed. When performing this chin-up with a close grip width, you most likely will pull in a way that predominantly trains shoulder extension in the sagittal plane as your elbows remain close to being aligned with your shoulders rather than flaring out to the sides.

The lat and teres major in the back function both as primary adductors and extensors and are consequently highly likely to be trained very effectively by pull-up variations regardless of the grip or plane of shoulder motion. However, the preponderance of available evidence indicates that the rear deltoid lacks meaningful leverage to contribute to shoulder adduction, but it clearly has favorable leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. The rear deltoid is correspondingly likely to be trained better with a closer grip emphasizing shoulder extension, which would be most comfortable when using an underhand or neutral grip. Alternatively the middle and lower regions of the pectoralis major (that constitute the sternoclavicular head) have large moment arms to function as shoulder adductors, but only the lower portion exhibits a shoulder extension moment arm, and this moment arm is quite small and only present in flexed shoulder positions. Consequently, wider grip pull-ups (that will be most comfortable with an overhand or neutral grip) involving greater frontal plane shoulder motion are likely to train the pectoralis major more effectively than closer grip pulldowns. Which regions of the lats are preferentially biased by a pull-up variation may also potentially be influenced by how much shoulder motion occurs in the frontal or sagittal planes. The middle and lower regions have relatively greater leverage to function as shoulder adductors, while the upper region of the lat has relatively greater leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. Plausibly, these biomechanical differences may facilitate the middle and lower regions of the lats being preferentially trained by wider grip pull-ups, while the upper region may be preferentially trained by closer grip pulling variations. With that said, the degree to which any preferential biasing may occur among different regions of the lats when comparing different pull-up grips is more speculative in nature and has not been tested by any longitudinal training interventions.

### **Why Overhand Grip Pull-Ups may be More Difficult than Chin-Ups or Neutral Grip Pull-Ups**

While exceptions exist, people often find overhand grip pull-ups to be more difficult on a per rep basis than chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. The discrepancy is most likely influenced by chin-ups and neutral grip pull-ups typically being performed with a technique that emphasizes shoulder extension in the sagittal plane, while overhand grip pull-ups are typically performed with relatively more shoulder adduction in the frontal plane. As demonstrated by studies such as those from Mayer et al. (1994) along with Shklar and Dvir (1995), on average, maximal torque for shoulder extension exceeds maximal torque for shoulder adduction. The sample of participants, velocity, and type of muscle action tested can all influence the degree to which torque differs between these two actions, but maximal shoulder adduction torque has been measured to be 63-90% of maximal shoulder extension depending on the specific test conditions. In addition to the effect from how the shoulder moves, the forearm position can also affect how much elbow flexion torque you can generate during your pull. Guenzkofer et al. (2012) found that up to 15% greater elbow flexion torque can be generated when the forearm is in supinated (used for an underhand grip) or neutral position compared to a pronated (used with an overhand grip) position. Together these relationships mean that most people should be able to generate greater torque at the shoulders and elbows while performing chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups in comparison to overhand grip pull-ups, which are likely to be more challenging when performing the same number of reps. Consequently, it is common not to be able to complete as many reps of overhand grip pull-ups in a set as you would be able to complete when performing chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. Nonetheless, individual differences among factors such as training history and the relative strength of different muscle groups can result in some people not noticing a difference in difficulty between these variations or even finding overhand grip pull-ups to be somewhat easier on a per rep basis.

### **Chin Over Bar?**

The technique instructions do not specify where any particular part of your body should be situated in relation to the bar at the end of the lifting phase because your full range of motion can differ from someone else’s full range of motion. Pulling until your chin is above the bar is a common standard that is utilized for pull-up testing by the United States Marines or competitions held by the World Pull-Up Organization (which permits use of an underhand or overhand grip), and you certainly can use this standard if you wish. However, when focusing on chin clearance, some people have a tendency to extend their necks in a manner that lifts their chins upward in order to clear the bar with lower range of motion through the pull itself compared to if they retained a neutral neck position. In this instance, focusing on the proxy for a full range of motion may lead to a different movement compared to focusing on what is the full range of motion for the individual. When competing or performing a standardized test that affects professional prospects, it is only sensible to perform the minimum amount of work required to complete each rep per the rules provided, but training to build muscle and strength is a different endeavor.

### **The Most Challenging Part of the Pull-Up**

The end range of the lifting phase will typically be the most challenging portion of chin-ups in large part due to how the torque our pulling muscles can generate meaningfully varies across different joint angles. When the elbow is flexed to 120 degrees, Bober et al. (2002) found maximal elbow flexion torque to be approximately 35% of the peak torque that can be generated in a moderately flexed elbow position (i.e., near 60-75 degrees of elbow flexion). With your elbows approaching full flexion toward the end range of a chin-up, your elbow flexor muscles are correspondingly operating from a rather disadvantaged position to help you complete the rep. On average, maximal shoulder extension torque is also lower when the shoulder is mildly elevated (like at the end of a chin-up’s lifting phase) compared to when it is moderately elevated (like during the middle range of a chin-up). The difference can be of a notable magnitude as indicated by Bober et al. (2002) finding maximal shoulder extension in a neutral shoulder position to be only 60% of its peak value that could be generated in 90 degrees of shoulder flexion. Together these strength curves of the muscle groups acting on the elbow and shoulder can contribute to your chin-ups consistently feeling the most difficult during the last quarter of the lifting phase.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your back muscle strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your back muscles. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up and firmly grasp it with your palms facing backward (i.e., an underhand grip), with your hands approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar than if you jumped up from the ground to reach it.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and meaningfully arch your back to angle your chest upward (rather than having your chest face straight ahead like it would without arching your back).
    1. Arching your back places allows you to lean back and places you into a position akin to an exaggerated “proud chest” posture.
2. As you continue keeping your back arched, pull your breastbone (sternum) up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. While pulling with your back notably arched, your upper torso will move into a progressively more horizontal position throughout the lifting phase, resulting in your breastbone facing up toward the bar.
    3. Strive to pull until your breastbone is as close to contacting the bar as your strength enables.
    4. At the end of the lifting phase, your elbows should be tucked close to your sides (rather than being flared out away from your sides), and your upper arms should be tucked close to your sides.
    5. You can keep your knees somewhat bent throughout your set.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. During the lower phase, your upper torso will transition from a horizontal position to a relatively more vertical position.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Based on your preference, it is up to you whether you would like to keep your back arched throughout the entire set or whether you would like to relax your arch at the end of a rep’s lowering phase before re-arching your back when commencing the next rep.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Stand under a very stable pull-up bar that is at least slightly above what you can reach with your feet on the ground.
    1. The bar should provide enough clearance so that your feet do not touch the ground when you hang from it with straight legs.
    2. In contrast to standard pull-ups, it is inherently unsafe to use a door frame pull-up bar for a kipping pull-up; these types of bars can be dislodged from the horizontal forces imparted by swinging. A pull-up bar from a power rack or rig will be much more secure.
2. Reach up overhead toward the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width and your palms facing forward.
    1. While a slightly outside shoulder-width grip is normally appropriate, you can try out somewhat different grip widths over time to determine where you personally feel best.
3. Jump up and firmly grasp the bar to hang on it with straight elbows and straight legs as your feet are together.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your shoulder blades down, which will lift your chest slightly up.
    1. This will prevent your torso from sagging, which would leave you in a shrugged-shoulder position.
2. While keeping your elbows and knees straightened, drive through your arms to push your chest back as you simultaneously sweep your legs moderately forward.
    1. You can think of this motion as starting with you lying on your back, with your arms fully back overhead, aligned with your legs. The movement on the bar should be akin to if you lifted your arms and legs up off the ground from that position. Since you are holding onto the bar, the arm motion (which is like the start of a straight arm lat pulldown) pushes your chest backward.
    2. From this position, which is known as a hollow body, your shoulders should be mildly behind the bar as your feet are in front of it.
    3. As soon as you have achieved this position, strive to fluidly transition into the next step, utilizing the momentum you just generated.
3. Actively reverse the movement into the opposite direction as you drive through your arms to push your chest forward while swinging your legs back.
    1. Now you are achieving the arched body position, where your shoulders are slightly ahead of the bar (if your mobility permits), while your feet are behind it.
4. Swing back into the hollow body position you adopted from step 2 as you push your chest back while sweeping your legs forward.
    1. Use the momentum you have already developed to allow a more vigorous swing back than you were able to achieve during step 1.
5. As you reach the apex of step 4’s swing, explosively pull your chest up toward the bar. While bending them progressively, drive your elbows down and toward your sides until your chin is above the bar.
    1. As you begin driving your elbows to pull yourself up, you can simultaneously march your knees mildly to generate even more momentum.
    2. Swinging into this step should allow your body to move in an arc rather than straight up, as you would during a standard pull-up.
    3. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows won't actually drive downward, but having that intention helps you use your back muscles to pull yourself up.
    4. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
6. Quickly reverse the arcing movement by lowering your body in preparation to swing fluidly into step 3’s arched body position with straight elbows.
    1. Strive to transfer momentum from the descent directly into the start of the next rep.
    2. To utilize momentum most effectively, you need to strike a proper balance between being sufficiently controlled and quick. If you lower yourself slowly, you will lose momentum, but if you drop into an uncontrolled free fall, you will be unable to transfer the momentum with appropriate technique.
7. Alternate between steps 3-6 until you have completed your set and are ready to stop swinging before letting go of the bar.
    1. Fluidly moving from step 6’s descent into step 3’s arching requires fairly precise timing that can take some time to learn. If you have to learn that coordinated motor skill proficiently, you can allow yourself to settle into the dead hang position after the descent of each rep before completing each rep as a discrete cycle of steps 2-6. With repeated practice, smoothly stringing together consecutive reps can feel more natural.
## Description
This exercise can train your ability to pull your chin up above the bar efficiently using momentum generated from your hip flexors and rectus abdominis to assist your lats and biceps.## Setting up
1. Pick a pair of weight plates with your desired load.
    1. Weight plates (like cast iron plates or calibrated steel plates) that have a completely flat surface on one side are ideal.
2. Position the plates upright and together on a sturdy elevated object (e.g., plyometric box, flat bench, aerobic step on risers) or the ground.
    1. If you are using plates that have a flat surface on one side and a textured surface with a lip on the other side, have the flat surfaces of the plates facing outward.
    2. If you are using plates that have grip holes (cutouts that allow the rim of a plate to function as a handle), rotate the plates to have a portion of their solid bodies directed upward. For a pinch grip, you want to be able to grasp the solid body directly rather than having your fingers contact the grip holes.
    3. Placing them on an elevated surface can make it easier to pick the plates up with the pinch grip and set them down at the end of the set, but you can perform the same exercise from the ground.
3. While holding the plates together with one hand from the side, use your other hand to grasp the top of the plates with flat fingers.
    1. Position your thumb against one plate and your other four fingers against the other plate.
    2. Your palm should be directed down toward the ground while your elbow is straight.
4. Release your hand from the side while continuing to grip the plates with your top hand.
    1. You can now keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting by your side.
## Exercise technique
1. Stand upright to lift the plates off the elevated object or ground, and hold the plates with your working arm by your side.
    1. You can think about trying to crush the weight plates as you squeeze your thumb and your other four fingers in toward each other.
2. Continue firmly holding the plates together while standing upright until you have completed your set’s target duration.
    1. This is a static exercise, so you will simply hold the plates together without moving.
    2. Keep your fingers fully straightened as you avoid letting them curl.
    3. Keep the plates completely pressed into each other rather than letting the bottom of the plates tilt away from each other.
    4. If you notice that the plates begin sliding slightly, end your set and use your free hand to help you guide the plates down to the ground or an elevated surface. Be careful not to lose your grip and drop the plates, which can rapidly occur if the plates start sliding.
3. Set the plates down and repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively strengthen your hand’s intrinsic finger flexors while also training your forearm’s three extrinsic finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Load an adjustable EZ bar with your desired weight, or approach a fixed-weight EZ bar.
    1. Either type of EZ bar can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down and firmly grasp it with your palms angled backward and in toward each other (resulting in a semi-overhand grip). Your hands should be about shoulder-width apart or mildly closer.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a shoulder-width or mildly closer semi-overhand grip is accessible on the bar.
    2. The proper hand placement should result in your wrists and elbows feeling comfortable during this exercise.
3. Stand upright with the bar, walk over to a flat exercise bench, and stand over one end of the bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space behind you. With this equipment, you would straddle the bench and walk forward toward the J-hooks.
    2. With a portable exercise bench, your back should be facing the head of the bench, like when you would bench press.
4. Lift the bar up slightly above your waist, and sit down on the bench with the bar on your lap.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
5. While keeping your elbows bent and the bar close to your torso, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench. Your head should be right by the end of the bench.
    1. As you transition from sitting to lying down, you optionally can march your thighs up against the bar to help reposition yourself. This can be helpful when using heavier loads.
    2. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent. You will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
6. In a bench pressing motion, press the bar up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a bench press rep), the bar should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    2. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar hard will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the bar in a controlled arcing manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Based on what feels most natural and comfortable to you, you can allow your elbows to flare out to your sides to a degree, or you can keep your elbows tucked in.
    2. Avoid letting your elbows drift back and down with the bar through shoulder motion. When viewed from the side, the proper elbow position should result in your upper arms remaining in a vertical position.
    3. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
2. Finish lowering the bar just before it reaches your forehead.
    1. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way, especially when the bar approaches your head.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the bar into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is vertically above your shoulders.
    2. Continue keeping your upper arms in a vertical position as you avoid pushing your elbows forward (toward your chest) through shoulder motion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the bar toward your lower chest or upper abdomen like you would during a bench press.
6. March your thighs up toward the weight, and then rock forward to sit up with the weight on your upper thighs.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, straddle the machine by standing on the foot plates as you are facing toward the weights.
    1. This variation specifically uses a T-bar row machine without any chest support, and other exercises using chest-supported T-bar row machines are included in this database.
    2. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
    3. The foot plates of most machines will require you to adopt a close to hip-width stance.
    4. Position your feet in a manner that places the machine’s handles just in front of you so that you do not have to reach far forward in order to grasp the handles initially.
2. With your arms pointing down toward the handles, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handles. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
3. Firmly grasp the parallel handles with a neutral grip so your palms face each other.
    1. Different T-bar row machines have different handle designs, which can affect the specific grip you can use.
    2. If your machine lacks parallel handles but has handles that angle diagonally backward and outward, you can select a different over T-bar variation from this database, such as the bent-over semi-neutral grip T-bar row.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from now on.
5. While keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and elbows straight, drive through your legs to lift the bar slightly up and assume a slightly more upright torso position.
    1. The objective is to use your hip and thigh muscles to lift the bar just high enough that you can experience resistance from the weight when your elbows are fully straight in the starting position of each rep.
    2. In this position, your torso should still be meaningfully inclined closer to horizontal than vertical, with your hips mostly bent.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides with this neutral grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar to its initial position by bending your knees and hips to lower the bar.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. If you have access to one, you can also use a fixed-weight straight barbell, which may be lighter than a typical 20kg (44lb) barbell that has sleeves on which to add weight plates.
2. Pick up the barbell with an underhand grip where your palms are facing forward away from you.
3. While holding the barbell, sit down on a flat exercise bench or chair and position your forearms on top of your thighs with your palms facing up and your wrists situated slightly in front of your knees.
    1. To achieve this position, you will be leaning forward at your hips when seated with your elbows bent.
4. Allow the force from the bar to bend your wrists and bring your hands downward such that your palms are facing forward.
    1. In this position, the bar should not be contacting your legs, so scoot your forearms forward if it is initially contacting your legs.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by curling your wrists through their available range of motion while keeping your forearms resting on your thighs.
    1. All motion should come from your wrists.
    2. About halfway through the lifting phase, your palms should be facing straight up when your hands are aligned with your forearms.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, when the bar is fully raised, your palms should be angled back toward you.
    4. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the palm side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed forward away from you.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit down on the seat.
2. Adjust the height of the machine’s thigh pad to a level that allows you to fit the lowest portion of your thigh just underneath it, while the front half of your foot is situated on the foot plate.
    1. To help find this point, you can initially place the front half of your foot on the foot plate while your heel is lower than your toes.
    2. Avoid setting the pad to a height that requires you to lift your heel up higher than your toes in order for your thigh to press snugly into the pad. The weakest portion of a calf raise occurs when your heel is fully raised. Consequently, it can be difficult to re-rack the weight at the end of a set if your calf is fatigued, and you have to raise your heel meaningfully before adjusting the machine’s stopper to accept the weight.
3. With the front half of your foot on the foot plate, scoot your knee under and in front of the thigh pad until your lower thigh is securely under the pad.
    1. You can optionally use your hands to help pull the thigh pad up as you scoot forward.
    2. Because this is a single leg variation, you can leave your other foot resting on the ground. All the instructions will apply to the leg on the foot plate, since that is the side you are about to train.
4. Raise your heel up just enough to disengage the machine’s stopper by operating its lever with one of your hands.
    1. You should be primarily bearing weight through the ball of your foot as the back half of your foot is hanging off the foot plate.
    2. For tracking purposes, you can consider your first full rep to start after you have completely lowered your heel when performing step 1 of the exercise technique for the first time in a set. The initial short range of motion heel raise you perform to disengage the stopper does not constitute a full rep.
## Exercise technique
1. In a controlled manner, allow your heel to descend toward the ground through the available range of motion of your ankle while continuing to keep the front half of your foot on top of the foot plate.
    1. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf and/or ankle at the bottom position.
    2. Specifically how far you are able to lower your heel will be dependent on your mobility, but individuals with normal ankle mobility can position their heels lower than the balls of their feet at the bottom.
2. Push through the ball of your foot to raise your heel into the air through the available range of motion of your ankle.
    1. At the top, your heel should be higher than the ball of your foot.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. At times during a set, you may feel that your foot slides back on the foot plate. If that occurs, you can use your hands to pull up the bottom of the thigh pad to make it easier for you to re-position your foot forward on the foot plate.
4. After finishing the lowering phase of your rep, raise your heel high enough to engage the machine’s stopper with your hand.
    1. If you performed as many reps as you possibly could during your set, you may have to rest in the bottom position for several seconds until your calf is ready to lift your heel high enough to engage the stopper.
5. With the stopper engaged, lower your heel to let the weight rest on the stopper before repeating the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your soleus, but it will not train your gastrocnemius nearly as well as calf raise variations performed with straight knees.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells by your thighs with straight elbows as your palms face your thighs.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hand while holding them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
    3. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    4. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    5. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back flat, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion to lift the dumbbells upward while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. Throughout the exercise, look straight ahead to keep your neck near a neutral position.
    2. As a cue, you can think about driving your shoulders up toward your earlobes.
    3. While you shrug your shoulders upward, the rest of your body should remain stationary as you continue standing upright with straight legs.
3. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend fully in a controlled manner to lower the dumbbells.
    1. At the bottom, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the dumbbells can provide.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the dumbbells abruptly yank you downward.
4. Alternate between steps 2-3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back flat rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. To set up for this row, you can use an underhand grip and perform the lifting phase of a conventional deadlift, followed by the lowering phase of a Romanian deadlift.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the middle of your feet under the middle of the bar.
3. With your arms pointing down toward the bar, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can reach the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up throughout the exercise.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, as when you are looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width underhand grip such that your palms are facing forward.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced from this point on.
6. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. While motion occurs at your hips and knees, your back should remain rigid.
7. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as your hamstring flexibility permits with a flat back.
    1. To avoid rounding your lower back, you should think about sitting back rather than bending over.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something. 
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical (or close to vertical) throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively pushing your knees forward depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    5. If you have the mobility to hinge until the plates touch the ground, hinge at your hips until the plates are just above the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar up toward your abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. As you do so, keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your lower body should remain nearly stationary.
    3. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. Rather than flaring your elbows out away from your sides, keep them tucked fairly close to your sides as you lift the bar.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your arms are directed down toward the ground with extended elbows.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
    2. The plates should not touch the ground when lowering the bar.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the floor by bending your knees further.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat height to a comfortable position that will allow the lower aspect of your triceps to rest against the machine’s upper arm pads.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, and sit down on its seat with an upright posture, your feet flat on the floor, and your back against the backrest.
3. After positioning your triceps against the machine’s upper arm pads, grasp the handles with an underhand grip that results in your palms facing forward.
## Exercise technique
1. Starting from a position where your elbows are straight, curl the handles upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms pinned against the machine’s arm pads (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully bent, reverse the motion by lowering the handles in a controlled manner until your elbows have straightened.
    1. Continue to keep your upper arms in contact with the arm pads.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable to approximately hip height, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Based on your preference, either connect a rope attachment to the cable or leave the cable without any attachment.
    1. Either option allows you to perform this exercise with essentially the same technique.
3. Stand facing the cable station with the cable just in front of one of your arms.
4. With the hand in front of the cable, firmly grasp the rope or the ball-shaped stopper with a neutral grip such that your palm is facing in toward your other side.
    1. If you decide to use the rope attachment, pull the rope to one side of the attachment and grip its end with your little finger and the adjacent aspect of your hand contacting the flat surface of the attachment’s rubber stopper as your thumb wraps over the outside of your other fingers.
    2. If you decide not to use any cable attachment, grasp the ball-shaped stopper at the end of the cable in the palm of your hand by wrapping your four smaller fingers around it and positioning your thumb on top of these fingers.
5. Take a step backward so that you feel tension from the cable as the weight stack separates.
6. As you keep your upper arm aligned with your torso, hinge at your hips to sit your buttocks back, and incline your torso so that it is fairly close to being in a horizontal position.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can place your free hand up against the cable station’s beam for additional stability.
    2. Maintain this bent-over-torso position throughout your set.
7. Drive your elbow backward and upward until your upper arm is approximately parallel to the ground while you allow tension from the cable to keep your elbow bent.
    1. Your elbow should now be somewhat above the side of your torso (which would look like behind your torso if you were standing upright) rather than being aligned with your torso.
    2. In this starting position, your elbow should be bent by at least 90 degrees.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arm parallel to the ground, progressively straighten your elbow to drive the cable backward and upward in an arcing motion until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. As you move your elbow, you should keep the rest of your body stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to bend in a controlled manner as you bring the cable downward and forward in an arcing fashion until you have returned to the starting position with your elbow bent by at least 90 degrees.
    1. Allow your elbow to continue bending as long as you feel tension from the cable pulling you toward a greater degree of bending.
    2. Continue keeping your upper arm parallel to the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise trains all three heads of your triceps, which together constitute the majority of muscle mass in your upper arms.## Setting up
1. Connect a lat pulldown bar to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. If you do not have a standard lat pulldown bar attachment, which has a straight body and mildly angled ends, you can use a different attachment that lets you grasp it with an overhand grip.
2. While facing the bar and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms face forward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
3. While holding the bar, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
4. In the seated position, slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight backward lean throughout your set.
    2. From this position, your elbows should be straight, with your arms overhead and slightly forward (rather than completely vertical), and you should feel tension from the cable station holding your arms in this position.
    3. If you notice the cable remains slack in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), widening out your grip may be needed to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar down toward your upper chest by driving your elbows down toward your sides as you progressively bend them.
    1. Simultaneosuly pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. Maintain the slight backward lean of your torso you adopted while setting up, rather than leaning further backward.
    3. When pulling through your full range of motion, you should finish with the bar touching your upper chest or very close to doing so.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the bar in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a bench with your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides with slightly bent elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
    1. You can opt to sit down on a flat bench without any back support or sit down on a seated bench with back support.
2. Assume a bent-over torso position by flexing forward at your hips and back until your torso is close to parallel with the ground.
    1. How far you are able to bend over will be affected by your mobility.
    2. If your mobility permits, you can bend over until your torso is parallel with the ground, but you can still effectively perform this exercise if you are not able to descend as far.
    3. While bent over, your arms should be approximately perpendicular to the ground with the dumbbells situated in front of your knees.
    4. Maintain this bent-over position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the dumbbells away from each other and straight out to your sides until your arms and torso form a “T-shape.”
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells toward each other in a controlled manner back until they have returned to their starting position in front of your knees.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand centered between its handles in a shoulder-width stance with your back facing the machine.
2. With extended elbows and your arms positioned by your sides, hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handles.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
3. Firmly grasp each handle with a neutral grip such that your palms are facing in toward each other.
4. By extending your hips and knees, stand upright with the handles while continuing to keep your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the handles upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the handles.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Stand upright with your back against a wall.
2. While keeping your buttocks and back against the wall, walk your feet forward before straightening your knees with your soles flat on the ground.
    1. As you walk your feet farther forward, the difficulty of the exercise can increase, so you can select your positioning based on the challenge you wish to have, which may take some trial and error.
    2. At any point during the exercise, you can walk your feet closer toward the wall or farther away from it if you wish to adjust the difficulty.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your heels on the ground, lift up the rest of your feet off the ground through your available ankle range of motion.
    1. As only your ankles move, continue keeping your knees straight while your buttocks and back remain in contact with the wall.
    2. You will likely feel the muscles on the front of your shins engage while doing so.
    3. With the balls of your feet lifting up rather than your heels, this is the opposite movement of a calf raise.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering your feet in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with the soles of your feet fully contacting the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train the four muscle ankle dorsiflexor muscles on the front of your lower leg, the largest of which is the tibialis anterior.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a low incline angle (e.g., near 30 degrees) and set a pair of dumbbells under the head of the bench.
    1. As a different variation, which you can use if your bench does not have a low incline setting, you can alternatively select a moderate incline angle (e.g., near 45 degrees) and otherwise use the same technique for this exercise.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
3. Grasp the dumbbells and position your arms so that they are perpendicular to the ground with extended elbows.
    1. In this position, you can direct your palms to face whichever direction feels most comfortable to you.
    2. Most individuals will feel comfortable when positioning their palms such that they face in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip), backward (i.e., an overhand grip), or at some angle in between these two positions.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your shoulder blades backward and toward each other as far as you can while you continue to keep your elbows extended and your chest in contact with the bench.
    1. This will lift the dumbbells upward.
    2. As a cue, you can imagine pinching a pencil between your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulder blades to move forward and spread apart from each other as far as they can while you continue to keep your elbows extended and your chest in contact with the bench.
    1. While doing so, the dumbbells will descend toward the ground.
    2. At the end of this motion, your shoulder girdles should resemble a slouched posture as they are pulled forward beyond their typical resting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains your rhomboids and all three regions of your trapezius in a more isolated manner than row variations do.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired weight onto the machine, adjust the seat or thigh pad height to a level that allows you to position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Certain machine models have an adjustable seat, while others have an adjustable thigh pad, and some have both an adjustable seat and an adjustable thigh pad.
    2. Depending on the specific model, plate-loaded lat pulldown machines may have the thigh pads positioned so you face toward or away from the weights, and these models often include a chest pad.
    3. While exceptions exist, the machines you sit facing away from the weights tend to have handles angled in a way that makes an overhand grip uncomfortable and awkward to use, whereas those facing the weights are typically preferable for this variation.
2. While facing the thigh pads and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with an overhand grip, with your palms facing forward and away from you.
    1. The grip width you can use will depend on the specific machine model you use, and you can feel free to select whichever grip width you prefer within the range your machine supports.
    2. Situating your hands slightly wider than shoulder-width apart, or even wider, will typically be most comfortable with this overhand grip variation.
3. While holding the handles, sit down on the machine’s seat, with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the thigh pad, then slightly lean your torso backward by moving your hips.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to extend in a controlled manner and your arms to raise up overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), lowering the seat height or widening out your grip may be needed to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down toward the sides of your torso while progressively bending them to pull the handles downward.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. Maintain the same slight backward lean of your torso that you adopted while setting up, rather than progressively leaning further backward.
    3. Allow your elbows to naturally flare out from the midline of your body, in a manner that feels comfortable to you, during the initial portion of the pull.
    4. Pull through your available range of motion until you can no longer drive your elbows closer toward your torso, which often results in the handles finishing below the height of your ears and a little higher than the top of your shoulders.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the handles in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to be near chest height, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Connect to the cable a rope attachment that is ideally a long rope attachment, or two standard rope attachments that are each fully pulled to one side of the attachment.
    1. Compared to using one standard-length rope attachment, using a long rope attachment or two standard rope attachments (that together replicate the length of a long rope attachment) can help you emphasize shoulder external rotation at the end of this exercise’s lifting phase. However, this exercise can still be effectively performed with a single standard-length rope attachment, so you do not need to use a long rope or a combination of two standard ropes.
    2. In addition to the attachments that are just longer versions of the standard rope attachments, long rope attachments functionally include the long strap attachments (that can be used for cable crunches) that have a pair of looped ends into which you can place your hands.
3. While facing the cable station, grasp each end of the rope with your index fingers and adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the rounded outer aspects of the stoppers.
    1. If you are using a long strap attachment with looped ends rather than ropes, you naturally will situate your hands within the loops and grasp the inner part of each loop.
4. With your elbows straightened and arms raised to be parallel with the cable, step backward until the machine’s weight plates separate and you feel tension from the cable.
    1. In this position, your palms should be directed down as your arms are outstretched in front of your torso while you are standing upright with your hands close to each other.
5. With your feet near hip width apart, slightly bend your knees, and adopt a staggered stance with one foot mildly in front of the other, or keep your feet symmetrical with each other.
    1. Utilize whichever stance helps you feel the most stable and balanced.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows backward while flaring them out away from your sides. Simultaneously pull your hands upward and apart from each other as you pull the cable up toward your eyes.
    1. At the end of this motion, your elbows should be pointing straight out to your sides, and your upper arms should be approximately parallel with the ground as they form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. The end position should resemble a double biceps flexing pose with your hands pulled as far backward as your elbows, as opposed to your hands remaining in front of your elbows like they would during a row.
    3. Finish pulling with your shoulder blades fully pulled back together and your chest up in a “proud chest” posture.
    4. If you are using a long rope attachment or two ropes together, strive to finish with your hands vertically above your elbows with your elbows bent by about 90 degrees. With a single standard rope attachment, you will not be able to spread your hands as far apart and will finish the pull with a greater degree of elbow bend.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your hands to move diagonally forward, downward, and in toward each other. Simultaneously, allow your elbows to move forward toward each other as they straighten.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally and move forward as your arms move in line with the cable’s resistance.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms outstretched in front of you.
    3. You do not have to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control it the entire time rather than letting the cable abruptly pull your arms forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also meaningfully training your rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, this exercise can effectively develop your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, along with the supraspinatus to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. If using an adjustable bench, position it at a moderate incline angle, which is near 45 degrees.
    1. The available incline angles will vary among different benches, with some benches having several angle configurations, while others may only be set to flat, upright, or a 45-degree angle.
    2. You can also use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on the incline bench with your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
    1. Keep your upper back and the back of your head in contact with the bench throughout the set.
3. Direct your palms so that they are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
    1. Maintain this neutral grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl both dumbbells upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. You can initially set the bar up on the floor or from an elevated position, such as from a power rack.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with extended elbows.
3. Grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder width grip and an underhand grip such that your palms are directed forward.
4. While keeping your elbows extended, stand upright while gripping the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward by bending at your elbows while simultaneously driving your upper arms back behind your torso.
    1. The combined motion at your shoulders and elbows should result in the bar moving vertically upward in a straight line (as opposed to the arcing bar path that occurs with a typical barbell biceps curl).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed and your upper arms are positioned behind your torso, reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by simultaneously bringing your upper arms forward and extending your elbows.
    1. After finishing the lower phase of this exercise, your elbows should have returned to a fully extended position, and your upper arms should once again be positioned at your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the bar up and lowering it down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a flat exercise bench or chair.
2. While holding the dumbbells, lean forward at your hips and position your forearms on top of your thighs with your palms facing upward and your wrists situated slightly in front of your knees.
3. Allow the force from the dumbbells to bend your wrists and bring your hands downward such that your palms are facing forward.
    1. In this position, the dumbbells should not be contacting your legs, so scoot your forearms forward if they are initially contacting your legs.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the dumbbells upward by curling your wrists through their available range of motion while keeping your forearms resting on your thighs.
    1. All motion should come from your wrists.
    2. About halfway through the lifting phase, your palms should be facing straight up when your hands are aligned with your forearms.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, when the dumbbells are fully raised, your palms should be angled back toward you.
    4. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the palm side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed forward away from you.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The initial steps of the setup are the same as a conventional deadlift.
2. Stand close to the middle of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance with straight elbows.
    1. You can set up with the bar approximately over the middle of your feet, which should situate the bar close to one inch or 2.5cm away from most individuals’ shins while initially standing upright.
3. Hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. Throughout this movement, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
6. Stand upright with the bar by extending your hips and knees while continuing to keep your elbows straight and your lower back near a neutral position.
    1. While your lower back muscles will be working to maintain a rigid trunk position, lifting the bar upward should chiefly occur from your hip and thigh muscles extending your hips and knees.
    2. Keep the bar very close to your legs as you lift the bar rather than allowing the bar to drift forward.
7. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso through the range of motion you can move without rounding your back.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight, back flat, and core braced.
    2. Allow your knees to bend naturally while sitting your hips back as if you were closing a car door with your buttocks, while your hands are carrying something.
    3. How close to horizontal you are able to position your torso without rounding your lower back will be dependent on your mobility.
    4. If you can hinge low enough that the plates touch the ground, adopt a slightly shallower hinged position to keep the plates off the ground.
    5. In the hinged position, it is natural to feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings.
## Exercise technique
1. Explosively pull the bar up toward your abdomen by driving your elbows backward and upward. Simultaneously generate momentum from your lower body by letting your hips snap forward.
    1. Rather than focusing on driving the hips forward, focusing on pulling the bar toward your abdomen while allowing your lower body to assist naturally is often a viable strategy. Using your lower body to build momentum is more mechanically efficient than keeping your lower body completely stationary with a strict row. Without consciously thinking about your lower body, you naturally may be able to “cheat” appropriately when emphasizing the outcome (moving the bar to your abdomen) while letting your hips move freely.
    2. Snapping your hips forward will lift your torso into a more upright position, but your torso should still be somewhat inclined at the top rather than completely upright like you would be when fully thrusting your hips forward to lock out a deadlift. An appropriate degree of torso lean at the top of the row should allow you to finish with your elbows at least slightly behind your torso while the bar touches your lower abdomen. This will not be feasible if you lift your torso excessively upright.
    3. Keep your back flat and core braced throughout your set. When your torso transitions to a moderately more upright position through hip motion, your spine should stay rigid.
    4. Your shoulder blades should naturally pull back to allow you to finish the row with your chest up.
    5. A sweet spot exists in terms of how much “cheating” is appropriate. If you do not feel meaningful tension in the back muscles you normally do during a strict row, you may be using excessive momentum from your lower body. When initially learning this exercise, it can be helpful to start with only mild assistance from your lower body to ensure that you are still training your back muscles primarily.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar as you sit your hips back. Allow your elbows to move downward and forward until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. At the end of the lowering phase, you should be in the hinged position with the bar just in front of your legs and the plates above the ground.
    2. Keep your lower back tight as you avoid rounding it.
    3. It is natural to lower the bar more quickly than you would during a strict row, but strive to control the bar to the best of your ability rather than letting gravity abruptly yank the bar down. A quick lowering phase does not necessitate an uncontrolled lowering phase.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
4. Set the bar down onto the ground by reversing the technique you used to pick it up (i.e., bending at your hips and knees as opposed to just rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your abdomen contacting the ground (i.e., in the prone position) and your head lifted slightly off the ground as you maintain a tucked chin position with your face directed toward the ground
2. With extended elbows, raise your arms straight out to your sides so that they form a “T-shape” with your torso while resting on the ground.
3. Rotate your arms so that your palms face forward.
    1. If you make a thumbs-up gesture in this position, your thumb should be pointing up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your arms up off the ground through your available shoulder range of motion while maintaining the “T-shape” and keeping your chest in contact with the ground.
    1. Your shoulder mobility will affect how high you can raise your arms.
    2. If you notice your chest lifting up off the ground during this movement, that indicates that you most likely are dynamically extending your lumbar spine, which can occur as a compensation pattern instead of lifting your arm only with your shoulder girdle muscles.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering your arms in a controlled manner until your hands lightly contact the ground while maintaining the “T-shape.”
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance. Hold the kettlebells at your sides with straight elbows and your palms facing in toward your thighs.
    1. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the kettlebells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced from this point on.
3. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as your hamstring flexibility permits. Keep your back flat and chest up.
    1. To avoid rounding your lower back, you should think about sitting back rather than bending over.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something. 
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical (or close to vertical) throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the kettlebells at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively pushing your knees forward depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    5. If you have the mobility to hinge until the weights touch the ground, hinge at your hips until the weights are just above the ground.
4. With your arms hanging straight down toward the ground, direct your palms to angle inward and backward, resulting in a semi-overhand grip.
    1. The bony bump on the back of each elbow should point diagonally outward and backward, which is the direction in which you will row.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows in an arcing motion backward, upward, and moderately out away from your sides to row the kettlebells. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Aim to flare your elbows about 45 degrees away from your sides as you move them equally backward and out to the side. Zero degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows purely backward and keeping them completely tucked by your sides, while 90 degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows straight out to the sides, which would result in your upper arms and torso forming a “T-shape” at the top.
    2. Throughout the set, rigidly maintain a flat lower back with the same degree of bend in your hips and knees. All the motion should occur from your upper body.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being slightly above your sides at the top.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
    5. When allowing your elbows to bend naturally, your forearms should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    6. The kettlebells should move in an arcing motion rather than move straight up toward your shoulders.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the kettlebells in a controlled arcing motion until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Allow the weights to pull your shoulder blades apart and forward to stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. Move your elbows diagonally inward and forward at a near 45-degree angle while they descend to reverse the moderate elbow flare you just utilized during the lifting phase.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weights pull your arms down rapidly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the weights down by bending your knees further.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to pick it up with straight elbows from a position where your knees are mildly bent, and your torso is mildly hinged forward at your hips.
    1. Your individual limb lengths and the specific type of machine you are using will influence how high the bar will be in this position, but the bar will be above the knees for many people.
    2. For some people, the lowest machine setting that places the bar above their knees may be too high for them to pick up the bar with extended elbows, so they may have to situate the bar closer to knee height.
    3. You will naturally have to position the machine’s safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) below the height of the bar.
2. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand with your back facing the middle of the bar in a shoulder-width stance and hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
3. With extended elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
4. By extending your hips and knees, start standing up with the bar and rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion prior to standing fully upright with the bar.
    1. While doing, continue to keep your lower back near a neutral position.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the bar upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. If your buttock obstructs the upward movement of the bar, you can re-rack the bar and position yourself slightly farther away from the bar before resuming your set.
    3. If your buttock still obstructs the upward movement of the bar after repositioning yourself, you can proceed to use a limited range of motion for this exercise or select another shrug variation. 
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the bar.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the machine’s weight can provide.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to re-rack the bar.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each cable to its lowest height.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you can position the arms so that they form an approximately 90-degree angle with each other. This would result in the arms being midway between pointing straight forward and straight out to the side.
    2. Dual cable stations that have their two weight stacks facing each other (often with a pull-up in between them) normally lack adjustable arms.
2. Connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
3. Squat down with one cable station by your side, and firmly grasp its handle with a straight elbow as your palm is directed toward you.
4. While keeping your elbow straight, stand upright with the handle, and walk over to grasp the handle from the other cable in the same manner.
    1. You can allow the resistance from the first cable you grasped to lift your arm out to the side before doing the same with the other cable during the next step.
5. Step to the side to center yourself between the cables.
6. Step forward while allowing your arms and palms to rotate in line with the resistance from the cables. Stop moving once the cables are angled diagonally by about 30-45 degrees.
    1. When you are initially standing with your torso directly between the pulleys, the cables will exert resistance directly out to your sides. This is the 0-degree starting point from which you will move forward to angle the cables by about 30-45 degrees.
    2. As you step forward, the cables will progressively apply more of a backward-directed force.
    3. If your pulleys are situated close together, you will not have to step as far forward to achieve the intended line of pull compared to if your cable stations are positioned farther apart.
    4. On each side, the back of your hand and the bony bump on the back of your elbow should be directed toward the cable station. This will allow the cables to resist your biceps most directly.
    5. Your wrists should be mostly straight throughout the exercise.
7. While mildly bending your knees, adopt a staggered stance with one foot in front of the other as your feet are approximately hip width apart.
    1. In this position, you should feel stable and balanced.
8. As you are standing with an upright torso, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to curl the handles upward, forward, and in toward each other. Keep your upper arms stationary as you avoid swinging them forward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully bent as your forearms contact your biceps.
    2. While you move your forearms through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to straighten in a controlled manner.
    1. Finish the lowering phase with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. The handles should simultaneously spread apart from each other while arcing downward and back toward the pulleys.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cables abruptly yank the handles back in an uncontrolled manner.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest level, select your desired weight with the loading pin, and attach a bar with a straight (or mostly straight) body to the cable.
    1. Any bar that enables you to perform this exercise with an overhand grip can be similarly used, including a standard straight bar, a lat pulldown bar (with a long straight body and angled ends), and a multi-grip EZ bar.
2. While facing the cable station, reach up and grasp the bar with an overhand grip such that your palms are facing away from you.
3. Take a step backward and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight torso lean throughout your set.
4. Pull your upper arms down and back until they are positioned at your sides with your elbows close to fully bent.
    1. Maintain this upper arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Push the bar downward by extending your elbows until they are straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, the bar should be situated in front of your thighs.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows through your available range of motion to raise the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbows as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a bench with an upright posture and your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows and your palms facing in toward you (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. You can opt to sit down on a flat bench without any back support or sit down on a seated bench with back support.
    2. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    3. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    4. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back flat.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the dumbbells upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the dumbbells.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. As an alternative to a standard barbell, in which you can load plates onto its sleeves, you can similarly use a fixed-weight barbell for this exercise.
    2. If you do not have any experience training Jefferson curls, it is recommended that you initially try out the bodyweight Jefferson curl to determine how your body feels with this movement prior to performing a loaded variation.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with straight elbows.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms are directed toward you.
4. While keeping your elbows straight, stand upright with the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Tuck your chin to your chest, and lower the bar in a slow and controlled manner by bending over and rounding your whole back through your comfortable range of motion while keeping your knees and elbows straight.
    1. As a cue, you can think about rounding forward each segment of your spine successively until your entire spine has achieved a rounded posture.
    2. As you progressively descend, it is natural to feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings and back muscles.
    3. How far you can descend will be influenced by your hamstring flexibility and the available bending range of motion in your spine.
    4. With a sufficient amount of mobility, you may be able to descend until the weight contacts the floor, but only move through a range that is painless and feels right to your body.
    5. Your arms should be directed down toward the ground throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by gradually transitioning from the bent over position to the upright position in which you started by uncoiling your back, which will lift the bar up.
    1. At the end of the ascent, untuck your chin and look straight ahead.
    2. Continue keeping your knees and elbows in a straightened position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep spinal extensor muscles that run along your entire back, while also training your spinal mobility and hamstring flexibility.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that places the handles near shoulder level once you are seated.
    1. This seat height is an appropriate starting point for most people, but the ideal seat height specifically should allow you to press through your full range of motion while initially gripping the handles with your elbows stacked under the handles.
    2. If the seat is excessively high, you will not be able to position your elbows directly under the handles (when viewed from the side).
    3. If the seat is excessively low, your range of motion for this exercise will be limited.
    4. Some trial and error may be needed to find the best height for you as an individual.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the backrest.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the backrest if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
4. With your palms facing forward and elbows bent, firmly grasp the handles that are directed out to the sides. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being vertical when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably position your elbows under the handles, mildly lower the seat height before resuming your setup.
    3. Situating your elbows under the handles and maintaining this alignment will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise.
5. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling, and maintain this posture throughout your set.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles up overhead until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain stacked under your wrists when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your back snuggly against the backrest and your feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. With the appropriate seat height, you should finish the lowering phase with the handles near shoulder level, as your elbows are lower than your shoulders.
    2. At the bottom, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers underneath them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows stacked underneath the handles, resulting in your forearms remaining in a vertical position when viewed from the side.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a bench with an upright posture and your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
    1. You can opt to sit down on a flat bench without any back support or sit down on a seated bench with back support.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the dumbbells out in front of you until your upper arms are approximately parallel with the ground.
    1. If you wish to do so, elevating the dumbbells higher up, beyond when your upper arms are parallel to the floor, is a viable modification to this exercise’s technique.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner back to your sides.
3. Alternate between raising the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a bench with an upright posture and your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
    1. You can opt to sit down on a flat bench without any back support or sit down on a seated bench with back support.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing forward.
    1. Maintain this underhand grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl both dumbbells upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Place a trap bar in a location where you have sufficient space to carry it, and position its high handles up if your bar has high handles.
    1. The setup for this exercise mirrors that of a trap bar deadlift, with the lifting phase of one trap bar deadlift rep.
    2. You can certainly use a trap bar without elevated handles, but it is easier to lift into the starting position when using high handles.
2. Add weight plates to the bar, and stand within the middle of the bar with a near hip-width stance and your arms at your sides.
3. Hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handles when your elbows are straight, and your arms are lifted slightly out to your sides (to position your hands directly over the handles).
    1. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back flat and chest up, rather than meaningfully rounding your back.
4. Firmly grasp the center of the handles with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Keep your elbows straight throughout the exercise.
5. While keeping your elbows straight, press your legs into the ground with enough force to feel the trap bar’s sleeves push up into the weight plates, but not enough force to lift the bar off the ground.
    1. Applying this pre-tension to the bar will likely produce an audible “clinking” sound.
    2. The initial submaximal pull allows you to check if you have grasped the handles correctly, which will be indicated by the handles not rotating forward or backward when applying the pre-tension.
    3. If you pick up a trap bar with your hands positioned slightly away from the middle of the handles, the trap bar can start rotating in a manner that makes it very difficult to hold and lift appropriately.
    4. Even for experienced lifters, it can be easy to grasp a trap bar’s handles without having their hands truly centered initially, so applying the pre-tension can make sure you are in your ideal starting position.
    5. If the bar starts rotating when you apply the pre-tension, re-center your grip, and apply the pre-tension again until the bar no longer rotates.
6. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
7. Drive through the ground to lift the bar upward as you extend (straighten) your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright.
    1. Continue keeping your back flat as you lift the bar with your hip and thigh muscles.
## Exercise technique
1. Walk forward while holding the trap bar while maintaining an upright posture with your shoulders approximately level with each other.
    1. While you will not swing your arms like you would when walking normally, your torso and lower body should look similar to how they would when you walk normally.
    2. Walk at a pace where you feel stable and balanced throughout the carry.
    3. If you use a weight that is fairly heavy to you, it is natural to take shorter strides compared to how you typically walk.
    4. Keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level throughout your set rather than letting them drop down to one side when taking each step.
2. Continue walking with the trap bar until you have completed your set and are ready to set it down by reversing the movement you used to pick it up initially.
    1. Remember to lower the weight by hinging at your hips while bending your knees and keeping your lower back near a neutral position.
## Description
Rather than concentrating the workload on one body region, this exercise trains a variety of muscle groups throughout your body in an integrated manner, including the muscles in your forearms, upper back, core, and lower body.

### **Straps for Carries**

Even when using chalk, the weight you can use or distance you can complete with a particular weight will commonly be limited by grip strength while performing a farmer’s carry, so this carry can be a viable means of training static grip strength. However, if you wish to increase the stimulus provided to muscles besides the forearm and hand muscles, you have the option of performing this carry with lifting straps. With the heavier loads or longer distances that straps typically facilitate, the exercise can be more challenging to your upper back and core muscles as they support your trunk and shoulder girdle in addition to your lower body muscles that stabilize your pelvis and propel you forward.

### **Training Many Muscles Simultaneously**

McGill et al. (2009) measured EMG activity of several core and lower body muscles as competitive strongmen performed a variety of movements that appear in strongman competitions, including the farmer’s walk, which was performed for several steps with a 75kg (167.6lb) weight in each hand. During the farmer’s walk, peak EMG activity was high for the internal abdominal obliques, lats, thoracic erector spinae, lumbar erector spinae, gluteus medius, gluteus maximus, and rectus femoris. The external abdominal obliques and biceps femoris also exhibited moderate peak EMG activity, while the rectus abdominis exhibited low EMG activity. The abdominal muscles and rectus femoris demonstrated their peak activity during the walking portion of the carry, while the lats, erector spinae and biceps femoris demonstrated their peak activity during the initial lifting phase. The gluteal muscles exhibited peak activity during the initial lifting phase and during the first step of the carry. As indicated by these findings, the farmer’s carry can train a large amount of musculature simultaneously, which can result in this exercise being metabolically demanding when performed at a high relative intensity, especially for longer duration sets. Correspondingly, challenging sets of farmer’s carries may be able to train your cardiovascular system more robustly than most resistance training exercises that localize the workload to a smaller amount of total muscle mass.## Setting up
1. Connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and sit down facing the cable station.
    1. You can readily perform this exercise with essentially the same technique whether you use a seated cable row machine or an adjustable cable station.
    2. If you use a seated cable row machine, you naturally will sit down on its seat, but if you are using an adjustable cable station, you will sit down on the ground after adjusting the cable to a height that is approximately level with your shoulders when seated on the floor with an upright torso.
2. Slightly scoot to one side to situate the shoulder on the side you are about to train in front of the cable.
    1. As opposed to keeping the center of your chest aligned with the cable (like you would when rowing with both sides simultaneously), this asymmetrical sitting position will allow you to row straight backward in line with the cable’s resistance.
    2. Cable row machines often have pulleys that do not pivot side to side, and pulling their cables at an angle out to the side could result in excess friction, deleterious to the cables, while increasing the risk that a cable slips out of its pulley’s groove.
3. On the side you are about to train, position your foot in contact with the machine’s foot plate or the base of the adjustable cable station. Position your other foot similarly, or flat on the ground with a bent knee, based on what feels comfortable and stable to you when using your specific cable machine.
    1. Allow your knees to bend to an angle that allows you to feel stable and comfortable.
4. Lean forward at your hips until you are able to use one hand to grasp the handle firmly with an extended elbow before leaning backward until you are sitting with an upright torso.
    1. With greater knee bend, you will be able to sit closer toward the cable, so you can bend your knees further if you initially cannot reach the handles when leaning forward.
    2. When seated upright, you should feel tension from the cable, so if the cable is still slack, you can extend your knees to scoot yourself backward until the machine’s weight stack separates and you feel the resistance.
5. Based on your preference and what feels most comfortable, you can rotate the handle in a manner that allows you to use an underhand grip with your palm facing up, an overhand grip with your palm facing down, or a neutral grip with your palm facing in toward the midline of your body.
    1. People often find that the neutral grip feels most natural.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handle back toward your torso by driving your elbow backward while simultaneously bending and pulling your shoulder blade backward.
    1. Move your elbow through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping it tucked close to your side with this variation rather than notably flaring your elbow up and away from your side.
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulder, shoulder blade, and elbow, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to move forward in a controlled manner as it progressively straightens to move the handle forward.
    1. Finish with your arm fully outstretched in front of you with a straight elbow.
    2. Your shoulder blade will naturally move forward, but you have the option of exaggerating this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull it further forward than your resting position at the bottom. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you to train them more effectively.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and most of the back of your upper arms (i.e., triceps) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The proper setting will typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. After selecting your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, sit down on the seat with your feet on the ground.
3. With one arm, position the back of your upper arm and elbow on top of the arm pad as your elbow is aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates. That is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    1. For additional stability, you can use your other hand to hold onto the top of the arm pad, or you can place it wherever you prefer, so long as it does not touch the handle.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm on top of the arm pad since that is the arm you will be training first.
4. While your arm is aligned with the front of your shoulder, firmly grasp the handle with your palm angled upward.
    1. If the handle is a little out of your reach, you could slightly stand up and lean forward to reach the handle before sitting back down after you have grasped the handle.
    2. Your wrist should be mostly straight throughout your set after grasping the handles.
    3. Machines commonly have angled handles that allow you to grasp them with your palm angled inward and upward (resulting in a hybrid between an underhand and neutral grip), but certain models may have horizontal handles that will allow you to use a purely underhand grip.
5. In the starting position, your elbow should be fully straightened to allow you to train through a full range of motion.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow and the back of your upper arm in contact with the arm pad, curl the handle upward and back toward you in an arcing motion by bending at your elbow until it is fully bent.
    1. While your one forearm moves through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
2. After your elbow has fully bent, reverse the motion by lowering the handle in a slow and controlled manner until your elbow has returned to a fully straightened position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow and the back of your upper arm in contact with the arm pad.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other arm.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance. Hold the dumbbells by your sides with your palms facing in toward your thighs.
2. Lift the dumbbells up near the front of your shoulders with your elbows bent by curling the weights and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbells into position if they are too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbells mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum while bending your elbows and mildly swinging them forward to lift the weight up.
    2. Your elbows should now be underneath and slightly in front of the dumbbells.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. Press the dumbbells up overhead in a shoulder press motion until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can point the back of your elbows forward with your palms facing in toward each other.
    2. The dumbbells should be vertically stacked over your shoulder.
    3. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the dumbbells behind your head in a controlled manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Predominantly keep your elbows tucked in as they are chiefly pointing forward rather than out to your side. However, you can allow your elbows to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you. If the dumbbells bump into each other, you are flaring your elbows excessively.
    2. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
    3. As you keep your elbows up, your upper arms should remain in a vertical position by the sides of your head.
2. Continue lowering the dumbbells until you have fully bent your elbows or can lightly tap the back of your shoulders with the dumbbells.
    1. It is normal to be able to bend the elbows beyond 90 degrees, but specifically how far you can bend your elbows may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps.
    2. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach their shoulders with the dumbbells, wherever you feel a notable stretching sensation in your triceps can be an appropriate end range.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weights the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the dumbbells into the starting position overhead.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the dumbbells are vertically stacked above your shoulders.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows up as all the motion occurs from your elbows without moving your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the dumbbells toward the front of your shoulders like you would during a shoulder press.
6. Lower the dumbbells to your sides as you straighten your elbows and swing your elbows back by your sides.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Securely attach your desired weight to your wrist roller.
    1. Depending on the model of your wrist roller, you may have to use weight plates (if your model uses a loading pin), or you may be able to use weight plates, kettlebells, or dumbbells (if your model uses a strap that you can loop around the weight).
    2. Leave enough of the rope initially uncoiled so that you can stand up with the roller without the weight lifting off the ground.
2. While the weight is on the ground, firmly grasp the roller’s handles with your palms facing back toward you (i.e., an overhand grip) and the uncoiled part of the rope on the side of the roller that is farther away from you.
    1. If the uncoiled part of the rope is on the side of the roller closer to you, you would be able to train wrist flexion when using an overhand grip, but this is the wrist extension variation.
3. Stand upright and raise your arms out in front of you with straight elbows until your arms are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
    2. Your palms should now be facing down toward the ground.
4. Take up the slack from the roller’s rope with alternating twisting motions of your wrists until you feel that the rope is taut from the weight’s resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your arms held out in front of you, extend one wrist by pointing that side’s palm down toward the ground as you curl your other wrist in the opposite direction by pointing that hand’s palm up and back toward you.
    1. You should grasp the handle loosely with the hand whose palm is curling toward you, as you grasp the handle firmly with the hand whose palm is pointing toward the ground.
    2. The hand whose palm is directed down is what rotates the roller to lift the weight up via wrist extension, while the other side’s wrist is repositioned into a flexed position in preparation to perform the lifting motion through wrist extension.
2. Alternate the motions in step 1 with the opposite hands as you lift the weight up by cyclically repeating these twisting movements with your wrists until you have fully coiled the rope around the roller.
3. Lower the weight in a controlled manner by reversing the same motions you just performed until the weight lightly taps the ground or is just about to do so.
4. Repeatedly perform steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the seven wrist extensor muscles on the side of your forearm opposite to your palm.## Setting up
1. With a cable station’s pulley positioned near hip height, connect a d-handle attachment, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Position yourself to one side of the cable station while facing away from it.
3. Squat down or hinge at your hips with an extended elbow before using your closer hand to grasp the handle with an underhand grip such that your palm is directed forward.
4. While keeping your elbow extended, stand upright while gripping the handle.
5. Step forward while keeping your elbow fully extended and allowing the cable’s resistance to pull your upper arm backward at an angle behind your torso.
    1. In this position, your forearm and upper arm should form a straight line with the cable that is diagonally angled forward and upward, and you should also feel tension from the cable rather than slack being present in the cable.
    2. How far you step forward from the cable station will be based on your personal preference, since you can effectively perform this exercise with variable shoulder positions.
        1. If you step forward further, you will be able to have your upper arm pulled further behind your body, which will place your biceps into a more lengthened position.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the handle upward by bending at your elbow while keeping your upper arm fixed in the same position that allowed your elbow to be fully extended at the start of the rep (rather than allowing your upper arm to swing forward).
2. After your elbow has fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the handle in a controlled manner until your elbow has returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arm in the same position used to start the rep.
3. Alternate between curling the handle upwards and lowering it down until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While also meaningfully developing your brachialis and brachioradialis, this exercise can effectively train biceps from a notably lengthened position.## Setting up
1. Securely attach your desired weight to your wrist roller.
    1. Depending on the model of your wrist roller, you may have to use weight plates (if your model uses a loading pin), or you may be able to use weight plates, kettlebells, or dumbbells (if your model uses a strap that you can loop around the weight).
    2. Leave enough of the rope initially uncoiled so that you can stand up with the roller without the weight lifting off the ground.
2. While the weight is on the ground, firmly grasp the roller’s handles with your palms facing toward you (i.e., an overhand grip) and the uncoiled part of the rope on the side of the roller that is farther away from you.
    1. If the uncoiled part of the rope is on the side of the roller closer to you, you would be able to train wrist flexion when using an overhand grip, but this is the wrist extension variation.
3. Stand upright while grasping the roller and bend your elbows by approximately 90 degrees while keeping your upper arms tucked by your sides.
    1. You will maintain this bent elbow position throughout your set.
4. Take up the slack from the roller’s rope with alternating twisting motions of your wrists until you feel that the rope is taut from the weight’s resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows bent, extend one wrist by pointing that side’s palm down toward the ground as you curl your other wrist in the opposite direction by pointing that hand’s palm up and back toward you.
    1. You should grasp the handle loosely with the hand whose palm is curling toward you, as you grasp the handle firmly with the hand whose palm is pointing toward the ground.
    2. The hand whose palm is directed down is what rotates the roller to lift the weight up via wrist extension, while the other side’s wrist is repositioned into a flexed position in preparation to perform the lifting motion through wrist extension.
2. Alternate the motions in step 1 with the opposite hands as you lift the weight up by cyclically repeating these twisting movements with your wrists until you have fully coiled the rope around the roller.
3. Lower the weight in a controlled manner by reversing the same motions you just performed until the weight lightly taps the ground or is just about to do so.
4. Repeatedly perform steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the seven wrist extensor muscles on the side of your forearm opposite to your palm.## Setting up
1. Load a bar with your desired weight, and firmly grasp it with a near shoulder-width overhand grip that results in your palms facing back toward you
    1. In addition to a standard barbell to which you can add weight plates, you can also utilize a fixed-weight bar, which can be useful if you wish to use a load that is lighter than 45lb (20kg), which is the typical weight of an unloaded standard barbell.
2. Stand upright with the bar, walk over to a flat exercise bench, and stand over one end of the bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space behind you. With this equipment, you would straddle the bench and walk forward toward the J-hooks.
    2. With a portable exercise bench, your back should be facing the head of the bench, like when you would bench press.
3. Lift the bar up slightly above your waist, and sit down on the bench with the bar on your lap.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
4. While keeping your elbows bent and the bar close to your torso, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench. Your head should be right by the end of the bench.
    1. As you transition from sitting to lying down, you optionally can march your thighs up against the bar to help reposition yourself. This can be helpful when using heavier loads.
    2. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent. You will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
5. In a bench pressing motion, press the bar up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a bench press rep), the bar should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    2. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar hard will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the bar in a controlled arcing manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Based on what feels most natural and comfortable to you, you can allow your elbows to flare out to your sides to a degree, or you can keep your elbows tucked in.
    2. Avoid letting your elbows drift back and down with the bar through shoulder motion. When viewed from the side, the proper elbow position should result in your upper arms remaining in a vertical position.
    3. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
2. Finish lowering the bar just before it reaches your forehead.
    1. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way, especially when the bar approaches your head.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the bar into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is vertically above your shoulders.
    2. Continue keeping your upper arms in a vertical position as you avoid pushing your elbows forward (toward your chest) through shoulder motion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the bar toward your lower chest or upper abdomen like you would during a bench press.
6. March your thighs up toward the weight, and then rock forward to sit up with the weight on your upper thighs.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance. Hold the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows and your palms facing in toward your thighs.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced from this point on.
3. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as your hamstring flexibility permits. Keep your back flat and chest up.
    1. To avoid rounding your lower back, you should think about sitting back rather than bending over.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical (or close to vertical) throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively pushing your knees forward depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    5. If you have the mobility to hinge until the weights touch the ground, hinge at your hips until the weights are just above the ground.
4. With your arms hanging straight down toward the ground, direct your palms to angle inward and backward, resulting in a semi-overhand grip.
    1. The bony bump on the back of each elbow should point diagonally outward and backward, which is the direction in which you will row.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows in an arcing motion backward, upward, and moderately out away from your sides to row the dumbbells. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Aim to flare your elbows about 45 degrees away from your sides as you move them equally backward and out to the side. Zero degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows purely backward and keeping them completely tucked by your sides, while 90 degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows straight out to the sides, which would result in your upper arms and torso forming a “T-shape” at the top.
    2. Throughout the set, rigidly maintain a flat lower back with the same degree of bend in your hips and knees. All the motion should occur from your upper body.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being slightly above your sides at the top.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
    5. When allowing your elbows to bend naturally, your forearms should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    6. The dumbbells should move in an arcing motion rather than move straight up toward your shoulders.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled arcing motion until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Allow the weights to pull your shoulder blades apart and forward to stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. Move your elbows diagonally inward and forward at a near 45-degree angle while they descend to reverse the moderate elbow flare you just utilized during the lifting phase.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weights pull your arms down rapidly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the weights down by bending your knees further.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Use the machine’s loading pin to select your desired level of assistance, and ensure that the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is positioned down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you select heavier loads from the assisted pull-up machine since the weight determines the extent to which the machine will support your bodyweight.
    2. Certain machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but essentially the same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates and reach up overhead to grasp the machine’s handles firmly with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider apart.
    1. If your machine has multiple handles, you can specifically select the handles that are most comfortable for you to grasp with your palms facing forward, which will typically be the outer handles on machines with multiple handles.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
3. One leg at a time, kneel onto the machine’s knee pad (if your machine has a knee pad) or step onto the machine’s foot bar (if your machine has a foot bar) while continuing to grasp the handles firmly.
    1. If using a machine with a knee pad, you will keep your knees bent on the pad throughout your set, but if you are using a machine with a foot bar, you will keep your knees straight while standing on the bar throughout your set.
    2. Certain machines with knee pads may not allow shorter individuals to pull through a full range of motion when kneeling on the pad, so you can switch to standing on the knee pad if the machine does not allow you to pull yourself fully up when kneeling on the pad.
4. Allow your body to descend in a controlled manner until your arms are fully overhead with straight elbows.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will place you in a more advantageous position to start your pull compared to if you kept your chest facing exclusively horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up from the force of your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back places you into a position akin to a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the handles by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the handles are fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. If you are using a machine with a knee pad and you notice the machine restricts your range of motion when kneeling on the pad, switch to standing on the pad before resuming your set to pull through your full range of motion.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a low incline angle (e.g., near 30 degrees) and set a pair of dumbbells under the head of the bench.
    1. You can alternatively set your bench at a moderate incline (e.g., near 45 degrees) if your bench lacks a low incline setting, or if you personally prefer to use a mildly greater incline.
    2. The dumbbells may be easiest to grasp while lying on the bench if you initially place one end of each dumbbell on top of the bench’s horizontal base leg (which is where wheels may be located) and the other end of each dumbbell on the floor closer to where you will be on the bench.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
3. Firmly grasp one dumbbell at a time, and angle your palms in toward each other while your arms hang down with straight elbows.
    1. The bony bump on the back of each elbow should be pointing backward, which is the direction in which you will row.
    2. Maintain this neutral grip throughout your set.
    3. To row through a full range of motion, the dumbbells should not contact the ground or the base of the bench, which may be more challenging to avoid for individuals with longer arms.
    4. To create greater clearance between the dumbbells and the ground, you can scoot further up toward the head of the bench.
    5. If it is difficult for you to grasp the dumbbells, which may be more likely if you have shorter arms, you can initially place the dumbbells closer to the bench’s seat before setting up with your torso scooted further down on the bench to situate your hands closer to the floor. After grasping the dumbbells, you can then press your feet into the ground to scoot further up on the bench so that your head can hang off the bench rather than having your face pressed into the head of the bench.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows backward and upward in an arcing motion to pull the dumbbells toward your hip. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your elbows tucked by your sides, rather than flaring them outward.
    2. Progressively bend your elbows to the extent that keeps your forearms close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    3. Keep your torso in contact with the bench throughout the set rather than arching your lower back to lift your chest up off the bench.
    4. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    5. While performing this arcing motion, the dumbbells should not move straight upward in a vertical path.
    6. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled arcing forward motion until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Allow the weights to pull your shoulder blades apart to stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weights rapidly pull your arms down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to place the dumbbells back onto the ground.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Pick up one kettlebell, and hold it directly next to your chest with bent elbows as your upper arms are slightly in front of your sides (i.e., in the goblet position).
    1. There are multiple ways in which you transfer the kettlebell to this position based on your preference.
    2. One such way is to tilt a kettlebell onto its side with the bottom of its base facing you, firmly grasp its horns (i.e., the corner handles) with the middle handle facing away from you, stand upright, and curl the kettlebell toward your chest.
    3. With a heavier weight, you can alternatively pick it up by its middle handle in a deadlift motion before setting it on top of a flat exercise bench with its handle facing up. Then you can squat down until the weight is right in front of your chest, firmly grasp it by the horns, and squat up with it close to your chest.
2. With your back facing the head of the position, sit down on a flat exercise bench while keeping the kettlebell close to your chest.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
3. Lean back to lie down on the bench while marching your thighs up to help when transitioning. Continue keeping the kettlebell close to your torso with your elbows by your sides.
    1. Let your elbows lower toward the ground when leaning back, which will allow the kettlebell to move slightly further away from your head compared to when you were sitting upright. When sitting upright, this would be the same motion as moving your elbows back slightly back toward your ribcage.
    2. Once you are lying down, return your feet to the ground in a stable position with your knees bent.
4. In a bench pressing motion, press the kettlebell up until your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling with straight elbows.
    1. In this starting position, you should be holding the kettlebell's horns with mostly straight wrists as the bottom of the kettlebell’s base is directed forward (away from your head).
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the kettlebell’s handle toward your head in a controlled arcing manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Aim to keep your wrists straight throughout the set, which should result in the bottom of the kettlebell’s base angling upward and its handle angling downward while lowering the weight.
    2. Based on what feels most natural and comfortable, you can keep your elbows tucked in, or you can allow your elbows to flare out to your sides slightly.
    3. Movement should chiefly occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows as you strive to avoid letting your elbows drift back and down with the kettlebells through shoulder motion. When viewed from the side, your elbows should remain aligned over your shoulders rather than moving behind (closer to your head) them.
    4. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
2. Finish lowering the kettlebell once its handle is just above your forehead.
    1. Most lifters will be able to finish with their elbows bent by approximately 90 degrees.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way, particularly as it approaches your head.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the kettlebell into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. Continue keeping your upper arms in a vertical position as you avoid pushing your elbows forward (toward your chest) through shoulder motion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, lower your elbows toward your sides while bending them to bring the kettlebell toward your chest.
    2. March your thighs up before rocking forward to sit upright while keeping the kettlebell close to your chest.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the entirety of your triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. With its heavier side facing backward, firmly grasp an asymmetrical weighted implement (or a viable alternative) by its lighter end at a position that can provide your desired level of resistance.
    1. Asymmetrical weighted implements include adjustable dumbbells with plates loaded only on one side, hammers, and end-loaded baseball bats.
    2. As an alternative to an asymmetrical weighted implement, you can grasp certain symmetrically weighted cylindrical implements closer toward one end, which can provide the same training effect. Examples include a fixed-weight straight bar, a fixed-weight EZ bar, an adjustable EZ bar, and a cable straight bar attachment (like a lat pulldown bar).
    3. The resistance your forearm muscles experience during this exercise will be dictated by how closely to one end you grasp the implement.
    4. For greater resistance, you can grasp farther away from the weighted end with an asymmetrically loaded implement or closer to whichever end you are holding with a symmetrically loaded implement.
    5. When performing this exercise for the first time with a particular implement, you may have to try out different grip positions to determine specifically where you should grasp the implement to provide the magnitude of resistance you wish to have.
2. Hold the implement by your side with your palm facing in toward your side and your elbow straight. Based on your preference, you can stand or sit down with an upright torso.
    1. Hold the implement with your thumb bent and wrapped around your other fingers, like the standard grip for many exercises.
## Exercise technique
1. Allow the implement’s heavier end to tilt down through your wrist’s comfortable range of motion to assume the starting position of the exercise.
2. Tilt your hand back toward the little finger-side of your forearm (which is facing backward) through your wrist’s full range of motion.
    1. This will angle your knuckles at least mildly backward.
    2. The part of the lighter part of the implement in front of your thumb and index finger should now be angled diagonally downward and forward.
    3. Similarly, the heavier part of the implement behind your little finger should now be angled diagonally upward and backward.
    4. How far you can tilt your hand will be affected by your wrist mobility, and this exercise does not involve a particularly long range of motion.
    5. While you move your hand through wrist motion, keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the exercise.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your hand to tilt toward the thumb-side of your forearm through your wrist’s comfortable range of motion.
    1. This will angle your knuckles at least slightly forward.
    2. The lighter part of the implement in front of your thumb and index finger should now be angled at least slightly upward.
    3. On the other hand, the heavier part of the implement behind your little finger should now be angled at least slightly downward.
    4. There is no need to perform this extremely slowly, but you should actively control the movement rather than letting the weight swing your hand.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your forearm’s flexor carpi ulnaris and extensor carpi ulnaris.## Setting up
1. Connect an attachment with a straight bar shaft to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and sit down facing the cable station.
    1. You can use any attachment that allows you to hold it comfortably with an overhand grip.
    2. The most commonly available attachment appropriate for this exercise is a standard lat pulldown bar attachment, which has a straight shaft with mildly angled ends and may be found attached to a cable lat pulldown machine.
    3. You can perform this exercise with essentially the same technique whether you use a seated cable row machine or an adjustable cable station, but a seated cable row machine is typically more stable and therefore the ideal option. 
    4. If you use a seated cable row machine, you naturally will sit down on its seat, but if you are using an adjustable cable station, you will sit down on the ground after adjusting the cable to a height that is approximately level with your shoulders when seated on the floor with an upright torso.
2. Position your feet in contact with the machine’s foot plates or the base of the adjustable cable station.
    1. Allow your knees to bend to an angle that allows you to feel stable and comfortable, which can be a mild to moderately mild knee bend for many people.
    2. With greater knee bend, you will be able to sit closer toward the cable, so you can bend your knees further if you initially cannot reach the bar when leaning forward by bending at your hip
3. Lean forward at your hips until you are able to grasp the bar firmly with straight elbows. Grasp it with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, as your palms face down.
4. While holding the bar with straight elbows, lean your torso backward at your hips until you are sitting with an upright torso.
    1. When seated upright, you should feel tension from the cable, so if the cable is still slack, you can extend your knees to scoot yourself backward until the machine’s weight stack separates and you feel the resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar back toward your torso by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Let your elbows naturally flare out away from your sides in a way that feels comfortable to you.
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion or until the bar is directly in front of your torso, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten to move the bar forward.
    1. Finish with your arms fully outstretched in front of you.
    2. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, without rounding your lower back. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you to train them more effectively.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable cable station’s pulley to its highest setting, or approach a cable lat pulldown machine.
    1. Either type of equipment can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin, and connect a straight bar attachment to the cable.
    1. As an alternative to a standard straight bar or a lat pulldown bar (with a long straight body and angled ends), you can use a multi-grip EZ bar if you wish to do so.
3. While facing the cable station, reach up and firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms are angled forward and downward.
    1. Keep your elbows and wrists straight.
4. While keeping your arms outstretched with straight elbows in line with the cable, walk backward until you feel tension from the cable as the weight stack separates.
5. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and moderately incline your torso while keeping your arms in line with the cable and your back flat.
    1. The appropriate degree of torso incline should allow you to draw a mostly straight line from your arms through your shoulders to your hips when viewed from the side. From this position, the cable’s resistance should situate your arms fully overhead relative to what your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straightened and your torso inclined, sweep your arms down and back toward your sides in an arcing motion until your hands are right by your thighs.
    1. During the initial half of this movement, you can focus on driving down, while you can focus on pulling back during the latter half of this movement.
    2. As your arms move through motion at your shoulders, the rest of your body should be stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arms to sweep forward and upward in a controlled arcing manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms fully overhead in line with the cable.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straightened and your torso inclined.
    2. While you do not have to reverse the movement with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the cable abruptly swing your arms upward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, you can stand upright and walk forward to return the cable to its initial position.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres major, and rear delt, which are assisted by the lowest portion of your pec major and the long head of your triceps in the overhead position of each rep.## Setting up
1. At a cable station, adjust the cable height to near the top of your head when standing, clip in a D-handle attachment, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing away from the cable station and standing slightly to its side (such that the back of one shoulder is in line with the cable station), use your hand on the side closer to the cable to grasp the handle and take a step forward.
3. On the side holding the handle, raise your upper arm approximately 90 degrees out to your side (so that it is parallel to the ground) while bending your elbow to approximately 90 degrees and keeping your wrist in a fairly straight position.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
4. Rotate your forearm through your available range of motion so that it is near a vertical position (or as close to that position as is comfortable for your shoulder), which will result in your palm facing forward.
    1. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable, so if that is not the case, take another step backward away from the cable station.
    2. Your shoulder mobility will affect how far you can rotate.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handle downward and forward by rotating your forearm away from the cable station until your forearm is situated parallel to the ground, as you continue to keep your upper arm held out to your side and parallel to the ground.
    1. All of this movement should occur from shoulder rotation as the bony prominence on the back of your elbow continues pointing straight out to the side throughout the rep.
    2. When thinking about this technique, it can be helpful to view your forearm as a clock’s hand, while your elbow is the center about which rotation occurs without any side-to-side motion.
2. Reverse the motion by rotating your forearm toward the cable station as you move the handle upward and backward in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set with one side and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rotator cuff’s subscapularis, while also training your pec major, lat, teres major, and front delt.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide upward during the dead hang, which pulls on your hand skin in a manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
    1. A dead hang is a static exercise, so you will simply hang on the bar while keeping your feet off the ground and not moving your body to perform it.
2. Continue hanging on the bar until you have completed your set’s target duration.
    1. Following the set, lower your feet onto the ground (or step) and let go of the bar.
## Description
While simultaneously improving overhead shoulder mobility, this exercise can increase your grip strength by training your forearm and hand muscles that function as finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Approach a surface (e.g., hand rail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
2. Place your hand onto the nearby surface for balance assistance.
    1. Maintain this hand contact throughout the set.
3. On the side you are not about to train, lift the foot up off the ground by bending that knee.
    1. Keep this foot in this position off the ground throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. With the leg that is standing on the ground, raise your heel into the air through your ankle’s available range of motion while keeping that side’s knee straight and your trunk upright.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heel to descend toward the ground while continuing to keep the knee straight on your working side.
    1. Gently tap the ground with your heel at the bottom.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat this exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise offers you a convenient way to train your gastrocnemius and soleus  in nearly any environment.## Setting up
1. After adding your desired load (in the form of weight plates or kettlebells) to a pull-up/dip belt you have around your waist, stand upright under a pull-up bar.
    1. The back of your belt should be securely resting on top of your buttocks, and the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) should be looped through the center hole of a weight plate or under the handle of a kettlebell. If you have a loading pin for your belt, you can alternatively loop the chain or strap through it after adding weight plates.
    2. It will be easiest if you carry the weight over to the pull-up bar and add it to your belt while you are under the bar, rather than adding it farther away from the bar before walking over.
    3. As an alternative to using a pull-up/dip belt, you can wear a weighted vest.
    4. While less stable and more awkward than using a belt or weighted vest, you also have the option of holding a dumbbell between your thighs or feet, but you will be limited in how much weight you can add when using a dumbbell.
2. Reach overhead and firmly grasp the pull-up bar with your palms facing backward (i.e., an underhand grip) and your hands shoulder-width apart.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. Particularly with this weighted variation, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar than if you jumped up from the ground.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
3. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will place you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back places you into a position akin to a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps; however, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.

### **How Your Grip and Pull-Up Technique Influence Back Muscle Development**

When repeatedly performing this exercise over time, you can feel free to try out mildly different grip widths to determine if any particular position feels most comfortable to you and/or allows you to perceive your target back muscles working particularly well. Pull-ups can be performed with a variety of hand grips, including different grip widths and overhand, underhand (resulting in the exercise being called a chin-up), neutral, or hybrid (i.e., a combination of neutral and underhand or overhand) grips. Additionally, shoulder motion during a pull-up can occur predominantly in the sagittal (front-to-back) plane, the frontal (side-to-side) plane, or a combination of the two. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the sagittal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out in front of you), the upper arms remain parallel to each other and do not deviate away from the mid-line of your body. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the frontal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out to your sides, such as with a snow angle motion), the upper arms move away from the mid-line of your body until they are parallel to the ground. Then they move closer to the mid-line of your body with further elevation. During the lifting phase of a pull-up, the shoulders could extend in the sagittal plane, adduct in the frontal plane, or exhibit a combination of extension and adduction at a hybrid angle between the sagittal and frontal planes. Rather than moving the shoulders exclusively through one plane, pull-up variations are commonly performed with a technique that allows for the shoulders to move in between these two planes, but one plane of shoulder motion often makes a greater contribution than the other.

Muscles acting on the shoulder joint have different leverage to generate extension torque in the sagittal plane versus adduction torque in the frontal plane, and the nervous system is most likely to recruit the particular muscles that are best leveraged to produce the intended joint action during any active movement. Correspondingly, the plane of motion in which the shoulders move during a pull-up can affect how well different muscles are likely to be trained. With any particular grip for a pull-up, you can intentionally rotate your shoulders in a manner that allows you to move your shoulder relatively more in the sagittal or frontal plane. However, most people will typically find that a particular path of shoulder motion feels most comfortable and stable to their shoulders, elbows, and wrists when using a specific grip. For instance, people will normally perform a wide overhand grip pull-up with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the frontal plane. In contrast, they will normally perform a close-grip chin-up (using an underhand grip) with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the sagittal plane. Performing either variation in the alternative plane of motion would require greater-than-normal rotational range of motion in the shoulders and forearms, and attempting to do so could feel very awkward and uncomfortable. People will typically feel most comfortable when positioning themselves so that, at the point in the pull-up when their upper arms are parallel to the ground, their elbows and wrists are vertically aligned or their elbows are slightly lateral (outside) to their wrists.

In general, widening a grip facilitates relatively more frontal plane shoulder motion, while narrowing a grip facilitates relatively more sagittal plane shoulder motion. An overhand, underhand, or neutral grip may be used with any grip width. Still, the combination of these grip variables will influence comfort and which plane of shoulder motion feels most natural. When pulling predominantly in the sagittal plane with a rather close grip, an underhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable than an overhand grip. Similarly, when pulling predominantly in the frontal plane with a rather wide grip, an overhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable and allow more frontal-plane motion than an underhand grip. With a moderate-width grip, people may commonly find that pulling mostly in the sagittal plane feels natural with an underhand or neutral grip, but pulling relatively more in the frontal plane feels natural with an overhand grip. Fundamentally, the shoulder motion is what matters to the back muscles, and the grip is only relevant to the back muscles based on how it influences which shoulder motion is performed. When performing this overhand grip pull-up with a moderate grip width, you most likely will pull in a way that biases shoulder adduction somewhat more than shoulder extension. Still, you can also perform the exercise in a way that results in a fairly even balance between these two motions.

The lat and teres major in the back function both as primary adductors and extensors and are therefore highly likely to be trained effectively with pull-up variations regardless of the grip or plane of shoulder motion. However, the preponderance of available evidence indicates that the rear deltoid lacks meaningful leverage to contribute to shoulder adduction, but it clearly has favorable leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. The rear deltoid is correspondingly likely to be trained better with a closer grip emphasizing shoulder extension, which would be most comfortable when using an underhand or neutral grip. Alternatively, the middle and lower regions of the pectoralis major (that constitute the sternoclavicular head) have large moment arms to function as shoulder adductors, but only the lower portion exhibits a shoulder extension moment arm, and this moment arm is quite small and only present in flexed shoulder positions. Consequently, wider grip pull-ups (which are most comfortable with an overhand or neutral grip) that involve greater frontal-plane shoulder motion are likely to train the pectoralis major more effectively than closer grip pulldowns. Which regions of the lats are preferentially biased by a pull-up variation may also potentially be influenced by how much shoulder motion occurs in the frontal or sagittal planes. The middle and lower regions have relatively greater leverage to function as shoulder adductors, while the upper region of the lat has relatively greater leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. Plausibly, these biomechanical differences may facilitate the middle and lower regions of the lats being preferentially trained by wider grip pull-ups, while the upper region may be preferentially trained by closer grip pulling variations. With that said, the degree to which any preferential biasing may occur across different regions of the lats when comparing pull-up grips is more speculative and has not been tested in any longitudinal training interventions.

### Why Overhand Grip Pull-Ups may be More Difficult than Chin-Ups or Neutral Grip Pull-Ups

While exceptions exist, people often find overhand grip pull-ups to be more difficult per rep than chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. The discrepancy is most likely influenced by chin-ups and neutral grip pull-ups typically being performed with a technique that emphasizes shoulder extension in the sagittal plane, while overhand grip pull-ups are typically performed with relatively more shoulder adduction in the frontal plane. As demonstrated by studies such as those by Mayer et al. (1994) and Shklar and Dvir (1995), on average, maximal torque for shoulder extension exceeds that for shoulder adduction. The sample of participants, velocity, and type of muscle action tested can all influence the degree to which torque differs between these two actions, but maximal shoulder adduction torque has been measured at 63-90% of maximal shoulder extension, depending on the specific test conditions. In addition to the effect of how the shoulder moves, the forearm position can also affect how much elbow flexion torque you can generate during your pull. Guenzkofer et al. (2012) found that up to 15% greater elbow flexion torque can be generated when the forearm is in a supinated (used for an underhand grip) or neutral position compared to a pronated (used with an overhand grip) position. Together, these relationships suggest that most people can generate greater torque at the shoulders and elbows during chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups than during overhand grip pull-ups, which are likely to be more challenging when performing the same number of reps. Consequently, it is common not to be able to complete as many reps of overhand grip pull-ups in a set as you would be able to complete when performing chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. Nonetheless, individual differences in factors such as training history and the relative strength of different muscle groups can result in some people not noticing a difference in difficulty between these variations, or even finding overhand grip pull-ups to be somewhat easier per rep.

### Chin Over Bar?

The technique instructions do not specify where any particular part of your body should be situated in relation to the bar at the end of the lifting phase because your full range of motion can differ from someone else’s full range of motion. Pulling until your chin is above the bar is a common standard used for testing by the United States Marines and for competitions held by the World Pull-Up Organization, and you can certainly use it if you wish. However, when focusing on chin clearance, some people tend to extend their necks, lifting their chins, to clear the bar with less range of motion through the pull, compared to when they retain a neutral neck position. In this instance, focusing on the proxy for a full range of motion may result in a different movement than focusing on the individual's full range of motion. When competing or taking a standardized test that affects professional prospects, it is only sensible to do the minimum required to complete each rep per the rules, but training to build muscle and strength is a different endeavor.

### **The Most Challenging Part of the Chin-Up**

The end range of the lifting phase is typically the most challenging portion of chin-ups, in large part because the torque our pulling muscles can generate meaningfully varies across different joint angles. When the elbow is flexed to 120 degrees, Bober et al. (2002) found maximal elbow flexion torque to be approximately 35% of the peak torque that can be generated in a moderately flexed elbow position (i.e., near 60-75 degrees of elbow flexion). With your elbows approaching full flexion toward the end range of a chin-up, your elbow flexor muscles are correspondingly operating from a rather disadvantaged position to help you complete the rep. On average, maximal shoulder extension torque is also lower when the shoulder is mildly elevated (like at the end of a chin-up’s lifting phase) compared to when it is moderately elevated (like during the middle range of a chin-up). The difference can be of notable magnitude, as indicated by Bober et al. (2002), who found that maximal shoulder extension in a neutral shoulder position was only 60% of the peak value achievable at 90 degrees of shoulder flexion. Together these strength curves of the muscle groups acting on the elbow and shoulder can contribute to your chin-ups consistently feeling the most difficult during the last quarter of the lifting phase.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your back muscle strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your back muscles. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
You can perform this exercise with an asymmetrically weighted implement by holding it by its lighter end, or you can use a symmetrically weighted implement by holding it closer to one end to provide a similar effect. Asymmetrical implements include wrist bars (a type of equipment specifically designed for wrist exercises that looks like an adjustable dumbbell where plates can be added only onto one side), adjustable dumbbells with plates loaded only on one side, kettlebells, hammers, and end-loaded baseball bats. Symmetrical implements that may be used include dumbbells, fixed-weight straight bars, EZ bars, and cable straight bar attachments.

1. Firmly grasp an asymmetrically weighted implement by its lighter end with its heavier end in front of you. If you are using a kettlebell, firmly grasp it by one of its horns (i.e., side handle) with its base facing forward. Alternatively, if you are using a symmetrically weighted implement, firmly grasp it sufficiently close toward one end to shift more of the weight in front of your thumb and index finger than behind your little finger.
    1. The resistance your forearm muscles experience during this exercise will increase when the weight’s center of mass is positioned further in front of your wrist, so where you specifically grasp the implement will affect the difficulty.
    2. For greater resistance, you can grasp farther away from the weighted end with an asymmetrically loaded implement or closer to whichever end you are holding with a symmetrically loaded implement.
    3. When performing this exercise for the first time with certain implements, you may have to try out different grip positions to determine specifically where you should grasp the implement to provide the magnitude of resistance you wish to have.
    4. If you are using a dumbbell, your little finger should be contacting one end of the weight while there is space between where your index finger is wrapped around the handle and the other end of the weight.
2. Hold the implement by your side with your palm facing in toward your side and your elbow straight. Based on your preference, you can stand or sit down with an upright torso.
    1. While you have the option of holding the implement with your thumb bent and wrapped around your other fingers (i.e., the standard grip for many exercises), positioning your thumb flat against the implement as it is pointing forward may be more comfortable. While not inherently injurious, certain individuals may experience thumb discomfort in the bottom position of this exercise when using a bent thumb position due to the loaded stretch it provides to a thumb muscle.
    2. If you are holding a long implement, like a lat pulldown bar, you may have to stand to avoid contacting the ground with the implement.
## Exercise technique
1. Let the weight tilt your hand down through your wrist’s comfortable range of motion to assume the starting position of the exercise.
2. Tilt your hand up toward the thumb-side of your forearm (which is facing forward) through your wrist’s full range of motion.
    1. This will angle your knuckles at least slightly forward.
    2. The heavier part of the implement in front of your thumb and index finger should now be angled at least slightly upward while mostly pointing forward. Similarly, the lighter part of the implement behind your little finger should now be angled at least slightly downward while mostly pointing backward.
    3. Individuals with normal wrist mobility can typically tilt their hands only mildly beyond a neutral wrist position (as when standing at rest with your knuckles pointing straight down) during this exercise. Consequently, this exercise does not involve a particularly long range of motion.
    4. While you move your hand through wrist motion, keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the exercise.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting your hand tilt away from the thumb-side of your forearm through your wrist’s comfortable range of motion.
    1. This will angle your knuckles at least mildly backward.
    2. The heavier part of the implement in front of your thumb and index finger should now be angled diagonally downward and forward. Simultaneously, the lighter part of the implement behind your little finger should now be angled diagonally upward and backward.
    3. Only move through the range of motion that is comfortable to your wrist and thumb.
    4. There is no need to perform this extremely slowly, but you should actively control the movement rather than letting the weight swing your hand down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train five forearm muscles, namely the two extensor carpi radialis muscles, flexor carpi radialis, extensor pollicis brevis, and abductor pollicis longus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to near mid-chest height.
    1. The optimal starting height should let you position the bar on your upper back with your knees slightly bent.
2. Add your desired weight to the bar and position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are just below the height of the bar.
3. After adjusting the bar height, face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance, and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
    3. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to then walk under the bar and situate the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    4. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    5. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar up excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    6. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    7. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
    8. If you need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground in order to position the bar on your upper back, you can lower the height of the bar and the stop pads before repositioning the bar on your back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to tighten your upper back muscles, and unrack it by starting to stand up as you straighten your knees.
2. After the bar rests fully on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars with a slight wrist flick before standing fully upright with the bar.
3. Raise your heels into the air through the available range of motion of your ankles while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
4. In a controlled manner, allow your heels to descend toward the ground while continuing to keep your knees straight.
    1. Aim to tap the ground lightly with your heels in the bottom position.
5. Alternate steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
6. After finishing the set, rotate the safety hooks above the safety bars and rest the hooks onto the safety bars by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can develop your gastrocnemius and soleus.## Setting up
In contrast to pin-loaded pullover machines, it is not unusual for plate-loaded pullover machines to lack a foot pedal that you can press to move the machine’s arms while setting up. Some of the specific setup details differ depending on whether your machine has a foot pedal, so the setup for each type of machine is described separately. However, the exercise technique can be very similar for either option, so one technique section is provided with a couple of details that depend on which type of machine you utilize.

### **Setting up a machine with a foot pedal**

1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that aligns your shoulders with the machine’s pivot points and allows you to rest your triceps (the back of your upper arms) and elbows comfortably on the arm pads once you are seated.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point (which is also known as the axis of rotation) is the center about which the machine’s lever arm will rotate during the exercise. Aligning your shoulders with these pivot points can help the exercise feel smooth and natural.
    2. The same seat height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points is what should allow you to rest your triceps and elbows on the arm pads throughout the exercise.
    3. Finding the ideal seat height for you may initially require some trial and error, since you can only determine if the height is appropriate by sitting on the seat and checking to see if the center of your shoulders is level with the pivot points.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit on the seat with an upright torso and a “proud chest” posture, as your back and the back of your hip bones are resting against the backrest.
    1. Situate your feet on the stationary foot bar if your machine has one. Otherwise, situate your feet flat on the ground if you can do so. If your feet cannot reach a foot bar or the ground, you can simply let your feet hang down or place a step or bumper plate under your feet if you prefer them not to dangle.
4. If your machine has one, fasten the seat belt securely around your waist for additional stability.
5. Place one foot against the machine’s foot pedal, and push the pedal forward with your leg.
    1. This lowers the arm pads and moves the handle forward to a position you can comfortably reach.
    2. Keep the pedal pushed away during the next two steps.
6. With bent elbows, raise your arms to situate your elbows and triceps snugly against the arm pads while grasping the handles.
    1. Different machines have different handle types that will influence specifically how you should grasp the handle (such as with an overhand or semi-neutral grip), but what chiefly matters is that your grip feels comfortable and stable.
    2. While uncommon, certain machines may have handles without any arm pads. If you are using one of these atypical machines, you will simply grasp the handles and ignore the details about arm pad usage while otherwise performing the exercise as instructed.
7. Slowly bring the foot pedal back to its starting position by bending your knee, which will shift the machine’s resistance to your arms. Simultaneously, allow the machine’s resistance to lift your elbows upward and backward through the full range of motion that is comfortable to your shoulders.
    1. Maintain the natural curvature of your lower back rather than excessively arching your lower back and flaring your ribs upward and away from your hip bones as you raise your elbows.
    2. The degree to which you can comfortably allow your elbows to lift upward will be influenced by your individual shoulder mobility, but it is common to be able to reach near the point at which the upper arms are fully vertical.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your shoulders and/or back muscles in the starting position, but you should move through the range in which you do not feel any shoulder joint pain or discomfort.
8. Once your arms are fully lifted up, lift your foot off the foot pedal and place it symmetrically with your other foot, whether that be on the stationary foot bar, on the ground, or hanging under the seat.

### **Setting up a machine without a foot pedal**

1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that aligns your shoulders with the machine’s pivot points and allows you to rest your triceps (the back of your upper arms) and elbows comfortably on the arm pads once you are seated.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point (which is also known as the axis of rotation) is the center about which the machine’s lever arm will rotate during the exercise. Aligning your shoulders with these pivot points can help the exercise feel smooth and natural.
    2. The same seat height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points is what should allow you to rest your triceps and elbows on the arm pads throughout the exercise.
    3. Finding the ideal seat height for you may initially require some trial and error, since you can only determine if the height is appropriate by sitting on the seat and checking to see if the centers of your shoulders are level with the pivot points.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit on the seat with an upright torso and a “proud chest” posture, as your back and the back of your hip bones are resting against the backrest.
    1. Situate your feet on the stationary foot bar if your machine has one. Otherwise, situate your feet flat on the ground if you can do so. If your feet cannot reach a foot bar or the ground, you can simply let your feet hang down or place a step or bumper plate under your feet if you prefer them not to dangle.
4. If your machine has one, fasten the seat belt securely around your waist for additional stability.
5. With bent elbows, raise your arms up overhead until your triceps are behind the arm pads.
    1. Maintain the natural curvature of your lower back rather than excessively arching your lower back and flaring your ribs upward and away from your hip bones as you raise your elbows.
    2. You cannot modify the starting position of the arm pads when using a machine without a foot pedal, which is not particularly problematic for the majority of lifters, since these machines are designed to have a starting position that is accessible for people with normal shoulder mobility. However, certain individuals with meaningful shoulder mobility limitations may not be able to raise their arms high enough to reach the pads without pushing into a position that causes shoulder joint discomfort or pain. If this applies to you, you can select an alternative variation (such as the straight arm cable lat pulldown) that allows you to individualize the starting position and train the same movement pattern through a comfortable range of motion.
6. Situate your elbows and triceps snugly against the arm pads while grasping the handles.
    1. Different machines have different handle types that will influence specifically how you should grasp the handle (such as with an overhand or semi-neutral grip), but what chiefly matters is that your grip feels comfortable and stable.
    2. While uncommon, certain machines may have handles without any arm pads. If you are using one of these atypical machines, you will simply grasp the handles and ignore the details about arm pad usage while otherwise performing the exercise as instructed.
    3. Certain unconventional machines may also have a foot brake that you need to press with your foot to disengage before starting your set to enable you to train through a full range of motion.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down toward your sides in an arcing motion until your elbows are by your sides.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up and back in contact with the backrest rather than slumping forward into a slouched posture.
    2. During the initial half of this movement, you can focus on pulling down, while you can focus on pulling back during the latter half of this movement.
    3. Emphasize driving your elbows down into the arm pads rather than pulling the handle with your hands.
    4. As your arms move through motion at your shoulders, the rest of your body should be stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to sweep upward in a controlled arcing manner through the full range of motion that is comfortable to your shoulders.
    1. You should return to the starting position with your elbows lifted as high up as your shoulder mobility comfortably permits. If you are using a machine without a foot pedal, the machine’s lever arms contacting the stoppers underneath them may restrict your end range before your shoulder mobility does, so you can lightly tap the lever arms against the stoppers to confirm that you have moved through your full range of motion.
    2. Like when setting up, avoid letting your lower back excessively arch from flaring your ribs upward and away from your hip bones.
    3. While you do not need to perform this phase with an extremely slow tempo, avoid letting the machine’s resistance abruptly swing your arms upward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If your machine has a foot pedal, push it forward with your leg (once your arms are fully overhead following your last rep) until you are no longer supporting any of the machine’s resistance with your upper body. Then let go of the handles while lifting your arms off the arm pads before bending your knee to bring the foot pedal back to its initial position.
    2. If your machine lacks a foot pedal or foot brake, simply rest the machine’s lever arms on the stoppers underneath them (after your arms are fully overhead following your last rep) before letting go of the handles while lifting arms off the arm pads.
    3. If your machine has a foot brake rather than a foot pedal, engage it with your foot before letting go of the handles.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres major, and rear delt, which are assisted by the lowest portion of your pec major in the overhead position of each rep.## Setting up
1. You can select either a fixed-weight EZ bar or an adjustable plate-loaded EZ bar, which are functionally interchangeable for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with extended elbows.
    1. If the EZ bar is already positioned in an elevated rack at mid-thigh level or above, you naturally would not have to squat down or hinge at your hips before picking it up.
3. Grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder width grip and a semi-underhand grip (AKA semi-neutral grip) such that your palms are directed at an angle that is facing forward and inward.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a shoulder width semi-underhand grip is accessible on the bar.
4. While keeping your elbows extended, stand fully upright while gripping the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the bar upwards and lowering it down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. After selecting your desired weight with the loading pin, adjust the height of the machine’s shoulder pad to a level that allows you to perform full range of motion calf raises.
    1. If the pad is too high, the machine may not be able to provide tension when your heels are fully lowered.
    2. If the pad is too low, the machine may restrict you from fully raising your heels.
2. With your knees slightly bent, position the front halves of your feet onto the step with your heels hanging off the edge while your shoulders are snugly resting under the shoulder pads.
3. Stand upright with your knees extended to receive the weight from the machine.
    1. Maintain this upright position throughout the set.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they utilize a hyperextended knee position during this exercise, so keeping a straight knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
4. While keeping one foot on the step, lift your other foot off the step by bending that knee.
    1. Keep this foot in this position off the step throughout the set while you keep the front half of your working leg’s foot on the step.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your heel into the air through your ankle’s available range of motion.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heel to descend toward the ground through your ankle’s available range of motion while continuing to keep the front half of your foot on top of the step.
    1. How far you are able to lower your heel will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, straddle the machine as you are facing toward the weights.
2. Place your feet onto the foot plates and your chest onto the chest pad such that your whole body is inclined forward.
    1. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
3. Depending upon your height and leg lengths, you may want to bend your knees somewhat or keep them extended to determine where your torso is situated on the chest pad.
    1. How you position your knees will be based on what feels most comfortable and stable to you, but you can initially see how you feel when positioning your knees to allow the uppermost aspect of the chest pad to be approximately level with your collarbone.
    2. Individuals who have longer legs will typically feel more comfortable when using a somewhat more bent knee position, while those with shorter legs will typically feel more comfortable with extended knees.
4. After determining which degree of knee bend places you in a comfortable position, firmly grasp the inner handles that are angled outward and backward such that your palms are facing at an angle in toward each other and backward (which is a hybrid between a neutral grip and overhand grip).
    1. Certain T-bar row machines have a pair of inner parallel handles (which allow a neutral grip) and outer handles directed straight out to the sides (which allow an overhand grip), but lack handles angled outward and backward.
    2. If your T-bar row machine does not have handles that are angled outward and backward, you can perform the chest-supported neutral grip machine T-bar row or chest-supported overhand grip machine T-bar row as an alternative.
5. Lift the bar up off the machine’s stopper, center the bar so that it no longer lies above the stopper, and lower it until your elbows are extended.
    1. In this bottom position, allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades apart from one another, which will likely prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in your upper back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve flaring them out moderately away from your sides, such that your upper arms form close to 45-degree angles with your torso toward the end of the lifting phase.
    2. Keep your torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set rather than arching your lower back to lift your chest up off the pad.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar back onto the machine’s stopper.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Approach a surface (e.g., hand rail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
2. Place your hand onto the nearby surface for balance assistance.
    1. Maintain this hand contact throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your heels into the air through the available range of motion of your ankles while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heels to descend toward the ground while continuing to keep your knees straight.
3. After your heels touch the ground, alternate between raising and lowering your heels until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise offers you a convenient way to train your gastrocnemius and soleus in nearly any environment.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your abdomen contacting the ground (i.e., in the prone position) and your head lifted slightly off the ground as you maintain a tucked chin position with your face directed toward the ground
2. With straight elbows, raise your arms outward so that they form a “Y-shape” with your torso while resting on the ground.
3. Rotate your arms so that your palms face at an angle toward each other and a little forward.
    1. If you make a thumbs-up gesture in this position, your thumb should be pointing up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your arms up off the ground through your available shoulder range of motion while maintaining the “Y-shape” and keeping your chest in contact with the ground.
    1. Your shoulder mobility will affect how high you can raise your arms.
    2. Most people will not be able to lift their arms particularly high before reaching the end of their available range of motion.
    3. If you notice your chest lifting up off the ground during this movement, that indicates that you most likely are dynamically extending your lumbar spine, which can occur as a compensation pattern instead of lifting your arm only with your shoulder girdle muscles.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering your arms in a controlled manner until your hands lightly contact the ground while maintaining the “Y-shape.”
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your traps along with your serratus anterior, front delt, and side delt.## Setting up
1. With a cable station positioned to its lowest height, select your weight with the loading pin, and clip in a straight bar attachment or a multi-grip curl bar attachment.
2. While standing directly in front of the middle of the bar, squat down with extended elbows to grasp the bar firmly with approximately a shoulder-width grip as your palms are directed back toward you (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. When squatting down, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
3. By extending your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar while continuing to keep your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
    1. When upright, you should feel resistance from the cable, so take a step backward if you do not initially feel any resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the bar upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the bar.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
    2. If you lose resistance from the cable machine when your shoulders are fully depressed, you can take a step backward and resume your set with resistance throughout the full range of motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Select your weight with the loading pin and adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad.
    1. While you can initially adjust the seat to a height that situates your shoulders approximately level with the horizontal handles, the seat height that is best for you depends on the specific machine you use, and trying out different positions may be helpful.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, adjust it to a position that allows you to grasp the horizontal handles when your arms are fully outstretched forward in front of you with extended elbows.
    1. To row through your full range of motion, ideally place the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with your elbows fully straight.
3. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet flat on the floor.
4. Reach fully forward with straight elbows to grasp the horizontally directed handles firmly with your palms facing down (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. If you were able to grasp the handles with any amount of elbow bend, you could let go of the handles and adjust the chest pad before grasping the handles again.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward you by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, rather than trying to keep them tucked close to your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a closer underhand or neutral grip).
    2. Keep your chest in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner, progressively straightening until they are outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you train them more effectively.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add your desired weight to an adjustable plate-loaded EZ bar on top of the preacher curl bench, or place a fixed weight EZ bar on top of the preacher curl bench.
2. Adjust the seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and most of the back of your upper arms (i.e., triceps) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The proper setting will typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
3. Sit down on the seat, and position the back of your upper arms and elbows on top of the arm pad with your feet on the ground.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder width semi-underhand grip (AKA semi-neutral grip) such that your palms are directed at an angle that is facing forward and inward.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it initially before a near shoulder width semi-underhand grip is accessible on the bar.
    2. If the bar is a little out of your reach, you could slightly stand up and lean forward to reach the bar before sitting back down after you have grasped the bar.
    3. Your wrists should be mostly straight throughout your set after grasping the bar.
5. In the starting position, your elbows should be fully straightened with the bar no longer in contact with the bench so that you can train through a full range of motion.
    1. If this is not the case, you can mildly scoot back with an upright torso until you have reached this starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows and the back of your upper arms in contact with the bench, curl the bar upward and back toward you in an arcing motion by bending at your elbows until they are fully bent.
    1. While your forearms move through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the bar past the point when your forearms are completely vertical and perpendicular to the ground, you can finish the lifting phase after your forearms are vertical since the biceps would no longer be working beyond this point.
2. After your elbows have fully bent, reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a slow and controlled manner until your elbows have returned to a fully straightened position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and the back of your upper arms in contact with the bench.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar back down onto the preacher bench.
## Description
Like other curls, this exercise can effectively train the biceps and brachialis in your upper arm along with the brachioradialis in your forearm.## Setting up
1. Pick up a dumbbell and stand upright with approximately a shoulder width stance while holding the dumbbell at your side with an extended elbow.
2. Direct your palm so that it is facing forward.
    1. Maintain this underhand grip on the dumbbell throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the dumbbell upward by bending at your elbow while keeping your upper arm fixed at your side (rather than allowing your upper arm to swing forward).
2. After your elbow has fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner until your elbow has returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arm positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbell up and lowering it down until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable pulley at its highest setting, or approach a cable lat pulldown machine.
    1. Either type of equipment can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. Connect a D-handle attachment to the cable and select your weight with the loading pin.
3. Stand facing in front of the cable and slightly to the side such that one of your shoulders is in line with the cable, and with your arm that is in front of the cable, reach up to grasp the handle firmly.
4. Perform a controlled lunging motion into a half-kneeling position with your knee on the side you are holding the cable, resting on the ground, after stepping backward with that side’s leg.
    1. As you do so, allow your elbow to straighten and your arm to elevate until it aligns with the cable.
    2. Maintain an upright torso position and keep the center of your breastbone facing the same way as the center of your hips rather than having your trunk rotated toward one side.
    3. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable machine with your elbow extended, so if the cable remains slack (which may occur if you have very long arms), you should position yourself a little farther away from the cable station before assuming the half-kneeling position.
5. Based on your preference and what feels most comfortable, you can rotate the handle in a manner that allows you to use a neutral grip with your palm facing in toward the midline of your body or an underhand grip with your palm facing backward.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbow downward and then backward in an arcing manner while progressively bending it to pull the handle down toward the side of your upper chest.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade down away from your ear.
    2. Keep your forearm aligned in the same direction as the cable, which will naturally occur with the appropriate degree of elbow bend.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the pull.
    4. During the initial portion of the pull, you will drive your elbow downward, but you will drive your elbow simultaneously backward and downward during the second half of the pull.
    5. After the initial portion of the pull, when your upper arm is approximately parallel with the ground, the bony prominence on the back of your elbow should be pointing predominantly forward as your upper arm is situated in front of your body, rather than having your elbow and upper arm flared out to your side.
2. Reverse the movement by bringing the handle upward in a controlled manner by allowing your elbow to move forward and upward while it progressively extends until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Finish this step with your elbow fully straightened and your whole arm aligned with the cable.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arm up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates while the barbell is on the ground or in an elevated position, such as a power rack.
    1. Setting up will be easier when using an elevated position, especially if you are using relatively heavy loads for this exercise.
    2. The bar will be easiest to pick up if positioned at a height slightly lower than the level of your hands when standing upright with your arms relaxed at your sides.
2. Stand with your back facing the middle of the bar with a hip-width stance and extended elbows before hinging at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
4. By extending your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar while continuing to keep your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the bar upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. If your buttock obstructs the upward movement of the bar, you can proceed to use a limited range of motion for this exercise or select another shrug variation.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the bar.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest level, connect a rope attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Stand in front of the cable station facing the rope.
3. Reach up and grasp each end of the rope with your little fingers and adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the outside of your other fingers.
    1. In this position, your palms should be facing inward.
4. Take a step backward and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight torso lean throughout the set.
5. Pull your upper arms down and back until they are at your sides, with your elbows close to fully bent, so your hands are close together in front of your chest.
    1. Maintain this upper arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Push the rope downward and spread it apart by extending your elbows until they are straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, your hands should be near the outer aspects of your thighs, and they should be farther apart from each other compared to when you were in the starting position.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows to raise the rope in a controlled manner as your hands move closer to each other until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbows as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. With a cable station positioned to its lowest height, clip in a straight bar attachment and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with extended elbows.
3. Grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder width grip and an overhand grip (AKA reverse grip) such that your palms are directed back toward you.
4. While keeping your elbows extended, stand upright while gripping the bar.
5. Make sure tension is present from the cable station while your elbows are fully extended, rather than slack being present in the cable.
    1. Depending on your arm length relative to your height and which specific cable station you are using, you likely will have to take a small step backward to take out the slack of the cable.
    2. When taking a small step backwards, the cable will now have a mildly diagonal line of pull rather than being vertical.
    3. To maintain a fully extended elbow position with a diagonal line of pull, you will position your upper arms slightly in front of your body by flexing at your shoulders instead of keeping your upper arms pinned to your sides (like you would with a curl that has a vertically directed resistance, such as free weights).
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed in the same position that allowed your elbows to be fully extended at the start of the rep (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing further forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms in the same position used to start the rep.
3. Alternate between curling the bar upwards and lowering it down until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, but it may train your biceps somewhat less effectively than a curl with an underhand or neutral grip.## Setting up
1. Load an adjustable EZ bar with your desired weight, or approach a fixed-weight EZ bar.
    1. Either type of EZ bar can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar in a near shoulder-width stance, squat down and firmly grasp it with your palms angled backward and in toward each other (resulting in a semi-overhand grip). Your hands should be about shoulder-width apart or mildly closer.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a shoulder-width or mildly closer semi-overhand grip is accessible on the bar.
    2. The proper hand placement should result in your wrists and elbows feeling comfortable during this exercise.
3. Stand upright with the bar just in front of your thighs.
4. Move the bar up to your shoulders with your elbows fully bent underneath and slightly in front of the bar.
    1. If the load is light enough to do so, you can just curl the bar up.
    2. With a heavier load, you can perform a cheat curl, where you generate momentum with your hips to help you curl the bar up. Initially sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and your torso to incline slightly forward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward while bending your elbows and mildly swinging them forward.
    3. The bar should now be in essentially the same position it would be for an overhead press.
    4. The bar should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    5. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar hard will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
5. Press the bar up overhead in a shoulder press motion until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. The bar should be vertically stacked above your shoulders, hips, and knees.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the bar behind your head in a controlled manner. Based on your preference and what feels most comfortable to your elbows, you can select one of two approaches with respect to your elbow position.
    1. Option 1: Keep your elbows completely up with your upper arms in a vertical position throughout the rep. This will result in the bar moving in an arcing manner exclusively from your forearms hinging at your elbows.
    2. Option 2: Mildly arc your elbows forward and down while bending your elbows to lower the bar. You can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest to create additional space for the bar. This option will result in the bar moving in a closer to vertical path from a combination of shoulder and elbow motion.
    3. Neither of the approaches is inherently superior to the other, so you can feel free to try out both ways and continue using the technique you prefer.
    4. With either option, predominantly keep your elbows tucked in. However, you can allow them to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
2. Continue lowering the bar until you have fully bent your elbows or can lightly tap the base of your neck or your upper back muscles with the bar.
    1. How far you can lower the bar may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps, particularly if you keep your elbows completely up. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach near their upper back with the bar, others will reach the limit to which they can comfortably bend their elbows before that point. Do not lower the bar beyond the range that your flexibility permits without discomfort.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the bar into the starting position overhead.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is vertically above your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the bar in front of your head toward your shoulders like you would during an overhead press.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbells just in front of your thighs with your palms facing back toward you and your elbows straight.
    1. If you do not have any experience training Jefferson curls, it is recommended that you initially try out the bodyweight Jefferson curl to determine how your body feels with this movement prior to performing a loaded variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Tuck your chin to your chest and lower the dumbbells in a slow and controlled manner by bending over and rounding your whole back through what is your comfortable range of motion while keeping your knees and elbows straight.
    1. As a cue, you can think about rounding forward each segment of your spine successively until your entire spine has achieved a rounded posture.
    2. As you progressively descend, it is natural to feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings and back muscles.
    3. How far you can descend will be influenced by your hamstring flexibility and the available bending range of motion in your spine.
    4. If you have a high degree of mobility, you may be able to descend until the dumbbells contact the floor, but only move through a range that is painless and feels right to your body.
    5. Your arms should be directed down toward the ground throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by gradually transitioning from the bent-over position to the upright position in which you started by uncoiling your back, which will lift the dumbbells up.
    1. At the end of the ascent, untuck your chin and look straight ahead.
    2. Continue keeping your knees and elbows in a straightened position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep spinal extensor muscles that run along your entire back, while also training your spinal mobility and hamstring flexibility.## Setting up
1. Connect an attachment to the cable that has parallel handles that are near shoulder width apart or closer, select your weight with the loading pin, and sit down facing the cable station.
    1. Multiple neutral grip attachments can be used to train the same muscles, including a V-bar row attachment, which enables a close grip and is also known as a double D-handle attachment. Alternatively, you can use a “U-shaped” bar that has a curved body.
    2. Using a neutral grip attachment with handles outside shoulder width facilitates a different pulling technique that preferentially trains different back muscles, so the seated neutral wide grip cable row is a distinct exercise in this database.
    3. You can perform this exercise with essentially the same technique whether you use a seated cable row machine or an adjustable cable station, but a seated cable row machine can be more stable and therefore is ideal.
    4. If you use a seated cable row machine, you naturally will sit down on its seat, but if you are using an adjustable cable station, you will sit down on the ground after adjusting the cable to a height that is approximately level with your shoulders when seated on the floor with an upright torso.
2. Position your feet in contact with the machine’s foot plates or the base of the adjustable cable station.
    1. Allow your knees to bend to an angle that allows you to feel stable and comfortable, which can be a mild to moderately mild knee bend for many people.
    2. With greater knee bend, you will be able to sit closer to the cable, so you can bend your knees further if you initially cannot reach the handles when leaning forward at your hips.
3. Lean forward at your hips until you can grasp the handles firmly with straight elbows and your palms facing each other.
4. While holding the handles with straight elbows, lean your torso backward at your hips until you are sitting with an upright torso.
    1. When seated upright, you should feel tension from the cable, so if the cable is still slack, you can extend your knees to scoot yourself backward until the machine’s weight stack separates and you feel the resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward your torso by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, typically keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides with this neutral grip, rather than flaring them away from your sides (which is more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion or until the handles are directly in front of your torso, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten to move the handles forward.
    1. Finish with your arms fully outstretched in front of you.
    2. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, without rounding your lower back. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you to train them more effectively.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Connect a lat pulldown bar to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. If you do not have a standard lat pulldown bar attachment, which has a straight body and mildly angled ends, you can use a different attachment that lets you comfortably grasp it with an underhand grip.
2. While facing the bar and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the bar with a closer than shoulder-width underhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
    1. Specifically how closely you position your hands is up to your preference, but they should be clearly closer than what you would use for a standard underhand grip lat pulldown with a moderate grip width.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
3. While holding the bar, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
4. In the seated position, slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight backward lean throughout your set.
    2. From this position, your elbows should be straight, with your arms overhead and slightly forward (rather than completely vertical), and you should feel tension from the cable station holding your arms in this position.
    3. If you notice the cable remains slack in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), widening out your grip may be needed to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar down toward your upper chest by driving your elbows down toward your sides as you progressively bend them.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. After the initial half of the pull, when your upper arms are approximately parallel with the ground, the bony prominence on the back of each elbow should be pointing predominantly forward as your upper arms are situated in front of your body, rather than having your elbows and upper arms predominantly flared out to your sides.
    3. During the second half of this pull, drive your elbows simultaneously backward and downward until they are in line with your torso or slightly behind it.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the bar in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on a bench with your feet flat on the floor and your torso upright. Hold the dumbbells at your sides with slightly bent elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
    1. You can opt to sit down on a flat bench without any back support or sit down on a seated bench with back support.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep the dumbbells out and away from your sides until your upper arms are close to parallel with the ground.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner back to your sides.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus while also training your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. At a cable station, adjust the cable to near the height of your elbows when standing with your arms at your sides, clip in a D-handle attachment, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing with your chest facing near a 135-degree angle away from the cable station, grasp the handle with your hand on the side closer to the cable.
3. On the side holding the handle, position your upper arm by your side and slightly forward while bending your elbow to approximately 90 degrees and keeping your wrist in a fairly straight position.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
    2. Situating your upper arm slightly forward rather than having it be completely vertical at your side can limit your torso from restricting your range of motion during this exercise.
4. Have your forearm rotated outward toward the cable station.
    1. Your shoulder mobility will affect how far you can rotate, and you should use as much range as feels comfortable for your shoulder.
    2. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable, so if that is not the case, take a step to the side away from the cable stack.
## Exercise technique
1. Rotate your forearm inward by pulling the handle away from the cable station and across the front of your torso while moving through your shoulder’s available range of motion as you continue to keep your upper arm positioned slightly in front of your side.
    1. At the end of this motion, your forearm should be contacting or close to contacting your abdomen while your palm is facing backward.
    2. All of this movement should occur from shoulder rotation, and your upper arm should not raise away from your torso.
    3. When thinking about this technique, it can be helpful to view your forearm as a clock’s hand, while your elbow is the center about which rotation occurs without any side-to-side motion.
2. Reverse the motion by rotating your forearm outward as you bring the handle closer toward the cable station in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set with one side and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rotator cuff’s subscapularis, while also training your pec major, lat, teres major, and front delt.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your abdomen contacting the ground (i.e., in the prone position) and your head lifted slightly off the ground as you maintain a tucked chin position with your face directed toward the ground.
2. Position your arms straight out in front of you with extended elbows such that your hands are approximately shoulder-width apart while resting on the ground.
    1. Your body position should resemble how you would look if you were reaching up with your arms fully overhead while standing.
3. Rotate your arms so that your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If you make a thumbs-up gesture in this position, your thumb should be pointing up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your arms straight up off the ground through your available shoulder range of motion while keeping your chest in contact with the ground.
    1. Your shoulder mobility will affect how high you can raise your arms.
    2. Most people will not be able to lift their arms particularly high before reaching the end of their available range of motion.
    3. If you notice your chest lifting up off the ground during this movement, that indicates that you most likely are dynamically extending your lumbar spine, which can occur as a compensation pattern instead of lifting your arm only with your shoulder girdle muscles.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering your arms in a controlled manner until your hands lightly contact the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your traps along with your serratus anterior, front delt, side delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit down on the seat.
2. Adjust the height of the machine’s thigh pad to a level that allows you to fit the lowest portions of your thighs just underneath it, while the front halves of your feet are situated on the foot plate.
    1. To help find this point, you can initially place the front halves of your feet on the foot plate while your heels are lower than the balls of your feet.
    2. Avoid setting the pad to a height that requires you to lift your heels up higher than the balls of your feet in order for your thighs to press snugly into the pad. The weakest portion of a calf raise occurs when your heels are fully raised. Consequently, it can be difficult to re-rack the weight at the end of a set if your calves are fatigued, and you have to raise your heels meaningfully before adjusting the machine’s stopper to accept the weight.
3. With the front halves of your feet on the foot plate, scoot your knees under and in front of the thigh pad until your lower thighs are securely under the pad.
    1. You can optionally use your hands to help pull the thigh pad up as you scoot forward.
4. Raise your heels up just enough to disengage the machine’s stopper by operating its lever with one of your hands.
    1. You should be primarily bearing weight through the balls of your feet as the back half of your feet are hanging off the foot plate.
    2. For tracking purposes, you can consider your first full rep to start after you have completely lowered your heels when performing step 1 of the exercise technique for the first time in a set. The initial short range of motion heel raise you perform to disengage the stopper does not constitute a full rep.
## Exercise technique
1. In a controlled manner, allow your heels to descend toward the ground through the available range of motion of your ankles while continuing to keep the front halves of your feet on top of the foot plate.
    1. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf/and or ankle at the bottom position.
    2. Specifically how far you are able to lower your heels will be dependent on your mobility, but individuals with normal ankle mobility can position their heels lower than the balls of their feet at the bottom.
2. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels into the air through the available range of motion of your ankles.
    1. At the top, your heels should be higher than the balls of your feet.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. At times during a set, you may feel that your feet slide back somewhat on the foot plate. If that occurs, you can use your hands to pull up the bottom of the thigh pad to make it easier for you to re-position your feet forward on the foot plate.
4. After finishing the lowering phase of your rep, raise your heels high enough to engage the machine’s stopper with your hand.
    1. If you performed as many reps as you possibly could during your set, you may have to rest in the bottom position for several seconds until your calves are ready to lift your heels up high enough to engage the stopper.
5. With the stopper engaged, lower your heels to let the weight rest on the stopper.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your soleus, but it will not train your gastrocnemius nearly as well as calf raise variations performed with straight knees.## Setting up
1. With a cable station positioned to its lowest height, clip in a d-handle attachment and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing the cable station, take a step to the side such that the handle is positioned directly in front of one hand (rather than having the midline of your body in front of the handle)
3. Squat down or hinge at your hips with an extended elbow before using your closer hand to grasp the handle with an underhand grip such that your palm is directed forward.
4. While keeping your elbow extended, stand upright while gripping the handle.
5. Make sure tension is present from the cable station while your elbow is fully extended, rather than slack being present in the cable.
    1. Depending on your arm length relative to your height and which specific cable station you are using, you likely will have to take a small step backward to take out the slack of the cable.
    2. When taking a small step backwards, the cable will now have a mildly diagonal line of pull rather than being vertical.
    3. To maintain a fully extended elbow position with a diagonal line of pull, you will position your upper arm slightly in front of your body by flexing at your shoulder instead of keeping your upper arm pinned to your sides (like you would with a curl that has a vertically directed resistance, such as free weights).
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the handle upward by bending at your elbow while keeping your upper arm fixed in the same position that allowed your elbow to be fully extended at the start of the rep (rather than allowing your upper arm to swing further forward).
2. After your elbow has fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the handle in a controlled manner until your elbow has returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arm in the same position used to start the rep.
3. Alternate between curling the handle upwards and lowering it down until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position the Smith machine’s bar slightly below the height of your hands when standing with your arms at your sides.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the bar, and stand upright in a near hip-width stance with the middle of the bar just in front of your legs.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly backward when lifting it up (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails). Facing this way will help the shrug feel most natural.
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar, sit your hips mildly backward while slightly bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar, and rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion.
3. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion to lift the bar upward while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. Throughout the exercise, look straight ahead to keep your neck near a neutral position.
    2. As a cue, you can think about driving your shoulders up toward your earlobes.
    3. While you shrug your shoulders upward, the rest of your body should remain stationary as you continue standing upright with straight legs.
4. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend fully in a controlled manner to lower the bar.
    1. At the bottom, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the machine’s weight can provide.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank you downward.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to re-rack the bar.
    1. Following your last rep, rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars, and mildly bend your hips and knees to rerack the bar onto the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the seat so that your upper arms can be parallel with the floor or a little lower while grasping the handles.
    1. After initially adjusting the seat height, spread your arms outward so that your hands are level with the handles.
    2. You may have to re-adjust the seat if your upper arms are not parallel with the floor or a little lower.
    3. Some people will find their shoulders feel more comfortable when the seat is set to a height that allows their hands to be a bit lower than their shoulders, compared to when their hands are completely level with their shoulders.
2. While sitting with your back against the backrest, spread your arms backward into a “T-position” through your available range of motion.
3. Adjust the pins above the handles to a position that allows you to grasp the vertical handles with a neutral grip (i.e., palms facing forward) when your arms are in this “T-position.”
    1. You may have to readjust the handles and recheck to see that the handles are not positioned too far behind, such that you cannot grab the handles, or positioned too far in front, such that your arms are not as far back into the “T-position” as your mobility allows.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your shoulder blades in this tucked back position throughout the set.
2. Spread your arms apart into the “T-position” with a slight bend in your elbows before grasping the handles with a neutral grip (i.e., palms facing forward).
3. Pull your arms together as the handles move toward each other until they are just about to contact each other or until they lightly contact each other.
4. Reverse the motion by spreading your arms in a controlled manner until you have returned to your starting position (i.e., the “T-position”).
5. Alternate between pulling your arms together and spreading them apart until you have completed the set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, select your weight with the loading pin, and connect a rope attachment to the cable.
2. While facing the middle of the rope, squat down with straight elbows and grasp the rope with a neutral grip (AKA hammer grip) as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. For a particularly stable grip, you can place the outside of your index fingers in contact with the flat surface of the hard plastic end caps of the rope attachment.
    2. Whether you wrap your thumbs around your other fingers or place your thumbs on top of the rope’s end caps will be based on your personal preference.
3. Stand upright with the rope while keeping your elbows straight.
4. Make sure tension is present from the cable station while your elbows are straight, rather than slack being present in the cable.
    1. Depending on your arm length relative to your height and which specific cable station you are using, you likely will have to take a small step backward to take out the slack of the cable.
    2. When taking a small step backward, the cable will now have a mildly diagonal line of pull rather than being vertical.
    3. To maintain a fully extended elbow position with a diagonal line of pull, you will position your upper arms slightly in front of your body by flexing at your shoulders instead of keeping your upper arms pinned to your sides (like you would with a curl that has a vertically directed resistance, such as free weights).
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the rope upward and back toward you in an arcing motion by bending your elbows until they are fully bent.
    1. Rather than swinging them further forward, keep your upper arms fixed in the same position in which they started (i.e., a little in front of you).
    2. While your forearms move through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set. 
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the rope in a controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows. 
    1. Nothing else should move except for your forearms lowering in an arcing manner through elbow motion.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should avoid letting the cable’s resistance abruptly swing the bar down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the J-hooks of a rack to near mid-chest height, place a barbell on the J-hooks, and add weight plates of your desired load.
    1. The appropriate J-hook height should allow you to walk out with the bar without having to rise up on the balls of your feet.
2. Position the safety pins just under the J-hooks.
3. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat wider than shoulder width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
4. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to then walk under the bar and situate the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar up excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    3. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    4. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    5. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
    6. If you find that deeper than a mild bend at your hips and knees is (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) is required to position the bar on your back, you can lift the bar out of the J-hooks, set it down, raise the height of the J-hooks, and place the bar back onto the J-hooks.
5. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back and stand up in a quarter squat-like manner by extending your knees and hips.
## Exercise technique
1. Take a couple of steps backward and position your feet into a shoulder-width stance.
2. Raise your heels into the air through the available range of motion of your ankles while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
3. In a controlled manner, allow your heels to descend toward the ground while continuing to keep your knees straight.
    1. Aim to tap the ground lightly with your heels in the bottom position.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
5. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before slightly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the machine’s shoulder pads to a level that initially allows you to stand under the pads with mildly bent knees, which will enable you to perform full range of motion calf raises.
    1. The appropriate shoulder pad height should make it easy for you to assume the starting position and also allow you to feel resistance from the machine both when your heels are fully lowered and fully raised.
    2. If the pads are too high, your knees may be straightened when initially standing under the pads, and you may lose tension from the machine when your heels are fully lowered.
    3. If the pads are too low, you may need to bend your knees excessively to stand under the pads, and you may be restricted from fully raising your heels.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin.
3. With your knees mildly bent, position the balls of your feet onto the foot plate with your heels hanging off the edge while your shoulders are snugly resting under the shoulder pads.
4. Straighten your knees to stand fully upright and receive the weight from the machine.
    1. Throughout the set, keep the balls of your feet firmly planted on the step.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they allow their knees to bend backward into hyperextension during this exercise, so keeping a straight knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels fully upward while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
    1. Once you have lifted your heels as high upward as your ankle mobility permits, you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. While you lift your body upward through ankle motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your heels to descend toward the ground through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits.
    1. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can below the balls of your feet to stretch your calves and train them most effectively.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calves (which you may feel on the backside of your knees or lower down your legs) at the bottom, you likely are not lowering through your full range of motion.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid quickly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep.
    4. You can optionally briefly pause in the fully stretched bottom position,
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After finishing your last rep, mildly bend your knees to lower the weight to its resting position (where it no longer provides any resistance to you) before stepping off the foot plate.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Firmly grasp an asymmetrical weighted implement (or a viable alternative) by its lighter end at a position that can provide your desired level of resistance.
    1. Asymmetrical weighted implements include adjustable dumbbells with plates loaded only on one side, hammers, and end-loaded baseball bats.
    2. As an alternative to an asymmetrical weighted implement, you can grasp certain symmetrically weighted cylindrical implements closer toward one end, which can provide the same training effect. Examples include a fixed-weight straight bar, a fixed-weight EZ bar, an adjustable EZ bar, and a cable straight bar attachment (like a lat pulldown bar).
    3. The resistance your forearm pronator muscles experience during this exercise will be dictated by how closely to one end you grasp the implement.
    4. For greater resistance, you can grasp farther away from the weighted end with an asymmetrically loaded implement or closer to whichever end you are holding with a symmetrically loaded implement.
    5. When performing this exercise for the first time with a particular implement, you may have to try out different grip positions to determine specifically where you should grasp the implement to provide the magnitude of resistance you wish to have.
2. Sit down on a seat (e.g., chair, flat exercise bench, plyometric box), and position your forearm (on the side holding the weight) on top of your thigh with a bent elbow and your hand slightly in front of your knee.
    1. You can situate your wrist just above your kneecap, which should allow the weighted implement to rotate freely without contacting any part of your leg.
3. If not already in this position, direct your palm to face in toward your opposite side with a straight wrist position and the heavier end of your weighted implement directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. From here, your wrist and forearms are in a neutral position as they would normally be when standing upright with your palms facing in toward your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your forearm resting on your thigh, progressively rotate your palm down toward the floor in a controlled manner through your available range of motion.
    1. Doing so will rotate the weighted end (if using an asymmetrically loaded object) or longer end (if using a symmetrically loaded object) in toward the midline of your body, such that this end moves in front of your torso rather than out to the side.
    2. Most people will have the forearm mobility to rotate until their palm is close to facing straight up, which will result in the weight’s handle being close to parallel to the floor, but some people may not be able to rotate quite as far, while others can rotate beyond that point.
    3. As you rotate your forearm, keep your wrist in a straight (neutral) position rather than letting the weight bend your wrist and pull your hand downward (which would bring the palm of your hand closer toward your knee if the wrist flexes).
2. After reaching your end range, reverse the movement until you have returned to the starting position by turning your palm to face in toward your opposite side.
    1. Doing so will lift the weighted end or longer end of the implement upward and outward in an arcing manner until the handle is vertically oriented.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your forearm’s supinator and your biceps.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s foot plate to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are standing on the foot plate.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the foot plate height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    3. To confirm whether you selected an appropriate foot plate height, you will have to stand on it and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
    4. A foot plate height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points will commonly allow you to hold the handles with slightly bent elbows.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin, and stand on the foot plate with the handles situated just in front of your thighs.
3. Firmly grasp the handles with slightly bent elbows as your palms are facing in toward each other.
4. Optionally lean your torso slightly forward at your hips by sitting your buttocks slightly backward with a slight knee bend.
    1. People commonly find lateral raises to feel more comfortable with a slight forward torso lean, but you can alternatively stand fully upright if you prefer to do so.
    2. Maintain your preferred torso position throughout your set, as you subsequently move your shoulders without moving your lower body or torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the handles by driving your elbows out and away from your sides until your upper arms are close to parallel with the ground.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    2. Focusing on moving your elbows in an arcing manner throughout this exercise can help you target the appropriate shoulder muscles.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by lowering your elbows in toward your sides to return the handles to the starting position.
    1. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arms downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to a barbell situated in a landmine attachment (or a viable substitute).
    1. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    2. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
    3. The diameter of the plates you select can influence the range of motion of this exercise, so you can use smaller diameter plates if you wish to row through a longer range of motion.
    4. Plates weighing 45lb or 20kg (which differ slightly), along with bumper plates of various weights, have a diameter near 18in (45cm) that can physically constrain the range of motion for your row by contacting your torso at the end of the lifting phase or by contacting the ground at the end of the lowering phase.
2. Stand directly to one side of the barbell and slightly behind the plates with the landmine’s pivot point behind you.
    1. Position the side you wish to train during this set closer to the bar.
    2. While you keep your opposite arm wherever you prefer throughout the exercise, the subsequent directions specifically refer to the arm closer to the barbell.
3. Sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hand is level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
4. Firmly grasp the end of the barbell’s shaft just behind the bearing (the cylinder separating the shaft from the sleeve where you add plates) with a straight elbow and a neutral grip, resulting in your palm facing in toward your side.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from now on.
6. While keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and elbow straight, drive through your legs to lift the bar slightly up and assume a slightly more upright torso position.
    1. The objective is to use your hip and thigh muscles to lift the bar just high enough that you can experience resistance from the weight when your elbows are fully straight in the bottom position of each rep, rather than having the plates touch the ground.
    2. In this position, your torso should ideally remain inclined closer to horizontal than to vertical, with your hips mostly bent. However, large-diameter plates may require a more upright torso to keep them off the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbow simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending your elbow and pulling your shoulder blade backward .
    1. Move your elbow through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping it tucked fairly close to your side with this neutral grip rather than notably flaring your elbow away from your side.
    2. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. If using large diameter plates, you may not be able to row as far backward compared to if you use smaller diameter plates.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbow to move downward and forward until your elbow has finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blade will naturally move forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar to its initial position by bending your knees and hips to lower the bar before repeating the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are seated.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the seat height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    3. To confirm whether you selected an appropriate seat height, you will have to sit down and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
    4. A seat height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points will commonly allow you to contact the arm pads with the outer aspects of your arms just above your elbows when keeping your upper arms near your sides.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin.
3. Sit on the seat with your upper arms by your sides and your elbows bent to near 90 degrees. Place the outer aspects of your arms against the arm pads, and grasp the handles with your palms facing in toward each other if your machine has handles.
    1. Seated lateral raise machines may be constructed with handles situated closer toward the machine's weight stack or further away in front of the machine’s weight stack.
    2. With a machine that has handles closer toward its weight stack, you should sit down with your chest facing the stack. These machines often have a chest pad onto which you can rest your chest.
    3. With a machine that has handles further away from its weight stack, you should sit down with your back directed toward the stack. These machines often have a back pad onto which you can rest your back.
    4. Some machine models lack handles for you to hold, but have a chest pad or a larger back pad that could inform you which is the standard direction for you to face while using them.
    5. A minority of machines may lack a chest pad, back pad, or handles, and correspondingly not have a clear way for you to face besides what may be presented on an instructional image on the side of the machine.
4. Based on your preference, you can sit with a fully upright torso, or you can lean slightly forward at your hips.
    1. Either torso position can allow you to perform this exercise effectively, so you can utilize whichever one feels most comfortable for your shoulders.
    2. You can try out each posture during a different set to determine your personal preference.
    3. If you opt to lean slightly forward and are using a machine with a chest pad, you may have to scoot slightly backward on the seat.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows out and away from your sides until your upper arms are close to parallel with the ground.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    2. Focusing on moving your elbows in an arcing manner throughout this exercise can help you target the appropriate shoulder muscles.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by lowering your elbows in toward your sides until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arms downward.
    3. Keep your arms snugly in contact with the arm pads throughout your set.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to a trap bar situated on the ground and positioning the high handles up (if your trap bar has high handles), stand within the middle of the trap bar with a hip-width to shoulder-width stance and your arms at your sides.
    1. You can certainly use a trap bar that does not have elevated handles, but the bar is easier to lift into the starting position when using high handles.
    2. Specifically how wide you situate your feet apart can be based on what feels most comfortable to you when picking up a weight off the floor with a deadlifting motion.
2. Hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handles when your elbows are straight, and your arms are lifted slightly out to your sides (to position your hands directly over the handles).
    1. Throughout this movement, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
3. Firmly grasp the center of the handles with your palms facing in toward each other.
4. While keeping your elbows straight, press your legs into the ground with enough force to feel the trap bar’s sleeves push up into the weight plates, but not enough force to lift the bar off the ground.
    1. Applying this pre-tension to the bar will likely produce an audible “clinking” sound.
    2. The initial submaximal pull allows you to check if you have grasped the handles correctly, which will be indicated by the handles not rotating forward or backward when applying the pre-tension.
    3. If you pick up a trap bar with your hands positioned slightly away from the middle of the handles, the trap bar can start rotating in a manner that makes it very difficult to hold and lift appropriately.
    4. Even for experienced lifters, it can be easy to grasp a trap bar’s handles without having their hands truly centered initially, so applying the pre-tension can make sure you are in your ideal starting position.
    5. If the bar starts rotating when you apply the pre-tension, re-center your grip, and apply the pre-tension again until the bar no longer rotates.
5. Brace your core and stand upright with the bar by extending your hips and knees while continuing to keep your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
    1. To brace effectively, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
    2. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    3. After the deep “belly breath,” you can continue thinking about tensing the front of your abdominals, sides of your abdominals, and lower back muscles simultaneously in a manner that allows you to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    4. As a helpful cue for your “360 degree brace,” you can tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you are bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    5. While your lower back muscles will work to maintain a rigid trunk position, you should primarily lift the bar with your hip and thigh muscles.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the bar upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the bar.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. If you have access to one, you can also use a fixed-weight straight barbell, which may be lighter than a typical 20kg (44lb) barbell that has sleeves on which to add weight plates.
2. Pick up the barbell with an underhand grip where your palms are facing forward away from you.
3. While holding the barbell, sit down on a flat exercise bench or chair and position your forearms on top of your thighs with your palms facing up and your wrists situated slightly in front of your knees.
    1. To achieve this position, you will be leaning forward at your hips when seated with your elbows bent.
4. Allow the force from the bar to bend your wrists and bring your hands downward such that your palms are facing forward.
    1. In this position, the bar should not be contacting your legs, so scoot your forearms forward if it is initially contacting your legs.
5. While maintaining your grip on the bar, let the bar roll down your fingers.
    1. This downward motion will occur from movement at the joints formed by your knuckles and the finger bones closest to your knuckles.
    2. After it rolls down your fingers, the bar should no longer be fully contacting the palm side of your knuckles, in contrast to when you typically maintain a firm grip on a bar with a fist-like hand position.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by curling your wrists and fingers simultaneously through their available range of motion while keeping your forearms resting on your thighs.
    1. All of the motion should be coming from your wrists and the joints your fingers form with your knuckles.
    2. About halfway through the lifting phase, your palms should be facing straight up when your hands are aligned with your forearms.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, when the bar is fully raised, your palms should be angled back toward you, and your fingers should be fully wrapped around the bar in a fist-like hand position.
    4. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the palm side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner as it rolls down your fingers until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed forward away from you.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm in addition to the hand muscles involved in gripping.## Setting up
1. Approach a step or a sturdy object that can function as a step. You should be by a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
    1. Based on the equipment at your disposal, a variety of objects can serve as your step. Make sure the object can support your weight, will not move once you are on it, and allows you to lower your heels through your full range of motion during the exercise.
    2. As an alternative to a staircase’s bottom step, you may select a power rack’s bottom beam if the beam is sufficiently tall.
    3. Additionally, a calf raise block, aerobic step, deadlift block, or thick bumper plate can each be positioned near a wall or power rack for stability. Depending on your bodyweight, anchoring an aerobic step with weight on top of it may be required to prevent the step from tipping.
2. After placing your hand onto the nearby surface for balance assistance, step onto the step with the front halves of your feet on the step while the back halves of your feet are hanging off the step.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your heels into the air through the available range of motion of your ankles while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heels to descend toward the ground through the available range of motion of your ankles while continuing to keep your knees straight.
    1. How far you are able to lower your heel will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. After your heels have fully descended, alternate between raising and lowering your heels until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest level, select your desired weight with the loading pin, and attach a bar with a straight (or mostly straight) body to the cable.
    1. Any bar that enables you to perform this exercise with an overhand grip can be similarly used, including a standard straight bar, a lat pulldown bar (with a long straight body and angled ends), and a multi-grip EZ bar.
2. While standing directly in front of the cable station and facing away from it, reach up overhead with bent elbows and fairly straight wrists to grasp the bar with an overhand grip such that your palms are facing forward.
    1. Maintain fairly straight wrists throughout your set.
3. While keeping your hands overhead and elbows bent, step forward and adopt a staggered stance with one foot forward in front of the other before moderately leaning forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this forward torso lean throughout your set.
    2. In this position, your hands should be behind your head, and you should feel tension from the cable. If that is not the case, walk forward until you feel tension from the cable.
4. Tuck your chin toward your chest and make sure your upper arms are situated parallel to the ground or raised up a little higher.
    1. Doing so will reduce the likelihood that the back of your head obstructs the path of the cable during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the bar forward and somewhat downward by extending your elbows until they are straightened, while not moving your upper arms.
    1. At the end of this motion, the bar should be situated in front of you.
    2. If the cable contacts the back of your head during this motion, you can lean forward a little more for additional clearance.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows through your available range of motion to move the bar backward and somewhat upward in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to continue keeping your upper arms stationary while all of the movement occurs from elbow motion rather than also involving shoulder motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the seat so that your upper arms can be parallel with the floor or a little lower while grasping the handles.
    1. After initially adjusting the seat height, spread your arms outward so that your hands are level with the handles.
    2. You may have to re-adjust the seat if your upper arms are not parallel with the floor or a little lower.
    3. Some people will find their shoulders feel more comfortable when the seat is set to a height that allows their hands to be a bit lower than their shoulders, compared to when their hands are completely level with their shoulders.
2. Adjust the pins above the handles to a position that allows you to grasp the handles with your hands as close as they can be to each other while your chest is facing the machine’s pad.
3. After sitting down with your chest against the pad, grasp the horizontal handles with an overhand grip such that your palms are facing down and slightly bend your elbows.
    1. Maintain the slight bend in your elbows throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Spread the handles apart and backward until your arms and torso form a “T-shape.”
2. Reverse the motion by bringing the handles forward and toward each other until they have returned to their starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates while the barbell is on the ground or in an elevated position, such as a power rack.
    1. Setting up will be easier when using an elevated position, especially if you are using relatively heavy loads for this exercise.
    2. The bar will be easiest to pick up if positioned at a height slightly lower than the level of your hands when standing upright with your arms relaxed at your sides.
2. Stand close in front of the middle of the bar with a hip-width stance and extended elbows before hinging at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
4. By extending your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar while continuing to keep your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the bar upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the bar.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a flat exercise bench or chair.
2. While holding the dumbbells, lean forward at your hips and position your forearms on top of your thighs with your palms facing upward and your wrists situated slightly in front of your knees.
3. Allow the force from the dumbbells to bend your wrists and bring your hands downward such that your palms are facing forward.
    1. In this position, the dumbbells should not be contacting your legs, so scoot your forearms forward if they are initially contacting your legs.
4. While maintaining your grip on them, let the dumbbells roll down your fingers.
    1. This downward motion will occur from movement at the joints formed by your knuckles and the finger bones closest to your knuckles.
    2. After they roll down your fingers, the dumbbells should no longer be fully contacting the palm side of your knuckles, in contrast to when you typically maintain a firm grip on dumbbells with a fist-like hand position.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the dumbbells upward by curling your wrists and fingers simultaneously through their available range of motion while keeping your forearms resting on your thighs.
    1. All of the motion should be coming from your wrists and the joints your fingers form with your knuckles.
    2. About halfway through the lifting phase, your palms should be facing straight up when your hands are aligned with your forearms.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, when the dumbbells are fully raised, your palms should be angled back toward you, and your fingers should be fully wrapped around the handles in a fist-like hand position.
    4. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the palm side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner as they roll down your fingers until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed forward away from you.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm in addition to the hand muscles involved in gripping.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. You can initially set the bar up on the floor or from an elevated position, such as from a power rack.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with extended elbows.
3. Grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder width grip and an overhand grip such that your palms are directed toward you.
4. While keeping your elbows extended, stand upright while gripping the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the bar up and lowering it down until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, but it may train your biceps somewhat less effectively than a curl with an underhand or neutral grip.## Setting up
1. Stand upright with your back up against a flat wall, so that the back of your heels, your buttocks, your upper back, and the back of your head are all in contact with the wall.
    1. Keep all of these body regions in contact with the wall throughout the exercise.
2. With your elbows bent to approximately 90-degree angles, raise your upper arms straight out to your sides until they are parallel to the floor.
    1. In this position, the backs of your elbows should be in contact with the wall or very close to the wall if the size of your upper arms limits contact.
3. Rotate your forearms upward and back toward the wall as you strive to position the backs of your forearms and wrists as close to contacting the wall as you can.
    1. How close you will be to contacting the wall fully with your forearms and wrists will be meaningfully affected by your shoulder mobility, so positioning will vary among different individuals, with some being able to reach full contact and others reaching their end range well before contacting the wall with their forearms.
    2. While it is natural to feel a stretch when you have reached your shoulder’s end range, you should not force yourself into any position that is aggravating if you happen to experience pain from a pre-existing issue.
## Exercise technique
1. Slide your arms up the wall through your available shoulder range of motion in a controlled fashion while you continue applying effort to rotate the backs of your forearms toward the wall.
    1. While you may not be able to rotate your forearms any closer to the wall, continuously having the intent to do so may help amplify the training effect of this exercise.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows, head, upper back, buttocks, and heels in full contact with the wall.
    3. This sliding motion should resemble the upper arm movement used while performing a jumping jack or making a snow angel.
    4. How far you are able to slide up the wall will be determined by your shoulder mobility, and you most likely will not be able to raise your arms as high as you would when lifting your arms out to the side in other body positions.
    5. Rather than focusing on keeping a 90-degree bend in your elbows, allow your elbows to extend if that feels natural to you while sliding your arms upward.
2. Reverse the motion by sliding your arms down the wall in a controlled fashion until your elbows are positioned fairly close to your sides.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows, head, upper back, buttocks, and heels in full contact with the wall.
    2. You can allow your elbows to bend if that feels natural to you while sliding your arms downward.
3. Alternate between sliding your arms up and down the wall until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can improve shoulder external rotation range of motion while engaging a variety of muscles situated on the back of your shoulder and in your upper back.## Setting up
1. Pick up a short looped elastic band and sit down on an object you can use as a seat such as a chair, exercise bench, or plyometric box.
    1. You will remain seated throughout this exercise.
2. Position the band around both of your feet with the band under and on top of the balls of your feet.
    1. The appropriate positioning will keep the band in place during the exercise.
3. Straighten your knees to move your feet out in front of you until only your heels remain on the ground, while the rest of your soles are off the ground.
4. If the band is slack, spread your thighs apart from each other until you feel meaningful tension from the band while allowing the band to rotate your feet inward (through ankle inversion).
    1. In this starting position for the exercise, the soles of your feet should be angled in toward the middle point between your feet (i.e., the midline of your body) and down toward the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your legs stationary, rotate your feet outward (via ankle eversion) against the band’s resistance through your available ankle motion.
    1. When imagining a laser pointer shooting out from the middle of your soles, the outward rotation of your feet should prompt the laser pointer to point out away from the middle point between your feet.
    2. When performing this exercise correctly, you should feel tension in the muscles on the outer aspects of your lower legs, a little in front of the outer aspect of your calf muscles.
    3. Particularly avoid letting your thighs rotate via hip motion, which would result in your knee caps moving.
    4. If you have difficulty keeping your legs stationary, you can hold onto your thighs to stabilize them while the motion exclusively comes from your ankles.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing the band to rotate your feet inward (via ankle inversion) in a controlled manner through your available ankle range of motion as you continue keeping your thighs stationary.
    1. When imagining a laser pointer shooting out from the middle of your soles, the inward rotation of your feet should prompt the laser pointer to point in toward the middle point between your feet.
    2. If the band becomes slack toward the end of this movement, you can spread your thighs somewhat further apart in order to experience resistance from the band throughout your full range of motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the fibularis muscles on the outer aspect of your lower leg that assist in actively stabilizing the ankle.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each pulley to its lowest height, connect a D-handle attachment, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. After standing centered between the cables with your sides facing the cable stacks, side step to grasp the handle on one side with your opposite hand, grasp the other handle with your free hand, and return to being centered between the cables.
    1. Your palms should be approximately facing each other as your hands are situated just in front of your thighs.
3. Slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips and slightly bend your elbows.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the handles out and away from your sides until your upper arms are slightly below parallel with the ground.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the handles back to your sides in a controlled manner.
3. Alternate between rsteps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a low incline angle (e.g., near 30 degrees) and set a pair of dumbbells under the head of the bench.
    1. As a different variation, which you can use if your bench does not have a low incline setting, you can alternatively select a moderate incline angle (e.g., near 45 degrees) and otherwise use the same technique for this exercise.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
3. Grasp the dumbbells, position your arms so that they are perpendicular to the ground with extended elbows, and rotate your arms so that your palms are facing in toward each other.
    1. Keep your elbows extended and your palms facing each other throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your arms straight up out in front of you through your available shoulder range of motion as you continue to keep your chest in contact with the bench.
    1. You can strive to raise your arms until they are close to parallel with the incline bench, but your shoulder mobility will affect how high you can raise your arms.
    2. If you notice your chest lifting up off the bench during this movement, that indicates that you most likely are dynamically extending your lumbar spine, which can occur as a compensation pattern instead of lifting your arm only with your shoulder girdle muscles.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering your arms in a controlled manner until your arms have returned to being perpendicular to the ground.
    1. There is no need to lower your arms extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the weights rapidly swing your arms down. 
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your traps along with your serratus anterior, front delt, side delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates.
    1. You can initially set the bar up on the floor or from an elevated position, such as from a power rack.
    2. As an alternative to a standard barbell, you can also use a fixed-weight straight bar.
2. While facing the middle of the bar in an approximately hip-width stance with your toes pointing forward, squat down or hinge at your hips in a deadlift-like motion with straight elbows until your hands are level with the bar.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder width underhand grip, resulting in your palms facing forward.
4. While keeping your elbows straightened, stand upright with the bar by extending (straightening) your hips and knees while keeping your back flat (instead of meaningfully rounding your lower back).
    1. Once upright, the bar should be directly in front of your thighs.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you use hip motion instead of lower back motion to help drive the bar up.
2. While keeping your elbows straight, sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and your torso to incline slightly forward.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
3. As you simultaneously drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body, curl the bar upward in an arcing manner by bending at your elbows while allowing your elbows to swing a bit forward.
    1. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. Continue keeping your lower back near a neutral position instead of arching back excessively.
    3. While you are intentionally using momentum to help your biceps and other arm muscles lift the weight, you should not be “cheating” to the extent that you feel out of control.
4. After your elbows have fully bent, lower the bar in a controlled manner, allowing your elbows to straighten and return to your sides until you return to the starting position with straight elbows and the bar just in front of your thighs.
    1. Keep your torso upright and hips stationary as your biceps and other arm muscles control the descent without assistance from the rest of your body.
    2. Your muscles are noticeably stronger during the lowering phase of an exercise compared to the lifting phase, so “cheating” during the lifting phase allows your biceps to experience greater loading during the more controlled lowering phase.
    3. If you are unable to control the lowering phase to any reasonable degree, you are likely using a weight that is excessively heavy relative to your current ability.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a seated bench or a flat exercise bench with your feet flat on the ground. As you are sitting down, place the dumbbells onto your thighs.
    1. If you use a seated bench, the back rest should be low enough that it will not be within the path of the dumbbell and restrict your range of motion for this exercise.
    2. The tall backrest of an adjustable bench, positioned fully upright, may be more likely to limit your range than a fixed seated bench with a backrest that does not reach your head.
2. Lift the dumbbells up to shoulder level with your elbows bent by curling the weight and marching your thighs up one at a time for assistance if needed.
    1. Your elbows should now be underneath and slightly in front of the dumbbells.
    2. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. Press the dumbbells up overhead in a shoulder press motion until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Point the back of your elbows forward with your palms facing in toward each other.
    2. The dumbbells should be vertically stacked over your shoulder.
    3. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the dumbbells behind your head in a controlled manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Predominantly keep your elbows tucked in as they are chiefly pointing forward rather than out to your side. However, you can allow your elbows to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you. If the dumbbells bump into each other, you are flaring your elbows excessively.
    2. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
    3. As you keep your elbows up, your upper arms should remain in a vertical position by the sides of your head.
2. Continue lowering the dumbbells until you have fully bent your elbows or can lightly tap the back of your shoulders with the dumbbells.
    1. It is normal to be able to bend the elbows beyond 90 degrees, but specifically how far you can bend your elbows may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps.
    2. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach their shoulders with the dumbbells, wherever you feel a notable stretching sensation in your triceps can be an appropriate end range.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weights the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the dumbbell into the starting position overhead.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the dumbbells are vertically above your shoulders.
    2. Continue keeping your elbow up as all the motion occurs from your elbows without moving your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the dumbbells toward the front of your shoulders like you would during a shoulder press.
6. Lower the dumbbells to your sides as you straighten your elbows and swing your elbows back by your sides.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable cable to its highest setting, or approach a cable lat pulldown machine.
    1. Either type of equipment can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. Connect a D-handle attachment to the cable and select your weight with the loading pin.
3. Stand with one side of your body facing the cable station so that your shoulder is in line with the cable. With your arm near the cable, grasp the handle firmly and direct your palm forward in the same direction your chest is facing.
4. Perform a controlled lunging motion into a half-kneeling position with your knee on the side you are holding the cable, resting on the ground, after stepping backward with that side’s leg.
    1. While doing so, allow your elbow to extend and your arm to elevate to be aligned with the cable.
    2. Maintain an upright torso and keep the center of your breastbone facing the same direction as the center of your hips, rather than rotating your trunk toward one side.
    3. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable machine with your elbow extended, so if the cable remains slack (which may occur if you have very long arms), you should position yourself a little farther away from the cable station before assuming the half-kneeling position.
    4. In this position, your arm should be elevated out to your side such that it forms half of a “Y-shape” with your torso, which resembles the position in which one of your arms would be at the top of an overhand wide grip lat pulldown.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbow downward and in toward your side, progressively bending it to pull the handle closer to your torso.
    1. During the initial portion of the pull, you will drive your elbow downward, but you will drive your elbow simultaneously in toward your side and downward during the second half of the pull.
    2. After the initial portion of the pull, when your upper arm is approximately parallel with the ground, the bony prominence on the back of your elbow should be pointing predominantly out to your side toward the cable station as your upper arm and elbow are flared out to your side, which forms half of a “T-shape” with your torso.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow being fairly close to the side of your torso at the end of the pull.
    4. Keep your forearm aligned in the same direction as the cable, which will naturally occur with the appropriate degree of elbow bend.
2. Reverse the movement by bringing the handle outward and upward in a controlled manner by allowing your elbow to move out away from your side and upward while it progressively extends until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. At the end of this motion, your elbow should be in an extended position, and your whole arm should be aligned in the same direction as the cable.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arm up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Approach a flat exercise bench or a sturdy object of similar height that allows you to place both hands on top of it, about shoulder-width apart.
    1. In addition to a plyometric box (or a pair of plyometric boxes, depending on the width), you can use an aerobic step elevated on top of risers at a gym.
    2. Outside a gym, you can use objects like a chair (that will not slide), a couch, or a park bench.
2. Sit down on the edge of the bench with an upright torso.
    1. You should be seated with the long part of the bench aligned with your sides.
3. Place your palms on the edge of the bench approximately shoulder width apart, resulting in your arms being directed straight down with straight elbows.
    1. The standard technique involves pointing your knuckles forward with your fingers wrapped around the edge of the bench.
    2. However, pointing your knuckles out to the sides with straight fingers may be preferable. Relative to the standard setup that internally rotates your shoulders, this approach can help you externally rotate your shoulders, as you point your biceps straight forward rather than angling them inward. This difference in shoulder position can result in some people finding bench dips to feel more comfortable for their shoulders when pointing their fingers out to the sides, compared to straight forward.
4. While supporting yourself with your hands, scoot forward until your buttocks are just off the bench. Position your legs out in front of you with straight knees.
    1. You should be bearing weight mostly through the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands).
    2. The back of your heels should be on the ground.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. As you keep them tucked close to your sides, bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your shoulder blades pulled back and down as you avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up. It can help to think about keeping your chest up.
    2. Keep your hips just in front of the bench rather than letting them drift forward.
    3. Avoid flaring your elbows out away from your sides. When viewed from behind, your elbows should remain aligned with your wrists rather than tracking noticeably outside your wrists.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their elbows are bent by at least 90 degrees, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training bench dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by pushing through the bases of your palms to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows straight down.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, scoot your hips back to sit on the bench.
## Description
While training your front delts and pec major to a lesser degree than other dip variations, this exercise will primarily develop your triceps with an emphasis on its lateral and medial heads.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the kettlebells with straight elbows.
2. Position your hands by the front of your outer thighs and direct your palms so that they are facing backward.
3. Slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain the slight forward lean throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the kettlebells upward by driving your elbows upward and out to your sides through a range of motion that feels comfortable to your shoulders while bending your elbows.
    1. Your mobility will affect how high you are comfortably able to lift the kettlebells, and there is no need to force your shoulders into a range that provokes any discomfort.
    2. While different individuals may be able to lift higher up and others not quite as high, people often can lift their upper arms until they are close to parallel to the ground for a comfortable and effective range of motion.
    3. The kettlebells should remain close to your torso throughout the exercise as they move vertically.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to descend in toward your sides as you lower the kettlebells in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with straight elbows.
    1. At the end of the lowering phase, the kettlebells should be situated in front of your thighs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad. 
    1. While you can initially adjust the seat to a height that situates your shoulders approximately level with the parallel handles, the seat height that is best for you depends on the specific machine you use, and trying out different positions may be helpful.
2. Adjust the chest pad to a position that allows you to grasp the parallel handles when your arms are fully outstretched in front of you with straight elbows.
    1. To row through your full range of motion, ideally place the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with your elbows fully straight.
3. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet flat on the floor before reaching fully forward with extended elbows to grasp the parallel handles firmly with your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. If you were able to grasp the handles with any amount of elbow bend, you could let go of the handles and adjust the chest pad before grasping the handles again.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward the sides of your torso by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked close to your sides with this grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Keep your chest in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner, progressively straightening until they are outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you train them more effectively.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. A typical barbell that weighs 20kg (44lb) can commonly be too heavy to use with appropriate technique for individuals who lack much wrist extension training experience, so a wrist extension variation that allows for lighter loads (e.g., seated dumbbell wrist extension) may initially be preferable.
    2. If you have access to one, you can also use a fixed-weight straight barbell, which may be lighter than a typical barbell that has sleeves on which to add weight plates.
2. Pick up the barbell with an overhand grip where your palms are facing back toward you.
3. While holding the barbell, sit down on a flat exercise bench or chair and position your forearms on top of your thighs with your palms facing down toward the floor and your wrists situated slightly in front of your knees.
    1. To achieve this position, you will be leaning forward at your hips when seated with your elbows bent.
4. Allow the force from the bar to bend your wrists and bring your hands downward such that your palms are directed at an angle down and back toward you.
    1. In this position, the bar should not be contacting your legs, so scoot your forearms forward if it is initially contacting your legs.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by extending your wrists through their available range of motion while keeping your forearms resting on your thighs.
    1. All of the motion should be coming from your wrists as your hands move upward.
    2. About halfway through the lifting phase, your palms should be facing straight down toward the floor when your hands are aligned with your forearms.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, when the bar is fully raised, your palms should be angled forward.
    4. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the top side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed at an angle down and back toward you.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the seven wrist extensor muscles on the side of your forearm opposite your palm.## Setting up
1. While the barbell is on the ground or in an elevated position, such as a power rack, prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or by leaving the barbell unloaded.
    1. Setting up will be easier when using an elevated position, especially if you are using relatively heavy loads for this exercise.
    2. The bar will be easiest to pick up if positioned at a height slightly lower than the level of your hands when standing upright with your arms relaxed at your sides.
    3. If you have access to one, you can also use a fixed-weight straight barbell, which may be lighter than a typical 20kg (44lb) barbell that has sleeves on which to add weight plates.
2. With your backside facing the bar, stand close in front of the middle of the bar with a hip-width stance and straight elbows before hinging at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
4. By extending your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar while continuing to keep your elbows straight and your lower back near a neutral position.
    1. Once upright, the bar should be directly behind your thighs.
    2. Maintain this upright position with straight elbows throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by curling your wrists through their available range of motion while keeping your arms stationary.
    1. All of the motion should be coming from your wrists, and the bar will move slightly farther behind your thighs as it simultaneously moves upward.
    2. Your palms should be angled upward once you have fully curled your wrists.
    3. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the palm side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed straight backward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine, sit down with your back in full contact with the backrest.
    1. The machine’s safety stoppers (which can be moved with one or two safety handles, depending upon the specific machine) should already be locked, and you will keep them locked throughout your set.
    2. If your machine has an adjustable seat, you can also initially adjust the seat to whichever position feels most comfortable to you, but the seat position is unlikely to affect this exercise.
    3. Your lower back should be in a fairly flat position rather than being meaningfully rounded, like in a slouched position, which could occur if you sit your hips excessively forward when sitting down.
2. With approximately a shoulder width stance, place the front halves of your feet onto the bottom of the foot plate with your heels hanging off the edge.
    1. Maintain this foot position throughout your set.
3. While grasping the hand grips, press the platform away from you by extending your knees until they are straight or only slightly bent.
    1. You should maintain this knee position (rather than bending and extending your knees) while performing this exercise until you are ready to finish your set.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they utilize a hyperextended knee position during this exercise, so keeping a straight or slightly bent knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the platform away from you by pointing the balls of your feet away from you (in a manner similar to stepping on a car’s gas pedal) through the available range of motion of your ankles.
    1. Motion should occur exclusively at your ankles, while knees and hips do not move.
2. In a controlled manner, reverse the motion by allowing the front halves of your feet to move back toward you through the available range of motion of your ankles.
    1. How far you are able to move will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to lower the platform back onto the safety stoppers by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Using an overhand grip, grasp an elastic band with both hands in front of your torso while standing upright with your feet in approximately a shoulder-width stance.
    1. Inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
2. Position your hands so that they are somewhat greater than shoulder width apart from each other, with slack taken out of the band.
3. With straight elbows, elevate your arms in front of your torso until they are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. In this position, your palms should be directed down toward the ground.
    2. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your hands apart and backward until your arms and torso form a “T-shape.”
    1. If you set up by grasping the band with your hands too close together, you may reach the limit of the band’s extensibility (as indicated by an abrupt increase in resistance from the band) prior to your hands being pulled entirely apart from each other. If you notice this occurring, bring your hands back together and adjust your setup by gripping the band with your hands further apart. Continuing to pull the band apart once a portion has fully stretched may cause the band to tear.
2. Reverse the motion by bringing your hands forward and toward each other until they have returned to their starting position.
    1. You should feel tension from the band throughout the entire range of motion.
    2. Do not bring your hands so close together that the band becomes slack.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a low incline angle (e.g., near 30 degrees) and set a pair of dumbbells under the head of the bench.
    1. As a different variation, which you can use if your bench does not have a low incline setting, you can alternatively select a moderate incline angle (e.g., near 45 degrees) and otherwise use the same technique for this exercise.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
3. Grasp the dumbbells, position your arms so that they are perpendicular to the ground with extended elbows, and rotate your arms so that your palms are facing forward.
    1. Keep your elbows extended and your palms facing this direction throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your arms upward and straight out to your sides through your available shoulder range of motion so that they form a “T-shape” with your torso as you continue to keep your chest in contact with the bench.
    1. Squeeze your shoulder blades together at the top as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
    2. You can strive to raise your arms until they are at least parallel to the floor, but your shoulder mobility will affect how high you can raise your arms.
    3. If you notice your chest lifting up off the bench during this movement, that indicates that you most likely are dynamically extending your lumbar spine, which can occur as a compensation pattern instead of lifting your arm only with your shoulder girdle muscles.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering your arms in a controlled manner until your arms have returned to being perpendicular to the ground.
    1. There is no need to lower your arms extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the weights rapidly swing your arms down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and sit down on a seated bench or a flat exercise bench with your feet flat on the ground. As you are sitting down, place the dumbbell onto one of your thighs as you are holding the handle with both hands wrapped around each other.
    1. If you use a seated bench, the back rest should be low enough that it will not be within the path of the dumbbell and restrict your range of motion for this exercise.
    2. The tall back rest of an adjustable bench positioned fully upright may be more likely to limit your range than a fixed seated bench with a back rest that does not reach your head.
2. Lift the dumbbell up to shoulder level with your elbows bent by curling the weight with both arms.
    1. To assist in moving the weight into position, you can march your thigh up while curling it.
3. Press the dumbbell up overhead with both arms in a shoulder press motion until your arms are vertical.
    1. The back of your elbows should be pointing forward.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
4. While keeping your elbows up, slightly bend your elbows to tilt one end of the dumbbell down.
5. Reposition your hands so that both palms are firmly contacting the higher end of the dumbbell. Cross your thumbs over each other firmly around the handle. Simultaneously cross your index fingers over each other, resulting in your thumbs and index fingers resembling close to a diamond shape.
    1. In this position, one end of the dumbbell should be above your hands, while the other end is below your hands.
    2. You will continue securely gripping the dumbbell in this manner throughout your set. Make sure that your thumbs stay wrapped around the handle for stability.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the dumbbell behind your head in a controlled manner while keeping your elbow up.
    1. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
    2. Predominantly keep your elbows tucked in as they are chiefly pointing forward rather than out to your side. However, you can allow your elbows to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    3. As you keep your elbows up, your upper arms should remain in a vertical position by the sides of your head.
2. Continue lowering the dumbbell until you have fully bent your elbows or can lightly tap your upper back with the dumbbell.
    1. It is normal to be able to bend the elbows beyond 90 degrees during this exercise, but specifically how far you can bend your elbows may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps.
    2. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach their upper back with the dumbbell, wherever you feel a notable stretching sensation in your triceps can be an appropriate end range.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the dumbbell overhead.
    1. You can finish the lifting phase with a slight bend in your elbows rather than fully straightening them. A slight bend will keep one end of the dumbbell tilted down and make it easier to maintain a secure grip with the higher end of the dumbbell resting on your palms.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows up as all the motion occurs from your elbow without moving your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you have completed the lifting phase of your last rep, firmly grasp the handle with your lower hand while keeping the palm of your upper hand against the side of the dumbbell.
    1. Grasping the handle with one hand will make it easier for you to lower the weight compared to if you kept both palms against one end of the dumbbell.
6. Use both hands to lower the dumbbell toward your chest before setting it down.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that places the handles slightly above shoulder level once you are seated.
    1. This seat height is an appropriate starting point for most people, but the ideal seat height specifically should allow you to press through your full range of motion while initially gripping the handles with your elbows stacked under the handles.
    2. If the seat is excessively high, you will not be able to position your elbows directly under the handles (when viewed from the side).
    3. If the seat is excessively low, your range of motion for this exercise will be limited.
    4. Some trial and error may be needed to find the best height for you as an individual.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine. 
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the backrest.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the backrest if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
4. With your palms facing in toward each other and elbows bent, firmly grasp the handles that are angled forward. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being vertical when viewed from the side.
    1. If your machine only has handles directed out to the sides that can allow an overhand grip but not a neutral grip, you cannot perform this specific variation. However, you can select the “overhand grip plate-loaded machine shoulder press” from this database as an alternative.
    2. Rather than having handles directed completely forward, which allows a pure neutral grip, some machines have handles angled between forward and out to the side, allowing a semi-neutral grip. 
    3. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    4. If you cannot comfortably position your elbows under the handles, mildly lower the seat height before resuming your setup.
    5. Situating your elbows under the handles and maintaining this alignment will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow tuck during this exercise.
5. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling, and maintain this posture throughout your set.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles up overhead until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain stacked under your wrists when viewed from the side. This will result in your elbows driving up in front of your body rather than being excessively flared out away from your sides.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your back snuggly against the backrest and your feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. With the appropriate seat height, you should finish the lowering phase with the handles slightly above shoulder level, as your elbows are lower than your shoulders.
    2. At the bottom, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers underneath them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows stacked underneath the handles, resulting in your forearms remaining in a vertical position when viewed from the side.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, upper pec major, triceps, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Position a barbell in the J-cups of a power rack set approximately to the height of your hips while standing or alternatively position a Smith machine’s bar to the same height.
    1. This exercise can be performed with the same technique whether you use a barbell and power rack or a Smith machine.
    2. If you use a power rack, situate the barbell snugly in contact with the back corner of the J-cups since the middle portion of certain J-cups can be wide enough to allow the barbell to roll forward or backward, which is undesirable for this exercise.
2. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your back facing the back of the power rack/Smith machine and firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside of shoulder width using an overhand grip that results in your palms facing down.
3. While holding the bar, walk your feet forward to recline your torso into a more horizontal position until your chest is underneath the bar. Simultanesouly allow your elbows to straighten, and bridge your hips up by squeezing your glutes.
    1. In the bridged position, which you will maintain throughout your set, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. For the standard technique, you will walk your feet forward until you can fully extend your knees with the back of your heels on the ground, while the soles of your feet are off the ground and facing forward.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your chest toward the bar by driving your elbows simultaneously downward and backward while progressively bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion while aiming for your chest to reach the bar.
    3. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your torso and lower body should remain nearly stationary in the bridged position that is akin to holding a reverse plank.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your chest move down away from the bar in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively straighten.
    1. Fully return to the starting position with your arms outstretched up in front of you.
    2. Continue keeping your knees, hips, and shoulders aligned.
    3. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally.
    4. While you do not have to lower yourself extremely slowly, you should actively resist the descent rather than abruptly dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that will allow your legs to be in an approximately horizontal position once you are in the starting position.
    1. After estimating where this setting may be, you can adjust it during later steps if warranted.
    2. Not all machines permit you to adjust the footplate height.
2. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow the pad to support the front of your hip bones without meaningfully restricting back motion when you set up with your feet on the footplate.
    1. If the footplate is positioned excessively close to the pad (resulting in the pad predominantly supporting your thighs), your hips will not be stabilized as well, which can make it more challenging to emphasize training your back muscles.
    2. If the footplate is positioned excessively far from the pad, the pad can restrict your lower back’s motion.
    3. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position.
3. Climb up onto the machine, and position your feet against the footplate with your lower legs between the leg rollers while the front of your hips are resting on the pad.
    1. The back of your lower legs should be snugly pressed into the top pair of leg rollers slightly above your heels.
    2. Your knees should be straight, and your soles should be firmly contacting the footplate.
    3. While you are setting your feet, you can use the handles for assistance.
    4. Some machines have a step that you can use to help you get into position.
    5. With the appropriate footplate setting, the front of your hip bones should be resting on the pad, while your torso is unsupported.
4. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
    2. Your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Round your entire back in a slow and controlled curling manner through the full range of motion that is comfortable for you.
    1. You may feel a stretching sensation in your back muscles in the bottom position, but you should not feel any discomfort at the end range. Only round your back as far as is comfortable.
    2. At the bottom, your abdomen may be curled around the rounded front half of the pad.
    3. Rather than moving only one part of your back, strive to round your lower, middle, and upper back.
2. Reverse the movement by extending (straightening) your back in a controlled manner without hinging at your hips.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, your back should be near a neutral position, which involves your lower back being mildly arched.
    2. For increasing the size and strength of your back muscles, there is no need to arch your back maximally like you would during a cobra yoga pose.
    3. Keep your glutes relaxed to the best of your ability as you focus on moving your spine rather than your hips.
    4. You should feel your spinal extensor muscles, which run along each side of the spine, contract to extend your back.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
4. After your last rep, you can grasp the handles to help you dismount the machine.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep intrinsic spinal extensor muscles that run along the length of your entire back.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a moderate incline angle, which is near 45 degrees.
    1. Whether the bench is set to a 45-degree angle or a somewhat steeper angle will not meaningfully affect the stimulus this exercise provides to your biceps and other arm muscles.  However, using an angle closer to 60 degrees may result in setting up being easier if you are using heavy weights.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and straddle the bench as you face toward the elevated head of the bench.
3. While keeping your toes on the ground, position the front of your torso on top of the bench with your chest at the head of the bench, so that your head is above the end of the bench.
    1. Transitioning to lying on top of the bench may be easier if you initially position the dumbbells against the head of the bench, lower your torso to the bench in a push-up-like motion, and then lift the dumbbells out to the sides off the bench to hang down.
    2. Once on the bench, your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are looking straight ahead when normally standing upright.
4. With your arms hanging straight down toward the ground with straight elbows, direct your palms to face forward as you hold the dumbbells with an underhand grip.
    1. You will maintain this underhand grip throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arms directed straight down toward the ground, curl the dumbbells upward in an arcing motion by bending your elbows until they are fully bent.
    1. While your forearms move through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    2. Avoid swinging your elbows forward.
2. After your elbows have fully bent, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled arcing manner until your elbows have returned to a fully straightened position with your forearms directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your upper arms directed straight down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hand while holding them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
    2. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    3. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    4. With the aforementioned strategy, make sure to set the dumbbells down onto the bench in a manner where they do not risk rolling off the bench.
    5. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
## Exercise technique
1. Walk forward while holding the dumbbells at your sides, maintaining an upright posture with your shoulders approximately level.
    1. While you will not swing your arms like you would when walking normally, your torso and lower body should look similar to how they would when you walk normally.
    2. Walk at a pace where you feel stable and balanced throughout the carry.
    3. If you use a weight that is fairly heavy to you, it is natural to take shorter strides compared to how you typically walk.
    4. Keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level throughout your set rather than letting them drop down to one side when taking each step.
2. Continue walking with the dumbbells until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down.
    1. Similar to the setup, it may be easiest to set the dumbbells down one at a time onto a flat exercise (or sturdy box) prior to picking up one dumbbell and reracking it, and then doing the same with the other dumbbell.
    2. The above approach can be particularly useful if you are rather fatigued after completing your set and could use a rest break prior to reracking the dumbbells.
## Description
Rather than concentrating the workload on one body region, this exercise trains a variety of muscle groups throughout your body in an integrated manner, including the muscles in your forearms, upper back, core, and lower body.

### **Straps for Carries**

Even when using chalk, the weight you can use or distance you can complete with a particular weight will commonly be limited by grip strength while performing a farmer’s carry, so this carry can be a viable means of training static grip strength. However, if you wish to increase the stimulus provided to muscles besides the forearm and hand muscles, you have the option of performing this carry with lifting straps. With the heavier loads or longer distances that straps typically facilitate, the exercise can be more challenging to your upper back and core muscles as they support your trunk and shoulder girdle in addition to your lower body muscles that stabilize your pelvis and propel you forward.

### Training Many Muscles Simultaneously

McGill et al. (2009) measured EMG activity of several core and lower body muscles as competitive strongmen performed a variety of movements that appear in strongman competitions, including the farmer’s walk, which was performed for several steps with a 75kg (167.6lb) weight in each hand. During the farmer’s walk, peak EMG activity was high for the internal abdominal obliques, lats, thoracic erector spinae, lumbar erector spinae, gluteus medius, gluteus maximus, and rectus femoris. The external abdominal obliques and biceps femoris also exhibited moderate peak EMG activity, while the rectus abdominis exhibited low EMG activity. The abdominal muscles and rectus femoris demonstrated their peak activity during the walking portion of the carry, while the lats, erector spinae and biceps femoris demonstrated their peak activity during the initial lifting phase. The gluteal muscles exhibited peak activity during the initial lifting phase and during the first step of the carry. As indicated by these findings, the farmer’s carry can train a large amount of musculature simultaneously, which can result in this exercise being metabolically demanding when performed at a high relative intensity, especially for longer duration sets. Correspondingly, challenging sets of farmer’s carries may be able to train your cardiovascular system more robustly than most resistance training exercises that localize the workload to a smaller amount of total muscle mass.## Setting up
1. After ensuring that your suspension trainer is appropriately anchored to a sufficiently sturdy elevated structure (such as the top of a power rack or a pull-up bar), adjust its straps to a length that situates the handles approximately at the level of your waist.
    1. If you are familiar with TRX straps but unfamiliar with the term “suspension trainer,” TRX is a specific brand name that popularized suspension trainers (the general type of equipment) to enough of a degree that people may colloquially refer to suspension trainers in general as TRX straps.
    2. To check that your suspension trainer is anchored firmly, you can tug down on the straps and make sure they are stable before continuing to set up for this exercise.
2. Sit down on the ground with your legs outstretched in front of you and the handles situated a little in front of your torso before firmly grasping the handles with a neutral grip where your palms are facing in toward each other.
3. While holding the handles, allow your elbows to straighten as you lean backward. Simulatenasouly drive the back of your heels into the ground while squeezing your glutes to bridge your hips upward with straight knees.
    1. In the bridged position, which you will maintain throughout your set, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. For the standard version of this exercise, you can aim to have the straps directed vertically down toward the ground, so you can walk your feet forward if you notice that the straps are initially angled somewhat backward.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your chest up toward the handles by driving your elbows simultaneously downward and backward while progressively bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked close to your sides with this exercise rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion while aiming for the front of your chest to reach near the level of the handles, which will typically result in your elbows being aligned with your torso or a little behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    3. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your torso and lower body should remain nearly stationary in the bridged position that is akin to holding a reverse plank.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your chest move down away from the handles in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively straighten.
    1. Fully return to the starting position with your arms outstretched up in front of you.
    2. Continue keeping your knees, hips, and shoulders aligned.
    3. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally.
    4. While you do not have to lower yourself extremely slowly, you should actively resist the descent rather than abruptly dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a dumbbell with one hand, carry it over to a flat bench, and position yourself near the base of the bench and directly to its side with your side that is holding the dumbbell farther away from the bench.
    1. If you have to carry the dumbbell a meaningful distance before reaching your bench and your grip is somewhat fatigued, you can place the dumbbell on the bench before picking it up with your opposite hand to start your set with the side that does not have a fatigued grip.
2. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips, reach out with your free arm to place its hand on the bench with a straight elbow, and place your knee (on the side not holding the dumbbell) onto the bench.
    1. In this position, your torso should now be reasonably close to being parallel to the ground, and your lower back should be flat rather than being meaningfully rounded.
    2. While setting up, keep your elbow straight on the side holding the dumbbell and allow that arm to hang down toward the ground with your shoulder blade naturally pulled forward toward the ground from the weight.
    3. Based on your comfort and preference, you can place the open palm of your free hand onto the bench, or you can make a fist and place your knuckles onto the bench.
    4. Your foot on the side of the dumbbell should remain firmly in contact with the floor and be sufficiently far behind the dumbbell that your leg does not restrict your row. If you initially do not feel well-balanced, you can try positioning this foot somewhat further out to the side to widen your base of support.
3. Direct your palm so that it is facing forward, and keep it facing forward to use an underhand grip throughout the row.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Drive your elbow backward and upward in an arcing motion to pull the dumbbell toward your hip. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade backward, and keep your elbow tucked by your side rather than flaring it outward.
    1. When allowing your elbow to bend naturally, your forearm should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    2. At the top of the row, your elbow should be aligned with your torso or slightly behind your torso.
    3. Keep your torso in a mostly fixed position with your chest facing a similar direction throughout this movement. A mild degree of torso rotation is not problematic, but avoid letting it become excessive and turning the exercise into a cheat row.
    4. While performing this arcing motion, the dumbbell should not move straight upward in a vertical path.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled arcing motion until your arm is directed down toward the ground with your elbow straightened.
    1. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blade forward, which will stretch your upper back muscles and result in your working side’s shoulder being mildly closer to the ground than your other side’s shoulder at the bottom of the row.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight rapidly pull your arm down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, raise your legs up and out in front of you with straight knees until your legs are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. In this position, your torso and legs should resemble an “L-shape” when viewed from the side, which results from your legs forming a right angle with your torso.
    2. If you do not have the hamstring flexibility to utilize what is considered the standard “textbook form” with your knees fully straight as your legs are parallel to the ground, you can situate your legs as closely as you can to this position.
    3. Individuals with limited hamstring flexibility can either keep their thighs parallel to the ground with their knees mildly bent, or they can lift their legs up as close to parallel to the ground as they can reach with straight knees.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows and continuing to keep your legs lifted parallel to the ground.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows being bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps, but different individuals may have different ranges of motion that their bodies enable.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, rectus abdominis, and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Adjust a pair of securely anchored gymnastic rings to be fairly close to shoulder width apart and elevated above your head.
    1. Ideally, the rings should be reasonably close to the height of your palms when standing upright with your arms raised straight up overhead. However, a lower height can be appropriate as long as you can hang on the rings without your legs touching the ground when your knees are bent.
    2. You may also use rings beyond your reach if you initially place a sturdy box, an aerobic step, or a bumper plate on the ground as a step to reach the rings. While not required, elevating yourself to reach high rings may help you set up with a more stable hand position compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the rings.
    3. If the rings have not been previously anchored, you will naturally have to anchor them to a sturdy structure that is able to support your bodyweight. Potential anchor points include the top of a power rack, a pull-up bar, a monkey rig, and a ceiling-mounted anchor that is designed for rings or suspension trainers.
    4. While positioning the rings at shoulder width is appropriate, you can adjust the exact distance between them to your preference.
2. Stand under the rings, reach up overhead, and firmly grasp them with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip)
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the rings with the rings pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the rings closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the rings can slide up your hands while hanging on them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
3. Lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the rings with straight elbows.
    1. You only need to bend your knees as much as is required to keep your feet above the ground.
    2. If you initially used a step to reach the rings and the step is positioned somewhat behind the rings, you may be able to keep your knees straight and still keep your feet off the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the rings with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will place you in a more advantageous position to start your pull compared to if you kept your chest facing exclusively horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to shrug up from the force of your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back places you into a position akin to a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the rings by driving your elbows down and toward your sides as you progressively bend them. Allow your palms to rotate naturally in a manner that feels comfortable to you.
    1. People commonly perform this exercise by progressively turning their palms toward each other, so that they face each other at the top of the pull-up, after facing forward at the bottom. That can certainly be an effective technique, but it is not the only viable technique, since rotating your palms in a way that feels right to you as an individual is most important.
    2. Since the rings are anchored, your elbows will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    3. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms near the sides of the torso and elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, individuals may have different ranges of motion that their bodies allow.
    4. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. If you rotate your palms to face each other while pulling yourself up, you can let them rotate forward as you descend.
    2. You do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than just letting gravity pull you down abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet to the ground.
## Description
This exercise can train your lat and teres major especially well while also effectively training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell on the side you are about to train, and stand over one end of a flat exercise bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space behind you. With this equipment, you would straddle the bench and walk forward toward the J-hooks.
    2. With a portable exercise bench, your back should face the head of the bench, as you would with a dumbbell bench press.
    3. All the directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
    4. Throughout the exercise, you can situate your free arm wherever is comfortable to you.
2. Sit down on the bench while lifting the dumbbell onto your upper thigh near your abdomen.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
    2. Your palm should be facing inward with your upper arm by your side.
    3. To move a heavy dumbbell efficiently into the proper position, you can initially situate the dumbbell on the front of your thigh while standing up. Then you can rest it on your thigh while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbell back toward your chest with your elbow bent, lean back to lie down on the bench. Your head should be right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent. You will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
4. In a bench pressing motion, press the dumbbell up until your elbow is straight and your arm is directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a single arm dumbbell bench press rep), the dumbbell should be sitting on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a reasonably straight wrist. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    2. While your wrist does not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the dumbbell with your wrist cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the dumbbell hard will usually keep your wrist from cocking back excessively.
    3. Based on what feels most comfortable, you can have your palm facing in toward your other side (with a neutral grip), forward (with an overhand grip), or angled inward and forward. During your set, you can similarly allow your palm to rotate within this range if that feels comfortable to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbow to lower the dumbbell in a controlled arcing manner while keeping your elbow up.
    1. Predominantly keep your elbow tucked in, but you can allow it to flare slightly out away from your side if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    2. Avoid letting your elbow drift back and down with the dumbbells through shoulder motion. When viewed from the side, the proper elbow position should result in your upper arm remaining in a vertical position.
    3. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearm hinging at your elbow without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbow as the center of an analog clock, while your forearm is the hand that rotates about the center elbow during this exercise.
2. Finish lowering the dumbbell just before it reaches your forehead or the bench.
    1. If you are using an overhand grip (with your palm facing forward to start), you will finish with the dumbbell just above your forehead.
    2. If you are using a neutral grip (with your palms facing inward), your head may not be in the way of the dumbbell, so you can finish with the dumbbell just above the end of the bench.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way, especially when the weight approaches your head.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbow to lift the dumbbell into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbow fully straightened as the dumbbell is vertically above your shoulder.
    2. Continue keeping your upper arm in a vertical position as you avoid pushing your elbow forward (toward your chest) through shoulder motion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the dumbbell toward your side like you would during a neutral grip dumbbell bench press.
    1. Regardless of the palm position you used for your set, it is easier to reposition yourself when your palm is facing in toward your other side.
6. Transfer the dumbbell to your other side and repeat the exercise.
    1. Once you have finished training your other side and have performed step 5, march your thighs up until they are close to being vertical before rocking forward to sit up with the dumbbell on your upper thigh.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar with parallel handles that are near shoulder-width apart, reach up and firmly grasp the handles with your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar than if you jumped up from the ground to reach it.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will put you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back puts you in a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending them.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows will not actually drive downward, but having this intent helps pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine and adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad.
    1. While you can initially adjust the seat to a height that situates your shoulders approximately level with the horizontal handles, the seat height that is best for you depends on the specific machine you use, and trying out different positions may be helpful.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, adjust it to a position that allows you to grasp the horizontal handles when your arms are fully outstretched forward in front of you with extended elbows.
    1. To row through your full range of motion, ideally place the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with your elbows fully straight.
3. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet flat on the floor.
4. Reach fully forward with straight elbows to grasp the horizontally directed handles firmly with your palms facing down (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. If your machine does not have an adjustable chest pad and the handles are far enough from you to make it difficult to grasp both of them simultaneously, it may be easier for you to grasp one handle at a time.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward you by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, rather than trying to keep them tucked close to your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a closer underhand or neutral grip).
    2. Keep your chest in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner, progressively straightening until they are outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you train them more effectively.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands well outside shoulder-width apart.
    1. With a wide grip, your arms should form a “Y-shape” with your torso.
    2. Specifically, how wide you grasp the bar is up to your preference, but it should be clearly wider than the grip width you would use for a standard moderate grip-width pull-up
    3. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    4. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up onto a sturdy box, an aerobic step, or a bumper plate to place the bar within reach.
    5. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar than if you jumped up from the ground to reach it.
    6. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    7. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will put you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back puts you in a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides as you bend them.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows will not actually drive downward, but having this intent helps pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you would like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, select your weight with the loading pin, and connect a D-handle attachment to the cable.
2. Stand with the side of one shoulder (on the same side of your body you intend to train) facing the cable station.
3. Squat down and grasp the handle firmly with the hand closer to the cable station.
    1. Keep your elbow straight and your palm facing in toward your side.
    2. When squatting down (and up during the next step), keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
4. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the handle while continuing to keep your elbow straight.
    1. You can situate your resting hand wherever you feel helps maintain your balance, such as raised out to the side or resting on your hip.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the side in which you are holding the cable.
5. Take a step out to the side against the cable’s resistance while keeping your straight arm aligned with the cable. Simultaneously allow the cable’s resistance to pull your shoulder blade downward and out toward the pulley.
    1. Your arm should naturally be directed diagonally out to the side and downward in line with the cable.
    2. Due to the cable’s resistance, your working side’s shoulder blade should be a bit lower and further away from the midline of your back compared to your resting side’s shoulder blade.
    3. You should currently feel resistance from the cable, so take another step away from the cable station if you do not feel resistance.
6. Position your feet about shoulder width apart with your knees slightly bent in an athletic stance and your chest up.
    1. Ultimately, you want to adopt a stance that helps you feel stable and balanced, so you can modify your stance based on what helps accomplish that for you personally.
## Exercise technique
1. As you keep your elbow straight, shrug your shoulder and shoulder blade upward while simultaneously pulling them in toward your body’s midline.
    1. The objective is to move your shoulder and shoulder blade diagonally in the opposite direction to which the cable is pulling them.
    2. Once you can no longer shrug upward and inward, you have reached the end range. In this position, your working side’s shoulder should be higher than your resting side’s shoulder.
    3. Throughout the set, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    4. Keep your torso upright as you avoid letting the cable’s resistance bend your torso toward the pulley, as would otherwise occur during a side-bend exercise.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by allowing your shoulder and shoulder blade to move diagonally downward and to the side toward the pulley.
    1. Move through your full range of motion, which may prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in the region of your upper trap (on the back of your neck and uppermost upper back) at the bottom.
    2. At the end of this motion, your working side’s shoulder should be positioned a little lower than your resting side’s shoulder due to the downward stretch that the cable can provide.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm toward the pulley.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your upper trap and levator scapulae while also training your middle trap and rhomboids to a greater degree than purely vertical shrugs.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead with one arm to firmly grasp the bar with your palm facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palm (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide upward during the dead hang, which pulls on your hand skin in a manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. With your elbow straight and your hand firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
    1. A dead hang is a static exercise, so you will simply hang on the bar while keeping your feet off the ground to perform it.
2. Continue hanging on the bar until you have completed your set’s target duration and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. Following the set, lower your feet onto the ground (or step) and let go of the bar.
## Description
While simultaneously improving overhead shoulder mobility, this exercise can increase your grip strength by training your forearm and hand muscles that function as finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
    1. Maintain this neutral grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl both dumbbells upward by fully bending your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position a barbell in the J-cups of a power rack set approximately to the height of your hips while standing or alternatively position a Smith machine’s bar to the same height.
    1. This exercise can be performed with the same technique whether you use a barbell and power rack or a Smith machine.
    2. If you use a power rack, situate the barbell snugly in contact with the back corner of the J-cups since the middle portion of certain J-cups can be wide enough to allow the barbell to roll forward or backward, which is undesirable for this exercise.
2. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your back facing the back of the power rack/Smith machine and firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width underhand grip that results in your palms facing up.
3. While holding the bar, walk your feet forward to recline your torso into a more horizontal position until your chest is underneath the bar. Simultanesouly allow your elbows to straighten, and bridge your hips up by squeezing your glutes.
    1. In the bridged position, which you will maintain throughout your set, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. For the standard technique, you will walk your feet forward until you can fully extend your knees with the back of your heels on the ground, while the soles of your feet are off the ground and facing forward.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your chest toward the bar by driving your elbows simultaneously downward and backward while progressively bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked close to your sides with this underhand grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Pull through your full range of motion while aiming for your chest to reach the bar.
    3. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your torso and lower body should remain nearly stationary in the bridged position that is akin to holding a reverse plank.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your chest move down away from the bar in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively straighten.
    1. Fully return to the starting position with your arms outstretched up in front of you.
    2. Continue keeping your knees, hips, and shoulders aligned.
    3. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally.
    4. While you do not have to lower yourself extremely slowly, you should actively resist the descent rather than abruptly dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to a barbell situated in a landmine attachment (or a viable substitute).
    1. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    2. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
2. Stand upright in a near hip-width stance with the free end of the bar between the middle of your feet.
3. Simultaneously bend your hips and knees to squat down until you are low enough to grasp the bar.
    1. Keep your back flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your back.
4. Firmly grasp the end of the bar with your fingers cupped around it, as one hand is directly in front of the other.
    1. The little finger of one hand should be touching the index finger of your other hand.
    2. Your palms should be facing in toward the bar.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
6. Explosively lift the bar up to chest height while rapidly repositioning your hands under the bar with your elbows bent. Allow the end of the bar to rest on the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with your fingers cupped around the bar as one hand is directly in front of the other.
    1. Your knees and hips should now be straight, like when you are normally standing upright, but allow your body to lean mildly forward at your ankles.
    2. Avoid leaning back with an excessively arched lower back position throughout the rest of the setup and during your set.
7. Move the bar to the side you are about to train so that its end is slightly in front of that side’s shoulder.
8. Maintain a firm grip on the end of the bar with the hand on the side you are about to train while releasing your other hand.
    1. You can now keep your free arm in whichever position you prefer, such as resting by your side.
    2. The subsequent directions will apply to the side on which you continue holding the bar, since that is the side you will be training.
9. Pivot your feet and your whole body so that the front of your shoulder is directly facing the base of the landmine.
    1. Your elbow should be positioned by your side or slightly in front of it, rather than being behind your side.
    2. Your wrist should be mostly straight rather than cocked back. Squeezing the bar hard can help you maintain the appropriate wrist position.
10. Take a small step forward on the side you are not training to adopt a staggered stance for greater stability.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes like you are trying to crush a walnut. Simultaneously, brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core and tightening your glutes throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the bar upward and mildly forward until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. As a cue, you think about punching upward and outward while driving your elbow in this direction.
    2. Your hand will move diagonally since the bar will naturally move in an arcing manner rather than in a purely vertical path. At the top, your arm should be angled upward and forward.
    3. Rather than intentionally trying to keep it pulled back and packed down, allow your shoulder blade to move freely. It is natural for the shoulder blade on your working side to move forward as you press.
    4. Keep your shoulder, elbow, and wrist stacked in alignment with the bar from your perspective of looking forward.
    5. Continue keeping your knees and hips stationary rather than using them to help you lift the weight.
    6. Throughout your set, avoid leaning your torso back via excessive lower back arching.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until its end is slightly in front of your shoulder.
    1. Move your elbow downward and back toward your side while progressively bending it.
    2. At the bottom, your elbow should be tucked near your side or slightly in front of it. Avoid bringing it back to the extent that it is behind your back, which would require lowering the bar excessively.
    3. Allow your shoulder blade to move naturally backward with your elbow.
    4. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
5. Once you have completed the lowering phase of your last rep, transfer the bar to your other side and repeat the exercise.
6. After finishing the set with your other side, firmly grasp the bar with your free hand as you pivot toward the side in which you just finished the set.
7. Guide the bar toward the ground as if you were performing the lowering phase of a row while sitting your hips back like during a deadlift.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your upper pec major, lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Pick up a dumbbell in one hand and sit down on a flat exercise bench with enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space.
2. Lift the dumbbell up onto your thigh and grasp its handle with both hands as your palms face inward.
3. While simultaneously marching your thigh up to help lift the dumbbell back toward your upper abdomen, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench, as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent, and you will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbell with both hands right above your upper abdomen.
4. Press the weight up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
5. Reposition your hands so that both of your palms are firmly contacting one end of the dumbbell with your thumbs crossed over each other and your index fingers crossed over each other, which can result in your thumbs and index fingers resembling close to a diamond shape.
    1. In this position, the dumbbell’s handle should be vertical, with one end of the weight below your hands and the other end above your hands.
    2. Continue securely gripping the dumbbell in this manner throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows fairly straight, lower the weight behind your head in a controlled arcing manner through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. You should avoid meaningfully bending your elbows, but you can allow them to bend mildly as your arms move through motion at your shoulders while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
    2. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back during the descent.
    3. People can often lower the weight until their upper arms are close to parallel with the ground, but how far you can descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    4. Once you feel that you have reached the end range to which your shoulders can safely stretch, you have reached the end range for you as an individual.
2. Reverse the movement by pulling your arms upward and forward in an arcing motion to lift the weight up until you have returned to the starting position with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows fairly straight as you move your arms through shoulder motion while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your lats, teres major, and rear delt, which are assisted by the lowest portion of your pec major in the overhead position of each rep.## Setting up
1. Connect a V-bar lat pulldown/row attachment (or a similar neutral grip attachment) to the cable and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. You can use any attachment that lets you grasp it with a closer-than-shoulder-width neutral grip.
    2. Note that a V-bar for pulldowns or rows, which is also known as a double-D handle attachment, differs from the V-bar attachment that can be used for triceps pushdowns.
2. Adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
3. While facing the bar and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the bar with a neutral grip, such that your palms face each other.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
4. While holding the bar, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
5. In the seated position, slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight backward lean throughout your set.
    2. From this position, your elbows should be straight, with your arms overhead and slightly forward (rather than completely vertical), and you should feel tension from the cable station holding your arms in this position.
    3. If you notice the cable remains slack in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), you may need to use a different attachment and a wider grip to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for your lat pulldown.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar down toward your upper chest by driving your elbows down toward your sides as you progressively bend them.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. After the initial half of the pull, when your upper arms are approximately parallel with the ground, the bony prominence on the back of each elbow should be pointing predominantly forward as your upper arms are situated in front of your body, rather than having your elbows and upper arms predominantly flared out to your sides.
    3. During the second half of this pull, drive your elbows simultaneously backward and downward until they are in line with your torso or slightly behind it.
    4. Maintain the slight backward lean of your torso you adopted while setting up, rather than leaning further backward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the bar in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The initial steps of the setup are the same as a conventional deadlift with an underhand grip.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the middle of your feet under the middle of the bar.
3. Hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. Throughout this movement, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width underhand grip such that your palms are facing forward.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
6. Stand upright with the bar by extending your hips and knees while continuing to keep your elbows straight and your lower back near a neutral position.
    1. While your lower back muscles will be working to maintain a rigid trunk position, lifting the bar upward should chiefly occur from your hip and thigh muscles extending your hips and knees.
    2. Keep the bar very close to your legs as you lift the bar rather than allowing the bar to drift forward.
7. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and moderately incline your torso until you have lowered the bar near your knees.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight, back flat, and core braced.
    2. Allow your knees to bend naturally while sitting your hips back as if you were closing a car door with your buttocks, while your hands are carrying something.
    3. Maintain this hinged position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar up toward your lower abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward as you pull your shoulder blades back.
    1. Throughout your set, maintain the same degree of torso inclination while keeping your back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    2. As movement occurs at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, your lower body should remain essentially stationary. Resist the urge to use your legs to lift your torso into a more upright position at the end of the lifting phase, which is the most difficult part of the exercise.
    3. Keep your elbows tucked close to your sides as they bend rather than meaningfully flaring your elbows out away from your sides.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner. Allow your elbows to move downward and forward until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. At the end of the lowering phase, you should be in the starting position with the bar just in front of your knees.
    2. Let your shoulder blades naturally spread apart.
    3. There is no need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but make sure that you actively control the weight rather than simply letting gravity abruptly yank the bar down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
4. Set the bar down onto the ground by reversing the technique you used to pick it up (i.e., bending at your hips and knees as opposed to just rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lats, teres major, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates while the barbell is on the ground or in an elevated position, such as a power rack.
    1. Setting up will be easier when using an elevated position, especially if you are using relatively heavy loads for this exercise.
    2. The bar will be easiest to pick up if positioned at a height slightly lower than the level of your hands when standing upright with your arms relaxed at your sides.
2. Stand close in front of the middle of the bar with a hip-width stance and extended elbows before hinging at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
4. By extending your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar while continuing to keep your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
## Exercise technique
1. Mildly hinge at your hips while slightly bending your knees to lower the bar until it is positioned slightly above your knees.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
2. Vigorously extend your hips and knees while simultaneously shrugging your shoulders upward in an explosive manner through your available range of motion to lift the bar upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your elbows extended, your lower back near a neutral position, and your neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
3. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend through your available range of motion while hinging at your hips and bending your knees until the bar has returned to slightly above your knees.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance. Hold the dumbbell at your side with a straight elbow as your palm faces in toward your thigh.
    1. Throughout the exercise, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting by your side. You can optionally  set up near a sturdy surface (like a power rack) where you can place your free hand for additional stability if you wish to do so.
    2. All the directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
2. Lift the dumbbell up near the front of your shoulder with your elbow bent by curling the weight and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbell into position if it is too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbell mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbow and mildly swinging it forward to lift the weight up.
    2. Your elbow should now be underneath and slightly in front of the dumbbell.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. Press the dumbbell up overhead in a shoulder press motion until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. You can point the back of your elbow forward and out to the side at an angle.
    2. The dumbbell should be vertically stacked over your shoulder.
    3. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbow to lower the dumbbell behind your head in a controlled manner while keeping your elbow up.
    1. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearm hinging at your elbow without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbow as the center of an analog clock, while your forearm is the hand that rotates about this center elbow during the exercise.
    2. As you keep your elbow up, your upper arm should remain in a vertical position by the side of your head.
2. Continue lowering the dumbbell until you have fully bent your elbow or can lightly tap your upper back with the dumbbell.
    1. It is normal to be able to bend the elbow beyond 90 degrees, but specifically how far you can bend your elbow may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps.
    2. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach their upper back with the dumbbell, wherever you feel a notable stretching sensation in your triceps can be an appropriate end range.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbow to lift the dumbbell into the starting position overhead.
    1. You should finish with your elbow fully straightened as the dumbbell is vertically above your shoulder.
    2. Continue keeping your elbow up as all the motion occurs from your elbow without moving your shoulder.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the dumbbell toward your shoulder like you would during a shoulder press.
6. Lower the dumbbell to your side as you straighten your elbow and swing your elbow back by your side.
7. Transfer the dumbbell to your other hand and repeat the exercise with that side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells, and stand over one end of a flat exercise bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space behind you. With this equipment, you would straddle the bench and walk forward toward the J-hooks.
    2. With a portable exercise bench, your back should be facing the head of the bench like when you would during a kettlebell bench press.
2. Sit down on the bench while holding the kettlebells by your sides with your palms facing forward.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
3. Curl the kettlebells up toward the front of your shoulders by bending your elbows, turning your palms to face in toward each other at the top. Move your elbows slightly in front of your ribcage, and allow the bodies of the kettlebells to rest against your forearms while keeping your wrists straight.
    1. You will be able to rest the bodies of the kettlebells against your forearms by angling them slightly inward as your wrists are slightly closer to the midline of your body than your elbows.
    2. To help get the weights into position, feel free to perform a cheat curl-like motion where you initially generate some momentum by swinging the weights mildly backward with straight elbows before swinging your elbows forward while curling them.
4. Lean back to lie down on the bench while marching your thighs up to help when transitioning. Keep your forearms in a mostly vertical position with the kettlebells contacting them as your elbows remain bent and close to your torso.
    1. To maintain a mostly vertical forearm position, you should let your elbows lower toward the ground when leaning back. When sitting upright, this would be the same motion as moving your elbows back slightly behind your ribcage.
    2. Once you are lying down, return your feet to the ground in a stable position with your knees bent.
    3. After completing this step, you should now be in essentially the same position as the bottom of a kettlebell bench press.
5. In a bench pressing motion, press the kettlebells up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling. Direct your palms forward at the top.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the top of a bench press rep), your wrists should be mostly straight while you are holding the kettlebells’ handles across your palms.
    2. Based on what feels most comfortable, you can have your palms facing in toward each other (with a neutral grip), forward (with an overhand grip), or angled inward and forward. During your set, you can similarly allow your palms to rotate within this range if that feels comfortable to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the kettlebells in a controlled arcing manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Based on what feels most natural and comfortable, you can keep your elbows tucked in, or you can allow your elbows to flare out to your sides slightly.
    2. Movement should chiefly occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows as you strive to avoid letting your elbows drift back and down with the kettlebells through shoulder motion. When viewed from the side, your elbows should remain aligned over your shoulders rather than moving behind (closer to your head) them.
    3. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
2. Finish lowering the kettlebells just before they reach the bench or until you have fully bent your elbows.
    1. When not letting the elbows drift back, the bench will determine how far the kettlebells can be lowered for most lifters.
    2. Normally, you should be able to finish with your elbows bent by greater than 90 degrees, but the size of your kettlebells may influence specifically how much you can bend your elbows.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weights the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the kettlebells into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as your wrists are vertically stacked above your shoulders.
    2. Continue keeping your upper arms in a vertical position as you avoid pushing your elbows forward (toward your chest) through shoulder motion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, lower your elbows and the kettlebells toward your sides like you would during a kettlebell bench press.
    2. March your thighs up before rocking forward while keeping your forearms mostly vertical to sit up with the kettlebells close to your shoulders.
    3. Turn your palms back toward your shoulders, and guide them down by letting your elbows straighten and swinging your elbows mildly back to help control the weights.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the entirety of your triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip), with your hands shoulder-width apart or slightly wider.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position than if you jumped up from the ground to reach it.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will put you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back puts you in a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. After hanging from the bar with your body stationary for a brief period of time (i.e., about two seconds), pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows will not actually drive downward, but having this intent helps pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
    4. Keep your neck in a neutral position (like when you are standing upright and looking straight ahead) rather than arching it backward.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. To perform the dead hang pull-up specifically, make sure that you briefly pause at the bottom with your elbows fully straightened after the lowering phase of each rep before commencing your next rep.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
You can set up for this exercise in essentially the same way you would for a close grip bench that situates your hands shoulder-width apart without arching your back.

1. Determine whether you will set up within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of JM press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descended by the end of the lowering phase of your JM press.
    1. The safety pins/spotter arms should be high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck.
    2. While the safety pins/spotter arms can provide additional safety, you should be able to close grip bench press the bar up if you happen to fail a JM press rep.
    3. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned above your neck, you can also avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your eyes are close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands near shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A shoulder-width grip is an appropriate starting point, but you can try mildly different grip widths over time to determine where you personally feel best. The proper grip width should allow your elbows, wrists, and shoulders to feel comfortable while emphasizing the triceps to a greater degree than your close grip bench press.
    2. The traditional technique for this exercise involves a thumbless grip with the thumbs placed on the same side of the bar as the other fingers. However, if you are new to this exercise, you have the option of wrapping your thumbs around the bar to fully enclose the bar with your hands, which can make it easier to hold the bar securely.
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
7. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Maintain the natural curvature of your lower back, like when you are normally standing upright. Regardless of whether you meaningfully arch your lower back when bench pressing, the JM press involves a mostly flat back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground, squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
3. Lower the bar toward your throat or chin in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows. Keep your elbows higher than your chest (when viewed from the side) while flaring your elbows out about 45 degrees from your sides.
    1. Avoid letting your wrist cock back, which would point your palms away from your forearms. Rather, curl your wrists mildly to angle your palms closer toward your forearms while securely holding the bar.
    2. In contrast to a strict lying triceps extension, you will somewhat lower your elbows, but to a meaningfully lesser degree than with a close grip bench press.
    3. The degree to which your forearms are angled relative to the ground can indicate whether you are moving your elbows (through shoulder motion) appropriately. By the end of the lowering phase, your forearms should be closer to horizontal than vertical. If your forearms remain closer to vertical at the bottom, you are excessively lowering your elbows and performing too much of a pressing movement. If your forearms angle beyond horizontal (with your wrists lower than your elbows), you are keeping your elbows excessively high and performing an exercise closer to a strict triceps extension than is intended.
    4. The amount of elbow flare is similar to what you can use for a close grip bench press. Strive to reach the middle ground between 90 degrees of elbow flare (which would cause your upper arms to form a “T-shape” with your torso) and 0 degrees of elbow flare (which would result in your upper arms being completely parallel to your torso).
    5. When lowering the bar toward your throat or chin, the bar will track slightly backward (toward your face) but to a notably lesser degree than with a strict triceps extension.
4. Continue lowering the bar until you have fully bent your elbows or until the bar is just about to contact your chin or throat.
    1. The end range can be determined by when your biceps contact your forearms, so what constitutes your personal end range can be influenced by the circumference of your arms.
    2. If the bar is about to touch your chin or throat before you have fully bent your elbows, you naturally can finish lowering the bar before achieving a full elbow bend.
5. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the bar up into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. Your elbows will move upward and inward during the lifting phase, but you can predominantly focus on extending your elbows to lift the weight.
    3. Uncurl your wrists while lifting the bar to maintain a secure grip.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
While training the pec major and front delts to a lesser degree than a close grip bench press, this exercise can develop the triceps particularly well.## Setting up
1. Firmly grasp an asymmetrical weighted implement (or a viable alternative) by its lighter end at a position that can provide your desired level of resistance.
    1. Asymmetrical weighted implements include adjustable dumbbells with plates loaded only on one side, hammers, and end-loaded baseball bats.
    2. As an alternative to an asymmetrical weighted implement, you can grasp certain symmetrically weighted cylindrical implements closer toward one end, which can provide the same training effect. Examples include a fixed-weight straight bar, a fixed-weight EZ bar, an adjustable EZ bar, and a cable straight bar attachment (like a lat pulldown bar).
    3. The resistance your forearm pronator muscles experience during this exercise will be dictated by how closely to one end you grasp the implement.
    4. For greater resistance, you can grasp farther away from the weighted end with an asymmetrically loaded implement or closer to whichever end you are holding with a symmetrically loaded implement.
    5. When performing this exercise for the first time with a particular implement, you may have to try out different grip positions to determine specifically where you should grasp the implement to provide the magnitude of resistance you wish to have.
2. Sit down on a seat (e.g., chair, flat exercise bench, plyometric box), and position your forearm (on the side holding the weight) on top of your thigh with a bent elbow and your hand slightly in front of your knee.
    1. You can situate your wrist just above your kneecap, which should allow the weighted implement to rotate freely without contacting any part of your leg.
3. If not already in this position, direct your palm to face in toward your opposite side with a straight wrist position and the heavier end of your weighted implement directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. From here, your wrist and forearms are in a neutral position as they would normally be when standing upright with your palms facing in toward your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your forearm resting on your thigh, progressively rotate your palm up toward the ceiling in a controlled manner through your available range of motion.
    1. Doing so will rotate the weighted end (if using an asymmetrically loaded object) or longer end (if using a symmetrically loaded object) out to your side, such that this end moves away from the midline of your body.
    2. How far you are able to rotate will be influenced by your individual forearm mobility, and you should aim to move through what is your comfortable full range of motion.
    3. Most people will have the forearm mobility to rotate until their palm is close to facing straight up, which will result in the weight’s handle being close to parallel to the floor, but some people may not be able to rotate quite as far, while others can rotate beyond that point.
    4. As you rotate your forearm, keep your wrist in a straight (neutral) position rather than letting the weight bend your wrist and pull your hand downward (which would bring the back of your hand closer toward your knee if the wrist extends).
2. After reaching your end range, reverse the movement until you have returned to the starting position by turning your palm to face in toward your opposite side.
    1. Doing so will lift the weighted end or longer end of the implement upward and inward in an arcing manner until the handle is vertically oriented.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the pronator teres and pronator quadratus on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
1. Securely attach your desired weight to your wrist roller.
    1. Depending on the model of your wrist roller, you may have to use weight plates (if your model uses a loading pin), or you may be able to use weight plates, kettlebells, or dumbbells (if your model uses a strap that you can loop around the weight).
    2. Leave enough of the rope initially uncoiled so that you can stand up with the roller without the weight lifting off the ground.
2. While the weight is on the ground, firmly grasp the roller’s handles with your palms facing back toward you (i.e., an overhand grip) and the uncoiled part of the rope on the side of the roller that is closer to you.
    1. If the uncoiled part of the rope is on the side of the roller farther away from you, you would be able to train wrist extension when using an overhand grip, but this is the wrist flexion variation.
3. Stand upright while grasping the roller and bend your elbows by approximately 90 degrees while keeping your upper arms tucked by your sides.
    1. Maintain this bent-elbow position throughout your set.
    2. Your palms should now be facing down toward the ground.
4. Take up the slack from the roller’s rope with alternating twisting motions of your wrists until you feel that the rope is taut from the weight’s resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows bent, bend one wrist to curl that side’s hand toward you as you extend your other wrist in the opposite direction by pointing that hand’s palm down toward the ground.
    1. You should grasp the handle firmly with the hand whose palm is curling toward you, as you grasp the handle loosely with the hand whose palm is pointing toward the ground.
    2. The hand whose palm is curling toward you is what rotates the roller to lift the weight up, while the other side’s wrist is repositioning in preparation to perform the curling motion.
2. Alternate the motions in step 1 with the opposite hands as you lift the weight up by cyclically repeating these twisting movements with your wrists until you have fully coiled the rope around the roller.
3. Lower the weight in a controlled manner by reversing the same motions you just performed until the weight lightly taps the ground or is just about to do so.
4. Repeatedly perform steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
1. Pick up a hand gripper with your desired resistance level. Initially hold it with what will be your non-working hand to help you set the gripper firmly within the hand you will be training.
    1. Based on your preference, you can be standing or seated for this exercise.
    2. For direction clarity, the set-up will be described with your working side’s elbow bent by 90 degrees as your upper arm is by your side, palm is facing in toward your other side, and forearm pointing forward. This can help communicate directionality (such as what “forward” involves), but you can optionally set up with your arm in a different position.
2. With the middle coil (where the handles meet) facing up, position one handle in your working hand’s palm as your thumb is directed forward
    1. The bottom end of the handle should be near the base of your palm on the side of your little finger.
    2. The handle should be deep enough in the middle of your palm that it is not resting on the wad of muscle at the base of your thumb.
3. Tightly wrap your index, middle, ring, and little fingers around the other handle with these four fingers close together. Position your little finger at the lowest point of the handle.
    1. To help you wrap your little finger firmly around the end of the handle, you can optionally use your non-working hand to squeeze the gripper until the front handle is close enough for your working hand’s little finger. This involves using your non-working hand’s thumb to press the back handle into your palm to stabilize it. Then use your non-working hand’s index finger to squeeze the front handle in toward your palm, so that you can tightly wrap your little finger around the front handle.
4. With the gripper now fully set, situate your arm in your preferred position.
    1. You can have your elbow straightened or bent, and you can keep your upper arm by your side or raised out in front of you. Over time, you can try out different arm positions to determine where the exercise feels best to you.
    2. So long as it does not touch the gripper or your working hand, you can place your non-working hand wherever you wish.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze the handles together until they touch.
    1. Hearing the clinking sound of the ends of the handles contacting each other confirms you have moved through the full range of motion.
    2. Strive to crush the gripper with all five fingers simultaneously. While your four long fingers pull the front handle toward the back handle, having the intent to crush with your thumb will keep the back handle stabilized.
    3. The back handle should remain firmly dug into your palm throughout the set.
    4. Allow your wrist to move naturally. Grip strength is normally greatest when the wrist is mildly cocked back, so your wrist may naturally cock back when squeezing the gripper without thinking about moving your wrist.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner as you allow the handles to separate into the starting position.
    1. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow opening phase, do not let the gripper abruptly yank your hand open.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively strengthen your forearm’s flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundus, along with your hand’s intrinsic finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder width stance while holding the kettlebells at your sides with extended elbows.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing forward.
    1. Maintain this underhand grip on the kettlebells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl both kettlebells upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the kettlebells in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the kettlebells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to a barbell situated in a landmine attachment (or a viable substitute), grab a V-bar row grip attachment (or attach a specific neutral grip landmine attachment to the bar before adding plates).
    1. Note that a V-bar for rows or pulldowns, which is also known as a double D-handle attachment, differs from the V-bar attachment that can be used for triceps pushdowns.
    2. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    3. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
    4. The diameter of the plates you select can influence the range of motion of this exercise, so you can use smaller diameter plates if you wish to row through a longer range of motion.
    5. Plates weighing 45lb or 20kg (which differ slightly), along with bumper plates of various weights, have a diameter near 18in (45cm) that can physically constrain the range of motion for your row by contacting your torso at the end of the lifting phase or by contacting the ground at the end of the lowering phase.
2. While holding the V-bar, straddle the barbell by standing a little behind the weight plates as your chest is directed toward the weight and your back is facing the landmine’s pivot point.
3. Sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can position the V-bar to the end of the barbell’s shaft, such that the V-bar contacts the bearing (the cylinder separating the shaft from the sleeve where you add plates) with its handles directed upward.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. In this position, the V-bar should be situated under and just in front of you such that you can grasp it without having to reach meaningfully forward.
4. Firmly grasp the V-bar’s handles with your palms facing each other, elbows straight, lower back flat, and chest up.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from now on.
6. While keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and elbows straight, drive through your legs to lift the bar slightly up and assume a slightly more upright torso position.
    1. The objective is to use your hip and thigh muscles to lift the bar just high enough that you can experience resistance from the weight when your elbows are fully straight in the bottom position of each rep, rather than having the plates touch the ground.
    2. In this position, your torso should ideally remain inclined closer to horizontal than to vertical, with your hips mostly bent. However, large-diameter plates may require a more upright torso to keep them off the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides with this neutral grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. If using large diameter plates, you may not be able to row as far backward compared to if you use smaller diameter plates.
    5. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar to its initial position by bending your knees and hips to lower the bar.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. With a cable station positioned to its lowest height, clip in a straight bar attachment and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with extended elbows.
3. Grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder width grip and an underhand grip such that your palms are directed forward.
4. While keeping your elbows extended, stand upright while gripping the bar.
5. Make sure tension is present from the cable station while your elbows are fully extended, rather than slack being present in the cable.
    1. Depending on your arm length relative to your height and which specific cable station you are using, you likely will have to take a small step backward to take out the slack of the cable.
    2. When taking a small step backward, the cable will now have a mildly diagonal line of pull rather than being vertical.
    3. To maintain a fully extended elbow position with a diagonal line of pull, you will position your upper arms slightly in front of your body by flexing at your shoulders instead of keeping your upper arms pinned to your sides (like you would with a curl that has a vertically directed resistance, such as free weights).
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed in the same position that allowed your elbows to be fully extended at the start of the rep (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing further forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms in the same position used to start the rep.
3. Alternate between curling the bar upwards and lowering it down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Connect a lat pulldown bar to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. If you do not have a standard lat pulldown bar attachment, which has a straight body and mildly angled ends, you can use a different attachment that lets you grasp it with an overhand grip.
2. While facing the bar and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the bar with a well outside shoulder width overhand grip, such that your palms are directed forward, and your arms form a “Y-shape” with your torso.
    1. Positioning your hands about 50% further apart than your shoulder width is appropriate, but a wide grip can encompass a range of grip widths, and specifically how wide you grasp the bar is up to your preference.
    2. What is personally considered a wide grip to you can also be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard pulldown, which you can somewhat widen to individualize your wide grip. For instance, for your wide grip, you can place your index finger on the bar just outside where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing your standard pulldown.
    3. Feel free to try out mildly different grip widths to determine if any particular position feels best to you.
3. While holding the bar, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to extend and your arms to raise overhead in line with the cable's resistance.
4. In the seated position, slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight backward lean throughout your set.
    2. From this position, your elbows should be straight, with your arms overhead and slightly forward (rather than completely vertical), and you should feel tension from the cable station holding your arms in this position.
    3. If you notice the cable remains slack in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), widening out your grip even further may be needed to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar down toward your upper chest by driving your elbows down toward your sides as you progressively bend them.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. Maintain the slight backward lean of your torso you adopted while setting up, rather than leaning further backward.
    3. When pulling through your full range of motion, you should finish with the bar touching your upper chest or very close to doing so.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the bar in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine and adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad.
    1. While you can initially adjust the seat to a height that situates your shoulders approximately level with the parallel handles, the seat height that is best for you depends on the specific machine you use, and trying out different positions may be helpful.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, adjust it to a position that allows you to grasp the parallel handles when your arms are fully outstretched forward in front of you with extended elbows.
    1. To row through your full range of motion, ideally place the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with your elbows fully straight.
3. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet flat on the floor.
4. Reach fully forward with straight elbows to grasp the parallel handles firmly with your palms facing each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. If your machine does not have an adjustable chest pad and the handles are far enough from you to make it difficult to grasp both of them simultaneously, it may be easier for you to grasp one handle at a time.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward the sides of your torso by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked close to your sides with this grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Keep your chest in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner, progressively straightening until they are outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you train them more effectively.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that places the handles slightly above shoulder level once you are seated.
    1. This seat height is an appropriate starting point for most people, but the ideal seat height specifically should allow you to press through your full range of motion while initially gripping the handles with your elbows stacked under the handles.
    2. If the seat is excessively high, you will not be able to position your elbows directly under the handles (when viewed from the side).
    3. If the seat is excessively low, your range of motion for this exercise will be limited.
    4. Some trial and error may be needed to find the best height for you as an individual.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the backrest.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the backrest if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
4. With your palms facing in toward each other and elbows bent, firmly grasp the handles that are directed forward. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being vertical when viewed from the side.
    1. If your machine only has handles directed out to the sides that can allow an overhand grip but not a neutral grip, you cannot perform this specific variation. However, you can select the “overhand grip pin-loaded machine shoulder press” from this database as an alternative.
    2. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    3. If you cannot comfortably position your elbows under the handles, mildly lower the seat height before resuming your setup.
    4. Situating your elbows under the handles and maintaining this alignment will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow tuck during this exercise.
5. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling, and maintain this posture throughout your set.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles up overhead until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain stacked under your wrists when viewed from the side. This will result in your elbows driving up in front of your body rather than being excessively flared out away from your sides.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your back snuggly against the backrest and your feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. With the appropriate seat height, you should finish the lowering phase with the handles slightly above shoulder level, as your elbows are lower than your shoulders.
    2. At the bottom, you can allow the weights you selected to tap the rest of the weight stack. A light tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows stacked underneath the handles, resulting in your forearms remaining in a vertical position when viewed from the side.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, upper pec major, triceps, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, and straddle the machine as you are facing toward the weights.
2. Place your feet onto the foot plates and your chest onto the chest pad such that your whole body is inclined forward.
    1. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
3. Depending upon your height and leg lengths, you may want to bend your knees somewhat or keep them straight to determine where your torso is situated on the chest pad.
    1. How you position your knees will be based on what feels most comfortable and stable to you, but you can initially see how you feel when positioning your knees to allow the uppermost aspect of the chest pad to be approximately level with your collarbone.
    2. Individuals who have longer legs will typically feel more comfortable when using a somewhat more bent knee position, while those with shorter legs will typically feel more comfortable with straighter knees.
4. Firmly grasp the outer horizontally directed handles with a mildly outside shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are directed behind you.
    1. During the row, the appropriate grip width should allow your elbows to flare naturally out away from your sides. This should result in a fairly even balance between having them fully tucked by your sides (which can naturally occur with a close grip) and having them fully flared out away from your sides (which can naturally occur with a wide grip).
5. Lift the bar up off the machine’s stopper, center the bar so that it no longer lies above the stopper, and lower it until your elbows are extended.
    1. In this bottom position, allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades apart from one another, which will likely prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in your upper back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward. Simultaneously bend your elbows and pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Allow your elbows to flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    2. Keep your torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set rather than arching your lower back to lift your chest up off the pad.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished straightening and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. While you do not have to lower the bar extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the bar drop abruptly in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar back onto the machine’s stopper.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance. Firmly hold the handle with both hands in front of your body as your hands are wrapped around each other.
2. Lift the dumbbell up to shoulder level with your elbows bent by curling the weight and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbell into position if it is too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbows and mildly swinging them forward to lift the weight.
3. Press the dumbbell up overhead with both arms in a shoulder press motion until your arms are vertical.
    1. The back of your elbows should be pointing forward.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
4. While keeping your elbows up, slightly bend your elbows to tilt one end of the dumbbell down.
5. Reposition your hands so that both palms are firmly contacting the higher end of the dumbbell. Cross your thumbs over each other firmly around the handle. Simultaneously cross your index fingers over each other, resulting in your thumbs and index fingers resembling close to a diamond shape.
    1. In this position, one end of the dumbbell should be above your hands, while the other end is below your hands.
    2. You will continue securely gripping the dumbbell in this manner throughout your set. Make sure that your thumbs stay wrapped around the handle for stability.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the dumbbell behind your head in a controlled manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
    2. Predominantly keep your elbows tucked in as they are chiefly pointing forward rather than out to your side. However, you can allow your elbows to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    3. As you keep your elbows up, your upper arms should remain in a vertical position by the sides of your head.
2. Continue lowering the dumbbell until you have fully bent your elbows or can lightly tap your upper back with the dumbbell.
    1. It is normal to be able to bend the elbows beyond 90 degrees during this exercise, but specifically how far you can bend your elbows may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps.
    2. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach their upper back with the dumbbell, wherever you feel a notable stretching sensation in your triceps can be an appropriate end range.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the dumbbell overhead.
    1. You can finish the lifting phase with a slight bend in your elbows rather than fully straightening them. A slight bend will keep one end of the dumbbell tilted down and make it easier to maintain a secure grip with the higher end of the dumbbell resting on your palms.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows up as all the motion occurs from your elbows without moving your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you have completed the lifting phase of your last rep, firmly grasp the handle with your lower hand while keeping the palm of your upper hand against the side of the dumbbell.
    1. Grasping the handle with one hand will make it easier for you to lower the weight compared to if you kept both palms against one end of the dumbbell.
6. Use both hands to lower the dumbbell toward your chest before setting it down.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. With a cable station positioned to its lowest height, select your weight with the loading pin, and connect a straight bar attachment.
2. Situate a flat exercise bench or chair a little in front of the cable station.
3. Squat down to pick up the bar attachment with an overhand grip where your palms are facing back toward you.
4. While holding the bar, sit down on a flat exercise bench or chair and position your forearms on top of your thighs with your palms facing down and your wrists situated slightly in front of your knees.
    1. To achieve this position, you will lean forward at your hips when seated, with your elbows bent.
5. Allow the force from the cable to bend your wrists and bring your hands downward such that your palms are directed at an angle down and back toward you.
    1. In this position, the bar should not be contacting your legs, so scoot your forearms forward if it is initially contacting your legs.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by extending your wrists through their available range of motion while keeping your forearms resting on your thighs.
    1. All motion should come from your wrists as your hands move upward.
    2. About halfway through the lifting phase, your palms should be facing straight down toward the floor when your hands are aligned with your forearms.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, when the bar is fully raised, your palms should be angled forward.
    4. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the top side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed at an angle down and back toward you.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the seven wrist extensor muscles on the side of your forearm opposite to your palm.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip), with your hands shoulder-width apart or slightly wider apart.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up onto a sturdy box, an aerobic step, or a bumper plate to place the bar within reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar than if you jumped up from the ground to reach it.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will put you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back puts you in a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows will not actually drive downward, but having this intent helps pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
Certain machines have a foot pedal that you can press to move the machine’s handle while setting up, but other machine models lack a foot pedal. Some of the specific setup details differ depending on which type of machine you are using, so the setup for each approach is described separately. However, the exercise technique can be essentially the same for either option, so one technique section is provided with a couple of details that depend on which type of machine you utilize.

### Setting up a machine without a foot pedal

1. Adjust the seat to a height that comfortably allows you to grasp the handle behind your head with your elbows fully bent once you are seated.
    1. This seat height commonly allows the base of someone’s neck to be reasonably close to being level with the handle once seated. However, your flexibility can influence where your ideal position lies, so trying out different seat heights may initially be required.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on its seat with an upright torso as your back is resting against the backrest.
    1. If your machine has a foot plate, position your soles against it. Otherwise, place your feet flat on the floor.
4. Raise your arms up with your elbows fully bent to grasp the handle firmly with fairly straight wrists.
    1. Different machines have different handle types that will influence specifically how you should grasp the handle, but handles that allow you to grasp them with your palms angled in toward each other are common. Some machines also allow you to connect various cable attachments, such as a multi-grip angled bar or a rope attachment, depending on what you prefer.
    2. What chiefly matters is that your grip feels stable and comfortable.
    3. From this position, your upper arms should be near the sides of your head with your elbows held high and tucked in (rather than being meaningfully flared out away from your sides).
    4. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your triceps.

### Setting up a machine with a foot pedal

1. Adjust the seat to a height that positions the handle at least a little lower than what you can reach behind your head with your elbows lifted up and fully bent once you are seated.
    1. This seat height commonly allows the base of someone’s neck to be at least mildly higher than the handle once seated. However, the foot pedal allows a range of seat heights to be viable for this exercise.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on its seat with an upright torso as your back is resting against the backrest.
4. Place one foot against the machine’s foot pedal, and push the pedal forward with your leg.
    1. This brings the handles up to a position you can comfortably reach.
    2. Keep the pedal pushed away during the next step, while keeping your other foot on the ground.
5. Raise your upper arms up near the sides of your head with your elbows bent enough to grasp the handle firmly with fairly straight wrists.
    1. Different machines have different handle types that will influence specifically how you should grasp the handle, but handles that allow you to grasp them with your palms angled in toward each other are common.
    2. What chiefly matters is that your grip feels stable and comfortable.
    3. From this position, hold your elbows high while keeping tucked in rather than being meaningfully flared out away from your sides.
6. Bring the foot pedal back to its starting position by bending your knee, which will shift the machine’s resistance to your arms. Simultaneously, lower the handle behind your head by fully bending your elbows while keeping your upper arms stationary.
    1. The degree to which you can bend your elbows while keeping your upper arms raised up will be dependent on your triceps flexibility.
    2. Allow your elbows to bend through the full range that feels comfortable, and you will normally feel a stretching sensation in your triceps.
7. Lift your foot off the foot pedal and place it flat on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the handle up overhead by straightening your elbows while keeping your upper arms stationary.
    1. At the top of the movement, your elbows should be fully straightened with your upper arms in the same position in which they started.
    2. While you move your forearms through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbows to lower the handle behind your head in a controlled manner.
    1. Lower the bar to the starting position with your elbows fully bent through your full range of motion.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows tucked in rather than meaningfully flaring them out to your sides.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the machine’s resistance rapidly pull the handle back down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you are using a machine with a foot pedal, push the pedal forward with your leg until you are no longer supporting any of the machine’s resistance with your arms. Then let go of the handle and bend your knee to return the foot pedal to its initial position.
    2. If your machine lacks a foot pedal, allow the plates you selected to rest fully on the rest of the weight stack before releasing the handle.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all three heads of your triceps, which constitute the majority of muscle mass in your upper arms.## Setting up
1. You can select either a fixed weight EZ bar or an adjustable plate-loaded EZ bar, which are functionally interchangeable for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with extended elbows.
    1. If the EZ bar, if already positioned in an elevated rack at mid-thigh level or above, you naturally would not have to squat down or hinge at your hips before picking it up.
3. Grasp the bar with a closer than shoulder width grip and a semi-underhand grip (AKA semi-neutral grip) such that your palms are directed at an angle that is facing forward and inward.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a closer than shoulder width semi-underhand grip is accessible on the bar.
4. While keeping your elbows extended, stand fully upright while gripping the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the bar upwards and lowering it down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curls, this exercise can effectively train the biceps and brachialis in your upper arm along with the brachioradialis in your forearm.## Setting up
1. Load a barbell with your desired weight, or approach a fixed weight straight bar.
2. While facing the middle of the bar in a near shoulder-width stance, squat down and firmly grasp it with an overhand grip (as your palms face backward). Your hands should be about shoulder-width apart or mildly closer.
3. Stand upright with the bar just in front of your thighs.
4. Move the bar up to your shoulders with your elbows fully bent underneath and slightly in front of the bar.
    1. If the load is light enough to do so, you can just curl the bar up.
    2. With a heavier load, you can perform a cheat curl, where you generate momentum with your hips to help you curl the bar up. Initially sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and your torso to incline slightly forward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward while bending your elbows and mildly swinging them forward.
    3. The bar should now be in essentially the same position it would be for an overhead press.
    4. The bar should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    5. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar hard will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
5. Press the bar up overhead in a shoulder press motion until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. The bar should be vertically stacked above your shoulders, hips, and knees.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the bar behind your head in a controlled manner. Based on your preference and what feels most comfortable to your elbows, you can select one of two approaches with respect to your elbow position.
    1. Option 1: Keep your elbows completely up with your upper arms in a vertical position throughout the rep. This will result in the bar moving in an arcing manner exclusively from your forearms hinging at your elbows.
    2. Option 2: Mildly arc your elbows forward and down while bending your elbows to lower the bar. You can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest to create additional space for the bar. This option will result in the bar moving in a closer to vertical path from a combination of shoulder and elbow motion.
    3. Neither of the approaches is inherently superior to the other, so you can feel free to try out both ways and continue using the technique you prefer.
    4. With either option, predominantly keep your elbows tucked in. However, you can allow them to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
2. Continue lowering the bar until you have fully bent your elbows or can lightly tap the base of your neck or your upper back muscles with the bar.
    1. How far you can lower the bar may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps, particularly if you keep your elbows completely up. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach near their upper back with the bar, others will reach the limit to which they can comfortably bend their elbows before that point. Do not lower the bar beyond the range that your flexibility permits without discomfort.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the bar into the starting position overhead.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is vertically above your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the bar in front of your head toward your shoulders like you would during an overhead press.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and most of the back of your upper arms (i.e., triceps) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The proper setting will typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. After selecting your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, sit down on the seat with your feet on the ground.
3. Position the back of your upper arms and elbows on top of the arm pad as your elbows are aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
4. Firmly grasp the handles with your hands approximately shoulder width apart as your palms are angled upward.
    1. If the handles are a little out of your reach, you could slightly stand up and lean forward to reach the handles before sitting back down after you have grasped the handles.
    2. Your wrists should be mostly straight throughout your set after grasping the handles.
    3. Machines commonly have angled handles that allow you to grasp them with your palms angled inward and upward (resulting in a hybrid between an underhand and neutral grip), but certain models may have horizontal handles that will allow you to use a purely underhand grip.
5. In the starting position, your elbows should be fully straightened to allow you to train through a full range of motion.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows and the back of your upper arms in contact with the arm pad, curl the handles upward and back toward you in an arcing motion by bending at your elbows until they are fully bent.
    1. While your forearms move through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
2. After your elbows have fully bent, reverse the motion by lowering the handles in a slow and controlled manner until your elbows have returned to a fully straightened position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and the back of your upper arms in contact with the arm pad.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a low incline angle (e.g., near 30 degrees) and set a pair of dumbbells under the head of the bench.
    1. As a different variation, which you can use if your bench does not have a low incline setting, you can alternatively select a moderate incline angle (e.g., near 45 degrees) and otherwise use the same technique for this exercise.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
3. Grasp the dumbbells, position your arms so that they are perpendicular to the ground with extended elbows, and rotate your arms so that your palms are facing at an angle in toward each other and somewhat forward.
    1. Keep your elbows extended and your palms facing this direction throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your arms upward and outward through your available shoulder range of motion so that they form a “Y-shape” with your torso as you continue to keep your chest in contact with the bench.
    1. You can strive to raise your arms until they are close to parallel with the incline bench, but your shoulder mobility will affect how high you can raise your arms.
    2. If you notice your chest lifting up off the bench during this movement, that indicates that you most likely are dynamically extending your lumbar spine, which can occur as a compensation pattern instead of lifting your arm only with your shoulder girdle muscles.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering your arms in a controlled manner until your arms have returned to being perpendicular to the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your traps along with your serratus anterior, front delt, and side delt.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and sit down on a seated bench or a flat exercise bench with your feet flat on the ground.
    1. If you use a seated bench, the back rest should be low enough that it will not be within the path of the dumbbell and restrict your range of motion for this exercise.
    2. The tall back rest of an adjustable bench positioned fully upright may be more likely to limit your range than a fixed seated bench with a back rest that does not reach your head.
    3. Throughout the exercise, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting by your side.
    4. All the directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
2. Lift the dumbbell up near the front of your shoulder with your elbow bent by curling the weight and swinging your elbow mildly forward.
    1. If the weight is too heavy for you to lift with a typical curling motion, you can initially situate the dumbbell on your lower thigh and march it up into position with assistance from your free hand.
    2. You should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
    3. Your forearm should now be in a close to vertical position.
3. While sitting fully upright, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
4. Press the dumbbell up overhead in a shoulder press motion until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. You can point the back of your elbow forward and out to the side at an angle.
    2. The dumbbell should be vertically stacked over your shoulder.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbow to lower the dumbbell behind your head in a controlled manner while keeping your elbow up.
    1. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearm hinging at your elbow without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbow as the center of an analog clock, while your forearm is the hand that rotates about this center elbow during the exercise.
    2. As you keep your elbow up, your upper arm should remain in a vertical position by the side of your head.
2. Continue lowering the dumbbell until you have fully bent your elbow or can lightly tap your upper back with the dumbbell.
    1. It is normal to be able to bend the elbow beyond 90 degrees, but specifically how far you can bend your elbow may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps.
    2. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach their upper back with the dumbbell, wherever you feel a notable stretching sensation in your triceps can be an appropriate end range.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbow to lift the dumbbell into the starting position overhead.
    1. You should finish with your elbow fully straightened as the dumbbell is vertically above your shoulder.
    2. Continue keeping your elbow up as all the motion occurs from your elbow without moving your shoulder.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the dumbbell toward your shoulder like you would during a shoulder press.
6. Lower the dumbbell to your side as you straighten your elbow and swing your elbow back by your side.
7. Transfer the dumbbell to your other hand and repeat the exercise with that side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. At a cable station, adjust the cable to near the height of your elbows when standing with your arms at your sides, clip in a D-handle attachment, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing with your chest facing near a 135-degree angle away from the cable station, grasp the handle with your hand on the side farther away from the cable.
3. On the side holding the handle, position your upper arm by your side and slightly forward while bending your elbow to approximately 90 degrees and keeping your wrist in a fairly straight position.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
    2. Situating your upper arm slightly forward rather than having it be completely vertical at your side can limit your torso from restricting your range of motion during this exercise.
4. Have your forearm rotated inward across the front of your torso such that your forearm is contacting or close to contacting your abdomen while your palm is facing backward.
    1. Your shoulder mobility will affect how far you can rotate, and you should use as much range as feels comfortable for your shoulder.
    2. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable, so if that is not the case, take a step to the side away from the cable stack.
## Exercise technique
1. Rotate your forearm outward by pulling the handle away from the cable station while moving through your shoulder’s available range of motion as you continue to keep your upper arm positioned slightly in front of your side.
    1. If the cable contacts your arm that is closer to the cable stack during this motion, you can position this arm away from the cable behind you with its elbow bent to near 90 degrees as its forearm rests on your lower back.
    2. All of this movement should occur from shoulder rotation, and your upper arm should not raise away from your torso.
    3. When thinking about this technique, it can be helpful to view your forearm as a clock’s hand, while your elbow is the center about which rotation occurs without any side-to-side motion.
2. Reverse the motion by rotating your forearm inward across your body as you bring the handle closer toward the cable station in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set with one side and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, along with a portion of the supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Connect a lat pulldown bar to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. If you do not have a standard lat pulldown bar attachment, which has a straight body and mildly angled ends, you can use a different attachment that lets you comfortably grasp it with an underhand grip.
2. While facing the bar and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width underhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
3. While holding the bar, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
4. In the seated position, slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight backward lean throughout your set.
    2. From this position, your elbows should be straight, with your arms overhead and slightly forward (rather than completely vertical), and you should feel tension from the cable station holding your arms in this position.
    3. If you notice the cable remains slack in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), widening out your grip may be needed to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar down toward your upper chest by driving your elbows down toward your sides as you progressively bend them.
    1. Simultaneosuly pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. Predominantly keep your elbows aligned with your wrists, rather than meaningfully flaring them out away from your sides.
    3. Maintain the slight backward lean of your torso you adopted while setting up, rather than leaning further backward.
    4. When pulling through your full range of motion, you should finish with the bar touching your upper chest or very close to doing so.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the bar in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the kettlebells at your sides with extended elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs.
    1. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the kettlebells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the kettlebells upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the kettlebells.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your abdomen contacting the ground (i.e., in the prone position) and your head lifted slightly off the ground as you maintain a tucked chin position with your face directed toward the ground.
2. Position your arms straight out in front of you with straight elbows such that your hands are approximately shoulder-width apart while resting on the ground.
    1. Your body position should resemble how you would look if you were reaching up with your arms fully overhead while standing.
3. Rotate your arms so that your palms face down toward the ground if they are not already doing so.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your arms straight up off the ground through your full available shoulder range of motion while keeping your chest in contact with the ground.
    1. You will keep your arms lifted off the ground throughout your set.
    2. Your shoulder mobility will affect how high you can raise your arms.
    3. Most people will not be able to lift their arms particularly high before reaching the end of their available range of motion.
    4. If you notice your chest lifting up off the ground during this movement, that indicates that you most likely are dynamically extending your lumbar spine, which can occur as a compensation pattern instead of lifting your arms only with your shoulder girdle muscles.
2. Bring your arms toward your sides in an arcing motion akin to the lowering phase of a jumping jack as you continue keeping your arms as high off the ground as your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Partway through this motion, your arms will point straight away from your sides as they form a “T-shape” with your torso.
3. After you have moved beyond the “T-shape” position, rotate your arms to point your palms up as you simultaneously continue moving your arms toward your sides.
4. Once your arms are near your sides, bend your elbows and continue to bring your hands together behind your back until one hand is on top of the other or as close to this position as your shoulder range of motion permits.
    1. The end range of this exercise places you into a position that resembles the position in which someone may be handcuffed while lying on the ground, which is why this exercise is sometimes called a prone handcuff.
5. Reverse the motions you just performed by bringing your arms out away from your sides, straightening your elbows, and rotating your arms to point your palms down before bringing your arms into the overhead starting position.
    1. You should move your arms in an arcing fashion akin to the upward phase of a jumping jack.
6. Alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. After learning the technique, you should be able to perform the different components of this exercise in a fluid motion.
## Description
This exercise can train your ability to move your shoulders actively through multiple motions while engaging various muscles throughout your shoulder girdle that allow you to do so.## Setting up
1. If using an adjustable bench, position it at a moderate incline angle, which is near 45 degrees.
    1. The available incline angles will vary among different benches, with some benches having several angle configurations, while others may only be set to flat, upright, or a 45-degree angle.
    2. You can also use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on the incline bench with your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
    1. Keep your upper back and the back of your head in contact with the bench throughout the set.
3. Direct your palms so that they are facing forward.
    1. Maintain this underhand grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl both dumbbells upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing backward (underhand grip) and your hands spaced about shoulder-width apart.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will place you in a more advantageous position to start your pull compared to if you kept your chest facing exclusively horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up from the force of your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back places you into a position akin to a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending them.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.

### **How Your Grip and Pull-Up Technique Influence Back Muscle Development**

When repeatedly performing this exercise over time, you can feel free to try out mildly different grip widths to determine if any particular position feels most comfortable to you and/or allows you to perceive your target back muscles working particularly well. Pull-ups can be performed with a variety of hand grips, including different grip widths and overhand, underhand (resulting in the exercise being called a chin-up), neutral, or hybrid (i.e., a combination of neutral and underhand or overhand) grips. Additionally, shoulder motion during a pull-up can occur predominantly in the sagittal (front-to-back) plane, the frontal (side-to-side) plane, or a combination of the two. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the sagittal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out in front of you), the upper arms remain parallel to each other and do not deviate away from the mid-line of your body. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the frontal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out to your sides, such as with a snow angle motion), the upper arms move away from the mid-line of your body until they are parallel to the ground. Then they move closer to the mid-line of your body with further elevation. During the lifting phase of a pull-up, the shoulders could extend in the sagittal plane, adduct in the frontal plane, or exhibit a combination of extension and adduction at a hybrid angle between the sagittal and frontal planes. Rather than moving the shoulders exclusively through one plane, pull-up variations are commonly performed with a technique that allows for the shoulders to move in between these two planes, but one plane of shoulder motion often makes a greater contribution than the other.

Muscles acting on the shoulder joint have different leverage to generate extension torque in the sagittal plane versus adduction torque in the frontal plane, and the nervous system is most likely to recruit the particular muscles that are best leveraged to produce the intended joint action during any active movement. Correspondingly, the plane of motion in which the shoulders move during a pull-up can affect how well different muscles are likely to be trained. With any particular grip for a pull-up, you can intentionally rotate your shoulders in a manner that allows you to move your shoulder relatively more in the sagittal or frontal plane. However, most people will typically find that a particular path of shoulder motion feels most comfortable and stable to their shoulders, elbows, and wrists when using a specific grip. For instance, people will normally perform a wide overhand grip pull-up with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the frontal plane. In contrast, they will normally perform a close-grip chin-up (using an underhand grip) with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the sagittal plane. Performing either variation in the alternative plane of motion would require greater-than-normal rotational range of motion in the shoulders and forearms, and attempting to do so could feel very awkward and uncomfortable. People will typically feel most comfortable when positioning themselves so that, at the point in the pull-up when their upper arms are parallel to the ground, their elbows and wrists are vertically aligned or their elbows are slightly lateral (outside) to their wrists.

In general, widening a grip facilitates relatively more frontal plane shoulder motion, while narrowing a grip facilitates relatively more sagittal plane shoulder motion. An overhand, underhand, or neutral grip may be used with any grip width. Still, the combination of these grip variables will influence comfort and which plane of shoulder motion feels most natural. When pulling predominantly in the sagittal plane with a rather close grip, an underhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable than an overhand grip. Similarly, when pulling predominantly in the frontal plane with a rather wide grip, an overhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable and allow more frontal-plane motion than an underhand grip. With a moderate-width grip, people may commonly find that pulling mostly in the sagittal plane feels natural with an underhand or neutral grip, but pulling relatively more in the frontal plane feels natural with an overhand grip. Fundamentally, the shoulder motion is what matters to the back muscles, and the grip is only relevant to the back muscles based on how it influences which shoulder motion is performed. When performing this overhand grip pull-up with a moderate grip width, you most likely will pull in a way that biases shoulder adduction somewhat more than shoulder extension. Still, you can also perform the exercise in a way that results in a fairly even balance between these two motions.

The lat and teres major in the back function both as primary adductors and extensors and are therefore highly likely to be trained effectively with pull-up variations regardless of the grip or plane of shoulder motion. However, the preponderance of available evidence indicates that the rear deltoid lacks meaningful leverage to contribute to shoulder adduction, but it clearly has favorable leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. The rear deltoid is correspondingly likely to be trained better with a closer grip emphasizing shoulder extension, which would be most comfortable when using an underhand or neutral grip. Alternatively, the middle and lower regions of the pectoralis major (that constitute the sternoclavicular head) have large moment arms to function as shoulder adductors, but only the lower portion exhibits a shoulder extension moment arm, and this moment arm is quite small and only present in flexed shoulder positions. Consequently, wider grip pull-ups (which are most comfortable with an overhand or neutral grip) that involve greater frontal-plane shoulder motion are likely to train the pectoralis major more effectively than closer grip pulldowns. Which regions of the lats are preferentially biased by a pull-up variation may also potentially be influenced by how much shoulder motion occurs in the frontal or sagittal planes. The middle and lower regions have relatively greater leverage to function as shoulder adductors, while the upper region of the lat has relatively greater leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. Plausibly, these biomechanical differences may facilitate the middle and lower regions of the lats being preferentially trained by wider grip pull-ups, while the upper region may be preferentially trained by closer grip pulling variations. With that said, the degree to which any preferential biasing may occur across different regions of the lats when comparing pull-up grips is more speculative and has not been tested in any longitudinal training interventions.

### Why Overhand Grip Pull-Ups may be More Difficult than Chin-Ups or Neutral Grip Pull-Ups

While exceptions exist, people often find overhand grip pull-ups to be more difficult per rep than chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. The discrepancy is most likely influenced by chin-ups and neutral grip pull-ups typically being performed with a technique that emphasizes shoulder extension in the sagittal plane, while overhand grip pull-ups are typically performed with relatively more shoulder adduction in the frontal plane. As demonstrated by studies such as those by Mayer et al. (1994) and Shklar and Dvir (1995), on average, maximal torque for shoulder extension exceeds that for shoulder adduction. The sample of participants, velocity, and type of muscle action tested can all influence the degree to which torque differs between these two actions, but maximal shoulder adduction torque has been measured at 63-90% of maximal shoulder extension, depending on the specific test conditions. In addition to the effect of how the shoulder moves, the forearm position can also affect how much elbow flexion torque you can generate during your pull. Guenzkofer et al. (2012) found that up to 15% greater elbow flexion torque can be generated when the forearm is in a supinated (used for an underhand grip) or neutral position compared to a pronated (used with an overhand grip) position. Together, these relationships suggest that most people can generate greater torque at the shoulders and elbows during chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups than during overhand grip pull-ups, which are likely to be more challenging when performing the same number of reps. Consequently, it is common not to be able to complete as many reps of overhand grip pull-ups in a set as you would be able to complete when performing chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. Nonetheless, individual differences in factors such as training history and the relative strength of different muscle groups can result in some people not noticing a difference in difficulty between these variations, or even finding overhand grip pull-ups to be somewhat easier per rep.

### Chin Over Bar?

The technique instructions do not specify where any particular part of your body should be situated in relation to the bar at the end of the lifting phase because your full range of motion can differ from someone else’s full range of motion. Pulling until your chin is above the bar is a common standard used for testing by the United States Marines and for competitions held by the World Pull-Up Organization, and you can certainly use it if you wish. However, when focusing on chin clearance, some people tend to extend their necks, lifting their chins, to clear the bar with less range of motion through the pull, compared to when they retain a neutral neck position. In this instance, focusing on the proxy for a full range of motion may result in a different movement than focusing on the individual's full range of motion. When competing or taking a standardized test that affects professional prospects, it is only sensible to do the minimum required to complete each rep per the rules, but training to build muscle and strength is a different endeavor.

### **The Most Challenging Part of the Chin-Up**

The end range of the lifting phase is typically the most challenging portion of chin-ups, in large part because the torque our pulling muscles can generate meaningfully varies across different joint angles. When the elbow is flexed to 120 degrees, Bober et al. (2002) found maximal elbow flexion torque to be approximately 35% of the peak torque that can be generated in a moderately flexed elbow position (i.e., near 60-75 degrees of elbow flexion). With your elbows approaching full flexion toward the end range of a chin-up, your elbow flexor muscles are correspondingly operating from a rather disadvantaged position to help you complete the rep. On average, maximal shoulder extension torque is also lower when the shoulder is mildly elevated (like at the end of a chin-up’s lifting phase) compared to when it is moderately elevated (like during the middle range of a chin-up). The difference can be of notable magnitude, as indicated by Bober et al. (2002), who found that maximal shoulder extension in a neutral shoulder position was only 60% of the peak value achievable at 90 degrees of shoulder flexion. Together these strength curves of the muscle groups acting on the elbow and shoulder can contribute to your chin-ups consistently feeling the most difficult during the last quarter of the lifting phase.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your back muscle strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your back muscles. As a simple approach, if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure you use the same technique for each condition, which means the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how the performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep's performance across these conditions on a second day, in which you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps on the first day, perform the set with straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue from the first set may influence the second. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting the performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout the set.
2. Raise the dumbbells out in front of you until your upper arms are approximately parallel with the ground.
    1. If you wish to do so, elevating the dumbbells higher up, beyond when your upper arms are parallel to the floor, is a completely viable modification to this exercise’s technique.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner back to your sides.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a bench with an upright posture and your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
    1. You can opt to sit down on a flat bench without any back support or sit down on a seated bench with back support.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position)
    1. Maintain this neutral grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl both dumbbells upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing in toward your sides (i.e., using a neutral grip position).
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one dumbbell resting at your side with an extended elbow, curl the other dumbbell upward by bending at your elbow while keeping your upper arm fixed at your side (rather than allowing your upper arm to swing forward).
    1. While curling the dumbbell upward, simultaneously rotate your forearm into an underhand grip position that results in your palm facing back toward you when your elbow is fully flexed.
2. After your elbow has fully flexed and your forearm has rotated from a neutral grip to an underhand grip, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner until your elbow has returned to an extended position.
    1. While lowering the dumbbell, simultaneously rotate your forearm back into a neutral grip position that results in your palm facing in toward your side when your elbow is fully extended.
    2. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arm positioned by your side.
3. Repeat steps 1 and 2 with your other arm.
4. Alternate between curling with each side until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine, sit down with your back in full contact with the backrest.
    1. The machine’s safety stoppers (which can be moved with one or two safety handles, depending upon the specific machine) should already be locked, and you will keep them locked throughout your set.
    2. If your machine has an adjustable seat, you can also initially adjust the seat to whichever position feels most comfortable to you, but the seat position is unlikely to affect this exercise meaningfully.
    3. Your low back should be in a fairly flat position rather than being meaningfully rounded, like in a slouched position, which could occur if you sit your hips excessively forward.
2. Place the front half of one foot onto the bottom of the foot plate with your heel hanging off the edge.
    1. Keep your other foot in a comfortable position, such as resting on the ground.
3. While grasping the hand grips, press the platform away from you by extending your knee until it is straight or only slightly bent.
    1. You should maintain this knee position (rather than bending and extending your knee) while performing this exercise until you are ready to finish your set.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they utilize a hyperextended knee position during this exercise, so keeping a straight or slightly bent knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the platform away from you by pointing the ball of your foot away from you (in a manner similar to stepping on a car’s gas pedal) through your ankle’s available range of motion.
    1. Motion should occur exclusively at your ankle, while your knee and hip do not move.
2. In a controlled manner, reverse the motion by allowing the front half of your foot to move back toward you through your ankle’s available range of motion.
    1. How far you are able to move will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. After completing your last rep with your other side, lower the platform back into its starting position by bending your knee.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near waist height, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Based on your preference, connect a rope attachment to the cable with the rope pulled fully to one side of the attachment, connect a D-handle to the cable, or leave the cable without any attachment.
    1. Essentially the same exercise can be performed with any of those options, so you can select whichever feels most comfortable for you to hold.
    2. Except for the next step, which includes details about how to grasp the cable without an attachment as an alternative option, the subsequent instructions refer to the usage of the rope as one example.
3. While facing the cable station, firmly grasp the end of the rope with the side you wish to train during this set.
    1. Your little finger and the adjacent aspect of your hand should be in contact with the flat surface of the rope attachment’s rubber stopper as your thumb wraps over the outside of your other fingers.
    2. If you opt to use the cable without any attachment, you can grasp the ball at the end of the cable, or you can grasp the end of the cable directly with your little finger, and the adjacent side of your hand in contact with the ball. If you decide to grasp the cable directly, be aware to avoid pinching any fingers when returning the cable to its initial position after finishing your set.
4. Directly above your hand already holding the rope, grasp the rope with the hand on the side you will not be training.
5. Pivot your body 180 degrees while simultaneously lifting your upper arms fully up with your elbows fully bent to reposition the rope behind your head.
    1. The arm movement performed during this step is akin to a controlled swinging-like motion.
    2. How high you are able to raise your upper arms and bend your elbows will be influenced by your mobility.
    3. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable pulling downward, so if that is not the case, you should walk forward until you feel tension from the cable.
6. On the side you will not be training, let go of the rope while continuing to hold the rope firmly with your other side.
    1. The purpose of initially holding the rope with both hands was to help reposition it behind your head.
    2. From this point onward, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting by your side or on the cable station’s beam for balance assistance.
    3. All the subsequent directions will refer to the arm that is holding the rope.
7. While slightly bending your knees, take a step forward with your foot on the side opposite the one holding the cable.
    1. In this staggard stance, you should feel stable and balanced with an upright torso.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched from leaning back toward the cable station, your lower back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow up, press the rope upward by extending your elbow until it is fully straightened.
    1. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearm hinging at your elbow without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbow as the center of an analog clock, while your forearm is the hand that rotates about this stationary center elbow.
    2. As you keep your elbow up, your upper arm should remain in a vertical position by the side of your head.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by bending your elbow and lowering the rope until you have returned to the starting position with your elbow fully bent.
    1. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your triceps at the end range.
    2. It is typical to be able to bend your elbow beyond 90 degrees during this exercise, but specifically how far you can bend it can be affected by the flexibility of the long head of your triceps.
    3. Continue keeping your elbow raised up with your biceps by the side of your head.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. After adding your desired load (in the form of weight plates or a kettlebell) to a pull-up/dip belt you have around your waist, stand upright under a pull-up bar.
    1. The back of your belt should be securely resting on top of your buttocks, and the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) should be looped through the center hole of a weight plate or under the handle of a kettlebell. If you have a loading pin for your belt, you can alternatively loop the chain or strap through it after adding weight plates.
    2. It will be easiest if you carry the weight over to the pull-up bar and add the weight to your belt while you are under the bar, rather than adding the weight to your belt farther away from the bar before walking over to the bar.
    3. As an alternative to using a pull-up/dip belt, you can wear a weighted vest.
    4. While less stable and more awkward than using a belt or weighted vest, you also have the option of holding a dumbbell between your thighs or feet, but you will be limited in how much weight you can add when using a dumbbell.
2. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the pull-up bar with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip), with your hands shoulder-width apart, slightly wider.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
3. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will put you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and let your bodyweight pull your shoulder blades up.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back places you into a position akin to a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows will not actually drive downward, but having this intent helps pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps; however, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your lat and teres major especially well while also effectively training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up one kettlebell and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the kettlebell just in front of your thighs. Direct your palms back toward you with your elbows straight.
    1. If you do not have any experience training Jefferson curls, it is recommended that you initially try out the bodyweight Jefferson curl to determine how your body feels with this movement prior to performing a loaded variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Tuck your chin to your chest and lower the kettlebell in a slow and controlled manner by bending over and rounding your whole back through what is your comfortable range of motion while keeping your knees and elbows straight.
    1. As a cue, you can think about rounding forward each segment of your spine successively until your entire spine has achieved a rounded posture.
    2. As you progressively descend, it is natural to feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings and back muscles.
    3. How far you can descend will be influenced by your hamstring flexibility and the available bending range of motion in your spine.
    4. If you have a high degree of mobility, you may be able to descend until the kettlebell contacts the floor, but only move through a range that is painless and feels right to your body.
    5. Your arms should be directed down toward the ground throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by gradually transitioning from the bent over position to the upright position in which you started by uncoiling your back, which will lift the kettlebell up.
    1. At the end of the ascent, untuck your chin and look straight ahead.
    2. Continue keeping your knees and elbows in a straightened position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep spinal extensor muscles that run along your entire back, while also training your spinal mobility and hamstring flexibility.## Setting up
1. Securely attach your desired weight to your wrist roller.
    1. Depending on the model of your wrist roller, you may have to use weight plates (if your model uses a loading pin), or you may be able to use weight plates, kettlebells, or dumbbells (if your model uses a strap that you can loop around the weight).
    2. Leave enough of the rope initially uncoiled so that you can stand up with the roller without the weight lifting off the ground.
2. While the weight is on the ground, firmly grasp the roller’s handles with your palms facing toward you (i.e., an overhand grip) and the uncoiled part of the rope on the side of the roller that is closer to you.
    1. If the uncoiled part of the rope is on the side of the roller farther away from you, you would be able to train wrist extension when using an overhand grip, but this is the wrist flexion variation.
3. Stand upright while grasping the roller as your elbows are straight, and your hands are slightly in front of your thighs with your arms directed down toward the ground.
4. Take up the slack from the roller’s rope with alternating twisting motions of your wrists until you feel that the rope is taut from the weight’s resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straightened, bend one wrist to curl that side’s hand toward you as you extend your other wrist in the opposite direction by pointing that hand’s palm down toward the ground.
    1. You should grasp the handle firmly with the hand whose palm is curling toward you, and grasp the handle loosely with the hand whose palm is pointing toward the ground.
    2. The hand whose palm is curling toward you is what rotates the roller to lift the weight up, while the other side’s wrist is repositioning in preparation to perform the curling motion.
2. Alternate the motions in step 1 with the opposite hands as you lift the weight up by cyclically repeating these twisting movements with your wrists until you have fully coiled the rope around the roller.
3. Lower the weight in a controlled manner by reversing the same motions you just performed until the weight lightly taps the ground or is just about to do so.
4. Repeatedly perform steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest setting, connect a rope attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing directly in front of the cable station and facing away from it, reach up overhead with bent elbows to grasp the ends of the rope with a neutral grip such that your palms are facing in toward each other.
    1. Your little fingers and the adjacent aspects of your hands should be in contact with the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers as your thumbs wrap over the outside of your other fingers.
3. While keeping your hands overhead and elbows bent, step forward and adopt a staggered stance with one foot forward in front of the other before moderately leaning forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this forward torso lean throughout your set.
    2. In this position, your hands should be behind your head, and you should feel tension from the cable. If that is not the case, walk forward until you feel tension from the cable.
4. Tuck your chin toward your chest and make sure your upper arms are situated parallel to the ground or raised up a little higher.
    1. Doing so will reduce the likelihood that the back of your head obstructs the path of the cable during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the rope forward and somewhat downward by extending your elbows until they are straightened, while not moving your upper arms.
    1. At the end of this motion, the rope should be situated in front of you.
    2. If the cable contacts the back of your head during this motion, you can lean forward a little more for additional clearance.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows through your available range of motion to move the rope backward and somewhat upward in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to continue keeping your upper arms stationary while all of the movement occurs from elbow motion rather than also involving shoulder motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. If you wish to use a precise snatch grip rather than a general approximation, you can start by determining your individual hand placement with an empty barbell or dowel rod.
    1. A true snatch grip results in the bar being level with the crease of your hip when holding the bar in a fully upright position.
    2. You can find which hand placement specifically places the bar in the aforementioned position by trying out different grip widths with an empty barbell or dowel rod until determining what precisely is your individual snatch grip width.
    3. As a general starting point, you can see where a bar is situated relative to your hip crease when grasping it with your arms spread out away from your sides with extended elbows so that each arm forms close to a 45-degree angle with your torso.
    4. Rather than trying out different grip widths, you can alternatively use that arm position as a general approximation to train this exercise effectively.
2. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates while the barbell is on the ground or in an elevated position, such as a power rack.
    1. Setting up will be easier when using an elevated position, especially if you are using relatively heavy loads for this exercise.
    2. The bar will be easiest to pick up if positioned near mid-thigh height.
3. Stand close in front of the middle of the bar with a hip-width stance and spread your arms out away from your sides with straight elbows until reaching what you determined to be your snatch grip width (or until each arm forms close to a 45-degree angle with your torso if you opt to use a general approximation).
4. Hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar prior to firmly grasping the bar with an overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
5. By extending your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar while continuing to keep your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the bar upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the bar.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Pick up a dumbbell with one hand, carry it to a flat bench, and position yourself near the base of the bench, directly to its side, with the side holding the dumbbell farther from the bench.
2. Hinge at your hips to lean your torso forward. Simultaneously reach out with your free arm to place your hand on the bench with a straight elbow, and place your knee on the side not holding the dumbbell onto the bench.
    1. Based on your comfort and preference, you can place the open palm of your free hand onto the bench, or you can make a fist and place your knuckles onto the bench.
    2. In this position, your torso should be fairly close to being parallel to the ground.
    3. Your foot on the side of the dumbbell should remain in contact with the floor, which, with your free hand and knee on the bench, results in three points of contact to stabilize yourself during the exercise.
3. On the side holding the dumbbell, direct your palm so that it is facing in toward the opposite side of your body, and maintain this neutral grip position throughout your set.
    1. From this position, your arm on the side of the dumbbell should be directed down toward the ground.
4. Drive your elbow backward and upward in an arcing motion until your upper arm is parallel to the ground while allowing your elbow to bend and keeping your forearm directed down toward the ground.
    1. After performing this rowing motion, your elbow should be bent by approximately 90 degrees, placing you into the starting position for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arm parallel to the ground, progressively straighten your elbow to drive the dumbbell backward and upward in an arcing motion until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. As you move your elbow, you should keep the rest of your body stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to bend in a controlled manner as you bring the dumbbell downward and forward in an arcing fashion until you have returned to the starting position with your forearm directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your upper arm stationary as it remains parallel to the ground.
    2. At the end of the lowering phase, your elbow should be bent by approximately 90 degrees.
    3. There is no need to bend your elbow beyond 90 degrees (when your forearm is perpendicular to the ground) since that additional bending motion would be training your biceps rather than your triceps.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise trains all three heads of your triceps, but other exercises can likely train the triceps more effectively.## Setting up
1. After selecting your desired weight with the loading pin, adjust the height of the machine’s back pad to a level that allows you to perform full range of motion calf raises.
    1. If the pad is too high, the machine may not be able to provide tension when your heels are fully lowered.
    2. If the pad is too low, it may be difficult for you to assume your starting position.
2. With your knees mildly bent and your torso flexed forward at your hips while facing toward the handles, position the front halves of your feet onto the foot plate. While grasping the handles and resting forearms on the nearby pad, let your heels hang off the edge of the foot plate.
    1. In this position, your lower back should be snugly resting under the back pad.
3. If your lower back is not snugly resting under the back pad or you find it challenging to situate your body into the starting position, you can re-adjust the height of the back pad.
4. Lift your hips up by extending your knees to receive the weight from the machine.
    1. Maintain your knees in a position of extension or a slight bend with the front halves of your feet on the foot plate throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your heels into the air through the available range of motion of your ankles.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heels to descend toward the ground through the available range of motion of your ankles while continuing to keep the front halves of your feet on top of the foot plate.
    1. How far you are able to lower your heels will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. After your heels have fully descended, alternate between raising and lowering your heels until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest level, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing with the cable station positioned in front of one of your shoulders, reach up with your closer hand to grasp the handle with an overhand grip such that your palm is facing forward and away from you while keeping your wrist fairly straight.
    1. Keep your wrist in a fairly straight position throughout your set.
3. Take a step backward and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight torso lean throughout your set.
4. Pull your upper arm down and back until it is positioned at your sides with your elbow being close to fully bent.
    1. Maintain this upper arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Push the handle downward by extending your elbow until it is straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, the handle should be situated by your side.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbow through your available range of motion to raise the handle in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbow as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Anchor a long looped elastic band of your desired tension level around the center of a pull-up bar.
    1. To anchor the band, you can initially drape the band over the bar, loop one end of the band into the middle of the other end, and pull that end until it is taut.
    2. If the bar is out of reach when standing on the ground, you can initially position it, then step up onto an aerobic step, sturdy box, or bumper plate to place the bar within reach while situating the band and grasping the bar during the next step. Position the step slightly behind the bar rather than directly underneath it, so you can keep your legs straight during the exercise without contacting the step.
    3. If your bar has rough knurling rather than a smooth surface, anchoring the band directly around the bar can increase the likelihood of the band tearing over time. Initially, placing a fat grip attachment or hand towel around a bar before anchoring the band can help prevent a bar with knurling from tearing the band.
    4. Before using it, inspect the band to confirm it has no small tears, as a small tear can quickly become a full tear when stretching the band.
2. Stand under the bar, and pull the band down with both arms.
    1. Each side of the band should be flat with no twists.
3. March up one leg and step into the band with the middle of your foot snugly against the bottom end of the band’s loop before lowering your foot while straightening your leg. Alternatively, you can bend one knee and loop the band around your upper shin.
    1. Since the band’s tension increases as you stretch it, situating the band around your foot with a straight knee will provide greater assistance compared to if you situate the band around your upper shin with your knee bent.
    2. If your pull-up bar is not tall enough for you to avoid contacting the ground with your foot at the bottom of the rep when keeping your leg straight, looping the band around your upper shin will be a better option. Otherwise, you can select whichever approach aligns with your preference while providing your desired level of assistance.
    3. With either setup, the band should feel secure rather than feeling like it may slide around during the exercise.
4. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the pull-up bar with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated slightly outside shoulder width.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
5. Hang on the bar with your elbows straightened. If you are standing on a step and have the band under your foot, step off the step and let both legs hang straight down toward the ground. If you have the band around your upper shin, lift your other foot off the ground by bending that knee and keeping both knees bent throughout the exercise.
    1. You can optionally place your other leg (which was not initially in contact with the band) within the band if you wish. 
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to assume a “proud chest” that is angled slightly upward.
    1. Maintain this “proud chest” posture throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows won't actually drive downward; having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms near the sides of the torso and elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, individuals may have different ranges of motion that their bodies allow.
    3. Rather than swinging your legs, statically keep them in a similar position throughout the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms fully overhead.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, you can return one foot onto the ground or step, shift your weight into step/ground, march your other leg up and out of the band, and release the bar.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up and grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can use a different grip, such as an underhand or a neutral (i.e., palms facing toward each other) grip, while otherwise using the same technique for this exercise.
2. Hang on the bar with straight elbows by bending your knees and lifting your feet off the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straight, pull your shoulder blades downward through your available range of motion.
    1. This will move your shoulders away from your ears, while the rest of your body, except for your arms, lifts upward to a small extent.
    2. While the range of motion for this exercise may vary among different individuals, this exercise involves a low amplitude of total movement.
    3. You can think about packing your armpits down into your pockets as a technique cue.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulder blades to move upward in a controlled manner until you have reached a position that is akin to when you shrug your shoulders upward.
    1. This will move your shoulders closer toward your ears, while the rest of your body, except for your arms, moves downward to a small extent.
    2. Continue to keep your elbows straight.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will train your lats, lower traps, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. At a cable station, adjust the cable to near the height of your waist when standing, clip in a D-handle attachment, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing in front of the cable station and slightly to the side (such that the front of one shoulder is in line with the cable station), use your hand on the side closer to the cable to grasp the handle with an overhand grip and take a step backward.
3. On the side holding the handle, raise your upper arm approximately 90 degrees out to your side (so that it is parallel to the ground) while bending your elbow to approximately 90 degrees and keeping your wrist in a fairly straight position.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
4. Rotate your forearm so that it is parallel to the ground, which will result in your palm facing down toward the ground.
    1. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable, so if that is not the case, take another step backward away from the cable station.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handle upward and backward by rotating your forearm away from the cable station while moving through your shoulder’s available range of motion as you continue to keep your upper arm held out to your side and parallel to the ground.
    1. If you have typical shoulder mobility, you should be able to finish this motion with your forearm near a vertical position, but not everyone will be able to rotate as far backward.
    2. All of this movement should occur from shoulder rotation as the bony prominence on the back of your elbow continues pointing straight out to the side throughout the rep.
    3. When thinking about this technique, it can be helpful to view your forearm as a clock’s hand, while your elbow is the center about which rotation occurs without any side-to-side motion.
2. Reverse the motion by rotating your forearm toward the cable station as you move the handle downward and forward in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set with one side and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, along with a portion of the supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Connect a lat pulldown bar to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. If you do not have a standard lat pulldown bar attachment, which has a straight body and mildly angled ends, you can use a different attachment that lets you grasp it with a wide overhand grip.
2. While facing the bar and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the bar with a well outside shoulder width overhand grip, such that your palms are directed forward, and your arms form a “Y-shape” with your torso.
    1. Positioning your hands about 50% further apart than your shoulder width is appropriate, but a wide grip can encompass a range of grip widths, and specifically how wide you grasp the bar is up to your preference.
    2. What is personally considered a wide grip to you can also be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard pulldown, which you can somewhat widen to individualize your wide grip. For instance, for your wide grip, you can place your index finger on the bar just outside where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing your standard pulldown.
    3. Feel free to try out mildly different grip widths to determine if any particular position feels best to you.
3. While holding the bar, sit down on the machine’s seat and scoot forward as far as you can with the upper portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to extend and your arms to raise overhead in line with the cable's resistance.
    2. Compared to the standard lat pulldown, where you pull the bar down to your chest, you sit farther forward on the seat with the behind-the-neck variation, so your head does not restrict the bar's path.
4. As you are seated upright, nod your head to bring your chin closer toward your chest, which reduces the likelihood of your head obstructing the bar path.
    1. From this position, your elbows should be in an extended position with your arms situated overhead, and you should feel tension from the cable station holding your arms in this position.
    2. If you notice the cable remains slack in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), widening out your grip even further may be needed to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar down behind your head toward your upper back by driving your elbows down toward your sides as you progressively bend them.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. How far you can pull the bar will be affected by your shoulder mobility.
    3. If your shoulders feel comfortable doing so, you can pull the bar downward until it contacts your upper back, but you should not force yourself into any range of motion that produces shoulder discomfort.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the bar in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Approach a step or a sturdy object that can function as a step. You should be by a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
    1. Based on the equipment at your disposal, a variety of objects can serve as your step. Make sure the object can support your weight, will not move once you are on it, and allows you to lower your heels through your full range of motion during the exercise.
    2. As an alternative to a staircase’s bottom step, you may select a power rack’s bottom beam if the beam is sufficiently tall.
    3. Additionally, a calf raise block, aerobic step, deadlift block, or thick bumper plate can each be positioned near a wall or power rack for stability. Depending on your bodyweight, anchoring an aerobic step with weight on top of it may be required to prevent the step from tipping.
2. After placing your hand onto the nearby surface for balance assistance, step onto the step with the front halves of your feet on the step while the back halves of your feet are hanging off the step.
3. While keeping one foot on the step, lift your other foot off the step by bending that knee.
    1. Keep this foot in this position off the step throughout the set while you keep the front half of your working leg’s foot on the step.
## Exercise technique
1. With the leg that is standing on the step, raise your heel into the air while keeping that side’s knee straight and your trunk upright.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heel to descend toward the ground through your ankle’s available range of motion while continuing to keep the knee straight on your working side.
    1. How far you are able to lower your heel will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. Reverse the motion by lifting your heel up through your ankle’s available range of motion.
4. Continue to perform the calf raises by alternating between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat this exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near waist height, select your desired weight with the loading pin, and attach a bar with a straight (or mostly straight) body to the cable.
    1. Any bar that enables you to perform this exercise with an overhand grip can be similarly used, including a standard straight bar, a lat pulldown bar (with a long straight body and angled ends), and a multi-grip EZ bar.
2. While facing the cable station, grasp the bar with an overhand grip.
3. Pivot your body 180 degrees while simultaneously repositioning your arms such that your upper arms are raised up high with your elbows close to being fully bent with fairly straight wrists as the cable is applying a downward force to the bar behind your head.
    1. The arm movement performed during this step is akin to a controlled swinging-like motion.
    2. How high you can raise your upper arms is influenced by your mobility.
4. Adopt a staggered stance by stepping forward with one foot, and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips
    1. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable, so if that is not the case, you should step further forward until you feel tension from the cable.
    2. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows high, press the bar upward by extending your elbows until they are straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, the bar should be situated above your head.
    2. If the cable contacts the back of your head during this motion, you can tuck your chin closer to your chest for clearance.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows through your available range of motion to lower the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbows as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Use both hands to pick up one kettlebell by its horns (the two side handles) with the base of the weighted bell facing forward, away from you. Stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance.
    1. Strive to keep your wrists fairly straight while setting up and performing the exercise. Firmly gripping the horns can help you do so, since the kettlebell can bend your wrists to the side of the weight unless you actively resist it. It is not inherently problematic for your wrists to bend to a degree during this exercise, but you should avoid letting your wrists bend to the extent that you experience any wrist discomfort.
    2. Your palms should be facing in toward each other.
2. Lift the kettlebell up in front of your chest with your elbows bent by curling the weight and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the kettlebell into position if it is too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly. Then drive your hips forcefully forward while bending your elbows and mildly swinging them forward to lift the weight.
    2. The base of the weighted bell should now be facing toward you.
3. Press the kettlebell up overhead with both arms in a shoulder press motion until your arms are vertical.
    1. The base of the weighted bell should now be facing behind you as your hands are level with the bell.
    2. The back of your elbows should be pointing forward.
    3. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position along with your neck, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the kettlebell behind your head in a controlled manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
    2. Predominantly keep your elbows tucked in as they are chiefly pointing forward rather than out to your side. However, you can allow your elbows to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    3. As you keep your elbows up, your upper arms should remain in a vertical position by the sides of your head.
2. Continue lowering the kettlebell until you have fully bent your elbows or can lightly tap your upper back with the kettlebell.
    1. It is normal to be able to bend the elbows beyond 90 degrees during this exercise, but specifically how far you can bend your elbows may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps.
    2. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach their upper back with the kettlebell, wherever you feel a notable stretching sensation in your triceps can be an appropriate end range.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the kettlebell overhead.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. Continue keeping your wrists reasonably straight and your elbows up, as all the motion occurs from your elbows without moving your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you complete the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the kettlebell toward your chest (like during the lowering phase of a shoulder press) before setting it down.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a flat exercise bench or chair.
2. While holding the dumbbells, lean forward at your hips and position your forearms on top of your thighs with your palms facing down toward the floor and your wrists situated slightly in front of your knees.
3. Allow the force from the dumbbells to bend your wrists and bring your hands downward such that your palms are directed at an angle down and back toward you.
    1. In this position, the dumbbells should not be contacting your legs, so scoot your forearms forward if they are initially contacting your legs.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the dumbbells upward by extending your wrists through their available range of motion while keeping your forearms resting on your thighs.
    1. All of the motion should be coming from your wrists as your hands move upward.
    2. About halfway through the lifting phase, your palms should be facing straight down toward the floor when your hands are aligned with your forearms.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, when the dumbbells are fully raised, your palms should be angled forward.
    4. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the top side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed at an angle down and back toward you.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the seven wrist extensor muscles on the side of your forearm opposite your palm.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and place it on the ground right next to the long side of a flat exercise bench (or similar object on which you can sit, such as a plyometric or a couch if training at home).
2. Sit down on the bench with your legs spread wide apart and the dumbbell in between your legs as your feet are on the floor.
3. Lean forward at your hips to grasp the dumbbell firmly with the side you are about to train.
    1. For additional stability, you can place your free hand on top of your thigh (on the same side as your free hand) and keep it there throughout your set.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the side in which you are holding the dumbbell since that is the arm you will be training first.
4. Lean your torso slightly back toward an upright position until the dumbbell lifts off the ground with your elbow fully straightened.
5. Position the back of your elbow against the inside of your thigh while holding the dumbbell with an underhand grip, which will direct your palm to be angled forward and in toward your other side.
    1. In this starting position, your entire arm should be directed straight down toward the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow pinned against your inner thigh, curl the dumbbell upward in an arcing motion by fully bending your elbow.
    1. While your one forearm moves through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    2. Maintain your underhand grip, which will result in your palm facing straight up at the point of the rep when your forearm is parallel to the ground.
2. After your elbow has fully bent, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner until your elbow has returned to a fully straightened position with your forearm directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow braced against your inner thigh.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your forearm down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other arm.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. You can select either a fixed-weight EZ bar or an adjustable plate-loaded EZ bar, which are functionally interchangeable for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with extended elbows.
    1. If the EZ bar is already positioned in an elevated rack at mid-thigh level or above, you naturally would not have to squat down or hinge at your hips before picking it up.
3. Grasp the bar with approximately a wider than shoulder width grip and a semi-underhand grip (AKA semi-neutral grip) such that your palms are directed at an angle that is facing forward and inward.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before an outside shoulder width semi-underhand grip is accessible on the bar.
4. While keeping your elbows extended, stand fully upright while gripping the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the bar upwards and lowering it down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the seat so that your upper arms can be parallel with the floor or a little lower while grasping the handles.
    1. After initially adjusting the seat height, spread your arms outward so that your hands are level with the handles.
    2. You may have to re-adjust the seat if your upper arms are not parallel with the floor or a little lower.
    3. Some people will find their shoulders feel more comfortable when the seat is set to a height that allows their hands to be a bit lower than their shoulders, compared to when their hands are completely level with their shoulders.
2. Adjust the pins above the handles to a position that allows you to grasp the handles with your hands as close as they can be to each other while your chest is facing the machine’s pad.
3. After sitting down with your chest against the pad, grasp the vertical handles with a neutral grip such that your palms face toward each other and slightly bend your elbows.
    1. Maintain the slight bend in your elbows throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Spread the handles apart and backward until your arms and torso form a “T-shape.”
2. Reverse the motion by bringing the handles forward and toward each other until they have returned to their starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent (i.e., the quadruped position, which is also known as being “on all fours”).
    1. In this position, your elbows should be stacked underneath your shoulders with your arms pointing straight down toward the ground.
2. Situate your hands slightly outside shoulder width apart from each other, with your fingers spread somewhat apart from each other.
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon which hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
4. Walk your feet forward while lifting your hips and angling your torso downward. Finish with your knees straight and your body as close to forming an inverted “V” shape (when viewed from the side) as your flexibility permits.
    1. How far you can walk your feet forward and consequently lift your hips up with straight knees can be determined by your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Based on your preference, you can situate your feet hip-width apart or closer together.
    3. Strive to keep your back flat and your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
5. While bearing weight through the bases of your palms (i.e., heels of your hands) and the balls of your feet, lift your heels off the ground in a calf raise-like motion.
    1. Maintain this foot position throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you keep your back flat.
2. While keeping your hips bent and high, lower your head toward the ground by bending your elbows in a controlled manner.
    1. Rather than keeping them completely tucked by your sides or fully flaring them out, allow your elbows to flare out mildly away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you.
    2. Avoid letting your hips drop down vertically closer to your chest, which would involve reducing the degree to which your hips are bent. As you bend your elbows, your hips will naturally descend as well, but they should lower at the same rate as your head and torso, while they all move together as one unit. This should result in your hips finishing at about the same height above your head at the bottom of the rep as at the top of the rep.
    3. Continue keeping your neck near a neutral position rather than meaningfully tilting your head up or down.
    4. You can finish the lowering phase once your head lightly taps the ground or just before this point.
    5. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid dropping down in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your head, torso, and hips up in unison until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Continue maintaining the same degree of bend in your hips with your knees still straightened.
    2. As a cue, you think about pushing the ground down and away from you.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your upper pec major, lateral delts, triceps, traps, serratus anterior, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a bench with an upright posture and your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
    1. You can opt to sit down on a flat bench without any back support or sit down on a seated bench with back support.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing back behind you, which positions you with a reverse (AKA overhand) grip.
    1. Maintain this reverse grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl both dumbbells upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, but it may train your biceps somewhat less effectively than a curl with an underhand or neutral grip.## Setting up
1. Approach a step or a sturdy object that can function as a step. You should be by a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
    1. Based on the equipment at your disposal, a variety of objects can serve as your step. Make sure the object can support your weight, will not move once you are on it, and allows you to lower your heels through your full range of motion during the exercise.
    2. As an alternative to a staircase’s bottom step, you may select a power rack’s bottom beam if the beam is sufficiently tall.
    3. Additionally, a calf raise block, aerobic step, deadlift block, or thick bumper plate can each be positioned near a wall or power rack for stability. Depending on your bodyweight, anchoring an aerobic step with weight on top of it may be required to prevent the step from tipping.
2. After placing your hand onto the nearby surface for balance assistance, stand with your heels on top of a step as the front half of your feet hang off the step.
    1. Once on the step, your knees should be straight throughout this exercise.
3. While keeping your heels on the step, allow your feet to point downward as the balls of your feet move toward the ground as low as your ankle mobility allows.
    1. After doing so, your toes should be situated closer to the ground than your heels.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your heels on the step, lift the rest of your feet upward away from the ground through your available ankle range of motion.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, the balls of your feet should be approximately level with your heels or above them if you have normal ankle mobility.
    2. Motion should occur exclusively at your ankle as the rest of your body remains stationary.
    3. You will likely feel the muscles on the front of your shins engage while doing so.
    4. With the balls of your feet lifting up rather than your heels, this is the opposite movement of a calf raise.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering your feet in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your toes pointing down toward the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train the four muscle ankle dorsiflexor muscles on the front of your lower leg, the largest of which is the tibialis anterior.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, straddle the machine as you are facing toward the weights.
2. Place your feet onto the foot plates and your chest onto the chest pad such that your whole body is inclined forward.
    1. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
3. Depending upon your height and leg lengths, you may want to bend your knees somewhat or keep them extended to determine where your torso is situated on the chest pad.
    1. How you position your knees will be based on what feels most comfortable and stable to you, but you can initially see how you feel when positioning your knees to allow the uppermost aspect of the chest pad to be approximately level with your collarbone.
    2. Individuals who have longer legs will typically feel more comfortable when using a somewhat more bent knee position, while those with shorter legs will typically feel more comfortable with extended knees.
4. After determining which degree of knee bend places you in a comfortable position, firmly grasp the parallel handles with a neutral grip such that your palms are directed in toward each other.
    1. Certain T-bar row machines have a pair of inner handles that are angled outward and backward (which allow you to use a semi-neutral grip) and outer handles directed straight out to the sides (which allow you to use an overhand grip) but lack parallel handles required to use a neutral grip.
    2. If your T-bar row machine does not have parallel handles, you can select the chest-supported semi-neutral grip  machine T-bar row as an alternative.
5. Lift the bar up off the machine’s stopper, center the bar so that it no longer lies above the stopper, and lower it until your elbows are extended.
    1. In this bottom position, allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades apart from one another, which will likely prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in your upper back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides with this neutral grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Keep your torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set rather than arching your lower back to lift your chest up off the pad.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar back onto the machine’s stopper.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and stand upright in a near hip width stance with your arms hanging down by your sides as your elbows are straight.
## Exercise technique
1. Direct your palms forward to adopt an underhand grip.
2. Curl the dumbbells upward in an arcing manner by bending your elbows without rotating your palms.
    1. Keep your wrists straight, torso upright, and your elbows by your sides, rather than swinging your elbows forward through shoulder motion.
    2. While your forearms move through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    3. Since you will maintain an underhand grip during this lifting phase, your palms should be facing upward when your forearms are parallel to the ground.
    4. At the top of the curl, your elbows should be fully bent with your forearms touching your biceps and your palms facing back toward your shoulders.
3. Rotate your palms forward away from you to adopt an overhand grip, which is also called a reverse grip.
4. While keeping your wrists straight, lower the dumbbells in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to straighten progressively without rotating your palms.
    1. Since you will maintain a reverse grip during this lowering phase, your palms should be facing down when your forearms are parallel to the ground before facing backward once you have fully straightened your elbows.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis while also training your wrist extensor muscles more effectively than most curl variations.## Setting up
1. Connect an attachment with a straight bar shaft to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and sit down facing the cable station.
    1. You can use any attachment that allows you to hold it comfortably with an underhand grip with your hands approximately shoulder width apart.
    2. The most commonly available attachment appropriate for this exercise is a standard lat pulldown bar attachment, which has a straight shaft with mildly angled ends and may be found attached to a cable lat pulldown machine.
    3. You can readily perform this exercise with essentially the same technique whether you use a seated cable row machine or an adjustable cable station.
    4. If you use a seated cable row machine, you naturally will sit down on its seat, but if you are using an adjustable cable station, you will sit down on the ground after adjusting the cable to a height that is approximately level with your shoulders when seated on the floor with an upright torso.
2. Position your feet in contact with the machine’s foot plates or the base of the adjustable cable station.
    1. Allow your knees to bend to an angle that allows you to feel stable and comfortable, which can be a mild to moderately mild knee bend for many people.
    2. With greater knee bend, you will be able to sit closer toward the cable, so you can bend your knees further if you initially cannot reach the bar when leaning forward by bending at your hip
3. Lean forward at your hips until you are able to grasp the bar firmly with straight elbows. Grasp it with a near shoulder-width underhand grip, such that your palms face upward.
4. While holding the bar with straight elbows, lean your torso backward at your hips until you are sitting with an upright torso.
    1. When seated upright, you should feel tension from the cable, so if the cable is still slack, you can extend your knees to scoot yourself backward until the machine’s weight stack separates and you feel the resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar back toward your torso by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides with this underhand grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion or until the bar is directly in front of your torso, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten to move the bar forward.
    1. Finish with your arms fully outstretched in front of you.
    2. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, without rounding your lower back. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you to train them more effectively.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near waist height, connect a rope attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Grasp the ends of the rope with a neutral grip such that your palms are facing in toward each other.
    1. Your little fingers and the adjacent aspects of your hands should be in contact with the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers as your thumbs wrap over the outside of your other fingers.
3. Pivot your body away from the pulley while simultaneously repositioning your arms such that your upper arms are raised up high with your elbows close to being fully bent as the cable is applying a downward force to the rope behind your head.
    1. To get into position with a heavier load, you can lunge down into a half-kneeling position during this step before standing upright once the rope is behind your head.
    2. The arm movement performed during this step is akin to a controlled swinging-like motion.
    3. How high you can raise your upper arms is influenced by your mobility.
4. Take a step forward, and slightly lean your torso forward by hinging at your hips while standing in a staggered stance with one foot in front of the other.
    1. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable, so if that is not the case, you should step further forward until you feel tension from the cable.
    2. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows high, press the rope upward by extending your elbows until they are straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, the rope should be situated above your head.
    2. If the cable contacts the back of your head during this motion, you can tuck your chin closer to your chest for clearance.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows through your available range of motion to lower the rope in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbows as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the Roman chair’s pad to a level that positions its top slightly below the top of your hip bones when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. The appropriate position should allow you to move your lower back freely while supporting your hips.
    2. While it can effectively stabilize your hips, positioning the top of the pad higher than the top of your hip bones can restrict your lower back’s motion, which is suboptimal for training lower back muscles.
    3. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones (rather than only slightly lower), the hips will not be stabilized as well, which can make it more challenging to emphasize training your back muscles.
    4. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position.
2. While placing your hands onto the handles for balance support, step onto the foot plate one leg at a time. Place the back of your heels against the back of the foot plate, as your soles fully contact the foot plate.
    1. Based on your preference, you can point your toes straight forward, or you can rotate them to point inward. Pointing the toes inward may help some people limit the hip muscle engagement while focusing on the back muscles.
    2. Situating your feet approximately hip width to shoulder width apart from each other will result in a viable stance width, but how wide of a stance you can use will be influenced by the width of your Roman chair’s foot plate.
    3. Your knees should be straight after you have finished setting your feet on the foot plate.
3. Check to make sure that the pad is positioned as described by step 1, and readjust the pad height if warranted before resuming your setup.
4. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
    2. Your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your back mostly flat, hinge at your hips through the range of motion that is permitted by the pad and your flexibility.
    1. Only during this step are you intentionally moving about your hips to assume the starting position.
    2. Once you have reached the end range of your hip motion, it will be easier to emphasize back motion.
    3. Depending on specifically where you have the pad positioned, the pad may limit how far you can hinge, or your hamstring flexibility may limit it.
2. Round your entire back in a slow and controlled curling manner through the full range of motion that is comfortable for you.
    1. The degree to which you can round your back will be affected by your spinal mobility.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your back muscles in the bottom position, but you should not feel any discomfort at the end range. Only round your back as far as is comfortable.
    3. Rather than moving only one part of your back, strive to round your lower, middle, and upper back simultaneously.
    4. As a cue, you can think about bringing the bottom of your ribs toward the top of your hip bones.
3. Reverse the movement by extending (straightening) your back in a controlled manner without hinging at your hips.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, your back should be near a neutral position, which involves your lower back being mildly arched.
    2. For increasing the size and strength of your back muscles, there is no need to arch your back maximally like you would during a cobra yoga pose.
    3. Keep your glutes relaxed to the best of your ability as you focus on moving your spine rather than your hips.
    4. You should feel your spinal extensor muscles, which run along each side of the spine, contract to extend your back.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep intrinsic spinal extensor muscles that run along the length of your entire back.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground, and stand close in front of the middle of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
    1. You can set up with the bar approximately over the middle of your feet, which should situate the bar close to one inch (2.5cm) away from most individuals’ shins while initially standing upright.
    2. The diameter of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. If you do not have a pair of full-diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates or any bumper plates) on the bar, this exercise can functionally serve as a deficit Pendlay row with a greater range of motion than what is typical for a standard Pendlay row. Rowing the bar from a lower than standard height is not recommended for individuals who lack the flexibility to reach the bar with a flat lower back position. If you have to round your lower back meaningfully to reach the bar, you can elevate the plates on top of aerobic steps, deadlift blocks, or stacks of full-diameter weight plates, like you could do when setting up for a block pull.
2. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something. Once you fully sit back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout the exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. Explosively pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward as you pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your torso in a hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set. All the movement should occur from motion at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows while your lower body remains stationary.
    2. Continue keeping your core braced, lower back flat, and chest up.
    3. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, which may result in your upper arms forming near 45-degree angles with your torso toward the end of the lifting phase.
    4. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar until it is fully resting on the ground.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, and your elbows will move downward and forward until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    2. Continue maintaining the same degree of forward torso lean with your chest up and lower back flat.
    3. The lowering phase of the Pendlay row is typically performed with a very quick tempo, but you can alternatively lower the bar in a more controlled manner if you wish to do so.
4. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than using momentum) before continuing to alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is resting on the ground. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Symmetrically elevate each end of a mobile flat bench on top of sturdy objects like aerobic steps with risers, bumper plates, plyometric boxes, or deadlift blocks. Elevate it to the level where you can lie face-down on the bench without your hands contacting the ground when your arms are hanging down on each side of the bench.
    1. If you have access to one, you can alternatively use a specific seal row bench, which is already elevated, but this is not a particularly common type of equipment.
    2. When elevating the bench, make sure that each base is level and that you stack the bench onto only objects that are able to handle the combined weight of you, your dumbbells, and the bench.
    3. Setting up this exercise can take more time than most row variations, and you can train your back muscles in a very similar manner with a shorter setup if you perform the “low incline neutral grip chest-supported dumbbell row%201a15c6f170d8802d969ae139f42062fa.md)” from this database.
2. Place a pair of dumbbells on an elevated surface near the head of your bench in a position where you can grasp them while lying down on the bench.
    1. If there is sufficient space, you would ideally place the dumbbells onto the object you used to elevate the bench.
    2. Alternatively, you can situate the dumbbells on top of bumper plates (or something similar) that you place near the head of the bench in a position you can grasp while lying on the bench, but would not restrict your rowing motion.
    3. With a sufficiently low setup, you also may be able to position the dumbbells on the floor underneath the bench and still be able to pick them up, but this would not be feasible with a more elevated setup.
3. Lie down with the front of your torso and thighs flat on the bench in a position that allows your arms to hang straight down toward the floor without being obstructed by the objects you used to elevate the bench.
    1. Based on your preference, you can either keep your knees extended or bent.
    2. Position your head in a comfortable position, such as tucking your chin and lifting your head slightly up to keep your face off the bench.
4. Firmly grasp each dumbbell, and angle your palms in toward each other while your arms hang down with straight elbows.
    1. The bony bump on the back of each elbow should be pointing backward, which is the direction in which you will row.
    2. Maintain this neutral grip throughout your set.
    3. To be able to row through a full range of motion, the dumbbells should not be in contact with anything in this position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows backward and upward in an arcing motion to pull the dumbbells toward your hip. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your elbows tucked by your sides, rather than flaring them outward.
    2. Progressively bend your elbows to the extent that keeps your forearms close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    3. Keep your torso in contact with the bench throughout the set rather than arching your lower back to lift your chest up off the bench.
    4. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    5. While performing this arcing motion, the dumbbells should not move straight upward in a vertical path.
    6. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled arcing forward motion until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Allow the weights to pull your shoulder blades apart to stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weights rapidly pull your arms down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells back down.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. If you have access to one, you can also use a fixed-weight straight barbell, which may be lighter than a typical 20kg (44lb) barbell that has sleeves on which to add weight plates.
2. Stand close in front of the middle of the bar with a hip-width stance and straight elbows before hinging at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. Keep your lower back near flat, rather than meaningfully rounding it while doing so.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width underhand grip such that your palms are facing forward away from you.
4. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar while continuing to keep your elbows straight.
    1. Once upright, the bar should be directly in front of your thighs.
    2. Maintain this upright position with straight elbows throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by curling your wrists through their available range of motion while keeping your arms stationary.
    1. All motion should come from your wrists while you keep the rest of your body stationary.
    2. Your palms should be angled upward once you have fully curled your wrists.
    3. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the palm side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed forward away from you.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your heels into the air through the available range of motion of your ankles while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heels to descend toward the ground while continuing to keep your knees straight.
3. After your heels touch the ground, alternate between raising and lowering your heels until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can develop your gastrocnemius and soleus.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. You can initially set the bar up on the floor or from an elevated position, such as from a power rack.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with extended elbows.
3. Grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder width grip and an underhand grip such that your palms are directed forward.
4. While keeping your elbows extended, stand upright while gripping the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the bar up and lowering it down until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise is a time-tested means of effectively training the biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each cable to a height approximately level with your shoulders or slightly above them, clip in a D-handle attachment, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. After standing centered between the cables with your sides facing the cable stacks, take a step backward, raise your arms in front of you until they are approximately parallel to the ground, and side-step toward one cable.
3. Reach across your body with your arm that is opposite to the cable to grasp its handle as your palm is facing the cable stack, before side-stepping toward the other cable and grasping its handle with your free hand.
4. Return to being centered between the cables with your arms crossed in front of your torso, with slightly bent elbows, as the cable stacks are situated a little in front of you.
    1. In this position, your arms should resemble an “X-shape” with each other.
    2. Maintain slightly bent elbows throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles out and away from each other as you move your arms backward until they form a “T-shape” with your torso.
2. Reverse the motion by bringing the handles inward and toward each other in a controlled manner until they have crossed and returned to their starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a kettlebell with one hand, carry it over to a flat bench, and position yourself near the base of the bench and directly to its side with your side that is holding the kettlebell farther away from the bench.
    1. If you have to carry the kettlebell a meaningful distance before reaching your bench and your grip is somewhat fatigued, you can place the kettlebell on the bench before picking it up with your opposite hand to start your set with the side that does not have a fatigued grip.
2. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips, reach out with your free arm to place its hand on the bench with a straight elbow, and place your knee (on the side not holding the kettlebell) onto the bench.
    1. In this position, your torso should now be reasonably close to being parallel to the ground, and your lower back should be flat rather than being meaningfully rounded.
    2. While setting up, keep your elbow straight on the side holding the kettlebell and allow that arm to hang down toward the ground with your shoulder blade naturally pulled forward toward the ground from the weight.
    3. Based on your comfort and preference, you can place the open palm of your free hand onto the bench, or you can make a fist and place your knuckles onto the bench.
    4. Your foot on the side of the kettlebell should remain firmly in contact with the floor and be sufficiently far behind the kettlebell that your leg does not restrict your row. If you initially do not feel well-balanced, you can try positioning this foot somewhat further out to the side to widen your base of support.
3. Direct your palm so that it is facing in toward the opposite side of your body, and maintain this neutral grip position throughout your set.
    1. The bony bump on the back of your elbow should be directed backward, which is the direction you will row.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Drive your elbow backward and upward in an arcing motion to pull the kettlebell toward your hip. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade backward, and keep your elbow tucked by your side rather than flaring it outward.
    1. When allowing your elbow to bend naturally, your forearm should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    2. At the top of the row, your elbow should be aligned with your torso or slightly behind your torso.
    3. Keep your torso in a mostly fixed position with your chest facing a similar direction throughout this movement. A mild degree of torso rotation is not problematic, but avoid letting it become excessive and turning the exercise into a cheat row.
    4. While performing this arcing motion, the kettlebell should not move straight upward in a vertical path.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the kettlebell in a controlled arcing motion until your arm is directed down toward the ground with your elbow straightened.
    1. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blade forward, which will stretch your upper back muscles and result in your working side’s shoulder being mildly closer to the ground than your other side’s shoulder at the bottom of the row.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight rapidly pull your arm down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing forward.
    1. Maintain this underhand grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl both dumbbells upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
You have the option of setting up this exercise with the plates resting on the ground or an elevated surface like the top of an exercise bench or plyometric box. Using the elevated set-up will make it easier to get into the starting position and set the weight down at the end of the set. While useful for heavy loads, the elevated setup requires more equipment, takes more time, and is not needed for lighter loads. The setup for each approach is described separately, but the exercise technique is the same for both options.

### Setting up from the ground

1. Add weight plates to a barbell situated in a landmine attachment (or a viable substitute).
    1. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    2. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
2. Stand upright in a near shoulder-width stance with the free end of the bar between the middle of your feet.
3. Simultaneously bend your hips and knees to squat down until you are low enough to grasp the bar.
    1. Keep your back flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your back.
4. Firmly grasp the end of the bar with your fingers cupped around it, as one hand is directly in front of the other.
    1. The little finger of one hand should be touching the index finger of your other hand.
    2. Your palms should be facing in toward the bar.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
6. Quickly lift the bar up to chest height while rapidly repositioning your hands under the bar with your elbows bent. Allow the end of the bar to rest on the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with your fingers interlocked and your thumbs against the tip of the bar.
    1. The tip of the bar should now be directly in front of your upper chest.
    2. Rather than being flared out or completely by your sides, your elbows should be mildly in front of your sides.
    3. Your knees and hips should now be straight, like when you are normally standing upright, but allow your body to lean mildly forward at your ankles.
    4. With the appropriate positioning, you should feel stable holding the bar.
7. While tucking your chin slightly back, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
    2. Avoid leaning back with an excessively arched lower back position.

### Setting up from an elevated position

1. Place a barbell in a landmine attachment or a viable substitute.
    1. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    2. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
2. Load the bar with one full diameter plate (such as a 45lb (20kg) plate or any bumper plate) or whichever plate will have the largest diameter of the plates you will use for your working weight.
3. Position a sturdy object with an elevated surface directly next to the plate you loaded onto the bar. Select an object that is tall enough to allow you to squat the weight up while holding it in front of your chest once the bar’s plate is resting on the object.
    1. Viable objects include a plyometric box at least 18in (46cm) in height, a flat exercise bench, or an aerobic step elevated on risers.
    2. How high the object needs to be for you to be able to squat the bar up will be influenced by your height and mobility.
    3. If the object you selected is too low for you, you can increase its height by stacking bumper plates on top of it. With an aerobic step, you can naturally increase its height by adding risers.
4. Lift the bar up by the free end and rest the weight plate on top of the elevated object.
5. Finish adding weight plates to the bar with your desired load.
6. Stand upright with the free end of the bar above the center point between the middle of your feet. Position your feet near shoulder width apart with your toes pointing mildly out to the sides or in whatever is your preferred squat stance.
7. Simultaneously bend your hips and knees to squat down until the tip of the bar is just in front of your upper chest.
    1. Keep your back flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your back.
8. With your elbows bent, position the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) firmly against the bottom of the end of the bar. Interlock your fingers, and place your thumbs against the tip of the bar.
    1. Rather than being flared out or completely by your sides, your elbows should be mildly in front of your sides.
9. Drive through the ground to stand upright while keeping the bar end of the bar directly in front of your chest.
    1. Your knees and hips should now be straight, like when you are normally standing upright, but allow your body to lean mildly forward at your ankles. A mild forward lean should feel natural due to the angle of the landmine.
10. While tucking your chin slightly back, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
    2. Avoid leaning back with an excessively arched lower back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes like you are trying to crush a walnut. Simultaneously, brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core and tightening your glutes throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the bar upward and outward by driving your elbows upward as you progressively straighten them.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as your arms are angled upward and forward.
    2. Your hands will move diagonally since the bar will naturally move in an arcing manner rather than in a purely vertical path.
    3. Continue keeping your knees and hips straightened rather than using them to help you lift the weight.
    4. Throughout your set, avoid leaning your torso back via excessive lower back arching.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until its end is directly in front of your upper chest.
    1. Move your elbows downward and backward toward your sides while progressively bending them.
    2. At the bottom, your elbows should be tucked slightly in front of where they would be when standing with your arms by your sides.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
5. Once you have completed the lowering phase of your last rep, set the weight down in the opposite manner to how you initially picked it up.
    1. If you set up from the floor, quickly and sequentially reposition your hands under the bar while guiding the bar down until you are holding it in front of you with straight elbows. After returning your hands to the cupping grip you used when initially setting up, squat down to set the weight onto the ground.
    2. If you set up with the plates on top of an elevated object, squat down until you can return the plates to fully resting on the object before letting go of the bar.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your upper pec major, lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and stand over one end of a flat exercise bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space behind you. With this equipment, you would straddle the bench and walk forward toward the J-hooks.
    2. With a portable exercise bench, your back should be facing the head of the bench, as you would when dumbbell bench pressing.
2. Sit down on the bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your upper thighs near your abdomen.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
    2. Your palms should be facing in toward each other with your upper arms by your sides.
    3. To move heavy dumbbells efficiently into the proper position, you can initially situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up. Then you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down on the bench. Your head should be right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent. You will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
4. In a bench pressing motion, press the dumbbells up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a bench press rep), the dumbbells should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    2. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the dumbbells with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the dumbbells hard will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    3. Based on what feels most comfortable, you can have your palms facing in toward each other (with a neutral grip), forward (with an overhand grip), or angled inward and forward. During your set, you can similarly allow your palms to rotate within this range if that feels comfortable to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the dumbbells in a controlled arcing manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Based on what feels most natural and comfortable, you can keep your elbows tucked in, or you can allow your elbows to flare out to your sides slightly.
    2. Avoid letting your elbows drift back and down with the dumbbells through shoulder motion. When viewed from the side, the proper elbow position should result in your upper arms remaining in a vertical position.
    3. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
2. Finish lowering the dumbbells just before they reach your forehead or the bench.
    1. If you are using an overhand grip (with your palms facing forward to start), you will finish with the dumbbells just above your forehead.
    2. If you are using a neutral grip (with your palms facing inward), your head may not be in the way of the dumbbells, so you can finish with the dumbbells just above the end of the bench.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way, especially when the weights approach your head.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the dumbbells into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the dumbbells are vertically above your shoulders.
    2. Continue keeping your upper arms in a vertical position as you avoid pushing your elbows forward (toward your chest) through shoulder motion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
5. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the dumbbells toward your sides like you would during a neutral grip dumbbell bench press.
    1. Regardless of the palm position you used for your set, it is easier to sit up with the dumbbells when your palms are facing in toward each other.
6. March your thighs up toward the weights, and then rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with slightly bent elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
2. Assume a bent-over torso position by hinging at your hips and mildly bending your knees until your torso is close to parallel with the ground.
    1. How far you are able to bend over will be affected by your mobility.
    2. If your mobility permits, you can bend over until your torso is parallel with the ground, but you can still effectively perform this exercise if you are not able to descend as far.
    3. While bent over, your arms should be approximately perpendicular to the ground with the dumbbells situated in front of your knees.
    4. Maintain this bent-over position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the dumbbells away from each other and straight out to your sides until your arms and torso form a “T-shape” without moving your hips or knees.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells toward each other in a controlled manner back until they have returned to their starting position in front of your knees.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright in a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing in toward your sides (i.e., using a neutral grip position).
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one dumbbell resting at your side with an extended elbow, curl the other dumbbell upward and across the front of your torso toward your opposite shoulder by bending at your elbow.
    1. As you start curling across your body, you will naturally rotate your shoulder inward such that your palm is directed toward the front of your torso.
    2. To allow the dumbbell to clear your torso, you will have to position your upper arm slightly in front of your body while curling (rather than keeping your upper arm right next to your side like you could with a standard hammer curl).
2. After your elbow has fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner until your elbow has returned to an extended position with the dumbbell and your upper back at your side.
3. With your other arm, repeat steps 1 and 2.
4. Alternate between curling with each side until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. If you have experience performing the dual kettlebell clean and kettlebell overhead press, you can set up by cleaning a pair of kettlebells and pressing them overhead. Otherwise, pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright in a near hip-width stance while holding the kettlebells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms are facing forward.
    1. The set-up described here will be less complicated than the technique for a dual kettlebell clean, but can be less efficient if you are using rather heavy weights that are difficult to curl for even one rep.
2. Curl the kettlebells up toward the front of your shoulders by bending your elbows and turning your palms to face in toward each other at the top. Allow the bodies of the kettlebells to rest against your forearms while keeping wrists straight.
    1. To help get the weights into position, feel free to perform a cheat curl-like motion where you initially generate some momentum by swinging the weights mildly backward with straight elbows before swinging your elbows forward while curling them.
3. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, keep your chest up in a “proud chest” posture, rather than letting your shoulders and upper back round forward into a hunched position.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain its natural curvature (like when you are normally standing upright) throughout the exercise.
4. Press the kettlebells up overhead by driving your elbows upward while straightening them. Direct your palms to face forward at the top.
    1. Strive to finish with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling with your elbows fully straightened, and your biceps by your ears.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the kettlebells behind your head in a controlled arcing manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Predominantly keep your elbows tucked in as they are chiefly pointing forward rather than out to your side. However, you can allow your elbows to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you. If the kettlebells bump into each other, you are flaring your elbows excessively.
    2. Movement should chiefly occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows as you strive to avoid lowering your elbows through shoulder motion.
    3. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
2. Continue lowering the kettlebells until you have fully bent your elbows or can lightly tap the back of your shoulders with the kettlebells.
    1. It is normal to be able to bend the elbows beyond 90 degrees, but specifically how far you can bend your elbows may be limited by the flexibility of the long head of your triceps.
    2. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach their shoulders with the kettlebells, wherever you feel a notable stretching sensation in your triceps can be an appropriate end range.
    3. You do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, but you should be actively controlling the weights the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the kettlebells up into the starting position overhead.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as your wrists are vertically stacked above your shoulders.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows up as you aim to avoid moving your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebells down in the opposite manner to how you initially lifted them into the starting position.
    1. After your last rep, lower the kettlebells toward the front of your shoulders while bending your elbows like you would during an overhead press. Then lower the kettlebells to your sides as you straighten your elbows in a controlled manner and swing your elbows mildly back to help control the weights.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the entirety of your triceps, including its long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. To set up for this row, you can perform the lifting phase of a conventional deadlift, followed by the lowering phase of a Romanian deadlift.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the middle of your feet under the middle of the bar.
3. With your arms pointing down toward the bar, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can reach the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up throughout the exercise.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, as when you are looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced from this point on.
6. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. While motion occurs at your hips and knees, your back should remain rigid.
7. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as your hamstring flexibility permits with a flat back.
    1. To avoid rounding your lower back, you should think about sitting back rather than bending over.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something. 
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical (or close to vertical) throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively pushing your knees forward depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    5. If you have the mobility to hinge until the plates touch the ground, hinge at your hips until the plates are just above the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. As you do so, keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your lower body should remain nearly stationary.
    3. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. While lifting the bar, allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, which may result in your upper arms forming near a 45-degree angle with your torso toward the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your arms are directed down toward the ground with extended elbows.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
    2. The plates should not touch the ground when lowering the bar.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the floor by bending your knees further.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near shoulder height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Stand facing the cable, slightly to the side, with one of your shoulders in line with it
    1. Stand with the side of your body you wish to train in line with the cable. The subsequent directions will refer to that side’s arm, while you can position your opposite arm wherever you prefer throughout the exercise.
3. Grasp the handle firmly with a straight elbow and your arm elevated to the height of the pulley. 
4. Step backward until the machine’s weight stack separates and you feel the resistance.
5. Mildly bend your hips and knees by sitting your buttocks somewhat backward with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart to assume a stable stance.
    1. In this position, the center of your breastbone should be facing the same way as the center of your hips, rather than having your trunk rotated toward one side.
6. Unless you prefer a different grip, direct your palm inward to hold the handle with a neutral grip.
    1. If you wish, you can alternatively rotate the handle to use an underhand grip with your palm facing up or an overhand grip with your palm facing down.
    2.  However, many people find the neutral grip feels most natural, so it is an appropriate starting point.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbow back toward your side while simultaneously bending it and pulling your shoulder blade backward to pull the handle back toward you.
    1. Rather than notably flaring your elbow up and away from your side, keep your elbow tucked near your side, which should naturally feel comfortable.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    3. Keep your forearm mostly aligned with the cable, which naturally promotes the appropriate degree of elbow bend.
    4. Movement should occur at your shoulder, shoulder blade, and elbow, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position as the center of your breastbone faces the same direction throughout the set.
    5. The cable will impart a rotational force to your trunk that your core muscles will resist to prevent your trunk from twisting during the row.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to move forward in a controlled manner as it progressively straightens to move the handle forward.
    1. Without rotating your torso, simultaneously let the cable’s resistance pull your shoulder blade beyond its resting position to stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. Finish this step with your elbow fully straightened and your whole arm aligned with the cable
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arm up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that aligns your shoulders with the machine’s pivot points and allows you to rest your triceps (the back of your upper arms) and elbows comfortably on the arm pads once you are seated.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point (which is also known as the axis of rotation) is the center about which the machine’s lever arm will rotate during the exercise. Aligning your shoulders with these pivot points can help the exercise feel smooth and natural.
    2. The same seat height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points is what should allow you to rest your triceps and elbows on the arm pads throughout the exercise.
    3. Finding the ideal seat height for you may initially require some trial and error, since you can only determine if the height is appropriate by sitting on the seat and checking to see if the centers of your shoulders are level with the pivot points.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit on the seat with an upright torso and a “proud chest” posture, as your back and the back of your hip bones are resting against the backrest.
    1. Situate your feet flat on the ground if you can do so, but some lifters may not be able to reach the ground with their feet when using certain machines. If that applies to you, you can simply let your feet hang down or place a step or bumper plate under your feet if you prefer them not to dangle.
4. If your machine has one, fasten the seat belt securely around your waist for additional stability.
5. Place one foot against the machine’s foot pedal, and push the pedal forward with your leg.
    1. This lowers the arm pads and moves the handle forward to a position you can comfortably reach.
    2. Keep the pedal pushed away during the next two steps.
6. With bent elbows, raise your arms to situate your elbows and triceps snugly against the arm pads while grasping the handles.
    1. Different machines have different handle types that will influence specifically how you should grasp the handle (such as with an overhand or semi-neutral grip), but what chiefly matters is that your grip feels comfortable and stable.
    2. While uncommon, certain machines may have handles without any arm pads. If you are using one of these atypical machines, you will simply grasp the handles and ignore the details about arm pad usage while otherwise performing the exercise as instructed.
7. Slowly bring the foot pedal back to its starting position by bending your knee, which will shift the machine’s resistance to your arms. Simultaneously, allow the machine’s resistance to lift your elbows upward and backward through the full range of motion that is comfortable to your shoulders.
    1. Maintain the natural curvature of your lower back rather than excessively arching your lower back and flaring your ribs upward and away from your hip bones as you raise your elbows.
    2. The degree to which you can comfortably allow your elbows to lift upward will be influenced by your individual shoulder mobility, but it is common to be able to reach near the point at which the upper arms are fully vertical.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your shoulders and/or back muscles in the starting position, but you should move through the range in which you do not feel any shoulder joint pain or discomfort.
8. Once your arms are fully lifted up, lift your foot off the foot pedal and place it flat on the ground with your other foot.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down toward your sides in an arcing motion until your elbows are by your sides.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up and back in contact with the backrest rather than slumping forward into a slouched posture.
    2. During the initial half of this movement, you can focus on pulling down, while you can focus on pulling back during the latter half of this movement.
    3. Emphasize driving your elbows down into the arm pads rather than pulling the handle with your hands.
    4. As your arms move through motion at your shoulders, the rest of your body should be stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to sweep upward in a controlled arcing manner through the full range of motion that is comfortable to your shoulders.
    1. You should return to the starting position with your elbows lifted as high up as your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    2. Like when setting up, avoid letting your lower back excessively arch from flaring your ribs upward and away from your hip bones.
    3. While you do not need to perform this phase with an extremely slow tempo, avoid letting the machine’s resistance abruptly swing your arms upward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once your arms are fully overhead following your last rep, push the pedal forward with your leg until you are no longer supporting any of the machine’s resistance with your upper body.
    2. Then let go of the handles while lifting your arms off the arm pads before bending your knee to bring the foot pedal back to its initial position.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres major, and rear delt, which are assisted by the lowest portion of your pec major in the overhead position of each rep.## Setting up
1. With a cable station positioned to its lowest height, select your weight with the loading pin, and connect a straight bar attachment.
2. Situate a flat exercise bench or chair a little in front of the cable station.
3. Squat down to pick up the bar attachment with an underhand grip where your palms are facing forward away from you.
4. While holding the bar, sit down on a flat exercise bench or chair and position your forearms on top of your thighs with your palms facing up and your wrists situated slightly in front of your knees.
    1. To achieve this position, you will lean forward at your hips when seated, with your elbows bent.
5. Allow the force from the cable to bend your wrists and bring your hands downward such that your palms are facing forward.
    1. In this position, the bar should not be contacting your legs, so scoot your forearms forward if it is initially contacting your legs.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by curling your wrists through their available range of motion while keeping your forearms resting on your thighs.
    1. All motion should come from your wrists.
    2. About halfway through the lifting phase, your palms should be facing straight up when your hands are aligned with your forearms.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, when the bar is fully raised, your palms should be angled back toward you.
    4. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel tension in the muscles on the palm side of your forearms.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your palms directed forward away from you.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that places the handles near shoulder level once you are seated.
    1. This seat height is an appropriate starting point for most people, but the ideal seat height specifically should allow you to press through your full range of motion while initially gripping the handles with your elbows stacked under the handles.
    2. If the seat is excessively high, you will not be able to position your elbows directly under the handles (when viewed from the side).
    3. If the seat is excessively low, your range of motion for this exercise will be limited.
    4. Some trial and error may be needed to find the best height for you as an individual.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the backrest.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the backrest if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
4. With your palms facing forward and elbows bent, firmly grasp the handles that are directed out to the sides. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being vertical when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably position your elbows under the handles, mildly lower the seat height before resuming your setup.
    3. Situating your elbows under the handles and maintaining this alignment will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise.
5. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling, and maintain this posture throughout your set.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles up overhead until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain stacked under your wrists when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your back snuggly against the backrest and your feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. With the appropriate seat height, you should finish the lowering phase with the handles near shoulder level, as your elbows are lower than your shoulders.
    2. At the bottom, you can allow the weights you selected to tap the rest of the weight stack. A light tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows stacked underneath the handles, resulting in your forearms remaining in a vertical position when viewed from the side.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing behind you, which positions you with a reverse (AKA overhand) grip.
    1. Maintain this reverse grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl both dumbbells upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed at your sides (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms positioned by your sides.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, but it may train your biceps somewhat less effectively than a curl with an underhand or neutral grip.## Setting up
1. With the empty bar, perform a couple of reps of the standard back squat or pause back squat through the full range of motion that your mobility permits with high-quality technique. Relative to the pinholes available in your rack, note the lowest point at which the bar descends during these reps.
2. Adjust the power rack’s safety pins to be mildly above the lowest point at which the bar descended during those reps.
    1. This height is generally an appropriate starting point, but you specifically want to set the pins near the lowest height that allows you to set up under the bar (once the bar is on pins) with high-quality positioning, which typically requires some trial and error to find.
    2. Setting up with high-quality positioning means that you can start under the bar with your back flat, heels fully on the ground, and each knee aligned with your second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or third toe of that side’s foot. If you round your back, lift your heels off the ground, or cave your knees inward once you are fully set up under the bar, you most likely set the pins lower than what is currently ideal for your mobility and body control.
    3. Particularly if you are not accustomed to this variation, it can be more difficult to set up with optimal positioning from the bottom of the squat compared to a standard back squat. Consequently, beginning with the bar set to the lowest point at which lifters normally can squat through a full range of motion may preclude them from setting up with optimal technique.
    4. For instance, people who have the mobility to squat no deeper than when their thighs are parallel to the ground often are not able to set up with the same quality of body positioning when setting the pins to a level that results in them starting with their thighs parallel to the ground. Setting the pins mildly above that position commonly allows them to set up with better technique.
3. Place the bar centered on the pins, and perform a couple of warm-up reps with the empty bar to confirm whether you selected the appropriate pin height.
    1. If you were unable to keep your back flat, heels down, and knees aligned with your toes, elevate the pins before repeating this step until you find the right pin height for you.
    2. If you think that you can begin from a lower pin height with quality technique, lower the pins before repeating this step.
4. Load the bar with your desired weight, and face the middle of the bar.
5. Grasp the bar with your standard back squat grip width, which commonly is a somewhat wider than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
6. Squat down and move under the bar until the middle of your feet and upper back are directly under the bar. Simultaneously, bend your elbows and pull them down by your sides.
    1. Aim to contact the bar specifically with the part of your upper back on which you normally rest the bar during your standard back squat, which varies from person to person.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck or contacting any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after starting to squat up with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
7. With the soles of your feet fully on the ground, position your feet in your standard back squat stance, which commonly is reasonably near shoulder width apart with the toes angled mildly outward.
    1. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which could involve a different stance width and a different degree of outward toe flare for you than other lifters prefer.
    2. Whatever stance feels best for your typical back squat is appropriate to use for this variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar as you tighten your back muscles with your chest up in a “proud chest” posture and your back flat.
    1. While they are presented distinctly for clarity, you can perform steps 1, 2, and 3 simultaneously with each other as they collectively serve to generate full-body tension before starting to squat up.
2. With your weight evenly distributed throughout your feet, “screw your feet into the ground” with the intent to push your knees out while developing tension through your hip muscles.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core throughout your rep.
4. Drive through the ground to stand upright with the bar by extending (straightening) your knees and hips while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Since you have no momentum to help you, the bar may remain motionless for a moment as you begin driving through the ground before it starts moving.
    2. Maintain the full body tightness you develop throughout the set.
    3. Continue keeping the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground throughout the set.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
5. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back, and progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your 2nd-3rd toes.
6. After you have squatted deep enough for the bar to contact the pins in a controlled manner, allow the weight from the bar to transmit into the pins fully as you completely come to a dead stop with the bar resting on the pins.
    1. While you do not have to finish the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you certainly should avoid crashing the bar into the pins in a jarring manner.
7. With the bar no longer transmitting any load into your back, relax in the bottom position for at least a couple of seconds to ensure you are able to train the subsequent rep in the same manner as the first.
    1. This is distinct from the standard pin squat, where you pause with the bar on the pins for a brief period while staying tight in between reps.
    2. Part of the objective of this variation is to train your ability to develop full-body tightness in a challenging position and lift each rep from a total dead stop.
    3. This is the ideal time to breathe fully during each rep, and the end of step 3 is also an appropriate time to breathe without losing your brace.
8. Alternate between steps 1-7 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the kettlebells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the kettlebells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    4. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. Continue keeping your elbows straight and arms directed straight down, which you will do throughout the entire duration of your set.
    7. If you are new to forward lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
2. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step backward with your front leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging forward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebells down.
    1. After finishing the last rep, set the kettlebells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate, aerobic step, or plyometric box) that you will use as a step onto the floor or approach the bottom step of a staircase.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your step, and the difficulty of the exercise will increase as the height increases.
    2. If you are new to forward step-downs, selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point, and you can subsequently adjust the height based on how challenging this height is for you.
    3. For reference, a step of a typical staircase is normally near seven inches (18cm) in height.
    4. If you have balance limitations, you can situate your step near a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
    5. When feasible, setting up where you can face a mirror is recommended to be able to receive visual feedback about your technique.
2. Step up onto the step with your feet hip-width apart, toes pointing straight ahead, and heels right by the edge.
    1. Your soles should be fully on the step.
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep your hands in your preferred position, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, crossed in front of your chest, or outstretched in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot, then lift your other foot up and back so it hangs over the edge of the step.
    1. Naturally, the side of your body to which you shift your weight will be the working side during this set.
2. On the side hovering off the step, pull your toes and the ball of your foot up toward your shin through an ankle motion that brings your heel closer to the ground than the ball of your foot.
    1. This is the opposite of the ankle motion that occurs when lifting your heels up during a calf raise, and you should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract.
    2. Throughout your set, maintain this foot position and the knee of your non-working side straight.
    3. You are now in the starting position for your first rep.
3. Sit your buttocks back to initiate the descent. Then progressively bend your working leg’s knee and hip in a controlled manner until you can gently tap the ground with the heel of your other foot.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hip to maintain your balance, but keep your back flat and chest up. When starting to descend, you can think about beginning to sit on a chair, which cues the intended hip motion that loads your glute.
    2. Keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down. Imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    3. While letting your working leg’s knee naturally track forward, strive to keep it aligned with your middle toes, rather than letting it inward relative to your foot (which you can check in a mirror, along with how level your hip bones are). Think about driving your knees out to the side if it starts drifting inward.
    4. Keep your elevated heel firmly planted on the step, along with the rest of your sole.
    5. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your free leg’s heel, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram). Lowering yourself slowly can help achieve this very light tap at the bottom.
4. Reverse the movement by driving through your elevated foot to step up into an upright position.
    1. By extending (straightening) your knee and hip, lift your body up completely through your working leg, as you avoid pushing off the ground with your other side’s foot.
    2. Continue keeping your hip bones level and your working leg’s knee aligned with your middle toes.
5. Alternate steps 3 and 4 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side on the step.
    1. Based on your preference, you can keep your non-working side’s foot hovering in the air between reps, or briefly place it on the step before lifting it off for the next rep. The latter approach can be useful if you feel that you need to reset your balance between reps, but either option is valid.
## Description
While training your quads, glutes, and adductor magnus, this exercise can improve your single leg stability.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance, and grasp the bar with your palms facing down as your hands are slightly outside shoulder width.
    1. For the full clean grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your 2nd-5th fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
3. While simultaneously bending your elbows, shallowly bend at your knees and hips with an upright torso to walk under the bar and situate it on the front of your shoulders just in front of the base of your neck.
    1. The bar should be resting on your front delts (which are the muscles on the front of your shoulders that attach to the outer aspects of your collarbones) and the outer portions of your collarbones, which together form a shelf with the base of your neck to cradle the bar.
    2. While the bar should be directly in front of your neck, it should not be pressing into your neck to the extent that it feels like it is choking you.
    3. If you are not accustomed to front squats, it is normal to experience discomfort from the pressure the bar places on the front of your shoulders (which may cause some skin bruising), but it should not be painful with proper bar placement.
4. With the bar now on the front of your shoulders, lift your elbows up until your upper arms are parallel to the ground while keeping your fingers wrapped around the bar.
    1. You can perform steps 3 and 4 simultaneously with each other.
    2. The bony prominence on the back of each elbow should now be pointing straight forward.
    3. Your hands can assist in keeping the bar balanced, but the bar should first and foremost be resting on the front of your shoulders without necessarily needing your hands to hold it there.
    4. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your wrists and/or forearm muscles in this position. However, if you find this position to be painful or lack the mobility to use a full clean grip, you can select a different front squat variation from this database that requires less mobility (such as the cross-arm grip front squat or fingertip clean grip front squat).
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of front squat reps with the form described in the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins before reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when front squatting.
7. Repeat steps 2-4 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arms parallel to the ground, stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the bar resting on your shoulders throughout the set.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest and elbows up.
    1. Throughout the set, avoid dropping your elbows or letting your chest cave down.
    2. As you descend, it is natural for your torso to lean forward mildly at your hips to maintain your balance, but maintain a mostly upright torso position with your back flat.
    3. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Position your shoulders snugly underneath the shoulder pads as your back is fully in contact with the back pad.
    1. The back of your hip bones should also be contacting the back pad.
    2. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
2. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot plate, near shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to hack press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when hack squatting through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the foot plate works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select should allow your heels (along with the rest of your feet) to be firmly on the foot plate throughout the whole set. Your foot placement should also allow you to keep the back of your hips snugly against the back pad at the bottom of each rep. The proper position should additionally feel comfortable for your knees.
    5. Placing your feet higher up may make it easier to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate, more difficult to keep your back flat against the back pad, and potentially bias the gluteus maximus to a greater degree. Placing your feet lower down may make it more difficult to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate, easier to keep your back flat against the back pad, and potentially bias the quads to a degree. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward and/or limits your range of motion.
    6. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to allow you to unlock the sled and begin your set. If you have very short legs, you potentially may have to situate your feet lower on the foot plate to start with slightly bent knees.
3. Without having any weight plates added to the machine, perform a few reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section before finishing your setup.
    1. These initial reps will help you determine what your full range of motion entails and inform you if any adjustments (such as to your foot placement or machine settings) are warranted.
    2. If you make any adjustments to your foot placement or the machine, it is recommended to try out a couple of reps without additional weights to make sure the adjustments were appropriate before adding weights.
4. If your machine allows you to adjust it, angle the foot plate to a setting that comfortably allows you to train through a full range of motion with your feet fully on the foot plate.
    1. Angling the foot plate closer toward a horizontal position can reduce the amount of ankle mobility required to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate at the bottom of the rep. If you noticed that your ability to keep your heels fully on the foot plate limited how deeply you were able to hack squat, it may be appropriate to try angling the foot plate closer toward a horizontal position. This may allow you to hack squat with a deeper knee bend compared to if the foot plate were angled more vertically.
    2. However, your feet may feel unstable and risk sliding if the foot plate is angled excessively close to being horizontal, so you want to find an appropriate balance.
5. If your machine has them, position the safety stoppers slightly under the lowest point at which the sled reaches at the bottom of your hack squat rep.
    1. Depending on how deeply you hack squat and the machine you use, the lowest safety stoppers available potentially may not be sufficiently low to allow you to descend through your full range of motion.
6. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and repeat steps 1 and 2 to assume the starting position with the foot placement you determined is appropriate for you.
    1. Note that the machine will provide resistance even without any additional weight plates. The weight of the sled will vary depending on the specific model, but it commonly weighs reasonably close to 100lb (45kg).
    2. Avoid adding so much weight that you no longer are able to train through your full range of motion.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handles with your hands to move the safety stoppers out of the sled’s way.
    1. The specific location of the safety handles and how to unlock them can vary among different machine models. However, the safety handles are often near where your hands will be when resting by your sides, and rotating the handles outward will commonly disengage the safety stoppers.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handles) and continue holding them throughout your set.
    1. The fixed handles are commonly located near the shoulder pads.
5. Squat down in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the foot plate.
    2. Continue keeping your back and the back of your hip bones snugly in contact with the back pad.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue squatting down through the full pain-free range of motion you can while keeping your heels fully on the foot plate, along with the back of your hips and back flat against the back pad.
    1. People can commonly descend until their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees, and some lifters can reach the point at which their thighs touch their calves with their knees fully bent. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. With certain machine models, you alternatively may reach the end range to which the machine permits before reaching the deepest position that your mobility enables.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track over your toes, hack squatting any deeper would cause your heels to start lifting off the foot plate.
    4. If you reached the end range to which your hips can bend, lowering the sled any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your hip bones starting to curl forward off the back pad.
    5. Hack squatting into a deep position is not inherently problematic to the knees, but there is no need to force yourself into the lowest position possible if you experience knee discomfort at your end range.
7. Reverse the movement by driving your feet into the foot plate to squat up as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when hack squatting, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, knees aligned over your feet, and your back and the back of your hip bones fully against the back pad.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set.
9. After your knees are straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, lock the safety handles with your hands to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the sled.
10. Lower the sled onto the safety stoppers by bending your knees.
## Description
While also training your gluteus maximus and multiple hip adductor muscles, this exercise primarily targets your quads.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to near mid-chest height.
    1. The optimal starting height should let you position the bar on your upper back and unrack it without lifting your heels off the ground.
2. Position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are slightly below the lowest height at which the bar descends at the bottom of your squat.
    1. To find this point for your body, you can perform a couple of squat reps with the unloaded bar as described in the “exercise technique” section, but you can initially put the safety stops somewhat below the top of your hip bones when standing upright as an approximation.
3. Add your desired weight to the bar, and face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, position yourself to face the side where the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (so the bottom of the rails is farther in front of you than the top).
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
4. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
5. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees to then walk under the bar and situate the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
6. Position the back of your heels slightly in front of the bar with your feet near shoulder width apart from each other as your toes are pointing somewhat out to the sides (rather than pointing straight ahead).
    1. In contrast to when you barbell back squat, placing your feet a bit in front of the bar when Smith machine back squatting can assist with comfortably achieving a deep squat.
    2. Your feet should not be so far in front of the bar that you feel that they may slide out in front of you when squatting.
    3. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    4. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    5. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to tighten your upper back muscles, and unrack it by starting to stand up as you straighten your knees.
2. After the bar rests fully on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars with a slight wrist flick before standing fully upright with the bar.
3. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright. Keep your neck in this position throughout the exercise.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward while leaving your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to help you maintain a rigid back position.
5. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. As you descend, it is natural for your torso to lean forward mildly at your hips, but avoid letting your lower back round or your chest cave down from upper back rounding.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet, especially the deeper your squat.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
6. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion that your mobility enables without rounding your back, lifting your heels up off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, heels lift off the ground, or knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
7. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips until you have returned to a fully upright position.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees stacked over your feet as you squat up.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
9. After finishing the set, rotate the safety hooks above the safety bars and rest the hooks onto the safety bars by bending your knees.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Position your shoulders snugly underneath the shoulder pads as your back is fully in contact with the back pad.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can rest your head against the pad or keep it slightly in front of the pad. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    3. If your machine’s back pad extends low enough, you should also be resting the back of your hip bones against the back pad.
2. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot plate, near shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to squat effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the foot plate works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select should allow your heels (along with the rest of your feet) to be firmly on the foot plate throughout the whole set. Your foot placement should also allow you to keep your whole back snugly against the back pad at the bottom of each rep. The proper position should additionally feel comfortable for your knees.
    5. Placing your feet higher up may make it easier to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate, more difficult to keep your back flat against the back pad, and potentially bias the gluteus maximus to a greater degree. Placing your feet lower down may make it more difficult to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate, easier to keep your back flat against the back pad, and potentially bias the quads to a degree. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward and/or limits your range of motion.
    6. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to allow you to unlock the machine and begin your set. Some machines have multiple starting pegs on which the safety hook could rest, allowing variable starting heights. If your machine’s safety hook is currently latched on a peg that is too high for you to use, you can ask a taller person to reposition the machine to a lower peg. If you are unable to adjust the machine to a lower starting height, situating your feet lower on the foot plate may help you assume the starting position with slightly bent knees.
3. Without having any weight plates added to the machine, perform a few reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section before finishing your setup.
    1. These initial reps will help you determine what your full range of motion entails and inform you if any adjustments (such as to your foot placement or machine settings) are warranted.
    2. If you make any adjustments to your foot placement or the machine, it is recommended to try out a couple of reps without additional weights to make sure the adjustments were appropriate before adding weights.
4. If your machine allows you to adjust it, angle the foot plate to a setting that comfortably allows you to train through a full range of motion with your feet fully on the foot plate.
    1. Angling the foot plate closer toward a horizontal position can reduce the amount of ankle mobility required to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate at the bottom of the rep. If you noticed that your ability to keep your heels fully on the foot plate limited how deeply you were able to squat, it may be appropriate to try angling the foot plate closer toward a horizontal position. This may allow you to squat with a deeper knee bend compared to if the foot plate were angled more vertically.
    2. However, your feet may feel unstable and risk sliding if the foot plate is angled excessively close to being horizontal, so you want to find an appropriate balance.
    3. Most pendulum squat machines do not allow you to adjust the foot plate.
5. If your machine has it, position the safety stopper slightly under the lowest point that you reached at the bottom of your pendulum squat rep.
    1. Depending on how deeply you squat and the machine you use, the lowest safety stoppers available potentially may not be sufficiently low to allow you to descend through your full range of motion.
6. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and repeat steps 1 and 2 to assume the starting position with the foot placement you determined is appropriate for you.
    1. Note that the machine will provide resistance even without any additional weight plates, so it can provide sufficiently high resistance to some people without adding any weight plates.
    2. Avoid adding so much weight that you are no longer able to train through your full range of motion.
    3. In addition to the weight sleeve by the back pad, certain machines will have an additional weight sleeve on the opposite end that serves as a counterbalance. Adding weight to the counterbalance sleeve will reduce the difficulty of the exercise compared to if no weight is added to the machine. It can be appropriate to add weight to the counterbalance sleeve if it is too challenging to squat through a full range of motion for your desired number of reps when the machine is unloaded.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your feet into the foot plate to push yourself away from it by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the safety hook should no longer be resting on the machine’s starting peg.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handle/bar with your hands to rotate the safety hook out of the starting peg’s way.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the hand grips/handlebar and continue holding them/it throughout your set.
5. Squat down in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the foot plate.
    2. Continue keeping your back snugly in contact with the back pad.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue squatting down through the full range of motion you can comfortably perform while keeping your heels fully on the foot plate, along with your back flat against the back pad.
    1. People can commonly descend until their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees, and some lifters can reach the point at which their thighs touch their calves with their knees fully bent. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. If you reach the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track over your toes, squatting any deeper can cause your heels to start lifting off the foot plate.
    3. If you reach the end range to which your hips can bend, descending any further can cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your hip bones starting to curl forward.
    4. Squatting into a deep position is not inherently problematic for the knees, but there is no need to force yourself into the lowest position possible if you experience knee discomfort at your end range.
7. Reverse the movement by driving your feet into the foot plate to squat up as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when pendulum squatting, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, knees aligned over your feet, and your back fully against the back pad.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set.
9. After your knees are straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, use your hand(s) to rotate the safety hook fully over the starting peg.
10. Lower the safety hook onto the starting peg by bending your knees.
## Description
While also training your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus, this exercise can effectively develop your quads.## Setting up
1. Stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance in a location that has sufficient space in front of you for lunging forward repeatedly.
    1. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc.
    2. Keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
    3. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. If you are new to walking lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
2. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
3. Progressively extend (straighten) your knees to lift your body up until you can repeat step 1 to step forward with your rear leg past your stationary leg in the same manner that you initially performed with your other side.
    1. Strive to begin stepping forward as you are lunging up in a fluid manner, which will advance your body forward.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat step 2 with your other side now in the forward position, as you lunge down just like you previously did.
5. Continue repeating steps 1-4 as you progressively lunge forward with alternating legs until you have completed your set or have to turn around and continue lunging in the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Determine what you hold with your hands for balance assistance.
    1. So long as what you use for assistance is sturdy and will be within your reach to hold throughout the exercise, you have multiple options for what you can select.
    2. With two hands, you can hold objects like the vertical posts of a rack, a door frame, anchored suspension trainer straps, or gymnastic rings.
    3. If you feel that you only require one hand for assistance, you can also use those objects in addition to a variety of others, such as a bedpost, couch, dumbbell rack, incline exercise bench, part of an exercise machine, etc.
2. Stand upright in a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead as you are close to what you hold onto to provide assistance with your arms. Grasp that object with one or two hands.
    1. Ensure that there is sufficient room for you to lift one of your legs forward and that you will be able to continue holding the object at the bottom of your rep, along with the top of it.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight into the foot on the side of your body you will train. Simultaneously pick your other foot up off the ground by lifting that thigh up until it is parallel to the ground with your knee as straight as your hamstring flexibility permits.
    1. Throughout the set, keep this leg close to parallel to the ground without letting its foot touch the ground.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the leg whose foot is on the ground.
2. Bend at your hip and knee to squat down in a controlled manner while assisting with your arms as needed.
    1. Throughout the rep, you can adjust how much assistance you use from your upper body, which will likely need to be greater near the bottom of the rep (where the exercise is more challenging) compared to near the top of the rep. Some people will simply use their hands to assist with balance, while others may need to utilize greater upper body involvement to reduce the demands on their lower body muscles.
    2. Keep the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your foot firmly in contact with the ground throughout your set.
    3. Strive to have your knee track straight forward in line with your second and third toes (when counting your big toe as your first toe) as you descend, while not letting your knee cave inward.
    4. To the best of your ability, keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Aim to keep your torso mostly upright within the constraints of your mobility and balance. The vast majority of people can only move through a full range of motion during this exercise if they let their backs round during the descent.
    6. Through motion at your working leg’s hip, your torso should naturally transition to an inclined position rather than remaining completely upright, but strive to keep your back near a neutral position (where it retains its natural curvature or looks flat) as you avoid excessively rounding your lower back.
3. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion that your mobility comfortably enables without letting your heel lift up off the ground or your knee cave inward.
    1. The greatest possible depth involves descending until your knee is fully bent with your hamstrings contacting your calves. If you have ankle mobility limitations, you will not be able to reach this point, but training this exercise over time can help improve your depth.
    2. If you start experiencing knee discomfort in the deepest position, you can abbreviate the range of motion to what feels right to your knee. This exercise is not inherently dangerous to the knees, but it can take some time to adapt to very deep pistol squats if you are unaccustomed to exercises that concentrate a high degree of loading into a fully bent knee position.
    3. While an extremely slow descent is unnecessary, controlling the entirety of the lowering phase is recommended rather than dropping down rapidly in an effort to bounce out of the bottom position.
4. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knee and hip to squat up until you have returned to standing upright.
    1. Like during the descent, continue keeping your foot fully in contact with the ground and your hip bones level as you avoid letting your knee cave inward.
    2. Greater assistance from your hands is commonly needed when squatting up compared to squatting down.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows, as your palms face toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. You can optionally place a soft object (like a cushioned balance pad, yoga block, folded thick towel, folded yoga mat, or a pillow) on the ground directly behind where you will stand if you are not confident in your ability to control the descent and tap your knee to the ground in a gentle manner.
    2. If you opt to place a soft object on the ground, you will use your knee to tap that object instead of the ground, and doing so will mildly reduce the range of motion and difficulty of this exercise compared to if you would lightly contact the ground with your knee.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and pick the other foot up off the ground by bending that side’s knee by greater than 90 degrees while keeping its thigh directed down toward the ground.
    1. The goal is to bend your knee enough that your foot will not contact the ground before your knee reaches the ground during the descent.
    2. Keep your free leg in this position throughout your set.
2. While simultaneously reaching the dumbbells mildly forward, progressively bend at your knee and hip with the leg in contact with the ground to squat down in a controlled manner until you can gently tap the ground with your free leg’s knee.
    1. It can be helpful to initiate the descent by mildly sitting your buttocks back through a hip hinge motion that mildly inclines your torso, as if you are closing a car door with your buttocks as your hands are carrying something.
    2. The forward movement of the dumbbells should come from your shoulders (as if you started to perform a front raise) as you keep your elbows fairly straight, and it should be a relatively small movement primarily to help you maintain your balance.
    3. Keep the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your working leg’s foot firmly in contact with the ground throughout your set.
    4. Strive to have your knee track straight forward in line with your second and third toes (when counting your big toe as your first toe) as you descend, while not letting your knee cave inward.
    5. To the best of your ability, keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down.
    6. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    7. Through motion at your working leg’s hip, your torso should naturally transition to a somewhat inclined position rather than remaining completely upright, but strive to keep your back near a neutral position (where it retains its natural curvature or looks flat) as you avoid excessively rounding your lower back.
    8. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your free leg’s knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
3. Reverse the motion you just performed by progressively extending (straightening) your working leg's knee and hip to squat yourself up as you simultaneously pull your arms back until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. Like during the descent, continue keeping your working leg’s foot fully in contact with the ground as you keep your hip bones level and avoid letting your knee cave inward.
    2. At the end of the ascent, your arms should once again be by your sides as they are directed down toward the ground.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise after switching which foot is in contact with the ground.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the dumbbells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    4. Positioning yourself in a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, take a fairly long step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Slightly bend the knee on the side of your stationary foot while doing so.
    2. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    3. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    6. Keep your arms by your sides, directed down toward the ground throughout the duration of your set.
2. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
3. While keeping both of your feet planted on the ground, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position.
    1. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the lower leg pad to be level with your upper calf, just below the back of your knees, when standing on the foot plate.
    1. The ideal pad height will allow your knees to move freely (which may not be the case if the pad is too high) during the exercise while appropriately supporting your lower legs (which may not be the case if the pad is too low).
2. Step onto the foot plate in an approximately hip-width stance with the tops of your feet under the machine’s foot roller and your upper calves snugly against the pad.
    1. The soles of your feet should be fully in contact with the foot plate as your toes are pointing straight ahead.
3. If your machine permits you to do so, adjust the foot roller so that it is snugly on top of your feet just in front of your ankles.
    1. Your feet and lower legs should now be securely locked into the machine in a position where they will not move during the exercise.
    2. While many do, certain machine models lack an adjustable foot roller.
4. Stand fully upright and place your arms in your preferred position, which may be crossed in front of your chest or held outstretched in front of you parallel to the ground.
    1. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your torso in a vertical position and your calves in contact with the leg pad, bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner until your thighs are approximately parallel to the ground or somewhat below this point.
    1. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the foot plate.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot.
    3. Strive to keep your shoulders vertically stacked over your hips, as you resist any forward torso lean. 
    4. When your thighs are parallel to the ground, your knees and hips should be bent by approximately 90 degrees (as demonstrated by the angle between your thighs and trunk for your hips and the angle between your shins and thighs for your knees).
    5. The intensity of this exercise will increase as your squat depth increases, so you can adjust your depth based on your ability if you currently cannot descend until your thigh is parallel to the ground for the number of reps you intend to perform during this set.
2. Reverse the movement by pressing through the foot plate to squat up as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips until you have returned to the starting position with your torso fully lifted up and your knees straightened.
    1. Like during the descent, continue keeping your torso in a vertical position, calves in contact with the leg pad, feet fully in contact with the ground, and knees in line with your feet.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads with relatively low loading of your gluteus maximus and hip adductors, in contrast to most squat variations.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    4. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. Continue keeping your elbows straight and arms directed straight down, which you will do throughout the entire duration of your set.
    7. If you are new to forward lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
2. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step backward with your front leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging forward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down.
    1. After finishing the last rep, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate, aerobic step, or plyometric box) that you will use as a step onto the floor.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your step, and the difficulty of the exercise will increase as the height increases.
    2. If you are new to weighted lateral step-downs, selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point, and you can subsequently adjust the height based on how challenging this height is for you.
    3. Alternatively, you can perform this exercise on the lowest step of a staircase, which is normally near seven inches (18cm) in height.
    4. When feasible, setting up where you can face a mirror is recommended to be able to receive visual feedback about your technique.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
3. Stand directly to the side of the step, and place one foot fully onto the step while leaving your other foot on the ground. Situate your feet about hip-width apart, and point your toes straight ahead.
    1. The entire sole (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) of your elevated foot should be firmly planted on the step throughout your set.
    2. Continue holding the dumbbells by your sides throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive through your elevated leg and straighten your knee to step up until your feet are level, keeping your non-working leg’s foot hanging off the step.
    1. Finish fully upright with the tops of your hip bones and shoulders level, while you bear all your bodyweight through the foot on the step.
2. On the side hovering off the step, pull your toes and the ball of your foot up toward your shin through an ankle motion that brings your heel closer to the ground than the ball of your foot.
    1. This is the opposite of the ankle motion that occurs when lifting your heels up during a calf raise, and you should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract.
    2. Throughout your set, maintain this foot position and the knee of your non-working side straight.
    3. You are now in the starting position for your first rep.
3. Sit your buttocks back to initiate the descent. Then progressively bend your working leg’s knee and hip in a controlled manner until you can gently tap the ground with the heel of your other foot.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hip to maintain your balance, but keep your back flat and chest up. When starting to descend, you can think about beginning to sit on a chair, which cues the intended hip motion that loads your glute.
    2. Keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down. Imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    3. While letting your working leg’s knee naturally track forward, strive to keep it aligned with your middle toes, rather than letting it inward relative to your foot (which you can check in a mirror, along with how level your hip bones are). Think about driving your knees out to the side if it starts drifting inward.
    4. Keep your elevated heel firmly planted on the step, along with the rest of your sole.
    5. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your free leg’s heel, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram). Lowering yourself slowly can help achieve this very light tap at the bottom.
4. Reverse the movement by driving through your elevated foot to step up into a fully upright position.
    1. By extending (straightening) your knee and hip, lift your body up completely through your working leg, as you avoid pushing off the ground with your other side’s foot.
    2. Continue keeping your hip bones level and your working leg’s knee aligned with your middle toes.
    3. Finish your free foot hovering in the air, not in contact with the step.
5. Alternate steps 3 and 4 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side on the step.
    1. Since each rep starts with the lowering phase, completion of step 2 does not constitute a full rep.
## Description
While training your quads, glutes, and adductor magnus, this exercise can improve your single leg stability.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate or an aerobic step) that you will use as a step onto the floor in a location that has sufficient space behind it for your split squat.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your stepping surface, but selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the dumbbells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
3. In approximately a hip-width stance, stand with the foot that you will first step up onto the step aligned with the middle of your step (which is primarily relevant if you are using a bumper plate to provide the greatest surface area for your foot).
## Exercise technique
1. With the foot aligned with the middle of your step, step up onto the step with the entirety of its sole in contact with the step while leaving your other foot on the ground.
    1. The knee on the side of your elevated foot should be mildly bent once on the step.
    2. Keep your arms by your sides, directed down toward the ground throughout the duration of your set.
2. Shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot before taking a fairly long step backward with your other foot and contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. To shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you bend that side’s knee slightly more.
    2. Once you step back and resume transferring some of your weight into your rear leg, you can return to keeping your torso in an upright position.
    3. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    4. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    5. Keep the entire sole of your elevated foot firmly planted on the step and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    6. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
3. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the step.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
4. While keeping both of your feet planted, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position on top of the step.
    1. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance in a location that has sufficient space in front of you for lunging forward.
    1. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc.
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
    3. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. If you are new to forward lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
2. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step backward with your front leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging forward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to a barbell situated in a landmine attachment (or a viable substitute).
    1. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    2. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
2. Stand upright with the free end of the bar between the middle of your feet. Position your feet near shoulder width apart with your toes pointing mildly out to the sides.
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. In the starting position, your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, and you will keep your neck in this position throughout your set.
3. Simultaneously bend your hips and knees to squat down until you are low enough to grasp the bar.
    1. Keep your back flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your back.
4. Firmly grasp the end of the bar with your fingers cupped around it, as one hand is directly in front of the other.
    1. The little finger of one hand should be touching the index finger of your other hand.
    2. Your palms should be facing in toward the bar.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
6. Explosively lift the bar up to chest height while rapidly repositioning your hands under the bar with your elbows bent. Allow the end of the bar to rest on the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with your fingers interlocked and your thumbs against the tip of the bar.
    1. The tip of the bar should now be directly in front of your chest.
    2. Your elbows should be tucked slightly in front of your sides.
    3. Your knees and hips should now be straight, like when you are normally standing upright, but allow your body to lean mildly forward at your ankles.
    4. With the appropriate positioning, you should feel stable holding the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner.
    1. Avoid rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. Keep the tip of the bar directly in front of your chest throughout your set.
2. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion that your mobility enables without rounding your back, lifting your heels up off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, heels lift off the ground, or knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground. Extend (straighten) your knees and hips until you have returned to the upright starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, knees stacked over your feet, and the weight held close to your chest as you squat up.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
5. Once you have completed the lifting phase of your last rep, quickly reposition your hands under the bar sequentially while guiding the bar down until you are holding it in front of you with straight elbows.
    1. You are returning your hands to the cupping grip you used when initially setting up.
6. Squat down to set the weight onto the ground.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensor muscles, this exercise can develop your gluteus maximus, quads, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright while firmly holding the kettlebells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the kettlebells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Adopt an approximately hip-width stance in a location that has sufficient space in front of you for lunging forward repeatedly.
    1. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. Continue keeping your elbows straight and arms directed straight down by your sides, which you will do throughout the entire duration of your set.
    7. If you are new to walking lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
2. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
3. Progressively extend (straighten) your knees to lift your body up until you can repeat step 1 to step forward with your rear leg past your stationary leg in the same manner that you initially performed with your other side.
    1. Strive to begin stepping forward as you are lunging up in a fluid manner, which will advance your body forward.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat step 2 with your other side now in the forward position, as you lunge down just like you previously did.
5. Continue repeating steps 1-4 as you progressively lunge forward with alternating legs until you have completed your set or have to turn around and continue lunging in the opposite direction.
    1. After finishing the last rep, set the kettlebells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a kettlebell or dumbbell and hold it right next to your chest with bent elbows, as your upper arms are slightly in front of your sides (i.e., in the goblet position).
    1. You can use either a kettlebell or a dumbbell, but some people find holding a kettlebell in the goblet position to feel more stable and comfortable, especially with a fairly heavy weight.
    2. Based on your preference and the load you use, you can transfer your kettlebell or dumbbell into the goblet position through a couple of different approaches.
    3. An effective approach is initially to pick up the weight by the handle through a deadlift motion and set it on an elevated surface (like a bench or plyometric box) with the handle facing up if you use a kettlebell or the handle directed vertically if using a dumbbell (as only one end of the dumbbell is on the surface). Then squat down until the weight is right in front of your chest, firmly grasp it in the goblet position, and squat up with the weight. If you use a kettlebell, grasp it by its horns (i.e., side handles). If you use a dumbbell, cup your hands around the top end of the weight with the base of your palms by the handle, and your fingers wrapped around the rest of that end of the weight.
    4. With a lighter kettlebell, you can tilt it onto its side with the weight base directed toward you, squat down to grasp its horns, stand upright with it, and curl it into the goblet position.
    5. With a lighter dumbbell, you can also pick it up with one hand and curl it up across your body to be in front of your chest. Then cup your free hand around the top end of the dumbbell with the base of its palm by the handle and fingers wrapped around the top end of the weight before repositioning your other hand to cup the other side of the weight in the same manner.
2. While holding the weight to your chest and looking straight ahead, stand upright with your feet near shoulder-width apart from each other as your toes are pointing somewhat out to the sides (rather than pointing straight ahead).
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. In the starting position, your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, and you will keep your neck in this position throughout your set.
    5. Continue holding the weight close to your chest in the goblet position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward, leaving your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Strive to create tension throughout the entirety of your core to form a “360-degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you maintain a rigid trunk position.
2. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your knees aligned with your feet.
    1. As you descend, it is natural for your torso to lean forward mildly at your hips to maintain balance, but aim to keep your torso mostly upright, with your back flat and chest up.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot, as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
3. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
4. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips until you have returned to a fully upright position.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, knees stacked over your feet, and the weight held close to your chest as you squat up.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat to a distance away from the foot platform that allows you to leg press through a full range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    1. Finding the ideal setting for you as an individual typically takes some trial and error. The only way to know if the seat setting is appropriate is to sit down on the seat and situate your feet on the platform in the same position you will use for your set.
    2. The appropriate setting will allow you to assume the starting position as your lower back maintains close to its natural curvature (like when you are normally standing upright). It should also allow you to keep your feet fully on the platform.
    3. If the seat is excessively close to the foot platform relative to your hip mobility, your lower back may have to round to place your feet on the platform while seated. Alternatively, you may not be able to place your heels firmly in contact with the platform if it is too close.
    4. You should ideally place the seat as close to the foot platform as you can without having to round your lower back or lift your heels off the platform. Placing the seat meaningfully farther back beyond this point may decrease how much muscle growth the exercise is likely to stimulate.
    5. As a general starting point, you can find the seat setting that allows your knees to be bent by about 90 degrees when your feet are on the platform. If you are able to avoid rounding your lower back when set up with this setting, you can incrementally bring the seat closer until you find your optimal starting position. Alternatively, if you have to round your lower back to set up with your knees bent by about 90 degrees, you can incrementally bring the seat back until you can assume the starting position without rounding your lower back.
2. Select your weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on the seat with your back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot platform, near shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to leg press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the platform works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select, your heels should be firmly on the platform along with the rest of your feet. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward or limits your range of motion.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
3. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner. 
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. Certain machine models will involve you pressing yourself away from a stationary foot platform rather than pressing the platform away from you. The technique remains the same regardless.
4. Reverse the movement by bending at your knees and hips in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, back against the back rest, and knees aligned over your feet.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time. A sign of an appropriately controlled movement is having the weights you selected lightly contact the rest of the weight stack at the end range. Having the weights lightly touch down will also confirm that you have moved through your full range of motion.
    3. With the appropriate seat position, you should be able to avoid rounding your lower back at the end range of this motion. If you notice your lower back starts rounding at the end, you could finish your rep and mildly move the seat backward before resuming your set.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your quads, gluteus maximus, and multiple hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of squat reps through the full range of motion your mobility allows, without rounding your back or lifting your heels off the ground
    1. Use the technique for a standard barbell back squat to help you determine the appropriate height of the safety pins for your pin squats.
3. Rerack the bar onto the J-hooks before loading the bar with your intended weight.
4. Position the safety pins slightly above the lowest point at which the bar descends during your typical back squat.
    1. If you wish to train through a shorter range of motion to address a specific weak point, you can alternatively set the pins a little higher.
    2. Your safety pins should be positioned to a height that allows you to set the bar on them at the end of each rep’s descent without having to descend beyond the range of motion in which you can squat with quality technique (e.g., keeping your back flat and feet fully on the ground).
5. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
6. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should be securely resting on the muscles of your upper back when squeezing your shoulder blades together.
    2. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    5. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. After you have squatted deep enough for the bar to contact the pins in a controlled manner, allow the weight from the bar to transmit into the pins as you come to a dead stop with the bar resting on the pins for a brief period of time (e.g., about two seconds).
    1. While you do not have to finish the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you certainly should avoid crashing the bar into the pins in a jarring manner.
    2. Continue keeping your core braced and your back flat while you remain stationary.
6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    4. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position with your arms directed down toward the ground as you do so.
    4. Strive to keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    7. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step backward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step forward with your rear leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright, arms directed down toward the ground, and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging backward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down.
    1. After finishing the last rep, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance, and elevate your arms to be parallel to the ground out in front of you.
3. Shallowly bend at your knees and hips with an upright torso to walk under the bar and situate it on the front of your shoulders just in front of the base of your neck.
    1. The bar should be resting on your front delts (which are the muscles on the front of your shoulders that attach to the outer aspects of your collarbones) and the outer portions of your collarbones, which together form a shelf with the base of your neck to cradle the bar.
    2. While the bar should be directly in front of your neck, it should not be pressing into your neck to the extent that it feels like it is choking you.
    3. With the appropriate bar positioning, you should feel that the bar would be stable on the front of your shoulders if you were to unrack the bar and walk out while keeping your arms outstretched in front of you with straight elbows.
    4. If you are not accustomed to front squats, it is normal to experience discomfort from the pressure the bar places on the front of your shoulders (which may cause some skin bruising), but it should not be painful with proper bar placement.
4. With the bar now on the front of your shoulders, bend your elbows to cross your forearms into an “X-shape” as your hands are on top of the bar with your palms facing down and your upper arms kept parallel to the ground.
    1. Based on what feels more stable to you, you can position your hands very close together, such that your thumbs are touching each other, or you can have your hands slightly wider apart, such that you have fingers contacting your front delts.
    2. The bony prominence on the back of each elbow should now be pointing straight forward.
    3. Your hands can assist in keeping the bar balanced, but the bar should first and foremost be resting on the front of your shoulders without necessarily needing your hands to hold it there.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of front squat reps with the form described in the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins before reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when front squatting.
7. Repeat steps 2-4 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arms parallel to the ground, stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the bar resting on your shoulders throughout the set.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest and elbows up.
    1. Throughout the set, avoid dropping your elbows or letting your chest cave down.
    2. As you descend, it is natural for your torso to lean forward mildly at your hips to maintain your balance, but maintain a mostly upright torso position with your back flat.
    3. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a kettlebell or dumbbell and hold it right next to your chest with bent elbows, as your upper arms are slightly in front of your sides (i.e., in the goblet position).
    1. You can use either a kettlebell or a dumbbell, but some people find holding a kettlebell in the goblet position to feel more stable and comfortable, especially with a fairly heavy weight.
    2. Based on your preference and the load you use, you can transfer your kettlebell or dumbbell into the goblet position through a couple of different approaches.
    3. An effective approach is initially to pick up the weight by the handle through a deadlift motion and set it on an elevated surface (like a bench or plyometric box) with the handle facing up if you use a kettlebell or the handle directed vertically if using a dumbbell (as only one end of the dumbbell is on the surface). Then squat down until the weight is right in front of your chest, firmly grasp it in the goblet position, and squat up with the weight. If you use a kettlebell, grasp it by its horns (i.e., side handles). If you use a dumbbell, cup your hands around the top end of the weight with the base of your palms by the handle, and your fingers wrapped around the rest of that end of the weight.
    4. With a lighter kettlebell, you can tilt it onto its side with the weight base directed toward you, squat down to grasp its horns, stand upright with it, and curl it into the goblet position.
    5. With a lighter dumbbell, you can also pick it up with one hand and curl it up across your body to be in front of your chest. Then cup your free hand around the top end of the dumbbell with the base of its palm by the handle and fingers wrapped around the top end of the weight before repositioning your other hand to cup the other side of the weight in the same manner.
2. While keeping your chest up, position your feet approximately hip-width apart, with your toes pointing straight ahead.
    1. In this starting position, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you are keeping your chest up and looking straight ahead.
    2. You will continue holding the weight close to your chest in the goblet position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Take a long step out to the side as you keep the toes of your stepping foot pointing forward.
    1. How wide a stance may be ideal for you will be influenced by your hip mobility, and some trial and error may be needed to find which stance feels best to you.
    2. As a general guideline, your feet should be further apart than one and a half times your shoulder width, but not so wide that you feel unstable.
    3. While you step to the side, your torso will shift over to that side.
2. As your stepping foot contacts the ground, bend at that side’s knee and hip while sitting your buttock somewhat backward to squat down with your stepping leg in a controlled manner through your available range of motion while keeping your other knee straight.
    1. While doing so, drive your stepping side’s knee out to the side to keep it aligned over your foot as opposed to letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot.
    2. Keep the entirety of the soles of both feet in contact with the ground as you descend.
    3. As you sit your buttocks back (like if you started to sit on a chair), allow your torso naturally to lean somewhat forward at your hips to maintain your balance. Simultaneously keep your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    4. People can commonly descend until the stepping leg’s thigh is close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes your full range of motion will be affected by your mobility.
    5. Descend as far as you can while keeping your stepping leg’s knee aligned with your foot, soles fully on the ground, and back near a neutral position.
    6. Strive to perform steps 1 and 2 fluidly as a continuous movement.
3. Reverse the movement by squatting up with your stepping leg as you drive through the ground by progressively extending (straightening) your knee and hip until you can push through the ground to step back with your feet together and your torso upright.
    1. Continue keeping your stepping leg’s knee aligned with your foot and your back near a neutral position as you squat up.
    2. At the end of this movement, you should have returned to the starting position as you are standing upright with your feet approximately hip-width apart from each other.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 as you use your other leg to step out to the side while performing your lateral lunge.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the weight down.
