6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before slightly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Stand upright with your feet approximately width apart from each other as your toes are pointing straight forward, and lift your arms up so that they are parallel to the ground.
    1. In this starting position, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you are keeping your chest up and looking straight ahead.
    2. Keep your arms outstretched parallel to the ground throughout your set to provide a counterbalance that helps keep your torso upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Take a long step out to the side as you keep the toes of your stepping foot pointing forward.
    1. The ideal stance width will be influenced by hip mobility, and some trial and error may be needed to determine which stance feels best to you.
    2. As a general guideline, your feet should be further apart than one and a half times your shoulder width, but not so wide that you feel unstable.
    3. While you step to the side, your torso will shift over to that side.
2. As your stepping foot contacts the ground, bend at that side’s knee and hip while sitting your buttock somewhat backward to squat down with your stepping leg in a controlled manner through your available range of motion while keeping your other knee straight.
    1. While doing so, drive your stepping side’s knee out to the side to keep it aligned over your foot as opposed to letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot.
    2. Keep the entirety of the soles of both feet in contact with the ground as you descend.
    3. As you sit your buttocks back (like if you started to sit on a chair), allow your torso naturally to lean somewhat forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but keep your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    4. People can commonly descend until the stepping leg’s thigh is close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes your full range of motion will be affected by your mobility.
    5. Descend as far as you can while keeping your stepping leg’s knee aligned with your foot, soles fully on the ground, and back near a neutral position.
    6. Strive to perform steps 1 and 2 fluidly as a continuous movement.
3. Reverse the movement by squatting up with your stepping leg as you drive through the ground by progressively extending (straightening) your knee and hip until you can push through the ground to step back with your feet together and your torso upright.
    1. Continue keeping your stepping leg’s knee aligned with your foot and your back near a neutral position as you squat up.
    2. At the end of this movement, you should have returned to the starting position as you are standing upright with your feet approximately hip-width apart from each other.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 as you use your other leg to step out to the side while performing your lateral lunge.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a kettlebell or dumbbell and hold it right next to your chest with bent elbows, as your upper arms are slightly in front of your sides (i.e., in the goblet position).
    1. You can use either a kettlebell or a dumbbell, but some people find holding a kettlebell in the goblet position to feel more stable and comfortable, especially with a fairly heavy weight.
    2. Based on your preference and the load you use, you can transfer your kettlebell or dumbbell into the goblet position through a couple of different approaches.
    3. An effective approach is initially to pick up the weight by the handle through a deadlift motion and set it on an elevated surface (like a bench or plyometric box) with the handle facing up if you use a kettlebell or the handle directed vertically if using a dumbbell (as only one end of the dumbbell is on the surface). Then squat down until the weight is right in front of your chest, firmly grasp it in the goblet position, and squat up with the weight. If you use a kettlebell, grasp it by its horns (i.e., side handles). If you use a dumbbell, cup your hands around the top end of the weight with the base of your palms by the handle, and your fingers wrapped around the rest of that end of the weight.
    4. With a lighter kettlebell, you can tilt it onto its side with the weight base directed toward you, squat down to grasp its horns, stand upright with it, and curl it into the goblet position.
    5. With a lighter dumbbell, you can also pick it up with one hand and curl it up across your body to be in front of your chest. Then cup your free hand around the top end of the dumbbell with the base of its palm by the handle and fingers wrapped around the top end of the weight before repositioning your other hand to cup the other side of the weight in the same manner.
2. Once you are standing upright while holding the weight to your chest, position your feet into a rather wide stance by taking a large step out to the side with one foot while keeping your other foot on the ground.
    1. How wide a stance may be ideal for you will be influenced by your hip mobility, and some trial and error may be needed to find which stance feels best to you.
    2. As a general guideline, your feet should be further apart than one and a half times your shoulder width, but not so wide that you feel unstable.
3. Point your toes somewhat outward in a position that feels comfortable to you.
    1. In this starting position, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you are keeping your chest up and looking straight ahead.
    2. You will continue holding the weight close to your chest in the goblet position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot. On the side to which you shift your weight, start bending at your knee and hip to squat down toward that side as you push your knee out to that side while keeping your other knee straight.
    1. While doing so, your torso will move closer to the foot on which you shifted your weight as it simultaneously descends.
    2. In the starting position, your knees are inside your feet, and one of the objectives of the descent is to drive your bending knee out toward that side’s foot until your knee is stacked over the middle of your foot.
2. Continue to squat down in a controlled manner to that side through the full range of motion comfortably permitted by your hip mobility and muscle flexibility.
    1. Throughout your rep, keep the sole of the foot to which you shifted your weight fully in contact with the ground as you keep that side’s knee aligned with your foot (as opposed to letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot).
    2. Based on your preference and what feels most comfortable, you can keep the sole of your other foot fully on the ground, or you can keep its heel on the ground while allowing the rest of the foot to lift up off the ground.
    3. As you descend, allow your torso naturally to lean somewhat forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but strive to keep your lower back flat and chest up
    4. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your thigh muscles the deeper you squat.
    5. If you wish, you can pause at the bottom to extend the time you spend stretching your muscles.
3. Reverse the movement by squatting up as you progressively extend (straighten) your knee and hip to drive your torso upward and back toward the center of your stance.
    1. Finish in the same position in which you started, where both knees are straight, and your torso is upright, symmetrically situated between your feet.
    2. If you opted to lift part of your foot (on the side with the straight knee) off the ground during the descent, you will return the entirety of its sole onto the ground during the ascent.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 as you perform your Cossack squat toward your opposite foot.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to bring your feet back together to a near hip-width stance.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus while also being able to improve your hip mobility due to the loaded stretch it provides.## Setting up
1. Adjust the J-hooks of a rack to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on the J-hooks.
    1. The setup for this exercise is essentially the same as for a back squat.
    2. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    3. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
2. Face the center of the bar with approximately hip-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
3. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back muscles, slightly below the base of your neck, in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Aim for the bar to rest on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts on the back of your shoulders and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine). This is the high bar position, which is generally recommended over the low bar position for split squats and lunges.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your traps will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    5. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    6. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase.
    1. While not required, setting up safety pins is recommended in case you lose your balance or fail a rep during your work sets.
6. Repeat steps 2 and 3 of the setup and proceed to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. While keeping your chest up, take a couple of steps backward and position your feet approximately hip-width apart, with your toes pointing straight ahead.
    1. Make sure that you walk far enough backward that the barbell will not contact the rack when lunging forward.
3. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. If you are new to forward lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
4. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
5. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step backward with your front leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
6. Repeat steps 3-5 while lunging forward with your opposite side.
7. Alternate between steps 3-6 until you have completed your set.
8. Walk forward until the bar contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Adopt an approximately hip-width stance in a location that has sufficient space in front of you for lunging forward repeatedly.
    1. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. Continue keeping your elbows straight and arms directed straight down by your sides, which you will do throughout the entire duration of your set.
    7. If you are new to walking lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
2. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
3. Progressively extend (straighten) your knees to lift your body up until you can repeat step 1 to step forward with your rear leg past your stationary leg in the same manner that you initially performed with your other side.
    1. Strive to begin stepping forward as you are lunging up in a fluid manner, which will advance your body forward.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat step 2 with your other side now in the forward position, as you lunge down just like you previously did.
5. Continue repeating steps 1-4 as you progressively lunge forward with alternating legs until you have completed your set or have to turn around and continue lunging in the opposite direction.
    1. After finishing the last rep, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to position the bar on your upper back and unrack it in a stable position.
    1. As an initial approximation, you can position the bar such that it is a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
2. Position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are slightly below the height of the bar when your rear knee taps the ground at the end of the lowering phase of your split squat.
    1. To find this point for your body, you can perform a rep or two of the split squat with the unloaded bar as described in the “exercise technique” section, but you can initially put the safety stops a little below the top of your hip bones when standing upright as an approximation.
3. Add your desired weight to the bar and face the center of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails).
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
4. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
5. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees to then walk under the bar and situate it on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar up excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
    7. If you need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground in order to position the bar on your upper back, you can remove the weights from the bar, lower the height of the bar, and add the weight plates back before repositioning the bar on your upper back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to tighten your upper back muscles, and unrack it by starting to stand up as you straighten your knees.
2. After the bar rests fully on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars with a slight wrist flick before standing fully upright with the bar.
3. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
4. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    7. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step backward during your next rep.
5. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step forward with your rear leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
6. Repeat steps 3-5 while lunging backward with your opposite side.
7. Alternate between steps 3-6 until you have completed your set and are ready to re-rack the bar.
    1. Re-rack the bar by bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train three of your four quad muscles, buttock’s gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus on the inside of your thigh.## Setting up
1. Stand upright with your back, pelvis (hip bone), and heels against a wall.
    1. Continue keeping your back and pelvis against the wall throughout the rest of your setup and while you perform your wall squat.
2. Step forward with one foot until its heel is just in front of your other foot’s big toe before doing the same with your opposite foot.
3. Step forward with your rear foot to position it symmetrically with your front foot, resulting in your feet now being about two “foot lengths” in front of the wall.
    1. While this strategy can consistently situate most people in a viable starting position for the exercise, somewhat of a range of foot placements can be effectively used for this exercise.
4. Situate your feet near shoulder width apart from each other and position your toes so that they are either facing straight ahead or rotated somewhat out based on what feels comfortable to you.
    1. So long as you do not place them on your legs, you can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides or crossed on your shoulders.
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
    3. After finishing adjusting your foot position, your knees should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend at your knees and hips to squat down as you slide your back down the wall in a controlled manner until your thighs are approximately parallel to the ground or somewhat below that point.
    1. Avoid descending beyond the point where you can still keep your pelvis in contact with the wall.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot.
    3. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground as you continue keeping your upper back and pelvis in contact with the wall.
    4. If you wish to prioritize training your quads, focus on transmitting pressure to the wall through your pelvis while minimizing how much pressure you transmit into the wall through your upper back.
2. Reverse the movement by pressing through the ground to squat up as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips until you have returned to the starting position with your torso fully lifted up and your knees straightened.
    1. Like during the descent, continue keeping your pelvis and upper back in contact with the wall, feet fully in contact with the ground, and your knees in line with your feet.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads with relatively low loading of your gluteus maximus and hip adductors, in contrast to most squat variations.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
3. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on the back of your shoulder muscles and shoulder blades in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. For the low bar squat, you are aiming for the bar to rest on your rear delts on the back of your shoulders, just below the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine).
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your rear delts will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel the bar may slide down your back after standing upright, you most likely have it placed too low.
    4. If you notice discomfort from the bar pressing on a bony ridge on your shoulder blades, shifting the bar slightly lower may be more comfortable.
    5. The bar should be clearly lower than the base of your neck, so you should not feel pressure on your neck from the weight pressing on any bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    6. When positioning the bar, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    7. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when squatting.
6. Repeat steps 2 and 3 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to position the bar on your upper back and un-rack it in a stable position.
    1. As an initial approximation, you can position the bar such that it is a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
2. Position a sturdy object near knee height (like a flat exercise bench, split squat roller stand, aerobic step with risers, or plyometric box) behind the Smith machine’s bar at a distance that is about twice the length of your feet.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you will face the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails) during this exercise, so you will place the object behind where you will face.
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face, so it does not matter which side you place the elevated object.
    3. To determine an appropriate distance, you can stand with the middle of your feet directly under the bar, step backward with one foot until its big toe is just behind the heel of your other foot, and do the same with your other foot.
    4. Where the heel of your rear foot reaches after taking these two steps backward is where you can place your elevated object just behind.
    5. While this strategy can consistently situate most people in a viable starting position for the exercise, a range of foot placements can be effectively used, including having the object foot two and a half “foot lengths” behind the bar (which may be even more comfortable for some people).
    6. If it is available, a split squat roller stand will normally be the most comfortable object you can use to elevate your foot during this exercise.
    7. If you are using an angled Smith machine, a bench may potentially be too wide to fit behind you within the machine.
3. Position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are slightly below the height of the bar when your rear knee taps the ground at the end of the lowering phase of your split squat.
    1. To find this point for your body, you can perform a rep or two of the split squat with the unloaded bar as described in the “exercise technique” section, but you can initially put the safety stops a little below the top of your hip bones when standing upright as an approximation.
4. Add your desired weight to the bar and face the center of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
5. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat wider than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
6. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees to then walk under the bar and situate the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
    7. If you need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground in order to position the bar on your upper back, you can remove the weights from the bar, lower the height of the bar, and add the weight plates back before repositioning the bar on your upper back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to tighten your upper back muscles, and unrack it by starting to stand up as you straighten your knees.
2. After the bar rests fully on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars with a slight wrist flick before standing fully upright with the bar.
3. Shift your bodyweight into your front foot before placing the top surface (on the side of your shoe laces) of your rear foot on top of the elevated surface with that side’s knee bent.
    1. To help shift your bodyweight into your front foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you slightly bend that side’s knee.
    2. Once you have situated your rear foot into the elevated position, you can return to keeping your torso in a fairly upright position, which should involve you maintaining the natural curvature of your lower back, like when you are normally standing upright (rather than excessively arching beyond that position).
    3. Strive to move your rear foot backward in a straight line to keep your feet approximately hip width apart rather than bringing your rear foot closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep the entire sole of your front foot firmly planted on the ground and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    6. If you are using a split squat roller to elevate your foot, you can contact the surface with the front of your ankle along with your foot, but only your foot will contact the elevated surface if it is flat, like a bench.
4. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep most of your weight shifted onto your front foot while you are primarily using your rear foot to assist with balance.
    2. Continue keeping your torso fairly upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially situated your elevated object excessively far behind you, you may reach the end of your available hip range of motion before your rear knee reaches the ground.
    7. If you initially situated your elevated object excessively close to you, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. In either case of suboptimal foot placement, you do not need to force your rear knee to tap the ground, and you can adjust how far in front your front foot is situated after finishing your rep.
5. While keeping your front foot firmly planted on the ground and your rear foot in its elevated position, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso fairly upright and knees aligned with your feet while having most of your weight shifted onto the front foot.
6. Repeat steps 4 and 5 until you have finished your set and are ready to step forward with your rear leg prior to repeating the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position and which leg is in the elevated position.
7. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, step forward with your rear leg before you rerack the bar by bending at your hips and knees as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright while firmly holding the kettlebells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the kettlebells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Stand in approximately a hip-width stance with the back of your legs against the edge of a flat exercise bench or similarly sturdy elevated object that will not slide (like a split squat roller, couch, or plyometric box with a somewhat compliant surface).
    1. While not as commonly utilized as a bench, the most comfortable surface will typically be a split squat roller or a barbell that has a squat/hip thrust pad on it and is placed on top of the J-cups of a power rack set to near knee height.
    2. The split squat roller is ideal since it allows for a quicker setup than the barbell technique, but split squat rollers are not as widely available.
3. While keeping your feet approximately hip-width apart, step forward with one foot until its heel is just in front of your other foot’s big toe before doing the same with your opposite foot.
    1. This results in your front foot now being about two “foot lengths” in front of where you started when your calves were touching the object you will use to elevate your rear foot.
    2. While this strategy can consistently situate most people in a viable starting position for the exercise, somewhat of a range of foot placements can be effectively used including having your front foot two and a half “foot lengths” in front of where you started (which may be even more comfortable for some people).
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your bodyweight into your front foot before placing the top surface (on the side of your shoe laces) of your rear foot on top of the elevated surface with that side’s knee bent.
    1. To help shift your bodyweight into your front foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you slightly bend that side’s knee.
    2. Once you have situated your rear foot into the elevated position, you can return to keeping your torso in a fairly upright position, which should involve you maintaining the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright (rather than excessively arching beyond that position).
    3. Strive to move your rear foot backward in a straight line to keep your feet approximately hip-width apart rather than bringing your rear foot closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep the entire sole of your front foot firmly planted on the ground and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    6. If you are using a cylindrical object to elevate your foot, you can contact the surface with the front of your ankle along with your foot, but only your foot will contact the elevated surface if it is flat, like a bench.
    7. Keep your arms by your sides, directed down toward the ground, as you continue holding the kettlebells throughout the duration of your set.
2. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep most of your weight shifted onto your front foot while you are primarily using your rear foot to assist with balance.
    2. Continue keeping your torso fairly upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially positioned your front foot excessively far in front of your elevated object, you may reach the end of your available hip range of motion before your rear knee reaches the ground.
    7. If you initially positioned your front foot too close to the elevated object, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. In either case of suboptimal foot placement, you do not need to force your rear knee to tap the ground, and you can adjust how far in front your front foot is situated after finishing your rep.
3. While keeping your front foot firmly planted on the ground and your rear foot in its elevated position, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso fairly upright and knees aligned with your feet while having most of your weight shifted onto the front foot.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position and which leg is in the elevated position.
    1. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, set the kettlebells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate, aerobic step, or plyometric box) that you will use as a step onto the floor or approach the bottom step of a staircase.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your step, and the difficulty of the exercise will increase as the height increases.
    2. If you are new to forward step-ups, selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point, and you can subsequently adjust the height based on how challenging this height is for you.
    3. For reference, a step of a typical staircase is normally near seven inches (18cm) in height.
    4. If you have balance limitations, you can situate your step near a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
    5. When feasible, setting up where you can face a mirror is recommended to be able to receive visual feedback about your technique.
2. Face the step with your feet approximately hip width apart from each other, as your toes are pointing straight ahead.
    1. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, crossed in front of your chest, or outstretched in front of you.
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift your other foot up to place its sole fully in contact with the step.
    1. Make sure that your lead leg’s heel is not hanging off the step.
2. Shift your weight into your lead foot as you mildly lean your torso forward from hinging at your hip.
    1. The forward torso should occur from you moving about your hip while you keep your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    2. In this starting position for the exercise, your rear leg’s knee should be straight.
3. Without pushing off the ground through your rear foot, drive through your lead leg as you push down into the step by extending (straightening) your knee and hip to lift yourself upward until you can place your rear foot onto the step.
    1. Your lead leg’s knee should be straight, and your torso should be upright by the time you place your other foot onto the step.
    2. Aim to lift yourself exclusively through your lead leg as you refrain from performing a calf raise-like motion to push off with your rear leg.
    3. Keep the entire sole (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) of your elevated foot firmly planted on the step and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your rear leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
4. While you are bearing your weight into the foot that started on the step, lift your other foot up and back off the step as you pull that foot’s toes upward through an ankle motion that results in your heel being closer to the ground than the ball of your foot.
    1. This is the opposite of the ankle motion that occurs when lifting your heels up during a calf raise.
5. With your leg that is on the step, sit your buttocks backward to initiate the descent, and bend your knee to lower yourself in a controlled manner until you can contact the ground with the heel of your other foot.
    1. As you sit your buttocks back (like if you started to sit on a chair), allow your torso naturally to lean mildly forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid rounding your back excessively.
    2. Continue keeping your working leg’s knee stacked over your foot, the sole of your foot fully on the step, and your hip bones level.
    3. While the descent does not have to be extremely slow, your lower body muscles should be actively lowering your body rather than gravity simply plopping you down onto the ground.
6. Repeat steps 3-5 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which foot starts elevated on top of the step.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the J-hooks of a rack to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on the J-hooks.
    1. The setup for this exercise is essentially the same as for a back squat.
    2. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    3. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
2. Face the center of the bar with approximately hip-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
3. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back muscles, slightly below the base of your neck, in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Aim for the bar to rest on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts on the back of your shoulders and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine). This is the high bar position, which is generally recommended over the low bar position for split squats and lunges.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your traps will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    5. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    6. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of split squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase.
    1. While not required, setting up safety pins is recommended in case you lose your balance or fail a rep during your work sets.
6. Repeat steps 2 and 3 of the setup and proceed to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. While keeping your chest up, take a couple of steps backward and position your feet approximately hip-width apart, with your toes pointing straight ahead.
3. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, take a fairly long step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Slightly bend the knee on the side of your stationary foot while doing so.
    2. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    3. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
4. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
5. While keeping both of your feet planted on the ground, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
6. Repeat steps 4 and 5 until you have finished your set. Then step forward with your rear leg to position your feet symmetrically.
    1. Now you have the option of proceeding directly into your next set with your other leg in the forward position, or you can rerack the bar and rest before doing so.
    2. When you are ready to rerack the bar, walk forward until it contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat to the most reclined position that allows your back to be well-supported by the backrest when seated with your feet on the foot platform.
    1. Starting with the most reclined position (which tilts the seat down in a closer to horizontal position) generally works well for most people.
    2. Compared to using a closer to upright seat angle, using an appropriately reclined position will allow you to leg press through a longer range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    3. The most reclined setting on certain machine models may be excessively reclined to the extent that the backrest may no longer be able to stabilize your body when leg pressing. If this occurs, your buttocks may lift off the seat, and your torso may slide back along the backrest due to the backrest being angled too close to a horizontal position. However, most leg press machines will not have an excessively reclined seat setting available.
    4. To determine if the seat angle you initially selected is appropriate for you, you can perform a couple of reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section without adding any weight plates to the leg press. If you feel stable and well-supported by the backrest, the seat is not excessively reclined, and you have selected a viable seat angle.
    5. Most machines allow you to adjust the seat angle, but some models may not permit you to adjust it.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. Note that the sled will provide resistance even without any additional weight plates. The weight of the sled will vary depending on the specific model, but it commonly weighs near 120lb (54kg).
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot platform, hip-width apart, with your toes pointing straight up.
    1. While not required, initially placing both feet on the platform before lifting one foot off the platform during the next step can help with positioning your working leg’s foot appropriately.
    2. Many people find that positioning their feet in this manner allows them to leg press effectively. However, specifically what is ideal foot placement may vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a shoulder-width stance compared to a hip-width stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with pointing their toes slightly out to the side instead of straight up.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different foot placements over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the platform works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select should let your heels remain firmly planted on the platform along with the rest of your feet. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward or limits your range of motion.
    5. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to allow you to unlock the sled and begin your set. If you have short legs, you potentially may have to situate your feet lower on the platform to reach the platform with slightly bent knees. If your legs are too short to achieve this position with a leg press that has multiple safety stopper heights, you can ask a taller lifter to reposition the platform to a lower starting height that you can reach. If you are using a machine that does not permit a lower starting height of the sled, that particular machine may not be feasible for your body type.
5. Lift one foot off the platform and place it in a comfortable position that will keep that leg from contacting the platform throughout your set.
    1. Situating the resting leg’s foot under the platform with a bent knee is commonly a comfortable position for a variety of machines.
    2. However, where it is best to position your resting leg can vary depending on the specific machine model you are using.
    3. The subsequent directions will apply to the leg whose foot is on the platform, since you will just keep your other leg out of the way during your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your foot into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knee.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knee should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handles with your hands to move the safety stoppers out of the sled’s way.
    1. The specific location of the safety handles and how to unlock them can vary among different machine models. However, the safety handles are often near where your hands will be when resting by your sides, and rotating the handles outward will commonly disengage the safety stoppers.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handles) and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Lower the sled toward you in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knee and hip.
    1. Keep the entire sole of your foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform.
    2. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    3. Strive to keep your knee aligned with your second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or third toe as you avoid letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot. It can help to think about pushing your knee out if you notice it starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue lowering the sled through the full range of motion you can while keeping your buttocks firmly on the seat, lower back flat against the back rest, and heel fully on the platform.
    1. People can commonly lower the sled until reaching at least 90 degrees of knee bend. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. If you reached the end range to which your hip can bend, lowering the sled any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your buttocks starting to curl up off the seat.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knee to track over your toes, lowering the sled any further would cause your heel to start lifting off the platform.
7. Drive your foot into the platform to push the sled away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knee and hip.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knee fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knee when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knee into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heel or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your foot.
    3. Continue keeping your foot fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, back against the back rest, and knee aligned over your foot.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
9. After your knee is straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, lock the safety handles with your hands to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the sled.
10. Lower the sled onto the safety stoppers by bending your knee.
11. Switch which leg is on the platform and perform a set with that side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your quads, gluteus maximus, and multiple hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. While holding the dumbbells, stand upright with your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, with your toes pointing somewhat out to the sides rather than pointing straight ahead.
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Continue holding the dumbbells by your sides throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your knees aligned with your feet.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but keep your back flat and chest up, as you avoid rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
3. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
4. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips until you have returned to a fully upright position.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees stacked over your feet as you squat up.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Stand upright with your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, as your toes are pointing somewhat out to the sides rather than pointing straight ahead.
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
2. Raise your arms out in front of you until they are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. You can alternatively keep your arms in a different position if you wish to do so, but keeping your arms outstretched throughout the set provides a counterbalance that often allows people to reach a greater squat depth.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your knees aligned with your feet.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but keep your back flat and chest up, as you avoid rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
3. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion that your mobility enables without rounding your back, lifting your heels up off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, heels lift off the ground, or knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
4. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips until you have returned to a fully upright position.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees stacked over your feet as you squat up.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the kettlebells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the kettlebells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    4. Positioning yourself in a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, take a fairly long step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Slightly bend the knee on the side of your stationary foot while doing so.
    2. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    3. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    6. Keep your arms by your sides, directed down toward the ground throughout the duration of your set.
2. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
3. While keeping both of your feet planted on the ground, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position.
    1. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, set the kettlebells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat to the most reclined position that allows your back to be well-supported by the backrest when seated with your feet on the foot platform.
    1. Starting with the most reclined position (which tilts the seat down in a closer to horizontal position) generally works well for most people.
    2. Compared to using a closer to upright seat angle, using an appropriately reclined position will allow you to leg press through a longer range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    3. The most reclined setting on certain machine models may be excessively reclined to the extent that the backrest may no longer be able to stabilize your body when leg pressing. If this occurs, your buttocks may lift off the seat, and your torso may slide back along the backrest due to the backrest being angled too close to a horizontal position. However, most leg press machines will not have an excessively reclined seat setting available.
    4. To determine if the seat angle you initially selected is appropriate for you, you can perform a couple of reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section without adding any weight plates to the leg press. If you feel stable and well-supported by the backrest, the seat is not excessively reclined, and you have selected a viable seat angle.
    5. Most machines allow you to adjust the seat angle, but some models may not permit you to adjust it.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. Note that the sled will provide resistance even without any additional weight plates. The weight of the sled will vary depending on the specific model, but it commonly weighs near 120lb (54kg).
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot platform, near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to leg press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the platform works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select, your heels should be firmly on the platform along with the rest of your feet. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward and/or limits your range of motion.
    5. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to allow you to unlock the sled and begin your set. If you have short legs, you potentially may have to situate your feet lower on the platform to reach the platform with slightly bent knees. If your legs are too short to achieve this position with a leg press that has multiple safety stopper heights, you can ask a taller lifter to reposition the platform to a lower starting height that you can reach. If you are using a machine that does not permit a lower starting height of the sled, that particular machine may not be feasible for your body type.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handles with your hands to move the safety stoppers out of the sled’s way.
    1. The specific location of the safety handles and how to unlock them can vary among different machine models. However, the safety handles are often near where your hands will be when resting by your sides, and rotating the handles outward will commonly disengage the safety stoppers.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handles) and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Lower the sled toward you in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform.
    2. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue lowering the sled through the full range of motion you can while keeping your buttocks firmly on the seat, lower back flat against the backrest, and heels fully on the platform.
    1. People can commonly lower the sled until their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. If you reached the end range to which your hips can bend, lowering the sled any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your buttocks starting to curl up off the seat.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track over your toes, lowering the sled any further would cause your heels to start lifting off the platform.
7. Drive your feet into the platform to push the sled away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, back against the backrest, and knees aligned over your feet.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set.
9. After your knees are straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, lock the safety handles with your hands to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the sled.
10. Lower the sled onto the safety stoppers by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your quads, gluteus maximus, and multiple hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. Place a cushioned object like a Pilates mat or foam pillow onto the ground.
    1. You can use any object that is comfortable for kneeling.
2. Kneel on the cushioned object with your knees approximately hip width apart from each other and your shoelaces (or the top surface of your feet) in contact with the ground as your heels are facing backward.
    1. Your ankles will be in the same position that they would be at the top of a calf raise.
    2. If you have limited ankle mobility and/or find this ankle position uncomfortable, you can place a half foam roller underneath the front of your ankles, which will allow you to adopt a closer to neutral ankle position.
3. Assume an upright torso position while kneeling and position your arms by your sides with straight elbows or in your preferred position.
    1. Where you position your arms can influence the difficulty of the exercise.
    2. If you wish to increase the intensity of the exercise beyond what is present when your arms are by your sides, you can cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows or hold them overhead with straight elbows for an even greater challenge.
    3. Keep your arms in your preferred position throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Keep your glutes squeezed and your core braced throughout the set to help you maintain a rigid trunk position.
2. Lean your torso backward in a controlled manner by bending at your knees through the range of motion that your strength and mobility permit.
    1. The motion should exclusively come from your knees, while your hips and lower back remain near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, which is facilitated by step 1.
    2. Throughout the exercise, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thigh through your hip to your torso, indicating that you are maintaining an appropriately rigid trunk position without moving your hips.
    3. In particular, avoid letting your lower back excessively arch, which can occur if you do not keep your glutes squeezed and core braced.
    4. The intensity of this exercise will increase as you lean back further, so you can adjust how far you descend based on your ability.
    5. The greatest possible range of motion involves leaning back until your upper back lightly contacts the ground with your knees fully bent, but most people cannot reach this depth without consistently training this exercise over time.
    6. As you progressively lean back, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in one of the quad muscles on the front of your upper thigh, and the flexibility of this muscle can limit how far backward certain individuals can move.
3. Reverse the movement by lifting your torso up through motion at your knees until you have returned to the starting position, where your torso is fully upright.
    1. Continue keeping your thighs and torso aligned with each other as you resist any back or hip motion, as you did not during the descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all four of your quad muscles, including your rectus femoris, which may not be trained as effectively by most squat, leg press, and lunge variations.## Setting up
1. While standing upright, position your feet into a rather wide stance by taking a large step out to the side with one foot while keeping your other foot on the ground.
    1. How wide a stance may be ideal for you will be influenced by your hip mobility, and some trial and error may be needed to find which stance feels best to you.
    2. As a general guideline, your feet should be further apart than one and a half times your shoulder width, but not so wide that you feel unstable.
    3. If you have balance limitations, you can situate yourself with a surface (e.g., power rack beam) in front of you where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
2. Point your toes somewhat outward in a position that feels comfortable to you and lift your arms up so that they are parallel to the ground.
    1. In this starting position, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you are keeping your chest up and looking straight ahead.
    2. You will keep your arms outstretched parallel to the ground throughout your set to provide you with a counterbalance that can assist with keeping your torso upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot. On the side to which you shift your weight, start bending at your knee and hip to squat down toward that side as you push your knee out to that side while keeping your other knee straight.
    1. While doing so, your torso will move closer to the foot on which you shifted your weight as it simultaneously descends.
    2. In the starting position, your knees are inside your feet, and one of the objectives of the descent is to drive your bending knee out toward that side’s foot until your knee is stacked over the middle of your foot.
2. Continue to squat down in a controlled manner to that side through the full range of motion comfortably permitted by your hip mobility and muscle flexibility.
    1. Throughout your rep, keep the sole of the foot to which you shifted your weight fully in contact with the ground as you keep that side’s knee aligned with your foot (as opposed to letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot).
    2. Based on your preference and what feels most comfortable, you can keep the sole of your other foot fully on the ground, or you can keep its heel on the ground while allowing the rest of the foot to lift up off the ground.
    3. As you descend, allow your torso naturally to lean somewhat forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but strive to keep your lower back flat and chest up
    4. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your thigh muscles the deeper you squat.
    5. If you wish, you can pause at the bottom to extend the time you spend stretching your muscles.
3. Reverse the movement by squatting up as you progressively extend (straighten) your knee and hip to drive your torso upward and back toward the center of your stance.
    1. Finish in the same position in which you started, where both knees are straight, and your torso is upright, symmetrically situated between your feet.
    2. If you opted to lift part of your foot (on the side with the straight knee) off the ground during the descent, you will return the entirety of its sole onto the ground during the ascent.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 as you perform your Cossack squat toward your opposite foot.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to bring your feet back together to a near hip-width stance.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus while also being able to improve your hip mobility due to the loaded stretch it provides.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the kettlebells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the kettlebells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    4. Positioning yourself in a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position with your arms directed down toward the ground as you do so.
    4. Strive to keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    7. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step backward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step forward with your rear leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright, arms directed down toward the ground, and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging backward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebells down.
    1. After finishing the last rep, set the kettlebells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the J-hooks of a rack to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on the J-hooks.
    1. The setup for this exercise is essentially the same as for a back squat.
    2. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    3. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
2. Face the center of the bar with an approximately hip-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
3. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back muscles, slightly below the base of your neck, in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Aim for the bar to rest on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts on the back of your shoulders and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine). This is the high bar position, which is generally recommended over the low bar position for split squats and lunges.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your traps will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    5. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    6. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase.
    1. While not required, setting up safety pins is recommended in case you lose your balance or fail a rep during your work sets.
6. Repeat steps 2 and 3 of the setup and proceed to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. While keeping your chest up, take a couple of steps backward and position your feet approximately hip-width apart, with your toes pointing straight ahead.
3. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
4. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. You should perform steps 3 and 4 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    7. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step backward during your next rep.
5. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step forward with your rear leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
6. Repeat steps 3-5 while lunging backward with your opposite side.
7. Alternate between steps 3-6 until you have completed your set.
8. Walk forward until the bar contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance with the back of your legs against the edge of the sturdy elevated object you will use to elevate your foot.
    1. If available, a padded cylindrical object positioned near knee height will normally be most comfortable and is recommended.
    2. A Smith machine bar with a squat/hip thrust pad on it, a barbell on top of the J-cups of a power rack, the leg pad of a lying hamstring curl machine, and a split squat roller stand are all high-quality cylindrical options that you can position to knee height.
    3. Alternatively, you can use an object like a flat exercise bench, couch, or plyometric box with a somewhat compliant surface, but objects with a flat surface tend not to be as comfortable for this exercise.
2. While keeping your feet approximately hip-width apart, step forward with one foot until its heel is just in front of your other foot’s big toe before doing the same with your opposite foot.
    1. This results in your front foot now being about two “foot lengths” in front of where you started when your calves were touching the object you will use to elevate your rear foot.
    2. While this strategy can consistently situate most people in a viable starting position for the exercise, a range of foot placements can be effectively used, including having your front foot two and a half “foot lengths” in front of where you started (which may be even more comfortable for some people).
    3. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc.
    4. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your bodyweight into your front foot before placing the top surface (on the side of your shoe laces) of your rear foot on top of the elevated surface with that side’s knee bent.
    1. To help shift your bodyweight into your front foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you slightly bend that side’s knee.
    2. Once you have situated your rear foot into the elevated position, you can return to keeping your torso in a fairly upright position, which should involve you maintaining the natural curvature of your lower back, like when you are normally standing upright (rather than excessively arching beyond that position).
    3. Strive to move your rear foot backward in a straight line to keep your feet approximately hip-width apart, rather than bringing your rear foot closer toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep the entire sole of your front foot firmly planted on the ground and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    6. If you are using a cylindrical object to elevate your foot, you can contact the surface with the front of your ankle along with your foot, but only your foot will contact the elevated surface if it is flat, like a bench.
2. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep most of your weight shifted onto your front foot while you are primarily using your rear foot to assist with balance.
    2. Continue keeping your torso fairly upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially positioned your front foot excessively far in front of your elevated object, you may reach the end of your available hip range of motion before your rear knee reaches the ground.
    7. If you initially positioned your front foot too close to the elevated object, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. In either case of suboptimal foot placement, you do not need to force your rear knee to tap the ground, and you can adjust how far in front your front foot is situated after finishing your rep.
3. While keeping your front foot firmly planted on the ground and your rear foot in its elevated position, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso fairly upright and knees aligned with your feet while having most of your weight shifted onto the front foot.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position and which leg is in the elevated position.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to position the bar on your upper back and un-rack it in a stable position.
    1. As an initial approximation, you can position the bar such that it is a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
2. Position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are slightly below the height of the bar when your rear knee taps the ground at the end of the lowering phase of your split squat.
    1. To find this point for your body, you can perform a rep or two of the split squat with the unloaded bar as described in the “exercise technique” section, but you can initially put the safety stops a little below the top of your hip bones when standing upright as an approximation.
3. Add your desired weight to the bar and face the center of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails).
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
4. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat wider than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
5. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees to then walk under the bar and situate it on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
    7. If you need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground in order to position the bar on your upper back, you can remove the weights from the bar, lower the height of the bar, and add the weight plates back before repositioning the bar on your upper back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to tighten your upper back muscles, and unrack it by starting to stand up as you straighten your knees.
2. After the bar rests fully on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars with a slight wrist flick before standing fully upright with the bar.
3. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, take a fairly long step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Slightly bend the knee on the side of your stationary foot while doing so.
    2. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    3. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
4. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
5. While keeping both of your feet planted on the ground, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
6. Repeat steps 4 and 5 until you have finished your set and are ready to step forward with your rear leg to return to having your feet symmetrically hip-width apart before repeating the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position.
7. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, step forward with your rear leg to return to having your feet symmetrically hip-width apart before re-racking the bar.
    1. Re-rack the bar by bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate or an aerobic step) that you will use as a step onto the floor in a location that has sufficient space behind it for your split squat.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your stepping surface, but selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point.
    2. If you have balance limitations, you can situate your step near a surface (e.g., hand rail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
2. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance with the foot on which you will first step up onto the step aligned with the middle of your step (which is primarily relevant if you are using a bumper plate to provide the greatest surface area for your foot).
    1. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc..
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. With the foot aligned with the middle of your step, step up onto the step with the entirety of its sole in contact with the step while leaving your other foot on the ground.
    1. The knee on the side of your elevated foot should be mildly bent once on the step.
2. Shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot before taking a fairly long step backward with your other foot and contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. To shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you bend that side’s knee slightly more.
    2. Once you step back and resume transferring some of your weight into your rear leg, you can return to keeping your torso in an upright position.
    3. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    4. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    5. Keep the entire sole of your elevated foot firmly planted on the step and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    6. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
3. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the step.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
4. While keeping both of your feet planted, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position on top of the step.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Anchor a long looped band at knee height around a power rack’s beam or similarly sturdy object with a smooth surface to which you can anchor your band.
    1. To anchor the band, you can initially fold the band around the beam while holding each end, loop one end of the band into the middle of the other end, and pull that end until it is taught.
    2. Inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
    3. You can optionally select an anchor point that, after you walk backward, places you near an elevated surface where you can place a hand to provide balance assistance during the exercise.
2. Step into the band with one foot and situate the band on the back of your knee.
    1. If you are wearing shorts, you can situate a hand towel between the back of your knee and the band for comfort since the band pulling on the skin can be uncomfortable (especially for individuals with notable leg hair).
3. Walk backward until the band provides the level of tension you wish to experience.
    1. Up to a certain point, you can progressively increase the band’s resistance by walking back farther. However, be careful not to walk back so far that you overstretch the band, which can be indicated by an abrupt increase in resistance from the band.
    2. The capacity to which a band can safely stretch can vary depending on the company that made it, but avoid stretching the band three times its resting length or greater as a general rule of thumb to minimize the risk of tearing the band.
    3. Naturally, you will not be measuring the length to which you elongate the band. Nonetheless, if the band does not provide sufficient resistance after taking a few steps back from the point at which you started feeling resistance, it may be appropriate to switch to a higher tension band instead of walking any farther back.
4. Position your feet about hip width apart as you are facing the band’s anchor point while standing fully upright.
    1. If you are near a surface that you can reach, you can place one hand on that surface to assist with maintaining your balance throughout the exercise, which can be helpful if you are using a high-tension band.
## Exercise technique
1. Through the leg that is banded, allow the band to pull your knee forward in a controlled manner as your heel lifts up off the ground while you keep the ball of your foot on the ground and maintain an upright torso position.
    1. As you bend your working leg’s knee, keep your other leg’s knee straight with its foot fully in contact with the ground.
    2. Once you feel that your working leg’s knee can no longer track forward with the previously described technique, you have reached the end range of this exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by pulling your knee backward until you have fully straightened your knee with your working leg’s foot flat on the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright as your resting leg’s knee straight.
3. Fully squeeze your quad muscles on the front of your working leg’s thigh for approximately 1-2 seconds in the fully straightened knee position.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will train the quads in a manner that may not be especially effective for muscle growth, but may be helpful as a dynamic warm-up or to regain full knee extension range of motion.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate or ideally an aerobic step) that you will use as a step onto the floor, somewhat in front of the Smith machine’s bar.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your stepping surface, but selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you will face and put the step in front of the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails).
    3. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face, so it does not matter which side in which you place the step.
    4. Strive to place your step far enough in front of the bar that you can keep your torso fairly upright when performing your split squat with one foot on the step, which may take a bit of trial and error if you are new to this exercise.
    5. Due to the fixed bar path of the Smith machine, situating the step directly under the bar would require you to adopt a bent-over torso position during this exercise.
    6. When using a sufficiently wide object (such as an aerobic step) as a step, you can simply place the step so that it is centered with the Smith machine’s bar.
    7. If what you use as a step does not have a large enough surface area for you to stand on it in a hip-width stance with both of your soles fully in contact with the step, situate your step slightly toward the side on which you initially will place your foot on the step.
    8. The appropriate step placement will allow you to perform your split squat with your elevated foot fully in contact with the step while your feet are hip-width apart from each other.
2. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to position the bar on your upper back and un-rack it in a stable position.
    1. As an initial approximation, you can position the bar such that it is a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
3. Position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are slightly below the height of the bar when your rear knee taps the ground at the end of the lowering phase of your split squat.
    1. To find this point for your body, you can perform a rep or two of the split squat with the unloaded bar as described in the “exercise technique” section, but you can initially put the safety stops a little below the top of your hip bones when standing upright as an approximation.
4. Add your desired weight to the bar and face the center of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
5. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat wider than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
6. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees to then walk under the bar and situate it on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
    7. If you need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground in order to position the bar on your upper back, you can remove the weights from the bar, lower the height of the bar, and add the weight plates back before repositioning the bar on your upper back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to tighten your upper back muscles, and unrack it by starting to stand up as you straighten your knees.
2. After the bar rests fully on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars with a slight wrist flick before standing fully upright with the bar.
3. Step up onto the step with the entirety of your sole in contact with the step while leaving your other foot on the ground.
    1. If you positioned your step closer toward one side of your body (due to using a step with a small surface area), you will step up with the foot aligned with the step, while you can use either side if your step is centered.
    2. The knee on the side of your elevated foot should be mildly bent once on the step.
4. Shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot before taking a fairly long step backward with your other foot and contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. To shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you bend that side’s knee slightly more.
    2. Once you step back and resume transferring some of your weight into your rear leg, you can return to keeping your torso in an upright position.
    3. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    4. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    5. Keep the entire sole of your elevated foot firmly planted on the step and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    6. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
5. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the step.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
6. While keeping both of your feet planted, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
7. Repeat steps 5 and 6 until you have finished your set and are ready to step forward with your rear leg prior to repeating the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position on top of the step.
    1. If you are using a small step that you initially situated closer toward one side, you can re-rack the weight after completing your set with your first foot elevated and adjust the step position before performing your set with your other side’s foot elevated.
8. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, step forward with your rear leg before you re-rack the bar by bending at your hips and knees as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the J-hooks of a rack to be slightly below the height of your elbows when standing with your arms at your sides.
2. Place a barbell on the J-hooks, and add weight plates of your desired load.
3. Optionally place a squat pad or hip thrust pad around the center of the bar to provide cushioning for your elbows.
    1. Wearing a fairly thick long-sleeved shirt (like a hoodie) or elbow sleeves can be another means of cushioning your elbows.
4. Face the center of the bar in a near shoulder-width stance.
5. Mildly bend your knees, and hook your elbows under the bar as your elbows are bent and pointing down toward the ground.
    1. Based on your preference, you can clasp your hands together or leave them unclasped.
6. Stand upright with the bar by straightening your knees and hips.
7. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of Zercher squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
8. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of the Zercher squat.
    1. If you train without safety pins and fail a rep, you can dump the weight forward, which may require shooting your hips up and pushing your knees into a more hinged position so that you can dump the bar without hitting your legs. This maneuver increases loading on your lower back (which is not inherently problematic but may be uncomfortable for some people), and it has the potential to damage certain types of flooring, so using the safety pins is recommended.
9. Repeat steps 4-6 of the setup and proceed to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Take a couple of steps backward, and position your feet shoulder-width apart or wider with your toes pointing somewhat out to the sides.
    1. The specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. In the starting position, your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright. Keep your neck in this position throughout your set.
2. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to create tension throughout the entirety of your core to form a “360-degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
3. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your knees aligned with your feet.
    1. As you descend, it is natural for your torso to lean forward mildly at your hips to maintain balance, but aim to keep your torso mostly upright, with your back flat and chest up.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot, as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. Keep the bar close to your torso with your bent elbows hooked under it throughout your set.
4. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
5. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips until you have returned to a fully upright position.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees stacked over your feet as you squat up.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
7. Rerack the bar by walking forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook, and you are ready to rest the bar on the J-hooks by once again bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to adjust it, angle the foot plate to a setting that comfortably allows you to train through a full range of motion with your feet fully on the foot plate.
    1. Angling the foot plate closer toward a horizontal position can reduce the amount of ankle mobility required to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate at the bottom of the rep. However, your feet may feel unstable and risk sliding if the foot plate is angled excessively close to being horizontal, so you want to find an appropriate balance.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal foot plate angle for you, but you can initially select an angle that is about halfway in between the two extremes before trying out different settings over time to determine which one you prefer.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. When learning the exercise, you can alternatively leave the machine unloaded, since it will still provide resistance without additional weight plates.
3. Stand on the foot plate, and position your shoulders snugly underneath the shoulder pads as your back is fully in contact with the back pad.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can rest your head against the pad or keep it slightly in front of the pad. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    3. If your machine’s back pad extends low enough, you should also be resting the back of your hip bones against the back pad.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot plate, near shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing mildly outward and the soles of your feet fully on the foot plate.
    1. People commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, but the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable while being able to train through a long range of motion, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the foot plate works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select should allow your heels (along with the rest of your feet) to be firmly on the foot plate throughout the whole set. Your foot placement should also feel comfortable on your knees and allow you to keep your whole back snugly against the back pad at the bottom of each rep.
    5. Placing your feet higher up may make it easier to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate, more difficult to keep your back flat against the back pad, and potentially bias the gluteus maximus to a greater degree. Placing your feet lower down may make it more difficult to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate, easier to keep your back flat against the back pad, and potentially bias the quads to a degree. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the plate if doing so feels awkward and/or limits your range of motion.
    6. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to allow you to unlock the machine and begin your set. While exceptions may exist, V-squat machines often do not allow you to adjust the starting height of the pads, so certain machines may not be suitable for rather short lifters.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your feet into the foot plate to push yourself away from it by straightening your knees.
    1. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handle with your hand before firmly grasping the hand grips for additional stability.
4. Squat down in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the foot plate.
    2. Continue keeping your back snugly in contact with the back pad.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should avoid dropping down in an uncontrolled fashion.
5. Continue squatting down through the full range of motion you can comfortably perform while keeping your heels fully on the foot plate, along with your back flat against the back pad.
    1. People can commonly descend until their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees, and some lifters can reach the point at which their thighs touch their calves with their knees fully bent. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. If you reach the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track forward over your toes, squatting any deeper can cause your heels to start lifting off the foot plate.
    3. If you reach the end range to which your hips can bend, descending any further can cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your hip bones starting to curl forward off the back pad.
    4. Squatting into a deep position is not inherently problematic to the knees, but there is no need to force yourself into the lowest position possible if you experience knee discomfort at your end range.
6. Reverse the movement by driving your feet into the foot plate to squat up as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees during this exercise, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, knees aligned over your feet, and your back fully against the back pad.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
8. After your knees are straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, engage the safety handle with one of your hands.
9. Bend your knees to lower the weight fully onto the safety arm before stepping off the foot plate.
## Description
While also training your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus, this exercise can effectively develop your quads.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the dumbbells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Stand in approximately a hip-width stance with the back of your legs against the edge of the sturdy elevated object you will use to elevate your foot.
    1. If available, a padded cylindrical object positioned near knee height will normally be most comfortable and is recommended.
    2. A Smith machine bar with a squat/hip thrust pad on it, a barbell on top of the J-cups of a power rack, the leg pad of a lying hamstring curl machine, and a split squat roller stand are all high-quality cylindrical options that you can position to knee height.
    3. Alternatively, you can use an object like a flat exercise bench, couch, or plyometric box with a somewhat compliant surface, but objects with a flat surface tend not to be as comfortable for this exercise.
3. While keeping your feet approximately hip width apart, step forward with one foot until its heel is just in front of your other foot’s big toe before doing the same with your opposite foot.
    1. This results in your front foot now being about two “foot lengths” in front of where you started when your calves were touching the object you will use to elevate your rear foot.
    2. While this strategy can consistently situate most people in a viable starting position for the exercise, a range of foot placements can be effectively used, including having your front foot two and a half “foot lengths” in front of where you started (which may be even more comfortable for some people).
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your bodyweight into your front foot before placing the top surface (on the side of your shoelaces) of your rear foot on top of the elevated surface with that side’s knee bent.
    1. To help shift your bodyweight into your front foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you slightly bend that side’s knee.
    2. Once you have situated your rear foot into the elevated position, you can return to keeping your torso in a fairly upright position, which should involve you maintaining the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright (rather than excessively arching beyond that position).
    3. Strive to move your rear foot backward in a straight line to keep your feet approximately hip-width apart, rather than bringing your rear foot closer toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep the entire sole of your front foot firmly planted on the ground and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    6. If you are using a cylindrical object to elevate your foot, you can contact the surface with the front of your ankle along with your foot, but only your foot will contact the elevated surface if it is flat, like a bench.
    7. Keep your arms by your sides, directed down toward the ground, as you continue holding the dumbbells throughout the duration of your set.
2. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep most of your weight shifted onto your front foot while you are primarily using your rear foot to assist with balance.
    2. Continue keeping your torso fairly upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially positioned your front foot excessively far in front of your elevated object, you may reach the end of your available hip range of motion before your rear knee can tap the ground. If you initially positioned your front foot too close to the elevated object, your depth may be limited by your ability to keep your front foot’s heel on the ground. In either case of suboptimal foot placement, you can simply descend through the range of motion in which you can move with proper technique before adjusting how far in front your front foot is situated after finishing your rep.
3. While keeping your front foot firmly planted on the ground and your rear foot in its elevated position, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso fairly upright and knees aligned with your feet while having most of your weight shifted onto the front foot.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position and which leg is in the elevated position.
    1. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Wrap a thick, long, looped band around a power rack’s beam (or similarly sturdy object with a smooth surface) just below knee height.
    1. Because the purpose of the band is to keep your lower legs upright in a stationary position, a high-tension band will be required (unless you have a viable inelastic substitute like a Spanish squat strap made of polyester).
    2. Inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
    3. The floor on which you perform this exercise should have sufficient traction so that your feet do not slide forward during your Spanish squat.
2. Step inside each end of the band’s loop with your feet, and situate the band on the top of your calves just below your knees.
    1. If you are wearing shorts, you can situate a hand towel between the back of your knees and the band for comfort since the band pulling on the skin can be uncomfortable (especially for individuals with notable leg hair).
3. Walk backward until the band is fully tensed, which should not require many steps when using an appropriately thick band.
    1. Up to a certain point, you can progressively increase the band’s resistance by walking back farther, but be careful not to walk back so far that you overstretch the band, which can be indicated by an abrupt increase in resistance from the band.
    2. The capacity to which a band can safely stretch can vary depending on the company that made it, but avoid stretching the band three times its resting length or greater as a general rule of thumb to minimize the risk of tearing the band.
    3. Naturally, you will not be measuring the length to which you elongate the band, but if the band does not provide sufficient resistance after taking a few steps back from the point at which you started feeling resistance, it may be appropriate to switch to a higher tension band instead of walking any farther back.
4. Position your feet about shoulder width apart as you are facing the band’s anchor point while standing fully upright with your toes either facing straight ahead or rotated slightly outward based on your preference.
    1. So long as you do not place them on your legs, you can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides or crossed on your shoulders.
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your torso and lower legs in a vertical position, bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner until your thighs are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot.
    3. Strive to keep your shoulders vertically stacked over your hips, as you resist any forward torso lean. 
2. Reverse the movement by pressing through the ground to squat up as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips until you have returned to the starting position with your torso fully lifted up and your knees straightened.
    1. Like during the descent, continue keeping your torso and lower legs in a vertical position, feet fully in contact with the ground, and knees in line with your feet.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads with relatively low loading of your gluteus maximus and hip adductors, in contrast to most squat variations.## Setting up
1. Firmly grasp one dumbbell with both hands interlocked around the handle before standing upright with it.
    1. Keep your elbows straight with your arms just in front of your torso. This will position the dumbbell between the inner aspect of your upper thighs. You will continue holding the dumbbell in this position throughout your set.
    2. The two weighted ends of the dumbbells should be horizontally level with each other.
    3. Some people may perform this exercise while holding one weighted end of the dumbbell with its handle in a vertical position. Holding it this way will limit your range of motion, since the lower end of the dumbbell will contact the floor and prevent you from squatting any deeper unless you are standing on a pair of aerobic steps.
2. Position your feet moderately outside shoulder width apart with your toes angled forward and moderately out to your sides.
    1. Initially situating your feet near 1.5 times your shoulder width apart can be an appropriate stance width for many lifters.
    2. Pointing your toes about 30-45 degrees out to the side is an appropriate amount of toe flare for most people. Zero degrees of toe flare would mean your toes are pointing straight forward, while 90 degrees would mean your toes are pointing directly out to your side.
    3. Ultimately, your feet should be situated in a manner that allows you to feel stable and comfortable (particularly in your hips) during this exercise.
    4. Specifically where it is optimal to position your feet can be influenced by your individual body structure and hip mobility. Consequently, you can try out different stance widths and degrees of toe flare over time to determine where you personally feel best.
3. In the starting position, your back and neck should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Start squatting down by sitting your buttocks back while bending at your hips and knees in a controlled manner. Drive your knees outward as you keep your back flat and chest up.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance. However, you should focus on sitting your hips back rather than pushing your knees forward.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
3. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion that your mobility enables without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a sumo squat will vary among different individuals. Your hip mobility will often determine how deeply you can sumo squat.
    2. If your back starts rounding, heels lift off the ground, or knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
4. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up. Drive your feet through the ground and thrust your hips forward until you are fully upright.
    1. Continue keeping your knees pushed out over your feet, chest up, back flat, and feet fully on the ground.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your quads, this squat variation can effectively develop your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its furthest back position that allows you to begin each rep with the greatest knee bend you can achieve.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the shin pad to a position that places it slightly above the front of your ankles on the lowest aspect of your shins when seated with your legs behind the shin pad with your knees bent.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the shin pad without moving the lever arm in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. Add weight plates to the machine, and sit down on its seat with your lower shins behind the shin pad, knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point, and back resting against the back pad.
    1. With the appropriate positioning, it should be comfortable for you to have your knees bent.
    2. Your knees are aligned with the machine’s pivot point if you can draw a straight line through the center of both knees to the pivot point.
5. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Through a controlled kicking motion, progressively extend (straighten) one knee to lift up the shin pad until you have fully straightened your knee while you keep your other knee bent.
    1. While you should not lift the weight in an intentionally slow manner, avoid kicking up in an uncontrolled, jerky motion.
    2. As your lower leg moves through motion at your knee, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set as your thighs remain in contact with the seat.
3. Reverse the movement as you allow your working leg’s knee to bend progressively in a controlled manner to lower the leg pad until you have returned to the starting position with your knee bent as much as the machine permits.
    1. Keep your resting leg’s knee bent as you lower the weight with your other leg.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower leg down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all four of your quad muscles, including your rectus femoris, which may not be trained as effectively by most squat, leg press, and lunge variations.

### **Training the entirety of your quad**

Exercises that exclusively resist knee extension like machine leg extensions or exercises that chiefly load knee extension while imparting minimal hip extensor loading (like reverse Nordic curls and sissy squats) can serve a distinct role in a training program. A wide array of exercises will train knee extension and hip extension simultaneously such as squats, lunges, step ups, and leg presses. These multi-joint exercises can certainly stimulate meaningful increases in the size and strength of the quads, but they are not ideal for training the rectus femoris, which is the one quad muscle that crosses the hip and functions as a hip flexor along with functioning as a knee extensor. Notably, the rectus femoris does not just weakly assist in flexing the hip, but rather functions as a primary hip flexor that has greater leverage for producing hip flexion torque than any other muscle in the human body at certain joint angles. Because contraction of the rectus femoris can produce a substantial amount of hip flexion torque, its recruitment during a movement that requires both hip and knee extension is less biomechanically efficient than recruitment of the other muscle bellies of the quadriceps. As the rectus femoris contracts more forcefully during a movement like the squat, the hip extensors must work harder to resist the hip flexion torque it produces. Striving to maximize efficiency, the nervous system preferentially recruits the three monoarticular (crossing only the knee joint) muscles of the quad to a greater degree than the rectus femoris during compound exercises where extension simultaneously occurs at the hip and knee joints.

Although some research indicates the rectus femoris does not grow significantly from multi-joint exercises like squats, other studies have reported statistically significant growth in this muscle following such training. When a fairly small magnitude of growth occurs (which is the case even in studies reaching the level of statistical significance), it is unsurprising that the change reaches statistical significance in certain studies but not in others. While the rectus femoris will be trained to a degree during these types of multi-joint exercises, the available body of evidence indicates that the monoarticular quad muscles are much more likely to experience a robust hypertrophy response. Correspondingly, your rectus femoris will almost certainly lag behind your other quad muscles if all of your quad training is performed through multi-joint exercises like squats.

However, during a single joint knee extension exercise where no movement occurs at the hip joint, the rectus femoris is just as active as the monoarticular muscles of the quad, if not more so. This is logical since the hip flexion torque that rectus femoris contraction generates is essentially inconsequential during an exercise that does not simultaneously resist the hip extensor muscles. Congruent with what would be expected, machine seated leg extensions have been demonstrated to produce a considerable degree of hypertrophy of the rectus femoris that is similar to or even more pronounced than that of the three monoarticular quad muscles. While much less studied than machine leg extensions, reverse Nordic curls have been found to yield significant increases in rectus femoris size and the biomechanically similar sissy squat would likely yield a similar result, although it has not been directly studied.

### **Modifying the leg extension for greater muscle growth**

When performed with a sufficiently high relative intensity and appropriate load, machine leg extensions can grow the rectus femoris regardless of how you position the seat, but the seat position can influence the magnitude to which the muscle may grow. Larsen et al. (2025) assessed changes in the thickness of the rectus femoris and vastus lateralis after untrained participants trained single leg machine leg extensions for 10 weeks. For each participant, one leg was trained with an upright torso positioning the hip into 90 degrees of flexion, while the other leg trained with a recline torso positioning the hip into only 40 degrees of flexion. The two interventions resulted in similar vastus lateralis growth, which is unsurprising since that muscle does not cross the hip joint. However, the rectus femoris experienced significantly greater growth from the leg extensions using a reclined torso position compared to the upright torso position. Because the rectus femoris functions as a hip flexor, it shortens in progressively greater angles of hip flexion, while it lengthens as the hip extends. When you are able to lean your torso back when seated, your hip is in a lower angle of flexion compared to when you are seated upright or leaned forward, so your rectus femoris can operate at a longer muscle length when you lean back. The available body of evidence indicates that biarticular muscles (which cross two joints) are likely to experience greater hypertrophy when trained at long lengths compared to shorter lengths, and the rectus femoris fits this trend along with the hamstrings, calves, and triceps.

If you wish to train your rectus femoris at longer lengths during machine leg extensions, you can intentionally position the back pad as far back as you can to allow you to lean further back at your hips when performing the exercise. With this approach, you would still want to align your knee joint with the machine’s pivot point, so you would position your thighs at the same part of the seat as you would if you had the back pad further forward to maintain an upright torso. When using the reclined technique, your upper back would still rest against the back pad, but your lower back would no longer contact the back pad in contrast to if you positioned the seat further forward. While Larsen et al. (2025) specifically utilized a machine that allowed participants to be meaningfully reclined, many leg extensions are not constructed in a manner that would allow users to set up appropriately with only 40 degrees of hip flexion. Consequently, the degree to which you practically can modify your hip position during a machine leg extension will likely be smaller than what was studied by Larsen et al. (2025). This may potentially result in using a reclined torso position on your leg extension machine having a smaller effect on rectus femoris hypertrophy compared to what was reported by Larsen et al. (2025).## Setting up
1. Adjust the J-hooks of a power rack or squat stand to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on the J-hooks.
    1. Other than setting up an object you use as a step during the next step, the setup for this exercise is the same as for a back squat.
2. Centered under the bar, position what you will use as your sturdy step that will not slide.
    1. As an alternative to a thick bumper plate (which can be the ideal option), you may be able to use an aerobic step that is situated in a manner ensuring that part of the step does not get in your way when setting up.
    2. When using a long aerobic step, it is typically most practical to position the step so that the back end is directly under the bar, while the front end is further in front of the bar outside the rack. Strive to avoid positioning the step where it can interfere with walking out the weight.
    3. You can use a range of different heights for your stepping surface, but selecting an object that is near 3-4 inches (8-10cm) high can be an appropriate starting point, which typically includes 45lb (20kg) bumper plates and aerobic steps without risers.
3. Face the center of the bar, and grasp it with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
4. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to then walk under the bar with your feet on each side of the step.
5. Situate the bar on your upper back muscles slightly below the base of your neck in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Aim for the bar to rest on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts on the back of your shoulders and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine). This is the high bar position, which is generally recommended over the low bar position for split squats and lunges.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your traps will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back and should situate the bar lower on your traps.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
6. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
7. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of split squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
8. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase.
    1. While not required, setting up safety pins is recommended in case you lose your balance or fail a rep during your work sets.
9. Repeat steps 3-6 of the setup and proceed to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your chest up, take a couple of steps backward and position your feet approximately hip-width apart, with your toes pointing straight ahead.
    1. Strive to step back far enough that you will be able to lower your rear knee below the height of your front foot during your set.
2. Step up onto the step with the entirety of your sole in contact with the step while leaving your other foot on the ground.
    1. To keep the feet hip-width apart with the front foot’s sole fully on the step (rather than part of the sole hanging off the step), some lifters may initially have to take a very small step to the side, so that one foot is fully aligned with the step.
3. From your split stance position, bend your knees to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the step.
    2. While the balls of your rear foot will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees tracking forward in line with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
4. Continue descending through the full range of motion you can while keeping your front foot’s sole fully in contact with the step.
    1. For some lifters, this may involve lightly tapping the ground with the rear knee in the bottom position.
    2. Other lifters may find that they reach the end of their hip’s available range of motion before the rear knee reaches the ground, which will produce a notable stretching sensation in the front leg’s glute as the hip is fully bent.
    3. If you feel a blocked sensation in your front ankle at the bottom before your rear knee descends below the level of your front foot, your rear foot is likely excessively close to your front foot, resulting in your ankle mobility restricting your range of motion. To descend any further would require lifting up your front foot’s heel off the step in this situation. To correct this, you should move your rear foot a bit further back once you are upright between reps.
5. While keeping both of your feet planted, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees and hips to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
6. Repeat steps 3-5 until you have finished your set. Then step down from the step to position your feet symmetrically.
    1. Now you have the option of proceeding directly into your next set with your other leg in the forward position, or you can rerack the bar and rest before doing so.
    2. When you are ready to rerack the bar, walk forward until it contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
To perform this exercise, you naturally have to position a bar on your back and have sufficient space available for you without any objects or people getting in your way. With a heavy load, it will be easiest to initially set up an Olympic barbell at mid-chest height in a squat stand or the outside of a power rack. However, many gyms will not have the necessary space to perform walking lunges starting from a squat stand/power rack. Alternatively, you can reverse curl or power clean a bar (whether that be an Olympic bar with weight plates or a fixed-weight straight bar) to your shoulders, press it up overhead, and lower it onto your upper back. With a fixed-weight straight bar, you will not require as much space to perform this exercise, but you will be limited in terms of your load potential. Regardless of how you set up, the exercise technique will be the same, but the different set-up methods are described in separate sections based on which one you wish to select.

### **Setting up with a fixed-weight straight bar**

1. Stand upright with a fixed-weight straight bar while holding it with straight elbows and a mildly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms are directed toward you.
2. Walk over with the bar to the spot where you could begin your walking lunges.
3. While keeping your elbows straight, sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and your torso to incline slightly forward.
4. As you simultaneously drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body, curl the bar upward in an arcing manner by bending at your elbows while allowing your elbows to swing a bit forward.
    1. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
5. Tuck your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
6. Press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can press the bar overhead with a push press technique, where you use leg drive.
7. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
8. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
9. Lower the bar behind your head onto your upper back muscles slightly below the base of your neck in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Aim for the bar to rest on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts on the back of your shoulders and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine). This is the high bar position, which is generally recommended over the low bar position for walking lunges.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your traps will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back and should situate the bar lower on your traps.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.

### **Setting up an Olympic barbell from a rack**

1. Adjust the J-hooks of a power rack or squat stand to a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
    1. If you are using a power rack, make sure that the J-hooks are on the outside of the power rack so that you can walk out with the bar.
2. Place the barbell on top of the J-hooks, and add plates of your desired load.
3. While facing the direction in which you will walk out, stand in front of the center of the bar with a near hip-width stance.
4. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
5. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to then walk under the bar.
6. Situate the bar on your upper back muscles slightly below the base of your neck in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Aim for the bar to rest on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts on the back of your shoulders and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine). This is the high bar position, which is generally recommended over the low bar position for walking lunges.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your traps will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back and should situate the bar lower on your traps.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
7. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand up in a quarter squat-like manner by extending (straightening) your knees and hips.
8. Walk out with the bar on your back until you have reached the space where you will perform your walking lunges.

### **Setting up an Olympic barbell from the floor**

1. Position an Olympic barbell in the spot where you could begin your walking lunges, and add weight plates of your desired load to the bar.
2. Sit your hips backward while allowing your knees to bend until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. Throughout the set-up, keep your back flat and chest up rather than letting your lower back round or chest collapse.
3. With your elbows straightened, grasp the bar with a mildly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms are directed toward you.
4. If you know how to clean (whether that be a power clean or full clean the bar), clean it into the front rack position and skip to step 8. Otherwise, follow steps 5-7 to deadlift the bar and perform a reverse cheat curl.
5. Drive through your feet, and thrust your hips forward to stand upright with the bar in a deadlift motion.
6. While keeping your elbows straight, sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and your torso to incline slightly forward.
7. As you simultaneously drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body, curl the bar upward in an arcing manner by bending at your elbows while allowing your elbows to swing a bit forward.
    1. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
8. Tuck your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
9. Press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can press the bar overhead with a push press technique, where you use leg drive.
10. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
11. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
12. Lower the bar behind your head onto your upper back muscles slightly below the base of your neck in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Aim for the bar to rest on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts on the back of your shoulders and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine). This is the high bar position, which is generally recommended over the low bar position for walking lunges.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your traps will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back and should situate the bar lower on your traps.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. If you are new to walking lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
2. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. Perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth, continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
3. Progressively extend (straighten) your knees to lift your body up until you can repeat step 1 to step forward with your rear leg past your stationary leg in the same manner that you initially performed with your other side.
    1. Strive to begin stepping forward as you are lunging up in a fluid manner, which will advance your body forward.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat step 2 with your other side now in the forward position, as you lunge down just like you previously did.
5. Continue repeating steps 1-4 as you progressively lunge forward with alternating legs until you have completed your set or have to turn around and continue lunging in the opposite direction.
    1. After finishing the last rep, set the weight down in the opposite manner to how you initially set up.
    2. You can press the weight up overhead, lower it to the front of your shoulders, and then lower it to the front of your thighs, or you can rerack the bar in a rack (if you initially set up in a rack).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate or an aerobic step) that you will use as a step onto the floor in a location that has sufficient space behind it for your split squat.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your stepping surface, but selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point.
2. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright while firmly holding the kettlebells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the kettlebells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
3. In approximately a hip-width stance, stand with the foot that you will first step up onto the step aligned with the middle of your step (which is primarily relevant if you are using a bumper plate to provide the greatest surface area for your foot).
## Exercise technique
1. With the foot aligned with the middle of your step, step up onto the step with the entirety of its sole in contact with the step while leaving your other foot on the ground.
    1. The knee on the side of your elevated foot should be mildly bent once on the step.
    2. Keep your arms by your sides, directed down toward the ground throughout the duration of your set.
2. Shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot before taking a fairly long step backward with your other foot and contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. To shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you bend that side’s knee slightly more.
    2. Once you step back and resume transferring some of your weight into your rear leg, you can return to keeping your torso in an upright position.
    3. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    4. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    5. Keep the entire sole of your elevated foot firmly planted on the step and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    6. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
3. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the step.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
4. While keeping both of your feet planted, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position on top of the step.
    1. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, set the kettlebells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its furthest back position that allows you to begin each rep with the greatest knee bend you can achieve.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the shin pad to a position that places it slightly above the front of your ankles on the lowest aspect of your shins when seated with your legs behind the shin pad with your knees bent.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the shin pad without moving the lever arm in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin and sit down on its seat with your lower shins behind the shin pad, knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point, and back resting against the back pad.
    1. With the appropriate positioning, it should be comfortable for you to have your knees bent.
    2. Your knees are aligned with the machine’s pivot point if you can draw a straight line through the center of both knees to the pivot point.
5. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Through a controlled kicking motion, progressively extend (straighten) your knees to lift up the shin pad until you have fully straightened your knees.
    1. While you do not lift the weight in an intentionally slow manner, avoid kicking up in an uncontrolled, jerky motion.
    2. As your lower legs move through motion at your knees, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set as your thighs remain in contact with the seat.
3. Reverse the movement as you allow your knees to bend progressively in a controlled manner to lower the shin pad until you have returned to the starting position with your knees bent as much as the machine permits.
    1. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower legs down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all four of your quad muscles, including your rectus femoris, which may not be trained as effectively by most squat, leg press, and lunge variations.

### **Training the entirety of your quad**

Exercises that exclusively resist knee extension like machine leg extensions or exercises that chiefly load knee extension while imparting minimal hip extensor loading (like reverse Nordic curls and sissy squats) can serve a distinct role in a training program. A wide array of exercises will train knee extension and hip extension simultaneously such as squats, lunges, step ups, and leg presses. These multi-joint exercises can certainly stimulate meaningful increases in the size and strength of the quads, but they are not ideal for training the rectus femoris, which is the one quad muscle that crosses the hip and functions as a hip flexor along with functioning as a knee extensor. Notably, the rectus femoris does not just weakly assist in flexing the hip, but rather functions as a primary hip flexor that has greater leverage for producing hip flexion torque than any other muscle in the human body at certain joint angles. Because contraction of the rectus femoris can produce a substantial amount of hip flexion torque, its recruitment during a movement that requires both hip and knee extension is less biomechanically efficient than recruitment of the other muscle bellies of the quadriceps. As the rectus femoris contracts more forcefully during a movement like the squat, the hip extensors must work harder to resist the hip flexion torque it produces. Striving to maximize efficiency, the nervous system preferentially recruits the three monoarticular (crossing only the knee joint) muscles of the quad to a greater degree than the rectus femoris during compound exercises where extension simultaneously occurs at the hip and knee joints.

Although some research indicates the rectus femoris does not grow significantly from multi-joint exercises like squats, other studies have reported statistically significant growth in this muscle following such training. When a fairly small magnitude of growth occurs (which is the case even in studies reaching the level of statistical significance), it is unsurprising that the change reaches statistical significance in certain studies but not in others. While the rectus femoris will be trained to a degree during these types of multi-joint exercises, the available body of evidence indicates that the monoarticular quad muscles are much more likely to experience a robust hypertrophy response. Correspondingly, your rectus femoris will almost certainly lag behind your other quad muscles if all of your quad training is performed through multi-joint exercises like squats.

However, during a single joint knee extension exercise where no movement occurs at the hip joint, the rectus femoris is just as active as the monoarticular muscles of the quad, if not more so. This is logical since the hip flexion torque that rectus femoris contraction generates is essentially inconsequential during an exercise that does not simultaneously resist the hip extensor muscles. Congruent with what would be expected, machine seated leg extensions have been demonstrated to produce a considerable degree of hypertrophy of the rectus femoris that is similar to or even more pronounced than that of the three monoarticular quad muscles. While much less studied than machine leg extensions, reverse Nordic curls have been found to yield significant increases in rectus femoris size and the biomechanically similar sissy squat would likely yield a similar result, although it has not been directly studied.

### Modifying the leg extension for greater muscle growth

When performed with a sufficiently high relative intensity and appropriate load, machine leg extensions can grow the rectus femoris regardless of how you position the seat, but the seat position can influence the magnitude to which the muscle may grow. Larsen et al. (2025) assessed changes in the thickness of the rectus femoris and vastus lateralis after untrained participants trained single leg machine leg extensions for 10 weeks. For each participant, one leg was trained with an upright torso positioning the hip into 90 degrees of flexion, while the other leg trained with a recline torso positioning the hip into only 40 degrees of flexion. The two interventions resulted in similar vastus lateralis growth, which is unsurprising since that muscle does not cross the hip joint. However, the rectus femoris experienced significantly greater growth from the leg extensions using a reclined torso position compared to the upright torso position. Because the rectus femoris functions as a hip flexor, it shortens in progressively greater angles of hip flexion, while it lengthens as the hip extends. When you are able to lean your torso back when seated, your hip is in a lower angle of flexion compared to when you are seated upright or leaned forward, so your rectus femoris can operate at a longer muscle length when you lean back. The available body of evidence indicates that biarticular muscles (which cross two joints) are likely to experience greater hypertrophy when trained at long lengths compared to shorter lengths, and the rectus femoris fits this trend along with the hamstrings, calves, and triceps.

If you wish to train your rectus femoris at longer lengths during machine leg extensions, you can intentionally position the back pad as far back as you can to allow you to lean further back at your hips when performing the exercise. With this approach, you would still want to align your knee joint with the machine’s pivot point, so you would position your thighs at the same part of the seat as you would if you had the back pad further forward to maintain an upright torso. When using the reclined technique, your upper back would still rest against the back pad, but your lower back would no longer contact the back pad in contrast to if you positioned the seat further forward. While Larsen et al. (2025) specifically utilized a machine that allowed participants to be meaningfully reclined, many leg extensions are not constructed in a manner that would allow users to set up appropriately with only 40 degrees of hip flexion. Consequently, the degree to which you practically can modify your hip position during a machine leg extension will likely be smaller than what was studied by Larsen et al. (2025). This may potentially result in using a reclined torso position on your leg extension machine having a smaller effect on rectus femoris hypertrophy compared to what was reported by Larsen et al. (2025).## Setting up
1. Stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance in a location that has sufficient space behind you to step backward.
    1. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc.
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
    3. If you have balance limitations, you can situate yourself near a surface (e.g., hand rail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
    4. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, take a fairly long step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Slightly bend the knee on the side of your stationary foot while doing so.
    2. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    3. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
3. While keeping both of your feet planted on the ground, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate, aerobic step, or plyometric box) that you will use as a step onto the floor or approach the bottom step of a staircase.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your step, and the difficulty of the exercise will increase as the height increases.
    2. If you are new to lateral step-downs, selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point, and you can subsequently adjust the height based on how challenging this height is for you.
    3. For reference, a step of a typical staircase is normally near seven inches (18cm) in height.
    4. If you have balance limitations, you can situate your step near a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
    5. When feasible, setting up where you can face a mirror is recommended to be able to receive visual feedback about your technique.
2. Stand directly to the side of the step, and place one foot fully onto the step while leaving your other foot on the ground. Situate your feet about hip-width apart, and point your toes straight ahead.
    1. The entire sole (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) of your elevated foot should be firmly planted on the step throughout your set.
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep your hands in your preferred position, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, crossed in front of your chest, or outstretched in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive through your elevated leg and straighten your knee to step up until your feet are level, keeping your non-working leg’s foot hanging off the step.
    1. Finish fully upright with the tops of your hip bones and shoulders level, while you bear all your bodyweight through the foot on the step.
2. On the side hovering off the step, pull your toes and the ball of your foot up toward your shin through an ankle motion that brings your heel closer to the ground than the ball of your foot.
    1. This is the opposite of the ankle motion that occurs when lifting your heels up during a calf raise, and you should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract.
    2. Throughout your set, maintain this foot position and the knee of your non-working side straight.
    3. Now you are in the starting position for your first rep.
3. Sit your buttocks back to initiate the descent. Then progressively bend your working leg’s knee and hip in a controlled manner until you can gently tap the ground with the heel of your other foot.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hip to maintain your balance, but keep your back flat and chest up. When starting to descend, you can think about beginning to sit on a chair, which cues the intended hip motion that loads your glute.
    2. Keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down. Imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    3. While letting your working leg’s knee naturally track forward, strive to keep it aligned with your middle toes, rather than letting it inward relative to your foot (which you can check in a mirror, along with how level your hip bones are). Think about driving your knees out to the side if it starts drifting inward.
    4. Keep your elevated heel firmly planted on the step, along with the rest of your sole.
    5. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your free leg’s heel, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram). Lowering yourself slowly can help achieve this very light tap at the bottom.
4. Reverse the movement by driving through your elevated foot to step up into a fully upright position.
    1. By extending (straightening) your knee and hip, lift your body up completely through your working leg, as you avoid pushing off the ground with your other side’s foot.
    2. Continue keeping your hip bones level and your working leg’s knee aligned with your middle toes.
    3. Finish your free foot hovering in the air, not in contact with the step.
5. Alternate steps 3 and 4 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side on the step.
    1. Since each rep starts with the lowering phase, completion of step 2 does not constitute a full rep.
## Description
While training your quads, glutes, and adductor magnus, this exercise can improve your single leg stability.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. You can alternatively leave the machine initially unloaded if you are new to this exercise and have yet to determine which belt attachment points are best for you.
2. Connect the belt to the machine’s carabiner at the belt’s attachment points that will allow you to squat through your full range of motion.
    1. This exercise’s potential range of motion and ease of setting up can be determined by the specific attachment point you select.
    2. While you may have to switch to different ones later on, you can initially try the attachment points closest to the middle of the belt, since using them will allow you to squat through the longest range of motion.
    3. Trying out different attachment points may be required because you have to continue setting up to determine if the pair you selected is ideal for you as an individual.
    4. If you select attachment points too close to the ends of the belt, the end of the cable that is linked to the belt will bottom out prematurely when squatting down, which will restrict you to performing partial range of motion squats.
    5. The con of using attachment points close to the middle of the belt is that they can make it more difficult to get into the starting position. Consequently, you ideally do not want to select attachments that are any closer toward the middle of the belt than what is needed for you to squat through a full range of motion.
3. Move the belt toward the front of the machine (away from the weights) on the ground.
4. Step both feet into the belt, and situate it around your hips while squatting down as needed to move the belt into position.
    1. The back of the belt should be securely resting on top of your buttocks in a comfortable position.
    2. Avoid pulling the cable to the extent that you have to work against the machine’s resistance when moving the belt.
    3. Rather than standing on the platform, it can often be easier to get the belt into position while standing on the ground, particularly with machines that have a platform that is noticeably elevated above the ground. However, it may be easier to get the belt around your hips while standing on the platform with certain machines, so you can try that approach if you initially have a difficult time setting the belt around your hips while standing on the ground.
    4. If you are unable to get the belt around your hips regardless of whether you are standing on the ground or platform, you may have to switch to belt attachment points closer toward the ends of the belt.
5. Step up onto the center of the platform with the cable pulling directly downward (rather than being angled forward or backward) between the middle of your feet.
    1. When stepping up, you can hold the machine's uprights for balance assistance if they are within your reach.
6. Position your feet reasonably near shoulder width apart with your toes pointing somewhat out to the sides and the soles of your feet fully on the platform.
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up close to this stance allows them to belt squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when belt squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Using a stance that is mildly wider than your typical squat stance may sometimes reduce discomfort from the pressure the belt can exert on the groin.
7. Raise your arms and grasp the handle with your hands near shoulder width apart.
8. While standing upright, pull the handle back toward you to disengage the safety arm.
    1. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner.
    1. Lightly use the handles to help you feel stable with a mostly upright torso while allowing your torso naturally to lean mildly forward at your hips to maintain your balance.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your entire set.
    3. Allow your knees to track forward to load your quads and help you maintain your balance.
    4. Aim to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. As you would during other squats, strive to keep your chest up and lower back flat rather than meaningfully rounding your back.
3. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion that is comfortably enabled by your mobility and your machine.
    1. When setting up appropriately and following the technique points listed under step 2, people can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground. However, what constitutes a full range of motion for a belt squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your heels lift off the platform or knees cave inward at the bottom, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
4. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the platform to extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Avoid pulling down on the handle with your arms, as you simply hold the handle for stability rather than using your upper body to help your legs.
    2. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, knees stacked over your feet, chest up, and lower back mostly flat.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
6. Once you are upright after your last rep, push the handle forward to engage the safety arm before squatting down to return the weight onto the safety arm.
## Description
While also training your gluteus maximus and multiple hip adductor muscles, this exercise primarily targets your quads with minimal spinal loading.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
3. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should be securely resting on the muscles of your upper back when squeezing your shoulder blades together.
    2. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    5. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when squatting.
6. Repeat steps 2 and 3 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in an intentionally slow and controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Aim for your descent to take a few seconds as you actively control every moment of the lowering phase with a tempo that is clearly slower than your standard squat.
    2. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    3. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Slowly continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. If you wish to incorporate a pause into your tempo back squat, pause in the bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., about two seconds) while staying tight.
    1. A pause is typically included in a tempo back squat, but it is not required.
    2. When using a pause, avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
7. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Strive to perform the lifting phase explosively with quality technique.
    2. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
8. Alternate between steps 4-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
9. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. Other than setting up the bands, you can use essentially the same setup and technique for this variation as you would for a standard back squat.
    2. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    3. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    4. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of squat reps through the full range of motion your mobility allows, without rounding your back or lifting your heels off the ground
    1. Use the technique for a standard barbell back squat to help you determine the appropriate height of the safety pins.
3. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when squatting.
4. On each side of the bar, securely anchor a long elastic band and loop it around the bar.
    1. Ultimately, you want to ensure that whatever you use as anchor points will not move during your set. Your anchor points should have a smooth surface that will not tear the band.
    2. The appropriate setup should keep the bands taut at the bottom of each rep, and the two bands should be stretched to the same degree to provide equal resistance. If the bands become slack at any point during your reps, you did not stretch them sufficiently in the starting position.
    3. Once positioned, the bands should be flat rather than having any twists, and each band should be pulling vertically downward (from the position in which you will be after walking out with the bar) rather than being angled to one side.
    4. If you have access to band pegs, anchoring the bands to the pegs and looping them around each side of the bar will be the ideal method of setting up.
    5. Without band pegs, you can alternatively anchor the band around the bottom frames of your rack if your rack has the appropriate design to do so.
    6. If the two aforementioned options are not available to you, you have the option of anchoring the bands to heavy dumbbells on each end of the bar. This method is less stable and less practical for high-tension bands. It can help to select the heaviest dumbbells you can and place a pair of weight plates on the sides of each dumbbell to reduce the likelihood that they will roll.
    7. Prior to using them, it is recommended that you inspect the bands to confirm they do not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
5. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
6. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should be securely resting on the muscles of your upper back when squeezing your shoulder blades together.
    2. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    5. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
    2. As you lift the bar higher up, the band tension will progressively increase.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its furthest back position that allows you to begin each rep with the greatest knee bend you can achieve.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the shin pad to a position that places it slightly above the front of your ankles on the lowest aspect of your shins when seated with your legs behind the shin pad with your knees bent.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the shin pad without moving the lever arm in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. Add weight plates to the machine, and sit down on its seat with your lower shins behind the shin pad, knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point, and back resting against the back pad.
    1. With the appropriate positioning, it should be comfortable for you to have your knees bent.
    2. Your knees are aligned with the machine’s pivot point if you can draw a straight line through the center of both knees to the pivot point.
5. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Through a controlled kicking motion, progressively extend (straighten) your knees to lift up the shin pad until you have fully straightened your knees.
    1. While you do not lift the weight in an intentionally slow manner, avoid kicking up in an uncontrolled, jerky motion.
    2. As your lower legs move through motion at your knees, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set as your thighs remain in contact with the seat.
3. Reverse the movement as you allow your knees to bend progressively in a controlled manner to lower the shin pad until you have returned to the starting position with your knees bent as much as the machine permits.
    1. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower legs down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all four of your quad muscles, including your rectus femoris, which may not be trained as effectively by most squat, leg press, and lunge variations.

### **Training the entirety of your quad**

Exercises that exclusively resist knee extension like machine leg extensions or exercises that chiefly load knee extension while imparting minimal hip extensor loading (like reverse Nordic curls and sissy squats) can serve a distinct role in a training program. A wide array of exercises will train knee extension and hip extension simultaneously such as squats, lunges, step ups, and leg presses. These multi-joint exercises can certainly stimulate meaningful increases in the size and strength of the quads, but they are not ideal for training the rectus femoris, which is the one quad muscle that crosses the hip and functions as a hip flexor along with functioning as a knee extensor. Notably, the rectus femoris does not just weakly assist in flexing the hip, but rather functions as a primary hip flexor that has greater leverage for producing hip flexion torque than any other muscle in the human body at certain joint angles. Because contraction of the rectus femoris can produce a substantial amount of hip flexion torque, its recruitment during a movement that requires both hip and knee extension is less biomechanically efficient than recruitment of the other muscle bellies of the quadriceps. As the rectus femoris contracts more forcefully during a movement like the squat, the hip extensors must work harder to resist the hip flexion torque it produces. Striving to maximize efficiency, the nervous system preferentially recruits the three monoarticular (crossing only the knee joint) muscles of the quad to a greater degree than the rectus femoris during compound exercises where extension simultaneously occurs at the hip and knee joints.

Although some research indicates the rectus femoris does not grow significantly from multi-joint exercises like squats, other studies have reported statistically significant growth in this muscle following such training. When a fairly small magnitude of growth occurs (which is the case even in studies reaching the level of statistical significance), it is unsurprising that the change reaches statistical significance in certain studies but not in others. While the rectus femoris will be trained to a degree during these types of multi-joint exercises, the available body of evidence indicates that the monoarticular quad muscles are much more likely to experience a robust hypertrophy response. Correspondingly, your rectus femoris will almost certainly lag behind your other quad muscles if all of your quad training is performed through multi-joint exercises like squats.

However, during a single joint knee extension exercise where no movement occurs at the hip joint, the rectus femoris is just as active as the monoarticular muscles of the quad, if not more so. This is logical since the hip flexion torque that rectus femoris contraction generates is essentially inconsequential during an exercise that does not simultaneously resist the hip extensor muscles. Congruent with what would be expected, machine seated leg extensions have been demonstrated to produce a considerable degree of hypertrophy of the rectus femoris that is similar to or even more pronounced than that of the three monoarticular quad muscles. While much less studied than machine leg extensions, reverse Nordic curls have been found to yield significant increases in rectus femoris size and the biomechanically similar sissy squat would likely yield a similar result, although it has not been directly studied.

### **Modifying the leg extension for greater muscle growth**

When performed with a sufficiently high relative intensity and appropriate load, machine leg extensions can grow the rectus femoris regardless of how you position the seat, but the seat position can influence the magnitude to which the muscle may grow. Larsen et al. (2025) assessed changes in the thickness of the rectus femoris and vastus lateralis after untrained participants trained single leg machine leg extensions for 10 weeks. For each participant, one leg was trained with an upright torso positioning the hip into 90 degrees of flexion, while the other leg trained with a recline torso positioning the hip into only 40 degrees of flexion. The two interventions resulted in similar vastus lateralis growth, which is unsurprising since that muscle does not cross the hip joint. However, the rectus femoris experienced significantly greater growth from the leg extensions using a reclined torso position compared to the upright torso position. Because the rectus femoris functions as a hip flexor, it shortens in progressively greater angles of hip flexion, while it lengthens as the hip extends. When you are able to lean your torso back when seated, your hip is in a lower angle of flexion compared to when you are seated upright or leaned forward, so your rectus femoris can operate at a longer muscle length when you lean back. The available body of evidence indicates that biarticular muscles (which cross two joints) are likely to experience greater hypertrophy when trained at long lengths compared to shorter lengths, and the rectus femoris fits this trend along with the hamstrings, calves, and triceps.

If you wish to train your rectus femoris at longer lengths during machine leg extensions, you can intentionally position the back pad as far back as you can to allow you to lean further back at your hips when performing the exercise. With this approach, you would still want to align your knee joint with the machine’s pivot point, so you would position your thighs at the same part of the seat as you would if you had the back pad further forward to maintain an upright torso. When using the reclined technique, your upper back would still rest against the back pad, but your lower back would no longer contact the back pad in contrast to if you positioned the seat further forward. While Larsen et al. (2025) specifically utilized a machine that allowed participants to be meaningfully reclined, many leg extensions are not constructed in a manner that would allow users to set up appropriately with only 40 degrees of hip flexion. Consequently, the degree to which you practically can modify your hip position during a machine leg extension will likely be smaller than what was studied by Larsen et al. (2025). This may potentially result in using a reclined torso position on your leg extension machine having a smaller effect on rectus femoris hypertrophy compared to what was reported by Larsen et al. (2025).## Setting up
1. Adjust the J-hooks of a power rack or squat stand to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on the J-hooks.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
2. Set up the sturdy object near knee height that you will use to elevate your rear foot sufficiently behind the bar.
    1. A cylindrical split squat roller pad is ideal to use if you have one, but flat exercise benches are most commonly used due to their availability, and a plyometric box with a somewhat compliant surface can also be viable. Objects with a flat hard surface (like a metal plyometric box or aerobic step elevated on risers) can be used to elevate the foot, but they can feel uncomfortable, so they are not the best options.
    2. With respect to placement, you could place the object about two “foot lengths” behind where your heels will be after you unrack the bar and take a couple of steps back away from the J-hooks. When initially walking back with the bar, the goal is to move far enough away that the bar will not risk bumping the J-hooks when your torso leans mildly forward during the exercise. From this position, step backward with one foot until its big toe is just behind your other foot’s heel before doing the same with your opposite foot. You can situate the front of the elevated object where the back of your rear foot’s heel is situated after those steps. While this strategy can consistently situate most people in a viable starting position for the exercise, a range of foot placements can be effectively used, including having your front foot two and a half “foot lengths” in front of where you started (which may be even more comfortable for some people).
3. Face the center of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
4. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back muscles, slightly below the base of your neck, in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Aim for the bar to rest on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts on the back of your shoulders and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine). This is the high bar position, which is generally recommended over the low bar position for split squats and lunges.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your traps will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    5. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    6. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. Additionally, use these initial reps to check if you have situated your elevated object an appropriate distance behind the bar. The right distance will typically allow your rear knee to tap the ground lightly at the bottom of the rep while keeping your front foot’s heel fully on the ground.
    2. If the object is excessively far behind you, your range of motion may be limited by your rear leg’s hip flexor flexibility, which would result in a deep stretching sensation in the front of your rear leg’s upper thigh before your rear knee reaches the ground.
    3. If the object is not far enough behind you, you may feel a blocked sensation in your front ankle at the bottom before your rear knee reaches the ground, indicating that you have reached the limit of your front ankle’s mobility from your knee having to track far forward. To descend any further would require lifting up your front foot’s heel off the ground in this situation.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase.
    1. While not required, setting up safety pins is recommended in case you lose your balance or fail a rep during your work sets.
7. Repeat steps 3 and 4 of the setup and proceed to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. While keeping your chest up, take a couple of steps backward and position your feet approximately hip-width apart, with your toes pointing straight ahead.
    1. Strive to step back far enough that your heels are now about “two foot lengths” in front of the object behind you.
3. Shift your body weight into one foot (that will be your front foot) before placing the top surface (on the side of your shoelaces) of your other foot on top of the elevated surface with that side’s knee bent.
    1. Strive to move your rear foot backward in a straight line to keep your feet approximately hip-width apart, rather than bringing your rear foot closer toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    2. Throughout the set, keep the entire sole of your front foot firmly planted on the ground and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    3. If you are using a cylindrical object to elevate your foot, you can contact the surface with the front of your ankle along with your foot, but only your foot will contact the elevated surface if it is flat, like a bench.
4. From your split stance position, bend your knees and hips to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Rather than letting your back round, keep your back flat and chest up throughout the exercise.
    2. You should keep your torso mostly upright, but you can allow your torso to lean forward mildly if that feels natural to you.
    3. Continue keeping the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground throughout the rep.
    4. Keep your knees tracking forward in line with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
5. Continue descending through the full range of motion you can while keeping your front foot’s sole fully in contact with the ground and your back flat.
    1. With the right set-up, this commonly involves lightly tapping the ground with the rear knee in the bottom position. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    2. Alternatively, some people may notice that their front leg’s hip reaches the end of its mobility before the rear knee can tap the ground, which will produce a notable stretching sensation in the front leg’s glute.
    3. What chiefly matters is moving through your individual full range of motion with high-quality technique.
    4. Regardless of where you end the descent, you should strive to move through the full range of motion you can with high-quality technique.
6. While keeping both of your feet planted, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees and hips to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso mostly upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
7. Repeat steps 4-6 until you have finished your set. Then lift your rear foot off the object and step forward to position your feet symmetrically.
    1. Now you have the option of proceeding directly into your next set with your other leg in the forward position, or you can rerack the bar and rest before doing so.
    2. When you are ready to rerack the bar, walk forward until it contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
3. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should be securely resting on the muscles of your upper back when squeezing your shoulder blades together.
    2. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    5. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when squatting.
6. Repeat steps 2 and 3 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. Once you reach the bottom of your squat, pause in that position, remaining completely stationary for a brief period (e.g., about two seconds) while staying tight.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
7. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
8. Alternate between steps 4-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
9. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. Which features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its furthest back position that allows you to begin each rep with the greatest knee bend you can achieve.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the shin pad to a position that places it slightly above the front of your ankles on the lowest aspect of your shins when seated with your legs behind the shin pad with your knees bent.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the shin pad without moving the lever arm in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin and sit down on its seat with your lower shins behind the shin pad, knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point, and back resting against the back pad.
    1. With the appropriate positioning, it should be comfortable for you to have your knees bent.
    2. Your knees are aligned with the machine’s pivot point if you can draw a straight line through the center of both knees to the pivot point.
5. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Through a controlled kicking motion, progressively extend (straighten) one knee to lift up the shin pad until you have fully straightened your knee while you keep your other knee bent.
    1. While you should not lift the weight in an intentionally slow manner, avoid kicking up in an uncontrolled, jerky motion.
    2. As your lower leg moves through motion at your knee, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set as your thighs remain in contact with the seat.
3. Reverse the movement as you allow your working leg’s knee to bend progressively in a controlled manner to lower the leg pad until you have returned to the starting position with your knee bent as much as the machine permits.
    1. Keep your resting leg’s knee bent such that this leg does not touch the lower leg pad until you have finished lowering it with your other leg.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower leg down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all four of your quad muscles, including your rectus femoris, which may not be trained as effectively by most squat, leg press, and lunge variations.

### **Training the entirety of your quad**

Exercises that exclusively resist knee extension like machine leg extensions or exercises that chiefly load knee extension while imparting minimal hip extensor loading (like reverse Nordic curls and sissy squats) can serve a distinct role in a training program. A wide array of exercises will train knee extension and hip extension simultaneously such as squats, lunges, step ups, and leg presses. These multi-joint exercises can certainly stimulate meaningful increases in the size and strength of the quads, but they are not ideal for training the rectus femoris, which is the one quad muscle that crosses the hip and functions as a hip flexor along with functioning as a knee extensor. Notably, the rectus femoris does not just weakly assist in flexing the hip, but rather functions as a primary hip flexor that has greater leverage for producing hip flexion torque than any other muscle in the human body at certain joint angles. Because contraction of the rectus femoris can produce a substantial amount of hip flexion torque, its recruitment during a movement that requires both hip and knee extension is less biomechanically efficient than recruitment of the other muscle bellies of the quadriceps. As the rectus femoris contracts more forcefully during a movement like the squat, the hip extensors must work harder to resist the hip flexion torque it produces. Striving to maximize efficiency, the nervous system preferentially recruits the three monoarticular (crossing only the knee joint) muscles of the quad to a greater degree than the rectus femoris during compound exercises where extension simultaneously occurs at the hip and knee joints.

Although some research indicates the rectus femoris does not grow significantly from multi-joint exercises like squats, other studies have reported statistically significant growth in this muscle following such training. When a fairly small magnitude of growth occurs (which is the case even in studies reaching the level of statistical significance), it is unsurprising that the change reaches statistical significance in certain studies but not in others. While the rectus femoris will be trained to a degree during these types of multi-joint exercises, the available body of evidence indicates that the monoarticular quad muscles are much more likely to experience a robust hypertrophy response. Correspondingly, your rectus femoris will almost certainly lag behind your other quad muscles if all of your quad training is performed through multi-joint exercises like squats.

However, during a single joint knee extension exercise where no movement occurs at the hip joint, the rectus femoris is just as active as the monoarticular muscles of the quad, if not more so. This is logical since the hip flexion torque that rectus femoris contraction generates is essentially inconsequential during an exercise that does not simultaneously resist the hip extensor muscles. Congruent with what would be expected, machine seated leg extensions have been demonstrated to produce a considerable degree of hypertrophy of the rectus femoris that is similar to or even more pronounced than that of the three monoarticular quad muscles. While much less studied than machine leg extensions, reverse Nordic curls have been found to yield significant increases in rectus femoris size and the biomechanically similar sissy squat would likely yield a similar result, although it has not been directly studied.

### **Modifying the leg extension for greater muscle growth**

When performed with a sufficiently high relative intensity and appropriate load, machine leg extensions can grow the rectus femoris regardless of how you position the seat, but the seat position can influence the magnitude to which the muscle may grow. Larsen et al. (2025) assessed changes in the thickness of the rectus femoris and vastus lateralis after untrained participants trained single leg machine leg extensions for 10 weeks. For each participant, one leg was trained with an upright torso positioning the hip into 90 degrees of flexion, while the other leg trained with a recline torso positioning the hip into only 40 degrees of flexion. The two interventions resulted in similar vastus lateralis growth, which is unsurprising since that muscle does not cross the hip joint. However, the rectus femoris experienced significantly greater growth from the leg extensions using a reclined torso position compared to the upright torso position. Because the rectus femoris functions as a hip flexor, it shortens in progressively greater angles of hip flexion, while it lengthens as the hip extends. When you are able to lean your torso back when seated, your hip is in a lower angle of flexion compared to when you are seated upright or leaned forward, so your rectus femoris can operate at a longer muscle length when you lean back. The available body of evidence indicates that biarticular muscles (which cross two joints) are likely to experience greater hypertrophy when trained at long lengths compared to shorter lengths, and the rectus femoris fits this trend along with the hamstrings, calves, and triceps.

If you wish to train your rectus femoris at longer lengths during machine leg extensions, you can intentionally position the back pad as far back as you can to allow you to lean further back at your hips when performing the exercise. With this approach, you would still want to align your knee joint with the machine’s pivot point, so you would position your thighs at the same part of the seat as you would if you had the back pad further forward to maintain an upright torso. When using the reclined technique, your upper back would still rest against the back pad, but your lower back would no longer contact the back pad in contrast to if you positioned the seat further forward. While Larsen et al. (2025) specifically utilized a machine that allowed participants to be meaningfully reclined, many leg extensions are not constructed in a manner that would allow users to set up appropriately with only 40 degrees of hip flexion. Consequently, the degree to which you practically can modify your hip position during a machine leg extension will likely be smaller than what was studied by Larsen et al. (2025). This may potentially result in using a reclined torso position on your leg extension machine having a smaller effect on rectus femoris hypertrophy compared to what was reported by Larsen et al. (2025).## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate, aerobic step, or plyometric box) that you will use as a step onto the floor.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your step, and the difficulty of the exercise will increase as the height increases.
    2. If you are new to weighted forward step-ups, selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point, and you can subsequently adjust the height based on how challenging this height is for you.
    3. Alternatively, you can perform this exercise on the lowest step of a staircase, which is normally near seven inches (18cm) in height.
    4. When feasible, setting up where you can face a mirror is recommended to be able to receive visual feedback about your technique.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
3. Face the step with your feet approximately hip width apart from each other, as your toes are pointing straight ahead.
    1. You will continue holding the dumbbells by your sides throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift your other foot up to place its sole fully in contact with the step.
    1. Make sure that your lead leg’s heel is not hanging off the step.
2. Shift your weight into your lead foot as you mildly lean your torso forward from hinging at your hip.
    1. The forward torso should occur from you moving about your hip while you keep your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    2. In this starting position for the exercise, your rear leg’s knee should be straight.
3. Without pushing off the ground through your rear foot, drive through your lead leg as you push down into the step by extending (straightening) your knee and hip to lift yourself upward until you can place your rear foot onto the step.
    1. Your lead leg’s knee should be straight, and your torso should be upright by the time you place your other foot onto the step.
    2. Aim to lift yourself exclusively through your lead leg as you refrain from performing a calf raise-like motion to push off with your rear leg.
    3. Keep the entire sole (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) of your elevated foot firmly planted on the step and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your rear leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
4. While you are bearing your weight into the foot that started on the step, lift your other foot up and back off the step as you pull that foot’s toes upward through an ankle motion that results in your heel being closer to the ground than the ball of your foot.
    1. This is the opposite of the ankle motion that occurs when lifting your heels up during a calf raise.
5. With your leg that is on the step, sit your buttocks backward to initiate the descent, and bend your knee to lower yourself in a controlled manner until you can contact the ground with the heel of your other foot.
    1. As you sit your buttocks back (like if you started to sit on a chair), allow your torso naturally to lean mildly forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid rounding your back excessively.
    2. Continue keeping your working leg’s knee stacked over your foot, the sole of your foot fully on the step, and your hip bones level.
    3. While the descent does not have to be extremely slow, your lower body muscles should be actively lowering your body rather than gravity simply plopping you down onto the ground.
6. Repeat steps 3-5 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which foot starts elevated on top of the step.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate, aerobic step, or plyometric box) that you will use as a step onto the floor or approach the bottom step of a staircase.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your step, and the difficulty of the exercise will increase as the height increases.
    2. If you are new to forward step-downs, selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point, and you can subsequently adjust the height based on how challenging this height is for you.
    3. For reference, a step of a typical staircase is normally near seven inches (18cm) in height.
    4. If you have balance limitations, you can situate your step near a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance while performing this exercise.
    5. When feasible, setting up where you can face a mirror is recommended to be able to receive visual feedback about your technique.
2. Step up onto the step with both feet and position them approximately hip-width apart from each other, with your toes pointing straight ahead, as the soles of your feet are fully on the step, with your toes right by the edge of the step.
3. Raise your arms out in front of you until they are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. You will keep your arms outstretched in this position throughout your set to provide a counterbalance that can assist in keeping your torso upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight into one foot and lift your other foot up and forward so that it is hanging over the edge of the step.
2. On the side hovering off the step, pull your toes and the ball of your foot upward through an ankle motion that results in your heel being closer to the ground than the ball of your foot.
    1. This is the opposite of the ankle motion that occurs when lifting your heels up during a calf raise.
    2. You will keep your foot in this position and that side’s knee straight throughout your set.
    3. Now you are in the starting position for your first rep.
3. With your leg that is on the step, bend your knee as you drive your knee forward over your foot while keeping your torso upright to lower yourself in a controlled manner until you can gently tap the ground with the heel of your other foot.
    1. Keep the entire sole (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) of your elevated foot firmly planted on the step and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down.
    2. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    3. Keep your working leg’s knee aligned with your foot rather than letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot (which you can check in a mirror, along with how level your hip bones are).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your free leg’s heel, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
4. Reverse the movement by driving through your elevated foot as you straighten your knee to lift yourself up until you have returned to the starting position with your knee fully straightened.
    1. During the ascent, continue keeping your working leg’s knee stacked over your foot, the sole of your foot fully on the step, hip bones level, and torso upright.
    2. While you lift yourself up through your elevated leg, avoid pushing off the ground with your foot.
5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which foot is elevated on top of the step.
## Description
While biasing your quads to a greater degree than your gluteus maximus, this exercise can help train your dynamic single leg stability.## Setting up
1. Stand upright with your back, pelvis (hip bone), and heels against a wall.
    1. Continue keeping your back and pelvis against the wall throughout the rest of your setup and while you perform your wall squat.
2. Step forward with one foot until its heel is just in front of your other foot’s big toe before doing the same with your opposite foot.
3. Step forward with your rear foot to position it symmetrically with your front foot, resulting in your feet now being about two “foot lengths” in front of the wall.
    1. While this strategy can consistently situate most people in a viable starting position for the exercise, somewhat of a range of foot placements can be effectively used for this exercise.
4. Situate your feet near shoulder width apart from each other and position your toes so that they are either facing straight ahead or rotated somewhat out based on what feels comfortable to you.
    1. So long as you do not place them on your legs, you can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides or crossed on your shoulders.
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
    3. After finishing adjusting your foot position, your knees should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend at your knees and hips to squat down as you slide your back down the wall in a controlled manner until your thighs are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. If you wish to prioritize training your quads, focus on transmitting pressure to the wall through your pelvis while minimizing how much pressure you transmit into the wall through your upper back.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot.
    3. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground as you continue keeping your back and pelvis in contact with the wall.
2. Statically maintain this wall squat position until you have finished your set and are ready to squat up by extending (straightening) your knees and hips until you have returned to the starting position.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads with relatively low loading of your gluteus maximus and hip adductors, in contrast to most squat variations.## Setting up
1. Determine what you hold with your hands for balance assistance during your sissy squat.
    1. So long as what you use for assistance is sturdy and will be within your reach to hold throughout the exercise, you have multiple options for what you can select.
    2. With two hands, you can hold objects such as a Smith machine bar locked in place near chest height, an elastic band anchored near a similar level, a door frame, or the posts of a power rack.
    3. If you feel that you only require one hand for assistance, you can also use a variety of objects such as a bedpost, couch, dumbbell rack, incline exercise bench, part of an exercise machine, etc.
2. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead, as you are close to what you hold onto to provide assistance with your arms. Simultaneously grasp that object with one or two hands.
    1. Ensure that there is sufficient room for your knee to track forward during the exercise and that you will be able to continue holding the object at the bottom of your rep, along with the top of it.
    2. If you use an object that you will hold with both hands, position yourself a little behind the object.
    3. If you use an object that you will hold with only one hand, you can position yourself to the side of the object.
    4. Specifically how you should hold the object will be dependent on what you selected
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Keep your glutes squeezed and your core braced throughout the set to help you maintain a rigid trunk position.
2. In a controlled manner, push your knees forward while leaning your torso backward. While bending your knees and keeping your thighs aligned with your torso, bear your weight through the balls of your feet while allowing your heels to lift off the ground.
    1. Throughout the exercise, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side, indicating that you are maintaining an appropriately rigid trunk position without moving your hips.
    2. Rather than letting your lower back excessively arch, strive to keep your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright. If you notice your lower back starting to arch beyond this point, it can help to emphasize further bracing your core and squeezing your glutes.
    3. While an extremely slow descent is unnecessary, using a fairly slow tempo is generally recommended to help you focus on achieving high-quality technique through the range of motion that feels right to your knees.
3. Continue slowly descending with step 2’s technique through the full range of motion that is both comfortable to your knees and permitted by your current level of strength and mobility.
    1. The greatest possible range of motion for this exercise involves fully bending the knee to the point where the hamstrings contact the calves, and the knees are able to tap the ground gently. Most individuals are not able to achieve this maximal depth if they are new to this exercise and can consequently move through an abbreviated range of motion.
    2. Prioritize moving through a range that does not produce knee discomfort. This exercise is not inherently dangerous to the knees, but it can meaningfully load the knees, especially in deeper degrees of knee bend. This can increase the risk of experiencing knee discomfort if you immediately move through a long range of motion without progressively building up to that point by training incrementally greater ranges of motion within your comfort zone.
    3. Only descend through the range in which you can avoid bending your hips. For some individuals, the range of motion will be limited by the flexibility of one of the quad muscles, which will be demonstrated by feeling a deep stretching sensation on the front of your hip and thigh.
4. Reverse the movement by straightening your knees to lift your thighs and torso as one unit into an upright position.
    1. Continue keeping your hips straightened so that you can still draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. Your knees should track backward until they finish vertically stacked under your hips, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. Allow your heels to return to the ground naturally.
    4. Aim to use your hands merely for balance assistance rather than pulling yourself up through your arms.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all four of your quad muscles, including the rectus femoris, which may not be trained as effectively by most squat, leg press, and lunge variations.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate, aerobic step, or plyometric box) that you will use as a step onto the floor.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your step, and the difficulty of the exercise will increase as the height increases.
    2. If you are new to weighted lateral step-downs, selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point, and you can subsequently adjust the height based on how challenging this height is for you.
    3. Alternatively, you can perform this exercise on the lowest step of a staircase, which is normally near seven inches (18cm) in height.
    4. When feasible, setting up where you can face a mirror is recommended to be able to receive visual feedback about your technique.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
3. Step up onto the step with your feet hip-width apart, toes pointing straight ahead, and heels right by the edge.
    1. Your soles should be fully on the step.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot, then lift your other foot up and back so it hangs over the edge of the step.
    1. Continue holding the dumbbells by your sides throughout your set.
    2. Naturally, the side of your body to which you shift your weight will be the working side during this set.
2. On the side hovering off the step, pull your toes and the ball of your foot up toward your shin through an ankle motion that brings your heel closer to the ground than the ball of your foot.
    1. This is the opposite of the ankle motion that occurs when lifting your heels up during a calf raise, and you should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract.
    2. Throughout your set, maintain this foot position and the knee of your non-working side straight.
    3. You are now in the starting position for your first rep.
3. Sit your buttocks back to initiate the descent. Then progressively bend your working leg’s knee and hip in a controlled manner until you can gently tap the ground with the heel of your other foot.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hip to maintain your balance, but keep your back flat and chest up. When starting to descend, you can think about beginning to sit on a chair, which cues the intended hip motion that loads your glute.
    2. Keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down. Imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    3. While letting your working leg’s knee naturally track forward, strive to keep it aligned with your middle toes, rather than letting it inward relative to your foot (which you can check in a mirror, along with how level your hip bones are). Think about driving your knees out to the side if it starts drifting inward.
    4. Keep your elevated heel firmly planted on the step, along with the rest of your sole.
    5. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your free leg’s heel, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram). Lowering yourself slowly can help achieve this very light tap at the bottom.
4. Reverse the movement by driving through your elevated foot to step up into an upright position.
    1. By extending (straightening) your knee and hip, lift your body up completely through your working leg, as you avoid pushing off the ground with your other side’s foot.
    2. Continue keeping your hip bones level and your working leg’s knee aligned with your middle toes.
5. Alternate steps 3 and 4 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side on the step.
    1. Based on your preference, you can keep your non-working side’s foot hovering in the air between reps, or briefly place it on the step before lifting it off for the next rep. The latter approach can be useful if you feel that you need to reset your balance between reps, but either option is valid.
## Description
While training your quads, glutes, and adductor magnus, this exercise can improve your single leg stability.## Setting up
1. Stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance in a location that has sufficient space behind you for lunging backward.
    1. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc.
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
    3. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    7. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step backward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step forward with your rear leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging backward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Position a sturdy box of your desired height centered within the rack, a couple of step lengths behind the J-hooks.
    1. To train your lower body muscles most effectively, you want to pick a box height that enables you to squat near the depth of what is your full range of motion back squat (i.e., the lowest point at which you can squat with quality technique while keeping your back flat and feet fully on the floor). This depth will vary from person to person, but people can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground.
    2. A squat box or plyometric box is an ideal choice for your box. You can position either type of box with one of its corners directed to the middle point between the J-hooks to give you the greatest freedom in how you situate your feet when box squatting.
    3. Plyometric boxes are often available in gyms at heights of 12in (30cm), 18in (46cm), and 24in (61cm). Box squatting with an 18in (46cm) box, which is near the typical height of a flat exercise bench, restricts the range of motion for most people who are not clearly taller than six feet (183cm). If you currently do not have the mobility to squat down to a 12in (30cm) box with proper form, you can increase the height of the box by placing a thick bumper plate on top of the box to elevate it.
    4. While others have fixed heights like plyometric boxes, the height of certain squat box models can be adjusted to accommodate how deeply you wish to box squat.
    5. If you do not have access to a squat box or plyometric box, you can use an aerobic step provided that the step is rated to withstand at least half the combined load of your bodyweight and the bar weight. You can adjust its height based on how many pairs of risers you stack underneath it.
    6. Alternatively, you may be able to use a flat exercise bench as your box. A bench is excessively tall for many lifters to squat through a full range of motion, but it can be suitable for rather tall individuals, people with limited mobility, and anyone who intentionally wishes to train through a partial range of motion. To fit either a bench or an aerobic step within a rack, you may have to position it with the shorter side facing the side of the rack where you will place the barbell.
    7. While not ideal due to its lower stability, you can also stack bumper plates on top of each other to form a “box” of your desired height. If you opt to do so, make sure that the plates are completely centered on top of each other.
3. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
4. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should be securely resting on the muscles of your upper back when squeezing your shoulder blades together.
    2. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    5. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of box squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when box squatting.
7. Repeat steps 3 and 4 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps backward until the box is just behind you. Position your feet near shoulder width apart from each other or mildly wider, with your toes pointing somewhat out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to box squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when box squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Based on your preference, you can use the same stance that you would when back squatting without a box, or you can use a somewhat wider stance, which can facilitate sitting back a bit more.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down until you can sit onto the box in a controlled manner, as you avoid plopping onto the box or rocking back onto the box.
    1. As you sit on the box, your torso should maintain the same degree of forward lean that you had right before reaching the box.
    2. If your torso transitions to a more upright position after sitting on the box compared to at the end of your descent, you have rocked back onto the box.
6. While keeping your core braced and back flat, and feet fully on the ground, allow about half of your weight to deload into the box as you pause on the box for approximately one second.
    1. As a cue, you can think about a scale being on top of the box. If you weigh 200lb (91kg) and have 100lb (45kg) on the bar, the scale should read within the vicinity of 150lb (68kg).
7. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Avoid rocking your torso forward to generate momentum before you start squatting upward.
    2. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
8. Alternate between steps 4-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
9. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat to a distance away from the foot platform that allows you to leg press through a full range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    1. Finding the ideal setting for you as an individual typically takes some trial and error. The only way to know if the seat setting is appropriate is to sit down on the seat and situate your foot on the platform in the same position you will use for your set.
    2. The appropriate setting will allow you to assume the starting position as your lower back maintains close to its natural curvature (like when you are normally standing upright). It should also allow you to keep your foot fully on the platform.
    3. If the seat is excessively close to the foot platform relative to your hip mobility, your lower back may have to round to place your feet on the platform while seated. Alternatively, you may not be able to place your heel firmly in contact with the platform if it is too close.
    4. You should ideally place the seat as close to the foot platform as you can without having to round your lower back or lifting your heel off the platform. Placing the seat meaningfully farther back beyond this point may decrease how much muscle growth the exercise is likely to stimulate.
    5. As a general starting point, you can find the seat setting that allows your knee to be bent by about 90 degrees when your foot is on the platform. If you are able to avoid rounding your lower back when set up with this setting, you can incrementally bring the seat closer until you find your optimal starting position. Alternatively, if you have to round your lower back to set up with your knee bent by about 90 degrees, you can incrementally bring the seat back until you can assume the starting position without rounding your lower back.
2. Select your weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on the seat with your back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot platform, hip-width apart, with your toes pointing straight up.
    1. While not required, initially placing both feet on the platform before lifting one foot off the platform during the next step can help with positioning your working leg’s foot appropriately.
    2. Many people find that positioning their feet in this manner allows them to leg press effectively. However, specifically what is ideal foot placement may vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a shoulder-width stance compared to a hip-width stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with pointing their toes slightly out to the side instead of straight up.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different foot placements over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the platform works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select should let your heels remain firmly planted on the platform along with the rest of your feet. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward or limits your range of motion.
5. Lift one foot off the platform and place it in a comfortable position that will keep that leg from contacting the platform throughout your set.
    1. Situating the resting leg’s foot under the platform with a bent knee is commonly a comfortable position for a variety of machines.
    2. However, where it is best to position your resting leg can vary depending on the specific machine model you are using.
    3. The subsequent directions will apply to the leg whose foot is on the platform, since you will just keep your other leg out of the way during your set.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
3. Drive your foot into the platform to push it away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knee and hip.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knee fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knee when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knee into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner. 
    2. Keep the entire sole of your foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform. Rather than pushing all through your heel or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your foot.
    3. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    4. Strive to keep your knee aligned with your second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or third toe as you avoid letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot.
    5. Certain machine models will involve you pressing yourself away from a stationary foot platform rather than pressing the platform away from you. The technique remains the same regardless.
4. Reverse the movement by bending at your knee and hip in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your foot fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, back against the back rest, and knee aligned over your foot.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time. A sign of an appropriately controlled movement is having the weights you selected lightly contact the rest of the weight stack at the end range. Having the weights lightly touch down will also confirm that you have moved through your full range of motion.
    3. With the appropriate seat position, you should be able to avoid rounding your lower back at the end range of this motion. If you notice your lower back starts rounding at the end, you could finish your rep and mildly move the seat backward before resuming your set.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your quads, gluteus maximus, and multiple hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead.
    1. You can optionally place a soft object (like a cushioned balance pad, yoga block, folded thick towel, folded yoga mat, or a pillow) on the ground directly behind where you will stand if you are not confident in your ability to control the descent and tap your knee to the ground in a gentle manner.
    2. If you opt to place a soft object on the ground, you will use your knee to tap that object instead of the ground, and doing so will mildly reduce the range of motion and difficulty of this exercise compared to if you would lightly contact the ground with your knee.
    3. If you lack the strength or mobility to perform a full range of motion skater squat, you can select the “partial range of motion bodyweight skater squat” from this database.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and pick the other foot up off the ground by bending that side’s knee by greater than 90 degrees while keeping its thigh directed down toward the ground.
    1. The goal is to bend your knee enough that your foot will not contact the ground before your knee reaches the ground during the descent.
    2. Keep your free leg in this position throughout your set.
2. Raise your arms up with straight elbows until they are approximately parallel with the ground.
    1. Keep your arms parallel to the ground throughout your set.
3. With the leg in contact with the ground, progressively bend at your hip and knee to squat down in a controlled manner until you can gently tap the ground with your free leg’s knee.
    1. It can be helpful to initiate the descent by mildly sitting your buttocks back through a hip hinge motion that somewhat inclines your torso, as if you are closing a car door with your buttocks as your hands are carrying something. 
    2. Keep the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your working leg’s foot firmly in contact with the ground throughout your set.
    3. Strive to have your knee track straight forward in line with your second and third toes (when counting your big toe as your first toe) as you descend, while not letting your knee cave inward.
    4. To the best of your ability, keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    6. Through motion at your working leg’s hip, your torso should naturally transition to an inclined position rather than remaining completely upright, but strive to keep your back near a neutral position (where it retains its natural curvature or looks flat) as you avoid excessively rounding your lower back.
    7. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your free leg’s knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
4. Reverse the motion you just performed by progressively extending (straightening) your working leg's knee and hip to squat yourself up until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. Like during the descent, continue keeping your working leg’s foot fully in contact with the ground as you keep your hip bones level and avoid letting your knee cave inward.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise after switching which foot is in contact with the ground.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable decline bench to your desired decline angle.
    1. An angle near 30 degrees is an appropriate starting point, but you can effectively perform this exercise with a range of decline angles.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the decline becomes steeper, so you can adjust the angle to modify the intensity.
    3. Some adjustable benches have a mild decline setting along with incline settings but lack a pad to secure your feet. This exercise specifically uses a decline with a foot/ankle pad.
2. Pick up your desired weight and hold it on one side.
    1. A weight plate, dumbbell, or kettlebell is a viable option that you can use. Select whichever option you find most comfortable to hold to your chest.
3. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
    2. You can use your free hand to help you get into position.
4. Hold the weight just above your chest with bent elbows as you firmly grasp each side of the weight.
5. Lie down in a controlled manner to position your back and head on the bench.
## Exercise technique
1. As you tighten your abdominal muscles, lift your back and head up off the bench by curling your back. Bring your chest toward your thighs.
    1. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that you have fully rounded your back and cannot bring your chest closer toward your thighs.
    2. Continue holding the weight close to your chest throughout your set.
    3. Given that this is a sit-up rather than a crunch, you will lift the entirety of your back up off the bench.
    4. To train your abdominal muscles dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    5. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    6. As a cue for your engaging abdominal muscles, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs toward your hip bones.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your back to the starting position.
    1. Rather than fully relaxing at the bottom, strive to tap the bench lightly with your upper back while keeping your abdominals tensed.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the entire movement.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, or leave the machine unloaded.
2. Confirm that the center of the knee pad is pointing straight forward toward the handles rather than pointing out to either side of the machine.
    1. The knee pad may be swiveled to either side to perform an asymmetrical exercise that emphasizes your obliques, but the pad should be centered for this symmetrical variation.
    2. Machine models commonly permit you to adjust only the load and the knee pad orientation. If your machine has any additional modifiable features (such as handle height), you can adjust them to your preferred settings at this time.
3. Stand directly to the side of the knee pad while facing the handles.
4. Grasp the handles for balance assistance, and kneel on the knee pad, one leg at a time, while resting your forearms and elbows on the arm pads.
    1. Situate your knees near the front of the pad to allow your shins to rest on the pad for stability.
    2. You should be in a tall kneeling position with your chest up and your hips slightly behind your knees, as opposed to sitting your buttocks onto your heels with your knees fully bent.
    3. Your neck and back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. This should result in your lower back having its natural curvature rather than being excessively arched or rounded.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your knees upward while curling your hip bones up toward your ribs through the range of motion permitted by your mobility and the machine.
    1. To train your abs dynamically, you should be intentionally rounding your lower back by curling your hip bones closer toward your ribs, since this motion is performed by contraction of your abs. As you drive your knees higher up, you will be able to curl your hip bones to a greater degree.
    2. If you lift your knees up while keeping your lower back flat or mildly arched, you may be emphasizing your hip flexors to a greater degree than your abs.
    3. When curling your hip bones appropriately, you should feel meaningful tension in your abs.
    4. You can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the midpoint between your hips, a little below where you would wear a belt buckle. Strive to angle this laser beam upward by curling your hip bones
    5. Keep your chest and arms stationary throughout the exercise as you avoid pulling through your arms or moving your upper body.
    6. Where you precisely end the lifting phase can be influenced by the specific machine you are using, along with your mobility, but finishing with your knees being close to level with your hips is often an appropriate end range.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your knees and uncurling your lower back until your knees have returned to being slightly in front of your hips.
    1. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should avoid letting gravity rapidly swing you down.
    2. With an appropriately controlled descent, your knees should not rock back behind your hips.
    3. Your lower back should initially uncurl from a rounded position into a flat position before finishing with the mild arch it naturally has. At the end of the lowering phase, your lower back should not be arched beyond what it normally is when standing upright. If your lower back is arching excessively, you most likely are letting your knees rock too far backward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to dismount the machine.
    1. When transitioning from rep to rep, minimize any momentum to the best of your ability as you prioritize controlled movement and avoid swinging.
## Description
While simultaneously training your hip flexors meaningfully, this exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground near an exercise bench or similar sturdy elevated surface, such as a plyometric box or bed.
2. Lift up your legs to position the back of your heels on top of the bench with straight knees.
    1. If you are too close to keep your knees straight on top of the bench, you can scoot backward until you can do so.
    2. You can situate your feet near shoulder-width apart from each other, but it is up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    3. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
    4. As a modification, you can alternatively perform this exercise with your knees slightly bent if you wish to do so.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground until your hips are fully extended.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your gluteus maximus and three of your four hamstring muscles.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. The vast majority of models allow you to adjust the lower leg pad, and many allow you to adjust the lever arm, but most machines lack adjustable forearm pads. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heels (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when lying down on the machine with your knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to a position that allows you to begin each rep with your knees straightened.
    1. If the lever arm is too high, you will start each rep with your knees somewhat bent rather than fully straightened. This would prevent you from training through a full range of motion.
    2. Certain machines may allow you to position the lever arm low enough that it could situate your knees beyond fully straightened into a hyperextended position (where your knees are bent in the opposite direction in which they normally bend). This may increase the likelihood of experiencing knee discomfort during this exercise.
    3. The optimal position will allow you to draw a straight line through your knees from your lower legs to your thighs once you are in the starting position.
3. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the forearm pad to a height that allows you to rest your forearms on it comfortably without excessively arcing your lower back.
4. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
5. Lie down on the body pad with your lower legs under the lower leg pad and your knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point (which will typically result in your knees hanging just off the edge of the body pad).
    1. The front of your thighs, hips, and torso should be in contact with the body pad.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position with its natural curvature like when you are normally standing upright.
    4. In this starting position, your knees should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
    1. If your machine has forearm pads, you should also rest your forearms on them.
2. Pull your toes and feet back toward your shins by moving at your ankles, and maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shins contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankles in the same position in which they would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heels up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankles in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
3. Drive your heels toward your buttocks by bending your knees through the full range of motion you can perform. You should typically be able to contact your buttocks or upper thighs with the lower leg pad at the end range.
    1. While your lower legs move through motion at your knees, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
    2. Avoid lifting your hips up off the pad or excessively arching your lower back.
4. Reverse the movement as you allow your knees to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knees fully straightened.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower legs down.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent and the soles of your feet flat on the ground, approximately hip width apart. 
    1. You can rest your head on the ground.
    2. You optionally can hook the tops of your feet under a sturdy object like a couch (while your soles are still flat on the ground), which is recommended to help stabilize your body during this exercise.
2. Place the fingertips of each hand lightly in contact with the sides of the back of your head as your upper arms and elbows are directed out away from your sides.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, looking straight ahead. Keep your neck in this position during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Without applying pressure to your head with your hands, lift your back and head up off the ground by curling your back to bring your chest toward your thighs as you tighten your abdominal muscles.
    1. Given that this is a sit-up rather than a crunch, you will lift the entirety of your back off the ground.
    2. To train your abdominal muscles dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    3. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    4. If a scale were fixed to the back of your head, it should not detect any force from your fingertips as you keep your hands relaxed throughout the exercise, rather than pulling your head.
    5. As a cue, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the top of your hip bones, which is the action that contraction of your abdominal muscles can produce.
    6. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that you have fully rounded your back and cannot bring your chest closer toward your thighs.
    7. At the end of the lifting phase, your chest should be facing forward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your head and back to the starting position on the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, denoted by a small dot or circle on some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heel (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when leaning on the machine with your knee aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. Certain machine models have an adjustable foot plate rather than an adjustable lower leg pad. If your machine has the adjustable foot plate, you should adjust its height until the lower leg pad is situated slightly above the back of your heel when standing on the foot plate.
2. Adjust the thigh pad to a position that allows you to place your lower thigh snugly against it when your knee is aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. This pad may be adjusted vertically or horizontally (or not at all) depending on which machine you are using.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Stand in front of the lower leg pad with the back of your lower legs contacting this pad and your thighs contacting the thigh pad.
5. Lean forward at your hips until you can firmly grasp the handles in a comfortable position. Continue holding them throughout your set.
    1. If your machine has forearm pads, you should also rest your forearms on them.
    2. If your machine has a torso pad (for your lower abdomen or chest) in addition to a thigh pad, you should also rest your torso on it while leaning forward.
    3. In this starting position, your knees should be straight and aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    4. Rather than being excessively arched or rounded, your lower back should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
    5. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. On the side you are about to train, pull your toes and foot up toward your shin by moving at your ankle. Maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankle in the same position in which it would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heel up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankle in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
2. Drive your heel toward your buttock by bending your knee through the full range of motion you can perform without moving your other leg.
    1. Some machines may allow you to contact your buttock or upper thigh with the lower leg pad at the end range, but other models may limit you from bending your knee to this extent.
    2. If you experience a notable cramping sensation in your hamstrings at the end range, you can stop curling the weight up just before the point where your hamstrings may cramp.
    3. While your lower leg moves through motion at your one knee, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
    4. Avoid lifting your thigh off the pad or excessively arching your lower back.
3. Reverse the movement as you allow your knee to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knee straightened.
    2. Continue keeping the foot of your resting leg planted on the ground/foot plate.
    3. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower leg down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back next to your weight with your knees bent as the soles of your feet are flat on the ground and approximately hip width apart.
    1. In addition to using a weight plate, a medicine ball, a dumbbell, or a kettlebell is a viable option that you can use.
    2. You optionally can hook the tops of your feet under a sturdy object like a couch (while your soles are still flat on the ground) to help stabilize your body during this exercise.
2. Pick up the weight and hold it just above your chest with bent elbows as you firmly grasp each side of the weight with your hands.
    1. After doing so, you can rest your head on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the weight close to your chest, tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the ground while you lift your head and shoulder blades up off the ground by curling your middle back.
    1. Given that this is a crunch rather than a sit-up, the lower half of your back should remain in contact with the ground while you only lift up the upper half of your back mildly off the floor.
    2. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    3. As a cue, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the top of your hip bones, which is the action that contraction of your abdominal muscles can produce.
    4. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that crunching up any further would require the lower half of your back to start lifting up off the floor.
    5. At the end of the lifting phase, your chest should be facing at an angle between forward and upward as opposed to only facing upward as it did in the starting position.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your middle back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your head and shoulder blades back to the starting position on the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground, and your knees bent at least 90 degrees.
    1. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
2. Position the soles of your feet flat against each other while allowing your knees to spread out to your sides and descend closer toward the ground.
    1. When you press your soles together, your knees will naturally spread out, and you do not have to try to push them further apart than this point.
    2. Your knees should be in a comfortable position, and the degree to which your knees will spread out will be influenced by your hip mobility.
    3. The outer aspect of your feet (on the side of your little toes) should be firmly in contact with the ground.
3. Tuck your chin toward your chest and lift your head slightly off the ground.
    1. This can help you keep your lower back flat rather than excessively arched during the exercise, but you have the option of resting your head on the ground if you wish to do so.
    2. You can position your arms in whichever comfortable position you prefer, such as resting by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving the outer aspects of your feet into the ground and squeezing your glutes. Keep the soles of your feet pressed together and your knees spread apart.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you ideally should be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side if you have the mobility to perform the full range of motion. If your hips feel “stuck” or quite uncomfortable before reaching this point, you may not have the hip mobility to move through the full range with your current knee position. Bringing your knees somewhat higher up and closer together may allow you to lift your hips up higher in a more comfortable manner.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively. Tensing your abdominal muscles may help limit your lower back from arching.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glutes engage during this exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. Continue keeping your soles in contact with each other.
    2. After determining which knee position allows you to comfortably move through your full range of motion, strive to keep your knees in the same position throughout your set.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will predominantly train your gluteus maximus in a rather shortened position.## Setting up
1. Place a dumbbell on the ground next to the side of an exercise bench via a squatting motion and sit down on the ground with the side of one of your hips directly next to the dumbbell and your upper back in contact with the bench.
    1. For an easier setup with a heavier dumbbell that has flat sides, you can position the dumbbell with one end on the ground and its handle directed vertically, resulting in one end remaining off the ground.
2. Position your feet out in front of you with your soles flat on the floor and your knees bent to at least 90 degrees.
    1. You can situate your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, but it is up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can opt to point your toes forward or somewhat out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
3. Tip the dumbbell onto the crease of your hips in a controlled manner using both hands to have the ends of the dumbbell symmetrically in contact with your upper thighs and the front of your hip bones.
    1. Use your hands to keep the dumbbell in the crease of your hips throughout your set.
4. Tuck your chin mildly toward your chest, and maintain this neck position throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your buttock muscles.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glutes engage during this exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your hip mobility comfortably permits without rounding or excessively arching your lower back.
    1. Some people can descend all the way down until they lightly contact the ground with their glutes, but you can prioritize lowering through the range in which you can achieve through hip motion rather than lower back motion.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will predominantly develop your gluteus maximus while also training your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Select your weight with the loading pin and position the footplates as closely together as the machine permits.
2. If your machine’s knee pads rotate (which is the case for combo machines that can be adjusted to train hip adduction or abduction), position the pads so that they are facing in toward each other.
    1. The knee pads may permanently face in toward each other in machines that exclusively permit you to train hip abduction.
3. If your machine allows you to adjust the backrest angle, position it fully upright.
    1. Most machines have backrests fixed in an upright position.
4. Sit down on the machine's seat with your torso upright against the backrest. Place your feet on the footplates with the outer aspect of your knees and/or lower thighs contacting the knee pads.
    1. If you have rather short legs, you may have to scoot slightly forward for your knees to reach the pads, which will require you to lean your torso somewhat backward to keep your back against the backrest.
## Exercise technique
1. Spread your legs apart from each other through your hips’ available range of motion.
    1. How far apart you can spread your legs will be affected by your hip mobility.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your legs to move in toward each other in a controlled manner.
    1. At the end range, you can allow the weights you selected to tap the rest of the weight stack. A light tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    2. After doing so, you should now be in the starting position with your legs as close together as the machine permits.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training several other smaller muscles on the side of your hip, this exercise can dynamically train your glutes, likely with an emphasis on the upper region of your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heels (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when lying down on the machine with your knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
2. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust its lever arm (that will align with your shins) to a position that lets you begin each rep with your knees straightened.
    1. If the lever arm is too high, you will start each rep with your knees somewhat bent rather than fully straightened. This would prevent you from training through a full range of motion.
    2. Certain machines may allow you to position the lever arm low enough that it could situate your knees beyond fully straightened into a hyperextended position (where your knees are bent in the opposite direction in which they normally bend). This may increase the likelihood of experiencing knee discomfort during this exercise.
    3. The optimal position will allow you to draw a straight line through your knees from your lower legs to your thighs once you are in the starting position.
3. Add weight plates to the machine.
4. Lie down on the body pad with your lower legs under the lower leg pad and your knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point (which will typically result in your knees hanging just off the edge of the body pad).
    1. The front of your thighs, hips, and torso should be in contact with the body pad.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
    4. In this starting position, your knees should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. On the side you are about to train, pull your toes and foot back toward your shin by moving at your ankle. Maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankle in the same position in which it would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heel up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankle in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
3. While keeping one knee straightened, use your other leg to drive your heel toward your buttock by bending your knee through the full range of motion you can perform. You should typically be able to contact your buttocks or upper thighs with the lower leg pad at the end range.
    1. While your lower leg moves through motion at your one knee, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
    2. Avoid lifting your hips up off the pad or excessively arching your lower back.
4. Reverse the movement as you allow your knee to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knee fully straightened.
    2. Continue keeping your other knee straightened.
    3. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower leg down.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the Roman chair’s pad to a level that positions the top of your hip bones somewhat above the top of the pads when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. This position will allow you to move freely about your hips in contrast to if the pad is contacting the top of your hip bones, which would restrict hip motion and prompt you to perform the exercise via spinal motion.
    2. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones (rather than only somewhat lower), some individuals may feel knee discomfort, so a sweet spot can exist for pad placement.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position after initially completing step 2.
2. While placing your hands onto the handles for balance support, step onto the foot plate one leg at a time with the back of your heels in contact with the back of the foot plate, so that the entirety of your soles are in contact with the foot plate.
    1. Position your feet symmetrically in a stance that feels comfortable to you, which can involve pointing your toes somewhat outward or pointing them fairly straight forward.
    2. Situating your feet approximately hip width to shoulder width apart from each other will result in a viable stance width, but how wide of a stance you can use will be influenced by the width of your Roman chair’s foot plate.
    3. Your knees should be straight after you have finished setting your feet on the foot plate.
3. Check to make sure that the pad is positioned as described by step 1, and readjust the pad height if it is not prior to resuming your setup. 
4. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes to keep your torso up such that you can draw approximately a straight line from your legs through your hip and to your torso as opposed to having your torso bent over.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your lower back and neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. This is the starting position of the exercise, where your body should look like it does when normally standing upright except at an incline.
2. Begin bracing your core by taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. After the deep “belly breath,” you can continue thinking about tensing the front of your abdominals, sides of your abdominals, and lower back muscles simultaneously in a manner that allows you to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. As a helpful cue for your “360 degree brace,” you can tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you are bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
3. While keeping your core braced, hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner.
    1. Nearly all of the motion should occur from you moving at your hips as you keep your trunk in a static position that maintains the natural curvature of your lower back (as opposed to bending over by rounding your lower back).
    2. Maintain a straight knee position, but you should avoid intentionally locking your knees out by squeezing your quads. 
    3. While hinging at your hips and keeping your hands on your shoulders, you should feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings that progressively intensifies.
    4. Most people will be able to descend until their shoulders are lower than their hips, but how deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    5. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch, you have finished descending through your available hip range of motion.
4. Reverse the movement by lifting your torso up through your hips until you have returned to the starting position, where your torso is aligned with your legs.
    1. You can finish the lifting phase by fully squeezing your glutes, which helps cue hip movement at the top as opposed to spinal movement.
    2. Avoid arching your lower back excessively at the top since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the hip motion you just performed during the lowering phase. 
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its highest position that allows you to begin each rep with your knees fully straightened.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heels (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when seated against the backrest, with your legs on top of the lower leg pad and your knees straightened.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the lower leg pad without moving the lever arm, in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. After adding weight plates to the machine, sit down on its seat with your back against the back pad and your lower legs on top of the lower leg pad.
5. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
    1. In this starting position, your knees should be straight and aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Pull your toes and feet back toward your shins by moving at your ankles, and maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shins contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankles in the same position in which they would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heels up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankles in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
3. Drive your heels down and back toward you as you bend your knees to pull the lower leg pad under your thighs through the available range of motion that the machine allows.
    1. At some point, the machine will stop you from bending your knees any further, but most machines will allow you to bend your knees by at least 90 degrees.
    2. While your lower legs move through motion at your knees, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
4. Reverse the movement as you allow your knees to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knees fully straightened.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower legs up.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Set the foot plate to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot point with your hips while standing on the center of the foot plate with your knees slightly bent.
    1. The pivot point will be the center of the machine’s wheel, about which the lever arm rotates.
    2. Strive to align the pivot point specifically with the bony bump (greater trochanter) that you could feel on the side of your hip.
    3. Some machines do not allow you to adjust the foot plate.
2. Position the machine’s lever arm to point straight down.
    1. This will help you adjust the machine to your body proportions before moving the lever arm into the exercise’s starting position.
3. Adjust the machine’s roller pad to be slightly above knee height when standing on the foot plate.
4. Kick one leg up as high as you can with a straight knee and note the height of your knee before lowering your leg.
5. Rotate the lever arm to arc the roller pad to the height at which you were able to lift your knee.
    1. This will commonly involve rotating the lever arm until it is close to parallel to the ground.
    2. The appropriate setting will enable the machine to provide resistance throughout the full range of motion your hamstring flexibility allows.
6. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
7. Stand in the center of the foot plate while facing the roller pad to align your hips with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. The roller should be in front of your leg that is closer to the weight stack.
    2. The leg further away from the weight stack should not be in the way of the roller pad when it arcs back toward the center of the machine during the exercise.
8. On the side closer to the weight stack, position the back of your knee on top of the roller pad with your knee fairly straight. Firmly grasp the handlebar in front of you with both hands.
    1. To move it into position, you can use your hands to pull down on the roller pad as you swing this leg in toward your other side while marching up before swinging it out onto the roller pad. It can be easier to march your leg with your knee bent before straightening it once it is on top of the roller pad.
    2. Your knee does not have to be completely straight, but it should be sufficiently close to fully straight that you feel a meaningful stretching sensation in your hamstrings. This can be achieved with a mild knee band.
    3. The leg further away from the weight stack should be standing with a slightly bent knee as its foot is flat on the foot plate.
    4. Based on what feels most stable to you, your torso should be upright or mildly leaned forward at your hips.
    5. In this starting position, your hips and shoulders should be level.
    6. The subsequent technique directions will apply to the leg on top of the roller pad, since that is the side you will train with this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your leg down and back in an arcing manner through your hip’s full range of motion. Keep your knee fairly straight throughout your set.
    1. Most people should be able to finish a little beyond the point at which the thigh is in a vertical position. However, how far you personally can drive your leg back will be influenced by your hip mobility.
    2. Strive to have all the motion come from your hip while keeping the rest of your body stationary. The foot of your other leg should remain firmly planted on the foot plate throughout your set.
    3. In particular, avoid excessively arching your lower back, which is a common technique error that can occur when trying to move the roller pad further back than what your hip mobility permits. Appropriately bracing your core can help limit unintended arching.
    4. At the end range, you should feel your glute contract. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glute engage during this exercise.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by bringing your leg forward and upward in an arcing motion.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your knee fairly straight and fully lifted up in front of you. In this position, you should feel a notable stretching sensation in your hamstrings.
    2. Avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your leg forward in an uncontrolled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. To train your other side, you will face the opposite direction.
## Description
While training other lower body muscles to a lesser degree, this exercise will predominantly develop your hamstrings, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. In a location where you have sufficient room to side-step, position a short looped elastic band around both of your lower shins just above your ankles.
    1. Initially inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
2. Stand upright with your feet near shoulder-width apart from each other and your toes pointing straight forward.
    1. Position your arms where you prefer. People often clasp their hands in front of their chest or rest them on their hips, but there is no single correct arm position.
    2. In the starting position, you ideally should feel tension from the band. The band may be slack for some people if they place it around their middle thighs instead of just above their knees.
## Exercise technique
1. Assume an athletic stance by mildly sitting your buttocks back while bending your hips and knees.
    1. Rather than being fully upright, your torso should now be mildly inclined.
    2. Keep your chest up and your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as opposed to being rounded or excessively arched.
    3. Maintain this mild bend in your hips and knees throughout your set.
2. Drive your knees out against the band’s resistance with the intent to align your knees with the middle of your feet.
    1. If you do not resist the band, it can pull your shins and knees inward to the extent that your knees are no longer stacked over the middle of your feet.
    2. Resist letting the band pull your shins or knees inward throughout the set.
3. Take a small step to the side while keeping both knees aligned with the middle of your feet.
    1. This requires continuing to drive both knees out to your sides, which should prompt you to feel meaningful tension in the muscles on the sides of your hips.
    2. Rather than leading with your foot, emphasize pushing out to the side with your lead leg’s knee as your foot remains vertically stacked under your knee.
    3. Your foot should not travel further out to the side than your knee. If it does (which can occur when leading with your foot instead of your knee and/or taking a long step), you will notice that your knee no longer remains aligned with the middle of your foot.
    4. At any moment during your set, including the moment in which your lead foot contacts the ground, you should be able to draw a straight line down from your knee through the middle of your foot. Achieving this alignment is typically only feasible with rather short steps.
    5. Keep your hips and shoulders level throughout the exercise as you avoid letting your trunk tilt to one side.
    6. Maintain the same degree of mild torso inclination rather than bobbing up and down.
4. Once your initial lead leg’s foot contacts the ground, step inward with your trail leg to return to an approximately shoulder-width stance.
    1. The appropriate step length should result in the band remaining tensed the entire set.
    2. If you notice the band becomes slack, you have taken an excessively long inward step with the trailing leg, resulting in your feet being too close together.
    3. As you step inward with your trailing leg, you need to continue driving your other knee outward to prevent the band from pulling it inward.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 to side-step until you are ready to switch which side is your lead leg, and repeat the exercise.
    1. If you reach the end of the space through which you can side-step and do not yet wish to switch your lead leg, you can simply pivot 180 degrees and walk back to the starting position to continue using the same lead leg.
    2. Based on your preference, you could complete all the distance you intend to complete in a set with one lead leg before switching to the other side, or you can switch lead legs multiple times in a set. For instance, you have the option of walking 20yd (18.3m) with your right leg leading before walking 20yd (18.3m) with your left leg leading. Alternatively, you can walk 10yd (9.1m) with your right leg leading before walking 10yd (9.1m) with your left leg leading, and then repeating this for another cycle.
## Description
This exercise can train your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and the upper region of your gluteus maximus, along with several smaller muscles on the side of your hip.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its highest position that allows you to begin each rep with your knees fully straightened.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heels (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when seated against the backrest, with your legs on top of the lower leg pad and your knees straightened.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the lower leg pad without moving the lever arm, in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. After selecting your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, sit down on its seat with your back against the back pad and your lower legs on top of the lower leg pad.
5. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
    1. In this starting position, your knees should be straight and aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Pull your toes and feet back toward your shins by moving at your ankles, and maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shins contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankles in the same position in which they would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heels up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankles in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
3. Drive your heels down and back toward you as you bend your knees to pull the lower leg pad under your thighs through the available range of motion that the machine allows.
    1. At some point, the machine will stop you from bending your knees any further, but most machines will allow you to bend your knees by at least 90 degrees.
    2. While your lower legs move through motion at your knees, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
4. Reverse the movement as you allow your knees to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knees fully straightened.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower legs up.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells by your thighs with straight elbows as your palms face your thighs.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hand while holding them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
    3. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    4. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    5. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Once upright, you can situate your palms right next to the outer aspect of the front of your thighs so that your palms face at an angle between backward and in toward each other.
    1. This results in a semi-overhand grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip that would direct your palms straight backward (with the dumbbells directly in front of your body) and a neutral grip that would direct your palms straight in toward your sides (with the dumbbells directly to the sides of your body).
    2. You will keep your palms facing this way throughout your set.
    3. Your neck should be near a neutral position when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead, and you will keep your neck in this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Bracing will help you maintain a rigid back position while you move about your hip throughout your set.
2. While allowing the other side’s foot to lift up off the floor, begin hinging at the hip on the side to which you shifted your weight by sitting your buttocks back as you let your knee mildly bend.
    1. As you do so, the dumbbells will naturally descend, and your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    2. Allow your free leg to swing backward as it moves with your torso in a see-saw-like manner, but focus on hinging at the hip of your working leg instead of kicking your other leg back.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows straightened, core braced, lower back near a neutral position, and working leg’s sole fully in contact with the ground while doing so.
    4. To the best of your ability, keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down.
    5. Most of the motion should come from your hip, and your shin should remain in a fairly vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knee fully straightened.
    6. You do not need to focus on bending your knee, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks back while not intentionally keeping your knee locked.
3. Continuing hinging at your hip in a controlled manner until you have reached the end of your available range of motion.
    1. While some people can descend until their torsos are close to parallel to the ground, how deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knee will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer descend without rounding your lower back, you have finished descending through your available range of motion.
4. Reverse the hinge movement by driving through your working leg’s hip to lift yourself up until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straightened, core braced, lower back near a neutral position, hip bones level, and working leg’s sole fully in contact with the ground.
    2. After you are fully upright, you could place your free leg’s foot back onto the ground if you could use some additional balance assistance.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which foot remains in contact with the ground.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    3. After finishing the last rep with your other side, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position a pair of deadlift blocks (or a similarly sturdy substitute capable of safely withstanding the load you will use) on the ground far enough apart from each other that the weight plates on a bar will be able to rest fully on the blocks in a stable position.
    1. For this block pull variation, your blocks should be of a low height that situates the bar near the middle of your shins when the loaded bar is resting on the blocks, which often involves no greater than a few inches of elevation  for most people.
    2. To appropriately position the blocks, it can be helpful to have a barbell on the ground and align the blocks about the same distance apart from each other as is the length of the barbell’s shaft (which is the middle part of the bar in between the two thick circular collars).
    3. Aerobic steps can often be used for block pulls while having the option to adjust the height by changing the number of risers you use, but you should be aware of the recommended weight limit if you opt to use heavy loads. The weight limit for aerobic steps will vary by brand, but some of the common brands recommend not exceeding 300lb (136kg) per step, which would result in a 600lb (272kg) limit when using a pair of steps for a block pull. The height of the aerobic step will also vary by brand, but the step itself is commonly near 4in (10cm) in height, with the risers being near 2in (5cm) in height.
    4. With thickness typically ranging from about 1-3.5in (2.5-8.9cm), depending on the weight and brand, bumper plates can also function as blocks that can be stacked on top of each other to provide a range of different heights.
2. After placing a barbell onto the blocks and loading weight plates to the bar, stand upright in approximately a hip width stance close in front of the middle of the bar as the bar is over the middle of your feet with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly turned out to the sides.
    1. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
3. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward to bring your shins right next to the bar.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. Immediately after the bar lifts off the blocks, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. The “push the floor away” cue only applies to the very start of the rep since thrusting your hips forward is chiefly what will allow you to lift the weight during this variation.
    2. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    3. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the blocks.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track slightly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the blocks for a moment (rather than simply tapping the blocks and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the blocks for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferring between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the blocks.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Connect a straight bar attachment or whichever attachment you prefer to the cable.
    1. As an alternative to a straight bar, you can use a multi-grip curl bar, two D-handles, or a V-bar row grip attachment.
    2. This page will describe the usage of the straight bar attachment, but the exercise can be performed in the same manner with the other attachments, except for how you hold them.
3. While standing directly in front of the middle of the bar, squat down with straight elbows to grasp it firmly with approximately a shoulder-width grip as your palms are directed back toward you (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. When squatting down, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
4. Extend (straighten) your hips and knees to squat upright with the bar while keeping your elbows straight and your lower back near a neutral position.
5. Take a couple of steps backward while allowing your straight arms to lift up in line with the cable’s resistance.
    1. The purpose of stepping backward is to ensure that the cable can provide resistance throughout the full range of motion of your Romanian deadlift.
    2. If you lose resistance from the weights you selected contacting the rest of the weight stack during the exercise, you should take another step backward.
6. Position your feet approximately hip width apart with your soles fully on the ground and toes pointing toward the pulley.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to stabilize your back and maximize performance.
2. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner while keeping your straight arms aligned with the cable.
    1. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal, and your arms will naturally move away from you and toward the pulley.
    2. Avoid trying to push or pull the cable with your arms. Your arms should function merely to transmit force from the cable to your torso, which transmits force to your hips.
    3. Avoid rounding your lower back as you keep your lower back mildly arched or flat throughout the exercise.
    4. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    5. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something, as you keep your chest up (rather than letting your chest collapse through back rounding).
    6. Keep the soles of your feet fully planted on the ground the entire time.
3. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. How deeply you are able to hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
4. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so you should continue keeping your arms aligned with the cable, feet fully on the ground, and lower back flat or mildly arched.
    2. Once you have returned to the starting position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid driving your hips in front of your shoulders at the top, which would prompt your lower back to arch excessively. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips appropriately stacked under your shoulders to prevent excessive arching.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
6. Once you are fully upright following your last rep, walk forward until you are directly in front of the pulley before squatting down to return the bar to the ground.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position a short, looped elastic band around both thighs.
    1. The band will provide the greatest resistance to your hip muscles if you place it just above your knees, while it will provide less resistance if you place it higher up your thighs.
    2. You can choose where you situate the band on your thighs based on the level of resistance you wish to experience, and if you are using a particular band for this exercise for the first time, you can adjust its placement during the set if you wish to change the difficulty after starting the set.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground or an exercise mat. Bend your knees to at least 90 degrees to position the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can position your feet shoulder-width apart, but it is up to you whether you would like them closer together or farther apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. You can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Push your knees outward against the band’s resistance to have them in line with your feet (as opposed to letting the band pull your knees closer together), and maintain this knee position throughout your set.
    1. You will most likely feel tension in the muscles on the sides of your hips while doing so.
2. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glutes engage.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you can lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 while continuing to keep your knees aligned with your feet until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus while statically training your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and short hip external rotator muscles.## Setting up
1. In a deadlift motion, pick up a kettlebell and set it onto the ground with its handle facing up.
    1. Particularly when using what you consider to be a heavy weight, initially lift it up using your hips and knees with your lower back near a flat position rather than being excessively rounded over at your lower back.
2. Stand upright with the kettlebell centered just in front of your toes.
3. Take a step back with one foot until its big toe is just behind the heel of your front foot, before stepping back the same distance with your front foot.
    1. This results in the kettlebell now being about one “foot length” in front of your toes.
4. Position your feet near shoulder width apart, with your toes slightly turned out to the sides or pointing straight ahead. Direct your arms toward the handle with straight elbows.
    1. While using a near shoulder-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility. Feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
5. Hinge at your hips while allowing your knees to bend until your hands are level with the handle. This will incline your torso forward with a flat back.
    1. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have hinged through your available hip range of motion (which should prompt you to feel a hamstring stretch), you can bend your knees further to reach the handle.
    2. More motion should come from your hips than your knees, and you should minimize forward knee tracking, since you are not squatting. Try to keep your shins as close to vertical as you can without rounding your back.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
6. With your hands close together and your elbows still straight, firmly grasp the handle with an overhand grip, resulting in your facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handle with the handle pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handle closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handle can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Pack your shoulder blades down to tilt the kettlebell slightly toward you on the closer edge of its base.
    1. In this starting position, you should have your back locked into a stable position with the muscles running up the length of your back fully tensed.
    2. The edge of the kettlebell’s base further in front of you should now be just off the ground
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you lift the weight with your hips.
3. Sweep the kettlebell back between your legs while keeping your torso inclined, lower back flat, and chest up.
    1. You should look like you are hiking a football (referring to American football).
    2. This is the only part of the exercise where you are intentionally using your upper body to move the weight. You should be moving your shoulders as you would during a straight arm lat pulldown.
    3. The objective is to generate some momentum and shift the weight into a position that loads your hips.
4. Once your forearms contact your inner thighs, explosively thrust your hips forward to drive your body into an upright position and swing the kettlebell out in front of you.
    1. Your arms and hands should respectively function as ropes and hooks that are attached to the kettlebell. While they maintain a tight grip and transmit force from your hips to the kettlebell, do not intentionally try to lift the weight with your arms.
    2. With an appropriate load, the power from your hips should be able to drive the kettlebell up until your arms are reasonably close to being parallel to the ground. It is common for the swing height from the first rep or two to be somewhat lower than during later reps since the initial reps help build momentum.
    3. Finish thrusting forward by momentarily squeezing your glutes as you avoid arching your lower back excessively. Stay tall while looking straight ahead rather than leaning back or tilting your head up at the top.
    4. While you focus on driving your hips, your knees will naturally straighten as well. At the top of your swing, you should essentially be able to draw a straight line (when viewed from the side) from your shoulders to your feet that moves through your hips and knees.
    5. Throughout your set, keep your neck and back locked in the same position in which you started while keeping the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground.
5. Allow the kettlebell to swing down and back between your legs while simultaneously hinging your hips with the intent of “catching” the weight.
    1. Rather than waiting for the weight to snap you down, start sitting your buttocks back to hinge as soon as the kettlebell begins arcing downward.
    2. Allow your knees naturally to bend from sitting your hips back while keeping your knees unlocked, but keep your shins close to vertical. You should be sitting back rather than squatting down. The appropriate amount of knee bend should allow you to feel a hamstring stretch at the bottom of the swing.
    3. Continue keeping your core braced, back flat, and chest up while your torso inclines forward from hinging your hips. Particularly avoid letting your lower back round, which may easily occur if you lose core tightness.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 in a rhythmic pendulum-like manner until you have completed your set.
    1. After completing the lowering phase of your last rep, stay tight while allowing the kettlebell to slow to a stop before setting it down.
## Description
This exercise can develop explosive hip extension strength while dynamically training your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. The vast majority of models allow you to adjust the lower leg pad, and many allow you to adjust the lever arm, but most machines lack adjustable forearm pads. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heels (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when lying down on the machine with your knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to a position that allows you to begin each rep with your knees straightened.
    1. If the lever arm is too high, you will start each rep with your knees somewhat bent rather than fully straightened. This would prevent you from training through a full range of motion.
    2. Certain machines may allow you to position the lever arm low enough that it could situate your knees beyond fully straightened into a hyperextended position (where your knees are bent in the opposite direction in which they normally bend). This may increase the likelihood of experiencing knee discomfort during this exercise.
    3. The optimal position will allow you to draw a straight line through your knees from your lower legs to your thighs once you are in the starting position.
3. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the forearm pad to a height that allows you to rest your forearms on it comfortably without excessively arcing your lower back.
4. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
5. Lie down on the body pad with your lower legs under the lower leg pad and your knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point (which will typically result in your knees hanging just off the edge of the body pad).
    1. The front of your thighs, hips, and torso should be in contact with the body pad.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
    4. In this starting position, your knees should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
    1. If your machine has forearm pads, you should also rest your forearms on them.
2. On the side you are about to train, pull your toes and foot back toward your shin by moving at your ankle. Maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankle in the same position in which it would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heel up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankle in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
3. While keeping one knee straightened, use your other leg to drive your heel toward your buttock by bending your knee through the full range of motion you can perform. You should typically be able to contact your buttocks or upper thighs with the lower leg pad at the end range.
    1. While your lower leg moves through motion at your one knee, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
    2. Avoid lifting your hips up off the pad or excessively arching your lower back.
4. Reverse the movement as you allow your knee to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knee fully straightened.
    2. Continue keeping your other knee straightened.
    3. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower leg down.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground or an exercise mat. Bend your knees to at least 90 degrees to position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your feet near hip-width apart.
    1. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    2. In this position, you can have your toes pointing forward, rest the back of your head on the ground, and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
2. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift the other foot off the ground by marching that side’s thigh up while keeping your knee bent, or by straightening your knee while leaving that side’s thigh aligned with the thigh of your other leg.
    1. Either position for the free leg is viable, so you can go with whichever you prefer, but keeping the knee straight may make the exercise mildly more challenging than keeping it bent.
    2. Keep one foot off the ground in the static position of your choice throughout the set you perform with your other side.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heel into the ground and squeezing your glute.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side (on the side of your body with the foot contacting the ground). Your thigh and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glute.
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glute engage.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to contact the ground with your buttocks lightly.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While your hamstrings and adductor magnus can assist to a degree, this exercise primarily targets your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. Position a short looped elastic band around both of your lower thighs in a location where you have sufficient room to side-step.
    1. The band will provide the greatest resistance to your hip muscles if you place it just above your knees, while it will provide less resistance if you place it higher up your thighs.
    2. You can choose specifically where you situate the band on your thighs based on the level of resistance you wish to experience. If you are using a particular band for this exercise for the first time, you can adjust its placement during the set if you wish to change the difficulty after starting the set.
    3. Inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
2. Stand upright with your feet near shoulder-width apart from each other and your toes pointing straight forward.
    1. Position your arms where you prefer. People often clasp their hands in front of their chest or rest them on their hips, but there is no single correct arm position.
    2. In the starting position, you ideally should feel tension from the band. The band may be slack for some people if they place it around their middle thighs instead of just above their knees.
## Exercise technique
1. Assume an athletic stance by mildly sitting your buttocks back while bending your hips and knees.
    1. Rather than being fully upright, your torso should now be mildly inclined.
    2. Keep your chest up and your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as opposed to being rounded or excessively arched.
    3. Maintain this mild bend in your hips and knees throughout your set.
2. Drive your knees out against the band with the intent to align your knees with the middle of your feet.
    1. If you do not resist the band, it can pull your knees inward to the extent that they are no longer stacked over the middle of your feet.
    2. Resist letting the band pull your knees inward throughout the set.
3. Take a small step to the side while keeping both knees aligned with the middle of your feet.
    1. This requires continuing to drive both knees out to your sides against the band, which should prompt you to feel meaningful tension in the muscles on the sides of your hips.
    2. Rather than leading with your foot, emphasize pushing out to the side with your lead leg’s knee as your foot remains vertically stacked under your knee.
    3. Your foot should not travel further out to the side than your knee. If it does (which can occur when leading with your foot instead of your knee and/or taking a long step), you will notice that your knee no longer remains aligned with the middle of your foot.
    4. At any moment during your set, including the moment in which your lead foot contacts the ground, you should be able to draw a straight line down from your knee through the middle of your foot. Achieving this alignment is typically only feasible with rather short steps.
    5. Keep your hips and shoulders level throughout the exercise as you avoid letting your trunk tilt to one side.
    6. Maintain the same degree of mild torso inclination rather than bobbing up and down.
4. Once your initial lead leg’s foot contacts the ground, step inward with your trail leg to return to an approximately shoulder width stance.
    1. The appropriate step length should result in the band remaining tensed the entire set.
    2. If you notice the band becomes slack, you have taken an excessively long inward step with the trail leg, resulting in your feet being too close together.
    3. As you step inward with your trail leg, you need to continue driving your other knee outward to prevent the band from pulling it inward.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 to side-step until you are ready to switch which side is your lead leg and repeat the exercise.
    1. If you reach the end of the space through which you can side-step and do not yet wish to switch your lead leg, you can simply pivot 180 degrees and walk back to the starting position to continue using the same lead leg.
    2. Based on your preference, you could complete all the distance you intend to complete in a set with one lead leg before switching to the other side, or you can switch lead legs multiple times in a set. For instance, you have the option of walking 20yd (18.3m) with your right leg leading before walking 20yd (18.3m) with your left leg leading. Alternatively, you can walk 10yd (9.1m) with your right leg leading before walking 10yd (9.1m) with your left leg leading, and then repeating this for another cycle.
## Description
This exercise can train your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and the upper region of your gluteus maximus, along with several smaller muscles on the side of your hip.## Setting up
1. Determine what you will use to anchor your feet securely during the exercise.
    1. You want to have something very stable positioned slightly above the back of your heels (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) while you are able to kneel comfortably on a relatively soft surface such as a foam pad, pillow, or rolled-up exercise mat.
    2. A person who is sufficiently strong and heavy can anchor your feet if you have access to an appropriate partner.
    3. Without specialized equipment, you can alternatively anchor your feet under a barbell with a squat pad that is sufficiently loaded with at least bodyweight. The barbell should be loaded with large-diameter plates to provide room for you to fit your lower legs under it with your toes on the ground. The bar should also be anchored so that it does not roll forward. Placing the barbell inside a power rack against the posts or placing heavy bumper plates in front of the barbell weight plates can stop it from rolling.
    4. If you have access to specialized Nordic hamstring curl equipment (e.g., a Nordic hamstring curl bench or strap), you can set that up. A strap will also require using an anchor point, like a flat exercise bench, power rack, or door, depending on which type of strap you are using. Specifically how you would set up different types of specialized equipment will vary based on what you are using.
2. Securely anchor your lower legs while kneeling on a comfortable surface with an upright torso. The anchor point should snugly contact the back of your lower leg, slightly above the back of your heels.
    1. The soles of your feet should be facing backward while not contacting the ground.
    2. Your knees should be approximately hip width apart as your thighs are completely vertical.
    3. You should be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    4. Your back and neck should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    5. You can position your feet in the way that feels most comfortable with your setup. One option involves pointing your feet like when hitting the gas pedal, which will result in your toes being behind your heels. Alternatively, you can situate your feet in a closer to vertical position with your toes under your heels.
    6. You can situate your arms by your side or have your palms facing forward a little in front of your chest with your elbows bent. Wherever you position your arms should allow you to be ready to use them for a push-up motion to control the end of this exercise’s lowering phase.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and tighten your abdominal muscles to brace your core.
    1. Continue doing so throughout the lowering phase to prevent your hips from bending or your lower back from excessively arching.
2. While your lower legs remain stationary, slowly lean the rest of your body forward as one unit through motion at your knees. Lean forward as far as you can in a controlled manner,
    1. Keep your torso and hips in a rigid position, while all the movement occurs from your knees gradually transitioning to a less bent position. You can think of your knees as the center of an analog clock, while the rest of your body above your knees forms one of its hands that arcs forward beyond twelve o’clock.
    2. Throughout your descent, you should be able to continue drawing a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders. To do so, you will have to avoid bending forward at your hips or dynamically arching your lower back.
    3. The degree of difficulty required to control the descent will markedly increase as your body progressively becomes closer to horizontal.
3. Once you can no longer control the descent, extend your hands out in front of you to catch yourself through the same upper body movement you perform during the lowering phase of a push-up.
    1. You can initially contact the ground with slightly bent elbows as your arms are raised to form about 90-degree angles with your torso.
    2. How much of the descent you can control before your body starts to fall rapidly (which is known as the break point) will naturally be dependent on your hamstring strength and experience with Nordic hamstring curls. Certain individuals who are remarkably proficient at this exercise may be able to control the descent all the way to the ground without having to catch themselves with their hands, but the ability to do so is extremely rare.
4. After catching yourself and reaching the bottom of your push-up position, rock your buttocks back toward your heels while bending your hips and knees, and walking yourself back with your hands.
    1. The purpose of this step is to reposition yourself and allow you to return to the starting position rather than training your hamstrings on the way up.
    2. This standard version of the Nordic hamstring curl exclusively trains the hamstrings during the lowering phase.
5. Return to the starting position with your torso fully upright above your knees.
6. Alternate between steps 1-5 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically engaging your gluteus maximus, this exercise can train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells by your thighs with straight elbows.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hand while holding them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
    3. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    4. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    5. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back flat, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
2. Situate your palms right next to the outer aspect of the front of your thighs so that your palms face at an angle between backward and in toward each other.
    1. This results in a semi-overhand grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip that would direct your palms straight backward (with the dumbbells directly in front of your body) and a neutral grip that would direct your palms straight in toward your sides (with the dumbbells directly to the sides of your body).
    2. Keep your palms facing this way throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Bracing your core will help you maintain a rigid torso position as you subsequently move about your hips rather than your back.
2. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the dumbbells just in front of your legs. Avoid letting the dumbbells drift forward away from you, which may be more likely to occur if you think about “bending over” rather than “sitting back.”
    2. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the dumbbells.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    5. Throughout the set, keep the soles of your feet fully planted on the ground and your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
3. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the dumbbells at least below their knees, but how deeply you are able to hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be in clear control of the entire descent.
4. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so you should continue keeping your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, feet fully on the ground, and the dumbbells close to your legs.
    2. When you have finished lifting the dumbbells, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips appropriately stacked under your shoulders to prevent excessive arching.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. Once you have finished your last rep, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back flat rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.

### Learning how to hip hinge

If you have minimal experience training hip hinge exercises such as Romanian deadlift and good morning variations, it is normal to have difficulty performing this movement pattern. Like riding a bicycle or shooting a basketball, hip hinging is a motor skill that may require repeated practice before you feel comfortable consistently using it. Broadly speaking, there are two types of movement substitution patterns that individuals who are in the process of learning how to hip hinge commonly perform when attempting to execute a hip hinge exercise.

One of these is using a stoop lift, which involves dynamically rounding the back (through flexion of the lumbar and thoracic spine) to bend over prior to uncoiling the spine into an upright position during the ascent. This often is accompanied by the buttock not moving particularly far backward and the arms drifting out in front of the legs in contrast to a strict hip hinge, which utilizes a visibly flat back position (with close to a neutral lumbar spine) and has the buttock noticeably moving backward during the descent. In terms of similarity, a stoop lift and a hip hinge both involve only a mild magnitude of knee bend during the descent while keeping the hips high. A clue that you may be performing more of a stoop lift than a hip hinge is feeling a stretching sensation in your back rather than feeling your back muscles remain statically tight throughout your set as these muscles function to maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hips. With a properly performed hip hinge, you should certainly feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the bottom of the lift, but you may also feel this stretching sensation during a stoop lift. Nonetheless, a hip hinge can allow you to feel the hamstring stretch earlier during the descent compared to a stoop lift since the hamstrings are shortened by the posterior pelvic tilt (tucking the tailbone under) that is coupled with lumbar spine flexion.

In addition to performing a stoop lift unintentionally when attempting to hip hinge, people can commonly utilize more of a squatting motion that is characterized by greater knee bend and forward tracking of the knees during the descent. Compared to a hip hinge, using more of a squatting movement can result in the buttock moving downward during the descent as opposed to backward, and the shins can also incline forward as opposed to remaining vertical. Because the hamstrings shorten as the knees bend, someone moving in a squat pattern when attempting to perform a hip hinge exercise is unlikely to feel much of a stretching sensation in the hamstrings at the bottom of the lift. Not feeling a hamstring stretch is the most straightforward indicator that you may be squatting your hip hinge exercise, but an additional clue would be if you perceive your quads to be working (e.g., feeling a “burning” sensation or lots of tension) more intensely than your hamstrings during the exercise.

When thinking about moving through hip hinge, stoop lift, and squat patterns, it is important to recognize that these movements can occur along a spectrum. You can perform a hip hinge exercise with a technique that is mostly a hip hinge while still utilizing elements of a stoop lift or a squat that may not be ideal for what you wish to accomplish from the exercise. Similarly, your technique can incrementally progress from being stoop lift-dominant or squat-dominant to becoming progressively more of a hip hinge as your body learns how to move in a different way through repeated practice. If your hip hinge technique currently has much room for improvement, it can be beneficial to start with lighter loads that may not be especially challenging to your muscles until you developed a reasonable level of competency with the movement itself. There is no need to let perfect be the enemy of good, so you do not need to wait to load a movement meaningfully until you have immaculate technique, but it would also be inadvisable to push your body to the limit without initially learning the basic fundamentals of the movement.

Depending on which element of hip hinging in which you currently are challenged, certain external cues can expedite how quickly you can learn how to hinge. When unintentionally moving in a manner that involves a greater degree of squatting or stoop lifting than is intended, a wall may help provide tactile feedback to cue sitting your buttock backward. To do so, you can perform your hip hinge with your back facing a nearby wall, and you will be able to receive confirmation as to whether you are actually sitting your hips backward by feeling the wall contact your buttock at the end of the descent. As an initial starting point, you can set up with a heel-to-toe tandem stance as the back of one heel contacts the wall. Then you can bring your rear foot forward to be aligned with your front foot in the symmetrical stance you will utilize for your hip hinge. This can provide a reasonable approximation as to how far people may commonly be able to sit their hips back when hinging, but you can adjust from there how far away you position yourself from the wall to individualize it to you.

Specifically if you have a tendency to perform a “squatty” hip hinge, practicing your hip hinge with your calves up against something like a flat exercise bench (or even a couch or chair at home) can be a rather productive means of honing your hinge. As you descend, you will receive immediate tactile feedback if your knees track forward from no longer feeling the contact with your calves, so it can be a great way to reinforce keeping your shins vertical throughout the movement. When using this method, make sure that you maintain full contact between the soles of your feet and the ground while you avoid leaning your bodyweight into the bench to the extent that you would fall backward if the bench was removed. If you successfully keep your calves in contact with the bench throughout the movement and feel a pronounced stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the end of the descent, you are right on track to achieving a high quality hip hinge.

As an additional learning tool, you can situate a long object like a dowel rod, PVC pipe, or yardstick on your back while hip hinging if you have a tendency to utilize more of a stoop lift technique with excessive back rounding. Either the calf-to-bench technique or the buttock-to-wall technique can be incorporated into an actual Romanian deadlift where you use weights, but using a rod as a cue without a partner will require you to hold the rod in place, so you would not be able to load this drill independently. To perform this movement, you would hold the rod behind against your back with one hand higher up near your head and your other hand lower down near your buttock. While standing fully upright and looking straight ahead, you should feel the rod contacting your sacrum (the bone that lies between your upper buttock cheeks and right above your tailbone), upper back between your shoulder blades, and the back of your head. You will then hip hinge while maintaining these three points of contact throughout the entirety of the movement. If you start rounding your back or moving your neck beyond the neutral position you adopt when standing fully upright, you will feel the rod lose contact with one of those three spots. This can be a rather effective form of instant feedback to help develop your ability to move predominantly through your lower body while keeping your trunk in a rigid position throughout the movement, which can transfer to improved technique for an array of different exercises. When maintaining those three contact points, you potentially can still perform a squatting motion rather than a hip hinge since moving through your lower body while minimizing back motion is also a component of squatting technique. However, if you maintain those three contact points and additionally feel your hamstrings notably stretched at the bottom of the descent, those two factors indicate that you have successfully hip hinged in all likelihood. If you find that the rod cuing shifts your movement from a stoop lift to more of a squat, you can use the rod cuing simultaneously with the calf-to-bench cuing, which can address multiple aspects of the hip hinge concurrently.

In the context of performing a hip hinge exercise, using a form that resembles more of a squat or stoop lift rather than a hip hinge can be considered technique errors, but squatting and stoop lifting are by no means faulty movement patterns inherently. They simply differ from a hip hinge, so they can be suboptimal means of performing an exercise that you intend to train specifically for the benefits that hip hinging can provide as opposed to other movements. The hip hinge can take some practice to learn, but investing the time and effort into doing so can be a prudent investment given that having the motor skill of hip hinging can meaningfully expand your freedom in how you can move both inside and outside of the gym.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your lower body or lower back strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your lower body and back muscles. Among trained lifters, grip strength can very commonly limit performance of Romanian deadlifts if they do not utilize straps since the posterior chain has the potential to move rather heavy loads for this exercise. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least a few minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.

### Romanian deadlift vs stiff-leg deadlift

The terms “Romanian deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” are often used interchangeably to describe the same exercise by scholarly articles and lifters in general. The nomenclature of the Romanian deadlift has been attributed to this exercise being performed by Nicu Vlad, a Romanian weightlifter who won gold, silver, and bronze medals respectively in the 1984, 1988, and 1996 Olympic games in addition to medaling in seven World Weightlifting Championships (with three of them being gold). American weightlifting coach Jim Schmitz reportedly saw Vlad performing the barbell hip hinge exercise and subsequently called it the Romanian deadlift since Vlad did not have a particular name for the variation he was performing.

While they may be commonly used to refer to the same exercise, some people will use the names “Romanian deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” to refer to different variations. They consider the stiff-leg deadlift to be what is described in this database as the “barbell stiff-leg deadlift from floor,” which involves each rep starting with the plates fully on the floor like a conventional deadlift. This variation differs from the Romanian deadlift, which starts from the upright position and uses a range of motion individualized to someone’s flexibility as the plates often do not contact the floor. If someone has the hamstring flexibility for the plates to contact the floor during a Romanian deadlift, a light tap to the ground is utilized, but the full weight of the bar is not transferred into the ground like how the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor is performed. In contrast to the Romanian deadlift, which is typically performed with a controlled lowering phase, this conception of the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor often involves a quicker lower phase with a tempo akin to how conventional deadlifts are commonly performed, which is to say a controlled drop. Both variations can involve a mild amount of knee flexion at the bottom of the hinge, but the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor can require that someone adopts a more flexed lumbar position in order to pull from the floor with the knee position while maintaining high hips. This differs from the Romanian deadlift being performed only through the range of motion where someone can maintain close to a neutral lumbar spine or a flat back position. 

As demonstrated by sources such as Hegyi et al. (2018) along with Piper and Waller (2001), another interpretation of the stiff-leg deadlift also exists as a truly straight leg deadlift. Under this conception of the exercise, the knees are kept extended to the extent that they are completely straight but not fully locked (which could otherwise allow for knee hyperextension in individuals with the mobility to do so). Such a technique would result in a movement that is more akin to “bending over” rather than “sitting back,” and the bar would correspondingly be likely to drift further in front of the legs compared to a Romanian deadlift, which utilizes a mild knee bend that allows you to “sit back.” In light of the different ways in which these names may be interpreted, hopefully this note provides some clarification if you have ever been confused by how different individuals may view Romanian deadlifts and stiff-leg deadlifts exercises as the same exercise or distinct variations.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your side with the side of your body that you are about to train facing up.
    1. In this side-lying position, you can rest the bottom side of your head on the hand of your bottom arm for comfort by situating your upper arm out to the side with your elbow bent.
    2. For extra stability, you can bend the elbow of your top arm and place that side’s hand on the ground in front of your torso.
2. Bend your knees by approximately 90 degrees and bring your thighs forward so they form near a 45-60-degree angle with your torso.
    1. Based on preference and what feels comfortable to you, you can position your thighs further forward so that they are closer to forming a right angle with your torso, or you can position them further backward so that they are closer to being aligned with your torso.
    2. In this position, the inner aspects of your knees and feet should be in contact with each other.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your top leg’s knee upward away from your bottom knee while keeping the inner aspect of your feet in contact with each other and the rest of your body stationary.
    1. Move through your hip’s available range of motion, and avoid rolling your hips backward during this exercise, which is a common technical error.
    2. To help prevent your hips from rolling, you can imagine that you are wearing a belt with a buckle that has a laser beam shooting out of it. Strive to keep the laser beam pointing straight forward, parallel to the ground, throughout the movement.
    3. If you start to roll your hips backward, your belt buckle’s laser beam will start to angle upward, the exercise will feel easier, and you will be able to lift your knee higher up than if you had avoided rolling backward.
    4. While performing this movement with the appropriate technique, you will likely feel the muscles on the side of your top hip contracting.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your top knee to descend in a controlled manner until it is low enough to contact the inner aspect of your lower knee.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side by lying on your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains the upper region of your gluteus maximus and the short hip external rotator muscles situated underneath your glutes.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent, as the soles of your feet are flat on the ground, approximately hip width apart.
    1. You can rest your head on the ground.
    2. You can optionally hook the tops of your feet under a sturdy object, such as a couch (while your soles remain flat on the ground), to help stabilize your body during this exercise.
2. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the ground while you lift your head and shoulder blades up off the ground by curling your middle back.
    1. Given that this is a crunch rather than a sit-up, the lower half of your back should remain in contact with the ground while you only lift up the upper half of your back mildly off the floor.
    2. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    3. As a cue, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the top of your hip bones, which is the action that contraction of your abdominal muscles can produce.
    4. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that crunching up any further would require the lower half of your back to lift off the floor.
    5. At the end of the lifting phase, your chest should be facing at an angle between forward and upward, rather than only facing upward as it did in the starting position.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your middle back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your head and shoulder blades back to the starting position on the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your obliques and especially your rectus abdominis, which is the “six-pack muscle.”## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to hold the handles comfortably with bent elbows once you are seated.
    1. If your machine has elbow pads, the proper seat height should also permit you to rest your elbows on them comfortably while grasping the handles.
    2. The appropriate setting should allow you to keep your arms mostly relaxed when grasping the handles.
    3. You should not have to shrug your shoulders up to reach the handles.
    4. Some trial and error may be required to find a seat height that feels appropriate for you.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit on the seat with your back snugly against the back pad as your lower back is comfortably arched.
    1. The degree to which you can comfortably arch your lower back can be affected by your mobility.
    2. Starting with an appropriate degree of lower back arching will situate your abs in a lengthened position that can facilitate training them through an extended range of motion during this exercise.
4. Position your feet in the stable position permitted by your machine, which will vary based on the specific model you are using.
    1. This may involve hooking your feet snugly under the foot roller pads, situating your feet on foot pegs, resting your feet on a foot plate, or placing your feet directly on the ground.
    2. Machines that allow your legs to move during the exercise will normally have foot roller pads, and certain machines allow you to adjust them. When using one of these models, you can situate the front of your ankles against the back of the pads and the top of your feet against the bottom of the pads
    3. Ultimately, you want to set up in a manner that allows your feet to feel stable throughout the exercise.
5. Reach up and grasp the handles with your palms facing in toward each other. If your machine has elbow pads, rest your elbows against them as well.
    1. In the starting position, your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl your spine as you pull the bottom of your ribs toward the tops of your hip bones in a controlled manner.
    1. Once you feel that you can no longer curl your spine through a range permitted by the machine that feels comfortable to your back, you have reached your end range. The machine may physically restrict your range of motion at a certain point, even if you have the mobility to crunch further.
    2. Depending on the specific model you are using, the bottom of the machine may lift up with your legs as you crunch, or it may remain stationary with your legs not moving. When using the former category of machines, moving the top of the machine through the crunching action will naturally prompt your knees and feet to lift upward, even if you do not consciously try to march your knees up. If you wish to train your hip flexors simultaneously with your abs, you can also intentionally think about driving your knees up, but make sure that you do so in addition to curling your spine rather than as an alternative to curling your spine.
    3. Avoid pulling down with your arms. Your arms should only move because they are attached to your torso, which is what you are directly moving. The function of your arms is merely to transmit force from your abs into the machine. You should not feel your back muscles contract like they would during a pulling exercise (such as a straight arm lat pulldown). If you feel like your back muscles are driving the movement, you can try loosening your grip to the minimum required to hold the handles in a stable fashion.
    4. To train your abs dynamically, you should be intentionally rounding your entire back, since this motion is performed by contraction of your abs. When rounding your back appropriately, you should feel meaningful tension in your abs. If you do not feel your abs engaging, you may be substituting hip or shoulder motion for the intended spinal motion.
    5. If you finish the movement with your back fairly flat as opposed to rounded, you most likely are primarily bending at your hips rather than curling your spine.
    6. It can help to think about crunching your elbows toward your knees.
    7. You can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the middle of your chest, and you are trying to point that closer toward the middle point between your knees.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner as you uncurl your back by flaring your ribs up and away from your hip bones.
    1. Move through the full range of motion permitted by the machine that feels comfortable to your lower back. This may involve allowing the weights you selected to tap the rest of the stack lightly at the end of this movement if the full range that the machine allows feels comfortable to you.
    2. When uncurling your spine, your lower back will initially move into a flat position before moving into an arched position.
    3. Depending on your mobility and the range of motion your machine permits, you may feel a stretching sensation in your abs as you allow your lower back to arch.
    4. The imaginary laser beam shooting out from your chest should angle progressively higher up as you uncurl your back and flare your ribs away from your hip bones.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you feel lower back discomfort at the end of either step, you can abbreviate the range of motion to what feels best to you. Performing this exercise through a full range of motion is by no means inherently injurious to the lower back, but certain individuals with pre-existing conditions may experience discomfort in rather rounded or rather arched positions.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that will allow your legs to be in an approximately horizontal position once you are in the starting position.
    1. After estimating where this setting may be, you can adjust it during later steps if warranted.
    2. Not all machines permit you to adjust the footplate height.
2. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow the pad to support your thighs without contacting your hip bones when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. The appropriate position should allow your hips to move freely while sufficiently stabilizing your legs.
    2. If the footplate is positioned excessively far back (resulting in your hips resting on the pad), the pad can restrict your hip motion and prompt you to perform the exercise via spinal motion.
    3. If the footplate is positioned excessively far forward to the extent that your knees are near the middle of the pad rather than your thighs, the exercise may provoke knee discomfort in some people.
    4. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position.
3. Climb up onto the machine, and position your feet against the footplate with your lower legs between the leg rollers while the front of your hips are resting on the pad.
    1. The back of your lower legs should be snugly pressed into the top pair of leg rollers slightly above your heels.
    2. Your knees should be straight, and your soles should be firmly contacting the footplate.
    3. While you are setting your feet, you can use the handles for assistance.
    4. Some machines have a step that you can use to help you get into position.
    5. With the appropriate footplate setting, the front of your hip bones should not be resting on the pad.
4. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes to keep your torso up such that you can draw approximately a straight line from your legs through your hips and to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your lower back and neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. This is the starting position of the exercise, where your body should look like it does when normally standing upright, except rotated to be horizontal.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips.
3. Without rounding your back, hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner to angle your torso downward.
    1. As opposed to bending over at your back, nearly all the motion should occur from you moving at your hips as you statically keep your trunk near a neutral position with your lower back mildly arched or flat.
    2. Maintain a straight knee position, but avoid intentionally locking your knees out by squeezing your quads.
    3. While hinging at your hips and keeping your hands on your shoulders, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings that progressively intensifies.
    4. How deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility. If you have the flexibility to hinge until your torso is completely vertical, stop once you reach this point. Otherwise, stop descending once you feel like you have reached the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch.
    5. If you initially did not position the footplate sufficiently forward, the pad may alternatively limit how deeply you can hinge.
4. Reverse the movement by lifting your torso up through your hips and squeezing your glutes.
    1. When moving through your hips, your entire torso should move together as one unit. You can think of your hips as the center of an analog clock, while your rigid torso is a hand that rotates about the center.
    2. At the end of the lifting phase, your torso should be close to a horizontal position aligned with your legs.
    3. Avoid arching your lower back excessively at the top since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the hip motion you just performed during the lowering phase. Focusing on finishing the movement by squeezing your glutes can help prevent excessive back arching.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
6. After your last rep, you can grasp the handles to help you dismount the machine.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
Standing pin-loaded glute kickback machines usually have an adjustable chest pad that permits a fairly upright torso position. A minority of machines lack a chest and have a fixed forearm pad as an alternative, which involves a more leaned-forward torso position to use. The exercise technique with either machine can be essentially the same, but certain setup details differ, which is why two different setup sections are provided for you to select based on the specific type of machine you have.

### Setting up a machine with an adjustable chest pad

1. Adjust the chest pad to a position that allows you to rest your chest comfortably on it while holding the handles after leaning forward onto the pad.
    1. The appropriate setting should allow you to feel stable and well-supported during the exercise.
    2. Some trial and error may be required to find a position that feels best to your body.
2. If your machine has a narrow adjustable foot bar (that can be moved to the right or left side), set it to the side you intend to train during this set.
    1. Other machine models have a wide foot bar that does not need to be adjusted to train your right or left sides.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, and stand directly next to the chest pad facing the handles.
    1. Depending on the machine you have, you may be able to stand with your feet hip-width apart, or you may have to angle your working leg wider out to the side if the foot bar is underneath where you would otherwise place that leg.
4. Lean forward at your hips to rest your chest on the chest pad, and firmly grasp the handles. Rest your forearms on the forearm pads if your machine has these pads.
    1. When you lean forward, keep your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright. You should lean forward at your hips rather than simply rounding over at your back.
    2. How far you have to lean forward will be influenced by your machine model and body type. With certain machines, only a rather slight forward lean may be needed to contact the chest pad, while others may involve a greater degree of forward lean.
5. On the side you intend to train, bend your knee to lift your foot off the ground, and position the back half of its sole in contact with the foot bar.
    1. Based on your preference and what feels stable to you, you can contact the foot bar with your heel or a little closer to your midfoot. The appropriate foot position should ensure that your foot does not slide during the exercise while also helping you to feel meaningful tension in your glute.
    2. When viewed from the front, your foot should be aligned with your knee rather than being positioned to either the right or left side of your knee.
    3. With a machine that has the foot bar starting under you, you will have to march your working side’s knee upward and forward to reach the foot bar. With a machine that has the foot bar starting behind you, you may be able to reach the foot bar by simply bending your knee without marching your thigh up.
    4. Keep the foot of your non-working leg firmly planted on the ground from this point onward.
    5. All the subsequent directions will apply to your working leg.

### Setting up a machine with a fixed forearm pad

1. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
2. Stand directly in front of the foot bar, facing the handles, with your feet hip-width apart.
    1. The machine may have a pair of footprint outlines indicating where it is appropriate to stand initially.
3. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, lean forward at your hips to rest your forearms on the forearm pad and grasp the handles.
    1. If you feel that you have to reach forward excessively to hold the handles (which may be more likely for short individuals), move your feet forward until you find that you can comfortably hold the handles and rest on the forearm pad. Because the pad is not adjustable, you have to modify the stance if the initial position does not suit your body.
    2. The appropriate position should allow you to feel stable throughout the exercise. Avoid walking any further forward than is needed to feel stable, since the exercise’s range of motion shortens when moving further away from the foot bar.
    3. When you lean forward, keep your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright. You should lean forward at your hips rather than simply rounding over at your back.
4. On the side you intend to train, bend your knee to lift your foot off the ground, and position the back half of its sole in contact with the foot bar.
    1. Based on your preference and what feels stable to you, you can contact the foot bar with your heel or a little closer to your midfoot. The appropriate foot position should ensure that your foot does not slide during the exercise while also helping you to feel meaningful tension in your glute.
    2. When viewed from the front, your foot should be aligned with your knee rather than being positioned to either the right or left side of your knee.
    3. Keep the foot of your non-working leg firmly planted on the ground from this point onward.
    4. All the subsequent directions will apply to your working leg.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your knee backward in an arcing manner to push your heel and the foot bar backward through the full range of motion that your hip mobility permits.
    1. Allow your knee to straighten naturally while focusing on moving your knee backward through hip motion.
    2. How far you personally can drive your knee backward will be influenced by your hip mobility.
    3. Strive to have all the movement come from your working leg while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
    4. During the entire set, keep your chest snugly against the chest pad and your imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead.
    5. Particularly avoid excessively arching your lower back or turning your imaginary belt buckle to the side, both of which are common technique errors that can occur when striving to push the foot bar further backward than what your hip mobility permits.
    6. At the end range, you should feel your glute contract. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glute engage during this exercise and help cue the intended hip motion.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your knee to arc forward while naturally bending.
    1. Avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your knee forward in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your knee positioned as far forward as the machine permits while keeping your foot on the foot bar.
    3. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack without allowing the weights to rest on the stack.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the barbell situated on the ground, stand upright close in front of the middle of the bar as the bar is approximately over the middle of your feet.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
    2. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of 45lb (20kg) plates (or 25kg plates common in gyms outside the USA) or bumper plates will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. When only using smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a deficit sumo deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize the largest diameter plates, you can perform a sumo block pull or rack pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with large diameter plates.
2. Spread your legs apart into a wide stance that is clearly outside shoulder width, with your feet moderately rotated out away from your sides.
    1. Provided that your feet are wider apart than your shoulders but closer together than the plates, you can use a range of stance widths for your sumo deadlift.
    2. The ideal stance width can meaningfully vary among different individuals based on someone’s body structure and hip mobility.
    3. Trying out different foot positions will normally be required to determine which stance specifically feels best to you.
    4. As a starting point, you can widen your feet as far apart as you feel comfortable and stable while being able to push your knees out approximately over your heels, resulting in close to vertical shins. You will push your knees out when descending to reach the bar, so subsequent aspects of the setup can help you gauge if you should adjust your stance.
    5. The ideal amount of toe flare also varies among different lifters, but you can start with your toes pointing about 30-45 degrees out to the side before adjusting from there based on what feels best to you. Zero degrees of toe flare would mean your toes are pointing straight forward, while 90 degrees would mean your toes are pointing directly out to your side.
3. Direct your arms straight down toward the ground with straight elbows as your shoulders are packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up).
    1. Maintain this position from here onward.
4. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips while bending your knees and pushing your knees out until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees, and your knees should essentially not track forward (although a slight degree of forward knee tracking can be used if that feels best to you).
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Try to push your knees far enough out to the sides that they are vertically stacked over your heels.
    4. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    5. Similarly, keep your chest up in a “proud chest” posture and your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    6. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
    7. The bar should be directly in front of your shins.
5. With your shoulders stacked vertically above the bar, firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath). This should expand the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles, like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360-degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. Continue bracing your core throughout your set. This will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. This can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” by extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. Keep your chest up, back flat, and knees pushed out.
    2. As your hips and chest rise at the same rate, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull. Avoid letting your torso incline further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
    3. Keep the bar very close to your legs throughout the rep.
    4. Try to avoid slowing down the start of your sumo deadlift just because you know you will be pausing very soon. You ideally should start your rep just like you would when performing a standard sumo deadlift.
4. Just after the plates lift off the floor, pause in that position for a brief period of time (e.g., 1-2 seconds) while staying tight with your chest up and back flat.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability as you visibly remain stationary.
5. Resume “pushing the floor away” until the bar reaches near your knees. Then focus on thrusting your hips forward until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back when viewed from the side.
6. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips and bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your hips back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally. After the bar has cleared your knees, you can further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
    2. Continue keeping your back flat and chest up.
7. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again. Then continue to alternate between steps 2-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the ground.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While also training your spinal extensor muscles, this exercise can train much of your lower body musculature, including your gluteus maximus, quads, and hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. Situate a barbell in front of a sufficiently anchored flat exercise bench (or viable alternative). The length of the bar should be parallel to the length of the bench.
    1. A specific hip thrust bench is ideal to use, but it is not commonly available.
    2. If you use a mobile exercise bench, make sure that it will not slide back during the exercise. To do so, you can position it against a power rack. Alternatively, you can position the bench against a plyometric box or bumper plates that are contacting a wall. When using a wall to anchor the bench, the intermediate objects are required to provide clearance for your head.
    3. You may also be able to use a soft plyometric box near knee height that is anchored in the same manner as a flat bench.
    4. What ultimately matters is selecting an object that is sturdy, stable, and reasonably close to being knee height while being comfortable enough to support your upper back.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the barbell.
    1. The diameter of the weight plates you select can influence how easily you can set up.
    2. It will be easiest to assume the starting position with at least one pair of full diameter plates, such as typical 45lb (20kg) Olympic plates or bumper plates. With full diameter plates, certain individuals who are very thick may need to utilize one of the strategies discussed below to set up under the bar.
    3. If you exclusively load the bar with smaller diameter plates, you may have to modify the standard setup in order to position your hips under the bar. When only using smaller diameter plates, you can elevate the bar just enough to fit your hips under it by situating the plates on top of deadlift blocks, aerobic steps, or stacks of full diameter weight plates. Alternatively, you may have a partner place the bar on top of your hip bones and load it with weight plates after you have assumed the starting position. If neither of these approaches is practical to you, you lack bumper plates that allow you to elevate the bar fully with light loads, and you do not currently have the strength to use 135lb (or 60kg) for this exercise, you have the option of performing other hip thrust variations. For instance, you can select a single leg bodyweight hip thrust or a dumbbell hip thrust to train the glutes in essentially the same manner as the barbell variation, but with an easier setup.
3. Optionally, obtain a pad (or an object that can function as a pad) that you will place between the bar and your hips.
    1. Using a pad is not required but strongly recommended because it can be very uncomfortable to have a barbell directly resting on your hip bones with a meaningful load.
    2. You can wrap a hip thrust pad or a squat pad around the middle of the bar.
    3. Alternatively, you can place a thick foam or folded exercise mat on the crease of your hip during the next step and subsequently keep it there to cushion your hip bones. Be aware that foam pads not specifically designed for hip thrusts may be somewhat damaged from this exercise.
4. Sit down on the ground with your upper back against the edge of the bench and your legs straight out in front of you under the bar.
    1. Specifically place the lower part of your shoulder blades against the edge of the bench. Feel free to adjust your upper back positioning mildly from this point based on what feels most stable to you.
    2. The middle of the bar should be aligned with the midline of your body.
5. Grasp the bar with your palms facing down and roll it toward the crease of your hips.
    1. So long as your hands are wider apart than your hips, you can adjust your grip width based on what feels stable and comfortable to you during this exercise.
    2. Continue holding the bar from this point onward.
6. Tuck your chin mildly toward your chest, and maintain this neck position throughout the exercise.
7. Bring your heels toward your buttocks by bending your knees greater than 90 degrees with the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can situate your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, but it is up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can opt to point your toes forward or somewhat out to the sides.
    3. The optimal degree of knee bend should allow your feet to feel stable while being firmly planted on the ground throughout the set. It is normally appropriate to position your feet far enough back that your knees will be bent by approximately 90 degrees at the top of your hip thrust. From this placement, you can adjust specifically where you situate your feet based on your preference.
8. Confirm that the bar is directly above the crease of your hips and completely centered before initiating the set.
    1. Positioning the bar appropriately will help you avoid the excessive discomfort that may arise from positioning the bar too high (i.e., directly on your lower abdomen) or too low (i.e., directly on your groin).
    2. Some people may experience discomfort when the bar looks aligned with the crease of their hips if the bar is directly resting on the bony bumps on the front of their hip bones. If you notice that this occurs for you during your set, you can roll the bar slightly further down away from your abdomen to shift the pressure away from these bony bumps. When making any adjustments during a set, only do so in between reps when the weights are resting on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to help stabilize your lower back.
    2. From this starting position, your back should either be flat or exhibit its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
2. Lift your hips up by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes. Keep the bar balanced on the crease of your hips the entire set.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glutes engage during this exercise.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
    5. Throughout the exercise, the soles of your feet should remain fully planted on the ground, and your upper back should remain firmly in contact with the bench.
    6. The purpose of holding the bar with your hands is to keep it stabilized on the crease of your hips. Avoid trying to lift the bar up or push it down with your hands.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your hip mobility comfortably permits without rounding or excessively arching your lower back.
    1. Some people can descend all the way down until they lightly contact the ground with the plates or their glutes, but you can prioritize lowering through the range in which you can achieve through hip motion rather than lower back motion.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly. If you hear the plates crashing into the ground, you are not lowering the bar with sufficient control.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps. The bar should come to a dead stop on the ground after each rep.
    2. After completing the lowering phase of your last rep, roll the bar forward before getting up in the reversed manner to how you set up initially.
## Description
This exercise will predominantly develop your gluteus maximus while also training your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your upper back on a flat exercise bench (or a sturdy object of similar height), your buttocks on the floor, and your feet out in front of you with your soles flat on the floor and your knees bent to at least 90 degrees.
    1. You can situate your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, but it is up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can opt to point your toes forward or somewhat out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. In this position, you can cross your arms across your chest or hold your upper arms somewhat out to your sides.
2. Tuck your chin mildly toward your chest, and maintain this neck position throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your buttock muscles.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glutes engage during this exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your hip mobility comfortably permits without rounding or excessively arching your lower back.
    1. Some people can descend all the way down until they lightly contact the ground with their glutes, but you can prioritize lowering through the range in which you can achieve through hip motion rather than lower back motion.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will predominantly develop your gluteus maximus while also training your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heels (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when lying down on the machine with your knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
2. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust its lever arm (that will align with your shins) to a position that lets you begin each rep with your knees straightened.
    1. If the lever arm is too high, you will start each rep with your knees somewhat bent rather than fully straightened. This would prevent you from training through a full range of motion.
    2. Certain machines may allow you to position the lever arm low enough that it could situate your knees beyond fully straightened into a hyperextended position (where your knees are bent in the opposite direction in which they normally bend). This may increase the likelihood of experiencing knee discomfort during this exercise.
    3. The optimal position will allow you to draw a straight line through your knees from your lower legs to your thighs once you are in the starting position.
3. Add weight plates to the machine.
4. Lie down on the body pad with your lower legs under the lower leg pad and your knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point (which will typically result in your knees hanging just off the edge of the body pad).
    1. The front of your thighs, hips, and torso should be in contact with the body pad.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
    4. In this starting position, your knees should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Pull your toes and feet back toward your shins by moving at your ankles, and maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shins contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankles in the same position in which they would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heels up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankles in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
3. Drive your heels toward your buttocks by bending your knees through the full range of motion you can perform. You should typically be able to contact your buttocks or upper thighs with the lower leg pad at the end range.
    1. While your lower legs move through motion at your knees, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
    2. Avoid lifting your hips up off the pad or excessively arching your lower back.
4. Reverse the movement as you allow your knees to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knees fully straightened.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower legs down.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground behind a sturdy object with an elevated surface, such as a flat exercise bench, stackable aerobic steps, plyometric box, stairs, or bed.
2. Lift up your legs to position your feet on top of the elevated surface with your knees bent by at least 90 degrees.
    1. If you are too far away from the elevated surface to achieve this bent knee position, you can scoot forward until you can do so.
    2. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (by scooting toward the elevated surface) is up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    3. Based on what feels stable to you, you can either position the back of your heels on top of the elevated surface or position the middle of your soles on the corner of the elevated surface.
    4. You can situate your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, but it is also up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    5. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can opt to point your toes forward or somewhat out to the sides.
    6. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
3. While leaving one foot on the elevated surface, lift the other foot off the elevated surface by marching that side’s thigh up while bending your knee, or by lifting that leg a little above the elevated surface while keeping your knee straight.
    1. Either position for the free leg is similarly viable, so you can go with whichever you prefer, but keeping the knee straight may make the exercise mildly more challenging compared to keeping it bent.
    2. Keep one foot off the elevated surface or ground in the static position of your choice throughout the set you perform with your other side.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your foot into the elevated surface and squeezing your glute.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side (on the side of your body with the foot contacting the surface). Your thigh and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glute.
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glute engage.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to contact the ground with your buttocks lightly.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. With a cable station’s pulley positioned at its lowest height, connect a rope attachment and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. In a near shoulder-width stance, stand above the rope with your back toward the cable station.
3. Squat down or hinge at your hips to grasp the ends of the rope firmly with your palms facing in toward each other and your elbows straightened.
    1. For a particularly stable grip, you can place the outside of your index fingers in contact with the flat surface of the hard plastic end caps of the rope attachment.
    2. Whether you wrap your thumbs around your other fingers or place your thumbs on top of the rope’s end caps will be based on your personal preference.
4. With your arms directed down toward the floor, take a few steps forward so that you can feel resistance from the cable station during the pull-through you are about to perform.
    1. After walking forward, you can adopt a near shoulder-width stance or a wider stance based on what feels comfortable and stable to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. After the deep “belly breath,” you can continue thinking about tensing the front of your abdominals, sides of your abdominals, and lower back muscles simultaneously in a manner that allows you to form a “360-degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. As a helpful cue for your “360-degree brace,” you can tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you are bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    4. Bracing will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hips throughout your set.
2. Hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner to move your buttocks backward while you allow your knees to bend mildly.
    1. Continue keeping your soles flat on the floor, elbows straightened, core braced, and lower back near a neutral position while doing so.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain close to vertical throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened.
    4. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    5. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    6. While you hinge at your hips, the rope will naturally move backward, and your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    7. How deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    8. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit your buttocks any farther back, you have finished hinging through your available range of motion.
    9. Tension from the cable should be present throughout the entire movement, so if you lose resistance from the cable while sitting back, you should take another step forward after completing the rep and returning to an upright position.
3. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back and neck near a neutral position while you move through your hips.
    2. If you wish to do so, you can squeeze your glutes for a moment at the end of this motion.
    3. When completing your rep, avoid excessively arching your back into a position that is hyperextended beyond the natural curvature of your spine (like you have when normally standing upright).
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to walk backward with the rope before setting it down.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.

### **Learning how to hip hinge**

If you have minimal experience training hip hinge exercises such as Romanian deadlift and good morning variations, it is normal to have difficulty performing this movement pattern. Like riding a bicycle or shooting a basketball, hip hinging is a motor skill that may require repeated practice before you feel comfortable consistently using it. Broadly speaking, there are two types of movement substitution patterns that individuals who are in the process of learning how to hip hinge commonly perform when attempting to execute a hip hinge exercise.

One of these is using a stoop lift, which involves dynamically rounding the back (through flexion of the lumbar and thoracic spine) to bend over prior to uncoiling the spine into an upright position during the ascent. This often is accompanied by the buttock not moving particularly far backward and the arms drifting out in front of the legs in contrast to a strict hip hinge, which utilizes a visibly flat back position (with close to a neutral lumbar spine) and has the buttock noticeably moving backward during the descent. In terms of similarity, a stoop lift and a hip hinge both involve only a mild magnitude of knee bend during the descent while keeping the hips high. A clue that you may be performing more of a stoop lift than a hip hinge is feeling a stretching sensation in your back rather than feeling your back muscles remain statically tight throughout your set as these muscles function to maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hips. With a properly performed hip hinge, you should certainly feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the bottom of the lift, but you may also feel this stretching sensation during a stoop lift. Nonetheless, a hip hinge can allow you to feel the hamstring stretch earlier during the descent compared to a stoop lift since the hamstrings are shortened by the posterior pelvic tilt (tucking the tailbone under) that is coupled with lumbar spine flexion.

In addition to performing a stoop lift unintentionally when attempting to hip hinge, people can commonly utilize more of a squatting motion that is characterized by greater knee bend and forward tracking of the knees during the descent. Compared to a hip hinge, using more of a squatting movement can result in the buttock moving downward during the descent as opposed to backward, and the shins can also incline forward as opposed to remaining vertical. Because the hamstrings shorten as the knees bend, someone moving in a squat pattern when attempting to perform a hip hinge exercise is unlikely to feel much of a stretching sensation in the hamstrings at the bottom of the lift. Not feeling a hamstring stretch is the most straightforward indicator that you may be squatting your hip hinge exercise, but an additional clue would be if you perceive your quads to be working (e.g., feeling a “burning” sensation or lots of tension) more intensely than your hamstrings during the exercise.

When thinking about moving through hip hinge, stoop lift, and squat patterns, it is important to recognize that these movements can occur along a spectrum. You can perform a hip hinge exercise with a technique that is mostly a hip hinge while still utilizing elements of a stoop lift or a squat that may not be ideal for what you wish to accomplish from the exercise. Similarly, your technique can incrementally progress from being stoop lift-dominant or squat-dominant to becoming progressively more of a hip hinge as your body learns how to move in a different way through repeated practice. If your hip hinge technique currently has much room for improvement, it can be beneficial to start with lighter loads that may not be especially challenging to your muscles until you developed a reasonable level of competency with the movement itself. There is no need to let perfect be the enemy of good, so you do not need to wait to load a movement meaningfully until you have immaculate technique, but it would also be inadvisable to push your body to the limit without initially learning the basic fundamentals of the movement.

Depending on which element of hip hinging in which you currently are challenged, certain external cues can expedite how quickly you can learn how to hinge. When unintentionally moving in a manner that involves a greater degree of squatting or stoop lifting than is intended, a wall may help provide tactile feedback to cue sitting your buttock backward. To do so, you can perform your hip hinge with your back facing a nearby wall, and you will be able to receive confirmation as to whether you are actually sitting your hips backward by feeling the wall contact your buttock at the end of the descent. As an initial starting point, you can set up with a heel-to-toe tandem stance as the back of one heel contacts the wall. Then you can bring your rear foot forward to be aligned with your front foot in the symmetrical stance you will utilize for your hip hinge. This can provide a reasonable approximation as to how far people may commonly be able to sit their hips back when hinging, but you can adjust from there how far away you position yourself from the wall to individualize it to you.

Specifically if you have a tendency to perform a “squatty” hip hinge, practicing your hip hinge with your calves up against something like a flat exercise bench (or even a couch or chair at home) can be a rather productive means of honing your hinge. As you descend, you will receive immediate tactile feedback if your knees track forward from no longer feeling the contact with your calves, so it can be a great way to reinforce keeping your shins vertical throughout the movement. When using this method, make sure that you maintain full contact between the soles of your feet and the ground while you avoid leaning your bodyweight into the bench to the extent that you would fall backward if the bench was removed. If you successfully keep your calves in contact with the bench throughout the movement and feel a pronounced stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the end of the descent, you are right on track to achieving a high quality hip hinge.

As an additional learning tool, you can situate a long object like a dowel rod, PVC pipe, or yardstick on your back while hip hinging if you have a tendency to utilize more of a stoop lift technique with excessive back rounding. Either the calf-to-bench technique or the buttock-to-wall technique can be incorporated into an actual Romanian deadlift where you use weights, but using a rod as a cue without a partner will require you to hold the rod in place, so you would not be able to load this drill independently. To perform this movement, you would hold the rod behind against your back with one hand higher up near your head and your other hand lower down near your buttock. While standing fully upright and looking straight ahead, you should feel the rod contacting your sacrum (the bone that lies between your upper buttock cheeks and right above your tailbone), upper back between your shoulder blades, and the back of your head. You will then hip hinge while maintaining these three points of contact throughout the entirety of the movement. If you start rounding your back or moving your neck beyond the neutral position you adopt when standing fully upright, you will feel the rod lose contact with one of those three spots. This can be a rather effective form of instant feedback to help develop your ability to move predominantly through your lower body while keeping your trunk in a rigid position throughout the movement, which can transfer to improved technique for an array of different exercises. When maintaining those three contact points, you potentially can still perform a squatting motion rather than a hip hinge since moving through your lower body while minimizing back motion is also a component of squatting technique. However, if you maintain those three contact points and additionally feel your hamstrings notably stretched at the bottom of the descent, those two factors indicate that you have successfully hip hinged in all likelihood. If you find that the rod cuing shifts your movement from a stoop lift to more of a squat, you can use the rod cuing simultaneously with the calf-to-bench cuing, which can address multiple aspects of the hip hinge concurrently.

In the context of performing a hip hinge exercise, using a form that resembles more of a squat or stoop lift rather than a hip hinge can be considered technique errors, but squatting and stoop lifting are by no means faulty movement patterns inherently. They simply differ from a hip hinge, so they can be suboptimal means of performing an exercise that you intend to train specifically for the benefits that hip hinging can provide as opposed to other movements. The hip hinge can take some practice to learn, but investing the time and effort into doing so can be a prudent investment given that having the motor skill of hip hinging can meaningfully expand your freedom in how you can move both inside and outside of the gym.## Setting up
1. Set the foot plate to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot point with your hips while standing on the center of the foot plate with your knees slightly bent.
    1. The pivot point will be the center of the machine’s wheel, about which the lever arm rotates.
    2. Strive to align the pivot point specifically with the bony bump (greater trochanter) that you could feel on the side of your hip.
    3. Some machines do not allow you to adjust the foot plate.
2. Position the machine’s lever arm to point straight down.
    1. This will help you adjust the machine to your body proportions before moving the lever arm into the exercise’s starting position.
3. Adjust the machine’s roller pad to be slightly above knee height when standing on the foot plate.
4. March one knee up as high as you can and note the height of your knee before lowering your leg.
5. Rotate the lever arm to arc the roller pad to the height at which you were able to march your knee. When facing the middle of the machine, you will rotate the lever away from the side you are about to train
    1. This will commonly involve rotating the lever arm up a little beyond the point at which it is parallel to the ground.
    2. The appropriate setting will enable the machine to provide resistance throughout the full range of motion your hip mobility allows.
6. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
7. Stand in the center of the foot plate while facing the roller pad to align your hips with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. The roller should be in front of your leg that is closer to the weight stack.
    2. The leg further away from the weight stack should not be in the way of the roller pad when it arcs back toward the center of the machine during the exercise.
8. On the side closer to the weight stack, position the back of your knee on top of the roller pad with your knee bent by about 90 degrees. Firmly grasp the handlebar in front of you with both hands.
    1. To move it into position, you can use your hands to pull down on the roller pad as you swing this leg in toward your other side while marching up before swinging it out onto the roller pad.
    2. The leg further away from the weight stack should be standing with a slightly bent knee as its foot is flat on the foot plate.
    3. Based on what feels most stable to you, your torso should be upright or mildly leaned forward at your hips.
    4. In this starting position, your hips and shoulders should be level.
    5. The subsequent technique directions will apply to the leg on top of the roller pad, since that is the side you will train with this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your knee down and back in an arcing manner through your hip’s full range of motion. Keep your knee bent throughout your set.
    1. Most people should be able to finish a little beyond the point at which the thigh is in a vertical position. However, how far you personally can drive your knee back will be influenced by your hip mobility.
    2. Strive to have all the motion come from your hip while keeping the rest of your body stationary. The foot of your other leg should remain firmly planted on the foot plate throughout your set.
    3. In particular, avoid excessively arching your lower back, which is a common technique error that can occur when trying to move the roller pad further back than what your hip mobility permits. Appropriately bracing your core can help limit unintended arching.
    4. At the end range, you should feel your glute contract. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glute engage during this exercise.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by bringing your knee forward and upward in an arcing motion.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your knee bent and fully lifted up in front of you.
    2. Avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your knee forward in an uncontrolled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. To train your other side, you will face the opposite direction.
## Description
While training other lower body muscles to a lesser degree, this exercise will predominantly develop your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. If you wish to use a precise snatch grip rather than a general approximation, you can start by determining your individual hand placement with an empty barbell or dowel rod.
    1. A true snatch grip results in the bar being level with the crease of your hip when holding the bar in a fully upright position.
    2. You can find which hand placement specifically places the bar in the aforementioned position by trying out different grip widths with an empty barbell or dowel rod until determining what precisely is your individual snatch grip width.
    3. As a general starting point, you can see where a bar is situated relative to your hip crease when grasping it with your arms spread out away from your sides with straight elbows so that each arm forms close to a 45-degree angle with your torso.
    4. Rather than trying out different grip widths, you can alternatively use that arm position as a general approximation to train this exercise effectively.
2. After adding weight plates to the barbell situated on the ground, stand upright close in front of the middle of the bar as the bar is over the middle of your feet.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
    2. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of 45lb (20kg) plates (or 25kg plates common in gyms outside the USA) or bumper plates will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. When only using smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a snatch grip deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize the largest diameter plates, you can perform a snatch grip block pull or rack pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with large diameter plates.
3. Situate your feet near hip width apart from each other or slightly wider, and position your toes slightly turned out to the sides or pointing straight ahead based on what feels best to you
    1. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
4. Spread your arms apart with straight elbows until your arms form close to 45-degree angles with your torso (if you opt to approximate your snatch grip width) or however far apart you determined from step 1 results in your specific snatch grip width.
5. With your arms held in that position and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track very far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift mildly forward to help you reach the bar rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
6. With your hands wide apart in the aforementioned snatch grip width, firmly grasp the bar with an overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the bar reaches near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the ground.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow you to position part of your buttocks just off the front edge of the pad when you set up with your feet on the footplate.
    1. The appropriate position should allow your thighs to rest comfortably on the pad.
    2. If the footplate is positioned excessively far from the pad, you will have to sit closer toward the middle of the pad rather than just off the front edge.
    3. If the footplate is positioned excessively close to the pad, the back of your upper thighs will not be in contact with the pad.
    4. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust the footplate height, select a height that lets you feel comfortable and secure with slightly bent knees once you are in the starting position.
    1. As a starting point, you can select a height that positions the lower pair of leg rollers level with the pad and the upper pair of leg rollers higher than the pad.
    2. Not all machines permit you to adjust the footplate height.
3. Climb up onto the machine, and hook your lower legs between the leg rollers with the top of your feet snugly pressed into the top roller as your soles are facing the footplate. Sit upright with your buttocks just hanging off the front edge of the pad.
    1. Your feet should be securely anchored so that they will not slide during the exercise. The top rollers should be just above the lowest part of your shins by your ankles, while the bottom rollers should be just under your Achilles tendons (which attach to the top of your heels).
    2. Depending on the machine you are using, you can step up onto a middle beam or a step to help you get into position.
    3. While you are setting your feet, you can reach back with your hands to hold the handles for stability.
4. Place the fingertips of each hand lightly in contact with the sides of the back of your head as your upper arms and elbows are directed out away from your sides.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, looking straight ahead. Keep your neck in this position during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Lean your torso back in a controlled manner until your torso is approximately parallel to the ground or slightly beyond this point.
    1. Allow your lower back to arch to a degree that feels comfortable to you.
    2. Specifically how much of an arch is comfortable will vary from person to person, but it is often appropriate to finish the lowering phase with your lower back arched to a similar degree as when you are normally standing upright. It is not inherently injurious to arch beyond this point, but meaningfully arching the lower back beyond this point can provoke lower back discomfort in some people.
    3. If you feel lower back discomfort at the bottom, you can strive to keep your lower back closer to a flat position with less of an arch, or you can reduce the range in which you lean back. Thinking about keeping your ribs tucked down to a greater degree as you avoid flaring them can help limit excessive arching.
2. Reverse the movement by curling your back to lift your torso up until your shoulders are vertically above your hips.
    1. To train your abs dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    2. As a cue to emphasize your abs, think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the tops of your hip bones while tightening your abs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
4. After your last rep, you can grasp the handles to help you dismount the machine.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Securely attach an ankle cuff just above the ankle of the leg you will train. Position the cuff so that one of its attachment rings is directed toward the inside of your leg.
3. Stand sideways directly next to the pulley with your working leg further away from the pulley and your feet together.
    1. For instance, if you are going to train your left side, the side of your right shoulder should be facing the cable station.
4. Take a step forward with the leg closer to the pulley.
    1. The goal is to have your non-working leg’s heel slightly in front of your working leg’s toes to provide sufficient clearance for your working leg to sweep inward across toward the pulley.
5. Bend over and connect the cable to your cuff’s attachment ring before standing upright.
    1. It will be easiest to connect the cable when your working leg is close enough to the pulley that the cable does not provide resistance when connecting it.
6. Grasp the cable station’s beam with your closer hand and continue holding it from now onward for balance assistance.
7. Take a step out to the side with your working leg against the cable’s resistance before taking a step with your other leg.
    1. Your feet should now be hip-width apart, with your non-working leg’s foot still in front of your working leg’s foot.
    2. The goal of this step is to step out just far enough so that the cable can provide resistance throughout the exercise’s full range of motion. If you notice that you lose resistance from the cable when your working leg is in the starting position of a rep, you should step a bit further out away from the pulley.
    3. Your chest and imaginary belt buckle should be pointing straight ahead.
    4. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Keep your back near this neutral position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Mildly lean your torso forward by hinging at your front leg’s hip until your working leg’s foot can lift off the ground with a straight knee.
    1. Keep your working leg’s thigh aligned with your torso when viewed from the side.
    2. To help your foot clear the ground, you can pull the top of your foot upward through ankle motion. This is the opposite motion that occurs during the lifting phase of a calf raise, and you should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract when performing it.
    3. Only lean as far forward as is required for you to be able to lift your foot off the ground.
    4. All the subsequent directions will apply to your working leg.
3. Allow your leg to sweep inward in line with the cable’s resistance while keeping your hip bones and shoulder level with each other.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your knee cap pointing straight ahead rather than rotating it inward or outward.
    2. Allow your leg to sweep inward through the full range that your hip mobility permits, which commonly prompts a stretching sensation on the side of your hip.
    3. People commonly have the mobility to let their working leg’s foot be pulled slightly horizontally closer to the pulley than the non-working leg’s foot.
4. Drive your leg straight out to the side and upward in an arcing manner through your hip’s available range of motion.
    1. During the entirety of the exercise, continue keeping the tops of your hip bones and shoulders level with each other as your chest and imaginary belt buckle are pointing straight ahead. Avoid twisting your torso, bending your torso to the side, or hiking up either hip bone.
    2. While the rest of your body remains stationary, the only motion that should occur is your leg arcing out to the side, like the hand of an analog clock moving about the center point that is your hip.
    3. Most people should be able to finish with the thigh angled at least 30 degrees from a vertical position. However, how far you personally can lift your leg out to the side will be influenced by your hip mobility, and you should only move as far as you can without using the rest of your body to compensate for hip motion.
    4. You should feel the muscles on the side of your hip contract.
5. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your leg to sweep downward and inward in an arcing motion.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your leg crossed across the midline of your body.
    2. Allow your leg to sweep back in through the full range that your hip mobility permits while keeping your knee cap, chest, and imaginary belt buckle pointing straight forward with your hip bones and shoulders level.
    3. Avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your leg inward in an uncontrolled manner.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. To train your other side, you will switch the cuff to your other leg and face the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the musculature on the side of your hip, including your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia latae.## Setting up
1. Position a short looped elastic band around both of your lower thighs.
    1. The band will provide the greatest resistance to your hip muscles if you place it just above your knees, while it will provide less resistance if you place it higher up your thighs.
    2. You can choose specifically where you situate the band on your thighs based on the level of resistance you wish to experience. If you are using a particular band for this exercise for the first time, you can adjust its placement during the set if you wish to change the difficulty after starting the set.
    3. Inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
2. Stand upright with your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, as your toes are pointing somewhat out to the sides rather than pointing straight ahead.
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
3. Raise your arms out in front of you until they are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. You can alternatively keep your arms in a different position if you wish to do so, but keeping your arms outstretched throughout the set provides a counterbalance that often allows people to reach a greater squat depth.
## Exercise technique
1. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down, while driving your knees out against the band to keep your knees stacked over your feet.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but keep your back flat and chest up, as you avoid rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting the band pull your knees inward past this point.
2. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion that your mobility enables without rounding your back, lifting your heels up off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, heels lift off the ground, or knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips until you have returned to a fully upright position.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees stacked over your feet as you squat up.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and short hip external rotator muscles, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and quads.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of full diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates or any bumper plates) will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. If you only use smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize 45lb (20kg) plates, you can perform a block pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with full diameter plates.
2. Stand with the middle of your feet under the bar, and spread your legs apart into a wide stance that is clearly outside shoulder width with your toes moderately rotated out away from your sides.
    1. Provided that your feet are wider apart than your shoulders but closer together than the plates, you can use a range of stance widths for your sumo deadlift.
    2. The ideal stance width can meaningfully vary among different individuals based on someone’s body structure and hip mobility.
    3. Trying out different foot positions will normally be required to determine which stance specifically feels best to you.
    4. As a starting point, you can widen your feet as far apart as you feel comfortable and stable while being able to push your knees out approximately over your heels, resulting in close to vertical shins. You will push your knees out when descending to reach the bar, so subsequent aspects of the setup can help you gauge if you should adjust your stance.
    5. The ideal amount of toe flare also varies among different lifters, but you can start with your toes pointing about 30-45 degrees out to the side before adjusting from there based on what feels best to you. Zero degrees of toe flare would mean your toes are pointing straight forward, while 90 degrees would mean your toes are pointing directly out to your side.
3. Direct your arms straight down toward the bar with your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up).
    1. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and help engage your back muscles.
4. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips while bending your knees and pushing your knees out until your hands are level with the bar. Rather than rounding your back, keep your lower back flat and chest up in a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Strive to keep your shins vertical as your knees essentially do not track forward (although a very slight degree of forward knee tracking can be used if that feels best to you).
    2. The proper balance of bending at your hips versus your knees should allow your shoulders to be stacked vertically above the bar and your hips to be higher than your knees at the bottom.
    3. Try to push your knees far enough out to the sides that they are vertically stacked over your heels.
    4. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
5. With your elbows fully straightened and the bar directly in front of your shins, firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can naturally use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish to do so.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. This can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
    3. Keep your soles fully in contact with the ground throughout your set.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” by extending (straightening) your hips and knees while pushing your knees out. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, the bar close to your legs, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As your hips and chest rise at the same rate, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull. Avoid letting your torso incline further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the bar reaches near your knees, focus on thrusting your hips forward until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
5. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips and bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and the bar close to your legs while driving your knees out with vertical shins.
    2. You can initially focus on sitting your hips back while allowing your knees to bend naturally. After the bar has cleared your knees, you can further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again. Then continue to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the ground rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can meaningfully train your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and quads.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable to its highest level, connect a rope attachment to it, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
    1. You optionally can place a foam pad (or similarly soft object) that is comfortable for kneeling a little in front of the cable station.
2. Stand under the rope with your back facing the cable station.
3. Reach up and grasp each end of the rope with your little fingers and adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the outside of your other fingers.
4. Pull your hands down until they are approximately in front of your shoulders with your elbows bent.
    1. You will keep your hands in this position throughout your set.
5. Kneel down with both knees on the ground (or the soft object you placed on the ground) about hip width apart from each other.
6. While keeping your back flat, moderately bend at your hips to position your torso closer to horizontal to the ground.
    1. The appropriate degree of hip bend should allow you to feel stable and balanced throughout your set. You can initially try bending your hips until your torso is approximately 45 degrees inclined and adjust from there based on what feels best to you.
    2. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable. If you do not, you should set up a little further away from the cable to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion of each rep.
## Exercise technique
1. Without moving your hips or arms, arch your back in a controlled manner through the range of motion that feels comfortable to your lower back.
    1. How far you can comfortably arch your back will be influenced by your mobility.
    2. Some people may experience lower back discomfort in a fully arched position, and there is no need to arch beyond the point that feels right to you.
    3. When thinking about an imaginary laser beam shooting out from the middle of your chest, the beam should angle progressively higher up as you arch your back.
    4. The bottom of your ribs should lift up and away from the top of your hip bones.
2. Curl your spine as you pull the bottom of your ribs toward the tops of your hip bones in a controlled manner. Avoid moving your hips or arms as you round your entire back.
    1. Once you feel that you can no longer curl your spine through a range that feels comfortable to your back, you have reached your end range.
    2. Try to point the imaginary laser beam shooting out from the middle of your chest closer toward the middle point between your knees.
    3. It can help to think about crunching your elbows toward your knees.
    4. If you finish the movement with your back fairly flat as opposed to rounded, you most likely are bending at your hips rather than curling your spine.
    5. Making sure to avoid sitting your buttocks back can help limit unintended hip motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent, as the soles of your feet are flat on the ground, approximately hip width apart. Place your arms by your sides and your palms on the ground.
    1. You can rest your head on the ground.
    2. You can optionally hook the tops of your feet under a sturdy object, such as a couch (while your soles remain flat on the ground), to help stabilize your body during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the ground while you lift your head and shoulder blades up off the ground by curling your middle back.
    1. Given that this is a crunch rather than a sit-up, the lower half of your back should remain in contact with the ground while you only lift up the upper half of your back mildly off the floor.
    2. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    3. As you progressively crunch up, allow your hands to slide forward slightly while remaining on the ground.
    4. As a cue, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the top of your hip bones, which is the action that contraction of your abdominal muscles can produce.
    5. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that crunching up any further would require the lower half of your back to lift off the floor.
    6. At the end of the lifting phase, your chest should be facing at an angle between forward and upward, rather than only facing upward as it did in the starting position.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your middle back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your head and shoulder blades back to the starting position on the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your obliques and especially your rectus abdominis, which is the “six-pack muscle.”## Setting up
1. In a deadlift motion (like what is subsequently described in the technique section), pick up a kettlebell and set it onto the ground with its handle facing up.
    1. Particularly when using what you consider to be a heavy weight, initially lift it up using your hips and knees with your lower back near a flat position rather than being excessively rounded over at your lower back.
2. Stand upright in a near shoulder-width stance with the handle of the kettlebell between the middle of your feet, as your toes are slightly turned out to the sides or pointing straight ahead.
    1. While using a near shoulder-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
3. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handle.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the handle.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
4. With your hands close together and your elbows still straight, firmly grasp the handle with an overhand grip, resulting in your facing palms backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handle with the handle pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handle closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handle can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the kettlebell, apply “pre-tension” into the handle as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force that would lift the weight if it were only half as heavy
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position and pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat, drive through your legs to begin lifting the kettlebell as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the handle reaches near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the kettlebell until it has returned to resting on the ground.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the handle has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the kettlebell.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the kettlebell up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the kettlebell on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the kettlebell on the ground.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to position the bar on your upper back and unrack it in a stable position.
    1. As an initial approximation, you can position the bar such that it is a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
2. Stand upright, facing the center of the unloaded bar.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails). Facing this way can often feel more natural for good mornings, but you can alternatively face the other direction if you personally prefer to do so.
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat wider than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
4. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to then walk under the bar and situate the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
5. Position the middle of your feet under the bar in an approximately shoulder-width to hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly out to the sides.
    1. You can determine specifically how wide a stance and where you point your toes based on what personally feels most comfortable and stable to you, since the ideal stance can vary among different individuals.
6. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of good morning reps with the form described under the exercise technique section before reracking the bar to determine the appropriate height of the safety stops.
7. Position the safety stops slightly under the lowest point or directly at the lowest point at which the bar descended during your good morning.
    1. The appropriate safety stop position should allow you to hinge through your full range of motion while being high enough to receive the bar if you fail a rep and bend your knees a bit more to set the bar on the safety stops.
8. Load the bar with your desired weight, and repeat steps 2-5.
## Exercise technique
1. While pulling down on the bar to keep it stable on your upper back, un-rack the bar by starting to stand up as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the bar on your upper back throughout your set.
2. After the bar is fully resting on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion prior to standing fully upright with the bar.
    1. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
    3. Keep your lower back and neck near a neutral position throughout your set.
3. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
4. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner.
    1. Avoid rounding your lower back as you keep your lower back mildly arched or flat throughout the exercise.
    2. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    3. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something, as you keep your chest up (rather than letting your chest collapse through back rounding).
    4. Keep the soles of your feet fully planted on the ground the entire time.
    5. While you hinge at your hips, the bar will naturally descend, and your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    6. How deeply you are able to hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    7. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range.
    8. If your end range corresponds to where you positioned the safety stops, you can gently tap the bar against the safety stops without resting the bar on the safety stops at the bottom.
5. Reverse the movement by driving your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so you should continue keeping your chest up without any back rounding.
    2. Once you have returned to the starting position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid driving your hips in front of your shoulders at the top, which would prompt your lower back to arch excessively. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips appropriately stacked under your shoulders to prevent excessive arching.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
7. After finishing the set, rerack the bar by bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your side with the side of your body that you are about to train facing up.
    1. In this side-lying position, you can rest the bottom side of your head on the hand of your bottom arm for comfort by situating your upper arm out to the side with your elbow bent.
    2. For extra stability, you can bend the elbow of your top arm and place that side’s hand on the ground in front of your torso.
2. While keeping the knee of your top leg straight and that side’s thigh aligned with your torso (like it would be when you are standing upright), march the thigh of your bottom leg toward your chest while moderately bending that side’s knee.
    1. After doing so, your bottom leg and foot should no longer be situated underneath your top leg, allowing your top leg’s foot to rest on the ground as that side’s thigh crosses in toward the midline of your body.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your top leg straight upward through your hip’s available range of motion while keeping that side’s knee straight and the rest of your body stationary.
    1. Avoid rolling your hips backward or bringing your top leg forward toward your chest during this exercise, which are common technical errors.
    2. To help prevent your hips from rolling, you can imagine that you are wearing a belt with a buckle that has a laser beam shooting out of it. Strive to keep the laser beam pointing straight forward, parallel to the ground, throughout the movement.
    3. If you start to roll your hips backward, your belt buckle’s laser beam will start to angle upward.
    4. When you lift your top leg straight upward rather than also bringing that leg forward in front of you, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thigh through your hip to your torso when your top thigh is parallel with the ground.
    5. While performing this movement with the appropriate technique, you will likely feel the muscles on the side of your top hip contracting.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your top leg to descend in a controlled manner until the inner aspect of its foot lightly contacts the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side by lying on your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the musculature on the side of your hip, including your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia latae.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Securely attach an ankle cuff just above the ankle of the leg you will train. Position the cuff so that one of its attachment rings is directed toward the inside of your leg.
3. Stand sideways directly next to the pulley with your working leg further away from the pulley and your feet together.
    1. For instance, if you are going to train your left side, the side of your right shoulder should be facing the cable station.
4. Take a step backward with the leg closer to the pulley.
    1. The goal is to have your non-working leg’s toes slightly behind your working leg’s heel to provide sufficient clearance for your working leg to sweep inward across your body toward the pulley.
5. Bend over and connect the cable to your cuff’s attachment ring before standing upright.
    1. It will be easiest to connect the cable when your working leg is close enough to the pulley that the cable does not provide resistance when connecting it.
6. Grasp the cable station beam with your closer hand and continue holding it from now onward for balance assistance.
7. Take a step out to the side with your working leg against the cable’s resistance before taking a step with your other leg.
    1. Your feet should now be hip-width apart, with your non-working leg’s foot still in front of your working leg’s foot.
    2. The goal of this step is to step out just far enough so that the cable can provide resistance throughout the exercise’s full range of motion. If you notice that you lose resistance from the cable when your working leg is in the starting position of a rep, you should step a bit further out away from the pulley.
    3. Your chest and imaginary belt buckle should be pointing straight ahead.
    4. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Keep your back near this neutral position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Shift your bodyweight into your non-working leg, and lift your working leg’s foot off the ground with a straight knee.
    1. All the subsequent directions will apply to your working leg.
3. Allow your leg to sweep inward in line with the cable’s resistance while keeping your hip bones and shoulder level with each other.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your knee cap pointing straight ahead rather than rotating it inward or outward.
    2. Allow your leg to sweep inward through the full range that your hip mobility permits, which commonly prompts a stretching sensation on the side of your hip.
    3. People commonly have the mobility to let their working leg’s foot be pulled slightly horizontally closer to the pulley than the non-working leg’s foot.
4. Drive your leg straight out to the side and upward in an arcing manner through your hip’s available range of motion.
    1. During the entirety of the exercise, continue keeping the tops of your hip bones and shoulders level with each other as your chest and imaginary belt buckle are pointing straight ahead. Avoid twisting your torso, bending your torso to the side, or hiking up either hip bone.
    2. While the rest of your body remains stationary, the only motion that should occur is your leg arcing out to the side, like the hand of an analog clock moving about the center point that is your hip.
    3. Most people should be able to finish with the thigh angled at least 30 degrees from a vertical position. However, how far you personally can lift your leg out to the side will be influenced by your hip mobility, and you should only move as far as you can without using the rest of your body to compensate for hip motion.
    4. You should feel the muscles on the side of your hip contract.
5. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your leg to sweep downward and inward in an arcing motion.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your leg crossed across the midline of your body.
    2. Allow your leg to sweep back in through the full range that your hip mobility permits while keeping your knee cap, chest, and imaginary belt buckle pointing straight forward with your hip bones and shoulders level.
    3. Avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your leg inward in an uncontrolled manner.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. To train your other side, you will switch the cuff to your other leg and face the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the musculature on the side of your hip, including your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia latae.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell in a spot where you have sufficient space to lie down on the ground.
    1. The diameter of the weight plates you select can influence how easily you can set up while also affecting the range of motion for this exercise.
    2. It will be easiest to assume the starting position with at least one pair of full diameter plates, such as typical 45lb (20kg) Olympic plates or bumper plates. With full diameter plates, certain individuals who are very thick may need to utilize one of the strategies discussed below to set up under the bar.
    3. If you exclusively load the bar with smaller diameter plates, you may have to modify the standard setup in order to position your hips under the bar. When only using smaller diameter plates, you can elevate the bar just enough to fit your hips under it by situating the plates on top of deadlift blocks, aerobic steps, or stacks of full diameter weight plates. Alternatively, you may have a partner place the bar on top of your hip bones and load it with weight plates after you have assumed the starting position. If neither of these approaches is practical to you, you lack bumper plates that allow you to elevate the bar fully with light loads, and you do not currently have the strength to use 135lb (or 60kg) for this exercise, you have the option of performing other glute bridge variations. For instance, you can select a single leg bodyweight glute bridge or a dumbbell glute bridge from this database to train the glutes in essentially the same manner as the barbell variation, but with an easier setup.
2. Optionally, obtain a pad (or an object that can function as a pad) that you will place between the bar and your hips.
    1. Using a pad is not required but strongly recommended because it can be very uncomfortable to have a barbell directly resting on your hip bones with a meaningful load.
    2. You can wrap a hip thrust pad or a squat pad around the middle of the bar.
    3. Alternatively, you can place a thick foam or folded exercise mat on the crease of your hip during the next step and subsequently keep it there to cushion your hip bones. Be aware that foam pads not specifically designed for glute bridges or hip thrusts may be somewhat damaged from this exercise.
3. Sit down on the ground with an upright torso and your legs straight out in front of you under the bar.
    1. The middle of the bar should be aligned with the midline of your body.
4. Grasp the bar with your palms facing down and roll it toward the crease of your hips.
    1. So long as your hands are wider apart than your hips, you can adjust your grip width based on what feels stable and comfortable to you during this exercise.
    2. Continue holding the bar from this point onward.
5. Lie down with your back and head resting on the ground.
    1. Tuck your chin slightly toward your chest while resting the back of your head on the ground throughout the exercise.
6. Bend your knees until your shins are in an approximately vertical position with the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can position your feet shoulder-width apart, but it is up to you whether you would like them closer together or farther apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    3. The optimal degree of knee bend should allow your feet to feel stable while being firmly planted on the ground throughout the set. Bringing your heels toward your buttocks until they are vertically stacked under your knees is normally appropriate, but you can adjust how much you bend your knees if a different position feels more stable to you.
7. Confirm that the bar is directly above the crease of your hips and completely centered before initiating the set.
    1. Positioning the bar appropriately will help you avoid the excessive discomfort that may arise from positioning the bar too high (i.e., directly on your lower abdomen) or too low (i.e., directly on your groin).
    2. Some people may experience discomfort when the bar looks aligned with the crease of their hips if the bar is directly resting on the bony bumps on the front of their hip bones. If you notice that this occurs for you during your set, you can roll the bar slightly further down away from your abdomen to shift the pressure away from these bony bumps. When making any adjustments during a set, only do so in between reps when the weights are resting on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to help stabilize your lower back.
2. Lift your hips up by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes. Keep the bar balanced on the crease of your hips the entire set.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glutes engage during this exercise.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
    5. The soles of your feet and your upper back should remain firmly in contact with the ground throughout the exercise.
    6. The purpose of holding the bar with your hands is to keep it stabilized on the crease of your hips. Avoid trying to lift the bar up or push it down with your hands.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid simply letting gravity pull the bar down abruptly, as opposed to you using your glutes to control the descent actively.
    2. If you hear the plates crashing into the ground, you are not lowering the bar with sufficient control.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps. The bar should come to a dead stop on the ground after each rep.
    2. After completing the lowering phase of your last rep, roll the bar forward before sitting up in the reversed manner to how you set up initially.
## Description
While your hamstrings and adductor magnus can assist to a degree, this exercise primarily targets your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that will allow your legs to be in an approximately horizontal position once you are in the starting position.
    1. After estimating where this setting may be, you can adjust it during later steps if warranted.
    2. Not all machines permit you to adjust the footplate height.
2. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow the pad to support your thighs without contacting your hip bones when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. The appropriate position should allow your hips to move freely while sufficiently stabilizing your legs.
    2. If the footplate is positioned excessively far back (resulting in your hips resting on the pad), the pad can restrict your hip motion and prompt you to perform the exercise via spinal motion.
    3. If the footplate is positioned excessively far forward to the extent that your knees are near the middle of the pad rather than your thighs, the exercise may provoke knee discomfort in some people.
    4. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position.
3. Position the weight you intend to use in a spot that you can comfortably reach after you set your feet on the footplate.
    1. For this variation, you can hold a weight close to your chest or hold it with straight arms that are directed down toward the ground. The position in which you hold it is up to preference, along with whether you use a weight plate, dumbbell, kettlebell, or medicine ball.
    2. Based on the weight you use, the machine model you have, your body proportions, and your mobility, you have multiple options for where you can place the weight.
    3. You may be able to place certain weights on top of the pad before picking up the weight after you mount the machine. However, not all weights can remain balanced on top of the pad, and this approach is not practical for some pad designs.
    4. With a sufficiently light weight plate or kettlebell, you may be able to hang the weight on one of the handles.
    5. Alternatively, you can place the weight on the ground just in front of the pad and pick it up at the bottom of your hip hinge after you finish setting up on the machine. This option naturally requires that you are able to reach the weight on the ground, which is not feasible for everyone.
    6. As an additional approach, you can have a partner hand you the weight after you are set up, which can be useful if you are using a heavy weight.
4. Climb up onto the machine, and position your feet against the footplate with your lower legs between the leg rollers while the front of your hips are resting on the pad.
    1. The back of your lower legs should be snugly pressed into the top pair of leg rollers slightly above your heels.
    2. Your knees should be straight, and your soles should be firmly contacting the footplate.
    3. While you are setting your feet, you can use the handles for assistance.
    4. Some machines have a step that you can use to help you get into position.
    5. With the appropriate footplate setting, the front of your hip bones should not be resting on the pad.
5. Pick up the weight, and hold it with both hands either close to your chest or with straight arms directed down toward the ground.
    1. The precise way in which you will pick up and hold it may vary based on the specific implement you selected and where you placed it initially. What matters is that you have a firm grip on the weight and are able to keep it in a stable position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes to keep your torso up such that you can draw approximately a straight line from your legs through your hips and to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your lower back and neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. This is the starting position of the exercise, where your body should look like it does when normally standing upright, except rotated to be horizontal.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips.
3. Without rounding your back, hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner to angle your torso downward.
    1. As opposed to bending over at your back, nearly all the motion should occur from you moving at your hips as you statically keep your trunk near a neutral position with your lower back mildly arched or flat.
    2. Maintain a straight knee position, but avoid intentionally locking your knees out by squeezing your quads.
    3. While hinging at your hips and keeping your hands on your shoulders, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings that progressively intensifies.
    4. How deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility. If you have the flexibility to hinge until your torso is completely vertical, stop once you reach this point. Otherwise, stop descending once you feel like you have reached the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch.
    5. If you initially did not position the footplate sufficiently forward, the pad may alternatively limit how deeply you can hinge.
4. Reverse the movement by lifting your torso up through your hips and squeezing your glutes.
    1. When moving through your hips, your entire torso should move together as one unit. You can think of your hips as the center of an analog clock, while your rigid torso is a hand that rotates about the center.
    2. At the end of the lifting phase, your torso should be close to a horizontal position aligned with your legs.
    3. Avoid arching your lower back excessively at the top since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the hip motion you just performed during the lowering phase. Focusing on finishing the movement by squeezing your glutes can help prevent excessive back arching.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
6. After your last rep, return the weight to the position in which you initially placed it before dismounting the machine.
    1. You can grasp the handles to help you dismount the machine.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and secure the weights with collars.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the weight plates may slide if you do not connect collars on a reverse hyper with a center pendulum and two sleeves.
2. If your machine has adjustable handles, position them at a setting that allows you to hold with mostly straight elbows during step 4.
    1. This can require some trial and error to find the ideal handle setting.
    2. A variety of machine models lack adjustable handles, but some have multiple pairs of handles that allow you to select the ones that you can grasp with mostly straight elbows.
3. Stand directly in front of the pad and the weights. If you are using leg straps rather than rollers, step into the straps.
4. After climbing on the pad, lie down with your abdomen on the pad, and firmly grasp the handles. Position the top of your hip bones just on the edge of the pad.
    1. The appropriate positioning should allow your legs to move freely throughout the exercise.
    2. If you position yourself excessively far forward, the pad will restrict your hip motion.
    3. If you position yourself excessively far backward, you will be less stable and may round your lower back more than intended.
    4. Some machine models will have a pair of steps to help you climb onto the pad.
5. If you are using leg straps, move your legs backward until your lower legs are snugly contacting the straps slightly above your ankles. If you are using leg rollers, position your lower legs snugly between the rollers slightly above your ankles.
    1. In the starting position, the straps should be taut rather than slack (if you are using straps).
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Drive your legs backward and upward in an arcing motion as you squeeze your glutes.
    1. Rather than aiming to lift your legs to a particular height, you can focus on forcefully squeezing your glutes, which can naturally promote the appropriate range.
    2. It is normal for the range of motion of the initial rep or two to be lower than subsequent reps, since the initial reps help build momentum.
    3. Throughout your set, keep your torso fully in contact with the pad and your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    4. As a cue, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between your glutes.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing your legs to arc downward and forward in a controlled manner.
    1. Keep the strap snugly against your lower legs the entirety of each rep if you are using a strap.
    2. The standard technique for this exercise involves a controlled swinging motion that the machine’s pendulum facilitates, but you can optionally use a very strict technique where you emphasize slowing the descent as much as you can. 
    3. While you can work with the pendulum’s momentum, avoid letting the momentum take over the movement in an uncontrolled manner. If you feel that the momentum is excessive despite your effort to control it, it may be appropriate to try a lighter load.
4. After your legs are close to pointing straight down, drive your legs backward and upward while squeezing your glutes.
    1. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable to you, you can begin driving your legs back once they are vertical or once they move mildly forward beyond this point. Do not let the pendulum yank your legs any further forward.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
    1. It is normal for some lower back motion to occur simultaneously with hip motion during this exercise. However, your lower back should not fully arch at the top or excessively round at the bottom when appropriately using your glutes through the proper range of motion.
6. While keeping your legs snugly against the strap or roller, allow the pendulum’s momentum to subside until it stops swinging before dismounting the machine.
    1. Guiding the pendulum through a few cycles of progressively shorter swings may be required for it to come to a dead stop.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus while also training your hamstrings, adductor magnus, and spinal erectors.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground or an exercise mat, and bend your knees to at least 90 degrees to position the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can position your feet shoulder-width apart, but it is up to you whether you would like them closer together or farther apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. You can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips off the ground by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glutes engage.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you can lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While other muscles may assist to some degree, this exercise primarily targets your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the barbell situated on the ground, position a large diameter weight plate (or a viable alternative) under the center of the bar that you will use to create a deficit.
    1. While you can use a variety of deficit heights, selecting a low deficit height (under 4in (10cm)) is initially recommended to facilitate the appropriate technique.
    2. You want to pick a height that allows you to keep your lower back flat at the bottom of the rep, and the height should still allow you to use a deadlift technique rather than squatting the weight up (which can occur with an excessively large deficit).
    3. A large diameter (e.g., 45lb (20kg)) cast iron weight plate with the flat surface facing up can be a suitable option to select since these plates are normally near 1.5in (3.8cm) thick.
    4. A bumper plate or stackable deadlift block can also be used for your deficit.
    5. Depending on your preferred stance width, you can also position two plates directly next to each other if the soles of your feet do not fit fully onto one plate.
    6. As an alternative to standing on a plate or step, you can also pull through an extended range of motion by exclusively loading smaller diameter plates onto the bar and standing on the ground.
2. Stand upright on the plate in approximately a hip-width stance, close in front of the middle of the bar, as the bar is over the middle of your feet, with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly turned out to the sides.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
    2. The soles of your feet should be fully in contact with the plate for maximal stability.
    3. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    4. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
3. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the bar reaches near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the ground.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can meaningfully train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground or an exercise mat, and bend your knees to at least 90 degrees to position the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can position your feet shoulder-width apart, but it is up to you whether you would like them closer together or farther apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. You can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes.
    1. Finish with your hips fully extended, allowing you to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
2. While keeping your hips up off the ground, lift one foot off the ground to march that side’s knee up toward your chest.
    1. March that thigh up through your hip’s available range of motion, which will vary among different individuals.
    2. Avoid letting your buttocks drop down, or either hip bone tilt down toward the ground as you march. Strive to keep your right and left hip bones fully level.
    3. As your one leg marches up, the hip muscles on the same side of your leg in contact with the ground will have to work more intensely to keep your buttocks bridged up.
3. Reverse the marching motion by lowering your thigh in a controlled manner until that side’s foot returns to contacting the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your buttocks fully lifted above the ground.
4. March up with your other side, and then lower that side’s foot back to the ground in the same way you just performed during steps 2 and 3.
5. With both feet in contact with the ground, lower your hips in a controlled manner until you can lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
6. Alternate between steps 1-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. You complete one rep each time you perform a cycle of steps 1-5.
## Description
While your hamstrings and adductor magnus can assist to a degree, this exercise primarily targets your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground or an exercise mat behind a sturdy object with an elevated surface, such as a flat exercise bench, aerobic step elevated on riser, plyometric box, stairs, or bed.
    1. So long as you can position your feet on the object while still lying on the ground, a range of heights can be used for your object, but selecting something near knee height is typically appropriate.
2. Lift up your legs to position your feet on top of the elevated surface with your knees bent by at least 90 degrees.
    1. If you are too far away from the elevated surface to achieve this bent knee position, you can scoot forward until you can do so.
    2. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (by scooting toward the elevated surface) is up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    3. Based on what feels stable to you, you can either position the back of your heels on top of the elevated surface or position the middle of your soles on the corner of the elevated surface.
    4. You can situate your feet near shoulder-width apart from each other, but it is also up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    5. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    6. You can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your feet into the elevated surface and squeezing your glutes.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glutes engage.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you can lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
This exercise can be performed with an ankle weight strapped to your ankle or a weight plate, dumbbell, or kettlebell held on top of your lower thigh. The technique is essentially the same regardless of which equipment you use, but certain setup details differ, which is why two different setup sections are presented.

### Setting up with ankle weights

1. Strap an ankle weight to each of your ankles and lie down on your side with the side of your body that you are about to train facing up.
    1. In this side-lying position, you can rest the bottom side of your head on the hand of your bottom arm for comfort by situating your upper arm out to the side with your elbow bent.
    2. For extra stability, you can bend the elbow of your top arm and place that side’s hand on the ground in front of your torso.
2. While keeping the knee of your top leg straight and that side’s thigh aligned with your torso (like it would be when you are standing upright), march the thigh of your bottom leg toward your chest while moderately bending that side’s knee.
    1. After doing so, your bottom leg and foot should no longer be situated underneath your top leg, allowing your top leg’s foot to rest on the ground as that side’s thigh crosses in toward the midline of your body.

### Setting up without ankle weights

1. Place the weight you will use for resistance on the ground next to where you will lie.
2. Lie down on your side with the side of your body that you are about to train facing up.
    1. In this side-lying position, you can rest the bottom side of your head on the hand of your bottom arm for comfort by situating your upper arm out to the side with your elbow bent.
    2. For extra stability, you can bend the elbow of your top arm and place that side’s hand on the ground in front of your torso.
3. While keeping the knee of your top leg straight and that side’s thigh aligned with your torso (like it would be when you are standing upright), march the thigh of your bottom leg toward your chest while moderately bending that side’s knee.
    1. After doing so, your bottom leg and foot should no longer be situated underneath your top leg, allowing your top leg’s foot to rest on the ground as that side’s thigh crosses in toward the midline of your body.
4. Place the weight on top of your thigh (that is facing up). Throughout the exercise, continue holding the weight in place with your hand as your top arm is by your side and your elbow is straight.
    1. How low down your thigh you can hold the weight will be affected by your body proportions. Because the resistance from the weight increases as you hold it further away from your hip, aim to situate the weight in the same spot on your thigh to help you track your progress accurately.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your top leg straight upward through your hip’s available range of motion while keeping that side’s knee straight and the rest of your body stationary.
    1. Avoid rolling your hips backward or bringing your top leg forward toward your chest during this exercise, which are common technical errors.
    2. To help prevent your hips from rolling, you can imagine that you are wearing a belt with a buckle that has a laser beam shooting out of it. Strive to keep the laser beam pointing straight forward, parallel to the ground, throughout the movement.
    3. If you start to roll your hips backward, your belt buckle’s laser beam will start to angle upward.
    4. When you lift your top leg straight upward rather than also bringing that leg forward in front of you, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thigh through your hip to your torso when your top thigh is parallel with the ground.
    5. While performing this movement with the appropriate technique, you should feel the muscles on the side of your top hip contracting.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your top leg to descend in a controlled manner until the inner aspect of its foot lightly contacts the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side by lying on your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the musculature on the side of your hip, including your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia latae.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The setup for this exercise is simply performing the lifting phase of a conventional deadlift.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the middle of your feet under the middle of the bar.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish.
    2. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    3. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    4. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than at the base of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hands while you hold it, which pulls on your hand skin uncomfortably and increases the risk of tearing a callus.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
6. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Keep your elbows straight and the bar very close to your legs as you lift the bar, rather than letting the bar drift forward.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the bar just in front of your legs. Avoid letting the bar drift forward away from you, which may be more likely to occur if you think about “bending over” rather than “sitting back.”
    2. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the bar.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    5. Throughout the set, keep your feet fully planted on the ground and your neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. If you have high hamstring flexibility and can hinge until the plates contact the ground, descend until you can tap the plates very lightly on the ground, but do not let the weight rest on the ground.
    4. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
3. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, feet fully on the ground, and the bar close to your legs.
    2. When you have finished lifting the bar, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips properly stacked under your shoulders, preventing excessive arching.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the floor.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe between reps in the fully upright position, rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. After finishing the last rep, set the bar down in the same manner in which you initially picked it up (e.g., keeping your lower back flat rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.

### Learning how to hip hinge

If you have minimal experience training hip hinge exercises such as Romanian deadlift and good morning variations, it is normal to have difficulty performing this movement pattern. Like riding a bicycle or shooting a basketball, hip hinging is a motor skill that may require repeated practice before you feel comfortable consistently using it. Broadly speaking, there are two types of movement substitution patterns that individuals who are in the process of learning how to hip hinge commonly perform when attempting to execute a hip hinge exercise.

One of these is using a stoop lift, which involves dynamically rounding the back (through flexion of the lumbar and thoracic spine) to bend over prior to uncoiling the spine into an upright position during the ascent. This often is accompanied by the buttock not moving particularly far backward and the arms drifting out in front of the legs in contrast to a strict hip hinge, which utilizes a visibly flat back position (with close to a neutral lumbar spine) and has the buttock noticeably moving backward during the descent. In terms of similarity, a stoop lift and a hip hinge both involve only a mild magnitude of knee bend during the descent while keeping the hips high. A clue that you may be performing more of a stoop lift than a hip hinge is feeling a stretching sensation in your back rather than feeling your back muscles remain statically tight throughout your set as these muscles function to maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hips. With a properly performed hip hinge, you should certainly feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the bottom of the lift, but you may also feel this stretching sensation during a stoop lift. Nonetheless, a hip hinge can allow you to feel the hamstring stretch earlier during the descent compared to a stoop lift since the hamstrings are shortened by the posterior pelvic tilt (tucking the tailbone under) that is coupled with lumbar spine flexion.

In addition to performing a stoop lift unintentionally when attempting to hip hinge, people can commonly utilize more of a squatting motion that is characterized by greater knee bend and forward tracking of the knees during the descent. Compared to a hip hinge, using more of a squatting movement can result in the buttock moving downward during the descent as opposed to backward, and the shins can also incline forward as opposed to remaining vertical. Because the hamstrings shorten as the knees bend, someone moving in a squat pattern when attempting to perform a hip hinge exercise is unlikely to feel much of a stretching sensation in the hamstrings at the bottom of the lift. Not feeling a hamstring stretch is the most straightforward indicator that you may be squatting your hip hinge exercise, but an additional clue would be if you perceive your quads to be working (e.g., feeling a “burning” sensation or lots of tension) more intensely than your hamstrings during the exercise.

When thinking about moving through hip hinge, stoop lift, and squat patterns, it is important to recognize that these movements can occur along a spectrum. You can perform a hip hinge exercise with a technique that is mostly a hip hinge while still utilizing elements of a stoop lift or a squat that may not be ideal for what you wish to accomplish from the exercise. Similarly, your technique can incrementally progress from being stoop lift-dominant or squat-dominant to becoming progressively more of a hip hinge as your body learns how to move in a different way through repeated practice. If your hip hinge technique currently has much room for improvement, it can be beneficial to start with lighter loads that may not be especially challenging to your muscles until you developed a reasonable level of competency with the movement itself. There is no need to let perfect be the enemy of good, so you do not need to wait to load a movement meaningfully until you have immaculate technique, but it would also be inadvisable to push your body to the limit without initially learning the basic fundamentals of the movement.

Depending on which element of hip hinging in which you currently are challenged, certain external cues can expedite how quickly you can learn how to hinge. When unintentionally moving in a manner that involves a greater degree of squatting or stoop lifting than is intended, a wall may help provide tactile feedback to cue sitting your buttock backward. To do so, you can perform your hip hinge with your back facing a nearby wall, and you will be able to receive confirmation as to whether you are actually sitting your hips backward by feeling the wall contact your buttock at the end of the descent. As an initial starting point, you can set up with a heel-to-toe tandem stance as the back of one heel contacts the wall. Then you can bring your rear foot forward to be aligned with your front foot in the symmetrical stance you will utilize for your hip hinge. This can provide a reasonable approximation as to how far people may commonly be able to sit their hips back when hinging, but you can adjust from there how far away you position yourself from the wall to individualize it to you.

Specifically if you have a tendency to perform a “squatty” hip hinge, practicing your hip hinge with your calves up against something like a flat exercise bench (or even a couch or chair at home) can be a rather productive means of honing your hinge. As you descend, you will receive immediate tactile feedback if your knees track forward from no longer feeling the contact with your calves, so it can be a great way to reinforce keeping your shins vertical throughout the movement. When using this method, make sure that you maintain full contact between the soles of your feet and the ground while you avoid leaning your bodyweight into the bench to the extent that you would fall backward if the bench was removed. If you successfully keep your calves in contact with the bench throughout the movement and feel a pronounced stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the end of the descent, you are right on track to achieving a high quality hip hinge.

As an additional learning tool, you can situate a long object like a dowel rod, PVC pipe, or yardstick on your back while hip hinging if you have a tendency to utilize more of a stoop lift technique with excessive back rounding. Either the calf-to-bench technique or the buttock-to-wall technique can be incorporated into an actual Romanian deadlift where you use weights, but using a rod as a cue without a partner will require you to hold the rod in place, so you would not be able to load this drill independently. To perform this movement, you would hold the rod behind against your back with one hand higher up near your head and your other hand lower down near your buttock. While standing fully upright and looking straight ahead, you should feel the rod contacting your sacrum (the bone that lies between your upper buttock cheeks and right above your tailbone), upper back between your shoulder blades, and the back of your head. You will then hip hinge while maintaining these three points of contact throughout the entirety of the movement. If you start rounding your back or moving your neck beyond the neutral position you adopt when standing fully upright, you will feel the rod lose contact with one of those three spots. This can be a rather effective form of instant feedback to help develop your ability to move predominantly through your lower body while keeping your trunk in a rigid position throughout the movement, which can transfer to improved technique for an array of different exercises. When maintaining those three contact points, you potentially can still perform a squatting motion rather than a hip hinge since moving through your lower body while minimizing back motion is also a component of squatting technique. However, if you maintain those three contact points and additionally feel your hamstrings notably stretched at the bottom of the descent, those two factors indicate that you have successfully hip hinged in all likelihood. If you find that the rod cuing shifts your movement from a stoop lift to more of a squat, you can use the rod cuing simultaneously with the calf-to-bench cuing, which can address multiple aspects of the hip hinge concurrently.

In the context of performing a hip hinge exercise, using a form that resembles more of a squat or stoop lift rather than a hip hinge can be considered technique errors, but squatting and stoop lifting are by no means faulty movement patterns inherently. They simply differ from a hip hinge, so they can be suboptimal means of performing an exercise that you intend to train specifically for the benefits that hip hinging can provide as opposed to other movements. The hip hinge can take some practice to learn, but investing the time and effort into doing so can be a prudent investment given that having the motor skill of hip hinging can meaningfully expand your freedom in how you can move both inside and outside of the gym.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your lower body or lower back strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your lower body and back muscles. Among trained lifters, grip strength can very commonly limit performance of Romanian deadlifts if they do not utilize straps since the posterior chain has the potential to move rather heavy loads for this exercise. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least a few minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.

### Romanian deadlift vs stiff-leg deadlift

The terms “Romanian deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” are often used interchangeably to describe the same exercise by scholarly articles and lifters in general. The nomenclature of the Romanian deadlift has been attributed to this exercise being performed by Nicu Vlad, a Romanian weightlifter who won gold, silver, and bronze medals respectively in the 1984, 1988, and 1996 Olympic games in addition to medaling in seven World Weightlifting Championships (with three of them being gold). American weightlifting coach Jim Schmitz reportedly saw Vlad performing the barbell hip hinge exercise and subsequently called it the Romanian deadlift since Vlad did not have a particular name for the variation he was performing.

While they may be commonly used to refer to the same exercise, some people will use the names “Romanian deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” to refer to different variations. They consider the stiff-leg deadlift to be what is described in this database as the “barbell stiff-leg deadlift from floor,” which involves each rep starting with the plates fully on the floor like a conventional deadlift. This variation differs from the Romanian deadlift, which starts from the upright position and uses a range of motion individualized to someone’s flexibility as the plates often do not contact the floor. If someone has the hamstring flexibility for the plates to contact the floor during a Romanian deadlift, a light tap to the ground is utilized, but the full weight of the bar is not transferred into the ground like how the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor is performed. In contrast to the Romanian deadlift, which is typically performed with a controlled lowering phase, this conception of the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor often involves a quicker lower phase with a tempo akin to how conventional deadlifts are commonly performed, which is to say a controlled drop. Both variations can involve a mild amount of knee flexion at the bottom of the hinge, but the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor can require that someone adopts a more flexed lumbar position in order to pull from the floor with the knee position while maintaining high hips. This differs from the Romanian deadlift being performed only through the range of motion where someone can maintain close to a neutral lumbar spine or a flat back position. 

As demonstrated by sources such as Hegyi et al. (2018) along with Piper and Waller (2001), another interpretation of the stiff-leg deadlift also exists as a truly straight leg deadlift. Under this conception of the exercise, the knees are kept extended to the extent that they are completely straight but not fully locked (which could otherwise allow for knee hyperextension in individuals with the mobility to do so). Such a technique would result in a movement that is more akin to “bending over” rather than “sitting back,” and the bar would correspondingly be likely to drift further in front of the legs compared to a Romanian deadlift, which utilizes a mild knee bend that allows you to “sit back.” In light of the different ways in which these names may be interpreted, hopefully this note provides some clarification if you have ever been confused by how different individuals may view Romanian deadlifts and stiff-leg deadlifts exercises as the same exercise or distinct variations.## Setting up
1. Fully lower the Smith machine’s safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails), and position the bar somewhat below knee height with the safety hooks resting on the safety bars.
    1. The proper starting height of the bar should comfortably allow you to get into the starting position. If the bar is too low, it can be difficult initially to move your hips under the bar with a bench behind you. If the bar is too high, you will be unable to lift your hips high enough to receive the bar initially without arching your back excessively.
2. Determine whether your Smith machine has angled or purely vertical sliding rails.
    1. If your machine has angled sliding rails, it will be ideal for you to position your bench (or other object during the next step) on the side that allows the bar to track slightly forward when lifting the bar up and slightly backward when lowering it. This means that the bottom of the rails should be horizontally closer to the bench than the top of the rails. However, the direction in which the rails angle can vary among different machines, and you may be restricted from setting up on a bench on the side of the machine’s frame. You can still hip thrust with the bar tracking backward during the lifting phase, but it may not feel as natural compared to if the bar tracks forward while lifting up.
    2. If your machine has angled sliding rails, it does not matter which side you face during this exercise.
3. Situate a flat exercise (or viable alternative) in front of the bar with the length of the bench parallel to the length of the bar.
    1. Position the bench at a distance from the bar that specifically enables you to rest your upper back comfortably on the edge of the bench while the bar is on the crease of your hips. Finding this position may require some trial and error, which is why it is recommended to perform a couple of hip thrust reps with the empty bar before you add weight plates.
    2. A specific hip thrust bench is ideal to use if you can move it, but it is not commonly available.
    3. If you use a mobile exercise bench, make sure that it will not slide back during the exercise. In contrast to a barbell hip thrust, it is not normally feasible to anchor a bench against a rack or wall when using an immobile Smith machine. However, you can place some heavy weight plates against the back of your bench if you are concerned it may slide back during your set. Note that certain benches may be more likely to slide than others.
    4. You may also be able to use a soft plyometric box near knee height that is anchored in the same manner as a flat bench.
    5. What ultimately matters is selecting an object that is sturdy, stable, and reasonably close to being knee height while being comfortable enough to support your upper back.
4. Optionally, obtain a pad (or an object that can function as a pad) that you will place between the bar and your hips.
    1. Using a pad is not required but strongly recommended because it can be very uncomfortable to have a barbell directly resting on your hip bones with a meaningful load.
    2. You can wrap a hip thrust pad or a squat pad around the middle of the bar.
    3. Alternatively, you can place a thick foam or folded exercise mat on the crease of your hip during the next step and subsequently keep it there to cushion your hip bones. Be aware that foam pads not specifically designed for hip thrusts may be somewhat damaged from this exercise.
5. Sit down on the ground with your upper back against the edge of the bench and your legs straight out in front of you under the bar.
    1. Specifically place the lower part of your shoulder blades against the edge of the bench. Feel free to adjust your upper back positioning mildly from this point based on what feels most stable to you.
    2. The middle of the bar should be aligned with the midline of your body.
6. Bring your heels toward your buttocks by bending your knees mildly greater than 90 degrees, with the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can situate your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, but it is up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can opt to point your toes forward or somewhat out to the sides.
    3. The optimal degree of knee bend should allow your feet to feel stable while being firmly planted on the ground throughout the set. It is normally appropriate to position your feet far enough back that your knees will be bent by approximately 90 degrees at the top of your hip thrust. From this placement, you can adjust specifically where you situate your feet based on your preference.
7. Grasp the bar with your palms facing down, and tuck your chin mildly toward your chest.
    1. So long as your hands are wider apart than your hips, you can adjust your grip width based on what feels stable and comfortable to you during this exercise.
    2. Continue holding the bar and keeping your chin mildly tucked throughout the exercise.
8. Using the form described in the exercise technique section, perform a couple of hip thrust reps with the empty bar to confirm that your bench placement lets the bar comfortably rest on the crease of your hips.
    1. If the bar is not positioned appropriately relative to your hips, adjust your bench placement prior to checking again until you have determined that you are set up correctly.
    2. Positioning the bar appropriately will help you avoid the excessive discomfort that may arise from positioning the bar too high (i.e., directly on your lower abdomen) or too low (i.e., directly on your groin).
    3. Some people may experience discomfort when the bar looks aligned with the crease of their hips if the bar is directly resting on the bony bumps on the front of their hip bones. If you notice that this occurs for you, you can move the bench slightly further back away from the bar to shift the pressure away from these bony bumps.
9. Load the bar with your intended weight, and assume the starting position by repeating steps 5-7.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to help stabilize your lower back.
    2. From this starting position, your back should either be flat or exhibit its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
2. Lift your hips up until the bar is fully resting on the crease of your hips and the safety hooks are no longer resting on the safety bars.
3. Rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion.
4. Squeeze your glutes to continue lifting up until you can draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side.
    1. Since the bar begins from an elevated position, you may not have to lift your hips up much further from when you initially received the bar to reach this position.
5. Lower your hips in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your machine permits.
    1. With most Smith machines, the depth to which you can lower your hips will be determined by when the bar reaches the bottom of the slide rails. These Smith machines reach their lowest position before the weight plates or your buttocks can contact the ground, resulting in a shorter range of motion than a barbell hip thrust. If you are using one of these machines, you can aim to lightly tap the bar to the bottom of the machine.
    2. Certain Smith machines permit you to lower the bar through the same range as if you used a barbell. With one of these less common machines, you can finish the lowering phase when either the weight plates lightly contact the ground, or you have reached the end point permitted by your hip mobility.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid simply letting gravity pull the bar down abruptly, as opposed to you using your glutes to control the descent actively. Do not bounce the bar out of the bottom position.
6. Lift your hips up by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glutes engage during this exercise.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
    5. Throughout the exercise, the soles of your feet should remain fully planted on the ground, and your upper back should remain firmly in contact with the bench.
    6. Continue holding the bar for stability, but avoid trying to lift the bar up or push it down with your hands.
7. Alternate between steps 5 and 6 until you have completed your set.
    1. For tracking purposes, each rep finishes after you have fully lifted your hips from the bottom position. The initial movement you performed during step 4 does not constitute a full rep.
8. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars and lower your hips until the safety hooks are fully resting on the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise will predominantly develop your gluteus maximus while also training your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, denoted by a small dot or circle on some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heel (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when leaning on the machine with your knee aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. Certain machine models have an adjustable foot plate rather than an adjustable lower leg pad. If your machine has the adjustable foot plate, you should adjust its height until the lower leg pad is situated slightly above the back of your heel when standing on the foot plate.
2. Adjust the thigh pad to a position that allows you to place your lower thigh snugly against it when your knee is aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. This pad may be adjusted vertically or horizontally (or not at all) depending on which machine you are using.
3. Add weight plates to the machine.
4. Stand in front of the lower leg pad with the back of your lower legs contacting this pad and your thighs contacting the thigh pad.
5. Lean forward at your hips until you can firmly grasp the handles in a comfortable position. Continue holding them throughout your set.
    1. If your machine has forearm pads, you should also rest your forearms on them.
    2. If your machine has a torso pad (for your lower abdomen or chest) in addition to a thigh pad, you should also rest your torso on it while leaning forward.
    3. In this starting position, your knees should be straight and aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    4. Rather than being excessively arched or rounded, your lower back should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
    5. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. On the side you are about to train, pull your toes and foot up toward your shin by moving at your ankle. Maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankle in the same position in which it would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heel up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankle in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
2. Drive your heel toward your buttock by bending your knee through the full range of motion you can perform without moving your other leg.
    1. Some machines may allow you to contact your buttock or upper thigh with the lower leg pad at the end range, but other models may limit you from bending your knee to this extent.
    2. If you experience a notable cramping sensation in your hamstrings at the end range, you can stop curling the weight up just before the point where your hamstrings may cramp.
    3. While your lower leg moves through motion at your one knee, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
    4. Avoid lifting your thigh off the pad or excessively arching your lower back.
3. Reverse the movement as you allow your knee to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knee straightened.
    2. Continue keeping the foot of your resting leg planted on the ground/foot plate.
    3. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower leg down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground near a sturdy object with an elevated surface that is no higher than the height of your upper thighs while standing.
    1. Objects like exercise flat benches, stackable aerobic steps, plyometric boxes, stairs, and beds may be used.
    2. The height of the object will affect the range of motion you can use for this exercise, and selecting an object that is near mid-thigh height for you may be preferable over shorter objects to train through a longer range of motion.
2. Lift up your legs to position the back of your heels on top of the elevated surface with your knees straight or slightly bent.
    1. If you are too close to keep your knees straight or slightly bent on top of the elevated surface, you can scoot backward until you can do so.
    2. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
3. While leaving one foot on the elevated surface, lift the other foot off the elevated surface by marching that side’s thigh up while bending your knee, or by lifting that leg a little above the elevated surface while keeping your knee straight.
    1. Either position for the free leg is viable, so you can go with whichever you prefer, but keeping the knee straight may make the exercise mildly more challenging than keeping it bent.
    2. Keep one foot off the elevated surface in the static position of your choice throughout the set you perform with your other side.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heel into the elevated surface until your hip is fully extended.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your gluteus maximus and three of your four hamstring muscles.## Setting up
1. Place one weighted end of a dumbbell on the ground with its handle directed vertically, resulting in one end remaining off the ground.
    1. Placing a dumbbell on the ground in this manner will make it easier to position onto your lap compared to if you placed both ends of the dumbbell on the ground. However, if the ends of your dumbbell are not flat (such as with a plate-loaded adjustable dumbbell), you will alternatively have to place both ends of the dumbbell on the ground with a horizontal handle.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground (or an exercise mat) and the side of one of your hips directly next to the dumbbell.
3. Roll onto your side toward the dumbbell, hold the dumbbell in contact with the front of your waist with both hands, and roll onto your back with the dumbbell situated in the crease of your hips.
4. While using both hands to keep the dumbbell in the crease of your hips, bend your knees to at least 90 degrees to position the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can position your feet shoulder-width apart, but it is up to you whether you would like them closer together or farther apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes while continuing to hold the dumbbell in the crease of your hips.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glutes engage.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you can lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. To remove the dumbbell from your lap, roll over to your side as you did when setting it up, and then place it on the ground.
## Description
While your hamstrings and adductor magnus can assist to a degree, this exercise primarily targets your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent and the soles of your feet flat on the ground, approximately hip width apart. 
    1. You can rest your head on the ground.
    2. You optionally can hook the tops of your feet under a sturdy object like a couch (while your soles are still flat on the ground), which is recommended to help stabilize your body during this exercise.
2. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. As you tighten your abdominal muscles, lift your back and head up off the ground by curling your back to bring your chest toward your thighs.
    1. Given that this is a sit-up rather than a crunch, you will lift the entirety of your back off the ground.
    2. To train your abdominal muscles dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    3. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    4. As a cue, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the top of your hip bones, which is the action that contraction of your abdominal muscles can produce.
    5. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that you have fully rounded your back and cannot bring your chest closer toward your thighs.
    6. At the end of the lifting phase, your chest should be facing forward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your head and back to the starting position on the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable decline bench to your desired decline angle.
    1. An angle near 30 degrees is an appropriate starting point, but you can effectively perform this exercise with a range of decline angles.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the decline becomes steeper, so you can adjust the angle to modify the intensity.
    3. Some adjustable benches have a mild decline setting along with incline settings but lack a pad to secure your feet. This exercise specifically uses a decline with a foot/ankle pad.
2. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
3. Lie down in a controlled manner to position your back and head on the bench.
4. Place the fingertips of each hand lightly in contact with the sides of the back of your head as your upper arms and elbows are directed out away from your sides.
    1. Keeping your hands and arms in this position can help you avoid inadvertently pulling down on your head during the exercise, which would otherwise be easier to do if you interlocked your fingers and directed your upper arms up toward the ceiling.
    2. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. As you tighten your abdominal muscles, lift your back and head up off the bench by curling your back. Bring your chest toward your thighs.
    1. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that you have fully rounded your back and cannot bring your chest closer toward your thighs.
    2. Given that this is a sit-up rather than a crunch, you will lift the entirety of your back up off the bench.
    3. To train your abdominal muscles dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    4. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    5. If a scale were fixed to your head, it should not detect any force from your fingertips as you keep your hands relaxed throughout the exercise, rather than pulling your head.
    6. As a cue for your engaging abdominal muscles, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs toward your hip bones.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your back to the starting position.
    1. Rather than fully relaxing at the bottom, strive to tap the bench lightly with your upper back while keeping your abdominals tensed.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the entire movement.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable to its highest level, connect a rope attachment to it, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. In a near shoulder width stance, stand under the rope with your back facing the cable station.
3. Reach up and grasp each end of the rope with your little fingers and adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the outside of your other fingers.
4. Pull your hands down until they are approximately in front of your shoulders with your elbows bent.
    1. You will keep your hands in this position throughout your set.
5. While allowing your knees to bend slightly, mildly lean forward at your hips. Simultaneously sit your buttocks back to contact the cable station’s beam.
    1. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable. If you do not, you should lean forward further by bending your hips until you feel tension to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion of each rep.
    2. You will continue keeping your backside against the cable station for additional stability throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Without moving your hips or arms, arch your back in a controlled manner through the range of motion that feels comfortable to your lower back.
    1. How far you can comfortably arch your back will be influenced by your mobility.
    2. Some people may experience lower back discomfort in a fully arched position, and there is no need to arch beyond the point that feels right to you.
    3. When thinking about an imaginary laser beam shooting out from the middle of your chest, the beam should angle progressively higher up as you arch your back.
    4. The bottom of your ribs should lift up and away from the top of your hip bones.
2. Curl your spine as you pull the bottom of your ribs toward the tops of your hip bones in a controlled manner. Avoid moving your hips or arms as you round your entire back.
    1. Once you feel that you can no longer curl your spine through a range that feels comfortable to your back, you have reached your end range.
    2. Try to point the imaginary laser beam shooting out from the middle of your chest closer toward the middle point between your feet.
    3. It can help to think about crunching your elbows toward your thighs.
    4. If you finish the movement with your back fairly flat as opposed to rounded, you most likely are bending at your hips rather than curling your spine.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Determine what you will use to anchor your looped elastic bands securely. You can use a variety of approaches based on your equipment availability, and the specific details for the setup can differ depending on which option you select.
    1. Ultimately you want to make sure that whatever you use as your anchor point will not move during your set. Your anchor point should have a smooth surface that will not tear the band.
    2. A deadlift platform with band pegs can provide ideal anchor points for long bands, but it is not available at most gyms.
    3. A power rack with band pegs at the bottom is also a very stable choice that allows you to use short or long bands within the rack. A power rack with base beams elevated above the ground can additionally be used to loop the bands around the base beams as anchor points.
    4. Without any of the aforementioned equipment, you can anchor a single long band by draping it around the center of the bar and standing with the middle of your feet on top of it. This method is convenient because it only requires a band, a barbell, and weight plates. However, it may be less stable compared to if you are able to anchor the bands to band pegs.
    5. Bands can alternatively be anchored to a pair of heavy dumbbells on each end of the bar, but this approach also requires that you use other weights to anchor the four dumbbells. This can be a more time-intensive set-up, and it may be difficult to keep the dumbbells stationary with a certain level of band tension. If you use dumbbells, you can place a pair of weight plates on the sides of each dumbbell to reduce the likelihood that they will roll.
2. After adding weight plates to the barbell situated on the ground, anchor your bands to the barbell with your chosen method.
    1. Inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
    2. Once positioned, the bands should be flat rather than twisted.
    3. Ideally the bands should be mildly tensed while the weight is on the ground. If the bands are slack, they will not be as stable and may move during your set.
    4. The bands should provide the same amount of tension on each side of the bar.
3. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance close in front of the middle of the bar, as the bar is over the middle of your feet, with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly turned out to the sides.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
    2. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    3. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
    4. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of 45lb (20kg) plates (or 25kg plates common in gyms outside the USA) or bumper plates will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. When only using smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize the largest diameter plates, you can perform a block pull or rack pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with large diameter plates.
4. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
5. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the bar reaches near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
    3. As you lift the bar higher up, the band tension will progressively increase.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the ground.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While also training your spinal extensors, this exercise can train much of your lower body musculature, including your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, quads, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Place a stability ball (which is also known as a Swiss ball or physio ball) on the ground.
2. After lying down with your back on the ground, place your heels and calves close together on top of the ball with straight knees.
    1. Make sure that your heels are on the half of the ball, further away from you, to provide sufficient room for you to drive your heels back without sliding off the ball.
    2. While the back of your heels are in contact with the ball, the soles of your feet should be facing forward and not contacting the ball.
    3. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ball as you keep your knees straight. Squeeze your glutes and tighten your abdominal muscles while doing so.
    1. You should now be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
2. While keeping your hips lifted up, drive your heels toward your buttocks to bend your knees and roll the ball back toward you.
    1. Once the soles of your feet are flat on the ball and you feel that you can no longer bend your knees, you have reached your end range. People can typically drive their heels back until their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees, but what constitutes a full range of motion may vary somewhat among different individuals.
    2. At the end range, you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    3. If you experience a notably uncomfortable cramping sensation in your hamstrings when your knees are fully bent, you can stop bending your knees just before reaching that point.
    4. Continue keeping your glutes and abdominal muscles tightened throughout your set. This will help prevent you from excessively arching your lower back or letting your hips sag down.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively straightening your knees in a controlled manner to roll the ball away from you.
    1. You should finish with your knees fully straightened, allowing you to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Continue keeping your hips bridged up.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically engaging your gluteus maximus, this exercise can train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to a trap bar situated on the ground and positioning the low handles up, stand within the middle of the trap bar with approximately a hip-width to shoulder-width stance and your arms at your sides.
    1. Specifically how wide you situate your feet apart can be based on what feels most comfortable to you when picking up a weight off the floor with a deadlifting motion.
    2. Certain trap models only have one pair of handles that are the same height as the low handles that other models have, in addition to a pair of high handles that allow you to grasp the bar with your hands elevated more than when grasping a barbell on the ground
2. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from hinging at your hips than bending your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift moderately forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have fully sat back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    4. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient starting position and help engage your back muscles.
3. With your elbows fully straightened, firmly grasp the center of the handles with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hands while holding the bar, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. The initial submaximal pull allows you to check if you have grasped the handles correctly, which will be indicated by the handles not rotating forward or backward when applying the pre-tension.
    2. If you pick up a trap bar with your hands positioned slightly away from the middle of the handles, the trap bar can start rotating in a manner that makes it very difficult to hold and lift appropriately.
    3. Even for experienced lifters, it can be easy to grasp a trap bar’s handles without having their hands truly centered initially, so applying the pre-tension can make sure you are in your ideal starting position.
    4. If the bar starts rotating when you apply the pre-tension, re-center your grip, and apply the pre-tension again until the bar no longer rotates.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. Once the bar is moving, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
5. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. Just like when lifting the bar, keep your lower back flat and chest up as you descend while actively avoiding letting your lower back round or chest collapse.
    2. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees. Then you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the ground rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.

### **Hip Hinging versus Squatting Your Trap Bar Deadlift**

Relative to conventional deadlifts or block pulls with a straight barbell, trap bar deadlifts can allow you greater liberty in how you perform them with respect to biasing more of a hip hinge or squat movement pattern. A conventional deadlift keeps the load in front of you and constrains forward knee travel, while a trap bar deadlift positions the load closer to your center of mass and permits pushing your knees farther forward than you would do during a conventional deadlift. Trap bar deadlifting with greater forward knee travel makes the movement more squat-like in nature compared to maintaining a closer to vertical shin position, which results in a comparatively more hip-hinge dominant movement. As discussed by Greg Nuckols in his Stronger By Science article “Trap Bar Deadlifts are Underrated”, squat and deadlift variations can be analyzed as existing along a “squat-hinge continuum” based on the demands they impart to the hip extensor muscles (e.g., gluteus maximus, hamstrings, adductor magnus) relative to the quads, which extend the knees. Compared to trap bar deadlifting with a vertical shin, the lever arm of the resistance acting on the knees is greater when deadlifting with your knees situated farther forward, resulting in a greater ratio of loading imparted to the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. In contrast, the lever arm of the resistance acting on the hips is greater when deadlifting with vertical shins, which prompts you to sit your hips farther back and experience a lower ratio of loading imparted to the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. While people tend to perform trap bar deadlifts with their knees situated farther forward than when they conventional deadlift, you certainly have the ability to trap bar deadlift with the same knee position you would use for conventional deadlifts. Correspondingly, you can trap bar deadlift with whichever technique you prefer in congruence with your training goals.

Based on research assessing participants using their self-selected technique (which likely is the technique in which they were most efficient for pulling), it is worth noting that low handle trap bar deadlifts have been found to be more hip-dominant than knee-dominant. Swinton et al. (2011) specifically assessed male powerlifters performing low handle trap bar deadlifts and conventional dreadlifts. Both exercises imparted meaningfully greater loading to the hip extensors and lumbar spinal extensors than the quads, but conventional deadlifts were relatively more hip dominant. When lifting 80% of the participants’ conventional deadlift one-rep maxes, the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee was 3.7 for conventional deadlifts and 1.8 for low handle trap bar deadlifts. This difference was mainly attributed to the low handle trap bar deadlift having greater peak resistive torque acting on the knee, which was 190% of the conventional deadlift. A smaller gap was present for hip extensor loading as the peak resistive torque acting on the hip for the low handle trap bar deadlift was 92% that of conventional deadlift. These findings indicate that the degree to which low handle trap bar deadlifts train the quads more than conventional deadlifts most likely exceeds the degree to which conventional deadlifts train the hip extensor muscles more than low handle trap bar deadlifts. In terms of which hip extensor muscles may be less involved during trap bar deadlifts, the biarticular hamstrings in particular will likely receive a lesser stimulus. Camara et al. (2016) and Andersen et al. (2018) both found the hamstring’s biceps femoris to exhibit lower EMG activity during the low handle trap bar deadlift compared to the conventional deadlift, which is congruent with what be anticipated in light of the mechanical differences between these two exercises. Since the trap bar deadlift imparts greater loading to the knee, the knee flexion torque that results from hamstring contraction will have more of a deleterious effect on performance than during the conventional deadlift. While the hip extension torque that the hamstrings produce is beneficial, their knee flexion torque will oppose the quads as they extend the knee, so the hamstrings are not the most efficient hip extensor muscles for the nervous system to recruit during an exercise that imparts meaningful resistance to the quads. To a lesser extent, this is the same principle that results in the hamstrings experiencing minimal hypertrophy after interventions of squatting, which imparts even greater loading of the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. Accompanying the lower hamstring EMG activity,  Camara et al. (2016) found the quad’s vastus lateralis (which was not assessed by Andersen et al.) to exhibit greater activity with the trap bar deadlift compared to the conventional deadlift. In contrast to the difference in hamstring EMG activity, Andersen et al. (2018) also found the gluteus maximus (which was not assessed by Camara et al.) to exhibit similar EMG activity during the two deadlift variations, which makes sense given that the gluteus maximus is able to generate hip extension without simultaneously generating knee flexion torque.

While the previously discussed data indicates that low handle trap bar deadlifts are typically performed with a more squat-like technique than conventional deadlifts, it is natural to wonder how they may compare to back squats. Choe et al. (2021) did not assess trap bar deadlifts, but they studied conventional deadlifts and back back squats performed with 70-85% of the mixed sex participants’ one-rep maxes. The researchers found the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee to be 3.0 for conventional deadlifts and 1.4 for squats. The participants of this study had some squat and deadlift training experience as the males had average deadlift and squat maxes that were respectively 143.3kg and 122.7kg, while the females had average deadlift and squat maxes that were respectively 95.0kg and 88.0kg. However, they were not nearly as strong as the powerlifter participants partaking in the study from Swinton et al. (2011) who had average conventional deadlift and low handle trap bar deadlift maxes that were respectively 244.5kg and 265.0kg. The participants from Swinton et al. (2011) correspondingly most likely used more efficient techniques, which may explain why they exhibited a higher ratio (3.8 versus 3.0) of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee when conventional deadlifting relative to the participants from Choe et al. (2021). When comparing the low handle trap bar deadlift findings from Swinton et al. (2011) to the back squat findings from Choe et al. (2021), the back squat was more knee dominant than the low handle trap bar deadlift, but the previously mentioned hip to knee torque ratio difference (1.7 versus 1.4) was not particularly pronounced.

This data suggests that the ratio of hip to knee loading of the low handle trap bar may be more similar to back squats than conventional deadlifts, but the strengths of our conclusions are limited when comparing findings from different studies assessing meaningfully different participants. For the most valid comparison of trap bar deadlifts to back squats, we ideally would want to analyze the findings from a study that assessed the same participants performing both exercises, which is what Stahl et al. (2024) precisely investigated. The researchers specifically compared the high handle trap bar deadlift (permitting chalk use but not straps) to the back squat (performed until thighs were parallel with the floor) as moderately trained mixed-sex participants lifted loads up to 100% of their maxes. They found the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee to be 0.9 for high trap bar deadlifts and 1.4 for squats (which was the same value determined by Choe et al.), indicating that high handle trap bar deadlifts can be even more knee-dominant than back squats. This difference was influenced by the resistive lever arm acting on the hip during back squats exceeding that during high trap bar deadlifts, while the resistive lever arm acting on the knee was similar between the two exercises. Comparing the data from Stahl et al. (2024) to the data from Swinton et al. (2011), high handle trap bar deadlifts appear to be more knee-dominant than low handle trap bar deadlifts with self-selected techniques, while back squats fall in between these two variations along the spectrum of hip to knee loading.

With that said, it is important to reiterate that you have the ability to perform trap bar deadlifts with either handle height in a more hip-dominant fashion if you wish to do so. Furthermore, it is also worth emphasizing that the aforementioned findings do not mean that high handle trap bar deadlifts are superior to back squats for training the quads. While the knee to hip loading ratios are relevant when appraising the ability of a deadlift or squat variation to train particular muscle groups, the capacity of an exercise to induce muscle hypertrophy is multi-factorial in nature. Multiple studies involving back squat training interventions have found back squats to induce significant quad growth, but the effect that high handle or low handle trap bar deadlifts may have on quad development has yet to be studied.

### **Trap Bar Deadlift versus Conventional Deadlift Strength**

Two different studies from Lockie et al. (2018) have compared one rep maxes without straps between conventional deadlifts and trap bar deadlifts with 4-inch (10.2cm) higher handles. Together they assessed a total of 54 mixed sex participants with at least two years of lifting experience that included conventional and trap bar deadlifts in addition to hitting maxes. Pooling the data from the two studies together, the average high handle trap bar max was 12% greater than the conventional deadlift max, resulting in a 39lb (17.7kg) mean difference. When comparing 1-rep maxes of the conventional deadlift to the low handle trap bar deadlift, Camara et al. (2016) found the maxes to be nearly identical to each other for the two lifts, but Swinton et al. (2011) and Lake et al. (2017) found their participants to lift an average of 6-8% greater loads during the low handle trap bar deadlift.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your lower body and spinal extensor muscles. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
1. Position a short looped elastic band around both of your thighs.
    1. The band will provide the greatest resistance to your hip muscles if you place it just above your knees, while it will provide less resistance if you place it higher up your thighs.
    2. You can choose where you situate the band on your thighs based on the level of resistance you wish to experience, and if you are using a particular band for this exercise for the first time, you can adjust its placement during the set if you wish to change the difficulty after starting the set.
2. Lie down with your upper back on a flat exercise bench (or a sturdy object of similar height), your buttocks on the floor, and your feet out in front of you with your soles flat on the floor and your knees bent to at least 90 degrees.
    1. You can situate your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, but it is up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can opt to point your toes forward or somewhat out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. In this position, you can cross your arms across your chest or hold your upper arms somewhat out to your sides.
    5. You can keep your neck near a neutral position, like you normally would when standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Push your knees outward against the band’s resistance to have them in line with your feet (as opposed to letting the band pull your knees closer together), and maintain this knee position throughout your set.
    1. You will most likely feel tension in the muscles on the sides of your hips while doing so.
2. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your buttock muscles.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glutes engage during this exercise.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your hip mobility comfortably permits without rounding or excessively arching your lower back.
    1. Some people can descend all the way down until they lightly contact the ground with their glutes, but you can prioritize lowering through the range in which you can achieve through hip motion rather than lower back motion.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 while continuing to keep your knees aligned with your feet until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus while statically training your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and short hip external rotator muscles.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the barbell situated on the ground, stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance close in front of the middle of the bar, as the bar is over the middle of your feet, with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly turned out to the sides.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
    2. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    3. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
    4. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of 45lb (20kg) plates (or 25kg plates common in gyms outside the USA) or bumper plates will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. When only using smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize the largest diameter plates, you can perform a block pull or rack pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with large diameter plates.
2. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the bar reaches near the middle of your shins, pause in that position for a brief period of time (e.g., 1-2 seconds) while staying tight with your chest up and back flat.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability as you visibly remain stationary.
5. Following the pause, thrust your hips forward as you resume extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
6. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
7. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the ground.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Position a short looped elastic band around both of your thighs.
    1. The band will provide the greatest resistance to your hip muscles if you place it just above your knees, while it will provide less resistance if you place it higher up your thighs.
    2. You can choose where you situate the band on your thighs based on the level of resistance you wish to experience. If you are using a particular band for this exercise for the first time, you can adjust its placement during the set if you wish to change the difficulty after starting the set.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground, and your knees bent at least 90 degrees.
    1. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
3. Position the soles of your feet flat against each other while spreading your knees apart to bring them closer to the ground.
    1. Spread your knees as far apart as is comfortable for your hips, but there is no need to push them further apart than they would naturally fall during a frog pump without a band. The degree to which your knees will spread out comfortably will be influenced by your hip mobility.
    2. The band exerts a force that will pull your knees together unless you actively push your knees out against the band to assume the appropriate position.
    3. The outer aspect of your feet (on the side of your little toes) should be firmly in contact with the ground.
4. Tuck your chin toward your chest and lift your head slightly off the ground.
    1. This can help you keep your lower back flat rather than excessively arched during the exercise, but you have the option of resting your head on the ground if you wish to do so.
    2. You can position your arms in whichever comfortable position you prefer, such as resting by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving the outer aspects of your feet into the ground and squeezing your glutes. Keep the soles of your feet pressed together and your knees spread apart.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you ideally should be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side if you have the mobility to perform the full range of motion. If your hips feel “stuck” or quite uncomfortable before reaching this point, you may not have the hip mobility to move through the full range with your current knee position. Bringing your knees somewhat higher up and closer together may allow you to lift your hips up higher in a more comfortable manner.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively. Tensing your abdominal muscles may help limit your lower back from arching.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glutes engage during this exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. Continue keeping your soles in contact with each other.
    2. After determining which knee position allows you to comfortably move through your full range of motion, strive to keep your knees in the same position throughout your set.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus while statically training your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and short hip external rotator muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright with approximately a hip width stance while holding the kettlebells by your thighs with straight elbows as your palms face your thighs.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hand while holding them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
    3. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    4. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    5. Pick up the kettlebells with a hip hinge or squat motion where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Once upright, you can situate your palms right next to the outer aspect of the front of your thighs so that your palms face at an angle between backward and in toward each other.
    1. This results in a semi-overhand grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip that would direct your palms straight backward (with the kettlebells directly in front of your body) and a neutral grip that would direct your palms straight in toward your sides (with the kettlebells directly to the sides of your body).
    2. You will keep your palms facing this way throughout your set.
    3. Your neck should be near a neutral position when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead, and you will keep your neck in this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Bracing will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hip throughout your set.
2. While allowing the other side’s foot to lift up off the floor, begin hinging at the hip on the side to which you shifted your weight by sitting your buttocks back as you let your knee mildly bend.
    1. As you do so, the kettlebells will naturally descend, and your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    2. Allow your free leg to swing backward as it moves with your torso in a see-saw-like manner, but focus on hinging at the hip of your working leg instead of kicking your other leg back.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows straightened, core braced, lower back near a neutral position, and working leg’s sole fully in contact with the ground while doing so.
    4. To the best of your ability, keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down.
    5. Most of the motion should come from your hip, and your shin should remain in a fairly vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knee fully straightened.
    6. You do not need to focus on bending your knee, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks back while not intentionally keeping your knee locked.
3. Continuing hinging at your hip in a controlled manner until you have reached the end of your available range of motion.
    1. While some people can descend until their torsos are close to parallel to the ground, how deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knee will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer descend without rounding your lower back, you have finished descending through your available range of motion.
4. Reverse the hinge movement by driving through your working leg’s hip to lift yourself up until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straightened, core braced, lower back near a neutral position, hip bones level, and working leg’s sole fully in contact with the ground.
    2. After you are fully upright, you could place your free leg’s foot back onto the ground if you could use some additional balance assistance.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which foot remains in contact with the ground.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    3. After finishing the last rep with your other side, set the kettlebells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to a trap bar situated on the ground and positioning the high handles up, stand within the middle of the trap bar with approximately a hip-width to shoulder-width stance and your arms at your sides.
    1. Specifically how wide you situate your feet apart can be based on what feels most comfortable to you when picking up a weight off the floor with a deadlifting motion.
    2. Many trap bar models have a pair of high handles that allow you to grasp the bar with your hands elevated more than when grasping a barbell on the ground, but certain trap bars lack a pair of high handles.
    3. If your trap bar does not have high handles, you can select the trap bar block pull from this database to be able to train essentially the same movement.
2. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from hinging at your hips than bending your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift moderately forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have fully sat back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    4. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and help engage your back muscles.
3. With your elbows fully straightened, firmly grasp the center of the handles with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hands while holding the bar, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. The initial submaximal pull allows you to check if you have grasped the handles correctly, which will be indicated by the handles not rotating forward or backward when applying the pre-tension.
    2. If you pick up a trap bar with your hands positioned slightly away from the middle of the handles, the trap bar can start rotating in a manner that makes it very difficult to hold and lift appropriately.
    3. Even for experienced lifters, it can be easy to grasp a trap bar’s handles without having their hands truly centered initially, so applying the pre-tension can make sure you are in your ideal starting position.
    4. If the bar starts rotating when you apply the pre-tension, re-center your grip, and apply the pre-tension again until the bar no longer rotates.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. Once the bar is moving, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
5. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. Just like when lifting the bar, keep your lower back flat and chest up as you descend while actively avoiding letting your lower back round or chest collapse.
    2. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees. Then you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the ground rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this can meaningfully train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.

### **Hip Hinging versus Squatting Your Trap Bar Deadlift**

Relative to conventional deadlifts or block pulls with a straight barbell, trap bar deadlifts can allow you greater liberty in how you perform them with respect to biasing more of a hip hinge or squat movement pattern. A conventional deadlift keeps the load in front of you and constrains forward knee travel, while a trap bar deadlift positions the load closer to your center of mass and permits pushing your knees farther forward than you would do during a conventional deadlift. Trap bar deadlifting with greater forward knee travel makes the movement more squat-like in nature compared to maintaining a closer to vertical shin position, which results in a comparatively more hip-hinge dominant movement. As discussed by Greg Nuckols in his Stronger By Science article “Trap Bar Deadlifts are Underrated”, squat and deadlift variations can be analyzed as existing along a “squat-hinge continuum” based on the demands they impart to the hip extensor muscles (e.g., gluteus maximus, hamstrings, adductor magnus) relative to the quads, which extend the knees. Compared to trap bar deadlifting with a vertical shin, the lever arm of the resistance acting on the knees is greater when deadlifting with your knees situated farther forward, resulting in a greater ratio of loading imparted to the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. In contrast, the lever arm of the resistance acting on the hips is greater when deadlifting with vertical shins, which prompts you to sit your hips farther back and experience a lower ratio of loading imparted to the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. While people tend to perform trap bar deadlifts with their knees situated farther forward than when they conventional deadlift, you certainly have the ability to trap bar deadlift with the same knee position you would use for conventional deadlifts. Correspondingly, you can trap bar deadlift with whichever technique you prefer in congruence with your training goals.

Based on research assessing participants using their self-selected technique (which likely is the technique in which they were most efficient for pulling), it is worth noting that low handle trap bar deadlifts have been found to be more hip-dominant than knee-dominant. Swinton et al. (2011) specifically assessed male powerlifters performing low handle trap bar deadlifts and conventional dreadlifts. Both exercises imparted meaningfully greater loading to the hip extensors and lumbar spinal extensors than the quads, but conventional deadlifts were relatively more hip dominant. When lifting 80% of the participants’ conventional deadlift one-rep maxes, the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee was 3.7 for conventional deadlifts and 1.8 for low handle trap bar deadlifts. This difference was mainly attributed to the low handle trap bar deadlift having greater peak resistive torque acting on the knee, which was 190% of the conventional deadlift. A smaller gap was present for hip extensor loading as the peak resistive torque acting on the hip for the low handle trap bar deadlift was 92% that of conventional deadlift. These findings indicate that the degree to which low handle trap bar deadlifts train the quads more than conventional deadlifts most likely exceeds the degree to which conventional deadlifts train the hip extensor muscles more than low handle trap bar deadlifts. In terms of which hip extensor muscles may be less involved during trap bar deadlifts, the biarticular hamstrings in particular will likely receive a lesser stimulus. Camara et al. (2016) and Andersen et al. (2018) both found the hamstring’s biceps femoris to exhibit lower EMG activity during the low handle trap bar deadlift compared to the conventional deadlift, which is congruent with what be anticipated in light of the mechanical differences between these two exercises. Since the trap bar deadlift imparts greater loading to the knee, the knee flexion torque that results from hamstring contraction will have more of a deleterious effect on performance than during the conventional deadlift. While the hip extension torque that the hamstrings produce is beneficial, their knee flexion torque will oppose the quads as they extend the knee, so the hamstrings are not the most efficient hip extensor muscles for the nervous system to recruit during an exercise that imparts meaningful resistance to the quads. To a lesser extent, this is the same principle that results in the hamstrings experiencing minimal hypertrophy after interventions of squatting, which imparts even greater loading of the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. Accompanying the lower hamstring EMG activity,  Camara et al. (2016) found the quad’s vastus lateralis (which was not assessed by Andersen et al.) to exhibit greater activity with the trap bar deadlift compared to the conventional deadlift. In contrast to the difference in hamstring EMG activity, Andersen et al. (2018) also found the gluteus maximus (which was not assessed by Camara et al.) to exhibit similar EMG activity during the two deadlift variations, which makes sense given that the gluteus maximus is able to generate hip extension without simultaneously generating knee flexion torque.

While the previously discussed data indicates that low handle trap bar deadlifts are typically performed with a more squat-like technique than conventional deadlifts, it is natural to wonder how they may compare to back squats. Choe et al. (2021) did not assess trap bar deadlifts, but they studied conventional deadlifts and back back squats performed with 70-85% of the mixed sex participants’ one-rep maxes. The researchers found the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee to be 3.0 for conventional deadlifts and 1.4 for squats. The participants of this study had some squat and deadlift training experience as the males had average deadlift and squat maxes that were respectively 143.3kg and 122.7kg, while the females had average deadlift and squat maxes that were respectively 95.0kg and 88.0kg. However, they were not nearly as strong as the powerlifter participants partaking in the study from Swinton et al. (2011) who had average conventional deadlift and low handle trap bar deadlift maxes that were respectively 244.5kg and 265.0kg. The participants from Swinton et al. (2011) correspondingly most likely used more efficient techniques, which may explain why they exhibited a higher ratio (3.8 versus 3.0) of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee when conventional deadlifting relative to the participants from Choe et al. (2021). When comparing the low handle trap bar deadlift findings from Swinton et al. (2011) to the back squat findings from Choe et al. (2021), the back squat was more knee dominant than the low handle trap bar deadlift, but the previously mentioned hip to knee torque ratio difference (1.7 versus 1.4) was not particularly pronounced.

This data suggests that the ratio of hip to knee loading of the low handle trap bar may be more similar to back squats than conventional deadlifts, but the strengths of our conclusions are limited when comparing findings from different studies assessing meaningfully different participants. For the most valid comparison of trap bar deadlifts to back squats, we ideally would want to analyze the findings from a study that assessed the same participants performing both exercises, which is what Stahl et al. (2024) precisely investigated. The researchers specifically compared the high handle trap bar deadlift (permitting chalk use but not straps) to the back squat (performed until thighs were parallel with the floor) as moderately trained mixed-sex participants lifted loads up to 100% of their maxes. They found the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee to be 0.9 for high trap bar deadlifts and 1.4 for squats (which was the same value determined by Choe et al.), indicating that high handle trap bar deadlifts can be even more knee-dominant than back squats. This difference was influenced by the resistive lever arm acting on the hip during back squats exceeding that during high trap bar deadlifts, while the resistive lever arm acting on the knee was similar between the two exercises. Comparing the data from Stahl et al. (2024) to the data from Swinton et al. (2011), high handle trap bar deadlifts appear to be more knee-dominant than low handle trap bar deadlifts with self-selected techniques, while back squats fall in between these two variations along the spectrum of hip to knee loading.

With that said, it is important to reiterate that you have the ability to perform trap bar deadlifts with either handle height in a more hip-dominant fashion if you wish to do so. Furthermore, it is also worth emphasizing that the aforementioned findings do not mean that high handle trap bar deadlifts are superior to back squats for training the quads. While the knee to hip loading ratios are relevant when appraising the ability of a deadlift or squat variation to train particular muscle groups, the capacity of an exercise to induce muscle hypertrophy is multi-factorial in nature. Multiple studies involving back squat training interventions have found back squats to induce significant quad growth, but the effect that high handle or low handle trap bar deadlifts may have on quad development has yet to be studied.

### **Trap Bar Deadlift versus Conventional Deadlift Strength**

Two different studies from Lockie et al. (2018) have compared one rep maxes without straps between conventional deadlifts and trap bar deadlifts with 4-inch (10.2cm) higher handles. Together they assessed a total of 54 mixed sex participants with at least two years of lifting experience that included conventional and trap bar deadlifts in addition to hitting maxes. Pooling the data from the two studies together, the average high handle trap bar max was 12% greater than the conventional deadlift max, resulting in a 39lb (17.7kg) mean difference. When comparing 1-rep maxes of the conventional deadlift to the low handle trap bar deadlift, Camara et al. (2016) found the maxes to be nearly identical to each other for the two lifts, but Swinton et al. (2011) and Lake et al. (2017) found their participants to lift an average of 6-8% greater loads during the low handle trap bar deadlift.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your lower body and spinal extensor muscles. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
1. Set a weight on the ground and lie down on your back next to it.
    1. A weight plate, medicine ball, dumbbell, or kettlebell is a viable option. Select whichever option you find most comfortable to hold to your chest.
2. Pick up the weight and hold it just above your chest with bent elbows as you firmly grasp each side of the weight.
3. Bend your knees to position the soles of your feet flat on the ground approximately hip width apart, and rest your head on the ground.
    1. You can optionally hook the tops of your feet under a sturdy object like a couch (while your soles are still flat on the ground), which is recommended to help stabilize your body during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. As you tighten your abdominal muscles, lift your back and head up off the ground by curling your back. Bring your chest toward your thighs.
    1. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that you have fully rounded your back and cannot bring your chest closer toward your thighs. At the top, your chest should be facing forward.
    2. Continue holding the weight close to your chest throughout your set.
    3. Given that this is a sit-up rather than a crunch, you will lift the entirety of your back up off the ground.
    4. To train your abdominal muscles dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    5. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    6. As a cue for engaging your abdominal muscles, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs toward your hip bones.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your back to the starting position.
    1. Rather than fully relaxing at the bottom, strive to tap the ground lightly with your upper back while keeping your abdominals tensed.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the entire movement.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Position the weight you intend to use at the base of the Roman chair between its handles such that you can reach the weight when your feet are on its foot plate.
    1. Based on your equipment availability and preference, you can select essentially any type of weight that you can hold. Dumbbells and kettlebells (which can be held as a pair or as one weight in both hands) can be used in addition to a weight plate, barbell, EZ curl bar, and medicine ball.
    2. Ideally select a weight that does not physically restrict you from hinging through your full range of motion when holding it.
    3. If you use a barbell to which you add plates, small diameter plates may be preferable over larger diameter plates to prevent the plates from limiting your motion.
    4. A fixed weight bar can be a quality choice since its weights are of a small diameter, and holding it with a wide grip can reduce the likelihood that it contacts the ground prematurely while you hinge.
2. Adjust the height of the Roman chair’s pad to a level that positions the top of your hip bones somewhat above the top of the pads when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. This position will allow you to move freely about your hips in contrast to if the pad is contacting the top of your hip bones, which would restrict hip motion and prompt you to perform the exercise via spinal motion.
    2. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones (rather than only somewhat lower), some individuals may feel knee discomfort, so a sweet spot can exist for pad placement.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position.
3. While placing your hands onto the handles for balance support, step onto the foot plate one leg at a time with the back of your heels in contact with the back of the foot plate, so that the entirety of your soles are in contact with the foot plate.
    1. Position your feet symmetrically in a stance that feels comfortable to you, which can involve pointing your toes somewhat outward or pointing them fairly straight forward.
    2. Situating your feet approximately hip width to shoulder width apart from each other will result in a viable stance width, but how wide of a stance you can use will be influenced by the width of your Roman chair’s foot plate.
    3. Your knees should be straight after you have finished setting your feet on the foot plate.
4. Check to make sure that the pad is positioned as described by step 1, and readjust the pad height if it is not prior to resuming your setup.
## Exercise technique
1. Begin bracing your core by taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. After the deep “belly breath,” you can continue thinking about tensing the front of your abdominals, sides of your abdominals, and lower back muscles simultaneously in a manner that allows you to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. As a helpful cue for your “360 degree brace,” you can tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you are bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
2. Hinge at your hips until you are low enough to firmly grasp the weight you selected with straight elbows as your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your lower back and neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. Nearly all of the motion should occur from you moving at your hips as you keep your trunk in a static position that maintains the natural curvature of your lower back (as opposed to bending over by rounding your lower back).
    3. Maintain a straight knee position throughout your set, but you should avoid intentionally locking your knees out by squeezing your quads.
    4. Depending on your limb lengths and the height of your Roman chair, you may have to round your upper back somewhat to grasp the weight initially.
    5. The precise way in which you will hold the weight may vary based on the specific implement you selected.
3. While holding the weight with your arms hanging down, lift your torso up through your hips until you can draw approximately a straight line from your legs through your hips and to your torso.
    1. You can finish the lifting phase by fully squeezing your glutes, which helps cue hip movement at the top as opposed to spinal movement.
    2. Avoid arching your lower back excessively at the top since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the hip motion you just performed during the lowering phase.
4. Lower your torso by hinging at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner as you continue keeping your trunk in a statically braced position.
    1. While hinging at your hips, you should feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings that progressively intensifies.
    2. Most people will be able to descend until their shoulders are lower than their hips, but how deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    3. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch, you have finished descending through your available hip range of motion.
    4. If you notice that the weight contacts the ground before you have reached the end of your hip hinge range of motion, bend your elbows just enough to permit your full range of motion without the weight contacting the ground.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the weight back down on the ground.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Fully lower the Smith machine’s safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) and the machine’s bar before adding weight plates to the bar and making sure that the safety ****hooks are rotated out away from the safety bars (rather than being underneath the safety bars).
2. Stand close in front of the middle of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can position yourself to face the side where the bar will track slightly backward when lowering it (which places the top of the rails farther in front of you than the bottom).
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3.  Hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar, while your elbows are straight.
    1. You can set up with the bar approximately over the middle of your feet, which should situate the bar close to one inch (2.5cm) away from most individuals’ shins while initially standing upright.
    2. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    3. Throughout this movement, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
5. Brace your core and stand upright with the bar by extending your hips and knees until they are straight, while continuing to keep your elbows straight and your lower back near a neutral position.
    1. To brace effectively, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
    2. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    3. After the deep “belly breath,” you can continue thinking about tensing the front of your abdominals, sides of your abdominals, and lower back muscles simultaneously in a manner that allows you to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    4. As a helpful cue for your “360 degree brace,” you can tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you are bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    5. While your lower back muscles will be working to maintain a rigid trunk position, lifting the bar upward should chiefly occur from your hip and thigh muscles extending your hips and knees.
    6. Keep the bar very close to your legs as you lift the bar rather than allowing the bar to drift forward.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the bar just in front of your legs. Avoid letting the bar drift forward away from you, which may be more likely to occur if you think about “bending over” rather than “sitting back.”
    2. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the bar.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    5. Throughout the set, keep your feet fully planted on the ground and your neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. If you have sufficient hamstring flexibility and can hinge until the bar reaches the bottom of the machine, tap the bottom lightly with the bar, but do not let the weight rest on the bottom.
    4. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
3. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, feet fully on the ground, and the bar close to your legs.
    2. When you have finished lifting the bar, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips properly stacked under your shoulders, preventing excessive arching.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    3. After finishing the last rep, set the bar down in the same manner in which you initially picked it up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.

### **Learning how to hip hinge**

If you have minimal experience training hip hinge exercises such as Romanian deadlift and good morning variations, it is normal to have difficulty performing this movement pattern. Like riding a bicycle or shooting a basketball, hip hinging is a motor skill that may require repeated practice before you feel comfortable consistently using it. Broadly speaking, there are two types of movement substitution patterns that individuals who are in the process of learning how to hip hinge commonly perform when attempting to execute a hip hinge exercise.

One of these is using a stoop lift, which involves dynamically rounding the back (through flexion of the lumbar and thoracic spine) to bend over prior to uncoiling the spine into an upright position during the ascent. This often is accompanied by the buttock not moving particularly far backward and the arms drifting out in front of the legs in contrast to a strict hip hinge, which utilizes a visibly flat back position (with close to a neutral lumbar spine) and has the buttock noticeably moving backward during the descent. In terms of similarity, a stoop lift and a hip hinge both involve only a mild magnitude of knee bend during the descent while keeping the hips high. A clue that you may be performing more of a stoop lift than a hip hinge is feeling a stretching sensation in your back rather than feeling your back muscles remain statically tight throughout your set as these muscles function to maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hips. With a properly performed hip hinge, you should certainly feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the bottom of the lift, but you may also feel this stretching sensation during a stoop lift. Nonetheless, a hip hinge can allow you to feel the hamstring stretch earlier during the descent compared to a stoop lift since the hamstrings are shortened by the posterior pelvic tilt (tucking the tailbone under) that is coupled with lumbar spine flexion.

In addition to performing a stoop lift unintentionally when attempting to hip hinge, people can commonly utilize more of a squatting motion that is characterized by greater knee bend and forward tracking of the knees during the descent. Compared to a hip hinge, using more of a squatting movement can result in the buttock moving downward during the descent as opposed to backward, and the shins can also incline forward as opposed to remaining vertical. Because the hamstrings shorten as the knees bend, someone moving in a squat pattern when attempting to perform a hip hinge exercise is unlikely to feel much of a stretching sensation in the hamstrings at the bottom of the lift. Not feeling a hamstring stretch is the most straightforward indicator that you may be squatting your hip hinge exercise, but an additional clue would be if you perceive your quads to be working (e.g., feeling a “burning” sensation or lots of tension) more intensely than your hamstrings during the exercise.

When thinking about moving through hip hinge, stoop lift, and squat patterns, it is important to recognize that these movements can occur along a spectrum. You can perform a hip hinge exercise with a technique that is mostly a hip hinge while still utilizing elements of a stoop lift or a squat that may not be ideal for what you wish to accomplish from the exercise. Similarly, your technique can incrementally progress from being stoop lift-dominant or squat-dominant to becoming progressively more of a hip hinge as your body learns how to move in a different way through repeated practice. If your hip hinge technique currently has much room for improvement, it can be beneficial to start with lighter loads that may not be especially challenging to your muscles until you developed a reasonable level of competency with the movement itself. There is no need to let perfect be the enemy of good, so you do not need to wait to load a movement meaningfully until you have immaculate technique, but it would also be inadvisable to push your body to the limit without initially learning the basic fundamentals of the movement.

Depending on which element of hip hinging in which you currently are challenged, certain external cues can expedite how quickly you can learn how to hinge. When unintentionally moving in a manner that involves a greater degree of squatting or stoop lifting than is intended, a wall may help provide tactile feedback to cue sitting your buttock backward. To do so, you can perform your hip hinge with your back facing a nearby wall, and you will be able to receive confirmation as to whether you are actually sitting your hips backward by feeling the wall contact your buttock at the end of the descent. As an initial starting point, you can set up with a heel-to-toe tandem stance as the back of one heel contacts the wall. Then you can bring your rear foot forward to be aligned with your front foot in the symmetrical stance you will utilize for your hip hinge. This can provide a reasonable approximation as to how far people may commonly be able to sit their hips back when hinging, but you can adjust from there how far away you position yourself from the wall to individualize it to you.

Specifically if you have a tendency to perform a “squatty” hip hinge, practicing your hip hinge with your calves up against something like a flat exercise bench (or even a couch or chair at home) can be a rather productive means of honing your hinge. As you descend, you will receive immediate tactile feedback if your knees track forward from no longer feeling the contact with your calves, so it can be a great way to reinforce keeping your shins vertical throughout the movement. When using this method, make sure that you maintain full contact between the soles of your feet and the ground while you avoid leaning your bodyweight into the bench to the extent that you would fall backward if the bench was removed. If you successfully keep your calves in contact with the bench throughout the movement and feel a pronounced stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the end of the descent, you are right on track to achieving a high quality hip hinge.

As an additional learning tool, you can situate a long object like a dowel rod, PVC pipe, or yardstick on your back while hip hinging if you have a tendency to utilize more of a stoop lift technique with excessive back rounding. Either the calf-to-bench technique or the buttock-to-wall technique can be incorporated into an actual Romanian deadlift where you use weights, but using a rod as a cue without a partner will require you to hold the rod in place, so you would not be able to load this drill independently. To perform this movement, you would hold the rod behind against your back with one hand higher up near your head and your other hand lower down near your buttock. While standing fully upright and looking straight ahead, you should feel the rod contacting your sacrum (the bone that lies between your upper buttock cheeks and right above your tailbone), upper back between your shoulder blades, and the back of your head. You will then hip hinge while maintaining these three points of contact throughout the entirety of the movement. If you start rounding your back or moving your neck beyond the neutral position you adopt when standing fully upright, you will feel the rod lose contact with one of those three spots. This can be a rather effective form of instant feedback to help develop your ability to move predominantly through your lower body while keeping your trunk in a rigid position throughout the movement, which can transfer to improved technique for an array of different exercises. When maintaining those three contact points, you potentially can still perform a squatting motion rather than a hip hinge since moving through your lower body while minimizing back motion is also a component of squatting technique. However, if you maintain those three contact points and additionally feel your hamstrings notably stretched at the bottom of the descent, those two factors indicate that you have successfully hip hinged in all likelihood. If you find that the rod cuing shifts your movement from a stoop lift to more of a squat, you can use the rod cuing simultaneously with the calf-to-bench cuing, which can address multiple aspects of the hip hinge concurrently.

In the context of performing a hip hinge exercise, using a form that resembles more of a squat or stoop lift rather than a hip hinge can be considered technique errors, but squatting and stoop lifting are by no means faulty movement patterns inherently. They simply differ from a hip hinge, so they can be suboptimal means of performing an exercise that you intend to train specifically for the benefits that hip hinging can provide as opposed to other movements. The hip hinge can take some practice to learn, but investing the time and effort into doing so can be a prudent investment given that having the motor skill of hip hinging can meaningfully expand your freedom in how you can move both inside and outside of the gym.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your lower body or lower back strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your lower body and back muscles. Among trained lifters, grip strength can very commonly limit performance of Romanian deadlifts if they do not utilize straps since the posterior chain has the potential to move rather heavy loads for this exercise. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least a few minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.

### **Romanian deadlift vs stiff-leg deadlift**

The terms “Romanian deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” are often used interchangeably to describe the same exercise by scholarly articles and lifters in general. The nomenclature of the Romanian deadlift has been attributed to this exercise being performed by Nicu Vlad, a Romanian weightlifter who won gold, silver, and bronze medals respectively in the 1984, 1988, and 1996 Olympic games in addition to medaling in seven World Weightlifting Championships (with three of them being gold). American weightlifting coach Jim Schmitz reportedly saw Vlad performing the barbell hip hinge exercise and subsequently called it the Romanian deadlift since Vlad did not have a particular name for the variation he was performing.

While they may be commonly used to refer to the same exercise, some people will use the names “Romanian deadlift” and “stiff-leg deadlift” to refer to different variations. They consider the stiff-leg deadlift to be what is described in this database as the “barbell stiff-leg deadlift from floor,” which involves each rep starting with the plates fully on the floor like a conventional deadlift. This variation differs from the Romanian deadlift, which starts from the upright position and uses a range of motion individualized to someone’s flexibility as the plates often do not contact the floor. If someone has the hamstring flexibility for the plates to contact the floor during a Romanian deadlift, a light tap to the ground is utilized, but the full weight of the bar is not transferred into the ground like how the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor is performed. In contrast to the Romanian deadlift, which is typically performed with a controlled lowering phase, this conception of the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor often involves a quicker lower phase with a tempo akin to how conventional deadlifts are commonly performed, which is to say a controlled drop. Both variations can involve a mild amount of knee flexion at the bottom of the hinge, but the stiff-leg deadlift from the floor can require that someone adopts a more flexed lumbar position in order to pull from the floor with the knee position while maintaining high hips. This differs from the Romanian deadlift being performed only through the range of motion where someone can maintain close to a neutral lumbar spine or a flat back position.

As demonstrated by sources such as Hegyi et al. (2018) along with Piper and Waller (2001), another interpretation of the stiff-leg deadlift also exists as a truly straight leg deadlift. Under this conception of the exercise, the knees are kept extended to the extent that they are completely straight but not fully locked (which could otherwise allow for knee hyperextension in individuals with the mobility to do so). Such a technique would result in a movement that is more akin to “bending over” rather than “sitting back,” and the bar would correspondingly be likely to drift further in front of the legs compared to a Romanian deadlift, which utilizes a mild knee bend that allows you to “sit back.” In light of the different ways in which these names may be interpreted, hopefully this note provides some clarification if you have ever been confused by how different individuals may view Romanian deadlifts and stiff-leg deadlifts exercises as the same exercise or distinct variations.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your upper back on a flat exercise bench (or a sturdy object of similar height), your buttocks on the floor, and your feet out in front of you with your soles flat on the floor and your knees bent to at least 90 degrees.
    1. You can situate your feet near shoulder width apart from each other, but it is up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can opt to point your toes forward or somewhat out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. In this position, you can cross your arms across your chest or hold your upper arms somewhat out to your sides.
    5. You can keep your neck near a neutral position, like you normally would when standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift the other foot off the ground by marching that side’s thigh up while keeping your knee bent, or by straightening your knee while leaving that side’s thigh aligned with the thigh of your other leg.
    1. Either position for the free leg is similarly viable, so you can go with whichever you prefer, but keeping the knee straight may make the exercise mildly more challenging compared to keeping it bent.
    2. Keep your one foot off the ground in the static position of your choice throughout the set you perform with your other side.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heel into the ground and squeezing your glute.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thigh through your hip to your torso (on the side of your body with the foot contacting the ground)
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glutes engage during this exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your hip mobility comfortably permits without rounding or excessively arching your lower back.
    1. Some people can descend all the way down until they lightly contact the ground with their glutes, but you can prioritize lowering through the range in which you can achieve through hip motion rather than lower back motion.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will predominantly develop your gluteus maximus while also training your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent, as the soles of your feet are flat on the ground, approximately hip width apart.
    1. You can rest your head on the ground.
    2. You can optionally hook the tops of your feet under a sturdy object, such as a couch (while your soles remain flat on the ground), to help stabilize your body during this exercise.
2. Place the fingertips of each hand lightly in contact with the sides of the back of your head as your upper arms and elbows are directed out away from your sides.
    1. Since you are not interlocking your fingers along the middle of the back of your head, you should not have to lift your head up off the ground to situate your hands in the appropriate position.
    2. Keeping your hands and arms in this position can help you avoid inadvertently pulling your head down during the exercise, which would be easier to do if you interlocked your fingers and raised your upper arms toward the ceiling.
    3. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Without applying pressure to your head with your hands, tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back into the ground while you lift your head and shoulder blades off the ground by curling your middle back.
    1. Given that this is a crunch rather than a sit-up, the lower half of your back should remain in contact with the ground while you only lift up the upper half of your back mildly off the floor.
    2. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    3. If a scale were fixed to the back of your head, it should not detect any force from your fingertips as you keep your hands relaxed throughout the exercise, rather than pulling your head.
    4. As a cue, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the top of your hip bones, which is the action that contraction of your abdominal muscles can produce.
    5. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that crunching up any further would require the lower half of your back to lift off the floor.
    6. At the end of the lifting phase, your chest should be facing at an angle between forward and upward, rather than only facing upward as it did in the starting position.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your middle back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your head and shoulder blades back to the starting position on the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your obliques and especially your rectus abdominis, which is the “six-pack muscle.”## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-to-lower-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back.
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
2. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
3. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to then walk under the bar and situate the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
    7. If you find that deeper than a mild bend at your hips and knees (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) is required to position the bar on your back, you can lift the bar out of the J-hooks, set it down, raise the height of the J-hooks, and place the bar back onto the J-hooks.
4. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back and stand up in a quarter squat-like manner by extending your knees and hips.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of good morning reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight and position the safety pins to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of the good morning.
## Exercise technique
1. With the hand placement and bar positioning determined while setting up, unrack the bar standing up in a quarter squat-like manner by extending your knees and hips while pulling down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back.
2. Take a couple of steps backward and position your feet into approximately a shoulder-width stance with the soles of your feet fully contacting the floor.
3. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. After the deep “belly breath,” you can continue thinking about tensing the front of your abdominals, sides of your abdominals, and lower back muscles simultaneously in a manner that allows you to form a “360-degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. As a helpful cue for your “360-degree brace,” you can tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you are bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    4. Bracing will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hips throughout your set.
4. Hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner to move your buttocks backward while you allow your knees to bend mildly.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than rounding your lower back while doing so.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened.
    4. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    5. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something, as you keep your chest up (rather than letting your chest collapse through back rounding).
    6. While you hinge at your hips, the bar will naturally descend, and your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    7. How deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    8. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit your buttocks any farther back, you have finished descending through your available range of motion.
    9. If you positioned them appropriately, the safety pins should not restrict how far you can hip hinge, but if you are able to hinge until the bar contacts the safety pins, you can lightly tap the safety pins with the bar without resting the bar on the pins.
5. Reverse the movement by pulling your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. Since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, all of the technique points from the lowering phase apply to how to perform the lifting phase, except for the details on how deeply to hip hinge.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
7. Rerack the bar by walking forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook, and you are ready to rest the bar on the J-hooks by once again bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.

### Reducing set up time

Relative to a variety of other exercises, exercises performed with a barbell on your back can require more time initially to set them up appropriately as is demonstrated by the fairly lengthy “setting up” section. However, subsequent sessions in which you perform the same exercise can involve a quicker set up relative to the first time in which you use this exercise. To save time during future sessions, you can record the height that you determined was appropriate to position the J-cups and safety pins. If your power rack has numbered pin holes, you can simply note the positions you used. Without numbered holes, you can note how many holes were above your J-cup position and how many holes were between your safety pin position and your J-cup position. Consequently, you will not need to take a trial and error approach when setting up your power rack each session.

### Use the safety pins

It is by no means rare for people to perform exercises with a bar on their back without setting up safety pins, but using safety pins when appropriate is strongly recommended. Positioning safety pins for an exercise such as the barbell good morning is the equivalent of wearing a seat belt when driving. In either situation, you may not actually intend to have to use them, but both safety mechanisms can reduce the risk of injury if an accident occurs. While you can try to refrain from performing a set of barbell good mornings with a load or number of reps that is beyond your capacity, sometimes lifters can overestimate what is left in the tank and unintentionally fail a rep. Furthermore a non-zero risk of injury exists during any reasonably challenging physical activity even when using high quality technique and an intelligently designed program that utilizes appropriate volumes, intensities, and rates of progression. For instance, the likelihood may be very low on a per rep basis, but it is certainly possible to sustain a sudden strain injury to a hamstring or a spinal extensor muscle during the descent of a good morning. If such an acute injury occurs, you can simply set the bar down onto the safety pins when the safety pins are positioned appropriately. However, if you did not previously set up the safety pins, you face a risk of sustaining further injury if an unforeseen injury acutely occurs while your torso is in an inclined position with a bar on your back. That unfortunate predicament can easily be avoided when investing a moment of time into just positioning the safety pins beforehand. As demonstrated by competitive powerlifters, weightlifters, and bodybuilding having lower rates of reported injuries than athletes in many other sports, strenuous resistance training is not uniquely dangerous and there is no need to be fearful about lifting weights. Nonetheless, the potential for an injury to occur acutely while training can never be eliminated, and it is prudent to take the simple precautionary actions that promote your safety in the weight room.

### Standardizing range of motion

As an alternative to positioning the safety pins just under the lowest point in which the bar would descend during your good morning, you can intentionally position them directly at the lowest point in which the bar reaches when you have finished fully sitting your buttock back and reaching your full hamstring stretch. With this approach, you can lightly tap the safety pins with the bar during each rep, which will provide confirmation that you reached the same depth every rep. Doing so can help you standardize your technique and know that any progressions in your reps per set or load do not come at the expense of reducing your range of motion, which can help with tracking your improvement over time. During exercises that lack an external stopping point such as barbell good mornings, people may have a tendency to reduce depth unintentionally during challenging reps, so you can know that will not be occurring if you lightly tap the pins at the bottom of each rep. It is worth noting using pins in this manner is distinct from the barbell pin good morning, which involves letting the bar come to a dead stop and fully resting on the pins at the bottom of each rep.

### **Learning how to hip hinge**

If you have minimal experience training hip hinge exercises such as Romanian deadlift and good morning variations, it is normal to have difficulty performing this movement pattern. Like riding a bicycle or shooting a basketball, hip hinging is a motor skill that may require repeated practice before you feel comfortable consistently using it. Broadly speaking, there are two types of movement substitution patterns that individuals who are in the process of learning how to hip hinge commonly perform when attempting to execute a hip hinge exercise.

One of these is using a stoop lift, which involves dynamically rounding the back (through flexion of the lumbar and thoracic spine) to bend over prior to uncoiling the spine into an upright position during the ascent. This often is accompanied by the buttock not moving particularly far backward and the arms drifting out in front of the legs in contrast to a strict hip hinge, which utilizes a visibly flat back position (with close to a neutral lumbar spine) and has the buttock noticeably moving backward during the descent. In terms of similarity, a stoop lift and a hip hinge both involve only a mild magnitude of knee bend during the descent while keeping the hips high. A clue that you may be performing more of a stoop lift than a hip hinge is feeling a stretching sensation in your back rather than feeling your back muscles remain statically tight throughout your set as these muscles function to maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hips. With a properly performed hip hinge, you should certainly feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the bottom of the lift, but you may also feel this stretching sensation during a stoop lift. Nonetheless, a hip hinge can allow you to feel the hamstring stretch earlier during the descent compared to a stoop lift since the hamstrings are shortened by the posterior pelvic tilt (tucking the tailbone under) that is coupled with lumbar spine flexion.

In addition to performing a stoop lift unintentionally when attempting to hip hinge, people can commonly utilize more of a squatting motion that is characterized by greater knee bend and forward tracking of the knees during the descent. Compared to a hip hinge, using more of a squatting movement can result in the buttock moving downward during the descent as opposed to backward, and the shins can also incline forward as opposed to remaining vertical. Because the hamstrings shorten as the knees bend, someone moving in a squat pattern when attempting to perform a hip hinge exercise is unlikely to feel much of a stretching sensation in the hamstrings at the bottom of the lift. Not feeling a hamstring stretch is the most straightforward indicator that you may be squatting your hip hinge exercise, but an additional clue would be if you perceive your quads to be working (e.g., feeling a “burning” sensation or lots of tension) more intensely than your hamstrings during the exercise.

When thinking about moving through hip hinge, stoop lift, and squat patterns, it is important to recognize that these movements can occur along a spectrum. You can perform a hip hinge exercise with a technique that is mostly a hip hinge while still utilizing elements of a stoop lift or a squat that may not be ideal for what you wish to accomplish from the exercise. Similarly, your technique can incrementally progress from being stoop lift-dominant or squat-dominant to becoming progressively more of a hip hinge as your body learns how to move in a different way through repeated practice. If your hip hinge technique currently has much room for improvement, it can be beneficial to start with lighter loads that may not be especially challenging to your muscles until you developed a reasonable level of competency with the movement itself. There is no need to let perfect be the enemy of good, so you do not need to wait to load a movement meaningfully until you have immaculate technique, but it would also be inadvisable to push your body to the limit without initially learning the basic fundamentals of the movement.

Depending on which element of hip hinging in which you currently are challenged, certain external cues can expedite how quickly you can learn how to hinge. When unintentionally moving in a manner that involves a greater degree of squatting or stoop lifting than is intended, a wall may help provide tactile feedback to cue sitting your buttock backward. To do so, you can perform your hip hinge with your back facing a nearby wall, and you will be able to receive confirmation as to whether you are actually sitting your hips backward by feeling the wall contact your buttock at the end of the descent. As an initial starting point, you can set up with a heel-to-toe tandem stance as the back of one heel contacts the wall. Then you can bring your rear foot forward to be aligned with your front foot in the symmetrical stance you will utilize for your hip hinge. This can provide a reasonable approximation as to how far people may commonly be able to sit their hips back when hinging, but you can adjust from there how far away you position yourself from the wall to individualize it to you.

Specifically if you have a tendency to perform a “squatty” hip hinge, practicing your hip hinge with your calves up against something like a flat exercise bench (or even a couch or chair at home) can be a rather productive means of honing your hinge. As you descend, you will receive immediate tactile feedback if your knees track forward from no longer feeling the contact with your calves, so it can be a great way to reinforce keeping your shins vertical throughout the movement. When using this method, make sure that you maintain full contact between the soles of your feet and the ground while you avoid leaning your bodyweight into the bench to the extent that you would fall backward if the bench was removed. If you successfully keep your calves in contact with the bench throughout the movement and feel a pronounced stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the end of the descent, you are right on track to achieving a high quality hip hinge.

As an additional learning tool, you can situate a long object like a dowel rod, PVC pipe, or yardstick on your back while hip hinging if you have a tendency to utilize more of a stoop lift technique with excessive back rounding. Either the calf-to-bench technique or the buttock-to-wall technique can be incorporated into an actual Romanian deadlift where you use weights, but using a rod as a cue without a partner will require you to hold the rod in place, so you would not be able to load this drill independently. To perform this movement, you would hold the rod behind against your back with one hand higher up near your head and your other hand lower down near your buttock. While standing fully upright and looking straight ahead, you should feel the rod contacting your sacrum (the bone that lies between your upper buttock cheeks and right above your tailbone), upper back between your shoulder blades, and the back of your head. You will then hip hinge while maintaining these three points of contact throughout the entirety of the movement. If you start rounding your back or moving your neck beyond the neutral position you adopt when standing fully upright, you will feel the rod lose contact with one of those three spots. This can be a rather effective form of instant feedback to help develop your ability to move predominantly through your lower body while keeping your trunk in a rigid position throughout the movement, which can transfer to improved technique for an array of different exercises. When maintaining those three contact points, you potentially can still perform a squatting motion rather than a hip hinge since moving through your lower body while minimizing back motion is also a component of squatting technique. However, if you maintain those three contact points and additionally feel your hamstrings notably stretched at the bottom of the descent, those two factors indicate that you have successfully hip hinged in all likelihood. If you find that the rod cuing shifts your movement from a stoop lift to more of a squat, you can use the rod cuing simultaneously with the calf-to-bench cuing, which can address multiple aspects of the hip hinge concurrently.

In the context of performing a hip hinge exercise, using a form that resembles more of a squat or stoop lift rather than a hip hinge can be considered technique errors, but squatting and stoop lifting are by no means faulty movement patterns inherently. They simply differ from a hip hinge, so they can be suboptimal means of performing an exercise that you intend to train specifically for the benefits that hip hinging can provide as opposed to other movements. The hip hinge can take some practice to learn, but investing the time and effort into doing so can be a prudent investment given that having the motor skill of hip hinging can meaningfully expand your freedom in how you can move both inside and outside of the gym.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that will allow your legs to be in an approximately horizontal position once you are at the bottom of a rep.
    1. After estimating where this setting may be, you can adjust it during later steps if warranted.
    2. If you wish to increase the intensity of the exercise beyond the typical version, you can elevate the footplate beyond this standard setting.
2. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow the lowest point of your thighs to contact the back (i.e., the end closer to the foot plate) of the pad once you are in the starting position.
    1. This is the standard set-up for a glute ham raise, as demonstrated by certain machines having a knee pad (in addition to the thigh pad) onto which you can kneel in the starting position when setting the foot plate in this manner.
    2. The proper footplate position should permit your knees to move freely throughout the exercise while feeling stable and comfortable.
    3. If your knees are resting on top of the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far forward for a standard glute ham raise, and the exercise can function more like a higher-intensity Nordic hamstring curl.
    4. If your middle or upper thighs are resting on the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far backward for a standard glute ham raise.
    5. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal footplate position.
3. Climb up onto the machine, and position the soles of your feet fully against the footplate with your lower legs between the leg rollers and your lower thigh resting against the thigh pad.
    1. The back of your lower legs should be snugly pressed into the top pair of leg rollers slightly above your heels.
    2. While you are setting your feet, you can use the handles for assistance.
    3. Some machines have a step that you can use to help you get into position.
4. Assume an upright torso position, where your shoulders and hips are vertically stacked above your knees when viewed from the side.
    1. Your back and neck should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
5. Situate your arms by your sides with straight elbows, so that they are aligned with your torso and thighs.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set to minimize the difficulty of each rep compared to other arm positions.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Keep your glutes squeezed and your core braced throughout the set to help you maintain a rigid trunk position.
2. In a controlled manner, lean your thighs and torso forward as one unit exclusively through knee motion.
    1. As all the movement occurs from your knees transitioning to a less bent position, keep your torso and hips in a rigid position while keeping your feet firmly against the footplate.
    2. Throughout your set, you should be able to continue drawing a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders. To do so, you will have to avoid bending forward at your hips or dynamically arching your lower back, either of which may occur if you do not appropriately continue squeezing your glutes and bracing your core.
    3. While you do not need to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should actively be in control the entire time rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you downward.
    4. As you lean forward, your knees will gradually slide upward and become closer to being vertically level with your feet.
3. Continue leaning forward until you have straightened your knees with your whole body in a horizontal position or until you have reached the end range that you can control.
    1. The exercise will become progressively more difficult as you descend, so not every lifter has the strength to lower through the full range of motion with quality technique, which includes keeping the thighs and torso aligned.
    2. If you are currently unable to control the descent until your knees have straightened, you can gradually increase the range of motion across multiple sessions to progress over time, or you can train through a full range of motion with the lower intensity band-assisted glute ham raise.
4. Reverse the movement by bending your knees to lift your torso and thighs as one unit until you have returned to having your hips and shoulders vertically stacked above your knees.
    1. It can help to think about driving the balls of your feet into the footplate.
    2. Since you are continuing to keep your thighs and torso aligned, your hips and shoulders should lift upward and backward in an arcing motion at the same rate. Avoid bending over at your torso while shooting your hips backward, which is a common error people make.
    3. Strive to have your torso and thighs form the hand of an analog clock that is arcing from 9 o’clock to 12 o’clock about the center point formed by your knees.
    4. During the lifting phase, your knees will naturally descend mildly toward the bottom of the thigh pad.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, you can bend at your hips and grasp the handles to help you dismount the machine.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your hamstrings, gracilis, and sartorius, which are assisted by your gastrocnemius and gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. Position a short looped elastic band around both of your thighs.
    1. The band will provide the greatest resistance to your hip muscles if you place it just above your knees, while it will provide less resistance if you place it higher up on your thighs.
    2. You can choose where you situate the band on your thighs based on the level of resistance you wish to experience, and if you are using a particular band for this exercise for the first time, you can adjust its placement during the set if you wish to change the difficulty after starting the set.
2. Lie down on your side with the side of your body that you are about to train facing up.
    1. In this side-lying position, you can rest the bottom side of your head on the hand of your bottom arm for comfort by situating your upper arm out to the side with your elbow bent.
    2. For extra stability, you can bend the elbow of your top arm and place that side’s hand on the ground in front of your torso.
3. Bend your knees by approximately 90 degrees and bring your thighs forward so they form near a 45-60-degree angle with your torso.
    1. Based on preference and what feels comfortable to you, you can position your thighs further forward so that they are closer to forming a right angle with your torso, or you can position them further backward so that they are closer to being aligned with your torso.
    2. In this position, the inner aspects of your knees and feet should be in contact with each other.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your top leg’s knee upward away from your bottom knee while keeping the inner aspect of your feet in contact with each other and the rest of your body stationary.
    1. Move through your hip’s available range of motion, and avoid rolling your hips backward during this exercise, which is a common technical error.
    2. To help prevent your hips from rolling, you can imagine that you are wearing a belt with a buckle that has a laser beam shooting out of it. Strive to keep the laser beam pointing straight forward, parallel to the ground, throughout the movement.
    3. If you start to roll your hips backward, your belt buckle’s laser beam will start to angle upward, the exercise will feel easier, and you will be able to lift your knee higher up than if you had avoided rolling backward.
    4. While performing this movement with the appropriate technique, you will likely feel the muscles on the side of your top hip contracting.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your top knee to descend in a controlled manner until it is low enough to contact the inner aspect of your lower knee.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side by lying on your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains the upper region of your gluteus maximus and the short hip external rotator muscles situated underneath your glutes.## Setting up
1. Position the weight you intend to use at the base of the Roman chair in between its handles such that you can reach the weight when your feet are on its foot plate.
    1. For this variation, you will be holding the weight to your chest, so a weight plate, dumbbell, kettlebell, or medicine ball is a viable option that you can select based on equipment availability and preference.
2. Adjust the height of the Roman chair’s pad to a level that positions the top of your hip bones somewhat above the top of the pads when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. This position will allow you to move freely about your hips in contrast to if the pad is contacting the top of your hip bones, which would restrict hip motion and prompt you to perform the exercise via spinal motion.
    2. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones (rather than only somewhat lower), some individuals may feel knee discomfort, so a sweet spot can exist for pad placement.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position after initially completing step 2.
3. While placing your hands onto the handles for balance support, step onto the foot plate one leg at a time with the back of your heels in contact with the back of the foot plate, so that the entirety of your soles are in contact with the foot plate.
    1. Position your feet symmetrically in a stance that feels comfortable to you, which can involve pointing your toes somewhat outward or pointing them fairly straight forward.
    2. Situating your feet approximately hip width to shoulder width apart from each other will result in a viable stance width, but how wide of a stance you can use will be influenced by the width of your Roman chair’s foot plate.
    3. Your knees should be straight after you have finished setting your feet on the foot plate.
4. Check to make sure that the pad is positioned as described by step 1, and readjust the pad height if it is not prior to resuming your setup.
## Exercise technique
1. Begin bracing your core by taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. After the deep “belly breath,” you can continue thinking about tensing the front of your abdominals, sides of your abdominals, and lower back muscles simultaneously in a manner that allows you to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. As a helpful cue for your “360 degree brace,” you can tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you are bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    4. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the upright position.
    5. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
2. Hinge at your hips until you are low enough to pick up your weight and hold it to your chest.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your lower back and neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. Nearly all of the motion should occur from you moving at your hips as you keep your trunk in a static position that maintains the natural curvature of your lower back (as opposed to bending over by rounding your lower back).
    3. Maintain a straight knee position throughout your set, but you should avoid intentionally locking your knees out by squeezing your quads.
    4. Depending on your limb lengths and the height of your Roman chair, you may have to round your upper back somewhat to grasp the weight initially.
    5. The precise way in which you will hold the weight to your chest may vary based on the specific implement you selected, but what matters is that you have a firm grip on the weight and are able to keep it in a stable position.
3. While keeping the weight held to your chest, lift your torso up through your hips until you can draw approximately a straight line from your legs through your hips and to your torso.
    1. You can finish the lifting phase by fully squeezing your glutes, which helps cue hip movement at the top as opposed to spinal movement.
    2. Avoid arching your lower back excessively at the top since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the hip motion you just performed during the lowering phase.
4. Lower your torso by hinging at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner as you continue keeping your trunk in a statically braced position.
    1. While hinging at your hips and keeping the weight by your chest, you should feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings that progressively intensifies.
    2. Most people will be able to descend until their shoulders are lower than their hips, but how deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    3. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch, you have finished descending through your available hip range of motion.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the weight back down on the ground.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position a pair of deadlift blocks (or a similarly sturdy substitute capable of safely withstanding the load you will use) on the ground far enough apart from each other that the weight plates on a bar will be able to rest fully on the blocks in a stable position.
    1. For this block pull variation, your blocks should be of a height that situates the bar below the knee and at least mid-shin height when the loaded bar is resting on the blocks. This can be considered a moderate height for a sumo block pull that elevates the bar to a greater degree than the sumo low block pull. If you will be using enough weight that the bar will visibly bend when starting the pull before the plates fully lift off the blocks, the correct block height should result in the bar still being below the knee at the moment when the plates completely lift off the blocks.
    2. To appropriately position the blocks, it can be helpful to have a barbell on the ground and align the blocks about the same distance apart from each other as is the length of the barbell’s shaft (which is the middle part of the bar in between the two thick circular collars).
    3. Aerobic steps can often be used for block pulls while having the option to adjust the height by changing the number of risers you use, but you should be aware of the recommended weight limit if you opt to use heavy loads. The weight limit for aerobic steps will vary by brand, but some of the common brands recommend not exceeding 300lb (136kg) per step, which would result in a 600lb (272kg) limit when using a pair of steps for a block pull. The height of the aerobic step will also vary by brand, but the step itself is commonly near 4in (10cm) in height, and risers are often near 2in (5cm) in height.
    4. With thickness typically ranging from about 1-3.5in (2.5-8.9cm), depending on the weight and brand, bumper plates can also function as blocks that can be stacked on top of each other to provide a range of different heights.
    5. It may be worthwhile to try out different block heights to determine which height places you in your intended starting position. However, you can initially try out a pair of approximately 4in (10cm) deadlift blocks, aerobic steps without risers, or thick 45lb (20kg) bumper plates, which should situate a bar loaded with large diameter plates (e.g., 45lb (20kg) plates or any bumper plates) reasonably close to a viable height for many people. If you are going to add only smaller diameter plates onto the bar, higher blocks will naturally be needed to position the bar near the middle of your shins.
2. After placing a barbell onto the blocks and loading weight plates onto the bar, stand upright close in front of the middle of the bar, as the bar is approximately over the middle of your feet.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
3. Spread your legs apart into a wide stance that is clearly outside shoulder width, with your feet moderately rotated out away from your sides.
    1. Provided that your feet are wider apart than your shoulders but closer together than the plates, you can use a range of stance widths for your sumo deadlift.
    2. The ideal stance width can meaningfully vary among different individuals based on someone’s body structure and hip mobility.
    3. Trying out different foot positions will normally be required to determine which stance specifically feels best to you.
    4. As a starting point, you can widen your feet as far apart as you feel comfortable and stable while being able to push your knees out approximately over your heels, resulting in close to vertical shins. You will push your knees out when descending to reach the bar, so subsequent aspects of the setup can help you gauge if you should adjust your stance.
    5. The ideal amount of toe flare also varies among different lifters, but you can start with your toes pointing about 30-45 degrees out to the side before adjusting from there based on what feels best to you. Zero degrees of toe flare would mean your toes are pointing straight forward, while 90 degrees would mean your toes are pointing directly out to your side.
4. Direct your arms straight down toward the ground with straight elbows as your shoulders are packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up).
    1. Maintain this position from here onward.
5. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips while bending your knees and pushing your knees out until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees, and your knees should essentially not track forward (although a slight degree of forward knee tracking can be used if that feels best to you).
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have hinged through your available hip range of motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Try to push your knees far enough out to the sides that they are vertically stacked over your heels.
    4. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    5. Similarly, keep your chest up in a “proud chest” posture and your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    6. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
    7. The bar should be directly in front of your shins.
6. With your shoulders stacked vertically above the bar, firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down, such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath). This should expand the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles, like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360-degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. Continue bracing your core throughout your set. This will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. This can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull you further into position, with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” by extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. Keep your chest up, back flat, and knees pushed out.
    2. As your hips and chest rise at the same rate, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull. Avoid letting your torso incline further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
    3. Keep the bar very close to your legs throughout the rep.
4. After the bar reaches near your knees, focus on thrusting your hips forward until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back when viewed from the side.
5. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips and bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the blocks.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your hips back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally. After the bar has cleared your knees, you can further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
    2. Continue keeping your back flat and chest up.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the blocks for a moment rather than simply tapping the blocks and immediately deadlifting the bar up again. Then continue to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the blocks for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferring between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the blocks.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While also training your spinal extensor muscles, this exercise can train much of your lower body musculature, including your gluteus maximus, quads, and hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow you to position part of your buttocks just off the front edge of the pad when you set up with your feet on the footplate.
    1. The appropriate position should allow your thighs to rest comfortably on the pad.
    2. If the footplate is positioned excessively far from the pad, you will have to sit closer toward the middle of the pad rather than just off the front edge.
    3. If the footplate is positioned excessively close to the pad, the back of your upper thighs will not be in contact with the pad.
    4. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that allows you to feel comfortable and secure with slightly bent knees once you are in the starting position.
    1. As a starting point, you can select a height that positions the lower pair of leg rollers level with the pad and the upper pair of leg rollers higher than the pad.
    2. Not all machines permit you to adjust the footplate height.
3. Climb up onto the machine, and hook your lower legs between the leg rollers with the top of your feet snugly pressed into the top roller as your soles are facing the footplate. Sit upright with your buttocks just hanging off the front edge of the pad.
    1. Your feet should be securely anchored so that they will not slide during the exercise. The top rollers should be just above the lowest part of your shins by your ankles, while the bottom rollers should be just under your Achilles tendons (which attach to the top of your heels).
    2. Depending on the machine you are using, you can step up onto a middle beam or a step to help you get into position.
    3. While you are setting your feet, you can reach back with your hands to hold the handles for stability.
4. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, looking straight ahead. Keep your neck in this position during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Lean your torso back in a controlled manner until your torso is approximately parallel to the ground or slightly beyond this point.
    1. Allow your lower back to arch to a degree that feels comfortable to you.
    2. Specifically how much of an arch is comfortable will vary from person to person, but it is often appropriate to finish the lowering phase with your lower back arched to a similar degree as when you are normally standing upright. It is not inherently injurious to arch beyond this point, but meaningfully arching the lower back beyond this point can provoke lower back discomfort in some people.
    3. If you feel lower back discomfort at the bottom, you can strive to keep your lower back closer to a flat position with less of an arch, or you can reduce the range in which you lean back. Thinking about keeping your ribs tucked down to a greater degree as you avoid flaring them can help limit excessive arching.
2. Reverse the movement by curling your back to lift your torso up until your shoulders are vertically above your hips.
    1. To train your abs dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    2. As a cue to emphasize your abs, think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the tops of your hip bones while tightening your abs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
4. After your last rep, you can grasp the handles to help you dismount the machine.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance while holding the kettlebells with straight elbows.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    3. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    4. Pick up the kettlebells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending (straightening) your hips and knees while keeping your back flat.
2. Position your palms right next to the outer aspect of the front of your thighs so that your palms angle diagonally backward and in toward each other.
    1. Keep your palms facing this way throughout the exercise.
    2. This results in a semi-overhand grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip that would direct your palms straight backward (with the kettlebells directly in front of your body) and a neutral grip that would direct your palms straight in toward your sides (with the kettlebells directly to the sides of your body).
    3. Your neck should be near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Bracing your core will help you maintain a rigid torso position as you subsequently move about your hips rather than your back.
2. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    2. Without thinking about “bending over,” your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the kettlebells when focusing on “sitting back.”
    3. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    4. Keep the kettlebells just in front of your legs and your feet fully planted on the ground throughout the set.
3. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees.
    1. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    2. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically hinge until their hands are at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    3. If you have the flexibility to hinge until the kettlebells contact the ground, descend until you can tap the weights very lightly on the ground, but do not let the weights rest on the ground.
    4. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
4. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    2. When you have finished lifting the kettlebells, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips properly stacked under your shoulders, preventing excessive arching.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebells down.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. After finishing the last rep, set the kettlebells down in a deadlift motion by bending your hips and knees while keeping your back flat.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent and the soles of your feet flat on the ground, approximately hip width apart. Place your arms by your sides with your palms on the ground.
    1. You can rest your head on the ground.
    2. You optionally can hook the tops of your feet under a sturdy object like a couch (while your soles are still flat on the ground), which is recommended to help stabilize your body during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. As you tighten your abdominal muscles, lift your back and head up off the ground by curling your back to bring your chest toward your thighs.
    1. Given that this is a sit-up rather than a crunch, you will lift the entirety of your back off the ground.
    2. To train your abdominal muscles dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    3. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    4. Allow your hands to slide forward on the ground progressively as your torso lifts up.
    5. As a cue, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the top of your hip bones, which is the action that contraction of your abdominal muscles can produce.
    6. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that you have fully rounded your back and cannot bring your chest closer toward your thighs.
    7. At the end of the lifting phase, your chest should be facing forward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your head and back to the starting position on the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its highest position that allows you to begin each rep with your knees fully straightened.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heels (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when seated against the backrest, with your legs on top of the lower leg pad and your knees straightened.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the lower leg pad without moving the lever arm, in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. After selecting your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, sit down on its seat with your back against the back pad and your lower legs on top of the lower leg pad.
5. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
    1. In this starting position, your knees should be straight and aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. On the side you are about to train, pull your toes and foot back toward your shin by moving at your ankle. Maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankle in the same position in which it would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heel up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankle in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
3. While keeping one knee fully straightened, use your other leg to drive your heel down and back toward you as you bend your knee to pull the lower leg pad under your thighs. Drive back through the available range of motion that the machine allows.
    1. At some point, the machine will stop you from bending your knee any further, but most machines will allow you to bend your knee by at least 90 degrees.
    2. While your lower leg moves through motion at your one knee, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
4. Reverse the movement as you allow your knee to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knee fully straightened.
    2. Continue keeping your other knee straightened.
    3. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower legs up.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to hold the handles comfortably with bent elbows once you are seated.
    1. If your machine has elbow pads, the proper seat height should also permit you to rest your elbows on them comfortably while grasping the handles.
    2. The appropriate setting should allow you to keep your arms mostly relaxed when grasping the handles.
    3. You should not have to shrug your shoulders up to reach the handles.
    4. Some trial and error may be required to find a seat height that feels appropriate for you.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit on the seat with your back snugly against the back pad as your lower back is comfortably arched.
    1. The degree to which you can comfortably arch your lower back can be affected by your mobility.
    2. Starting with an appropriate degree of lower back arching will situate your abs in a lengthened position that can facilitate training them a long range of motion during this exercise.
4. Hook your feet snugly under the foot roller pads in a secure position.
    1. Specifically how you should set your feet may vary from machine to machine, but situating the front of your ankles against the back of the pads and the top of your feet against the bottom of the pads can commonly promote a stable position. If your machine allows you to adjust these pads, you can set them to where you achieve this position.
    2. Certain machine models may have foot pegs onto which you simply rest your feet, rather than roller pads that can lock your feet into position.
    3. Ultimately, you want to set up in a manner that allows your feet to feel stable throughout the exercise.
5. Reach up and grasp the handles with your palms facing in toward each other. If your machine has elbow pads, rest your elbows against them as well.
    1. In the starting position, your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl your spine as you pull the bottom of your ribs toward the tops of your hip bones in a controlled manner.
    1. This action will naturally prompt your knees and feet to lift upward, even if you do not consciously try to march your knees up. If you wish to train your hip flexors simultaneously with your abs, you can also intentionally think about driving your knees up, but make sure that you do so in addition to curling your spine rather than as an alternative to curling your spine.
    2. Once you feel that you can no longer curl your spine through a range permitted by the machine that feels comfortable to your back, you have reached your end range. The machine may physically restrict your range of motion at a certain point, even if you have the mobility to crunch further.
    3. Avoid pulling down with your arms. Your arms should only move because they are attached to your torso, which is what you are directly moving. The function of your arms is merely to transmit force from your abs into the machine. You should not feel your back muscles contract like they would during a pulling exercise (such as a straight arm lat pulldown). If you feel like your back muscles are driving the movement, you can try loosening your grip to the minimum required to hold the handles in a stable fashion.
    4. To train your abs dynamically, you should be intentionally rounding your entire back, since this motion is performed by contraction of your abs. When rounding your back appropriately, you should feel meaningful tension in your abs. If you do not feel your abs engaging, you may be substituting hip or shoulder motion for the intended spinal motion.
    5. If you finish the movement with your back fairly flat as opposed to rounded, you most likely are primarily bending at your hips rather than curling your spine.
    6. It can help to think about crunching your elbows toward your knees.
    7. You can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the middle of your chest, and you are trying to point that closer toward the middle point between your knees.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner as you uncurl your back by flaring your ribs up and away from your hip bones.
    1. Allow your knees to descend away from your elbows.
    2. Move through the full range of motion permitted by the machine that feels comfortable to your lower back. This may involve letting the machine’s lever arms (onto which you loaded weight plates) lightly tap the stoppers underneath them if the full range enabled by the machine feels comfortable to you.
    3. When uncurling your spine, your lower back will initially move into a flat position before moving into an arched position.
    4. Depending on your mobility and the range of motion your machine permits, you may feel a stretching sensation in your abs as you allow your lower back to arch.
    5. The imaginary laser beam shooting out from your chest should angle progressively higher up as you uncurl your back and flare your ribs away from your hip bones.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you feel lower back discomfort at the end of either step, you can abbreviate the range of motion to what feels best to you. Performing this exercise through a full range of motion is by no means inherently injurious to the lower back, but certain individuals with pre-existing conditions may experience discomfort in rather rounded or rather arched positions.
## Description
While also training your hip flexors, this exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of full-diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates or any bumper plates) will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. If you only use smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize 45lb (20kg) plates, you can perform a block pull or rack pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with full diameter plates.
2. While facing the center of the bar, stand in an approximately hip-width stance with the middle of your feet under the bar and your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly out to the sides.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
    2. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    3. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from hinging at your hips than bending your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have fully sat back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    4. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and help engage your back muscles.
4. With your elbows fully straightened, firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can naturally use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish to do so.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, the bar close to your legs, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the bar reaches near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
5. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. Just like when lifting the bar, keep your lower back flat and chest up as you descend while actively avoiding letting your lower back round or chest collapse.
    2. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees. Then you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the ground rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep. 
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this time-tested exercise can meaningfully train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable decline bench to your desired decline angle.
    1. An angle near 30 degrees is an appropriate starting point, but you can effectively perform this exercise with a range of decline angles.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the decline becomes steeper, so you can adjust the angle to modify the intensity.
    3. Some adjustable benches have a mild decline setting along with incline settings, but they lack a pad to secure your feet. This exercise specifically uses a decline with a foot/ankle pad.
2. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
3. Lie down in a controlled manner to position your back and head on the bench.
4. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. As you tighten your abdominal muscles, lift your back and head up off the bench by curling your back. Bring your chest toward your thighs.
    1. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that you have fully rounded your back and cannot bring your chest closer toward your thighs.
    2. Given that this is a sit-up rather than a crunch, you will lift the entirety of your back off the bench.
    3. To train your abdominal muscles dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    4. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    5. As a cue for your engaging abdominal muscles, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs toward your hip bones.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner as you lower your back to the starting position.
    1. Rather than fully relaxing at the bottom, strive to tap the bench lightly with your upper back while keeping your abdominals tensed.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the entire movement.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its highest position that allows you to begin each rep with your knees fully straightened.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the lower leg pad to a position that places it slightly above the back of your heels (i.e., on your Achilles tendons) when seated against the backrest, with your legs on top of the lower leg pad and your knees straightened.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the lower leg pad without moving the lever arm, in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. After adding weight plates to the machine, sit down on its seat with your back against the back pad and your lower legs on top of the lower leg pad.
5. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
    1. In this starting position, your knees should be straight and aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. On the side you are about to train, pull your toes and foot back toward your shin by moving at your ankle. Maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankle in the same position in which it would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heel up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankle in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
3. While keeping one knee fully straightened, use your other leg to drive your heel down and back toward you as you bend your knee to pull the lower leg pad under your thighs. Drive back through the available range of motion that the machine allows.
    1. At some point, the machine will stop you from bending your knee any further, but most machines will allow you to bend your knee by at least 90 degrees.
    2. While your lower leg moves through motion at your one knee, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
4. Reverse the movement as you allow your knee to straighten progressively in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your knee fully straightened.
    2. Continue keeping your other knee straightened.
    3. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower legs up.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Position a power rack’s safety pins near the height of the middle of your shins when standing upright.
    1. Certain power racks will not allow you to position safety pins near this level, so this variation will not be feasible in every power rack. If your rack is not suitable for this variation, you can select the “conventional mid-shin block pull” from this database to provide a similar training effect.
    2. If the closest safety pin setting of your rack situates the bar somewhat higher than mid-shin, you may be able to perform a mid-shin block pull by elevating your feet like you would for a deficit deadlift. To do so, you can place a large diameter weight plate or deadlift block in the center of your rack, and you will stand on top of it to perform your rack pull from a closer to mid-shin starting position. Depending on your preferred stance width, you can also position two plates or deadlift blocks directly next to each other if the soles of your feet do not fit fully onto one plate or deadlift block.
2. Place a barbell centered on top of the safety pins, and add your desired weight plates to it.
    1. Particularly as the load on the bar increases, rack pulls risk damaging a bar if you do not utilize a well-controlled lowering phase, which people often do not.
    2. Certain gyms have bars that were previously bent from rack pulls and are now specifically designated to be used for rack pulls to avoid damaging other bars.
    3. When using a well-controlled lowering phase during every rep, you most likely can avoid damaging the bar when performing a rack pull.
    4. If you do not feel confident in your ability to avoid damaging a bar when performing a rack pull, it is not recommended to perform rack pulls with a bar you do not own unless you receive approval from the gym owner or are using a bar designated for rack pulls.
3. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance close in front of the middle of the bar, as the bar is over the middle of your feet, with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly turned out to the sides.
    1. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
4. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward to bring your shins right next to the bar.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
5. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down, such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force that would lift the bar if it were only half as heavy.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. Immediately after the bar lifts off the pins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. The “push the floor away” cue only applies to the very start of the rep, since thrusting your hips forward is chiefly what will allow you to lift the weight during this variation.
    2. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    3. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar in a controlled manner until the bar has returned to resting on the pins.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track slightly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you absolutely should not slam the bar onto the pins in a jarring manner.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the pins for a moment (rather than simply tapping the pins and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the pins for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferring between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the pins.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Sit on the ground with your knees bent by approximately 90 degrees and feet together.
2. Moderately lean your torso back at your hips, and lift your feet slightly off the ground.
    1. Keep your feet off the ground, and your torso reclined throughout this exercise.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
3. Place your hands together in front of your chest with your elbows bent and your upper arms by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Rotate your torso to one side through your trunk’s available range of motion as you direct your chest toward that side.
    1. You can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the center of your chest as you rotate that laser beam out to the side. This should result in your elbow (on the side to which you are twisting) moving behind your torso.
    2. People often emphasize tapping the ground with their hands rather than actually rotating their torsos during this exercise. It is not inherently problematic to tap the ground with your hands, but focusing on your hands can prompt you to move your arms through shoulder motion rather than actually twisting your trunk (which is the function of your obliques).
    3. How far you can rotate will be influenced by your spinal mobility.
    4. Continue keeping your legs stationary throughout your set as you avoid letting your legs swing to the side.
2. Reverse the movement by rotating your torso back to the starting position.
3. Cyclically perform steps 1 and 2 as you alternate to which side you are rotating until you have completed your set.
    1. Strive to rotate fluidly from one side to the other rather than pausing once you have returned to the center position.
    2. Move in a controlled manner the entire time.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your obliques while statically training your rectus abdominis and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s chest pad to a setting that will situate it at chest height once you are kneeling on the knee pad.
    1. To confirm if the chest pad is positioned appropriately, you will have to be kneeling on the knee pad.
    2. A minority of machine models may lack a chest pad and simply have fixed handles for you to hold. If you are using one of those machines, disregard the instructions about the chest pad and just aim to keep your chest pointing straight forward throughout the exercise while holding the handles.
2. Rotate the kneeling pad toward one direction. Select a setting that allows you to perform this exercise through the full range of motion that is comfortable to you.
    1. If you have normal back mobility and a healthy back, you can initially rotate it fully to one side. If you cannot comfortably assume the starting position from this setting, you can rotate the pad closer toward the center (where your imaginary belt buckle and chest are both facing forward) until you can set up comfortably.
    2. As you rotate the pad further away from the centered position, you will be able to train your core muscles in more lengthened positions that may be conducive to developing them effectively.
    3. It is up to you whether you begin with the pad rotated to your right or left, since you will adjust it to the opposite side after your first set to train both sides equally.
    4. Your optimal setting will be affected by your back mobility, and finding this ideal setting may require some trial and error.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Kneel on the knee pad with an upright torso as your thighs are vertical. Squeeze your glutes to promote the proper posture.
    1. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Keeping your glutes squeezed from this point onward can help you maintain the appropriate lower back position throughout the exercise.
    2. Your knees should be vertically stacked underneath your hips.
5. Rotate your chest toward the chest pad and snugly contact the pad with your chest while firmly gripping the handles.
    1. In the starting position, it is common to feel a stretching sensation in your back when the machine is adjusted to train through your full range of motion. However, you should not feel lower back discomfort in this position, so you should adjust the degree to which the knee pad is rotated if you experience discomfort when moving into the starting position. If you cannot rotate your chest enough to reach the chest pad, you naturally should adjust the knee  pad as well until you can reach it.
## Exercise technique
1. Rotate your imaginary belt buckle in a controlled manner away from the side it initially faces. Move through the full range of motion your mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your chest snug against the chest pad and your glutes squeezed while you maintain the tall kneeling position with your knees firmly on the knee pad.
    2. Your legs should only turn because you are rotating your torso, and your legs are connected to your torso.
    3. Focusing on turning your imaginary belt buckle can help cue the rotational trunk motion that your core muscles perform. 
    4. During the first half of the movement, you will rotate your imaginary belt buckle so that it faces straight forward with your chest. Then you will continue rotating to have your imaginary belt buckle face the opposite direction from which it started.
2. Reverse the movement as you rotate your imaginary belt buckle in the opposite direction to return to the starting position.
    1. While you do not need to utilize an extremely slow tempo, you should actively control the entire movement rather than letting the machine’s resistance abruptly twist you back into the starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with the knee pad rotated toward the opposite side from which you started.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your obliques and your small rotatores muscles through a long range of motion.## Setting up
1. Approach a flat exercise bench or a similarly sturdy object with an elevated surface that can support your foot.
    1. As an alternative to a bench, you can use a plyometric box or an aerobic step stacked on risers. At home, you can use pieces of furniture such as a bed, couch, or chair that will not slide. If the object you use has a hard surface, placing a cushioned object (such as a foam pad) on top of it may make this exercise more comfortable.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the object’s height increases, so you can utilize a taller object for a greater intensity. However, a height near 18in (46cm), which applies to a typical flat exercise bench, is recommended to use as a minimum for most people.
2. Lie down on your side with the inner aspect of your top foot and ankle on top of the elevated surface, as that leg’s knee is straight. Align your leg with your torso so that you can draw a straight line from your leg through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from above.
    1. The outer aspect of your lower hip should be resting on the ground.
    2. If you are on a hard surface, you can set up on an exercise mat for comfort. You chiefly want a comfortable surface to be under the forearm that you will be placing on the ground.
3. Align your lower thigh directly under your top thigh.
    1. If the object you are using has open space underneath its top surface (like with a bench), keep your knee straight on your lower leg.
    2. If your object lacks space for your lower foot under its top surface (like with a soft plyometric box), keep your bottom knee bent.
4. Position your bottom forearm on the ground with your elbow bent by about 90 degrees. Your elbow should be close to being stacked directly underneath your shoulder.
    1. With your bottom hand, you can make a fist and keep the little finger-side of your hand in contact with the ground. Alternatively, you can keep your palm flat on the ground if you find that to feel more comfortable and stable.
    2. You can keep your top arm in a comfortable position, such as resting on top of your side with a straight elbow. Alternatively, you can reach that arm toward the ceiling with a straight elbow or place that side’s hand on your top hip with a bent elbow.
    3. Your bottom upper arm should be in a vertical position.
    4. You can perform steps 2-4 simultaneously.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your bottom hip and leg fully off the ground by driving your top leg down into the bench while pushing your bottom forearm into the ground.
    1. At the top, you should now be able to draw a straight line from your head through your belly button to the center point between your knees when viewed from the front.
    2. You should also be able to draw a straight line from your heel through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the top.
    3. Your thighs (which should be close together), torso, and head should be in essentially the same alignment as they would be when standing upright.
    4. The intent to pull your top leg down is what should lift your bottom leg, rather than simply lifting your bottom leg up by itself. Since the bench stops your top leg from moving down, this intent lifts you up.
    5. An indicator that you are appropriately driving through your top leg is when your top hip becomes approximately level with your top foot. If you lift your bottom leg by itself, your top hip will remain lower than your top foot, which you do not want.
2. Slowly reverse the movement by lowering your hips until your bottom leg lightly taps the ground. Keep your bottom thigh close to parallel to the ground throughout the exercise.
    1. At the bottom, your top hip should now be lower than your top foot, since you should be moving through your top hip.
    2. Avoid lowering your bottom leg by itself, which would result in your bottom knee being lower than your bottom hip. Your bottom knee should remain essentially level with your bottom hip throughout the set.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your opposite side elevated.
    1. When learning this exercise, it can be useful to face a mirror to receive visual feedback about your positioning.
    2. Avoid letting your buttocks drift backward during the set by bending your hips. This would prevent you from being able to draw a straight line through your whole body when viewed from the top. Squeezing your glutes can help prevent you from unintentionally bending your hips.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hip adductor muscles and a variety of core muscles, including your obliques and quadratus lumborum.## Setting up
1. If your machine has an adjustable seat, position it to a height that allows you to contact the lower back pad with the back of your hip bones and your lower back.
    1. Most machines lack an adjustable seat.
2. Initially position the roller pad far enough back that you can comfortably sit on the seat.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Sit down on the seat with the back of your hip bones snugly against the lower back pad. 
5. Place your feet on the foot bar, and adjust the foot bar to a comfortable setting if it is adjustable on your machine.
    1. If your machine permits you to adjust the foot bar or presents multiple foot bars that cannot be adjusted, the ideal foot bar position should allow your feet to feel stable while your hip bones are well-supported by the lower back pad. The optimal foot bar position should also readily enable you to perform this exercise through spinal motion while limiting hip motion.
    2. Some people may find it easier to emphasize training the back muscles through spinal motion when setting the foot bar far enough back to allow their knees to be bent by about 90 degrees or somewhat greater. However, you may still be able to perform this exercise when setting the foot bar further in front to a position that allows your knees to be mildly bent. A range of foot bar positions can be viable, so you can feel free to try out different positions over time to determine where you personally feel best if you can utilize different settings with your machine.
6. Securely fasten the seat belt around your waist if your machine has a belt.
7. Round your lower and middle back through the full range of motion that is comfortable for you. Set the roller pad so that it contacts your upper back in this rounded position or is as close to doing so as your machine permits.
    1. You may notice that setting the roller pad as far forward as you can results in the pad not contacting your back when you have rounded your back through your full comfortable range. If this occurs with your machine, you will have to start each rep from a less rounded back position in order to reach the pad.
    2. As you move the roller pad further forward, you will be able to achieve a greater degree of back rounding in the starting position, which allows you to train your back muscles in a more lengthened position that may be conducive to developing them effectively.
    3. Your mobility can affect the degree to which you can comfortably round your back and, consequently, which roller pad starting position is ideal for you.
    4. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Uncurl your back by flaring your ribs up and away from your hip bones through a comfortable range of motion. Avoid leaning your torso backward primarily through hip motion as you emphasize moving the roller pad backward through spinal motion.
    1. If you just focus on driving the roller pad backward rather than on how you move it, you most likely will be meaningfully using your hip muscles to lift the weight rather than using your back muscles in a more targeted manner. Regardless of your intent, the hip muscles (like your glutes) will inevitably be engaged to at least some degree, since a normal rhythm exists between hip and spinal motions, and the machine does not fully stabilize the hips. However, you should strive to move predominantly at your spine while minimizing hip motion to the best of your ability when you are striving to target your back muscles.
    2. When uncurling your spine, your lower back will initially move into a flat position before moving into an arched position.
    3. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable to you as an individual, you can finish the lifting phase once your lower back is close to having the mild curvature it naturally has when sitting upright, or you can finish with a greater degree of lower back arching. What chiefly matters is moving through a range that does not prompt you to feel lower back discomfort.
    4. When you are appropriately moving your spine, you should feel tension in the lower back muscles running along each side of your spine. Thinking about flaring your ribs up and away from your hip bones can help cue you to move at your spine rather than your hips.
    5. Both during this lifting phase and the subsequent lowering phase, it is recommended to move in a slow and controlled manner while prioritizing technique quality.
    6. Throughout your set, keep your buttocks on the seat, hip bones snugly against the lower back pad, and feet on the foot bar.
2. Reverse the movement by rounding your back through the full range of motion that is comfortable to you to bring the roller pad forward.
    1. Continue emphasizing spinal motion while minimizing hip motion. If you finish the movement with your lower back flat or arched as opposed to rounded, you most likely are primarily bending at your hips rather than rounding your spine.
    2. It can help to think about crunching your ribs down toward your hip bones as you move into a slouched position with your chest angled closer toward your knees.
    3. If you initially positioned the roller pad to the appropriate setting, you should return to the starting position, where you can gently tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack to confirm you have moved through your full range of motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you feel lower back discomfort at the end of either step, you can abbreviate the range of motion to what feels best to you. Performing this exercise through a full range of motion is by no means inherently injurious to the lower back, but certain individuals with pre-existing conditions may experience discomfort in rather rounded or rather arched positions.
    2. If you experience lower back discomfort specifically at the end of step 2, you can pause your set and adjust the starting position of the roller pad to be further back to limit you from training beyond your comfortable range of motion.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep spinal extensor muscles in your lower and middle back.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with straight legs, as your feet are approximately hip width apart.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can lie down on an exercise mat.
2. Place the fingertips of each hand lightly in contact with the sides of the back of your head as your upper arms and elbows are directed out away from your sides.
    1. Since you are not interlocking your fingers along the middle of the back of your head, you should not have to lift your head up off the ground to situate your hands in the appropriate position.
    2. Keeping your hands and arms in this position can help you avoid inadvertently pulling down on your head during the exercise, which would otherwise be easier to do if you interlocked your fingers and directed your upper arms up toward the ceiling.
    3. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abs to flatten your lower back and press it lightly into the ground. Keep your lower back flattened against the ground throughout all subsequent steps.
    1. In contrast to if your abs were relaxed and your lower back exhibited its natural arch, you should not be able to slide a hand under your lower back.
2. March your left leg up and back toward your chest while bending its knee.
    1. How far you can march this leg back will be affected by your hip mobility.
    2. So long as you follow the same movement pattern described here, you can choose whether you begin with your left or right leg marched, but mentioning the sides as an example can help with technique clarity.
3. With a straight knee, lift your right leg slightly off the ground or higher up for a lower intensity.
    1. As you lift this leg higher up, the difficulty of the exercise will decrease, so you can determine specifically how high you lift it based on your current ability.
    2. If you notice that you cannot keep your lower back flat with your leg slightly off the ground, you can try lifting it up to a height that allows you to avoid aching your lower back.
4. Tuck your chin slightly toward your chest to lift your head just above the ground.
5. In a crunch-like motion, mildly curl your upper back to lift your shoulder blades slightly up off the ground.
    1. While they are presented as distinct steps for instructional clarity, you can perform the actions from steps 1-5 simultaneously with each other once you understand the proper positioning that each step achieves.
6. March your right leg up and back toward your chest while bending its knee as you lower your left leg while straightening its knee. Simultaneously rotate your chest to the right to bring your left elbow toward your right knee.
    1. Strive to move your legs reciprocally at the same speed in sync with your torso rotation. This should result in you finishing lowering your left leg at the same time you have finished marching your right leg and turning your chest to the right.
    2. Move through the range of motion permitted by your spine and hip mobility. You have reached your end range once you feel that you can no longer rotate your chest to the right while marching your right leg back. This may involve achieving contact between the knee and elbow for some people, but touching your elbow to your knee is not the focus of this exercise.
    3. Your elbows should only move because you are twisting your torso, and your arms are attached to your torso through your shoulders. Keep your arms mostly relaxed to avoid moving your arms by themselves through shoulder motion.
    4. Finish lowering your left leg once it is slightly above the ground or has reached the starting height you initially opted to use for your right leg.
    5. You can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the center of your chest as you rotate that laser beam out to the right. If your chest and the laser beam do not turn toward the right, you are not appropriately rotating your torso.
    6. Avoid letting your lower back arch up as you continue keeping it flattened against the ground by tightening your abs.
7. Fluidly reverse the movement by performing the opposite motion with each leg and rotating your chest the other way.
    1. This will involve lowering your right leg, marching your left leg up, and turning your chest to the left based on the directionality of the last step.
8. Alternate between steps 6 and 7 until you have completed your set and are ready to rest your feet on the ground.
## Description
This exercise can develop your obliques and rectus abdominis while additionally training your hip flexors.## Setting up
1. After initially standing in the center of the captain’s chair with your back facing its back pad, step up onto its foot plates while placing your hands against the arm pads for any balance assistance as needed.
2. Bend your elbows and place the bottom of your forearms onto the arm pads while firmly grasping the handles with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Your back should be contacting the backrest while your torso is in an upright position.
3. While bearing the force from your bodyweight through your elbows into the arm pads, pick your feet up off the foot plates and allow them to hang straight down toward the ground.
    1. Keep your shoulder blades and shoulders near the natural position in which they are when normally standing upright, rather than letting your shoulder blades and shoulders shrug up from gravity pulling your torso down.
    2. You should be able to draw a straight line vertically down from your torso through your legs.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your legs up and toward your torso through your available range of motion while curling your hips forward and only allowing your knees to bend mildly.
    1. If you keep your knees locked in a fully straightened position throughout the movement, your hamstring flexibility can limit how high you can lift your legs.
    2. Ideally keep your knees as close to straight as you can without your hamstring flexibility restricting your range of motion, which should result in your feet clearly being in front of your knees.
    3. You can keep your knees completely straight during the initial portion of the movement and allow them to bend as needed while progressively lifting your legs, since the hamstrings progressively lengthen as the thighs lift higher up (through hip flexion).
    4. Curling your hips forward will round your lower back and result in your buttocks facing forward at the end of the lifting phase.
    5. Keep your upper body stationary.
    6. Your legs should move in an arcing manner as they initially move upward and forward before moving upward and backward after they lift beyond being parallel to the ground.
    7. How high you are able to raise your legs up will be influenced by the mobility in your hips and lower back, along with your strength.
    8. Whenever you feel like you cannot bring your legs any higher up without excessively bending your knees, you have reached the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your legs to descend while uncurling your lower back until you have returned to the starting position with your thighs directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Actively lower your legs throughout the descent rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you back down, which could result in your legs swinging back behind the starting position as momentum takes over.
    2. Your knees can progressively straighten as you descend.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet onto the foot plates and step down from the captain’s chair.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.

### Straight knee leg raise versus bent knee leg raise

With the exception of utilizing a straighter knee position, the technique for the straight knee leg raise is otherwise essentially the same as the bent knee leg raise, so both variations primarily train the same muscle groups. In terms of the training effect these exercises provide, the main difference is that the straight knee leg raise is a higher intensity variation due to how it shifts the center of mass of your legs farther forward relative to your trunk and hip. This results in a longer lever arm of resistance acting on your trunk and hip, which requires your abdominal muscles and hip flexor muscles respectively generating greater trunk flexion and hip flexion torque to overcome. Resistive torque can be increased by increasing the weight you move or by increasing the lever arm in which the weight acts on a particular joint, so the straight knee leg raise can function similar to a weighted knee raise without requiring additional weights. The greater magnitude of resistance from the straight knee leg raise almost certainly explains why McGill et al. (2014) found this variation to yield noticeably greater EMG activity of the abdominal muscles than the bent knee leg raise when participants performed three reps of each exercise.

Nonetheless, this does not mean that the straight knee leg raise is inherently superior to the bent knee leg raise for developing the abdominal muscles in the same way that squatting 200lb (91kg) is not necessarily better than squatting 150lb (68kg) for inducing quad hypertrophy. If you have the strength to perform both leg raise variations with sound technique, you simply will have to perform greater reps per set with the bent knee position compared to the straight knee position to achieve the same hypertrophic stimulus. If an equal number of sets are performed until the same proximity failure (e.g., performing as many reps as you can with each condition), a very similar degree of muscle growth may be produced by 6-rep sets, 30-rep sets, or anywhere in between. Correspondingly, the bent knee variation that provides less resistance can still be just as productive for developing the abdominals as the straight knee variation for most people when leaving the same number of reps in reserve for sets of either exercise. The one exception may be for individuals with especially strong abdominals who are able to perform a very high number of bent knee leg raise reps per set before reaching a pronounced degree of muscle fatigue, indicating that the resistance from these reps are very low relative to their strength. At some point an exercise may just be providing too low of a resistance to optimize how much muscle growth that a set can stimulate even if the set is performed until failure. Specifically where that threshold lies is currently unclear, but that should not be any concern for you unless you are able to perform over 30 reps per set, which will not be an issue for the vast majority of people performing bent knee leg raises with a controlled technique through a full range of motion.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
    1. Your upper arms should be directed straight down such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. You should now be in the same position as the top of a push-up.
2. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes to maintain a flat back position while keeping your elbows and knees straight.
    1. Doing so keeps your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting them sag downward, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. In this position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
    4. Keep your neck in a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright.
3. Statically hold the position you adopted after performing steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise statically trains your rectus abdominis and obliques as they work to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Anchor a long looped band at chest height around a power rack’s beam or similarly sturdy object with a smooth surface to which you can anchor your band.
    1. To anchor the band, you can initially fold it around the beam while holding each end, then loop one end into the middle of the other, and pull that end until it is taut.
    2. Inspect the band to confirm it has no small tears, as a small tear can quickly become a full tear when stretching a band.
    3. If you have a D-handle attachment that you can connect to the band, you can optionally use it and grasp it during step 3 instead of grasping the band directly.
2. Stand with the side of one shoulder facing the band’s anchor point, and take a small step backward so that the band is directly in front of you.
3. Firmly grasp the free end of the band with your hands interlocked and palms facing each other. Your elbows should be bent with your upper arms by your sides, and your hands should be just in front of your chest.
    1. One palm should face toward the anchor point, while the other faces straight out to the side, away from the anchor point.
4. While keeping your hands close to your chest, side-step away from the anchor point until you feel the degree of tension you wish to experience from the band.
    1. Prevent your torso from rotating when side-stepping.
    2. The two halves of the band should be flat rather than twisted.
    3. Up to a certain point, you can progressively increase the band’s resistance by stepping out farther. However, be careful not to step out so far that you overstretch the band, which an abrupt increase in band resistance can indicate.
    4. The capacity to which a band can safely stretch varies depending on the manufacturer, but, as a general rule of thumb, avoid stretching the band three times its resting length or more to minimize the risk of tearing.
    5. Naturally, you will not be measuring the length to which you elongate the band. Nonetheless, if the band does not provide sufficient resistance after taking a few steps out from the point at which you started feeling resistance, it may be appropriate to switch to a higher tension band instead of stepping out any farther.
5. Position your feet near shoulder-width apart, and slightly sit your buttocks backward to bend your hips and knees slightly.
    1. Rather than being excessively arched or rounded, your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. In this starting position, you should be able to form a 90-degree angle between the band and an imaginary laser beam shooting straight forward from the center of your chest. You should also be able to draw a straight line between your shoulders that is parallel to the band.
    3. The purpose of the slight hip and knee bend is to help you feel stable and balanced during your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping your chest facing straight ahead, press your hands forward, away from your chest.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as your arms are outstretched in front of you close to parallel to the ground.
    2. All the motion should occur from your arms moving at your shoulders and elbows, while the rest of your body remains completely stationary.
    3. Particularly avoid letting the band's resistance rotate your torso. If your core muscles do not stabilize your trunk sufficiently, the imaginary laser beam shooting out from the center of your chest will start angling closer toward the band’s anchor point. The center of your chest should be facing the same direction throughout your entire set.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by bringing your hands straight back toward your chest as you bend your elbows.
    1. You should now be back to the starting position, with your hands directly in front of your chest and your upper arms by your sides.
    2. Continue keeping the center of your chest facing straight forward, with your shoulders aligned when viewed from the side.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise, this time facing the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise will statically train your obliques as they function to resist trunk rotation.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your side with straight legs as one leg is resting on top of the other. Align your legs with your torso so that you can draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso when viewed from above.
    1. The outer aspect of your lower foot and lower hip should be resting on the ground.
    2. If you are on a hard surface, you can set up on an exercise mat for comfort. You chiefly want a comfortable surface to be under the forearm that you will be placing on the ground.
    3. This side plank will simultaneously train the hip muscles on both sides of your body along with the core and shoulder muscles on the side closer to the ground.
2. Position your bottom forearm on the ground with your elbow bent to about 90 degrees. Your elbow should be close to being stacked directly underneath your shoulder.
    1. With your bottom hand, you can make a fist and keep the little finger-side of your hand in contact with the ground. Alternatively, you can keep your palm flat on the ground if you find that to feel more comfortable and stable.
    2. You can keep your top arm in a comfortable position, such as resting on top of your side with a straight elbow. Alternatively, you can reach that arm toward the ceiling with a straight elbow or place that side’s hand on your top hip with a bent elbow.
    3. Your bottom upper arm should be in a vertical position.
    4. You can perform this step simultaneously with step 1.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your bottom hip off the ground by pushing through your bottom foot and forearm. You should now be able to draw a straight line from your head through your belly button to the center point between your feet when viewed from the front.
    1. You should also be able to draw a straight line from your heel through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the top.
    2. Your legs, torso, and head should be in the same alignment in which they would be when standing upright with your feet together.
2. Lift your top leg straight up through your hip’s available range of motion. Keep the rest of your body completely stationary in the same position you assumed from step 1.
    1. Specifically how high you are able to lift your top leg will be influenced by your hip mobility and strength.
    2. Avoid rotating your top leg when lifting it up. Your kneecap and toes should be pointing straight forward the whole time. If your kneecap and toes are pointing diagonally up and forward instead of straight forward, you have rotated your leg while lifting it up.
3. Statically hold this position in proper alignment until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your opposite side contacting the ground.
    1. Avoid letting your lower hip sag down, which would prevent you from being able to draw the line through the midline of your body when viewed from the front. If you notice your hip starting to drop down, it can help to think about pushing more forcefully through your bottom foot. When learning this exercise, it can be useful to face a mirror to receive visual feedback if your lower hip begins sagging down.
    2. Avoid letting your buttocks drift backward or letting your top leg drift forward by bending your hips. This would prevent you from being able to draw the straight line through your whole body when viewed from the top. Squeezing your glutes can help prevent you from unintentionally bending your hips.
    3. To the best of your ability, strive to limit your top leg from drifting down. However, it is natural for this leg to descend gradually as your hip muscles become fatigued, and you do not necessarily need to end your set if this starts occurring. The key is that you are able to keep your top leg high enough that it does not touch your bottom leg.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, lateral deltoid, and a variety of core muscles, including your obliques and quadratus lumborum.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your side with straight legs, as one leg is resting on top of the other. Align your legs with your torso so that you can draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso when viewed from above.
    1. The outer aspect of your lower foot and lower hip should be resting on the ground.
    2. If you are on a hard surface, you can set up on an exercise mat for comfort. You chiefly want a comfortable surface under the forearm you will place on the ground.
    3. The muscles on the side closest to the ground are predominantly trained during your side plank.
2. Position your bottom forearm on the ground with your elbow bent to about 90 degrees. Your elbow should be close to being stacked directly underneath your shoulder.
    1. With your bottom hand, you can make a fist and keep the little finger-side of your hand in contact with the ground. Alternatively, you can keep your palm flat on the ground if you find that to feel more comfortable and stable.
    2. You can keep your top arm in a comfortable position, such as resting on top of your side with a straight elbow. Alternatively, you can reach that arm toward the ceiling with a straight elbow or place that side’s hand on your top hip with a bent elbow.
    3. Your bottom upper arm should be in a vertical position.
    4. You can perform this step simultaneously with step 1.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your bottom hip off the ground by pushing through your bottom foot and forearm. You should now be able to draw a straight line from your head through your belly button to the center point between your feet when viewed from the front.
    1. You should also be able to draw a straight line from your heel through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the top.
    2. Your legs, torso, and head should be in the same alignment as they would be when standing upright with your feet together.
2. Statically hold this position in proper alignment until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your opposite side contacting the ground.
    1. Avoid letting your lower hip sag, which would prevent you from drawing a line through the midline of your body when viewed from the front. If you notice your hip starting to drop down, it can help to think about pushing more forcefully through your bottom foot. When learning this exercise, it can be useful to face a mirror to receive visual feedback if your lower hip begins sagging down.
    2. Avoid letting your buttocks drift backward by bending your hips. This would prevent you from drawing a straight line through your whole body when viewed from above. Squeezing your glutes can help prevent you from unintentionally bending your hips.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, lateral deltoid, and a variety of core muscles, including your obliques and quadratus lumborum.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to approximately chest height, connect a D-handle attachment to it, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. As an alternative to the D-handle attachment, you can also use a rope attachment, but this page will describe the usage of the D-handle.
2. Stand with the side of one shoulder facing the cable station, as the handle is just in front of your chest.
3. Firmly grasp the handle with your hands interlocked and palms facing each other. Your elbows should be bent with your upper arms by your sides, and the handle in front of your chest.
    1. One palm should be facing in toward the cable, as the other palm faces straight out to the side, away from the cable.
4. While keeping your chest pointing straight ahead and the handle close to it, take a few steps to the side away from the cable station against its resistance.
    1. Walk far enough outward that the cable can still provide resistance at the point during the exercise when your torso is twisted toward the cable station. If you lose resistance at any point during this exercise, you can take another step away.
    2. Do not let the cable’s resistance twist your chest when stepping outward.
5. Position your feet near shoulder width apart, and slightly sit your buttocks backward to bend your hips and knees slightly.
    1. Rather than being excessively arched or rounded, your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. In this starting position, two imaginary laser beams shooting out from the center of your chest and where you would wear a belt buckle should be pointing straight forward, parallel to each other. These two laser beams should form approximately 90-degree angles with the cable.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the handle straight forward away from your chest. Keep your elbows straight throughout the later steps.
    1. Your arms should now be outstretched in front of you and close to parallel to the ground.
2. Rotate your chest toward the cable station to move the handle toward the station in a controlled manner. Keep your imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead and the handle directly in front of your chest throughout the set.
    1. Rotate your chest in a controlled manner through the full range of motion that your spinal mobility comfortably permits. Because spinal mobility varies among people, the end range will vary among different individuals.
    2. When appropriately rotating your trunk, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your back at your end range.
    3. As you move, a progressively greater angle should form between the mobile imaginary laser beam exiting your chest and the stationary imaginary laser beam shooting out from your belt buckle.
    4. If your imaginary belt buckle turns with your chest, you are rotating at your hips rather than rotating your trunk in an isolated manner.
    5. If your hands no longer remain directly in front of your chest’s imaginary laser beam, you are moving your arms through shoulder motion.
3. Rotate your chest fully away from the cable station to move the handle away from the station in an arcing manner.
    1. Continue keeping your imaginary belt buckle facing straight ahead and the handle directly aligned with the center of your chest.
    2. When rotating your trunk (rather than just your hips), you should feel the muscles on the sides of your torso contract.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise while facing the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your obliques and your small rotatores muscles through a long range of motion.## Setting up
1. Pick up one kettlebell and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while firmly holding the kettlebell at your side with your elbow straight, as your palm faces in toward your thigh.
    1. Initially pick up the kettlebell with a hip hinge or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow, hips, and knees extended, slowly lower the kettlebell toward the side of your knee in a controlled manner as you bend your torso toward the side of the kettlebell.
    1. Move through your comfortable full range of motion, which will normally result in you experiencing a stretching sensation in the muscles on the opposite side of your torso (including your lower back).
    2. Your mobility will affect how far you can side bend, as some people will reach their end range when the kettlebell is at the level of the lower thigh, while others may be able to reach knee level or even a little lower.
    3. The motion should exclusively involve your trunk bending to the side, so you should not be bending your lower back forward or arching it backward during this exercise.
    4. The tops of your hip bones should remain level throughout this movement, like they do when you are normally standing upright.
2. Reverse the motion by bringing the kettlebell back up your side as you bend your torso toward the opposite side in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your torso upright and your shoulders approximately level with each other.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your obliques and quadratus lumborum, while also training your erector spinae and intertransversarii lumborum.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your side with one leg resting on top of the other and your knees bent to about 90 degrees. Align your thighs with your torso so that, when viewed from above, you can draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    1. The outer aspect of your lower foot, knee, and hip should be resting on the ground.
    2. If you are on a hard surface, you can set up on an exercise mat for comfort. You chiefly want a comfortable surface to be under the knee and forearm that you will be placing on the ground.
    3. The muscles on the side closer to the ground are what will be predominantly trained by your side plank.
2. Position your bottom forearm on the ground with your elbow bent to about 90 degrees. Your elbow should be close to being stacked directly underneath your shoulder.
    1. With your bottom hand, you can make a fist and keep the little finger-side of your hand in contact with the ground. Alternatively, you can keep your palm flat on the ground if you find that to feel more comfortable and stable.
    2. You can keep your top arm in a comfortable position, such as resting on top of your side with a straight elbow. Alternatively, you can reach that arm toward the ceiling with a straight elbow or place that side’s hand on your top hip with a bent elbow.
    3. Your bottom upper arm should be in a vertical position.
    4. You can perform this step simultaneously with step 1.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your bottom hip off the ground by pushing through your bottom knee and forearm. You should now be able to draw a straight line from your head through your belly button to the center point between your knees when viewed from the front.
    1. You should also be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the top.
    2. Your thighs, torso, and head should be in the same alignment as they would be when standing upright with your feet together.
2. Statically hold this position in proper alignment until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your opposite side contacting the ground.
    1. Avoid letting your lower hip sag, which would prevent you from drawing a line through the midline of your body when viewed from the front. If you notice your hip starting to drop down, it can help to think about pushing more forcefully through your bottom knee. When learning this exercise, it can be useful to face a mirror to receive visual feedback if your lower hip begins sagging down.
    2. Avoid letting your buttocks drift backward by bending your hips. This would prevent you from being able to draw a straight line through your whole body when viewed from the top. Squeezing your glutes can help prevent you from unintentionally bending your hips.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, lateral deltoid, and a variety of core muscles, including your obliques and quadratus lumborum.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent to about 90 degrees.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can lie down on an exercise mat.
2. With straight elbows, raise both arms straight up toward the ceiling as your elbows and wrists are vertically stacked above your shoulders.
    1. You can point your palms in toward each other.
3. While keeping your knees bent to about 90 degrees, march both legs up until your knees are vertically stacked above your hips.
    1. In this position, your thighs should be perpendicular to the ground, and your shins should be parallel to the ground.
4. Based on your preference, you can keep your head resting on the ground, or you can slightly tuck your chin toward your chest to lift your head just barely off the ground.
    1. Lifting your head slightly off the ground can mildly increase the difficulty and allow you to train your neck muscles statically, but doing so is optional. If you opt to lift your head just off the ground, keep in that position throughout your set. Otherwise, continue resting your head on the ground during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. In this starting position, your lower back should be near a neutral position with the natural mild curvature you have when normally standing upright rather than being excessively arched or flattened.
2. Sweep your right arm backward and downward while simultaneously straightening your left leg and lowering it toward the ground. Rigidly maintain the natural curvature of your lower back throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As you lower your arm and leg, you will have to continue bracing your core to prevent your lower back from arching to a greater degree. Unintended arching would be demonstrated by an increase in the space between the ground and your lower back, so you can focus on maintaining this space each rep.
    2. Strive to move your arm and leg at the same rate through the full range of motion that you can without letting your lower back dynamically arch.
    3. With sufficient core control, you can finish with your arm and leg parallel to the ground as they are fully straightened. From this position, you can gently tap the ground with your hand and heel simultaneously. If your lower back starts arching to a greater degree before this point, shorten the range of motion to what you can perform without letting your lower back arch further.
    4. All the movement should occur from your right shoulder, left hip, and left knee as your spine remains motionless.
    5. Move in a slow and controlled manner throughout your set as you focus on high quality technique through every degree of motion.
    6. Keep your left arm and right leg completely stationary.
    7. So as long as you move an arm and leg on opposite sides of your body, you can choose which sides in which you will move to begin your set, but mentioning the sides as an example can help with technique clarity.
3. Reverse the movement by lifting your right arm and left leg up to return to the starting position.
    1. Since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the lowering phase, no motion should occur at your lower back as you continue bracing your core.
    2. While keeping your elbow straight, bend your left knee as you lift that thigh up to finish with your knee bent by approximately 90 degrees.
    3. At the top, both of your knees should be stacked vertically above your hips, and your arms should be vertically stacked above your shoulders.
4. Proceed to perform steps 2 and 3 with your left arm and right leg with the same technique you just utilized for your right arm and left leg.
5. Alternate between steps 2 through 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your rectus abdominis and obliques to maintain a rigid back position as you refine your ability to move through your hips and shoulders with a stable trunk.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to be near head height, connect a rope attachment to it, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. As an alternative to the rope attachment, you can also use a D-handle attachment, but this page will describe the use of the rope attachment.
2. Pull the rope fully to one side of the attachment.
3. Stand with the cable station at your side, and firmly grasp the rope with your palms about shoulder-width apart while facing down.
4. While keeping your chest pointing straight ahead and the rope close to it, take a couple of steps to the side away from the cable station against its resistance.
    1. Allow the rope to align diagonally with the cable, which will result in your hand that is closer to the cable being higher than your hand on the other side.
    2. Step far enough outward that the cable can still provide resistance at the point during the exercise when your torso is twisted toward the cable station. If you lose resistance at any point during this exercise, you can set up farther away.
5. Lunge backward with your foot closer to the station to assume a half-kneeling position with your knee on the side closer to the station on the ground. Simultaneously straighten your elbows so that the rope stays in close to the same position while you lunge your torso back away from it.
    1. You can optionally rest your lower knee on a comfortable surface (e.g., exercise mat, foam cushion, folded towel) in this position.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched or rounded, your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Rotate your chest diagonally up and toward the cable station to move the rope diagonally up toward the station in a controlled manner. Keep your chest facing the rope and your elbows straightened throughout the set.
    1. Rotate your chest in a controlled manner through the full range of motion that your spinal mobility comfortably permits. Because spinal mobility varies among people, the end range will vary among different individuals.
    2. During this exercise, you can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the middle of your chest, and you want to keep that laser beam pointing toward the middle of the rope. Turn that laser beam diagonally up toward the cable station during this step.
    3. If the middle of the rope no longer remains directly in front of your chest’s imaginary laser beam, you are moving your arms through shoulder motion rather than exclusively moving your trunk.
    4. Keep your imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead with your legs planted on the ground throughout this exercise.
2. Rotate your chest diagonally down and away from the cable station to move the rope diagonally down and away from the station.
    1. Continue keeping your imaginary belt buckle facing straight ahead and the rope directly aligned with the center of your chest.
    2. Avoid focusing on pulling with your arms to emphasize training your core. Your arms function to link the cable to your torso, and you are chiefly turning your chest to move the cable.
    3. When rotating your trunk appropriately (rather than only moving through your shoulders and/or hips), you should feel the muscles on the sides of your torso contract, particularly on the side further away from the cable station.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise while facing the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your obliques while also training your rectus abdominis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground, as your elbows are straight and your knees are bent.
    1. Your upper arms should be directed straight down under your shoulders such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    2. You can spread your fingers apart for stability. Depending upon which hand position feels most comfortable, you can point your middle fingers straight ahead or mildly out to the sides.
## Exercise technique
1. While bearing weight through your arms, straighten one knee to move your foot behind you and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. You should now look like you are at the top of a push-up.
2. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes to flatten your lower back.
    1. From this tall plank position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. Contracting your abs and glutes appropriately will prevent your hips from sagging down, which would prompt your lower back to arch rather than remain flat.
    3. During the entire set, do not allow your chest to sag down, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together. If you notice your chest starting to sag, push down into the ground more forcefully through your straight arms to spread your shoulder blades apart.
    4. Keep your neck near a neutral position as you would normally do when standing upright.
3. March one knee forward and up toward your chest while keeping the opposite leg on the ground and maintaining the same stable trunk position you assumed during step 2.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back flat while the only movement occurs from your marching leg as the rest of your body remains stationary.
    2. Particularly avoid letting your hip bones sag down, hike up, or tilt to either side.
    3. How far you can march your knee forward will be influenced by your hip mobility.
4. Reverse the movement by driving your knee (that you just marched) backward and straightening it until you are once again in a tall plank position with both feet on the ground.
    1. Move your leg in a controlled manner as you emphasize keeping your torso and hip bones stable.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 while switching which leg you march up each time until you have completed your set.
    1. During this variation, you are moving only one leg at a time while focusing on maintaining a strict plank position with your trunk.
    2. Wait to begin marching forward your opposite side’s knee until your other side’s foot has returned to the ground.
## Description
While also dynamically training your hip flexors, this exercise can statically train your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Pick up an ab wheel and kneel on a comfortable surface (e.g., exercise mat, foam cushion, pillow, folded towel) with your knees approximately hip-width apart.
    1. A comfortable surface is optional but definitely preferred by most people.
2. Place the ab wheel on the ground directly in front of you, then firmly grip the handles with your palms facing back toward you (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. Keep your wrists straight throughout this exercise.
3. With your hips stacked vertically above your knees, position the ab wheel directly under your shoulders as your elbows are straight.
    1. Your thighs and arms should now be directed straight down, such that they are perpendicular to the ground, which places your torso closer to a horizontal position than a vertical position.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Scoop your hip bones toward your ribs, while keeping your ribs pulled down toward your hip bones and tightening your abs. Maintain this position throughout your set.
    1. When starting with your lower back in its natural mild arch, this motion (known as posterior pelvic tilting) will flatten your lower back, since it is the opposite of arching it.
    2. Your abs attach to your pubic bone (which you can feel as a bony ridge in the middle of your pubic region) and your ribs, and this scooping motion should tilt your pubic bone up toward your ribs as you contract your abs.
    3. It can help to imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the midpoint between your hip bones, slightly below where you would wear a belt buckle. Before you scoop your hips, this laser beam will point down toward your abdomen. Scooping your hips appropriately should angle this laser beam forward toward the ab wheel.
2. Slowly push the ab wheel forward by simultaneously moving your hips and arms forward in unison. Keep your torso rigidly braced as you avoid letting your lower back arch throughout the movement.
    1. Only move the ab wheel as far forward as you can while maintaining a flat lower back position.
    2. Keeping your lower back flat will become progressively more difficult as you roll out further, so you can determine your range of motion based on your current ability.
    3. If you are new to this exercise, a rather short range may be appropriate. If you have the strength to do so with proper form, you can finish rolling out when your chest is almost touching the ground.
    4. Keep your elbows fully straightened as you move your hips and shoulders together.
    5. Avoid letting your hips sag down or your ribs flare up, as this can arch your lower back. If you can roll out until you are reasonably close to being able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your lower back, squeezing your glutes at this point can help you prevent your hips from sagging.
3. Reverse the movement by pulling the ab wheel back toward the starting position. Drive your arms down and back while allowing your hips to rock back in a controlled manner.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back in the same flat position you maintained while rolling out.
    2. In contrast to the exaggerated hip motion people often perform by rocking their back behind their knees, your hips should finish vertically above your knees with your thighs perpendicular to the ground.
    3. To avoid rocking your hips back excessively, focus on sweeping your arms back while letting your hips rock back naturally, rather than pulling through your hips.
    4. Your arms should finish directly under your shoulders, pointing straight down toward the ground.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your lats and teres major while statically training your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Hold a weight plate to your chest with both hands, and carry it toward a flat exercise bench.
    1. For comfort, you can optionally wear a beanie on your head or hold a small towel against the inner aspect of the plate. Resting the plate directly on your head without a soft fabric over your head can be uncomfortable, so using a beanie or towel is recommended.
    2. If you are new to this exercise, it is recommended to use a light plate, such as a 2.5-5lb (or 1.25-2.5kg) plate.
2. Lie down with your back and buttocks on the bench. Rest your shoulder blades on the head of the bench with your head and neck fully off the bench.
    1. Place your legs and feet in a position that feels comfortable and stable to you. This may involve positioning your feet flat on the ground or the bench with your knees bent.
3. While securely holding the plate with both hands, position the center of the weight plate against the middle of your forehead.
    1. Throughout the set, you will use your hands to help keep the plate in contact with your forehead.
## Exercise technique
1. Slowly tilt your head down and back to bring your chin away from your chest. Move through your neck’s comfortable range of motion.
    1. How far you can comfortably tilt your head back will be affected by your neck mobility, so the end position will vary from person to person.
    2. At the bottom position, your face should be directed primarily backward rather than upward, like in the starting position.
    3. Do not exceed the range of motion that is comfortable for your neck.
    4. This is the same neck motion that you would perform when standing up and looking up toward the ceiling.
    5. Continue holding the plate against your forehead.
2. Reverse the movement by slowly nodding your head up. Bring your chin toward your collarbone without using your arms to lift the weight.
    1. Aim to lift the weight exclusively with your neck muscles as your arms simply hold the plate against your head.
    2. People can commonly perform this movement until the chin is very close to touching the collarbone, but your neck mobility will also influence the degree to which you can nod.
    3. When performing this movement correctly, you should feel the muscles on the front of your neck contract.
    4. Prioritize actively controlling the weight throughout the entirety of your set. This is an intentionally slow exercise.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After completing your last rep, use your arms to lift the plate off your forehead and bring it to your chest before sitting up.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train several muscles on the front of your neck, the largest and strongest of which is your sternocleidomastoid.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while firmly holding the dumbbell at your side with your elbow straight, as your palm faces in toward your thigh.
    1. Initially pick up the dumbbell with a hip hinge or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow, hips, and knees extended, slowly lower the dumbbell toward the side of your knee in a controlled manner as you bend your torso toward the side of the dumbbell.
    1. Move through your comfortable full range of motion, which will normally result in you experiencing a stretching sensation in the muscles on the opposite side of your torso (including your lower back).
    2. Your mobility will affect how far you can side bend, as some people will reach their end range when the dumbbell is at the level of the lower thigh, while others may be able to reach knee level or even a little lower.
    3. The motion should exclusively involve your trunk bending to the side, so you should not be bending your lower back forward or arching it backward during this exercise.
    4. The tops of your hip bones should remain level throughout this movement, like they do when you are normally standing upright.
2. Reverse the motion by bringing the dumbbell back up your side as you bend your torso toward the opposite side in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your torso upright and your shoulders approximately level with each other.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your obliques and quadratus lumborum, while also training your erector spinae and intertransversarii lumborum.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbell at your side with a straight elbow as your palm faces in toward your thigh.
    1. Initially pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    2. Throughout the exercise, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer, such as resting by your side or held out to the side for balance assistance.
    3. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
2. Lift the dumbbell up near the front of your shoulder with your elbow bent by curling the weight and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbell into position if it is too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbell mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbow to curl the weight upward while swinging your elbow forward. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbell right by your shoulder with your forearm in a vertical position, and your wrist stacked over your elbow as the handle of the dumbbell is parallel to your upper arm.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, pull your shoulder blades back together to adopt a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back along with your neck, like when you are normally standing upright.
4. Now your palm should be facing inward (with a neutral grip) as your upper arm is in front of your body with the back of your elbow pointing downward and forward (as opposed to being flared out away from your side).
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. Prior to walking with the weight, you will press it up through a push press or strict overhead press without leg drive. The directions describe how to perform a push press, since it will efficiently allow you to assume the overhead position with a heavy weight, but you can alternatively press the weight up without leg drive if you have the strength to do so.
2. Without moving your arm, mildly bend your knees in a quarter squat-like manner to dip down while maintaining an upright torso position with your chest up.
    1. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground during this dip phase.
    2. Your knees should track straight forward toward your toes rather than caving inward.
3. Directly after reaching the bottom of your dip, forcibly push through your feet as you rapidly extend (straighten) your knees and hips to generate upward momentum.
    1. The intent of this leg drive phase should be akin to a jump without lifting your feet fully off the ground.
    2. At the end of this drive, you can either keep the soles of your feet fully on the ground, or you can allow your heels to lift up slightly off the ground while keeping the front of your feet firmly in contact with the ground if a slight heel lift feels natural to you.
4. Immediately after you have finished extending your legs, explosively push the dumbbell upward by forcibly driving your elbow upward as you swiftly straighten your elbow.
    1. At the end of the pressing phase, your knees, hips, and elbow should be fully straightened, and your arm should be directed straight up toward the ceiling with your elbow and dumbbell situated vertically over your shoulder.
    2. To transfer your leg drive into pushing the dumbbell upward in an explosive manner, you should perform steps 3 and 4 as a continuous movement.
    3. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, your forearm should be in a vertical position throughout the rep as your wrist and dumbbell remain stacked over your elbow.
    4. Throughout the exercise, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back.
    5. Regardless of whether you lifted your heels up off the ground after extending your legs during the leg drive phase, your heels should be flat on the ground with the rest of your feet at the end of the pressing phase.
5. While keeping the dumbbell overhead with your elbow straightened and your chest up, walk forward in a controlled manner while maintaining an upright posture with your shoulders approximately level with each other.
    1. You will not swing your arm like you would when normally walking, and you will keep your trunk in a rigidly braced position, but your lower body should look similar to how it would when you walk normally.
    2. Based on your preference and what feels natural to you, you can either swing your free arm back and forth as you walk or keep it by your side.
    3. Walk at a pace where you feel stable and balanced throughout the carry.
    4. If you use a weight that is fairly heavy to you, it is natural to take shorter strides compared to how you typically walk.
    5. You should feel your core muscles on the side opposite to the dumbbell working to maintain an upright torso, as these muscles prevent the weight from bending your torso to the side of the dumbbell.
    6. Avoid leaning your torso away from the dumbbell, and keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level throughout your set rather than letting them drop down to one side when taking each step.
6. Continue walking with the dumbbell overhead until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the carry while holding the dumbbell with your other side.
7. Lower the dumbbell in a controlled manner as your elbow progressively bends while simultaneously moving downward.
8. As you approach the end of the lowering phase, shallowly bend your knees and hips as you did during step 2 (the dip phase) to help control the weight until you have returned to the starting position with the dumbbell in front of your shoulders.
    1. Lower the dumbbell as far as you can while keeping your wrist in a straight position stacked over your elbow as your forearm remains vertical, which should result in your elbow being lower than your shoulder.
    2. Avoid descending beyond the point at which you can keep your forearm vertical.
    3. If you are using a heavy, bulky dumbbell, the dumbbell contacting your shoulder may also determine your end range of the lowering phase.
9. After you have fully lowered the weight, extend your knees and hips to stand upright before initiating your next rep.
10. After your dumbbell is by your shoulder, lower the dumbbell to your side as you straighten your elbow and swing your elbow back by your side.
11. Set the dumbbell down and pick it up with your other side before repeating the exercise with that side.
## Description
While also engaging your thoracic erectors, this exercise can statically train much of your shoulder girdle musculature, including your front delts, lateral delts, serratus anterior, traps, and rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. Ideally use a bar that is high enough to allow you to hang from it with straight legs without your feet touching the ground.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground and lifting your heels up, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. If you use an object as a step to reach the bar, position that object a little behind the bar so that your feet do not touch that object when you are hanging from the bar with straight legs.
    5. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    6. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide upward during the dead hang, which pulls on your hand skin in a manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet off the ground to begin hanging on the bar. Allow your legs to hang down toward the ground with straight knees.
    1. While hanging, you should be able to draw a straight line vertically down from your torso through your legs.
    2. You will maintain a firm grip on the bar with straightened elbows throughout your set.
    3. If your bar is not high enough to keep your feet off the ground when your legs are directed straight down toward the ground (which may often be the case with doorway pull-up bars), bend your knees by the minimum needed to lift your feet just off the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your legs up and toward your torso through your available range of motion while curling your hips forward and only allowing your knees to bend mildly.
    1. If you keep your knees locked in a fully straightened position throughout the movement, your hamstring flexibility can limit how high you can lift your legs.
    2. Ideally keep your knees as close to straight as you can without your hamstring flexibility restricting your range of motion, which should result in your feet clearly being in front of your knees.
    3. You can keep your knees completely straight during the initial portion of the movement and allow them to bend as needed while progressively lifting your legs, since the hamstrings progressively lengthen as the thighs lift higher up (through hip flexion).
    4. Curling your hips forward will round your lower back and result in your buttocks facing forward at the end of the lifting phase.
    5. Keep your chest facing straight ahead rather than leaning your torso backward.
    6. Your legs should move in an arcing manner as they initially move upward and forward before moving upward and backward after they lift beyond being parallel to the ground.
    7. How high you are able to raise your legs up will be influenced by the mobility in your hips and lower back, along with your strength.
    8. Whenever you feel like you cannot bring your legs any higher up without excessively bending your knees, you have reached the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your legs to descend while uncurling your lower back until you have returned to the starting position with your thighs directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Actively lower your legs throughout the descent rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you back down, which could result in your legs swinging back behind the starting position as momentum takes over.
    2. Your knees can progressively straighten as you descend.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet to the ground.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.

### **Straight knee leg raise versus bent knee leg raise**

With the exception of utilizing a straighter knee position, the technique for the straight knee leg raise is otherwise essentially the same as the bent knee leg raise, so both variations primarily train the same muscle groups. In terms of the training effect these exercises provide, the main difference is that the straight knee leg raise is a higher intensity variation due to how it shifts the center of mass of your legs farther forward relative to your trunk and hip. This results in a longer lever arm of resistance acting on your trunk and hip, which requires your abdominal muscles and hip flexor muscles respectively generating greater trunk flexion and hip flexion torque to overcome. Resistive torque can be increased by increasing the weight you move or by increasing the lever arm in which the weight acts on a particular joint, so the straight knee leg raise can function similar to a weighted knee raise without requiring additional weights. The greater magnitude of resistance from the straight knee leg raise almost certainly explains why McGill et al. (2014) found this variation to yield noticeably greater EMG activity of the abdominal muscles than the bent knee leg raise when participants performed three reps of each exercise.

Nonetheless, this does not mean that the straight knee leg raise is inherently superior to the bent knee leg raise for developing the abdominal muscles in the same way that squatting 200lb (91kg) is not necessarily better than squatting 150lb (68kg) for inducing quad hypertrophy. If you have the strength to perform both leg raise variations with sound technique, you simply will have to perform greater reps per set with the bent knee position compared to the straight knee position to achieve the same hypertrophic stimulus. If an equal number of sets are performed until the same proximity failure (e.g., performing as many reps as you can with each condition), a very similar degree of muscle growth may be produced by 6-rep sets, 30-rep sets, or anywhere in between. Correspondingly, the bent knee variation that provides less resistance can still be just as productive for developing the abdominals as the straight knee variation for most people when leaving the same number of reps in reserve for sets of either exercise. The one exception may be for individuals with especially strong abdominals who are able to perform a very high number of bent knee leg raise reps per set before reaching a pronounced degree of muscle fatigue, indicating that the resistance from these reps are very low relative to their strength. At some point an exercise may just be providing too low of a resistance to optimize how much muscle growth that a set can stimulate even if the set is performed until failure. Specifically where that threshold lies is currently unclear, but that should not be any concern for you unless you are able to perform over 30 reps per set, which will not be an issue for the vast majority of people performing bent knee leg raises with a controlled technique through a full range of motion.

### Hanging ab straps

If you have access to hanging ab straps, you have the option of using them for this exercise by hanging them from your pull-up bar about shoulder width apart from each other and resting your upper arms on top of the straps as you otherwise use the same form described under the exercise technique section. Using straps would ensure that your grip strength would not be able to function as the limiting factor during your set without otherwise affecting how your abdominal and hip flexor muscles are trained. Correspondingly, utilizing straps would remove the stimulus this exercise can provide to your forearm and hand muscles involved with gripping. Performing the exercise with straps would also result in the shoulders maintaining a moderately flexed position (as the upper arms remain parallel to the ground) in contrast to the fully flexed shoulder position that is adopted when hanging on the bar. This can result in strap usage removing the stimulus to improve or maintain shoulder mobility in an overhead position that can be experienced when hanging on the bar. On the other hand, the shoulder extensor muscles (e.g., lats, teres major, rear delt) will have to act isometrically (statically) to maintain the moderately flexed shoulder position when using straps. While these muscles are likely more engaged when performing the exercise with straps compared to without straps, they are still unlikely to be loaded enough to provide any meaningful training effect for most lifters.

### Is your grip strength limiting you?

As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance rather than abdominal muscle strength, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual. If you find that you can complete meaningfully more reps when using straps, your grip strength is likely limiting you during this exercise, which can consequently limit the stimulus it provides to your abdominal muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a kettlebell or dumbbell and hold it right next to your chest with bent elbows, as your upper arms are slightly in front of your sides (i.e., in the goblet position).
    1. You can use either a kettlebell or a dumbbell, but some people find holding a kettlebell in the goblet position to feel more stable and comfortable, especially with a fairly heavy weight.
    2. Based on your preference and the load you use, you can transfer your kettlebell or dumbbell into the goblet position through a couple of different approaches.
    3. An effective approach is initially to pick up the weight by the handle through a deadlift motion and set it on an elevated surface (like a bench or plyometric box) with the handle facing up if you use a kettlebell or the handle directed vertically if using a dumbbell (as only one end of the dumbbell is on the surface). Then squat down until the weight is right in front of your chest, firmly grasp it in the goblet position, and squat up with the weight. If you use a kettlebell, grasp it by its horns (i.e., side handles). If you use a dumbbell, cup your hands around the top end of the weight with the base of your palms by the handle, and your fingers wrapped around the rest of that end of the weight.
    4. With a lighter kettlebell, you can tilt it onto its side with the weight base directed toward you, squat down to grasp its horns, stand upright with it, and curl it into the goblet position.
    5. With a lighter dumbbell, you can also pick it up with one hand and curl it up across your body to be in front of your chest. Then cup your free hand around the top end of the dumbbell with the base of its palm by the handle and fingers wrapped around the top end of the weight before repositioning your other hand to cup the other side of the weight in the same manner.
## Exercise technique
1. Walk forward, holding the weight close to your chest in the goblet position, maintaining an upright posture with your shoulders approximately level.
    1. Walk at a pace where you feel stable and balanced throughout the carry.
    2. If you use a weight that is fairly heavy to you, it is natural to take shorter strides compared to how you typically walk.
    3. While you will not swing your arms like you would when walking normally, your torso and lower body should look similar to how they would when you walk normally.
    4. Focus on keeping your chest up, which will help engage your upper back muscles to prevent the weight from excessively rounding your upper back into a slouched posture.
    5. Maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like you have when normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back, which would lean your torso backward. 
    6. Keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level throughout your set rather than letting them drop down to one side when taking each step.
2. Continue walking with the weight in the goblet position using the technique described in step 1 until you have completed your set or have to turn around and walk in the opposite direction.
    1. After finishing the set, set the weight down in the same manner in which you initially picked it up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains the erector spinae and deep spinal extensor muscles in your upper and middle back, which help keep your chest up.## Setting up
1. Position the footplates far enough apart that you can fully spread your legs apart when you are seated with your feet on the footplates.
    1. If your machine’s knee pads rotate (which is the case for combo machines that can be adjusted to train hip adduction or abduction), position the pads so that they are facing away from each other.
    2. Determining specifically where you should position the footplates can take some trial and error if you are not accustomed to this exercise. The ideal position will allow you to feel resistance from the machine when your legs are spread as far apart as your flexibility comfortably permits. Avoid placing the footplates any further apart than that point to help you more easily transition into and out of the starting position.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust the backrest angle, position it fully upright.
    1. Most machines have backrests fixed in an upright position.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Sit down on the machine's seat with your torso upright against the backrest.
5. Spread your legs apart to place your feet on the footplates with the inner aspect of your knees and/or lower thighs contacting the knee pads.
    1. It may be easiest initially to position one leg and then use your arms to help pull the knee pads inward before positioning your other leg. The stretching force from the machine typically allows you to spread your legs further apart than you actively could by yourself, so it can be difficult to achieve the fully spread position for this exercise without using your arms to assist in setting up.
    2. If you have rather short legs, you may have to scoot slightly forward on the seat for your knees to reach the pads, which will require you to lean your torso somewhat backward to keep your upper back against the backrest.
6. If you feel that you can spread your legs further apart than is permitted by the initial machine setting you selected, you can adjust the pads to be further away from each other until you feel that your inner thigh muscles are sufficiently stretched.
    1. It is recommended to remove at least one leg from the footplate when adjusting the machine.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your knees in toward each other until your legs are situated directly in front of you.
    1. You can tap the inner aspect of the knee pads against each other at the end of this motion.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your knees to move out and away from each other in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your flexibility comfortably permits.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your legs spread fully apart.
    2. With the appropriate machine setting, you should feel a stretching sensation in your inner thighs.
    3. While you do not have to perform this phase of the movement with an extremely slow tempo, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach your end range is recommended.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Like when setting up, you can use your hands to apply an inward pressure to the knee pads to assist with lifting your legs up one at a time.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hip adductor muscles in an isolated manner.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your abdomen contacting the ground (i.e., in the prone position) and your head lifted slightly off the ground as you maintain a mildly tucked chin position with your face directed toward the ground.
2. Position your arms straight out in front of you with straight elbows such that your hands are approximately shoulder-width apart while resting on the ground.
    1. Your body position should resemble how you would look if you were reaching up with your arms fully overhead while standing.
3. Rotate your arms so that your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If you make a thumbs-up gesture in this position, your thumb should be pointing up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your chest, arms, and feet as high off the ground as you comfortably can by arching your lower back in a controlled manner and squeezing your glutes while keeping your knees and elbows straight.
    1. How far you are able to lift up will be influenced by your back mobility, and you can aim to lift up through the full range of motion that your mobility comfortably permits, but it is normal for the range of motion to be fairly small overall.
    2. You should feel the muscles running up and down your back on each side of the spine contract, especially in your lower back.
    3. Throughout the set, continue keeping your chin mildly tucked without moving your neck as your abdomen and the front of your hips remain in contact with the ground.
2. After you have fully lifted up, statically hold this position for a brief period of time, such as about two seconds.
3. Reverse the movement by gradually lowering your chest, arms, and feet back onto the ground.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically and statically train the erector spinae and deep intrinsic spinal extensor muscles that run along the length of your entire back.## Setting up
1. Pick up one kettlebell and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the kettlebell at your side with your elbow straight as your palm faces in toward your thigh.
    1. Initially pick up the kettlebell with a hip hinge or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back flat, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
    2. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handle with the handle pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the handle closer toward the middle of your palm (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handle can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Walk forward while holding the kettlebell at your side while maintaining an upright posture with your shoulders approximately level with each other.
    1. While you will not swing your arm like you would when walking normally, your torso and lower body should look similar to how they would when you walk normally.
    2. Based on your preference and what feels natural to you, you can either swing your free arm back and forth as you walk or keep it by your side.
    3. Walk at a pace where you feel stable and balanced throughout the carry.
    4. If you use a weight that is fairly heavy to you, it is natural to take shorter strides compared to how you typically walk.
    5. You should feel your core muscles on the side opposite to the kettlebell working to maintain an upright torso, as these muscles prevent the weight from bending your torso to the side of the kettlebell.
    6. Avoid leaning your torso away from the kettlebell, which can be a common compensation to rest the kettlebell partially on the outside of your thigh.
    7. Keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level throughout your set rather than letting them drop down to one side when taking each step.
2. Continue walking with the kettlebell until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the suitcase carry while holding the kettlebell with your other side.
## Description
This exercise statically trains your obliques, quadratus lumborum, and erector spinae as they work to keep your trunk upright. 

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your core muscle strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your core muscles. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
1. With a cable station positioned to its lowest height, select your weight with the loading pin, and connect a D-handle attachment to the cable.
2. While standing with the side of one shoulder facing the cable station, squat down to grasp the handle firmly with the hand closer to the cable station as you keep your elbow straight and your palm facing in toward your side (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. When squatting down, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
3. By extending your hips and knees, stand upright with the handle while continuing to keep your elbow straight and your lower back near a neutral position.
    1. When upright, you should feel resistance from the cable.
4. Take a step to the side away from the cable station and allow the cable’s tension to raise your arm somewhat out away from your side, such that your arm is directed in a straight line with the cable.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow, hips, and knees extended, slowly lower the handle toward the base of the cable station in a controlled manner as you bend your torso toward the side of the cable station.
    1. Move through your comfortable full range of motion, which will normally result in you experiencing a stretching sensation in the muscles on the opposite side of your torso (including your lower back).
    2. Continue to keep your arm directed in a straight line with the cable, which should naturally occur and result in the handle moving in a diagonal path involving motion directed both downward and out away from your side.
    3. Your mobility will affect how far you can side bend.
    4. Avoid bending your lower back forward or arching it backward during this exercise.
    5. The tops of your hip bones should remain level throughout this movement, like they do when you are normally standing upright.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your torso away from the cable station in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your torso upright and your shoulders approximately level with each other.
    1. Doing so will pull the handle diagonally out and away from the cable station.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your obliques and quadratus lumborum, while also training your erector spinae and intertransversarii lumborum.## Setting up
1. Place a weight plate on the ground directly in front of the head of a flat exercise bench.
    1. For comfort, you can optionally wear a beanie on your head or place a small towel on top of the plate. Resting the plate directly on your head without a soft fabric over your head can be uncomfortable, so using a beanie or towel is recommended.
    2. If you are new to this exercise, it is recommended to learn the proper technique while using a light weight, such as a 2.5-5lb (or 1.25-2.5kg) plate. For lifters opting to use heavier weights, it is worth noting that a large diameter plate (e.g., a bumper plate or a 45lb (20kg) plate) may limit the range of motion for this exercise’s lifting phase by contacting the back of your neck.
2. Lie down with your abdomen on the bench. Rest your chest on the head of the bench with your head and neck fully off the bench.
    1. Place your legs and feet in a position that feels comfortable and stable to you. This may involve placing your legs close together on the bench with your feet hanging off the bench, which may be practical for people with relatively narrow statures. Alternatively, you can spread your legs mildly apart to straddle the bench with your toes on the ground, which may be more suitable for individuals with wider frames.
3. Firmly grasp the weight plate (with the towel on top of it if you are using a towel) with both hands.
4. Nod your head by bringing your chin toward your collarbone.
    1. This will be the starting position for your head during each rep.
5. Position the center of the weight plate against the back of your head while securely holding the plate with both hands.
    1. The plate should not be touching your upper neck, since it should be directly on the back of your skull.
    2. During the exercise, you will use your hands to help keep the plate in contact with your head.
## Exercise technique
1. Slowly tilt your head up to bring your chin away from your chest without using your arms to lift the weight. Move through your neck’s comfortable range of motion.
    1. How far you can comfortably lift your head will be affected by your neck mobility, so the end position will vary from person to person.
    2. At the top position, your face should be angled forward.
    3. Do not exceed the range of motion that is comfortable to your neck.
    4. This is the same neck motion that you would perform when standing up and looking up toward the ceiling.
    5. Aim to lift the weight exclusively with your neck muscles as you use your arms simply to hold the plate against your head throughout your set.
    6. When performing this movement correctly, you should feel the muscles on the back of your neck contract.
    7. Continue keeping your chest on the bench.
2. Reverse the movement by slowly nodding your head down. Bring your chin toward your collarbone to return to the starting position.
    1. People can commonly perform this movement until the chin is very close to touching the collarbone, but your neck mobility will also influence the degree to which you can nod down.
    2. Prioritize actively controlling the weight throughout the entirety of your set. This is an intentionally slow exercise.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After completing your last rep, use your arms to lift the plate off your head and set it on the ground before sitting up.

### **Description**

This exercise can effectively develop the musculature on the back of your neck, including the splenius muscles, semispinalis muscles, and cervical portions of the erector spinae and multifidus.## Setting up
1. Pick up an ab wheel and kneel on a comfortable surface (e.g., exercise mat, foam cushion, pillow, folded towel) with your knees approximately hip-width apart.
    1. A comfortable surface is optional but definitely preferred by most people.
2. Place the ab wheel on the ground directly in front of you, then firmly grip the handles with your palms facing back toward you (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. Keep your wrists straight throughout this exercise.
3. With your hips stacked vertically above your knees, position the roller directly under your shoulders, keeping your elbows straight.
    1. Your thighs and arms should now be directed straight down, such that they are perpendicular to the ground, which places your torso closer to a horizontal position than a vertical position.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Scoop your hip bones forward toward your ribs, while keeping your ribs pulled down toward your hip bones and tightening your abs. Maintain this position throughout your set.
    1. When starting with your lower back exhibiting its natural mild arch, this motion (known as posterior pelvic tilting) will flatten your lower back, since it is the opposite of arching your lower back.
    2. Your abs attach to your pubic bone (which you can feel as a bony ridge in the middle of your pubic region) and your ribs, and this scooping motion should tilt your pubic bone up toward your ribs as you contract your abs.
    3. It can help to imagine a laser beam shooting out to the midpoint between your hip bones, slightly below where you would wear a belt buckle. Before you scoop your hips, this laser beam will point down toward your abdomen. Scooping your hips appropriately should angle this laser beam forward toward the ab wheel.
2. Lower your hips forward until you can draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your lower back when viewed from the side. Keep the ab wheel under your shoulders and your lower back flat.
    1. This lower-body motion will push the ab wheel moderately forward, but you are not moving your shoulders during this step. Your arms move forward simply because they are connected to the rest of your body.
    2. Your thighs, torso, and arms should now resemble a tall plank position.
    3. Avoid lowering your hips so far forward that your lower back begins arching. Your lower back position should not change from the last step.
3. Squeeze your glutes, and keep them squeezed for the rest of your set.
    1. Actively squeezing your glutes can help you avoid arching your lower back.
    2. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them.
4. Slowly push the ab wheel forward by sweeping your arms forward. Keep your torso rigidly braced as you avoid letting your lower back arch throughout the set.
    1. Only move the ab wheel as far forward as you can while maintaining a flat lower back position.
    2. Keeping your lower back flat will become progressively more difficult as you roll out further, so you can determine your range of motion based on your current ability.
    3. If you are new to this exercise, a rather short range may be appropriate. If you have the strength to do so with proper form, you can finish rolling out when your chest is almost touching the ground.
    4. Keep your elbows fully straightened.
    5. While you push the ab wheel forward through shoulder motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    6. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag or your ribs flare, as this can arch your lower back. Continuing to squeeze your glutes and tighten your abs will help you keep your lower back flat.
5. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by pulling the ab wheel back toward the starting position. Drive your arms back until they are once again under your shoulders without moving your hips.
    1. In contrast to the traditional rollout technique, you will not bend your hips or move them back during each rep with this variation.
    2. Your arms should now be pointing straight down toward the ground.
    3. Continue keeping your lower back in the same flat position you maintained while rolling out.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set and are ready to move your hips back before standing up.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your lats and teres major while statically training your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your legs straight and your feet approximately hip-width apart.
    1. On the ground, your legs and torso should be in the same position you exhibit when normally standing upright.
    2. While you can leave your arms resting on the ground by your sides, you can feel free to situate your hands wherever you prefer.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your knees straight, lift both feet slightly (about 6 inches (or 15 centimeters)) off the ground, tightening your abdominal muscles to press your lower back into the ground.
    1. Pressing your lower back into the ground will flatten it out from abdominal muscle contraction and prevent you from sliding a hand under it.
    2. If you do not contract your abdominals, lifting both legs up would prompt your lower back to develop an exaggerated arch that would allow you to slide a hand under your lower back.
2. As you hold the position described in step 1, tuck your chin toward your chest to lift your head off the ground and mildly curl your upper back (in a crunch-like motion) to lift your shoulder blades slightly off the ground.
    1. Doing so should prompt you to feel your upper abdominal muscles further tightening up.
3. Statically hold the position you reached after performing steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Along with training your hip flexor muscles on the front of your thigh, this exercise can statically train your abdominal muscles, including your obliques and rectus abdominis, which is the “six-pack muscle.”

### How the abdominal and hip flexor muscles work together

As with many other abdominal exercises, the six-inch leg raise trains the abdominal muscles concurrently with the hip flexor muscles due to the opposing forces these muscles can generate at the lumbopelvic region (i.e., the pelvis and lower back). For the sake of having clearer directions when referring to “up” or “down” during this explanation, we can imagine that you are standing upright rather than lying down, as you would when performing this exercise. To lift the thigh via hip flexion, the hip flexor muscles contract, creating a pulling force from the front of the pelvis toward the thigh. Muscle contraction generates a force at both ends to which a muscle attaches, and the hip flexor muscles either originate on the front of the pelvis or cross the front of the pelvis as they course toward their insertion on the femur (thigh bone) or tibia (shin bone). If left unopposed, the force from the hip flexor muscles will rotate the front of the pelvis down toward the thigh as the back of the pelvis rotates up toward the lower back via a motion known as anterior (forward) pelvic tilting. When the pelvis tilts in this manner, the lumbar spine extends correspondingly as the natural arch of the lower back becomes exaggerated into a sway-back posture.

Contraction of the abdominal muscles is able to produce the opposite motions, given that they arise from the ribs and attach to the top surface of the front aspect of the pelvis. To pull the ribs closer to the top of the pelvis, the lumbar spine flexes, initially flattening the natural curvature of the lower back and then rounding it with further flexion. Contraction of the abdominal muscles can also pull the top surface of the front aspect of the pelvis up toward the ribs as the back of the pelvis rotates down toward the thigh via a motion known as posterior (backward) pelvic tilting. Due to the posterior pelvic tilt function of the abdominals and the anterior pelvic tilt function of the hip flexors, these two muscle groups can operate in tandem. Whenever the legs lift off the ground via hip flexion, the abdominal muscles stabilize the lumbopelvic region by preventing the lower back from arching beyond its natural curvature, through a combination of anterior pelvic tilt and lumbar spine extension that would otherwise occur with abdominal muscle contraction.

### Six-inch leg raise progression

Performing the six-inch leg raise with the head and upper back lifted up off the ground serves as a more advanced variation of the six-inch leg raise with the head on the ground. When the upper back is off the ground, the abdominals have to work to prevent the thoracic spine from extending, while also functioning to prevent the lumbar spine from extending, which increases the intensity of the exercise.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, connect a rope attachment to it, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. As an alternative to the rope attachment, you can also use a D-handle attachment, but this page will describe the use of the rope attachment.
2. Pull the rope fully to one side of the attachment.
3. Squat down with the cable station at your side and firmly grasp the rope with an overhand shoulder-width  grip.
4. Stand upright while allowing the rope to align diagonally with the cable, which will result in your hand that is closer to the cable being lower than your hand on the other side.
5. While keeping your chest pointing straight ahead and the rope close to your body, take a couple of steps to the side away from the cable station against its resistance.
    1. Step far enough outward that the cable can still provide resistance at the point during the exercise when your torso is twisted toward the cable station. If you lose resistance at any point during this exercise, you can set up farther away.
6. Lunge backward with your foot closer to the station to assume a half-kneeling position with your knee on the side closer to the station on the ground. Simultaneously straighten your elbows so that the rope stays in close to the same position while you lunge your torso back away from it.
    1. You can optionally rest your lower knee on a comfortable surface (e.g., exercise mat, foam cushion, folded towel) in this position.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched or rounded, your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Rotate your chest diagonally down and toward the cable station to move the rope diagonally down toward the station in a controlled manner. Keep your chest facing the rope and your elbows straightened throughout the set.
    1. Rotate your chest in a controlled manner through the full range of motion that your spinal mobility comfortably permits. Because spinal mobility varies among people, the end range will vary among different individuals.
    2. During this exercise, you can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the middle of your chest, and you want to keep that laser beam pointing toward the middle of the rope. Turn that laser beam diagonally down toward the cable station during this step.
    3. If the middle of the rope no longer remains directly in front of your chest’s imaginary laser beam, you are moving your arms through shoulder motion rather than exclusively moving your trunk.
    4. Keep your imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead with your legs planted on the ground throughout this exercise.
2. Rotate your chest diagonally up and away from the cable station to move the rope diagonally up and away from the station.
    1. Continue keeping your imaginary belt buckle facing straight ahead and the rope directly aligned with the center of your chest.
    2. Avoid focusing on pulling with your arms to emphasize training your core. Your arms function to link the cable to your torso, and you are chiefly turning your chest to move the cable.
    3. When rotating your trunk appropriately (rather than only moving through your shoulders and/or hips), you should feel the muscles on the sides of your torso contract.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise while facing the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your obliques and rotatores muscles, which are assisted by your erector spinae and other deep spinal extensor muscles.## Setting up
1. Position yourself on your hands, knees, and toes, with your elbows straight and your knees bent.
2. Prop your forearms onto the ground with your upper arms directed straight down such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    1. Based on your preference, you can either make a fist with each hand and direct your palms in toward each other or place your palms flat on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. You should be bearing your bodyweight into the ground through your elbows and feet.
2. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes to maintain a flat back position while keeping your knees straight and elbows bent.
    1. Doing so keeps your hips up (but not excessively hiked) rather than letting them sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch in your lower back.
    2. In this position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, as this can cause your shoulder blades to pinch together.
    4. Keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
3. Press through your feet to rock your body back forward as you move at your ankles like you would during the lifting phase of a calf raise (also known as a heel raise).
    1. Doing so will prompt your elbows to bend further.
    2. Continue keeping your abdominal muscles and glutes tightened to maintain the same rigid trunk position you adopted in step 2.
4. Reverse the motion you just performed by rocking your body backward at your ankles as you continue keeping your abdominal muscles and glutes tightened.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your core muscles, including your rectus abdominis (AKA  the “six-pack muscle”) and your abdominal obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Add a pair of round weight plates to a barbell situated on the ground with sufficient room in front of the bar for you to roll out.
    1. If you roll out through the full range of motion, using small-diameter weight plates can make this exercise slightly more difficult than using large-diameter plates.
    2. If you stop rolling out before your arms are fully outstretched, the plate diameter is inconsequential.
    3. Your bodyweight will primarily provide the resistance for this exercise, so the weight of the plates you select is not particularly impactful.
    4. Hexagonal weight plates cannot be used for this exercise.
2. Optionally place a comfortable surface (e.g., an exercise mat, foam cushion, pillow, or folded towel) slightly behind the middle of the bar.
3. Kneel behind the bar so that your body is completely centered with the bar and your knees are approximately hip width apart.
4. With your elbows and wrists straightened, firmly grasp the bar with your hands at least slightly outside shoulder width.
    1. Use an overhand grip with your palms facing back toward you.
    2. So long as it is at least slightly outside shoulder width, you decide specifically how wide a grip you will use.
    3. Keep your wrists and elbows straight throughout this exercise.
5. With your hips stacked vertically above your knees, position the bar directly under your shoulders.
    1. Your thighs and arms should now be directed straight down, such that they are perpendicular to the ground, which places your torso closer to a horizontal position than a vertical position.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Scoop your hip bones forward toward your ribs, while keeping your ribs pulled down toward your hip bones and tightening your abs. Maintain this position throughout your set.
    1. When starting with your lower back exhibiting its natural mild arch, this motion (known as posterior pelvic tilting) will flatten your lower back, since it is the opposite of arching your lower back.
    2. Your abs attach to your pubic bone (which you can feel as a bony ridge in the middle of your pubic region) and your ribs, and this scooping motion should tilt your pubic bone up toward your ribs as you contract your abs.
    3. It can help to imagine a laser beam is shooting out from the midpoint between your hip bones, slightly below where you would wear a belt buckle. Before you scoop your hips, this laser beam will point down toward your abdomen. Scooping your hips appropriately should angle this laser beam forward toward the bar.
2. Slowly push the bar forward by simultaneously moving your hips and arms forward in unison. Keep your torso rigidly braced as you avoid letting your lower back arch throughout the set.
    1. Only move the bar as far forward as you can while maintaining a flat lower back position.
    2. Keeping your lower back flat will become progressively more difficult as you roll out further, so you can determine your range of motion based on your current ability.
    3. If you are new to this exercise, a rather short range may be appropriate. If you have the strength to do so with proper form, you can finish rolling out when your arms are fully outstretched in line with your torso, and your chest is close to the ground.
    4. Keep your elbows fully straightened as you move your hips and shoulders together.
    5. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag down or your ribs flare up, which will arch your lower back. If you can roll out until you are reasonably close to drawing a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your lower back, squeezing your glutes at this point can help prevent your hips from sagging.
3. Reverse the movement by pulling the bar back toward the starting position. Drive your arms down and back while allowing your hips to rock back in a controlled manner.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back in the same flat position you maintained while rolling out.
    2. In contrast to the exaggerated hip motion people often perform by rocking their back behind their knees, your hips should finish vertically above your knees with your thighs perpendicular to the ground.
    3. To avoid rocking your hips back excessively, focus on sweeping your arms back while letting your hips rock back naturally, rather than pulling through your hips.
    4. Your arms should finish directly under your shoulders as they point straight down toward the ground.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your lats and teres major while statically training your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
If your sled has vertical posts, you have the option of grasping the posts directly as handles, or you can connect a pair of straps with handles to the sled. Without vertical posts, you will naturally have to connect straps to the sled to drag it. For clarity, the setup for each method is described separately, but the exercise technique is the same. If you have the ability to utilize either setup, using the straps is recommended.

### **Setting up with straps**

1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a sled situated in an area where you have sufficient space for pulling it backward.
    1. With certain wheeled sleds, you may also be able to adjust your resistance level with a lever that changes its friction. For these sleds, increasing the friction will increase the intensity of the exercise, similar to adding weight plates.
    2. Note that the type of surface on which you will move the sled will affect how much resistance you experience during this exercise. When tracking progress, it is consequently best to compare sets performed on the same surface.
2. Attach to the base of the sled a pair of straps with handles like TRX suspension trainer straps.
    1. Since a range of strap lengths can be used for this exercise, you can adjust the straps to a length that feels comfortable to use and is practical with the space that is available to you.
3. While standing centered behind the sled, firmly grasp the handles.
4. Walk backward until the straps have become taut with your arms aligned with the straps, elbows straight, and palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Your arms should now be angled diagonally toward the base of the sled.
5. Position your feet approximately hip-width apart with your toes pointing straight ahead.
6. Adopt a “proud chest” posture with your shoulder blades pulled mildly backward.
7. While keeping your torso upright, bend your knees into a near half squat position or your preferred degree of knee bend.
    1. For stimulating increases in quad muscle size and developing strength through a longer range of motion, it is appropriate to reach at least a moderate degree (i.e., about 60 degrees) of knee bend during this exercise. However, you have the option of utilizing a milder degree of knee bend if you wish to do so.

### **Setting up without straps**

1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a sled situated in an area where you have sufficient space for pulling it backward.
    1. With certain wheeled sleds, you may also be able to adjust your resistance level with a lever that changes its friction. For these sleds, increasing the friction will increase the intensity of the exercise, similar to adding weight plates.
    2. Note that the type of surface on which you will move the sled will affect how much resistance you experience during this exercise. When tracking progress, it is consequently best to compare sets performed on the same surface.
2. Stand centered behind the sled with your feet approximately hip width apart and your toes pointing straight ahead.
3. With your palms facing in toward each other and elbows mildly bent, firmly grasp the posts near waist height.
4. While keeping your torso mostly upright, bend your knees until your arms are approximately parallel to the ground. Simultaneously allow your elbows to straighten and take a step backward if needed to have room to straighten them.
    1. This places most people reasonably near a half squat position. If you wish to perform your sled drag with a deeper or shallower angle of peak knee bend, you have the option of gripping the posts lower or higher, respectively, while bending your knees to the point that places your arms in a horizontal position.
5. Adopt a “proud chest” posture with your shoulder blades pulled mildly backward.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Begin dragging the sled backward by straightening one leg’s knee while simultaneously bending your other leg’s knee to step backward with its foot.
    1. While using your legs to move the sled, the rest of your body should remain in essentially a static position during your set.
    2. Keep your back flat and chest up to maintain an upright torso position throughout the drag.
    3. Rather than using your upper body muscles to pull the sled, your arms should simply function to transmit force from your legs to the sled, so you should keep your elbows straight during this exercise.
3. At the moment you finish fully straightening your one knee, plant your other foot onto the ground.
    1. While straightening your knee, you should feel tension in that side’s quad.
    2. With the proper technique, you should first and foremost be using your quads to drag the sled.
4. Continue fluidly dragging the sled backward as you reciprocally straighten one leg’s knee while stepping backward with your other leg’s foot until you have reached your target distance or the end of your available space and have to switch to dragging it the opposite direction.
    1. Throughout the set, be aware of any individuals who may potentially enter the path in which you are pulling to avoid an unintended collision.
    2. If your sled has an attachment point or a pair of vertical posts on each side of the sled, you can connect the straps to the other side or grasp the posts on the other side before dragging the sled in the opposite direction. Otherwise, you naturally will have to rotate your sled 180 degrees before dragging it back in the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your quads while being easier from which to recover compared to most quad exercises.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a sled situated in an area where you have sufficient space for pushing it.
    1. With certain wheeled sleds, you may also be able to adjust your resistance level with a lever that changes its friction. For these sleds, increasing the friction will increase the intensity of the exercise, similar to adding weight plates.
    2. Note that the type of surface on which you will move the sled will affect how much resistance you experience during this exercise. When tracking progress, it is consequently best to compare sets performed on the same surface.
2. Stand centered directly behind the sled in a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead.
3. With mostly straight wrists, firmly grasp the handles in a position that is comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Position the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) directly behind the handles so that you can push primarily through the bases of your palms.
    2. Sleds commonly have vertical posts that function as handles, but some models have horizontal handles.
    3. With vertical handles, you can direct your palms in toward each other and adjust how high up you grasp them. Initially gripping the handles a little below hip height is an appropriate starting point. However, a range of grip heights can be viable, so feel free to try out different hand positions over time to determine what feels best to you.
    4. With horizontal handles, you can situate your hands about shoulder width apart with your palms facing down.
4. Take a step backward with one foot and lean your torso forward while moderately bending your front leg’s knee and keeping your rear leg’s knee mildly bent. Keep your elbows straight, chest up, and back flat.
    1. Rather than simply rounding your back, the forward torso lean should arise from you hinging at your front leg’s hip.
    2. The degree to which you should lean your torso will be affected by the height of your hands when holding the handles. As a general rule of thumb, it is often appropriate to lean your torso forward by about 45 degrees when grasping handles a little below hip height, but you should feel free to try out different degrees of forward lean to determine what personally feels best to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position as you push through your legs.
2. Begin pushing the sled forward by pushing through your rear leg. Drive your rear knee backward while straightening it and pushing off through your foot in a calf raise-like ankle motion.
    1. It can help to think about pushing the ground away from you.
    2. Strive to push through your rear leg until you can draw a straight line from that leg through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side.
    3. While the toes and the ball of your foot remain on the ground, the rest of your rear foot’s sole should be off the ground and facing backward.
    4. Since you are starting with your rear knee only mildly bent, this initial step involves a short range of motion to start moving the sled
3. As you finish driving through your rear leg, push through your front leg to continue pushing the sled forward. Simultaneously, march what initially was your rear leg forward as you drive that knee forward while bending it.
    1. Keep your back flat and chest up the whole time while you maintain the same degree of forward torso lean.
    2. All the motion should come from your hips, knees, and ankles while the rest of your body remains in essentially a static position throughout your set.
    3. Rather than using your upper body to push the sled, your arms should simply function to transmit force from your legs to the sled.
4. Continue pushing the sled forward as you reciprocally move your legs until you have reached your target distance or the end of your available space, and have to switch to pushing it in the opposite direction.
    1. With some sled models, you may have to rotate your sled 180 degrees to be able to access the same type of handles before pushing the sled in the opposite direction. With other models, you may be able to walk to the other side of the sled and access another pair of handles.
## Description
This exercise will train your gluteus maximus, quads, adductor magnus, gastrocnemius, and soleus as they function to extend your hips, knees, and ankles simultaneously.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the dumbbell at your side with your elbow straight as your palm faces in toward your thigh.
    1. Initially pick up the dumbbell with a hip hinge or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back flat, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
    2. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handle with the handle pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the handle closer toward the middle of your palm (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handle can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Walk forward while holding the dumbbell at your side while maintaining an upright posture with your shoulders approximately level with each other.
    1. While you will not swing your arm like you would when walking normally, your torso and lower body should look similar to how they would when you walk normally.
    2. Based on your preference and what feels natural to you, you can either swing your free arm back and forth as you walk or keep it by your side.
    3. Walk at a pace where you feel stable and balanced throughout the carry.
    4. If you use a weight that is fairly heavy to you, it is natural to take shorter strides compared to how you typically walk.
    5. You should feel your core muscles on the side opposite to the dumbbell working to maintain an upright torso, as these muscles prevent the weight from bending your torso to the side of the dumbbell.
    6. Avoid leaning your torso away from the dumbbell, which can be a common compensation to rest the dumbbell partially on the outside of your thigh.
    7. Keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level throughout your set rather than letting them drop down to one side when taking each step.
2. Continue walking with the dumbbell until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the suitcase carry while holding the dumbbell with your other side.
## Description
This exercise statically trains your obliques, quadratus lumborum, and erector spinae as they work to keep your trunk upright. 

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your core muscle strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your core muscles. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
1. Place a stability ball (also known as a Swiss ball or physio ball) on the ground.
    1. Ideally, select a ball with a diameter exceeding your shoulder width.
2. Kneel with the ball in front of you as your elbows are bent to approximately 90 degrees.
3. Hinge at your hips to lean your torso forward, and position your bent elbows on top of the ball about shoulder width apart.
    1. Lean forward enough that you can stack your shoulders directly above your elbows.
    2. With a sufficiently large ball, your forearms can rest on the ball with your elbows, but you will chiefly be bearing weight through your elbows.
    3. The appropriate elbow placement should ensure that the ball does not roll when you press down through your elbows.
    4. Once on the ball, you can optionally clench each hand into a fist to promote full-body tension.
## Exercise technique
1. Walk your legs back until your knees are fully straightened with the bottom of your toes dug into the ground.
    1. Position your feet approximately hip-width apart with your soles facing backward.
    2. You should be bearing your bodyweight into the ground through your feet and into the ball through your elbows.
2. Tighten your abs and squeeze your glutes to maintain a rigid back position while pushing your elbows into the ball and keeping your knees straight.
    1. Doing so keeps your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting them sag downward, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. In this position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, as this can cause your shoulder blades to pinch together. Thinking about pushing your elbows down into the ball can help keep your shoulder blades apart.
    4. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like you would normally do when standing upright.
3. Smoothly move both elbows in a small circular motion on the ball in a controlled manner. Otherwise, maintain the same plank position you adopted from step 2.
    1. Only your arms should be moving through shoulder motion while you keep the rest of your body rigidly stationary.
    2. If someone were to watch your body from your middle back through your feet, you should ideally look like you are performing a static stability ball plank.
    3. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the circumference of the circular motion increases, so you can determine the range of this movement based on your current ability. Only move through a range that allows you to execute all the technical aspects from step 2. If your hips or chest start sagging down, you may be moving through a range that presently exceeds what you can currently stabilize effectively. Moving in a very small circle is recommended if you are new to this exercise.
    4. You decide whether you start with a clockwise or counterclockwise motion, since you will be training both directions.
4. Cyclically repeat step 3 for your desired number of reps, then start stirring your elbows in the opposite direction.
    1. Specifically, how many revolutions you complete in a particular direction before switching is up to your preference. For instance, if you are striving to perform 20 total reps, you may perform five clockwise reps before performing five counterclockwise reps and then repeat this for another round. Alternatively, you may perform 10 clockwise reps followed by 10 counterclockwise reps. If you wish to do so, you can even switch directions every rep.
5. Alternate between bouts of stirring clockwise and counterclockwise until you have completed your set and are ready to bend your knees to return to kneeling on the ground.
    1. Having a small difference in the number of reps you perform clockwise and counterclockwise is inconsequential. However, you can aim to finish your set with close to equal reps performed in each direction.
    2. Strive to have your torso and lower body look the same during the last second of your set as they did during the first second after getting into position
## Description
This exercise can statically train your core muscles, including your rectus abdominis (AKA the “six-pack muscle”) and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Using an underhand grip, grasp an elastic band with both hands in front of your torso while standing upright with your feet in approximately a shoulder-width stance.
    1. Inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
2. Position your hands so that they are somewhat greater than shoulder width apart from each other, with slack taken out of the band.
3. With straight elbows, elevate your arms in front of your torso until they are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. In this position, your palms should be directed down toward the ground.
    2. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your hands apart and backward until your arms and torso form a “T-shape.”
    1. If you set up by grasping the band with your hands too close together, you may reach the limit of the band’s extensibility (as indicated by an abrupt increase in resistance from the band) prior to your hands being pulled entirely apart from each other. If you notice this, bring your hands back together and adjust your setup by gripping the band with your hands farther apart. Continuing to pull the band apart once a portion has fully stretched may cause the band to tear.
2. Reverse the motion by bringing your hands forward and toward each other until they have returned to their starting position.
    1. You should feel tension from the band throughout the entire range of motion.
    2. Do not bring your hands so close together that the band becomes slack.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that will situate the chest pad to chest height once you are seated.
    1. You will have to sit down to check if you selected the appropriate seat height, so you may have to adjust the seat height multiple times before finding the right setting for you.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit on the seat with an upright torso as your knees are snugly contacting the knee pads.
    1. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. If your machine has foot plates, situate your feet on top of them, but if your machine lacks foot plates, place your feet on the ground.
4. Rotate the chest pad toward one direction. Select a setting that allows you to perform this exercise through the full range of motion that is comfortable to you.
    1. If you have normal back mobility and a healthy back, you can initially rotate it fully to one side. If you cannot comfortably assume the starting position from this setting, you can rotate the pad closer toward the center (where your imaginary belt buckle and chest are both facing forward) until you can set up comfortably.
    2. As you rotate the pad further away from the centered position, you will be able to train your core muscles in more lengthened positions that may be conducive to developing them effectively.
    3. It is up to you whether you begin with the pad rotated to your right or left, since you will adjust it to the opposite side after your first set to train both sides equally.
    4. Your optimal setting will be affected by your back mobility, and finding this ideal setting may require trying out different settings.
5. Rotate your chest toward the chest pad and snugly contact the pad with your chest while firmly gripping the handles.
    1. In the starting position, it is common to feel a stretching sensation in your back when the machine is adjusted to train through your full range of motion. However, you should not feel lower back discomfort in this position, so you should adjust the degree to which the seat is rotated if you experience discomfort when moving into the starting position. If you cannot rotate your chest enough to reach the chest pad, you naturally should adjust the seat as well until you can reach it.
## Exercise technique
1. In a controlled manner, rotate your chest away from the side that it is initially facing. Move through the full range of motion your mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your chest snugly against the chest pad, buttocks on the seat, knees against the knee pads, and feet stationary.
    2. Continue holding the handles for stability, but do not actively try to push or pull the machine with your arms. The function of your arms is merely to help transmit force from your core muscles into the machine.
    3. As a cue, you can think about turning an imaginary laser beam that is shooting out from the middle of your chest without moving your imaginary belt buckle.
    4. During the first half of the movement, you will rotate your chest so that it faces straight forward with your imaginary belt buckle. Then you will continue rotating to have your chest face the opposite direction from which it started.
2. Reverse the movement as you rotate your chest in the opposite direction to return to the starting position.
    1. While you do not need to utilize an extremely slow tempo, you should actively control the entire movement rather than letting the machine’s resistance abruptly twist you back into the starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with the chest pad rotated toward the opposite side from which you started.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your obliques and your small rotatores muscles through a long range of motion.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your hands, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight and your knees are bent.
2. Prop your forearms onto the ground with your upper arms directed straight down such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    1. Based on your preference, you can either make a fist with each hand and direct your palms in toward each other or place your palms flat on the ground.
3. One at a time, move your arms forward so that your upper arms are now directed forward and downward at an angle while your forearms are in contact with the ground.
    1. Positioning your elbows so that they are under your eyes (while still being shoulder width apart) is an appropriate starting point.
    2. However, it is up to you specifically how far forward you wish to position your arms so long as you are still able to keep your forearms and elbows on the ground.
    3. The difficulty of this exercise will progressively increase as you shift your arms farther forward, so you could determine your specific arm position based on the intensity you intend this exercise to have.
## Exercise technique
1. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. You should be bearing your bodyweight into the ground through your elbows and feet.
2. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes to maintain a flat back position while keeping your knees straight and elbows bent.
    1. Doing so keeps your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting them sag downward, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. In this position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag down, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
    4. Keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
3. Statically hold the position you adopted after performing steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your core muscles, including your rectus abdominis (AKA  the “six-pack muscle”) and your abdominal obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with straight legs as your feet are together.
2. Raise your arms straight back behind your head with straight elbows so that your arms are aligned with your legs and torso.
    1. Except for being rotated by 90 degrees, your body should be in the same position when you are standing upright and reaching your arms straight up overhead.
    2. Your palms should be facing up away from the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While only allowing your knees to bend mildly, lift both legs up simultaneously with your torso as you crunch up to touch your feet with your hands.
    1. Sweep your arms toward your feet in an arcing manner until you can touch them with your arms reaching forward and upward. Avoid rapidly swinging your arms in an effort to build momentum.
    2. To touch your feet, sweep your arms forward until they are angled upward and forward.
    3. Keep your elbows straight and your knees as close to straight as your hamstring flexibility permits.
    4. Lift the entirety of your back off the ground, resulting in only your buttocks contacting the ground at the top of the movement.
    5. Rather than trying to keep your back flat, you should intentionally curl your spine to train your abs dynamically.
    6. Keep your neck near a neutral position throughout the exercise, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your legs and torso at the same time.
    1. At the bottom, lightly tap your heels to the ground, and keep your abs tensed to avoid excessively arching your lower back.
    2. Fluidly lower your legs and torso at the same rate, resulting in your upper back contacting the ground at the same time as your heels.
    3. Finish with your whole body forming a straight line like the starting position.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, make sure that you actively control the entire movement rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis (the “six-pack muscle”) while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back and head on the ground as your legs are straight and your feet are together.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can lie down on an exercise mat.
2. Place your arms straight out to your sides with your palms on the ground and elbows straight.
    1. When viewed from above, your arms should now form a “T-shape” with your torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Press your hands into the ground, and tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the ground. Continue keeping your hands pressed into the ground and your abs tensed throughout the set.
    1. Pressing your hands into the ground will enhance your stability during the exercise.
    2. Pressing your lower back into the ground will flatten it out from abdominal muscle contraction and prevent you from being able to slide a hand under your lower back.
2. While keeping your knees mostly straight, lift your legs up off the ground until your thighs are approximately vertical.
    1. Strive to keep your knees as close to straight as your hamstring flexibility comfortably permits.
    2. If you do not keep your abdominals contracted, lifting both legs up would prompt your lower back to develop an exaggerated arch that would allow you to slide a hand under your lower back.
3. While keeping your hips bent and knees as straight as your hamstring flexibility enables, rotate your legs toward one side.
    1. Rotate through the full range of motion your mobility comfortably permits while keeping your upper back on the ground. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your back.
    2. While some lifters will naturally be able to rotate their legs closer to the ground than others, you should be able to rotate your legs at least 45 degrees beyond vertical if you have normal mobility.
    3. There is no need to rotate your legs extremely slowly, but you should lower them in a controlled manner rather than letting them drop down abruptly.
4. Reverse the movement as you sweep your legs up toward your midline and then fully to the other side in the same manner you just performed during step 3.
5. Cyclically rotate your legs from side to side in a windshield wiper-like fashion until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise chiefly trains your obliques while your rectus abdominis and hip flexors serve a stabilizing role.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide upward during the dead hang, which pulls on your hand skin in a manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
    1. So long as your feet are above the ground, it is up to you how bent your knees initially are when hanging on the bar.
    2. While hanging, your thighs should be directed straight down toward the ground, and your torso should be in a vertical position.
    3. You will maintain a firm grip on the bar with straightened elbows throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. March up both knees toward your chest through your available range of motion while curling your hips forward and allowing your knees to progressively bend as they lift higher up.
    1. Curling your hips forward will round your lower back and result in your buttocks facing forward at the end of the lifting phase.
    2. Keep your chest facing straight ahead rather than leaning your torso backward.
    3. You do not consciously need to think about progressively bending your knees since they will naturally bend when marching your knees up unless you intentionally try to straighten them.
    4. How closely you are able to raise your knees up toward your chest will be influenced by the mobility in your hips and lower back, along with your strength.
    5. Whenever you feel like you cannot bring your knees any closer toward your chest, you have reached the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your knees to descend while uncurling your lower back until you have returned to the starting position with your thighs directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Actively lower your legs throughout the descent rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you back down, which could result in your legs swinging back behind the starting position as momentum takes over.
    2. Your knees can naturally transition to a less bent position as you lower your legs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet to the ground.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.

### **Straight knee leg raise versus bent knee leg raise**

With the exception of utilizing a straighter knee position, the technique for the straight knee leg raise is otherwise essentially the same as the bent knee leg raise, so both variations primarily train the same muscle groups. In terms of the training effect these exercises provide, the main difference is that the straight knee leg raise is a higher intensity variation due to how it shifts the center of mass of your legs farther forward relative to your trunk and hip. This results in a longer lever arm of resistance acting on your trunk and hip, which requires your abdominal muscles and hip flexor muscles respectively generating greater trunk flexion and hip flexion torque to overcome. Resistive torque can be increased by increasing the weight you move or by increasing the lever arm in which the weight acts on a particular joint, so the straight knee leg raise can function similar to a weighted knee raise without requiring additional weights. The greater magnitude of resistance from the straight knee leg raise almost certainly explains why McGill et al. (2014) found this variation to yield noticeably greater EMG activity of the abdominal muscles than the bent knee leg raise when participants performed three reps of each exercise.

Nonetheless, this does not mean that the straight knee leg raise is inherently superior to the bent knee leg raise for developing the abdominal muscles in the same way that squatting 200lb (91kg) is not necessarily better than squatting 150lb (68kg) for inducing quad hypertrophy. If you have the strength to perform both leg raise variations with sound technique, you simply will have to perform greater reps per set with the bent knee position compared to the straight knee position to achieve the same hypertrophic stimulus. If an equal number of sets are performed until the same proximity failure (e.g., performing as many reps as you can with each condition), a very similar degree of muscle growth may be produced by 6-rep sets, 30-rep sets, or anywhere in between. Correspondingly, the bent knee variation that provides less resistance can still be just as productive for developing the abdominals as the straight knee variation for most people when leaving the same number of reps in reserve for sets of either exercise. The one exception may be for individuals with especially strong abdominals who are able to perform a very high number of bent knee leg raise reps per set before reaching a pronounced degree of muscle fatigue, indicating that the resistance from these reps are very low relative to their strength. At some point an exercise may just be providing too low of a resistance to optimize how much muscle growth that a set can stimulate even if the set is performed until failure. Specifically where that threshold lies is currently unclear, but that should not be any concern for you unless you are able to perform over 30 reps per set, which will not be an issue for the vast majority of people performing bent knee leg raises with a controlled technique through a full range of motion.

### Hanging ab straps

If you have access to hanging ab straps, you have the option of using them for this exercise by hanging them from your pull-up bar about shoulder width apart from each other and resting your upper arms on top of the straps as you otherwise use the same form described under the exercise technique section. Using straps would ensure that your grip strength would not be able to function as the limiting factor during your set without otherwise affecting how your abdominal and hip flexor muscles are trained. Correspondingly, utilizing straps would remove the stimulus this exercise can provide to your forearm and hand muscles involved with gripping. Performing the exercise with straps would also result in the shoulders maintaining a moderately flexed position (as the upper arms remain parallel to the ground) in contrast to the fully flexed shoulder position that is adopted when hanging on the bar. This can result in strap usage removing the stimulus to improve or maintain shoulder mobility in an overhead position that can be experienced when hanging on the bar. On the other hand, the shoulder extensor muscles (e.g., lats, teres major, rear delt) will have to act isometrically (statically) to maintain the moderately flexed shoulder position when using straps. While these muscles are likely more engaged when performing the exercise with straps compared to without straps, they are still unlikely to be loaded enough to provide any meaningful training effect for most lifters.

### Is your grip strength limiting you?

As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance rather than abdominal muscle strength, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual. If you find that you can complete meaningfully more reps when using straps, your grip strength is likely limiting you during this exercise, which can consequently limit the stimulus it provides to your abdominal muscles.## Setting up
1. Approach a flat exercise bench or a similarly sturdy object with an elevated surface that can support your foot.
    1. As an alternative to a bench, you can use a plyometric box or an aerobic step stacked on risers. At home, you can use pieces of furniture such as a bed, couch, or chair that will not slide. If the object you use has a hard surface, placing a cushioned object (such as a foam pad) on top of it may make this exercise more comfortable.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the object’s height increases, so you can utilize a taller object for a greater intensity. However, a height of around 18in (46cm), which applies to a typical flat exercise bench, is recommended to use as a minimum for most people.
2. Lie down on your side with the inner aspect of your top knee (which is bent by about 90 degrees) and lower leg on top of the elevated surface. Align this thigh with your torso so that you can draw a straight line from your thigh through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from above.
    1. The outer aspect of your lower hip should be resting on the ground.
    2. If you are on a hard surface, you can set up on an exercise mat for comfort. You chiefly want a comfortable surface to be under the forearm that you will be placing on the ground.
3. Align your lower thigh directly under your top thigh.
    1. If the object you are using has open space underneath its top surface (like with a bench), keep your knee straight on your lower leg.
    2. If your object lacks space for your lower foot under its top surface (like with a soft plyometric box), keep your bottom knee bent.
4. Position your bottom forearm on the ground with your elbow bent by about 90 degrees. Your elbow should be close to being stacked directly underneath your shoulder.
    1. With your bottom hand, you can make a fist and keep the little finger-side of your hand in contact with the ground. Alternatively, you can keep your palm flat on the ground if you find that to feel more comfortable and stable.
    2. You can keep your top arm in a comfortable position, such as resting on top of your side with a straight elbow. Alternatively, you can reach that arm toward the ceiling with a straight elbow or place that side’s hand on your top hip with a bent elbow.
    3. Your bottom upper arm should be in a vertical position.
    4. You can perform steps 2-4 simultaneously.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your bottom hip and leg fully off the ground by driving your top knee down into the bench while pushing your bottom forearm into the ground.
    1. At the top, you should now be able to draw a straight line from your head through your belly button to the center point between your knees when viewed from the front.
    2. You should also be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the top.
    3. Your thighs (which should be close together), torso, and head should be in essentially the same alignment as they would be when standing upright.
    4. The intent to pull your top knee down toward your bottom knee is what should lift your bottom leg, rather than simply lifting your bottom leg up by itself. Since the bench stops your knee from moving down, this intent lifts you up.
    5. An indicator that you are appropriately driving through your top leg is when your top hip becomes approximately level with your top knee. If you lift your bottom leg by itself, your top hip will remain lower than your top foot, which you do not want.
2. Slowly reverse the movement by lowering your hips until your bottom leg lightly taps the ground. Keep your bottom thigh close to parallel to the ground throughout the exercise.
    1. At the bottom, your top hip should now be lower than your top knee, since you should be moving through your top hip.
    2. Avoid lowering your bottom leg by itself, which would result in your bottom knee being lower than your bottom hip. Your bottom knee should remain essentially level with your bottom hip throughout the set.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your opposite side elevated.
    1. When learning this exercise, it can be useful to face a mirror to receive visual feedback about your positioning.
    2. Avoid letting your buttocks drift backward during the set by bending your hips. This would prevent you from being able to draw a straight line through your whole body when viewed from the top. Squeezing your glutes can help prevent you from unintentionally bending your hips.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hip adductor muscles and a variety of core muscles, including your obliques and quadratus lumborum.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with straight legs and your feet approximately hip-width apart. The back of your head should either rest on the ground or on your hands, which you can clasp behind your head.
    1. If you do not want to position your hands behind your head, feel free to place your arms wherever you prefer (e.g., resting on the ground by your sides).
    2. On the ground, your legs and torso should be in the same position in which you exhibit when normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your knees straightened, lift both of your feet mildly (i.e., approximately 6 inches (15 centimeters)) up off the ground while you tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the ground.
    1. Pressing your lower back into the ground will flatten it out from abdominal muscle contraction and prevent you from being able to slide a hand under your lower back.
    2. If you do not contract your abdominals, lifting both legs would prompt your lower back to develop an exaggerated arch, allowing you to slide a hand under it.
    3. For this variation, you will either rest your head on the ground or on your hands (as you prefer).
2. As you keep one leg stationary, lift your other leg somewhat (e.g., about 6 to 10 inches (15 to 25 centimeters)) higher up while keeping both knees straight.
    1. Specifically how high you lift this leg is up to your preference since a range of heights can effectively be used for this exercise.
3. In a scissor-like manner, lower your top leg toward the starting position (about 6 inches (15 centimeters)) off the ground while simultaneously lifting up your lower leg to the same height you just did with your other leg in step 2.
    1. How quickly you move is up to you, but strive to move each leg at the same speed such that they finish switching positions at the same time.
    2. Continue keeping your lower back pressed into the ground with your abdominal muscles tensed throughout your set as you avoid arching your lower back.
4. Cyclically repeat step 3 while switching which leg moves up and down each time until you have completed your set.
## Description
Along with training your hip flexor muscles on the front of your thigh, this exercise can statically train your abdominal muscles including your obliques and particularly your rectus abdominis, which is the “six-pack muscle.”## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station to approximately chest height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. As an alternative to the D-handle attachment, you also can perform a Pallof press with a rope attachment, but this page will describe use of the D-handle.
2. Stand with the side of one shoulder facing the cable station as the handle is just in front of your chest.
3. Firmly grasp the handle with your hands interlocked and palms facing each other. Your elbows should be bent with your upper arms by your sides and the handle in front of your chest.
    1. One palm should be facing in toward the cable as the other palm faces straight out to the side away from the cable.
4. While keeping the handle close to your chest, side-step away from the cable station until you feel tension from the cable.
    1. Prevent your torso from rotating when side-stepping.
5. Position your feet near shoulder-width apart, and slightly sit your buttocks backward to bend your hips and knees slightly.
    1. Rather than being excessively arched or rounded, your back and neck should be near a neutral position like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. In this starting position, you should be able to form a 90-degree angle between the cable and an imaginary laser beam shooting straight forward from the center of your chest. You should also be able to draw a straight line between your shoulders that is parallel to the cable.
    3. The purpose of the slight hip and knee bend is to help you feel stable and balanced during your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full body tension, and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping the center of your chest facing straight ahead, press the handle straight forward away from your chest.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as your arms are outstretched in front of you close to parallel to the ground.
    2. All the motion should occur from your arms moving at your shoulders and elbows while the rest of your body remains completely stationary.
    3. Particularly avoid letting the resistance from the cable rotate your torso. If your core muscles do not stabilize your trunk sufficiently, the imaginary laser beam shooting out from the center of your chest will start angling closer toward the cable station. The center of your chest should be facing the same direction throughout your entire set.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by bringing the handle straight back toward your chest as you bend your elbows.
    1. You should now be back to the starting position with the handle directly in front of your chest as your upper arms are right by your sides.
    2. Continue keeping the center of your chest facing straight forward as your shoulders remain aligned when viewed from the side.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise while facing the opposite direction.
## Description
While also engaging your pec major, this exercise will statically train your obliques as they function to resist trunk rotation.## Setting up
1. Pick up a weighted exercise ball of your desired weight that is appropriate to slam onto the ground.
    1. Not every medicine ball is designed to withstand the impact of slamming. Balls with a soft shell (which includes balls specifically known as slam balls) are typically suitable to slam, but balls with a rigid coat are often inappropriate for a high-impact exercise.
    2. If you are unsure whether the ball you have is appropriate to slam, you can look up the details of the model you have or ask the gym staff.
    3. It is recommended to use a ball that does not bounce if you have access to one.
2. Carry the ball to an area with sufficient space around you and a surface appropriate for slamming.
3. While standing upright with your feet near shoulder width apart, firmly hold the ball in front of your thighs with both hands and your fingers spread apart.
    1. Your palms should be facing in toward each other on each side of the ball.
    2. Your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Quickly lift the ball up until it is directly overhead with mildly bent elbows.
    1. Rather than overhead pressing the ball up, you can sweep it up out in front of you with mildly bent elbows as you could do during a front raise that ends overhead.
    2. You can optionally lift your heels up as you lift the ball up.
    3. While you should have a “proud chest” posture with your chest up, avoid arching your lower back excessively.
    4. If you do not have the shoulder mobility to lift the ball directly overhead without leaning back, simply raise your arms up as high as you can without letting your ribs flare up (which occurs from lower back arching). Strive to position your biceps close to your ears.
2. Explosively slam the ball down with the intent to break the ground. Simultaneously sweep your arms down while sitting your buttocks back as you hinge at your hips and bend your knees.
    1. To fully benefit from this exercise, it is paramount that you slam the ball as forcefully as you physically can.
    2. The arm motion you perform is akin to a straight arm lat pulldown performed as explosively as possible.
    3. Make sure to fully follow through with your arms rather than halting the slam after releasing the ball. If you can stop moving directly after the ball leaves your hands, you are not slamming the ball as violently as you can. You should drive your arms down so forcefully that they inevitably fly fully back behind your torso after letting go of the ball.
    4. Finish the slam with your elbows fully straightened.
    5. The slamming motion should look similar to when someone dips down in preparation to perform a vertical jump. At the end of the slamming phase, your body should look like you are ready to jump up.
    6. As your torso transitions closer to a horizontal angle from sitting your buttocks back, avoid collapsing your chest or rounding your shoulders forward into a slumped posture.
    7. If you opted to lift your heels up during the lifting phase, you naturally will bring your heels to the ground during the slamming phase.
3. Allow the ball to come to a dead stop on the ground before picking it up and assuming the same position you did while setting up.
    1. Certain balls will stop immediately as they hit the ground, while other balls will bounce up.
    2. When using a ball that bounces up, people often catch the ball right after it bounces following the impact, but this can be suboptimal. Aiming to catch the ball very soon after releasing it can interfere with the maximally explosive intent when initially slamming the ball. It can be difficult to catch the ball in front of you after fully following through with the slam, so striving to catch the ball can prompt people to stop the slamming motion prematurely, which contributes to reduced explosiveness.
    3. Use your lower body to pick up the ball in a squat or hip hinge movement rather than excessively rounding and straightening your lower back to do so.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While engaging a variety of muscles throughout the body, this exercise can particularly improve explosive strength of your lats, teres major, and rear delts as they function to extend the shoulders.## Setting up
1. Follow the setup instructions for the single arm kettlebell clean, and perform the lifting phase of the single arm kettlebell clean described in steps 1-5 of its exercise technique instructions.
    1. After doing so, you should now be holding the kettlebell in the front rack position. This involves situating it in front of your shoulder with your elbow bent slightly in front of your ribcage, palm facing in toward your other side, and the weight resting on your forearm, which is mostly vertically with your wrist slightly closer to the midline of your body than your elbow. The handle should also be diagonally contacting your palm, such that the side of the handle by your thumb is angled mildly higher than the side of the handle by your little finger.
    2. As an alternative to cleaning the kettlebell, you can curl it up while using your free hand to help lift the weight up under its base and guide the kettlebell into the same front rack position described above. Initially grasp the handle with your little finger closer to one of the corner horns than your thumb to help the handle rest diagonally across your palm in the front rack position. Feel free to perform a cheat curl-like motion where you initially generate some momentum by swinging the weight mildly backward with a straight elbow before swinging your elbow forward while curling it and assisting with your free hand. This is a less technically complicated means of moving the kettlebell into the front rack, but it is not as efficient when using a heavy weight for lifters who are proficient with cleaning the kettlebell.
    3. Once the kettlebell is in the front rack position, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the kettlebell.
2. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, keep your chest up in a “proud chest” posture, rather than letting your shoulders and upper back round forward into a hunched position.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain its natural curvature like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced throughout your set.
2. Based on your preference and familiarity with each technique, you can press the kettlebell fully up overhead through a push press using leg drive or a strict overhead press without leg drive.
    1. The following directions describe how to perform a push press, since it will efficiently allow you to assume the overhead position with a heavy weight, but you can alternatively press the weight up without leg drive if you have the strength to do so.
3. Without moving your arm, mildly bend your knees in a quarter squat-like manner to dip down while maintaining an upright torso position with your chest up.
    1. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground during this dip phase.
    2. Your knees should track straight forward toward your toes rather than caving inward.
4. Directly after reaching the bottom of your dip, forcibly push through your feet as you rapidly extend (straighten) your knees and hips to generate upward momentum.
    1. The intent of this leg drive phase should be akin to a jump without lifting your feet fully off the ground.
    2. At the end of this drive, you can either keep the soles of your feet fully on the ground, or you can allow your heels to lift up slightly off the ground while keeping the front of your feet firmly in contact with the ground if a slight heel lift feels natural to you.
5. Immediately after you have finished extending your legs, explosively push the kettlebell upward by forcibly driving your elbow upward as you swiftly straighten your elbow. Allow your elbow to flare out away from your side when driving up, so that your palm faces forward at the top.
    1. At the end of the pressing phase, your knees, hips, and elbow should be fully straightened, and your arm should be directed straight up toward the ceiling with your elbow and wrist situated vertically over your shoulder.
    2. To transfer your leg drive into pushing the kettlebell upward in an explosive manner, you should perform steps 4 and 5 as a continuous movement.
    3. While your forearm starts slightly angled in the front rack position, it should otherwise remain essentially in a vertical position with your elbow stacked under your wrist throughout the press.
    4. Throughout the exercise, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back.
    5. Regardless of whether you lifted your heels up off the ground after extending your legs during the leg drive phase, your heels should be flat on the ground with the rest of your feet at the end of the pressing phase.
6. While keeping the kettlebell overhead with your elbow straightened and your chest up, walk forward in a controlled manner while maintaining an upright posture with your shoulders approximately level with each other.
    1. You will not swing your working arm like you would when normally walking, and you will keep your trunk in a rigidly braced position, but your lower body should look similar to how it would when you walk normally.
    2. Based on your preference and what feels natural to you, you can either swing your free arm back and forth as you walk or keep it by your side.
    3. Walk at a pace where you feel stable and balanced throughout the carry.
    4. If you use a weight that is fairly heavy to you, it is natural to take shorter strides compared to how you typically walk.
    5. You should feel your core muscles on the side opposite to the kettlebell working to maintain an upright torso, as these muscles prevent the weight from bending your torso to the side of the kettlebell.
    6. Avoid leaning your torso away from the kettlebell, and keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level throughout your set rather than letting them drop down to one side when taking each step.
7. Continue walking with the kettlebell overhead until you have completed your target distance and are ready to repeat the carry while holding the kettlebell with your other side.
    1. Based on the space available to you, you naturally may have to pivot around and walk the opposite direction after covering a particular distance, depending on the distance you are striving to reach during this set.
8. Lower the kettlebell in a controlled manner as you let your elbow bend while descending and arcing forward toward the front rack position.
9. As you approach the end of the lowering phase, shallowly bend your knees and hips as you did during step 3 (the dip phase) to help control the weight until you have returned to the starting position with the kettlebell in front of your shoulder.
10. Set the kettlebell down in the opposite manner to how you lifted it into the front rack position when setting up.
    1. If you initially cleaned the kettlebell up, you can let the kettlebell fall forward as you swing your arm down while straightening your elbow and hinging at your hips to absorb the force of the lowering phase.
    2. If you initially curled the kettlebell up with assistance from your free hand, you can similarly use your free hand to help guide the kettlebell down while letting your working arm’s elbow straighten in a controlled manner.
11. Transfer the kettlebell to your other side before repeating the exercise with that side.
## Description
While also engaging your thoracic erectors, this exercise can statically train much of your shoulder girdle musculature, including your front delts, lateral delts, serratus anterior, traps, and rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. Sit on the ground with an upright torso and your legs straight out in front of you.
    1. The back of your heels should be on the ground as your feet are close together.
2. Place your hands on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart, slightly behind your hips, with your fingers spread apart and elbows straight.
    1. Rotating your hands such that your middle fingers point straight out to your sides is appropriate. However, you can feel free to rotate your hands to point in whichever direction you find most comfortable.
    2. The proper hand placement should allow your arms to point straight down toward the ground during the exercise.
3. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Rather than being slumped forward, your chest should be directed slightly upward.
    2. As a cue, you can think about pinching a pencil with your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes.
    1. At the top, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. Keep your elbows straight as the weight you bear through your arms predominantly goes through the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) rather than closer to your knuckles.
    3. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back with a “proud chest” as you avoid letting your shoulders round forward.
    4. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    5. Squeezing your glutes can help you avoid excessively arching your lower back, which should exhibit its natural mild curvature. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them.
2. Statically hold the rigid position you adopted from step 1 until you have completed your set’s target duration.
    1. Strive to have your body look the same during the last second of your set as it did during the first second after getting into position.
    2. Resist letting your hips gradually lower toward the ground as time elapses.
3. After completing your set, bend your hips to return to sitting on the ground.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and spinal extensor muscles that run along your entire back.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your hands, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight and your knees are bent.
2. Prop your forearms onto the ground with your upper arms directed straight down such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    1. Based on your preference, you can make a fist with each hand and direct your palms in toward each other, or you can place your palms flat on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. You should be bearing your bodyweight into the ground through your elbows and feet.
2. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes to maintain a flat back position while keeping your knees straight and elbows bent.
    1. Doing so keeps your hips up (but not excessively hiked) rather than letting them sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch in your lower back.
    2. In this position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag down, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
    4. Keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
3. Statically hold the position you adopted after performing steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your core muscles, including your rectus abdominis (AKA the “six-pack muscle”) and your abdominal obliques, which help maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent and the soles of your feet  flat on the ground, approximately hip width apart.
    1. You can rest your head on the ground while your arms are by your sides with your palms in contact with the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift both legs off the ground and back toward you until your thighs are approximately vertical. Keep your knees bent, and tighten your abs to press your lower back down into the ground.
    1. Pressing your lower back into the ground will flatten it out from abdominal muscle contraction and prevent you from being able to slide a hand under your lower back.
    2. If you do not contract your abdominals, lifting both legs up would prompt your lower back to develop an exaggerated arch that would allow you to slide a hand under your lower back.
2. Continue driving your bent knees toward your face while lifting your buttocks and hip bones up off the floor as you curl up your lower back.
    1. Doing so will prompt your buttocks to face forward rather than face straight down toward the ground.
    2. Move through what feels like your full range of motion that is comfortable to you, which will be influenced by the mobility in your hips and lower back.
    3. Aim to transition fluidly from step 1 to step 2 in a continuous motion rather than pausing after completing step 1.
    4. You should naturally feel your abdominal muscles further tighten while doing so.
3. Reverse the movement from step 2 by allowing your buttocks and hip bones to lower down to the floor in a controlled manner as you uncurl your lower back.
4. Reverse the movement from step 1 by allowing your legs to descend in a controlled manner until the soles of your feet lightly contact the ground.
    1. Continue tightening your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the ground as you lower your legs.
5. Alternate between performing steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Pick up a dumbbell with one hand, carry it over to a flat bench, and position yourself near the base of the bench and directly to its side with your side that is holding the dumbbell farther away from the bench.
    1. If you have to carry the dumbbell a meaningful distance before reaching your bench and your grip is somewhat fatigued, you can place the dumbbell on the bench before picking it up with your opposite hand to start your set with the side that does not have a fatigued grip.
2. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips, reach out with your free arm to place its hand on the bench with a straight elbow, and place your knee (on the side not holding the dumbbell) onto the bench.
    1. In this position, your torso should now be reasonably close to being parallel to the ground, and your lower back should be flat rather than being meaningfully rounded.
    2. While setting up, keep your elbow straight on the side holding the dumbbell and allow that arm to hang down toward the ground with your shoulder blade naturally pulled forward toward the ground from the weight.
    3. Based on your comfort and preference, you can place the open palm of your free hand onto the bench, or you can make a fist and place your knuckles onto the bench.
    4. Your foot on the side of the dumbbell should remain firmly in contact with the floor and be sufficiently far behind the dumbbell that your leg does not restrict your row. If you initially do not feel well-balanced, you can try positioning this foot somewhat further out to the side to widen your base of support.
3. Direct your palm so that it is facing in toward the opposite side of your body, and maintain this neutral grip position throughout your set.
    1. The bony bump on the back of your elbow should be directed backward, which is the direction you will row.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Drive your elbow backward and upward in an arcing motion to pull the dumbbell toward your hip. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade backward, and keep your elbow tucked by your side rather than flaring it outward.
    1. When allowing your elbow to bend naturally, your forearm should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    2. At the top of the row, your elbow should be aligned with your torso or slightly behind your torso.
    3. Keep your torso in a mostly fixed position with your chest facing a similar direction throughout this movement. A mild degree of torso rotation is not problematic, but avoid letting it become excessive and turning the exercise into a cheat row.
    4. While performing this arcing motion, the dumbbell should not move straight upward in a vertical path.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled arcing motion until your arm is directed down toward the ground with your elbow straightened.
    1. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blade forward, which will stretch your upper back muscles and result in your working side’s shoulder being mildly closer to the ground than your other side’s shoulder at the bottom of the row.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight rapidly pull your arm down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. While placing one hand on each of a kettlebell’s horns (i.e, the side handles) with your palms facing in toward each other, pick up the kettlebell and stand upright in a near hip-width stance. Hold the kettlebell in front of your thighs with straight elbows.
    1. It may be awkward to use this grip to pick up a kettlebell directly from the floor (especially if you are using a heavier weight), so you have the option of initially deadlifting the kettlebell with a double overhand grip from the top handle and setting it on an elevated surface like a bench before grasping its horns with your palms facing in.
    2. Due to the shape of the kettlebell, it is not feasible to have the top handle pointing straight upward (like it would when the bottom base of the kettlebell is resting on the ground) while your elbows are fully extended, so you will have to angle the kettlebell’s base toward you.
    3. To hold the kettlebell in front of your upper thighs with extended elbows, you will need to move your upper arms slightly in front of your torso (rather than keeping your upper arm positioned directly by your sides like you could when curling two kettlebells or dumbbells).
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the kettlebell upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms fixed just in front of your torso (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the kettlebell in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to an extended position.
    1. During the lowering phase, continue to keep your upper arms in the same position with which you started.
3. Alternate between curling the kettlebell up and lowering it down until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise will effectively train the biceps and brachialis in your upper arm along with the brachioradialis in your forearm with the practical benefit of only requiring one kettlebell.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with extended elbows.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing in toward your sides (i.e., using a neutral grip position).
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one dumbbell resting at your side with an extended elbow, curl the other dumbbell upward and across the front of your torso toward your opposite shoulder by bending at your elbow.
    1. As you start curling across your body, you will naturally rotate your shoulder inward such that your palm is directed toward the front of your torso.
    2. To allow the dumbbell to clear your torso, you will have to position your upper arm slightly in front of your body while curling (rather than keeping your upper arm right next to your side like you could with a standard hammer curl).
2. After your elbow has fully flexed, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner until your elbow has returned to an extended position with the dumbbell and your upper back at your side.
3. Alternate between curling the dumbbell upwards and lowering it down until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Select your desired weight with the loading pin and adjust the machine’s seat to a position that allows your knees to be mildly bent when your feet are on the foot plate.
    1. If the seat is positioned too far back, the machine may not be able to provide tension throughout your full ankle range of motion.
2. Sit down with your back in full contact with the backrest.
    1. Your low back should be in a fairly flat position rather than being meaningfully rounded, like in a slouched position, which could occur if you sit your hips excessively forward.
3. With approximately a shoulder width stance, place the front halves of your feet onto the bottom of the foot plate with your heels hanging off the edge.
    1. Maintain this foot position throughout your set.
4. While grasping the hand grips, press the platform away from you by extending your knees until they are straight or only slightly bent.
    1. Maintain this knee position (rather than bending and extending your knees) while performing this exercise until you are ready to finish your set.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they utilize a hyperextended knee position during this exercise, so keeping a straight or slightly bent knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the platform away from you by pointing the balls of your feet away from you (in a manner similar to stepping on a car’s gas pedal) through the available range of motion of your ankles.
    1. Motion should occur exclusively at your ankles, while knees and hips do not move.
2. In a controlled manner, reverse the motion by allowing the front halves of your feet to move back toward you through the available range of motion of your ankles.
    1. How far you are able to move will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to lower the platform back into its starting position by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the machine’s shoulder pads to a level that initially allows you to stand under the pads with mildly bent knees, which will enable you to perform full range of motion calf raises.
    1. The appropriate shoulder pad height should make it easy for you to assume the starting position and also allow you to feel resistance from the machine both when your heels are fully lowered and fully raised.
    2. If the pads are too high, your knees may be straightened when initially standing under the pads, and you may lose tension from the machine when your heels are fully lowered.
    3. If the pads are too low, you may need to bend your knees excessively to stand under the pads, and you may be restricted from fully raising your heels.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. With your knees mildly bent, position the balls of your feet onto the foot plate with your heels hanging off the edge while your shoulders are snugly resting under the shoulder pads.
4. Straighten your knees to stand fully upright and receive the weight from the machine.
    1. Throughout the set, keep the balls of your feet firmly planted on the step.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they allow their knees to bend backward into hyperextension during this exercise, so keeping a straight knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels fully upward while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
    1. Once you have lifted your heels as high upward as your ankle mobility permits, you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. While you lift your body upward through ankle motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your heels to descend toward the ground through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits.
    1. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can below the balls of your feet to stretch your calves and train them most effectively.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calves (which you may feel on the backside of your knees or lower down your legs) at the bottom, you likely are not lowering through your full range of motion.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid quickly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep.
    4. You can optionally briefly pause in the fully stretched bottom position,
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After finishing your last rep, mildly bend your knees to lower the weight to its resting position (where it no longer provides any resistance to you) before stepping off the foot plate.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine, adjust the height of the machine’s back pad to a level that allows you to perform full range of motion calf raises.
    1. If the pad is too high, the machine may not be able to provide tension when your heels are fully lowered.
    2. If the pad is too low, it may be difficult for you to assume your starting position.
2. With your knees mildly bent and your torso flexed forward at your hips while facing toward the handles, position the front halves of your feet onto the foot plate. While grasping the handles and resting forearms on the nearby pad, let your heels hang off the edge of the foot plate.
    1. In this position, your lower back should be snugly resting under the back pad.
3. If your lower back is not snugly resting under the back pad or you find it challenging to situate your body into the starting position, you can re-adjust the height of the back pad.
4. Lift your hips up by extending your knees to receive the weight from the machine.
    1. Maintain your knees in a position of extension or a slight bend with the front halves of your feet on the foot plate throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your heels into the air through the available range of motion of your ankles.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heels to descend toward the ground through the available range of motion of your ankles while continuing to keep the front halves of your feet on top of the foot plate.
    1. How far you are able to lower your heels will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. After your heels have fully descended, alternate between raising and lowering your heels until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine, adjust the height of the machine’s shoulder pad to a level that allows you to perform full range of motion calf raises.
    1. If the pad is too high, the machine may not be able to provide tension when your heels are fully lowered.
    2. If the pad is too low, the machine may restrict you from fully raising your heels.
2. With your knees slightly bent, position the front halves of your feet onto the step with your heels hanging off the edge while your shoulders are snugly resting under the shoulder pads.
3. Stand upright with your knees extended to receive the weight from the machine.
    1. Maintain this upright position throughout the set.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they utilize a hyperextended knee position during this exercise, so keeping a straight knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
4. While keeping one foot on the step, lift your other foot off the step by bending that knee.
    1. Keep this foot in this position off the step throughout the set while you keep the front half of your working leg’s foot on the step.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your heel into the air through your ankle’s available range of motion.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heel to descend toward the ground through your ankle’s available range of motion while continuing to keep the front half of your foot on top of the step.
    1. How far you are able to lower your heel will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Select your desired weight with the loading pin and adjust the machine’s seat to a position that allows your knees to be mildly bent when your feet are on the foot plate.
    1. If the seat is positioned too far back, the machine may not be able to provide tension throughout your full ankle range of motion.
2. Sit down with your back in full contact with the backrest.
    1. Your low back should be in a fairly flat position rather than being meaningfully rounded, like in a slouched position, which could occur if you sit your hips excessively forward.
3. Place the front half of one foot onto the bottom of the foot plate with your heel hanging off the edge.
    1. Keep your other foot in a comfortable position, such as resting on the ground.
4. While grasping the hand grips, press the platform away from you by extending your knee until it is straight or only slightly bent.
    1. You should maintain this knee position (rather than bending and extending your knees) while performing this exercise until you are ready to finish your set.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they utilize a hyperextended knee position during this exercise, so keeping a straight or slightly bent knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the platform away from you by pointing the ball of your foot away from you (in a manner similar to stepping on a car’s gas pedal) through your ankle’s available range of motion.
    1. Motion should occur exclusively at your ankle, while your knee and hip do not move.
2. In a controlled manner, reverse the motion by allowing the front half of your foot to move back toward you through your ankle’s available range of motion.
    1. How far you are able to move will be dependent on your mobility.
    2. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. After completing your last rep with your other side, lower the platform back into its starting position by bending your knee.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are seated.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the seat height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    3. To confirm whether you selected an appropriate seat height, you will have to sit down and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
    4. A seat height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points will commonly allow you to contact the arm pads with the outer aspects of your arms just above your elbows when keeping your upper arms near your sides.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit on the seat with your upper arms by your sides and your elbows bent to near 90 degrees. Place the outer aspects of your arms against the arm pads, and grasp the handles with your palms facing in toward each other if your machine has handles.
    1. Seated lateral raise machines may be constructed with handles situated closer toward the machine's weight stack or further away in front of the machine’s weight stack.
    2. With a machine that has handles closer toward its weight stack, you should sit down with your chest facing the stack. These machines often have a chest pad onto which you can rest your chest.
    3. With a machine that has handles further away in front of its weight stack, you should sit down with your back directed toward the stack. These machines often have a back pad onto which you can rest your back.
    4. Some machine models lack handles for you to hold, but have a chest pad or a larger back pad that could inform you which is the standard direction for you to face while using them.
    5. A minority of machines may lack a chest pad, back pad, or handles, and correspondingly not have a clear way for you to face besides what may be presented on an instructional image on the side of the machine.
4. Based on your preference, you can sit with a fully upright torso, or you can lean slightly forward at your hips.
    1. Either torso position can allow you to perform this exercise effectively, so you can utilize whichever one feels most comfortable for your shoulders.
    2. You can try out each posture during a different set to determine your personal preference.
    3. If you opt to lean slightly forward and are using a machine with a chest pad, you may have to scoot slightly backward on the seat.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows out and away from your sides until your upper arms are close to parallel with the ground.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    2. Focusing on moving your elbows in an arcing manner throughout this exercise can help you target the appropriate shoulder muscles.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by lowering your elbows in toward your sides until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the machine’s lever arms (onto which you loaded the plates) to the stoppers underneath them.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arms downward.
    3. Keep your arms snugly in contact with the arm pads throughout your set.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. As the side of your shoulder (that you will not train during this set) is facing the cable station, stand in a staggered stance with one foot a little in front of the pulley and your other foot a little behind the pulley.
    1. If you step forward with your foot on the side that is closer to the pulley, the cable may be less likely to brush up against your leg during the exercise.
3. Lunge down and grasp the handle between your legs with your hand on the side opposite the cable.
    1. Your palm should be facing in toward the pulley.
4. Grasp the cable station’s vertical beam for stability with your closer free hand, and stand up while maintaining a slight bend in your knees.
    1. Your working side’s arm should now be in a vertical position close to your body with a straight or slightly bent elbow (depending on what feels most comfortable to you).
    2. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable, so if that is not the case, take a step away from the cable stack.
    3. Continue using your free hand to assist with stabilizing yourself throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep the handle out and away from your side until your upper arm is close to parallel with the ground.
    1. At the top of the raise, your working arm should form half of a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from the front or back.
    2. While you raise your arm through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blade to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your arm in toward your side in a controlled arcing manner until your arm lightly contacts your torso.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. At a cable station, adjust the cable to its lowest height, clip in a D-handle attachment, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing to the side of the cable, grasp the handle with your hand on the side opposite the cable, stand up, and take a step out to the side away from the cable station.
    1. Your palm should be approximately facing the cable station as your hand is situated just in front of your thigh.
3. Slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips, slightly bend your elbow, and move your arm mildly across the front of your body until your hand is positioned approximately in front of your opposite thigh.
    1. Maintain this slightly hinged position and slightly bent elbow throughout your set.
    2. With your arm in this diagonal position, you will likely feel a stretching sensation on the side of your shoulder.
    3. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable, so if that is not the case, take another step away from the cable stack. 
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the handle out and away from your side until your upper arm is close to parallel with the ground.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the handle in a controlled manner back to the starting position with your hand just in front of your opposite thigh.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a bench with your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides as your palms are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
    1. You can opt to sit down on a flat bench without any back support or sit down on a seated bench with back support.
2. Bend your elbows to approximately right angles.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the dumbbells out and away from your sides until your upper arms are close to parallel with the ground.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner back to your sides.
3. Alternate between raising the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides as your palms are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
2. Slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips, and bend your elbows to approximately right angles.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the dumbbells out and away from your sides until your upper arms are close to parallel with the ground.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner back to your sides.
3. Alternate between raising the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. With your palms facing each other (i.e., using a neutral grip), grasp an elastic band with both hands in front of your torso while standing upright with your feet in approximately a shoulder-width stance.
    1. Inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
2. Position your hands so that they are somewhat greater than shoulder width apart from each other, with slack taken out of the band.
3. With straight elbows, elevate your arms in front of your torso until they are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. In this position, your palms should be directed down toward the ground.
    2. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your hands apart and backward until your arms and torso form a “T-shape.”
    1. If you set up by grasping the band with your hands too close together, you may reach the limit of the band’s extensibility (as indicated by an abrupt increase in resistance from the band) prior to your hands being pulled entirely apart from each other. If you notice this occurring, bring your hands back together and adjust your setup by gripping the band with your hands further apart. Continuing to pull the band apart once a portion has fully stretched may cause the band to tear.
2. Reverse the motion by bringing your hands forward and toward each other until they have returned to their starting position.
    1. You should feel tension from the band throughout the entire range of motion.
    2. Do not bring your hands so close together that the band becomes slack.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with slightly bent elbows as your palms are facing back toward you (i.e., in an overhand grip position).
2. Assume a bent-over torso position by hinging at your hips and mildly bending your knees until your torso is close to parallel with the ground.
    1. How far you are able to bend over will be affected by your mobility.
    2. If your mobility permits, you can bend over until your torso is parallel with the ground, but you can still effectively perform this exercise if you are not able to descend as far.
    3. While bent over, your arms should be approximately perpendicular to the ground with the dumbbells situated in front of your knees.
    4. Maintain this bent-over position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the dumbbells away from each other and straight out to your sides until your arms and torso form a “T-shape” without moving your hips or knees.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells toward each other in a controlled manner back until they have returned to their starting position in front of your knees.
3. Alternate between raising the dumbbells up and lowering them down until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s foot plate to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are standing on the foot plate.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the foot plate height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    3. To confirm whether you selected an appropriate foot plate height, you will have to stand on it and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
    4. A foot plate height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points will commonly allow you to hold the handles with slightly bent elbows.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and stand on the foot plate with the handles situated just in front of your thighs.
3. Firmly grasp the handles with slightly bent elbows as your palms are facing in toward each other.
4. Optionally lean your torso slightly forward at your hips by sitting your buttocks slightly backward with a slight knee bend.
    1. People commonly find lateral raises to feel more comfortable with a slight forward torso lean, but you can alternatively stand fully upright if you prefer to do so.
    2. Maintain your preferred torso position throughout your set, as you subsequently move your shoulders without moving your lower body or torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the handles by driving your elbows out and away from your sides until your upper arms are close to parallel with the ground.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    2. Focusing on moving your elbows in an arcing manner throughout this exercise can help you target the appropriate shoulder muscles.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by lowering your elbows in toward your sides to return the handles to the starting position.
    1. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arms downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a bench with your feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells at your sides with slightly bent elbows as your palms are facing back behind you (i.e., in an overhand grip position).
    1. You can opt to sit down on a flat bench without any back support or sit down on a seated bench with back support.
2. Assume a bent-over torso position by flexing forward at your hips and back until your torso is close to parallel with the ground.
    1. How far you are able to bend over will be affected by your mobility.
    2. If your mobility permits, you can bend over until your torso is parallel with the ground, but you can still effectively perform this exercise if you are not able to descend as far.
    3. While bent over, your arms should be approximately perpendicular to the ground with the dumbbells situated in front of your knees.
    4. Maintain this bent-over position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise the dumbbells away from each other and straight out to your sides until your arms and torso form a “T-shape.”
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells toward each other in a controlled manner back until they have returned to their starting position in front of your knees.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each cable to a height approximately level with your shoulders or slightly above them, remove any handle attachment if it is clipped onto the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pins.
2. After standing centered between the cables with your sides facing the cable stacks, take a step backward, raise your arms in front of you until they are approximately parallel with the ground, and side step toward one cable.
3. Reach across your body with your arm that is opposite to the cable to grasp the ball at the end of the cable with an overhand grip as your palm is facing down, before side-stepping toward the other cable and grasping its ball with your free hand.
    1. Alternatively, you have the option to grasp the end of the cable directly with your little finger and the adjacent side of your hand contacting the ball.
    2. Use whichever hand placement feels most comfortable and stable to you, but if you decide to grasp the cable directly, be aware to avoid pinching any fingers when returning the cable to its initial position after finishing your set.
4. Return to being centered between the cables with your arms crossed in front of your torso, with slightly bent elbows, as the cable stacks are situated a little in front of you.
    1. In this position, your arms should resemble an “X-shape” with each other.
    2. Maintain slightly bent elbows throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your arms out and away from each other as you move your arms backward until they form a “T-shape” with your torso.
2. Reverse the motion by bringing your arms inward and toward each other in a controlled manner until your arms have crossed and returned to their starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable to a height that is approximately level with your elbows when your arms are resting at your sides while standing.
2. Connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
3. While facing the cable station, grasp the handle with one hand and direct your palm downward as you raise your arm to be parallel with the cable.
4. With your elbow in an extended position, step backward until the machine’s weight plates separate and you feel tension from the cable
    1. In this position, your arm should be outstretched in front of your torso.
## Exercise technique
1. While bending your elbow, pull the handle backward and upward simultaneously until your upper arm is in line with your torso and approximately parallel with the ground while your palm is facing forward.
    1. While doing so, the cable should move in a diagonal motion that involves more horizontal motion than vertical motion.
    2. At the end of this motion, your elbow should be bent to near a 90-degree angle.
2. Reverse the motion by moving the handle forward and downward simultaneously as your elbow extends until you have returned to the starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While also meaningfully training your rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, this exercise can effectively develop your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, along with the supraspinatus to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest level, connect a straight bar attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing the cable station, reach up and grasp the bar with an underhand grip such that your palms are facing back toward you.
3. Take a step backward and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight torso lean throughout your set.
4. Pull your upper arms down and back until they are positioned at your sides with your elbows close to fully bent.
    1. Maintain this upper arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Push the bar downward by extending your elbows until they are straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, the bar should be situated in front of your thighs.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows through your available range of motion to raise the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbows as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest level and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Based on your preference, either connect a rope attachment or leave the cable without any attachment.
    1. Either option allows you to perform this exercise with essentially the same technique.
3. While standing with the cable station positioned in front of one of your shoulders, reach up with your closer hand to grasp the rope or the ball-shaped stopper with a neutral grip such that your palm is facing in toward your other side.
    1. If you decide to use the rope attachment, pull the rope to one side of the attachment and grip its end with your little finger and the adjacent aspect of your hand contacting the flat surface of the attachment’s rubber stopper as your thumb wraps over the outside of your other fingers.
    2. If you decide not to use any cable attachment, grasp the ball-shaped stopper at the end of the cable in the palm of your hand by wrapping your four smaller fingers around it and positioning your thumb on top of these fingers.
4. Take a step backward and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight torso lean throughout your set.
5. Pull your upper arm down and back until it is positioned at your sides with your elbow being close to fully bent.
    1. Maintain this upper arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Push the cable downward by extending your elbow until it is straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, your hand should be situated by your side.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbow through your available range of motion to raise the cable in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbow as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest level, connect a V-bar attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing the cable station, reach up and grasp the V-bar with your palms facing at an angle downward and inward while keeping your wrists fairly straight.
    1. Keep your wrists in a fairly straight position throughout your set.
3. Take a step backward and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight torso lean throughout your set.
4. Pull your upper arms down and back until they are at your sides, with your elbows close to fully bent.
    1. Maintain this upper arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Push the V-bar downward by extending your elbows until they are straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, the V-bar should be situated in front of your thighs.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows through your available range of motion to raise the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbows as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, which constitute the majority of muscle mass in your upper arms.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable to approximately hip height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Stand facing the cable station with the cable just in front of one of your arms.
3. With the hand in front of the cable, firmly grasp the handle with an underhand grip that directs your palm up with a bent elbow.
4. Take a step backward so that you feel tension from the cable as the weight stack separates.
5. As you keep your upper arm aligned with your torso, hinge at your hips to sit your buttocks back, and incline your torso so that it is fairly close to being in a horizontal position.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can place your free hand up against the cable station’s beam for additional stability.
    2. Maintain this bent-over-torso position throughout your set.
6. Drive your elbow backward and upward until your upper arm is approximately parallel to the ground while you allow tension from the cable to keep your elbow bent.
    1. Your elbow should now be somewhat above the side of your torso (which would look like behind your torso if you were standing upright) rather than being aligned with your torso.
    2. In this starting position, your elbow should be bent by at least 90 degrees.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arm parallel to the ground, progressively straighten your elbow to drive the handle backward and upward in an arcing motion until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. As you move your elbow, you should keep the rest of your body stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to bend in a controlled manner as you bring the handle downward and forward in an arcing fashion until you have returned to the starting position with your elbow bent by at least 90 degrees.
    1. Allow your elbow to continue bending as long as you feel tension from the cable pulling you toward a greater degree of bending.
    2. Continue keeping your upper arm parallel to the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise trains all three heads of your triceps, which together constitute the majority of muscle mass in your upper arms.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest level, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing with the cable station positioned in front of one of your shoulders, reach up with your closer hand to grasp the handle with an underhand grip such that your palm is facing back and toward you while keeping your wrist fairly straight.
    1. Keep your wrist in a fairly straight position throughout your set.
3. Take a step backward and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight torso lean throughout your set.
4. Pull your upper arm down and back until it is positioned at your sides with your elbow being close to fully bent.
    1. Maintain this upper arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Push the handle downward by extending your elbow until it is straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, the handle should be situated by your side.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbow through your available range of motion to raise the handle in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbow as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable to its highest setting, clip in a V-bar attachment, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing directly in front of the cable station and facing away from it, reach up overhead with bent elbows and fairly straight wrists to grasp the V-bar with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Maintain fairly straight wrists throughout your set.
3. While keeping your hands overhead and elbows bent, step forward and adopt a staggered stance with one foot forward in front of the other before moderately leaning forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this forward torso lean throughout your set.
    2. In this position, your hands should be behind your head, and you should feel tension from the cable. If that is not the case, walk forward until you feel tension from the cable.
4. Tuck your chin toward your chest and make sure your upper arms are situated parallel to the ground or raised up a little higher.
    1. Doing so will reduce the likelihood that the back of your head obstructs the path of the cable during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the V-bar forward and somewhat downward by extending your elbows until they are straightened, while not moving your upper arms.
    1. At the end of this motion, the V-bar should be situated in front of you.
    2. If the cable contacts the back of your head during this motion, you can lean forward a little more for additional clearance.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows through your available range of motion to move the V-bar backward and somewhat upward in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to continue keeping your upper arms stationary while all of the movement occurs from elbow motion rather than also involving shoulder motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near waist height, connect  a V-bar attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing the cable station, grasp the V-bar with your palms facing in toward each other.
3. Pivot your body 180 degrees while simultaneously repositioning your arms such that your upper arms are raised up high with your elbows close to being fully bent with fairly straight wrists as the cable is applying a downward force to the V-bar behind your head.
    1. The arm movement performed during this step is akin to a controlled swinging-like motion.
    2. How high you can raise your upper arms is influenced by your mobility.
4. Adopt a staggered stance by stepping forward with one foot, and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips
    1. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable, so if that is not the case, you should step further forward until you feel tension from the cable.
    2. Maintain this position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows high, press the V-bar upward by extending your elbows until they are straightened.
    1. At the end of this motion, the V-bar should be situated above your head.
    2. If the cable contacts the back of your head during this motion, you can tuck your chin closer to your chest for clearance.
2. Reverse the motion by bending your elbows through your available range of motion to lower the V-bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Strive to have your elbows as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up and grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can use a different grip, such as an underhand or a neutral (i.e., palms facing toward each other) grip, while otherwise using the same technique for this exercise.
2. Hang on the bar with straight elbows by bending your knees and lifting your heels off the ground while keeping the balls of your feet and your toes in contact with the ground.
    1. In this position, you can adjust how much of your bodyweight you are supporting through your legs and how much you are supporting through hanging on the bar.
    2. At any time during your set, you can shift how much of the force from your bodyweight is transmitted through the ground as opposed to the bar to alter the difficulty of this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straight and part of your feet on the ground, pull your shoulder blades downward through your available range of motion.
    1. This will move your shoulders away from your ears, while the rest of your body, except for your arms, lifts upward to a small extent.
    2. While the range of motion for this exercise may vary among different individuals, this exercise involves a low amplitude of total movement.
    3. You can think about packing your armpits down into your pockets as a technique cue.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulder blades to move upward in a controlled manner until you have reached a position that is akin to when you shrug your shoulders upward.
    1. This will move your shoulders closer toward your ears, while the rest of your body, except for your arms, moves downward to a small extent.
    2. Continue to keep your elbows straight.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will train your lats, lower traps, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates its handles slightly below the level of your hands when seated with your arms resting at your sides.
2. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, sit down facing away from the machine with your feet flat on the floor.
3. With extended elbows, lean forward by hinging at your hips until your hands are low enough to grasp the handles firmly with a neutral grip such that your palms are facing in toward each other.
    1. While leaning forward, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back into a slouched posture.
4. Sit upright by moving about your hips while continuing to keep your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the handles upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the handles.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
    2. If you lose resistance from the machine (from its mobile arms contacting its base beams) when your shoulders are fully depressed, you can set the handles down, elevate the seat height, and resume your set with resistance throughout the full range of motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates its handles slightly below the level of your hands when seated with your arms resting at your sides.
2. After selecting your desired amount of weight with the machine’s loading pin, sit down facing away from the machine with your feet flat on the floor.
3. With extended elbows, lean forward by hinging at your hips until your hands are low enough to grasp the handles firmly with a neutral grip such that your palms are facing in toward each other.
    1. While leaning forward, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back into a slouched posture.
4. Sit upright by moving about your hips while continuing to keep your elbows extended and your lower back near a neutral position.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion while keeping your elbows extended, which will lift the handles upward.
    1. While doing so, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend in a controlled manner through your available range of motion, which will lower the handles.
    1. At the end of this motion, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
    2. If you lose resistance from the machine (from its mobile arms contacting its base beams) when your shoulders are fully depressed, you can set the handles down, elevate the seat height, and resume your set with resistance throughout the full range of motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Connect to the lat pulldown cable an attachment with a pair of parallel handles that are approximately shoulder-width apart
    1. For this variation, you can use any neutral grip attachment that allows you to grip it with your hands about shoulder-width apart.
    2. If you do not have access to this type of attachment, you can select a similar lat pulldown variation (such as the neutral close grip cable lat pulldown or underhand grip cable lat pulldown) from this database to train the same muscles.
2. Select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
3. While facing the handles and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with a neutral grip, such that your palms face each other.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
4. While holding the handles, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
5. In the seated position, slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight backward lean throughout your set.
    2. From this position, your elbows should be straight, with your arms overhead and slightly forward (rather than completely vertical), and you should feel tension from the cable station holding your arms in this position.
    3. If you notice the cable remains slack in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), you may need to use a different attachment and a wider grip to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for your lat pulldown.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles down toward your upper chest by driving your elbows down toward your sides as you progressively bend them.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. Predominantly keep your elbows aligned with your wrists, rather than meaningfully flaring them out away from your sides.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which should result in your elbows finishing in line with your torso or slightly behind it at the bottom.
    4. Maintain the slight backward lean of your torso you adopted while setting up, rather than leaning further backward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the handles in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Connect to the lat pulldown cable an attachment with a pair of parallel handles that are outside shoulder-width apart.
    1. If you do not have access to this type of attachment, you can train the same muscles by selecting a similar lat pulldown variation from this database, such as the overhand wide grip cable lat pulldown, which can be performed with more common attachments.
2. Select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
3. While facing the bar and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with a neutral grip, such that your palms are directed in toward each other.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
4. While holding the bar, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
5. In the seated position, slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight backward lean throughout your set.
    2. From this position, your elbows should be straight with your arms situated overhead and slightly forward (rather than being completely vertical), and you should feel tension from the cable station holding your arms in this position.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar down toward your upper chest by driving your elbows down toward your sides as you progressively bend them.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. When pulling through your full range of motion, you should finish with the bar touching your upper chest or very close to doing so.
    3. Maintain the slight backward lean of your torso you adopted while setting up, rather than leaning further backward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the bar in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Connect a lat pulldown bar to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. If you do not have a standard lat pulldown bar attachment, which has a straight body and mildly angled ends, you can use a different attachment that lets you comfortably grasp it with an underhand wide grip.
2. While facing the bar and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the bar with an outside shoulder-width underhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
    1. As a general rule of thumb, you can place your index finger on the bar just outside where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing your standard underhand grip pulldown, which uses a near shoulder-width grip.
    2. Do not grasp the bar wider than feels comfortable to you because an excessively wide underhand grip may provoke discomfort in your wrists, elbows, or shoulders.
    3. Feel free to try out mildly different outside shoulder-width grips to determine if any particular position feels best to you.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
3. While holding the bar, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
4. In the seated position, slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight backward lean throughout your set.
    2. From this position, your elbows should be straight, with your arms overhead and slightly forward (rather than completely vertical), and you should feel tension from the cable station holding your arms in this position.
    3. If you notice the cable remains slack in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), widening out your grip may be needed to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar down toward your upper chest by driving your elbows down toward your sides as you progressively bend them.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. Maintain the slight backward lean of your torso you adopted while setting up, rather than leaning further backward.
    3. When pulling through your full range of motion, you should finish with the bar touching your upper chest or very close to doing so.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the bar in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, straddle the machine by standing on the foot plates as you are facing toward the weights.
    1. This variation specifically uses a T-bar row machine without a chest pad, in contrast to other variations in the database that use chest-supported T-bar row machines.
    2. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
    3. The foot plates of most machines will require you to adopt a near hip-width stance.
    4. Position your feet in a manner that places the machine’s handles just in front of you so that you do not have to reach far forward in order to grasp the handles initially.
2. With your arms pointing down toward the handles, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handles. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
3. Firmly grasp the handles that are directed straight out to each side with a slightly outside shoulder width overhand grip such that your palms are directed backward.
    1. The handles that are available vary among different T-bar row machines.
    2. Various models have a pair of horizontally directed handles (which allow an overhand grip) that are contiguous with inner handles that angle out to the sides and backward (which allow a semi-neutral grip that is a hybrid between an overhand and neutral grip).
    3. This overhand grip variation specifically uses the horizontally directed handles, so you can select a different bent-over T-bar variation from this database if you wish to perform a T-bar row with a different grip.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from now on.
5. While keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and elbows straight, drive through your legs to lift the bar slightly up and assume a slightly more upright torso position.
    1. The objective is to use your hip and thigh muscles to lift the bar just high enough that you can experience resistance from the weight when your elbows are fully straight in the starting position of each rep.
    2. In this position, your torso should still be meaningfully inclined closer to horizontal than vertical, with your hips mostly bent.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides to a comfortable degree.
    2. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar to its initial position by bending your knees and hips to lower the bar.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, straddle the machine by standing on the foot plates as you are facing toward the weights.
    1. This variation specifically uses a T-bar row machine without any chest support, and other exercises using chest-supported T-bar row machines are included in this database.
    2. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
    3. The foot plates of most machines will require you to adopt a close to hip-width stance.
    4. Position your feet in a manner that places the machine’s handles just in front of you so that you do not have to reach far forward in order to grasp the handles initially.
2. With your arms pointing down toward the handles, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handles. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
3. Firmly grasp the outer handles that are angled out to the sides and backward with a semi-neutral grip (a hybrid between an overhand and neutral grip) such that your palms are directed at an angle backward and in toward each other.
    1. The handles that are available vary among different T-bar row machines.
    2. Various models have a pair of outer angled handles (which allow a semi-neutral grip) that are contiguous with inner horizontally directed handles (which allow an overhand grip), but certain models lack a pair of angled handles.
    3. This semi-neutral grip variation specifically uses the angled handles, so you can select a different bent-over T-bar variation from this database if you wish to perform a T-bar row with a different grip.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from now on.
5. While keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and elbows straight, drive through your legs to lift the bar slightly up and assume a slightly more upright torso position.
    1. The objective is to use your hip and thigh muscles to lift the bar just high enough that you can experience resistance from the weight when your elbows are fully straight in the starting position of each rep.
    2. In this position, your torso should still be meaningfully inclined closer to horizontal than vertical, with your hips mostly bent.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides to whichever degree you prefer and feel comfortable to you.
    2. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
    2. While you do not have to lower the bar extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the bar drop abruptly in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar to its initial position by bending your knees and hips to lower the bar.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, straddle the machine as you are facing toward the weights.
2. Place your feet onto the foot plates and your chest onto the chest pad such that your whole body is inclined forward.
    1. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
3. Depending upon your height and leg lengths, you may want to bend your knees somewhat or keep them extended to determine where your torso is situated on the chest pad.
    1. How you position your knees will be based on what feels most comfortable and stable to you, but you can initially see how you feel when positioning your knees to allow the uppermost aspect of the chest pad to be approximately level with your collarbone.
    2. Individuals who have longer legs will typically feel more comfortable when using a somewhat more bent knee position, while those with shorter legs will typically feel more comfortable with extended knees.
4. After determining which degree of knee bend places you in a comfortable position, firmly grasp the outer handles that are directed straight out to the sides with an underhand grip such that your palms are facing forward.
    1. The handles that are available vary among different T-bar row machines, and certain models have a pair of outer handles that are angled out to the sides and backward instead of a pair of handles that extend from the machine in a purely horizontal orientation.
    2. Handles that are angled out to the sides and backward are not suitable to grasp with an underhand grip row (due to the abnormally high mobility demands), so this underhand variation can only be practically performed on a machine that has handles that are directed horizontally straight outward.
    3. If your T-bar row machine lacks horizontally directed handles, you can perform another chest-supported machine T-bar row variation from this database, such as the neutral grip variation, which can train the same muscles as the underhand variation.
    4. Positioning your hands as close to shoulder width apart as you can will typically be most comfortable when rowing with an underhand grip, but various machine models have outer handles that are spaced out too far for many individuals to utilize a shoulder-width underhand grip.
    5. Rowing with a wider than shoulder-width underhand grip is not inherently problematic, but if using such a grip prompts you to feel discomfort in your shoulders, elbows, or wrists, you can readily select a chest-supported machine T-bar row variation with a different grip from this database.
5. Lift the bar up off the machine’s stopper, center the bar so that it no longer lies above the stopper, and lower it until your elbows are extended.
    1. In this bottom position, allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades apart from one another, which will likely prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in your upper back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides with this underhand grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wide overhand grip).
    2. Keep your torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set rather than arching your lower back to lift your chest up off the pad.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar back onto the machine’s stopper.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Slide a landmine attachment with horizontally directed handles onto the sleeve at the end of a barbell situated in a landmine attachment (or a viable substitute), and then add weight plates to the barbell.
    1. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    2. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
    3. The diameter of the plates you select can influence the range of motion of this exercise, so you can use smaller diameter plates if you wish to row through a longer range of motion.
    4. Plates weighing 45lb or 20kg (which differ slightly), along with bumper plates of various weights, have a diameter near 18in (45cm) that can physically constrain the range of motion for your row by contacting your torso at the end of the lifting phase or by contacting the ground at the end of the lowering phase.
2. Straddle the barbell by standing with the attachment’s handles just in front of you as your back is facing the landmine’s pivot point.
3. With your arms pointing down toward the handles, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handles. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
4. Firmly grasp the handles that are directed straight out to each side with a slightly outside shoulder width overhand grip such that your palms are directed backward.
    1. If the handles are long enough to accommodate a range of grip widths, grasp them with your hands slightly outside shoulder width.
    2. How far apart you can position your hands depends on your attachment model, since some attachments have fairly narrow handles that constrain you to a very limited grip width range.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from now on.
6. While keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and elbows straight, drive through your legs to lift the bar slightly up and assume a slightly more upright torso position.
    1. The objective is to use your hip and thigh muscles to lift the bar just high enough that you can experience resistance from the weight when your elbows are fully straight in the bottom position of each rep, rather than having the plates touch the ground.
    2. In this position, your torso should ideally remain inclined closer to horizontal than to vertical, with your hips mostly bent. However, large-diameter plates may require a more upright torso to keep them off the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides to a comfortable degree.
    2. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. If using large diameter plates, you may not be able to row as far backward compared to if you use smaller diameter plates.
    5. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar to its initial position by bending your knees and hips to lower the bar.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, straddle the machine by standing on the foot plates as you are facing toward the weights.
    1. This variation specifically uses a T-bar row machine without any chest support, and other exercises using chest-supported T-bar row machines are included in this database.
    2. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
    3. The foot plates of most machines will require you to adopt a close to hip-width stance.
    4. Position your feet in a manner that places the machine’s handles just in front of you so that you do not have to reach far forward in order to grasp the handles initially.
2. With your arms pointing down toward the handles, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handles. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
3. Firmly grasp the handles that are directed straight out to the sides with a near shoulder-width underhand grip, such that your palms face forward. 
    1. Different T-bar row machines have different handle designs, which can affect the specific grip width you can use.
    2. If your machine lacks purely horizontal handles or has handles that are too wide to row comfortably with an underhand grip, you can select a different bent-over T-bar variation from this database.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from now on.
5. While keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and elbows straight, drive through your legs to lift the bar slightly up and assume a slightly more upright torso position.
    1. The objective is to use your hip and thigh muscles to lift the bar just high enough that you can experience resistance from the weight when your elbows are fully straight in the starting position of each rep.
    2. In this position, your torso should still be meaningfully inclined closer to horizontal than vertical, with your hips mostly bent.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward while progressively bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides with this underhand grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished extending and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar to its initial position by bending your knees and hips to lower the bar.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Connect an attachment to the cable with a pair of parallel handles that are outside shoulder width apart, select your weight with the loading pin, and sit down facing the cable station.
    1. While you can use any attachment that allows you to use a neutral wide grip, this type of attachment is not quite as widely available as other attachments.
    2. If you do not have access to this type of attachment, you can train the same muscles by selecting a similar variation from this database, such as the seated overhand wide grip cable row, which can be performed with more common attachments.
    3. You can perform this exercise with essentially the same technique whether you use a seated cable row machine or an adjustable cable station, but a seated cable row machine is typically more stable and therefore the ideal option. 
    4. If you use a seated cable row machine, you naturally will sit down on its seat, but if you are using an adjustable cable station, you will sit down on the ground after adjusting the cable to a height that is approximately level with your shoulders when seated on the floor with an upright torso.
2. Position your feet in contact with the machine’s foot plates or the base of the adjustable cable station.
    1. Allow your knees to bend to an angle that allows you to feel stable and comfortable, which can be a mild to moderately mild knee bend for many people.
    2. With greater knee bend, you will be able to sit closer to the cable, so you can bend your knees further if you initially cannot reach the handles when leaning forward by bending at your hips.
3. Lean forward at your hips until you are able to grasp the bar’s parallel handles firmly with straight elbows and a neutral grip, such that your palms face each other.
4. While holding the bar with straight elbows, lean your torso backward at your hips until you are sitting with an upright torso.
    1. When seated upright, you should feel tension from the cable, so if the cable is still slack, you can extend your knees to scoot yourself backward until the machine’s weight stack separates and you feel the resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar back toward your torso by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, rather than trying to keep them tucked close to your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a closer grip).
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion or until the bar is directly in front of your torso, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten to move the bar forward.
    1. Finish with your arms fully outstretched in front of you.
    2. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, without rounding your lower back. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you to train them more effectively.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your traps, rhomboids, and rear delts while also training your biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. Connect an attachment with a straight bar shaft to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and sit down facing the cable station.
    1. You can use any attachment that allows you to hold it comfortably with an overhand wide grip.
    2. The most commonly available attachment appropriate for this exercise is a standard lat pulldown bar attachment, which has a straight shaft with mildly angled ends and may be found attached to a cable lat pulldown machine.
    3. You can perform this exercise with essentially the same technique whether you use a seated cable row machine or an adjustable cable station, but a seated cable row machine is typically more stable and therefore the ideal option. 
    4. If you use a seated cable row machine, you naturally will sit down on its seat, but if you are using an adjustable cable station, you will sit down on the ground after adjusting the cable to a height that is approximately level with your shoulders when seated on the floor with an upright torso.
2. Position your feet in contact with the machine’s foot plates or the base of the adjustable cable station.
    1. Allow your knees to bend to an angle that allows you to feel stable and comfortable, which can be a mild to moderately mild knee bend for many people.
    2. With greater knee bend, you will be able to sit closer toward the cable, so you can bend your knees further if you initially cannot reach the bar when leaning forward by bending at your hip
3. Lean forward at your hips until you are able to grasp the bar firmly with straight elbows. Grasp it with an overhand wide grip, such that your palms are clearly outside shoulder width while facing down.
    1. The appropriate grip width for this upper-back focused variation should naturally let your elbows flare prominently out away from your sides. At the end of your row, this should result in your upper arms being closer to forming a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from behind, rather than being closer to being tucked by your sides (which can naturally occur with a closer grip).
    2. To find a viable wide grip based on your typical grip, you can place your index fingers on the bar where you would otherwise place your little fingers when performing your standard overhand grip row.
4. While holding the bar with straight elbows, lean your torso backward at your hips until you are sitting with an upright torso.
    1. When seated upright, you should feel tension from the cable, so if the cable is still slack, you can extend your knees to scoot yourself backward until the machine’s weight stack separates and you feel the resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar back toward your torso by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, rather than trying to keep them tucked close to your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a closer underhand or neutral grip).
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion or until the bar is directly in front of your torso, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten to move the bar forward.
    1. Finish with your arms fully outstretched in front of you.
    2. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, without rounding your lower back. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you to train them more effectively.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your traps, rhomboids, and rear delts while also training your biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable above the height of your shoulders when standing, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. Whether you position the cable to its highest setting or only moderately above your shoulder height is up to your preference.
    2. As an alternative to using an adjustable cable station, you can also perform this exercise with the same technique on a cable lat pulldown machine.
2. Stand facing in front of the cable and slightly to the side such that one of your shoulders is in line with the cable.
3. With your arm that is in front of the cable, grasp the handle firmly with an extended elbow and your arm elevated to be aligned with the cable before stepping backward until the machine’s weight stack separates and you feel the resistance.
4. Mildly bend your hips and knees by sitting your buttocks somewhat backward with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart to assume a stable stance.
    1. In this position, the center of your breastbone should be facing the same way as the center of your hips, rather than having your trunk rotated toward one side.
5. Based on your preference and what feels most comfortable, you can rotate the handle to use an underhand grip with your palm facing up, an overhand grip with your palm facing down, or a neutral grip with your palm facing in toward the midline of your body.
    1. People often find that the neutral grip feels most natural.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handle back toward your torso by driving your elbow simultaneously downward and backward while progressively bending your elbow.
    1. Move your elbow through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping it tucked close to your side with this variation rather than notably flaring your elbow up and away from your side.
    2. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    3. Keep your forearm aligned in the same direction as the cable, which will naturally occur with the appropriate degree of elbow bend.
    4. Your shoulder blade will naturally move backward with your elbow.
    5. Movement should occur at your shoulder, shoulder blade, and elbow, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position as the center of your breastbone faces the same direction throughout the set.
    6. The cable will impart a rotational force to your trunk that your core muscles will resist to prevent your trunk from twisting during the row.
2. Reverse the movement by bringing the handle toward the cable station in a controlled manner by allowing your elbow to move forward and upward while it progressively extends until the handle has returned to the starting position.
    1. Your shoulder blade will naturally move forward with your elbow.
    2. At the end of this motion, your elbow should be in an extended position, and your arm should be aligned in the same direction as the cable.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a low incline angle (e.g., near 30 degrees) and set a pair of dumbbells under the head of the bench.
    1. You can alternatively set your bench at a moderate incline (e.g., near 45 degrees) if your bench lacks a low incline setting, or if you personally prefer to use a mildly greater incline.
    2. The dumbbells may be easiest to grasp while lying on the bench if you initially place one end of each dumbbell on top of the bench’s horizontal base leg (which is where wheels may be located) and the other end of each dumbbell on the floor closer to where you will be on the bench.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
3. Grasp one dumbbell at a time, position your arms so that they are perpendicular to the ground with extended elbows, and direct your palms backward to utilize an overhand grip.
    1. The bony bump on the back of each elbow should point directly out to the side (which is the direction you will row), rather than pointing backward.
    2. To be able to row through a full range of motion, the dumbbells should not be in contact with the ground or the base of the bench, which may be more challenging to avoid for individuals with longer arms.
    3. To create greater clearance between the dumbbells and the ground, you can scoot further up toward the head of the bench.
    4. If it is difficult for you to grasp the dumbbells, which may be more likely if you have shorter arms, you can initially place the dumbbells closer to the bench’s seat before setting up with your torso scooted further down on the bench to situate your hands closer to the floor. After grasping the dumbbells, you can then press your feet into the ground to scoot further up on the bench so that your head can hang off the bench rather than having your face pressed into the head of the bench.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows straight out to your sides and upward in an arcing motion to row the dumbbells up. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. To focus on your upper back, emphasize flaring your elbows straight out from your sides so your upper arms and torso form a “T-shape” at the top of the row. 
    2. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being approximately level with your shoulders at the end of the lifting phase.
    3. Keep your palms facing backward and torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    4. When allowing your elbows to bend naturally, your forearms should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    5. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells inward in a controlled arcing motion until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Allow the weights to pull your shoulder blades apart and forward to stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. Avoid tucking your elbows back toward your sides to keep the emphasis on your upper back.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weights pull your arms down rapidly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to place the dumbbells back onto the ground.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your traps, rhomboids, and rear delts while also training your biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. Position a barbell in the J-cups of a power rack set approximately to the height of your hips while standing, or alternatively position a Smith machine’s bar to the same height.
    1. This exercise can be performed with the same technique whether you use a barbell and power rack or a Smith machine.
    2. If you use a power rack, situate the barbell snugly in contact with the back corner of the J-cups since the middle portion of certain J-cups can be wide enough to allow the barbell to roll forward or backward, which is undesirable for this exercise.
2. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your back facing the back of the power rack/Smith machine and firmly grasp the bar with your hands clearly outside of shoulder width using an overhand grip that results in your palms facing down.
    1. Specifically how wide you grasp the bar is up to your preference and what feels comfortable to you.
3. While holding the bar, walk your feet forward to recline your torso into a more horizontal position until your chest is underneath the bar. Simultanesouly allow your elbows to straighten, and bridge your hips up by squeezing your glutes.
    1. In the bridged position, which you will maintain throughout your set, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. For the standard technique, you will walk your feet forward until you can fully extend your knees with the back of your heels on the ground, while the soles of your feet are off the ground and facing forward.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your chest toward the bar by driving your elbows simultaneously downward and backward while progressively bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, rather than trying to keep them tucked close to your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a closer underhand grip).
    2. Pull through your full range of motion while aiming for your chest to reach the bar.
    3. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your torso and lower body should remain nearly stationary in the bridged position that is akin to holding a reverse plank.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your chest move down away from the bar in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively straighten.
    1. Fully return to the starting position with your arms outstretched up in front of you.
    2. Continue keeping your knees, hips, and shoulders aligned.
    3. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally.
    4. While you do not have to lower yourself extremely slowly, you should actively resist the descent rather than abruptly dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your traps, rhomboids, and rear delts while also training your biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. Symmetrically elevate each end of a mobile flat bench on top of sturdy objects like aerobic steps with risers, bumper plates, plyometric boxes, or deadlift blocks. Elevate it to the level where you can lie face-down on the bench without your hands contacting the ground when your arms are hanging down on each side of the bench.
    1. If you have access to one, you can alternatively use a specific seal row bench, which is already elevated, but this is not a particularly common type of equipment.
    2. When elevating the bench, make sure that each base is level and that you stack the bench onto only objects that are able to handle the combined weight of you, your dumbbells, and the bench.
    3. Setting up this exercise can take more time than most row variations, and you can train your back muscles in a very similar manner with a shorter setup by performing the low incline chest-supported elbows-out dumbbell row%202ab5c6f170d880839baed369debc0459.md).
2. Place a pair of dumbbells on an elevated surface near the head of your bench in a position where you can grasp them while lying down on the bench.
    1. If there is sufficient space, you would ideally place the dumbbells onto the object you used to elevate the bench.
    2. Alternatively, you can situate the dumbbells on top of bumper plates (or something similar) that you place near the head of the bench in a position you can grasp while lying on the bench, but would not restrict your rowing motion.
    3. With a sufficiently low setup, you also may be able to position the dumbbells on the floor underneath the bench and still be able to pick them up, but this would not be feasible with a more elevated setup.
3. Lie down with the front of your torso and thighs flat on the bench in a position that allows your arms to hang straight down toward the floor without being obstructed by the objects you used to elevate the bench.
    1. Based on your preference, you can either keep your knees extended or bent.
    2. Position your head in a comfortable position, such as tucking your chin and lifting your head slightly up to keep your face off the bench.
4. Firmly grasp each dumbbell, and angle your palms diagonally backward and in toward each other while your arms are hanging down with straight elbows.
    1. Rather than just rotating your palm as your elbow and upper arm remain still, rotate your whole arm through shoulder motion, resulting in the bony bump on the back of each elbow pointing diagonally outward and backward. This is the direction in which you will row.
    2. Throughout your set, maintain this grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip and a neutral grip.
    3. To be able to row through a full range of motion, the dumbbells should not be in contact with anything in this position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows in an arcing motion backward, upward, and moderately out away from your sides to row the dumbbells. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Aim to flare your elbows about 45 degrees away from your sides as you move them equally backward and out to the side. Zero degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows purely backward and keeping them completely tucked by your sides, while 90 degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows straight out to the sides, which would result in your upper arms and torso forming a “T-shape” at the top.
    2. Keep your torso in contact with the bench throughout the set rather than arching your lower back to lift your chest up off the bench.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the top.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
    5. When allowing your elbows to bend naturally, your forearms should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    6. The dumbbells should move in an arcing motion rather than move straight up toward your shoulders.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled arcing motion until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Allow the weights to pull your shoulder blades apart and forward to stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. Move your elbows diagonally inward and forward at a near 45-degree angle while they descend to reverse the moderate elbow flare you just utilized during the lifting phase.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weights pull your arms down rapidly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells back down.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains much of the musculature of your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Connect a D-handle attachment to the lat pulldown machine’s cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the height of the thigh pad to a level that allows you to position your thigh snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Whether you use a machine with one cable or two cables will affect how you subsequently position yourself for the exercise.
    2. As an alternative to a lat pulldown machine, you can perform this exercise using the same technique with an adjustable cable station set to its highest setting, and you can sit on a mobile bench, a plyometric box, or the floor.
2. If you are using a machine with only one cable, sit with both feet on the same side of the seat, and press the thigh closest to the middle of the seat under the thigh pad.
    1. As opposed to keeping the center of your chest aligned with the cable (as you would when training both sides simultaneously), this asymmetrical sitting position allows you to pull down in line with the cable’s resistance.
    2. Lat pulldown machines often have pulleys that do not pivot side to side, and pulling their cables at an angle out to the side can create excessive friction, which is deleterious to the cables, while increasing the risk that a cable slips out of its pulley’s groove.
    3. If you are using a machine with two cables (which is less common than single-cable machines), you can sit symmetrically, as you normally would, with each thigh against the thigh pad.
3. With your arm closer to the cable, reach overhead to grasp the handle firmly with your elbow straight, then lean your torso slightly backward at the hips.
    1. If you are unable to grasp the handle from the seated position, you can stand up to grasp the handle before sitting back down.
    2. If you notice the cable remains slack in this position (which may occur for individuals who are tall and have long arms), you can lean back slightly further until you feel tension, thereby providing resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
    3. To assist with stabilizing your trunk, you optionally can use your free hand to hold onto the middle of the seat pad throughout your set.
4. Based on your preference and what feels most comfortable, you can rotate the handle to use a neutral grip with your palm facing in toward your midline or an underhand grip with your palm facing backward.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbow downward and then backward in an arcing manner while progressively bending it to pull the handle down toward the side of your upper chest.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade down away from your ear.
    2. Keep your forearm aligned in the same direction as the cable, which will naturally occur with the appropriate degree of elbow bend.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the pull.
    4. During the initial portion of the pull, you will drive your elbow downward, but you will drive your elbow simultaneously backward and downward during the second half of the pull.
    5. After the initial portion of the pull, when your upper arm is approximately parallel with the ground, the bony prominence on the back of your elbow should be pointing predominantly forward as your upper arm is situated in front of your body, rather than having your elbow and upper arm flared out to your side.
2. Reverse the movement by bringing the handle upward in a controlled manner by allowing your elbow to move forward and upward while it progressively extends until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Let your shoulder blade move naturally as it elevates with your arm.
    2. Finish with your elbow fully straightened and your arm overhead, aligned with the cable.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arm up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired weight onto the machine, adjust the seat or thigh pad height to a level that allows you to position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Some machine models have an adjustable seat, others have an adjustable thigh pad, and some have both.
    2. Depending on the specific model, plate-loaded lat pulldown machines may have the thigh pads positioned so you face toward or away from the weights, and these models often include a chest pad.
    3. The machines in which you sit facing toward the weights may be more likely to have handles angled in a manner that results in an underhand grip being uncomfortable and awkward to use, in contrast to machines where you face away from the weights, which often have handles well-suited for an underhand grip.
2. While facing the thigh pads and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with an underhand grip, with your palms facing backward.
    1. The grip width you can use will depend on the specific machine model you use, and you can feel free to select whichever grip width you prefer within the range your machine allows. An approximately shoulder-width grip will often be quite comfortable.
3. While holding the handles, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to extend in a controlled manner and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), you may need to lower the seat height to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down toward your sides while progressively bending them to pull the handles downward.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears.
    2. Keep your torso in the same position throughout your set rather than progressively leaning backward during the pull.
    3. During the initial portion of the pull, you will drive your elbows downward, but you will drive your elbows simultaneously backward and downward during the second half of the pull.
    4. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically leave your elbows level with or slightly behind your torso at the end of the pull.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the handles in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground and moderately bend your knees to a degree that lifts up the back of your legs off the ground but does not allow you to contact the ground with the soles of your feet.
    1. Compared to a standard glute bridge (where the soles of your feet would contact the ground), your knees will not be as bent with this hamstring bridge, so only the back of your heels will be in contact with the ground.
    2. You can situate your feet near shoulder-width apart from each other, but it is up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    3. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground until your hips are fully extended.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your gluteus maximus and three of your four hamstring muscles.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground and moderately bend your knees to a degree that lifts up the back of your legs off the ground but does not allow you to contact the ground with the soles of your feet.
    1. Compared to a standard glute bridge (where the soles of your feet would contact the ground), your knees will not be as bent with this hamstring bridge, so only the back of your heels will be in contact with the ground.
    2. You can situate your feet near shoulder-width apart from each other, with your toes pointing forward
    3. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
2. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift the other foot off the ground by marching that side’s thigh up while keeping your knee bent, or by straightening your knee while leaving that side’s thigh aligned with the thigh of your other leg.
    1. Either position for the free leg is similarly viable, so you can go with whichever you prefer, but keeping the knee straight may make the exercise mildly more challenging compared to keeping it bent.
    2. Keep one foot off the ground in the static position of your choice throughout the set you perform with your other side.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heel into the ground until your hip is fully extended.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your gluteus maximus and three of your four hamstring muscles.## Setting up
1. With an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip that results in your palms facing back toward you, firmly grasp a bar loaded with your desired weight and stand upright.
    1. In addition to a standard barbell to which you can add weight plates, you can also utilize a fixed-weight bar, which can be useful if you wish to use a load that is lighter than 45lb (20kg), which is the typical weight of an unloaded standard barbell.
2. After walking over to a flat exercise bench with the bar, lift the bar up slightly above your waist and sit down on the bench with the bar on your lap and enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space.
3. While keeping your elbows bent and the bar close to your torso, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. As you transition from sitting to lying down, you optionally can march your thighs up against the bar to help reposition yourself.
    2. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent, and you will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the bar up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a bench press rep), your wrist should be fairly straight rather than bent backward, and you should keep your wrist fairly straight throughout the exercise.
2. While keeping your elbows fairly straight, lower the bar back behind your head in a controlled arcing manner through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. You should avoid meaningfully bending your elbows, but you can allow them to bend mildly as your arms move through motion at your shoulders while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
    2. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back during the descent.
    3. People can often lower the bar until their upper arms are close to parallel with the ground, but how far you can descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    4. Once you feel that you have reached the end range to which your shoulders can safely stretch, you have reached the end range for you as an individual.
3. Reverse the movement by pulling your arms upward and forward in an arcing motion to lift the bar until you have returned to the starting position with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows fairly straight as you move your arms through shoulder motion while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your lats, teres major, and rear delt, which are assisted by the lowest portion of your pec major in the overhead position of each rep.## Setting up
1. Position a short, looped elastic band around both thighs.
    1. The band will provide the greatest resistance to your hip muscles if you place it just above your knees, while it will provide less resistance if you place it higher up your thighs.
    2. You can choose where you situate the band on your thighs based on the level of resistance you wish to experience, and if you are using a particular band for this exercise for the first time, you can adjust its placement during the set if you wish to change the difficulty after starting the set.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground or an exercise mat behind a sturdy object with an elevated surface, such as a flat exercise bench, aerobic step with risers, plyometric box, stairs, or bed.
    1. So long as you can position your feet on the object while still lying on the ground, a range of heights can be used for your object, but selecting something near knee height is typically appropriate.
3. Lift up your legs to position your feet on top of the elevated surface with your knees bent by at least 90 degrees.
    1. If you are too far away from the elevated surface to achieve this bent knee position, you can scoot forward until you can do so.
    2. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (by scooting toward the elevated surface) is up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    3. Based on what feels stable to you, you can either position the back of your heels on top of the elevated surface or position the middle of your soles on the corner of the elevated surface.
    4. You can situate your feet near shoulder-width apart from each other, but it is also up to your preference if you would like to position them somewhat closer together or wider apart.
    5. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can opt to point your toes forward or somewhat out to the sides.
    6. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Push your knees outward against the band’s resistance to have them in line with your feet (as opposed to letting the band pull your knees closer together), and maintain this knee position throughout your set.
    1. You will most likely feel tension in the muscles on the sides of your hips while doing so.
2. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your feet into the elevated surface and squeezing your buttock muscles.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glutes engage.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you can lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 while continuing to keep your knees aligned with your feet until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus while statically training your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and short hip external rotator muscles.## Setting up
1. Position a short looped elastic band around both of your legs.
    1. The band will provide the greatest resistance to your hip muscles if you place it at ankle level, while it will provide less resistance if you place it higher up on your legs.
    2. You can choose where you situate the band on your thighs based on the level of resistance you wish to experience, and if you are using a particular band for this exercise for the first time, you can adjust its placement during the set if you wish to change the difficulty after starting the set.
2. Lie down on your side with the side of your body that you are about to train facing up and your legs aligned with your torso with straight knees (like they would be when you are standing upright).
    1. In this side-lying position, you can rest the bottom side of your head on the hand of your bottom arm for comfort by situating your upper arm out to the side with your elbow bent.
    2. For extra stability, you can bend the elbow of your top arm and place that side’s hand on the ground in front of your torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your top leg straight upward through your hip’s available range of motion while keeping that side’s knee straight and the rest of your body stationary.
    1. Avoid rolling your hips backward or bringing your top leg forward toward your chest during this exercise, which are common technical errors.
    2. To help prevent your hips from rolling, you can imagine that you are wearing a belt with a buckle that has a laser beam shooting out of it. Strive to keep the laser beam pointing straight forward, parallel to the ground, throughout the movement.
    3. If you start to roll your hips backward, your belt buckle’s laser beam will start to angle upward.
    4. When you lift your top leg straight upward rather than also bringing that leg forward in front of you, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thigh through your hip to your torso when your top thigh is parallel with the ground.
    5. While performing this movement with the appropriate technique, you will likely feel the muscles on the side of your top hip contracting.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your top leg to descend in a controlled manner until your leg is low enough that the band becomes slack or until the inner aspect of your top foot contacts your bottom foot.
    1. If you lower your leg until beyond the point when the band becomes slack, you naturally will no longer be experiencing resistance from the band, but the weight of your top leg will still be resisting your hip muscles, so you have the option of deciding when you finish the rep.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side by lying on your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the musculature on the side of your hip, including your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia latae.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the kettlebells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hand while holding them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
    2. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    3. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    4. Pick up the kettlebells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
## Exercise technique
1. Walk forward while holding the kettlebells at your sides, maintaining an upright posture with your shoulders approximately level.
    1. While you will not swing your arms like you would when walking normally, your torso and lower body should look similar to how they would when you walk normally.
    2. Walk at a pace where you feel stable and balanced throughout the carry.
    3. If you use a weight that is fairly heavy to you, it is natural to take shorter strides compared to how you typically walk.
    4. Keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level throughout your set rather than letting them drop down to one side when taking each step.
2. Continue walking with the kettlebells until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebells down.
    1. Similar to the setup, it may be easiest to set the kettlebells down one at a time onto a flat exercise (or sturdy box) prior to picking up one kettlebell and reracking it, and then doing the same with the other kettlebell.
    2. The above approach can be particularly useful if you are rather fatigued after completing your set and could use a rest break prior to reracking the kettlebells.
## Description
Rather than concentrating the workload on one body region, this exercise trains a variety of muscle groups throughout your body in an integrated manner, including the muscles in your forearms, upper back, core, and lower body.## Setting up
1. To elevate the bar to your desired height, position a trap bar on top of a pair of deadlift blocks or a similarly sturdy substitute capable of safely withstanding the load you will use.
    1. If your trap bar has a pair of high handles and low handles, you can decide which handles to situate facing up based on the position in which you wish to start your trap bar block pull, which will also be influenced by the height of the blocks you select. It is generally recommended to use a block height that will position the handles you will use at least below your knees once you have placed the trap bar on top of the blocks.   
    2. Aerobic steps can often be used for block pulls while having the option to adjust the height by changing the number of risers you use, but you should be aware of the recommended weight limit if you opt to use heavy loads.
    3. The weight limit for aerobic steps will vary by brand, but some of the common brands recommend not exceeding 300lb (136kg) per step, which would result in a 600lb (272kg) limit when using a pair of steps for a block pull.
    4. The height of the aerobic step will also vary by brand, but the step itself is commonly near 4in (10cm) in height, with the risers being near 2in (5cm) in height.
    5. With thickness typically ranging from about 1-3.5in (2.5-8.9cm), depending on the weight and brand, bumper plates can also function as blocks that can be stacked on top of each other to provide a range of different heights.
2. Add weight plates to the trap bar, and stand within the middle of it with approximately a hip-width to shoulder-width stance and your arms at your sides.
    1. Specifically how wide you situate your feet apart can be based on what feels most comfortable to you when picking up a weight off the floor with a deadlifting motion.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from hinging at your hips than bending your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift moderately forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have fully sat back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    4. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and help engage your back muscles.
4. With your elbows fully straightened, firmly grasp the center of the handles with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hands while holding the bar, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. The initial submaximal pull allows you to check if you have grasped the handles correctly, which will be indicated by the handles not rotating forward or backward when applying the pre-tension.
    2. If you pick up a trap bar with your hands positioned slightly away from the middle of the handles, the trap bar can start rotating in a manner that makes it very difficult to hold and lift appropriately.
    3. Even for experienced lifters, it can be easy to grasp a trap bar’s handles without having their hands truly centered initially, so applying the pre-tension can make sure you are in your ideal starting position.
    4. If the bar starts rotating when you apply the pre-tension, re-center your grip, and apply the pre-tension again until the bar no longer rotates.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. Once the bar is moving, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
5. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the blocks.
    1. Just like when lifting the bar, keep your lower back flat and chest up as you descend while actively avoiding letting your lower back round or chest collapse.
    2. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees. Then you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the blocks for a moment (rather than simply tapping the blocks and immediately lifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the blocks for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the blocks rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this can meaningfully train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.

### Why Pull from Blocks Instead of the Floor?

When performing a low handle trap bar deadlift from the floor, your body proportions will determine the magnitude of hip flexion and knee flexion that is required in order to grasp the handles. Along with bumper plates, plates weighing 45lb or 20kg (which differ slightly) typically have a diameter near 45cm (17.7in) that result in the center of a bar (and a trap bar’s low handles) to which they are loaded being about 22.5cm (8.9in) above the ground. Based on differences in body proportions such as arm length relative to height, certain individuals may find that the handles at this level of elevation require deeper joint angles than they wish to train. In light of a particular person’s mobility, training goals, and injury history, the ideal starting height of any deadlift variation will vary among different individuals. For instance someone with short arms relative to his/her height and limited hip mobility may find pulling from the floor to be quite awkward, but elevating the handles mildly allows for a more comfortable starting position that feels better to that individual. Many trap models have a pair of high handles that allow for a lifter to pull from a height akin to a block pull, but some models lack such handles, so you can perform a trap bar block pull to replicate the high handle trap bar deadlift if you do not have access to a trap bar with high handles. Even when using a trap bar with high handles, a trap bar block pull can still serve a purpose because the height of the handles is fixed. The high handles of trap bars are commonly elevated 4in (10.2cm) above the low handles, but some models have slightly lower high handles, while others have high handles that are up to about 8in (20.3cm) above the low handles. Based on your specific trap bar, body dimensions, and training goals, high handle trap deadlifting from the floor may not provide quite enough handle elevation or it may provide excessive elevation. Correspondingly, you can adjust the starting position to your preference by determining the height of the blocks and which handles you use. 

### **Hip Hinging versus Squatting Your Trap Bar Deadlift**

Relative to conventional deadlifts or block pulls with a straight barbell, trap bar deadlifts can allow you greater liberty in how you perform them with respect to biasing more of a hip hinge or squat movement pattern. A conventional deadlift keeps the load in front of you and constrains forward knee travel, while a trap bar deadlift positions the load closer to your center of mass and permits pushing your knees farther forward than you would do during a conventional deadlift. Trap bar deadlifting with greater forward knee travel makes the movement more squat-like in nature compared to maintaining a closer to vertical shin position, which results in a comparatively more hip-hinge dominant movement. As discussed by Greg Nuckols in his Stronger By Science article “Trap Bar Deadlifts are Underrated”, squat and deadlift variations can be analyzed as existing along a “squat-hinge continuum” based on the demands they impart to the hip extensor muscles (e.g., gluteus maximus, hamstrings, adductor magnus) relative to the quads, which extend the knees. Compared to trap bar deadlifting with a vertical shin, the lever arm of the resistance acting on the knees is greater when deadlifting with your knees situated farther forward, resulting in a greater ratio of loading imparted to the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. In contrast, the lever arm of the resistance acting on the hips is greater when deadlifting with vertical shins, which prompts you to sit your hips farther back and experience a lower ratio of loading imparted to the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. While people tend to perform trap bar deadlifts with their knees situated farther forward than when they conventional deadlift, you certainly have the ability to trap bar deadlift with the same knee position you would use for conventional deadlifts. Correspondingly, you can trap bar deadlift with whichever technique you prefer in congruence with your training goals.

Based on research assessing participants using their self-selected technique (which likely is the technique in which they were most efficient for pulling), it is worth noting that low handle trap bar deadlifts have been found to be more hip-dominant than knee-dominant. Swinton et al. (2011) specifically assessed male powerlifters performing low handle trap bar deadlifts and conventional dreadlifts. Both exercises imparted meaningfully greater loading to the hip extensors and lumbar spinal extensors than the quads, but conventional deadlifts were relatively more hip dominant. When lifting 80% of the participants’ conventional deadlift one-rep maxes, the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee was 3.7 for conventional deadlifts and 1.8 for low handle trap bar deadlifts. This difference was mainly attributed to the low handle trap bar deadlift having greater peak resistive torque acting on the knee, which was 190% of the conventional deadlift. A smaller gap was present for hip extensor loading as the peak resistive torque acting on the hip for the low handle trap bar deadlift was 92% that of conventional deadlift. These findings indicate that the degree to which low handle trap bar deadlifts train the quads more than conventional deadlifts most likely exceeds the degree to which conventional deadlifts train the hip extensor muscles more than low handle trap bar deadlifts. In terms of which hip extensor muscles may be less involved during trap bar deadlifts, the biarticular hamstrings in particular will likely receive a lesser stimulus. Camara et al. (2016) and Andersen et al. (2018) both found the hamstring’s biceps femoris to exhibit lower EMG activity during the low handle trap bar deadlift compared to the conventional deadlift, which is congruent with what be anticipated in light of the mechanical differences between these two exercises. Since the trap bar deadlift imparts greater loading to the knee, the knee flexion torque that results from hamstring contraction will have more of a deleterious effect on performance than during the conventional deadlift. While the hip extension torque that the hamstrings produce is beneficial, their knee flexion torque will oppose the quads as they extend the knee, so the hamstrings are not the most efficient hip extensor muscles for the nervous system to recruit during an exercise that imparts meaningful resistance to the quads. To a lesser extent, this is the same principle that results in the hamstrings experiencing minimal hypertrophy after interventions of squatting, which imparts even greater loading of the quads relative to the hip extensor muscles. Accompanying the lower hamstring EMG activity,  Camara et al. (2016) found the quad’s vastus lateralis (which was not assessed by Andersen et al.) to exhibit greater activity with the trap bar deadlift compared to the conventional deadlift. In contrast to the difference in hamstring EMG activity, Andersen et al. (2018) also found the gluteus maximus (which was not assessed by Camara et al.) to exhibit similar EMG activity during the two deadlift variations, which makes sense given that the gluteus maximus is able to generate hip extension without simultaneously generating knee flexion torque.

While the previously discussed data indicates that low handle trap bar deadlifts are typically performed with a more squat-like technique than conventional deadlifts, it is natural to wonder how they may compare to back squats. Choe et al. (2021) did not assess trap bar deadlifts, but they studied conventional deadlifts and back back squats performed with 70-85% of the mixed sex participants’ one-rep maxes. The researchers found the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee to be 3.0 for conventional deadlifts and 1.4 for squats. The participants of this study had some squat and deadlift training experience as the males had average deadlift and squat maxes that were respectively 143.3kg and 122.7kg, while the females had average deadlift and squat maxes that were respectively 95.0kg and 88.0kg. However, they were not nearly as strong as the powerlifter participants partaking in the study from Swinton et al. (2011) who had average conventional deadlift and low handle trap bar deadlift maxes that were respectively 244.5kg and 265.0kg. The participants from Swinton et al. (2011) correspondingly most likely used more efficient techniques, which may explain why they exhibited a higher ratio (3.7 versus 3.0) of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee when conventional deadlifting relative to the participants from Choe et al. (2021). When comparing the low handle trap bar deadlift findings from Swinton et al. (2011) to the back squat findings from Choe et al. (2021), the back squat was more knee dominant than the low handle trap bar deadlift, but the previously mentioned hip to knee torque ratio difference (1.7 versus 1.4) was not particularly pronounced.

This data suggests that the ratio of hip to knee loading of the low handle trap bar may be more similar to back squats than conventional deadlifts, but the strengths of our conclusions are limited when comparing findings from different studies assessing meaningfully different participants. For the most valid comparison of trap bar deadlifts to back squats, we ideally would want to analyze the findings from a study that assessed the same participants performing both exercises, which is what Stahl et al. (2024) precisely investigated. The researchers specifically compared the high handle trap bar deadlift (permitting chalk use but not straps) to the back squat (performed until thighs were parallel with the floor) as moderately trained mixed-sex participants lifted loads up to 100% of their maxes. They found the ratio of peak resistive torque acting on the hip to the peak resistive torque acting on the knee to be 0.9 for high trap bar deadlifts and 1.4 for squats (which was the same value determined by Choe et al.), indicating that high handle trap bar deadlifts can be even more knee-dominant than back squats. This difference was influenced by the resistive lever arm acting on the hip during back squats exceeding that during high trap bar deadlifts, while the resistive lever arm acting on the knee was similar between the two exercises. Comparing the data from Stahl et al. (2024) to the data from Swinton et al. (2011), high handle trap bar deadlifts appear to be more knee-dominant than low handle trap bar deadlifts with self-selected techniques, while back squats fall in between these two variations along the spectrum of hip to knee loading.

With that said, it is important to reiterate that you have the ability to perform trap bar deadlifts with either handle height in a more hip-dominant fashion if you wish to do so. Furthermore, it is also worth emphasizing that the aforementioned findings do not mean that high handle trap bar deadlifts are superior to back squats for training the quads. While the knee to hip loading ratios are relevant when appraising the ability of a deadlift or squat variation to train particular muscle groups, the capacity of an exercise to induce muscle hypertrophy is multi-factorial in nature. Multiple studies involving back squat training interventions have found back squats to induce significant quad growth, but the effect that high handle or low handle trap bar deadlifts may have on quad development has yet to be studied.

### **Trap Bar Deadlift versus Conventional Deadlift Strength**

Two different studies from Lockie et al. (2018) have compared one rep maxes without straps between conventional deadlifts and trap bar deadlifts with 4-inch (10.2cm) higher handles. Together they assessed a total of 54 mixed sex participants with at least two years of lifting experience that included conventional and trap bar deadlifts in addition to hitting maxes. Pooling the data from the two studies together, the average high handle trap bar max was 12% greater than the conventional deadlift max, resulting in a 39lb (17.7kg) mean difference. When comparing 1-rep maxes of the conventional deadlift to the low handle trap bar deadlift, Camara et al. (2016) found the maxes to be nearly identical to each other for the two lifts, but Swinton et al. (2011) and Lake et al. (2017) found their participants to lift an average of 6-8% greater loads during the low handle trap bar deadlift.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your lower body and spinal extensor muscles. As a simple approach you can use if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure that you are using the same technique for each condition, which will mean using the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep performance between these conditions on a second day where you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps prior to the set using straps on the first day, you perform the set using straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue resulting from the first set may influence the second set. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    4. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide upward during the dead hang, which pulls on your hand skin in a manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, bend your knees to lift your heels up off the ground while keeping the balls of your feet and your toes in contact with the ground.
2. While supporting some of your bodyweight through your feet on the ground, hang on the bar to support the rest of your bodyweight.
    1. Based on the proportion of your bodyweight you wish to use for your dead hang, you can adjust how much of your bodyweight you are supporting through your legs as opposed to your hands.
    2. A dead hang is a static exercise, so you will simply hang on the bar while not moving your body to perform it.
3. Continue hanging on the bar until you have completed your set’s target duration.
## Description
While simultaneously improving overhead shoulder mobility, this exercise can increase your grip strength by training your forearm and hand muscles that function as finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Stand under a pull-up bar with parallel handles that are closer than shoulder-width apart.
    1. While not necessarily rare for multi-grip pull-up bars to have, these parallel handles that allow you to use a neutral close grip are not as commonly available as parallel handles that enable you to use a moderate-width neutral grip (i.e., approximately shoulder width).
    2. If you lack a pull-up bar with close parallel handles, you can select from this database the neutral grip pull-up that uses a moderate grip width to train the same muscle groups as would be trained by this close grip variation.
2. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the close parallel handles with your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
3. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will place you in a more advantageous position to start your pull compared to if you kept your chest facing exclusively horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up from the force of your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back places you into a position akin to a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending them.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.

### **How Your Grip and Pull-Up Technique Influence Back Muscle Development**

When repeatedly performing this exercise over time, you can feel free to try out mildly different grip widths to determine if any particular position feels most comfortable to you and/or allows you to perceive your target back muscles working particularly well. Pull-ups can be performed with a variety of hand grips, including different grip widths and overhand, underhand (resulting in the exercise being called a chin-up), neutral, or hybrid (i.e., a combination of neutral and underhand or overhand) grips. Additionally, shoulder motion during a pull-up can occur predominantly in the sagittal (front-to-back) plane, the frontal (side-to-side) plane, or a combination of the two. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the sagittal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out in front of you), the upper arms remain parallel to each other and do not deviate away from the mid-line of your body. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the frontal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out to your sides, such as with a snow angle motion), the upper arms move away from the mid-line of your body until they are parallel to the ground. Then they move closer to the mid-line of your body with further elevation. During the lifting phase of a pull-up, the shoulders could extend in the sagittal plane, adduct in the frontal plane, or exhibit a combination of extension and adduction at a hybrid angle between the sagittal and frontal planes. Rather than moving the shoulders exclusively through one plane, pull-up variations are commonly performed with a technique that allows for the shoulders to move in between these two planes, but one plane of shoulder motion often makes a greater contribution than the other.

Muscles acting on the shoulder joint have different leverage to generate extension torque in the sagittal plane versus adduction torque in the frontal plane, and the nervous system is most likely to recruit the particular muscles that are best leveraged to produce the intended joint action during any active movement. Correspondingly, the plane of motion in which the shoulders move during a pull-up can affect how well different muscles are likely to be trained. With any particular grip for a pull-up, you can intentionally rotate your shoulders in a manner that allows you to move your shoulder relatively more in the sagittal or frontal plane. However, most people will typically find that a particular path of shoulder motion feels most comfortable and stable to their shoulders, elbows, and wrists when using a specific grip. For instance, people will normally perform a wide overhand grip pull-up with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the frontal plane. In contrast, they will normally perform a close-grip chin-up (using an underhand grip) with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the sagittal plane. Performing either variation in the alternative plane of motion would require greater-than-normal rotational range of motion in the shoulders and forearms, and attempting to do so could feel very awkward and uncomfortable. People will typically feel most comfortable when positioning themselves so that, at the point in the pull-up when their upper arms are parallel to the ground, their elbows and wrists are vertically aligned or their elbows are slightly lateral (outside) to their wrists.

In general, widening a grip facilitates relatively more frontal plane shoulder motion, while narrowing a grip facilitates relatively more sagittal plane shoulder motion. An overhand, underhand, or neutral grip may be used with any grip width. Still, the combination of these grip variables will influence comfort and which plane of shoulder motion feels most natural. When pulling predominantly in the sagittal plane with a rather close grip, an underhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable than an overhand grip. Similarly, when pulling predominantly in the frontal plane with a rather wide grip, an overhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable and allow more frontal-plane motion than an underhand grip. With a moderate-width grip, people may commonly find that pulling mostly in the sagittal plane feels natural with an underhand or neutral grip, but pulling relatively more in the frontal plane feels natural with an overhand grip. Fundamentally, the shoulder motion is what matters to the back muscles, and the grip is only relevant to the back muscles based on how it influences which shoulder motion is performed. When performing this overhand grip pull-up with a moderate grip width, you most likely will pull in a way that biases shoulder adduction somewhat more than shoulder extension. Still, you can also perform the exercise in a way that results in a fairly even balance between these two motions.

The lat and teres major in the back function both as primary adductors and extensors and are therefore highly likely to be trained effectively with pull-up variations regardless of the grip or plane of shoulder motion. However, the preponderance of available evidence indicates that the rear deltoid lacks meaningful leverage to contribute to shoulder adduction, but it clearly has favorable leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. The rear deltoid is correspondingly likely to be trained better with a closer grip emphasizing shoulder extension, which would be most comfortable when using an underhand or neutral grip. Alternatively, the middle and lower regions of the pectoralis major (that constitute the sternoclavicular head) have large moment arms to function as shoulder adductors, but only the lower portion exhibits a shoulder extension moment arm, and this moment arm is quite small and only present in flexed shoulder positions. Consequently, wider grip pull-ups (which are most comfortable with an overhand or neutral grip) that involve greater frontal-plane shoulder motion are likely to train the pectoralis major more effectively than closer grip pulldowns. Which regions of the lats are preferentially biased by a pull-up variation may also potentially be influenced by how much shoulder motion occurs in the frontal or sagittal planes. The middle and lower regions have relatively greater leverage to function as shoulder adductors, while the upper region of the lat has relatively greater leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. Plausibly, these biomechanical differences may facilitate the middle and lower regions of the lats being preferentially trained by wider grip pull-ups, while the upper region may be preferentially trained by closer grip pulling variations. With that said, the degree to which any preferential biasing may occur across different regions of the lats when comparing pull-up grips is more speculative and has not been tested in any longitudinal training interventions.

### Why Overhand Grip Pull-Ups may be More Difficult than Chin-Ups or Neutral Grip Pull-Ups

While exceptions exist, people often find overhand grip pull-ups to be more difficult per rep than chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. The discrepancy is most likely influenced by chin-ups and neutral grip pull-ups typically being performed with a technique that emphasizes shoulder extension in the sagittal plane, while overhand grip pull-ups are typically performed with relatively more shoulder adduction in the frontal plane. As demonstrated by studies such as those by Mayer et al. (1994) and Shklar and Dvir (1995), on average, maximal torque for shoulder extension exceeds that for shoulder adduction. The sample of participants, velocity, and type of muscle action tested can all influence the degree to which torque differs between these two actions, but maximal shoulder adduction torque has been measured at 63-90% of maximal shoulder extension, depending on the specific test conditions. In addition to the effect of how the shoulder moves, the forearm position can also affect how much elbow flexion torque you can generate during your pull. Guenzkofer et al. (2012) found that up to 15% greater elbow flexion torque can be generated when the forearm is in a supinated (used for an underhand grip) or neutral position compared to a pronated (used with an overhand grip) position. Together, these relationships suggest that most people can generate greater torque at the shoulders and elbows during chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups than during overhand grip pull-ups, which are likely to be more challenging when performing the same number of reps. Consequently, it is common not to be able to complete as many reps of overhand grip pull-ups in a set as you would be able to complete when performing chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. Nonetheless, individual differences in factors such as training history and the relative strength of different muscle groups can result in some people not noticing a difference in difficulty between these variations, or even finding overhand grip pull-ups to be somewhat easier per rep.

### Chin Over Bar?

The technique instructions do not specify where any particular part of your body should be situated in relation to the bar at the end of the lifting phase because your full range of motion can differ from someone else’s full range of motion. Pulling until your chin is above the bar is a common standard used for testing by the United States Marines and for competitions held by the World Pull-Up Organization, and you can certainly use it if you wish. However, when focusing on chin clearance, some people tend to extend their necks, lifting their chins, to clear the bar with less range of motion through the pull, compared to when they retain a neutral neck position. In this instance, focusing on the proxy for a full range of motion may result in a different movement than focusing on the individual's full range of motion. When competing or taking a standardized test that affects professional prospects, it is only sensible to do the minimum required to complete each rep per the rules, but training to build muscle and strength is a different endeavor.

### The Most Challenging Part of the Pull-Up

The end range of the lifting phase is typically the most challenging portion of pull-ups, in large part because the torque our pulling muscles can generate meaningfully varies across different joint angles. When the elbow is flexed to 120 degrees, Bober et al. (2002) found maximal elbow flexion torque to be approximately 35% of the peak torque that can be generated in a moderately flexed elbow position (i.e., near 60-75 degrees of elbow flexion). With your elbows approaching full flexion toward the end range of a pull-up, your elbow flexor muscles are correspondingly operating from a rather disadvantaged position to help you complete the rep. On average, maximal shoulder extension and adduction (a combination of which occurs during a pull-up) torques are also lower when the shoulder is mildly elevated (like at the end of a pull-up’s lifting phase) compared to when it is moderately elevated (like during the middle range of a pull-up). Together, these strength curves of the muscle groups acting on the elbow and shoulder can contribute to your pull-ups consistently feeling the most difficult during the last quarter of the lifting phase.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your back muscle strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your back muscles. As a simple approach, if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure you use the same technique for each condition, which means the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how the performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep's performance across these conditions on a second day, in which you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps on the first day, perform the set with straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue from the first set may influence the second. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting the performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up and firmly grasp it with your palms facing backward (i.e., an underhand grip) and your hands outside shoulder width apart.
    1. With a wide grip, your arms should form a “Y-shape” with your torso.
    2. Specifically, how wide you grasp the bar is up to your preference, but it should be wider than the grip width you would use for a standard moderate grip chin-up.
    3. Do not grip the bar wider than feels comfortable to you because an excessively wide underhand grip may provoke discomfort in your wrists, elbows, or shoulders.
    4. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    5. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up onto a sturdy box, an aerobic step, or a bumper plate to place the bar within reach.
    6. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar than if you jumped up from the ground to reach it.
    7. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    8. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will place you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back puts you in a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending them.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows will not actually drive downward, but having this intent helps pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.

### **How Your Grip and Pull-Up Technique Influence Back Muscle Development**

When repeatedly performing this exercise over time, you can feel free to try out mildly different grip widths to determine if any particular position feels most comfortable to you and/or allows you to perceive your target back muscles working particularly well. Pull-ups can be performed with a variety of hand grips, including different grip widths and overhand, underhand (resulting in the exercise being called a chin-up), neutral, or hybrid (i.e., a combination of neutral and underhand or overhand) grips. Additionally, shoulder motion during a pull-up can occur predominantly in the sagittal (front-to-back) plane, the frontal (side-to-side) plane, or a combination of the two. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the sagittal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out in front of you), the upper arms remain parallel to each other and do not deviate away from the mid-line of your body. When symmetrically moving the shoulders strictly in the frontal plane (like would occur when lifting your arms straight out to your sides, such as with a snow angle motion), the upper arms move away from the mid-line of your body until they are parallel to the ground. Then they move closer to the mid-line of your body with further elevation. During the lifting phase of a pull-up, the shoulders could extend in the sagittal plane, adduct in the frontal plane, or exhibit a combination of extension and adduction at a hybrid angle between the sagittal and frontal planes. Rather than moving the shoulders exclusively through one plane, pull-up variations are commonly performed with a technique that allows for the shoulders to move in between these two planes, but one plane of shoulder motion often makes a greater contribution than the other.

Muscles acting on the shoulder joint have different leverage to generate extension torque in the sagittal plane versus adduction torque in the frontal plane, and the nervous system is most likely to recruit the particular muscles that are best leveraged to produce the intended joint action during any active movement. Correspondingly, the plane of motion in which the shoulders move during a pull-up can affect how well different muscles are likely to be trained. With any particular grip for a pull-up, you can intentionally rotate your shoulders in a manner that allows you to move your shoulder relatively more in the sagittal or frontal plane. However, most people will typically find that a particular path of shoulder motion feels most comfortable and stable to their shoulders, elbows, and wrists when using a specific grip. For instance, people will normally perform a wide overhand grip pull-up with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the frontal plane. In contrast, they will normally perform a close-grip chin-up (using an underhand grip) with shoulder motion predominantly occurring in the sagittal plane. Performing either variation in the alternative plane of motion would require greater-than-normal rotational range of motion in the shoulders and forearms, and attempting to do so could feel very awkward and uncomfortable. People will typically feel most comfortable when positioning themselves so that, at the point in the pull-up when their upper arms are parallel to the ground, their elbows and wrists are vertically aligned or their elbows are slightly lateral (outside) to their wrists.

In general, widening a grip facilitates relatively more frontal plane shoulder motion, while narrowing a grip facilitates relatively more sagittal plane shoulder motion. An overhand, underhand, or neutral grip may be used with any grip width. Still, the combination of these grip variables will influence comfort and which plane of shoulder motion feels most natural. When pulling predominantly in the sagittal plane with a rather close grip, an underhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable than an overhand grip. Similarly, when pulling predominantly in the frontal plane with a rather wide grip, an overhand or neutral grip will typically be more comfortable and allow more frontal-plane motion than an underhand grip. With a moderate-width grip, people may commonly find that pulling mostly in the sagittal plane feels natural with an underhand or neutral grip, but pulling relatively more in the frontal plane feels natural with an overhand grip. Fundamentally, the shoulder motion is what matters to the back muscles, and the grip is only relevant to the back muscles based on how it influences which shoulder motion is performed. When performing this overhand grip pull-up with a moderate grip width, you most likely will pull in a way that biases shoulder adduction somewhat more than shoulder extension. Still, you can also perform the exercise in a way that results in a fairly even balance between these two motions.

The lat and teres major in the back function both as primary adductors and extensors and are therefore highly likely to be trained effectively with pull-up variations regardless of the grip or plane of shoulder motion. However, the preponderance of available evidence indicates that the rear deltoid lacks meaningful leverage to contribute to shoulder adduction, but it clearly has favorable leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. The rear deltoid is correspondingly likely to be trained better with a closer grip emphasizing shoulder extension, which would be most comfortable when using an underhand or neutral grip. Alternatively, the middle and lower regions of the pectoralis major (that constitute the sternoclavicular head) have large moment arms to function as shoulder adductors, but only the lower portion exhibits a shoulder extension moment arm, and this moment arm is quite small and only present in flexed shoulder positions. Consequently, wider grip pull-ups (which are most comfortable with an overhand or neutral grip) that involve greater frontal-plane shoulder motion are likely to train the pectoralis major more effectively than closer grip pulldowns. Which regions of the lats are preferentially biased by a pull-up variation may also potentially be influenced by how much shoulder motion occurs in the frontal or sagittal planes. The middle and lower regions have relatively greater leverage to function as shoulder adductors, while the upper region of the lat has relatively greater leverage to function as a shoulder extensor. Plausibly, these biomechanical differences may facilitate the middle and lower regions of the lats being preferentially trained by wider grip pull-ups, while the upper region may be preferentially trained by closer grip pulling variations. With that said, the degree to which any preferential biasing may occur across different regions of the lats when comparing pull-up grips is more speculative and has not been tested in any longitudinal training interventions.

### Why Overhand Grip Pull-Ups may be More Difficult than Chin-Ups or Neutral Grip Pull-Ups

While exceptions exist, people often find overhand grip pull-ups to be more difficult per rep than chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. The discrepancy is most likely influenced by chin-ups and neutral grip pull-ups typically being performed with a technique that emphasizes shoulder extension in the sagittal plane, while overhand grip pull-ups are typically performed with relatively more shoulder adduction in the frontal plane. As demonstrated by studies such as those by Mayer et al. (1994) and Shklar and Dvir (1995), on average, maximal torque for shoulder extension exceeds that for shoulder adduction. The sample of participants, velocity, and type of muscle action tested can all influence the degree to which torque differs between these two actions, but maximal shoulder adduction torque has been measured at 63-90% of maximal shoulder extension, depending on the specific test conditions. In addition to the effect of how the shoulder moves, the forearm position can also affect how much elbow flexion torque you can generate during your pull. Guenzkofer et al. (2012) found that up to 15% greater elbow flexion torque can be generated when the forearm is in a supinated (used for an underhand grip) or neutral position compared to a pronated (used with an overhand grip) position. Together, these relationships suggest that most people can generate greater torque at the shoulders and elbows during chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups than during overhand grip pull-ups, which are likely to be more challenging when performing the same number of reps. Consequently, it is common not to be able to complete as many reps of overhand grip pull-ups in a set as you would be able to complete when performing chin-ups or neutral grip pull-ups. Nonetheless, individual differences in factors such as training history and the relative strength of different muscle groups can result in some people not noticing a difference in difficulty between these variations, or even finding overhand grip pull-ups to be somewhat easier per rep.

### Chin Over Bar?

The technique instructions do not specify where any particular part of your body should be situated in relation to the bar at the end of the lifting phase because your full range of motion can differ from someone else’s full range of motion. Pulling until your chin is above the bar is a common standard used for testing by the United States Marines and for competitions held by the World Pull-Up Organization, and you can certainly use it if you wish. However, when focusing on chin clearance, some people tend to extend their necks, lifting their chins, to clear the bar with less range of motion through the pull, compared to when they retain a neutral neck position. In this instance, focusing on the proxy for a full range of motion may result in a different movement than focusing on the individual's full range of motion. When competing or taking a standardized test that affects professional prospects, it is only sensible to do the minimum required to complete each rep per the rules, but training to build muscle and strength is a different endeavor.

### The Most Challenging Part of the Pull-Up

The end range of the lifting phase is typically the most challenging portion of pull-ups, in large part because the torque our pulling muscles can generate meaningfully varies across different joint angles. When the elbow is flexed to 120 degrees, Bober et al. (2002) found maximal elbow flexion torque to be approximately 35% of the peak torque that can be generated in a moderately flexed elbow position (i.e., near 60-75 degrees of elbow flexion). With your elbows approaching full flexion toward the end range of a pull-up, your elbow flexor muscles are correspondingly operating from a rather disadvantaged position to help you complete the rep. On average, maximal shoulder extension and adduction (a combination of which occurs during a pull-up) torques are also lower when the shoulder is mildly elevated (like at the end of a pull-up’s lifting phase) compared to when it is moderately elevated (like during the middle range of a pull-up). Together, these strength curves of the muscle groups acting on the elbow and shoulder can contribute to your pull-ups consistently feeling the most difficult during the last quarter of the lifting phase.

### **Straps**

If you find that your grip strength functions as the limiting factor as opposed to your back muscle strength when performing this exercise, you can utilize chalk or straps in an effort to amplify the stimulus each set provides your back muscles. As a simple approach, if you are unsure whether grip strength is limiting your performance, you can perform a set of as many quality reps as you can with a particular weight without using straps and compare your rep total to a set using straps. To perform an appropriate comparison, you would want to ensure you use the same technique for each condition, which means the same range of motion and tempo. Additionally, you would want to perform each condition when you are similarly recovered. Practically, resting at least three minutes between the two sets should allow most fatigue to dissipate by the time you perform the second set, therefore enabling a useful comparison in terms of how the performance of this exercise may be affected by your grip strength. However, if you really wish to optimize the validity of your self-assessment, you can compare your rep's performance across these conditions on a second day, in which you reverse the order of the conditions. If you performed the set without straps on the first day, perform the set with straps before the set without straps on a different day to control for how any fatigue from the first set may influence the second. When comparing how many reps you complete during each condition, you will be well informed as to how grip strength may be affecting the performance of this exercise for you as an individual.## Setting up
1. Stand under a pull-up bar with parallel handles that are spaced outside shoulder width.
    1. While not necessarily rare for multi-grip pull-up bars to have, these parallel handles that allow you to use a neutral wide grip are not as commonly available as parallel handles that enable you to use a moderate width neutral grip (i.e., approximately shoulder width apart).
    2. If you lack a pull-up bar with wide parallel handles, you can select from this database the “overhand wide grip pull-up,” which would be the most similar variation in terms of which muscles are most effectively trained.
2. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the wide parallel handles with your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
3. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will place you in a more advantageous position to start your pull compared to if you kept your chest facing exclusively horizontally and allowed your shoulderblades to be shrugged up from the force of your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back places you into a position akin to a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.

### **How Your Grip and Pull-Up Technique Influence Back Muscle Development**

