3. Once your chest and thighs are resting on the ground, lift your hands just slightly off the ground by driving your elbows up toward the ceiling.
    1. While you lift your hands up through the same shoulder motion that occurs at the end of a row, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    2. This is the same shoulder motion that occurs during the lowering phase of the push-up.
4. Place your hands onto the ground in the same position they previously were.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
