    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This classic exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your front delts, triceps, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
    1. Your elbows should be stacked under your shoulders with your arms pointing straight down toward the ground.
    2. For comfort, you optionally can set up on an exercise mat.
2. Situate your hands mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other, with your fingers spread somewhat apart from each other.
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon which hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a quick but controlled manner until it is just (i.e., about one inch (2.5cm)) above the ground.
    1. Let your elbows flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you. This commonly results in the upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with the torso (when viewed from above) at the bottom of the push-up. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are fully tucked to your sides, remaining shoulder-width the whole rep, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows point straight out to your sides, letting your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” at the bottom instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
3. Immediately after reaching the bottom, forcefully push the ground away with a maximally explosive intent to launch yourself up as high as you can with your hands off the ground while keeping your feet on the ground.
    1. Your shoulders and elbows should simply reverse the motion that occurred during the descent while moving through the same path that you would during a standard push-up. Consequently, your hands should only lift off the ground after you have fully straightened your elbows, and the momentum generated by your explosive push is what lifts your hands up.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Without thinking about it, your shoulder blades will naturally spread apart as you push up while focusing on throwing the ground away from you.
    4. If you wish, you can clap your hands together after you have fully straightened your elbows, with your hands off the ground, but this is completely optional and requires a certain amount of air time to execute properly. Using the clapping maneuver is not recommended for individuals new to plyometric push-ups or who can only lift their hands slightly off the ground.
4. With your elbows slightly bent, land softly with your hands in the same position they just were (i.e., mildly outside shoulder width).
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Strive to transition fluidly between reps, moving from step 4 of one rep directly into step 2 of the next.
## Description
While developing horizontal pressing explosive strength, this advanced push-up variation can effectively train your pec major,  front delts, triceps, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Place a pair of push-up bars or hex dumbbells onto the ground, mildly outside shoulder-width apart, with their handles parallel to each other.
    1. While push-up bars will normally be more comfortable to use, the same movement can be trained whether you use push-up bars or hex dumbbells.
    2. If you use hex dumbbells, the size (which scales with weight) you select will affect how elevated your hands are above the ground. Ideally, your hands should be elevated high enough that you can descend through your full available shoulder range of motion without the ground restricting your push-up range of motion. For many people, dumbbells of a moderate size and weight will be sufficient to permit the desired full range, but individuals with a high degree of shoulder mobility may require dumbbells of a greater size and weight to do so.
2. With your knees and toes on the ground and your knees bent, grasp the handles with your palms facing in toward each other and your elbows straight.
    1. In this position, your elbows should be underneath the outer aspect of your shoulders rather than being in front of your shoulders (i.e., horizontally closer to your head) or behind your shoulders (i.e., horizontally closer to your hips).
    2. You should be holding the handles with mostly straight wrists and bearing weight predominantly through the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands).
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. How far you can descend depends on your individual shoulder mobility, but you should be able to reach the point where your chest is at least slightly lower than your hands if you have normal mobility.
    2. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    3. Let your elbows flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you. This commonly results in the upper arms forming approximately 30-60-degree angles with the torso (when viewed from above) at the bottom of the push-up. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your elbows are fully tucked to your sides, remaining shoulder-width the whole rep, while 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows point straight out to your sides, letting your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    4. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” at the bottom instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    5. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    6. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    7. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the handles to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your front delts, triceps, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Load an adjustable EZ bar with your desired weight or approach a fixed-weight EZ bar.
    1. Either type of EZ bar can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down and firmly grasp it with your palms angled backward and in toward each other (resulting in a semi-overhand grip) as your hands are about shoulder width apart or somewhat closer.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a shoulder-width or closer semi-overhand grip is accessible on the bar.
    2. Since a range of grip widths can be effectively used for this exercise, you can determine your specific grip width based on what feels most comfortable to you.
3. Stand upright with the bar, and walk over to a flat exercise bench before turning your back to the bench.
4. Lift the bar up slightly above your waist and sit down on the bench with the bar on your lap and enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space.
5. While keeping your elbows bent and the bar close to your torso, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. As you transition from sitting to lying down, you optionally can march your thighs up against the bar to help reposition yourself.
    2. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent, and you will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the bar up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a bench press rep), your wrist should be reasonably straight rather than bent backward, and you should keep your wrist reasonably straight throughout the exercise.
2. While keeping your elbows fairly straight, lower the bar back behind your head in a controlled arcing manner through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. You should avoid meaningfully bending your elbows, but you can allow them to bend mildly as your arms move through motion at your shoulders while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
    2. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back during the descent.
    3. People can often lower the bar until their upper arms are close to parallel with the ground, but how far you can descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    4. Once you feel that you have reached the end range to which your shoulders can safely stretch, you have reached the end range for you as an individual.
3. Reverse the movement by pulling your arms upward and forward in an arcing motion to lift the bar until you have returned to the starting position with your arms directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows fairly straight as you move your arms through shoulder motion while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your lats, teres major, and rear delt, which are assisted by the lowest portion of your pec major in the overhead position of each rep.## Setting up
1. Position a pair of deadlift blocks (or a similarly sturdy substitute capable of safely withstanding the load you will use) on the ground far enough apart from each other that the weight plates on a bar will be able to rest fully on the blocks in a stable position.
    1. For this block pull variation, your blocks should be of a height that situates the bar close to slightly below your knees when the loaded bar is resting on the blocks. This results in a moderate height block pull with a somewhat greater elevation than the mid-shin block pull also present in this database.
    2. To appropriately position the blocks, it can be helpful to have a barbell on the ground and align the blocks about the same distance apart from each other as is the length of the barbell’s shaft (which is the middle part of the bar in between the two thick circular collars).
    3. Aerobic steps can often be used for block pulls while having the option to adjust the height by changing the number of risers you use, but you should be aware of the recommended weight limit if you opt to use heavy loads. The weight limit for aerobic steps will vary by brand, but some of the common brands recommend not exceeding 300lb (136kg) per step, which would result in a 600lb (272kg) limit when using a pair of steps for a block pull. The height of the aerobic step will also vary by brand, but the step itself is commonly near 4in (10cm) in height, with the risers being near 2in (5cm) in height.
    4. With thickness typically ranging from about 1-3.5in (2.5-8.9cm), depending on the weight and brand, bumper plates can also function as blocks that can be stacked on top of each other to provide a range of different heights.
    5. It may be worthwhile to try out different block heights to determine which height places you in your intended starting position. However, you can initially try out a pair of approximately 6-8in (15-20cm) deadlift blocks, aerobic steps with 1-2 pairs of risers, or a pair of two thick 45lb (20kg) bumper plates stacked on top of each other. Your individual body proportions will dictate where the bar is specifically positioned relative to your knees, but the aforementioned set-up options should situate a bar loaded with large diameter plates (e.g., 45lb (20kg) plates or any jumper plates) reasonably close to slightly below the knees for many people and allow you to adjust the height as needed. If you are going to add only smaller diameter plates onto the bar, higher blocks will naturally be needed to position the bar at the appropriate height.
2. After placing a barbell onto the blocks and loading weight plates to the bar, stand upright in approximately a hip width stance close in front of the middle of the bar as the bar is over the middle of your feet with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly turned out to the sides.
    1. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
3. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while allowing your knees to bend until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your shins should remain close to being vertical, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward to bring your shins right next to the bar.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees a bit further to reach the bar.
    3. When hinging your hips properly, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings on the back of your thighs at the bottom of your hip hinge.
    4. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    5. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    6. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. Immediately after the bar begins lifting up, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. The “push the floor away” cue only applies to the very start of the rep since thrusting your hips forward is chiefly what will allow you to lift the weight during this variation.
    2. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    3. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while allowing your knees to bend to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the blocks.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track slightly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the blocks for a moment (rather than simply tapping the blocks and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the blocks for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferring between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the blocks.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position a power rack’s safety pins a little below the height of your knees when standing upright.
    1. Certain power racks will not allow you to position safety pins near this level, so this variation will not be feasible in every power rack. If your rack is not suitable for this variation, you can select the “conventional below-the-knee block pull” from this database to provide a similar training effect.
    2. If the closest safety pin setting of your rack situates the bar somewhat higher than your knees, you may be able to perform a below-the-knee block pull by elevating your feet like you would for a deficit deadlift. To do so, you can place a large diameter weight plate or deadlift block in the center of your rack, and you will stand on top of it to perform your rack pull from a closer to below-the-knee starting position. Depending on your preferred stance width, you can also position two plates or deadlift blocks directly next to each other if the soles of your feet do not fit fully onto one plate or deadlift block.
2. Place a barbell centered on top of the safety pins, and add your desired weight plates to it.
    1. Particularly as the load on the bar increases, rack pulls risk damaging a bar if you do not utilize a well-controlled lowering phase, which people often do not.
    2. Certain gyms have bars that were previously bent from rack pulls and are now specifically designated to be used for rack pulls to avoid damaging other bars.
    3. When using a well-controlled lowering phase during every rep, you most likely can avoid damaging the bar when performing a rack pull.
    4. If you do not feel confident in your ability to avoid damaging a bar when performing a rack pull, it is not recommended to perform rack pulls with a bar you do not own unless you receive approval from the gym owner or are using a bar designated for rack pulls.
3. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance close in front of the middle of the bar, as the bar is over the middle of your feet, with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly turned out to the sides.
    1. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
4. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while allowing your knees to bend until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your shins should remain close to being vertical, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward to bring your shins right next to the bar.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees a bit further to reach the bar.
    3. When hinging your hips properly, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings on the back of your thighs at the bottom of your hip hinge.
    4. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    5. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    6. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
5. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down, such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force that would lift the bar if it were only half as heavy.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. Immediately after the bar lifts off the pins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. The “push the floor away” cue only applies to the very start of the rep since thrusting your hips forward is chiefly what will allow you to lift the weight during this variation.
    2. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    3. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar in a controlled manner until the bar has returned to resting on the pins.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track slightly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you absolutely should not slam the bar onto the pins in a jarring manner.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the pins for a moment (rather than simply tapping the pins and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the pins for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferring between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the pins.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent (i.e., the quadruped position, which is also known as being “on all fours”).
    1. In this position, your elbows should be stacked underneath your shoulders with your arms pointing straight down toward the ground.
2. Situate your hands mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other, with your fingers spread somewhat apart from each other.
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart from each other as your heels are facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes, which you will continue doing throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag downward, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should allow you to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag down, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso in a rigid position and your legs straight, lower your chest toward the ground in a controlled manner as you bend your elbows until you are able to contact the ground with your chest.
    1. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact the ground instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    2. As you descend, allow your elbows to flare out moderately away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you. This can result in the upper arms forming approximately 30-60-degree angles with the torso for many people at the bottom of the push-up (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag down below your chest or hike up excessively above your chest.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your whole body as you progressively straighten your elbows until you have returned to the starting position with fully straightened elbows.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally from each other as you push up.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will allow you to still draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
4. Immediately after you finish straightening your elbows, continue pushing the ground away from you as you further spread your shoulder blades apart as far as you can while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. While prompting your upper back to round mildly, this will cause your chest to lift up a little higher above the ground, but it is a fairly small amplitude of motion.
    2. If you performed this shoulder blade motion when standing with your arms outstretched parallel to the ground, your shoulder blades would move forward.
    3. As a cue, you can imagine what it would feel like if you were leaning forward with your arms outstretched in front of you, and you are trying to touch something just beyond your reach with your elbows fully straightened. Pushing your shoulder blades forward as they spread apart would push your hands slightly forward to touch that object, and you should be performing the same shoulder blade motion during this step.
    4. Steps 3 and 4 should occur as a fluid movement.
5. Allow your shoulder blades to move back and toward each other until they have returned to being near their typical neutral position, like at the top of a standard push-up.
    1. Doing so will lower your chest slightly closer to the ground.
6. Alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. You should fluidly transition from step 5 back to the lowering phase of step 2.
    2. Steps 2 and 3 are the same as during a standard push-up, so the “plus” of a push-up plus simply refers to the additional shoulder blade motion performed at the top of each rep.
## Description
While training your pec major, front delt, and triceps like a standard push-up, this variation can also effectively develop your large serratus anterior and small pec minor.## Setting up
1. Position a power rack’s safety pins to just above the height of your knees when standing upright.
    1. Certain power racks will not allow you to position safety pins near this level, so this variation will not be feasible in every power rack. If your rack is not suitable for this variation, you can select the “conventional above-the-knee block pull” from this database to provide a similar training effect.
    2. If the closest safety pin setting of your rack situates the bar a little too high, you may be able to perform an above-the-knee block pull by elevating your feet like you would for a deficit deadlift. To do so, you can place a large diameter weight plate or deadlift block in the center of your rack, and you will stand on top of it to perform your rack pull from a closer to above-the-knee starting position. Depending on your preferred stance width, you can also position two plates or deadlift blocks directly next to each other if the soles of your feet do not fit fully onto one plate or deadlift block.
2. Place a barbell centered on top of the safety pins, and add your desired weight plates to it.
    1. Particularly as the load on the bar increases, rack pulls risk damaging a bar if you do not utilize a well-controlled lowering phase, which people often do not.
    2. Certain gyms have bars that were previously bent from rack pulls and are now specifically designated to be used for rack pulls to avoid damaging other bars.
    3. When using a well-controlled lowering phase during every rep, you most likely can avoid damaging the bar when performing a rack pull.
    4. If you do not feel confident in your ability to avoid damaging a bar when performing a rack pull, it is not recommended to perform rack pulls with a bar you do not own unless you receive approval from the gym owner or are using a bar designated for rack pulls.
3. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance close in front of the middle of the bar, as the bar is touching your lower thighs with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly turned out to the sides.
    1. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
4. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while allowing your knees to bend a bit until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your shins should essentially remain vertical.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, which will naturally facilitate a mild degree of knee bend.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
5. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down, such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force that would lift the bar if it were only half as heavy.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. Immediately after the bar lifts off the pins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. The “push the floor away” cue only applies to the very start of the rep since thrusting your hips forward is chiefly what will allow you to lift the weight during this variation.
    2. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    3. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while allowing your knees to bend mildly to lower the bar in a controlled manner until the bar has returned to resting on the pins.
    1. Focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical, and your knees unlocked, which will facilitate a natural degree of mild knee bend.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you absolutely should not slam the bar onto the pins in a jarring manner.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the pins for a moment (rather than simply tapping the pins and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the pins for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferring between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the pins.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
This exercise will not train your lower body muscles nearly as effectively as longer range of motion pulling variations, but it can heavily load your traps and rhomboids as they stabilize your shoulder girdle.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can set up on an exercise mat.
2. Form a diamond shape with your index fingers and thumbs by bringing your hands together underneath your chest, rotating your hands inward, and spreading your thumbs out away from your other fingers while keeping your other four fingers together.
    1. Your elbows should still be straight while they are stacked under the inner aspect of your shoulders.
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso in a rigid position and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner until it lightly touches your hands.
    1. Rather than excessively flaring your elbows out away from your sides, keep your elbows reasonably tucked by your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you as you descend.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact your hands instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    5. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ground to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your triceps to a preferentially greater degree than a standard push-up while also training your pec major, front delts, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. In a deadlift motion (like what is subsequently described in the technique section) pick up a kettlebell and set it onto the ground with its handle facing up.
    1. Particularly when using what you consider to be a heavy weight, initially lift it up using your hips and knees with your lower back near a flat position rather than being excessively rounded over at your lower back.
2. Place a pair of aerobic steps, large diameter weight plates, or deadlift blocks on each side of the kettlebell close enough together that you can place your feet fully on them with a near shoulder width stance.
    1. While you can use a variety of deficit heights, selecting no greater than a moderate deficit height (less than or equal to 4in (10cm)) is initially recommended to facilitate the appropriate technique.
    2. You want to pick a height that allows you to keep your lower back flat at the bottom of the rep, and the height should still allow you to use a deadlift technique rather than squatting the weight up (which can occur with an excessively large deficit).
    3. Any of the aforementioned objects can be effectively used depending on your equipment availability and the height you wish to use.
    4. Aerobic steps without risers are typically near 4in (10cm), bumper plates usually range from about 1-3.5in (2.5-8.9cm) in thickness, and 45lb (20kg) cast iron weight plates are often near 1in (2.5cm) thick.
    5. If you use weight plates, you can stack them on top of each other to adjust your height.
3. With the soles of your feet completely on the elevated surfaces, stand upright in a near shoulder width stance with the handle of the kettlebell between the middle of your feet as your toes are slightly turned out to the sides or pointing straight ahead.
    1. While using a near shoulder width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
4. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handle.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttock as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the handle.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position like when you are normally standing upright rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
5. With your hands close together and your elbows still straight, firmly grasp the handle with an overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handle with the handle pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handle closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handle can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the kettlebell, apply “pre-tension” into the handle as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force that would lift the weight if it was only half as heavy
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position and pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat, drive through your legs to begin lifting the kettlebell as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the handle reaches near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
5. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the kettlebell until it has returned to resting on the ground.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttock back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the handle has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the kettlebell.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the kettlebell up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the kettlebell on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferring between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the kettlebell on the ground.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While also training your back’s spinal extensor muscles, this exercise can train much of lower body musculature including your buttock’s gluteus maximus along with the hamstrings, quads, and hip adductor muscles in your thighs.## Setting up
1. Position a pair of deadlift blocks (or a similarly sturdy substitute capable of safely withstanding the load you will use) on the ground far enough apart from each other that the weight plates on a bar will be able to rest fully on the blocks in a stable position.
    1. For this block pull variation, your blocks should be of a height that situates the bar slightly above your knees when the loaded bar is resting on the blocks. This results in a high block pull with a greater elevation than the other block pulls included in this database.
    2. To appropriately position the blocks, it can be helpful to have a barbell on the ground and align the blocks about the same distance apart from each other as is the length of the barbell’s shaft (which is the middle part of the bar in between the two thick circular collars).
    3. Aerobic steps can often be used for block pulls while having the option to adjust the height by changing the number of risers you use, but you should be aware of the recommended weight limit if you opt to use heavy loads. The weight limit for aerobic steps will vary by brand, but some of the common brands recommend not exceeding 300lb (136kg) per step, which would result in a 600lb (272kg) limit when using a pair of steps for a block pull. The height of the aerobic step will also vary by brand, but the step itself is commonly near 4in (10cm) in height, with the risers being near 2in (5cm) in height.
    4. With thickness typically ranging from about 1-3.5in (2.5-8.9cm), depending on the weight and brand, bumper plates can also function as blocks that can be stacked on top of each other to provide a range of different heights.
    5. It may be worthwhile to try out different block heights to determine which height places you in your intended starting position. However, you can initially try out a pair of approximately 10in (25cm) deadlift blocks, aerobic steps with three pairs of risers, or a pair of three thick 45lb (20kg) bumper plates stacked on top of each other. Your individual body proportions will dictate where the bar is specifically positioned relative to your knees, but the aforementioned set-up options should situate a bar loaded with large diameter plates (e.g., 45lb (20kg) plates or any jumper plates) reasonably close to slightly above the knees for many people and allow you to adjust the height as needed. If you are going to add only smaller diameter plates onto the bar, higher blocks will naturally be needed to position the bar at the appropriate height.
2. After placing a barbell onto the blocks and loading weight plates onto the bar, stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance close in front of the middle of the bar, as the bar is touching your lower thighs with your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly turned out to the sides.
    1. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
3. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while allowing your knees to bend a bit until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your shins should essentially remain vertical.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, which will naturally facilitate a mild degree of knee bend.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
