## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower-to-middle chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists (when viewed from the side). This will keep your forearms close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set and promote the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare.
    2. When lowering the bar toward your lower-to-middle chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line. Relative to when you perform a standard bench press, you can aim to lower the bar slightly higher up on your chest with this board press.
    3. To reach that point with the bar, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
4. Continue lowering the bar until you are able to tap the board with the bar (if you are using a wooden board) or able to tap the board attachment to your chest.
    1. You should be in full control of your light tap, since you will not be bouncing out of the bottom position.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
While effectively training your pec major, front delts, and triceps, this exercise can improve bench press strength throughout the range of motion it trains.## Setting up
1. Place one dumbbell on the ground.
    1. Both of its weighted ends should be on the ground.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground, with the dumbbell by the side of your body you are about to train. When your upper arm (closer to the dumbbell) is by your side, the dumbbell’s handle should be parallel with your upper arm and out to the side of your elbow.
    1. Specifically, the dumbbell should be slightly greater than the length of your forearm away from your elbow to help you pick it up.
3. On the side closer to the dumbbell, bend your elbow by about 90 degrees with your upper arm right by your side.
4. Roll onto your side toward the dumbbell.
5. Firmly grasp the dumbbell with your palm on the side of your lower arm facing up toward the ceiling. Tightly wrap your other hand over your lower hand, so that your palms are facing each other.
6. While pulling the dumbbell up with your top arm, roll over onto your back. Move your torso and arms together as one unit, such that the dumbbell remains directly in front of your elbow (on the side holding the dumbbell directly) throughout the roll.
    1. When appropriately transitioning to lying on your back, you should not feel that you are stressing your shoulder on the side directly holding the dumbbell. You are primarily using the rest of your body to reposition yourself and lift the dumbbell rather than using your shoulder muscles (on the side directly holding the dumbbell) to rotate the forearm.
    2. Once you are on your back, you should be holding the dumbbell on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. On the side holding the dumbbell, your upper arm should be resting on the ground with your palm facing in toward your opposite side, your elbow tucked close to your side, and your forearm vertical.
7. Move your arm that is not directly holding the dumbbell into a comfortable position, such as resting by your side.
    1. Some lifters may prefer to use the free hand to hold onto a sturdy object (like the beam of a power rack) for additional stability, but you can position that hand wherever you like.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
8. If you have not already, position your legs straight out in front of you with straight knees.
    1. The back of your heels should now be on the ground along with your buttocks, upper back, and head.
9. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the dumbbell upward until your elbow is fully straightened with your arm directed straight up toward the ceiling. It may help to focus on driving your elbow upward.
    1. Your palm should continue facing in toward your opposite side as you avoid flaring your elbow out away from your side.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, your forearm should be in a vertical position throughout the press. This will result in your wrist and dumbbell remaining stacked over your elbow.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbell should be situated vertically over your shoulder.
    4. Throughout your set, continue keeping your heels, buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner. Move your elbow downward while bending it and keeping it tucked fairly close to your side.
    1. Continue keeping your wrist stacked over your elbow as your forearm remains vertical.
    2. You should finish with the back of your upper arm and elbow contacting the ground.
    3. While you do not have to finish the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you certainly should avoid slamming your arm onto the ground. You will not be bouncing your arm off the ground.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. It can be awkward to transfer the weight to your other hand with proper positioning while lying down if you are using a weight that is at least fairly heavy to you.
    2. Consequently, you can set the dumbbell down in an opposite manner to how you initially set up before switching to your other side.
5. After finishing the lowering phase of the last rep, wrap your free hand around your hand holding the dumbbell.
6. Roll onto your side (in which you are holding the dumbbell) while using your outer hand’s arm to help control the weight onto the ground. Move your torso and arms together as one unit, such that the dumbbell remains directly in front of your elbow (on the side holding the dumbbell directly) throughout the roll.
7. Let go of the dumbbell before setting up on the opposite side of the dumbbell to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can train your pec major, front delts and triceps in a manner with lesser shoulder strain than a variety of other horizontal pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed-height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to board press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, board pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, determine what you will use as your two-board.
    1. If you are using a standard wooden two-board, a partner will hold the board on your chest after you unrack the weight. The partner should stand next to you out of the bar’s way.
    2. Alternatively, you may use a specialized board press attachment that you would connect to the middle of the bar to board press without a partner. This type of equipment commonly allows you to perform variable-height board presses based on which side you connect to the bar. For this variation, the thickness of the attachment under the bar should be approximately 3in (7.6cm), which is the standard thickness of a two-board.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of board press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your board press.
6. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you prefer to perform your standard bench press with a greater arch, you can also use the same degree of arching for this variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your middle chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists (when viewed from the side). This will keep your forearms close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set and promote the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare.
    2. When lowering the bar toward your middle chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line. Relative to when you perform a standard bench press, you can aim to lower the bar slightly higher up on your chest with this board press.
    3. To reach that point with the bar, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out (which would result in your upper arms forming a “T-shape” with your torso).
4. Continue lowering the bar until you are able to tap the board with the bar (if you are using a wooden board) or able to tap the board attachment to your chest.
    1. You should be in full control of your light tap, since you will not be bouncing out of the bottom position.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
While primarily training your pec major, front delts, and triceps, this exercise can improve bench press strength throughout the range of motion it trains.## Setting up
1. Load an adjustable EZ bar with your desired weight, or approach a fixed-weight EZ bar.
    1. Either type of EZ bar can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down and firmly grasp it with your palms angled backward and in toward each other (resulting in a semi-overhand grip). Your hands should be about shoulder-width apart or mildly closer.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a shoulder-width or mildly closer semi-overhand grip is accessible on the bar.
    2. The proper hand placement should result in your wrists and elbows feeling comfortable during this exercise.
3. Stand upright with the bar, walk over to a flat exercise bench, and stand over one end of the bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space behind you. With this equipment, you would straddle the bench and walk forward toward the J-hooks.
    2. With a portable exercise bench, your back should be facing the head of the bench, like when you would bench press.
4. Lift the bar up slightly above your waist, and sit down on the bench with the bar on your lap.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
5. While keeping your elbows bent and the bar close to your torso, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench. Your head should be right by the end of the bench.
    1. As you transition from sitting to lying down, you optionally can march your thighs up against the bar to help reposition yourself. This can be helpful when using heavier loads.
    2. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent. You will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
6. In a bench pressing motion, press the bar up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a bench press rep), the bar should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    2. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar hard will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
## Exercise technique
1. Lower the bar behind your head in a controlled manner by bending your elbows while simultaneously moving your elbows backward. Based on your preference, you can select one of two approaches with respect to specifically where you lower the bar.
    1. Option 1: Lower the bar until it lightly taps the bench just behind your head. While the back muscles will still be engaged, this technique will predominantly train the triceps.
    2. Option 2: Set up with your head near the edge of the bench, lower the bar just behind your head while flexing your elbows, and then continue lowering the bar through the full range that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits. This option involves more of a pullover movement (arising from shoulder motion) than the former and will more heavily engage the back muscles along with the triceps.
    3. Neither of the approaches is inherently superior to the other, so you can feel free to try out both ways and continue using the technique you prefer.
    4. With either option, predominantly keep your elbows tucked in. However, you can allow them to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    5. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
2. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows while arcing them forward to lift the bar into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is vertically above your shoulders.
    2. For targeting the triceps, it can be helpful to focus on straightening your elbows to a greater degree than arcing your elbows forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
4. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the bar toward your lower chest or upper abdomen like you would during a bench press.
5. March your thighs up toward the weight, and then rock forward to sit up with the weight on your upper thighs.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your triceps, which are assisted by your lat, teres major, and rear delt.## Setting up
1. Position the safety pins of a power rack within the final third of the range of motion for your standard overhead press.
    1. For this variation, the pins should be higher than the top of your head, but somewhat of a range of pin heights can fall within the final third of your standard press. Within this range, you can decide specifically where you set the pins based on your preferences and the pin heights your rack permits.
    2. This is intentionally a short range of motion variation. However, for it to transfer effectively to improved lockout strength of your standard overhead press, the pins should generally allow you to press the bar at least 2in (5cm) up.
    3. Certain power racks have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes that may not allow you to set the pins at an appropriate height for a lockout pin press. If this applies to you, it may not be feasible to perform a lockout pin press with your rack. However, you may be able to select the overhead midpoint pin press as an alternative that you can perform with your equipment.
2. Symmetrically place a barbell in the middle of the safety pins and load it with your desired weight.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width and your palms facing away from you.
    1. Situating your index fingers directly outside the outer aspects of your shoulders is generally an effective approach, but what is personally your ideal grip may be a bit wider or closer. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Step forward until the bar is directly over your head and the middle of your feet. Situate your feet near shoulder width apart with your knees straight.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
    2. You can perform this step simultaneously with step 6.
6. Position your elbows under the bar. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Your forearms should now be close to vertical.
7. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Since the bar is already starting overhead, you will not have to tuck your chin back during this variation, in contrast to a standard overhead press.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
3. While keeping your legs straight, press the straight bar up until your elbows are fully straightened.
    1. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position throughout your rep.
    2. At the top of the press, you should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
4. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar straight down in a controlled manner.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down. Do not let the bar crash into the pins.
5. Once the bar contacts the pins, allow the weight to transmit fully into the pins. Remain completely stationary with the bar resting on the pins for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe when the bar is resting on the pins. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating your next rep.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. In contrast to a standard overhead press, you will finish your set with the bar resting on the pins rather than racking it onto J-hooks.
## Description
While training the triceps, front delts, and other shoulder girdle muscles, this exercise can improve lockout strength for your overhead press.## Setting up
1. Position the safety pins of a power rack near the midpoint of your standard overhead press.
    1. For many lifters, situating the pins reasonably close to forehead height will allow them to begin this exercise within the middle third of the range of a standard overhead press. However, specifically where you can set the pins can be influenced by where your power rack’s pinholes are situated.
    2. Certain power racks have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes. This may result in the two closest settings being near the start and end of your press. If this applies to you, and you cannot set them reasonably close to your midpoint (i.e., within the middle third of the press), it may not be feasible to perform an overhead midpoint pin press. However, you can select the overhead low pin press or the overhead lockout pin press as an alternative that you can perform with your equipment.
2. Symmetrically place a barbell onto the middle of the safety pins and load it with your desired weight.
3. While standing in front of the middle of the bar, firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width and your palms facing away from you.
    1. Situating your index fingers directly outside the outer aspects of your shoulders is generally an effective approach, but what is personally your ideal grip may be a bit wider or closer. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
4. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
    2. Maintain this posture until you finish your set.
5. Tuck your chin back to move your head a little further away from the bar. Keep it tucked as you finish setting up.
    1. The combination of tucking your chin and mildly arching your lower back should create additional space between your face and the bar. This will allow you to position yourself in essentially the same position in which you would be during the midpoint of your standard overhead press.
6. Step forward until the bar is directly in front of your face with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart. The middle of your feet should be under the bar, and your knees should be straight.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
    2. Like during a standard overhead press, the bar should be as close to being directly over your collarbone as possible.
    3. You can perform this step simultaneously with step 6.
7. Position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Your forearms should now be close to a vertical position when viewed from the front.
    3. From this position, your elbows should be reasonably close to being flared 45 degrees out from your sides. 0 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the front. 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the side.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
3. While keeping your legs straight, press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. To the best of your ability, avoid pushing the bar forward out in front of your shoulders. This can be challenging if you are new to this exercise, since you want to avoid hitting your face with the bar. You can still meaningfully increase your strength and grow muscle if the bar drifts a bit forward, but strive to minimize any forward bar motion as well as you can for an efficient overhead press.
    2. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position throughout your rep.
4. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
5. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
6. Reverse the movement by tucking your chin back and lowering the bar toward your collarbone in a controlled manner.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down. Do not let the bar crash into the pins.
7. Once the bar contacts the pins, allow the weight to transmit fully into the pins. Remain completely stationary with the bar resting on the pins for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe when the bar is resting on the pins. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating your next rep.
8. Alternate between steps 4-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. In contrast to a standard overhead press, you will finish your set with the bar resting on the pins rather than racking it onto J-hooks.
## Description
While training the triceps, front delts, and other shoulder girdle muscles, this exercise can improve overhead press strength from the midpoint of the press through the lockout.## Setting up
1. Position the J-hooks of a power rack (or squat stand) near the height of your upper chest.
    1. As an alternative to starting from a rack, you can begin with the barbell on the floor and power clean it into position. However, this page will describe how to set up from a rack.
2. Place a barbell onto the J-hooks and load it with your desired weight.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width as your palms are facing away from you.
    1. Situating your index fingers directly outside the outer aspects of your shoulders is generally an effective approach, but what is personally your ideal grip may be a bit wider or closer. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Slightly bend your knees, and step forward to situate your collarbone or upper chest directly under the bar. Simultaneously, position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Your forearms should now be close to a vertical position when viewed from the front.
    3. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    4. From this position, your elbows should be reasonably close to being flared 45 degrees out from your sides. 0 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the front. 90 degrees of flare would mean your elbows are directly aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the side.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Fully stand upright while holding the bar.
    1. With your knees straightened, the bar should now be above the tops of the J-hooks.
3. Take a couple of steps backward and set your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
4. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
5. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
6. Tuck or tilt your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
7. While keeping your legs straight, press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. To the best of your ability, avoid pushing the bar forward out in front of your shoulders. This can be challenging if you are new to this exercise, since you want to avoid hitting your face with the bar. You can still meaningfully increase your strength and grow muscle if the bar drifts a bit forward, but strive to minimize any forward bar motion as well as you can for an efficient overhead press.
    2. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position throughout your rep.
    3. Avoid using your legs to help with driving the bar up. This is not a push press.
8. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
9. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe after locking out the weight overhead. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to lowering the bar.
10. Reverse the movement by tucking your chin back and lowering the bar toward your collarbone in a controlled manner.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
11. Once the bar is on your collarbone, pause in that position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds) while staying tight.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
12. Alternate between steps 7-11 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
13. After completing the lowering phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second before walking forward.
14. After the bar contacts the back of both J-hooks, bend your knees slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, traps, upper pec major, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and stand over one end of a flat exercise bench.
    1. If you are using a bench that is built into a rack, you should set up facing toward the base of the rack for sufficient space behind you. With this equipment, you would straddle the bench and walk forward toward the J-hooks.
    2. With a portable exercise bench, your back should be facing the head of the bench, like when you would dumbbell bench press.
2. Sit down on the bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your upper thighs near your abdomen.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
    2. Your palms should be facing in toward each other with your upper arms by your sides.
    3. To move heavy dumbbells efficiently into the proper position, you can initially situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up. Then you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down on the bench. Your head should be right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent. You will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
4. In a bench pressing motion, press the dumbbells up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a bench press rep), the dumbbells should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    2. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the dumbbells with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the dumbbells hard will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    3. Based on what feels most comfortable, you can have your palms facing in toward each other (with a neutral grip), forward (with an overhand grip), or angled inward and forward. During your set, you can similarly allow your palms to rotate within this range if that feels comfortable to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Lower the dumbbells behind your head in a controlled manner by bending your elbows while simultaneously moving your elbows backward. Based on your preference, you can select one of two approaches with respect to specifically where you lower the dumbbells.
    1. Option 1: Lower the dumbbells until they lightly tap the bench just behind your head. While the back muscles will still be engaged, this technique will predominantly train the triceps.
    2. Option 2: Set up with your head near the edge of the bench, lower the dumbbells just behind your head while flexing your elbows, and then continue lowering the dumbbells through the full range that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits. This option involves more of a pullover movement (arising from shoulder motion) than the former and will more heavily engage the back muscles along with the triceps.
    3. Neither of the approaches is inherently superior to the other, so you can feel free to try out both ways and continue using the technique you prefer.
    4. With either option, predominantly keep your elbows tucked in. However, you can allow them to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    5. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
2. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows while arcing them forward to lift the dumbbells into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the dumbbells are vertically above your shoulders.
    2. For targeting the triceps, it can be helpful to focus on straightening your elbows to a greater degree than arcing your elbows forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
4. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the dumbbells toward your sides like you would during a neutral grip dumbbell bench press.
    1. Regardless of the palm position you used for your set, it is easier to sit up with the dumbbells when your palms are facing in toward each other.
5. March your thighs up toward the weights, and then rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your triceps, which are assisted by your lat, teres major, and rear delt.## Setting up
1. Load a bar with your desired weight in front of a portable flat exercise bench (not fused within a rack like a bench press station).
    1. In addition to a standard barbell to which you can add weight plates, you can also utilize a fixed-weight bar, which can be useful if you wish to use a load that is lighter than 45lb (20kg), which is the typical weight of an unloaded standard barbell.
    2. Your back should be facing the head of the bench, like when you would bench press.
2. Firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width overhand grip that results in your palms facing back toward you.
3. Stand upright with the bar as the bench is right behind you.
4. Lift the bar up slightly above your waist, and sit down on the bench with the bar on your lap.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
5. While keeping your elbows bent and the bar close to your torso, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench. Your head should be right by the end of the bench.
    1. As you transition from sitting to lying down, you optionally can march your thighs up against the bar to help reposition yourself. This can be helpful when using heavier loads.
    2. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent. You will keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
6. In a bench pressing motion, press the bar up until your elbows are straight and your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a bench press rep), the bar should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    2. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar hard will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
## Exercise technique
1. Lower the bar behind your head in a controlled manner by bending your elbows while simultaneously moving your elbows backward. Based on your preference, you can select one of two approaches with respect to specifically where you lower the bar.
    1. Option 1: Lower the bar until it lightly taps the bench just behind your head. While the back muscles will still be engaged, this technique will predominantly train the triceps.
    2. Option 2: Set up with your head near the edge of the bench, lower the bar just behind your head while flexing your elbows, and then continue lowering the bar through the full range that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits. This option involves more of a pullover movement (arising from shoulder motion) than the former and will more heavily engage the back muscles along with the triceps.
    3. Neither of the approaches is inherently superior to the other, so you can feel free to try out both ways and continue using the technique you prefer.
    4. With either option, predominantly keep your elbows tucked in. However, you can allow them to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    5. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
2. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows while arcing them forward to lift the bar into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is vertically above your shoulders.
    2. For targeting the triceps, it can be helpful to focus on straightening your elbows to a greater degree than arcing your elbows forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
4. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the bar toward your lower chest or upper abdomen like you would during a bench press.
5. March your thighs up toward the weight, and then rock forward to sit up with the weight on your upper thighs.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your triceps, which are assisted by your lat, teres major, and rear delt.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a 90-degree angle, or approach a mobile exercise bench with a permanently fixed upright backrest.
    1. If you have access to one, a seated bench with a backrest that does not reach your head is ideal because it can allow you to move your head back away from the bar during the exercise.
2. Center the bench underneath a Smith machine’s bar in a spot that will allow you to touch near your upper chest with the bar at the bottom of the press.
    1. After initially estimating which bench placement allows this to occur, you may have to move the bench a bit forward or backward during later steps if you notice the bar does not reach near your upper chest.
    2. Specifically how you should set up can be influenced by whether your Smith machine has sliding rails that are purely vertical or angled.
    3. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, place the side of the bench where you will place your feet on the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends. This will result in the top of the rails being horizontally closer to your head than the bottom of the rails when seated on the bench. An angled Smith machine will prompt the bar to track mildly back toward your head when pressing the bar up.
    4. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which side of the bar you place your feet.
3. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to grasp it with slightly bent elbows once you are sitting on the bench.
    1. The ideal height will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently.
    2. If the bar is situated too high, you may have to shrug your shoulders excessively to lift the bar off the safety bars (on which the safety hooks that are attached to the bar rest). This can place you in a less stable position to start your set.
    3. If the bar is situated excessively low, you will have to grasp the bar initially with a greater degree of elbow bend, and unracking it will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    4. If neither of the two closest bar heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    5. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best starting bar height for you.
4. After positioning the bar to the appropriate height, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of overhead press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section. Use these reps to confirm that you positioned the bench the ideal distance away from the bar prior to reracking the bar and adjusting the bench as needed.
    1. The bench should not be any further away from the bar than what is required for the bar to clear your head when pressing up.
    2. Even a little bit of additional distance between the bar and your face can contribute to the exercise feeling awkward.
    3. At this time, you can optionally place the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails under the bar) slightly under the height of your upper chest when seated.
5. After loading the bar with your intended weight, sit down with your buttocks, back, and head in contact with the bench. Firmly place your feet flat on the ground approximately shoulder-width apart with your knees bent.
    1. Your hips should be fully back on the seat.
6. Tuck your chin back to move your head slightly further back away from the bar.
7. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
8. Reach up and firmly grasp the bar with your hands slightly outside shoulder width as your palms are facing forward.
    1. Situating your index fingers directly outside the outer aspects of your shoulders is generally an effective approach, but what is personally your ideal grip may be a bit wider or closer. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Press the bar up off the safety bars by straightening your elbows before rotating the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion. After unracking it, hold the bar stationary for a moment before starting to lower it.
3. Lower the bar in a controlled manner until you are able to tap near your upper chest lightly with the bar, or until you have fully moved through your available shoulder range of motion.
    1. Keep your elbows mostly stacked under the bar rather than meaningfully tracking in front of the bar or behind. However, you can allow your elbows to track slightly forward in front of the bar at the bottom of the rep if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    2. Your elbows should neither be fully flared, such that they are pointing completely out to your sides, nor should they be fully tucked, such that they are pointing directly forward. Aligning your elbows close to directly under the bar will naturally promote the appropriate balance between elbow flare and elbow tuck.
    3. Most lifters can comfortably lower the bar to their upper chest, but certain individuals with limited shoulder mobility may not be able to touch their chest with the bar without discomfort. If this applies to you, lower the bar as far as you can without discomfort.
    4. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up overhead until your elbows are fully straightened.
    1. Keep your buttocks pressed up against the bench as you avoid scooting your hips forward or excessively arching your lower back.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows stacked under the bar.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
    1. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second.
6. Rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars by flicking your wrists before bending your elbows to lower the safety hooks securely onto the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, supraspinatus, upper pec major, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Position the J-hooks of a power rack (or squat stand) near the height of your upper chest.
2. Place a barbell onto the J-hooks and load it with your desired weight.
    1. It is recommended to use an empty barbell if you are new to this exercise.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands clearly outside shoulder width as your palms are facing away from you (i.e., with an overhand grip).
    1. What is personally considered a wide grip to you can be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard behind-the-neck press, which you can somewhat widen to individualize your wide grip. For instance, for your wide grip, you can place your index finger on the bar where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing your standard behind-the-neck press. This approach provides many lifters with an appropriately wide grip, but there is a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine specifically where you feel best.
    2. The standard behind-the-neck press normally uses a wider grip than when overhead pressing to the front. Consequently, the wide grip behind the neck press can have a meaningfully wider grip than where you feel strongest when overhead pressing to the front.
    3. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Slightly bend your knees, and step forward to situate your collarbone or upper chest directly under the bar. Simultaneously, position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Your elbows should naturally be flared out (pointing out to the side) to a greater extent during this variation compared to your standard behind-the-neck press.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to help create full-body tension.
2. Fully stand upright while holding the bar.
    1. With your knees straightened, the bar should now be above the tops of the J-hooks.
3. Take a couple of steps backward and set your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
4. Squeeze your glutes and quads from now until you finish your last rep.
    1. This will create a stable platform from which to press.
5. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
6. Tuck your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
7. Press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position.
8. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar. Simultaneously arc the bar slightly backward while pressing it up.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
9. Finish the initial press with your elbows straightened as the bar is overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath during your set, you can breathe after locking out the weight overhead. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to lowering the bar.
    3. You are now in the starting position for your behind-the-neck press.
10. In a slow and controlled manner, lower the bar behind your head through the range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. If you have the mobility to do so comfortably, you can lower the bar until it lightly contacts your upper back muscles just under the base of your neck. However, what constitutes your end range will be determined by your individual shoulder mobility.
    2. If you start experiencing shoulder pain or joint discomfort at the bottom of the lowering phase, you likely are moving beyond the current limit of your shoulder mobility. Training this exercise over time may progressively improve your shoulder mobility and gradually permit you to descend through a greater range, but there is no need to force yourself into a position that currently does not feel right for your shoulders.
    3. With sufficient shoulder mobility, you should not have to push your head meaningfully forward to avoid scraping your head or neck with the bar. However, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest if you wish to do so for additional clearance.
    4. Strive to keep your elbows stacked under the bar when viewed from the side. To do so, your elbows should be pointing out away from your sides.
    5. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the bar the entire way down.
11. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up until you have fully straightened your elbows with the bar overhead.
12. Alternate between steps 10 and 11 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
13. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, tuck your chin back and lower the bar to your collarbone.
14. With the bar now in front of your body, walk forward until the bar contacts the back of both J-hooks
15. Bend your knees slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts and lateral delts while also training your triceps, supraspinatus, serratus anterior, and traps.## Setting up
1. Place a weight plate under the base of a flat exercise bench (on the end where you place your feet) to position the head of the bench at a slight decline.
    1. A plate with a thickness of approximately 1-2in (2.5-5cm) can be appropriate for this variation, which often includes 25-45lb (and 10-20kg) plates.
    2. If your plate has different surfaces, place the flat part of the weight plate facing up against the bench.
    3. The bench should be very stable once it is on the plate.
    4. This variation intentionally uses a degree of decline that should allow you to feel stable once you are lying down with your feet flat on the ground. If you feel like you may slide back on the bench once you are lying down, the decline is excessively high to use without a bench that has a foot/ankle pad to secure your feet.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on the bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your upper thighs near your abdomen with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Position yourself so that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    2. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    3. To move heavy dumbbells efficiently into the proper position, you can initially situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up. Then you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
5. Flare your elbows moderately out to your sides such that your upper arms form approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    1. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable for your shoulders, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbows out within this range.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the dumbbells up by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten them until your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press, as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner toward the sides of your chest through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Let your elbows moderately flare out to your sides as they descend and bend enough to keep your wrists stacked above them with vertical forearms. Lifters often find that at least a mild degree of elbow tuck makes this exercise feel most comfortable for the shoulders compared to fully flaring the elbows straight out to the sides.
    2. How far you can lower the dumbbells with vertical forearms will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility, but most people have the mobility to descend until their hands are at least level with their chest.
    3. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    4. There is no need to lower the dumbbells extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way you initially set up.
    1. After your dumbbells are by your chest following the lowering phase of the last rep, bring your upper arms in line with your sides to tuck your elbows and position the dumbbells closer to your thighs.
    2. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
While not loading your triceps as effectively as a barbell bench press, this exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delts.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable decline bench to your desired decline angle.
    1. An angle near 30 degrees is an appropriate starting point, but you can effectively perform this exercise with a range of decline angles.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the decline becomes steeper, so you can adjust the angle to modify the intensity.
    3. Some adjustable benches have a mild decline setting along with incline settings, but they lack a pad to secure your feet. This exercise specifically uses a decline with a foot/ankle pad.
2. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
3. Lie down in a controlled manner to position your back and head on the bench.
4. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the bench. Simultaneously lift your head and shoulder blades up off the bench by curling your middle back.
    1. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that crunching up any further would require the lower half of your back to start lifting up off the bench.
    2. Given that this is a crunch rather than a sit-up, the lower half of your back should remain in contact with the bench while you only lift up the upper half of your back mildly up.
    3. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    4. As a cue for your engaging abdominal muscles, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs toward your hip bones.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner until you can lightly tap the bench with your shoulder blades.
    1. Rather than fully relaxing at the bottom, keep your abdominals tensed at the bottom.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the entire movement.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Avoid using momentum to rock your body from rep to rep.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable decline bench to your desired decline angle.
    1. An angle near 30 degrees is an appropriate starting point, but you can effectively perform this exercise with a range of decline angles.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the decline becomes steeper, so you can adjust the angle to modify the intensity.
    3. Some adjustable benches have a mild decline setting along with incline settings, but they lack a pad to secure your feet. This exercise specifically uses a decline with a foot/ankle pad.
2. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
3. Lie down in a controlled manner to position your back and head on the bench.
4. Place the fingertips of each hand lightly in contact with the sides of the back of your head as your upper arms and elbows are directed out away from your sides.
    1. Keeping your hands and arms in this position can help you avoid inadvertently pulling down on your head during the exercise, which would otherwise be easier to do if you interlocked your fingers and directed your upper arms up toward the ceiling.
    2. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the bench. Simultaneously lift your head and shoulder blades up off the bench by curling your middle back.
    1. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that crunching up any further would require the lower half of your back to start lifting up off the bench.
    2. Given that this is a crunch rather than a sit-up, the lower half of your back should remain in contact with the bench while you only lift the upper half of your back mildly up.
    3. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    4. If a scale were fixed to your head, it should not detect any force from your fingertips as you keep your hands relaxed throughout the exercise, rather than pulling your head.
    5. As a cue for your engaging abdominal muscles, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs toward your hip bones.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner until you can lightly tap the bench with your shoulder blades.
    1. Rather than fully relaxing at the bottom, keep your abdominals tensed at the end.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the entire movement.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Avoid using momentum to rock your body from rep to rep.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Approach a flat exercise bench or a similarly sturdy object with an elevated surface that can support your foot.
    1. As an alternative to a bench, you can use a plyometric box or an aerobic step stacked on risers. At home, you can use pieces of furniture such as a bed, couch, or chair that will not slide. If the object you use has a hard surface, placing a cushioned object (such as a foam pad) on top of it may make this exercise more comfortable.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the object’s height increases, so you can utilize a taller object for a greater intensity. However, a height near 18in (46cm), which applies to a typical flat exercise bench, is recommended to use as a minimum for most people.
2. Lie down on your side with the inner aspect of your top knee (which is bent by about 90 degrees) and lower leg on top of the elevated surface. Align this thigh with your torso so that you can draw a straight line from your thigh through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from above.
    1. The outer aspect of your lower hip should be resting on the ground.
    2. If you are on a hard surface, you can set up on an exercise mat for comfort. You chiefly want a comfortable surface to be under the forearm that you will be placing on the ground.
3. Align your lower thigh directly under your top thigh.
    1. If the object you are using has open space underneath its top surface (like with a bench), keep your knee straight on your lower leg.
    2. If your object lacks space for your lower foot under its top surface (like with a soft plyometric box), keep your bottom knee bent.
4. Position your bottom forearm on the ground with your elbow bent by about 90 degrees. Your elbow should be close to being stacked directly underneath your shoulder.
    1. With your bottom hand, you can make a fist and keep the little finger-side of your hand in contact with the ground. Alternatively, you can keep your palm flat on the ground if you find that to feel more comfortable and stable.
    2. You can keep your top arm in a comfortable position, such as resting on top of your side with a straight elbow. Alternatively, you can reach that arm toward the ceiling with a straight elbow or place that side’s hand on your top hip with a bent elbow.
    3. Your bottom upper arm should be in a vertical position.
    4. You can perform steps 2-4 simultaneously.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your bottom hip and leg fully off the ground by driving your top knee down into the bench while pushing your bottom forearm into the ground.
    1. At the top, you should now be able to draw a straight line from your head through your belly button to the center point between your knees when viewed from the front.
    2. You should also be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the top.
    3. Your thighs (which should be close together), torso, and head should be in essentially the same alignment as they would be when standing upright.
    4. The intent to pull your top knee down toward your bottom knee is what should lift your bottom leg, rather than simply lifting your bottom leg up by itself. Since the bench stops your knee from moving down, this intent lifts you up.
    5. An indicator that you are appropriately driving through your top leg is when your top hip becomes approximately level with your top knee. If you lift your bottom leg by itself, your top hip will remain lower than your top knee, which you do not want.
2. Statically hold this position in proper alignment until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your opposite side elevated.
    1. Avoid letting your hips sag down, which would prevent you from being able to draw the line through the midline of your body when viewed from the front. If you notice your hips starting to drop down, it can help to think about driving more forcefully through your top knee. When driving through your top leg, you should feel its inner thigh muscles contract.
    2. When learning this exercise, it can be useful to face a mirror to receive visual feedback if your hips begin sagging.
    3. Avoid letting your buttocks drift backward by bending your hips. This would prevent you from being able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the top. Squeezing your glutes can help prevent you from unintentionally bending your hips.
## Description
This exercise can statically train all your hip adductor muscles and a variety of core muscles, including your obliques and quadratus lumborum.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable decline bench to your desired decline angle.
    1. An angle near 30 degrees is an appropriate starting point, but you can effectively perform this exercise with a range of decline angles.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the decline becomes steeper, so you can adjust the angle to modify the intensity.
    3. Some adjustable benches have a mild decline setting along with incline settings, but they lack a pad to secure your feet. This exercise specifically uses a decline with a foot/ankle pad.
2. Pick up your desired weight and hold it on one side.
    1. A weight plate, dumbbell, or kettlebell is a viable option that you can use. Select whichever option you find most comfortable to hold to your chest.
3. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
    2. You can use your free hand to help you get into position.
4. Hold the weight just above your chest with bent elbows as you firmly grasp each side of the weight.
5. Lie down in a controlled manner to position your back and head on the bench.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the bench. Simultaneously lift your head and shoulder blades up off the bench by curling your middle back.
    1. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that crunching up any further would require the lower half of your back to start lifting up off the bench.
    2. Continue holding the weight close to your chest throughout your set.
    3. Given that this is a crunch rather than a sit-up, the lower half of your back should remain in contact with the bench while you only lift up the upper half of your back mildly.
    4. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
    5. As a cue for your engaging abdominal muscles, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs toward your hip bones.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your back to uncurl in a controlled manner until you can lightly tap the bench with your shoulder blades.
    1. Rather than fully relaxing at the bottom, keep your abdominals tensed.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the entire movement.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Avoid using momentum to rock your body from rep to rep.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your rectus abdominis (the “six-pack muscle”), which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Approach a flat exercise bench or a similarly sturdy object with an elevated surface that can support your foot.
    1. As an alternative to a bench, you can use a plyometric box or an aerobic step stacked on risers. At home, you can use pieces of furniture such as a bed, couch, or chair that will not slide. If the object you use has a hard surface, placing a cushioned object (such as a foam pad) on top of it may make this exercise more comfortable.
    2. The difficulty of this exercise will increase as the object’s height increases, so you can utilize a taller object for a greater intensity. However, a height near 18in (46cm), which applies to a typical flat exercise bench, is recommended to use as a minimum for most people.
2. Lie down on your side with the inner aspect of your top foot and ankle on top of the elevated surface, as that leg’s knee is straight. Align your leg with your torso so that you can draw a straight line from your leg through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from above.
    1. The outer aspect of your lower hip should be resting on the ground.
    2. If you are on a hard surface, you can set up on an exercise mat for comfort. You chiefly want a comfortable surface to be under the forearm that you will be placing on the ground.
3. Align your lower thigh directly under your top thigh.
    1. If the object you are using has open space underneath its top surface (like with a bench), keep your knee straight on your lower leg.
    2. If your object lacks space for your lower foot under its top surface (like with a soft plyometric box), keep your bottom knee bent.
4. Position your bottom forearm on the ground with your elbow bent by about 90 degrees. Your elbow should be close to being stacked directly underneath your shoulder.
    1. With your bottom hand, you can make a fist and keep the little finger-side of your hand in contact with the ground. Alternatively, you can keep your palm flat on the ground if you find that to feel more comfortable and stable.
    2. You can keep your top arm in a comfortable position, such as resting on top of your side with a straight elbow. Alternatively, you can reach that arm toward the ceiling with a straight elbow or place that side’s hand on your top hip with a bent elbow.
    3. Your bottom upper arm should be in a vertical position.
    4. You can perform steps 2-4 simultaneously.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your bottom hip and leg fully off the ground by driving your top leg down into the bench while pushing your bottom forearm into the ground.
    1. At the top, you should now be able to draw a straight line from your head through your belly button to the center point between your knees when viewed from the front.
    2. You should also be able to draw a straight line from your heel through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the top.
    3. Your thighs (which should be close together), torso, and head should be in essentially the same alignment as they would be when standing upright.
    4. The intent to pull your top leg down is what should lift your bottom leg, rather than simply lifting your bottom leg up by itself. Since the bench stops your top leg from moving down, this intent lifts you up.
    5. An indicator that you are appropriately driving through your top leg is when your top hip becomes approximately level with your top foot. If you lift your bottom leg by itself, your top hip will remain lower than your top foot, which you do not want.
2. Statically hold this position in proper alignment until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your opposite side elevated.
    1. Avoid letting your hips sag down, which would prevent you from being able to draw the line through the midline of your body when viewed from the front. If you notice your hips starting to drop down, it can help to think about driving more forcefully through your top leg. When driving through your top leg, you should feel its inner thigh muscles contract.
    2. When learning this exercise, it can be useful to face a mirror to receive visual feedback if your hips begin sagging.
    3. Avoid letting your buttocks drift backward by bending your hips. This would prevent you from being able to draw a straight line through your whole body when viewed from the top. Squeezing your glutes can help prevent you from unintentionally bending your hips.
## Description
This exercise can statically train all your hip adductor muscles and a variety of core muscles, including your obliques and quadratus lumborum.## Setting up
1. Place a barbell in a landmine attachment or a viable substitute.
    1. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    2. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
2. Load the bar with one full-diameter plate (such as a 45lb (20kg) plate or any bumper plate) or whichever plate will have the largest diameter of the plates you will use for your working weight.
3. Position a sturdy object with an elevated surface directly next to the plate you loaded onto the bar. Select an object with a height that will allow the bar to start at the bottom of your squat once the bar’s plate is resting on the object.
    1. The optimal object height will allow you to squat through your full range of motion. If it is excessively high, the plate will touch the object before you reach the end range of your squat, therefore restricting your squat depth.
    2. If the object’s height is excessively low relative to how deeply you can squat, you will not be able to pick it up initially in the goblet position, and it will be more difficult to set the weight down after your set.
    3. While not ideal, it is preferable to select an object that slightly limits your squat depth rather than an object that is too low for you to assume the appropriate starting position without meaningfully rounding your back.
    4. Trying out different object heights may be needed to determine which height is ideal for you as an individual. After you find what places you in your optimal position, you can make a note of what you used to expedite your set-up for subsequent sessions.
    5. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    6. As a general starting point, you can try out a flat exercise bench, a plyometric box with a height of 18in (46cm), or an aerobic step with seven risers on each side. With an aerobic step, you can naturally adjust the height via the addition or subtraction of risers. To increase the height with a bench or plyometric box, you can stack bumper plates on top of it. While more time-consuming, you can alternatively use a stack of only bumper plates to form your elevated object.
4. Lift the bar up by the free end and rest the weight plate on top of the elevated object.
5. Perform a couple of reps with only one plate on the bar to confirm you selected the appropriate object height. If you initially selected an object that is too tall or too short, adjust the height of the object before proceeding to add more weight to the bar.
    1. Skip the next setup step and follow the subsequent setup steps before following the “exercise technique” section’s instructions to perform a couple of reps before adding plates to the bar.
    2. The proper starting height should allow you to keep your back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees in line with your feet at the bottom of your squat. If you cannot satisfy these criteria, a taller object is likely warranted. Alternatively, a lower object may be warranted if you feel that you can squat deeper with quality technique than what the object permits.
    3. If you only have access to objects near the height of a flat bench and find that you can squat deeper than they allow, you can exclusively load the bar with smaller diameter weight plates to extend your squat’s range of motion.
6. Finish adding weight plates to the bar with your desired load.
7. Stand upright with the free end of the bar above the center point between the middle of your feet. Position your feet shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing mildly out to the sides.
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. In the starting position, your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, and you will keep your neck in this position throughout your set.
8. Simultaneously bend your hips and knees to squat down until the tip of the bar is just in front of your upper chest.
    1. Keep your back flat, rather than meaningfully rounding it.
9. With your elbows bent, position the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) firmly against the bottom of the end of the bar. Interlock your fingers, and place your thumbs against the tip of the bar.
    1. Your elbows should be tucked slightly in front of your sides. They should be pointing slightly forward rather than pointing straight down, like they would if your upper arms were completely by your sides.
    2. You are now in the goblet position that you will maintain throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Drive through the ground to stand up while keeping the end of the bar directly in front of your chest. Push through your legs until you have straightened your knees and hips.
    1. Avoid rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. Allow your body to lean mildly forward at your ankles to keep the bar close to your chest at the end of the lifting phase.
3. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner until you can lightly tap the elevated object with the weight plates.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, knees aligned over your feet, and the weight held close to your chest as you squat down.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time.
    3. Avoid bouncing the plates off the elevated object instead of gently contacting it with the weight.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
5. Once you have completed the lowering phase of your last rep, allow the weight to fully rest on the elevated object before letting go of the bar.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensor muscles, this exercise can develop your gluteus maximus, quads, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that places the handles near your lower chest or slightly lower once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, but the ideal seat height specifically should allow you to train through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably allows.
    2. Individuals with limited mobility may have to position the seat higher to reach the handles, while those with greater mobility may benefit from positioning the seat lower to train through a full range of motion.
    3. If the seat is excessively high, your range of motion for this exercise will be limited.
    4. If the seat is excessively low, you may not be able to reach the handles with proper positioning. Forcing your elbows back beyond what your mobility permits in an effort to reach the handles may increase the risk of experiencing shoulder discomfort.
    5. Some trial and error may be needed to find the best height for you as an individual. When continuing to set up, you can always adjust the seat height if you determine the initial height is suboptimal for you.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your feet flat on the ground.
    1. If your machine has a back pad, position your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against it.
    2. If your machine lacks a back pad, sit with a fully upright torso.
4. If your machine has a thigh pad, snugly position it on top of your thighs. If your machine has a seat belt, securely fasten it around your waist.
    1. Either option will help you anchor your body.
    2. Certain machines lack a thigh pad or seat belt, which can contribute to the exercise being less stable.
5. If your machine allows you to rotate its handles inward or outward, position them inward unless your thighs restrict the range of motion for the closer setting or if you simply find the wider setting to feel more comfortable.
    1. The closer setting facilitates a greater degree of elbow tuck, which is typically desirable when striving to emphasize the triceps and/or the front delts to a greater degree than the pec major during this exercise.
    2. However, the closer setting may position the handles excessively close for individuals with rather wide body types.
    3. Even if your body does not restrict your range of motion with the closer handle setting, you can also select the wider setting if it just feels more comfortable to you.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back, and move your elbows fully back behind your torso while bending them by at least 90 degrees.
    1. Position your elbows close to being directly behind your wrists and the handles. Keeping your elbows tucked close to your sides rather than flaring them out away from your sides will limit the degree to which this exercise trains the pec major.
    2. If the handles are now just within your reach, the seat height you initially selected is appropriate.
    3. If you cannot reach the handles without shrugging your shoulders up or tipping them forward, raise the seat before resuming your setup.
    4. If the handles are lower than what you can comfortably reach from this position, you can lower the seat to facilitate a greater degree of elbow bend before resuming your setup.
7. Firmly grasp the handles with the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) against them.
    1. While almost all machines allow you to use a neutral grip with your palms facing in toward each other, some machines also have handles that allow you to use an overhand grip with your palms facing backward. If your machine enables you to use both grips, select whichever feels most comfortable to you.
    2. Hold the handles with mostly straight wrists. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Push the handles down by driving your elbows down until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Throughout your set, continue keeping your torso fully upright and feet planted on the ground.
    2. You should finish with your elbows close to your sides and your arms directed down toward the ground.
    3. While driving downward and straightening, your elbows will simultaneously move forward.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing the handles to lift up to the starting position. Bend your elbows while arcing them upward and backward.
    1. Keep your elbows tucked so that they remain behind your wrists rather than flaring them out away from your sides.
    2. Avoid letting your shoulders shrug up or roll forward.
    3. With the appropriate seat height, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders at the end range, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain. If you feel joint discomfort at the end range, increase the seat height before resuming your set.
    4. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms to the stoppers under them to confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the lateral and medial heads of your triceps while also training your front delts and pec major to a lesser degree.## Setting up
Certain machines have a foot pedal that you can press to move the machine’s handle while setting up, but other machine models lack a foot pedal. Some of the specific setup details differ depending on which type of machine you are using, so the setup for each approach is described separately. However, the exercise technique can be essentially the same for either option, so one technique section is provided with a couple of details that depend on which type of machine you utilize.

### **Setting up a machine without a foot pedal**

1. Adjust the seat to a height that comfortably allows you to grasp the handle behind your head with your elbows fully bent once you are seated.
    1. This seat height commonly allows the base of someone’s neck to be reasonably close to being level with the handle once seated. However, your flexibility can influence where your ideal position lies, so trying out different seat heights may initially be required.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down on its seat with an upright torso as your back is resting against the backrest.
    1. If your machine has a foot plate, position your soles against it. Otherwise, place your feet flat on the floor.
4. Raise your arms up with your elbows fully bent to grasp the handle firmly with fairly straight wrists.
    1. Different machines have different handle types that will influence specifically how you should grasp the handle, but what chiefly matters is that your grip feels stable and comfortable.
    2. From this position, your upper arms should be near the sides of your head with your elbows held high and tucked in (rather than being meaningfully flared out away from your sides).
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your triceps.

### **Setting up a machine with a foot pedal**

1. Adjust the seat to a height that positions the handle at least a little lower than what you can reach behind your head with your elbows lifted up and fully bent once you are seated.
    1. This seat height commonly allows the base of someone’s neck to be at least mildly higher than the handle once seated. However, the foot pedal allows a range of seat heights to be viable for this exercise.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down on its seat with an upright torso as your back is resting against the backrest.
4. Place one foot against the machine’s foot pedal, and push the pedal forward with your leg.
    1. This brings the handles up to a position you can comfortably reach.
    2. Keep the pedal pushed away during the next step, while keeping your other foot on the ground.
5. Raise your upper arms up near the sides of your head with your elbows bent enough to grasp the handle firmly with fairly straight wrists.
    1. Different machines have different handle types that will influence specifically how you should grasp the handle, but what chiefly matters is that your grip feels stable and comfortable.
    2. From this position, hold your elbows high while keeping tucked in rather than being meaningfully flared out away from your sides.
6. Bring the foot pedal back to its starting position by bending your knee, which will shift the machine’s resistance to your arms. Simultaneously, lower the handle behind your head by fully bending your elbows while keeping your upper arms stationary.
    1. The degree to which you can bend your elbows while keeping your upper arms raised up will be dependent on your triceps flexibility.
    2. Allow your elbows to bend through the full range that feels comfortable, and you will normally feel a stretching sensation in your triceps.
7. Lift your foot off the foot pedal and place it flat on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Press the handle up overhead by straightening your elbows while keeping your upper arms stationary.
    1. At the top of the movement, your elbows should be fully straightened with your upper arms in the same position in which they started.
    2. While you move your forearms through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbows to lower the handle behind your head in a controlled manner.
    1. Lower the bar to the starting position with your elbows fully bent through your full range of motion.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows tucked in rather than meaningfully flaring them out to your sides.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the machine’s resistance rapidly pull the handle back down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you are using a machine with a foot pedal, push the pedal forward with your leg until you are no longer supporting any of the machine’s resistance with your arms. Then let go of the handle and bend your knee to return the foot pedal to its initial position.
    2. If your machine lacks a foot pedal, allow the machine’s arms to rest fully on the stoppers underneath them before releasing the handle.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all three heads of your triceps, which constitute the majority of muscle mass in your upper arms.## Setting up
1. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, and sit down on the seat.
2. Adjust the height of the machine’s thigh pad to a level that allows you to fit the lowest portion of your thigh just underneath it, while the front half of your foot is situated on the foot plate.
    1. To help find this point, you can initially place the front half of your foot on the foot plate while your heel is lower than your toes.
    2. Avoid setting the pad to a height that requires you to lift your heel up higher than your toes in order for your thigh to press snugly into the pad. The weakest portion of a calf raise occurs when your heel is fully raised. Consequently, it can be difficult to re-rack the weight at the end of a set if your calf is fatigued, and you have to raise your heel meaningfully before adjusting the machine’s stopper to accept the weight.
3. With the front half of your foot on the foot plate, scoot your knee under and in front of the thigh pad until your lower thigh is securely under the pad.
    1. You can optionally use your hands to help pull the thigh pad up as you scoot forward.
    2. Because this is a single leg variation, you can leave your other foot resting on the ground. All the instructions will apply to the leg on the foot plate, since that is the side you are about to train.
4. Raise your heel up just enough to disengage the machine’s stopper by operating its lever with one of your hands.
    1. You should be primarily bearing weight through the ball of your foot as the back half of your foot is hanging off the foot plate.
    2. For tracking purposes, you can consider your first full rep to start after you have completely lowered your heel when performing step 1 of the exercise technique for the first time in a set. The initial short range of motion heel raise you perform to disengage the stopper does not constitute a full rep.
## Exercise technique
1. In a controlled manner, allow your heel to descend toward the ground through the available range of motion of your ankle while continuing to keep the front half of your foot on top of the foot plate.
    1. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf and/or ankle at the bottom position.
    2. Specifically how far you are able to lower your heel will be dependent on your mobility, but individuals with normal ankle mobility can position their heels lower than the balls of their feet at the bottom.
2. Push through the ball of your foot to raise your heel into the air through the available range of motion of your ankle.
    1. At the top, your heel should be higher than the ball of your foot.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. At times during a set, you may feel that your foot slides back on the foot plate. If that occurs, you can use your hands to pull up the bottom of the thigh pad to make it easier for you to re-position your foot forward on the foot plate.
4. After finishing the lowering phase of your rep, raise your heel high enough to engage the machine’s stopper with your hand.
    1. If you performed as many reps as you possibly could during your set, you may have to rest in the bottom position for several seconds until your calf is ready to lift your heel high enough to engage the stopper.
5. With the stopper engaged, lower your heel to let the weight rest on the stopper before repeating the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your soleus, but it will not train your gastrocnemius nearly as well as calf raise variations performed with straight knees.## Setting up
1. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, and sit down on the seat.
2. Adjust the height of the machine’s thigh pad to a level that allows you to fit the lowest portions of your thighs just underneath it, while the front halves of your feet are situated on the foot plate.
    1. To help find this point, you can initially place the front halves of your feet on the foot plate while your heels are lower than the balls of your feet.
    2. Avoid setting the pad to a height that requires you to lift your heels up higher than the balls of your feet in order for your thighs to press snugly into the pad. The weakest portion of a calf raise occurs when your heels are fully raised. Consequently, it can be difficult to re-rack the weight at the end of a set if your calves are fatigued, and you have to raise your heels meaningfully before adjusting the machine’s stopper to accept the weight.
3. With the front halves of your feet on the foot plate, scoot your knees under and in front of the thigh pad until your lower thighs are securely under the pad.
    1. You can optionally use your hands to help pull the thigh pad up as you scoot forward.
4. Raise your heels up just enough to disengage the machine’s stopper by operating its lever with one of your hands.
    1. You should be primarily bearing weight through the balls of your feet as the back half of your feet are hanging off the foot plate.
    2. For tracking purposes, you can consider your first full rep to start after you have completely lowered your heels when performing step 1 of the exercise technique for the first time in a set. The initial short range of motion heel raise you perform to disengage the stopper does not constitute a full rep.
## Exercise technique
1. In a controlled manner, allow your heels to descend toward the ground through the available range of motion of your ankles while continuing to keep the front halves of your feet on top of the foot plate.
    1. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can, and you will likely feel a stretching sensation in your calf/and or ankle at the bottom position.
    2. Specifically how far you are able to lower your heels will be dependent on your mobility, but individuals with normal ankle mobility can position their heels lower than the balls of their feet at the bottom.
2. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels into the air through the available range of motion of your ankles.
    1. At the top, your heels should be higher than the balls of your feet.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. At times during a set, you may feel that your feet slide back somewhat on the foot plate. If that occurs, you can use your hands to pull up the bottom of the thigh pad to make it easier for you to re-position your feet forward on the foot plate.
4. After finishing the lowering phase of your rep, raise your heels high enough to engage the machine’s stopper with your hand.
    1. If you performed as many reps as you possibly could during your set, you may have to rest in the bottom position for several seconds until your calves are ready to lift your heels up high enough to engage the stopper.
5. With the stopper engaged, lower your heels to let the weight rest on the stopper.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your soleus, but it will not train your gastrocnemius nearly as well as calf raise variations performed with straight knees.## Setting up
1. With your hands close together and palms facing back toward you, firmly grasp the handle of one kettlebell.
2. In an approximately shoulder width stance, stand upright while holding the kettlebell in front of your legs with straight elbows.
3. Slightly (such as by about 10 degrees) lean forward at your hips by sitting your buttocks backward slightly.
    1. While performing a very small hip hinge motion that results in your knees barely bending, keep your chest up and lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. As an alternative to this slight forward lean, you can keep your torso completely upright if you prefer to do so, but some people find that slightly hinging their hips can help the exercise feel more comfortable for their shoulders.
    3. Regardless of whether you lean forward slightly or stay fully upright, you should maintain the same torso position throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the kettlebell upward by driving your elbows upward and out to your sides through a range of motion that feels comfortable to your shoulders.
    1. Your mobility will affect how high you are comfortably able to lift the kettlebell, and there is no need to force your shoulders into a range that causes any discomfort.
    2. While different individuals may be able to lift higher up and others not quite as high, people often can lift their upper arms until they are close to parallel to the ground for a comfortable and effective range of motion.
    3. For cuing your side delts, it can help to focus on leading with your elbows and allowing your hands to follow them naturally as your elbows naturally bend.
    4. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    5. Throughout the exercise, keep the kettlebell close to your body, and avoid moving your legs or lower back.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering your elbows in toward your sides in a controlled manner to lower the kettlebell.
    1. You finish in the starting position as your elbows are straight, and the kettlebell is directly in front of your legs.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weight abruptly pull you down in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt, upper trap, and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt.## Setting up
A chest press machine with vertical handles is required to perform this neutral grip variation. If your machine only has horizontal handles, you can select the “seated overhand grip pin-loaded machine chest press” from this database as an alternative.

Certain chest press machines have a foot pedal that you can press to move the machine’s arms while setting up. Other machines lack a foot pedal but commonly allow you to modify the starting position with adjustable arms or a back pad that can be moved forward and backward. Some of the specific setup details differ depending on which type of machine you are using, so the setup for each approach is described separately. However, the exercise technique can be very similar for either option, so one technique section is provided with a couple of details that depend on which type of machine you utilize.

### **Setting up a machine without a foot pedal**

1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with the lower half of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable and the pressing motion to feel natural during the exercise.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust either aspect, position its arms or back pad to a setting that enables you to press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Some machines allow you to increase the range of motion by placing the arms further back toward the back pad, while others allow you to move the back pad forward, closer toward the arms. However, certain machine models may not permit you to adjust either part.
    2. If you have normal shoulder mobility, you can initially position the arms or the seat to a setting that results in the handles being near the front of your chest (when viewed from the side) once you are seated. This commonly involves setting the arms as far backward or the seat as far forward as the machine enables.
    3. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles with proper positioning during step 5, you can adjust the machine to increase the distance between the back pad and the handles until you can do so.
    4. The ideal setting should allow your elbows to start as far behind your torso as is comfortable for your shoulders while keeping your elbows level with the handles.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. With your palms facing in toward each other and elbows bent, firmly grasp the vertical handles. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles and position your elbows directly behind them, you may have to position the machine’s arms further forward or the back pad further back before resuming your setup.
    3. Aligning your elbows directly behind the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow tuck during this exercise.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.

### **Setting up a machine with a foot pedal**

1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with the lower half of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable and the pressing motion to feel natural during the exercise.
2. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
4. Place one foot against the machine’s foot pedal, and push the pedal forward with your leg.
    1. This brings the handles forward to a position you can comfortably reach.
    2. Keep the pedal pushed away during the next two steps, while keeping your other foot on the ground.
5. With your palms facing in toward each other and elbows bent, firmly grasp the vertical handles. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbows directly behind the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow tuck during this exercise.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
7. Bring the foot pedal back to its starting position by bending your knee, which will shift the machine’s resistance to your upper body. Simultaneously, allow your elbows to move backward through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Keep your elbows level with your wrists and the handles.
    2. How far your elbows can comfortably move backward while keeping them aligned with the handles will be dependent on your mobility.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range, but you should not allow the machine to pull you into a position that causes shoulder discomfort.
8. Lift your foot off the foot pedal and place it flat on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles forward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. Keep your elbows stacked behind your wrists and the handles while driving your elbows forward.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handles to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and wrists aligned behind the handles as your elbows progressively move backward while bending.
    2. Move through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
    3. If your machine lacks a foot pedal, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack at the end range to confirm that you have moved through the full range in a controlled manner. If your machine has a foot pedal, you can finish the lowering phase once you can no longer comfortably move your elbows backward while keeping your elbows directly behind the handles.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. If your machine has a foot pedal, push the pedal forward with your leg until you are no longer supporting any of the machine’s resistance with your upper body. Then let go of the handles and bend your knee to bring the foot pedal back to its initial position.
    2. If your machine lacks a foot pedal, simply rest the machine’s arms on the stoppers after the lowering phase of your last rep before letting go of the handles.
## Description
This exercise predominantly trains your pec major, front delts, and triceps with a technique that some people find feels more comfortable for their shoulders compared to other presses.## Setting up
A chest press machine with vertical handles is required to perform this neutral grip variation. If your machine only has horizontal handles, you can select the overhand grip variation from this database, which uses the name “seated plate-loaded machine chest press.” 

1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with the lower half of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable and the pressing motion to feel natural during the exercise.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust either aspect, position its arms or back pad to a setting that enables you to press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Some machines allow you to increase the range of motion by placing the arms further back toward the back pad, while others allow you to move the back pad forward, closer toward the arms. However, certain machine models may not permit you to adjust either part.
    2. If you have normal shoulder mobility, you can initially position the arms or the seat to a setting that results in the handles being near the front of your chest (when viewed from the side) once you are seated. This commonly involves setting the arms as far backward or the seat as far forward as the machine enables.
    3. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles with proper positioning during step 5, you can adjust the machine to increase the distance between the back pad and the handles until you can do so.
    4. The ideal setting should allow your elbows to start as far behind your torso as is comfortable for your shoulders while keeping your elbows level with the handles.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. With your palms facing in toward each other and elbows bent, firmly grasp the vertical handles. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles and position your elbows directly behind them, you may have to position the machine’s arms further forward or the back pad further back before resuming your setup.
    3. Aligning your elbows directly behind the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow tuck during this exercise.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles forward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. Keep your elbows stacked behind your wrists and the handles while driving your elbows forward.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handles to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and wrists aligned behind the handles as your elbows progressively move backward while bending.
    2. Move through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
    3. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers behind them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise predominantly trains your pec major, front delts, and triceps with a technique that some people find feels more comfortable for their shoulders compared to other presses.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. You can alternatively leave the machine initially unloaded if you are new to this exercise and have yet to determine which lever arm and/or belt attachment points are best for you.
2. Grab the belt, and step up onto the center of the platform with the machine’s lever arm between your feet.
3. Situate the belt around your hips so that the back of the belt is securely resting on top of your buttocks in a comfortable position.
4. Squat down, and connect the belt to the lever arm’s attachment point in a way that will comfortably allow you to squat through your full range of motion.
    1. Different machine models vary in their design, since some machines have a lever arm with a single attachment point, while others have a lever arm with multiple attachment points. Similarly, some belts have a single attachment point that can be connected to the lever arm, while others have multiple attachment points.
    2. When using a lever arm with multiple attachment points, the machine will provide less resistance when using a point near the base of the lever arm compared to the free end of the lever arm. Consequently, the specific lever attachment point can affect the difficulty of each rep, in addition to how much weight you add to the machine via plates.
    3. Which lever arm attachment point you use may also affect how comfortable the exercise feels to you, and there is no way to know which one feels best to you without personally using them. If you are new to this exercise, you can initially pick one attachment point and then try out different points over time to find which one you most prefer.
    4. If your belt has multiple attachment points (including a chain that can be varied in length to form different attachment points), which one you select can affect the exercise’s range of motion. While you may have to switch to different ones later on, you can initially try the attachment points closest to the middle of the belt, since using them will allow you to squat through the longest range of motion. Trying out different belt attachment points may be required because you have to continue setting up to determine if the pair you selected is ideal for you as an individual.
    5. If you select attachment points too close to the ends of the belt, the end of the cable that is linked to the belt will bottom out prematurely when squatting down, which will restrict you to performing partial range of motion squats. The con of using attachment points close to the middle of the belt is that they can make it more difficult to get into the starting position. Consequently, you ideally do not want to select attachments that are any closer toward the middle of the belt than what is needed for you to squat through a full range of motion.
5. Stand upright, and align your feet so that the lever arm’s attachment point is between the middle of your feet (rather than being in front of your feet or behind them).
6. Position your feet reasonably near shoulder width apart with your toes pointing somewhat out to the sides and the soles of your feet fully on the platform.
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up close to this stance allows them to belt squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when belt squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Using a stance that is mildly wider than your typical squat stance may sometimes reduce discomfort from the pressure the belt can exert on the groin.
7. Raise your arms and grasp the handle with your hands near shoulder width apart.
    1. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
8. Pull the handle back toward you to disengage the safety arm if you are using a machine that allows you to do so.
    1. Some machine models have the safety arm automatically disengage once you stand upright, but others require you to disengage it manually.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner.
    1. Lightly use the handles to help you feel stable with a mostly upright torso while allowing your torso naturally to lean mildly forward at your hips to maintain your balance.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your entire set.
    3. Allow your knees to track forward to load your quads and help you maintain your balance.
    4. Aim to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. As you would during other squats, strive to keep your chest up and lower back flat rather than meaningfully rounding your back.
3. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion that is comfortably enabled by your mobility and your machine.
    1. When setting up appropriately and following the technique points listed under step 2, people can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground. However, what constitutes a full range of motion for a belt squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your heels lift off the platform or knees cave inward at the bottom, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
4. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the platform to extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Avoid pulling down on the handle with your arms, as you simply hold the handle for stability rather than using your upper body to help your legs.
    2. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, knees stacked over your feet, chest up, and lower back mostly flat.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
6. Once you are upright after your last rep, push the handle or safety lever forward to engage the safety arm before squatting down to return the weight onto the safety arm.
## Description
While also training your gluteus maximus and multiple hip adductor muscles, this exercise primarily targets your quads with minimal spinal loading.## Setting up
1. If you are using a multi-press machine that can be adjusted to a flat or incline press setting, set the back pad to its horizontal position.
2. If your machine lets you adjust the starting height of the handles, set the handles to a position that lets you press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Setting the handles to where they are approximately level with your chest when lying down on the back pad is an appropriate starting point for many lifters.
    2. From that starting point, you can specifically set the handles to the lowest height at which you can grasp them without any shoulder discomfort while keeping your elbows vertically stacked under your wrists.
    3. If your machine does not let you adjust the handle height and you cannot comfortably assume the starting position, a different chest press variation may be preferable for you as an individual.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Lie down with your back, buttocks, and head on the pad. Position yourself so that your lower-to-middle chest is aligned with the handles.
    1. Your elbows will naturally tuck closer to your sides as you position yourself further back on the pad (situating your shoulders horizontally further behind the handles).
    2. You can decide specifically where you lie down on the pad based on what feels comfortable to your shoulders throughout the exercise, while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If you experience shoulder discomfort when pressing with the handles near mid-chest, you can try positioning yourself with the handles by the lowest part of your chest to be able to press with a lower degree of elbow flare.
    4. Your feet should be flat on the ground in a stable position, stacked under your bent knees.
5. With your palms facing forward and elbows bent, firmly grasp the horizontally directed handles. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being in a vertical position.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbows directly under (rather than being in front of or behind when viewed from the side) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you positioned your body appropriately on the pad.
    3. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best while stacking your elbows under the handles.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the handles upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain stacked under your wrists and the handles when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back and buttocks snugly against the pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Keep your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles as you allow your elbows to move outward and downward while bending.
    2. Descend through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    3. If you can comfortably lower the handles fully to the starting position, you can lightly tap the weights you selected to the rest of the weight stack. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the stoppers (that are positioned under the arms) to a height that allows you to press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with vertical forearms, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
    3. Some machine models do not allow you to adjust the stoppers.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine after determining your appropriate stopper height.
3. Lie down with your back, buttocks, and head on the pad. Position yourself so that your lower-to-middle chest is aligned with the middle of the parallel handles.
    1. The proper positioning should allow you to grasp the parallel handles with your elbows directly under the handles as your forearms are in a vertical position. This will contribute to the exercise feeling natural and comfortable.
    2. Your elbows will naturally tuck closer to your sides as you position yourself further back on the pad (situating your shoulders horizontally further behind the handles).
    3. Your feet should be flat on the ground in a stable position, stacked under your bent knees.
4. With your palms facing in toward each other and elbows bent, firmly grasp the parallel handles. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being in a vertical position.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles and position your elbows directly under them, you may have to increase the height of the machine’s stoppers before resuming your setup.
    3. Aligning your elbows directly under the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow tuck during this exercise, provided that you positioned your body appropriately on the pad.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain stacked under your wrists and the handles when viewed from the side.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back and buttocks snugly against the pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers under them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles as you allow your elbows to move downward while bending.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise predominantly trains your pec major, front delts, and triceps with a technique that some people find feels more comfortable for their shoulders compared to other presses.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the stoppers (that are positioned under the arms) to a height that allows you to press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with vertical forearms, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
    3. Some machine models do not allow you to adjust the stoppers.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine after determining your appropriate stopper height.
3. Lie down with your back, buttocks, and head on the pad. Position yourself so that your lower-to-middle chest is aligned with the handles.
    1. Your elbows will naturally tuck closer to your sides as you position yourself further back on the pad (situating your shoulders horizontally further behind the handles).
    2. You can decide specifically where you lie down on the pad based on what feels comfortable to your shoulders throughout the exercise, while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If you experience shoulder discomfort when pressing with the handles near mid-chest, you can try positioning yourself with the handles by the lowest part of your chest to be able to press with a lower degree of elbow flare.
    4. Your feet should be flat on the ground in a stable position, stacked under your bent knees.
4. With your palms facing forward and elbows bent, firmly grasp the horizontally directed handles. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being in a vertical position.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles and position your elbows directly under them, you may have to increase the height of the machine’s stoppers before resuming your setup.
    3. Aligning your elbows directly under (rather than being in front or behind when viewed from the side) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you positioned your body appropriately on the pad.
    4. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best while stacking your elbows under the handles.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain stacked under your wrists and the handles when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back and buttocks snugly against the pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers under them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles as you allow your elbows to move outward and downward while bending.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that places the handles near your lower chest or slightly lower once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, but the ideal seat height specifically should allow you to train through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably allows.
    2. Individuals with limited mobility may have to position the seat higher to reach the handles, while those with greater mobility may benefit from positioning the seat lower to train through a full range of motion.
    3. If the seat is excessively high, your range of motion for this exercise will be limited.
    4. If the seat is excessively low, you may not be able to reach the handles with proper positioning. Forcing your elbows back beyond what your mobility permits in an effort to reach the handles may increase the risk of experiencing shoulder discomfort.
    5. Some trial and error may be needed to find the best height for you as an individual. When continuing to set up, you can always adjust the seat height if you determine the initial height is suboptimal for you.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your feet flat on the ground.
    1. If your machine has a back pad, position the back of your hip bones against it.
    2. If your machine has a chest pad, sit down toward the back of the seat to provide some space for you to lean forward mildly during a subsequent step.
4. If your machine has a thigh pad, snugly position it on top of your thighs. If your machine has a seat belt, securely fasten it around your waist.
    1. Either option will help you anchor your body.
    2. Certain machines lack a thigh pad or seat belt, which can contribute to the exercise being less stable.
5. If your machine allows you to rotate its handles inward or outward, position them outward.
    1. Relative to the closer handle setting, the wider setting facilitates a greater degree of elbow flare that can help you train your chest more effectively during your dip.
6. Mildly lean forward at your hips while keeping your back flat and pulling your shoulder blades back.
    1. A mild forward lean can help you emphasize your chest to a greater degree compared to if you sat fully upright.
    2. Specifically how far you lean forward is up to your preference and what feels comfortable to you during the exercise.
    3. Pulling your shoulder blades back will place you into a “proud chest” posture and prevent your shoulders from rounding forward into a slouched position. Keep your shoulder blades pulled back while continuing to set up.
7. Move your elbows back behind your torso while flaring them somewhat out away from your sides and bending them reasonably close to 90 degrees.
    1. In contrast to a triceps-focused dip, you are intentionally flaring your elbows out to a degree for this variation rather than keeping them tucked very close to your sides. However, make sure to avoid excessively flaring them.
    2. The appropriate degree of elbow flare should feel comfortable to your shoulders while also permitting you to feel meaningful tension in your chest during the exercise. Specifically how much elbow flare someone should utilize will vary among different individuals and be influenced by shoulder mobility.
    3. Aiming to flare your elbows less than 45 degrees is generally appropriate for most people. Zero degrees of flare would mean your elbows are pointing straight back, parallel to each other, while 90 degrees of flare would mean they are pointing straight out to the sides.
8. After moving your arms to the end range that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits, confirm whether the handles are at an appropriate height for you.
    1. If the handles are now just within your reach, the seat height you initially selected is appropriate.
    2. If you cannot reach the handles without shrugging your shoulders up or tipping them forward, raise the seat before resuming your setup.
    3. If the handles are lower than what you can comfortably reach from this position, you can lower the seat to facilitate a greater degree of elbow bend before resuming your setup.
9. Firmly grasp the handles with the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) against them.
    1. While almost all machines allow you to use a neutral grip with your palms facing in toward each other, some machines also have handles that allow you to use an overhand grip with your palms facing backward. If your machine enables you to use both grips, select whichever feels most comfortable to you.
    2. Hold the handles with mostly straight wrists. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Push the handles down until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. While driving your elbows downward, they will simultaneously move forward and inward.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your torso mildly leaned forward and feet planted on the ground. Due to your torso lean, you should finish with your elbows in front of your sides rather than being directly by your sides.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing the handles to lift up to the starting position.
    1. Bend your elbows while allowing them to move upward, backward, and mildly to moderately out away from your sides.
    2. Avoid letting your shoulders shrug up or roll forward.
    3. With the appropriate seat height, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders and/or chest at the end range, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain. If you feel joint discomfort at the end range, increase the seat height before resuming your set.
    4. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers under them to confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also effectively developing the lateral and medial heads of your triceps, this exercise can train your pec major and front delts.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with your middle-to-upper chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is excessively low or high, the exercise may feel awkward and uncomfortable.
2. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the stoppers (that are positioned under the arms) to a height that allows you to press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with proper positioning, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
    3. Certain machine models do not allow you to adjust the stoppers.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Lie down on the machine’s back pad with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against it.
    1. Your buttocks should be resting on the seat, and your feet should be flat on the ground under your bent knees.
    2. If the back pad is sufficiently tall, rest your head against it. Otherwise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. Firmly grasp the handles while positioning your elbows directly under your wrists such that your forearms are in a vertical position when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Whether you can grasp the handles with your palms facing each other (i.e., neutral grip) or facing forward (i.e., overhand grip) depends on how the handles are designed for the specific machine you use.
    3. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
    4. Aligning your elbows directly under your wrists and the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows upward and in toward each other while progressively straightening them.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles to maintain vertical forearms when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, buttocks on the seat, back against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides to the degree that keeps your elbows vertically stacked under your wrists and the handles.
    2. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers behind them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
