## Description
While also effectively developing the lateral and medial heads of your triceps, this exercise can train your pec major and front delts.## Setting up
1. If you are using a multi-press machine that can be adjusted to a flat or incline press setting, position the back pad to the incline setting.
2. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with your middle-to-upper chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is excessively low or high, the exercise may feel awkward and uncomfortable.
3. If your machine lets you adjust the starting height of the handles, set the handles to a position that lets you press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Setting the handles to where they are approximately level with your chest when lying down on the back pad is an appropriate starting point for many lifters.
    2. From that starting point, you can specifically set the handles to the lowest height at which you can grasp them without any shoulder discomfort while keeping your elbows vertically stacked under your wrists.
    3. If your machine does not let you adjust the handle height and you cannot comfortably assume the starting position, a different incline press variation may be preferable for you as an individual.
4. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
5. Lie down on the machine’s back pad with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against it.
    1. Your buttocks should be resting on the seat, and your feet should be flat on the ground under your bent knees.
    2. If the back pad is sufficiently tall, rest your head against it. Otherwise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
6. Firmly grasp the horizontally directed handles with an overhand grip. Position your elbows directly under your wrists such that your forearms are in a vertical position when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
    3. Aligning your elbows directly under your wrists and the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
7. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows upward and in toward each other while progressively straightening them.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles to maintain vertical forearms when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, buttocks on the seat, back against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides to the degree that keeps your elbows vertically stacked under your wrists and the handles.
    2. You can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack at the end range to confirm that you have moved through the full range in a controlled manner
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
1. Use the machine’s loading pin to select your desired level of assistance, and ensure that the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is positioned down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you select heavier loads from the assisted dip machine, since the weight determines the extent to which the machine will support your bodyweight.
    2. Certain machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but essentially the same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates.
3. If your machine allows you to rotate its parallel handles inward or outward, position them outward.
    1. Relative to the closer handle setting, the wider setting facilitates a greater degree of elbow flare that can help you train your chest more effectively during your dip.
    2. While using wider handles is standard when seeking to emphasize chest training during a dip, you have the option of selecting the closer setting if using the wide handles feels uncomfortable for your shoulders. The wide handle setting on certain machines may be wider than ideal for some lifters, particularly for people who have rather narrow frames and/or those with pre-existing shoulder issues.
    3. Note that a variety of machines do not allow you to adjust the handle position.
4. Firmly grasp the parallel handles with your palms facing each other. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
5. One leg at a time, kneel onto the machine’s knee pad (if your machine has a knee pad) or step onto the machine’s foot bar (if your machine has a foot bar) while continuing to grasp the handles firmly. Maintain straight elbows when doing so.
    1. If using a machine with a knee pad, you will keep your knees bent on the pad throughout your set, but if you are using a machine with a foot bar, you will keep your knees straight while standing on the bar throughout your set.
6. Mildly lean forward at your hips while keeping your back flat and pulling your shoulder blades back. Avoid letting your shoulders shrug up.
    1. A mild forward lean can help you emphasize your chest more than if your torso were fully upright.
    2. Specifically how far you lean forward is up to your preference and what feels comfortable to you during the exercise.
    3. Pulling your shoulder blades back will place you into a “proud chest” posture and prevent your shoulders from rounding forward into a slouched position.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While allowing them to flare out somewhat away from your sides, bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, continue keeping your torso mildly leaned forward and avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up.
    2. In contrast to a triceps-focused dip, you are intentionally flaring your elbows out to a degree for this variation rather than keeping them tucked very close to your sides. However, make sure to avoid excessively flaring them.
    3. The appropriate degree of elbow flare should feel comfortable for your shoulders while also permitting you to feel meaningful tension in your chest during the exercise. Specifically how much elbow flare someone should utilize will vary among different individuals and be influenced by shoulder mobility.
    4. Aiming to flare your elbows less than 45 degrees is generally appropriate for most people. Zero degrees of flare would mean your elbows are pointing straight back, parallel to each other, while 90 degrees of flare would mean they are pointing straight out to the sides.
    5. If your machine allows you to use moderately wide handles, the appropriate degree of elbow flare can often naturally occur without having to think about flaring your elbows out, provided that you do not intentionally try to tuck your elbows inward.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their upper arms are at least parallel to the ground, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by pushing through the handles to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows simultaneously in toward your sides and downward.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down onto the foot plate.
## Description
While also training the front delt and upper pec major, this exercise will particularly develop the middle and lower regions of the pec major, along with the triceps.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine to utilize your desired level of assistance and ensure that the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is positioned down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you add heavier loads to the assisted dip/pull-up machine since the weight determines the extent to which the machine will support your body weight.
    2. Certain machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but essentially the same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates.
3. If your machine allows you to rotate its parallel handles inward or outward, position them outward.
    1. Relative to the closer handle setting, the wider setting facilitates a greater degree of elbow flare that can help you train your chest more effectively during your dip.
    2. While using wider handles is standard when seeking to emphasize chest training during a dip, you have the option of selecting the closer setting if using the wide handles feels uncomfortable for your shoulders. The wide handle setting on certain machines may be wider than ideal for some lifters, particularly for people who have rather narrow frames and/or those with pre-existing shoulder issues.
    3. Note that a variety of machines do not allow you to adjust the handle position.
4. Firmly grasp the parallel handles with your palms facing each other. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
5. One leg at a time, kneel onto the machine’s knee pad (if your machine has a knee pad) or step onto the machine’s foot bar (if your machine has a foot bar) while continuing to grasp the handles firmly. Maintain straight elbows when doing so.
    1. If using a machine with a knee pad, you will keep your knees bent on the pad throughout your set, but if you are using a machine with a foot bar, you will keep your knees straight while standing on the bar throughout your set.
6. Mildly lean forward at your hips while keeping your back flat and pulling your shoulder blades back. Avoid letting your shoulders shrug up.
    1. A mild forward lean can help you emphasize your chest more than if your torso were fully upright.
    2. Specifically how far you lean forward is up to your preference and what feels comfortable to you during the exercise.
    3. Pulling your shoulder blades back will place you into a “proud chest” posture and prevent your shoulders from rounding forward into a slouched position.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While allowing them to flare out somewhat away from your sides, bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, continue keeping your torso mildly leaned forward and avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up.
    2. In contrast to a triceps-focused dip, you are intentionally flaring your elbows out to a degree for this variation rather than keeping them tucked very close to your sides. However, make sure to avoid excessively flaring them.
    3. The appropriate degree of elbow flare should feel comfortable for your shoulders while also permitting you to feel meaningful tension in your chest during the exercise. Specifically how much elbow flare someone should utilize will vary among different individuals and be influenced by shoulder mobility.
    4. Aiming to flare your elbows less than 45 degrees is generally appropriate for most people. Zero degrees of flare would mean your elbows are pointing straight back, parallel to each other, while 90 degrees of flare would mean they are pointing straight out to the sides.
    5. If your machine allows you to use moderately wide handles, the appropriate degree of elbow flare can often naturally occur without having to think about flaring your elbows out, provided that you do not intentionally try to tuck your elbows inward.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point in which their upper arms are at least parallel to the ground, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by pushing through the handles to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows simultaneously in toward your sides and downward.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down onto the foot plate.
## Description
While also training the front delt and upper pec major, this exercise will particularly develop the middle and lower regions of the pec major, along with the triceps.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine to utilize your desired level of assistance and ensure that the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is positioned down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you add heavier loads to the assisted dip/pull-up machine since the weight determines the extent to which the machine will support your body weight.
    2. Certain machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but essentially the same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates.
3. If your machine allows you to rotate its parallel handles inward or outward, position them inward unless your body’s width restricts the range of motion for the closer setting.
    1. The closer setting facilitates a greater degree of elbow tuck, which is typically desirable when striving to emphasize the triceps and/or the front delts to a greater degree than the chest during this exercise.
    2. However, the closer setting may position the handles excessively close for individuals with rather wide body types.
    3. Even if your body does not restrict your range of motion with the closer handle setting, you have the option of selecting the wider setting if it just feels more comfortable to you. However, that may contribute to the exercise being more chest-dominant.
    4. Note that a variety of machines do not allow you to adjust the handle position.
4. Firmly grasp the parallel handles with your palms facing each other. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
5. One leg at a time, kneel onto the machine’s knee pad (if your machine has a knee pad) or step onto the machine’s foot bar (if your machine has a foot bar) while continuing to grasp the handles firmly. Maintain straight elbows when doing so.
    1. If using a machine with a knee pad, you will keep your knees bent on the pad throughout your set, but if you are using a machine with a foot bar, you will keep your knees straight while standing on the bar throughout your set.
6. Assume a “proud chest” posture as you pull your shoulder blades back with an upright torso.
    1. Avoid letting your shoulders shrug up throughout the exercise.
    2. In contrast to a “chest dip,” you want to avoid leaning forward at your hips during this “triceps dip.”
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While keeping them tucked close to your sides, bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, continue keeping your torso upright and avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up.
    2. Avoid flaring your elbows out away from your sides. When viewed from behind, your elbows should remain stacked over your wrists and the handles rather than having your elbows move outside your wrists.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their elbows are bent by at least 90 degrees, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by pushing through the handles to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows straight down.
    2. You should finish with your elbows close to your sides and your arms directed down toward the ground.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down onto the foot plate.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the lateral and medial heads of your triceps while also training the long head to a lesser degree, along with your front delts and pec major.## Setting up
1. Approach the pair of bars/handles that you will use for your dip.
    1. Ideally, select a pair of bars that enables you to use a mildly outside shoulder-width grip for this variation.
    2. A variety of pieces of equipment can be used to perform this exercise.
    3. You can use bars that are completely parallel to each other or angled “V-grip” bars that converge to be closer together near the base and wider apart at the free ends. In contrast to stationary parallel bars, angled bars allow you to adjust your grip width based on specifically where you grasp them. Gymnastics rings can also be used to train dips, but the ring dip is presented as a distinct variation in this database.
    4. Gyms commonly have a power tower (which has dip bars protruding from its vertical frame and often has a pull-up bar and/or forearm pads to use for leg raises) or a dip stand/station (which is specifically designed for dips).
    5. You can alternatively use a dip attachment that is connected to a power rack.
    6. Some assisted dip/pull-up machines also permit you to flip the knee pad or foot bar up, therefore enabling you to train dips without machine assistance. Certain machine models allow you to adjust the distance between the bars and position them mildly outside shoulder width apart.
    7. Other options include portable parallettes (which can be positioned closer or wider apart to adjust your grip width) or parallel bars located at a playground.
2. Firmly grasp the bars with your palms facing each other and your elbows straight. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Before you can grasp the bars with straight elbows, you may have to step up onto a step (which may be built into the equipment you use or require you to place a mobile step near the bars), depending on the design of your equipment.
    2. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
    3. If you are using angled bars, you can grasp the bars at the point where they are mildly outside shoulder width apart for you. The grip width you use ideally should let your elbows naturally flare out somewhat away from your sides without prompting the exercise to feel uncomfortable for your shoulders. Feel free to try out different grip widths over time to determine what feels best to you.
    4. When using dip bars attached to a power rack, some people may have to face away from the rack to train through the full range of motion. The leaned forward position from a chest dip can result in the base of the attachment restricting depth for certain lifters if they face toward the base of the dip bars.
3. While keeping your elbows straightened, lift your feet off the ground.
    1. Depending on the equipment you use, you may have to bend your knees in order for your feet to clear the ground, or you may be able to keep your knees straight.
    2. With straight knees, you can keep the inner aspect of your feet against each other. If you bend your knees, you can cross your ankles.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
5. Lean your torso and head moderately forward while placing your legs in a position that helps you naturally maintain this forward lean.
    1. The appropriate degree of lean should result in your chest clearly being in front of your hips (when viewed from the side), but your torso should still be closer to a vertical position than a horizontal position.
    2. Leaning forward near 30 degrees from vertical is generally appropriate, but specifically how far you lean forward is up to your preference and what feels comfortable to you during the exercise.
    3. Since there is no single correct leg position, you can also position them where you personally prefer. The key is to select a position that allows you to lean your torso forward. Some people find that tucking their legs forward helps them shift their hips back and correspondingly lean their torso forward, while others prefer to keep their thighs aligned with their torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While allowing them to flare out somewhat away from your sides, bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, continue keeping your torso moderately leaned forward, and avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up.
    2. Rather than letting them swing, keep your legs stationary relative to your hips until you finish your set.
    3. In contrast to a triceps-focused dip, you are intentionally flaring your elbows out to a degree for this variation rather than keeping them tucked very close to your sides. However, make sure to avoid excessively flaring them.
    4. The appropriate degree of elbow flare should feel comfortable for your shoulders while also permitting you to feel meaningful tension in your chest during the exercise. Specifically how much elbow flare someone should utilize will vary among different individuals and be influenced by shoulder mobility.
    5. Aiming to flare your elbows less than 45 degrees is generally appropriate for most people. Zero degrees of flare would mean your elbows are pointing straight back, parallel to each other, while 90 degrees of flare would mean they are pointing straight out to the sides.
    6. Provided that you do not intentionally try to tuck your elbows inward, the appropriate degree of elbow flare can often naturally occur without having to think about flaring your elbows out if your bars are mildly outside shoulder width.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their upper arms are at least parallel to the ground, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Once you have finished lowering to your end range, pause in that bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Continue to stay braced with your shoulder blades pulled back and down.
    2. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Reverse the movement by pushing through the bars to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows simultaneously in toward your sides and downward.
6. Alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down from the bars.
## Description
While also training the front delt and upper pec major, this exercise will particularly develop the middle and lower regions of the pec major, along with the triceps.## Setting up
1. Approach the pair of bars/handles that you will use for your dip.
    1. Ideally, select a pair of bars that enables you to use a mildly outside shoulder-width grip for this variation.
    2. A variety of pieces of equipment can be used to perform this exercise.
    3. You can use bars that are completely parallel to each other or angled “V-grip” bars that converge to be closer together near the base and wider apart at the free ends. In contrast to stationary parallel bars, angled bars allow you to adjust your grip width based on specifically where you grasp them. Gymnastics rings can also be used to train dips, but the ring dip is presented as a distinct variation in this database.
    4. Gyms commonly have a power tower (which has dip bars protruding from its vertical frame and often has a pull-up bar and/or forearm pads to use for leg raises) or a dip stand/station (which is specifically designed for dips).
    5. You can alternatively use a dip attachment that is connected to a power rack.
    6. Some assisted dip/pull-up machines also permit you to flip the knee pad or foot bar up, therefore enabling you to train dips without machine assistance. Certain machine models allow you to adjust the distance between the bars and position them mildly outside shoulder width apart.
    7. Other options include portable parallettes (which can be positioned closer or wider apart to adjust your grip width) or parallel bars located at a playground.
2. While standing in front of the bars, add your desired load (in the form of weight plates or a kettlebell) to a dip/pull-up belt you have around your waist.
    1. The back of your belt should be securely resting on top of your buttocks. You can loop the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) through the center hole of a weight plate or under the handle of a kettlebell. If you have a loading pin for your belt, you would alternatively loop the chain or strap through the loading pin after adding weight plates to it.
    2. It will be easiest if you carry the weight over to the dip bars and add the weight to your belt while you are under the bar. Adding the weight to your belt farther away from the bar before walking over to the bar can be awkward.
    3. As an alternative to using a pull-up/dip belt, you can wear a weighted vest.
    4. While less stable and more awkward than using a belt or weighted vest, you also have the option of holding a dumbbell between your thighs or feet. This approach is suboptimal and will limit how much weight you can use.
3. Firmly grasp the bars with your palms facing each other and your elbows straight. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Before you can grasp the bars with straight elbows, you may have to step up onto a step (which may be built into the equipment you use or require you to place a mobile step near the bars), depending on the design of your equipment.
    2. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
    3. If you are using angled bars, you can grasp the bars at the point where they are mildly outside shoulder width apart for you. The grip width you use ideally should let your elbows naturally flare out somewhat away from your sides without prompting the exercise to feel uncomfortable for your shoulders. Feel free to try out different grip widths over time to determine what feels best to you.
    4. When using dip bars attached to a power rack, some people may have to face away from the rack to train through the full range of motion. The leaned forward position from a chest dip can result in the base of the attachment restricting depth for certain lifters if they face toward the base of the dip bars.
4. While keeping your elbows straightened, lift your feet off the ground, and position your legs against the weight (if you are using a dip belt).
    1. Depending on the equipment you use, you may have to bend your knees in order for your feet to clear the ground, or you may be able to keep your knees straight.
    2. Based on your preference, you can spread your legs just enough to position the weight between them, or you can position your legs together with the weight just in front of them. Which option is most comfortable will be influenced by how you set up your belt and the shape of your weight(s). With either option, positioning your thighs in a close to vertical position is typically the most practical. Contacting the weight with your legs can help you prevent the weight from excessively swinging during your set.
    3. If you opt to bend your knees, you can cross your ankles for additional stability.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
6. Lean your torso and head moderately forward. 
    1. The appropriate degree of lean should result in your chest clearly being in front of your hips (when viewed from the side), but your torso should still be closer to a vertical position than a horizontal position.
    2. Leaning forward near 30 degrees from vertical is generally appropriate, but specifically how far you lean forward is up to your preference and what feels comfortable to you during the exercise.
