    1. Precisely how you should position your arms will be dependent on the specific machine model you are using.
    2. Some machines have handles to hold with your hands.
    3. Certain machines have a cylindrical chest pad underneath which you can hook your arms, while others have a rectangular pad on top of which you can rest your upper arms with your hands crossed and resting on your shoulders.
    4. In the starting position, your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl your spine as you pull the bottom of your ribs toward the tops of your hip bones in a controlled manner.
    1. Once you feel that you can no longer curl your spine through a range permitted by the machine that feels comfortable to your back, you have reached your end range. The machine may physically restrict your range of motion at a certain point, even if you have the mobility to crunch further.
    2. Avoid pulling down with your arms. Your arms should only move indirectly because they are attached to your torso, which is what you are directly moving. The function of your arms is merely to help keep the chest pad stabilized against your chest. You should not feel your back muscles contract like they would during a pulling exercise (such as a lat pulldown). If you feel like your back muscles are driving the movement, you can try relaxing your arms to the minimum required to keep the chest pad against your chest.
    3. To train your abs dynamically, you should be intentionally rounding your entire back, since this motion is performed by contraction of your abs. When rounding your back appropriately, you should feel meaningful tension in your abs. If you do not feel your abs engaging, you may be substituting hip or shoulder motion for the intended spinal motion.
    4. If you finish the movement with your back fairly flat as opposed to rounded, you most likely are primarily bending at your hips rather than curling your spine.
    5. It can help to think about crunching your elbows toward your knees.
    6. You can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the middle of your chest, and you are trying to point that closer toward the middle point between your knees.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner as you uncurl your back by flaring your ribs up and away from your hip bones.
    1. Move through the full range of motion permitted by the machine that feels comfortable to your lower back. This may involve letting the machine’s lever arm (onto which you loaded weight plates) lightly tap the stopper underneath it if the full range enabled by the machine feels comfortable to you.
    2. When uncurling your spine, your lower back will initially move into a flat position before moving into an arched position.
    3. Depending on your mobility and the range of motion your machine permits, you may feel a stretching sensation in your abs as you allow your lower back to arch.
    4. The imaginary laser beam shooting out from your chest should angle progressively higher up as you uncurl your back and flare your ribs away from your hip bones.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you feel lower back discomfort at the end of either step, you can abbreviate the range of motion to what feels best to you. Performing this exercise through a full range of motion is by no means inherently injurious to the lower back, but certain individuals with pre-existing conditions may experience discomfort in rather rounded or rather arched positions.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your abdominal muscles, including your obliques and especially your rectus abdominis, which is the “six-pack muscle.”## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s foot bar to a setting that allows you to rest the soles of your feet comfortably against it while seated with the back of your hip bones snugly against the hip pad.
    1. So long as you are able to achieve the stable position described above, a range of foot bar settings may be viable for this exercise, so you can feel free to try out different settings over time to determine where you personally feel best.
    2. As you move the foot bar further back, you will have to bend your knees to a greater degree and lift your knees higher up to reach it.
    3. Some people may find it easier to emphasize moving the spine (via ab contraction) rather than the hips (via hip flexor contraction) when setting the foot bar far enough back to allow their knees to be bent by at least 90 degrees. However, you may still be able to perform this exercise when setting the foot bar further in front to a position that allows your knees to be mildly bent.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust them, set the handles to the furthest back (toward the hip pad) setting that permits you to grasp them with straight elbows once you are seated with your lower back comfortably arched.
    1. The starting position of the handles can normally only be adjusted with combination machines that allow you to perform a back extension (if the handles are situated further forward) or a crunch.
    2. As the handles are situated further back, you will be able to achieve a greater degree of lower back arching in the starting position, which allows you to train your abs in a more lengthened position that may be conducive to developing them effectively.
    3. Your mobility can affect the degree to which you can comfortably arch your lower back and consequently which handle starting position is ideal for you.
    4. Only place the handles as far back as allows your lower back to feel comfortable once you are in the starting position, which may require trying out different handle positions if your lower back is sensitive to being meaningfully arched.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Sit on the seat with the back of your hip bones snugly against the hip pad and your feet on the foot bar.
    1. Keep your feet stationary throughout your set.
5. Arch your lower back to a degree that feels comfortable, and grasp the handles with straight elbows as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. With your hip bones against the hip pad, leaning your chest back while flaring your ribs up and away from your hip bones will arch your lower back.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl your spine as you pull the bottom of your ribs toward the tops of your hip bones in a controlled manner.
    1. Once you feel that you can no longer curl your spine through a range permitted by the machine that feels comfortable to your back, you have reached your end range. The machine may physically restrict your range of motion at a certain point, even if you have the mobility to crunch further.
    2. Keep your elbows straight as you avoid actively pushing the handles with your arms. Your arms should only move indirectly because they are attached to your torso, which is what you are directly moving. The function of your arms is merely to transmit force from your abs into the machine.
    3. To train your abs dynamically, you should be intentionally rounding your entire back, since this motion is performed by contraction of your abs. When rounding your back appropriately, you should feel meaningful tension in your abs. If you do not feel your abs engaging, you may be substituting hip or shoulder girdle motion for the intended spinal motion.
    4. If you finish the movement with your back fairly flat as opposed to rounded, you most likely are primarily bending at your hips rather than curling your spine.
    5. You can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the middle of your chest, and you are trying to point that closer toward the middle point between your knees.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner as you uncurl your back by flaring your ribs up and away from your hip bones.
    1. Move through the full range of motion permitted by the machine that feels comfortable to your lower back. This may involve allowing the weights you selected to tap the rest of the stack lightly at the end of this movement if the full range that the machine allows feels comfortable to you.
    2. When uncurling your spine, your lower back will initially move into a flat position before moving into an arched position.
    3. Depending on your mobility and the range of motion your machine permits, you may feel a stretching sensation in your abs as you allow your lower back to arch.
    4. The imaginary laser beam shooting out from your chest should angle progressively higher up as you uncurl your back and flare your ribs away from your hip bones.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you feel lower back discomfort at the end of either step, you can abbreviate the range of motion to what feels best to you. Performing this exercise through a full range of motion is by no means inherently injurious to the lower back, but certain individuals with pre-existing conditions may experience discomfort in rather rounded or rather arched positions.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your abdominal muscles, including your obliques and especially your rectus abdominis, which is the “six pack muscle.”## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, or leave the machine unloaded.
    1. Machines commonly permit you to adjust only the load and the knee pad orientation. If your machine has any additional modifiable features (such as handle height), you can adjust them to your preferred settings at this time.
2. Stand to the side of the knee pad with both of the pad’s front corners pointing forward.
    1. It can be easier to set up the pad symmetrically centered before swiveling it to the side.
3. Grasp the handles for balance assistance, and kneel on the knee pad, one leg at a time, while resting your forearms and elbows on the arm pads.
    1. Situate your knees near the front of the pad to allow your shins to rest on the pad for stability.
    2. You should be in a tall kneeling position with your chest up and your hips fairly close to being vertically above your knees, as opposed to sitting your buttocks onto your heels with your knees fully bent.
    3. Your neck and back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. This should result in your lower back having its natural curvature rather than being excessively arched or rounded.
4. While keeping your chest pointing straight forward, twist your torso to swivel the pad toward one side.
    1. Swivel the pad away from the side that you wish to predominantly train first. For instance, if you swivel the pad to the right by twisting your lower torso to the right, you will be in a position to train your left obliques predominantly.
    2. If laser beams were shooting out from your chest and the middle of your waist, where you would wear a belt buckle, those laser beams should now be angled in different directions.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the pad swiveled to the side, drive your knees up across your body through the range of motion your mobility allows.
    1. On the side that you are primarily training, you can think about crunching your hip bone diagonally up toward your ribs, which is a primary function of your obliques. For instance, if you swiveled the pad to the right, you can strive to pull your left hip bone up toward your left ribs. Once you can no longer bring this hip bone closer toward these ribs, you have reached the end of the lifting phase.
    2. When keeping the pad rotated and your knees snugly on top of it, driving your knees up toward your elbows should naturally prompt you to feel meaningful tension in your working obliques (situated on the side of your abdomen).
    3. This exercise essentially trains a hybrid of a crunching motion with a side bending motion.
    4. Keep your chest and arms stationary throughout the exercise as you avoid pulling through your arms or moving your upper body.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your knees until they are close to being under your hips.
    1. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should avoid letting gravity rapidly swing you down.
    2. With an appropriately controlled descent, your knees should not rock back behind the point at which they are directly under your hips.
    3. As you lower your knees, your working side’s hip bone will naturally move away from your ribs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to swivel the pad to the opposite side and repeat the exercise.
    1. When transitioning from rep to rep, minimize any momentum to the best of your ability as you prioritize controlled movement and avoid swinging.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your obliques, which are assisted by your rectus abdominis.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground, as your elbows are straight and your knees are bent.
    1. Your upper arms should be directed straight down under your shoulders such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    2. You can spread your fingers apart for stability. Depending upon which hand position feels most comfortable, you can point your middle fingers straight ahead or mildly out to the sides.
## Exercise technique
1. While bearing weight through your arms, straighten one knee to move your foot behind you and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. You should now look like you are at the top of a push-up.
2. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes to flatten your lower back.
    1. From this tall plank position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. Contracting your abs and glutes appropriately will prevent your hips from sagging down, which would prompt your lower back to arch rather than remain flat.
    3. During the entire set, do not allow your chest to sag down, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together. If you notice your chest starting to sag, push down into the ground more forcefully through your straight arms to spread your shoulder blades apart.
    4. Keep your neck near a neutral position as you would normally do when standing upright.
3. Explosively march one knee forward and up toward your chest while maintaining the same stable trunk position you assumed during step 2.
    1. How far you can march your knee forward will be influenced by your hip mobility.
4. Reverse the movement by quickly driving your knee (that you just marched) backward and straightening it. Simultaneously march your other knee explosively forward.
    1. Start marching one leg forward at the same time as you begin driving the other leg backward, which will result in both feet being off the ground for a brief moment until your foot moving backward reaches the ground.
    2. Move each leg reciprocally at the same speed as if you were running. This should result in one leg reaching its fully marched position at the same moment that the other leg’s foot contacts the ground.
    3. While you are moving your legs explosively through motion at your hip and knee joints, strive to keep the rest of your body stationary. Your trunk and hip bones should remain stable in a rigid position with your shoulders stacked over your wrists throughout the set. Moving your legs quickly is not an excuse to let your form become sloppy.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hip bones hike up, sag down, or tilt to either side.
    5. Also avoid letting your arms rock back and forth.
5. Cyclically repeat step 4 while switching which leg you march up and which leg you drive back each time until you have completed your set.
    1. Minimize how long either foot remains on the ground by rapidly marching your leg up immediately after its foot reaches the ground.
    2. Due to the speed of each rep, people commonly track set duration for this variation rather than the total number of reps.
    3. After finishing your last rep, return both feet to the ground before bending your knees and standing up.
## Description
While also dynamically training your hip flexors, this exercise can statically train your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Add a pair of round weight plates to a barbell situated on the ground with sufficient room in front of the bar for you to roll out.
    1. If you roll out through the full range of motion, using small-diameter weight plates can make this exercise slightly more difficult than using large-diameter plates.
    2. If you stop rolling out before your arms are fully outstretched, the plate diameter is inconsequential.
    3. Your bodyweight will primarily provide the resistance for this exercise, so the weight of the plates you select is not particularly impactful.
    4. Hexagonal weight plates cannot be used for this exercise.
2. Optionally place a comfortable surface (e.g., an exercise mat, foam cushion, pillow, or folded towel) slightly behind the middle of the bar.
3. Kneel behind the bar as your body is completely centered with the bar and your knees are approximately hip-width apart.
4. With your elbows and wrists straightened, firmly grasp the bar with your hands at least slightly outside shoulder width.
    1. Use an overhand grip with your palms facing back toward you.
    2. So long as it is at least slightly outside shoulder width, you decide specifically how wide a grip you will use.
    3. Keep your wrists and elbows straight throughout this exercise.
5. With your hips stacked vertically above your knees, position the bar directly under your shoulders.
    1. Your thighs and arms should now be directed straight down, such that they are perpendicular to the ground, which places your torso closer to a horizontal position than a vertical position.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Scoop your hip bones forward toward your ribs, while keeping your ribs pulled down toward your hip bones and tightening your abs. Maintain this position throughout your set.
    1. When starting with your lower back in its natural mild arch, this motion (known as posterior pelvic tilting) will flatten your lower back, since it is the opposite of arching it.
    2. Your abs attach to your pubic bone (which you can feel as a bony ridge in the middle of your pubic region) and your ribs, and this scooping motion should tilt your pubic bone up toward your ribs as you contract your abs.
    3. It can help to imagine that a laser beam is shooting out for the midpoint between your hip bones, slightly below where you would wear a belt buckle. Before you scoop your hips, this laser beam will point down toward your abdomen. Scooping your hips appropriately should angle this laser beam forward toward the bar.
2. Lower your hips forward until you can draw a straight line from your thighs through your hips to your lower back when viewed from the side. Keep the bar under your shoulders and your lower back flat.
    1. This lower-body motion will push the bar moderately forward, but you are not moving your shoulders during this step. Your arms move forward simply because they are connected to the rest of your body.
    2. Your thighs, torso, and arms should now resemble a tall plank position.
    3. Avoid lowering your hips so far forward that your lower back begins arching. Your lower back position should not change from the last step.
3. Squeeze your glutes, and keep them squeezed for the rest of your set.
    1. Actively squeezing your glutes can help you avoid arching your lower back.
    2. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them.
4. Slowly push the bar forward by sweeping your arms forward. Keep your torso rigidly braced as you avoid letting your lower back arch throughout the set.
    1. Only move the bar as far forward as you can while maintaining a flat lower back position.
    2. Keeping your lower back flat will become progressively more difficult as you roll out further, so you can determine your range of motion based on your current ability.
    3. If you are new to this exercise, a rather short range may be appropriate. If you have the strength to do so with proper form, you can finish rolling out when your chest is almost touching the ground.
    4. Keep your elbows fully straightened.
    5. While you push the bar forward through shoulder motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    6. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag or your ribs flare, as this can arch your lower back. Continuing to squeeze your glutes and tighten your abs will help you keep your lower back flat.
5. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by pulling the bar back toward the starting position. Drive your arms back until they are once again under your shoulders without moving your hips.
    1. In contrast to the traditional rollout technique, you will not bend your hips or move them back during each rep with this variation.
    2. Your arms should now be pointing straight down toward the ground.
    3. Continue keeping your lower back in the same flat position you maintained while rolling out.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set and are ready to move your hips back before standing up.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your lats and teres major while statically training your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Pick up an ab wheel, and kneel on the ground with your knees approximately hip-width apart.
    1. You can optionally kneel on a soft surface, such as an exercise mat, foam cushion, pillow, or folded towel, but you will only be kneeling while setting up.
2. Place the ab wheel on the ground in front of you while firmly gripping the handles with your palms facing back toward you (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. Keep your wrists straight throughout this exercise.
3. Position your hips vertically above your knees, and position the roller directly under your shoulders with your elbows straightened.
    1. Your thighs and arms should now be directed straight down, such that they are perpendicular to the ground, which places your torso closer to a horizontal position than a vertical position.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While bearing weight through your arms into the ab wheel, straighten one knee to move your foot behind you and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. Throughout the entire set, do not allow your chest to sag, as this would cause your shoulder blades to pinch together.
2. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes to flatten your lower back. Continue doing throughout your entire set.
    1. You should now be in a tall plank position where you can draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side. Maintain this alignment during the subsequent steps.
    2. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them.
    3. When tightening your abs, think about slightly crunching the bottom of your ribs toward your hip bones.
    4. Contracting your abs and glutes appropriately will prevent your hips from sagging down or your ribs from flaring away from your bones, either of which would prompt your lower back to arch.
3. Sweep your arms forward to push the ab wheel through the range where you can keep your lower back flat.
    1. Keeping your lower back flat will become progressively more difficult as you roll out farther, so you can determine specifically how far you move based on your current ability. If you are new to this exercise, aiming to move the ab wheel a couple of inches (or a few centimeters) forward can be appropriate. If you have a high level of core strength, you can move farther forward through a range that provides your desired intensity.
    2. While you push the ab wheel forward through shoulder motion, keep your elbows straightened, and keep the rest of your body stationary.
    3. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag down or your ribs flare up, which will arch your lower back.
4. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by pulling the ab wheel back toward the starting position. Drive your arms back until they are once again under your shoulders without moving your hips.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back in the same flat position you maintained while rolling out.
    2. Your arms should now be pointing straight down toward the ground.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set, then bend your knees, return to kneeling, and stand up.
## Description
While also dynamically training your lats and teres major, this exercise can statically train your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Add a pair of round weight plates to a barbell situated on the ground with sufficient room in front of the bar for you to roll out.
    1. If you roll out through the full possible range of motion (which is extremely rare for this variation), using small-diameter weight plates can allow this exercise to be slightly more difficult than if you used large-diameter plates.
    2. If you will stop rolling out before your arms are fully outstretched (which applies to at least 99% of people), the plate diameter is inconsequential.
    3. Your bodyweight will primarily provide the resistance for this exercise, so the weight of the plates you select is not particularly impactful.
    4. Hexagonal weight plates cannot be used for this exercise.
2. Optionally place a comfortable surface (e.g., exercise mat, foam cushion, pillow, folded towel) a little behind the middle of the bar.
3. Kneel behind the bar as your body is completely centered with the bar and your knees are approximately hip-width apart.
4. With your elbows and wrists straightened, firmly grasp the bar with your hands at least slightly outside shoulder width.
    1. Use an overhand grip with your palms facing back toward you.
    2. So long as it is at least slightly outside shoulder width, you decide specifically how wide a grip you will use.
    3. Keep your wrists and elbows straight throughout this exercise.
5. With your hips stacked vertically above your knees, position the bar directly under your shoulders.
    1. Your thighs and arms should now be directed straight down, such that they are perpendicular to the ground, which places your torso closer to a horizontal position than a vertical position.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. While bearing weight through your arms into the bar, straighten one knee to move your foot behind you and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. Throughout the entire set, do not allow your chest to sag, as this would cause your shoulder blades to pinch together.
2. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes to flatten your lower back. Continue doing throughout your entire set.
    1. You should now be in a tall plank position where you can draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side. Maintain this alignment during the subsequent steps.
    2. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them.
    3. When tightening your abs, think about slightly crunching the bottom of your ribs toward your hip bones.
    4. Contracting your abs and glutes appropriately will prevent your hips from sagging or your ribs from flaring away from your spine, both of which would prompt your lower back to arch.
3. Sweep your arms forward to push the bar forward through the range in which you can keep your lower back flat.
    1. Keeping your lower back flat will become progressively more difficult as you roll out farther, so you can determine specifically how far you move based on your current ability. If you are new to this exercise, aiming to move the bar a couple of inches (or a few centimeters) forward can be appropriate. If you have a high level of core strength, you can move farther forward through a range that provides your desired intensity.
    2. While you push the bar forward through shoulder motion, keep your elbows straightened, and keep the rest of your body stationary.
    3. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag down or your ribs flare up, which will arch your lower back.
4. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by pulling the bar back toward the starting position. Drive your arms back until they are once again under your shoulders without moving your hips.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back in the same flat position you maintained while rolling out.
    2. Your arms should now be pointing straight down toward the ground.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set, then bend your knees, return to kneeling, and stand up.
## Description
While also dynamically training your lats and teres major, this exercise can statically train your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat to a height that allows your lower back to be aligned with the machine’s pivot point once you are seated.
    1. The proper alignment should place the top of your hip bones slightly above the pivot point when you are seated.
2. Initially position the roller pad far enough back that you can comfortably sit on the seat.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the seat with your lower back aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. If your machine has a lower back pad, position the back of your hip bones snugly against it, which should result in you being appropriately aligned.
5. Place your feet on the foot bar under the foot rollers (if present), and adjust the foot bar to a comfortable setting if it is adjustable on your machine.
    1. The ideal foot bar position should allow your feet to feel stable while your hip bones are well-supported by the lower back pad. The optimal foot bar position should also readily enable you to perform this exercise through spinal motion while limiting hip motion.
    2. Some people may find it easier to emphasize training the back muscles through spinal motion when setting the foot bar far enough back to allow their knees to be bent by about 90 degrees or somewhat greater. However, you may still be able to perform this exercise when setting the foot bar further in front to a position that allows your knees to be mildly bent. A range of foot bar positions can be viable, so you can feel free to try out different positions over time to determine where you personally feel best if you can utilize different settings with your machine.
6. Round your lower and middle back through the full range of motion that is comfortable for you. Set the roller pad so that it contacts your upper back in this rounded position, or is as close to doing so as your machine permits.
    1. You may notice that setting the roller pad as far forward as you can results in the pad not contacting your back when you have rounded your back through your full comfortable range. If this occurs with your machine, you will have to start each rep from a less rounded back position in order to reach the pad.
    2. As you move the roller pad further forward, you will be able to achieve a greater degree of back rounding in the starting position, which allows you to train your back muscles in a more lengthened position that may be conducive to developing them effectively.
    3. Your mobility can affect the degree to which you can comfortably round your back and, consequently, which roller pad starting position is ideal for you.
    4. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Uncurl your back by flaring your ribs up and away from your hip bones through a comfortable range of motion. Avoid leaning your torso backward primarily through hip motion as you emphasize moving the roller pad backward through spinal motion.
    1. If you just focus on driving the roller pad backward rather than on how you move it, you most likely will be meaningfully using your hip muscles to lift the weight rather than using your back muscles in a more targeted manner. Regardless of your intent, the hip muscles (like your glutes) will inevitably be engaged to at least some degree, since a normal rhythm exists between hip and spinal motions, and the machine does not fully stabilize the hips. However, you should strive to move predominantly at your spine while minimizing hip motion to the best of your ability when you are striving to target your back muscles.
    2. When uncurling your spine, your lower back will initially move into a flat position before moving into an arched position.
    3. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable to you as an individual, you can finish the lifting phase once your lower back is close to having the mild curvature it naturally has when sitting upright, or you can finish with a greater degree of lower back arching. What chiefly matters is moving through a range that does not prompt you to feel lower back discomfort.
    4. When you are appropriately moving your spine, you should feel tension in the lower back muscles running along each side of your spine. Thinking about flaring your ribs up and away from your hip bones can help cue you to move at your spine rather than your hips.
    5. Both during this lifting phase and the subsequent lowering phase, it is recommended to move in a slow and controlled manner while prioritizing technique quality.
    6. Avoid sliding your buttocks as you keep them planted on the seat, in addition to keeping your feet on the foot bar throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by rounding your back through the full range of motion that is comfortable to you to bring the roller pad forward.
    1. Continue emphasizing spinal motion while minimizing hip motion. If you finish the movement with your lower back flat or arched as opposed to rounded, you most likely are primarily bending at your hips rather than rounding your spine.
    2. It can help to think about crunching your ribs down toward your hip bones as you move into a slouched position with your chest angled closer toward your knees.
    3. If you initially positioned the roller pad to the appropriate setting, you should return to the starting position, where you can gently tap the machine’s lever arm (onto which you loaded weight plates) against the stopper underneath it to confirm you have moved through your full range of motion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you feel lower back discomfort at the end of either step, you can abbreviate the range of motion to what feels best to you. Performing this exercise through a full range of motion is by no means inherently injurious to the lower back, but certain individuals with pre-existing conditions may experience discomfort in rather rounded or rather arched positions.
    2. If you experience lower back discomfort specifically at the end of step 2, you can pause your set and adjust the starting position of the roller pad to be further back to limit you from training beyond your comfortable range of motion.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep spinal extensor muscles in your lower and middle back.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that will situate the chest pad to chest height once you are seated.
    1. You will have to sit down to check if you selected the appropriate seat height, so you may have to adjust the seat height multiple times before finding the right setting for you.
2. Rotate the seat toward one direction. Select a setting that allows you to perform this exercise through the full range of motion that is comfortable to you.
    1. If you have normal back mobility and a healthy back, you can initially rotate it fully to one side. If you cannot comfortably assume the starting position from this setting, you can rotate the pad closer toward the center (where your imaginary belt buckle and chest are both facing forward) until you can set up comfortably.
    2. As you rotate the pad further away from the centered position, you will be able to train your core muscles in more lengthened positions that may be conducive to developing them effectively.
    3. It is up to you whether you begin with the pad rotated to your right or left, since you will adjust it to the opposite side after your first set to train both sides equally.
    4. Your optimal setting will be affected by your back mobility, and finding this ideal setting may require some trial and error.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Sit on the seat with an upright torso as your knees contact the knee pads and your feet rest on the foot plates.
    1. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
5. Rotate your chest toward the chest pad and snugly contact the pad with your chest while firmly gripping the handles.
    1. In the starting position, it is common to feel a stretching sensation in your back when the machine is adjusted to train through your full range of motion. However, you should not feel lower back discomfort in this position, so you should adjust the degree to which the seat is rotated if you experience discomfort when moving into the starting position. If you cannot rotate your chest enough to reach the chest pad, you naturally should adjust the seat as well until you can reach it.
## Exercise technique
1. Rotate your imaginary belt buckle in a controlled manner away from the side it initially faces. Move through the full range of motion your mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Throughout the set, continue keeping your chest snugly against the chest pad, buttocks on the seat, knees against the knee pads, and feet on the foot plates.
    2. Your legs should only turn because you are rotating your torso, and your legs are connected to your torso.
    3. Focusing on turning your imaginary belt buckle can help cue the rotational trunk motion that your core muscles perform.
    4. During the first half of the movement, you will rotate your imaginary belt buckle so that it faces straight forward with your chest. Then you will continue rotating to have your imaginary belt buckle face the opposite direction from which it started.
2. Reverse the movement as you rotate your imaginary belt buckle in the opposite direction to return to the starting position.
    1. While you do not need to utilize an extremely slow tempo, you should actively control the entire movement rather than letting the machine’s resistance abruptly twist you back into the starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with the seat rotated toward the opposite side from which you started.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your obliques and your small rotatores muscles through a long range of motion.## Setting up
1. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
2. Sit on the seat with an upright torso. Position the back of your hip bones against the back of the seat and your knees snugly against the knee pad (or pads).
    1. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. If your machine has foot plates, situate your feet on top of them, but if your machine lacks foot plates, place your feet on the ground.
    3. This machine style normally lacks the ability to adjust the seat height. If you are rather short and do not feel like you are positioned appropriately once you have assumed the starting position at the end of the setup, elevating your torso by sitting on something like a foam cushion may help you feel better positioned during the exercise.
3. Rotate the handles (attached to the arm/shoulder pads) toward one direction. Select a setting that allows you to perform this exercise through the full range of motion that is comfortable to you.
    1. If you have normal back mobility and a healthy back, you can initially rotate them fully to one side. If you cannot comfortably assume the starting position from this setting, you can rotate the handles closer toward the center (where your imaginary belt buckle and chest are both facing forward) until you can set up comfortably.
    2. As you rotate the handles further away from the centered position, you will be able to train your core muscles in more lengthened positions that may be conducive to developing them effectively.
    3. It is up to you whether you begin with the handles rotated to your right or left, since you will adjust them to the opposite side after your first set to train both sides equally.
    4. Your optimal setting will be affected by your back mobility, and finding this ideal setting may require trying out different settings.
4. Rotate your chest toward the middle point between the handles, and firmly grasp the handles. If your machine has arm pads, spread your arms apart so that the inner aspects of your arms are contacting the outer aspects of the pads. If your machine has shoulder pads, pull them down until they are snugly resting on top of your shoulders before grasping the handles.
    1. In the starting position, it is common to feel a stretching sensation in your back when the machine is adjusted to train through your full range of motion. However, you should not feel lower back discomfort in this position, so you should adjust the degree to which the seat is rotated if you experience discomfort when moving into the starting position. If you cannot rotate your chest enough to reach the chest pad, you naturally should adjust the seat as well until you can reach it.
    2. While it determines where you situate your arms, the same exercise technique can be used whether your machine has arm pads or shoulder pads.
## Exercise technique
1. In a controlled manner, rotate your chest away from the side that it is initially facing. Move through the full range of motion your mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your buttocks on the seat, knees against the knee pad (or pads), and feet stationary while keeping your arms or shoulders (depending on your machine) in contact with the pads.
    2. Continue holding the handles for stability, but do not actively try to push or pull the machine with your arms. The function of your arms is merely to help transmit force from your core muscles into the machine.
    3. As a cue, you can think about turning an imaginary laser beam that is shooting out from the middle of your chest without moving your imaginary belt buckle.
    4. During the first half of the movement, you will rotate your chest so that it faces straight forward with your imaginary belt buckle. Then you will continue rotating to have your chest face the opposite direction from which it started.
2. Reverse the movement as you rotate your chest in the opposite direction to return to the starting position.
    1. While you do not need to utilize an extremely slow tempo, you should actively control the entire movement rather than letting the machine’s resistance abruptly twist you back into the starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with the handles rotated toward the opposite side from which you started.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train your obliques and your small rotatores muscles through a long range of motion.## Setting up
When front squatting, you can hold the bar in different ways depending on your mobility. These options include the full hand clean grip, fingertip clean grip, and cross-arm grip, in addition to using straps. The full hand clean grip requires the most mobility to utilize effectively, followed by the fingertip clean grip and then the cross arm grip and strap technique, both of which have minimal mobility requirements. Each of these positions is described as its own front squat page in the database to communicate specifically how you would set up with each grip. Because the grip does not affect how your lower body or back muscles are trained in any meaningful way, you should utilize whichever grip feels most comfortable and stable to you. This page describes the front squat instructions that are shared in common among each grip, but the setup does not describe each position in detail, so you can click the link to your desired grip to see the additional details for how to use that specific grip. Regardless of your grip, the exercise technique will be the same, along with how you begin and finish setting up. 

1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Face the center of the bar in approximately a shoulder-width stance.
3. Based on your preference, set up with a full hand clean grip, fingertip clean grip, cross-arm grip, or straps.
4. While simultaneously bending your elbows, shallowly bend at your knees and hips with an upright torso to walk under the bar and situate it on the front of your shoulders just in front of the base of your neck.
    1. The bar should be resting on your front delts (which are the muscles on the front of your shoulders that attach to the outer aspects of your collarbones) and the outer portions of your collarbones, which together form a shelf with the base of your neck to cradle the bar.
    2. While the bar should be directly in front of your neck, it should not be pressing into your neck to the extent that it feels like it is choking you.
    3. If you are not accustomed to front squats, it is normal to experience discomfort from the pressure the bar places on the front of your shoulders (which may cause some skin bruising), but it should not be painful with proper bar placement.
5. With the bar now on the front of your shoulders, lift your elbows up until your upper arms are parallel to the ground.
    1. You can perform steps 4 and 5 simultaneously with each other.
    2. The bony prominence on the back of each elbow should now be pointing straight forward.
    3. Your hands can assist in keeping the bar balanced, but the bar should first and foremost be resting on the front of your shoulders without necessarily needing your hands to hold it there.
6. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of front squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
7. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when front squatting.
8. Repeat steps 2-5 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arms parallel to the ground, stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the bar resting on your shoulders throughout the set.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest and elbows up.
    1. Throughout the set, avoid dropping your elbows or letting your chest cave down.
    2. As you descend, it is natural for your torso to lean forward mildly at your hips to maintain your balance, but maintain a mostly upright torso position with your lower back flat.
    3. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your lower back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your lower back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the torso pad to a height that allows you to rest your torso comfortably on it while resting your forearms on the forearm pads and holding the handles.
    1. The appropriate setting should allow you to feel stable and well-supported during the exercise while distributing the pressure from your bodyweight comfortably throughout the forearms pads, torso pad, and knee pad.
    2. If the torso pad is too high, you may be unable to rest your forearms fully on the forearm pads without rounding your shoulders or back excessively forward. Without the ability to support a meaningful portion of your bodyweight through your forearms, you may feel that the torso pad applies excessive pressure through your abdomen or chest (depending on whether your machine has an abdomen or chest pad).
    3. If the torso pad is too low, it may not be able to support your torso unless you excessively arch your lower back, which could prompt you to feel less stable during the exercise.
    4. Some trial and error may be required to find the pad height that feels best to your body.
2. Set the knee pad to the side you intend to train during this set if it is adjustable.
3. Initially position the lever arm (to which the foot bar is attached) to the furthest forward setting if it is adjustable.
    1. If you cannot comfortably reach the foot bar when continuing to set up or notice that you have to round your back in order to reach the foot bar, you can move the lever arm further backward until you can comfortably reach the foot bar without rounding your back.
    2. Compared to setting it further backward, setting the lever arm further forward permits you to train through a longer range of motion that may amplify the muscle growth stimulus and increase your strength through a greater range of motion. Consequently, it is sensible to start with the furthest forward setting and adjust from there if warranted.
4. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
5. Kneel on the knee pad with the side you will not train during this set, and lean forward to rest your torso on the torso pad, rest your forearms on the forearm pads, and grasp the handles.
    1. With the proper set-up, your lower back should be near a neutral position (like when you are normally standing upright) rather than being excessively arched or rounded.
6. On the side you intend to train, bend your knee to lift your foot off the ground, and position its sole firmly against the foot bar or foot plate (depending on what your machine has).
    1. If your machine has a short foot bar (that is not tall enough for your entire sole to contact), position the back half of your sole against the foot bar. Based on your preference and what feels stable to you, you can contact the foot bar with your heel or a little closer to your midfoot. The appropriate foot position should ensure that your foot does not slide during the exercise while also helping you to feel meaningful tension in your glute.
    2. If your machine has a tall foot plate, place the entirety of your sole against it. Certain machines may also have a foot roller that can help secure the top part of your foot (where your shoelaces are) while the bottom of your foot contacts the foot plate.
    3. When viewed from the front, your foot should be aligned with your knee rather than being positioned to either the right or left side of your knee.
    4. Keep the knee of your non-working leg firmly planted on the knee pad from this point onward.
    5. All the subsequent directions will apply to your working leg.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your knee backward in an arcing manner to push your heel and the foot bar/plate backward through the full range of motion that your hip mobility permits.
    1. While focusing on moving your knee backward through hip motion, allow your knee to move naturally with respect to straightening or remaining bent. Depending on the specific machine you are using, your knee may naturally straighten, or it may continue to be fairly bent throughout the exercise.
    2. Strive to have all the movement come from your working leg while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
    3. During the entire set, keep your torso snugly against the torso pad and your imaginary belt buckle pointing in the same direction as it was in the starting position.
    4. Particularly avoid excessively arching your lower back or turning your imaginary belt buckle to the side, both of which are common technique errors that can occur when striving to push the foot bar/plate further backward than what your hip mobility permits.
    5. At the end range, you should feel your glute contract. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glute engage during this exercise and help cue the intended hip motion.
    6. If you are using a machine with a foot plate (rather than a foot bar), think about pushing through your heel instead of your toes to help cue the intended glute engagement.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your knee to arc forward while naturally bending.
    1. Avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your knee forward in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your knee positioned as far forward as the machine permits while keeping your foot on the foot bar/plate.
    3. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the lever arm (onto which you loaded weight plates) to the stopper underneath it without allowing the lever arm to rest on the stopper.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
2. Stand directly next to the pad, facing the handles.
3. Lean forward at your hips to rest on the pad and grasp the handles.
    1. When you lean forward, keep your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright. You should lean forward at your hips rather than simply rounding over at your back.
    2. Based on the specific machine you are using, your height, and what feels most comfortable and stable to you, you can decide specifically what you rest on the pad. Some pads may be suitable for contacting the front of your torso, while others may be better for supporting your forearms and elbows as you keep your torso a little above the pad. Ultimately, you want to feel stable and comfortable no matter how you position yourself on the pad, which may warrant trying out different positions.
    3. The pad height cannot normally be adjusted, so shorter individuals may feel like they are unable to rest on the pad comfortably while leaning forward at their hips. Shorter lifters may also feel like they have to shrug their shoulders awkwardly to rest on the pad. If this applies to you, you can elevate your feet with a thick bumper plate or an aerobic step.
    4. If you are using an uncommon standing plate-loaded glute kickback machine that has an adjustable torso pad, you can adjust it to a setting that allows you to rest your torso comfortably on it.
4. On the side you intend to train, bend your knee to lift your foot off the ground, and place the entirety of its sole firmly in contact with the foot plate.
    1. It is appropriate initially to place your foot on the middle of the foot plate with respect to its height, but you can feel free to move your foot somewhat higher up or lower down on the foot plate based on what feels best to you.
    2. When viewed from the front, your foot should be aligned with your knee rather than being positioned to either the right or left side of your knee.
    3. Keep the foot of your non-working leg firmly planted on the ground from this point onward.
    4. All the subsequent directions will apply to your working leg.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your knee backward in an arcing manner as you push through your heel to move the foot plate backward through the full range of motion that your hip mobility permits.
    1. Allow your knee to straighten naturally while focusing on moving your knee backward through hip motion.
    2. How far you personally can drive your knee backward will be influenced by your hip mobility.
    3. Strive to have all the movement come from your working leg while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
    4. During the entire set, maintain the same body contact with the pad you had when setting up and keep your imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead.
    5. Particularly avoid excessively arching your lower back or turning your imaginary belt buckle to the side, both of which are common technique errors that can occur when striving to push the foot bar further backward than what your hip mobility permits.
    6. Thinking about pushing through your heel rather than your toes can help cue the intended glute engagement.
    7. At the end range, you should feel your glute contract. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glute engage during this exercise and help cue the intended hip motion.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your knee to arc forward while naturally bending.
    1. Avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your knee forward in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your knee positioned as far forward as the machine permits while keeping your foot on the foot plate.
    3. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the lever arm (onto which you loaded weight plates) to the stopper underneath it without allowing the lever arm to rest on the stopper.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Adjust the torso pad to a height that allows you to rest your torso comfortably on it while resting your forearms on the forearm pads and holding the handles.
    1. The appropriate setting should allow you to feel stable and well-supported during the exercise while distributing the pressure from your bodyweight comfortably throughout the forearms pads, torso pad, and knee pad.
    2. If the torso pad is too high, you may be unable to rest your forearms fully on the forearm pads without rounding your shoulders or back excessively forward. Without the ability to support a meaningful portion of your bodyweight through your forearms, you may feel that the torso pad applies excessive pressure through your abdomen or chest (depending on whether your machine has an abdomen or chest pad).
    3. If the torso pad is too low, it may not be able to support your torso unless you excessively arch your lower back, which could prompt you to feel less stable during the exercise.
    4. Some trial and error may be required to find the pad height that feels best to your body.
2. Set the knee pad to the side you intend to train during this set if it is adjustable.
3. Initially position the lever arm (to which the foot bar is attached) to the furthest forward setting if it is adjustable.
    1. If you cannot comfortably reach the foot bar when continuing to set up or notice that you have to round your back in order to reach the foot bar, you can move the lever arm further backward until you can comfortably reach the foot bar without rounding your back.
    2. Compared to setting it further backward, setting the lever arm further forward permits you to train through a longer range of motion that may amplify the muscle growth stimulus and increase your strength through a greater range of motion. Consequently, it is sensible to start with the furthest forward setting and adjust from there if warranted.
4. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
5. Kneel on the knee pad with the side you will not train during this set, and lean forward to rest your torso on the torso pad, rest your forearms on the forearm pads, and grasp the handles.
    1. With the proper set-up, your lower back should be near a neutral position (like when you are normally standing upright) rather than being excessively arched or rounded.
6. On the side you intend to train, bend your knee to lift your foot off the ground, and position its sole firmly against the foot bar or foot plate (depending on what your machine has).
    1. If your machine has a short foot bar (that is not tall enough for your entire sole to contact), position the back half of your sole against the foot bar. Based on your preference and what feels stable to you, you can contact the foot bar with your heel or a little closer to your midfoot. The appropriate foot position should ensure that your foot does not slide during the exercise while also helping you to feel meaningful tension in your glute.
    2. If your machine has a tall foot plate, place the entirety of your sole against it. Certain machines may also have a foot roller that can help secure the top part of your foot (where your shoelaces are) while the bottom of your foot contacts the foot plate.
    3. When viewed from the front, your foot should be aligned with your knee rather than being positioned to either the right or left side of your knee.
    4. Keep the knee of your non-working leg firmly planted on the knee pad from this point onward.
    5. All the subsequent directions will apply to your working leg.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your knee backward in an arcing manner to push your heel and the foot bar/plate backward through the full range of motion that your hip mobility permits.
    1. While focusing on moving your knee backward through hip motion, allow your knee to move naturally with respect to straightening or remaining bent. Depending on the specific machine you are using, your knee may naturally straighten, or it may continue to be fairly bent throughout the exercise.
    2. Strive to have all the movement come from your working leg while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
    3. During the entire set, keep your torso snugly against the torso pad and your imaginary belt buckle pointing in the same direction as it was in the starting position.
    4. Particularly avoid excessively arching your lower back or turning your imaginary belt buckle to the side, both of which are common technique errors that can occur when striving to push the foot bar/plate further backward than what your hip mobility permits.
    5. At the end range, you should feel your glute contract. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glute engage during this exercise and help cue the intended hip motion.
    6. If you are using a machine with a foot plate (rather than a foot bar), think about pushing through your heel instead of your toes to help cue the intended glute engagement.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your knee to arc forward while naturally bending.
    1. Avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your knee forward in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your knee positioned as far forward as the machine permits while keeping your foot on the foot bar/plate.
    3. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack without allowing the weights to rest on the stack.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine’s weight sleeve, and straddle the machine while facing the weights.
    1. The weight plates you select should not touch the ground during this exercise, which will not be an issue for most lifters regardless of which plates they use.
    2. Full-diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates) can reach the ground and restrict the range of motion for this exercise for some lifters with rather long arms. If you notice that the plates contacting the ground restrict your range of motion at the bottom of your reps, you should load the bar exclusively with smaller diameter plates that will not reach the ground.
2. Place your feet fully onto the foot plates and your chest onto the chest pad to incline your whole body forward.
    1. If you notice that most of your chest is situated above the top of the pad when your knees are straight (which is likely to occur with tall lifters), you could bend your knees to slide your torso down the pad until the pad supports your chest appropriately. Avoid bending your knees to the extent that your collarbone is resting on the chest pad.
    2. The proper position should feel comfortable and stable to you throughout the exercise.
3. Firmly grasp whichever handles you prefer.
    1. For this variation, you can effectively use an overhand grip (with handles directed out to the sides), a neutral grip (with the parallel handles), or a semi-neutral grip (with handles angled diagonally outward and backward).
    2. To determine which feels best to you, feel free to try out different grips along with different grip widths if the handles are long enough to permit a range of possible grip widths.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar up off the machine’s stopper in a short rowing motion by driving your elbows mildly backward while bending them.
2. Center the bar so that it no longer lies above the stopper, and lower it until your elbows are straight.
    1. In this bottom position, allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades fully apart from one another.
3. Pull your shoulder blades backward and in toward each other to lift the bar upward while keeping your elbows straight and your chest in contact with the pad.
    1. Pull your shoulder blades as far back as you can, which should result in you feeling your upper back muscles contracting.
    2. As a cue, you can imagine pinching a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Like when performing a standard shrugging exercise, the bar will only move a short distance during this variation, since you are isolating motion of your shoulder blades without moving your arms directly.
4. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulder blades to move forward and spread apart from each other as far as they can to lower the bar in a controlled manner.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straightened and chest snugly in contact with the pad.
    2. At the end of this motion, your shoulder girdles should resemble a slouched posture as they are pulled forward beyond their normal resting position, which often produces a stretching sensation in your upper back muscles.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to lift the bar up onto the stopper before setting it down.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains your rhomboids and all three regions of your trapezius in a more isolated manner than row variations do.## Setting up
1. Add your desired load (in the form of weight plates or a kettlebell) to a pull-up/dip belt you have around your waist, and stand upright under a pull-up bar with parallel handles.
    1. The back of your belt should be securely resting on top of your buttocks, and the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) should be looped through the center hole of a weight plate or under the handle of a kettlebell. If you have a loading pin for your belt, you can alternatively loop the chain or strap through it after adding weight plates.
    2. It will be easiest if you carry the weight over to the pull-up bar and add the weight to your belt while you are under the bar, rather than adding the weight to your belt farther away from the bar before walking over to the bar.
    3. As an alternative to using a pull-up/dip belt, you can wear a weighted vest.
    4. While less stable and more awkward than using a belt or weighted vest, you also have the option of holding a dumbbell between your thighs or feet, but you will be limited in how much weight you can add when using a dumbbell.
2. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the bar’s parallel handles with your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. If your bar has greater than two parallel handles, grasp the two that situate your hands near your shoulders apart.
    2. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    3. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up onto a sturdy box, an aerobic step, or a bumper plate to place the bar within reach.
    4. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    5. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    6. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
3. Lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar with straight elbows.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will put you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back puts you in a “proud chest” posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending them.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows will not actually drive downward, but having this intent helps pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. It is up to your preference whether you would like to keep your thighs directed vertically downward (resulting in a similar hip position as when you stand) or whether you like to lift your knees moderately up (resulting in a more flexed hip position, like when you are seated, but to a lesser degree). Regardless, avoid swinging your legs during the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad.
    1. You can initially adjust the seat so your shoulders are approximately level with the horizontal handles when seated. However, the ideal seat height for you can depend on the specific machine you use, so trying out different positions may be helpful.
2. Adjust the chest pad to a position that allows you to grasp the horizontal handles when your arms are fully outstretched in front of you with straight elbows.
    1. To train through your full range of motion, situate the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with straight elbows. This should place the handles just within your reach.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet flat on the floor.
5. Reach fully forward with straight elbows to grasp your preferred handles firmly.
    1. For this variation, you can effectively use any grip, including an overhand grip with handles directed out to the sides or a neutral grip with parallel handles.
    2. To determine which feels best to you, feel free to try out different grips along with different grip widths if the handles are long enough to permit a range of possible grip widths.
    3. If you can grasp the handles with any amount of elbow bend, you will not be able to train through your full range of motion, so you could let go of the handles and adjust the chest pad before grasping the handles again.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your shoulder blades backward and in toward each other to pull the handles backward while keeping your elbows straight and your chest in contact with the pad.
    1. Pull your shoulder blades as far back as you can, which should result in you feeling your upper back muscles contracting.
    2. As a cue, you can imagine pinching a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. In contrast to a row, the handles will only move a short distance during this variation, since you are isolating motion of your shoulder blades without moving your arms directly.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulder blades to move forward and spread apart from each other as far as they can in a controlled manner.
    1. This will move the handles forward toward the starting position.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows straightened and chest snugly in contact with the pad.
    3. At the end of this motion, your shoulder girdles should resemble a slouched posture as they are pulled forward beyond their normal resting position, which often produces a stretching sensation in your upper back muscles.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains your rhomboids and all three regions of your trapezius in a more isolated manner than row variations do.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad.
    1. You can initially adjust the seat so your shoulders are approximately level with the horizontal handles when seated. However, the ideal seat height for you can depend on the specific machine you use, so trying out different positions may be helpful.
2. Adjust the chest pad to a position that allows you to grasp the horizontal handles when your arms are fully outstretched in front of you with straight elbows.
    1. To train through your full range of motion, situate the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with straight elbows. This should place the handles just within your reach.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet flat on the floor.
5. Reach fully forward with straight elbows to grasp your preferred handles firmly.
    1. For this variation, you can effectively use any grip, including an overhand grip with handles directed out to the sides or a neutral grip with parallel handles.
    2. To determine which feels best to you, feel free to try out different grips along with different grip widths if the handles are long enough to permit a range of possible grip widths.
    3. If you can grasp the handles with any amount of elbow bend, you will not be able to train through your full range of motion, so you could let go of the handles and adjust the chest pad before grasping the handles again.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your shoulder blades backward and in toward each other to pull the handles backward while keeping your elbows straight and your chest in contact with the pad.
    1. Pull your shoulder blades as far back as you can, which should result in you feeling your upper back muscles contracting.
    2. As a cue, you can imagine pinching a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. In contrast to a row, the handles will only move a short distance during this variation, since you are isolating motion of your shoulder blades without moving your arms directly.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulder blades to move forward and spread apart from each other as far as they can in a controlled manner.
    1. This will move the handles forward toward the starting position.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows straightened and chest snugly in contact with the pad.
    3. At the end of this motion, your shoulder girdles should resemble a slouched posture as they are pulled forward beyond their normal resting position, which often produces a stretching sensation in your upper back muscles.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains your rhomboids and all three regions of your trapezius in a more isolated manner than row variations do.## Setting up
When comparing different models, hip thrust machines can vary in terms of how they are designed and which features can be adjusted. In terms of how to use them, the most notable difference between machines is whether they have safety arms that you have to disengage to begin your set and re-engage to finish your set. Machines with these safety arms, which are often known as glute drive machines, have you begin your set from the top of the hip thrust and typically have a long, non-adjustable back pad that can extend from your upper back to your hip bones. In contrast, your set starts from the bottom position with your hips fully bent when using a hip thrust machine without safety arms, which often has a shorter adjustable upper back pad and an adjustable seat. Because some of the setup and technique steps differ between the two types of machines, this database describes the usage of these machines through distinct pages to provide clear instructions.

Regardless of which machine you are using, you ultimately want to ensure that you set up in a manner that positions the crease of your hips under the hip belt/pad to align the machine’s pivot point with your hip joints. The proper set-up should also result in your knees being bent by about 90 degrees at the top of your hip thrust, feet feeling stable on the foot plate, upper back feeling well-supported by the back pad, and lower back feeling comfortable throughout the set. Determining how you should set up to achieve these objectives may require some trial and error during various steps if you are using a machine that is unfamiliar to you.

1. Move the back pad forward or backward to a setting that allows you to rest your upper back comfortably on it while your hips are aligned with the hip belt/pad and your feet are on the foot plate with your knees bent by at least 90 degrees.
    1. Depending on your machine model, you may be able to move the entire carriage, the upper back pad itself, or neither of them.
2. Adjust the seat to a height that can result in your thighs and torso forming reasonably close to a 90-degree angle (when viewed from the side) when you are in the starting position.
    1. Aiming to achieve near this 90-degree angle is an appropriate starting point for most people, but the ideal seat height specifically should allow you to set up with your lower back in a flat position while training through a meaningful range of motion.
    2. An excessively low seat can require you to round your lower back to move into the starting position.
    3. An excessively high seat can limit the degree to which your hips are initially bent and restrict the range of motion for this exercise.
    4. The seat height that is personally best for you can be affected by your stature and your hip mobility.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Lie down with your upper back on the back pad, hips aligned with the hip belt/pad, and the soles of your feet fully on the foot plate, with your knees bent at greater than 90-degree angles.
    1. If your machine has a fairly short upper back pad, you can place the lower part of your shoulder blades against the front of the pad. If your machine has a long back pad, you may be able to rest your middle and upper back against it.
    2. In addition to moving the back pad forward or backward, you can move your feet further forward or backward on the foot plate to modify the degree to which your knees are bent.
    3. The degree to which your knees are bent will decrease as you thrust your hips up during a rep, so initially setting up with a little over 90 degrees of knee bend can help you achieve about 90 degrees of knee bend at the top of the rep.
5. Situate your feet near shoulder width apart with your toes pointing straight ahead or mildly out to the sides, depending on what is most comfortable.
    1. A near shoulder-width stance is appropriate for most people, but feel free to adjust your stance based on what feels best to you, since what specifically is the most comfortable stance width can vary among different individuals, just like the most comfortable degree of toe flare.
6. Depending on what your machine has, snugly fasten the hip belt around the crease of your hips, or set the hip pad snugly on top of the crease of your hips.
    1. If you feel notable discomfort from the pressure of the belt/pad during your set, you can try shifting your hips slightly further forward or backward to adjust where the pressure is concentrated. When making any adjustments during a set, only do so in between reps when the machine’s lever arms (onto which you loaded plates) are resting on the stoppers underneath them.
    2. If you continue to feel discomfort from the pressure of the belt/pad, you can try cushioning your hip bones by placing a folded exercise mat or thick foam between your hips and the belt/pad.
7. Tuck your chin mildly toward your chest, and maintain this neck position throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to help stabilize your lower back.
    2. From this starting position, your back should be flat.
2. Lift your hips up by driving your heels into the foot plate and squeezing your glutes.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glutes engage during this exercise.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them or tightly holding onto a million-dollar check with your glutes.
    5. Throughout the exercise, keep your upper back snugly in contact with the back pad and the soles of your feet fully planted on the foot plate while concentrating pressure through your heels.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your hips through the full range of motion in which you can keep your lower back flat.
    1. If you were able to position the seat to a height that allowed you to assume the starting position with a flat lower back, you can lower your hips until you are able to tap the seat gently with your buttocks.
    2. If you do not have a seat under you that you can reach while maintaining a flat lower back position, simply lower your hips as much as you can without rounding your lower back. Most lifters will be able to reach the point at which their thighs and torso form at least a 90-degree angle, but your individual end range can be affected by your hip mobility.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid simply letting gravity pull you down abruptly, as opposed to using your glutes to control the descent actively.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After completing the lowering phase of your last rep, allow the machine’s lever arms to rest fully on the stoppers underneath them before unfastening the hip belt or lifting up the hip pad.
## Description
This exercise will predominantly develop your gluteus maximus while also training your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
When comparing different models, hip thrust machines can vary in terms of how they are designed and which features can be adjusted. In terms of how to use them, the most notable difference between machines is whether they have safety arms that you have to disengage to begin your set and re-engage to finish your set. Machines with these safety arms, which are often known as glute drive machines, have you begin your set from the top of the hip thrust and typically have a long, non-adjustable back pad that can extend from your upper back to your hip bones. In contrast, your set starts from the bottom position with your hips fully bent when using a hip thrust machine without safety arms, which often has a shorter adjustable upper back pad and an adjustable seat. Because some of the setup and technique steps differ between the two types of machines, this database describes the usage of these machines through distinct pages to provide clear instructions.

Regardless of which machine you are using, you ultimately want to ensure that you set up in a manner that positions the crease of your hips under the hip belt/pad to align the machine’s pivot point with your hip joints. The proper set-up should also result in your knee being bent by about 90 degrees at the top of your hip thrust, your foot feeling stable on the foot plate, your upper back feeling well-supported by the back pad, and your lower back feeling comfortable throughout the set. Determining how you should set up to achieve these objectives may require some trial and error during various steps if you are using a machine that is unfamiliar to you.

1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
2. Lie down with your hips aligned with the hip belt/pad, back on the back pad, and the soles of your feet fully on the foot plate with your knees bent by close to 90 degrees.
    1. If your machine has a long back pad, you may be able to rest your whole back and the back of your hip bones against it. If your machine has a fairly short upper back pad, you can place the lower part of your shoulder blades against the front of the pad to rest your upper back on the pad.
    2. You can move your feet further forward or backward on the foot plate to modify the degree to which your knees are bent.
    3. You perform this step simultaneously with the next step.
3. Place your feet about hip width apart, with your toes pointing straight ahead.
4. Depending on what your machine has, snugly fasten the hip belt around the crease of your hips, or set the hip pad snugly on top of the crease of your hips.
    1. If you feel notable discomfort from the pressure of the belt/pad during your set, you can try shifting your hips slightly further forward or backward to adjust where the pressure is concentrated. When making any adjustments during a set, only do so in between reps when the machine’s lever arms (onto which you loaded plates) are resting on the stoppers underneath them.
    2. If you continue to feel discomfort from the pressure of the belt/pad, you can try cushioning your hip bones by placing a folded exercise mat or thick foam between your hips and the belt/pad.
5. Tuck your chin mildly toward your chest, and maintain this neck position throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to help stabilize your lower back.
    2. From this starting position, your lower back should be flat.
2. Lift your hips up by driving your heels into the foot plate and squeezing your glutes to lift the machine’s lever arms off the safety arms.
3. Move the handles with your hands to disengage the machine’s safety arms, so that they are no longer under the lever arms.
    1. You can subsequently keep your arms wherever you prefer until you finish your set and are ready to re-engage the safety arms.
4. After shifting your weight into your other side’s foot, lift your one foot (on the side you will not train during this set) off the foot plate, and situate it in a comfortable position throughout your set.
    1. Based on your preference, you can lift your leg up with a straight knee or a bent knee. Either position for the free leg is viable, but keeping the knee straight may make the exercise mildly more challenging compared to keeping it bent.
    2. The subsequent directions will apply to the side on which your foot remains on the foot plate.
5. Bend your hip in a controlled manner to lower your hips through the full range of motion in which you can keep your lower back flat.
    1. Most lifters should be able to reach the point at which the thigh and torso form at least a 90-degree angle (when viewed from the side), but your individual end range can be affected by your hip mobility.
    2. If your lower back begins rounding, which can be indicated by your hip bones starting to curl up off the back pad (provided that the back pad is long enough to contact the back of your hip bones initially), you are descending excessively far relative to your mobility.
    3. Throughout the exercise, keep your back snugly in contact with the back pad and your foot fully planted on the foot plate while concentrating pressure through your heel.
    4. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid simply letting gravity pull you down abruptly, as opposed to using your glutes to control the descent actively.
6. Reverse the movement by lifting your hips up as you drive your heel into the foot plate and squeeze your glute.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thigh and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glute.
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid arching your lower back excessively. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glute engage during this exercise.
7. Alternate between steps 5 and 6 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
8. After you have fully lifted your hips to complete your final rep, return your free foot to the foot plate, shift your weight into that side, and lift up your other foot before training your opposite side.
9. When you have finished your sets for both sides, re-engage the safety arms with the handles before lowering the machine’s lever arms onto the safety arms by lowering your hips.
    1. Wait for the lever arms to be fully resting on the safety arms before unfastening the hip belt or lifting up the hip pad.
