This exercise can effectively train your pec major, front delt, and triceps while being able to improve both your horizontal and vertical pressing strength.## Setting up
While the chest and triceps will be engaged during any dip, you can intentionally bias one muscle group or the other based on the specific setup and technique you utilize. This database contains pages specifically detailing chest-focused and triceps-focused dip variations, but you can alternatively opt to train dips in whichever manner feels personally best to your body, rather than consciously trying to modify the exercise to emphasize your chest or triceps. With the more general approach explained by this page, you ultimately want to set up and perform your dip in the manner that feels most comfortable to you, which will allow you to train both your chest and your triceps.

1. Add weight plates to the machine to utilize your desired level of assistance and ensure that the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is positioned down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you add heavier loads to the dip/assisted pull-up machine since the weight determines the extent to which the machine will support your bodyweight.
    2. Certain machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but essentially the same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates.
3. If your machine allows you to rotate its parallel handles inward or outward, position them to whichever setting is closest to being slightly outside shoulder width for you.
