## Description
This exercise can effectively train your pec major, front delt, and triceps while being able to improve both your horizontal and vertical pressing strength.## Setting up
1. Pick up the sides of a weight plate with both hands as your palms are facing in toward each other.
    1. If you are using a load that is lighter than 45lb (20kg) and have access to full diameter bumper plates, using a bumper plate may be more comfortable than a smaller diameter plate, which would require grasping it with a narrower than shoulder width grip.
2. With straight elbows and wrists, firmly hold the plate directly in front of your legs while standing upright.
    1. While you do not necessarily need to stand with an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid standing with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout the set.
2. Raise the plate out in front of you until your arms are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. Keep your elbows straight throughout the set.
    2. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    3. All the movement should occur from shoulder motion while you keep your legs and torso stationary throughout the exercise.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering the plate in a controlled manner until it is directly in front of your legs.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weight downward.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Pick up the sides of a weight plate with both hands as your palms are facing in toward each other.
    1. If you are using a load that is lighter than 45lb (20kg) and have access to full diameter bumper plates, using a bumper plate may be more comfortable than a smaller diameter plate, which would require grasping it with a narrower than shoulder width grip.
2. With straight elbows and wrists, firmly hold the plate directly in front of your legs while standing upright.
    1. While you do not necessarily need to stand with an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid standing with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core and squeezing your glutes throughout the set to help prevent you from excessively arching your lower back.
2. Raise the plate out in front of you and up overhead through your available shoulder range of motion while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. People can typically lift their arms up until they are close to being fully vertical, but your specific end range will be influenced by your individual shoulder mobility.
    2. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back and leaning backward, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright. Excessive lower back can be demonstrated by your ribs flaring up and away from your hip bones.
    4. All the movement should occur from shoulder motion while you keep your legs and torso stationary throughout the exercise.
    5. The plate should move in an arcing motion, as it initially sweeps forward and upward before sweeping backward and upward.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering the plate in a controlled manner until it is directly in front of your legs.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weight downward.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, traps, serratus anterior, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Alternatively, you can use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on the incline bench with your feet flat on the ground.
    1. Keep your upper back and the back of your head in contact with the bench throughout the set.
    2. While you do not necessarily need to have an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid sitting with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
3. With your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip), direct your arms down toward the ground with straight or slightly bent elbows.
    1. Whether you keep your elbows straight or slightly bent is up to your preference, but you should statically maintain your preferred elbow position throughout the set.
    2. You can perform this step simultaneously with step 2.
## Exercise technique
1. Without bending your elbows, lift the dumbbells outward and away from your sides in an arcing motion until your hands are near shoulder height.
    1. While you raise your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    2. If your shoulders feel comfortable doing so, you can lift your arms straight out away from your sides. However, if you experience some discomfort with this technique, you can try moving your arms mildly forward while still predominantly lifting them out away from your sides, which is a modification that may acutely reduce shoulder discomfort for certain lifters.
    3. If you experience shoulder discomfort at the top when your upper arms are approximately parallel to the ground, you can reduce the range of motion to what feels comfortable to your shoulders.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in toward your sides in a controlled manner.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your arms pointing down toward the ground.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weights downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus while likely training your front delt more effectively than most lateral raise variations.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Alternatively, you can use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on the incline bench with your feet flat on the ground. Direct your arms straight down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Keep your upper back and the back of your head in contact with the bench throughout the set.
    2. While you do not necessarily need to have an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid sitting with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
3. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, direct your palms so that they are facing in toward each other (to use a neutral grip) or backward (to use an overhand grip).
    1. Either grip can be utilized for this exercise, so feel free to try out both grips and continue using whichever one personally feels best to you.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straight, sweep the dumbbells forward and upward in an arcing motion until your hands are near shoulder height.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    2. All the movement should occur from shoulder girdle motion, while you keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the set.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your arms are pointing straight down toward the ground.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weights downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees, and pick up a pair of dumbbells.
    1. Alternatively, you can use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
    1. With the appropriate positioning, your feet should feel stable on the ground, and your torso should be well-supported by the bench.
    2. Shorter lifters may be able to assume this position with straight knees, but taller lifters may have to bend their knees in order to rest their chest comfortably on the head of the bench.
3. Allow your arms to hang down toward the ground with straight elbows and your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straight, lift the dumbbells straight outward and away from your sides in an arcing motion until your hands are near shoulder height.
    1. Continue keeping your chest in contact with the bench rather than lifting it up through lower back arching.
    2. While you raise your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    3. Rather than moving in front of your shoulders or behind them, the dumbbells should remain aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the side, since you are raising them straight out away from your sides.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering the dumbbells inward until they are vertically underneath your shoulders.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your arms pointing down toward the ground.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weights downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt, rear delt, and supraspinatus while also training your traps, rhomboids, infraspinatus, and teres minor to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Approach a barbell loaded with your intended weight or a fixed-weight straight bar.
2. In a near shoulder stance, squat down to grasp the bar firmly with your hands approximately shoulder width apart and your palms facing back toward you (i.e., an overhand grip).
3. Stand upright with the bar directly in front of your legs, as your elbows are straight and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. While you do not necessarily need to stand with an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid standing with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout the set.
2. While keeping your elbows straight, sweep your arms forward and upward in an arcing manner to lift the bar to approximately shoulder height.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    2. All the movement should occur from shoulder motion while you keep your legs and torso stationary throughout the exercise.
    3. At the top, your arms should be outstretched straight in front of you, parallel to the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until it is directly in front of your legs.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your arms pointing down toward the ground.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weight downward.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Connect a rope attachment or a straight bar attachment to the cable.
    1. Except for specifically how you grasp the attachment, you can perform this exercise with the same technique with either implement, so you can select whichever one you prefer and feel free to try each one out to determine if one feels more comfortable than the other for you.
3. In a shoulder width stance, stand with the cable station directly behind your back as the attachment is just in front of your feet, and the pulley is centered between your feet.
4. Squat down and firmly grasp the attachment with both hands as your wrists are mostly straight.
    1. If you are using a rope attachment, grasp the ends of the rope with a neutral grip as your palms are facing in toward each other. Place the outside of your index fingers in contact with the flat surface of the hard plastic end caps of the rope attachment.
    2. If you are using a straight bar attachment, grasp it with a near shoulder width overhand grip as your palms are facing backward.
5. Stand upright while holding the attachment close to your body with straight elbows and your arms pointing down toward the ground.
6. For additional stability, sit your buttocks slightly backward to adopt a very mild forward torso lean.
    1. While performing a very small hip hinge motion that results in your knees barely bending, keep your chest up and lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. In the starting position, you should feel tension from the cable to provide resistance through this exercise’s full range of motion. If you do not currently feel tension from the cable, take a step forward to prompt the weight stack to separate.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout the set.
2. While keeping your elbows straight, sweep your arms forward and upward in an arcing manner until your arms are approximately parallel to the ground, outstretched in front of you.
    1. While your arm moves from motion at your shoulders, your legs and torso should remain stationary throughout your set.
    2. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing your arms to sweep downward and backward in a controlled arcing manner.
    1. You should finish with your arms pointing down toward the ground with your hands just in front of your legs.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable’s resistance abruptly yank your arms downward.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. If you wish to use a precise snatch grip rather than a general approximation, you can start by determining your individual hand placement with an empty barbell or dowel rod.
    1. A true snatch grip results in the bar being level with the crease of your hip when holding the bar in a fully upright position.
    2. You can find which hand placement specifically places the bar in the aforementioned position by trying out different grip widths with an empty barbell or dowel rod until determining what precisely is your individual snatch grip width.
    3. As a general starting point, you can see where a bar is situated relative to your hip crease when grasping it with your arms spread out away from your sides with straight elbows so that each arm forms close to a 45-degree angle with your torso.
    4. Rather than trying out different grip widths, you can alternatively use that arm position as a general approximation to train this exercise effectively.
2. After adding weight plates to the barbell situated on the ground, stand upright close in front of the middle of the bar as the bar is over the middle of your feet.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
    2. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of 45lb (20kg) plates (or 25kg plates common in gyms outside the USA) or bumper plates will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. When only using smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a snatch grip deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize the largest diameter plates, you can perform a snatch grip block pull or rack pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with large diameter plates.
3. Situate your feet near hip width apart from each other or slightly wider, and position your toes slightly turned out to the sides or pointing straight ahead based on what feels best to you
    1. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
4. Spread your arms apart with straight elbows until your arms form close to 45-degree angles with your torso (if you opt to approximate your snatch grip width) or however far apart you determined from step 1 results in your specific snatch grip width.
5. With your arms held in that position and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track very far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift mildly forward to help you reach the bar rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, and after you have hinged through your available hip range motion, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back looking flat near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, rather than excessively rounding your lower back while doing so.
    4. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    5. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and engage your back muscles.
6. With your hands wide apart in the aforementioned snatch grip width, firmly grasp the bar with an overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward as you tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Strive to tighten the entirety of your core to form a “360 degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move through your hips and knees.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. While keeping your chest up and back flat with the bar close to your shins, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the bar reaches near the middle of your shins, pause in that position for a brief period of time (e.g., 1-2 seconds) while staying tight with your chest up and back flat.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability as you visibly remain stationary.
5. Following the pause, thrust your hips forward as you resume extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. You should lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your back.
6. While keeping your back flat, reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees, after which you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
7. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps with the bar on the ground.
    3. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can meaningfully train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your triceps (on the backs of your upper arms) comfortably on the angled arm pad when sitting on the seat with your elbows aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. The pivot point (which is also known as the axis of rotation) is the center about which the machine’s lever arm will rotate during the exercise, and it may be denoted by a small dot or circle in a variety of machines. This exercise will feel the most comfortable when you align your elbows with the pivot point, such that you can draw a straight line between your elbows and the pivot point.
    2. The proper seat setting will typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits.
    3. If you have to shrug your shoulders up while getting into the starting position, the seat is too low.
    4. If you have to slump forward and round your back while getting into the starting position, the seat is too high.
    5. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height for your body.
2. If your machine permits you to adjust the starting position of the handles, set them as far back as you can comfortably grip to start each rep with your elbows close to fully bent.
    1. They are always adjustable in combo machines that enable you to perform a preacher curl (when the handles are positioned forward) or a triceps extension (when the handles are positioned backward), but the handles often cannot be adjusted in dedicated triceps extension machines.
    2. The proper handle position should ensure that you can still align your elbows with the pivot point and rest your triceps comfortably on the arm pad.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Sit down on the seat with your feet resting on the foot bar (if your machine has one) or the ground.
    1. If your machine has a lower back pad, situate the back of your hip bones snugly against it.
    2. Remain seated with your feet planted on the foot bar or ground throughout your set.
5. Position the back of your upper arms and elbows on top of the arm pad as your elbows are aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. You potentially may have to lean slightly forward at your hips to achieve this alignment.
    2. Maintain this position throughout your set.
6. Firmly grasp the handles with your elbows bent and palms facing in toward each other.
    1. With the appropriate set-up, you should now feel stable in the starting position
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your triceps in contact with the arm pad, press the handles away from you by straightening your elbows.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and your upper arms in the same position in which they started.
    2. As the rest of your body remains stationary, all the movement should occur from your forearms arcing forward through elbow motion, like the hands of an analog clock rotating about the center points formed by your elbows.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to bend in a controlled manner to bring the handles backward.
    1. Move through the full range of motion permitted by the machine to return to the starting position with your elbows as close to fully bent as you can achieve.
    2. To confirm you have moved through the full range in a controlled manner, you can let the weights you selected lightly tap the remainder of the weight stack without allowing the weights to rest at the bottom.
    3. While you do not need to perform this phase with an extremely slow tempo, avoid letting the weight abruptly swing your forearms backward in an unresisted fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a position that allows you to rest your triceps (on the backs of your upper arms) comfortably on the angled arm pad when sitting on the seat with your elbows aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. The pivot point (which is also known as the axis of rotation) is the center about which the machine’s lever arm will rotate during the exercise, and it may be denoted by a small dot or circle in a variety of machines. This exercise will feel the most comfortable when you align your elbows with the pivot point, such that you can draw a straight line between your elbows and the pivot point.
    2. The proper seat setting will typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits.
    3. If you have to shrug your shoulders up while getting into the starting position, the seat is too low.
    4. If you have to slump forward and round your back while getting into the starting position, the seat is too high.
    5. In addition to having an adjustable height, some machines let you move the seat forward or backward.
    6. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height for your body.
2. If your machine lets you adjust the starting position of the handles/hand pads, set them as far back as you can comfortably grip to start each rep with your elbows close to fully bent.
    1. Combo machines that enable you to perform a preacher curl (when the handles are positioned forward) or a triceps extension (when the handles are positioned backward) always have an adjustable handle.
    2. Instead of having handles, dedicated triceps extension machines commonly have hand pads that cannot be adjusted, but they often let you move the seat forward or backward. For these machines, moving the seat forward can help you achieve a greater degree of elbow similar to setting the handles further back with combo machines.
    3. The proper position should allow you to still align your elbows with the pivot point and rest your triceps comfortably on the arm pad.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the seat with your feet resting on the foot bar (if your machine has one) or the ground.
    1. If your machine has a lower back pad, situate the back of your hip bones snugly against it.
    2. Remain seated with your feet planted on the foot bar or ground throughout your set.
5. Position the back of your upper arms and elbows on top of the arm pad as your elbows are aligned with the machine’s pivot point.
    1. You potentially may have to lean slightly forward at your hips to achieve this alignment.
    2. Maintain this position throughout your set.
6. If your machine has handles, firmly grasp them with your elbows bent. If your machine has hand pads, place your hands and/or lower forearms snugly against them in a position that feels comfortable to you, with your elbows bent.
    1. When using a machine with handles, whether it is most comfortable to grasp them with your palms facing each other (i.e., neutral grip) or facing forward (i.e., overhand grip) depends on how the handles are designed.
    2. When using a machine with hand pads, you have the option of holding the pads with your palms against them, or you can place the little finger side of your lower forearms against the pads while closing your hands into fists. Select whichever approach feels most comfortable and stable to you.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your triceps in contact with the arm pad, press the handles/hand pads away from you by straightening your elbows.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and your upper arms in the same position in which they started.
    2. As the rest of your body remains stationary, all the movement should occur from your forearms arcing forward through elbow motion, like the hands of an analog clock rotating about the center points formed by your elbows.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to bend in a controlled manner to bring the handles/hand pads backward.
    1. Move through the full range of motion permitted by the machine to return to the starting position with your elbows as close to fully bent as you can achieve.
    2. To confirm you have moved through the full range in a controlled manner, you can let the machine’s lever arm (onto which you loaded weight plates) lightly tap the stopper underneath it without resting at the bottom.
    3. While you do not need to perform this phase with an extremely slow tempo, avoid letting the weight abruptly swing your forearms backward in an unresisted fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to position the bar on your upper back and unrack it in a stable position.
    1. As an initial approximation, you can position the bar such that it is a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone when you are standing in a wide stance.
2. Face the center of the unloaded bar with your feet clearly outside shoulder width stance and your toes moderately to mildly pointing outward.
    1. You can determine specifically how wide apart you position your feet and the degree to which you rotate your toes outward based on what feels comfortable and stable to you, since what is ideal for a wide stance varies among different individuals. Avoid taking so wide a stance that you feel unbalanced.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails). Facing this way can often feel more natural for good mornings, but you can alternatively face the other direction if you personally prefer to do so.
    3. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat wider than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
4. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to then walk under the bar and situate the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. The soles of your feet should be fully planted on the ground.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    3. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    5. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    6. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    7. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of good morning reps with the form described under the exercise technique section before reracking the bar to determine the appropriate height of the safety stops.
6. Position the safety stops slightly under the lowest point or directly at the lowest point at which the bar descended during your good morning.
    1. The appropriate safety stop position should allow you to hinge through your full range of motion while being high enough to receive the bar if you fail a rep and bend your knees a bit more to set the bar on the safety stops.
7. Load the bar with your desired weight, and repeat steps 2-4.
## Exercise technique
1. While pulling down on the bar to keep it stable on your upper back, un-rack the bar by starting to stand up as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the bar on your upper back throughout your set.
2. After the bar is fully resting on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion prior to standing fully upright with the bar.
    1. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, your lower back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Your neck should similarly be near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
    3. Keep your lower back and neck near a neutral position throughout your set.
3. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Tense these muscles like you would do if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
4. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner.
    1. Avoid rounding your lower back as you keep your lower back mildly arched or flat throughout the exercise.
    2. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    3. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something, as you keep your chest up (rather than letting your chest collapse through back rounding).
    4. Keep the soles of your feet fully planted on the ground the entire time.
    5. While you hinge at your hips, the bar will naturally descend, and your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    6. How deeply you are able to hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    7. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range.
    8. If your end range corresponds to where you positioned the safety stops, you can gently tap the bar against the safety stops without resting the bar on the safety stops at the bottom.
5. Reverse the movement by driving your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so you should continue keeping your chest up without any back rounding.
    2. Once you have returned to the starting position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid driving your hips in front of your shoulders at the top, which would prompt your lower back to arch excessively. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips appropriately stacked under your shoulders to prevent excessive arching.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
7. After finishing the set, rerack the bar by bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that will allow your legs to be in an approximately horizontal position once you are at the bottom of a rep.
    1. After estimating where this setting may be, you can adjust it during later steps if warranted.
    2. If you wish to increase the intensity of the exercise beyond the typical version, you can elevate the footplate beyond this standard setting.
2. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow the lowest point of your thighs to contact the back (i.e., the end closer to the foot plate) of the pad once you are in the starting position.
    1. This is the standard set-up for a glute ham raise, as demonstrated by certain machines having a knee pad (in addition to the thigh pad) onto which you can kneel in the starting position when setting the foot plate in this manner.
    2. The proper footplate position should permit your knees to move freely throughout the exercise while feeling stable and comfortable.
    3. If your knees are resting on top of the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far forward for a standard glute ham raise, and the exercise can function more like a higher-intensity Nordic hamstring curl.
    4. If your middle or upper thighs are resting on the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far backward for a standard glute ham raise.
    5. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal footplate position.
3. Position the weight you intend to use in a spot that you can comfortably reach after you set your feet on the footplate.
    1. While a weight plate is most commonly used, you can select any type of weight that you can hold to your chest, including a medicine ball, kettlebell, or dumbbell.
    2. Based on the weight you use, the machine model you have, your body proportions, and your mobility, you have multiple options for where you can place the weight.
    3. You may be able to place certain weights on top of the pad before picking up the weight after you mount the machine. However, not all weights can remain balanced on top of the pad, and this approach is not practical for some pad designs.
    4. With a sufficiently light weight plate or kettlebell, you may be able to hang the weight on one of the handles.
    5. As an additional approach, you can have a partner hand you the weight after you are set up, which can be useful if you are using a heavy weight.
4. Climb up onto the machine, and position the soles of your feet fully against the footplate with your lower legs between the leg rollers and your lower thigh resting against the thigh pad.
    1. The back of your lower legs should be snugly pressed into the top pair of leg rollers slightly above your heels.
    2. While you are setting your feet, you can use the handles for assistance.
    3. Some machines have a step that you can use to help you get into position.
5. Pick up the weight, hold it with both hands close to your chest, and continue doing so throughout your set.
    1. The precise way in which you will pick up and hold it may vary based on the specific implement you selected and where you placed it initially. What matters is that you have a firm grip on the weight and are able to keep it in a stable position throughout your set.
6. Assume an upright torso position, where your shoulders and hips are vertically stacked above your knees when viewed from the side.
    1. Your back and neck should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Keep your glutes squeezed and your core braced throughout the set to help you maintain a rigid trunk position.
2. In a controlled manner, lean your thighs and torso forward as one unit exclusively through knee motion.
    1. As all the movement occurs from your knees transitioning to a less bent position, keep your torso and hips in a rigid position while keeping your feet firmly against the footplate.
    2. Throughout your set, you should be able to continue drawing a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders. To do so, you will have to avoid bending forward at your hips or dynamically arching your lower back, either of which may occur if you do not appropriately continue squeezing your glutes and bracing your core.
    3. While you do not need to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should actively be in control the entire time rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you downward.
    4. As you lean forward, your knees will gradually slide upward and become closer to being vertically level with your feet.
3. Continue leaning forward until you have straightened your knees with your whole body in a horizontal position.
4. Reverse the movement by bending your knees to lift your torso and thighs as one unit until you have returned to having your hips and shoulders vertically stacked above your knees.
    1. It can help to think about driving the balls of your feet into the footplate.
    2. Since you are continuing to keep your thighs and torso aligned, your hips and shoulders should lift upward and backward in an arcing motion at the same rate. Avoid bending over at your torso while shooting your hips backward, which is a common error people make.
    3. Strive to have your torso and thighs form the hand of an analog clock that is arcing from 9 o’clock to 12 o’clock about the center point formed by your knees.
    4. During the lifting phase, your knees will naturally descend mildly toward the bottom of the thigh pad.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, return the weight to the position in which you initially placed it, bend at your hips, and dismount the machine while using your hands for assistance.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your hamstrings, gracilis, and sartorius, which are assisted by your gastrocnemius and gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that will allow your legs to be in an approximately horizontal position once you are at the bottom of a rep.
    1. After estimating where this setting may be, you can adjust it during later steps if warranted.
    2. If you wish to increase the intensity of the exercise beyond the typical version, you can elevate the footplate beyond this standard setting.
2. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow the lowest point of your thighs to contact the back (i.e., the end closer to the foot plate) of the pad once you are in the starting position.
    1. This is the standard set-up for a glute ham raise, as demonstrated by certain machines having a knee pad (in addition to the thigh pad) onto which you can kneel in the starting position when setting the foot plate in this manner.
    2. The proper footplate position should permit your knees to move freely throughout the exercise while feeling stable and comfortable.
    3. If your knees are resting on top of the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far forward for a standard glute ham raise, and the exercise can function more like a higher-intensity Nordic hamstring curl.
    4. If your middle or upper thighs are resting on the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far backward for a standard glute ham raise.
    5. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal footplate position.
3. Climb up onto the machine, and position the soles of your feet fully against the footplate with your lower legs between the leg rollers and your lower thigh resting against the thigh pad.
    1. The back of your lower legs should be snugly pressed into the top pair of leg rollers slightly above your heels.
    2. While you are setting your feet, you can use the handles for assistance.
    3. Some machines have a step that you can use to help you get into position.
4. Assume an upright torso position, where your shoulders and hips are vertically stacked above your knees when viewed from the side.
    1. Your back and neck should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
5. Place the fingertips of each hand lightly in contact with the sides of the back of your head as your upper arms and elbows are directed out away from your sides.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Keep your glutes squeezed and your core braced throughout the set to help you maintain a rigid trunk position.
2. In a controlled manner, lean your thighs and torso forward as one unit exclusively through knee motion.
    1. As all the movement occurs from your knees transitioning to a less bent position, keep your torso and hips in a rigid position while keeping your feet firmly against the footplate.
    2. Throughout your set, you should be able to continue drawing a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders. To do so, you will have to avoid bending forward at your hips or dynamically arching your lower back, either of which may occur if you do not appropriately continue squeezing your glutes and bracing your core.
    3. While you do not need to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should actively be in control the entire time rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you downward.
    4. As you lean forward, your knees will gradually slide upward and become closer to being vertically level with your feet.
3. Continue leaning forward until you have straightened your knees with your whole body in a horizontal position.
    1. The exercise will become progressively more difficult as you descend, so not every lifter has the strength to lower through the full range of motion with quality technique, which includes keeping the thighs and torso aligned.
    2. If you are currently unable to control the descent until your knees have straightened with this variation, you can select a lower intensity glute ham raise variation, such as where you keep your arms crossed in front of your chest or by your sides.
4. Reverse the movement by bending your knees to lift your torso and thighs as one unit until you have returned to having your hips and shoulders vertically stacked above your knees.
    1. It can help to think about driving the balls of your feet into the footplate.
    2. Since you are continuing to keep your thighs and torso aligned, your hips and shoulders should lift upward and backward in an arcing motion at the same rate. Avoid bending over at your torso while shooting your hips backward, which is a common error people make.
    3. Strive to have your torso and thighs form the hand of an analog clock that is arcing from 9 o’clock to 12 o’clock about the center point formed by your knees.
    4. During the lifting phase, your knees will naturally descend mildly toward the bottom of the thigh pad.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, you can bend at your hips and grasp the handles to help you dismount the machine.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your hamstrings, gracilis, and sartorius, which are assisted by your gastrocnemius and gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that will allow your legs to be in an approximately horizontal position once you are at the bottom of a rep.
    1. After estimating where this setting may be, you can adjust it during later steps if warranted.
2. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow the lowest point of your thighs to contact the back (i.e., the end closer to the foot plate) of the pad once you are in the starting position.
    1. This is the standard set-up for a glute ham raise, as demonstrated by certain machines having a knee pad (in addition to the thigh pad) onto which you can kneel in the starting position when setting the foot plate in this manner.
    2. The proper footplate position should permit your knees to move freely throughout the exercise while feeling stable and comfortable.
    3. If your knees are resting on top of the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far forward for a standard glute ham raise, and the exercise can function more like a higher-intensity Nordic hamstring curl.
    4. If your middle or upper thighs are resting on the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far backward for a standard glute ham raise.
    5. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal footplate position.
3. Anchor a long looped elastic band of your desired assistance level around the back of the machine on a smooth surface.
    1. Select a band with a tension level that provides sufficient assistance for you to be able to train through a full range of motion for your desired number of glute ham raise reps.
    2. Specifically how you could anchor the band may vary depending on the design of the specific machine you are using. However, with a variety of machines, you can anchor the band around the metal post to which the roller pads attach and pull the band up over the top of the post.
    3. If you have equipment that can be used as an anchor point (like a power rack) close behind your glute ham developer, you can alternatively anchor your band to that.
    4. Ultimately, you want to ensure that the band is securely anchored while being able to exert a force directed back toward the footplate during the exercise.
    5. It is recommended initially to inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
4. Climb up onto the machine, and position the soles of your feet fully against the footplate with your lower legs between the leg rollers and your lower thigh resting against the thigh pad.
    1. The back of your lower legs should be snugly pressed into the top pair of leg rollers slightly above your heels.
    2. While you are setting your feet, you can use the handles for assistance.
    3. Some machines have a step that you can use to help you get into position.
5. Pull the free end of the band up over your head to your chest, where you can hold it with both hands.
    1. Based on what feels most comfortable and stable, you can pull the band over your shoulders like the straps of a backpack, or you can pull it a little lower under your armpits.
    2. Regardless of how you situate the band, it should not be applying any pressure to your neck.
    3. From this point onward, use your hands to help keep the band stabilized against your chest as you maintain the same arm position throughout the set.
6. Assume an upright torso position, where your shoulders and hips are vertically stacked above your knees when viewed from the side.
    1. Your back and neck should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Keep your glutes squeezed and your core braced throughout the set to help you maintain a rigid trunk position.
2. In a controlled manner, lean your thighs and torso forward as one unit exclusively through knee motion.
    1. As all the movement occurs from your knees transitioning to a less bent position, keep your torso and hips in a rigid position while keeping your feet firmly against the footplate.
    2. Throughout your set, you should be able to continue drawing a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders. To do so, you will have to avoid bending forward at your hips or dynamically arching your lower back, either of which may occur if you do not appropriately continue squeezing your glutes and bracing your core.
    3. While you do not need to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should actively be in control the entire time rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you downward.
    4. As you lean forward, your knees will gradually slide upward and become closer to being vertically level with your feet.
3. Continue leaning forward until you have straightened your knees with your whole body in a horizontal position.
4. Reverse the movement by bending your knees to lift your torso and thighs as one unit until you have returned to having your hips and shoulders vertically stacked above your knees.
    1. It can help to think about driving the balls of your feet into the footplate.
    2. Since you are continuing to keep your thighs and torso aligned, your hips and shoulders should lift upward and backward in an arcing motion at the same rate. Avoid bending over at your torso while shooting your hips backward, which is a common error people make. If you cannot complete the lifting phase without bending at your hips, a greater level of band assistance may be required.
    3. Strive to have your torso and thighs form the hand of an analog clock that is arcing from 9 o’clock to 12 o’clock about the center point formed by your knees.
    4. During the lifting phase, your knees will naturally descend mildly toward the bottom of the thigh pad.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, you can remove the band, bend at your hips, and grasp the handles to help you dismount the machine.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your hamstrings, gracilis, and sartorius, which are assisted by your gastrocnemius and gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that will allow your legs to be in an approximately horizontal position once you are at the bottom of a rep.
    1. After estimating where this setting may be, you can adjust it during later steps if warranted.
    2. If you wish to increase the intensity of the exercise beyond the typical version, you can elevate the footplate beyond this standard setting.
2. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow the lowest point of your thighs to contact the back (i.e., the end closer to the foot plate) of the pad once you are in the starting position.
    1. This is the standard set-up for a glute ham raise, as demonstrated by certain machines having a knee pad (in addition to the thigh pad) onto which you can kneel in the starting position when setting the foot plate in this manner.
    2. The proper footplate position should permit your knees to move freely throughout the exercise while feeling stable and comfortable.
    3. If your knees are resting on top of the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far forward for a standard glute ham raise, and the exercise can function more like a higher-intensity Nordic hamstring curl.
    4. If your middle or upper thighs are resting on the thigh pad in the starting position, the footplate is excessively far backward for a standard glute ham raise.
    5. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal footplate position.
3. Climb up onto the machine, and position the soles of your feet fully against the footplate with your lower legs between the leg rollers and your lower thigh resting against the thigh pad.
    1. The back of your lower legs should be snugly pressed into the top pair of leg rollers slightly above your heels.
    2. While you are setting your feet, you can use the handles for assistance.
    3. Some machines have a step that you can use to help you get into position.
4. Assume an upright torso position, where your shoulders and hips are vertically stacked above your knees when viewed from the side.
    1. Your back and neck should be near a neutral position with its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
5. Cross your arms across your chest with bent elbows to position each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Keep your glutes squeezed and your core braced throughout the set to help you maintain a rigid trunk position.
2. In a controlled manner, lean your thighs and torso forward as one unit exclusively through knee motion.
    1. As all the movement occurs from your knees transitioning to a less bent position, keep your torso and hips in a rigid position while keeping your feet firmly against the footplate.
    2. Throughout your set, you should be able to continue drawing a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders. To do so, you will have to avoid bending forward at your hips or dynamically arching your lower back, either of which may occur if you do not appropriately continue squeezing your glutes and bracing your core.
    3. While you do not need to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should actively be in control the entire time rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you downward.
    4. As you lean forward, your knees will gradually slide upward and become closer to being vertically level with your feet.
3. Continue leaning forward until you have straightened your knees with your whole body in a horizontal position.
    1. The exercise will become progressively more difficult as you descend, so not every lifter has the strength to lower through the full range of motion with quality technique, which includes keeping the thighs and torso aligned.
    2. If you are currently unable to control the descent until your knees have straightened with this variation, you can select the mildly lower intensity glute ham raise, where you keep your arms by your sides, or the band-assisted glute ham raise as an alternative.
4. Reverse the movement by bending your knees to lift your torso and thighs as one unit until you have returned to having your hips and shoulders vertically stacked above your knees.
    1. It can help to think about driving the balls of your feet into the footplate.
    2. Since you are continuing to keep your thighs and torso aligned, your hips and shoulders should lift upward and backward in an arcing motion at the same rate. Avoid bending over at your torso while shooting your hips backward, which is a common error people make.
    3. Strive to have your torso and thighs form the hand of an analog clock that is arcing from 9 o’clock to 12 o’clock about the center point formed by your knees.
    4. During the lifting phase, your knees will naturally descend mildly toward the bottom of the thigh pad.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, you can bend at your hips and grasp the handles to help you dismount the machine.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your hamstrings, gracilis, and sartorius, which are assisted by your gastrocnemius and gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to just below the height of your collarbone, and place a safety squat bar on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back.
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
2. Place the safety squat bar on top of the J-hooks with the handles angled down and toward the direction in which you will face during the good morning.
    1. When resting on J-hooks, most safety squat bar models are designed in a way that results in the handles being mildly angled forward or backward rather than being directed straight down.
    2. The direction in which the handles from those bars are directed will influence how far forward the weight is situated relative to the upper back pad, which influences the lever arm of the weight.
    3. If you set up with the handles angled back toward you, the weight will be pitched relatively farther forward and increase the difficulty of the exercise compared to positioning the bar in the standard way.
    4. If you wish to do so, you can intentionally position the bar in the opposite manner as its own variation, but be aware that lighter loads will be required with that approach.
    5. Certain safety squat bar models are designed with the front and back sides being identical, so their handles can be directed straight down, and how you place the bar on the J-hooks is irrelevant.
3. While facing the center of the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to then walk under the bar and grasp its handles while situating the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Your palms should be facing in toward each other while grasping the handles with bent elbows.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    3. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    5. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
    6. If you find that deeper than a mild bend at your hips and knees (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) is required to position the bar on your back, you can lift the bar out of the J-hooks, set it down, raise the height of the J-hooks, and place the bar back onto the J-hooks.
4. With the bar resting on your upper back muscles, stand up in a quarter squat-like manner by extending your knees and hips.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of good morning reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight and position the safety pins to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of the good morning.
## Exercise technique
1. With the hand placement and bar positioning determined while setting up, un-rack the bar standing up in a quarter squat-like manner by extending your knees and hips while keeping the bar positioned on your upper back.
2. Take a couple of steps backward, and position your feet clearly outside shoulder-width stance with your toes moderately to mildly pointing outward.
    1. You can determine specifically how wide apart you position your feet and the degree to which you rotate your toes outward based on what feels comfortable and stable to you, since what is ideal for a wide stance varies among different individuals. Avoid taking so wide a stance that you feel unbalanced.
    2. The soles of your feet should be fully planted on the ground.
3. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. After the deep “belly breath,” you can continue thinking about tensing the front of your abdominals, sides of your abdominals, and lower back muscles simultaneously in a manner that allows you to form a “360-degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. As a helpful cue for your “360-degree brace,” you can tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you are bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    4. Bracing will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hips throughout your set.
4. Hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner to move your buttocks backward while you allow your knees to bend mildly.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than rounding your lower back while doing so.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened.
    4. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    5. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something, as you keep your chest up (rather than letting your chest collapse through back rounding).
    6. While you hinge at your hips, the bar will naturally descend, and your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    7. How deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    8. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit your buttocks any farther back, you have finished descending through your available range of motion.
    9. If you positioned them appropriately, the safety pins should not restrict how far you can hip hinge, but if you are able to hinge until the bar contacts the safety pins, you can lightly tap the safety pins with the bar without resting the bar on the pins.
5. Reverse the movement by driving your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so you should continue keeping your chest up without any back rounding.
    2. Once you have returned to the starting position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid driving your hips in front of your shoulders at the top, which would prompt your lower back to arch excessively. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips appropriately stacked under your shoulders to prevent excessive arching.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
7. Rerack the bar by walking forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook, and you are ready to rest the bar on the J-hooks by once again bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner.
    1. Depending on how wide a stance you adopted, you may feel more balanced if you initially bring your feet closer together before walking forward to re-rack the weight.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. If your machine has one, set the range limiter (situated in one of the machine’s back corners) just under the lowest point at which the lever arm will reach during your rep.
    1. If you are new to this exercise, you will have to perform a rep to determine which setting corresponds to this position. Consequently, you can initially set the range limiter toward the bottom, continue setting up, and then adjust from there if warranted.
    2. The range limiter should be low enough that you are able to train through the full range of motion (or very close to it) comfortably permitted by your shoulder mobility.
    3. The range limiter should be high enough that it will be able to receive the weight if you fail a rep and relax your upper back in the bottom position.
    4. After finding the appropriate setting, you can make a note of it and quickly know which one to use in the future, but initially finding it can take some trial and error.
    5. Prior to performing a challenging set, it is recommended to perform a warm-up set to confirm that you set the range limiter correctly if you are new to this machine.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Step on the footplate and stand upright in a hip-width to shoulder-width stance with your back fully against the back pad.
    1. If the pad is long enough for you to do so, rest your head and/or the back of your hip bones against the pad as well.
    2. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, you should be standing with your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. Note where the foot pedal is located, since you will have to press it at the end of your set.
4. Firmly grasp a pair of handles at chest height with your palms facing down. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    1. Machines typically have multiple pairs of handles at different heights from which you can select, but certain models may have a pair of handles that you manually have to adjust to your desired height. If your machine has adjustable handles, set them to a position that situates them at chest height when you are standing on the platform. Certain machine models will also let you adjust the width of the handles in addition to their height. With one of those machines, setting the handles close to the point where they are moderately outside shoulder width is often appropriate.
    2. It is recommended to pick a pair of handles that let your hands be close to level with your lower chest at the bottom of each rep. Finding this point may require trying out a couple of different handle heights, since the machine’s lever arms arc mildly downward during the rep’s lowering phase.
    3. The appropriate handles should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the set while also feeling meaningful tension in your chest.
    4. If the handles are excessively high, resulting in the hands being near shoulder height at the bottom of a rep, some people may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort.
    5. If the handles are excessively low, resulting in the hands being fully below the chest at the bottom of a rep, the chest may not be trained as effectively.
    6. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    7. Aligning your elbows directly behind the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform for pressing.
2. Press the handles forward until you have straightened your elbows, which will disengage the safety arm (i.e., what the machine lever arms initially rest upon).
    1. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the footplate.
3. Let the handles move backward in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Keep your elbows and wrists aligned behind the handles as your elbows progressively move backward while bending.
    2. People commonly have the mobility to reach at least the point at which the handles are horizontally near chest level, but the end range will vary among different individuals.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom, but you should not force yourself into an end range that causes any shoulder joint discomfort.
    4. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should actively control the weight the entire time, rather than letting it quickly swing your arms  back.
4. Reverse the movement as you press the handles forward by driving your elbows forward while straightening them.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows stacked behind your wrists and the handles.
    2. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
5. Alternate between steps 3-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Note that the initial press from step 2 does not constitute a full rep. Its function is to assume the starting position.
6. Once you have locked out your final rep, press the foot pedal with one foot to engage the safety arm.
7. Move the handles back until you can rest the weight on the safety arm before letting go of the handles.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your entire pec major, which is assisted by your front delt, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. Plates weighing 45lb (or 20kg) may be awkward to remove from this machine, especially if you are adding at least two of them, so using no greater than 35lb (or 15kg) plates may make it easier to remove the plates at the end of the set.
2. In a hip-width to shoulder-width stance, stand centered directly in front of the handles.
3. Lift your arms slightly forward, so that your palms are just in front of the handles with your palms facing backward and elbows straight.
4. Mildly lean forward at your hips until you can hook the tips of your four long fingers around the bottom handle and firmly grasp it with straight wrists.
    1. Point your thumbs mildly forward so that they will be out of the way of the top handle.
    2. The handle should be resting on the joints of your fingers formed by your fingertip bones and the bones directly next to them that are closer toward your palms. These joints are most apparent when you make a fist.
## Exercise technique
1. Stand upright, using your hips to lift the bottom handle toward the top handle while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. Depending on your height, you may have to maintain a slight forward lean throughout your set, or you may be able to stand fully upright.
2. Move your thumbs over the top handle on its side closer to you, and snugly contact the top handle with the base of each palm.
    1. The appropriate grip should result in the webspaces between your thumbs and index fingers touching the bar.
3. Curl your four long fingers to lift the bottom handle upward until it taps the top handle.
    1. Throughout this exercise, you will predominantly be moving your fingers through motion at your knuckle joints.
    2. This motion is similar to the “come here” hand gesture performed with your four long fingers.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows and wrists straight while your thumbs remain stationary around the top handle throughout your set.
4. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by uncurling your fingers to lower the bottom handle.
    1. You should finish with the finger bones attached to your knuckles pointing straight down while continuing to keep your fingertips hooked around the bottom handle.
    2. If you feel as if the bottom handle is sliding out of your fingers, you should wrap your fingertips further around it to secure your grip.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weight rapidly uncurl your fingers in an uncontrolled fashion.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
6. Drive your elbows up in an upright rowing motion to lift the bases of your palms off the top handle and point your thumbs mildly forward before leaning forward at your hips to set the plates down.
    1. Release your four long fingers from the bottom handle after the plates are fully resting on the bottom of the machine.
## Description
While also effectively training the majority of your intrinsic hand muscles, this exercise can develop your forearms’s flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundas.## Setting up
1. Position your shoulders snugly underneath the shoulder pads as your back is fully in contact with the back pad.
    1. The back of your hip bones should also be contacting the back pad.
    2. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
2. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot plate, near shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to hack press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when hack squatting through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the foot plate works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select should allow your heels (along with the rest of your feet) to be firmly on the foot plate throughout the whole set. Your foot placement should also allow you to keep the back of your hips snugly against the back pad at the bottom of each rep. The proper position should additionally feel comfortable for your knees.
    5. Placing your feet higher up may make it easier to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate, more difficult to keep your back flat against the back pad, and potentially bias the gluteus maximus to a greater degree. Placing your feet lower down may make it more difficult to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate, easier to keep your back flat against the back pad, and potentially bias the quads to a degree. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward and/or limits your range of motion.
    6. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to allow you to unlock the sled and begin your set. If you have very short legs, you potentially may have to situate your feet lower on the foot plate to start with slightly bent knees.
3. Without having any weight plates added to the machine, perform a few reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section before finishing your setup.
    1. These initial reps will help you determine what your full range of motion entails and inform you if any adjustments (such as to your foot placement or machine settings) are warranted.
    2. If you make any adjustments to your foot placement or the machine, it is recommended to try out a couple of reps without additional weights to make sure the adjustments were appropriate before adding weights.
4. If your machine allows you to adjust it, angle the foot plate to a setting that comfortably allows you to train through a full range of motion with your feet fully on the foot plate.
    1. Angling the foot plate closer toward a horizontal position can reduce the amount of ankle mobility required to keep your heels in contact with the foot plate at the bottom of the rep. If you noticed that your ability to keep your heels fully on the foot plate limited how deeply you were able to hack squat, it may be appropriate to try angling the foot plate closer toward a horizontal position. This may allow you to hack squat with a deeper knee bend compared to if the foot plate were angled more vertically.
    2. However, your feet may feel unstable and risk sliding if the foot plate is angled excessively close to being horizontal, so you want to find an appropriate balance.
5. If your machine has them, position the safety stoppers slightly under the lowest point at which the sled reaches at the bottom of your hack squat rep.
    1. Depending on how deeply you hack squat and the machine you use, the lowest safety stoppers available potentially may not be sufficiently low to allow you to descend through your full range of motion.
6. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and repeat steps 1 and 2 to assume the starting position with the foot placement you determined is appropriate for you.
    1. Note that the machine will provide resistance even without any additional weight plates. The weight of the sled will vary depending on the specific model, but it commonly weighs reasonably close to 100lb (45kg).
    2. Avoid adding so much weight that you no longer are able to train through your full range of motion.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handles with your hands to move the safety stoppers out of the sled’s way.
    1. The specific location of the safety handles and how to unlock them can vary among different machine models. However, the safety handles are often near where your hands will be when resting by your sides, and rotating the handles outward will commonly disengage the safety stoppers.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handles) and continue holding them throughout your set.
    1. The fixed handles are commonly located near the shoulder pads.
5. Squat down in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the foot plate.
    2. Continue keeping your back and the back of your hip bones snugly in contact with the back pad.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue squatting down through the full range of motion you comfortably can while keeping your heels fully on the foot plate, along with the back of your hips and back flat against the back pad.
    1. People can commonly descend until their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees, and some lifters can reach the point at which their thighs touch their calves with their knees fully bent. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. With certain machine models, you alternatively may reach the end range to which the machine permits before reaching the deepest position that your mobility enables.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track over your toes, hack squatting any deeper would cause your heels to start lifting off the foot plate.
    4. If you reached the end range to which your hips can bend, lowering the sled any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your hip bones starting to curl forward off the back pad.
    5. Hack squatting into a deep position is not inherently problematic to the knees, but there is no need to force yourself into the lowest position possible if you experience knee discomfort at your end range.
7. Once you have reached the bottom of your hack squat, pause in that position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., 1-2 seconds) while staying tight.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
8. Reverse the movement by driving your feet into the foot plate to squat up as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when hack squatting, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, knees aligned over your feet, and your back and the back of your hip bones fully against the back pad.
9. Alternate between steps 5-8 until you have completed your set.
10. After your knees are straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, lock the safety handles with your hands to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the sled.
11. Lower the sled onto the safety stoppers by bending your knees.
## Description
While also training your gluteus maximus and multiple hip adductor muscles, this exercise primarily targets your quads.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells by your thighs with straight elbows as your palms face your thighs.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hand while holding them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
    3. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    4. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    5. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back flat, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
2. Situate your palms right next to the outer aspect of the front of your thighs so that your palms face at an angle between backward and in toward each other.
    1. This results in a semi-overhand grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip that would direct your palms straight backward (with the dumbbells directly in front of your body) and a neutral grip that would direct your palms straight in toward your sides (with the dumbbells directly to the sides of your body).
    2. Keep your palms facing this way throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Bracing your core will help you maintain a rigid torso position as you subsequently move about your hips rather than your back.
2. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the dumbbells just in front of your legs. Avoid letting the dumbbells drift forward away from you, which may be more likely to occur if you think about “bending over” rather than “sitting back.”
    2. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the dumbbells.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    5. Throughout the set, keep the soles of your feet fully planted on the ground and your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
3. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the dumbbells at least below their knees, but how deeply you are able to hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be in clear control of the entire descent.
4. Once you have reached the end range to which you can hinge at your hips, pause in the bottom position for a brief period of time (e.g., about two seconds) while staying tight with your chest up and back flat.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability as you visibly remain stationary.
5. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so you should continue keeping your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, feet fully on the ground, and the dumbbells close to your legs.
    2. When you have finished lifting the dumbbells, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips appropriately stacked under your shoulders to prevent excessive arching.
6. Alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. Once you have finished your last rep, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back flat rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    3. Pick up the dumbbells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Stand in approximately a hip-width stance with the back of your legs against the edge of the sturdy elevated object you will use to elevate your foot.
    1. If available, a padded cylindrical object positioned near knee height will normally be most comfortable and is recommended.
    2. A Smith machine bar with a squat/hip thrust pad on it, a barbell on top of the J-cups of a power rack, the leg pad of a lying hamstring curl machine, and a split squat roller stand are all high-quality cylindrical options that you can position to knee height.
    3. Alternatively, you can use an object like a flat exercise bench, couch, or plyometric box with a somewhat compliant surface, but objects with a flat surface tend not to be as comfortable for this exercise.
3. While keeping your feet approximately hip-width apart, step forward with one foot until its heel is just in front of your other foot’s big toe before doing the same with your opposite foot.
    1. This results in your front foot now being about two “foot lengths” in front of where you started when your calves were touching the object you will use to elevate your rear foot.
    2. While this strategy can consistently situate most people in a viable starting position for the exercise, somewhat of a range of foot placements can be effectively used including having your front foot two and a half “foot lengths” in front of where you started (which may be even more comfortable for some people).
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your bodyweight into your front foot before placing the top surface (on the side of your shoelaces) of your rear foot on top of the elevated surface with that side’s knee bent.
    1. To help shift your bodyweight into your front foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you slightly bend that side’s knee.
    2. Once you have situated your rear foot into the elevated position, you can return to keeping your torso in a fairly upright position, which should involve you maintaining the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright (rather than excessively arching beyond that position).
    3. Strive to move your rear foot backward in a straight line to keep your feet approximately hip-width apart, rather than bringing your rear foot closer toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep the entire sole of your front foot firmly planted on the ground and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    6. If you are using a cylindrical object to elevate your foot, you can contact the surface with the front of your ankle along with your foot, but only your foot will contact the elevated surface if it is flat, like a bench.
    7. Keep your arms by your sides, directed down toward the ground, as you continue holding the dumbbells throughout the duration of your set.
2. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until your rear leg’s kneecap is just barely above the ground.
    1. Keep most of your weight shifted onto your front foot while you are primarily using your rear foot to assist with balance.
    2. Continue keeping your torso fairly upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. If you initially positioned your front foot excessively far in front of your elevated object, you may reach the end of your available hip range of motion before your rear knee descends to just above the ground. If you initially positioned your front foot too close to the elevated object, your depth may be limited by your ability to keep your front foot’s heel on the ground. In either case of suboptimal foot placement, you can simply descend through the range of motion in which you can move with proper technique before adjusting how far in front your front foot is situated after finishing your rep.
3. Once your rear knee is just barely above the ground, pause in that position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., about two seconds).
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
4. While keeping your front foot firmly planted on the ground and your rear foot in its elevated position, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso fairly upright and knees aligned with your feet while having most of your weight shifted onto the front foot.
5. Repeat steps 2-4 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position and which leg is in the elevated position.
    1. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back near a neutral position rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, select your weight with the loading pin, and connect a D-handle attachment to the cable.
2. Stand with the side of one shoulder (on the same side of your body you intend to train) facing the cable station.
3. Squat down and grasp the handle firmly with the hand closer to the cable station.
    1. Keep your elbow straight and your palm facing in toward your side.
    2. When squatting down (and up during the next step), keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
4. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the handle while continuing to keep your elbow straight.
    1. You can situate your resting hand wherever you feel helps maintain your balance, such as raised out to the side or resting on your hip.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the side in which you are holding the cable.
5. Take a step out to the side against the cable’s resistance while keeping your straight arm aligned with the cable. Simultaneously allow the cable’s resistance to pull your shoulder blade downward and out toward the pulley.
    1. Your arm should naturally be directed diagonally out to the side and downward in line with the cable.
    2. Due to the cable’s resistance, your working side’s shoulder blade should be a bit lower and further away from the midline of your back compared to your resting side’s shoulder blade.
    3. You should currently feel resistance from the cable, so take another step away from the cable station if you do not feel resistance.
6. Position your feet about shoulder-width apart with your knees slightly bent in an athletic stance and your chest up.
    1. Ultimately, you want to adopt a stance that helps you feel stable and balanced, so you can modify your stance based on what helps accomplish that for you personally.
## Exercise technique
1. As you keep your elbow straight, shrug your shoulder and shoulder blade upward while simultaneously pulling them in toward your body’s midline.
    1. The objective is to move your shoulder and shoulder blade diagonally in the opposite direction to which the cable is pulling them.
    2. Once you can no longer shrug upward and inward, you have reached the end range. In this position, your working side’s shoulder should be higher than your resting side’s shoulder.
    3. Throughout the set, keep your neck in a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    4. Keep your torso upright while avoiding letting the cable’s resistance bend your torso toward the pulley, as would occur during a side-bend exercise.
2. Continue squeezing your shoulder blade and shoulder upward and inward as you pause in the fully shrugged-in position for a brief period of time (e.g., about two seconds).
    1. You should feel meaningful tension in the region of your upper trap (at the base of your neck and uppermost upper back).
3. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by allowing your shoulder and shoulder blade to move diagonally downward and to the side toward the pulley.
    1. Move through your full range of motion, which may prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in your upper trap at the bottom.
    2. At the end of this motion, your working side’s shoulder should be positioned a little lower than your resting side’s shoulder due to the downward stretch that the cable can provide.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm toward the pulley.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your upper trap and levator scapulae while also training your middle trap and rhomboids to a greater degree than purely vertical shrugs.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to a barbell on the ground, and stand close to the middle of the bar in approximately a hip-width stance.
    1. Stand with the bar over the middle of your feet, which should place it about one inch (2.5cm) from most individuals’ shins when standing upright.
    2. The setup for this exercise is simply performing the lifting phase of a conventional deadlift.
2. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
3. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish.
    2. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    3. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    4. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than at the base of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hands while you hold it, which pulls on your hand skin uncomfortably and increases the risk of tearing a callus.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
5. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Keep your elbows straight and the bar very close to your legs as you lift the bar, rather than letting the bar drift forward.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the bar just in front of your legs. Avoid letting the bar drift forward away from you, which may be more likely to occur if you think about “bending over” rather than “sitting back.”
    2. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the bar.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    5. Throughout the set, keep your feet fully planted on the ground and your neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. If you have high hamstring flexibility and can hinge until the plates contact the ground, descend until you can tap the plates very lightly on the ground, but do not let the weight rest on the ground.
    4. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
3. Once you have reached the end range to which you can hinge at your hips, pause in the bottom position for a brief period of time (e.g., about two seconds) while staying tight with your chest up and back flat.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability, as you remain visibly stationary.
4. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, feet fully on the ground, and the bar close to your legs.
    2. When you have finished lifting the bar, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips properly stacked under your shoulders, preventing excessive arching.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the floor.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe between reps in the fully upright position, rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. After finishing the last rep, set the bar down in the same manner in which you initially picked it up (e.g., keeping your lower back near flat rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground, and stand close in front of the middle of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
    1. You can set up with the bar approximately over the middle of your feet, which should situate the bar close to one inch (2.5cm) away from most individuals’ shins while initially standing upright.
    2. The setup for this exercise is simply performing the lifting phase of a snatch grip deadlift.
2. With straight elbows, spread your arms apart until each arm forms close to a 45-degree angle with your torso to approximate your snatch grip.
    1. This estimation is sufficient to widen your grip appropriately and perform this exercise very effectively. However, you can take the time to determine what is precisely your individual snatch grip width if you wish to do so by following the instructions explained in the exercise notes section that follows the technique section.
    2. If you subsequently notice that the grip feels particularly uncomfortable, you can try a slightly closer grip that is still well outside shoulder width.
3. Sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track very far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift naturally forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
4. With straight elbows and your palms facing backward, firmly grasp the bar with your arms still spread wide apart.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
6. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Keep your elbows straight and the bar very close to your legs as you lift the bar rather than allowing the bar to drift forward.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the bar just in front of your legs. Avoid letting the bar drift forward away from you, which may be more likely to occur if you think about “bending over” rather than “sitting back.”
    2. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the bar.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    5. Throughout the set, keep the soles of your feet fully planted on the ground and your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you are able to hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. If you have high hamstring flexibility and are able to hinge until the plates contact the ground, descend until you can tap the plates very lightly on the ground, but do not let the weight rest on the ground.
    4. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be in clear control of the entire descent.
3. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so you should continue keeping your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, feet fully on the ground, and the bar close to your legs.
    2. When you have finished lifting the bar, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back, which would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips appropriately stacked under your shoulders to prevent excessive arching.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the floor.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. After finishing the last rep, set the bar down in the same manner in which you initially picked it up (e.g., keeping your lower back near flat rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the preacher bench’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbow and most of your triceps (on the back of your upper arm) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The appropriate seat height should allow the exercise to feel stable and comfortable, and it will also typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits once you are seated.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. On the side you wish to train, pick up a dumbbell and straddle the seat. Hold the dumbbell firmly with your palm facing forward (resulting in an underhand grip).
    1. Initially stand with enough space between you and the pad that you will be able to curl the dumbbell upward.
    2. Maintain this underhand grip and avoid rotating your palm as you finish setting up and perform the exercise.
    3. Unless stated otherwise, the directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell.
3. Curl the dumbbell upward by fully bending your elbow while lifting it upward to point straight ahead.
    1. Your arm and dumbbell should now be fully above the arm pad, which will make it easier to get into the starting position.
4. Step forward until your torso is very close to the pad before sitting down on the seat and resting your triceps and elbow on the pad. Continue keeping your elbow bent.
    1. You can use your free hand to help guide your other arm into the starting position if you wish to do so.
    2. Once you are in position, you can hold the arm pad with your free hand for additional stability throughout the set.
    3. In the starting position and throughout the exercise, your wrist should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow and triceps in contact with the arm pad, lower the dumbbell in a slow and controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. While your forearm arcs forward and downward through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbell down.
    3. After initially pointing back toward you in the starting position, your palm should be angled forward and upward at the bottom of the rep. 
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbow to curl the dumbbell upward and back toward you in an arcing motion until your elbow is fully bent.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow and triceps snugly against the arm pad.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the dumbbell past the point when your forearm is completely vertical, you can finish curling once your forearm is fully vertical (rather than once your elbow is fully bent), since your biceps will not be loaded beyond this point.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other arm.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep with your elbow fully bent, stand upright to lift your arm off the pad, and step backward before lowering the dumbbell.
    2. If you become too fatigued to finish a rep during your set, you can use your free hand to assist with lifting it up and dismounting the preacher bench.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
This page describes setting up a barbell hold the same way as an above-the-knee rack pull, since that is a common and efficient setup. However, you have the option of starting with the bar from a lower pin height, blocks, or the floor if you wish to do so, since all you need to do is wind up in the lockout position of a deadlift while holding the bar in front of you.

1. Position a power rack’s safety pins slightly above the height of your knees when standing upright.
    1. Specifically, you want to select the highest pin settings that allow you to hold the bar with straight elbows without the bar contacting the pins during this exercise.
    2. For some racks with large spaces between available pinholes, that point may result in the bar starting at or mildly below knee level. If these lower pinholes are the highest ones that you can use with your rack, you have the option of elevating your feet like you would for a deficit deadlift. To do so, you can place a large diameter weight plate or deadlift block in the center of your rack, and you will stand on top of it to perform your rack pull from a closer to above-the-knee starting position. Depending on your preferred stance width, you can also position two plates or deadlift blocks directly next to each other if the soles of your feet do not fit fully onto one plate or deadlift block.
2. Place a barbell centered on top of the safety pins, and add your desired weight plates to it.
3. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance, close in front of the middle of the bar with your toes pointing straight ahead.
    1. The bar should be touching your lower thighs.
4. With your arms directed down toward the ground, straight elbows, and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), hinge at your hips while allowing your knees to bend a bit until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something, which will naturally facilitate a mild degree of knee bend.
    2. Throughout this exercise, keep your lower back flat rather than rounding over.
    3. Similarly, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
5. Firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. Your arms should be directed straight down, such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Keep your elbows straight and the bar very close to your legs as you lift the bar rather than allowing the bar to drift forward.
    2. When you have finished lifting the bar, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the top can help you finish with your hips appropriately stacked under your shoulders to prevent excessive arching.
3. Statically grip the bar while staying upright and keeping your core braced until you have reached your target hold duration.
    1. Continue keeping your shoulders stacked above your hips as you avoid leaning back to rest the bar on your thighs.
    2. Whenever you take a breath, strive to take a belly breath rather than a chest breath to facilitate your core bracing.
4. Lower the bar onto the safety pins in a slow and controlled manner by sitting your hips backward and allowing your knees to bend mildly while keeping your lower back flat and chest up.
    1. A controlled descent is important to avoid permanently damaging the bar on the pins, so you should start lowering the bar if you feel it slipping from your hands to prevent you from dropping it.
## Description
This exercise can improve your grip strength by statically training your forearm and hand muscles that function as finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Adjust the preacher bench’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbow and most of your triceps (on the back of your upper arm) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The appropriate seat height should allow the exercise to feel stable and comfortable, and it will also typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits once you are seated.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. On the side you wish to train, pick up a dumbbell and straddle the seat. Hold the dumbbell firmly with your palm facing in toward your side (resulting in a neutral grip that is also called a hammer grip).
    1. Initially stand with enough space between you and the pad that you will be able to curl the dumbbell upward.
    2. Maintain this neutral grip and avoid rotating your palm as you finish setting up and perform the exercise.
    3. Unless stated otherwise, the directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell.
3. Curl the dumbbell upward by fully bending your elbow while lifting your elbow upward to point straight ahead.
    1. Your arm and dumbbell should now be fully above the arm pad, which will make it easier to get into the starting position.
4. Step forward until your torso is very close to the pad before sitting down on the seat and resting your triceps and elbow on the pad. Continue keeping your elbow bent.
    1. You can use your free hand to help guide your other arm into the starting position if you wish to do so.
    2. Once you are in position, you can hold the arm pad with your free hand for additional stability throughout the set.
    3. In the starting position and throughout the exercise, your wrist should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow and triceps in contact with the arm pad, lower the dumbbell in a slow and controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. While your forearm arcs forward and downward through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary with your palm facing inward throughout your set.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbell down.
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbow to curl the dumbbell upward and back toward you in an arcing motion until your elbow is fully bent.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow and triceps snugly against the arm pad.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the dumbbell past the point when your forearm is completely vertical, you can finish curling once your forearm is fully vertical (rather than once your elbow is fully bent), since the target muscles will not be resisted beyond this point.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other arm.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep with your elbow fully bent, stand upright to lift your arm off the pad, and step backward before lowering the dumbbell.
    2. If you become too fatigued to finish a rep during your set, you can use your free hand to assist with lifting it up and dismounting the preacher bench.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the power rack’s J-hooks to a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
    1. Except for positioning the safety pins higher, the setup for this exercise should be the same as for your back squat.
2. Place a barbell on top of the J-hooks and load it with your desired weight.
    1. If you are not confident that you selected the ideal J-hook height for you, you should walk out with just the bar before adding weight plates, since it can be time-consuming to adjust the J-hooks after you load the bar with plates.
    2. The proper J-hook height should allow you to unrack the bar in no deeper than a quarter squat-like manner while ensuring that the bar will not bump the J-hooks when walking out without rising onto the balls of your feet.
3. Set the safety pins directly under the J-hooks.
4. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. Use whichever hand placement you prefer when back squatting.
    2. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility, so feel free to try out different hand placements.
5. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to then walk under the bar and situate it on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar up excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand up in a quarter squat-like manner by extending (straightening) your knees and hips.
    1. The bar should now be above the tops of the J-hooks such that they would not contact the bar when walking out backward. If you need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too low. If this is the case, you need to lower the safety pins and J-hooks after reracking the bar, removing the plates, and lifting the bar off the J-hooks
3. Take a couple of steps backward, and position your feet near shoulder width apart from each other as your toes are pointing somewhat out to the sides (rather than pointing straight ahead).
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. For this variation to transfer most effectively to your standard squat, you should ultimately set up in your preferred squat stance.
    3. Keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level with each other when walking out, rather than letting either one drop down the side.
4. Continue standing upright with the bar on your back for your target duration while staying tight with your core braced, chest up, and lower back flat.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes).
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position as you look straight ahead.
    3. Whenever you take a breath, strive to take a belly breath rather than a chest breath to facilitate your core bracing.
5. Once you have reached your intended static hold time, walk forward until the bar firmly contacts the back of each J-hook.
6. Rerack the bar by bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner to rest the bar fully on the J-hooks.
## Description
While this exercise does not effectively increase the size of any particular muscle, it can train your ability to walk out and support heavy loads on your back.## Setting up
1. Adjust the preacher bench’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and most of your triceps (on the back of your upper arms) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The appropriate seat height should allow the exercise to feel stable and comfortable, and it will also typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpit once you are seated.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and straddle the seat while holding them firmly with your palms facing forward (resulting in an underhand grip).
    1. Initially stand with enough space between you and the pad that you will be able to curl the dumbbells upward.
    2. Maintain this underhand grip and avoid rotating your palms as you finish setting up and performing the exercise.
3. Curl the dumbbells upward by fully bending your elbows while lifting them upward to point straight ahead.
    1. Your arms and dumbbells should now be fully above the arm pad, which will make it easier to get into the starting position.
4. Step forward until your torso is very close to the pad before sitting down on the seat and resting your triceps and elbows on the pad.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows bent.
    2. In the starting position and throughout the exercise, your wrists should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows and triceps in contact with the arm pad, lower the dumbbells in a slow and controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. While your forearms arc forward and downward through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
    3. After initially pointing back toward you in the starting position, your palms should be angled forward and upward at the bottom of the rep. 
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbows to curl the dumbbells upward and back toward you in an arcing motion until your elbows are fully bent.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and triceps snugly against the arm pad.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the dumbbells past the point when your forearms are completely vertical, you can finish curling once your forearms are fully vertical (rather than once your elbows are fully bent), since your biceps will not be loaded beyond this point.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep with your elbows fully bent, stand upright to lift your arms off the pad, and step backward before lowering the dumbbells.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Load an adjustable EZ bar with your desired weight, or approach a fixed-weight EZ bar.
    1. Either type of EZ bar can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down and firmly grasp it with your palms angled backward and in toward each other (resulting in a semi-overhand grip) as your hands are about shoulder width apart.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a shoulder width grip is accessible on the bar.
3. While keeping your elbows straight, stand fully upright and hold the bar directly in front of your legs with your arms by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward by bending your elbows while keeping your elbows and upper arms fixed by your sides.
    1. Finish lifting the bar once your elbows are fully bent with your biceps contacting your forearms.
    2. While your forearms lift upward in an arcing manner through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    3. Avoid swinging your elbows and upper arms forward through shoulder motion.
    4. Since you should not be leaning backward, your shoulders should remain vertically aligned with your hips and knees when viewed from the side.
    5. Keep your wrists straight rather than letting the resistance from the weight bend them.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Nothing else should move except for your forearms lowering in an arcing manner through elbow motion.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the bar down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, but it may train your biceps somewhat less effectively than a curl with an underhand or neutral grip.## Setting up
1. Adjust the preacher bench’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and most of your triceps (on the back of your upper arms) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The appropriate seat height should allow the exercise to feel stable and comfortable, and it will also typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits once you are seated.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and straddle the seat while holding them firmly with your palms facing in toward each other (resulting in a neutral grip that is also called a hammer grip).
    1. Initially stand with enough space between you and the pad that you will be able to curl the dumbbells upward.
    2. Maintain this neutral grip and avoid rotating your palms as you finish setting up and perform the exercise.
3. Curl the dumbbells upward by fully bending your elbows while lifting your elbows upward to point straight ahead.
    1. Your arms and dumbbells should now be fully above the arm pad, which will make it easier to get into the starting position.
4. Step forward until your torso is very close to the pad before sitting down on the seat and resting your triceps and elbows on the pad.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows bent.
    2. In the starting position and throughout the exercise, your wrists should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows and triceps in contact with the arm pad, lower the dumbbells in a slow and controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. While your forearms arc forward and downward through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary with your palms facing in toward each other throughout your set.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbows to curl the dumbbells upward and back toward you in an arcing motion until your elbows are fully bent.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and triceps snugly against the arm pad.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the dumbbells past the point when your forearms are completely vertical, you can finish curling once your forearms are fully vertical (rather than once your elbows are fully bent), since the target muscles will not be resisted beyond this point.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep with your elbows fully bent, stand upright to lift your arms off the pad, and step backward before lowering the dumbbells.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Position a sturdy box of your desired height centered within the rack, a couple of step lengths behind the J-hooks.
    1. To train your lower body muscles most effectively, you want to pick a box height that enables you to squat near the depth of what is your full range of motion back squat (i.e., the lowest point at which you can squat with quality technique while keeping your back flat and feet fully on the floor). This depth will vary from person to person, but people can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground.
    2. A squat box or plyometric box is an ideal choice for your box. You can position either type of box with one of its corners directed to the middle point between the J-hooks to give you the greatest freedom in how you situate your feet when box squatting.
    3. Plyometric boxes are often available in gyms at heights of 12in (30cm), 18in (46cm), and 24in (61cm). Box squatting with an 18in (46cm) box, which is near the typical height of a flat exercise bench, restricts the range of motion for most people who are not clearly taller than six feet (183cm). If you currently do not have the mobility to squat down to a 12in (30cm) box with proper form, you can increase the height of the box by placing a thick bumper plate on top of the box to elevate it.
    4. While others have fixed heights like plyometric boxes, the height of certain squat box models can be adjusted to accommodate how deeply you wish to box squat.
    5. If you do not have access to a squat box or plyometric box, you can use an aerobic step provided that the step is rated to withstand at least half the combined load of your bodyweight and the bar weight. You can adjust its height based on how many pairs of risers you stack underneath it.
    6. Alternatively, you may be able to use a flat exercise bench as your box. A bench is excessively tall for many lifters to squat through a full range of motion, but it can be suitable for rather tall individuals, people with limited mobility, and anyone who intentionally wishes to train through a partial range of motion. To fit either a bench or an aerobic step within a rack, you may have to position it with the shorter side facing the side of the rack where you will place the barbell.
    7. While not ideal due to its lower stability, you can also stack bumper plates on top of each other to form a “box” of your desired height. If you opt to do so, make sure that the plates are completely centered on top of each other.
3. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
4. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees and walk under the bar. Situate the bar on your upper back muscles slightly below the base of your neck in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. For this high bar variation, you are aiming for the bar to rest on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts (on the back of your shoulders) and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine).
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable on your traps will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back as you squeeze your shoulder blades back together.
    3. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    5. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    6. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    7. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of box squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when squatting.
7. Repeat steps 3 and 4 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps backward until the box is just behind you. Position your feet near shoulder width apart from each other or mildly wider, with your toes pointing somewhat out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to box squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when box squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Based on your preference, you can use the same stance that you would when back squatting without a box, or you can use a somewhat wider stance, which can facilitate sitting back a bit more.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down until you can sit onto the box in a controlled manner, as you avoid plopping onto the box or rocking back onto the box.
    1. As you sit on the box, your torso should maintain the same degree of forward lean that you had right before reaching the box.
    2. If your torso transitions to a more upright position after sitting on the box compared to at the end of your descent, you have rocked back onto the box.
6. While keeping your core braced and back flat, and feet fully on the ground, allow about half of your weight to deload into the box as you pause on the box for approximately one second.
    1. As a cue, you can think about a scale being on top of the box. If you weigh 200lb (91kg) and have 100lb (45kg) on the bar, the scale should read within the vicinity of 150lb (68kg).
7. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Avoid rocking your torso forward to generate momentum before you start squatting upward.
    2. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
8. Alternate between steps 4-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
9. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the preacher bench’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbow and most of your triceps (on the back of your upper arm) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The appropriate seat height should allow the exercise to feel stable and comfortable, and it will also typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits once you are seated.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. On the side you wish to train, pick up a dumbbell and straddle the seat. Hold the dumbbell firmly with your palm facing backward (resulting in an overhand grip that is also called a reverse grip).
    1. Initially stand with enough space between you and the pad that you will be able to curl the dumbbell upward.
    2. Maintain this reverse grip and avoid rotating your palm as you finish setting up and perform the exercise.
    3. Unless stated otherwise, the directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell.
3. Curl the dumbbell upward by fully bending your elbow while lifting it upward to point straight ahead.
    1. Your arm and dumbbell should now be fully above the arm pad, which will make it easier to get into the starting position.
4. Step forward until your torso is very close to the pad before sitting down on the seat and resting your triceps and elbow on the pad. Continue keeping your elbow bent, which should result in your palm now facing forward.
    1. You can use your free hand to help guide your other arm into the starting position if you wish to do so.
    2. Once you are in position, you can hold the arm pad with your free hand for additional stability throughout the set.
    3. In the starting position and throughout the exercise, your wrist should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow and triceps in contact with the arm pad, lower the dumbbell in a slow and controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. While your forearm arcs forward and downward through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbell down.
    3. After initially pointing forward away from you in the starting position, your palm should be angled backward and downward at the bottom of the rep. 
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbow to curl the dumbbell upward and back toward you in an arcing motion until your elbow is fully bent.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow and triceps snugly against the arm pad.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the dumbbell past the point when your forearm is completely vertical, you can finish curling once your forearm is fully vertical (rather than once your elbow is fully bent), since the target muscles will not be resisted beyond this point.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other arm.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep with your elbow fully bent, stand upright to lift your arm off the pad, and step backward before lowering the dumbbell.
    2. If you become too fatigued to finish a rep during your set, you can turn your palm to switch to a stronger underhand grip in addition to using your free hand to assist with lifting the weight and dismounting the preacher bench.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, but it may train your biceps somewhat less effectively than a curl with an underhand or neutral grip.## Setting up
1. Add your desired weight to an adjustable plate-loaded EZ bar on top of the preacher curl bench, or place a fixed weight EZ bar on top of the preacher curl bench.
2. Adjust the seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and most of the back of your upper arms (i.e., triceps) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The proper setting will typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
3. Sit down on the seat, and position the back of your upper arms and elbows on top of the arm pad with your feet on the ground.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms angled backward and in toward each other (resulting in a semi-overhand grip) as your hands are about shoulder width apart.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it initially before a near shoulder width semi-overhand grip is accessible on the bar to perform this reverse curl.
    2. If the bar is a little out of your reach, you could slightly stand up and lean forward to reach the bar before sitting back down after you have grasped the bar.
    3. Your wrists should be mostly straight throughout your set after grasping the bar.
5. In the starting position, your elbows should be fully straightened with the bar no longer in contact with the bench so that you can train through a full range of motion.
    1. If this is not the case, you can mildly scoot back with an upright torso until you have reached this starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows and the back of your upper arms in contact with the bench, curl the bar upward and back toward you in an arcing motion by bending at your elbows until they are fully bent.
    1. While your forearms move through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the bar past the point when your forearms are completely vertical and perpendicular to the ground, you can finish the lifting phase after your forearms are vertical, since the biceps would no longer be working beyond this point.
2. Reverse the motion by lowering the bar in a slow and controlled manner until your elbows have returned to a fully straightened position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and the back of your upper arms in contact with the bench.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar back down onto the preacher bench.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, but it may train your biceps somewhat less effectively than a curl with an underhand or neutral grip.## Setting up
1. Adjust the preacher bench’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and most of your triceps (on the back of your upper arms) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The appropriate seat height should allow the exercise to feel stable and comfortable, and it will also typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits once you are seated.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and straddle the seat while holding them firmly with your palms facing backward (resulting in an overhand grip that is also called a reverse grip).
    1. Initially stand with enough space between you and the pad that you will be able to curl the dumbbells upward.
    2. Maintain this reverse grip and avoid rotating your palms as you finish setting up and perform the exercise.
3. Curl the dumbbells upward by fully bending your elbows while lifting them upward to point straight ahead.
    1. Your arms and dumbbells should now be fully above the arm pad, which will make it easier to get into the starting position.
4. Step forward until your torso is very close to the pad before sitting down on the seat and resting your triceps and elbows on the pad.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows bent, which should result in your palms now facing forward.
    2. In the starting position and throughout the exercise, your wrists should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows and triceps in contact with the arm pad, lower the dumbbells in a slow and controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. While your forearms arc forward and downward through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
    3. After initially pointing forward away from you in the starting position, your palms should be angled backward and downward at the bottom of the rep. 
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbows to curl the dumbbells upward and back toward you in an arcing motion until your elbows are fully bent.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and triceps snugly against the arm pad.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the dumbbells past the point when your forearms are completely vertical, you can finish curling once your forearms are fully vertical (rather than once your elbows are fully bent), since the target muscles will not be resisted beyond this point.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep with your elbows fully bent, stand upright to lift your arms off the pad, and step backward before lowering the dumbbells.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, but it may train your biceps somewhat less effectively than a curl with an underhand or neutral grip.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
    1. Maintain this neutral grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout the exercise, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you use hip motion instead of lower back motion to help lift the dumbbells.
2. While keeping your elbows straight, sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and your torso to incline slightly forward.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat or mildly arched rather than rounding it or excessively arching it.
3. As you simultaneously thrust your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body, curl the dumbbells upward by bending your elbows while allowing them to swing forward mildly.
    1. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. Refrain from leaning back through excessive lower back arching, which can be easier to avoid if you think about squeezing your glutes.
    3. While you are intentionally using momentum to help your arm muscles lift the weight, you should not be “cheating” to the extent that you feel out of control.
    4. You should finish the lifting phase with your elbows fully bent as your forearms contact your biceps and your palms are facing in toward each other.
4. Lower the dumbbells in a controlled manner as you progressively allow your elbows to straighten and return near your sides.
    1. You should finish with your elbows straight and the dumbbells by your sides.
    2. Keep your torso upright and hips stationary as only your arms move during the descent.
    3. Your muscles are noticeably stronger during the lowering phase of an exercise compared to the lifting phase, so “cheating” during the lifting phase allows your arm muscles to experience greater loading during the more controlled lowering phase.
    4. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should not let gravity just swing the dumbbells back down. If you are unable to control the lowering phase to any reasonable degree, you are likely using a weight that is excessively heavy relative to your current ability.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead.
    1. Throughout this exercise, you can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides or in front of your chest.
    2. You can optionally set up near an elevated object where you can place your phone with a timer or stopwatch that you can start once you begin your lunge hold. Alternatively, you can use a wristwatch or position yourself in front of a clock with a second hand to track your set duration.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to descend until your rear knee is just above the ground with your front thigh close to parallel to the ground. This may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until your rear leg’s kneecap is just barely above the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet, rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    4. Perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
3. Hold the lunge position for your target duration as you remain completely stationary with your rear knee hovering above the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet, the sole of your front foot fully on the ground, and your torso upright.
    2. Since this is a static exercise, you will track how many seconds you hold the lunge rather than reps.
4. Step forward with your rear leg while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to lift your body up into a standing position.
    1. If you are particularly fatigued following the completion of your lunge hold, you can alternatively just lower your rear knee to rest on the ground.
5. Repeat steps 1-4 while switching which leg you place in the lead position.
    1. Before proceeding to your next set, it is recommended to rest, since your rear leg can be meaningfully engaged during a lunge hold, which can result in fatigue interfering with the performance of your next set if you do not rest at all between them.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, and place a barbell on top of them.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back. 
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Position a sturdy box of your desired height centered within the rack, a couple of step lengths behind the J-hooks.
    1. To train your lower body muscles most effectively, you want to pick a box height that enables you to squat near the depth of what is your full range of motion back squat (i.e., the lowest point at which you can squat with quality technique while keeping your back flat and feet fully on the floor). This depth will vary from person to person, but people can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground.
    2. A squat box or plyometric box is an ideal choice for your box. You can position either type of box with one of its corners directed to the middle point between the J-hooks to give you the greatest freedom in how you situate your feet when box squatting.
    3. Plyometric boxes are often available in gyms at heights of 12in (30cm), 18in (46cm), and 24in (61cm). Box squatting with an 18in (46cm) box, which is near the typical height of a flat exercise bench, restricts the range of motion for most people who are not clearly taller than six feet (183cm). If you currently do not have the mobility to squat down to a 12in (30cm) box with proper form, you can increase the height of the box by placing a thick bumper plate on top of the box to elevate it.
    4. While others have fixed heights like plyometric boxes, the height of certain squat box models can be adjusted to accommodate how deeply you wish to box squat.
    5. If you do not have access to a squat box or plyometric box, you can use an aerobic step provided that the step is rated to withstand at least half the combined load of your bodyweight and the bar weight. You can adjust its height based on how many pairs of risers you stack underneath it.
    6. Alternatively, you may be able to use a flat exercise bench as your box. A bench is excessively tall for many lifters to squat through a full range of motion, but it can be suitable for rather tall individuals, people with limited mobility, and anyone who intentionally wishes to train through a partial range of motion. To fit either a bench or an aerobic step within a rack, you may have to position it with the shorter side facing the side of the rack where you will place the barbell.
    7. While not ideal due to its lower stability, you can also stack bumper plates on top of each other to form a “box” of your desired height. If you opt to do so, make sure that the plates are completely centered on top of each other.
3. Face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance and grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width varies by person, and what feels best to you can depend on your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
4. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should be securely resting on the muscles of your upper back when squeezing your shoulder blades together.
    2. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    5. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a barbell on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of box squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when squatting.
7. Repeat steps 3 and 4 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back, and stand upright by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Maintain this bar position throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps backward until the box is just behind you. Position your feet clearly outside shoulder width, and point your toes moderately out to the sides.
    1. Ultimately, you want to use a wide stance for this variation, but what is ideal for a wide stance varies among different individuals, so trying out different stances over time can be helpful.
    2. You can determine specifically how wide apart you position your feet and the degree to which you rotate your toes outward based on what feels comfortable and stable to you.
    3. Unless you typically squat with a rather wide stance, you can also use your standard stance as a reference point and moderately widen your stance from there.
    4. As a general rule of thumb, a stance width that is at least 150% of your shoulder width with your toes pointing about 30-45 degrees out to the side is an appropriate starting point.
    5. The soles of your feet should be fully planted on the ground, while your lower back is flat and your head is looking straight ahead.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down until you can sit onto the box in a controlled manner, as you avoid plopping onto the box or rocking back onto the box.
    1. As you sit on the box, your torso should maintain the same degree of forward lean that you had right before reaching the box.
    2. If your torso transitions to a more upright position after sitting on the box compared to at the end of your descent, you have rocked back onto the box.
6. While keeping your core braced and back flat, and feet fully on the ground, allow about half of your weight to deload into the box as you pause on the box for approximately one second.
    1. As a cue, you can think about a scale being on top of the box. If you weigh 200lb (91kg) and have 100lb (45kg) on the bar, the scale should read within the vicinity of 150lb (68kg).
7. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Avoid rocking your torso forward to generate momentum before you start squatting upward.
    2. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
8. Alternate between steps 4-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
9. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. Depending on how wide a stance you adopted, you may feel more balanced if you initially bring your feet closer together before walking forward to re-rack the weight.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells while firmly holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    1. Prior to picking up the dumbbells, you optionally can set up a stopwatch on your phone in a location where you can view throughout your set, such as propped up against a dumbbell near you. Alternatively, you can position yourself in front of a clock with a second hand to track your set duration.
    2. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    3. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    4. Pick up the dumbbells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead.
    1. Throughout the exercise, continue holding the dumbbells by your sides with your arms directed straight down toward the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to descend until your rear knee is just above the ground with your front thigh close to parallel to the ground. This may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until your rear leg’s kneecap is just barely above the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet, rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    4. Perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
3. Hold the lunge position for your target duration as you remain completely stationary with your rear knee hovering above the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet, the sole of your front foot fully on the ground, and your torso upright.
    2. Since this is a static exercise, you will track how many seconds you hold the lunge rather than reps.
4. Step forward with your rear leg while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to lift your body up into a standing position.
    1. If you are particularly fatigued following the completion of your lunge hold, you can alternatively just lower your rear knee to rest on the ground and set the dumbbells down.
5. Repeat steps 1-4 while switching which leg you place in the lead position.
    1. Before proceeding to your next set, it is recommended to set the dumbbells down and rest, since your rear leg can be meaningfully engaged during a lunge hold, which can result in fatigue interfering with the performance of your next set if you do not rest at all between them.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to a little higher than your head, and select your desired load with the loading pin
2. Based on your preference, leave the cable without any handle attachment or connect a D-handle attachment to the cable.
    1. Either option allows you to train your rear delts with essentially the same technique except for the specific grip you will use, so feel free to try out both and use whichever feels most comfortable to you.
3. Stand with the side of your shoulder (on the side you will not train during this set) facing the cable station, and take a step backward.
4. With your arm that is opposite to the cable, reach across your body with a slightly bent elbow and firmly grasp the ball at the end of the cable (if you are not using any handle attachment) or the D-handle (if you are using a handle attachment). Simultaneously grasp the cable station’s beam with your free hand for additional stability.
    1. Continue using your free hand to help stabilize your body throughout the exercise.
    2. Keep your working side’s elbow slightly bent from this point onward.
5. If you do not currently feel resistance from the cable, take a small step backward out to the side away from the pulley until you do.
    1. You ideally want to experience resistance from the cable in the starting position of each rep.
    2. Rather than having your torso twisted with your chest facing the pulley, your chest and imaginary belt buckle should be facing the same direction. Having your chest rotated toward the pulley may prompt you not to feel tension from the cable in the starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arm out away from the pulley while driving it diagonally downward and backward.
    1. Move through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility permits, which should result in your elbow being slightly behind your shoulder or at least horizontally level with your shoulder at the end range.
    2. Strive to achieve a roughly even balance between sweeping horizontally outward and vertically downward.
    3. Keep your torso upright with your chest up throughout the set, while all the motion occurs from your arm moving at your shoulder.
    4. Continue keeping your chest and imaginary belt buckle facing straight ahead in the same direction as you avoid twisting your torso.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your arm to move diagonally forward, upward, and in toward the pulley across your body until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. When you set up appropriately, the angle of resistance from the cable will naturally guide your arm through this intended diagonal path.
    2. You do not have to perform this movement with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm toward the pulley.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delts while also training your traps, rhomboids, lats, teres majores, and two rotator cuff muscles to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Use the machine’s loading pin to select your desired level of assistance, and ensure that the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is positioned down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you select heavier loads from the assisted dip machine, since the weight determines the extent to which the machine will support your bodyweight.
    2. Certain machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but essentially the same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates.
3. If your machine allows you to rotate its parallel handles inward or outward, position them to whichever setting is closest to being slightly outside shoulder width for you.
    1. A slightly outside shoulder-width grip is appropriate for many lifters, but a mildly wider grip or a shoulder-width grip may also be used. If they are adjustable on your machine, feel free to try out both handle positions to determine which one personally feels best to you.
4. Firmly grasp the parallel handles with your palms facing each other. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
5. One leg at a time, kneel onto the machine’s knee pad (if your machine has a knee pad) or step onto the machine’s foot bar (if your machine has a foot bar) while continuing to grasp the handles firmly. Maintain straight elbows when doing so.
    1. If using a machine with a knee pad, you will keep your knees bent on the pad throughout your set, but if you are using a machine with a foot bar, you will keep your knees straight while standing on the bar throughout your set.
6. Rather than keeping your torso fully upright, mildly lean your torso forward and maintain this mild lean throughout your set.
    1. The appropriate degree of lean should result in your chest being slightly in front of your hips (when viewed from the side) while helping your shoulders feel comfortable throughout the set.
    2. Feel free to try out different degrees of torso lean over time to determine what personally feels best to you.
7. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up. If you notice that either motion starts occurring, strive to pull your shoulder blades further backward and/or downward.
    2. Your elbows should be predominantly pointing backward rather than out to your sides from excessive elbow flare. However, you do not have to keep your elbows completely tucked by your sides, and you can allow them to flare mildly outward if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their upper arms are at least parallel to the ground, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Once you have finished lowering to your end range, pause in that bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Continue to stay braced with your shoulder blades pulled back and down.
    2. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Reverse the movement by pushing through the bars to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your hands and elbows downward to lift your body upward.
6. Alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down onto the foot plate.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your pec major, front delt, and triceps while being able to improve both your horizontal and vertical pressing strength.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to the height of your chest when you are in a half-kneeling position, which should correspond to being reasonably near hip height when standing.
2. Connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. As an alternative to the D-handle attachment, you also can perform a Pallof press with a rope attachment, but this page will describe the use of the D-handle.
3. Stand with the side of one shoulder facing the cable station, and grasp the handle with your closer hand.
4. Take a step to the side while keeping your arm (holding the handle) aligned with the cable.
5. Perform a controlled lunging motion into a half-kneeling position by stepping back with your leg closer to the cable and resting that side’s knee on the ground.
    1. In this position, your imaginary belt buckle should be facing about 90 degrees away from the cable station.
6. Turn your chest toward the cable station, and reach across your body with your free hand to firmly grasp the handle, interlocking your hands and facing your palms toward each other.
7. Pull the handle close to your chest with elbows bent and by your sides before turning your chest to face the same direction as your imaginary belt buckle.
    1. Rather than being bent to one side, twisted, rounded, or excessively arched, your torso should be in a symmetrical upright position aligned with your hips, like it would normally be when you are standing upright.
    2. In this starting position, you should be able to form close to a 90-degree angle between the cable and an imaginary laser beam shooting straight forward from the center of your chest. You should also be able to draw a straight line between your shoulders that is parallel to the cable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension, and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping the center of your chest facing straight ahead, press the handle straight forward away from your chest.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as your arms are outstretched in front of you, close to parallel to the ground.
    2. All the motion should occur from your arms moving at your shoulders and elbows, while the rest of your body remains completely stationary.
    3. Particularly avoid letting the cable's resistance rotate your torso. If your core muscles do not stabilize your trunk sufficiently, the imaginary laser beam shooting out from the center of your chest will start angling closer toward the cable station. The center of your chest should be facing the same direction in line with your imaginary belt buckle throughout your entire set.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by bringing the handle straight back toward your chest as you bend your elbows.
    1. You should now be back to the starting position with the handle directly in front of your chest as your upper arms are right by your sides.
    2. Continue keeping the center of your chest facing straight forward as your shoulders remain aligned when viewed from the side.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise, this time facing the opposite direction.
## Description
While also engaging your pec major, this exercise will statically train your obliques as they function to resist trunk rotation.## Setting up
1. Bring a head harness and an elastic band to a flat exercise bench or any object on which you can comfortably sit.
    1. Either a tube elastic band or a long looped elastic band can be used for this exercise.
    2. You can alternatively perform this exercise standing, but this page will describe the seated position.
2. After optionally donning a beanie for comfort, place a head harness on top of your head, and adjust its straps as needed until it feels snug and secure.
    1. The appropriate fit should ensure that the harness does not slide when nodding your head up and down.
3. While sitting down, connect one end of the band to one of the harness's side attachment points before connecting the other end of the band to the other side attachment point.
4. Drape the band behind your body, and grasp the bottom of the band with your hands to tension the band with straight arms.
## Exercise technique
1. Slowly nod your head down as you tilt your chin toward your chest through your neck’s comfortable range of motion.
    1. People can commonly perform this movement until the chin is very close to touching the collarbone, but your neck mobility will also influence the degree to which you can nod down.
    2. It is natural to feel a stretching sensation in some of the muscles on the back of your neck in the bottom position, but you should not experience any neck discomfort.
    3. Prioritize actively controlling the weight throughout the entirety of your set. This is an intentionally slow exercise.
    4. While you move your head through neck motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary as you continue holding the band throughout the set.
2. Reverse the movement by nodding your head up in a controlled manner as you tilt your chin away from your chest through your neck’s comfortable range of motion.
    1. Finishing with the face angled diagonally forward and upward is generally an appropriate end position for many people, but the specific end point will vary among different individuals.
    2. What chiefly matters is finishing in a position that feels comfortable to your neck.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After completing your last rep, lift the band up with your hands by bending your elbows until the band becomes slack and you can remove the harness.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train several muscles on the front of your neck, the largest and strongest of which is your sternocleidomastoid.## Setting up
1. Position an exercise bench at a low to moderate incline that will allow you to rest your chest against the head of the bench when you are bent over with your torso close to horizontal.
    1. The ideal bench angle should allow you to position your torso as close to horizontal as your mobility permits without your lower back.
    2. A lower incline may be appropriate for shorter lifters and/or those with good mobility, while a moderate incline may be better for taller lifters and/or those who have mobility limitations.
    3. You can optionally place a soft object (e.g., a squat pad, a rolled-up towel, or a rolled-up exercise mat) onto the head of the bench for cushioning.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and hold them by your sides with straight elbows.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    2. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
3. In a near hip-width stance, face the back of the bench while standing close enough to it that you will be able to rest your chest on it during the next step.
4. Sit your hips back while allowing your knees to bend mildly to hinge at your hips and incline your torso until you can rest your chest on the head of the bench.
    1. Keep your lower back flat throughout the exercise.
    2. The proper position should allow your head to be above the bench with your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
5. As your arms hang down toward the ground with straight elbows, angle your palms diagonally backward and in toward each other.
    1. Throughout your set, maintain this grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip and a neutral grip.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from now on.
2. Drive your elbows in an arcing motion backward, upward, and moderately out away from your sides to row the dumbbells. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Aim to flare your elbows about 45 degrees out away from your side as you move your elbows equal parts backward and out to the side. Zero degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows purely backward and keeping them completely tucked by your sides, while 90 degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows straight out to the sides, which would result in your upper arms and torso forming a “T-shape” at the top.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the top.
    3. Keep your chest in contact with the bench throughout the set, as all the motion occurs from your shoulder girdle and arms.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
    5. When allowing your elbows to bend naturally, your forearms should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    6. The dumbbells should move in an arcing motion rather than moving straight upward in a vertical path.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled arcing motion until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Allow the weights to pull your shoulder blades apart and forward, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. Move your elbows diagonally inward and forward at a near 45-degree angle while they descend to reverse the moderate elbow flare you just utilized during the lifting phase.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weights rapidly pull your arms down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, thrust your hips forward to stand upright with your chest off the bench before setting the dumbbells down.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Fully lower the Smith machine’s safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) and the machine’s bar.
    1. The safety hooks attached to the bar should be rotated out away from the safety bars (which are the bars onto which the safety hooks can latch).
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the bar, and stand close in front of the middle of the bar with approximately a hip width to shoulder width stance.
    1. Stand with the bar over the middle of your feet, which should situate the bar close to one inch (2.5cm) away from most individuals’ shins while initially standing upright.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly backward when lifting it up (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails). Facing this way will help the row feel most natural.
    3. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. Explosively pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward as you pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your torso in a hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set. All the movement should occur from motion at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows while your lower body remains stationary.
    2. Continue keeping your core braced, lower back flat, and chest up.
    3. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, which may result in your upper arms forming near a 45-degree angle with your torso toward the end of the lifting phase.
    4. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar until it is fully resting on the bottom of the machine.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, and your elbows will move downward and forward until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    2. Continue maintaining the same degree of forward torso lean with your chest up and lower back flat.
    3. The lowering phase of the Pendlay row is typically performed with a very quick tempo, but you can alternatively lower the bar in a more controlled manner if you wish to do so.
4. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop at the bottom for a moment (rather than using momentum) before continuing to alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is resting on the bottom of the machine. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, select your weight with the loading pin, and connect an EZ bar (also known as a multi-grip curl bar) attachment to the cable.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips to grasp the bar firmly with approximately a shoulder width semi-underhand grip such that your palms are directed at an angle that is facing forward and inward.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar attachment, you may have to rotate it initially before a near shoulder width semi-underhand grip is accessible on the bar.
3. While keeping your elbows straight, stand upright with the bar just in front of your legs.
4. If you do not currently feel resistance from the cable or are standing too close to the pulley to curl the bar without bumping the station, take a step backward while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. The appropriate positioning should allow you to feel resistance from the cable throughout the curl’s full range of motion.
    2. When stepping backward, the cable will now have a mildly diagonal line of the pull rather than being vertical. To maintain a straight elbow position with a mildly diagonal line of pull, you will have to raise your arms slightly in front of your body instead of keeping them pinned to your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward and back toward you in an arcing motion by bending your elbows until they are fully bent.
    1. Rather than swinging them further forward, keep your upper arms fixed in the same position in which they started (i.e., a little in front of you).
    2. While your forearms move through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Nothing else should move except for your forearms lowering in an arcing manner through elbow motion.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should avoid letting the cable’s resistance abruptly swing the bar down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, step forward and squat down or hinge at your hips to set the bar down.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows.
2. Direct your palms so that they are facing in toward your sides (i.e., in a neutral grip position).
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout the exercise, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you use hip motion instead of lower back motion to help lift the dumbbells.
2. While keeping your elbows straight, sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and your torso to incline slightly forward.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat or mildly arched rather than rounding it or excessively arching it.
3. Thrust your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body. Simultaneously curl the dumbbells upward by bending your elbows while turning your palms to face back toward you at the top.
    1. Allow your elbows to swing forward mildly rather than keeping them pinned to your sides, like during a strict curl.
    2. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    3. Refrain from leaning back through excessive lower back arching, which can be easier to avoid if you think about squeezing your glutes.
    4. While you are intentionally using momentum to help your arm muscles lift the weight, you should not be “cheating” to the extent that you feel out of control.
    5. You should finish the lifting phase with your elbows fully bent as your forearms are contacting your biceps and you are holding the dumbbells with an underhand grip (rather than the neutral grip with which you started).
4. Lower the dumbbells in a controlled manner as you progressively allow your elbows to straighten and return near your sides. Simultaneously turn your palms in toward each other.
    1. You should finish with your elbows straight while you are holding the dumbbells in a neutral grip by your sides.
    2. Keep your torso upright and hips stationary as only your arms move during the descent.
    3. Your muscles are noticeably stronger during the lowering phase of an exercise compared to the lifting phase, so “cheating” during the lifting phase allows your biceps to experience greater loading during the more controlled lowering phase.
    4. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should not let gravity just swing the dumbbells back down. If you are unable to control the lowering phase to any reasonable degree, you are likely using a weight that is excessively heavy relative to your current ability.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells by your thighs with straight elbows as your palms face your thighs.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hand while holding them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
    3. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    4. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    5. Pick up the dumbbells with a hip hinge or squat motion, where you lift them by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back flat, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Dip down slightly by bending your knees while keeping your chest up.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat or mildly arched rather than rounding it or excessively arching it.
    2. Quickly proceed to the next step as soon as you finish dipping down.
3. Forcefully drive your feet into the ground as you rapidly straighten your knees to generate momentum from your lower body. Simultaneously shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion to lift the dumbbells upward while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. While you are intentionally using momentum to help your upper traps lift the weight, you should not be “cheating” to the extent that you feel out of control.
    2. Throughout the exercise, look straight ahead to keep your neck near a neutral position.
    3. As a cue, you can think about driving your shoulders up toward your earlobes.
4. Allow your shoulders to descend fully in a controlled manner to lower the dumbbells while keeping your torso upright and legs stationary.
    1. Your muscles are noticeably stronger during the lowering phase of an exercise compared to the lifting phase, so “cheating” during the lifting phase allows your upper traps to experience greater loading during the more controlled lowering phase.
    2. At the bottom, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the dumbbells can provide.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the dumbbells abruptly yank you downward. If you are unable to control the lowering phase to any reasonable degree, you are likely using weights that are excessively heavy relative to your current ability.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep, set the dumbbells down in the same manner in which you initially picked them up (e.g., keeping your lower back flat rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable cable station’s pulley to its highest setting, or approach a cable lat pulldown machine.
    1. Either type of equipment can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin or add weight plates if you are using a plate-loaded cable machine.
3. Based on your preference, connect a rope attachment, straight bar attachment, or multi-grip EZ bar attachment to the cable.
    1. You can optionally place an exercise mat on the ground for comfort.
4. While facing the cable station, reach up and firmly grasp the cable attachment with straight elbows while keeping your wrists fairly straight.
    1. With a rope attachment, you can point your palms in toward each other and grasp each end of the rope with your little fingers and the adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the outside of your other fingers.
    2. With a straight bar or multi-grip EZ bar, you can grasp it with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing away from you.
5. While keeping your arms outstretched with straight elbows in line with the cable, kneel down onto the ground.
    1. In this position, you should feel tension from the cable. If you do not, you should situate yourself further back away from the cable until the weight stack separates.
6. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and moderately incline your torso while keeping your arms in line with the cable and your back flat.
    1. The appropriate degree of torso incline should allow you to draw a mostly straight line from your arms through your shoulders to your hips when viewed from the side. From this position, the cable’s resistance should situate your arms fully overhead relative to what your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straightened and your torso inclined, sweep your arms down and back toward your sides in an arcing motion until your hands are right by your thighs.
    1. During the initial half of this movement, you can focus on driving down, while you can focus on pulling back during the latter half of this movement.
    2. As your arms move through motion at your shoulders, the rest of your body should be stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arms to sweep forward and upward in a controlled arcing manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms fully overhead in line with the cable.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straightened and your torso inclined.
    2. While you do not have to reverse the movement with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the cable abruptly swing your arms upward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, you can return to an upright torso position, step one leg forward to assume a lunge position, stand upright in a lunging motion, and return the cable to its initial position.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres major, and rear delt, which are assisted by the lowest portion of your pec major and the long head of your triceps in the overhead position of each rep.## Setting up
1. In an approximately hip-width stance, face the stable object with a flat, elevated surface that you will use for this push-up variation.
    1. So long as the object can support your body weight and will not slide, you can use a variety of objects based on the degree of difficulty you wish to have and what is available to you.
    2. As the height of the surface decreases, you will be able to adopt a torso position that is closer to horizontal, which increases the difficulty of your push-up as gravity is able to provide greater resistance.
    3. A flat exercise bench (which typically has a surface near 18in (46cm) above the ground) can be appropriate to use in a gym as an initial starting point for many people who struggle to perform more than a couple of reps of standard push-ups through a full range of motion.
    4. In addition to a plyometric box, an aerobic step elevated on top of risers can also be a quality object to use, since you can adjust the height (typically in 2in (5cm) increments) to match the specific level of difficulty you wish to have.
    5. For a lower intensity variation at home, furniture like a bed can alternatively be used to elevate your hands.
2. After leaning forward at your hips, place your palms on top of the elevated object with the base of your palms on the corner as your elbows are straight, and your hands are about shoulder width apart.
    1. In this position, your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
    2. Based on your preference, you can spread your fingers somewhat apart from each other or keep them close together.
3. While bearing weight through your hands and allowing your heels to lift off the ground, walk your feet back until your hips and knees are straightened as your arms form approximately 90-degree angles with your torso.
    1. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    3. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner until it lightly touches the elevated surface.
    1. Rather than excessively flaring your elbows out away from your sides, keep your elbows reasonably tucked by your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you as you descend.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact the ground instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    5. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the surface to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    2. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise trains your pec major, front delts, and triceps like a standard close grip push-up but with a lower intensity.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground, and position a large diameter weight plate (or a viable alternative) under the center of the bar that you will use to create a deficit.
    1. While you can use a variety of deficit heights, selecting a low deficit height (under 4in (10cm)) is initially recommended to facilitate the appropriate technique.
    2. Ideally pick a height that allows you to keep your lower back flat at the bottom of the rep while fully hinging at your hips to incline your torso as close to horizontal as your flexibility permits.
    3. A large diameter (e.g., 45lb (20kg)) cast iron weight plate with the flat surface facing up can be a suitable option to select initially. For a greater deficit, you can alternatively select a thicker bumper plate, deadlift block, stack of two cast iron plates, or aerobic step rated to withstand the combined loads of your bodyweight and the bar’s weight.
    4. Whichever object you select should be stable when you are standing with the soles of your feet fully on it in a near hip-width stance. Some people may not be able to situate both feet fully on one plate and can alternatively situate two plates next to each other to enable full foot contact.
    5. As an alternative to standing on an elevated object, you can also achieve the same extended range of motion for your row by only adding smaller diameter weight plates to the bar instead of loading at least one pair of full diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates or any bumper plates).
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand upright on the plate (or other object) with the middle of your feet directly under the middle of the bar as your toes are pointing straight ahead or slightly out to the sides (depending on what feels most comfortable).
    1. Throughout the exercise, the soles of your feet should be fully in contact with the plate (or other object) for maximal stability.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something. Once you fully sit back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout the exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. Explosively pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward as you pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your torso in a hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set. All the movement should occur from motion at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows while your lower body remains stationary.
    2. Continue keeping your core braced, lower back flat, and chest up.
    3. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, which may result in your upper arms forming near 45-degree angles with your torso toward the end of the lifting phase.
    4. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar until it is fully resting on the ground.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, and your elbows will move downward and forward until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    2. Continue maintaining the same degree of forward torso lean with your chest up and lower back flat.
    3. The lowering phase of the Pendlay row is typically performed with a very quick tempo, but you can alternatively lower the bar in a more controlled manner if you wish to do so.
4. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than using momentum) before continuing to alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is resting on the ground. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley near mid-shin height, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Securely attach an ankle cuff just above the ankle of the leg you will train. Position the cuff so that one of its attachment rings is directed out to the side (away from your other leg).
3. Stand sideways with your working leg directly next to the pulley, and connect the cable to your cuff’s outer attachment ring.
    1. It will be easiest to connect the cable when your cuffed ankle is close enough to the pulley that the cable does not provide resistance when connecting it. This may involve marching your cuffed side’s foot mildly above the ground when attaching the cable
4. Grasp the cable station’s beam near shoulder height with your closer hand, and continue holding it from now onward for additional stability.
5. Step out to the side away from the pulley until your elbow (of the arm holding the cable station) has fully straightened.
    1. Keeping your toes pointing straight forward, such that lines drawn from your toes to your heels can form approximately 90-degree angles with the cables.
    2. The objective is to situate yourself as far out to the side, away from the pulley, as you can while still being able to help stabilize your body with your arm.
6. Take a step backward with the leg that is not cuffed.
    1. The goal is to have your non-working leg’s toes slightly behind your cuffed leg’s heel to provide sufficient clearance for your cuffed leg to sweep across your body away from the pulley.
    2. In the starting position, your chest and imaginary belt buckle should be pointing straight ahead in the same direction, and your shoulders should be level.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Shift your bodyweight into your non-working side’s foot, and allow the cable’s resistance to sweep your cuffed leg out to the side toward the pulley. Keep your hip bones and shoulders level with each other throughout the exercise.
    1. Let your leg angle as far out to the side as the cable’s resistance enables.
    2. The subsequent directions will apply to your cuffed side’s leg as you keep your other leg still.
3. Sweep your leg inward away from the pulley and across your body while keeping your knee pointing straight forward.
    1. Individuals with normal hip mobility can typically finish with their legs slightly crossed over each other.
    2. As you move your leg via hip motion, strive to keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the set.
    3. With the appropriate technique, you should feel tension in your adductors within the inner aspect of your thigh.
4. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting your leg sweep out to the side toward the pulley, like during step 2.
    1. You can aim to let your leg move outward until the weights you selected are just about to tap the rest of the weight stack.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the cable abruptly swing your leg outward in an uncontrolled fashion.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hip adductor muscles in an isolated manner.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up and firmly grasp it with a slightly outside shoulder-width grip, palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. Depending on the specific pull-up bar you are using, your height, and your wingspan, the bar may be beyond your reach.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground, you can initially step up onto a sturdy box, an aerobic step, or a bumper plate to place the bar within reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar than if you jumped up from the ground to reach it.
    4. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    5. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight, begin hanging on the bar by lifting your feet just off the ground (or the elevated object if you initially stood on something as a step to reach the bar).
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar, pull your shoulder blades down, which will pull your shoulders away from your ears.
    1. Maintain this shoulder position throughout the exercise as you avoid letting your shoulders shrug up toward your ears from the force of your bodyweight.
2. Sweep your legs up and out in front of you, keeping your knees straight, until your legs are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. Keep your torso vertical and your elbows straight as you avoid swinging your body to build momentum.
    2. In this position, your torso and legs should resemble an “L shape” when viewed from the side, which results from your legs forming a right angle with your torso.
    3. If you do not have the hamstring flexibility to utilize what is considered the standard textbook form with your knees fully straight as your legs are parallel to the ground, you can situate your legs as close as you can to this position.
    4. Individuals with limited hamstring flexibility can either keep their thighs parallel to the ground with their knees mildly bent, or lift their legs as close to parallel to the ground as they can reach with straight knees.
    5. Rather than remaining mildly arched like when you are normally standing upright, your lower back should naturally flatten from the contraction of your abs.
3. Hold the L-sit position for your target duration as you strive to remain completely stationary.
    1. Since this is a static exercise, you will track how many seconds you hold the position rather than reps.
4. After reaching your target hold duration, lower your legs in a controlled manner until you can return your feet to the ground (or the elevated object you used as a step to reach the bar).
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your hip flexors and rectus abdominis (the “six pack muscle”) while also engaging your obliques secondarily.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. In a mildly outside shoulder width stance, stand with the handle between your feet as your back is facing the cable station.
3. Hinge at your hips or squat down to firmly grasp the handle with the side of your body you will train during this set.
    1. Hold the handle with your palm facing forward and your elbow straight.
4. Take a step or two forward until the weight stack separates and you feel tension from the cable.
    1. You can keep your torso somewhat inclined forward while stepping forward.
5. On the same side of the body in which you are holding the cable, angle your knee and foot out to the side while situating your feet fairly wide apart.
6. Sit your hips back while allowing your knees to bend and torso to incline further forward until you can rest your working side’s elbow against the inside of your thigh. Simultaneously, brace your free arm’s forearm against your other side’s thigh for additional stability.
    1. Your working side’s arm should be directed down toward the ground with a straight elbow as the cable is exerting a pulling force backward.
    2. With the appropriate set-up, you should feel balanced in the starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow pinned against your inner thigh, bend your elbow to curl the handle forward and upward in an arcing manner.
    1. You can finish curling once your forearm is approximately parallel to the ground. Resistance from the cable will be minimal when bending your elbow beyond that point.
    2. While you move your forearm through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to straighten in a controlled manner as you let your forearm arc downward and back toward the pulley.
    1. Finish this step with your elbow fully straightened.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank the handle back in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine to utilize your desired level of assistance and ensure that the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is positioned down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you add heavier loads to the assisted dip/pull-up machine since the weight determines the extent to which the machine will support your body weight.
    2. Certain machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but essentially the same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates.
3. If your machine allows you to rotate its parallel handles inward or outward, position them to whichever setting is closest to being slightly outside shoulder width for you.
    1. A slightly outside shoulder-width grip is appropriate for many lifters, but a mildly wider grip or a shoulder-width grip may also be used. If they are adjustable on your machine, feel free to try out both handle positions to determine which one personally feels best to you.
4. Firmly grasp the parallel handles with your palms facing each other. Hold the handles directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the handles tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
5. One leg at a time, kneel onto the machine’s knee pad (if your machine has a knee pad) or step onto the machine’s foot bar (if your machine has a foot bar) while continuing to grasp the handles firmly. Maintain straight elbows when doing so.
    1. If using a machine with a knee pad, you will keep your knees bent on the pad throughout your set, but if you are using a machine with a foot bar, you will keep your knees straight while standing on the bar throughout your set.
6. Rather than keeping your torso fully upright, mildly lean your torso forward and maintain this mild lean throughout your set.
    1. The appropriate degree of lean should result in your chest being slightly in front of your hips (when viewed from the side) while helping your shoulders feel comfortable throughout the set.
    2. Feel free to try out different degrees of torso lean over time to determine what personally feels best to you.
7. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up. If you notice that either motion starts occurring, strive to pull your shoulder blades further backward and/or downward.
    2. Your elbows should be predominantly pointing backward rather than out to your sides from excessive elbow flare. However, you do not have to keep your elbows completely tucked by your sides, and you can allow them to flare mildly outward if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point where their upper arms are at least parallel to the ground, but how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Once you have finished lowering to your end range, pause in that bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Continue to stay braced with your shoulder blades pulled back and down.
    2. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Reverse the movement by pushing through the bars to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your hands and elbows downward to lift your body upward.
6. Alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step down onto the foot plate.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your pec major, front delt, and triceps while being able to improve both your horizontal and vertical pressing strength.## Setting up
1. Situate a weight plate or kettlebell of your desired load next to a flat exercise bench or any object on which you can comfortably sit.
    1. You can alternatively perform this exercise standing with your torso hinged forward, but this page will describe the seated position.
2. After optionally donning a beanie for comfort, place a head harness on top of your head, and adjust its straps as needed until it feels snug and secure.
    1. The appropriate fit should ensure that the harness does not slide when nodding your head up and down.
3. Connect one end of a chain to one of the harness's side attachment points while sitting down with your legs moderately spread apart.
4. After picking up your weight, slide the free end of the chain through the center hole of a weight plate or under the handle of a kettlebell before connecting that end of the chain to the other side attachment point of the harness.
5. Moderately to mildly lean forward at your hips, and lower the weight until the chain is tensed.
    1. The weight should now be hanging between your legs.
## Exercise technique
1. Slowly nod your head down as you tilt your chin toward your chest through your neck’s comfortable range of motion.
    1. People can commonly perform this movement until the chin is very close to touching the collarbone, but your neck mobility will also influence the degree to which you can nod down.
    2. It is natural to feel a stretching sensation in some of the muscles on the back of your neck in the bottom position, but you should not experience any neck discomfort.
    3. Prioritize actively controlling the weight throughout the entirety of your set. This is an intentionally slow exercise.
    4. While you move your head through neck motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by nodding your head up in a controlled manner as you tilt your chin away from your chest through your neck’s comfortable range of motion.
    1. Based on your preference and what feels best to you, you can finish nodding up once you have returned to a neutral position (like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright), or you can extend a bit beyond that point.
    2. Regardless of which end range you opt to use, your face should be angled forward in the top position. There is no need to nod your head all the way up until you are looking up at the ceiling.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After completing your last rep, lift the weight up with your hand to unload the harness before unfastening the chain from one side of the harness and removing the chain from the weight.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the musculature on the back of your neck, including the splenius muscles, semispinalis muscles, and cervical portions of the erector spinae and multifidus.## Setting up
1. Fully lower the Smith machine’s safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) and position the bar slightly below the height of your hands when standing with your arms at your sides.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the bar, and stand upright in a near hip-width stance with the middle of the bar just in front of your legs.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly backward when lifting it up (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails). Facing this way will help the row feel most natural.
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar, sit your hips mildly backward while slightly bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar, and rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion.
3. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as far as your flexibility comfortably permits without letting the bar contact the bottom of the machine.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight, lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and the soles of your feet fully on the ground.
    2. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    3. The degree to which you can hinge at your hips and consequently angle your torso closer to horizontal will be affected by your hamstring flexibility. If you have the flexibility to hinge until the bar reaches the bottom of the machine, you can stop just before you reach that point. Otherwise, you can sit your hips back as far as you comfortably can while keeping your back flat and shins vertical.
4. Pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward as you pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your torso in a hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set. All the movement should occur from motion at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows while your lower body remains stationary.
    2. Continue keeping your core braced, lower back flat, and chest up.
    3. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, which may result in your upper arms forming near a 45-degree angle with your torso toward the end of the lifting phase.
    4. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
5. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Continue maintaining the same degree of forward torso lean with your chest up and lower back flat, which should result in the bar not reaching the bottom of the machine.
    2. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another as your elbows move downward and forward while straightening.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be in clear control of the entire descent.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set.
7. Following your last rep, thrust your hips forward to stand upright with the bar before rotating the safety hooks toward the safety bars, and mildly bending your hips and knees to rerack the bar onto the safety bars.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Place a firm medicine ball on the ground.
    1. The ball should have a diameter that is large enough for you to situate both hands on top of it.
2. With the ball under your shoulders, position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
3. Place your hands on top of the ball with the heels of your hands (i.e., bases of your palms) firmly pressed into the ball and your fingers spread somewhat apart.
    1. The proper hand placement should allow you to feel stable on top of the ball without wrist discomfort during this exercise. Determining the precise hand placement that personally feels best to you with the specific ball you are using may require some trial and error.
4. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. Rather than rolling, the ball should remain stationary under your shoulders throughout the exercise.
    2. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    3. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
2. While keeping your torso rigid and your legs straight, bend your elbows to lower your chest in a controlled manner until it lightly touches the ball.
    1. Rather than excessively flaring your elbows out away from your sides, keep your elbows reasonably tucked by your sides to a degree that feels comfortable and natural to you as you descend.
    2. Without shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears, allow your shoulder blades to pull back together as you lower yourself, which will result in you having a “proud chest posture” by the time you contact the ball instead of having your shoulders rounded forward.
    3. While motion occurs at your elbows and shoulder girdles, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set, so you should still be able to draw a straight line from your thighs through your torso at the bottom of the rep.
    4. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag from lower back arching or hike up from bending your hips.
    5. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent, rather than dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by pressing into the ball to lift your whole body, progressively straightening your elbows until you return to the tall plank position.
    1. You can emphasize driving the heels of your hands into the ball with the intent to push the ball down through the ground, which will correspondingly push you up.
    2. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart from each other naturally as you push up, such that they are near their neutral position (like when you are normally relaxed) by the time you have finished the lifting phase.
    3. Continue keeping your legs aligned with your torso as your hips and chest lift up in unison, which will still let you draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    4. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, you can bend your knees to kneel on the ground before rocking your hips back and releasing the ball.
## Description
This exercise can train your triceps to a preferentially greater degree than a standard push-up while also training your pec major, front delts, serratus anterior, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Fully lower the Smith machine’s safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) and position the bar near the height of your hands when standing with your arms at your sides.
2. Determine whether your machine has angled sliding rails, which will affect the direction you will face during the exercise and on which side of the bar you should subsequently place the object to create your deficit.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you should face the side in which the bar will track slightly backward when lifting it up to help the row feel most natural. Compared to the top of the rails, this will result in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of where you will place the object for your deficit, and then stand during the exercise.
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3. Position a sturdy object that you will use to create your deficit under the middle of the bar and slightly closer to the side of the bar on which you will stand.
    1. Objects you can use include a thick bumper plate, a deadlift block, and an aerobic step rated to withstand the combined loads of your bodyweight and the machine’s weight. With the flat surface facing up, you can also use a large diameter (e.g., 45lb (20kg)) cast iron weight plate for a smaller deficit or stack multiple plates for a larger deficit.
    2. Ideally, the object should be tall enough that you are able to achieve as close to a horizontal torso position as your flexibility permits without the bar reaching the bottom of the machine during your row. A 45lb (20kg) bumper plate or aerobic step without risers should be sufficiently tall for most lifters to accomplish this objective.
    3. Whichever object you select should be stable when you are standing with the soles of your feet fully on it in a near hip-width stance. Some people may not be able to situate both feet fully on one plate and can alternatively situate two plates next to each other to enable full foot contact.
4. Add weight plates of your desired load to the bar, and stand upright on the elevated object in a near hip-width stance with the middle of the bar above the middle of your feet.
5. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar, sit your hips mildly backward while slightly bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
6. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width underhand grip such that your palms are facing forward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar, and rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion.
3. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as far as your flexibility comfortably permits
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight, lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and the soles of your feet fully on the elevated object.
    2. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    3. The degree to which you can hinge at your hips and consequently angle your torso closer to horizontal will be affected by your hamstring flexibility. You have achieved your fully bent-over position once you can no longer comfortably sit your hips back while keeping your back flat and shins vertical. With a sufficiently tall object you selected for your deficit, the bar should not reach the bottom of the machine in this position.
4. Pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward as you pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your torso in a hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set. All the movement should occur from motion at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows while your lower body remains stationary.
    2. Continue keeping your core braced, lower back flat, and chest up.
    3. Keep your elbows tucked fairly close to your sides rather than meaningfully flaring them outward.
    4. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
5. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Continue maintaining the same degree of forward torso lean with your chest up and lower back flat, which should result in the bar not reaching the bottom of the machine.
    2. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another as your elbows move downward and forward while straightening.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be in clear control of the entire descent.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set.
7. Following your last rep, thrust your hips forward to stand upright with the bar before rotating the safety hooks toward the safety bars, and mildly bending your hips and knees to rerack the bar onto the safety bars.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Fully lower the Smith machine’s safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) and position the bar slightly below the height of your hands when standing with your arms at your sides.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the bar, and stand upright in a near hip-width stance with the middle of the bar just in front of your legs.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly backward when lifting it up (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails). Facing this way will help the row feel most natural.
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar, sit your hips mildly backward while slightly bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width underhand grip such that your palms are facing forward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar, and rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion.
3. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as far as your flexibility comfortably permits without letting the bar contact the bottom of the machine.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight, lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and the soles of your feet fully on the ground.
    2. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    3. The degree to which you can hinge at your hips and consequently angle your torso closer to horizontal will be affected by your hamstring flexibility. If you have the flexibility to hinge until the bar reaches the bottom of the machine, you can stop just before you reach that point. Otherwise, you can sit your hips back as far as you comfortably can while keeping your back flat and shins vertical.
4. Pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward as you pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your torso in a hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set. All the movement should occur from motion at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows while your lower body remains stationary.
    2. Continue keeping your core braced, lower back flat, and chest up.
    3. Keep your elbows tucked fairly close to your sides rather than meaningfully flaring them outward.
    4. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
5. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Continue maintaining the same degree of forward torso lean with your chest up and lower back flat, which should result in the bar not reaching the bottom of the machine.
    2. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another as your elbows move downward and forward while straightening.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be in clear control of the entire descent.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set.
7. Following your last rep, thrust your hips forward to stand upright with the bar before rotating the safety hooks toward the safety bars, and mildly bending your hips and knees to rerack the bar onto the safety bars.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each pulley to a little higher than your head, and select your desired load at the two with stacks.
2. Based on your preference, leave the cables without any handle attachments or connect a D-handle attachment to each cable.
    1. Either option allows you to train your rear delts with essentially the same technique except for the specific grip you will use, so feel free to try out both and use whichever feels most comfortable to you.
3. After standing centered between the cables with your sides facing the weight stacks, take a step backward before side-stepping toward one cable.
4. With your arm that is opposite to the cable, reach across your body and firmly grasp the ball at the end of the cable (if you are not using any handle attachment) or the D-handle (if you are using a handle attachment).
5. Side step toward the other cable, and reach across your body to grasp it with your free hand.
6. Return to being centered between the cables with your arms crossed in front of your torso, with slightly bent elbows, as the pulleys are situated a little in front of you.
    1. In this position, your arms should resemble an “X-shape” with each other as your hands are above shoulder height in line with the resistance from the cables.
    2. Maintain slightly bent elbows throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arms out and away from each other while driving them diagonally downward and backward.
    1. Move through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility permits, which should result in your elbows being slightly behind your shoulders or at least horizontally level with your shoulders at the end range.
    2. Strive to achieve a roughly even balance between sweeping horizontally outward and vertically downward.
    3. Keep your torso upright with your chest up throughout the set, while all the motion occurs from your arms moving at your shoulder joints.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your arms to move diagonally forward, upward, and in toward each other until they have crossed each other and returned to the starting position.
    1. When you set up appropriately, the angle of resistance from the cables will naturally guide your arms through the intended diagonal crossing path.
    2. You do not have to perform this movement with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the cables abruptly yank your arms in toward each other.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After completing your last rep, set the cables back in the opposite way in which you set up initially. Side-step toward one pulley until the cable loses tension, then release it and walk over to the other pulley to do the same.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delts while also training your traps, rhomboids, lats, teres majores, and two rotator cuff muscles to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, connect a rope attachment to a cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
    1. As an alternative to using an adjustable cable station, you can perform this exercise on a seated cable row machine that allows you to connect a rope attachment.
2. Sit down on the ground with your feet positioned against the base of the cable station.
3. Lean forward at your hips and reach forward to grasp each end of the rope with your index fingers and the adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers.
    1. Wrap your thumbs over the rounded outer aspects of the stoppers.
4. While holding the rope with straight elbows, lean your torso back until you are sitting with an upright torso. If you do not feel tension from the cable, straighten your knees to scoot back away from the pulley until the weight stack separates and you feel resistance.
    1. The appropriate position should prompt you to feel tension from the cable in the starting position of every rep.
## Exercise technique
1. While bending your elbows and progressively spreading your hands apart, pull the rope backward and upward simultaneously until the middle of the rope is situated close to your face at approximately eye level.
    1. At the end of this motion, each elbow should be bent by a mildly greater than 90-degree angle with your arms resembling a double biceps flexing pose. Your upper arms and torso should also form a “T-shape” while your upper arms are approximately parallel with the ground and your palms are facing your shoulders.
    2. The cable should move in a diagonal motion that involves more horizontal motion than vertical motion.
    3. Your shoulder blades should naturally pull back together when you drive your elbows backward.
2. Reverse the motion by moving the cable forward and downward simultaneously as your hands come closer together, and your elbows straighten.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally and move forward.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms stretched out in front of you.
    3. You do not have to perform this reversal phase with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control it the entire time rather than letting the cable abruptly pull your arms forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also meaningfully training your rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, this exercise can effectively develop your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, along with the supraspinatus to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Position a flat exercise bench centered underneath a Smith machine’s bar in a spot that will allow the bar to descend toward your throat or chin when lying down on the bench.
    1. Specifically how you should set up can be influenced by whether your Smith machine has sliding rails that are purely vertical or angled.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, place the side of the bench where you will place your feet on the side in which the bar will track slightly backward as it descends. This will result in the bottom of the rails being horizontally closer to your head than the top of the rails when lying down on the bench. An angled Smith machine will allow you to use a bar path that most closely resembles that of a barbell JM press.
    3. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which side of the bar you place your feet.
2. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to grasp it with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently.
    1. The ideal height allows you to unrack the bar while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together without requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    2. If the bar is situated too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart to lift the bar off the safety bars (on which the safety hooks that are attached to the bar rest). This can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find the best starting bar height for you.
3. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of JM press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section. Use these reps to determine the appropriate height of the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails under the bar) prior to re-racking the bar.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety stops to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descended by the end of the lowering phase of your JM press.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench, knees bent, and feet on the floor approximately shoulder-width apart. Position yourself so that the bar can finish above your throat or chin (based on your preference) at the bottom of the JM press.
    1. If your Smith machine has vertical slide rails, situate your chin or throat directly under the bar.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can alternatively set up with your upper chest under the bar.
    3. With the fixed bar path from the Smith machine, it may be worthwhile to try scooting slightly forward or backward on the bench in between sets if the exercise feels a bit awkward with your initial positioning.
    4. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    5. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing forward. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A shoulder-width grip is an appropriate starting point, but you can try mildly different grip widths over time to determine where you personally feel best. The proper grip width should allow your elbows, wrists, and shoulders to feel comfortable while emphasizing the triceps to a greater degree than your close grip bench press.
    2. The traditional technique for this exercise involves a thumbless grip with the thumbs placed on the same side of the bar as the other fingers. However, if you are new to this exercise, you have the option of wrapping your thumbs around the bar to fully enclose the bar with your hands, which can make it easier to hold the bar securely.
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
7. Assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Maintain the natural curvature of your lower back, like when you are normally standing upright. Regardless of whether you meaningfully arch your lower back when bench pressing, the JM press involves a mostly flat back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground, squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the safety bars by straightening your elbows before rotating the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion. After unracking it, hold the bar stationary for a moment before starting to lower it.
    1. Keep your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your throat or chin in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows. Keep your elbows higher than your chest (when viewed from the side) while flaring your elbows out about 45 degrees from your sides.
    1. Avoid letting your wrist cock back, which would point your palms away from your forearms. Rather, curl your wrists mildly to angle your palms closer toward your forearms while securely holding the bar.
    2. In contrast to a strict lying triceps extension, you will lower your elbows somewhat, but to a meaningfully lesser degree than with a close grip bench press.
    3. The degree to which your forearms are angled relative to the ground can indicate whether you are moving your elbows (through shoulder motion) appropriately. By the end of the lowering phase, your forearms should be closer to horizontal than vertical. If your forearms remain closer to vertical at the bottom, you are excessively lowering your elbows and performing too much of a pressing movement. If your forearms angle beyond horizontal (with your wrists lower than your elbows), you are keeping your elbows excessively high and performing an exercise closer to a strict triceps extension than is intended.
    4. The amount of elbow flare is similar to what you can use for a close grip bench press. Strive to reach the middle ground between 90 degrees of elbow flare (which would cause your upper arms to form a “T-shape” with your torso) and 0 degrees of elbow flare (which would result in your upper arms being completely parallel to your torso).
4. Continue lowering the bar until you have fully bent your elbows or until the bar is just about to contact your chin or throat.
    1. The end range can be determined by when your biceps contact your forearms, so what constitutes your personal end range can be influenced by the circumference of your arms.
    2. If the bar is about to touch your chin or throat before you have fully bent your elbows, you naturally can finish lowering the bar before achieving a full elbow bend.
5. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the bar up into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. Your elbows will move upward and inward during the lifting phase, but you can predominantly focus on straightening your elbows to lift the weight with your triceps.
    3. Uncurl your wrists while lifting the bar to maintain a secure grip.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
    1. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second.
7. Rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars by flicking your wrists before bending your elbows to lower the safety hooks securely onto the safety bars.
## Description
While training the pec major and front delts to a lesser degree than a close grip bench press, this exercise can develop the triceps particularly well.## Setting up
1. Place a pair of furniture sliders on the ground hip-width apart from each other.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground, and situate the bottom of your heels on top of the sliders with your knees fully bent.
    1. You can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides or in whichever position you prefer.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels down into the sliders, squeezing your glutes, and tightening your abs.
    1. After performing this glute bridge motion, you should now be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
    3. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to hold onto a hundred-dollar bill by tightly squeezing it between your glutes.
2. Without bending your hips, move your feet forward by progressively straightening your knees in a controlled manner until they are almost fully straightened.
    1. Continue keeping your glutes squeezed, abs tensed, and pressure concentrated through your heels throughout the exercise.
    2. All the motion should occur from your shins moving at your knee joints, while you are still able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders.
    3. At the end of this lowering phase, your buttocks should just barely be above the ground.
    4. You do not have to perform this movement with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting your feet abruptly slide forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by driving your heels toward your buttocks to bend your knees and pull the sliders backward.
    1. You should finish with your knees close to being fully bent as the sliders are vertically under your knees.
    2. If you experience a notably uncomfortable cramping sensation in your hamstrings when your knees are fully bent, you can stop bending your knees just before reaching that point.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to lower your buttocks to the ground by bending at your hips.
## Description
While statically engaging your gluteus maximus, this exercise can train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Fully lower the Smith machine’s safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) and position the bar near the height of your hands when standing with your arms at your sides.
2. Determine whether your machine has angled sliding rails, which will affect the direction you will face during the exercise and on which side of the bar you should subsequently place the object to create your deficit.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you should face the side in which the bar will track slightly backward when lifting it up to help the row feel most natural. Compared to the top of the rails, this will result in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of where you will place the object for your deficit, and then stand during the exercise.
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3. Position a sturdy object that you will use to create your deficit under the middle of the bar and slightly closer to the side of the bar on which you will stand.
    1. Objects you can use include a thick bumper plate, a deadlift block, and an aerobic step rated to withstand the combined loads of your bodyweight and the machine’s weight. With the flat surface facing up, you can also use a large diameter (e.g., 45lb (20kg)) cast iron weight plate for a smaller deficit or stack multiple plates for a larger deficit.
    2. Ideally, the object should be tall enough that you are able to achieve as close to a horizontal torso position as your flexibility permits without the bar reaching the bottom of the machine during your row. A 45lb (20kg) bumper plate or aerobic step without risers should be sufficiently tall for most lifters to accomplish this objective.
    3. Whichever object you select should be stable when you are standing with the soles of your feet fully on it in a near hip-width stance. Some people may not be able to situate both feet fully on one plate and can alternatively situate two plates next to each other to enable full foot contact.
4. Add weight plates of your desired load to the bar, and stand upright on the elevated object in a near hip-width stance with the middle of the bar above the middle of your feet.
5. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar, sit your hips mildly backward while slightly bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
6. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar, and rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion.
3. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as far as your flexibility comfortably permits
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight, lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and the soles of your feet fully on the elevated object.
    2. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    3. The degree to which you can hinge at your hips and consequently angle your torso closer to horizontal will be affected by your hamstring flexibility. You have achieved your fully bent-over position once you can no longer comfortably sit your hips back while keeping your back flat and shins vertical. With a sufficiently tall object you selected for your deficit, the bar should not reach the bottom of the machine in this position.
4. Pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward as you pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Keep your torso in a hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set. All the movement should occur from motion at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows while your lower body remains stationary.
    2. Continue keeping your core braced, lower back flat, and chest up.
    3. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, which may result in your upper arms forming near a 45-degree angle with your torso toward the end of the lifting phase.
    4. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
5. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Continue maintaining the same degree of forward torso lean with your chest up and lower back flat, which should result in the bar not reaching the bottom of the machine.
    2. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another as your elbows move downward and forward while straightening.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be in clear control of the entire descent.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set.
7. Following your last rep, thrust your hips forward to stand upright with the bar before rotating the safety hooks toward the safety bars, and mildly bending your hips and knees to rerack the bar onto the safety bars.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. On the side of your body you are about to train, pick up one dumbbell and hold it by your side with your elbow straightened.
    1. Continue holding the dumbbell by your side throughout the exercise.
2. Approach a step or a sturdy object that can function as a step. You should be by a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance.
    1. Based on the equipment at your disposal, a variety of objects can serve as your step. Make sure the object can support your weight, will not move once you are on it, and allows you to lower your heel through the full range of motion your ankle mobility permits.
    2. As an alternative to a staircase’s bottom step, you may select a power rack’s bottom beam if the beam is sufficiently tall.
    3. Additionally, a calf raise block, aerobic step, deadlift block, or thick bumper plate can each be positioned near a wall or power rack for stability. Depending on your bodyweight, anchoring an aerobic step with weight on top of it may be required to prevent the step from tipping.
3. Place your free hand onto the nearby surface for balance assistance, and maintain this hand contact throughout the set.
    1. If you are using a surface that you can grasp (like a power rack) rather than a wall, do not grip it tightly.
4. Step onto the step with the front halves of your feet on the step while the back halves of your feet are hanging off the step.
5. While keeping your foot on the same side as the dumbbell on the step, lift your other foot off the step by bending that knee.
    1. Keep that foot off the step throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. With the leg that is standing on the step, push through the ball of your foot to raise your heel upward while keeping that side’s knee straight and your trunk upright.
    1. Only use your free hand for balance assistance as you avoid pushing down in a manner that can help you lift yourself upward.
    2. Once you have lifted your heel as high upward as your ankle mobility permits, you have finished the lifting phase. There is no need to pause at the top.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heel to descend toward the ground through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits while continuing to keep your working leg’s knee straight.
    1. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can below the ball of your foot to stretch your calf and train it the most effectively.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full range of motion.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid quickly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep.
    4. You can optionally pause in the bottom position for a brief period of time (e.g., 1-2 seconds).
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat this exercise with your other side.
    1. If you ever feel that your foot is starting to slide down the step, you can return your free leg’s foot to the step for a moment to help you scoot your working leg’s foot forward onto the step before resuming your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Place a dumbbell on the ground next to the side of a flat exercise bench (or a sturdy object of similar height).
    1. If your dumbbell has flat sides, you can position the dumbbell with one end on the ground and its handle directed vertically, resulting in one end remaining off the ground.
2. Sit down on the ground with the side of one of your hips directly next to the dumbbell and your upper back in contact with the bench.
3. Position your feet out in front of you with your soles flat on the floor, hip-width apart, toes pointing forward, and knees bent by a little greater than 90 degrees.
    1. The optimal degree of knee bend should allow your feet to feel stable while being firmly planted on the ground throughout the set. It is normally appropriate to position your feet far enough back that your knees will be bent by approximately 90 degrees at the top of your hip thrust. From this placement, you can adjust specifically where you situate your feet based on your preference.
4. Move the dumbbell onto the crease of your hips in a controlled manner using both hands. Based on your preference, you can situate the weight centered along the crease of both hips, or you can position one flat end of the dumbbell on the uppermost thigh of what will be your working leg.
    1. If you position the dumbbell onto only one side, the handle should be angled diagonally upward and backward, and you can hold it in place with your closer hand while placing your other hand across the bench for additional stability during the set.
    2. If you position the dumbbell across both hips, the handle should be horizontal, and you can use both hands to keep the weight in contact with the front of your hips throughout the set.
    3. Neither set-up approach is inherently better than the other, so feel free to try both out and continue using whichever one feels best to you.
5. Tuck your chin mildly toward your chest, and maintain this neck position throughout the exercise.
6. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift the other foot off the ground by marching that side’s thigh up.
    1. You have the option of keeping your free leg’s knee bent or straight. Either position for the free leg is similarly viable, so you can go with whichever you prefer, but keeping the knee straight may make the exercise mildly more challenging compared to keeping it bent.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to help stabilize your lower back.
2. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heel into the ground and squeezing your glute.
    1. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your thigh through your hip to your shoulder (on the side of your body with the foot contacting the ground).
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
    3. Strive to keep your hip bones level on each side, rather than letting your free leg’s hip bone drop down or hike up.
    4. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may assist with feeling your glute engage during this exercise.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your hip mobility comfortably permits without rounding or excessively arching your lower back.
    1. Some people can descend all the way down until they lightly contact the ground with their glutes, but you can prioritize lowering through the range in which you can achieve through hip motion rather than lower back motion.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will predominantly develop your gluteus maximus while also training your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back and head on a flat bench, with your legs straight and feet together.
    1. As an alternative to a bench, you can lie down on the ground with something sturdy (like a couch or bed) behind you that you can grasp for stability. If you select this option, you can optionally place an exercise mat under where you will lie for comfort.
2. Reach behind your head and grasp the bottom of the bench’s pad with both hands.
    1. If you opted to lie down on the ground, you can alternatively reach behind you and grasp whatever sturdy object you are positioned against.
    2. Continue holding onto the bench (or alternative object) for stability throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abdominal muscles to press your lower back down into the ground. Simultaneously lift your legs up off the ground until your thighs are approximately vertical while keeping your knees mostly straight.
    1. Strive to keep your knees as close to straight as your hamstring flexibility comfortably permits.
2. While keeping your legs directed straight up, lift your buttocks, lower back, and middle back off the floor.
    1. You should now be bearing your weight predominantly through your upper back.
3. In a controlled manner, allow your legs to arc forward and downward while keeping your buttocks and most of your back off the bench until your thighs are about to contact the bench.
    1. In contrast to the lifting phase of this exercise that you perform during steps 1 and 2, this lowering phase should be a continuous movement as you lower your legs back together. Correspondingly, your buttocks should only contact the bench when your legs are angled mostly forward rather than upward.
    2. At the bottom of this movement, your legs should be directed straight forward with your back contacting the bench.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should strive to control the descent to the best of your ability rather than just dropping down abruptly.
    4. As you descend further, controlling the movement will become progressively more challenging.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your rectus abdominis, with assistance from your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Position the Smith machine’s bar slightly below the height of your hands when standing with your arms at your sides.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the bar, and stand upright in a near hip-width stance with the middle of the bar just in front of your legs.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly backward when lifting it up (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails). Facing this way will help the shrug feel most natural.
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar, sit your hips mildly backward while slightly bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the bar, and rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion.
3. Slightly hinge at your hips by sitting your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat or mildly arched rather than rounding it or excessively arching it.
    2. Quickly proceed to the next step as soon as you finish this step.
4. Forcefully thrust your hips forward while straightening your knees to generate momentum from your lower body. Simultaneously shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion to lift the bar upward while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position. Refrain from leaning back through excessive lower back arching, which can be easier to avoid if you think about squeezing your glutes.
    2. While you are intentionally using momentum to help your upper traps lift the weight, you should not be “cheating” to the extent that you feel out of control.
    3. Throughout the exercise, look straight ahead to keep your neck near a neutral position.
    4. As a cue, you can think about driving your shoulders up toward your earlobes.
5. Allow your shoulders to descend fully in a controlled manner to lower the bar while keeping your torso upright and legs stationary.
    1. Your muscles are noticeably stronger during the lowering phase of an exercise compared to the lifting phase, so “cheating” during the lifting phase allows your upper traps to experience greater loading during the more controlled lowering phase.
    2. At the bottom, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the machine’s weight can provide.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank you downward. If you are unable to control the lowering phase to any reasonable degree, you are likely using a weight that is excessively heavy relative to your current ability.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to re-rack the bar.
    1. Following your last rep, rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars, and mildly bend your hips and knees to rerack the bar onto the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on a bench with an upright torso while holding the dumbbells by your sides with straight elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides.
    1. You can opt to sit down on a seated bench with your back resting against the back support, or you can sit on a flat bench without any back support.
    2. While you do not necessarily need to have an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid sitting with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your arms out and away from your sides in an arcing motion while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. Keep your elbows straight throughout the exercise.
    2. You should resemble making a snow angel with your arms as they move straight outward rather than forward.
2. As your arms approach being parallel to the ground, point your palms forward and continue sweeping your arms up overhead through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. While you raise your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    2. If you have the mobility to do so comfortably, you can finish the lifting phase once your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling. However, if you start feeling shoulder discomfort in the fully overhead position, you can simply move through the greatest range of motion that is comfortable to you.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering the dumbbells toward your sides in a smooth arcing motion.
    1. As your arms become approximately parallel to the ground, turn your palms down, which will result in your palms facing in toward your sides once your arms are by your sides again.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the dumbbells swing down in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While still meaningfully training your lateral and front delts, this variation biases your upper trap and serratus anterior to a greater degree than standard lateral raises.## Setting up
1. Place a stability ball (which is also known as a Swiss ball or physio ball) on the ground, and optionally place an exercise mat or cushion behind it for comfort.
    1. Ideally, select a ball with a diameter exceeding your shoulder width.
2. With your knees hip width apart, kneel with the ball in front of you as your torso and thighs are fully upright.
    1. From this tall kneeling position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to your knees when viewed from the side.
3. Place your palms on the ball with straight elbows, while positioning the ball as far in front of you while keeping your torso fully upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Keep your glutes squeezed and your core braced throughout the set to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. Slowly lean your torso and thighs forward as one unit as you let the ball roll forward and your arms angle forward. Keep your elbows straight and torso rigidly braced as you avoid letting your lower back arch throughout the set.
    1. Only roll the ball as far forward as you can while maintaining a flat lower back position and keeping your knees, hips, and shoulders fully aligned when viewed from the side.
    2. Keeping your lower back flat will become progressively more difficult as you roll out further, so you can determine your range of motion based on your current ability.
    3. If you are new to this exercise, a rather short range may be appropriate. If you have the strength to do so with proper form, you can finish rolling out when your head is almost contacting the ball.
    4. The forward movement of the arms is the same type of shoulder motion that would occur when lifting your arms straight forward and up overhead.
    5. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag or your ribs flare, as this can arch your lower back. Continuing to squeeze your glutes and tighten your abs will help you keep your lower back flat.
    6. Keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by driving your arms backward to roll the ball back toward the starting position while lifting your torso and thighs up together.
    1. Continue avoiding letting your hips bend or arching your lower back.
    2. You should still be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. At the end of this step, your torso and thighs should be fully upright.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also dynamically training your lats and teres major, this exercise can statically train your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with your lower chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, but feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is too low (placing the handles near shoulder level or above), some lifters may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort from having to flare their elbows outward excessively.
    4. If the seat is excessively high (placing the handles near your abdomen), the exercise may feel awkward and be suboptimal for developing the chest.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Depending on the specific machine model you are using and what feels most stable to you, you can place your feet flat on the floor or on a foot bar.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
4. With your palms facing down, elbows bent, and hands moderately outside shoulder width, firmly grasp the handles. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbows directly behind (rather than being above or below) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    3. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While keeping your elbows level with your wrists when viewed from the side, press the handles forward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows forward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet stationary.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handles to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers behind them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows and wrists aligned as they remain level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arms abruptly back in an uncontrolled fashion.
    4. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Position a sturdy object (such as a thick bumper plate or ideally an aerobic step) that you will use as a step onto the floor, somewhat in front of the Smith machine’s bar.
    1. You can use a range of different heights for your stepping surface, but selecting an object that is near four inches (10cm) high (which is a typical height of many aerobic steps without additional risers) can be an appropriate starting point.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you will face and put the step in front of the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails).
    3. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face, so it does not matter which side you place the step.
    4. Strive to place your step far enough in front of the bar that you can keep your torso fairly upright when performing your split squat with one foot on the step, which may take a bit of trial and error if you are new to this exercise.
    5. Due to the fixed bar path of the Smith machine, situating the step directly under the bar would require you to adopt a bent-over torso position during this exercise.
    6. When using a sufficiently wide object (such as an aerobic step) as a step, you can simply place the step so that it is centered with the Smith machine’s bar.
    7. If what you use as a step does not have a large enough surface area for you to stand on it in a hip-width stance with both of your soles fully in contact with the step, situate your step slightly toward the side on which you initially will place your foot on the step.
    8. The appropriate step placement will allow you to perform your split squat with your elevated foot fully in contact with the step while your feet are hip-width apart from each other.
2. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
    1. The ideal starting height should allow you to position the bar on your upper back and un-rack it in a stable position with the soles of your feet fully on the ground.
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground in order to unrack the bar, you initially set it excessively high.
3. Position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are slightly below the height of the bar when your rear knee taps the ground at the end of the lowering phase of your split squat.
    1. To find this point for your body, you can perform a rep or two of the split squat with the unloaded bar as described in the “exercise technique” section, but you can initially put the safety stops a little below the top of your hip bones when standing upright as an approximation.
4. Add your desired weight to the bar and face the center of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
5. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat wider than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
6. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees to then walk under the bar and situate it on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to tighten your upper back muscles, and unrack it by starting to stand up as you straighten your knees.
2. After the bar rests fully on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars with a slight wrist flick before standing fully upright with the bar.
3. Step up onto the step with the entirety of your sole in contact with the step while leaving your other foot on the ground.
    1. If you positioned your step closer toward one side of your body (due to using a step with a small surface area), you will step up with the foot aligned with the step, while you can use either side if your step is centered.
    2. The knee on the side of your elevated foot should be mildly bent once on the step.
4. Shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot before taking a fairly long step backward with your other foot and contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. To shift your bodyweight into your elevated foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you bend that side’s knee slightly more.
    2. Once you step back and resume transferring some of your weight into your rear leg, you can return to keeping your torso in an upright position.
    3. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    4. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    5. Keep the entire sole of your elevated foot firmly planted on the step and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    6. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
5. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until your rear leg’s kneecap is just barely above the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the step.
    2. While the forefoot of your rear leg will stay in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
6. Hold the split squat position for your target duration as you remain completely stationary with your rear knee hovering above the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet, the sole of your front foot fully on the step, and your torso upright.
    2. Since this is a static exercise, you will track how many seconds you hold the split squat rather than reps.
7. While keeping both of your feet planted, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
8. Re-rack the bar by bending at your hips and knees as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
9. After you have sufficiently rested, repeat the exercise while switching which leg is in the forward position on top of the step.
    1. Since your rear leg can be meaningfully engaged during a split squat hold, it is recommended to rest before performing your next set with your sides switching positions.
    2. If you wish, you can alternatively perform your sets continuously without reracking the bar and resting, but fatigue may interfere with your performance of the second set if you switch lead legs without any rest.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its furthest back position that allows you to begin each rep with the greatest knee bend you can achieve.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the shin pad to a position that places it slightly above the front of your ankles on the lowest aspect of your shins when seated with your legs behind the shin pad with your knees bent.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the shin pad without moving the lever arm, in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin and sit down on its seat with your lower shins behind the shin pad, knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point, and back resting against the back pad.
    1. With the appropriate positioning, it should be comfortable for you to have your knees bent.
    2. Your knees are aligned with the machine’s pivot point if you can draw a straight line through the center of both knees to the pivot point.
5. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Through a controlled kicking motion, progressively extend (straighten) both knees to lift the shin pad up until you have fully straightened your knees.
    1. While you do not lift the weight in an intentionally slow manner, avoid kicking up in an uncontrolled jerky motion.
    2. As your lower legs move through motion at your knees, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set as your thighs remain in contact with the seat.
3. Fully bend one knee to pull that side’s leg away from the shin pad while continuing to keep your other leg against the pad.
4. With the leg still in contact with the shin pad, allow your knee to bend in a controlled manner to lower the shin pad until you have returned to the starting position with your knee bent as much as the machine permits.
    1. There is no need to use an extremely slow lowering tempo, but you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting it abruptly swing your lower leg down.
    2. Keep your free leg’s knee bent as you avoid touching the shin pad with your free leg throughout the lowering phase.
5. Repeatedly perform steps 2-4 as you alternate each rep which leg you keep in contact with the shin pad during steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can alternatively complete one set where you perform the lowering phase of each rep exclusively with one side before resting, and then complete another set where you perform the lowering phase of every rep with your opposite side.
    2. During each rep, continue to move the shin pad up with both legs as you control it on the way down with only one leg.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all four of your quad muscles, including your rectus femoris, which may not be trained as effectively by most squat, leg press, and lunge variations.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a few finger widths below the height of your collarbone.
    1. The ideal starting height should let you position the bar on your upper back and unrack it in a stable position with the soles of your feet fully on the ground.
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground in order to unrack the bar, you initially set it excessively high.
2. Position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are slightly below the height of the bar when your rear knee taps the ground at the end of the lowering phase of your split squat.
    1. To find this point for your body, you can perform a rep or two of the split squat with the unloaded bar as described in the “exercise technique” section, but you can initially put the safety stops a little below the top of your hip bones when standing upright as an approximation.
3. Add your desired weight to the bar and face the center of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, position yourself to face the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (which results in the bottom of the rails being farther in front of you than the top of the rails).
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
4. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat outside than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
5. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees to then walk under the bar and situate it on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    3. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    4. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    5. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to tighten your upper back muscles, and unrack it by starting to stand up as you straighten your knees.
2. After the bar rests fully on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars with a slight wrist flick before standing fully upright with the bar.
3. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to descend until your rear knee is just above the ground with your front thigh close to parallel to the ground. This may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep your torso in a mostly upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
4. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until your rear leg’s kneecap is just barely above the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your torso mostly upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    4. Perform steps 3 and 4 as a smooth and continuous movement.
5. Hold the lunge position for your target duration as you remain completely stationary with your rear knee hovering above the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet, the sole of your front foot fully on the ground, and your torso upright.
    2. Since this is a static exercise, you will track how many seconds you hold the lunge rather than reps.
6. Step forward with your rear leg while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to lift your body up into a standing position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart.
7. Rerack the bar by bending at your hips and knees as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
8. After you have sufficiently rested, repeat the exercise while switching which leg you place in the lead position.
    1. Since your rear leg can be meaningfully engaged during a lunge hold, it is recommended to rest before performing your next set with your other leg forward
    2. If you wish to do so, you can alternatively perform your sets continuously without reracking the bar and resting, but fatigue may interfere with the performance of the next set if you do not rest at all between them.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Optionally place a full diameter weight plate (like a thick bumper plate or a 45lb (20kg) plate with a flat surface facing up) slightly in front of the pulley and just to one side of the pulley.
    1. While not required, standing on a mildly elevated object with your non-working leg can help provide clearance above the ground for your working leg’s foot.
    2. The appropriate positioning of the plate (or a viable alternative like an aerobic step or deadlift block) should allow your working leg’s foot to hang fully off it while your knees are about hip width apart and pointing straight forward. The plate should also be sufficiently far in front of the pulley that you can experience resistance from the cable in the starting position of each rep when your feet are level with each other. If you situate the plate excessively close to the pulley, you will lose tension from the cable at the start of each rep when your working leg’s knee is straight.
3. Securely attach an ankle cuff just above the ankle of the leg you will train. Position the cuff so that one of its attachment rings is directed straight forward.
4. While facing the cable station in a hip-width stance, stand in front of the pulley with your non-working leg’s foot on the plate and your working leg’s foot directly in front of the pulley.
    1. With the appropriate placement of the plate, your working leg’s foot should be slightly further in front of your other foot in this position.
5. Bend over and connect the cable to your cuff’s attachment ring before standing mostly upright with your non-working side’s knee slightly bent.
    1. It will be easiest to connect the cable when your working leg is close enough to the pulley that the cable does not provide resistance when connecting it.
6. With your torso mildly leaned forward at your hips, grasp part of the cable station (with your fingers away from the weight stack) with your hands.
    1. Continue holding part of the cable station from now onward for additional stability.
7. Take a small step backward with your working side’s foot until your feet are aligned with each other and you feel resistance from the cable.
    1. If you do not feel resistance from the cable in this position, you should move the plate you are using as a step mildly further back away from the pulley. Then resume setting up.
8. Straighten your non-working side’s knee to lift your other foot off the ground without letting the cable pull your working leg forward.
    1. In this starting position, your hip bones and knees should be symmetrically level with each other.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to your working side’s leg.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your toes upward and back toward your shin by moving at your ankle. Maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankle in the same position in which it would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heel up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankle in this position can increase your hamstring curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
2. Drive your heel toward your buttock by bending your knee while keeping your thigh fixed in a vertical position.
    1. Strive to bend your knee through your full comfortable range of motion, which should result in you bending your knee by at least 90 degrees.
    2. If you experience a notable cramping sensation in your hamstrings when your knee is fully bent, you can stop the lifting phase just before the point where your hamstrings may cramp.
    3. While your lower leg arcs backward and upward through knee motion, aim to keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set. This will involve keeping your hip bones level with each other and avoiding letting your knee swing forward or backward via hip motion.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing your knee to extend progressively in a controlled manner until it has fully straightened.
    1. As you let your lower leg arc downward and forward in line with the cable’s resistance, continue keeping the rest of your body stationary as your thighs remain symmetrically aligned.
    2. There is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, but you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the cable abruptly swing your lower leg down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Place an inflated stability ball (which is also known as a Swiss ball or physio ball) on the ground.
2. Sit down on the center of the ball with your torso upright, knees bent, and feet on the floor.
3. Walk your feet forward while leaning your torso backward and rolling your buttocks closer to the front of the ball until your back is resting on top of the ball. Simultaneously allow your back to arch naturally.
    1. Your back and the back of your hip bones should be supported by the ball.
    2. After walking forward, your knees should be bent by about 90 degrees with your feet flat on the floor.
4. Place the fingertips of each hand lightly in contact with the sides of the back of your head as your upper arms and elbows are directed out away from your sides.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
    2. If you wish to reduce the difficulty of this exercise, you can alternatively cross your arms across your chest.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl your spine as you pull the bottom of your ribs toward the tops of your hip bones in a controlled manner while tightening your abs and lifting your upper back off the ball.
    1. Once you feel that you can no longer curl your lower and middle back while keeping your hip bones and lower back against the ball, you have reached your end range. In this position, your shoulder blades should be above the ball.
    2. To train your abs dynamically, you should be intentionally rounding your back, since this motion is performed by contraction of your abs.
    3. You can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the middle of your chest, and you are trying to point that closer toward the middle point between your knees.
    4. Keep your feet planted on the ground and your lower body stationary throughout the set.
    5. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
2. Reverse the movement by uncurling your back in a controlled manner as you lower your upper back to the ball.
    1. After transitioning from a rounded position to a flat position, allow your lower back to arch around the ball.
    2. The imaginary laser beam shooting out from your chest should angle progressively backward as you let your ribs flare away from your hip bones.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should actively control it the entire time rather than dropping down in an abrupt fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. If you are using an adjustable incline bench, position it in the fully upright setting. Otherwise, proceed to the next step.
    1. You can interchangeably use an adjustable bench that is positioned fully upright or a seated bench that is permanently fixed in an upright position.
    2. Alternatively, you can use a bench that is permanently fixed in a flat position. This will preclude you from resting your back against the bench, but otherwise allow you to use the same technique that the exercise involves when using back support.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and hold them with your palms facing in toward your sides.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
3. Sit down on a seated bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your lower thighs without leaning back onto the back rest.
    1. To move the dumbbells into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
    2. When seated, your elbows should be tucked by your sides with your palms facing in toward each other.
4. March your thighs up one at a time as you lift the dumbbells up near your shoulders with your elbows bent. Simultaneously lean back to situate your back against the bench.
    1. After doing so, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder-width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. Hold the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip you will maintain throughout your set.
5. With your elbows and upper arms in front of your torso, direct your palms straight backward as your wrists are in front of your shoulders.
    1. Your forearms should be in a vertical position, and your wrists should be stacked over your elbows.
    2. In contrast to a standard dumbbell overhead press, your elbows should not be flared out away from your sides.
6. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, keep your chest up in a “proud chest” posture, rather than letting your shoulders and upper back round forward into a hunched position.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While simultaneously flaring your elbows out away from your sides and rotating your palms forward, press the dumbbells upward.
    1. At the top of the press, the bony bumps on the back of your elbows should be pointing out away from your sides (rather than forward), your palms should be facing forward, and your elbows should be straight with your arms fully overhead.
    2. Rather than turning the exercise into a segmented movement, strive to have the outward elbow flare and palm rotation fluidly occur at the same rate at which you are driving your elbows upward.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your feet flat on the floor along with your buttocks and upper back in contact with the bench.
    4. Keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering the dumbbells while simultaneously moving your elbows in front of your torso and rotating your palms backward.
    1. Move your elbows forward and rotate your palms backward progressively throughout the lowering phase, rather than just performing these motions during the initial part of the descent and lowering the dumbbells straight down in front of your body. This should result in you starting to lower the dumbbells with your elbows predominantly pointing out to the sides and your elbows only being fully in front of your shoulders at the bottom of the rep.
    2. Fully return to the starting position by lowering the dumbbells as far as you can while keeping your wrists in a straight position stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical.
    3. You do not have to lower the dumbbells with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting them drop down in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After the lowering phase of the last rep, direct your palms in toward each other, march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and lower the dumbbells onto your thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Anchor a long looped elastic band of your desired tension level around the center of a pull-up bar.
    1. To anchor the band, initially drape it over the bar, loop one end through the middle of the other, and pull that end until it is taut.
    2. If the bar is out of reach when standing on the ground, initially position yourself on an aerobic step, sturdy box, or bumper plate to bring the bar within reach. Then set the band and grasp the bar during the next step. Position the step slightly behind the bar rather than directly underneath it, so you can keep your legs straight during the exercise without contacting the step.
    3. If your bar has rough knurling rather than a smooth surface, anchoring the band directly around the bar can increase the risk of the band tearing over time. Placing a fat grip attachment or hand towel around the bar before anchoring the band can help prevent the bar with knurling from tearing the band.
    4. Before using it, inspect the band to confirm there are no small tears, as a small tear can quickly become a full tear when stretching the band.
2. Stand under the bar, and pull the band down with both arms.
    1. Each side of the band should be flat without any twists.
3. March up one leg and step into the band with the middle of your foot snugly against the bottom end of the band’s loop before lowering your foot while straightening your leg. Alternatively, you can bend one knee and loop the band around your upper shin.
    1. Since the band’s tension increases as you stretch it, situating the band around your foot with a straight knee will provide greater assistance compared to if you situate the band around your upper shin with your knee bent.
    2. If your pull-up bar is not tall enough to keep your foot from contacting the ground at the bottom of the rep while keeping your leg straight, looping the band around your upper shin will be a better option. Otherwise, you can select whichever approach best suits your preference while providing the level of assistance you prefer.
    3. With either setup, the band should feel secure rather than feeling like it may slide around during the exercise.
4. Reach overhead and firmly grasp the pull-up bar with your palms facing forward (an overhand grip) and your hands well outside shoulder width.
    1. Positioning your hands about 50% further apart than your shoulder width is appropriate, but a wide grip can encompass a range of grip widths, and specifically, how wide you grasp the bar is up to your preference.
    2. What is considered a wide grip to you can also be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your standard pull-up. For instance, with a wide grip, place your index finger on the bar just outside where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing a standard pull-up.
    3. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    4. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palm (rather than at the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your skin in an uncomfortable way and increases the risk of tearing a callus.
5. Hang on the bar with your elbows straightened. If you are standing on a step with the band under your foot, step off the step and let both legs hang straight down toward the ground. If you have the band around your upper shin, lift your other foot off the ground by bending that knee and keeping both knees bent throughout the exercise.
    1. You can optionally situate your other leg (that was not initially contacting the band) within the band if you wish to do so.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to assume a “proud chest” that is angled slightly upward.
    1. Maintain this “proud chest” posture throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows being bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion that their bodies enable.
    3. Rather than swinging your legs, statically keep them in a similar position throughout the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms fully overhead.
    1. You do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, you can return one foot onto the ground or step, shift your weight into step/ground, march your other leg up and out of the band, and release the bar.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
Note that this accentuated eccentric variation cannot be performed on iso-lateral machine models, which have a lever arm on each side of the machine and two separate shin pads that permit you to move the right and left sides independently of each other. This variation requires that your machine have a single lever arm with one long shin pad that enables you to perform the lifting phase with both legs and the lowering phase with only one leg.

The key goal of your setup is to adjust the machine and position yourself in a manner that results in your knee joint being aligned with the machine’s pivot point, which is the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates, that is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines. After setting up appropriately, you should be in a position that results in you feeling comfortable and stable during the exercise. The features you can adjust will vary based on the specific machine model you use, so certain setup steps described in the instructions may not be applicable to a particular machine model. As you set up, some trial and error may be needed while you adjust the machine and position yourself to find what works best for your individual body proportions.

1. Adjust the seat’s back pad to a position that aligns your knees with the machine’s pivot point while sitting up against the back pad with your thighs resting on the seat.
2. Adjust the machine’s lever arm (that will align with your shins) to its furthest back position that allows you to begin each rep with the greatest knee bend you can achieve.
    1. Certain machines can be used for leg extensions or hamstring curls based on how the lever arm is positioned.
3. Adjust the shin pad to a position that places it slightly above the front of your ankles on the lowest aspect of your shins when seated with your legs behind the shin pad with your knees bent.
    1. Adjusting this setting exclusively moves the shin pad without moving the lever arm, in contrast to step 2, where the machine lever arm and shin pad move together.
4. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
5. Sit down on the seat with your lower shins behind the shin pad, knees aligned with the machine’s pivot point, and back resting against the back pad.
    1. With the appropriate positioning, it should be comfortable for you to have your knees bent.
    2. Your knees are aligned with the machine’s pivot point if you can draw a straight line through the center of both knees to the pivot point.
6. Adjust the thigh pad to press down snugly against your lower thighs.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Through a controlled kicking motion, progressively extend (straighten) both knees to lift the shin pad up until you have fully straightened your knees.
    1. While you do not lift the weight in an intentionally slow manner, avoid kicking up in an uncontrolled jerky motion.
    2. As your lower legs move through motion at your knees, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set as your thighs remain in contact with the seat.
3. Fully bend one knee to pull that side’s leg away from the shin pad while continuing to keep your other leg against the pad.
4. With the leg still in contact with the shin pad, allow your knee to bend in a controlled manner to lower the shin pad until you have returned to the starting position with your knee bent as much as the machine permits.
    1. There is no need to use an extremely slow lowering tempo, but you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting it abruptly swing your lower leg down.
    2. Keep your free leg’s knee bent as you avoid touching the shin pad with your free leg throughout the lowering phase.
5. Repeatedly perform steps 2-4 as you alternate each rep which leg you keep in contact with the shin pad during steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can alternatively complete one set where you perform the lowering phase of each rep exclusively with one side before resting, and then complete another set where you perform the lowering phase of every rep with your opposite side.
    2. During each rep, continue to move the shin pad up with both legs as you control it on the way down with only one leg.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all four of your quad muscles, including your rectus femoris, which may not be trained as effectively by most squat, leg press, and lunge variations.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent to about 90 degrees.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can lie down on an exercise mat.
2. With straight elbows, raise both arms straight up toward the ceiling as your elbows and wrists are vertically stacked above your shoulders.
    1. You can point your palms in toward each other.
3. While keeping your knees bent to about 90 degrees, march both legs up until your knees are vertically stacked above your hips.
    1. In this position, your thighs should be perpendicular to the ground, and your shins should be parallel to the ground.
4. Based on your preference, you can keep your head resting on the ground, or you can slightly tuck your chin toward your chest to lift your head just barely off the ground.
    1. Lifting your head slightly off the ground can mildly increase the difficulty and allow you to train your neck muscles statically, but doing so is optional. If you opt to lift your head just off the ground, keep it there throughout your set. Otherwise, continue resting your head on the ground during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
2. Press your lower back lightly into the floor to flatten your lower back, and continue doing so throughout your set.
    1. This can prompt you to feel your abs contract further as they keep the bottom of your ribs pulled down toward your hip bones.
    2. It is the opposite of if you were to arch your lower back, which would flare the bottom of your ribs away from your hip bones.
    3. If you are relaxed while lying down, your lower back naturally has a mild arch, allowing you to slide a hand between the ground and your lower back. When you use your abs to flatten your lower back, there should be no space between your lower back and the ground, preventing you from sliding a hand between them.
    4. Before proceeding to the next step, you can optionally try sliding one hand between your lower back and the ground. Being unable to slide your hand under your lower back confirms that you have effectively flattened it. If you choose to do so, lift your arm to the starting position before beginning the next step.
3. Sweep arms backward and downward while simultaneously straightening your legs and lowering them toward the ground.
    1. As you lower your arms and legs, you will have to continue bracing your core to prevent your lower back from arching up off the ground.
    2. Strive to move your arms and legs at the same rate through the full range of motion that you can while keeping your lower back flat against the ground.
    3. With sufficient core control, you can finish with your arms and legs parallel to the ground as they are fully straightened. From this position, you can gently tap the ground with your hands and heels simultaneously. If your lower back starts arching before this point, shorten the range of motion to what you can perform while maintaining a flat lower back.
    4. All the movement should occur from your shoulders, hips, and knees as your spine remains motionless.
    5. Move slowly and deliberately throughout your set, focusing on high-quality technique at every degree of motion.
4. Reverse the movement by lifting your arms and legs up to return to the starting position.
    1. Since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the lowering phase, no motion should occur at your lower back as you continue bracing your core.
    2. While keeping your elbows straight, bend your knees as you march your thighs up to finish with your knees bent to approximately 90 degrees.
    3. At the top, both of your knees should be stacked vertically above your hips, and your arms should be vertically stacked above your shoulders.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your rectus abdominis and obliques to maintain a rigid back position as you refine your ability to move through your hips and shoulders with a stable trunk.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Lift the dumbbells up near the front of your shoulders with your elbows bent by curling the weights and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbells into position if they are too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbells mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbows to curl the weights upward while swinging your elbows forward. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. Hold the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip you will maintain throughout your set.
3. With your elbows and upper arms in front of your torso, direct your palms straight backward as your wrists are in front of your shoulders.
    1. Your forearms should be in a vertical position, and your wrists should be stacked over your elbows.
    2. In contrast to a standard dumbbell overhead press, your elbows should not be flared out away from your sides.
4. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, keep your chest up in a “proud chest” posture, rather than letting your shoulders and upper back round forward into a hunched position.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes like you are trying to crush a walnut, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core and tightening your glutes throughout your set.
2. While simultaneously flaring your elbows out away from your sides and rotating your palms forward, press the dumbbells upward.
    1. At the top of the press, the bony bumps on the back of your elbows should be pointing out away from your sides (rather than forward), your palms should be facing forward, and your elbows should be straight with your arms fully overhead.
    2. Rather than turning the exercise into a segmented movement, strive to have the outward elbow flare and palm rotation fluidly occur at the same rate at which you are driving your elbows upward.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your knees and hips straightened as you avoid using your legs to help lift the weights or leaning your torso back via lower back arching.
    4. Keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering the dumbbells while simultaneously moving your elbows in front of your torso and rotating your palms backward.
    1. Move your elbows forward and rotate your palms backward progressively throughout the lowering phase, rather than just performing these motions during the initial part of the descent and lowering the dumbbells straight down in front of your body. This should result in you starting to lower the dumbbells with your elbows predominantly pointing out to the sides and your elbows only being fully in front of your shoulders at the bottom of the rep.
    2. Fully return to the starting position by lowering the dumbbells as far as you can while keeping your wrists in a straight position stacked over your elbows as your forearms remain vertical.
    3. You do not have to lower the dumbbells with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting them drop down in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down in the opposite way in which you initially set up.
    1. After the lowering phase of the last rep, lower the dumbbells to your sides as you straighten your elbows and swing your elbows back by your sides.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, triceps, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and stand upright in a near hip-width stance. Hold the dumbbells by your sides with straight elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides.
    1. While you do not necessarily need to have an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid standing with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Raise your arms out and away from your sides in an arcing motion while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. Throughout the exercise, keep your elbows and legs straight while maintaining an upright torso.
    2. You should resemble making a snow angel with your arms as they move straight outward rather than forward.
2. As your arms approach being parallel to the ground, point your palms forward and continue sweeping your arms up overhead through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. While you raise your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    2. If you have the mobility to do so comfortably, you can finish the lifting phase once your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling. However, if you start feeling shoulder discomfort in the fully overhead position, you can simply move through the greatest range of motion that is comfortable to you.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering the dumbbells toward your sides in a smooth arcing motion.
    1. As your arms become approximately parallel to the ground, turn your palms down, which will result in your palms facing in toward your sides once your arms are by your sides again.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the dumbbells swing down in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While still meaningfully training your lateral and front delts, this variation biases your upper trap and serratus anterior to a greater degree than standard lateral raises.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and stand centered between its handles in a near hip-width stance with your back facing the machine.
2. With straight elbows and your arms directed toward the handles, sit your buttocks back while bending your knees to hinge at your hips until your hands are level with the handles.
    1. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
3. Firmly grasp each handle with a neutral grip such that your palms are facing in toward each other.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the handles before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand fully upright.
3. Dip down slightly by bending your knees while keeping your chest up.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat or mildly arched rather than rounding it or excessively arching it.
    2. Quickly proceed to the next step as soon as you finish dipping down.
4. Forcefully drive your feet into the ground as you rapidly straighten your knees to generate momentum from your lower body. Simultaneously shrug your shoulders upward through your available range of motion to lift the handles upward while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. While you are intentionally using momentum to help your upper traps lift the weight, you should not be “cheating” to the extent that you feel out of control.
    2. Throughout the exercise, look straight ahead to keep your neck near a neutral position.
    3. As a cue, you can think about driving your shoulders up toward your earlobes.
5. Allow your shoulders to descend fully in a controlled manner to lower the handles while keeping your torso upright and legs stationary.
    1. Your muscles are noticeably stronger during the lowering phase of an exercise compared to the lifting phase, so “cheating” during the lifting phase allows your upper traps to experience greater loading during the more controlled lowering phase.
    2. At the bottom, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weight can provide.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank you downward. If you are unable to control the lowering phase to any reasonable degree, you are likely using weights that are excessively heavy relative to your current ability.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep, sit your buttocks back while bending your knees to hinge at your hips and set the handles down.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. Anchor a long looped elastic band of your desired tension level around the center of a pull-up bar with parallel handles that are near shoulder width apart.
    1. To anchor the band, initially drape it over the bar, loop one end through the middle of the other, and pull that end until it is taut.
    2. If the bar is out of reach when standing on the ground, initially position yourself on an aerobic step, sturdy box, or bumper plate to bring the bar within reach. Then set the band and grasp the bar during the next step. Position the step mildly behind the bar rather than directly underneath it, so that you have the option of keeping your legs straightened during the exercise without contacting the step.
    3. If your bar has rough knurling rather than a smooth surface, anchoring the band directly around the bar can increase the risk of the band tearing over time. Placing a fat grip attachment or hand towel around a bar before anchoring the band can help stop a bar with knurling from tearing a band.
    4. Before using it, inspect the band to confirm there are no small tears, as a small tear can quickly become a full tear when stretching the band.
2. Stand under the bar, and pull the band down with both arms.
    1. Each side of the band should be flat without any twists.
3. March up one leg and step into the band with the middle of your foot snugly against the bottom end of the band’s loop before lowering your foot while straightening your leg. Alternatively, you can bend one knee and loop the band around your upper shin.
    1. Since the band’s tension increases as you stretch it, situating the band around your foot with a straight knee will provide greater assistance compared to if you situate the band around your upper shin with your knee bent.
    2. If your pull-up bar is not tall enough to keep your foot from contacting the ground at the bottom of the rep while keeping your leg straight, looping the band around your upper shin will be a better option. Otherwise, you can select whichever approach best suits your preference while providing the level of assistance you prefer.
    3. With either setup, the band should feel secure rather than feeling like it may slide around during the exercise.
4. Reach overhead and firmly grasp the parallel handles with your palms facing each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
5. Hang on the bar with your elbows straightened. If you are standing on a step and have the band under your foot, step forward off the step and allow both legs to hang straight down toward the ground. If you have the band around your upper shin, lift your other foot off the ground by bending that knee and keeping both knees bent throughout the exercise.
    1. You can optionally situate your other leg (that was not initially contacting the band) within the band if you wish to do so.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to assume a “proud chest” that is angled slightly upward.
    1. Maintain this “proud chest” posture throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and back toward your sides as you progressively bend your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows will not actually drive downward. However, the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows being bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion that their bodies enable.
    3. Rather than swinging your legs, statically keep them in a similar position throughout the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms fully overhead.
    1. You do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, you can return one foot onto the ground or step, shift your weight into step/ground, march your other leg up and out of the band, and release the bar.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the seat so that your upper arms can be parallel with the floor or a little lower while grasping the handles.
    1. Some people notice that their shoulders feel more comfortable when the seat is set to a height that allows their elbows to be slightly lower than their shoulders, compared to when their elbows are completely level with their shoulders. Consequently, if you find that your shoulders do not feel the best when performing this exercise with your elbows at shoulder height, you can try increasing the seat height to let you utilize a slightly lesser degree of elbow flare.
    2. If your machine lacks handles, you can adjust the seat to a height that allows you to rest your arms comfortably against the arm pads when your upper arms are reasonably close to being parallel to the ground. When using a machine without handles, you can naturally ignore the instructions about grasping the handles and otherwise use the same setup and exercise technique.
2. Select your weight with the loading pin, and sit down on the seat with your back against the back pad and your feet flat on the floor.
3. With your elbows bent by about 90 degrees, spread your arms backward into a “T-position” through your full range of motion.
    1. Note how far backward your shoulder mobility permits you to spread your arms apart comfortably, since this will indicate where you should adjust the lever arms.
4. Adjust the machine’s lever arms to a position that allows you to situate your arms just behind the arm pads when your upper arms are fully spread apart in the “T-position.”
    1. The ideal starting position of the lever arms should allow you to perform this exercise through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your chest or the front of the shoulders after you assume the starting position, you likely can move the lever arms a bit further back.
    3. If you feel shoulder discomfort in the starting position with your upper arms fully spread apart, you likely set the lever arms excessively far back relative to your shoulder mobility.
    4. Some trial and error may be required to find what is personally the best setting for you.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to adopt a “proud chest” posture.
6. With your elbows bent by about 90 degrees, spread your arms backward into a “T-position” to rest your arms against the arm pads and grasp the handles.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows fully in toward each other in an arcing manner to pull the machine’s lever arms together.
    1. You can finish once your elbows are directly in front of your shoulders or once you lightly tap the lever arms against each other.
    2. While you do not have to keep your shoulder blades fully pulled back together as your elbows approach each other, avoid letting your shoulders round forward into a slouched posture.
    3. Maintain the same degree of elbow bend throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to spread apart from each other and arc backward through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. You should finish in a “proud chest” posture with your upper arms fully spread apart from each other as they form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, avoid letting the machine’s resistance abruptly swing your arms apart in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed the set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the stoppers (that are positioned under the arms) to a height that allows you to press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with vertical forearms, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down on the bench while snugly positioning the front of your ankles and the top of your feet under the foot/ankle pad.
    1. Your legs should feel securely locked in place and not permit any sliding.
4. Lie down with your back and head on the pad. Ideally align your lower chest with the handles if you can do so.
    1. Since you have to set up with your feet under the foot/ankle pad, you can be limited in terms of positioning your body in a manner that aligns your lower chest with the handles. This can result in not all body types being well-suited to use this type of machine. If the handles are situated horizontally behind your shoulders or meaningfully further forward than your chest (resulting in the handles being level with your abdomen) when lying down, this machine may not be the best tool for you.
5. Firmly grasp the handles with your bent elbows stacked directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being in a vertical position.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. How wide a grip you can use, along with whether you can use an overhand or neutral grip, can be determined by the design of your machine’s handles.
    3. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles and position your elbows directly under them, you may have to increase the height of the machine’s stoppers before resuming your setup. If you still find that the machine forces you into an uncomfortable position to grasp the handles, a different exercise may be more suitable for you.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists, press the handles upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back and buttocks snugly against the pad, and feet locked in place.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms to the stoppers under them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles as you allow your elbows to move outward and downward while bending.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Place a furniture slider on the ground.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground, and situate the bottom of one heel on top of the slider with your knees fully bent and feet hip-width apart.
    1. You can rest the back of your head on the ground and leave your arms on the ground by your sides or in whichever position you prefer.
3. On the side not in contact with the slider, lift your foot off the ground by marching that side’s thigh up with your knee bent.
    1. Statically keep this leg lifted off the ground throughout the set you perform with your other side.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the leg contacting the slider.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heel down into the slider, squeezing your glute, and tightening your abs.
    1. After performing this glute bridge motion, you should now be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side.
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid arching your lower back excessively.
    3. Throughout the set, keep your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down or hike up above your working leg’s hip bone.
2. Without bending your hip, move your foot forward by progressively straightening your knee in a controlled manner until it is almost fully straightened.
    1. Continue keeping your glute squeezed, abs tensed, and pressure concentrated through your heel throughout the exercise.
    2. All the motion should occur from your shin moving at your knee joint, while you are still able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder.
    3. At the end of this lowering phase, your buttocks should just barely be above the ground.
    4. You do not have to perform this movement with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting your foot abruptly slide forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Reverse the movement by driving your heel toward your buttock to bend your knee and pull the slider backward.
    1. You should finish with your knee close to being fully bent as the slider is vertically under your knee.
    2. If you experience a notably uncomfortable cramping sensation in your hamstrings when your knee is fully bent, you can stop bending your knee just before reaching that point.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to lower your buttocks to the ground by bending at your hip before repeating the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While statically engaging your gluteus maximus, this exercise can train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. On the side you wish to train, pick up a dumbbell and stand behind a seated bench that is permanently fixed to an upright setting or a standing preacher curl bench that has a vertical arm pad on one side.
    1. For this Scott curl, you specifically want to use a bench with a vertical pad in contrast to a Preacher curl, which uses a sloped arm pad.
    2. While they are not available in most gyms, some standing Preacher curl benches have a sloped arm pad on one side and a vertical arm pad on the other side. To use this bench, you should stand on the side of the sloped arm pad in order to rest your arm on the vertical side.
    3. More commonly available are fixed seated benches with back pads that are shorter than adjustable exercise benches set to a fully upright position. To perform this exercise, the top of the pad should be no higher than your armpit, which precludes adjustable benches positioned fully upright from being used except for very tall lifters.
    4. If the seated bench or standing Preacher curl bench is a little too tall for you, you can elevate yourself by positioning and then standing on a bumper plate, a stack of standard full-diameter weight plates, an aerobic step, or a deadlift block behind the bench.
2. While initially standing with enough space between you and the bench that you will be able to curl the dumbbell upward, direct your palm forward, resulting in an underhand grip.
    1. Maintain this underhand grip and avoid rotating your palm as you finish setting up and perform the exercise.
    2. Unless stated otherwise, the directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell.
3. Curl the dumbbell upward by fully bending your elbow while lifting it upward to point straight ahead.
    1. Your arm and dumbbell should now be fully above the arm pad, which will make it easier to get into the starting position.
    2. Keep your wrist straight when setting up and performing the exercise.
4. While keeping your elbow bent, step forward until your torso is very close to the pad before resting your upper arm and elbow on the pad.
    1. You can use your free hand to help guide your other arm into the starting position if you wish to do so.
    2. Your elbow should now be vertically situated under your shoulder.
5. Hold the bench pad with your free hand for additional stability. While keeping your torso upright, adjust your stance and degree of knee bend to whichever position situates the top of the bench just below your armpit and allows most of your upper arm to contact the pad.
    1. The ideal position should allow your upper arm to feel well-supported and your feet to feel balanced.
    2. If you initially find that the pad only contacts your elbow and the lower part of your triceps, widening your stance and bending your knees to dip down with an upright torso can lower your arm and facilitate greater support from the bench. Keeping the feet closer to hip width apart with straighter knees may alternatively be better for shorter lifters if the top of the bench is fairly close to armpit height when standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow and triceps in contact with the bench, lower the dumbbell in a slow and controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. While your forearm arcs forward and downward through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary with your palm facing inward throughout your set.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbell down.
    3. After initially pointing back toward you in the starting position, your palm should be angled forward and upward at the bottom of the rep.
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbow to curl the dumbbell upward and back toward you in an arcing motion until your elbow is fully bent with your forearm contacting your biceps.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow and triceps snugly against the arm pad.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other arm.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep, lift your elbow up above the bench through shoulder motion while keeping it bent, and step backward before lowering the dumbbell. Feel free to assist your working arm with your free hand. Alternatively, you may set the dumbbell down onto the bench after your last rep.
    2. If you become too fatigued to finish a rep during your set, you can also use your free hand to assist with curling the dumbbell up. Recognize that an assisted rep should not be recorded as a full rep for tracking progress.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up a dumbbell with one hand, carry it over to a flat bench, and position yourself near the base of the bench and directly to its side with your side that is holding the dumbbell farther away from the bench.
    1. If you have to carry the dumbbell a meaningful distance before reaching your bench and your grip is somewhat fatigued, you can place the dumbbell on the bench before picking it up with your opposite hand to start your set with the side that does not have a fatigued grip.
2. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips, reach out with your free arm to place its hand on the bench with a straight elbow, and place your knee (on the side not holding the dumbbell) onto the bench.
    1. In this position, your torso should now be reasonably close to being parallel to the ground, and your lower back should be flat rather than being meaningfully rounded.
    2. While setting up, keep your elbow straight on the side holding the dumbbell and allow that arm to hang down toward the ground with your shoulder blade naturally pulled forward toward the ground from the weight.
    3. Based on your comfort and preference, you can place the open palm of your free hand onto the bench, or you can make a fist and place your knuckles onto the bench.
    4. Your foot on the side of the dumbbell should remain firmly in contact with the floor and be sufficiently far behind the dumbbell that your leg does not restrict your row. If you initially do not feel well-balanced, you can try positioning this foot somewhat further out to the side to widen your base of support.
3. Direct your palm so that it is angled between facing backward and in toward the opposite side of your body.
    1. Throughout your set, maintain this grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip and a neutral grip.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Drive your elbow in an arcing motion backward, upward, and moderately out away from your side to row the dumbbell. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade backward.
    1. Aim to flare your elbow about 45 degrees out away from your side as your elbow moves equal parts backward and out to the side. Zero degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbow purely backward and keeping it completely tucked by your side, while 90 degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbow straight out to the side, which would result in your upper arm and torso forming half of a “T-shape” at the top.
    2. When allowing your elbow to bend naturally, your forearm should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    3. At the top of the row, your elbow should be close to level (with respect to height above the ground) with your torso when viewed from the side or front.
    4. Keep your torso in a mostly fixed position with your chest facing a similar direction throughout this movement. A mild degree of torso rotation is not problematic, but avoid letting it become excessive and turning the exercise into a cheat row.
    5. While performing this arcing motion, the dumbbell should not move straight upward in a vertical path.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled arcing motion until your arm is directed down toward the ground with your elbow straightened.
    1. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blade forward, which will stretch your upper back muscles and result in your working side’s shoulder being mildly closer to the ground than your other side’s shoulder at the bottom of the row.
    2. Move your elbow diagonally inward and forward at a near 45-degree angle while it descends to reverse the moderate elbow flare you just utilized during the lifting phase.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight rapidly pull your arm down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on a flat exercise bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your upper thighs near your abdomen, with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Position yourself so that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    2. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
    3. To move heavy dumbbells efficiently into the proper position, you can initially situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up. Then you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
2. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
4. Flare your elbows moderately out to your sides such that your upper arms form approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    1. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable for your shoulders, you can decide specifically how much you flare your elbows out within this range.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. One at a time, lift your feet off the ground and straighten your knees with your legs close together.
    1. While your thighs are on the bench, your calves may or may not be resting on the bench as well, depending on your height and the length of your bench.
3. Press the dumbbells up by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner as you progressively straighten them until your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your forearms in a vertical position throughout the press, as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, keep your feet off the floor while keeping your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench.
    3. At the end of the lifting phase, the dumbbells should be situated vertically over your shoulders.
4. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner toward the sides of your chest through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Let your elbows moderately flare out to your sides as they descend and bend enough to keep your wrists stacked above them with vertical forearms. Lifters often find that at least a mild degree of elbow tuck makes this exercise feel most comfortable for the shoulders compared to fully flaring the elbows straight out to the sides.
    2. How far you can lower the dumbbells with vertical forearms will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility, but most people have the mobility to descend until their hands are at least level with their chest.
    3. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    4. There is no need to lower the dumbbells extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
5. Alternate between steps 3-4 until you have completed your set.
6. Following the lowering phase of your last rep, bend your knees to return your feet to the ground.
7. Bring your upper arms in line with your sides to tuck your elbows and position the dumbbells closer to your thighs.
8. March your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
While not loading your triceps as effectively as a barbell bench press, this exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delts.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of full-diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates or any bumper plates) will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. If you only use smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize 45lb (20kg) plates, you can perform a block pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with full diameter plates.
2. Stand with the middle of your feet under the bar, and spread your legs apart into a wide stance that is clearly outside shoulder width with your toes moderately rotated out away from your sides.
    1. Provided that your feet are wider apart than your shoulders but closer together than the plates, you can use a range of stance widths for your sumo deadlift.
    2. The ideal stance width can meaningfully vary among different individuals based on someone’s body structure and hip mobility.
    3. Trying out different foot positions will normally be required to determine which stance specifically feels best to you.
    4. As a starting point, you can widen your feet as far apart as you feel comfortable and stable while being able to push your knees out approximately over your heels, resulting in close to vertical shins. You will push your knees out when descending to reach the bar, so subsequent aspects of the setup can help you gauge if you should adjust your stance.
    5. The ideal amount of toe flare also varies among different lifters, but you can start with your toes pointing about 30-45 degrees out to the side before adjusting from there based on what feels best to you. Zero degrees of toe flare would mean your toes are pointing straight forward, while 90 degrees would mean your toes are pointing directly out to your side.
3. Direct your arms straight down toward the bar with your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up).
    1. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and help engage your back muscles.
## Exercise technique
1. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips while bending your knees and pushing your knees out until your hands are level with the bar. Rather than rounding your back, keep your lower back flat and chest up in a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Strive to keep your shins vertical as your knees essentially do not track forward (although a very slight degree of forward knee tracking can be used if that feels best to you).
    2. The proper balance of bending at your hips versus your knees should allow your shoulders to be stacked vertically above the bar and your hips to be higher than your knees at the bottom.
    3. Try to push your knees far enough out to the sides that they are vertically stacked over your heels.
    4. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
2. With your elbows fully straightened and the bar directly in front of your shins, firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can naturally use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish to do so.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
4. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. This can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
    3. Keep your soles fully in contact with the ground throughout your set.
5. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” by extending (straightening) your hips and knees while pushing your knees out. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, the bar close to your legs, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As your hips and chest rise at the same rate, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull. Avoid letting your torso incline further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
6. After the bar reaches near your knees, focus on thrusting your hips forward until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
7. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips and bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and the bar close to your legs while driving your knees out with vertical shins.
    2. You can initially focus on sitting your hips back while allowing your knees to bend naturally. After the bar has cleared your knees, you can further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
8. Once the weight is fully resting on the ground, release the bar and stand upright to reset between reps.
9. Alternate between steps 1-8 until you have completed your set.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the ground.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can meaningfully train your gluteus maximus,  adductor magnus, and quads.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of full-diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates or any bumper plates) will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. If you only use smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize 45lb (20kg) plates, you can perform a block pull or rack pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with full diameter plates.
2. While facing the center of the bar, stand in an approximately hip-width stance with the middle of your feet under the bar and your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly out to the sides.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
    2. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    3. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
## Exercise technique
1. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from hinging at your hips than bending your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have fully sat back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    4. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and help engage your back muscles.
2. With your elbows fully straightened, firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can naturally use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish to do so.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
4. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
5. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, the bar close to your legs, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
6. After the bar reaches near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
7. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. Just like when lifting the bar, keep your lower back flat and chest up as you descend while actively avoiding letting your lower back round or chest collapse.
    2. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees. Then you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
8. Once the weight is fully resting on the ground, release the bar and stand upright to reset between reps.
9. Alternate between steps 1-8 until you have completed your set.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the ground.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can meaningfully train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the Roman chair’s pad to a level that positions the top of your hip bones somewhat above the top of the pads when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. This position will allow you to move freely about your hips in contrast to if the pad is contacting the top of your hip bones, which would restrict hip motion and prompt you to perform the exercise via spinal motion.
    2. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones (rather than only somewhat lower), some individuals may feel knee discomfort, so a sweet spot can exist for pad placement.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position after initially completing step 2.
2. While placing your hands onto the handles for balance support, step onto the foot plate one leg at a time with the back of your heels in contact with the back of the foot plate, so that the entirety of your soles are in contact with the foot plate.
    1. Position your feet symmetrically in a stance that feels comfortable to you, which can involve pointing your toes somewhat outward or pointing them fairly straight forward.
    2. Situating your feet approximately hip width to shoulder width apart from each other will result in a viable stance width, but how wide of a stance you can use will be influenced by the width of your Roman chair’s foot plate.
    3. Your knees should be straight after you have finished setting your feet on the foot plate.
3. Check to make sure that the pad is positioned as described by step 1 and readjust the pad height if it is not prior to repeating step 2.
4. With your elbows straightened, lift your arms up fully overhead to form a “Y-shape” with your torso and arms.
    1. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
    2. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can point your palms in toward each other or forward.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze your glutes to keep your torso up such that you can draw approximately a straight line from your legs through your hips and to your shoulders as opposed to having your torso bent over.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your lower back and neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. This is the starting position of the exercise, where your body should look like it does when normally standing upright with your arms raised except at an incline.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
3. Hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner.
    1. Rather than simply bending over by rounding your lower back, nearly all the motion should occur from your torso moving as one unit about your hips, like an analog clock’s hand rotating about its center point.
    2. Maintain a straight knee position, but you should avoid intentionally locking your knees out by squeezing your quads.
    3. While hinging at your hips and keeping your arms raised, you should feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings that progressively intensifies.
    4. Most people will be able to descend until their shoulders are lower than their hips, but how deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    5. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch, you have finished descending through your available hip range of motion.
4. Reverse the movement by lifting your torso up through your hips until you have returned to the starting position, where your torso is aligned with your legs.
    1. You can finish the lifting phase by fully squeezing your glutes, which helps cue the intended hip movement.
    2. Avoid arching your lower back excessively at the top, since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the hip hinge you just performed during the lowering phase.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the preacher bench’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbow and most of your triceps (on the back of your upper arm) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The appropriate seat height should allow the exercise to feel stable and comfortable, and it will also typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits once you are seated.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. Slide a fat grip attachment onto a dumbbell’s handle, and pick it up on the side you wish to train. Hold the dumbbell firmly with your palm facing in toward your side (resulting in a neutral grip that is also called a hammer grip).
    1. Maintain this neutral grip and avoid rotating your palm as you finish setting up and perform the exercise.
    2. Unless stated otherwise, the directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell.
3. Straddle the seat with enough space between you and the pad that you will be able to curl the dumbbell upward.
4. Curl the dumbbell upward by fully bending your elbow while lifting it upward to point straight ahead.
    1. Your arm and dumbbell should now be fully above the arm pad, which will make it easier to get into the starting position.
    2. Keep your wrist straight when setting up and performing the exercise.
5. While keeping your elbow bent, step forward until your torso is very close to the pad before sitting down on the seat and resting your triceps and elbow on the pad.
    1. You can use your free hand to help guide your other arm into the starting position if you wish to do so.
    2. Once you are in position, you can hold the arm pad with your free hand for additional stability throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow and triceps in contact with the arm pad, lower the dumbbell in a slow and controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbow.
    1. Maintain a tight grip on the dumbbell throughout the entirety of this exercise.
    2. While your forearm arcs forward and downward through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary with your palm facing inward throughout your set.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbell down.
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbow to curl the dumbbell upward and back toward you in an arcing motion until your elbow is fully bent.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow and triceps snugly against the arm pad.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the dumbbell past the point when your forearm is completely vertical, you can finish curling once your forearm is fully vertical (rather than once your elbow is fully bent), since the target muscles will not be resisted beyond this point.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other arm.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep with your elbow fully bent, stand upright to lift your arm off the pad, and step backward before lowering the dumbbell.
    2. If you become too fatigued to finish a rep during your set, you can use your free hand to assist with lifting it up and dismounting the preacher bench.
## Description
While dynamically training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this exercise can statically train your forearm’s extrinsic finger flexors and your hand’s intrinsic finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Anchor a long looped elastic band of your desired tension level around the center of a pull-up bar.
    1. To anchor the band, initially drape it over the bar, loop one end through the middle of the other, and pull that end until it is taut.
    2. If the bar is out of reach when standing on the ground, initially position yourself on an aerobic step, sturdy box, or bumper plate to bring the bar within reach. Then set the band and grasp the bar during the next step. Position the step slightly behind the bar rather than directly underneath it, so you can keep your legs straight during the exercise without contacting the step.
    3. If your bar has rough knurling rather than a smooth surface, anchoring the band directly around the bar can increase the risk of the band tearing over time. Initially placing a fat grip attachment or hand towel around a bar before anchoring the band can help stop a bar with knurling from tearing a band.
    4. Before using it, inspect the band to confirm there are no small tears, as a small tear can quickly become a full tear when stretching the band.
2. Stand under the bar, and pull the band down with both arms.
    1. Each side of the band should be flat without any twists.
3. March up one leg and step into the band with the middle of your foot snugly against the bottom end of the band’s loop before lowering your foot while straightening your leg. Alternatively, you can bend one knee and loop the band around your upper shin.
    1. Since the band’s tension increases as you stretch it, situating the band around your foot with a straight knee will provide greater assistance compared to if you situate the band around your upper shin with your knee bent.
    2. If your pull-up bar is not tall enough to keep your foot from contacting the ground at the bottom of the rep while keeping your leg straight, looping the band around your upper shin will be a better option. Otherwise, you can select whichever approach aligns with your preference while providing your desired level of assistance.
    3. With either setup, the band should feel secure rather than feeling like it may slide around during the exercise.
4. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing backward (i.e., an underhand grip) about shoulder-width apart.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
5. Hang on the bar with your elbows straightened. If you are standing on a step and have the band under your foot, step forward off the step and allow both legs to hang straight down toward the ground. If you have the band around your upper shin, lift your other foot off the ground by bending that knee and keeping both knees bent throughout the exercise.
    1. You can optionally situate your other leg (that was not initially contacting the band) within the band if you wish to do so.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to assume a “proud chest” that is angled slightly upward.
    1. Maintain this “proud chest” posture throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and back toward your sides while progressively bending your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows naturally will not actually drive downward, but having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows being bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, individuals may have different ranges of motion.
    3. Rather than swinging your legs, statically keep them in a similar position throughout the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms fully overhead.
    1. You do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, you can return one foot onto the ground or step, shift your weight into step/ground, march your other leg up and out of the band, and release the bar.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Slide a pair of fat grip attachments onto two dumbbells, and firmly grasp them.
2. In an approximately hip width stance, stand upright and hold the dumbbells by your sides with straight elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides (resulting in a neutral grip that is also called a hammer grip).
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the dumbbells upward in an arcing manner by bending your elbows while keeping palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Keep your upper arms fixed by your sides rather than allowing them to swing forward through shoulder motion.
    2. While your forearms move through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    3. Maintain a tight grip on the dumbbells with straight wrists throughout the entirety of this exercise.
    4. At the top of the curl, your elbows should be fully bent with your forearms touching your biceps.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While dynamically training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this exercise can statically train your forearm’s extrinsic finger flexors and your hand’s intrinsic finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Slide a pair of fat grip attachments onto two dumbbells, and pick them up while firmly grasping them.
2. Sit down on a bench with your torso upright and feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells by your sides with straight elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides (resulting in a neutral grip that is also called a hammer grip).
    1. You can opt to sit down on a seated bench with your back resting against the back support, or you can sit on a flat bench without any back support.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the dumbbells upward in an arcing manner by bending your elbows while turning your palms to face up and then back toward you at the top.
    1. Keep your upper arms fixed by your sides rather than allowing them to swing forward through shoulder motion.
    2. While your forearms move through elbow joint motion and your palms rotate, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    3. Maintain a tight grip on the dumbbells with straight wrists throughout the entirety of this exercise.
    4. At the top of the curl, your elbows should be fully bent with your forearms touching your biceps as you are holding the dumbbells with an underhand grip rather than the neutral grip you used to start.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner and turning your palms inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows straightened and your palms facing in toward your sides.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While dynamically training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this exercise can statically train your forearm’s extrinsic finger flexors and your hand’s intrinsic finger flexors.## Setting up
