2. Sit down on a bench with your torso upright and feet flat on the floor while holding the dumbbells by your sides with straight elbows as your palms are facing in toward your sides (resulting in a neutral grip that is also called a hammer grip).
    1. You can opt to sit down on a seated bench with your back resting against the back support, or you can sit on a flat bench without any back support.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the dumbbells upward in an arcing manner by bending your elbows while keeping palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Keep your upper arms fixed by your sides rather than allowing them to swing forward through shoulder motion.
    2. While your forearms move through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    3. Maintain a tight grip on the dumbbells with straight wrists throughout the entirety of this exercise.
    4. At the top of the curl, your elbows should be fully bent with your forearms touching your biceps.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While dynamically training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this exercise can statically train your forearm’s extrinsic finger flexors and your hand’s intrinsic finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Adjust the preacher bench’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and most of your triceps (on the back of your upper arms) on the sloped arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The appropriate seat height should allow the exercise to feel stable and comfortable, and it will also typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits once you are seated.
    2. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. Slide a pair of fat grip attachments onto two dumbbells, and pick them up. Hold them firmly with your palms facing in toward each other (resulting in a neutral grip that is also called a hammer grip).
    1. Maintain this neutral grip and avoid rotating your palms as you finish setting up and perform the exercise.
3. Straddle the seat with enough space between you and the pad that you will be able to curl the dumbbells upward.
4. Curl the dumbbells upward by fully bending your elbows while lifting them upward to point straight ahead.
    1. Your arms and dumbbells should now be fully above the arm pad, which will make it easier to get into the starting position.
5. While keeping your elbows bent, step forward until your torso is very close to the pad before sitting down on the seat and resting your triceps and elbows on the pad.
    1. In the starting position and throughout the exercise, your wrists should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows and triceps in contact with the arm pad, lower the dumbbells in a slow and controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Maintain a tight grip on the dumbbells throughout the entirety of this exercise.
    2. While your forearms arc forward and downward through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary with your palms facing in toward each other throughout your set.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
2. Reverse the movement by bending your elbows to curl the dumbbells upward and back toward you in an arcing motion until your elbows are fully bent.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and triceps snugly against the arm pad.
    2. If your preacher bench allows you to curl the dumbbells past the point when your forearms are completely vertical, you can finish curling once your forearms are fully vertical (rather than once your elbows are fully bent), since the target muscles will not be resisted beyond this point.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have finished your last rep with your elbows fully bent, stand upright to lift your arms off the pad, and step backward before lowering the dumbbells.
## Description
While dynamically training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this exercise can statically train your forearm’s extrinsic finger flexors and your hand’s intrinsic finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Stand with the side of your shoulder (that you will not train during this set) facing the cable.
3. Squat down, and reach across your body to grasp the handle with your arm on the side opposite the cable.
4. After standing upright, use your free hand to grasp a stable part of the cable station.
    1. Throughout the exercise, continue using this arm to help stabilize yourself.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm holding the handle.
5. Take a step forward with your foot that is further away from the pulley. Allow the cable’s resistance to pull your arm across your body and in toward the pulley with your elbow straightened.
    1. You should feel tension from the cable and will likely feel a stretching sensation on the side of your shoulder.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow straight, sweep your arm in a smooth arcing motion diagonally upward and outward away from the pulley.
    1. After sweeping your arm as if you were drawing a sword, you should finish with your arm angled out to the side and mildly upward.
    2. In contrast to a typical lateral raise, your arm should initially arc forward in front of you as it moves across your body. This will result in the movement functioning like a hybrid between a lateral raise and a front raise, with it involving a greater proportion of lateral raise motion than front raise motion.
    3. While you raise your arm through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blade to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary. This includes keeping your chest and imaginary belt buckle directed straight ahead throughout your set rather than rotating either one to the side.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your arm in toward the pulley and across your body in a controlled arcing manner through your full range of motion.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt, front delt, and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your upper trap.## Setting up
1. Adjust each pulley from a dual cable station to near its lowest height, and select your desired weight with the loading pin of each stack.
    1. A dual cable station that can situate the two pulleys no further apart than your wingspan is ideal to use for this variation.
    2. A dual cable station that has its pulleys rather far apart from each other (often with a long upper beam connecting each end of the station) is not as practical to use. With this type of equipment, the single arm cross-body cable Y-raise may be preferable.
2. Securely attach an ankle cuff just above each wrist. Position each cuff so that one of its attachment rings is facing the same direction as your palm.
3. Stand just in front of one pulley while facing in the direction of its weight stack.
4. Squat down, and connect the cable to the cuff on the side that is further away from the opposite pulley.
    1. For instance, if the opposite pulley is on your left side, connect your right arm’s cuff to the cable in front of you.
5. Connect the opposite cable to your other cuff, and stand upright centered in between the pulleys.
    1. Your left arm’s cuff should now be connected to the cable on your left side, while your right arm’s cuff is connected to the cable on your right side.
6. Pivot your body to face the opposite direction while stepping over one cable and allowing the cables to cross over behind your back.
    1. With your back facing in the direction of the weight stacks, your left arm’s cuff should now be connected to the cable on your right side, while your right arm’s cuff is connected to the cable on your left side.
7. Take a step forward into a staggered stance with one foot further in front than the other, and your lead knee slightly bent.
    1. Your shoulders should be mildly in front of the pulleys rather than being directly between them, resulting in the cables pulling diagonally backward but mostly inward.
    2. In this staggered stance, you should feel stable and balanced.
8. Assume a “proud chest” posture rather than slumping your shoulders forward. Allow the cables to pull your arms in toward your sides with your elbows straight and palms facing the pulleys if they are not already in this position.
    1. Due to the diagonal line of pull from the cables, your wrists should be slightly behind your shoulders in the starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. In a smooth arcing motion, sweep your arms diagonally upward, outward, and mildly forward away from the pulleys.
    1. Finish with your wrists near shoulder height as your arms are angled outward and slightly forward.
    2. While you raise your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary as you keep your elbows straight.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your arms in toward your sides and allowing them to move mildly back toward the pulleys.
    1. Strive to finish with your wrists near your buttocks as your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cables abruptly yank your arms downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt, front delt, and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your upper trap.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat to a distance away from the foot platform that allows you to leg press through a full range of motion while keeping your lower back flat and heels fully on the platform in the starting position.
    1. Finding the ideal setting for you as an individual typically takes some trial and error. The only way to know if the seat setting is appropriate is to sit down on the seat and situate your feet on the platform in the same position you will use for your set.
    2. If the seat is excessively close to the foot platform relative to your hip mobility, your lower back may have to round to place your feet on the platform while seated. Alternatively, you may not be able to place your heels firmly in contact with the platform if it is too close.
    3. You should ideally place the seat as close to the foot platform as you can without having to round your lower back or lift your heels off the platform. Placing the seat meaningfully farther back beyond this point may decrease how much muscle growth the exercise is likely to stimulate.
    4. As a general starting point, you can find the seat setting that allows your knees to be bent by about 90 degrees when your feet are on the platform. If you are able to avoid rounding your lower back or lifting your heels up with this setting, you can incrementally bring the seat closer until you find your optimal starting position.
2. Select your weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on the seat with your back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the lower half of the platform, near shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to leg press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    3. While placing your feet lower on the platform may help bias your quads during this variation, make sure that your heels are still fully in contact with the platform rather than hanging off it.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
3. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. Certain machine models will involve you pressing yourself away from a stationary foot platform rather than pressing the platform away from you. The technique remains the same regardless.
4. Reverse the movement by bending at your knees and hips in a controlled manner through your full range of motion to return to the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, back against the back rest, and knees aligned over your feet.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time. A sign of an appropriately controlled movement is having the weights you selected lightly contact the rest of the weight stack at the end range. Having the weights lightly touch down will also confirm that you have moved through your full range of motion.
    3. With the appropriate seat position, you should be able to avoid rounding your lower back or lifting your heels off the platform at the end range of this motion. If you notice your lower back starts rounding at the end or heels start lifting up, you could finish your rep and mildly move the seat backward before resuming your set.
    4. You can optionally briefly pause in the bottom position.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop three of your four quad muscles while also meaningfully training your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station with pulleys not meaningfully further apart than your wingspan, adjust each pulley to its lowest height.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you can position the arms so that their pulleys are moderately outside shoulder width apart. Specifically, you can aim for about the same distance between your elbows when your arms are raised straight out away from your sides, parallel to the ground.
    2. A dual cable station that has its pulleys situated well outside your wingspan is not appropriate for this particular variation.
2. Connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
3. Squat down with one cable station by your side, and firmly grasp its handle with a straight elbow.
4. While keeping your elbow straight, stand upright with the handle, and walk over to grasp the handle from the other cable in the same manner.
    1. You can allow the resistance from the first cable you grasped to lift your arm out to the side before doing the same with the other cable during the next step.
5. Step to the side to center yourself between the cables, and direct your palms forward.
6. Bend your elbows while allowing them to swing mildly forward to curl the handles up to your shoulders.
    1. Use momentum from your lower body as needed in a cheat curl-like manner to help lift the handles into position.
7. With your wrist vertically stacked above your elbows, turn your palms in toward each other, and assume a “proud chest” posture with your chest up.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back along with your neck, like when you are normally standing upright.
8. Flare your elbows at least moderately out to your sides to a degree that feels comfortable for your shoulders.
    1. When 0 degrees of elbow flare means that your wrists are directly in front of your shoulders with your palms facing each other, and 90 degrees of elbow flare means that your wrists are fully out to your sides with your palms facing straight forward, using about 60-75 degrees of elbow flare is commonly appropriate. In this position, your elbows will point more out to the side than forward, but they will not be fully flared out.
    2. If your shoulders feel comfortable when pressing with a greater degree of elbow flare, you can certainly flare your elbows beyond that point, but some people find that utilizing a closer to moderate degree of elbow flare feels most comfortable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles upward by driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner until you are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. While your elbows progressively straighten, your forearms should be in a vertical position throughout the press as your wrists and handles remain stacked over your elbows.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your knees and hips straightened as you avoid using your legs to help lift the weights or leaning your torso back via lower back arching.
    3. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner as you allow your elbows to move outward and downward while bending.
    1. Continue keeping your wrist stacked over your elbows to maintain a vertical forearm position.
    2. Strive to lower the handles as far as you comfortably can while keeping your forearms vertical, which should result in your elbows bending by greater than 90 degrees.
    3. There is no need to perform this lowering phase extremely slowly, but you should actively resist the cables rather than letting them abruptly yank your arms down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delts while also training your lateral delts, supraspinatus, traps, and serratus anterior.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat to a distance away from the foot platform that allows you to leg press through a full range of motion while keeping your lower back flat and heels fully on the platform in the starting position.
    1. Finding the ideal setting for you as an individual typically takes some trial and error. The only way to know if the seat setting is appropriate is to sit down on the seat and situate your feet on the platform in the same position you will use for your set.
    2. If the seat is excessively close to the foot platform relative to your hip mobility, your lower back may have to round to place your feet on the platform while seated. Alternatively, you may not be able to place your heels firmly in contact with the platform if it is too close.
    3. You should ideally place the seat as close to the foot platform as you can without having to round your lower back or lift your heels off the platform. Placing the seat meaningfully farther back beyond this point may decrease how much muscle growth the exercise is likely to stimulate.
    4. As a general starting point, you can find the seat setting that allows your knees to be bent by about 90 degrees when your feet are on the platform. If you are able to avoid rounding your lower back or lifting your heels up with this setting, you can incrementally bring the seat closer until you find your optimal starting position.
2. Select your weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. Sit down on the seat with your back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the upper half of the foot platform, mildly outside shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing mildly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to leg press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    3. While placing your feet higher on the platform may help bias your glutes during this variation, make sure that your toes are still fully in contact with the platform rather than hanging above it.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
3. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. You can emphasize pushing through your heels while still keeping the entirety of your soles (including the middle of your feet and the balls of your feet) firmly in contact with the platform.
    3. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. Certain machine models will involve you pressing yourself away from a stationary foot platform rather than pressing the platform away from you. The technique remains the same regardless.
4. Reverse the movement by bending at your knees and hips in a controlled manner through your full range of motion to return to the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, back against the back rest, and knees aligned over your feet.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time. A sign of an appropriately controlled movement is having the weights you selected lightly contact the rest of the weight stack at the end range. Having the weights lightly touch down will also confirm that you have moved through your full range of motion.
    3. With the appropriate seat position, you should be able to avoid rounding your lower back or lifting your heels off the platform at the end range of this motion. If you notice your lower back starts rounding at the end or heels start lifting up, you could finish your rep and mildly move the seat backward before resuming your set.
    4. You can optionally briefly pause in the bottom position.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus while also meaningfully training three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, position each pulley near hip height.
    1. This variation will feel the most natural when using a dual cable station that can situate the two pulleys no further apart than your wingspan.
    2. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you can position the arms straight forward, parallel to each other.
    3. With a dual cable station that has its pulleys rather far apart from each other (often with a long upper beam connecting each end of the station), it may be more practical to use the single arm variation of this exercise.
2. Connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
3. While standing with the sides of your shoulders facing the two pulleys, firmly grasp one of the handles before side-stepping to then grasp the other handle.
4. Center yourself between the cables before walking forward with your palms facing forward and wrists straight. Allow the resistance from the cables to pull your arms backward with straight elbows.
    1. You can stop walking forward once you feel that your arms are angled about 45 degrees behind your torso, or once you have reached the end range to which your shoulder mobility comfortably permits your arms to pull backward.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders and/or biceps with your arms pulled backward, but you should not experience shoulder joint discomfort when you have appropriately walked forward.
5. While mildly bending your knees, adopt a staggered stance with one foot in front of the other as your feet are approximately hip width apart from each other.
    1. In this position, you should feel stable and balanced.
6. As you are standing with an upright torso, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to curl the handles forward and upward in an arcing manner. Keep your upper arms stationary as you avoid swinging them forward through shoulder motion.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully bent as your forearms contact your biceps.
    2. While you move your forearms through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set, which will result in your elbows remaining behind your torso.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing the handles to arc downward and back toward the pulleys as your elbows straighten.
    1. Finish the lowering phase with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cables abruptly yank the handles back in an uncontrolled manner.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, walk backward until the cables are no longer tensed before releasing the handles.
## Description
While also meaningfully developing your brachialis and brachioradialis, this exercise can effectively train biceps from a notably lengthened position.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each pulley’s height to be mildly above the top of your head, connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you can position the arms to a setting that places the pulleys about as far apart as your wingspan.
2. While standing to the side of one cable, grasp its handle, and then step over to grasp the handle from the other cable with your palms facing forward and your elbows slightly bent.
    1. Keep your elbows slightly bent throughout the exercise.
3. Center yourself between the pulleys, and take a step forward with one leg to adopt a staggered stance.
4. Lean your torso forward closer to horizontal by hinging at your front leg’s hip and bending your front leg’s knee while keeping your rear knee straight. Allow the cables to spread your arms apart while pulling them up and back through a range that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Strive to lean forward until the cables form approximately 90-degree angles with your torso when viewed from the side. This will result in your shoulders being in essentially the same position as if you positioned the pulleys to shoulder height and set up while standing fully upright.
    2. Avoid rounding your back as you lean forward through hip motion while keeping your back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. In this position, you should feel resistance from the cables with your arms fully spread apart. If you currently do not, you should take a step forward so that the cables can exert resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that you will maintain throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep the handles in toward each other by arcing them diagonally forward and downward while keeping your elbows mildly bent.
    1. You should finish with your hands about to touch each other as your arms form approximately 90-degree angles with your torso when viewed from the side.
    2. The wide arcing motion should resemble hugging a thick barrel, as all the motion comes from your shoulders while you keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the set.
    3. Keep your chest up as you avoid hunching over with your shoulders slouched forward.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your hands to spread apart from each other in a controlled manner as they move diagonally backward and upward in line with the resistance from the cables.
    1. At the end, your arms should be fully spread apart through the full range your shoulder mobility comfortably permits, which normally produces a stretching sensation in the chest and shoulders.
    2. Aim to maintain the same 90-degree angles between the cables and your torso when viewed from the side, which should naturally occur when following the direction of resistance from the cables.
    3. Avoid bending your elbows any further and turning this exercise into a chest press.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley near shoulder height or slightly higher, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Stand with the side of your body that you will not train during this set facing the cable station.
3. Reach across your body (with your hand further away from the pulley), and grasp the handle with your palm facing backward. To assist with getting into position, you can use your other hand to grasp the ball of the cable next to the pulley.
4. Take a step out to the side, and lunge backward into a half-kneeling position with your knee further away from the pulley resting on the ground.
    1. Keep your torso upright with the center of your chest and your imaginary belt buckle facing straight ahead in the same direction.
    2. Allow the cable’s resistance to keep your arm (that is holding the handle) pulled across your body.
    3. Your knee on the same side as the pulley should now be in front of you, while your other knee is under your torso.
5. While holding the handle with your working side’s hand, let go of the cable’s ball with your other hand. Brace that free hand against your lower thigh for additional stability.
    1. Continue bracing that hand against your thigh throughout the exercise to help avoid twisting your chest toward the pulley.
    2. All the subsequent instructions will refer to the arm whose hand is holding the cable.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout the set.
2. Drive your elbow diagonally outward and down toward your side while keeping your forearm aligned with the cable and palm facing backward.
    1. You should finish with your elbow bent by your side, as the handle is in front of your torso.
    2. Continue keeping the center of your chest and imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead throughout the set, as you avoid twisting your chest to either side.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing the cable to pull your elbow diagonally inward and upward across your body.
    1. While letting your elbow straighten, strive to move through your shoulder’s full range of motion with your arm fully across your body, angled toward the pulley.
    2. At the end of this step, it is common to feel a stretching sensation in your back muscles with your elbow in front of your breastbone or slightly further across your body.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the cable yank your arm across your body in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side while facing the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop train your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Lie down with your back on the ground, knees straight out in front of you, and your arms straight by your sides.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can lie down on an exercise mat.
2. Lift your arms slightly off the ground, pointing forward, and keep them pointing forward throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. As you tighten your abdominal muscles, curl your back to lift it off the ground while keeping the backs of your hip bones and legs on the ground.
    1. To train your abdominal muscles dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain flat (which could happen if you predominantly lift your torso through your hip flexors). With the appropriate focus on curling your back, you should feel meaningful tension in your abs rather than in your hip flexors on the front of your upper thighs.
    2. As a cue, you can think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the top of your hip bones, which is the action that contraction of your abdominal muscles can produce.
    3. You have reached the end of the lifting phase once you feel that you have fully curled your back and crunched the bottom of your ribs down toward the top of your hips.
    4. In contrast to a sit-up, your shoulders should remain clearly behind your hips at the top of this lift, since you should exclusively be moving through back motion without any hip motion.
    5. Based on what feels most comfortable to you, you can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest at the start of the lifting phase, or you can keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
2. Reverse the movement by uncurling your back in a controlled manner as you lower your back to the ground.
    1. When transitioning from having a rounded back to a straighter back, your ribs should flare away from your hip bones.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should avoid dropping onto the ground in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis, which is assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Place a pair of dumbbells on the floor wide enough apart that you can lie down between them and grasp their handles with your arms by your sides.
    1. You can optionally situate an exercise mat on the ground where you will lie for comfort.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground, your arms near your sides, and your palms under the dumbbells’ handles as they are facing up.
3. Bend your knees and situate the soles of your feet flat on the floor in a position where you feel comfortable and stable.
4. Firmly grasp the dumbbells and curl them up by bending your elbows until your forearms are vertical.
5. Direct your palms toward each other to use a neutral grip.
6. In a bench pressing motion, press the dumbbells upward until you have straightened your elbows with your arms directed up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a bench press rep), the dumbbells should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. Maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the dumbbells in a controlled arcing manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Based on what feels most natural and comfortable, you can keep your elbows tucked in, or you can allow your elbows to flare out to your sides slightly.
    2. Avoid letting your elbows drift back and down with the dumbbells through shoulder motion. When viewed from the side, the proper elbow position should result in your upper arms remaining in a vertical position.
    3. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
    4. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the dumbbells the entire way.
2. Based on your preference, finish lowering the dumbbells once they are just barely above the floor or once they gently tap the floor.
    1. You can optionally briefly pause in this bottom position if you wish to do so.
    2. If you decide to tap the dumbbells to the ground (which can help standardize your range of motion each rep), emphasize a very light contact as you avoid bouncing the dumbbells off the floor.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the dumbbells into the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your upper arms in a vertical position as you avoid pushing your elbows forward (toward your chest) through shoulder motion.
    2. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the dumbbells are vertically above your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, lower your elbows and triceps to the floor (like you would during a neutral grip dumbbell floor press) before returning the dumbbells to the floor by straightening your elbows (like during the lowering phase of a biceps curl).
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. If your machine permits you to adjust it, set the seat to the most reclined position that allows your back to be well-supported by the backrest when seated with your feet on the foot platform.
    1. Starting with the most reclined position (which tilts the seat down in a closer to horizontal position) generally works well for most people.
    2. Compared to using a closer to upright seat angle, using an appropriately reclined position will allow you to leg press through a longer range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    3. The most reclined setting on certain machine models may be excessively reclined to the extent that the backrest may no longer be able to stabilize your body when leg pressing. If this occurs, your buttocks may lift off the seat, and your torso may slide back along the backrest due to the backrest being angled too close to a horizontal position. However, most leg press machines will not have an excessively reclined seat setting available.
    4. To determine if the seat angle you initially selected is appropriate for you, you can perform a couple of reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section without adding any weight plates to the leg press. If you feel stable and well-supported by the backrest, the seat is not excessively reclined, and you have selected a viable seat angle.
    5. Note that not all machine models permit you to adjust the seat angle.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. Note that the sled will provide resistance even without any additional weight plates. The weight of the sled will vary depending on the specific model, but it commonly weighs near 120lb (54kg).
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck in a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the lower half of the foot platform, near shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to leg press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    3. While placing your feet lower on the platform may help bias your quads during this variation, make sure that your heels are still fully in contact with the platform rather than hanging off it.
    4. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to allow you to unlock the sled and begin your set. If your legs are too short to achieve this position with a leg press that has multiple safety stopper heights, you can ask a taller lifter to reposition the platform to a lower starting height that you can reach. If you are using a machine that does not permit a lower starting height of the sled, that particular machine may not be feasible for your body type.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handles with your hands to move the safety stoppers out of the sled’s way.
    1. The specific location of the safety handles and how to unlock them can vary among different machine models. However, the safety handles are often near where your hands will be when resting by your sides, and rotating the handles outward will commonly disengage the safety stoppers.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handles) and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Lower the sled toward you in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform.
    2. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue lowering the sled through the full range of motion you can while keeping your buttocks firmly on the seat, lower back flat against the back rest, and heels fully on the platform.
    1. What constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and will be influenced by your individual ankle and hip mobility.
    2. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track over your toes, lowering the sled any further would cause your heels to start lifting off the platform.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your hips can bend, lowering the sled any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your buttocks starting to curl up off the seat.
    4. You can optionally briefly pause in the bottom position.
7. Drive your feet into the platform to push the sled away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, back against the back rest, and knees aligned over your feet.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set.
9. After your knees are straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, lock the safety handles with your hands to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the sled.
10. Lower the sled onto the safety stoppers by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop three of your four quad muscles while also meaningfully training your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. If your machine permits you to adjust it, set the seat to the most reclined position that allows your back to be well-supported by the backrest when seated with your feet on the foot platform.
    1. Starting with the most reclined position (which tilts the seat down in a closer to horizontal position) generally works well for most people.
    2. Compared to using a closer to upright seat angle, using an appropriately reclined position will allow you to leg press through a longer range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    3. The most reclined setting on certain machine models may be excessively reclined to the extent that the backrest may no longer be able to stabilize your body when leg pressing. If this occurs, your buttocks may lift off the seat, and your torso may slide back along the backrest due to the backrest being angled too close to a horizontal position. However, most leg press machines will not have an excessively reclined seat setting available.
    4. To determine if the seat angle you initially selected is appropriate for you, you can perform a couple of reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section without adding any weight plates to the leg press. If you feel stable and well-supported by the backrest, the seat is not excessively reclined, and you have selected a viable seat angle.
    5. Note that not all machine models permit you to adjust the seat angle.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. Note that the sled will provide resistance even without any additional weight plates. The weight of the sled will vary depending on the specific model, but it commonly weighs near 120lb (54kg).
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position ,like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the upper half of the foot platform, mildly outside shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing mildly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to leg press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    3. While placing your feet higher on the platform may help bias your glutes during this variation, make sure that your toes are still fully in contact with the platform rather than hanging above it.
    4. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to allow you to unlock the sled and begin your set. If your legs are too short to achieve this position with a leg press that has multiple safety stopper heights, you can ask a taller lifter to reposition the platform to a lower starting height that you can reach. If you are using a machine that does not permit a lower starting height of the sled, that particular machine may not be feasible for your body type for this specific variation, but situating your feet lower on the platform potentially may allow you to still use the leg press.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handles with your hands to move the safety stoppers out of the sled’s way.
    1. The specific location of the safety handles and how to unlock them can vary among different machine models. However, the safety handles are often near where your hands will be when resting by your sides, and rotating the handles outward will commonly disengage the safety stoppers.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handles) and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Lower the sled toward you in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform.
    2. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue lowering the sled through the full range of motion you can while keeping your buttocks firmly on the seat, lower back flat against the back rest, and heels fully on the platform.
    1. What constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and will be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility.
    2. If you reached the end range to which your hips can bend, lowering the sled any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your buttocks starting to curl up off the seat, which can more easily occur when situating your feet high on the platform for this glute-biased variation.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track over your toes (which is uncommon for this glute-biased variation), lowering the sled any further would cause your heels to start lifting off the platform.
    4. You can optionally briefly pause in the bottom position.
7. Drive your feet into the platform to push the sled away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Focus on especially pushing through your heels while still keeping the entirety of each sole fully in contact with the platform.
    3. Continue keeping your buttocks on the seat, back against the back rest, and knees aligned over your feet.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set.
9. After your knees are straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, lock the safety handles with your hands to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the sled.
10. Lower the sled onto the safety stoppers by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus while also meaningfully training three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. On the side of your body you are about to train, pick up one dumbbell and hold it by your side with your elbow straightened.
    1. Continue holding the dumbbell by your side throughout the exercise.
2. Approach a step or a sturdy object that can function as a step. You should be by a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance.
    1. Based on the equipment at your disposal, a variety of objects can serve as your step. Make sure the object can support your weight, will not move once you are on it, and allows you to lower your heel through the full range of motion your ankle mobility permits.
    2. As an alternative to a staircase’s bottom step, you may select a power rack’s bottom beam if the beam is sufficiently tall.
    3. Additionally, a calf raise block, aerobic step, deadlift block, or thick bumper plate can each be positioned near a wall or power rack for stability. Depending on your bodyweight, anchoring an aerobic step with weight on top of it may be required to prevent the step from tipping.
3. Place your free hand onto the nearby surface for balance assistance, and maintain this hand contact throughout the set.
    1. If you are using a surface that you can grasp (like a power rack) rather than a wall, do not grip it tightly.
4. Step onto the step with the balls of your feet on the step while the back halves of your feet are hanging off the step.
5. While keeping your foot on the same side as the dumbbell on the step, lift your other foot off the step by bending that knee.
    1. Keep that foot off the step throughout the exercise.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the leg in contact with the step.
6. Slightly bend your knee while allowing your heel to fully lower below the ball of your foot.
## Exercise technique
1. While simultaneously straightening your knee quickly, forcefully push through the ball of your foot to raise your heel upward as you keep the ball of your foot planted on the step.
    1. Once you have lifted your heel as high upward as your ankle mobility permits with your knee fully straightened and torso upright, you have finished the lifting phase. There is no need to pause at the top.
    2. As you use your calf to perform most of the work with some assistance from your quad, this quick movement should resemble a jump rope motion, but without the ball of your foot leaving the step.
    3. Only use your free hand for balance assistance as you avoid pushing down in a manner that can help you lift yourself upward.
2. In a controlled manner, allow your heel to descend toward the ground through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits while slightly bending your knee.
    1. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can below the ball of your foot to stretch your calf and train it the most effectively.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full range of motion.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid rapidly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep. Only the lifting phase should be quick.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat this exercise with your other side.
    1. If you ever feel that your foot is starting to slide down the step, you can return your free leg’s foot to the step for a moment to help you scoot your working leg’s foot forward onto the step before resuming your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley near its lowest height before connecting a bar with a straight (or mostly straight) body to the cable and selecting your weight with the loading pin.
    1. The pulley should be set to a height that allows you to reach back and grasp the bar once you are lying on the bench. The appropriate pulley should simultaneously allow the cable to exert resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise. The lowest pulley height or the second-to-lowest pulley height typically satisfies these criteria.
    2. Any bar that enables you to perform this exercise with a near shoulder-width overhand grip can be similarly used, including a standard straight bar, a lat pulldown bar (with a long straight body and angled ends), and a multi-grip EZ bar.
2. Position a flat exercise bench slightly in front of the pulley.
    1. The bench should be close enough to the pulley that you can grasp the bar when setting up and then be able to direct your arms straight up toward the ceiling (in the starting position of each rep) without the cable contacting the bench or your head.
3. Lie down on your back on the bench, with your head resting on the end near the cable.
    1. Place your feet flat on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent.
4. Reach back behind your head with your elbows bent, and firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width overhand grip.
    1. The overhand grip should result in your palms being angled backward in this position.
    2. If you cannot grasp the bar, you may have to mildly increase the pulley height before resuming your setup.
5. Pull the bar upward and forward by driving your elbows upward and forward until your upper arms are vertical. Simultaneously straighten your elbows so that your arms are directed straight up toward the ceiling.
    1. If the cable brushes up against the back of the bench or your head when your arms are fully vertical with your elbows stacked over your shoulders, you can keep your arms angled slightly backward. This will result in your elbows being slightly horizontally closer to the pulley than your shoulders.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the bar in a controlled arcing manner while keeping your elbows up.
    1. Based on what feels most natural and comfortable to you, you can allow your elbows to flare out to your sides to a degree, or you can keep your elbows tucked in.
    2. Avoid letting your elbows drift back and down with the bar through shoulder motion.
    3. Essentially all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbows as the center of an analog clock, while your forearms are the hands that rotate about these center elbows during this exercise.
2. Finish lowering the bar just before it reaches your forehead or until you have bent your elbows as much as you can without letting your elbows drift backward.
    1. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way, especially when the bar approaches your head.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the bar into the starting position.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is vertically above you.
    2. Continue keeping your upper arms mostly stationary as you avoid pushing your elbows forward (toward your chest) through shoulder motion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, lower the bar behind your head toward the pulley while bending your elbows until you lose tension from the cable and can set the bar down.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a position that allows your knees to be bent by about 45 degrees when your feet are on the bottom of the foot plate.
    1. The appropriate seat position should permit you to bend your knees mildly each rep, which can involve situating the seat further forward than you would for a standard leg press calf raise.
    2. If you notice that you lose resistance from the machine at the bottom of your reps during the set, you should position the seat further back to experience tension throughout the full range of motion for this variation.
2. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the back rest.
    1. Your back should be flat rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, which may occur if you do not situate your hips far enough back against the back rest.
3. With a near hip width stance, place the balls of your feet onto the bottom of the foot plate with your heels hanging off the edge.
    1. Ensure that the balls of your feet are firmly on the foot plate to the extent that they will not slide down during the exercise.
4. While grasping the hand grips, press the platform away from you by straightening your knees until they are only mildly bent.
## Exercise technique
1. While simultaneously straightening your knees quickly, forcefully push through the balls of your feet to point your feet away from you (like when stepping on a car’s gas pedal) and press the platform away.
    1. Keep the balls of your feet firmly in contact with the foot plate throughout the exercise.
    2. Once you have pointed your feet as far as your ankle mobility permits with your knees fully straightened, you have finished the lifting phase. There is no need to pause at the top.
    3. As you use your calves to perform most of the work with some assistance from your quads, this quick movement should resemble a jump rope motion but without the ball of your foot leaving the platform.
    4. Aiming to fully straighten your knees does not mean hyperextending your knees, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. If you have the mobility to hyperextend your knees, there is no need to forcefully lock your knees into a hyperextended position, since straightening them is appropriate.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner, by allowing your feet to point back closer toward you through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits while mildly bending your knees.
    1. While ensuring that they remain firmly on the foot plate, strive to bring the balls of your feet fully back toward your shins to stretch your calves and train them most effectively.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calves or ankles at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full ankle range of motion.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid rapidly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep. Only the lifting phase should be quick.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the platform to its initial position by further bending your knees until the weights you selected are resting on the rest of the weight stack.
    1. If you have rather flexible calves and high ankle mobility, you may feel most stable when bringing the platform back if you do so when your ankles are near a neutral position (like near the mid-range of each rep) rather than when your feet are bent fully back toward you (like at the bottom of each rep).
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to be above head height, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. So long as the pulley is set to a height that allows the cable to move diagonally downward during this exercise’s pulling phase, a range of pulley heights can be viably used for this variation. Feel free to try out different pulley heights to determine which one feels best to you.
2. Connect to the cable a rope attachment that is ideally a long rope attachment, or two standard rope attachments that are each fully pulled to one side of the attachment.
    1. Compared to using a single standard-length rope attachment, using a long rope attachment or two standard-length rope attachments (together replicating the length of a long rope attachment) can help you emphasize shoulder external rotation at the end of this exercise’s lifting phase. However, this exercise can still be effectively performed with a single standard-length rope attachment, so you do not need to use a long rope or a combination of two standard ropes.
    2. In addition to the attachments that are just longer versions of the standard rope attachments, long rope attachments functionally include the long strap attachments (that can be used for cable crunches) that have a pair of looped ends into which you can place your hands.
3. While facing the cable station, grasp each end of the rope with your index fingers and adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the rounded outer aspects of the stoppers.
    1. If you are using a long strap attachment with looped ends rather than ropes, you naturally will situate your hands within the loops and grasp the inner part of each loop.
4. With your elbows straightened and arms raised to be parallel with the cable, step backward until the machine’s weight plates separate and you feel tension from the cable.
    1. In this position, your palms should be directed down as your arms are outstretched in front of your torso while you are standing upright with your hands close to each other.
5. With your feet near hip width apart, slightly bend your knees, and adopt a staggered stance with one foot mildly in front of the other, or keep your feet symmetrical with each other.
    1. Utilize whichever stance helps you feel the most stable and balanced.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows diagonally backward and down toward shoulder height while flaring them out away from your sides. Simultaneously spread your hands apart as you pull the cable back toward your eyes.
    1. At the end of this motion, your elbows should be pointing straight out to your sides, and your upper arms should be approximately parallel with the ground as they form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. The end position should resemble a double biceps flexing pose with your hands pulled as far backward as your elbows, rather than your hands remaining in front of your elbows like they would during a row.
    3. Finish pulling with your shoulder blades fully pulled back together and your chest up in a “proud chest” posture.
    4. If you are using a long rope attachment or two ropes together, strive to finish with your hands vertically above your elbows with your elbows bent by about 90 degrees. With a single standard rope attachment, you will not be able to spread your hands as far apart and will finish the pull with a greater degree of elbow bend.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your hands and elbows to move diagonally forward toward the pulley as they come together.
    1. Allow your elbows to straighten and your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally as your arms move in line with the cable’s resistance.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms outstretched in front of you.
    3. You do not have to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control it the entire time rather than letting the cable abruptly pull your arms forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also meaningfully training your rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, this exercise can effectively develop your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, along with the supraspinatus to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Position a flat exercise bench centered underneath a Smith machine’s bar in a spot that will situate the bar over your chest when lying down on the bench.
    1. Specifically how you should set up can be influenced by whether your Smith machine has sliding rails that are purely vertical or angled. With either type, you want to set up in a manner that results in the bar being able to touch your lower chest at the bottom of the press.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, place the side of the bench where you will place your feet on the side in which the bar will track slightly forward as it descends. This will result in the top of the rails being horizontally closer to your head than the bottom of the rails when lying down on the bench. An angled Smith machine will allow you to use a bar path that most closely resembles that of a standard barbell bench press.
    3. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which side of the bar you place your feet.
2. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to a height that allows you to grasp it with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently.
    1. The ideal height allows you to unrack the bar while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together without requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    2. If the bar is situated too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart to lift the bar off the safety bars (on which the safety hooks that are attached to the bar rest). This can place you in a less stable position to start your set since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    3. If the bar is situated excessively low, you will have to grasp the bar initially with a greater degree of elbow bend, and unracking it will involve a pressing range of motion that is greater than ideal. This will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    4. If neither of the two closest bar heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    5. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best starting bar height for you.
3. After positioning the bar to the appropriate height, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of bench press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section. Use these reps to determine the appropriate height of the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails under the bar) prior to re-racking the bar.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety stops to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could untuck your shoulder blades and flatten out your back to lose your arch. This will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety stops.
    2. Certain Smith machine models have fairly wide spaces between where you could position safety stops. This may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your bench press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. If this applies to your Smith machine, it is not ideal, and you have the option of using a spotter. However, the safety stops can still enhance safety if they are high enough to stop the bar from reaching your neck if you are pinned under a bar and try to scoot out.
    3. If you cannot press the bar fully up and lack a spotter or safety stops that are able to receive the bar when flattening your back, you may be able to re-rack the bar on the closest safety bars above your chest if you are able to complete part of the press. If you happen to become pinned in this situation and are initially unable to reach the nearest safety bars above you, you can let the bar rest on your chest until you feel recovered enough to press the bar up high enough to re-rack the bar on the closest safety bars.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your chest is underneath the bar, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor approximately shoulder width apart.
    1. If your Smith machine has vertical slide rails, situate your lower chest under the bar.
    2. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, situate your upper chest under the bar to allow the bar to reach your lower chest at the bottom of the rep.
    3. With the fixed bar path from the Smith machine, it may be worthwhile to try scooting slightly forward or backward on the bench if the exercise feels a bit awkward with your initial positioning.
    4. Starting with your feet on the floor, as you would with a standard bench press, can help you set up optimally and feel more stable when unracking the weight compared to if you lifted your feet off the ground before unracking the bar.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning to bench press, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you are specifically training to bench press as much weight as possible, you can optionally exaggerate your lower back arch, which may allow you to shorten the range of motion a bit and potentially lift heavier weights.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
2. Press the bar off the safety bars by straightening your elbows before rotating the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a slight wrist flick motion.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. One at a time, lift your feet off the ground and straighten your knees with your legs close together while keeping the bar stationary above your shoulders.
    1. Keep your legs in this position until you finish your set.
    2. While your thighs are on the bench, your calves may or may not be resting on the bench as well, depending on your height and the length of your bench.
4. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner. Progressively bend your elbows and allow them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending until you can tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    2. To touch your lower chest, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    3. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up until you have fully straightened your elbows. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. Continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together and your feet off the ground as your buttocks, upper back, and head remain firmly planted on the bench.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
    1. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless for about one second.
7. Rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars by flicking your wrists before bending your elbows to lower the safety hooks securely onto the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the machine’s shoulder pads to a level that initially allows you to stand under the pads with mildly bent knees, which will enable you to perform full range of motion calf raises.
    1. The appropriate shoulder pad height should make it easy for you to assume the starting position and also allow you to feel resistance from the machine both when your heels are fully lowered and fully raised.
    2. If the pads are too high, your knees may be straightened when initially standing under the pads, and you may lose tension from the machine when your heels are fully lowered.
    3. If the pads are too low, you may need to bend your knees excessively to stand under the pads, and you may be restricted from fully raising your heels.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin.
3. With your knees mildly bent, position the balls of your feet onto the foot plate with your heels hanging off the edge while your shoulders are snuggly resting under the shoulder pads.
4. Straighten your knees to stand fully upright and receive the weight from the machine.
    1. Throughout the set, keep the balls of your feet firmly planted on the step.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they allow their knees to bend backward into hyperextension during this exercise, so keeping a straight knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels fully upward while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
    1. Once you have lifted your heels as high upward as your ankle mobility permits, you have finished the lifting phase.
2. Mildly bend one knee, and lift that side’s foot off the foot plate while keeping your other foot firmly planted on the foot plate.
3. In a controlled manner, allow your working side’s heel to descend toward the ground through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits.
    1. Continue keeping your free leg’s foot not in contact with the foot plate while maintaining a straight knee position with your working leg.
    2. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can below the ball of your foot to stretch your calf and train it the most effectively.
    3. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full range of motion.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid quickly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep.
4. Once you have fully lowered your working leg’s heel, return your free leg’s foot to the foot plate to have both feet symmetrically on the foot plate.
5. Repeatedly perform steps 1-4 as you alternate each rep which foot you keep in contact with the foot plate during steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After finishing your last rep, mildly bend your knees to lower the weight to its resting position (where it no longer provides any resistance to you) before stepping off the foot plate.
    2. If you wish to do so, you can alternatively complete one set where you perform the lowering phase of each rep exclusively with one side, rest, and then complete another set where you perform the lowering phase of every rep with your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near waist height, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Securely attach an ankle cuff just above your wrist on the side you will train this set. Position the cuff so that one of its attachment rings is facing the same direction as your palm.
3. Stand with the side of your other shoulder (that you will not train during this set) facing the cable, and take a small step backward, so that the cable is slightly in front of you by your side.
4. Reach across your body and attach the cuff to the cable with your palm facing the pulley.
5. With your closer free hand, grasp part of the cable station for stability, and step out to the side away from the pulley until you have straightened that side’s elbow.
    1. Throughout the exercise, continue using this arm to help stabilize yourself.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm using the cuff.
6. Allow the cable’s resistance to pull your arm across your body and in toward the pulley with your elbow straightened.
    1. In this position, your hand should be close to being just in front of your opposite side’s thigh with your palm facing the pulley.
    2. You should feel tension from the cable and will likely feel a stretching sensation on the side of your shoulder.
    3. While predominantly pointing downward, your arm should be angled forward just enough to allow it to sweep across your body without your torso getting in the way. If your arm is outstretched forward beyond this point, take a small step forward to position the rest of your body closer to the handle.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow straight, sweep your arm out and away from the pulley until your arm is close to parallel with the ground.
    1. At the top of the raise, your palm should be facing down, and your arm should form half of a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from the front or back.
    2. While you raise your arm through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blade to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your arm in toward the pulley and across your body in a controlled arcing manner through your full range of motion.
    1. You should finish with your elbow just in front of your torso.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to be near the height of the top of your head, select your desired weight with the loading pin, and leave the cable without any attachment.
    1. The appropriate pulley height should allow you to move the cable at about a 45-degree angle during this exercise.
2. Stand sideways with the side of your body you will not train during this set closer to the pulley.
3. With the arm further away from the pulley, reach up across your body, and firmly grasp the ball-shaped stopper at the end of the cable. For additional stability, you can grasp part of the cable station with your free hand.
    1. Hold the stopper in the palm of your hand by wrapping your four smaller fingers around it and positioning your thumb on top of these fingers.
    2. All the subsequent directions will refer to the arm holding the cable.
4. Drive your elbow diagonally downward and across your body until it is level with your side when viewed from the side. Simultaneously let your elbow bend from the cable’s resistance, and keep your forearm aligned with the cable.
    1. When viewed facing the front of your body, you should be able to draw essentially a straight line from your elbow that passes your wrist and the cable to the pulley.
    2. To achieve this alignment, you should finish with your upper arm flared out reasonably close to 45 degrees from your side, resulting in your elbow being angled diagonally outward and downward. Zero degrees of elbow flare would mean your upper arm is pinned directly by your side, while 90 degrees of elbow flare would mean your upper arm is parallel to the ground.
    3. In this context, having your elbow level with your side means it is not in front of your torso or behind your torso when viewed from the side.
5. While maintaining the same arm position, take a small step backward so that the pulley is now slightly in front of you
    1. In this starting position, your elbow should be fully bent or very close to being fully bent.
## Exercise technique
1. Without moving your upper arm, drive your forearm diagonally outward and downward in an arcing manner by straightening your elbow.
    1. Finish with your elbow fully straightened.
    2. All the motion should occur from your forearm hinging at your elbow, without any shoulder motion. You can think of your elbow as the center of an analog clock, while your forearm is the hand that rotates about this stationary center elbow.
    3. Keeping your upper arm angled about 45 degrees out from your side without letting your elbow move forward or backward will indicate that you are successfully avoiding any shoulder motion.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your forearm to arc diagonally upward and in toward the pulley as you bend your elbow in a controlled manner.
    1. Continue keeping your upper arm stationary, and finish with your elbow fully bent.
    2. There is no need to perform this extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your hand toward the pulley in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and hold it by your side with your elbow straightened.
    1. Continue holding the dumbbell by your side throughout the exercise.
2. Approach a step or a sturdy object that can function as a step. You should be by a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance.
    1. Based on the equipment at your disposal, a variety of objects can serve as your step. Make sure the object can support your weight, will not move once you are on it, and allows you to lower your heel through the full range of motion your ankle mobility permits.
    2. As an alternative to a staircase’s bottom step, you may select a power rack’s bottom beam if the beam is sufficiently tall.
    3. Additionally, a calf raise block, aerobic step, deadlift block, or thick bumper plate can each be positioned near a wall or power rack for stability. Depending on your bodyweight, anchoring an aerobic step with weight on top of it may be required to prevent the step from tipping.
3. Place your free hand onto the nearby surface for balance assistance, and maintain this hand contact throughout the set.
    1. If you are using a surface that you can grasp (like a power rack) rather than a wall, do not grip it tightly.
4. Step onto the step with the balls of your feet on the step while the back halves of your feet are hanging off the step.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels upward while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
    1. Only use your free hand for balance assistance as you avoid pushing down in a manner that can help you lift yourself upward.
    2. Once you have lifted your heels as high upward as your ankle mobility permits, you have finished the lifting phase.
2. Mildly bend one knee, and lift that side’s foot off the step while keeping your other foot firmly planted on the step.
3. In a controlled manner, allow your working side’s heel to descend toward the ground through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits.
    1. Continue keeping your free leg’s foot not in contact with the step while maintaining a straight knee position with your working leg.
    2. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can below the ball of your foot to stretch your calf and train it the most effectively.
    3. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full range of motion.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid quickly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep.
4. Once you have fully lowered your working leg’s heel, return your free leg’s foot to the step to have both feet symmetrically on the step.
5. Repeatedly perform steps 1-4 until you have completed your set. Each rep, alternate which foot you keep in contact with the step during steps 2 and 3.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can alternatively complete one set where you perform the lowering phase of each rep exclusively with one side, rest, and then complete another set where you perform the lowering phase of every rep with your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Connect to a cable, a rope attachment that is ideally a long rope attachment, or two standard rope attachments that are each fully pulled to one side of the attachment.
    1. Compared to using a single standard-length rope attachment, using a long rope attachment or two standard-length rope attachments (together replicating the length of a long rope attachment) can help you emphasize shoulder external rotation at the end of this exercise’s lifting phase. However, this exercise can still be effectively performed with a single standard-length rope attachment, so you do not need to use a long rope or a combination of two standard ropes.
    2. In addition to the attachments that are just longer versions of the standard rope attachments, long rope attachments functionally include the long strap attachments (that can be used for cable crunches) that have a pair of looped ends into which you can place your hands.
2. Select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the cable station’s pulley to a height that allows you to just reach the rope when you are sitting on the ground.
    1. The appropriate pulley height should allow you to experience resistance from the cable when your arms are fully outstretched in the starting position. If you do not feel resistance from the cable in the starting position, a higher pulley height is warranted.
    2. For comfort, you optionally can situate an exercise mat on the ground near the cable where you will lie.
3. Sit down on the ground with an upright torso as you are facing the cable.
    1. You can bend your knees and position both feet flat on the floor, or you can place one foot against the cable column for additional stability.
4. Reach your arms toward the rope, and grasp each of its ends with your index fingers and adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the rounded outer aspects of the stoppers.
    1. If you are using a long strap attachment with looped ends rather than ropes, you naturally will situate your hands within the loops and grasp the inner part of each loop.
5. With your elbows straightened and arms aligned with the cable, lean backward to rest your back on the ground.
    1. Based on your preference, you can rest your head on the ground, or you can keep your head up, looking toward the pulley.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down to the floor while flaring them out away from your sides. Simultaneously spread your hands apart while pulling the cable down toward your eyes.
    1. At the end of this motion, your elbows should be pointing straight out to your sides, allowing your upper arms to form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. Strive to pull your hands toward the floor horizontally behind your elbows (i.e., further away from the cable station). This should result in the end position resembling a double biceps flexing pose.
    3. Finish with your shoulder blades fully pulled back together and your chest up in a “proud chest” posture.
    4. If you are using a long rope attachment or two ropes together, aim to finish with your hands as far apart from each other as your elbows, which should be bent by about 90 degrees. With a single standard rope attachment, you will not be able to spread your hands as far apart and will finish the pull with a greater degree of elbow bend.
2. Pause in the bottom position with your arms on the ground for a brief period of time (e.g., about two seconds).
    1. During this pause, you should feel meaningful tension in the muscles on the back of your shoulders.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your hands and elbows to move diagonally up toward the pulley as they come together.
    1. Allow your elbows to straighten and your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally as your arms move in line with the cable’s resistance.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your arms fully outstretched toward the pulley.
    3. You do not have to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the cable abruptly pull your arms upward in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also meaningfully training your rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, this exercise can effectively develop your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, along with the supraspinatus to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Set the foot plate to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot point with your hips while standing on the center of the foot plate.
    1. The pivot point will be the center of the machine’s wheel, about which the lever arm rotates.
    2. Strive to align the pivot point specifically with the bony bump (greater trochanter) that you could feel on the side of your hip.
    3. Some machines do not allow you to adjust the foot plate.
2. Position the machine’s lever arm to point straight down.
    1. This will help you adjust the machine to your body proportions before moving the lever arm into the exercise’s starting position.
3. Adjust the machine’s roller pad to be slightly above knee height when standing on the foot plate.
4. Rotate the lever arm out to the side to arc the roller pad to a height that allows the machine to provide resistance when your leg is raised as far out to the side as your flexibility comfortably permits.
    1. How high up you should rotate the lever arm will be determined by your flexibility, and some trial and error may initially be required to find your ideal setting.
    2. The appropriate setting should allow you to stretch the muscles on the inside of your thigh when your leg is fully lifted.
    3. As a starting point, you can rotate the lever arm about 60 degrees beyond vertical out to the side of your body you will train during this set. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in the muscles within your inner thigh when your leg is fully lifted with this setting, you should rotate the lever arm closer to horizontal until you are able to train through the full range of motion your flexibility comfortably enables.
5. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, and stand on the foot plate facing the weight stack
6. Align the leg that is on the same side as the roller pad with the machine’s pivot point. Stand with that leg just behind the path of the roller pad as your other leg is aligned with the path of the roller pad.
    1. This will result in you adopting a split stance with what will be your working leg behind what will be your resting stance leg.
7. While grasping the roller pad with your hand, pull the lever arm down into a vertical position.
8. Step forward with your rear leg and situate its lower inner thigh snugly against the outer aspect of the roller pad before moving your hand off the roller pad.
9. While standing upright with your legs together, grasp the handlebars by your sides with both hands for stability.
    1. In this starting position, your kneecaps, chest, and imaginary belt buckle should be directed straight ahead toward the center of the machine.
## Exercise technique
1. Allow the machine’s resistance to sweep your leg out to the side and upward in a controlled arcing manner. Let your leg lift as high upward as your flexibility comfortably permits.
    1. Continue keeping your kneecap, chest, and imaginary belt buckle pointing straight forward throughout your set.
    2. Keep the foot of your resting leg firmly planted on the ground.
    3. With the appropriate lever arm setting, you should feel a meaningful stretching sensation within the muscles on the inner aspect of your thigh when your leg is fully lifted.
    4. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine rapidly swing your leg up in an uncontrolled fashion.
2. Reverse the movement by driving your leg downward and inward in an arcing manner.
    1. You can finish this step by tapping the roller against the inner aspect of your resting stance leg.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. After your last rep, you can hold the roller pad down with your hand before lifting your leg off the roller pad and guiding the roller pad up to its starting setting with your hand.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hip adductor muscles in an isolated manner.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a moderate incline angle (near 45 degrees).
    1. Naturally, you can also use a bench permanently fixed to a moderate incline.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on the incline bench with your upper back resting on it and your feet flat on the floor.
    1. While you do not necessarily need to have an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid sitting with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
3. With your elbows straight and arms hanging down toward the ground, rotate your arms to direct your palms and the front of your biceps out to the sides.
    1. If you lack the mobility to point your palms fully out to the sides, simply rotate them as close to this position as you comfortably can.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to curl the dumbbells upward in an arcing manner while keeping the front of your biceps pointing out to the side.
    1. Since you will not rotate your arms throughout the exercise, the dumbbells should initially arc outward away from your sides before arcing in toward the sides of your shoulders after your forearms have lifted beyond the point where they are parallel to the floor.
    2. While your forearms move through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    3. At the top of the curl, your elbows should be fully bent with your forearms touching your biceps and the backside of your hands close to pointing straight out to the side.
2. Without rotating your arms, reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until your elbows have fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable’s pulley to its lowest setting, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. For comfort, you can optionally place an exercise mat on the ground where you will lie in front of the pulley.
2. With your chest facing down, lie down on the ground so that your shoulder (on the side of your body that you will train) is aligned with the pulley.
3. Position yourself where you can just barely reach the handle with the arm in front of the pulley.
    1. If you are situated any closer to the pulley, the cable will not provide resistance when your arm is fully outstretched in the starting position. Provided that the weight you selected is not too heavy to do so, you may be able to crawl back mildly away from the pulley after grasping the handle if you initially are too close to the pulley to experience tension from the cable with your elbow fully straightened.
    2. If you are situated any further away from the pulley, you will not be able to grasp the handle.
4. On the side of your body that you will train, bend your knee and position out to the side, such that your inner thigh is facing the ground.
5. On the side of your body that you will not train, prop your forearm onto the ground, and use this arm to brace against the ground throughout the exercise.
    1. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm that is aligned with the cable, since that is the side you will train.
6. Reach forward toward the pulley and firmly grasp the handle with your elbow straight.
    1. Based on your preference, you can angle your palm down toward the ground or in toward your other side.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbow back toward your side while progressively bending it to pull the handle toward you.
    1. Move your elbow through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which may involve keeping your elbow tucked closer to your side (and moving closer to the floor) or flaring it out further from your side (resulting in the handle staying slightly higher above the ground). Feel free to try out different degrees of elbow flare to determine what personally feels best to you.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion, which typically results in your upper arm ending directly by your side.
    3. The appropriate degree of elbow should result in your forearm remaining aligned with the cable throughout the set.
    4. While motion occurs at your working side’s shoulder girdle and elbow, the rest of your body should remain stationary as you continue bracing against the ground.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing the handle to move toward the pulley as you let your elbow move forward away from your side in a controlled manner.
    1. Allow your elbow to arc fully toward the pulley until it straightens to train through a full range of motion.
    2. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can train your lat and teres major especially well while also training your rear delt, biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position a seated exercise bench centered between two pulleys from a dual cable station. Situate the bench so that your shoulders will be aligned with the pulleys (rather than being behind or in front of the pulleys) once you are sitting on it.
    1. As an alternative to a seated bench with a backrest or an adjustable bench positioned fully upright, you may use a flat bench and perform the exercise without any back support.
2. Adjust each pulley to be near the height of the top of your head when you are seated on the bench.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, position the ends of its arms further apart than your wingspan. The proper setting should allow you to feel tension from the cables when your arms are fully outstretched to your sides.
    2. Dual cable stations that have their two weight stacks facing each other (often with a pull-up in between them) normally lack adjustable arms. If the two pulleys are not situated further apart than your wingspan, this specific exercise may not be feasible for you to perform with that equipment. If that applies to your dual cable station, you can select an alternative cable curl variation from this database, such as the low pulley dual cable biceps curl.
3. Connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
4. While standing with the sides of your shoulders facing the two pulleys, reach out to your side and firmly grasp one of the handles with a straight elbow.
5. While keeping your elbow straight, walk over to grasp the handle from the other cable in the same manner.
    1. Allow the resistance from the first cable you grasped to keep your arm raised out to the side before doing the same with the other cable during the next step.
6. Step to the side to center yourself between the cables, and sit down on the seated bench with an upright torso.
    1. Your elbows should be straightened, and your arms should be fully aligned with the cables, such that you can draw a straight line from each shoulder through your arm to the cable and then the pulley.
7. Rotate your palms to point up toward the ceiling if they are not already facing this way.
    1. Your wrists should be mostly straight and remain mostly straight throughout the exercise.
8. Pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to curl the handles in toward your ears. Keep your upper arms stationary as they remain directed toward the pulleys.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully bent as your forearms are contacting your biceps or are close to doing so. Depending on the length of your forearms, you may not be able to fully bend your elbows if your hands are about to contact your head.
    2. At the end of this movement, you should look like you are flexing for a double biceps pose.
    3. While you move your forearms through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to straighten to move the handles outward in a controlled manner.
    1. Finish with your elbows fully straightened, like they were in the starting position.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cables abruptly yank the handles outward in an uncontrolled manner.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to be clearly lower than chest height, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. So long as the pulley is set to a height that allows the cable to move diagonally upward during this exercise’s lifting phase, a range of pulley heights can be viably used for this variation. Setting the pulley near hip height can be an appropriate starting point, but feel free to try out different pulley heights to determine what feels best to you.
2. Connect to the cable a rope attachment (that is ideally a long rope attachment), or two standard rope attachments that are each entirely pulled to one side of the attachment.
    1. Compared to using one standard length rope attachment, using a long rope attachment or two standard rope attachments (that together replicate the length of a long rope attachment) can help you emphasize shoulder external rotation at the end of this exercise’s lifting phase. However, this exercise can still be effectively performed with a single standard-length rope attachment, so you do not need to use a long rope or a combination of two standard ropes.
    2. In addition to the attachments that are just longer versions of the standard rope attachments, long rope attachments functionally include the long strap attachments (that can be used for cable crunches) that have a pair of looped ends into which you can place your hands.
3. While facing the cable station, grasp each end of the rope with your index fingers and adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the rounded outer aspects of the stoppers.
    1. If you are using a long strap attachment with looped ends rather than ropes, you naturally will situate your hands within the loops and grasp the inner part of each loop.
4. With your elbows straightened and arms raised to be parallel with the cable, step backward until the machine’s weight plates separate and you feel tension from the cable.
    1. In this position, your palms should be directed down as your arms are outstretched in front of your torso while you are standing upright with your hands close to each other.
5. With your feet hip-width apart, slightly bend your knees, and adopt a staggered stance with one foot slightly in front of the other, or keep your feet symmetrical.
    1. Utilize whichever stance helps you feel the most stable and balanced.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows diagonally backward and upward while flaring them out away from your sides. Simultaneously spread your hands apart as you pull the cable up toward your eyes.
    1. At the end of this motion, your elbows should be pointing straight out to your sides, and your upper arms should be approximately parallel with the ground as they form a “T-shape” with your torso.
    2. The end position should resemble a double biceps flexing pose with your hands pulled as far backward as your elbows, as opposed to your hands remaining in front of your elbows like they would during a row.
    3. Finish pulling with your shoulder blades fully pulled back together and your chest up in a “proud chest” posture.
    4. If you are using a long rope attachment or two ropes together, strive to finish with your hands vertically above your elbows with your elbows bent by about 90 degrees. With a single standard rope attachment, you will not be able to spread your hands as far apart and will finish the pull with a greater degree of elbow bend.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your hands and elbows to move diagonally forward and downward while coming closer together as your elbows straighten.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally and move forward as your arms move in line with the cable’s resistance.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms angled forward and downward in front of you.
    3. You do not have to perform this lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control it the entire time rather than letting the cable abruptly pull your arms forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also meaningfully training your rear delt, lateral delt, traps, and rhomboids, this exercise can effectively develop your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, along with the supraspinatus to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near hip height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Stand with the pulley by the side of your body you will train during this set, and firmly grasp the handle with that side’s hand.
3. With your palm directed forward, take a step out to the side away from the pulley until the weight stack separates and you feel resistance from the cable.
    1. Allow the cable’s resistance to lift your arm out to the side with a straight elbow, which should result in your arm being angled diagonally outward and downward.
    2. Situate your free arm wherever you prefer throughout the exercise, since all the instructions apply to the side on which you are holding the handle.
4. Take a step forward with your foot further away from the cable to adopt a staggered stance that allows you to feel stable and balanced.
5. As you are standing with an upright torso, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. In the starting position, your shoulders should be level as your chest is facing the same direction as your imaginary belt buckle, rather than being twisted toward the pulley.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbow to curl the handle in toward your shoulder while keeping your upper arm stationary.
    1. You should finish with your elbow fully bent as your forearm is contacting your biceps or very close to doing so.
    2. While you move your forearm through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set.
    3. Resist the urge to swing your elbow forward through shoulder motion, and keep your wrist mostly straight.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to straighten in a controlled manner as you let your forearm arc outward toward the pulley.
    1. Finish this step with your elbow fully straightened.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank the handle back in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees, and pick up a pair of dumbbells.
    1. Alternatively, you can use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
    1. With the appropriate positioning, your feet should feel stable on the ground, and your torso should be well-supported by the bench.
    2. Shorter lifters may be able to assume this position with straight knees, but taller lifters may have to bend their knees in order to rest their chest comfortably on the head of the bench.
3. Allow your arms to hang down toward the ground with straight elbows as your palms are angled diagonally in toward each other and backward.
    1. Maintain this grip (which is a hybrid between an overhand grip and a neutral grip) throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arms diagonally backward and outward away from each other in an arcing motion through the full range your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straightened and chest in contact with the bench throughout the exercise.
    2. Strive to sweep your arms equal parts backward and outward, which should result in your arms forming approximately 45-degree angles near the top of the exercise when viewed from behind.
    3. When moving through your full range of motion, your arms should finish at least slightly behind/above your torso.
    4. While you lift your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your arms diagonally forward and inward until they are pointing straight down toward the ground.
    1. You should finish with dumbbells vertically aligned under your shoulders.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weights downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delts while also training your traps, rhomboids, lats, teres majores, and two rotator cuff muscles to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near shoulder height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing with the side of your shoulder (on the side of your body you will train during this set) facing the pulley, reach out to your side and firmly grasp the handle with a straight elbow.
3. Take a step out to the side, away from the pulley, while allowing the resistance from the cable to keep your arm raised out to the side.
4. Perform a controlled lunging motion into a half-kneeling position by stepping back with one leg and resting its knee on the ground.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can decide which knee you situate on the ground.
    2. Keep your torso upright, shoulders level with each other, and your arm aligned with the cable, such that you can draw a straight line from your shoulder through your arm to the cable and then the pulley.
    3. In this position, your imaginary belt buckle should be facing about 90 degrees away from the cable station.
5. Rotate your palm to point up toward the ceiling with a straight wrist if it is not already facing this way.
    1. Situate your free hand wherever you like, such as against your front leg’s lower thigh for additional stability. Keep your free hand in your preferred position throughout the exercise.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbow to curl the handle in toward your ear. Keep your upper arm stationary as it remains directed toward the pulley.
    1. You should finish with your elbow fully bent as your forearm is contacting your biceps or is close to doing so. Depending on the length of your forearm, you may not be able to fully bend your elbow if your hand is about to contact your head.
    2. At the end of this movement, you should look like you are flexing for a biceps pose.
    3. While you move your forearm through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to straighten to move the handle outward in a controlled manner.
    1. Finish with your elbow fully straightened, like in the starting position.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank the handle outward in an uncontrolled manner.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side as you face the opposite direction.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
Note that this exercise only functions as a variation that is distinct from the standard barbell bench press for lifters who set up with a meaningfully arched lower back when performing their typical bench press. If you typically bench press without exaggerating your lower back’s natural curvature, this flat-back bench press will be the same as your standard bench press. All aspects of the flat-back barbell bench press and standard barbell bench press are the same, except for the former, which intentionally does not use an arched setup.

1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed-height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of bench press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your bench press.
    1. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to an appropriate height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning to bench press, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
7. Tightly pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling. Maintain your lower back’s natural curvature rather than arching it any further.
    1. When lying down, your lower back normally exhibits a mild arch, so there is no need to try to flatten it fully. The objective is simply not to exaggerate this natural curvature, which results in you having a closer-to-flat back compared to if you fully arched your back actively.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending until you are able to tap your lower chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    2. When aiming to touch the bar to your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. To touch your lower chest, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and stand upright in a near hip width stance with your arms hanging down by your sides as your elbows are straight.
## Exercise technique
1. Turn your palms in toward your sides to adopt a neutral grip, which is also called a hammer grip.
2. Curl the dumbbells upward in an arcing manner by bending your elbows while keeping your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Keep your wrists straight, torso upright, and elbows by your sides, rather than swinging your elbows forward through shoulder motion.
    2. While your forearms move through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary
    3. At the top of the curl, your elbows should be fully bent with your forearms touching your biceps.
3. Rotate your palms back toward your shoulders to adopt an underhand grip.
4. Lower the dumbbells in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to straighten progressively without rotating your palms.
    1. Since you will maintain an underhand grip during this lowering phase, your palms should be facing upward when your forearms are parallel to the ground before facing forward once you have fully straightened your elbows.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a low incline angle (e.g., near 30 degrees) and set a pair of dumbbells under the head of the bench.
    1. You can alternatively set your bench at a moderate incline (e.g., near 45 degrees) if your bench lacks a low incline setting, or if you personally prefer to use a mildly greater incline.
    2. The dumbbells may be easiest to grasp while lying on the bench if you initially place one end of each dumbbell on top of the bench’s horizontal base leg (which is where wheels may be located) and the other end of each dumbbell on the floor closer to where you will be on the bench.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
3. Firmly grasp one dumbbell at a time, and angle your palms diagonally backward and in toward each other while your arms are hanging down with straight elbows.
    1. Rather than just rotating your palm as your elbow and upper arm remain still, rotate your whole arm through shoulder motion, resulting in the bony bump on the back of each elbow pointing diagonally outward and backward. This is the direction in which you will row.
    2. Throughout your set, maintain this grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip and a neutral grip.
    3. To be able to row through a full range of motion, the dumbbells should not be in contact with the ground or the base of the bench, which may be more challenging to avoid for individuals with longer arms. To create greater clearance between the dumbbells and the ground, you can scoot further up toward the head of the bench.
    4. If it is difficult for you to grasp the dumbbells, which may be more likely if you have shorter arms, you can initially place the dumbbells closer to the bench’s seat before setting up with your torso scooted further down on the bench to situate your hands closer to the floor. After grasping the dumbbells, you can then press your feet into the ground to scoot further up on the bench so that your head can hang off the bench rather than having your face pressed into the head of the bench.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows in an arcing motion backward, upward, and moderately out away from your sides to row the dumbbells. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Aim to flare your elbows about 45 degrees away from your sides as you move them equally backward and out to the side. Zero degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows purely backward and keeping them completely tucked by your sides, while 90 degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows straight out to the sides, which would result in your upper arms and torso forming a “T-shape” at the top.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the top.
    3. Keep your torso in contact with the bench throughout the set rather than arching your lower back to lift your chest up.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
    5. When allowing your elbows to bend naturally, your forearms should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    6. The dumbbells should move in an arcing motion rather than move straight up toward your shoulders.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled arcing motion until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. Allow the weights to pull your shoulder blades apart to stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. Move your elbows diagonally inward and forward at a near 45-degree angle while they descend to reverse the moderate elbow flare you just utilized during the lifting phase.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weights pull your arms down rapidly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to place the dumbbells back onto the ground.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. If your machine has an adjustable seat, you can also initially adjust the seat to whichever position feels most comfortable to you, but the seat position is unlikely to affect exercise.
    2. Note that the sled will provide resistance even without any additional weight plates. The weight of the sled will vary depending on the specific model, but it commonly weighs near 120lb (54kg).
2. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the back rest.
    1. Your back should be flat rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, which may occur if you do not situate your hips far enough back against the back rest.
    2. The machine’s safety stoppers (which can be moved with one or two safety handles depending upon the specific machine) should already be locked, and you will keep them locked throughout your set.
3. With a near hip width stance, place the balls of your feet onto the bottom of the foot plate with your heels hanging off the edge.
    1. Ensure that the balls of your feet are firmly on the foot plate to the extent that they will not slide down during the exercise.
4. While grasping the hand grips, press the platform away from you by straightening your knees until they are only mildly bent.
## Exercise technique
1. While simultaneously straightening your knees quickly, forcefully push through the balls of your feet to point your feet away from you (like when stepping on a car’s gas pedal) and press the platform away.
    1. Keep the balls of your feet firmly in contact with the foot plate throughout the exercise.
    2. Once you have pointed your feet as far as your ankle mobility permits with your knees fully straightened, you have finished the lifting phase. There is no need to pause at the top.
    3. As you use your calves to perform most of the work with some assistance from your quads, this quick movement should resemble a jump rope motion but without the ball of your foot leaving the platform.
    4. Aiming to fully straighten your knees does not mean hyperextending your knees, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. If you have the mobility to hyperextend your knees, there is no need to forcefully lock your knees into a hyperextended position, since straightening them is appropriate.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner, by allowing your feet to point back closer toward you through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits while mildly bending your knees.
    1. While ensuring that they remain firmly on the foot plate, strive to bring the balls of your feet fully back toward your shins to stretch your calves and train them most effectively.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calves or ankles at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full ankle range of motion.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid rapidly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep. Only the lifting phase should be quick.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to lower the platform back onto the safety stoppers by further bending your knees.
    1. If you have rather flexible calves and high ankle mobility, you may feel most stable when lowering the platform if you do so when your ankles are near a neutral position (like near the mid-range of each rep) rather than when your feet are bent fully back toward you (like at the bottom of each rep).
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Situate a pair of dumbbells on an elevated surface (like an exercise bench) or the ground with their handles oriented vertically from one side of each dumbbell facing up.
2. Spread your fingers apart, and firmly grasp the top end of each dumbbell with your palms directed down.
3. In a near hip-width stance, stand upright with your arms by your sides and your elbows straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While forcefully squeezing the dumbbells, curl them upward in an arcing manner by bending your elbows. Strive to keep the dumbbells’ handles fairly aligned with your forearms, such that the handles and your forearms point in a similar direction throughout the curl.
    1. When your elbows are bent by about 90 degrees, and your forearms are in a horizontal position, the dumbbells’ handles should be mostly horizontal. This will require cocking your wrists back and pointing your palms forward rather than down toward the ground with straight wrists, which would situate the handles in a vertical position.
    2. When your elbows are fully bent at the top of the curl, the dumbbells’ handles should be angled upward like your forearms.
    3. Keep your upper arms fixed by your sides rather than allowing them to swing forward through shoulder motion.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until you have straightened your elbows. Continue keeping the handles pointing in a similar direction as your forearms while you resist letting your wrists straighten.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While dynamically training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this exercise can statically train your hand's intrinsic finger flexors and your forearm’s wrist extensors.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the machine’s shoulder pads to a level that initially allows you to stand under the pads with mildly bent knees, which will enable you to perform full range of motion calf raises.
    1. The appropriate shoulder pad height should make it easy for you to assume the starting position and also allow you to feel resistance from the machine both when your heels are fully lowered and fully raised.
    2. If the pads are too high, your knees may be straightened when initially standing under the pads, and you may lose tension from the machine when your heels are fully lowered.
    3. If the pads are too low, you may need to bend your knees excessively to stand under the pads, and you may be restricted from fully raising your heels.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. With your knees mildly bent, position the balls of your feet onto the foot plate with your heels hanging off the edge while your shoulders are snuggly resting under the shoulder pads.
4. Straighten your knees to stand fully upright and receive the weight from the machine.
    1. Throughout the set, keep the balls of your feet firmly planted on the step.
    2. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they allow their knees to bend backward into hyperextension during this exercise, so keeping a straight knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels fully upward while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
    1. Once you have lifted your heels as high upward as your ankle mobility permits, you have finished the lifting phase.
2. Mildly bend one knee, and lift that side’s foot off the foot plate while keeping your other foot firmly planted on the foot plate.
3. In a controlled manner, allow your working side’s heel to descend toward the ground through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits.
    1. Continue keeping your free leg’s foot not in contact with the foot plate while maintaining a straight knee position with your working leg.
    2. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can below the ball of your foot to stretch your calf and train it the most effectively.
    3. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full range of motion.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid quickly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep.
4. Once you have fully lowered your working leg’s heel, return your free leg’s foot to the foot plate to have both feet symmetrically on the foot plate.
5. Repeatedly perform steps 1-4 as you alternate each rep which foot you keep in contact with the foot plate during steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After finishing your last rep, mildly bend your knees to lower the weight to its resting position (where it no longer provides any resistance to you) before stepping off the foot plate.
    2. If you wish to do so, you can alternatively complete one set where you perform the lowering phase of each rep exclusively with one side, rest, and then complete another set where you perform the lowering phase of every rep with your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near knee height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Stand with the pulley by the side of your body you will train during this set, squat down, and firmly grasp the handle with that side’s hand.
3. Stand upright, and take a step out to the side away from the pulley before taking a step backward.
    1. Allow the cable’s resistance to lift your arm out to the side with a straight elbow, which should result in your arm being angled diagonally outward and downward in addition to mildly forward.
4. Perform a controlled lunging motion into a half-kneeling position by stepping back with your leg closer to the cable and resting its knee on the ground.
5. Brace your free hand against your front knee for additional stability, and direct your other side’s palm forward.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. In the starting position, your shoulders should be level as your chest is facing the same direction as your imaginary belt buckle, rather than being twisted toward the pulley.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbow to curl the handle across your body toward your opposite shoulder while keeping your upper arm stationary.
    1. You should finish with your elbow fully bent as your forearm is contacting your biceps or very close to doing so.
    2. While you move your forearm through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set.
    3. Resist the urge to swing your elbow forward through shoulder motion, and keep your wrist mostly straight.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to straighten in a controlled manner as you let your forearm arc outward toward the pulley.
    1. Finish this step with your elbow fully straightened.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank the handle back in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. On the side you will train during this set, pick up a dumbbell and hold it with a straight arm as your palm is facing in toward your side.
2. Stand with the side of your opposite shoulder (on the side not holding the dumbbell) facing a sturdy surface onto which you can place one hand and lean.
    1. A variety of objects can be used, such as a cable station’s vertical beam, power rack, or wall.
3. Raise your free arm out to the side until it is close to parallel to the ground with a straight elbow, and place its palm against the surface.
4. While leaning into the surface, take a small step out to the side, and situate both feet together.
    1. Through the hand holding the surface, predominantly bear your weight through the base of your palm (i.e., heel of your hand).
    2. Allow your arm holding the dumbbell to rest against your side. The subsequent directions will apply specifically to this arm.
    3. Specifically how far out you position your feet and consequently lean to the side is up to your preference, but you should be stable and balanced in this position.
    4. While being tilted to the side, your torso and legs should be aligned, like when you are standing upright with your feet together.
## Exercise technique
1. Without bending your elbow, sweep your arm out and away from your side until it is parallel to the ground or a little above that point.
    1. While you raise your arm through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blade to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    2. At the top of the raise, your palm should be directed down toward the ground with a straight wrist.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your arm in toward your side in a controlled arcing manner until the dumbbell lightly contacts your side.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arm downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus while also meaningfully training your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. As the side of your shoulder (that you will not train during this set) is facing the cable station, stand in a staggered stance with one foot a little in front of the pulley and your other foot a little behind the pulley.
    1. If you step forward with your foot on the side that is closer to the pulley, the cable may be less likely to brush up against your leg during the exercise.
3. Lunge down, grasp the handle between your legs with your hand on the side opposite to the cable, and stand upright.
    1. Your working side’s arm should now be in a vertical position close to your body with your palm facing in toward the pulley and your elbow straight or slightly bent (depending on what feels most comfortable to you).
4. With your closer free hand, firmly grasp the cable station’s vertical beam near shoulder height, and lean your entire body to the side away from the cable station until your supporting arm’s elbow has straightened.
    1. In this starting position, you should feel stable with your hand firmly holding onto the cable station.
    2. Throughout the set, maintain this leaned body position, and continue supporting yourself with your one arm.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep the handle out and away from your side until your upper arm is close to parallel with the ground.
    1. At the top of the raise, your working arm should form half of a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from the front or back.
    2. While you raise your arm through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blade to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your arm in toward your side in a controlled arcing manner until your arm lightly contacts your torso.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus while also training your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Situate one side of a dumbbell on an elevated surface (like an exercise bench) or the ground with its handle oriented vertically.
2. On the side you will train during this set, spread your fingers apart, and firmly grasp the top end of the dumbbell with your palm directed down.
    1. Keep your free arm wherever you prefer throughout the set.
3. In a near hip-width stance, stand upright with your arm by your side and your elbow straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While forcefully squeezing the dumbbell, curl it upward in an arcing manner by bending your elbow. Strive to keep the dumbbell’s handle fairly aligned with your forearm, such that the handle and your forearm point in a similar direction throughout the curl.
    1. When your elbow is bent by about 90 degrees, and your forearm is in a horizontal position, the dumbbell’s handle should be mostly horizontal. This will require cocking your wrist back and pointing your palm forward rather than down toward the ground with a straight wrist, which would situate the handle in a vertical position.
    2. When your elbow is fully bent at the top of the curl, the dumbbell’s handle should be angled upward like your forearm.
    3. Keep your upper arm fixed by your side rather than allowing it to swing forward through shoulder motion.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner until you have straightened your elbow. Continue keeping the handle pointing in a similar direction as your forearm while you resist letting your wrist straighten.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbell down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While dynamically training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this exercise can statically train your hand's intrinsic finger flexors and your forearm’s wrist extensors.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the stoppers (that are positioned under the arms) to a height that allows you to press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with vertical forearms, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
    3. Some machine models do not allow you to adjust the stoppers.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine after determining your appropriate stopper height.
3. Lie down with your back, buttocks, and head on the pad. Position yourself so that your lower-to-middle chest is aligned with the handles.
    1. Your elbows will naturally tuck closer to your sides as you position yourself further back on the pad (situating your shoulders horizontally further behind the handles).
    2. You can decide specifically where you lie down on the pad based on what feels comfortable to your shoulders throughout the exercise, while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If you experience shoulder discomfort when pressing with the handles near mid-chest, you can try positioning yourself with the handles by the lowest part of your chest to be able to press with a lower degree of elbow flare.
    4. You can initially keep your feet on the ground until you are ready to finish setting up.
4. With your palms facing forward and elbows bent, firmly grasp the horizontally directed handles. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being in a vertical position.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles and position your elbows directly under them, you may have to increase the height of the machine’s stoppers before resuming your setup.
    3. Aligning your elbows directly under (rather than being in front or behind when viewed from the side) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you positioned your body appropriately on the pad.
    4. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best while stacking your elbows under the handles.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
6. One at a time, lift your feet off the ground and straighten your knees with your legs close together.
    1. Keep your legs in this position until you finish your set.
    2. While your thighs are on the pad, your calves may or may not be resting on the pad as well, depending on your height and the length of your pad.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows upward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain stacked under your wrists and the handles when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back and buttocks snugly against the pad, and feet off the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers under them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles as you allow your elbows to move outward and downward while bending.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable bench at a low incline angle (e.g., near 30 degrees), and pick up a dumbbell on the side you are about to train.
    1. As a different variation, which you can use if your bench does not have a low incline setting, you can alternatively select a moderate incline angle (e.g., near 45 degrees) and otherwise use the same technique for this exercise.
2. Sit down sideways on the bench with your arm holding the dumbbell further away from the elevated head of the bench.
3. Lie down with your side (not holding the dumbbell) on the bench in a manner that feels comfortable and stable to you.
    1. There is no single correct way specifically how to situate your body when lying on your side, since individuals with different body types will differ in terms of which exact positions are most comfortable.
    2. For instance, some people will prefer to situate both feet on the ground with fairly straight knees, while others may prefer to keep their feet off the ground with a more pronounced knee bend.
    3. Similarly, certain lifters may feel most comfortable using their free arm to support their head with their upper arm lying on the bench and elbow bent. Others may feel better wrapping the free arm around the bench and holding it for stability.
    4. Feel free to try out different body positions to determine what personally feels most comfortable and stable to you.
    5. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell.
4. With a straight elbow, position your arm moderately in front of your torso so that it can hang down toward the ground without being restricted by contacting the rest of your body.
    1. When setting up, you can begin with your arm directly by your torso (which would naturally prevent your arm from hanging down any lower) and move it somewhat forward beyond this position.
    2. With your arm hanging down across the midline of your body, you should feel a stretching sensation in your shoulder musculature.
    3. To train your lateral delt effectively, avoid positioning your arm meaningfully further forward than is needed (such as where your wrist would be straight forward in front of your chest), which would situate more so to emphasize your rear delt. Your wrist should be closer to your imaginary belt buckle than your chest.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arm diagonally upward and backward until it is aligned with the side of your torso and your elbow is moderately above the height of your top shoulder.
    1. While you do not need to keep it locked, you should keep your elbow essentially straight throughout the exercise.
    2. Having your arm become aligned with the side of your torso means that your arm is no longer in front of your torso when viewed straight on from the lower side of the bench (where your feet are) due to the backward motion of this step
    3. At the top, your arm should be angled diagonally upward and out to the side. When viewed from the front of your body, you can aim to lift your arm upward until it is reasonably close to forming a 90-degree angle with your torso.
    4. As you raise your arm through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blade to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
2. While moving mildly backward, lower your arm behind your torso in a smooth arcing manner through your shoulder’s full comfortable range of motion.
    1. Strive to move your arm just far enough backward that the side of your body will not contact the dumbbell and physically restrict your range of motion.
    2. How far you can lower your arm will be affected by your shoulder mobility, but individuals with normal mobility can often finish with the dumbbell behind the higher buttock. When moving through your full range of motion, it is common to feel a stretching sensation in the muscles on the side of your shoulder.
    3. At the end of this step, the dumbbell should be closer to your buttocks than your imaginary belt buckle.
    4. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should actively control the entirety of this motion.
3. Reverse step 2’s movement by sweeping your arm upward and mildly forward until you have returned to the end position of step 1 with the dumbbell above you.
    1. Your arm should once again be angled upward and outward, as it is no longer behind your torso.
4. Reverse step 1’s movement by allowing your arm to sweep diagonally downward and forward in a controlled manner
    1. Move through your shoulder’s full range of motion while allowing your arm to point downward and forward with your wrist closer to your imaginary buckle than your chest.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. Each rep involves completing steps 1-4.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt while also training your front delt, rear delt, supraspinatus , infraspinatus, and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on a bench with your torso upright, feet flat on the floor, and arms hanging straight down.
    1. You can opt to sit down on a seated bench with your back resting against the back support, or you can sit on a flat bench without any back support.
## Exercise technique
1. While holding the dumbbells by your sides with straight elbows, turn your palms in toward your sides, resulting in a neutral grip that is also called a hammer grip.
2. Curl the dumbbells upward in an arcing manner by bending your elbows while keeping your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Keep your wrists straight and your elbows by your sides, rather than swinging your elbows forward through shoulder motion.
    2. While your forearms move through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    3. At the top of the curl, your elbows should be fully bent with your forearms touching your biceps.
3. Rotate your palms back toward your shoulders to adopt an underhand grip.
4. Lower the dumbbells in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to straighten progressively without rotating your palms.
    1. Since you will maintain an underhand grip during this lowering phase, your palms should be facing upward when your forearms are parallel to the ground before facing forward once you have fully straightened your elbows.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near waist height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Stand with the side of your shoulder (that you will not train during this set) facing the cable, and take a small step backward, so that the cable is slightly in front of you by your side.
3. With your arm on the side opposite the cable, reach across your body to grasp the handle.
4. With your closer free hand, grasp part of the cable station for stability, and step out to the side away from the pulley until you have straightened that side’s elbow.
    1. Throughout the exercise, continue using this arm to help stabilize yourself.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm holding the handle.
5. Allow the cable’s resistance to pull your arm across your body and in toward the pulley with your elbow straightened.
    1. In this position, your hand should be close to being just in front of your opposite side’s thigh with your palm facing the pulley.
    2. You should feel tension from the cable and will likely feel a stretching sensation on the side of your shoulder.
    3. While predominantly pointing downward, your arm should be angled forward just enough to allow it to sweep across your body without your torso getting in the way. If your arm is outstretched forward beyond this point, take a small step forward to position the rest of your body closer to the handle.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow straight, sweep your arm out and away from the pulley until your arm is close to parallel with the ground.
    1. At the top of the raise, your palm should be facing down, and your arm should form half of a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from the front or back.
    2. While you raise your arm through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blade to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your arm in toward the pulley and across your body in a controlled arcing manner through your full range of motion.
    1. You should finish with your elbow just in front of your torso.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
This variation requires a dual cable station that specifically allows you to position its pulleys reasonably close to shoulder-width apart. These machines (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) typically have adjustable arms that can be swiveled close together or further apart. Dual cable stations that have their pulleys situated further apart will not be feasible for this exact variation, but can be used for the single arm cable triceps press.

1. At a dual cable station, adjust each pulley to be near shoulder-width apart and near head height.
2. Select your desired load with the two weight stacks, and leave the cables without any handle attachments.
3. While facing away from the base of the cable station, stand centered between the pulleys, slightly behind the pulleys.
4. Reach up with bent elbows, and firmly grasp the ball-shaped stoppers at the end of each cable.
    1. Hold the stoppers in the palms of your hands by wrapping your four long fingers around them and positioning your thumbs on top of these fingers.
5. Take a couple of steps forward, and adopt a staggered stance with one foot further in front than the other.
6. For balance, slightly bend your front knee and slightly lean your torso forward by hinging at your front leg’s hip.
7. If you did not already do so, hold your upper arms approximately parallel to the ground with your elbows bent to assume the starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your forearms forward in an arcing manner by straightening your elbows. Keep your elbows up as your upper arms remain essentially stationary throughout the set.
    1. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. Strive to have all the movement occurring from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion.
    3. You can think of each elbow as the center of an analog clock, while your forearm is the hand that rotates about this stationary center elbow.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to bend in a controlled manner as your forearms arc back toward the pulleys.
    1. Let your elbows bend through the full range of motion provided by the cables until your forearms are aligned with them.
    2. There is no need to perform this extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the cables abruptly yank your hands toward the pulleys in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, step backward and bring the cable ends toward the pulleys until the cables lose tension and you can release them.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. At a dual cable station, adjust each pulley to near waist height, select your desired load with the two weight stacks, and leave the cables without any handle attachments.
    1. A dual cable station that can situate the two pulleys no further apart than your wingspan is ideal to use for this variation.
    2. A dual cable station that has its pulleys rather far apart from each other (often with a long upper beam connecting each end of the station) is not as practical to use. With this type of equipment, the single arm cable overhead triceps extension may be preferable.
2. Stand centered between the pulleys facing toward the base of the cable station.
3. Firmly grasp the ball at the end of each cable with the hand that is closer to that cable.
4. Pivot your body 180 degrees while simultaneously bending your elbows and lifting them upward to cross the cables over each other.
    1. Finish with your hands close to each other behind your neck as your elbows are fully bent and raised to head height.
    2. The cable you are holding with your right hand should now be exerting a force angled to your left, while the cable you are holding with your left hand is exerting a force angled to your right.
5. Adopt a staggered stance by stepping forward with one foot, and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips
    1. In this starting position, your upper arms should be angled out to your sides, upward, and somewhat forward as they are aligned with the cables exerting a force that is angled inward, downward, and somewhat backward.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your hands upward, outward, and mildly forward in an arcing motion by extending your elbows until they are fully straightened. Keep your elbows up as your upper arms remain essentially stationary throughout the set.
    1. While your upper arms remain aligned with the direction in which the cables are pulling, this movement should resemble drawing a pair of katana from their sheaths behind your back.
    2. During this exercise, nearly all the motion should occur from your forearms hinging at your elbows without any shoulder motion. You can think of each elbow as the center of an analog clock, while your forearm is the hand that rotates about this stationary center elbow.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by bending your elbows and allowing your hands to move downward, in toward each other, and back behind your neck.
    1. Allow your elbows to bend fully, which typically produces a stretching sensation in your triceps.
    2. There is no need to perform this extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the cables abruptly yank your hands toward the pulleys in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, perform the opposite of the pivoting maneuver you utilized while setting up to uncross the cables and face toward the base of the cable station.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust each pulley from a dual cable station to be near hand height when standing with your arms relaxed by your sides.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you can set the arms so that their ends are as close to shoulder width apart as you can.
    2. Dual cable stations that have their pulleys rather far apart from each other (often with a long upper beam connecting each end of the station) are not as practical to use for this variation compared to dual cable stations that have their pulleys closer together. If you use cables situated fairly far apart from each other, the cables will exert a force angled out to the sides to a notable degree, which will influence the technique compared to if the cables are chiefly pulling backward.
2. Position a seated exercise bench (or an adjustable bench positioned fully upright) centered in front of two pulleys.
    1. Place the bench far enough in front of the pulleys that the cables will be able to provide resistance to you when your arms are situated fully back behind you in the starting position.
3. Connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
4. Stand in between the pulleys while facing toward the bench.
5. Grasp the two handles with your elbows straightened and palms facing forward.
6. While keeping your arms reasonably close to your torso, walk forward on one side of the bench until you can straddle the bench and sit down with your back snugly against the back pad.
    1. Keep your elbows bent with the handles close to you while walking forward against the cables’ resistance.
    2. Place your feet flat on the floor or against the foot bar if your seated bench has a foot bar.
7. Pull your shoulder blades back together with your chest up to assume a “proud chest” posture.
8. With straight elbows, allow the cables to pull your arms back behind your torso through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to curl the handles forward and upward in an arcing manner. Keep your upper arms stationary as you avoid swinging them forward through shoulder motion.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully bent as your forearms contact your biceps.
    2. While you move your forearms through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set, which will result in your elbows remaining behind your torso.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing the handles to arc downward and back toward the pulleys as your elbows straighten.
    1. Finish this step with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cables abruptly yank the handles back in an uncontrolled manner.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, drive your elbows forward to bring your arms to your sides before standing up. Then step to one side of the bench, and walk backward until the cables are no longer tensed before releasing the handles.
## Description
While also meaningfully developing your brachialis and brachioradialis, this exercise can effectively train biceps from a notably lengthened position.## Setting up
1. Pick up a weight plate with each hand, and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance. Hold the plates by your sides with straight elbows and your palms facing your thighs.
    1. Plates that have grip holes (cutouts that allow the rim of a plate to function as a handle) will be the easiest to hold.
    2. If you are using weight plates that lack grip holes but have a raised outer lip, you can hook the tips of your four long fingers under the lip while positioning your thumbs on the other side of the plate.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Shrug your shoulders straight up toward your earlobes through the full range of motion you can to lift the plates upward.
    1. You should not turn this shrug into an upright row, but it is not problematic if your elbows naturally bend a bit rather than remaining completely straight.
    2. Throughout the exercise, continue standing upright with straight legs, and look straight ahead to keep your neck near a neutral position.
3. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulders to descend fully in a controlled manner to lower the plates.
    1. At the bottom, your shoulders should ideally be positioned a little lower than where they are normally situated while standing at rest due to the downward stretch that the weights can provide.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the weights abruptly yank you downward.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps and levator scapulae.## Setting up
1. At a cable station, adjust the pulley to be near the height of your lower chest, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you can position the arm of the cable you will not be using in a position where you can grasp it with your free hand for stability during the exercise.
2. Stand with the side of your body that you will train during this set facing the handle.
3. Using your closer hand, firmly grasp the handle with your elbow bent and forearm approximately parallel to the ground as it is pointing straight ahead.
    1. Based on your preference, you could hold the handle with your palm facing in toward you, down toward the ground, or angled diagonally in between these two directions. Feel free to try out different positions and utilize whichever feels best to you.
4. Pivot your body about 45 degrees away from the pulley, and use your free hand to grasp part of the cable station for additional stability if your cable station allows you to do so.
    1. Keep your chest and imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead in the same direction rather than letting the cable twist your body when turning.
    2. Not all cable stations will have a surface available against which to brace with your free hand.
    3. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the handle.
5. If you are not currently experiencing resistance from the cable, step forward until you do.
6. Adopt a staggered stance with one foot further in front than your other and your knees slightly bent. Your elbow should be flared out away from your side toward the pulley in line with where the cable’s resistance naturally pulls it.
    1. In this position, you should feel sufficiently stable and balanced, as your elbow is flared out away from your side toward the pulley
7. As you are standing with an upright torso, pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the handle across your body by driving your elbow inward as it straightens.
    1. Press through the full range of motion across your body. This often results in the elbow finishing in front of the breastbone, and the hand finishing in front of the opposite shoulder.
    2. You should feel like you are squeezing your pec, particularly at the end of this step, with your arm fully across your body.
    3. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, strive to keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
    4. Continue keeping your chest and imaginary belt buckle pointing straight ahead in the same direction as you avoid twisting your body.
    5. Keep your elbow level with your straight wrist, which will align your forearm with the direction in which you are pressing
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your elbow to move out toward the pulley while naturally bending.
    1. Allow the cable to pull your elbow fully out to the side as you avoid twisting your chest toward the pulley.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm outward in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will primarily develop your pec major, which is assisted by your front delt, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Connect a standard lat pulldown attachment with a straight bar shaft to a seated cable row machine, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. If your machine allows you to adjust the seat height, you can set it to a height that allows the cable to run approximately horizontally during this exercise rather than meaningfully angling upward or downward. A seat height that situates the pulley a little lower than your chest (once you are seated with an upright torso) is normally appropriate.
2. Sit down on the seat, and position your feet in contact with the machine’s foot plates as your knees are mildly bent.
    1. The appropriate degree of knee bend should allow you to feel stable and comfortable throughout the exercise while experiencing resistance throughout its full range of motion. If you lose resistance (from the weight you selected contacting the rest of the weight stack) at any point during your set, it can be preferable to adopt a lesser degree of knee bend to scoot yourself further back.
3. Fully reach your arms forward, and lean forward by rounding your back until you can grasp the bar firmly with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing downward.
    1. If you initially cannot reach the bar, you can scoot forward closer toward the cable by bending your knees further.
    2. Rather than rounding just one part of your back, round your lower, middle, and upper back together through a range that feels comfortable to your back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar back toward your torso by driving your elbows backward and pulling your shoulder blades together. Simultaneously straighten your back to assume an upright torso position.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you.
    2. At the end of this step, your lower back should exhibit the mild curvature it naturally has when you are standing upright, which should result in your shoulders being vertically aligned above your hips. Avoid excessively arching your lower back beyond this point, which can occur if you exaggerate leaning back.
    3. You should finish the row in a “proud chest” posture with your chest up and shoulder blades fully back. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together as if you were trying to pinch a pencil between them.
    4. Aim to drive your elbows fully backward, which should result in the bar being directly in front of your torso with your elbows slightly behind your torso at the end.
    5. Rather than performing the two motions in a segmented manner, strive to row the bar toward you in sync with the same rate at which you are straightening your back. This should result in the bar reaching just in front of your torso at the same moment in which your torso becomes fully upright.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing the cable to pull your elbows forward as you simultaneously round your back through a comfortable range and let your shoulder blades spread apart.
    1. You should finish with your back comfortably rounded and arms fully outstretched in front of you with straight elbows. This position should resemble if you were seated and leaning forward to try to reach an object just barely within your reach.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation throughout your back in this position, but you should not experience lower back discomfort. If you begin feeling lower back discomfort at the end range, it may be appropriate to not round your back quite as much, since you should only move through what is your back’s comfortable range of motion.
    3. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should certainly avoid letting the cable abruptly yank you forward. Using a fairly slow tempo can help reinforce the controlled technique desirable for this variation.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop most of your back musculature, including your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, rhomboids, and erector spinae.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the seat so that the handles are level with your shoulders once you are seated with an upright torso.
2. Adjust the lever arms to position the handles as close together (on the side of the chest pad) as your machine enables.
3. Select your desired weight with the loading pin.
4. Sit down sideways on the seat with what will be your working side further away from the chest pad.
    1. Rest your feet on the floor with your knees bent.
    2. Compared to sitting with your chest against the pad, sitting sideways can let you train through a longer range of motion while loading your rear delt in a more lengthened position. However, you alternatively can set up with your chest symmetrically against the pad if you wish.
5. With your arm further away from the chest pad, reach across your body to grasp the horizontal handle with your palm facing down. Simultaneously turn your chest toward the handle to help you reach it.
    1. To grasp the handle, you can initially stand up before sitting back down during the next step if that helps you reach it.
6. While holding the handle with a straight or slightly bent elbow, turn your chest until it is facing the same direction as your imaginary belt buckle.
    1. In this starting position, your working arm should be situated fully across your body, which should prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in the back of your shoulder.
    2. Position your free hand wherever you prefer throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Without bending your elbow, smoothly sweep your arm outward and across your body in a wide arcing motion.
    1. For the standard version of this exercise, you can finish once your arm is directed straight out to the side of your shoulder, resulting in your torso and arm forming half of a “T-shape.”
    2. While you move your arm through shoulder motion, keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arm to sweep fluidly in toward the starting position through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Just like when you finished setting up, you should feel a stretching sensation in the back of your shoulder as your arm is fully across your body.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should actively resist the weight rather than letting it abruptly yank your arm inward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Attach your desired load (e.g., weight plates or a kettlebell) to a pull-up/dip belt around your waist, and stand upright under a pull-up bar.
    1. The back of your belt should be securely resting on top of your buttocks, and the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) should be looped through the center hole of a weight plate or under the handle of a kettlebell. If you have a loading pin on your belt, you can alternatively loop the chain or strap through the loading pin after adding weight plates.
    2. It will be easiest if you carry the weight over to the pull-up bar and add the weight to your belt while you are under the bar, rather than adding the weight to your belt farther away from the bar before walking over to the bar.
    3. As an alternative to using a pull-up/dip belt, you can wear a weighted vest.
    4. While less stable and more awkward than using a belt or weighted vest, you also have the option of holding a dumbbell between your thighs or feet, but you will be limited in how much weight you can add when using a dumbbell.
2. Reach up overhead and firmly grasp the pull-up bar with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated well outside shoulder width.
    1. Positioning your hands approximately 50% wider than shoulder width is appropriate; however, a wide grip encompasses a range of grip widths, and the specific width you grasp the bar is up to your preference.
    2. What you consider a wide grip may also be influenced by the grip width you use for your standard pull-up, which you can widen to individualize your grip. For instance, for a wide grip, place your index finger on the bar just outside where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing a standard pull-up.
    3. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    4. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
3. With your elbows straightened, lift your feet up off the ground by bending your knees to begin hanging on the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging on the bar with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to assume a “proud chest” that is angled slightly upward.
    1. Maintain this “proud chest” posture throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the bar by driving your elbows down and toward your sides as you progressively bend your elbows.
    1. Since the bar is fixed, your elbows will not naturally drive downward; however, maintaining the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows being bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, individuals may have different ranges of motion.
    3. Rather than swinging your legs, statically keep them in a similar position throughout the rep.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms fully overhead.
    1. You do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet to the ground before releasing the bar.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. If you are using a step, position it slightly in front of the pull-up bar. The step should be positioned so that you can readily reach the bar without the step interfering with your feet or your weight during the exercise.
    1. Objects that can be used as a step include a plyometric box, an aerobic step, a bumper plate, and a flat exercise bench.
    2. Ideally, the step will be tall enough that you can grasp the bar with your elbows fairly bent, which will minimize how high you have to hop.
2. Attach your desired load (e.g., weight plates or a kettlebell) to a pull-up/dip belt around your waist, and stand upright under a pull-up bar.
    1. The back of your belt should be securely resting on top of your buttocks, and the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) should be looped through the center hole of a weight plate or under the handle of a kettlebell. If you have a loading pin on your belt, you can alternatively loop the chain or strap through the loading pin after adding weight plates.
    2. It will be easiest if you carry the weight over to the pull-up bar and add the weight to your belt while you are very close to the bar, rather than adding the weight to your belt farther away from the bar before walking over to the bar.
    3. As an alternative to using a pull-up/dip belt, you can wear a weighted vest.
3. While standing under the pull-up bar on the step or ground, reach up and firmly grasp it with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing forward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Hop up while pulling down on the bar to bring your chest toward the bar, keeping your elbows fully bent at your sides.
    1. You should now be in the top position of a pull-up.
    2. The degree to which you have to use your legs to hop up will naturally be affected by how high the bar is above your chest at the end of the setup. With a sufficiently tall step, you may be able to step forward without hopping up.
    3. Since you can use meaningful assistance from your legs or start with your chest already close to the bar, this step should not feel particularly strenuous to your back and arm muscles. The objective is to make getting your chest up to the bar feel relatively easy, so that your muscles are fresh and ready to control the descent.
2. While keeping your chest by the bar for a moment, bend your knees as much as is required to avoid your feet contacting the step or ground during the lowering phase. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. If you can do so without the step getting in your way, you can allow your thighs to hang straight down toward the ground.
3. In a controlled manner, lower your chest away from the bar by allowing your elbows to straighten while lifting up and away from your sides.
    1. Maintain a “proud chest” posture as you avoid letting your shoulders shrug up toward your ears from the force of your bodyweight.
    2. Finish with your elbows fully straightened as your arms are in an overhead position.
    3. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should use your back and arm muscles to control the entire descent rather than letting your body drop quickly.
    4. If you are using a load that exceeds your ability to perform a full weighted pull-up, it may be beneficial to aim to pull your chest up to the bar by driving your arms down toward your sides. Without the strength to lift yourself up, striving to do so can help you control the descent.
4. Return your feet to the step or ground to assume the same position you had when you finished setting up.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to its highest level, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. If you have access to one, connect a long rope attachment to the cable. Otherwise, connect two standard rope attachments to the cable, and pull each rope fully to one side of the attachment.
    1. Whatever type of equipment you select should be long enough that you can spread your hands about shoulder width apart at the bottom of the rep to perform this specific variation.
    2. In addition to the attachments that are just longer versions of the standard rope attachments, long rope attachments functionally include the long strap attachments (that can be used for cable crunches) that have a pair of looped ends into which you can place your hands.
    3. If you only have a single standard rope attachment, you can train your triceps just as effectively with the standard cable rope triceps pushdown.
3. Stand in front of the cable station facing the rope.
4. Reach up and grasp each end of the rope with your little fingers and adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the outside of your other fingers.
    1. If you are using a long strap attachment with looped ends rather than ropes, you naturally will situate your hands within the loops and grasp the inner part of each loop.
    2. Your palms should be facing inward.
5. Take a step backward and slightly lean forward by hinging at your hips.
    1. Maintain this slight torso lean throughout the set.
6. Pull your upper arms down and back until they are at your sides, with your elbows close to fully bent, so your hands are close together in front of your chest.
    1. In this starting position, you should feel tension from the cable. If you do not, take another step back until the weight stack separates.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arms and elbows aligned with your torso, push the rope downward and spread it apart by straightening your elbows.
    1. Allow your elbows to flare slightly out away from your sides while spreading the rope apart, which can result in your hands finishing slightly out to the sides of your torso.
    2. At the end of this motion, your elbows should be fully straightened, and you can optionally emphasize squeezing your triceps for a moment if you wish to do so.
    3. Throughout the exercise, avoid swinging your elbows backward or forward through shoulder motion.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to bend in a controlled manner to raise the rope as your hands move closer together.
    1. Strive to have your elbows as close to fully bent as you can by the time you have finished this movement to train your triceps through a full range of motion.
    2. You do not have to bend your elbows extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the cable abruptly pull your forearms upward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, which constitute the majority of muscle mass in your upper arms.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your torso against the chest pad and enables you to row comfortably with your elbows tucked fairly close to your sides.
    1. The appropriate seat height often naturally allows you to finish the lifting phase with your elbows bent by about 90 degrees.
    2. If the seat is too low, the upward angle in which the handles move during the lifting phase can cause the row to feel awkward as you have to shrug your shoulders up and/or meaningfully flare your elbows out away from your sides.
    3. If the seat is too high, you may notice that you have to pack your shoulder blades down to reach the handles with your arms directed further downward than forward. This could limit your range of motion and prompt the row to not feel as natural as it otherwise could.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, set it to a position that allows you to just grasp the handles when your arms are fully outstretched with straight elbows.
    1. Machines often lack an adjustable chest pad.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit down on the seat with your torso against the chest pad.
    1. Based on your preference, you can position your feet against the machine’s foot plates (if your machine model has foot plates), or you can place your feet flat on the floor.
    2. As an alternative to sitting on the seat, you optionally can adopt a staggered stance with one foot forward and the other backward as you lightly contact the seat with your buttocks while predominantly bearing weight through your feet.
    3. Set up in whichever position feels most stable and comfortable to you.
4. Reach forward and firmly grasp the handles with your palms angled in toward each other. 
    1. Some machines have handles that can freely rotate, but most have a pair of fixed handles angled downward that you can grasp with your palms facing in toward each other.
    2. Hold the handles with straight wrists and elbows.
    3. If the handles are situated far enough in front of you to make it difficult to grasp both of them simultaneously, it may be easier for you to grasp one handle at a time.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward you by driving your elbows backward while bending them. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Rather than meaningfully flaring them out away from your sides, keep your elbows tucked reasonably close to your sides to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you. This should keep your forearms aligned with the direction in which you are rowing.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being slightly behind your torso or at least level with your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    3. Instead of moving straight backward in a fully horizontal direction, this machine type will normally have its handles move at an upward angle while you pull them backward.
    4. Keep your torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    5. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten until your arms are fully outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, straddle the machine by standing on the foot plates as you are facing toward the weights.
    1. This variation specifically uses a T-bar row machine without any chest support, and other exercises using chest-supported T-bar row machines are included in this database.
    2. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
    3. The foot plates of most machines will require you to adopt a close to shoulder-width stance.
    4. Position your feet in a manner that places the machine’s handles just in front of you so that you do not have to reach far forward in order to grasp the handles initially.
2. With your arms pointing down toward the handles, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the handles. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
3. Firmly grasp the outer handles with a wide grip, such that your hands are clearly outside shoulder width with palms angled backward.
    1. The appropriate grip width for this upper-back focused variation should naturally let your elbows flare prominently out away from your sides. At the top of your row, this should result in your upper arms being closer to forming a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from behind, rather than being closer to being tucked by your sides (which can naturally occur with a closer grip).
    2. To find a viable wide grip based on your typical grip, you can place your index fingers on the handles where you would otherwise place your little fingers when performing your standard overhand grip T-bar row.
    3. The handles that permit a wide grip are completely horizontal (which points your palms straight backward) on some machines, but angled outward and backward on other machines (which points your palms backward and inward at an angle). Consequently, the specific grip you can use will vary based on your machine model.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from now on.
5. While keeping your lower back flat, chest up, and elbows straight, drive through your legs to lift the bar slightly up and assume a slightly more upright torso position.
    1. The objective is to use your hip and thigh muscles to lift the bar just high enough that you can experience resistance from the weight when your elbows are fully straight in the starting position of each rep.
    2. In this position, your torso should still be meaningfully inclined closer to horizontal than vertical, with your hips mostly bent.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward. Simultaneously bend your elbows and pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Rather than trying to keep them tucked close to your sides, allow your elbows to flare out meaningfully away from your sides to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    2. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished straightening and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
    2. While you do not have to lower the bar extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the bar drop abruptly in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar to its initial position by bending your knees and hips to lower the bar.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your traps, rhomboids, and rear delts while also training your biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. If you are using a step, position what you will use as your step slightly in front of the pull-up bar. The step should be situated where you can readily reach the bar without the step getting in the way of your feet during the exercise.
    1. Objects that can be used as a step include a plyometric box, aerobic step, bumper plate, and flat exercise bench.
    2. Ideally the step will be tall enough that you can grasp the bar with your elbows fairly bent, which will minimize how high you have to hop.
2. While standing under the pull-up bar on the step or ground, reach up and firmly grasp it with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing forward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Hop up while pulling down on the bar to bring your chest up toward the bar with your elbows fully bent by your sides.
    1. You should now be in the top position of a pull-up.
    2. The degree to which you have to use your legs to hop up will naturally be affected by how high the bar is above your chest at the end of the setup. With a sufficiently tall step, you may be able to just step forward off the step without having to hop up.
    3. Since you can use meaningful assistance from your legs or start with your chest already close to the bar, this step should not feel particularly strenuous to your back and arm muscles. The objective is to have getting your chest up to the bar feel fairly easy, so that your muscles will feel fresh and ready to control the descent.
2. While keeping your chest by the bar for a moment, bend your knees as much as is required to avoid your feet contacting the step or ground during the lowering phase. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. If you can do so without the step getting in your way, you can allow your thighs to hang straight down toward the ground.
3. In a controlled manner, lower your chest away from the bar by allowing your elbows to straighten while lifting up and away from your sides.
    1. Maintain a “proud chest” posture as you avoid letting your shoulders shrug up toward your ears from the force of your bodyweight.
    2. Finish with your elbows fully straightened as your arms are in an overhead position.
    3. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should use your back and arm muscles to control the entirety of the descent rather than letting your body quickly drop down.
    4. If you are not currently strong enough to perform a full pull-up, it can be beneficial to have the intent to try to pull your chest up to the bar by driving your arms down toward your side. Without the strength to lift yourself up, striving to do so can help you control the descent.
4. Return your feet to the step or ground to assume the same position you had when you finished setting up.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine’s weight sleeve, and straddle the machine as you are facing toward the weights.
2. Place your feet onto the foot plates and your chest onto the chest pad such that your whole body is inclined forward.
    1. You will be most stable when the entirety of the soles of your feet are in contact with the foot plates.
3. Depending upon your height and leg lengths, you may want to bend your knees somewhat or keep them straight to determine where your torso is situated on the chest pad.
    1. How you position your knees will be based on what feels most comfortable and stable to you, but you can initially see how you feel when positioning your knees to allow the uppermost aspect of the chest pad to be approximately level with your collarbone.
    2. Individuals who have longer legs will typically feel more comfortable when using a somewhat more bent knee position, while those with shorter legs will typically feel more comfortable with straighter knees.
4. Firmly grasp the outer horizontally directed handles with a wide overhand grip, such that your hands are clearly outside shoulder width with your palms facing backward.
    1. The appropriate grip width for this upper-back focused variation should naturally let your elbows flare prominently out away from your sides. At the end of your row, this should result in your upper arms being closer to forming a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from behind, rather than being closer to being tucked by your sides (which can naturally occur with a closer grip).
    2. To find a viable wide grip based on your typical grip, you can place your index fingers on the handles where you would otherwise place your little fingers when performing your standard overhand grip T-bar row. If your machine’s handles are not long enough to let you widen your grip to this degree, you can just use as wide a grip as your handles permit. For machines with short handles, the difference between a standard grip width and a wide grip may be small. 
5. Lift the bar up off the machine’s stopper, center the bar so that it no longer lies above the stopper, and lower it until your elbows are extended.
    1. In this bottom position, allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades apart from one another, which will likely prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in your upper back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar upward and back toward your torso by driving your elbows simultaneously upward and backward. Simultaneously bend your elbows and pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Rather than trying to keep them tucked close to your sides, allow your elbows to flare out meaningfully away from your sides to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    2. Keep your torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set rather than arching your lower back to lift your chest up off the pad.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your elbows have finished straightening and the bar has returned to the starting position.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. While you do not have to lower the bar extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the bar drop abruptly in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return the bar back onto the machine’s stopper.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your traps, rhomboids, and rear delts while also training your biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine, adjust the seat or thigh pad height to a level that allows you to position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Certain machine models have an adjustable seat, others have an adjustable thigh pad, and some have both an adjustable seat and an adjustable thigh pad.
    2. Depending on the specific model, plate-loaded lat pulldown machines may position the thigh pads either face toward or away from the weights, and these models often include a chest pad.
    3. While exceptions exist, the machines in which you sit facing away from the weights tend to have handles angled in a manner that results in an overhand grip being uncomfortable and awkward to use, in contrast to machines where you face the weights, which are typically preferable for this variation
2. While facing the thigh pads and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) as they are fairly wide apart.
    1. Your hands should be clearly outside shoulder width, resulting in your arms forming a “Y-shape” with your torso.
    2. While being influenced by the length of your machine’s handles, specifically how wide a grip you use is up to your preference.
3. While holding the handles, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad. Slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten in a controlled manner and your arms to raise up overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for individuals who are tall and have long arms), lowering the seat height or widening your grip may be necessary to achieve resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down toward the sides of your torso while progressively bending them to pull the handles downward.
    1. Maintain the same slight backward lean of your torso that you adopted while setting up, rather than progressively leaning further backward.
    2. Allow your elbows to naturally flare outward from the midline of your body in a manner that feels comfortable to you during the initial portion of the pull.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion until you can no longer drive your elbows closer toward your torso, which often results in the handles finishing below the height of your ears and slightly above the tops of your shoulders.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten in a controlled manner.
    1. You should finish with your arms positioned fully overhead with straight elbows.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance swing your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your lat and teres major especially well while also effectively training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Approach a step or a sturdy object that can function as a step. You should be by a surface (e.g., handrail, wall, power rack) where you can place one hand to assist with maintaining your balance.
    1. Based on the equipment at your disposal, a variety of objects can serve as your step. Make sure the object can support your weight, will not move once you are on it, and allows you to lower your heel through the full range of motion your ankle mobility permits.
    2. As an alternative to a staircase’s bottom step, you may select a power rack’s bottom beam if the beam is sufficiently tall.
    3. Additionally, a calf raise block, aerobic step, deadlift block, or thick bumper plate can each be positioned near a wall or power rack for stability. Depending on your bodyweight, anchoring an aerobic step with weight on top of it may be required to prevent the step from tipping.
2. Place one free hand onto the nearby surface for balance assistance, and maintain this hand contact throughout the set.
    1. If you are using a surface that you can grasp (like a power rack) rather than a wall, do not grip it tightly.
    2. Keep your other hand wherever you prefer throughout your set.
3. Step onto the step with the balls of your feet on the step while the back halves of your feet are hanging off the step.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels upward while keeping your knees straight and your trunk upright.
    1. Only use your free hand for balance assistance as you avoid pushing down in a manner that can help you lift yourself upward.
    2. Once you have lifted your heels as high upward as your ankle mobility permits, you have finished the lifting phase.
2. Mildly bend one knee, and lift that side’s foot off the step while keeping your other foot firmly planted on the step.
3. In a controlled manner, allow your working side’s heel to descend toward the ground through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits.
    1. Continue keeping your free leg’s foot not in contact with the step while maintaining a straight knee position with your working leg.
    2. Strive to lower your heel as far as you can below the ball of your foot to stretch your calf and train it the most effectively.
    3. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full range of motion.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid quickly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep.
4. Once you have fully lowered your working leg’s heel, return your free leg’s foot to the step to have both feet symmetrically on the step.
5. Repeatedly perform steps 1-4 until you have completed your set. Each rep, alternate which foot you keep in contact with the step during steps 2 and 3.
    1. If you wish to do so, you can alternatively complete one set where you perform the lowering phase of each rep exclusively with one side, rest, and then complete another set where you perform the lowering phase of every rep with your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. After selecting your weight using the machine’s loading pin, adjust the seat or thigh pad height to a level that allows you to position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Certain machine models have an adjustable seat, others have an adjustable thigh pad, and some have both an adjustable seat and an adjustable thigh pad.
2. While facing the thigh pads and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. If your machine has rotating handles (rather than fixed parallel handles), you naturally can rotate your palms inward for this variation.
3. While holding the handles, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad. Slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. When seated, allow your elbows to straighten in a controlled manner and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for individuals who are tall and have long arms), lowering the seat height may be necessary to achieve resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down toward your sides while progressively bending them to pull the handles downward.
    1. Pull through your full range of motion until you can no longer drive your elbows closer to your torso, which often results in the handles finishing below the height of your ears and slightly above the tops of your shoulders.
    2. Maintain the same slight backward lean of your torso that you adopted while setting up, rather than progressively leaning further backward.
    3. Allow your elbows to move naturally in a manner that feels comfortable with the path of your machine’s handles. With a machine whose handles spread apart as you pull them down, it is typically most comfortable to let your elbows flare outward from your sides during the initial portion of the pull. With a machine that has handles that remain closer together throughout the rep, it is typically most comfortable to keep your elbows tucked closer to shoulder width apart rather than meaningfully flaring them outward.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten in a controlled manner.
    1. You should finish with your arms positioned fully overhead with straight elbows.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance swing your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your lat and teres major especially well while also effectively training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. After selecting your weight using the machine’s loading pin, adjust the seat or thigh pad height to a level that allows you to position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Certain machine models have an adjustable seat, others have an adjustable thigh pad, and some have both an adjustable seat and an adjustable thigh pad.
2. While facing the thigh pads and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip).
    1. Use a mildly outside shoulder-width grip if the machine’s handles permit you to do so.
    2. If your machine has rotating handles (rather than longer, fixed horizontal handles), you cannot adjust your grip width, but you can rotate your palms to face forward for this variation.
3. While holding the handles, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad. Slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. When seated, allow your elbows to straighten in a controlled manner and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for individuals who are tall and have long arms), lowering the seat height or widening your grip may be necessary to achieve resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down toward the sides of your torso while progressively bending them to pull the handles downward.
    1. Maintain the same slight backward lean of your torso that you adopted while setting up, rather than progressively leaning further backward.
    2. Allow your elbows to naturally flare outward from the midline of your body in a manner that feels comfortable to you during the initial portion of the pull.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion until you can no longer drive your elbows closer toward your torso, which often results in the handles finishing below the height of your ears and slightly above the tops of your shoulders.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten in a controlled manner.
    1. You should finish with your arms positioned fully overhead with straight elbows.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance swing your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your lat and teres major especially well while also effectively training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable cable station’s pulley to its highest setting, or approach a cable lat pulldown machine.
    1. Either type of equipment can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin, and connect a rope attachment to the cable.
3. While facing the cable station, reach up and firmly grasp the ends of the rope with straight elbows while keeping your wrists fairly straight.
    1. You can point your palms in toward each other and grasp each end of the rope with your little fingers and the adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Simultaneously wrap your thumbs over the outside of your other fingers for a stable grip.
4. While keeping your arms outstretched with straight elbows in line with the cable, walk backward until you feel tension from the cable as the weight stack separates.
5. Sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and moderately incline your torso while keeping your arms in line with the cable and your back flat.
    1. The appropriate degree of torso incline should allow you to draw a mostly straight line from your arms through your shoulders to your hips when viewed from the side. From this position, the cable’s resistance should situate your arms fully overhead relative to what your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straightened and your torso inclined, sweep your arms down and back toward your sides in an arcing motion until your hands are right by your thighs.
    1. During the initial half of this movement, you can focus on driving down, while you can focus on pulling back during the latter half of this movement.
    2. As your arms move through motion at your shoulders, the rest of your body should be stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arms to sweep forward and upward in a controlled arcing manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms fully overhead in line with the cable.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straightened and your torso inclined.
    2. While you do not have to reverse the movement with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the cable abruptly swing your arms upward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, you can stand upright and walk forward to return the cable to its initial position.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres major, and rear delt, which are assisted by the lowest portion of your pec major and the long head of your triceps in the overhead position of each rep.## Setting up
1. After selecting your weight using the machine’s loading pin, adjust the seat or thigh pad height to a level that allows you to position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Certain machine models have an adjustable seat, others have an adjustable thigh pad, and some have both an adjustable seat and an adjustable thigh pad.
2. While facing the thigh pads and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) as they are fairly wide apart.
    1. Your hands should be clearly outside shoulder width, resulting in your arms forming a “Y-shape” with your torso.
    2. While being influenced by the length of your machine’s handles, specifically how wide a grip you use is up to your preference.
3. While holding the handles, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad. Slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten in a controlled manner and your arms to raise up overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for individuals who are tall and have long arms), lowering the seat height or widening your grip may be necessary to achieve resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down toward the sides of your torso while progressively bending them to pull the handles downward.
    1. Maintain the same slight backward lean of your torso that you adopted while setting up, rather than progressively leaning further backward.
    2. Allow your elbows to naturally flare outward from the midline of your body in a manner that feels comfortable to you during the initial portion of the pull.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion until you can no longer drive your elbows closer to your torso, which often results in the handles finishing below the height of your ears and slightly above the tops of your shoulders.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten in a controlled manner.
    1. You should finish with your arms positioned fully overhead with straight elbows.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance swing your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your lat and teres major especially well while also effectively training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Stand tall upright with an upright posture and your feet about hip width apart.
    1. Position your arms wherever you prefer throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Take a deep breath, and then fully exhale until you feel that you have released all the air you can.
2. Without inhaling, suck your belly button in toward your spine with the intent to narrow your waist fully.
3. Statically hold this position for your target duration (e.g., 10-15 seconds) before inhaling and relaxing your abdomen.
    1. Regardless of whether you reached your target duration, you should resume breathing if you start feeling lightheaded when holding your stomach vacuum.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While engaging your transversus abdominis, training stomach vacuums can help improve posing for bodybuilders.## Setting up
1. Position a seated exercise bench (or an adjustable bench positioned fully upright) centered in front of two pulleys from a dual cable station.
    1. Place the bench far enough in front of the pulleys that the cables will be able to provide resistance to you when your arms are fully spread apart in the starting position.
2. Adjust each pulley to be near the height of your lower chest when you are seated on the bench.
    1. If you are using a machine (like the Freemotion dual cable cross) that has its pulleys connected to adjustable arms that can be swiveled forward or backward, you can set the arms so that their ends are somewhat less than the length of your wingspan apart from each other. Ideally, the ends of the lever arms can be situated just close enough together that you can grasp both handles at the same time rather than having to grasp one cable and then step over to the other cable to reach it.
3. Connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
4. After standing with one pulley by your side while facing forward toward the bench, lunge down to assume a half-kneeling position.
5. With your hand that is closer to the pulley, grasp the handle with your elbow fully bent, palm angled inward and forward, and upper arm by your side. If the other pulley is close enough for you to do so, grasp its handle the same way.
    1. This arm position should resemble the starting position of a neutral grip dumbbell overhead press.
6. Stand upright while maintaining the arm position you just adopted. If the pulleys were too far apart for you to grasp both handles during the last step, stand upright, and step over to the other pulley to lunge down and grasp its handle in the same manner you just did before standing upright again.
7. Walk forward on one side of the bench until you can straddle the bench and sit down with your back snugly against the back pad.
    1. Keep your elbows bent with the handles close to you while walking forward against the cables’ resistance.
    2. Place your feet flat on the floor or against the foot bar if your seated bench has a foot bar.
8. Pull your shoulder blades back together with your chest up to assume a “proud chest” posture.
9. Flare your elbows out away from your sides to chest height and point them toward the pulleys. Simultaneously, allow the cables to pull your elbows behind your torso through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. You should now look like you are in the starting position of a seated cable chest press.
10. Point your palms in toward each other before letting your elbows straighten until they are only mildly bent.
    1. Once your elbows are mildly bent, your palms should be pointing forward.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arms forward and in toward each other in an arcing manner while keeping your elbows mildly bent.
    1. You should finish with your hands about to touch each other in front of your chest.
    2. The wide arcing motion should resemble hugging a thick barrel, as all the motion comes from your shoulders while you keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the set.
    3. As your hands approach each other, it is natural for your shoulder blades to move mildly forward, but you should avoid letting your shoulders round forward into a slouched posture.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arms to spread apart from each other in a controlled manner as they arc backward through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. You should finish in a “proud chest” posture with your chest up and shoulder blades pulled back.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders with your arms fully spread apart, but you should not experience any shoulder discomfort.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows only mildly bent as avoid bending your elbows any further and turning this exercise into a chest press.
    4. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, avoid letting the cables abruptly swing your arms apart in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, bend your elbows to bring the handles in front of your shoulders to return to the position you adopted when initially sitting down. Then stand up, step to one side of the bench, and walk backward while keeping your arms close to your torso before returning the cables to their starting position.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Select your weight with the machine’s loading pin, and adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad.
    1. As a starting point, you can set the seat to a height that situates the horizontal handles level with the middle of your chest when you are seated. From this point, you can adjust the seat height based on what feels best to you with the specific machine model you are using.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, set it to a position that allows you to grasp the horizontal handles when your arms are fully outstretched forward in front of you with straight elbows.
    1. To be able to row through your full range of motion, you should ideally situate the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with fully straightened elbows.
3. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet flat on the floor.
4. Reach forward to grasp the horizontally directed handles firmly with your palms facing down (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands clearly outside shoulder width.
    1. The appropriate grip width for this upper-back focused variation should naturally let your elbows flare prominently out away from your sides. At the end of your row, this should result in your upper arms being closer to forming a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from behind, rather than being closer to being tucked by your sides (which can naturally occur with a closer grip).
    2. To find a viable wide grip based on your typical grip, you can place your index fingers on the handles where you would otherwise place your little fingers when performing your standard overhand grip machine row.
    3. If your machine does not have an adjustable chest pad and the handles are far enough from you to make it difficult to grasp both of them simultaneously, it may be easier for you to grasp one handle at a time.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward you by driving your elbows backward and outward while bending them. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Rather than trying to keep them tucked close to your sides, allow your elbows to flare out meaningfully away from your sides to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    2. Keep your chest in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten until your arms are fully outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your traps, rhomboids, and rear delts while also training your biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. Add weights plates of your desired load to the machine, and adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad.
    1. As a starting point, you can set the seat to a height that situates the horizontal handles level with the middle of your chest when you are seated. From this point, you can adjust the seat height based on what feels best to you with the specific machine model you are using.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, set it to a position that allows you to grasp the horizontal handles when your arms are fully outstretched forward in front of you with straight elbows.
    1. To be able to row through your full range of motion, you should ideally situate the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with fully straightened elbows.
3. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet flat on the floor.
4. Reach forward to grasp the horizontally directed handles firmly with your palms facing down (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands clearly outside shoulder width.
    1. The appropriate grip width for this upper-back focused variation should naturally let your elbows flare prominently out away from your sides. At the end of your row, this should result in your upper arms being closer to forming a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from behind, rather than being closer to being tucked by your sides (which can naturally occur with a closer grip).
    2. To find a viable wide grip based on your typical grip, you can place your index fingers on the handles where you would otherwise place your little fingers when performing your standard overhand grip machine row.
    3. If your machine does not have an adjustable chest pad and the handles are far enough from you to make it difficult to grasp both of them simultaneously, it may be easier for you to grasp one handle at a time.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward you by driving your elbows backward and outward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Rather than trying to keep them tucked close to your sides, allow your elbows to flare out meaningfully away from your sides to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    2. Keep your chest in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten until your arms are fully outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop much of your upper back, including your rear delt, traps, rhomboids, infraspinatus, and teres minor.## Setting up
1. Lie down on your back with your knees bent to about 90 degrees.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can lie down on an exercise mat.
2. With straight elbows, raise both arms straight up toward the ceiling as your elbows and wrists are vertically stacked above your shoulders.
    1. You can point your palms in toward each other.
3. While keeping your knees bent to about 90 degrees, march both legs up until your knees are vertically stacked above your hips.
    1. In this position, your thighs should be perpendicular to the ground, and your shins should be parallel to the ground.
4. Based on your preference, you can keep your head resting on the ground, or you can slightly tuck your chin toward your chest to lift your head just barely off the ground.
    1. Lifting your head slightly off the ground can mildly increase the difficulty and allow you to train your neck muscles statically, but doing so is optional. If you opt to lift your head just off the ground, keep it that position throughout your set. Otherwise, continue resting your head on the ground during the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. In this starting position, your lower back should be near a neutral position with the natural mild curvature you have when normally standing upright, rather than being excessively arched or flattened.
2. Sweep arms backward and downward while simultaneously straightening your legs and lowering them toward the ground. Throughout the entire exercise, rigidly maintain the mild curvature of your lower back that naturally have.
    1. As you lower your arms and legs, you will need to continue bracing your core to prevent your lower back from arching further. Unintended arching would be demonstrated by an increase in the space between the ground and your lower back, so you can focus on maintaining this space each rep.
    2. Strive to move your arms and legs at the same rate through the full range of motion you can without dynamically arching your lower back.
    3. With sufficient core control, you can finish with your arms and legs parallel to the ground as they are fully straightened. From this position, you can gently tap the ground with your hands and heels simultaneously. If your lower back starts arching to a greater degree before this point, shorten the range of motion to what you can perform without further arching.
    4. All the movement should occur from your shoulders, hips, and knees as your spine remains motionless.
    5. Move slowly and deliberately throughout your set, focusing on high-quality technique at every degree of motion.
3. Reverse the movement by lifting your arms and legs up to return to the starting position.
    1. Since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the lowering phase, no motion should occur at your lower back as you continue bracing your core.
    2. While keeping your elbows straight, bend your knees as you march your thighs up to finish with your knees bent to approximately 90 degrees.
    3. At the top, both of your knees should be stacked vertically above your hips, and your arms should be vertically stacked above your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your rectus abdominis and obliques to maintain a rigid back position as you refine your ability to move through your hips and shoulders with a stable trunk.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on a bench with your torso upright, feet flat on the floor, and arms hanging straight down.
    1. You can opt to sit down on a seated bench with your back resting against the back support, or you can sit on a flat bench without any back support.
## Exercise technique
1. While holding the dumbbells by your sides with straight elbows, direct your palms forward to adopt an underhand grip.
2. Curl the dumbbells upward in an arcing manner by bending your elbows without rotating your palms.
    1. Keep your wrists straight and your elbows by your sides, rather than swinging your elbows forward through shoulder motion.
    2. While your forearms move through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    3. Since you will maintain an underhand grip during this lifting phase, your palms should be facing upward when your forearms are parallel to the ground.
    4. At the top of the curl, your elbows should be fully bent with your forearms touching your biceps and your palms facing back toward your shoulders.
3. Rotate your palms forward away from you to adopt an overhand grip, which is also called a reverse grip.
4. While keeping your wrists straight, lower the dumbbells in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to straighten progressively without rotating your palms.
    1. Since you will maintain a reverse grip during this lowering phase, your palms should be facing down when your forearms are parallel to the ground before facing backward once you have fully straightened your elbows.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the dumbbells down.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can develop your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis while also training your wrist extensor muscles more effectively than most curl variations.## Setting up
1. In an approximately hip-width stance, face a stable object with a flat, elevated surface that you will use for this push-up variation.
    1. So long as the object can support your bodyweight and will not slide, you can use a variety of objects based on the degree of difficulty you wish to have and what is available to you.
    2. As the height of the surface decreases, the difficulty of the exercise will increase from being able to adopt a closer to horizontal torso position, where gravity can provide greater resistance.
    3. A flat exercise bench (which typically has a surface near 18in (46cm) above the ground) can be appropriate to use in a gym as an initial starting point for many people.
    4. In addition to a plyometric box, an aerobic step elevated on top of risers can also be a quality object to use, since you can adjust the height (typically in 2in (5cm) increments) to match the specific level of difficulty you wish to have.
    5. For a lower intensity variation at home, furniture like a bed can alternatively be used to elevate your hands.
2. After leaning forward at your hips, place your palms on top of the elevated object with the base of your palms on the corner as your elbows are straight, and your hands are mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other.
    1. In this position, your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
    2. Based on your preference, you can spread your fingers somewhat apart from each other or keep them close together.
3. While bearing weight through your hands and allowing your heels to lift off the ground, walk your feet back until your hips and knees are straightened as your arms form approximately 90-degree angles with your torso.
    1. After doing so, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
    2. Your toes and the balls of your feet should be on the ground as your feet are approximately hip-width apart.
    3. Keep your neck in a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze diagonally down and forward.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes, which you will continue doing throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag downward, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should allow you to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
2. Push the ground away from you by spreading your shoulder blades apart as far as you can while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. While prompting your upper back to round mildly, this will lift your chest up a little higher above the ground, but it is a fairly small amplitude of motion.
    2. If you performed this shoulder blade motion when standing with your arms outstretched parallel to the ground, your shoulder blades would move forward.
    3. As a cue, you can imagine what it would feel like if you were leaning forward with your arms outstretched in front of you, and you are trying to touch something just beyond your reach with your elbows fully straightened. Pushing your shoulder blades forward as they spread apart would push your hands slightly forward to touch that object, and you should be performing the same shoulder blade motion during this step.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your blades to move fully back together without bending your elbows.
    1. This will lower your chest mildly toward the ground and result in your chest being closer to the ground than your shoulders.
    2. Allow your shoulder blades to move back as close together as they can without letting your hips sag down through lower back arching.
    3. You do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control the descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically engaging the rectus abdominis, this exercise can effectively develop the large serratus anterior and the smaller pec minor in a targeted manner.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your palms, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent (i.e., the quadruped position, which is also known as being “on all fours”).
    1. In this position, your elbows should be stacked underneath your shoulders with your arms pointing straight down toward the ground.
2. Situate your hands mildly outside shoulder width apart from each other, with your fingers spread somewhat apart from each other.
    1. Your middle fingers should be pointing slightly out to the sides or straight ahead, depending upon what hand position feels most comfortable and stable to you.
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart from each other as your heels are facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this tall plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes, which you will continue doing throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag downward, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should allow you to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
2. Push the ground away from you by spreading your shoulder blades apart as far as you can while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. While prompting your upper back to round mildly, this will lift your chest up a little higher above the ground, but it is a fairly small amplitude of motion.
    2. If you performed this shoulder blade motion when standing with your arms outstretched parallel to the ground, your shoulder blades would move forward.
    3. As a cue, you can imagine what it would feel like if you were leaning forward with your arms outstretched in front of you, and you are trying to touch something just beyond your reach with your elbows fully straightened. Pushing your shoulder blades forward as they spread apart would push your hands slightly forward to touch that object, and you should be performing the same shoulder blade motion during this step.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your blades to move fully back together without bending your elbows.
    1. This will lower your chest mildly toward the ground and result in your chest being closer to the ground than your shoulders.
    2. Allow your shoulder blades to move back as close together as they can without letting your hips sag down through lower back arching.
    3. You do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control the descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically engaging the rectus abdominis, this exercise can effectively develop the large serratus anterior and the smaller pec minor in a targeted manner.## Setting up
1. Connect your preferred bar attachment to a seated cable row machine, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. You can perform this exercise with essentially any row attachment that you can comfortably hold throughout the set, so it is up to you which specific attachment you use. If you are unsure what to select initially, you can use a standard lat pulldown attachment with a straight shaft.
    2. If your machine allows you to adjust the seat height, you can set it to a height that allows the cable to run approximately horizontally during this exercise rather than meaningfully angling upward or downward. A seat height that situates the pulley a little lower than your chest (once you are seated with an upright torso) is normally appropriate.
2. Sit down on the seat, and position your feet in contact with the machine’s foot plates as your knees are mildly bent.
    1. The appropriate degree of knee bend should allow you to feel stable and comfortable throughout the exercise while experiencing resistance throughout its full range of motion. If you lose resistance (from the weight you selected contacting the rest of the weight stack) before you reach the end range to which you can comfortably round your back during the exercise, you can adopt a lesser degree of knee bend to scoot yourself further back.
3. Fully reach your arms forward, and lean forward at your hips until you can firmly grasp the bar you attached.
    1. Specifically how you grasp the bar will be influenced by which attachment you selected, but the grip you use should feel comfortable and stable.
    2. If you initially cannot reach the bar, you can scoot forward closer toward the cable by bending your knees further.
4. While holding the bar with straight elbows, lean your torso backward by moving at your hips until you are sitting with an upright torso.
## Exercise technique
1. In a slow and controlled manner, lean forward by rounding your entire back through your full comfortable range of motion.
    1. You should finish with your back comfortably rounded and arms fully outstretched in front of you with straight elbows.
    2. Rather than moving only one part of your back, strive to round your lower, middle, and upper back simultaneously, which will bring the bottom of your ribs toward the top of your hip bones.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation throughout your back in this position, but you should not experience lower back discomfort. If you begin feeling lower back discomfort at the end range, it may be appropriate not to round your back quite as much, since you should only move through what is your back’s comfortable range of motion.
    4. While you do not need to perform this step extremely slowly, using a reasonably slow tempo is recommended to help reinforce the controlled technique desirable for this variation.
2. Reverse the movement by extending (straightening) your back in a controlled manner until you are sitting with a fully upright torso position.
    1. At the end of this step, you should have a “proud chest” posture, and your lower back should exhibit the mild curvature it naturally has when you are standing upright. This should result in your shoulders being vertically aligned above your hips rather than behind your hips, which could otherwise occur if you excessively arch your lower back from exaggerating leaning back.
    2. Keep your elbows straight as you uncurl your back through spinal motion while minimizing any hip motion.
    3. You should feel your spinal extensor muscles, which run along each side of the spine, contract to extend your back.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep intrinsic spinal extensor muscles that run along the length of your entire back.## Setting up
1. Position yourself with your hands, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight, and your knees are bent.
2. Prop your forearms onto the ground with your upper arms directed straight down such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    1. Based on your preference, you can make a fist with each hand and direct your palms in toward each other, or you can place your palms flat on the ground.
3. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. You should be bearing your bodyweight into the ground through your elbows and feet.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
## Exercise technique
1. From this forearm plank position, tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes, which you will continue doing throughout your set.
    1. Contracting your abs and glutes will help you keep your back in a mostly flat position with your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting your hips sag downward, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. This should allow you to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
2. Push the ground away from you by spreading your shoulder blades apart as far as you can while keeping your elbows and forearms on the ground.
    1. While prompting your upper back to round mildly, this will lift your chest up a little higher above the ground, but it is a fairly small amplitude of motion.
    2. If you performed this shoulder blade motion when standing with your arms outstretched parallel to the ground, your shoulder blades would move forward.
    3. As a cue, you can imagine what it would feel like if you were leaning forward with your arms outstretched in front of you, and you are trying to touch something just beyond your reach with your elbows fully straightened. Pushing your shoulder blades forward as they spread apart would push your hands slightly forward to touch that object, and you should be performing the same shoulder blade motion during this step.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your blades to move back as close together as they can without letting your hips sag down through lower back arching.
    1. This will lower your chest mildly toward the ground and result in your chest being closer to the ground than your shoulders.
    2. You do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control the descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically engaging the rectus abdominis, this exercise can effectively develop the large serratus anterior and the smaller pec minor in a targeted manner.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a sled situated in an area where you have sufficient space for pushing it.
    1. With certain wheeled sleds, you may also be able to adjust your resistance level with a lever that changes its friction. For these sleds, increasing the friction will increase the intensity of the exercise, similar to adding weight plates.
    2. Note that the type of surface on which you will move the sled will affect how much resistance you experience during this exercise. When tracking progress, it is consequently best to compare sets performed on the same surface.
2. Stand centered directly behind the sled in a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead.
3. With mostly straight wrists, firmly grasp the handles in a position that is comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Position the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) directly behind the handles so that you can push primarily through the bases of your palms.
    2. Sleds commonly have vertical posts that function as handles, but some models have horizontal handles or handles that are angled diagonally. For this bent-arm variation, it is best to use handles that are vertical or angled diagonally.
    3. With vertical handles, you can direct your palms in toward each other and adjust how high up you grasp them. Initially gripping the handles a little below hip height is an appropriate starting point. However, a range of grip heights can be viable, so feel free to try out different hand positions over time to determine what feels best to you.
    4. With angled handles, you can grasp them with palms angled inward and downward.
4. Take a step backward with one foot and lean your torso forward while moderately bending your front leg’s knee and keeping your rear leg’s knee mildly bent. Allow your elbows to bend naturally while leaning into the sled as you keep your elbows aligned with your wrists, chest up, and back flat.
    1. Rather than simply rounding your back, the forward torso lean should arise from you hinging at your front leg’s hip.
    2. The degree to which you should lean your torso will be affected by the height of your hands when holding the handles. As a general rule of thumb, it is often appropriate to lean your torso forward by about 45 degrees, but you should feel free to try out different degrees of forward lean to determine what personally feels best to you.
    3. When leaning into the sled, it is natural for your elbows to bend greater than 90 degrees, with your forearms reasonably close to being parallel to the ground during this variation. Specifically how much you bend your elbows should be based on which position feels the most stable and comfortable while pushing the sled.
    4. Rather than being closer together or farther apart than your wrists (when viewed from the front or back), your elbows should be stacked behind your wrists for stability.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position as you push through your legs.
2. Begin pushing the sled forward by pushing through your rear leg. Drive your rear knee backward while straightening it and pushing off through your foot in a calf raise-like ankle motion.
    1. It can help to think about pushing the ground away from you.
    2. Strive to push through your rear leg until you can draw a straight line from that leg through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side.
    3. While the toes and the ball of your foot remain on the ground, the rest of your rear foot’s sole should be off the ground and facing backward.
    4. Since you are starting with your rear knee only mildly bent, this initial step involves a short range of motion to start moving the sled
3. As you finish driving through your rear leg, push through your front leg to continue pushing the sled forward. Simultaneously, march what initially was your rear leg forward as you drive that knee forward while bending it.
    1. Keep your back flat and chest up the whole time while you maintain the same degree of forward torso lean.
    2. All the motion should come from your hips, knees, and ankles while the rest of your body remains in essentially a static position throughout your set.
    3. Rather than using your upper body to push the sled, your arms should simply function to transmit force from your legs to the sled.
4. Continue pushing the sled forward as you reciprocally move your legs until you have reached your target distance or the end of your available space, and have to switch to pushing it in the opposite direction.
    1. With some sled models, you may have to rotate your sled 180 degrees to be able to access the same type of handles before pushing the sled in the opposite direction. With other models, you may be able to walk to the other side of the sled and access another pair of handles.
## Description
This exercise will train your gluteus maximus, quads, adductor magnus, gastrocnemius, and soleus as they function to extend your hips, knees, and ankles simultaneously.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to be near chest height, connect a rope attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing the cable station, grasp each end of the rope with your index fingers and adjacent aspects of your hands contacting the flat surfaces of the attachment’s rubber stoppers. Wrap your thumbs over the rounded outer aspects of the stoppers.
    1. If you are using a long strap attachment with looped ends rather than ropes, you naturally will situate your hands within the loops and grasp the inner part of each loop.
3. With your elbows straightened and arms raised to be parallel with the cable, step backward until the machine’s weight plates separate and you feel tension from the cable.
    1. In this position, your palms should be directed down as your arms are outstretched in front of your torso while you are standing upright with your hands close to each other.
4. With your feet near hip width apart, slightly bend your knees, and adopt a staggered stance with one foot mildly in front of the other, or keep your feet symmetrical with each other.
    1. Utilize whichever stance helps you feel the most stable and balanced.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows backward while flaring them straight out away from your sides to pull the rope toward your shoulders.
    1. While emphasizing driving your elbows back behind you, your elbows will naturally bend without having to focus on pulling with your hands.
    2. Your elbows should finish pointing straight out to your sides as your upper arms are approximately parallel with the ground, forming a “T-shape” with your torso
    3. Finish pulling with your shoulder blades fully pulled back together and your chest up in a “proud chest” posture.
    4. In contrast to a face pull, your hands should remain in front of your shoulders rather than above your shoulders during this high row.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to arc forward and in toward each other while naturally straightening.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to spread apart naturally from each other as your arms move in line with the cable’s resistance.
    2. You should finish in the starting position with your elbows straightened and arms outstretched in front of you.
    3. You do not have to perform this phase with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your traps, rhomboids, and rear delts while also training your biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your torso against the chest pad and enables you to row comfortably with your elbow tucked fairly close to your side.
    1. The appropriate seat height often naturally allows you to finish the lifting phase with your elbow bent by about 90 degrees.
    2. If the seat is too low, the upward angle in which the handle moves during the lifting phase can cause the row to feel awkward as you have to shrug your shoulder up and/or meaningfully flare your elbow out away from your side.
    3. If the seat is too high, you may notice that you have to pack your shoulder blade down to reach the handles with your arm directed further downward than forward. This could limit your range of motion and prompt the row to not feel as natural as it otherwise could.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, set it to a position that allows you to just grasp the handle when your arm is fully outstretched with a straight elbow.
    1. Machines often lack an adjustable chest pad.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit down on the seat with your torso against the chest pad.
    1. Based on your preference, you can position your feet against the machine’s foot plates (if your machine model has foot plates), or you can place your feet flat on the floor.
    2. As an alternative to sitting on the seat, you optionally can adopt a staggered stance with one foot forward and the other backward as you lightly contact the seat with your buttocks while predominantly bearing weight through your feet.
    3. Set up in whichever position feels most stable and comfortable to you.
4. With the side you are going to train, reach forward and firmly grasp the handle with your palm angled inward (i.e., a neutral or semi-neutral grip).
    1. Some machines have handles that can freely rotate, but most have a pair of fixed handles angled downward that you can grasp with your palms facing inward.
    2. Hold the handle with a straight wrist and elbow.
    3. If your machine has a stationary center handle (that is not connected to either of the machine’s mobile lever arms), you can grasp it with your free hand and continue holding it throughout the set for additional stability. Otherwise, place your free hand wherever you prefer.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handle back toward you by driving your elbow backward while bending it. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade fully back.
    1. Rather than meaningfully flaring it out away from your side, keep your elbow tucked reasonably close to your side to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you. This should keep your forearm aligned with the direction in which you are rowing.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow being slightly behind your torso or at least level with your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    3. Instead of moving straight backward in a fully horizontal direction, this machine type will normally have its handle move at an upward angle while you pull it backward.
    4. Keep your torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to move forward in a controlled manner as it progressively straightens until your arm is fully outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blade will naturally move forward and away from the center of your back, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull it further forward than your resting position at the end, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arm forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley near mid-shin height, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. Securely attach an ankle cuff just above the ankle of the leg you will train. Position the cuff so that one of its attachment rings is directed straight forward.
    1. If you lack an ankle cuff, you can alternatively perform this exercise by connecting a D-handle attachment to the cable and then stepping your foot into the attachment with its handle contacting the back third of your sole. However, this page will describe setting up with the ankle cuff, which is ideal to use given its greater stability.
3. While facing the cable station in a hip-width stance, stand with your cuffed leg directly in front of the pulley.
4. Bend over and connect the cable to your cuff’s attachment to its front ring before grasping part of the cable station (with your fingers away from the weight stack) with your hands.
    1. Continue holding part of the cable station (such as by its vertical beam) from now onward for additional stability.
    2. It will be easiest to connect the cable when your working leg is close enough to the pulley that the cable does not provide resistance when connecting it.
5. While leaning your torso forward at your hips, step backward until you are in a position where you can march your cuffed leg toward the pulley while still experiencing resistance from the cable.
    1. It is generally appropriate to adopt a degree of torso lean that positions your torso somewhat closer to horizontal than vertical, but you can decide specifically how much forward torso lean you utilize based on what feels best to you.
    2. After stepping backward, your knees should be slightly bent with your feet approximately hip-width apart.
    3. Aim to walk backward far enough that you will not lose tension from the cable at the start of each rep.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Shift your bodyweight into your non-working side’s foot, and allow the cable’s resistance to sweep your cuffed leg forward as your knee naturally bends.
    1. Your cuffed leg should now look like it is in a marching position.
    2. Specifically how far forward the cable pulls your cuffed leg can be affected by the degree to which you leaned your torso forward.
    3. All the subsequent directions will apply to your cuffed side’s leg as you keep your other leg still.
3. Drive your knee backward in an arcing manner through your hip’s full range of motion while squeezing your glute and allowing your knee to straighten naturally.
    1. How far you personally can drive your knee backward will be influenced by your hip mobility. With normal hip mobility, you should be able to at least reach the point where you can draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side.
    2. Strive to have almost all the motion come from your working leg while keeping the rest of your body stationary. This should result in your torso maintaining essentially the same degree of inclination, with your hip bones fairly level and your back mostly flat. It is not inherently problematic for a mild degree of motion to occur beyond your working leg, but having the intent to minimize it can help prevent it from becoming excessive.
    3. Aim to avoid arching your lower back excessively at the top, which can occur when kicking your leg upward as high as you can rather than driving it back until you have fully squeezed your glute.
    4. Try to keep your imaginary belt buckle facing the same direction throughout the set instead of letting it angle out to the side from hiking your working leg’s hip bone up.
    5. Rather than focusing on kicking your foot backward, thinking about sweeping your knee back through hip motion may help cue the intended glute engagement. If you wish to do so, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment, which may additionally assist with feeling your glute engage.
4. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting your knee arc forward in line with the cable’s resistance while naturally bending.
    1. You should finish with your working leg’s knee mildly in front of your other knee.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the cable abruptly swing your leg forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with your middle-to-upper chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is excessively low or high, the exercise may feel awkward and uncomfortable.
2. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the stoppers (that are positioned under the arms) to a height that allows you to press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with proper positioning, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
    3. Various machine models do not allow you to adjust the stoppers.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground under your bent knees.
    2. If the back pad is sufficiently tall, rest your head against it. Otherwise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. On the side of your body you will train during this set, firmly grasp one of the handles with your palm angled down. Stack your elbow with your wrist such that your forearm is aligned with the incline angle at which you will press.
    1. Grasping the handle where it would result in a moderately outside shoulder-width grip if you used both handles works well for many lifters, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best while being able to achieve the aforementioned stacking of your elbow and wrist.
    2. In contrast to a purely horizontal seated chest press, where you would position your elbow at the height of your wrist, your elbow should be lower than your wrist during this incline variation. Specifically how much lower you should position your elbow will be affected by the machine model you are using, but machines commonly allow you to press at an angle that is 30-45 degrees beyond horizontal. Consequently, you can initially position your elbow so that your forearm forms approximately a 30-45 angle with the ground. After starting your set, you can allow this forearm angle based on what feels most natural and comfortable with the machine you are using.
    3. Aligning your forearm with the incline angle at which you will press will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    4. Hold the handle on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip throughout your set. Squeezing the handle firmly can help promote this wrist position.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping your forearm aligned with the angle at which you are pressing, press the handle forward and upward until you have straightened your elbow.
    1. You can think about driving your elbow diagonally forward and upward as it progressively straightens.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handle to move downward and backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your forearm aligned with the angle of your press.
    2. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arm to the stopper underneath it or stop right before that point.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arm abruptly back in an uncontrolled fashion.
    4. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulder at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to be slightly above shoulder height, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. While standing with the side of your shoulder (on the side of your body you will train during this set) facing the pulley, reach out to your side and firmly grasp the handle with a straight elbow.
3. Take a step out to the side away from the pulley while allowing the resistance from the cable to keep your arm raised out to the side.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can stand in a staggered stance with one foot further in front than the other, or you can utilize a near shoulder width symmetrical stance.
4. Rotate your palm to point up toward the ceiling with a straight wrist if it is not already facing this way.
    1. Situate your free hand wherever you like throughout the exercise.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbow to curl the handle in toward your ear. Keep your upper arm stationary as it remains directed toward the pulley.
    1. You should finish with your elbow fully bent as your forearm is contacting your biceps or is close to doing so. Depending on the length of your forearm, you may not be able to fully bend your elbow if your hand is about to contact your head.
    2. At the end of this movement, you should look like you are flexing for a biceps pose.
    3. While you move your forearm through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain still throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to straighten to move the handle outward in a controlled manner.
    1. Finish with your elbow fully straightened, like in the starting position.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank the handle outward in an uncontrolled manner.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side as you face the opposite direction.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Securely anchor a long elastic band of your desired tension level above head height.
    1. Based on your equipment availability and preference, you can use a looped band or a tube band.
    2. With a looped elastic band, you can anchor around a pull-up bar or the top of a power rack. To do so, you can initially drape the band over the bar, loop one end of the band into the middle of the other end, and pull that end until it is taut.
    3. In addition to draping the middle of a tube band over a pull-up bar or the top of a power rack, you can also set up a tube band by sliding it through a specific tube band anchor point that can be secured between a closed door and its door frame. Tube bands are commonly sold with D-handle attachments that you can optionally connect to each end of the band if you prefer to grip the handles rather than the band directly during this exercise.
    4. If you are anchoring your band to a pull-up bar that has rough knurling rather than a smooth surface, anchoring the band directly around the bar can increase the likelihood that the band will tear when using it over time. Initially placing a fat grip attachment or hand towel around a bar before anchoring the band can help stop a bar with knurling from tearing a band.
    5. Prior to using it, it is recommended to inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
2. Stand directly in front of the band and firmly grasp its ends with your elbows fully bent by your sides. Keep your wrists fairly straight.
    1. If you are not using a band with D-handle attachments, you can directly grasp the ends of the bands with a neutral grip as your palms are facing in toward each other.
    2. If you opted to connect D-handle attachments to your bands, you can grasp the handles with an overhand grip as your palms are angled forward and downward.
    3. Based on the length of your band and how high up you anchored it, you may initially have to reach up to grasp it before pulling your elbows back down by your sides against the band’s resistance.
3. If the band is slack when standing upright directly next to the anchor point, step backward, and mildly lean forward at your hips until the band is taut.
    1. Alternatively, you can kneel on the ground with your torso upright to remove any slack from the band if the band is not particularly close to being taut during step 2.
    2. The appropriate starting position should allow you to feel tension from the band with your elbows fully bent by your sides. Simultaneously, you should be able to straighten your elbows fully without overstretching the band, which can be indicated by an abrupt increase in resistance from the band at the bottom of the rep.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows pinned by your sides, push the ends of the band downward by straightening your elbows
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened, where you optionally can focus on squeezing your triceps for a moment if you wish to do so.
    2. While your forearms arc down toward your sides through motion at your elbows, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the set. This will involve keeping your shoulders, torso, and hips still, whether you are standing or kneeling.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to bend in a controlled manner to raise your forearms and the ends of the band upward.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows pinned by your sides rather than letting them swing forward through shoulder motion.
    2. To train your triceps through a full range of motion, strive to finish with your elbows fully bent as your forearms are contacting your biceps.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should actively control it rather than letting the band abruptly yank your hands upward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can develop all three heads of your triceps, which constitute the majority of muscle mass in your upper arms.## Setting up
1. Set a pair of kettlebells close together (without touching each other) on the ground with their handles facing up. Angle them so that their handles form a “V-shape” together.
2. On the side of the kettlebells where their handles form a “V-shape” when looking down, stand upright in a near shoulder-width stance with the kettlebells centered mildly in front of your toes.
    1. When standing on the appropriate side of the kettlebells, the horns (i.e., side handles) closer to you should be closer together than the horns further in front of you. If the handles form an inverted “V-shape,” you are standing on the incorrect side of the kettlebells.
    2. You specifically can position your toes one “foot length” behind the weights by initially standing with the kettlebells directly in front of your toes. Then take a step back with one foot until its big toe is just behind the heel of your front foot, before stepping back the same distance with your front foot.
3. While keeping your back flat, hinge at your hips to incline your torso while allowing your knees to bend until the handles are within your reach.
    1. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have hinged through your available hip range of motion (which should prompt you to feel a hamstring stretch), you can bend your knees further to reach the handle.
    2. More motion should come from your hips than your knees, and you should minimize forward knee tracking, since you are not squatting. Try to keep your shins as close to vertical as you can without rounding your back.
    3. Keep your neck and back near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Reach forward with your hands and grasp the kettlebells by the closer corners of their handles. Angle your palms in toward you with the handles running diagonally across your palms.
    1. Your hands should be directly next to where the top handles meet the horns, and your wrists should be fairly straight.
    2. Due to the diagonal angling of the kettlebells, the bases of your thumbs should be angled backward and in toward each other.
## Exercise technique
1. Tilt the kettlebells slightly toward you on the closer edge of their bases by pulling your shoulders slightly away from your ears (opposite to if you were shrugging your shoulders up).
    1. In this starting position, you should have your back locked into a stable position with the muscles running up the length of your back fully tensed.
    2. The edge of the kettlebell’s base further in front of you should now be just off the ground
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help maintain a rigid trunk.
3. Sweep the kettlebells back between your legs while keeping your torso inclined, lower back flat, and chest up.
    1. You should look like you are hiking two footballs (referring to American football) while moving your shoulders as you would do during a straight arm lat pulldown.
    2. The objective is to generate some momentum and shift the weight into a position that loads your hips.
4. Once your forearms contact your inner thighs, explosively thrust your hips forward to drive your body into an upright position and swing the kettlebells out in front of you.
    1. Instead of pulling with your arms, the power from your hips is what should chiefly drive the kettlebells up. First and foremost, a kettlebell clean is a kettlebell swing, and your hips are the key to swinging the kettlebells into the front rack position.
    2. Finish thrusting forward by momentarily squeezing your glutes as you avoid arching your lower back excessively. Stay tall while looking straight ahead rather than leaning back or tilting your head up at the top.
    3. While you focus on driving your hips, your knees will naturally straighten as well. At the top of your swing, you should essentially be able to draw a straight line (when viewed from the side) from your shoulders to your feet that moves through your hips and knees.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck and back locked in the same position in which you started while keeping the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground.
5. As the kettlebells reach the peak of the swing, roll your arms around them while bending your elbows and pulling them back to move your forearms into a mostly vertical position. Simultaneously, shoot your hands through the handles to allow the kettlebells to rest against the back of your forearms at the top with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Keep the kettlebells close to your torso as you let the kettlebells roll around your forearms in a controlled manner, rather than flipping the kettlebells over and having them crash into your forearms.
    2. At the end of this step, the kettlebells should feel supported by your forearms rather than you having to grip the weights forcefully to keep them in position. Rather than keeping them fully vertical, allowing the forearms to angle slightly inward (such that your wrists are slightly closer to the midline of your body than your elbows) can maximize stability in this front rack position.
    3. You should finish with your palms facing in toward each other and your elbows slightly in front of your ribcage.
    4. If you are not experienced with holding kettlebells in the front rack position and/or you are using rather heavy weights, it is normal to experience some discomfort from the kettlebell pressing into your forearms, but the appropriate position should not feel painful.
6. Reverse the movement by guiding the kettlebells back down in the opposite manner to how you cleaned them. Let the kettlebells fall forward as you swing your arms down while straightening your elbows and hinging at your hips to absorb the force of the lowering phase.
    1. Like when setting up, sit your hips back while allowing your knees to bend naturally and keeping your back flat to control the downward swing with your hip muscles.
    2. Let your palms rotate so that they finish facing diagonally back toward you, while your thumbs are angled in toward each other.
7. Alternate between 4-6 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebells down
    1. As an alternative to transitioning continuously from one rep directly into the next, you have the option of setting the kettlebell back down onto the ground and resetting between reps. This approach can help with learning the technique if you find it difficult to transition fluidly between reps.
## Description
This exercise can develop explosive hip extension strength while dynamically training your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Follow the set-up instructions for the single arm kettlebell clean, and perform the lifting phase of the single arm kettlebell clean described in steps 1-5 of its exercise technique instructions.
    1. After doing so, you should now be holding the kettlebell in the front rack position. This involves situating it in front of your shoulder with your elbow bent slightly in front of your ribcage, palm facing in toward your other side, and the weight resting on your forearm, which is mostly vertically with your wrist slightly closer to the midline of your body than your elbow. The handle should also be diagonally contacting your palm, such that the side of the handle by your thumb is angled mildly higher than the side of the handle by your little finger.
    2. As an alternative to cleaning the kettlebell, you can curl it up while using your free hand to help lift the weight up under its base and guide the kettlebell into the same front rack position described above. Initially grasp the handle with your little finger closer to one of the corner horns than your thumb to help the handle rest diagonally across your palm in the front rack position. Feel free to perform a cheat curl-like motion where you initially generate some momentum by swinging the weight mildly backward with a straight elbow before swinging your elbow forward while curling it and assisting with your free hand. This is a less technically complicated means of moving the kettlebell into the front rack, but it is not as efficient when using a heavy weight for lifters who are proficient with cleaning the kettlebell.
    3. Once the kettlebell is in the front rack position, you can keep your free hand wherever you prefer. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the kettlebell.
2. While looking straight ahead with your neck near a neutral position, keep your chest up in a “proud chest” posture, rather than letting your shoulders and upper back round forward into a hunched position.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain its natural curvature like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced throughout your set.
2. Without moving your arm, mildly bend your knees in a quarter squat-like manner to dip down while maintaining an upright torso position with your chest up.
    1. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground during this dip phase.
    2. Your knees should track straight forward toward your toes rather than caving inward.
3. Directly after reaching the bottom of your dip, forcibly push through your feet as you rapidly extend (straighten) your knees and hips to generate upward momentum.
    1. The intent of this leg drive phase should be akin to a jump without lifting your feet fully off the ground.
    2. At the end of this drive, you can either keep the soles of your feet fully on the ground, or you can allow your heels to lift up slightly off the ground while keeping the front of your feet firmly in contact with the ground if a slight heel lift feels natural to you.
4. Immediately after you have finished extending your legs, explosively push the kettlebell upward by forcibly driving your elbow upward as you swiftly straighten your elbow. Allow your elbow to flare out away from your side when driving up, so that your palm faces forward at the top.
    1. At the end of the pressing phase, your knees, hips, and elbow should be fully straightened, and your arm should be directed straight up toward the ceiling with your elbow and wrist situated vertically over your shoulder.
    2. To transfer your leg drive into pushing the kettlebell upward in an explosive manner, you should perform steps 3 and 4 as a continuous movement.
    3. While your forearm starts slightly angled in the front rack position, it should otherwise remain essentially in a vertical position with your elbow stacked under your wrist throughout the press.
    4. Throughout the exercise, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back.
    5. Regardless of whether you lifted your heels up off the ground after extending your legs during the leg drive phase, your heels should be flat on the ground with the rest of your feet at the end of the pressing phase.
5. Lower the kettlebell in a controlled manner as you let your elbow bend while descending and arcing forward toward the front rack position.
6. As you approach the end of the lowering phase, shallowly bend your knees and hips as you did during step 2 (the dip phase) to help control the weight until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Finish with your wrist in front of your shoulder, palm facing in toward your side, and elbow bent slightly in front of your ribcage.
    2. While keeping it vertical throughout most of the lowering phase, allow your forearm to angle slightly inward at the end, resulting in your straight wrist being slightly closer to your midline than your elbow.
7. After you have fully lowered the weight, extend your knees and hips to stand upright out of the dip position before initiating your next rep.
8. Alternate between steps 2-7 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
9. Following your last rep, set the kettlebell down in the opposite manner to how you lifted it into the front rack position when setting up.
    1. If you initially cleaned the kettlebell up, you can let the kettlebell fall forward as you swing your arm down while straightening your elbow and hinging at your hips to absorb the force of the lowering phase.
    2. If you initially curled the kettlebell up with assistance from your free hand, you can similarly use your free hand to help guide the kettlebell down while letting your working arm’s elbow straighten in a controlled manner.
10. Transfer the kettlebell to your other side before repeating the exercise with that side.
## Description
While developing explosive overhead pressing strength and engaging the quads to generate leg drive, this exercise can train your front delts, triceps, lateral delts, serratus anterior, traps, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. In a deadlift motion, pick up a kettlebell and set it onto the ground with its handle facing up.
2. Stand upright in a near shoulder-width stance with the kettlebell centered mildly in front of your toes. The kettlebell should be angled such that the front of its body is facing toward the foot on the same side you are about to train.
    1. The angling of the kettlebell should result in one corner of the kettlebell being slightly closer to you than the other.
    2. You can specifically position your toes one “foot length” behind the weight by initially standing with a kettlebell directly in front of your toes. Then take a step back with one foot until its big toe is just behind the heel of your front foot, before stepping back the same distance with your front foot.
3. Hinge at your hips while allowing your knees to bend until the handle is within your reach. This will incline your torso forward with a flat back.
    1. As a cue for hinging, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have hinged through your available hip range of motion (which should prompt you to feel a hamstring stretch), you can bend your knees further to reach the handle.
    2. More motion should come from your hips than your knees, and you should minimize forward knee tracking, since you are not squatting. Try to keep your shins as close to vertical as you can without rounding your back.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck and back near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Reach forward with one hand, and grasp the kettlebell by the closer corner of its handle with the handle running diagonally across your palm and palm angled toward you.
    1. Your hand should be directly next to where the top handle meets the horn (the side handle), and your wrist should be fairly straight.
    2. Due to the diagonal angling of the kettlebell, the base of your thumb should be slightly closer to you than your little finger.
## Exercise technique
1. Tilt the kettlebell slightly toward you on the closer edge of its base.
    1. In this starting position, you should have your back locked into a stable position with the muscles running up the length of your back fully tensed.
    2. The edge of the kettlebell’s base further in front of you should now be just off the ground
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help maintain a rigid trunk.
3. Sweep the kettlebell back between your legs while keeping your torso inclined, lower back flat, and chest up.
    1. You should look like you are hiking a football (referring to American football).
    2. You should be moving your shoulder as you would during a straight arm lat pulldown.
    3. The objective is to generate some momentum and shift the weight into a position that loads your hips.
4. Once your forearm contacts your inner thighs, explosively thrust your hips forward to drive your body into an upright position and swing the kettlebell out in front of you.
    1. Instead of pulling with your arm, the power from your hips is what should chiefly drive the kettlebell up. First and foremost, a kettlebell clean is a kettlebell swing, and your hips are the key to swinging the kettlebell into the front rack position.
    2. Finish thrusting forward by momentarily squeezing your glutes as you avoid arching your lower back excessively. Stay tall while looking straight ahead rather than leaning back or tilting your head up at the top.
    3. While you focus on driving your hips, your knees will naturally straighten as well. At the top of your swing, you should essentially be able to draw a straight line (when viewed from the side) from your shoulders to your feet that moves through your hips and knees.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck and back locked in the same position in which you started while keeping the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground.
5. As the kettlebell reaches the peak of the swing, roll your arm around it while bending your elbow and pulling it back to move your forearm into a mostly vertical position. Simultaneously, shoot your hand through the handle to allow the kettlebell to rest against the back of your forearm at the top with a mostly straight wrist.
    1. Keep the kettlebell close to your torso as you let the kettlebell roll around your forearm in a controlled manner, rather than flipping the kettlebell over and having it crash into your forearm.
    2. At the end of this step, the kettlebell should feel supported by your forearm rather than you having to grip the weight forcefully to keep it in position. Rather than keeping it fully vertical, allowing the forearm to angle slightly inward (such that your wrist is slightly closer to the midline of your body than your elbow) can maximize stability in this front rack position.
    3. You should finish with your palm facing in toward your other side and your elbow slightly in front of your ribcage.
    4. If you are not experienced with holding a kettlebell in the front rack position and/or you are using a rather heavy weight, it is normal to experience some discomfort from the kettlebell pressing into your forearm, but the appropriate position should not feel painful.
6. Reverse the movement by guiding the kettlebell back down in the opposite manner to how you cleaned it. Let the kettlebell fall forward as you swing your arm down while straightening your elbow and hinging at your hips to absorb the force of the lowering phase.
    1. Like when setting up, sit your hips back while allowing your knees to bend naturally and keeping your back flat to control the downward swing with your hip muscles.
    2. Let your palm rotate so that it finishes facing diagonally back toward you, as the base of your thumb is closer to you than your little finger.
7. Alternate between 4-6 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebell down
    1. As an alternative to transitioning continuously from one rep directly into the next, you have the option of setting the kettlebell back down onto the ground and resetting between reps. This approach can help with learning the technique if you find it difficult to transition fluidly between reps.
## Description
This exercise can develop explosive hip extension strength while dynamically training your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust each pulley from a dual cable station to its lowest height, connect a D-handle attachment to each cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin of each stack.
    1. A dual cable station that can situate the two pulleys no further apart than your wingspan is ideal to use for this variation.
    2. A dual cable station that has its pulleys rather far apart from each other (often with a long upper beam connecting each end of the station) is not as practical to use. With this type of equipment, the single arm cross-body cable Y-raise may be preferable.
2. Stand just in front of one pulley while facing in the direction of its weight stack.
3. Squat down, and grasp its handle with the arm on the side that is further away from the opposite pulley.
    1. For instance, if the opposite pulley is on your left side, grasp the cable in front of you with your right arm
4. Grasp the opposite cable with your free hand, and stand upright centered in between the pulleys.
    1. Your left arm should now be holding the cable on your left side, while your right arm is holding the cable on your right side.
5. Pivot your body to face the opposite direction while stepping over one cable and allowing the cables to cross over behind your back.
    1. With your back facing in the direction of the weight stacks, your left arm should now be holding the cable on your right side, while your right arm is holding the cable on your left side.
6. Take a step forward into a staggered stance with one foot further in front than the other, and your lead knee slightly bent.
    1. Your shoulders should be mildly in front of the pulleys rather than being directly between them, resulting in the cables pulling diagonally backward but mostly inward.
    2. In this staggered stance, you should feel stable and balanced.
7. Assume a “proud chest” posture rather than slumping your shoulders forward. Allow the cables to pull your arms in toward your sides with your elbows straight and palms facing the pulleys if they are not already in this position.
    1. Due to the diagonal line of pull from the cables, your wrists should be slightly behind your shoulders in the starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. In a smooth arcing motion, sweep your arms diagonally upward, outward, and mildly forward away from the pulleys.
    1. Finish with your wrists near shoulder height as your arms are angled outward and slightly forward.
    2. While you raise your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary as you keep your elbows straight.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your arms in toward your sides and allowing them to move mildly back toward the pulleys.
    1. Strive to finish with your wrists near your buttocks as your arms are directed down toward the ground.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cables abruptly yank your arms downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt, front delt, and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your upper trap.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a sled situated in an area where you have sufficient space for pulling it backward.
    1. With certain wheeled sleds, you may also be able to adjust your resistance level with a lever that changes its friction. For these sleds, increasing the friction will increase the intensity of the exercise, similar to adding weight plates.
    2. Note that the type of surface on which you will move the sled will affect how much resistance you experience during this exercise. When tracking progress, it is consequently best to compare sets performed on the same surface.
2. Attach a strap to the base of the sled before connecting a belt to the other end of the strap.
    1. With an appropriate strap, viable belt options include a sled drag belt (that is specifically designed for sled drags), a belt squat belt (that is designed for machine belt squats), or a dip/pull-up belt.
    2. A belt that has padding will naturally be the most comfortable for this exercise.
3. Stand centered behind the sled, step into the belt, and situate the belt around your waist.
    1. Rather than positioning the belt higher on your lower back, it is generally appropriate to have the belt resting on the back of your upper hip bones.
4. While using your hands to keep the belt in place, walk backward until the strap becomes taut with the belt snuggly around your waist.
5. Release your hands from the belt, and position your feet approximately hip-width apart with your toes pointing straight ahead.
6. With your torso upright, adopt a “proud chest” posture with your shoulder blades pulled mildly backward.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. While keeping your torso upright, bend your knees into a near half squat position or your preferred degree of knee bend.
    1. For stimulating increases in quad muscle size and developing strength through a longer range of motion, it is appropriate to reach at least a moderate degree (i.e., about 60 degrees) of knee bend during this exercise. However, you have the option of utilizing a milder degree of knee bend if you wish to do so.
3. Begin dragging the sled backward by straightening one leg’s knee while simultaneously bending your other leg’s knee to step backward with its foot.
    1. While using your legs to move the sled, the rest of your body should remain in essentially a static position during your set as the belt stays around your waist.
    2. Keep your back flat and chest up to maintain an upright torso position throughout the drag.
    3. Throughout the exercise, allow your arms to move naturally, like when you would normally walk backward.
4. At the moment you finish fully straightening your one knee, plant your other foot onto the ground.
    1. While straightening your knee, you should feel tension in that side’s quad.
    2. With the proper technique, you should first and foremost be using your quads to drag the sled.
5. Continue fluidly dragging the sled backward as you reciprocally straighten one leg’s knee while stepping backward with your other leg’s foot until you have reached your target distance or the end of your available space and have to switch to dragging it the opposite direction.
    1. Throughout the set, be aware of any individuals who may potentially enter the path in which you are pulling to avoid an unintended collision.
    2. If your sled has an attachment point on each side of the sled, you can connect the straps to the other side before dragging the sled in the opposite direction.
    3. If your sled only has an attachment point on one side, you will naturally have to rotate your sled 180 degrees before dragging it back in the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your quads while being easier from which to recover compared to most quad exercises.## Setting up
1. Determine the object that will serve as the box onto which you will sit during this exercise. The object should be of a height that allows you to reach your desired squat depth, sufficiently stable that it will not slide, and sturdy enough to support your body weight.
    1. Provided that they satisfy the aforementioned criteria, a variety of objects can be effectively used.
    2. Viable objects include a squat box, plyometric box, aerobic step elevated on risers, flat exercise bench, stack of deadlift blocks, couch, and chair (that will not slide).
    3. While not ideal due to its lower stability, you can also stack bumper plates on top of each other to form a “box” of your desired height. If you opt to do so, make sure that the plates are completely centered on top of each other.
    4. As the height of your object decreases, the intensity of each rep will naturally increase in turn, so the weight that is appropriate for you will be influenced by the height of your object. For most goals, it is typically appropriate to select an object that is near the lowest height that allows you to complete your intended number of reps with high-quality technique. It is generally recommended to progress initially to an object of a low height that allows you to train through a full range of motion prior to progressing to a heavier weight.
2. Pick up a kettlebell or dumbbell and hold it right next to your chest with bent elbows, as your upper arms are slightly in front of your sides (i.e., in the goblet position).
    1. You can use either a kettlebell or a dumbbell, but some people find holding a kettlebell in the goblet position to feel more stable and comfortable, especially with a fairly heavy weight.
    2. Based on your preference and the load you use, you can transfer your kettlebell or dumbbell into the goblet position through a couple of different approaches.
    3. An effective approach is initially to pick up the weight by the handle through a deadlift motion and set it on an elevated surface (like a bench or plyometric box) with the handle facing up if you use a kettlebell or the handle directed vertically if using a dumbbell (as only one end of the dumbbell is on the surface). Then squat down until the weight is right in front of your chest, firmly grasp it in the goblet position, and squat up with the weight. If you use a kettlebell, grasp it by its horns (i.e., side handles). If you use a dumbbell, cup your hands around the top end of the weight with the base of your palms by the handle, and your fingers wrapped around the rest of that end of the weight.
    4. With a lighter kettlebell, you can tilt it onto its side with the weight base directed toward you, squat down to grasp its horns, stand upright with it, and curl it into the goblet position.
    5. With a lighter dumbbell, you can also pick it up with one hand and curl it up across your body to be in front of your chest. Then cup your free hand around the top end of the dumbbell with the base of your palm by the handle and fingers wrapped around the top end of the weight before repositioning your other hand to cup the other side of the weight in the same manner.
3. In a near hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead, stand upright while holding the weight to your chest with the box behind you.
    1. The box should be close enough to you that you can sit on it.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot (on the side you will train), and lift your other foot up off the ground by lifting that side’s leg up with a mostly straight knee.
    1. Keep your free leg’s foot off the ground throughout the set.
2. With the leg in contact with the ground, progressively bend at your knee and hip to sit back in a controlled manner.
    1. It can be helpful to initiate the descent by mildly sitting your buttocks back through a hip hinge motion that somewhat inclines your torso, as if you are closing a car door with your buttocks as your hands are carrying something.
    2. Keep the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your working leg’s foot firmly in contact with the ground throughout your set.
    3. Strive to have your knee track straight forward in line with your second and third toes (when counting your big toe as your first toe) as you descend, while not letting your knee cave inward.
    4. Throughout the set, continue keeping the weight close to your chest and your chest up rather than letting your chest collapse through excessive upper back rounding.
    5. To the best of your ability, keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down.
    6. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    7. Through motion at your working leg’s hip, your torso should naturally transition to at least a mildly inclined position rather than remaining completely upright. While you should not intentionally round your lower back, it is common to experience a degree of rounding toward the bottom of the rep if you are using a low box.
    8. There is no need to descend with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid dropping down abruptly. If you are having a difficult time keeping your hip bones level or preventing your knee from caving inward, a slower descent may help improve your technique quality.
3. Continue to squat down until you can sit onto the box in a controlled manner, as you avoid plopping onto the box or rocking back onto the box.
    1. As you sit on the box, strive to maintain the same degree of forward lean that you had right before reaching the box. If your torso transitions to a more upright position after sitting on the box compared to at the end of your descent, you have rocked back onto the box.
    2. Try not to speed up your descent right before reaching the box, which can increase the likelihood that you plop onto the box.
4. Briefly pause on the box as your body remains visibly stationary for at least a moment.
5. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground while extending (straightening) your knee and hip until you are fully upright.
    1. Avoid rocking your torso forward to generate momentum before you start squatting upward.
    2. Like during the descent, continue keeping your working leg’s foot fully in contact with the ground as you keep your hip bones level and avoid letting your knee cave inward.
6. Alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Place a dumbbell on the ground.
    1. Optionally place an exercise mat on the ground near the dumbbell for comfort.
2. Lie down on your side with the side of your body that you are about to train facing up and the dumbbell in front of your torso.
    1. In this side-lying position, you can rest the bottom side of your head on the hand of your bottom arm for comfort by situating your upper arm out to the side with your elbow bent.
    2. Place your legs in whichever position feels most comfortable and stable to you, which people commonly find involves about 90 degrees of knee bend.
3. Grasp the dumbbell with your top arm as your elbow is bent by about 90 degrees and your palm is facing down.
    1. All the subsequent directions will apply to this top arm.
4. Position your upper arm and elbow on the front aspect of your torso’s side (which is facing up) while allowing the weight to rotate your forearm down toward the ground.
    1. Aligning your upper arm with the front aspect of your torso’s side rather than the middle of your side can increase the range of motion you can use for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow bent by about 90 degrees and pinned by your side, rotate your forearm upward through your shoulder’s full range of motion.
    1. How high up you can rotate your forearm will be affected by your shoulder mobility, but people with normal mobility should finish with the forearm angled upward and forward, as the wrist is clearly above shoulder height.
    2. Throughout the exercise, all the motion should occur from shoulder rotation as you avoid moving your elbow away from your side.
    3. When thinking about this technique, it can be helpful to view your forearm as a clock’s hand, while your elbow is the center about which rotation occurs.
    4. When performing this exercise appropriately, you should feel the muscles on the back of your shoulder working.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your forearm to rotate down toward in a controlled arcing motion.
    1. You should finish with your forearm contacting your torso while it is angled downward.
    2. There is no need to lower the weight extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting it swing your forearm down in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus and teres minor, along with a portion of the supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Place a stability ball (which is also known as a Swiss ball or physio ball) on the ground.
    1. Ideally select a ball with a diameter exceeding your shoulder width.
2. Kneel with the ball in front of you as your elbows are bent by approximately 90 degrees.
3. Hinge at your hips to lean your torso forward, and position your bent elbows on top of the ball about shoulder width apart.
    1. Lean forward enough that you can stack your shoulders directly above your elbows.
    2. With a sufficiently large ball, your forearms can rest on the ball with your elbows, but you will chiefly be bearing weight through your elbows.
    3. The appropriate elbow placement should make sure that the ball does not roll once you press down into the ball through your elbows.
    4. Once on the ball, you optionally can clench each hand into a fist to promote full body tension.
## Exercise technique
1. Walk your legs back until your knees are fully straightened with the bottom of your toes dug into the ground.
    1. Position your feet approximately hip-width apart with your soles facing backward.
    2. You should be bearing your bodyweight into the ground through your feet and into the ball through your elbows.
2. Tighten your abs and squeeze your glutes to maintain a rigid back position while pushing your elbows into the ball and keeping your knees straight.
    1. Doing so keeps your hips held up (but not excessively hiked up) rather than letting them sag downward, which would result in an exaggerated arch of your lower back.
    2. In this position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag down, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together. Thinking about pushing your elbows down into the ball can help keep your shoulder blades apart.
    4. Keep your neck near a neutral position like you would normally do when standing upright.
3. Drive your elbows forward to push the ball forward through the range in which you can keep your lower back flat.
    1. Keeping your lower back flat will become progressively more difficult as you roll out farther, so you can determine specifically how far you move based on your current ability. If you are new to this exercise, aiming to move the ball a couple of inches (or a few centimeters) forward can be appropriate. If you have a high level of core strength, you can move farther forward through a range that provides your desired intensity.
    2. While you push the ball forward through shoulder motion and allow your elbows to straighten naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    3. Particularly avoid letting your hips sag down or your ribs flare up, which will arch your lower back.
4. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by pulling the ball back toward the starting position. Drive your elbows backward until they are once again under your shoulders without moving your hips.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back in the same flat position you maintained while rolling out.
    2. After being closer to straight at the end of the last step, your elbows should naturally return to being bent by about 90 degrees by the end of this step.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to bend your knees and return to kneeling before standing up.
## Description
While also dynamically training your lats and teres major, this exercise can statically train your rectus abdominis and obliques as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates to the machine, adjust the seat or thigh pad height to a level that lets you position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Certain machine models have an adjustable seat, others have an adjustable thigh pad, and some have both an adjustable seat and an adjustable thigh pad.
2. While facing the thigh pads and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip).
    1. If your machine has rotating handles (rather than fixed parallel handles), you naturally can rotate your palms inward for this variation.
3. While holding the handles, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad. Slightly lean your torso backward at your hips.
    1. When seated, allow your elbows to straighten in a controlled manner and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for individuals who are tall and have long arms), lowering the seat height may be necessary to achieve resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down toward your sides while progressively bending them to pull the handles downward.
    1. Pull through your full range of motion until you can no longer drive your elbows closer to your torso, which often results in the handles finishing below the height of your ears and slightly above the tops of your shoulders.
    2. Maintain the same slight backward lean of your torso that you adopted while setting up, rather than progressively leaning further backward.
    3. Allow your elbows to move naturally in a manner that feels comfortable with the path of your machine’s handles. With a machine whose handles spread apart as you pull them down, it is typically most comfortable to let your elbows flare outward from your sides during the initial portion of the pull. With a machine that has handles that remain closer together throughout the rep, it is typically most comfortable to keep your elbows tucked closer to shoulder width apart rather than meaningfully flaring them outward.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten in a controlled manner.
    1. You should finish with your arms positioned fully overhead with straight elbows.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance swing your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can train your lat and teres major especially well while also effectively training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
Using a dedicated seal row bench is ideal for this exercise due to its convenience and its ability to help you train through a full range of motion, but you can alternatively take the time to elevate a standard flat bench without a seal row bench. Each approach is described in its own setup section, but the exercise technique can be essentially the same with either technique, so they share the technique section.

### **Setting up with a seal row bench**

1. Adjust the height of the pad or the bar holders to a setting that lets you grasp the bar with straight or slightly bent elbows once you are lying down on the pad.
    1. Which part of the bench can be adjusted varies based on the model you are using, and certain models may lack any adjustable features.
    2. Some trial and error may initially be needed to find the setting that makes it easy for you to unrack and rerack the bar.
2. Place a barbell onto the bar holders and load it with weight plates that will not touch the ground when your arms are hanging down with straight elbows when holding the bar in the starting position of your seal row.
    1. The diameter of the weight plates added to the bar is often irrelevant with an appropriately adjusted bench.
    2. However, for some people (particularly those with longer arms using non-adjustable benches), adding full diameter plates (such as 45lb (20kg) or any bumper plates) may cause the plates to contact the ground before the elbows can fully straighten during a rep. If this is the case for you, you exclusively load the bar with smaller diameter plates that will not restrict your row’s range of motion.
3. Lie down with the front of your torso flat on the bench as your neck is situated vertically above the bar.
    1. Position your head in a comfortable position, such as tucking your chin and lifting your head slightly up to keep your face off the bench.
    2. Situating yourself in a spot where your neck is situated above the bar (instead of your shoulders) will help you avoid hitting the bar holders during your set.
    3. While some seal row benches have one continuous flat pad to support your torso and legs in alignment, others have a torso pad and a lower leg pad onto which you can kneel with your hips bent.
4. Reach down and firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width underhand grip such that your palms are facing forward.
5. Unrack the bar by lifting it up just above the bar holders before moving the bar slightly backward to position it directly under your shoulders. Let your arms hang with straight elbows as your shoulder blades are pulled apart from the bar’s weight.

### **Setting up without a seal row bench**

1. Symmetrically elevate each end of a flat bench on top of sturdy objects like aerobic steps with risers, bumper plates, plyometric boxes, or deadlift blocks. Elevate it to the level where you can just grasp a loaded barbell under you while lying face down on the bench
    1. A non-adjustable flat bench is generally preferable to an adjustable bench, since the former type of bench typically has more space under it for bar clearance.
    2. When elevating the bench, make sure that each base is level and that you stack the bench onto only objects that are able to handle the combined weight of you, your barbell, and the bench.
    3. To row through your longest range of motion, the bench should ideally be high enough that you can grasp the bar with straight elbows at the end of your setup. If you can grasp the bar with a bent elbow position, a greater degree of bench elevation would be preferable. Some trial and error may be needed to determine specifically how high a bench elevation is optimal for you.
2. Place a barbell centered under the bench near one of its ends, and load it with weight plates.
    1. The barbell should be situated in a spot where you can reach it while the bench supports your torso and thighs, which will involve positioning the barbell close to one end of the bench.
    2. If you are using an adjustable bench, place the barbell under the part of the bench that has the most space available under it.
    3. The diameter of the largest plates you add to the bar will affect how high off the ground the barbell lies, so both the size of your plates and your bench’s height can affect your row’s range of motion and your ability to reach the bar initially.
3. Lie down with the front of your torso and thighs flat on the bench as your shoulders are positioned above the bar.
    1. Based on your preference, you can either keep your knees straight or bent.
    2. Position your head in a comfortable position, such as tucking your chin and lifting your head slightly up to keep your face off the bench.
4. Reach down and firmly grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width underhand grip such that your palms are facing forward.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced from this point on.
2. Pull the bar up toward your abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward while pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Rather than lifting your chest up through excessive lower back arching, keep your chest and the rest of your torso in contact with the bench throughout your set.
    2. Row through your full range of motion, which ideally results in your elbows finishing at shoulder height or close to this point. Your setup can affect your end range, since the bottom of your bench may physically determine how high you may lift the bar.
    3. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
    4. Rather than meaningfully flaring your elbows out away from your sides, naturally keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides should feel the most comfortable.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. If your bench is tall enough to do so, lower the bar until your elbows have fully straightened.
    2. If the plates reach the ground before your elbows have fully straightened, you can finish lowering the bar once the plates lightly tap the ground.
    3. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades apart to stretch your upper back muscles.
    4. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight pull your arms down rapidly.
4. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you are using a seal row bench, you can rerack the bar following your last rep.
    2. If you are using a flat bench elevated on objects, you set the bar onto the ground once you have finished your set.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains much of the musculature of your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
Using a dedicated seal row bench is ideal for this exercise due to its convenience and its ability to help you train through a full range of motion, but you can alternatively take the time to elevate a standard flat bench without a seal row bench. Each approach is described in its own setup section, but the exercise technique can be essentially the same with either technique, so they share the technique section.

### **Setting up with a seal row bench**

1. Adjust the height of the pad or the bar holders to a setting that lets you grasp the bar with straight or slightly bent elbows once you are lying down on the pad.
    1. Which part of the bench can be adjusted varies based on the model you are using, and certain models may lack any adjustable features.
    2. Some trial and error may initially be needed to find the setting that makes it easy for you to unrack and rerack the bar.
2. Place a barbell onto the bar holders and load it with weight plates that will not touch the ground when your arms are hanging down with straight elbows when holding the bar in the starting position of your seal row.
    1. The diameter of the weight plates added to the bar is often irrelevant with an appropriately adjusted bench.
    2. However, for some people (particularly those with longer arms using non-adjustable benches), adding full diameter plates (such as 45lb (20kg) or any bumper plates) may cause the plates to contact the ground before the elbows can fully straighten during a rep. If this is the case for you, you exclusively load the bar with smaller diameter plates that will not restrict your row’s range of motion.
3. Lie down with the front of your torso flat on the bench as your neck is situated vertically above the bar.
    1. Position your head in a comfortable position, such as tucking your chin and lifting your head slightly up to keep your face off the bench.
    2. Situating yourself in a spot where your neck is situated above the bar (instead of your shoulders) will help you avoid hitting the bar holders during your set.
    3. While some seal row benches have one continuous flat pad to support your torso and legs in alignment, others have a torso pad and a lower leg pad onto which you can kneel with your hips bent.
4. Reach down and firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms face backward.
5. Unrack the bar by lifting it up just above the bar holders before moving the bar slightly backward to position it directly under your shoulders. Let your arms hang with straight elbows as your shoulder blades are pulled apart from the bar’s weight.

### **Setting up without a seal row bench**

1. Symmetrically elevate each end of a flat bench on top of sturdy objects like aerobic steps with risers, bumper plates, plyometric boxes, or deadlift blocks. Elevate it to the level where you can just grasp a loaded barbell under you while lying face down on the bench
    1. A non-adjustable flat bench is generally preferable to an adjustable bench, since the former type of bench typically has more space under it for bar clearance.
    2. When elevating the bench, make sure that each base is level and that you stack the bench onto only objects that are able to handle the combined weight of you, your barbell, and the bench.
    3. To row through your longest range of motion, the bench should ideally be high enough that you can grasp the bar with straight elbows at the end of your setup. If you can grasp the bar with a bent elbow position, a greater degree of bench elevation would be preferable. Some trial and error may be needed to determine specifically how high a bench elevation is optimal for you.
2. Place a barbell centered under the bench near one of its ends, and load it with weight plates.
    1. The barbell should be situated in a spot where you can reach it while the bench supports your torso and thighs, which will involve positioning the barbell close to one end of the bench.
    2. If you are using an adjustable bench, place the barbell under the part of the bench that has the most space available under it.
    3. The diameter of the largest plates you add to the bar will affect how high off the ground the barbell lies, so both the size of your plates and your bench’s height can affect your row’s range of motion and your ability to reach the bar initially.
3. Lie down with the front of your torso and thighs flat on the bench as your shoulders are positioned above the bar.
    1. Based on your preference, you can either keep your knees straight or bent.
    2. Position your head in a comfortable position, such as tucking your chin and lifting your head slightly up to keep your face off the bench.
4. Reach down and firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms face backward.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced from this point on.
2. Pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen/lower chest by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward while pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Rather than lifting your chest up through excessive lower back arching, keep your chest and the rest of your torso in contact with the bench throughout your set.
    2. Row through your full range of motion, which ideally results in your elbows finishing at shoulder height or close to this point. Your setup can affect your end range, since the bottom of your bench may physically determine how high you may lift the bar.
    3. Rather than fully flaring your elbows straight out to the sides or keeping them fully tucked by your sides, let your elbows naturally flare out away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable to you.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. If your bench is tall enough to do so, lower the bar until your elbows have fully straightened.
    2. If the plates reach the ground before your elbows have fully straightened, you can finish lowering the bar once the plates lightly tap the ground.
    3. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades apart to stretch your upper back muscles.
    4. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight pull your arms down rapidly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you are using a seal row bench, you can rerack the bar following your last rep.
    2. If you are using a flat bench elevated on objects, you set the bar onto the ground once you have finished your set.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains much of the musculature of your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Adjust a pair of securely anchored gymnastic rings to be slightly outside shoulder width apart.
2. Set the rings to a height that allows your elbows to be aligned with the center of the rings or to whatever is your preferred starting height. Set up a step (like a plyometric box, aerobic step on risers, or a couple of bumper plates stacked on each other) slightly behind the rings so that you can grasp the rings with straight elbows once you are standing on it while mildly leaning your torso forward.
    1. The appropriate ring height should allow you to train dips through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits, while also allowing you to get into the proper starting position with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. The right ring height should also enable you to set your feet onto the ground or step while holding onto the rings if you fail a rep.
    3. While setting up the rings so that their centers are near elbow height is an appropriate starting point for many people, the ideal ring height can vary among different individuals. Someone’s shoulder mobility and preferred knee position during dips can affect where it is best to set the rings.
    4. While not required since you can jump up into the starting position, setting up a step is highly recommended if you are not already accustomed to performing ring dips, where you jump up from the ground. Correspondingly, the directions will describe the usage of the step.
3. Step up onto the step while facing the rings.
4. Firmly grasp the rings with your palms facing each other. Hold the rings directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the rings tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
5. Step forward off the step, and hold yourself up with straight elbows.
    1. Keep your knees as straight as you can without your feet restricting your range of motion from contacting the ground before you reach the bottom of your rep. Initially, you can maintain a slight knee bend and then adjust your knee position during your set if warranted.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
7. Rather than keeping your torso fully upright, mildly lean your torso forward and maintain this mild lean throughout your set.
    1. The appropriate degree of lean should result in your chest being slightly in front of your hips (when viewed from the side) while helping your shoulders feel comfortable throughout the set.
    2. Feel free to try out different degrees of torso lean over time to determine what personally feels best to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up. If you notice that either motion starts occurring, strive to pull your shoulder blades further backward and/or downward.
    2. Your elbows should be predominantly pointing backward rather than out to your sides from excessive elbow flare. However, you do not have to keep your elbows completely tucked by your sides, and you can allow them to flare mildly outward if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    3. Rather than letting them swing, keep your legs stationary relative to your hips until you finish your set.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point in which their upper arms are at least parallel to the ground, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Once you have finished lowering to your end range, pause in that bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds).
    1. Continue to stay braced with your shoulder blades pulled back and down.
    2. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Reverse the movement by pushing through the rings to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your hands and elbows downward to lift your body upward.
6. Alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step back onto the step before releasing the rings.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your pec major, front delt, and triceps while being able to improve both your horizontal and vertical pressing strength.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired weight onto the machine, adjust the thigh pad or seat to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Most machines have an adjustable thigh pad, but some models have an adjustable seat, while others let you adjust both.
2. While standing above the seat and facing the torso pad, firmly grasp the handles with a grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. Which grip (e.g., overhand, underhand, neutral) is most comfortable will be affected by the angle of your specific machine’s handles.
3. While holding the handles, sit down on the seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the thigh pad and your torso contacting the torso pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten in a controlled manner and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), you may need to lower the seat height to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbows down and back toward your sides while progressively bending them to pull the handles toward you.
    1. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and back together. At the end of the pull, you can think about squeezing them together as if you were trying to pinch a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    2. Keep your torso in the same position throughout your set rather than progressively leaning backward.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows finishing slightly behind your torso.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to move forward and upward while straightening in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally forward as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your elbows fully straightened and the handles fully returned to the starting position.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell on the side you are about to train, and stand over one end of a flat exercise bench.
    1. All the directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the dumbbell, since that is the side you will be training first.
    2. Throughout the exercise, you can situate your free arm wherever is comfortable to you.
2. Sit down on the bench while lifting the dumbbell onto your upper thigh near your abdomen.
    1. Ensure that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie down with your head on the end of the bench.
    2. Your palm should be facing inward with your upper arm by your side.
3. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbell back toward your chest with your elbow bent, lean back to lie down on the bench. Your head should be right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be on the ground in a stable position with your knees bent. Keep them in this position throughout the exercise.
4. In a bench pressing motion, press the dumbbell up until your elbow is straight and your arm is directed up toward the ceiling.
    1. In this starting position (that should look like the end of a single arm dumbbell bench press rep), the dumbbell should be sitting on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a reasonably straight wrist. Maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
5. Rotate your arm to point your palm forward and the bony bump on the back of your elbow out to the side.
## Exercise technique
1. Without bending your elbow, lower your arm across your body in a wide arcing motion toward your opposite shoulder.
    1. Move through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits, which should prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in the back of your shoulder at the bottom.
    2. Let your shoulder blade move naturally throughout the exercise, but keep your back resting on the bench rather than twisting your torso to the side where you are lowering the dumbbell.
    3. There is no need to lower the weight extremely slowly, but you should actively control the descent rather than letting the weight pull your arm down rapidly.
2. Reverse the movement by fluidly sweeping your arm upward and outward until it is vertical or almost vertical.
    1. When your arm is fully vertical, the dumbbell will no longer provide any resistance to your rear delt, so you decide whether you finish with the dumbbell over your shoulder or the middle of your chest. Either approach can be similarly effective, so you can select whichever end position you prefer.
    2. Continue keeping your elbow straight.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Place a dumbbell on the floor near one of the two longer sides of a flat exercise bench.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can place an exercise mat or cushion on the floor next to the dumbbell where you will kneel.
2. Kneel on the floor next to the dumbbell, and pick up the dumbbell with the arm you will train during this set.
3. On the side holding the dumbbell, place your forearm on the bench with your palm facing up, elbow straight, and wrist hanging just off the edge of the bench.
4. Rest your free forearm on the bench and grasp its edge with your hand for additional stability.
    1. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the weight.
5. Sit your hips back toward your heels by bending your knees and inclining your torso until your elbow is comfortably supported by the bench.
    1. Depending on how close you initially kneeled next to the bench, you may have to scoot your knees a bit back at this point to rest your elbow on the bench.
    2. The appropriate position should let you keep your shoulder relaxed due to the support the bench provides.
6. Let the force from the dumbbell bend your wrist to angle your palm forward.
    1. If the dumbbell touches the bench in this starting position, scoot your forearm forward until it no longer does.
7. Let the dumbbell roll down your fingers while maintaining your grip on the dumbbell.
    1. This downward motion will occur from movement at the joints formed by your knuckles and the finger bones closest to your knuckles.
    2. After it rolls down your fingers, the dumbbell should no longer be fully contacting the palm side of your knuckles, in contrast to when you typically maintain a firm grip on a dumbbell with a fist-like hand position.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the dumbbell upward by simultaneously curling your wrist and fingers until your palm is facing toward you and your fingers are fully wrapped around the dumbbell’s handle.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your forearm and elbow supported on the bench as you lift the dumbbell through motion at your wrists and the joints your fingers form with your knuckles.
    2. You should feel tension in the muscles on the palm side of your forearm and finish with your knuckles higher than your wrist.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner as it rolls down your fingers while you angle your palm forward.
    1. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the muscles on the palm side of your forearm at the bottom of the rep when your knuckles are below your wrist.
    2. Avoid letting the dumbbell roll excessively down your fingers to the extent that it could slip out of your hand. Your fingertips should remain wrapped around the handle.
    3. There is no need to lower the dumbbell extremely slowly, but you should actively control the weight rather than letting it pull your hand down abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm, along with the hand muscles involved in gripping.## Setting up
1. Place a dumbbell on the floor near one of the two longer sides of a flat exercise bench.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can place an exercise mat or cushion on the floor next to the dumbbell where you will kneel.
2. Kneel on the floor next to the dumbbell, and pick up the dumbbell with the arm you will train during this set.
3. On the side holding the dumbbell, place your forearm on the bench with your palm facing down, elbow straight, and wrist hanging just off the edge of the bench.
4. Rest your free forearm on the bench and grasp its edge with your hand for additional stability.
    1. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the weight.
5. Sit your hips back toward your heels by bending your knees and inclining your torso until your elbow is comfortably supported by the bench.
    1. Depending on how close you initially kneeled next to the bench, you may have to scoot your knees a bit back at this point to rest your elbow on the bench.
    2. The appropriate position should let you keep your shoulder relaxed due to the support the bench provides.
6. If you did not already do so, let the force from the dumbbell bend your wrist to angle your palm down and back toward you.
    1. If the dumbbell touches the bench in this starting position, scoot your forearm forward until it no longer does.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hand upward and angle your palm forward through your wrist’s available range of motion.
    1. Throughout the set, continue keeping your forearm and elbow supported on the bench as you lift the dumbbell exclusively through wrist motion.
    2. After straightening your wrist during the first half of the movement, you should finish with your wrist cocked back and your knuckles higher than your forearm.
    3. You should feel tension in the muscles on the top side of your forearm.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hand in a controlled manner and letting your wrist bend through its full comfortable range of motion.
    1. You should finish with your palm angled downward and back toward you with your knuckles below your wrist.
    2. There is no need to lower the dumbbell extremely slowly, but you should actively control the weight rather than letting it pull your hand down abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the seven wrist extensor muscles on the side of your forearm opposite your palm.## Setting up
Single arm cable wrist curls can be performed in a variety of positions, including sitting on a bench with your forearm supported on your thigh, standing with a bent elbow facing a low pulley, and standing with a bent elbow facing a high pulley. Those are all viable ways to perform the exercise, but these instructions will describe how to perform it while standing facing away from the pulley with your arm straight by your side.

1. With a cable station’s pulley positioned near hip height, connect a d-handle attachment, and select your weight with the loading pin.
2. While facing away from the cable station, stand with the back of your shoulder (on the side of your body you will train during this set) in front of the pulley.
3. With a straight elbow, firmly grasp the handle with your palm facing forward (i.e., an underhand grip) away from the pulley.
4. While positioning your arm directly by your side, step forward a little beyond the point where you start feeling resistance from the cable.
    1. You should be far enough away from the cable that you feel tension from it throughout the full range of motion of this exercise.
    2. If you lose tension from the cable at any point during a rep, you should take another step forward.
5. For additional stability, you optionally can use your free hand to hold your working arm’s triceps (just above your elbow) and keep your working side’s arm pinned to your side.
    1. If unopposed, the cable’s resistance will pull your working arm back behind your torso, so you can use your non-working arm to help counteract that force and keep your working arm stationary in a vertical position.
    2. Alternatively, you can keep your non-working arm in whichever position you prefer and use your working side’s shoulder muscles to stabilize your arm through a static front raise effort.
    3. All the subsequent directions apply to your working side’s arm.
## Exercise technique
1. Allow the force from the cable to pull your hand back toward the pulley. While maintaining a grip on the handle with your lower finger wrapped around it, simultaneously let the handle roll down your fingers away from your knuckles.
    1. The finger motion will occur from movement at the joints formed by your knuckles and the finger bones closest to your knuckles.
    2. After it rolls down your fingers, the handle should no longer be fully contacting the palm side of your knuckles, in contrast to when you typically maintain a firm grip on a handle with a closed-fist hand position.
2. Simultaneously curl your wrist and fingers to pull your hand and the handle forward away from the pulley while forming a tight fist. Finish with your fingers fully wrapped around the handle as your palm is angled diagonally forward and upward.
    1. With your wrist fully curled, your knuckles should finish in front of your wrist rather than being directly under your wrist (like when your palm is angled purely forward) or behind your wrist (like in the starting position).
    2. While motion occurs at your wrist and finger joints, keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the exercise, which will involve keeping your arm pinned by your side and your elbow straight.
3. Reverse the movement by letting your hand arc backward toward the pulley while the handle rolls down your fingers away from your knuckles.
    1. Move through the full range of motion that is comfortable to your wrist, which normally produces a stretching sensation in the muscles on the front of your forearm at the end of the rep when your knuckles are behind your wrist, and your palm is angled down.
    2. Avoid letting the handle roll excessively far down your fingers to the extent that it could slip out of your hand. Your fingertips should remain firmly wrapped around the handle.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should actively control the weight rather than letting the cable pull your hand back abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm, along with the hand muscles involved in gripping.## Setting up
1. Place a dumbbell on the floor near one of the two longer sides of a flat exercise bench.
    1. For comfort, you can optionally place an exercise mat or cushion on the floor next to the dumbbell where you will kneel.
2. Kneel on the floor next to the dumbbell, and pick up the dumbbell with the arm you will train during this set.
3. On the side holding the dumbbell, place your forearm on the bench with your palm facing up, elbow straight, and wrist hanging just off the edge of the bench.
4. Rest your free forearm on the bench and grasp its edge with your hand for additional stability.
    1. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm in which you are holding the weight.
5. Sit your hips back toward your heels by bending your knees and inclining your torso until your elbow is comfortably supported by the bench.
    1. Depending on how close you initially knelt next to the bench, you may have to scoot your knees a bit back at this point to rest your elbow on the bench.
    2. The appropriate position should let you keep your shoulder relaxed due to the support the bench provides.
6. If you did not already do so, let the force from the dumbbell bend your wrist to angle your palm forward.
    1. If the dumbbell touches the bench in this starting position, scoot your forearm forward until it no longer does.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hand and the dumbbell upward by curling your wrist until your palm is facing toward you.
    1. Throughout the set, continue keeping your forearm and elbow supported on the bench as you lift the dumbbell exclusively through wrist motion.
    2. You should feel tension in the muscles on the palm side of your forearm and finish with your knuckles higher than your wrist.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner through your wrist’s full comfortable range of motion.
    1. You should finish with your palm angled forward and your knuckles below your wrist.
    2. There is no need to lower the dumbbell extremely slowly, but you should actively control the weight rather than letting it pull your hand down abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
This exercise can be performed with an ankle weight strapped to your ankle or a dumbbell, weight plate, or kettlebel held on the outer side of your leg. The technique is essentially the same regardless of which equipment you use, but certain setup details differ, which is why two different setup sections are presented.

### **Setting up with ankle weights**

1. Strap an ankle weight to each of your ankles and stand with what will be your non-working side next to a wall or other sturdy surface onto which you can place one hand and lean.
    1. The vertical beam of a power rack, cable station, or Smith machine can also be used to support yourself.
2. Raise your non-working side’s arm out to the side until it is close to parallel to the ground with a straight elbow, and place its palm against the wall.
3. While leaning into the surface, take a small step out to the side, and situate both feet together.
    1. Through the hand holding the wall, predominantly bear your weight through the base of your palm (i.e., the heel of your hand).
    2. Specifically how far out you position your feet and consequently lean to the side is up to your preference, but you should be stable and balanced in this position.
    3. While being tilted to the side, your torso and legs should be aligned with your feet pointing in the same direction, like when you are standing upright with your feet together.
    4. Position your free arm wherever you prefer throughout the exercise.

### **Setting up without ankle weights**

1. Pick up the dumbbell, weight plate, or kettlebell you will use for resistance and hold it with a straight elbow by the side of your body you will train during this set.
2. Stand with the side of your body not holding the weight next to a wall or other sturdy surface onto which you can place one hand and lean.
    1. The vertical beam of a power rack, cable station, or Smith machine can also be used to support yourself.
3. Raise your free arm out to the side until it is close to parallel to the ground with a straight elbow, and place its palm against the wall.
4. While leaning into the surface, take a small step out to the side, and situate both feet together while resting the weight on the outer side of your leg that is further away from the wall.
    1. Through the hand holding the wall, predominantly bear your weight through the base of your palm (i.e., the heel of your hand).
    2. Specifically how far out you position your feet and consequently lean to the side is up to your preference, but you should be stable and balanced in this position.
    3. While being tilted to the side, your torso and legs should be aligned with your feet pointing in the same direction, like when you are standing upright with your feet together.
    4. Continue holding the weight against your working side’s leg throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight into your foot that is closer to the wall against which you are leaning.
2. Sweep your working leg straight out to the side through your hip’s full range of motion while keeping the rest of your body stationary.
    1. Keep your kneecap and toes pointing straight ahead rather than rotating your leg to turn them out to the side and angle toward the direction in which you are lifting your leg. If laser beams were shooting out from your kneecap and imaginary belt buckle, they should continue pointing straight forward throughout the exercise instead of angling further away from the wall.
    2. Avoiding pushing your hip bones out to the side away from the wall or letting them sag toward the wall through motion of your non-working hip. Throughout the set, you should be able to draw a line on your non-working side from your knee through your hip to your chest, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. While performing this movement with the appropriate technique, you should feel the muscles on the side of your hip further away from the wall contracting.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your working leg in toward your opposite leg in a controlled manner.
    1. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting your leg abruptly swing back down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side by lying on your opposite side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the musculature on the side of your hip, including your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia latae.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a low incline with an angle up to 30 degrees.
    1. If your bench can be adjusted to a moderate incline near 45 degrees but does not have a low incline setting, you can alternatively select the moderate incline barbell bench press from this database.
2. Place the bench centered within a power rack with the head of the bench between the posts, where you will adjust the J-hooks.
    1. You should position the bench in a spot that allows your mouth to be directly under the barbell after setting up the rack and lying down on the bench.
3. Position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the incline bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. Then place the bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of incline press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your incline press.
    1. If you happen to fail a rep without a spotter, you could flatten out your back to lose your arch, which will lower your chest and ideally allow the bar to rest on the safety pins.
    2. Certain power rack models have fairly wide spaces between safety pinholes that may result in the highest setting that does not restrict your incline press range of motion being too low to receive the bar after you flatten your back. It is not ideal if this applies to your rack, but the safety pins can still enhance safety if they are high enough to receive the bar if you dump it forward, in case you are completely pinned without anyone to assist you.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and lie down with your back and head on the incline bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Firmly situate the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    2. Based on what feels most stable, you can position your heels directly under your knees with vertical shins, or you can bend your knees a bit beyond this point to position your heels slightly behind your knees with slightly angled shins.
    3. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
    4. If you are not training with safety pins that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for bench press variations is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground, squeeze your glutes, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set. This will help create full-body tension and allow your body to function as a stable platform from which to press.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. You will maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your middle chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending until you are able to tap your middle chest lightly with the bar.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set.
    2. When aiming to touch the bar to your middle chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    3. To touch your middle chest, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your middle chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    4. Make sure to control the entire lowering phase before gently contacting your chest with the bar, since you will not be bouncing the bar off your chest.
4. Once the bar contacts your chest, pause in that bottom position as you remain completely stationary for a brief period of time (e.g., approximately two seconds) while staying tight.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your middle chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major, front delts, and triceps, with a slightly greater emphasis on the upper pec than flat presses.## Setting up
1. Connect a lat pulldown bar to a seated cable row machine, and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. While a lat pulldown bar is typically used, you can perform this exercise with any attachment you can comfortably hold throughout the set, so it is up to you which specific attachment you use.
    2. If your machine lets you adjust the seat, you can set it to a height that positions the pulley approximately level with your upper chest once you are seated with an upright torso. This height will keep your arms close to parallel to the ground during the exercise. The exercise can still be very effective with a different seat height, so it is not problematic if your machine does not enable this position.
2. Sit down on the seat and place your feet in contact with the foot plates, as your knees are bent enough to position the bar within your reach after leaning forward.
3. Lean forward at your hips and firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing down.
4. Assume an upright torso position while pressing your feet into the foot plates to scoot your hips back until you are in a position where you feel stable and comfortable.
    1. People commonly find that a mildly bent knee position feels most comfortable.
## Exercise technique
1. Without bending your elbows or leaning your torso back, pull your shoulder blades backward and in toward each other as far as you can.
    1. As a cue, you can imagine pinching a pencil between your shoulder blades, which should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting.
    2. In contrast to a row, the bar will only move a short distance during this variation, since you are isolating motion of your shoulder blades without moving your arms directly.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your shoulder blades to move forward and spread apart from each other as far as they can in a controlled manner.
    1. Avoid bending your elbows, leaning forward at your hips, or rounding your lower back.
    2. At the end of this motion, your shoulder girdles should resemble a slouched posture as they are pulled forward beyond their normal resting position, which often produces a stretching sensation in your upper back muscles.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains your rhomboids and all three regions of your trapezius in a more isolated manner than row variations do.## Setting up
1. With a cable station’s pulley positioned to knee height or lower, connect a straight bar attachment and select your weight with the loading pin.
    1. You can use any attachment that lets you use a shoulder-width underhand grip.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down and firmly grasp it with straight elbows and your hands near shoulder-width apart as your palms face forward, resulting in an underhand grip.
3. Stand upright with the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the bar upward until your forearms are parallel to the ground by bending your elbows by about 90 degrees. Let the force from the cable bend your wrists to angle your palms forward and pull your knuckles below your wrists.
2. While keeping your forearms parallel to the ground, fully curl your wrists to angle your palms back toward you and lift your knuckles up above your wrists.
    1. With the exception of your hand moving through wrist motion, you should keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the set.
    2. To maintain the same degree of elbow bend that keeps your forearms in a horizontal position, you will be performing a static biceps curl during this exercise.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by uncurling your wrists to angle your palms away from you and move your knuckles lower than your wrists.
    1. Move through the full range of motion that is comfortable to your wrists as you continue keeping your forearms parallel to the ground.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should actively control the weight rather than letting the cable yank your hands down abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, let your forearms straighten to lower the bar before squatting down to set the bar on the ground.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train the six wrist flexor muscles on the palm side of your forearm.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench to an incline of about 30-45 degrees.
    1. A 30-degree incline can provide greater resistance to your delts in the starting position of each rep than a 45-degree incline, but the moderately higher incline is still completely viable for this exercise.
    2. As an alternative to an adjustable bench, you have the option of using a fixed moderate incline bench.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on the incline bench with your feet flat on the ground.
    1. Keep your upper back in contact with the bench throughout the set. Based on your preference, you can rest your head on the bench or keep it slightly above it.
    2. While you do not necessarily need to have an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid sitting with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
3. With your palms angled in toward each other and slightly backward, direct your arms down toward the ground with straight or slightly bent elbows.
    1. Whether you keep your elbows straight or slightly bent is up to your preference, but you should statically maintain your preferred elbow position throughout the set.
    2. You can perform this step simultaneously with step 2.
## Exercise technique
1. In a smooth arcing motion, sweep your arms diagonally upward, outward, and mildly forward through a range of motion comfortable to your shoulders.
    1. Lifting until the hands are near ear height is often appropriate, but you can determine specifically how high up you finish based on your personal preference and what feels comfortable. The right range of motion for you should not prompt you to feel any shoulder discomfort in the top position.
    2. As you raise your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in toward your sides in a controlled manner.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your arms pointing down toward the ground.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weights downward.
    3. The dumbbells should predominantly arc downward and inward, while simultaneously tracking slightly backward to reverse the forward motion that occurred during step 1.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your lateral delt, front delt, and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your upper trap.## Setting up
1. Follow the set-up instructions for the dual kettlebell clean, and perform the lifting phase of the dual kettlebell clean described in steps 1-5 of its exercise technique instructions.
    1. After doing so, you should now be holding the kettlebell in the front rack position. This involves situating them in front of your shoulders with your elbows bent slightly in front of your ribcage, palms facing in toward each other, and the weights resting on your forearms, which are mostly vertically with your wrists slightly closer to the midline of your body than your elbows. The handle should also be diagonally contacting your palms, such that the sides of the handles by your thumbs are angled mildly higher than the sides of the handles by your little fingers.
    2. As an alternative to cleaning the kettlebells, you can curl them up into the same front rack position described above if you are using loads that are light enough to do so. With this technique, initially grasp the handles with your little fingers directly next to one of the horns (i.e., side handles) on each kettlebell to help the handles rest diagonally across your palms in the front rack position. Feel free to perform a cheat curl-like motion where you initially generate some momentum by swinging the weights mildly backward with straight elbows before swinging your elbows forward while curling them. This is a less technically complicated means of moving the kettlebells into the front rack, but it is not as efficient when using heavy weights for lifters who are proficient with cleaning kettlebells.
2. Assume a “proud posture” with your chest up, as you avoid letting the weights pull you into a hunched position with your upper back and shoulders excessively rounded.
    1. Maintain the natural curvature of your lower back and neck, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
3. Position your feet near shoulder-width apart from each other, and point your toes at least slightly out to your sides.
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
2. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest and elbows up.
    1. Throughout the set, avoid dropping your elbows or letting your chest cave down.
    2. As you descend, it is natural for your torso to lean forward mildly at your hips to maintain your balance, but maintain a mostly upright torso position with your back flat.
    3. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
3. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
4. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
6. Following your last rep, set the kettlebells down in the opposite manner to how you initially lifted them into the front rack position.
    1. If you initially cleaned the kettlebells, you can let the kettlebells fall forward as you swing your arms down while straightening your elbows and hinging at your hips to absorb the force of the lowering phase.
    2. If you initially curled the kettlebells up, you can similarly guide them down by letting your elbows straighten and swinging your elbows mildly back to help control the weights.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance while holding the kettlebells with straight elbows.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you are using kettlebells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both kettlebells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging.
    3. For an easier setup, you can pick up one kettlebell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other kettlebell onto the bench before picking up both kettlebells from a mechanically advantaged position.
    4. Pick up the kettlebells with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift them by extending (straightening) your hips and knees while keeping your back flat.
2. Position your palms right next to the outer aspect of the front of your thighs so that your palms angle diagonally backward and in toward each other.
    1. This results in a semi-overhand grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip that would direct your palms straight backward (with the kettlebells directly in front of your body) and a neutral grip that would direct your palms straight in toward your sides (with the kettlebells directly to the sides of your body).
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep your palms facing this way and your neck should be near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and take a small step backward with your other foot so that its toes are just slightly behind your lead foot’s heel while keeping your feet hip-width apart.
    1. Naturally let your knees slightly bend.
    2. Avoid stepping inward to the extent that your rear foot is directly behind your lead foot. Keeping your feet hip-width apart can help keep you stable.
2. Place the balls and toes of your rear foot on the ground while keeping its midfoot and heel off the ground.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you maintain a rigid back position.
4. While continuing to bear almost all your weight through your lead foot, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as you let your knees mildly bend.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat, chest up, and lead foot fully planted on the ground.
    2. Your rear leg’s purpose is to assist with maintaining your balance so that the exercise can feel more stable than a true single leg Romanian deadlift.
    3. If scales were under both of your feet, the scale under your lead foot should ideally show that you are bearing at least 90% of your weight through your front leg.
    4. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the kettlebells just in front of your legs. Avoid letting the kettlebells drift forward away from you, which may be more likely to occur if you think about “bending over” rather than “sitting back.”
    5. Strive to keep your lead leg’s shin vertical with your weight distributed throughout the entirety of your lead foot. If you concentrate all your weight through your heel, your knee may track backward into an excessively straightened position. If you bear too much weight through the front of your foot, your knee may track forward and bend excessively.
    6. While motion will occur at both hips, you should focus on sitting your lead leg’s hip back (as if you were trying to close a car door with your buttocks) while letting your rear leg’s hip naturally move with it.
5. In a controlled manner, continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically hinge until their hands are at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
6. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, lead foot fully on the ground, and the kettlebells close to your legs.
    2. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Following your last rep, step forward with your rear foot to position your feet symmetrically before repeating the exercise with your other leg in the lead position.
    1. Once you are ready to set the kettlebells down, step forward again to position your feet symmetrically before sitting your hips back and bending your knees to lower the kettlebells onto the ground (or rerack them).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Pick up one kettlebell and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while firmly holding the kettlebell at your side with a straight elbow and level shoulders.
    1. You will be training the leg opposite to the side in which you hold the kettlebell.
    2. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handle with the handle pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. You can pick up the kettlebell with a deadlift or squat motion, where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back flat.
2. Once upright, you can situate your palm right next to the outer aspect of the front of your thigh so that your palm faces at an angle between backward and inward.
    1. Throughout the exercise, keep your palm facing this way, elbow straightened, and neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. Keep your free hand in a comfortable position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto the foot opposite to the side in which you are holding the kettlebell. Take a small step backward with your other foot so that its toes are just slightly behind your lead foot’s heel while keeping your feet hip-width apart.
    1. Naturally let your knees slightly bend.
    2. Avoid stepping inward to the extent that your rear foot is directly behind your lead foot. Keeping your feet hip-width apart can help keep you stable.
2. Place the balls and toes of your rear foot on the ground while keeping its midfoot and heel off the ground.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you maintain a rigid back position.
4. While continuing to bear almost all your weight through your lead foot, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as you let your knees mildly bend.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat, chest up, and lead foot fully planted on the ground.
    2. Your rear leg’s purpose is to assist with maintaining your balance so that the exercise can feel more stable than a true single leg Romanian deadlift.
    3. If scales were under both of your feet, the scale under your lead foot should ideally show that you are bearing at least 90% of your weight through your front leg.
    4. Keep your shoulders level with each other as you avoid letting your torso rotate or bend to the side of the kettlebell.
    5. Strive to keep your lead leg’s shin vertical with your weight distributed throughout the entirety of your lead foot. If you concentrate all your weight through your heel, your knee may track backward into an excessively straightened position. If you bear too much weight through the front of your foot, your knee may track forward and bend excessively.
    6. While motion will occur at both hips, you should focus on sitting your lead leg’s hip back (as if you were trying to close a car door with your buttocks) while letting your rear leg’s hip naturally move with it.
5. In a controlled manner, continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. How deeply you can hinge without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
6. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, shoulders level, and lead foot fully on the ground.
    2. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Following your last rep, step forward with your rear foot to position your feet symmetrically before repeating the exercise with your other leg in the lead position.
    1. Once you are ready to set the kettlebell down, step forward again to position your feet symmetrically before reracking it or sitting your hips back and bending your knees to lower the kettlebell onto the ground.
