## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The setup for this exercise is simply performing the lifting phase of a conventional deadlift.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the middle of your feet under the middle of the bar.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
5. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Throughout the exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and take a small step backward with your other foot so that its toes are just slightly behind your lead foot’s heel while keeping your feet hip-width apart.
    1. Naturally let your knees slightly bend.
    2. Avoid stepping inward to the extent that your rear foot is directly behind your lead foot. Keeping your feet hip-width apart can help keep you stable.
2. Place the balls and toes of your rear foot on the ground while keeping its midfoot and heel off the ground.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you maintain a rigid back position.
4. While continuing to bear almost all your weight through your lead foot, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as you let your knees mildly bend.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat, chest up, and lead foot fully planted on the ground.
    2. Your rear leg’s purpose is to assist with maintaining your balance so that the exercise can feel more stable than a true single leg Romanian deadlift.
    3. If scales were under both of your feet, the scale under your lead foot should ideally show that you are bearing at least 90% of your weight through your front leg.
    4. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the bar just in front of your legs. Avoid letting the bar drift forward away from you, which may be more likely to occur if you think about “bending over” rather than “sitting back.”
    5. Strive to keep your lead leg’s shin vertical with your weight distributed throughout the entirety of your lead foot. If you concentrate all your weight through your heel, your knee may track backward into an excessively straightened position. If you bear too much weight through the front of your foot, your knee may track forward and bend excessively.
    6. While motion will occur at both hips, you should focus on sitting your lead leg’s hip back (as if you were trying to close a car door with your buttocks) while letting your rear leg’s hip naturally move with it.
5. In a controlled manner, continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. If you have high hamstring flexibility and can hinge until the plates contact the ground, descend until you can tap the plates very lightly on the ground, but do not let the weight rest on the ground.
6. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, lead foot fully on the ground, and the bar close to your legs.
    2. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Following your last rep, step forward with your rear foot to position your feet symmetrically before repeating the exercise with your other leg in the lead position.
    1. Once you are ready to set the bar down, step forward again to position your feet symmetrically before sitting your hips back and bending your knees to lower the bar onto the ground.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust a pair of securely anchored gymnastic rings to be slightly outside shoulder width apart.
2. Set the rings to a height that allows your elbows to be aligned with the center of the rings or to whatever is your preferred starting height. Set up a step (like a plyometric box, aerobic step on risers, or a couple of bumper plates stacked on each other) slightly behind the rings so that you can grasp the rings with straight elbows once you are standing on it while mildly leaning your torso forward.
    1. The appropriate ring height should allow you to train dips through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits, while also allowing you to get into the proper starting position with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. The right ring height should also enable you to set your feet onto the ground or step while holding onto the rings if you fail a rep.
    3. While setting up the rings so that their centers are near elbow height is an appropriate starting point for many people, the ideal ring height can vary among different individuals. Someone’s shoulder mobility and preferred knee position during dips can affect where it is best to set the rings.
    4. While not required since you can jump up into the starting position, setting up a step is highly recommended if you are not already accustomed to performing ring dips, where you jump up from the ground. Correspondingly, the directions will describe the usage of the step.
3. While standing in front of the rings, add your desired load (in the form of weight plates or a kettlebell) to a dip/pull-up belt you have around your waist.
    1. The back of your belt should be securely resting on top of your buttocks. You can loop the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) through the center hole of a weight plate or under the handle of a kettlebell. If you have a loading pin for your belt, you would alternatively loop the chain or strap through the loading pin after adding weight plates to it.
    2. It will be easiest if you carry the weight over to the rings and add the weight to your belt while you are by them. Adding the weight to your belt farther away from the bar before walking over to the bar can be awkward.
    3. As an alternative to using a pull-up/dip belt, you can wear a weighted vest.
    4. While less stable and more awkward than using a belt or weighted vest, you also have the option of holding a dumbbell between your thighs or feet. This approach is suboptimal and will limit how much weight you can use.
4. Step up onto the step while facing the rings.
5. Firmly grasp the rings with your palms facing each other. Hold the rings directly under the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists.
    1. Squeezing the rings tightly can help you avoid letting your wrists excessively cock back.
6. Step forward off the step, hold yourself up with straight elbows, and position your legs against the weight (if you are using a dip belt).
    1. Keep your knees as straight as you can without your feet restricting your range of motion from contacting the ground before you reach the bottom of your rep. Initially, you can maintain a slight knee bend and then adjust your knee position during your set if warranted.
    2. Based on your preference, you can spread your legs just enough to position the weight between them, or you can position your legs together with the weight just in front of them. Which option is most comfortable will be influenced by how you set up your belt and the shape of your weight(s). With either option, positioning your thighs in a close to vertical position is typically the most practical. Contacting the weight with your legs can help you prevent the weight from excessively swinging during your set.
7. Pull your shoulder blades back and down to assume a “proud chest” posture.
    1. You chiefly want to prevent your shoulders from rolling forward or shrugging up during this exercise.
    2. It is not required to keep your shoulder blades packed down further than they normally are at rest, but the intent to pack them down will prevent your torso from sagging down, which would place you into a shrugged shoulder position.
    3. Similarly, you do not necessarily need to have your shoulder blades fully back in the starting position, but pulling them back can make it easier to avoid tilting your shoulders forward, which is undesirable.
8. Rather than keeping your torso fully upright, mildly lean your torso forward and maintain this mild lean throughout your set.
    1. The appropriate degree of lean should result in your chest being slightly in front of your hips (when viewed from the side) while helping your shoulders feel comfortable throughout the set.
    2. Feel free to try out different degrees of torso lean over time to determine what personally feels best to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Bend your elbows to lower your body in a controlled manner.
    1. Throughout your set, avoid letting your shoulders roll forward or shrug up. If you notice that either motion starts occurring, strive to pull your shoulder blades further backward and/or downward.
    2. Your elbows should be predominantly pointing backward rather than out to your sides from excessive elbow flare. However, you do not have to keep your elbows completely tucked by your sides, and you can allow them to flare mildly outward if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    3. Rather than letting them swing, keep your legs stationary relative to your hips until you finish your set. If you are using a dip belt, keeping your legs in contact with the weight can help you stabilize it. If the weight is between your legs, squeezing your thighs together can stop the weight from swinging.
3. Continue descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. It is common for people with healthy shoulders to reach the point at which their upper arms are at least parallel to the ground, but specifically how deeply you should descend will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. At your end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the front of your shoulders, but you should not feel any shoulder discomfort or pain.
    3. If you feel joint discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely lowered yourself excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training dips over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into an aggravating range.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a slow and controlled tempo as you approach the bottom position is recommended.
4. Reverse the movement by pushing through the rings to lift yourself up until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your hands and elbows downward to lift your body upward.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Once you have fully straightened your elbows after your last rep, step back onto the step before releasing the rings.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains your pec major, front delt, and triceps while improving both your horizontal and vertical pressing strength.## Setting up
1. Select your desired weight with the loading pin and adjust the machine’s seat to a position that allows your knees to be mildly bent when your feet are on the foot platform.
    1. If the seat is positioned too far back, the machine may not be able to provide tension throughout your full ankle range of motion.
2. Sit down with your back in full contact with the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture from sitting your hips excessively forward, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
3. With a hip-width to shoulder-width stance, place the balls of your feet onto the foot platform with your heels hanging off the platform.
4. Grasp the handles for additional stability and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. While letting your heels move slightly forward to stretch your calves, straighten your knees to press your feet into the platform.
    1. After doing so, your knees should either be straight or very slightly bent. If you have the mobility to hyperextend your knees (which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position), you can avoid hyperextending them.
    2. The purpose of this step is to place your calves into a stretched starting position to help you train through a longer range of ankle motion compared to if you started with your ankles in a neutral position (like when you are normally standing upright and your foot and shin form a 90-degree angle). Ideally, you should now feel a stretching sensation in your calves.
    3. While this type of machine often has a foot platform that can pivot and a seat that is stationary after adjusting it, some machine models have a stationary foot platform and a mobile seat. If your machine has a mobile seat, you will press yourself away from the platform during this step.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the balls of your feet to press the platform fully away from you and point your feet forward, in a manner similar to stepping on a car’s gas pedal.
    1. If your machine has a mobile seat and a fixed platform, you can have the same intent, but you will press yourself away from the platform instead of pressing the platform away from you. With this machine style, you will similarly perform the reverse of that motion during the next step.
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep the balls of your feet firmly planted on the platform as movement occurs exclusively at your ankles without any knee or hip motion.
2. Reverse the movement by letting the balls of your feet arc back toward your shins in a controlled manner through the full range of motion permitted by your calf flexibility and machine.
    1. Ideally, you should feel a deep stretching sensation in your calves at the end of this step.
    2. Avoid letting your knees bend.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, avoid rapidly moving and trying to bounce into the next rep.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, bend your knees to lower the weight you selected onto the rest of the weight stack.
    2. If you feel your feet start sliding down on the platform during your set, make sure to shift them back up to keep the balls of your feet on the platform.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the gastrocnemius and soleus that constitute your calves.## Setting up
1. If your machine has an adjustable seat height, set it to a level that aligns your shoulders or chest with the handles when lying down on the pad.
    1. If you grasp the handles when they are aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the side, your arms will form 90-degree angles with your torso when viewed from the front.
    2. Some people find that their shoulders feel more comfortable when grasping the handles if they are aligned with the middle-to-lower chest, which reduces the degree to which the elbows will be flared away from the sides.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height for you.
    4. Certain machine models lack an adjustable seat height and, alternatively, have rather long handles that let you vary your shoulder position based on how high up you grasp them.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. Certain machine models position their loading sleeves close enough together that you avoid loading them with full-diameter weight plates (e.g., 45lb (20kg) plates) to be able to train through a full range of motion.
3. Lie down on the machine’s back pad with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against it while your buttocks are resting on the seat.
    1. Place your feet flat on the ground under your bent knees.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the pad or keep it slightly off the pad. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. One at a time, grasp the handles with your arms flared out about 90 degrees from your sides or a little lower if that feels more comfortable to you.
    1. Rotate your body as needed to grasp the handles without exceeding the comfortable limit of your shoulder mobility.
    2. Some people may prefer to grasp the first handle initially sitting upright on the seat before lying down and then grasping the other handle.
    3. The specific approach that may feel best to you to grasp the handles may vary based on which machine model you use and your shoulder mobility.
5. After setting your grip, hold the handles with mildly bent elbows and pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Maintain this “proud chest” posture throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your arms together to move the handles upward and in toward each other until they are just about to touch.
    1. You can think about sweeping your arms together in a wide arcing motion, as if you were hugging a thick barrel.
    2. All the motion should come from your shoulders, as you keep your elbows mildly bent throughout the set.
    3. As your hands approach each other, it is natural for your shoulder blades to move slightly forward, but you should avoid letting your shoulders excessively round forward into a slouched posture.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arms to spread apart in a controlled manner as they arc backward through the full range of motion that is comfortable for your shoulders.
    1. Finish in a “proud chest” posture with your chest up and shoulder blades pulled back.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows mildly bent as you resist letting them bend any further, which would shift the exercise into more of a chest press rather than a fly.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders with your arms fully spread apart, but you should not experience any shoulder pain when moving through your comfortable range of motion.
    4. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, avoid letting the machine abruptly swing your arms apart in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed the set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. If your machine is not currently next to a seat, place it near a seat on which you can comfortably sit while your feet are on the machine’s foot platform.
    1. Having the seat be close enough to the machine where your knees can be bent by a bit less than 90 degrees once your feet are on the foot platform is commonly a comfortable position for this exercise.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine using plates with diameters that do not touch the ground.
    1. Most machines have loading sleeves that are close enough to the ground that plates larger than typical 10lb (or 5kg) plates may touch the ground and restrict your range of motion for the exercise.
3. Sit down on the seat, and place the soles of your feet onto the machine’s foot platform.
4. Slide your feet forward on the platform until the tops of your feet are snugly pressed against the foot pad and your ankles are aligned with the machine’s pivot points.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center point about which the platform rotates. When your ankles are aligned with the pivot points (such that a straight line can pass from one pivot point through the center of your ankles and to the other pivot point), the exercise will feel the most comfortable and natural.
5. If you did not already do so, allow the machine’s resistance to fully point your toes down away from you to assume the exercise’s starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. Fully pull your toes and the balls of your feet upward and back toward your shins by moving at your ankles.
    1. As the balls of your feet and toes lift up, your heels will correspondingly arc downward.
    2. How high you will be able to lift up will be affected by your ankle mobility, but people with normal ankle mobility should be able to finish with the balls of their feet and toes at least slightly higher than the bottom of their heels.
    3. Throughout the set, keep the soles of your feet against the foot platform while the tops of your feet are against the foot pad.
    4. While motion occurs at your ankles, the rest of your body should remain stationary during this exercise.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting the balls of your feet and toes arc down away from your shins to lower the weight.
    1. Descend through the full range of motion your ankle mobility comfortably permits, which often results in a stretching sensation in the muscles on the front of your shins when your feet are fully pointed down with your toes and the balls of your feet lower than your heels.
    2. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your feet down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to slide your feet back off the platform.
## Description
This exercise can train the four muscle ankle dorsiflexor muscles on the front of your lower leg, the largest of which is the tibialis anterior.## Setting up
To perform this exercise, you can use a specific incline plate-loaded calf press machine or a seated hack squat machine (AKA linear hack squat machine) with a foot plate that comfortably lets you hang your heels off the bottom edge of its foot platform. Some seated hack squat machines have foot plates that extend directly under the sled’s rails, which would prevent you from hanging your heels off its edge while keeping your knees straight. With one of those seated hack squat machines, you could still train calf raises, but you would not be able to perform through the full range of motion discussed in the instructions.

1. After adding weight plates of your desired load to the machine, sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your lower back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture or excessively arched, your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright while looking straight ahead.
2. With a hip-width to shoulder-width stance, place the balls of your feet and toes onto the bottom of the foot platform with your heels hanging off the edge.
    1. Instead of starting with your heels off the platform, you have the option of starting with your heels just on the platform and performing the next step with this foot placement before walking your feet down until your heels hang off the edge. This approach may be preferable for lifters with longer legs who start with their knees more notably bent.
    2. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent. If your legs are too short to achieve this position with a machine that has multiple safety stopper heights, you can ask a taller lifter to reposition the sled to a lower starting height. Without a sled that lets you adjust its starting position, a different calf raise variation may be more suitable for your body type if your machine does not let you assume the appropriate starting position for this exercise.
3. While grasping the fixed handles (not the movable safety handle), push yourself away from the platform by straightening your knees.
    1. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a slightly hyperextended knee position is normal for many people and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they utilize a hyperextended knee position during this exercise, so keeping a straight or very slightly bent knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels fully and press yourself away from the platform.
    1. Once you have lifted your heels as high upward as your ankle mobility permits, you have finished the lifting phase.
2. While keeping the balls of your feet planted on the platform and your knees straight, lower your heels in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits.
    1. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can below the balls of your feet to stretch your calves and train them the most effectively.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full range of motion.
    3. If your machine has a safety lock that stops the sled before you have reached the end range permitted by your flexibility, you will have to unlock the safety handle with your hand to train through your full range of motion.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid quickly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, lower the sled onto the safety stoppers by bending your knees.
    2. If you feel your feet start sliding down on the platform during your set, make sure to shift them back up to keep the balls of your feet on the platform.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the seat so that the middle points of the handles will be level with your mid-chest once you are seated.
    1. After initially adjusting the seat, you will naturally have to sit down to confirm whether the height is appropriate.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit with your back against the backrest, and spread your arms backward into a “T-position” through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. People with normal shoulder mobility are able to spread their arms back until their elbows are slightly behind their shoulders.
4. Adjust the pins above the handles to a position that lets you comfortably grasp the handles when your arms are fully spread apart in this “T-position.”
    1. After initially adjusting the handles, you can recheck to see if they are positioned appropriately for you.
    2. If the handles are excessively far back relative to your shoulder mobility, it may be difficult or uncomfortable to grasp them.
    3. If the handles are not sufficiently far back, the range of motion you can use for this exercise will be limited, and you will not be able to train your chest in a stretched position.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Maintain this “proud chest” posture throughout your set.
6. Spread your arms apart into the “T-position” with a slight bend in your elbows before grasping the handles with your palms facing forward.
    1. It may be easiest to grasp the handles one at a time.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your arms together to move the handles toward each other until they are just about to touch or lightly make contact.
    1. You can think about sweeping your arms together in a wide arcing motion, as if you were hugging a thick barrel.
    2. All the motion should come from your shoulders, as you keep your elbows mildly bent throughout the set.
    3. As your hands approach each other, it is natural for your shoulder blades to move slightly forward, but you should avoid letting your shoulders round forward into a slouched posture.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arms to spread apart in a controlled manner as they arc backward through the full range of motion that is comfortable for your shoulders.
    1. Finish in a “proud chest” posture with your chest up and shoulder blades pulled back.
    2. If you were initially able to adjust the handles to start as far back as your mobility permits, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders with your arms fully spread apart
    3. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, avoid letting the machine abruptly swing your arms apart in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed the set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and the backside of your upper arms on the arm pad when sitting on the seat.
    1. The proper setting will typically result in the top of the pad being close to your armpits.
    2. Avoid setting the seat too low to the point where you have to shrug your shoulders up.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height.
2. After selecting your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, sit down on the seat with your feet on the ground.
    1. If your machine lets you adjust its handle attachment, you can connect a straight bar attachment or an angled EZ-bar attachment, based on your preference.
3. Position the back of your upper arms and elbows on top of the arm pad with your armpits just behind the pad.
4. Firmly grasp the handles with an approximately shoulder-width grip as your palms face upward.
    1. If the handles are a little out of your reach, you could slightly stand up and lean forward to reach the handles before sitting back down after you have grasped the handles.
    2. Your wrists should be mostly straight throughout your set after grasping the handles.
5. In the starting position, your elbows should be fully straightened to allow you to train through a full range of motion.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows and triceps in contact with the arm pad, fully bend your elbows to curl the bar toward you.
    1. While your forearms move in an arc through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
2. Reverse the motion by letting the bar move forward in a controlled manner until your elbows have returned to a fully straightened position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows and triceps in contact with the arm pad.
    2. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance abruptly yank your forearms forward.
    3. If you lose resistance from the machine when your elbows fully straighten, you can stop just before the point where this loss of tension would occur.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and adjust the height of the seat so that your upper arms can be parallel with the floor or a little lower while grasping the handles.
    1. After initially adjusting the seat height, spread your arms outward so that your hands are level with the handles.
    2. You may have to re-adjust the seat if your upper arms are not parallel with the floor or a little lower.
    3. Some people will find their shoulders feel more comfortable when the seat is set to a height that allows their hands to be a bit lower than their shoulders, compared to when their hands are completely level with their shoulders.
2. Adjust the pins above the handles to a position that allows you to grasp the handles with your hands as close as they can be to each other while your chest is facing the machine’s pad.
3. After sitting down with your chest against the pad, grasp the vertical handles with your palms facing in toward each other, and slightly bend your elbows.
    1. Maintain the slight bend in your elbows throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Spread the handles apart and backward until your arms and torso form a “T-shape.”
2. Reverse the motion by bringing the handles forward and toward each other until they have returned to their starting position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with your lower chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, but feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best for your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is too low (placing the handles near shoulder level or above), some lifters may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort from having to flare their elbows outward excessively.
    4. If the seat is excessively high (placing the handles near your abdomen), the exercise may feel awkward and be suboptimal for developing the chest.
2. Select your weight with the loading pin at each weight stack.
    1. This style of machine normally has two weight stacks that independently provide resistance to each side of the machine.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Depending on the specific machine model you are using and what feels most stable to you, you can place your feet flat on the floor or on a foot bar.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
4. With your palms facing down, elbows bent, and hands moderately outside shoulder width, firmly grasp the handles. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbows directly behind (rather than being above or below) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    3. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands moderately outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While keeping your forearms aligned with the angle at which you are pressing, press the handles forward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows forward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet stationary.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handles to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your forearms aligned with the angle of your press.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arms abruptly back in an uncontrolled fashion.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range.
    4. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers behind them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine to use your desired level of assistance, and ensure the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you add heavier loads to the assisted pull-up machine since the weight determines the extent to which the machine will support your bodyweight.
    2. Certain machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but essentially the same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates and reach overhead to grasp the handles that let you comfortably direct your palms backward (i.e., an underhand grip) with your hands near shoulder-width apart.
    1. Some machines have fully horizontal handles that let you use a pure underhand grip as your palms point straight backward. Others have handles that are angled diagonally outward and backward, which let you use a grip that is a hybrid between an underhand grip and a neutral grip, as your palms angle backward and inward. Either type of handles can be used for this variation.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
3. One leg at a time, kneel onto the machine’s knee pad (if your machine has a knee pad) or step onto the machine’s foot bar (if your machine has a foot bar) while continuing to grasp the handles firmly.
    1. If using a machine with a knee pad, keep your knees bent on the pad throughout your set. If using a machine with a foot bar, keep your knees straight while standing on the bar throughout your set.
    2. Certain machines with knee pads may not allow shorter individuals to pull through a full range of motion when kneeling on the pad, so you can switch to standing on the knee pad if the machine does not allow you to fully pull yourself up while kneeling.
4. Allow your body to descend in a controlled manner until your arms are fully overhead with straight elbows.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will put you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back puts you in a "proud chest" posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the handles by driving your elbows down and toward your sides as you progressively bend your elbows.
    1. Since the handles are fixed, your elbows won't actually drive downward; having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. If you are using a machine with a knee pad and notice it restricts your range of motion when kneeling on the pad, switch to standing on the pad before resuming your set to pull through your full range of motion.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Place one weighted end of a dumbbell on the ground with its handle directed vertically, resulting in one end remaining off the ground.
    1. Placing a dumbbell on the ground in this manner will make it easier to position onto your lap compared to if you placed both ends of the dumbbell on the ground. However, if the ends of your dumbbell are not flat (such as with a plate-loaded adjustable dumbbell), you will alternatively have to place both ends of the dumbbell on the ground with a horizontal handle.
2. Lie down with your back on the ground (or an exercise mat) and the side of one of your hips directly next to the dumbbell.
3. Roll onto your side toward the dumbbell, hold the dumbbell in contact with the front of your waist with both hands, and roll onto your back with the dumbbell situated in the crease of your hips.
    1. With a sufficiently light weight, you can simply pick up the dumbbell while seated, but the rolling technique is most efficient for transferring a heavier weight into position.
4. While using both hands to keep the dumbbell in the crease of your hips, bend your knees to at least 90 degrees to position the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can position your feet shoulder-width apart, but it is up to you whether you would like them closer together or farther apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground.
5. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift your other foot off the ground by straightening your knee.
    1. Keep one foot off the ground throughout the set you perform with your other side.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heel into the ground and squeezing your glute while continuing to hold the dumbbell in the crease of your hips.
    1. At the top, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side (on the side of your body with the foot contacting the ground). Your thigh and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glute.
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glute engage.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you can lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side by switching which foot you keep on the ground.
    1. To remove the dumbbell from your lap after your next set, roll over to your side as you did when setting it up, and then place it on the ground.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell in a spot where you have sufficient space to lie down on the ground.
    1. The diameter of the weight plates you select can influence how easily you can set up while also affecting the range of motion for this exercise.
    2. It will be easiest to assume the starting position with at least one pair of full diameter plates, such as typical 45lb (20kg) Olympic plates or bumper plates. With full diameter plates, certain individuals who are very thick may need to utilize one of the strategies discussed below to set up under the bar.
    3. If you exclusively load the bar with smaller diameter plates, you may have to modify the standard setup in order to position your hips under the bar. When only using smaller diameter plates, you can elevate the bar just enough to fit your hips under it by situating the plates on top of deadlift blocks, aerobic steps, or stacks of full diameter weight plates. Alternatively, you may have a partner place the bar on top of your hip bones and load it with weight plates after you have assumed the starting position. If neither of these approaches is practical to you, you lack bumper plates that allow you to elevate the bar fully with light loads, and you do not currently have the strength to use 135lb (or 60kg) for this exercise, you have the option of performing other glute bridge variations. For instance, you can select a single leg dumbbell glute bridge or the standard barbell glute bridge.
2. Optionally, obtain a pad (or an object that can function as a pad) that you will place between the bar and your hips.
    1. Using a pad is not required but strongly recommended because it can be very uncomfortable to have a barbell directly resting on your hip bones with a meaningful load.
    2. You can wrap a hip thrust pad or a squat pad around the middle of the bar.
    3. Alternatively, you can place a thick foam or folded exercise mat on the crease of your hip during the next step and subsequently keep it there to cushion your hip bones. Be aware that foam pads not specifically designed for glute bridges or hip thrusts may be somewhat damaged from this exercise.
3. Sit down on the ground with an upright torso and your legs straight out in front of you under the bar.
    1. The middle of the bar should be aligned with the midline of your body.
4. Grasp the bar with your palms facing down and roll it toward the crease of your hips.
    1. So long as your hands are wider apart than your hips, you can adjust your grip width based on what feels stable and comfortable to you during this exercise.
    2. Continue holding the bar from this point onward.
5. Lie down with your back and head resting on the ground.
    1. Tuck your chin slightly toward your chest while resting the back of your head on the ground throughout the exercise.
6. Bend your knees until your shins are in an approximately vertical position with the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can position your feet shoulder-width apart, but it is up to you whether you would like them closer together or farther apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    3. The optimal degree of knee bend should allow your feet to feel stable while being firmly planted on the ground throughout the set. Bringing your heels toward your buttocks until they are vertically stacked under your knees is normally appropriate, but you can adjust how much you bend your knees if a different position feels more stable to you.
7. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift your other foot off the ground by straightening your knee.
    1. Keep one foot off the ground throughout the set you perform with your other side.
8. Confirm that the bar is directly above the crease of your hips and completely centered before initiating the set.
    1. Positioning the bar appropriately will help you avoid the excessive discomfort that may arise from positioning the bar too high (i.e., directly on your lower abdomen) or too low (i.e., directly on your groin).
    2. Some people may experience discomfort when the bar looks aligned with the crease of their hips if the bar is directly resting on the bony bumps on the front of their hip bones. If you notice that this occurs for you during your set, you can roll the bar slightly further down away from your abdomen to shift the pressure away from these bony bumps. When making any adjustments during a set, only do so in between reps when the weights are resting on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to help stabilize your lower back.
2. Lift your hips up by driving your heel into the ground and squeezing your glute. Keep the bar balanced on the crease of your hips the entire set.
    1. At the top, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side (on the side of your body with the foot contacting the ground). Your thigh and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glute.
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glute engage.
    4. The purpose of holding the bar with your hands is to keep it stabilized on the crease of your hips. Avoid trying to lift the bar up or push it down with your hands.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you are able to lightly contact the ground with your buttocks or the plates.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should avoid simply letting gravity pull the bar down abruptly, as opposed to you using your glutes to control the descent actively.
    2. If you hear the plates crashing into the ground, you are not lowering the bar with sufficient control.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the plates on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps. The bar should come to a dead stop on the ground after each rep.
    2. After completing the lowering phase of your last rep, roll the bar forward before sitting up in the reversed manner to how you set up initially.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Use the machine’s loading pin to select your desired level of assistance, and ensure the machine’s knee pad or foot bar is down.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the difficulty of this exercise decreases as you select heavier loads on the assisted pull-up machine, since the weight determines how much the machine will support your bodyweight.
    2. Some machine models have a knee pad, and others have a foot bar, but the same exercise technique can be used with either type.
2. Step up onto the machine’s elevated foot plates and reach overhead to grasp the handles that let you comfortably direct your palms backward (i.e., an underhand grip) with your hands near shoulder-width apart.
    1. Some machines have fully horizontal handles that let you use a pure underhand grip as your palms point straight backward. Others have handles that are angled diagonally outward and backward, which let you use a grip that is a hybrid between an underhand grip and a neutral grip, as your palms angle backward and inward. Either type of handles can be used for this variation.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), it can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which can pull on your hand skin in an uncomfortable way and increase the risk of tearing a callus.
3. One leg at a time, kneel onto the machine’s knee pad (if your machine has a knee pad) or step onto the machine’s foot bar (if your machine has a foot bar) while continuing to grasp the handles firmly.
    1. If using a machine with a knee pad, keep your knees bent on the pad throughout your set. If using a machine with a foot bar, keep your knees straight while standing on the bar throughout your set.
    2. Certain machines with knee pads may not allow shorter individuals to pull through a full range of motion when kneeling on the pad, so you can switch to standing on the knee pad if the machine does not allow you to fully pull yourself up while kneeling.
4. Allow your body to descend in a controlled manner until your arms are fully overhead with straight elbows.
## Exercise technique
1. While hanging with straight elbows, simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. Doing so will put you in a more advantageous position to start your pull than if you kept your chest facing horizontally and allowed your shoulder blades to be shrugged up by your bodyweight.
    2. Arching your middle and upper back puts you in a "proud chest" posture, which you should maintain throughout your set.
2. Pull your chest up toward the handles by driving your elbows down and toward your sides as you progressively bend your elbows.
    1. Since the handles are fixed, your elbows won't actually drive downward; having the intention helps you pull yourself upward with an appropriate technique that effectively trains your back muscles.
    2. Strive to pull through your full available range of motion, which commonly results in the upper arms being close to the sides of the torso and the elbows bent enough that the forearms contact the biceps. However, different individuals may have different ranges of motion enabled by their bodies.
    3. If you are using a machine with a knee pad and notice it restricts your range of motion when kneeling on the pad, switch to standing on the pad before resuming your set to pull through your full range of motion.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your body in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with your arms positioned overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than simply dropping down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and the backside of your upper arms comfortably on the arm pads while aligning your elbows with pivot points when sitting on the seat.
    1. The pivot points are the axes about which the machine’s lever arms rotate, and they are commonly denoted by small dots or circles.
    2. If the seat is too low, you may need to shrug your shoulders up to rest your triceps on the arm pads.
    3. If the seat is too high, you may restrict the range of motion for the exercise.
    4. Some trial and error is typically needed to find the ideal seat height for you.
    5. If the exercise feels awkward, you most likely did not select the seat height that is optimal for your body proportions.
2. After adding weight plates of your desired load to the machine, sit down on the seat
    1. You optionally can grasp the handles initially while standing above the seat before sitting down.
3. With your palms facing up, reach forward and firmly grasp the handles while resting the backside of your upper arms on the arm pads, and aligning your elbows with the pivot points on each side of the machine.
    1. If the handles are a little out of your reach, you could slightly stand up and lean forward to reach the handles before sitting back down after you have grasped the handles.
    2. Your wrists should be mostly straight throughout your set after grasping the handles.
    3. In the starting position, your elbows should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the backside of your upper arms snugly in contact with the arm pads, fully bend your elbows to curl the handles back toward you.
    1. While your forearms move in an arc through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set as you continue keeping your elbows aligned with the pivot points.
2. Reverse the motion by letting the handles move forward in a controlled manner as your elbows straighten toward the starting position.
    1. Aim to move the full range of motion through which the machine provides resistance to finish with your elbows close to being fully straightened.
    2. These machines often stop providing resistance just before the point at which the elbows can fully straighten.
    3. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance abruptly yank your forearms forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. After adding weight plates of your desired load to the machine, sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your lower back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture or excessively arched, your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright while looking straight ahead.
2. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot platform, near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to perform this exercise effectively, so it is an appropriate starting point. However, the specific foot placement that feels best can vary among different lifters, so trying out different positions can often help you determine specifically what works best for you as an individual.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight forward, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward. How high up on the platform feels best to set the feet can also vary from person to person.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable with your feet fully planted on the platform throughout the set when training through your full range of motion.
    4. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to let you unlock the sled and begin your set. If you have short legs, you potentially may have to situate your feet lower on the platform to start with slightly bent knees. If your legs are too short to achieve this position with a machine that has multiple safety stopper heights, you can ask a taller lifter to reposition the sled to a lower starting height that you can reach. Without a sled that lets you adjust its starting position, you can remove any plates from the machine and have someone help you lower the sled to its bottom position. Then you can set up from the bottom position and start each rep with the lifting phase of the exercise instead of the lowering phase.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. Straighten your knees by pushing yourself away from the platform.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handles with your hand to move the safety stoppers out of the sled’s way.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handle) and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Bend at your knees and hips to lower the sled toward the platform in a controlled manner.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform.
    2. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your lower back snugly up against the backrest.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue lowering the sled through the full range of motion you can while keeping your buttocks firmly on the seat, lower back flat against the backrest, and heels fully on the platform.
    1. People can commonly lower the sled until their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your mobility, body proportions, and foot placement, along with the design of the machine you are using.
    2. If you reached the end range to which your hips can bend, lowering the sled any further would cause your lower back to start rounding, which would be demonstrated by your hip bones starting to curl forward away from the backrest. Setting up with your feet excessively high on the platform can reduce how deeply you can descend before reaching the limit to which your hips can bend.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track over your toes, lowering the sled any further would cause your heels to start lifting off the platform. Setting up with your feet excessively low on the platform can reduce how deeply you can descend before reaching this point.
    4. If you can descend with good technique until the sled reaches the bottom of the rails, you can lightly tap the sled against the bottom without resting in the bottom position.
7. Drive your feet into the platform to push the sled away from the platform as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees during this exercise, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, lower back flat against the back rest, and knees aligned over your feet.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set.
9. After your knees are straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, lock the safety handles with your hands to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the sled.
10. Lower the sled onto the safety stoppers by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your quads, gluteus maximus, and multiple hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near lower chest height.
    1. Setting the J-hooks a bit lower for a Marrs bar than for a straight bar is appropriate  for most lifters, but the optimal starting height should specifically place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back.
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Place the Marrs bar on top of the J-hooks with the handles angled down so they will be in front of your torso when facing the direction in which you will squat.
    1. The padded center of the bar will rest on your upper back.
    2. Note that the bar can start rotating after you place it on the hooks. When this happens, you can rotate the bar back to the appropriate starting position while setting up.
3. Face the center of the bar in a shoulder-width stance.
4. Shallowly bend your hips and knees, walk under the bar, grasp its handles, and position your shoulders snugly under the shoulder pads with the base of the bar resting on your back.
    1. Your palms should be facing inward while holding the handles with bent elbows.
    2. The bar should be securely resting in a comfortable and stable spot where you would not even need to use your hands to keep the bar in position, but holding the handles can provide additional stability.
    3. The appropriate bar placement should not be applying pressure to the base of your neck.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when squatting.
7. Repeat steps 3 and 4 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Stand upright with the bar by straightening your knees and hips.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. If your machine lets you adjust it, set the pads to a starting height that allows you to stand with your shoulders snugly under the pads while your knees are slightly bent.
    1. Assuming the starting position with at least a slight knee bend will let you unrack the machine.
    2. If you are using a machine that lacks an adjustable starting height and are not tall enough to unrack the machine, a different variation can be more suitable for your body type.
2. In a near shoulder-width stance with your toes pointing slightly outward, stand with your feet fully on the footplate facing toward the machine’s base. Place your shoulders snugly underneath the shoulder pads in a position that feels comfortable and stable to you.
    1. While many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this lets them squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a hip-width stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting through your full range of motion, which may involve trying out different stances over time to test for yourself.
    2. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Depending on the size of your machine’s footplate, you may have some space to vary specifically how far forward you place your feet. Starting near the middle of the footplate is often appropriate, but you can feel free to try out different foot placements to determine which position personally feels best to you.
3. Without having any weight plates added to the machine, perform a couple of reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section before finishing your setup.
    1. These initial reps will help you determine what your full range of motion entails and inform you if any adjustments (such as to your foot placement or machine settings) are warranted.
4. If your machine has an adjustable safety stopper, set it where it will be just below the machine’s lever arm at the bottom of your squat.
    1. The ideal safety stopper height should be low enough to let you train through your full range of motion while being high enough to receive the lever arm if you fail a rep.
5. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
6. Repeat step 2 and proceed to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Straighten your knees to stand upright and lift the machine’s lever arm off the stopper.
2. Use your hand to disengage the stopper, and grasp the machine’s stationary handles (not the stopper release) for additional stability if your machine has them.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
4. Simultaneously bend at your hips and knees to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and lower back flat.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the footplate.
    2. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
5. Continue squatting down through the full range of motion you can comfortably perform without lifting your heels off the footplate or rounding your back.
    1. People can commonly descend until their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees, and some lifters can reach the point at which their thighs touch their calves with their knees fully bent. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement and the machine model you are using.
    2. If you reach the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track forward over your toes, squatting any deeper can cause your heels to start lifting off the footplate.
    3. If you reach the end range to which your hips can bend, descending any further can cause your lower back to start rounding.
    4. With certain machines, you may reach the limit to which the lever arm can physically lower at the bottom of your squat.
    5. Squatting into a deep position is not inherently problematic for the knees, but there is no need to force yourself into the lowest position possible if you experience knee discomfort at your end range.
6. Reverse the movement by driving your feet into the footplate to squat up as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when squatting, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, aim to push evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the footplate, knees aligned over your feet, chest up, and lower back flat.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Once you are upright following your last rep, use your hand to engage the stopper before bending your knees to rerack the weight.
## Description
While also training your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus, this exercise can effectively develop your quads.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles approximately level with your upper chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in a bit of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The optimal seat height should let the exercise’s pressing motion feel natural and comfortable for your shoulders.
    3. If the seat is excessively low or high, the exercise may feel awkward and/or uncomfortable.
2. If your machine has adjustable stoppers (that are positioned under the arms of some machines), set them to a height that lets you press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with proper positioning, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Your feet should be flat on the ground under your bent knees.
    2. Based on your preference and the height of the back pad, you can rest your head against the back pad or keep it just in front of the pad with your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead when standing upright.
5. Firmly grasp the handles while stacking your elbows with your wrists such that your forearms align with the incline angle at which you will press.
    1. With a super incline press, you will be pressing at a high incline that should result in your forearms angling diagonally upward and forward. After starting your set, you can modify your initial forearm angle based on what feels most natural and comfortable with the machine you are using.
    2. Aligning your forearms with the incline angle at which you will press will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    3. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    4. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Placing your hands mildly outside shoulder width can work well for many people, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Throughout your set, avoid letting your shoulders round forward into a slumped posture.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout the exercise.
2. While keeping your forearms aligned with the angle at which you are pressing, press the handles upward and moderately forward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. With a greater degree of vertical motion than horizontal motion due to the high incline, you can think about driving your elbows diagonally forward and upward while they progressively straighten.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your chest up, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handles to move downward and backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your forearms aligned with the angle of your press.
    2. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers behind them or stop just before that point.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arms abruptly back in an uncontrolled fashion.
    4. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While not training your lower and middle pec major as effectively as lower incline presses, this exercise can develop your upper pec major, front delts, and triceps.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit on the seat facing the handles.
    1. Place your feet on the floor under your bent knees.
2. Reach forward with both arms and firmly hook the tips of your four long fingers around the front handles with your thumbs pointing up, so that they are not in between the front and back handles.
    1. This type of machine typically has a pair of higher handles (where gripping angles your palms downward and inward) and a pair of lower handles (where gripping angles your palms upward and inward).
    2. People most often use the higher handles, but you can use whichever handles you prefer.
3. By driving your elbows back in a rowing motion, pull the handles back toward you.
4. Once the front handles and back handles are sufficiently close together, firmly hook your thumbs around the back handles, with these handles snugly contacting the bases of your palms.
    1. The appropriate grip should result in the webspaces between your thumbs and index fingers touching the back handles.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze the handles together by curling your four long fingers toward your palms.
    1. Throughout this exercise, you will predominantly be moving your fingers through motion at your knuckle joints.
    2. This motion is similar to the “come here” hand gesture performed with your four long fingers.
    3. Continue keeping your elbows and wrists still while your thumbs remain stationary around the back handle throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by uncurling your fingers to let the handles spread apart from each other.
    1. You should finish with the finger bones attached to your knuckles pointing straight forward away from your wrists while continuing to keep your fingertips hooked around the front handles.
    2. If you feel as if the front handles are sliding out of your fingers, you should wrap your fingertips further around them to secure your grip.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weight rapidly pull the handles apart in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
4. Drive your elbows back in a rowing motion to lift the bases of your palms off the back handles and point your thumbs mildly forward before setting the front handles down.
    1. Release your four long fingers from the bottom handle after the machine is no longer providing any resistance.
## Description
While also effectively training the majority of your intrinsic hand muscles, this exercise can develop your forearms’s flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundas.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat height to a comfortable position that will allow the lower aspect of your triceps to rest against the machine’s upper arm pads.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit down on its seat with an upright posture, your feet flat on the floor, and your back against the backrest.
3. After positioning your triceps against the machine’s upper arm pads, grasp the handles with an underhand grip that results in your palms facing forward.
## Exercise technique
1. Starting from a position where your elbows are straight, curl the handles upward by bending at your elbows while keeping your upper arms pinned against the machine’s arm pads (rather than allowing your upper arms to swing forward).
2. After your elbows have fully bent, reverse the motion by lowering the handles in a controlled manner until your elbows have straightened.
    1. Continue to keep your upper arms in contact with the arm pads.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your elbows and the backside of your upper arms comfortably on the arm pads while aligning your elbows with pivot points when sitting on the seat.
    1. The pivot points are the axes about which the machine’s lever arms rotate, and they are commonly denoted by small dots or circles.
    2. If the seat is too low, you may need to shrug your shoulders up to rest your triceps on the arm pads.
    3. If the seat is too high, you may restrict the range of motion for the exercise.
    4. Some trial and error is typically needed to find the ideal seat height for you.
    5. If the exercise feels awkward, you most likely did not select the seat height that is optimal for your body proportions.
2. After selecting your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin(s), sit down on the seat
    1. These machines commonly include two separate weight stacks, where each weight stack provides resistance to the handle on its side.
    2. You optionally can grasp the handles initially while standing above the seat before sitting down.
3. With your palms facing up, reach forward and firmly grasp the handles while resting the backside of your upper arms on the arm pads, and aligning your elbows with the pivot points on each side of the machine.
    1. If the handles are a little out of your reach, you could slightly stand up and lean forward to reach the handles before sitting back down after you have grasped the handles.
    2. Your wrists should be mostly straight throughout your set after grasping the handles.
    3. In the starting position, your elbows should be straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the backside of your upper arms snugly in contact with the arm pads, fully bend your elbows to curl the handles back toward you.
    1. While your forearms move in an arc through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set as you continue keeping your elbows aligned with the pivot points.
2. Reverse the motion by letting the handles move forward in a controlled manner as your elbows straighten toward the starting position.
    1. Aim to move the full range of motion through which the machine provides resistance to finish with your elbows close to being fully straightened.
    2. These machines often stop providing resistance just before the point at which the elbows can fully straighten.
    3. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance abruptly yank your forearms forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to an axle bar (AKA thick bar) situated on the ground.
    1. Except for using a different bar, the setup and technique for an axle deadlift are the same as for a conventional deadlift.
    2. If you lack an axle bar, you can replicate the grip demands of an axle deadlift by connecting fat grip attachments to a standard barbell shoulder-width apart.
    3. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of full-diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates or any bumper plates) will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. If you only use smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to perform a deficit deadlift with appropriate technique. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize 45lb (20kg) plates, you can perform a block pull or rack pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with full diameter plates.
2. While facing the center of the bar, stand in an approximately hip-width stance with the middle of your feet under the bar and your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly out to the sides.
    1. For most lifters, this situates the bar about one inch (2.5cm) away from the shins.
    2. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    3. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from hinging at your hips than bending your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have fully sat back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    4. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and help engage your back muscles.
4. With your elbows fully straightened, firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into your most advantageous starting position.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. As you forcefully squeeze the bar, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, the bar close to your legs, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the bar reaches near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
5. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. Just like when lifting the bar, keep your lower back flat and chest up as you descend while actively avoiding letting your lower back round or chest collapse.
    2. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees. Then you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the ground rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
## Description
This exercise emphasizes grip training to a greater degree than other deadlift variations while still training the other muscles involved with deadlifting.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed-height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a Buffalo bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. The appropriate height of safety pins can be lower when using a cambered bar compared to a straight bar.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of your rep’s lowering phase.
    1. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Use your hands to rotate the bar so that its cambered center is directed straight up if it is not already facing this way.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward and your hands mildly to moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists as you squeeze the bar to prevent it from rotating.
    1. Some people may find that their shoulders feel more comfortable when using a grip that is a bit closer than their standard grip width with a straight bar, but others find using the same grip width to work well for them.
    2. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best when using the cambered bar.
    3. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning to bench press, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    4. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    5. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting, like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Continue squeezing the bar throughout your set to prevent it from rotating and keep the center part of its shaft pointing straight up.
    2. Prioritize keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness during the unrack.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner. Progressively bend your elbows and allow them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set when viewed from the side. While some lifters can lower the bar fully to the touch the chest with quality technique, how far you can lower the bar while maintaining that alignment will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility.
    2. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    3. If you feel shoulder discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely descended excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training this exercise over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into a range that does not feel right for your shoulders.
    4. When lowering the bar toward your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    5. This bar path will require that you keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of fully flaring them out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    6. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a controlled and fairly slow tempo is recommended, particularly as you approach the bottom position.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to bench press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed-height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to bench press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, bench pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a cambered bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. The appropriate height of safety pins can be lower when using a cambered bar compared to a straight bar.
4. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of your rep’s lowering phase.
    1. If you are not training with safety pins/spotter arms that can be positioned to the ideal height, you can avoid adding a pair of collars to the end of the bar. This would allow you to dump the weight to one side and allow the weights to slide off the bar if you become pinned underneath the bar after failing a rep. The con of not using collars is that the plates can accidentally slide to one side when pressing if you happen to tilt the bar asymmetrically, but it can be an acceptable tradeoff to make sure that you can avoid being pinned if you fail a rep.
5. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
6. Use your hands to rotate the bar so that its cambered center is directed straight up if it is not already facing this way.
7. Firmly grasp the straight sides of the bar’s shaft with your palms facing forward and your hands mildly to moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists as you squeeze the bar to prevent it from rotating.
    1. The minimum grip width you can use will be dictated by the design of the specific cambered bar you have, because you will not be grasping the angled parts of the bar or the center portion of the bar that should now be situated higher above your chest than the outer parts of its shaft. Certain bars may require people (particularly those with narrower frames) to use a wider-than-typical grip to grasp the bar. If this applies to you and you find that the closest grip you can use feels uncomfortably wide when pressing, a different pressing variation may be better suited for you.
    2. Some people may find that their shoulders feel more comfortable when using a grip that is a bit closer than their standard grip width with a straight bar, but others find using the same grip width to work well for them.
    3. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best when using the cambered bar.
    4. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re just learning to bench press, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    5. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    6. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneously brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Continue squeezing the bar throughout your set to prevent it from rotating and keep the center part of its shaft pointing straight up.
    2. Prioritize keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness during the unrack.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower chest in a controlled manner. Progressively bend your elbows and allow them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your wrists as your forearms remain close to vertical throughout the set when viewed from the side. How far you can lower the bar while maintaining that alignment will be affected by your individual shoulder mobility.
    2. When moving through your full range of motion, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom.
    3. If you feel shoulder discomfort or pain at the bottom, you most likely descended excessively far relative to your current mobility. Training this exercise over time may improve your mobility, but you should not force yourself into a range that does not feel right for your shoulders.
    4. When lowering the bar toward your lower chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line.
    5. This bar path will require that you keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of fully flaring them out, and the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare can naturally occur when keeping your elbows stacked under your wrists as you bring the bar to your lower chest. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
    6. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, using a controlled and fairly slow tempo is recommended, particularly as you approach the bottom position.
4. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your lower chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
6. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
7. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts and triceps.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, place a cambered squat bar on top of them, and face the center of the bar with a near shoulder-width stance.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back.
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Based on your preference, grasp the horizontal bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you, or grasp the vertical posts.
    1. If you have the mobility to grasp the horizontal bar comfortably, you can set up just as you would when back squatting with a straight bar.
    2. If back squatting with a straight bar prompts you to feel pain or discomfort in your shoulders, elbows, and/or wrists due to mobility limitations and/or prior injuries, holding the vertical posts may let you squat more comfortably.
    3. Which part of the vertical posts is ideal to hold can vary based on the design of your specific bar and your mobility. The appropriate height for your hand placement should feel comfortable and stable to you once you are under the bar, while allowing you to keep your upper back tight. Finding which hand position best accomplishes these objectives for you as an individual can require some trial and error.
3. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should be securely resting on the muscles of your upper back when squeezing your shoulder blades together.
    2. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    3. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    4. While you can use a lower bar placement, this variation is often performed with the bar resting on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts (on the back of your shoulders) and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine).
    5. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when squatting.
6. Repeat steps 2 and 3 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. With your shoulder blades pulled back and your chest up in a proud posture, stand upright with the bar by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. If the bar starts swinging, let it settle for a second before walking it out.
    2. Keep the bar snugly on your upper back throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps back, and position your feet near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly out to the sides.
    1. While people often find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the ideal stance width and degree of toe flare will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip-width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside shoulder width and their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Once the bar is motionless, initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Avoid starting your descent if the bar is still oscillating following your walkout. You may need to stand still for a moment longer compared to when you use a straight bar.
    2. To prevent the bar from swinging excessively, it may be helpful to descend a bit slower than you typically do when back squatting.
    3. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    4. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    5. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    6. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down through the full range of motion your mobility allows without rounding your back, lifting your heels off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, your heels lift off the ground, or your knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting too deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Avoid letting your hips shoot back excessively, which could prompt the bar to swing and pitch your torso forward.
    2. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Walk forward until the bar contacts the back of each J-cup before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the machine’s thigh pad to a level that positions the tops of your hip bones somewhat above the top of the pad when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. This position will allow you to move freely about your hips in contrast to if the pad is contacting the top of your hip bones, which would restrict hip motion and prompt you to perform the exercise via spinal motion.
    2. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones (rather than only somewhat lower), some individuals may feel knee discomfort, so a sweet spot can exist for pad placement.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position after initially completing step 4.
2. Select your weight with the machine’s loading pin.
3. While placing your hands onto the thigh pad or handles for balance support, step onto the foot plate and position your lower calves snugly against the lower leg pads with your heels on the back of the foot plate.
    1. Position your feet symmetrically in a stance that feels comfortable to you, which can involve pointing your toes somewhat outward or pointing them fairly straight forward.
    2. Situating your feet approximately hip width to shoulder apart will result in a viable stance width, but how wide of a stance you can use will be influenced by the width of your machine’s foot plate.
    3. To set your lower calves against the pads, your heels may hang off the edge of the foot plate, but the rest of your soles should be firmly planted on the foot plate.
    4. Your knees should be straight after you have finished setting your feet on the foot plate.
4. With your knees straightened, snugly rest your thighs against the thigh pad, let your arms hang down, and confirm that the tops of your hip bones are now somewhat above the top of the pad.
    1. If the pad is not positioned appropriately, readjust before getting into this starting position again.
## Exercise technique
1. Hinge at your hips to lower your torso until you are low enough to grasp the bar firmly with your elbows straight, palms facing backward, and hands about shoulder-width apart.
    1. Keep your lower back flat or slightly arched, rather than rounding your lower back to reach the bar.
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Maintain a straight knee position throughout your set, but avoid intentionally locking your knees out by squeezing your quads.
    4. Depending on your limb lengths and the height of your machine, you may have to round your upper back to reach the bar.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
3. While keeping your back in a rigid position, lift your torso up through your hips until you can fully squeeze your glutes with your torso aligned with your legs.
    1. Throughout your set, aim to have all the movement occur from hip motion.
    2. At the top, you should essentially be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side, like you could do when standing upright.
    3. Avoid arching your lower back excessively at the top, which can occur if you try to lift up as high as you physically can, rather than finishing once your glutes are fully squeezed and your torso is in alignment with your legs.
4. Reverse the movement to lower your torso by hinging at your hips through your full range of motion in a controlled manner without rounding your lower back.
    1. How deeply you are able to hinge without rounding your lower back will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch, you have finished descending through your available hip range of motion.
    3. With sufficient flexibility, you may be able to lower the bar until the weight you selected lightly taps the rest of the weight stack, indicating you have moved the full range of motion in which the machine can provide resistance.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar back down and dismount the machine.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Place a pair of farmer’s walk handles on the ground about shoulder width apart and add weight plates of your desired load to them.
    1. Position the handles parallel to each other.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand centered between the handles with your shoulders aligned with the middle points of the handles.
3. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, sit your hips back while bending your knees until you can reach the handles.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the center of each handle with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles. If you situate the handles closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the handles can slide down your hand while holding them, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
6. Drive through the ground to lift the handles upward as you extend (straighten) your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Walk forward while holding the handles at your sides, maintaining an upright posture with your shoulders approximately level.
    1. While you will not swing your arms like you would when walking normally, your torso and lower body could otherwise look similar to how they would when you walk normally, with the understanding that your body will naturally make adjustments when carrying weights. If you use a weight that is fairly heavy to you, it is natural to take shorter strides compared to how you typically walk.
    2. Walk at a pace where you feel stable and balanced throughout the carry. Some lifters may wish to move at a very brisk pace when aiming to cover a particular duration in the least amount of time, while others may wish to walk with a more controlled tempo.
    3. Strive to keep the tops of your hip bones approximately level throughout your set rather than letting them drop down to one side when taking each step.
2. Continue walking with the handles until you have completed your set and are ready to set the handles down.
    1. It is common to have to change direction before completing your set. At this point, it will be most efficient to set the handles down before turning around, picking them up, and continuing to walk in the opposite direction.
    2. Rather than setting the handles down, you alternatively can pivot to face the opposite direction while holding the handles, but doing so can be challenging with heavy loads.
    3. Whenever you are ready to set the handles down, sit your hips back while bending your knees as you keep your lower back flat and chest up.
## Description
Rather than concentrating the workload on one body region, this exercise trains a variety of muscle groups throughout your body in an integrated manner, including the muscles in your forearms, upper back, core, and lower body.## Setting up
1. Determine which type of weight you will use to provide additional resistance for this exercise and set it up under a pull-up bar.
    1. Based on your equipment availability and preference, you can wear a pull-up/dip belt around your waist with a weight plate looped through it, hold a medicine ball between your feet, or strap ankle weights around your ankles.
    2. If you use a pull-up/dip belt, its back should be securely resting on top of your buttocks, and the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) should be looped through the center hole of a weight plate. Situate the plate flat against the front of your legs (rather than between your legs). If you have bumper plates available that can provide your intended weight, using at least one of them is recommended over other weight plates. If you are using multiple plates of different diameters, position the largest diameter plate directly against your legs with the smaller diameter plate in front of the larger one.
    3. If you are using a medicine ball, center it under the pull-up bar. Based on your height and the bar’s height, you may have to elevate the ball on something like an aerobic step or bumper plate to position it within the reach of your feet once you are hanging from the bar. Note that holding a medicine ball between your feet can be less stable than using a pull-up/dip belt or ankle weights.
2. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. Ideally use a bar that is high enough to allow you to hang from it with straight legs without your feet touching the ground.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground and lifting your heels up, you can hop up from the ground to reach the bar or initially stand on a step to place the bar within your reach. If you use an object as a step to reach the bar, position that object a little behind the bar so that your feet do not touch that object when you are hanging from the bar with straight legs.
    3. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles. Setting up this grip can be more difficult if you have to jump up to reach the bar. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide upward when hanging, which pulls on your hand skin in a manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
3. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet off the ground to begin hanging on the bar. Allow your legs to hang down toward the ground with straight knees.
    1. While hanging, you should be able to draw a straight line vertically down from your torso through your legs.
    2. You will maintain a firm grip on the bar with straightened elbows throughout your set.
    3. If your bar is not high enough to keep your feet off the ground when your legs are directed straight down toward the ground (which may often be the case with doorway pull-up bars), bend your knees by the minimum needed to lift your feet just off the ground.
4. If you are using a medicine ball, firmly grip it between the inner parts of your feet and keep the ball squeezed between your feet throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your legs up and toward your torso through your available range of motion while curling your hips forward and only allowing your knees to bend mildly.
    1. If you keep your knees locked in a fully straightened position throughout the movement, your hamstring flexibility can limit how high you can lift your legs.
    2. Ideally keep your knees as close to straight as you can without your hamstring flexibility restricting your range of motion, which should result in your feet clearly being in front of your knees.
    3. You can keep your knees completely straight during the initial portion of the movement and allow them to bend as needed while progressively lifting your legs, since the hamstrings progressively lengthen as the thighs lift higher up (through hip flexion).
    4. Curling your hips forward will round your lower back and result in your buttocks facing forward at the end of the lifting phase.
    5. Keep your chest facing straight ahead rather than leaning your torso backward.
    6. Your legs should move in an arcing manner as they initially move upward and forward before moving upward and backward after they lift beyond being parallel to the ground.
    7. How high you are able to raise your legs up will be influenced by the mobility in your hips and lower back, along with your strength.
    8. Whenever you feel like you cannot bring your legs any higher up without excessively bending your knees, you have reached the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your legs to descend while uncurling your lower back until you have returned to the starting position with your thighs directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Actively lower your legs throughout the descent rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you back down, which could result in your legs swinging back behind the starting position as momentum takes over.
    2. Your knees can progressively straighten as you descend.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet to the ground.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Position a cable station’s pulley to its lowest height, select your weight with the loading pin, and connect a D-handle attachment to the cable.
2. With your feet near shoulder-width apart, stand with the side of one shoulder (on the same side of your body you intend to train) facing the cable station.
3. Squat down and grasp the handle firmly with the hand closer to the cable station as your palm faces in toward your side and your elbow is straight.
    1. Avoid rounding your lower back while setting up.
4. By extending (straightening) your hips and knees, stand upright with the handle while keeping your lower back flat, elbow straight, and arm aligned with the cable.
    1. If it helps you feel more stable, you can optionally maintain a slight bend in your knees rather than fully straightening them.
    2. You can situate your resting hand wherever you feel helps maintain your balance, such as raised out to the side or resting on your hip.
    3. All the subsequent directions will apply to the side in which you are holding the cable.
5. In the starting position, you should feel tension from the cable pulling your shoulder girdle down lower than your other side. If you do not feel tension when standing directly above the pulley, step out to the side away from the pulley until you do.
    1. While the cable’s resistance should pull your shoulder girdle down, avoid letting it bend your whole torso to the side through spinal motion.
    2. Aim to keep your chest up and back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Without bending your elbow, shrug your shoulder up toward your earlobe through the full range of motion you can to lift the handle upward.
    1. Keep your torso upright as you avoid letting the cable’s resistance bend your torso toward the pulley, as would otherwise occur during a side-bend exercise.
    2. Throughout the exercise, look straight ahead to keep your neck near a neutral position.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting the cable’s resistance pull your shoulder fully down.
    1. Move through your full range of motion, which may prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in the region of your upper trap (on the back of your neck and uppermost upper back) at the bottom.
    2. At the end of this motion, your working side’s shoulder should be positioned a little lower than your resting side’s shoulder due to the downward stretch that the cable can provide.
    3. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper trap and the smaller levator scapulae situated deep to your upper trap.## Setting up
1. Place a pair of dumbbells on the ground, about shoulder-width apart, with their handles parallel to each other.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can move one dumbbell into position at a time.
    2. When initially picking up the dumbbells and setting them down, use the deadlift motion described in the exercise technique section, where you avoid rounding your lower back excessively.
2. Stand between the dumbbells in a near hip-width stance with the center of each handle aligned with the middle of your feet.
    1. Based on what feels most comfortable, point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the sides.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the handles, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the handles. Strive to keep your chest up as you avoid rounding your lower back excessively.
    1. Since this is a deadlift rather than a squat, more motion should come from your hips than your knees, and your hips should remain well above your knees as your knees do not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. With typical dumbbell handles being closer to the ground than a barbell that is loaded with full diameter plates (e.g., 45lb (20kg) plates or bumper plates), you will need to descend a greater distance to grasp dumbbells compared to a barbell (loaded with full diameter plates).
    3. When descending primarily through a hip hinge rather than a squat (which would involve lowering the hips closer to knee height while bending the knees more and moving them further forward), the low handle height can prompt some people to reach the limit to which they can bend at their hips before reaching the dumbbells if they do not let their backs move to any visible degree.
    4. Consequently, it is not unusual to have to assume a mildly rounded back position to get into the bottom position of this exercise. Strive to have this modest rounding distributed throughout the entirety of your lower, middle, and upper back rather than only rounding your lower back.
    5. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles. Your palms should be facing in toward your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While squeezing the dumbbells, start submaximally pressing the middle of your feet into the ground to develop “pre-tension” in your lower body and back muscles.
    1. When combined with bracing your core, this step can help position you in your most efficient starting position and lock your back into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
    2. If the weights were lighter, this maneuver would prompt you to begin lifting them up off the ground.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the dumbbells as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees. Strive to keep your chest up and back tight while you avoid rounding your lower back.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the handles reach near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout the exercise, keep your arms directed straight down toward the ground.
5. While keeping your chest up, reverse the movement by sitting your hips back and bending your knees to lower the dumbbells until they have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. While recognizing that a mild degree of back rounding may naturally occur toward the end of the lowering phase, refrain from excessive lower back rounding as you chiefly hinge at your hips.
    2. To avoid turning the movement into a squat, you can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until you feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings. Then you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the dumbbells.
6. Allow the dumbbells to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than trying to bounce the weights off the ground) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the dumbbells on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weights are on the ground rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can train your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus, along with your hamstrings and quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Determine which type of weight you will use to provide additional resistance for this exercise and set it up under a pull-up bar.
    1. Based on your equipment availability and preference, you can wear a pull-up/dip belt around your waist with a weight plate looped through it, hold a medicine ball or dumbbell between your feet, or strap ankle weights around your ankles.
    2. If you use a pull-up/dip belt, its back should be securely resting on top of your buttocks, and the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) should be looped through the center hole of a weight plate. Situate the plate flat against the front of your legs (rather than between your legs). If you have bumper plates available that can provide your intended weight, using at least one of them is recommended over other weight plates. If you are using multiple plates of different diameters, position the largest diameter plate directly against your legs with the smaller diameter plate in front of the larger one.
    3. If you are using a medicine ball or dumbbell, place it centered under the pull-up bar. With a dumbbell, you can situate it with one end on the ground and its handle in a vertical position. Based on your height and the bar’s height, you may have to elevate the weight on something like an aerobic step or bumper plate to position the weight within the reach of your feet once you are hanging from the bar. Note that holding a medicine ball or dumbbell between your feet can be less stable than using a pull-up/dip belt or ankle weights.
2. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. Ideally use a bar that is high enough to allow you to hang from it with straight legs without your feet touching the ground.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground and lifting your heels up, you can hop up from the ground to reach the bar or initially stand on a step to place the bar within your reach. If you use an object as a step to reach the bar, position that object a little behind the bar so that your feet do not touch that object when you are hanging from the bar with straight legs.
    3. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles. Setting up this grip can be more difficult if you have to jump up to reach the bar. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide upward when hanging, which pulls on your hand skin in a manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
3. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet off the ground to begin hanging on the bar. Allow your legs to hang down toward the ground with straight knees.
    1. While hanging, you should be able to draw a straight line vertically down from your torso through your legs.
    2. You will maintain a firm grip on the bar with straightened elbows throughout your set.
    3. If your bar is not high enough to keep your feet off the ground when your legs are directed straight down toward the ground (which may often be the case with doorway pull-up bars), bend your knees by the minimum needed to lift your feet just off the ground.
4. 
5. 
## Exercise technique
1. March up both knees toward your chest through your available range of motion while curling your hips forward and allowing your knees to progressively bend as they lift higher up.
    1. Curling your hips forward will round your lower back and result in your buttocks facing forward at the end of the lifting phase.
    2. Keep your chest facing straight ahead rather than leaning your torso backward.
    3. You do not consciously need to think about progressively bending your knees since they will naturally bend when marching your knees up unless you intentionally try to straighten them.
    4. How closely you are able to raise your knees up toward your chest will be influenced by the mobility in your hips and lower back, along with your strength.
    5. Whenever you feel like you cannot bring your knees any closer toward your chest, you have reached the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your knees to descend while uncurling your lower back until you have returned to the starting position with your thighs directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Actively lower your legs throughout the descent rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you back down, which could result in your legs swinging back behind the starting position as momentum takes over.
    2. Your knees can naturally transition to a less bent position as you lower your legs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet to the ground.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Determine which type of weight you will use to provide additional resistance for this exercise and set it up by the captain’s chair.
    1. Based on your equipment availability and preference, you can wear a pull-up/dip belt around your waist with a weight plate looped through it, hold a medicine ball or dumbbell between your feet, or strap ankle weights around your ankles.
    2. If you use a pull-up/dip belt, its back should be securely resting on top of your buttocks, and the belt’s chain or strap (depending on the belt model) should be looped through the center hole of a weight plate. Situate the plate flat against the front of your legs (rather than between your legs). If you have bumper plates available that can provide your intended weight, using at least one of them is recommended over other weight plates. If you are using multiple plates of different diameters, position the largest diameter plate directly against your legs with the smaller diameter plate in front of the larger one.
    3. If you are using a medicine ball or dumbbell, place it in the center of the captain’s chair. With a dumbbell, you can situate it with one end on the ground and its handle in a vertical position. Based on your height and the captain’s chair, you may have to elevate the weight on something like an aerobic step or bumper plate to position the weight within the reach of your feet once you are set up. Note that holding a medicine ball or dumbbell between your feet can be less stable than using a pull-up/dip belt or ankle weights.
2. After initially standing in the center of the captain’s chair with your back facing its back pad, step up onto its foot plates while placing your hands against the arm pads for any balance assistance as needed.
3. Bend your elbows and place the bottom of your forearms onto the arm pads while firmly grasping the handles with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Your back should be contacting the backrest while your torso is in an upright position.
4. While bearing the force from your bodyweight through your elbows into the arm pads, pick your feet up off the foot plates and allow them to hang straight down toward the ground.
    1. Keep your shoulder blades and shoulders near the natural position in which they are when normally standing upright, rather than letting your shoulder blades and shoulders shrug up from gravity pulling your torso down.
    2. You should be able to draw a straight line vertically down from your torso through your legs.
5. If you are using a medicine ball or dumbbell, firmly grip it between the inner parts of your feet and keep the weight squeezed between your feet throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. March up both knees toward your chest through your available range of motion while curling your hips forward and allowing your knees to progressively bend as they lift higher up.
    1. Curling your hips forward will round your lower back and result in your buttocks facing forward at the end of the lifting phase.
    2. You do not consciously need to think about progressively bending your knees since they will naturally bend when marching your knees up unless you intentionally try to straighten them.
    3. How closely you are able to raise your knees up toward your chest will be influenced by the mobility in your hips and lower back, along with your strength.
    4. Whenever you feel like you cannot bring your knees any closer toward your chest, you have reached the end of the lifting phase.
    5. Keep your upper body stationary.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your knees to descend while uncurling your lower back until you have returned to the starting position with your thighs directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Actively lower your legs throughout the descent rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you back down, which could result in your legs swinging back behind the starting position as momentum takes over.
    2. Your knees can naturally transition to a less bent position as you lower your legs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet onto the foot plates and step down from the captain’s chair.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to approximately shoulder height, and select your desired load with the loading pin.
2. Based on your preference, leave the cable without any handle attachment or connect a D-handle attachment to the cable.
    1. Either option allows you to train your rear delt effectively, so feel free to try out both options and use whichever feels most comfortable to you.
3. Stand with the side of your shoulder (on the side you will not train during this set) facing the cable station, and take a step backward.
4. With your arm that is opposite to the cable, reach across your body with a slightly bent elbow and firmly grasp the ball at the end of the cable (if you are not using any handle attachment) or the D-handle (if you are using a handle attachment). Optionally grasp the cable station’s beam with your free hand for additional stability throughout the exercise.
    1. Keep your working side’s elbow slightly bent from this point onward.
    2. If you grasp the cable directly, your palm should face down, but if you grasp a D-handle, your palm should face in toward the pulley.
5. If you do not currently feel resistance from the cable with your arm reaching fully across your body, take a small step out to the side away from the pulley until you do.
    1. You ideally want to experience resistance from the cable in the starting position of each rep.
    2. Rather than having your torso twisted with your chest facing the pulley, your chest and imaginary belt buckle should be facing the same direction. Having your chest rotated toward the pulley may prompt you not to feel tension from the cable in the starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. Without bending your elbow, smoothly sweep your arm out away from the pulley and across your body in a wide arcing motion.
    1. You can finish once your arm is directed straight out to the side of your shoulder, resulting in your torso and arm forming half of a “T-shape.”
    2. Keep your torso upright with your chest up throughout the set, while all the motion occurs from your arm moving at your shoulder.
    3. Continue keeping your chest and imaginary belt buckle facing straight ahead in the same direction as you avoid twisting your torso.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arm to sweep fluidly in toward the starting position through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Just like when you finished setting up, you should feel a stretching sensation in the back of your shoulder when your arm is positioned fully across your body.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should actively resist the weight rather than letting it abruptly yank your arm inward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps, rhomboids, lateral delt, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. Fully lower the Smith machine’s safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) and the machine’s bar before adding weight plates to the bar and making sure that the safety hooks are rotated out away from the safety bars (rather than being underneath the safety bars).
2. Stand close in front of the middle of the bar with approximately a hip-width stance.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, you can position yourself to face the side where the bar will track slightly backward when lifting it (which places the bottom of the rails farther in front of you than the top of the rails). With an angled Smith machine, stand with the middle of your feet under the bar to leave a bit of space between it and your legs. If you stand too close to the bar, your torso can restrict the bar path when lifting it, so you adjust where you stand if that occurs during the set.
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face, and you can stand with the bar just in front of your legs.
3. Sit your hips back and bend your knees until you can firmly grasp the bar with a mildly outside shoulder-width grip as your elbows are straight and your palms point backward.
    1. A mildly outside shoulder-width grip works well for many lifters, but feel free to try out somewhat different grip widths to determine if any particular hand positions feel best to you as an individual.
4. While keeping your elbows straight, stand upright with the bar.
    1. You can optionally maintain a slight forward torso lean throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by driving your elbows upward and out to your sides through a range of motion that feels comfortable to your shoulders while bending your elbows.
    1. Your mobility will affect how high you are comfortably able to lift the bar, and there is no need to force your shoulders into a range that provokes any discomfort.
    2. While different individuals may be able to lift higher up and others not quite as high, people often can lift their upper arms until their elbows are near shoulder height for a comfortable and effective range of motion.
    3. While motion occurs at your shoulder girdles and elbows, keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to descend in toward your sides as you lower the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with straight elbows.
    1. There is no need to lower the bar with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control the descent rather than letting the weight abruptly pull your arms down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt, upper trap, and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand centered between its handles in a near hip-width stance while facing toward the base of the machine.
    1. If your machine model lets you adjust the handle height, you can initially set the handles to the lowest setting. If you cannot grasp the handles during the next step without rounding your lower back, you can subsequently raise the handles to a height that lets you grasp them with a flat-back position.
    2. Compared to facing away from the base of the machine, facing toward it can often reinforce sitting back to perform a high-quality hip hinge during the exercise.
2. While keeping your lower back flat, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles, it is generally recommended to grasp the lower handles to let you hinge through your full range of motion during the exercise.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
4. Drive through your legs and thrust your hips forward to stand upright as you continue keeping your back flat and elbows straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Focus on “sitting back” rather than “bending over,” and your torso will naturally transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    2. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit back without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    3. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    4. Throughout the set, keep your feet fully planted on the ground and your neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically hinge until their hands are at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. If you have high hamstring flexibility and can hinge until the machine’s lever arms contact the stoppers underneath them, descend until you can tap the stoppers very lightly with the lever arms without resting in the bottom position.
    4. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
3. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. This should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and feet fully on the ground.
    2. At the end of the lifting phase, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips properly stacked under your shoulders, preventing excessive arching.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the handles down by sitting your hips back and bending your knees.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe between reps in the fully upright position, rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the Roman chair’s pad to a level that positions its top slightly below the top of your hip bones when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. The appropriate position should allow you to move your lower back freely while supporting your hips.
    2. Positioning the top of the pad higher than the top of your hip bones can restrict your range of motion.
    3. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones, you may feel less stable.
2. Face the side of the Roman chair with the side of your body you will train during the set closer toward the back base of the Roman chair.
3. While using your hands for balance assistance, step onto the foot plate one leg at a time while facing sideways relative to the thigh pad. Position the heel of one foot in front of your other foot’s toes as you rest the side of your hip and upper thigh against the thigh pad.
    1. Your feet should now be in a tandem stance as they are pointing forward to the side of the Roman chair with your lower legs against the lower leg pad and your knees straight.
    2. Based on what personally feels more comfortable and stable, you can position either foot in the forward position. Some people perform this exercise with the foot of the leg closer to the thigh pad in the front position, while others perform it with the opposite side further forward. Neither setup is inherently superior to the other for everyone, so it is recommended to try both and continue with the setup that feels best to you.
    3. Rather than being angled to the front or back of the Roman chair, your imaginary belt buckle (at the center point between your hip bones) and chest should be facing straight to the side of the Roman chair in the same direction.
4. Once you have set your legs, cross your arms across your chest or place them into whichever position you prefer.
    1. Your arm position will affect the difficulty of each rep, so you can adjust your position based on the challenge you wish to have.
    2. Crossing your arms across your chest is an appropriate starting position when learning the exercise, but holding your hands by the sides of your head can increase the intensity, and holding them straight up fully overhead can further increase the difficulty.
    3. Keep your arms in your preferred position throughout the set.
5. In the starting position, your torso and neck should near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    1. In a neutral position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your imaginary belt buckle through the center of your chest to your nose when viewed from the side of the Roman chair facing the front of your torso.
    2. Rather than being twisted or bent to one side, your back should be symmetrical, and it should exhibit its natural curvature instead of being rounded or excessively arched.
## Exercise technique
1. In a controlled manner, bend your torso sideways toward the ground through the full range of motion comfortable to you.
    1. Your mobility will affect how far you can comfortably side bend, and at your personal end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the muscles on the side of your torso (including your lower back) that is further away from the ground.
    2. On the side further away from the ground, your ribs should move away from your hip bone, while your lower side’s shoulder should move closer to the ground.
    3. While aiming to bend to your side without any other torso motions, you should avoid bending your lower back forward, arching it backward, or twisting it during this exercise.
    4. If laser beams were shooting out from the center of your chest and your imaginary belt buckle, they should continue pointing straight forward, parallel to each other and the ground. If your chest’s imaginary laser beam starts angling upward or downward, you are twisting your torso.
    5. When viewed from the front of the Roman chair, your shoulders should remain aligned, rather than one shoulder moving closer to the right or left side of the Roman chair, which would occur from torso twisting.
    6. Keep your legs and hips stationary throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by bending your torso to the opposite side to lift up and away from the ground.
    1. It can be appropriate to finish once you have returned to the starting position with a neutral back, but you can alternatively continue side bending beyond that point if you prefer.
    2. On the side further away from the ground, your ribs should move closer toward your hip bone, while your lower side’s shoulder should move away from the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your obliques and quadratus lumborum, while also training your erector spinae and intertransversarii lumborum.## Setting up
1. Pick up a weight plate on the side you are about to train.
    1. For comfort, you can optionally wear a beanie on your head or hold a small towel against the inner aspect of the plate. Resting the plate directly on your head without a soft fabric over your head can be uncomfortable, so using a beanie or towel is recommended.
    2. If you are new to this exercise, it is recommended to use a light plate, such as a 2.5-5lb (or 1.25-2.5kg).
2. Lie down with the other side of your body (not holding the plate) on a flat bench in a position that feels comfortable and stable to you, while having clearance for your neck and head to hang off the bench.
    1. You can situate your armpit directly on the edge of the bench’s head, letting your lower arm hang down with its hand touching the ground, or you can position the side of your bottom shoulder directly on the head of the bench with your arm under your torso.
    2. Some people prefer to keep their legs straight in alignment with the torso, but others may prefer to keep their knees bent and positioned slightly further forward than their hips.
    3. There is no single correct way specifically how to situate your body when lying on your side, since individuals with different body types will differ in terms of which exact positions feel the most comfortable and stable.
    4. Elevating yourself on a bench (or a piece of furniture like a bed or couch) can help you train through a full range of motion, but you can alternatively perform this exercise while lying on the ground.
3. Position the plate against the side of your skull (further away from the ground), a bit above the ear, and approximately centered between the front and back surfaces of your skull.
    1. When positioning the plate, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. The proper placement should feel stable while not applying excessive pressure to your ear or the uppermost part of your jaw.
## Exercise technique
1. Slowly tilt your head down as you bring your bottom ear toward your bottom shoulder. Move through your neck’s comfortable range of motion while using your hand to keep the plate positioned against the side of your skull throughout the set.
    1. How far you can comfortably tilt your head down will be affected by your neck mobility, so the end position will vary from person to person.
    2. When moving your neck’s full range of motion for side bending, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the muscles on the back of your neck on the same side as the plate, but you should not exceed the range of motion that is comfortable for your neck.
    3. Keep your face directed straight ahead to avoid rotating your neck toward the ground, which would angle your face toward the ground.
    4. As the top of your head tilts down closer toward the ground, the bottom of your jaw should naturally tilt up closer toward the ceiling.
    5. As you perform a neck side bending motion and use your top arm to keep the plate stable, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the movement through the full range of motion comfortable for your neck by tilting your head up as you bring your top ear toward your top shoulder.
    1. Aim to lift the plate exclusively with your neck muscles as you merely use your top arm to keep the plate against your head, rather than using your arm to assist in lifting the weight.
    2. At the top of the lifting phase, the top of your head should be higher above the ground than your jaw.
    3. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should prioritize moving your head in a well-controlled manner throughout the entirety of your set.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After completing your last rep, you can lower your head to where your neck is in a neutral position (with your head level rather than tilted to either side) before lifting the plate off your head and sitting up.
## Description
This exercise can train a variety of neck muscles, including your sternocleidomastoid, upper trap, cervical erector spinae, levator scapulae, and scalenes.## Setting up
1. You can select either a fixed-weight EZ bar or an adjustable plate-loaded EZ bar, which are functionally interchangeable for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down or hinge at your hips with straight elbows.
    1. If the EZ bar is already positioned in an elevated rack at mid-thigh level or above, you naturally would not have to squat down or hinge at your hips before picking it up.
3. Grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder-width grip and a semi-underhand grip (AKA semi-neutral grip) such that your palms are directed at an angle that is facing forward and inward.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a shoulder-width semi-underhand grip is accessible on the bar.
4. While keeping your elbows straight, stand fully upright while gripping the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you use hip motion instead of lower back motion to help drive the bar up.
2. While keeping your elbows straight, sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and your torso to incline slightly forward.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so.
3. As you simultaneously drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body, curl the bar upward in an arcing manner by bending at your elbows while allowing your elbows to swing a bit forward.
    1. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. Continue keeping your lower back near a neutral position instead of arching back excessively.
    3. While you are intentionally using momentum to help your biceps and other arm muscles lift the weight, you should not be “cheating” to the extent that you feel out of control.
4. After your elbows have fully bent, lower the bar in a controlled manner, allowing your elbows to straighten and return to your sides until you return to the starting position with straight elbows and the bar just in front of your thighs.
    1. Keep your torso upright and hips stationary as your biceps and other arm muscles control the descent without assistance from the rest of your body.
    2. Your muscles are noticeably stronger during the lowering phase of an exercise compared to the lifting phase, so “cheating” during the lifting phase allows your biceps to experience greater loading during the more controlled lowering phase.
    3. If you are unable to control the lowering phase to any reasonable degree, you are likely using a weight that is excessively heavy relative to your current ability.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near shoulder height, select your desired weight with the loading pin, and remove any handle attachment if it is connected to the cable.
2. Stand with the side of what will be working your shoulder facing the cable.
3. With your closer hand, firmly grasp the ball at the end of the cable, or the end of the cable directly with your little finger and the adjacent side of your hand contacting the ball. You can use your free hand to assist with setting your working side’s grip.
    1. Use whichever hand placement feels most comfortable and stable to you, but if you decide to grasp the cable directly, be aware to avoid pinching any fingers when returning the cable to its initial position after finishing your set.
    2. All the subsequent directions refer to the arm in which you are holding the cable. Keep your free arm in your preferred comfortable position throughout the exercise.
4. With your palm facing forward, walk out to the side away from the cable until you feel tension from the cable with your arm raised out away from your side in alignment with the cable.
    1. Your wrist should be straight as the cable is contacting the little finger-side of your hand. Keep your wrist straight throughout the exercise.
5. Set your feet about hip-width apart while standing upright with a mostly straight elbow (which can be slightly bent or fully straight based on what feels most comfortable to you).
    1. While you do not necessarily need to have an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid standing with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow mostly straight, sweep your arm upward and inward in an arcing manner to pull your biceps toward your ear.
    1. Strive to finish with your arm fully overhead or as close to fully overhead as your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    2. While you raise your arm through shoulder motion and let your shoulder blade move naturally, keep the rest of your body stationary.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your arm toward the pulley until your elbow is close to shoulder height.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm downward.
    2. At the end of this step, your arm should be outstretched close to parallel to the ground.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While still meaningfully training your lateral and front delts, this variation biases your upper trap and serratus anterior to a greater degree than standard lateral raises.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the Roman chair’s pad to a level that positions its top slightly below the top of your hip bones when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. The appropriate position should allow you to move your lower back freely while supporting your hips.
    2. Positioning the top of the pad higher than the top of your hip bones can restrict your range of motion.
    3. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones, you may feel less stable.
2. Position the weight you intend to use at the base of the Roman chair in between its handles such that you can reach the weight when your feet are on its foot plate.
    1. In order from most to least generally recommended, you can use a dumbbell, kettlebell, or weight plate for this exercise.
    2. If you have difficulty reaching a dumbbell once set up on the Roman chair, a kettlebell may be preferable due to its elevated handle.
    3. With a sufficiently light weight plate or kettlebell, you may be able to hang the weight on one of the handles and pick up the weight from the elevated position rather than the ground.
3. Face the side of the Roman chair with the side of your body you will train during the set closer toward the back base of the Roman chair.
4. While using your hands for balance assistance, step onto the foot plate one leg at a time while facing sideways relative to the thigh pad. Position the heel of one foot in front of your other foot’s toes as you rest the side of your hip and upper thigh against the thigh pad.
    1. Your feet should now be in a tandem stance as they are pointing forward to the side of the Roman chair with your lower legs against the lower leg pad and your knees straight.
    2. Based on what personally feels more comfortable and stable, you can position either foot in the forward position. Some people perform this exercise with the foot of the leg closer to the thigh pad in the front position, while others perform it with the opposite side further forward. Neither setup is inherently superior to the other for everyone, so it is recommended to try both and continue with the setup that feels best to you.
    3. Rather than being angled to the front or back of the Roman chair, your imaginary belt buckle (at the center point between your hip bones) and chest should be facing straight to the side of the Roman chair in the same direction.
5. In the starting position, your torso and neck should near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    1. In a neutral position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your imaginary belt buckle through the center of your chest to your nose when viewed from the side of the Roman chair facing the front of your torso.
    2. Rather than being twisted or bent to one side, your back should be symmetrical, and it should exhibit its natural curvature instead of being rounded or excessively arched.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your torso sideways toward the ground until you can firmly grasp the weight.
    1. To reach the weight, you can fully outstretch your arm toward it with a straight elbow.
2. While holding the weight with your arm pointing toward the ground, bend your torso to the opposite side to lift up and away from the ground.
    1. It can be appropriate to finish once you have returned to the starting position with a neutral back, but you can alternatively continue side bending beyond that point if you prefer.
    2. On the side further away from the ground, your ribs should move closer toward your hip bone, while your lower side’s shoulder should move away from the ground.
    3. While aiming to bend to your side without any other torso motions, you should avoid bending your lower back forward, arching it backward, or twisting it during this exercise.
    4. If laser beams were shooting out from the center of your chest and your imaginary belt buckle, they should continue pointing straight forward, parallel to each other and the ground. If your chest’s imaginary laser beam starts angling upward or downward, you are twisting your torso.
    5. When viewed from the front of the Roman chair, your shoulders should remain aligned, rather than one shoulder moving closer to the right or left side of the Roman chair, which would occur from torso twisting.
    6. Keep your legs and hips stationary throughout the exercise.
3. In a controlled manner, bend your torso sideways toward the ground through the full range of motion comfortable to you.
    1. Your mobility will affect how far you can comfortably side bend, and at your personal end range, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the muscles on the side of your torso (including your lower back) that is further away from the ground.
    2. To avoid letting the weight reach the ground and restrict your range of motion, you can keep your shoulder blade pulled back toward your top side’s shoulder blade, rather than letting the weight pull it outward, which would functionally lengthen your reach. Alternatively, you can maintain a mild degree of elbow bend while otherwise letting your arm hang down.
    3. On the side further away from the ground, your ribs should move away from your hip bone, while your lower side’s shoulder should move closer to the ground.
    4. There is no need to descend with an extremely slow tempo, but you should not let the weight pull you down abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the weight down and repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your obliques and quadratus lumborum, while also training your erector spinae and intertransversarii lumborum.## Setting up
1. Pick up a weight plate in each hand with your middle, ring, and little fingers in their holes, gripping the sides of the plates opposite to your thumbs and index fingers, which should be flat against the outer sides of the plates.
    1. Inserting three fingers into the holes is commonly performed and an appropriate grip with which to start, but some people with thick fingers may feel most comfortable when only inserting their middle and ring fingers.
2. Stand upright in a near hip-width stance, and hold the plates just in front of your legs with slightly bent elbows and your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. While you do not necessarily need to have an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid standing with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arms out and away from your sides in an arcing motion while keeping your elbows slightly bent.
    1. Throughout the exercise, keep your legs straight and torso upright as you avoid bending your elbows any further than the slight bend you exhibited in the starting position.
    2. You should resemble making a snow angel with your arms as they move outward rather than forward.
2. As your arms approach being parallel to the ground, angle your palms forward and continue sweeping your arms up overhead through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. While you raise your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
    2. If you have the mobility to do so comfortably, you can finish the lifting phase once your arms are fully overhead with your biceps by your ears. However, if you start feeling shoulder discomfort in the fully elevated position, you can simply move through the greatest range of motion that is comfortable to you.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering the plates in a smooth arcing motion until they have returned to being just in front of your legs.
    1. As your arms reach close to parallel to the ground, rotate your palms down, which will result in your palms facing in toward each other in the bottom position.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the plates swing down in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While still meaningfully training your lateral and front delts, this variation biases your upper trap and serratus anterior to a greater degree than standard lateral raises.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and sit down on a flat exercise bench on a flat exercise bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your upper thighs near your abdomen, with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Position yourself so that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
2. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
3. Tuck your chin toward your chest to lift your head off the bench. Simultaneously perform a slight crunching motion to lift the uppermost part of your upper back just off the bench without rolling your shoulders forward into a slouched position.
    1. Maintain this position throughout your set.
4. Point your palms backward to assume an underhand grip.
## Exercise technique
1. Straighten your elbows in a controlled manner and let the weights pull your arms down toward the ground through a range of motion comfortable to your shoulders.
    1. If your shoulders feel comfortable doing so, you can finish letting your arms descend once the dumbbells are barely above the ground.
    2. To prevent the dumbbells from contacting the bench, you may have to spread your arms slightly out away from your sides.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your biceps and the front of your shoulders in the bottom position, but you should not experience any shoulder discomfort when moving into the position appropriate for you.
2. Curl the dumbbells upward by fully bending your elbows without moving your upper arms.
    1. While your forearms move through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary as you avoid swinging your elbows upward through shoulder motion.
    2. Once you have fully bent your elbows at the top of the curl, your palms should be facing back toward you, since you should maintain an underhand grip with straight wrists throughout the set.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Continue keeping your upper arms still as all the motion occurs from your forearms hinging about your elbows like the hand of an analog clock rotating about its center.
    2. To prevent your elbows from drifting down, you will have to perform a static front raise effort, since the force from the dumbbells will pull your upper arms down through the shoulder if you do not resist this force.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last full rep, you can repeat step 2 to curl the dumbbells again before turning your palms inward to face each other and lifting your elbows up to be tucked by your sides. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs as you would when following a set of dumbbell bench pressing.
    2. If your mobility and arm length allow you to do so comfortably, you can alternatively set the dumbbells on the ground while lying down on the bench after resting your head and upper back fully on the bench and letting your arms drift downward. In contrast to the first approach described, setting the dumbbells down onto the ground is not recommended for everyone because it requires a greater degree of shoulder mobility than some people have. However, the first technique described requires you to curl the dumbbells up, which may not be feasible if you reach true muscular failure during the set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The size of the weight plates you add to the bar will determine how high the bar lies above the ground, which consequently affects your range of motion. Adding a pair of full-diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates or any bumper plates) will elevate the bar to the standard height (about 9in (23cm)) when deadlifting from the floor. If you only use smaller diameter plates, the exercise will function as a deficit deadlift with a greater range of motion that may exceed the range of motion in which some people wish to train if they lack the mobility to use appropriate technique through the deeper range. If you do not have access to bumper plates and are using loads that are not heavy enough to utilize 45lb (20kg) plates, you can perform a block pull or rack pull with the bar elevated to where it would be when deadlifting from the floor with full diameter plates.
2. With your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly out to the sides, stand in an approximately hip-width stance with the center of the bar just behind you.
    1. While using a near hip-width stance places most people into a viable position, the precise stance width that is ideal for you as an individual may be influenced by your body structure and mobility, so you can feel free to modify your stance a bit based on where you feel strong and comfortable.
    2. You can similarly decide whether to point your toes straight ahead or slightly out to the side based on what feels best to you.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar and your shoulders packed down (opposite to if you shrugged your shoulders up), sit your hips back and down while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar.
    1. Based on your preference, you can let your back modestly round or aim to keep it flat with your chest up. If you adopt a mild degree of back rounding, strive to have it distributed throughout the entirety of your lower, middle, and upper back rather than only rounding your lower back. Setting up with a somewhat rounded back can help you keep your hips not as far behind the bar, which reduces the degree to which your hamstrings can get in the way of the bar when lifting it up. However, you can set up with a flat back if that feels more comfortable to you.
    2. Compared to a conventional deadlift, you can bend your knees to a greater degree and let them track further forward while not sitting your hips as far back during this variation.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
    4. As a cue to pack your shoulders down, you can think about making yourself have long arms, which can place you into an efficient start position and help engage your back muscles.
4. With your elbows fully straightened, firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can naturally use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish to do so.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hand while holding it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position through a maneuver known as pulling the slack out of the bar.
    2. When creating “pre-tension,” pressing through the ground should pull yourself further into position with your back locked into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees without losing back tightness.
    1. Regardless of whether you set up with a modestly rounded back or a flat back, you should keep your back locked in a stable position when starting the rep, rather than letting it dynamically round.
    2. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the lift rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. Once the bar reaches near the middle of your knees, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your knees and hips until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. It is normal for the bar to drag up your hamstrings during this step when appropriately keeping the bar close to your body.
    2. Thrusting your hips forward is key to minimizing how much your hamstrings may get in the way of the bar (which can be more notable for lifters with thick thighs).
    3. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    4. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
5. Reverse the movement by bending your knees and hips to lower the bar until the plates have returned to resting on the ground.
    1. Like when setting up, you could keep your back flat or let it round modestly, but you should avoid an excessive degree of lower back rounding regardless of which approach you prefer to use.
    2. Letting your knees move further forward than you would with a conventional deadlift can help keep your hamstrings from sitting onto the bar. By repeatedly performing this exercise, you can better determine specifically how much forward knee travel feels best with your body proportions.
6. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop on the ground for a moment (rather than simply tapping the ground and immediately deadlifting the bar up again) before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause with the bar on the ground for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the ground rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
## Description
While still training your erector spinae and gluteus maximus, this exercise can train your quads more effectively than most other deadlift variations.## Setting up
1. Horizontally adjust the machine’s footplate to a setting that will allow you to position part of your buttocks just off the front edge of the pad when you set up with your feet on the footplate.
    1. The appropriate position should allow your thighs to rest comfortably on the pad.
    2. If the footplate is positioned excessively far from the pad, you will have to sit closer toward the middle of the pad rather than just off the front edge.
    3. If the footplate is positioned excessively close to the pad, the back of your upper thighs will not be in contact with the pad.
    4. Since a sweet spot exists, some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust the height of the footplate, select a height that allows you to feel comfortable and secure with slightly bent knees once you are in the starting position.
    1. As a starting point, you can select a height that positions the lower pair of leg rollers level with the pad and the upper pair of leg rollers higher than the pad.
    2. Not all machines permit you to adjust the footplate height.
3. Position the weight you intend to use in a spot that you can comfortably reach after you set your feet on the footplate.
    1. When using a weight plate or kettlebell, you can hang the weight on one of the machine’s handles.
    2. For the easiest setup, you can have a partner hand you the weight after you set your feet on the footplate during the next step. Besides a weight plate or kettlebell, a partner can hand you a medicine ball or dumbbell, which cannot be hung on the handles. This partner approach is ideal if you are using a heavy weight, since it can otherwise be awkward to get into the starting position with the weight independently.
    3. Some lifters will alternatively set the weight on the ground directly in front of the machine between its handles and fully lean backward to pick up the weight after setting their feet on the footplate. Not everyone has the mobility to set up in this manner, and it is most practical when using a large medicine ball, which makes it less difficult to pick up from the ground.
4. Climb up onto the machine, and hook your lower legs between the leg rollers with the top of your feet snugly pressed into the top roller as your soles are facing the footplate.
    1. Your feet should be securely anchored so that they will not slide during the exercise. The top rollers should be just above the lowest part of your shins by your ankles, while the bottom rollers should be just under your Achilles tendons (which attach to the top of your heels).
    2. Depending on the machine you are using, you can step up onto a middle beam or a step to help you get into position.
    3. While you are setting your feet, you can hold the base of the leg rollers for stability.
5. Pick up the weight, hold it with both hands close to your chest, and sit upright with your buttocks just hanging off the front edge of the pad.
    1. The precise way in which you will pick up and hold the weight will naturally vary based on the specific implement you selected and where you placed it initially. What matters is that you have a firm grip on the weight and are able to keep it in a stable position throughout your set.
    2. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, looking straight ahead. Throughout your set, keep your neck in this position and the weight close to your chest.
## Exercise technique
1. Lean your torso back in a controlled manner until your torso is approximately parallel to the ground or slightly beyond this point.
    1. Allow your lower back to arch to a degree that feels comfortable to you.
    2. Specifically how much of an arch is comfortable will vary from person to person, but it is often appropriate to finish the lowering phase with your lower back arched to a similar degree as when you are normally standing upright. It is not inherently injurious to arch beyond this point, but meaningfully arching the lower back beyond this point can provoke lower back discomfort in some people.
    3. If you feel lower back discomfort at the bottom, you can strive to keep your lower back closer to a flat position with less of an arch, or you can reduce the range in which you lean back. Thinking about keeping your ribs tucked down to a greater degree as you avoid flaring them can help limit excessive arching.
2. Reverse the movement by curling your back to lift your torso up until your shoulders are vertically above your hips.
    1. To train your abs dynamically, your back should progressively round rather than remain in a flat position (which could occur if you predominantly lift your torso up through your hip flexor muscles).
    2. As a cue to emphasize your abs, think about driving the bottom of your ribs down toward the tops of your hip bones while tightening your abs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, set the weight down in the opposite way in which you initially picked it up before dismounting the machine.
    2. Like when setting up, you can grasp the base of the rollers for stability when getting off the machine.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis while also training your obliques and hip flexors.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to near wrist height when standing with your arms by your sides, connect a D-handle attachment to the cable, and select your desired weight with the loading pin.
2. Stand with the side of your shoulder (that you will not train during this set) facing the cable, and take a small step forward, so that the cable is slightly behind you by your side.
3. With your arm on the side opposite the cable, reach behind your body toward the pulley to grasp the handle with your elbow straightened or slightly bent (based on what feels more comfortable).
    1. While predominantly pointing downward, your arm should be angled backward just enough to prevent your buttocks from getting in the way of your forearm. If your arm is directed backward beyond this point, take a small step backward to position the rest of your body closer to the handle.
    2. In this position, your hand should be just behind your buttocks with your palm facing the pulley.
4. With your closer free hand, grasp part of the cable station for stability, and step out to the side away from the pulley until you have mostly straightened that side’s elbow.
    1. Throughout the exercise, continue using this arm to help stabilize yourself.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the arm holding the handle.
    3. Once you have stepped out away from the pulley, you should feel tension from the cable and will likely feel a stretching sensation on the side of your shoulder.
## Exercise technique
1. Without bending your elbow, sweep your arm out and away from the pulley until your arm is close to parallel with the ground.
    1. While predominantly sweeping outward, move your arm slightly forward so that your elbow finishes in alignment with your shoulders when viewed from your side, rather than remaining behind your shoulders.
    2. At the top of the raise, your palm should be facing down, and your arm should form half of a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from the front or back.
    3. While you raise your arm through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blade to move naturally, the rest of your body should remain stationary.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your arm in toward the pulley and behind your back in a controlled arcing manner through the full range of motion that is comfortable to your shoulder.
    1. Specifically where you finish the movement can be influenced by your personal shoulder mobility.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the cable abruptly yank your arm toward the pulley.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Select your weight with the machine’s loading pin(s), and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Some machines will have a single weight stack that provides resistance to each cable, while others will have one weight stack for each cable.
    2. If you notice that your thighs do not snugly rest under the thigh pad when sitting down on the seat, you can readjust the pad before resuming your setup.
2. While facing the center of the machine and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
3. While holding the handles, sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten and your arms to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
4. Slightly lean your torso backward at your hips, and maintain this slight backward lean throughout your set.
    1. From this position, you should feel tension from the cables holding your arms overhead with straight elbows.
    2. If you notice that the cables remain slack when your elbows are fully straightened (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), you may have to lean a bit further back for the cables to provide resistance in the starting position. If that does not take the slack out of the cables, the machine may not be designed to provide resistance through the full range of motion for people with your stature, so you may have to perform a different pulldown variation on another machine if you wish to train through a full range of motion.
5. Let your palms face a direction that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    1. The dual handles allow you to use whichever grip you prefer, so you have the ability to point your palms forward for an overhand grip, inward for a neutral grip, backward for an underhand grip, or at an angle between two of those grips for a hybrid grip.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears. Simultaneously drive your elbows down toward your sides to pull the handles down toward the sides of your upper chest.
    1. Let your palms freely rotate in a manner that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    2. Bend your elbows to the extent that your forearms remain aligned with the cables, allowing you to draw straight lines from your elbows through your wrists to the cables.
    3. Maintain the slight backward lean of your torso you adopted while setting up, rather than leaning further backward.
    4. When pulling through your full range of motion, you should finish with your elbows close to the sides of your torso.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the handles in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Allow your palms to rotate if that feels natural to you.
    3. Finish with your arms overhead and your elbows fully straightened.
    4. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat and teres major while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin(s), and adjust the seat to a height that lets you rest your lower chest comfortably against the chest pad once you are seated.
    1. The proper seat height should let the exercise feel smooth and natural while ideally letting you experience resistance from the machine in the starting position with your elbows fully straightened.
    2. An excessively high seat can prevent the machine from providing tension in the starting position and restrict your range of motion.
    3. When using this machine for the first time, trying out different seat heights may be helpful to determine specifically which setting helps this exercise feel best to you based on your body proportions.
2. If your machine has a thigh pad, adjust it to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting down.
3. While facing the chest pad and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with a grip that feels comfortable to you based on the design of your machine.
4. Sit down on the seat with your torso against the chest pad. Let your elbows straighten and your arms raise in line with the machine’s resistance.
    1. Set your feet on the foot bar or the floor, based on your preference and machine design.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position with straight elbows, you may need to lower the seat height to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion.
## Exercise technique
1. While pulling your shoulder blades down and back, drive your elbows down and back to pull the handles toward you.
    1. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows finishing slightly behind your torso.
    2. Strive to keep your forearms aligned with the angle in which you are pulling, which should naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow bend.
    3. Keep your torso upright against the chest pad throughout your set rather than leaning backward during the row.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil between them.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to move forward and upward while straightening in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move fully forward as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Select your weight with the loading pin, and sit down on the machine with your feet in contact with its foot plates.
    1. Allow your knees to bend to an angle that allows you to feel stable and comfortable, which can be a mild to moderately mild knee bend for many people.
    2. With greater knee bend, you will be able to sit closer toward the handles, so you can bend your knees further if you initially cannot reach them during the next step.
2. Lean forward at your hips until you are able to grasp the handles firmly with straight elbows.
    1. How you angle your palms (e.g., in toward each other or downward) when setting up is up to your preference and what feels comfortable to you with the machine you are using.
3. Lean your torso backward at your hips until you are sitting with an upright torso as your arms are aligned with the cables and your palms are angled in toward each other.
    1. When seated upright with straight elbows, you should feel tension from the cables, so if the cables are still slack, you can scoot yourself backward by extending your knees until you feel the resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward you by driving your elbows backward while pulling your shoulder blades together into a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Let your elbows move through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, whether that involves keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides or moderately flaring them out. Simulatnesouly let your palms rotate if that feels natural to you.
    2. Keep your forearms aligned with the machine’s arms connected to the handles, which naturally promotes the appropriate degree of elbow bend.
    3. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position throughout the set as you keep your torso upright.
    4. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows finishing slightly behind your torso.
    5. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten to move the handles forward.
    1. Finish with your arms fully outstretched in front of you.
    2. Like when rowing the handles toward you, let your palms rotate if doing so feels natural and comfortable to you.
    3. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, without rounding your lower back. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you to train them more effectively.
    4. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. If your machine has an adjustable seat, set it to the most reclined position that allows your back to be well-supported by the backrest when seated with your feet on the foot platform.
    1. Starting with the most reclined position (which tilts the seat down in a closer-to-horizontal position) generally works well for most people.
    2. Compared to using a closer to upright seat angle, using an appropriately reclined position will allow you to leg press through a longer range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    3. The most reclined setting on certain machine models may be excessively reclined to the extent that the backrest may no longer be able to stabilize your body when leg pressing. If this occurs, your buttocks may lift off the seat, and your torso may slide back along the backrest due to the backrest being angled too close to a horizontal position. However, most leg press machines will not have an excessively reclined seat setting available.
    4. To determine if the seat angle you initially selected is appropriate for you, you can perform a couple of reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section without adding any weight plates to the leg press. If you feel stable and well-supported by the backrest, the seat is not excessively reclined, and you have selected a viable seat angle.
    5. Note that not all machines have an adjustable seat.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot platform, near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to leg press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the platform works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select, your heels should be firmly on the platform along with the rest of your feet. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward and/or limits your range of motion.
    5. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to let you unlock the leg press and begin your set. If you have short legs, you potentially may have to situate your feet lower on the platform to reach the platform with slightly bent knees. If you are using a machine that does not permit a lower starting height of the sled, that particular machine may not be feasible for your body type.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue doing so throughout your set, which will help create full-body tension to amplify your stability and performance.
2. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handle with your hand to disengage the safety stopper.
    1. The specific location of the safety handle and how to unlock it can vary among different machine models. However, the safety handle is often near where one of your hands will be when resting by your side.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handle) and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Lower the foot platform toward you in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knees and hips.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform.
    2. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue bending your knees and hips through the full range of motion you can while keeping your buttocks firmly on the seat, lower back flat against the backrest, and heels fully on the platform.
    1. People can reach the point where their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. If you reached the end range to which your hips can bend, continuing any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your buttocks starting to curl up off the seat.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track over your toes, continuing any further would cause your heels to start lifting off the platform.
7. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, strive to push evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, back against the backrest, and knees aligned over your feet.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set.
9. After your knees are straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, lock the safety handle with your hand to engage the safety stopper.
10. Bend your knees to return the weight fully onto the safety stopper.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your quads, gluteus maximus, and multiple hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. Select your desired load with the machine’s loading pin, and set the knee pad to the side you will not train during this set if your machine has an adjustable knee pad.
    1. Some machines have a pair of fixed knee pads rather than a single adjustable knee pad.
    2. Your kneeling leg will be the non-working leg during this exercise.
2. With what will be your non-working leg, kneel on the knee pad with your thigh snugly against the thigh pad.
    1. Since you will keep this non-working leg in a kneeling position throughout your set, all the subsequent directions will apply to your working leg.
3. Position your working leg’s thigh snugly against the other thigh pad with the back of your lower leg contacting the front of the roller pad.
4. Lean forward at your hips until you can firmly grasp the handles in a comfortable position with your forearms resting on the forearm pads.
    1. In this starting position, your knee should be aligned with the machine’s pivot point (i.e., the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates), which should naturally occur when your thigh contacts the thigh pad. Keeping your knee aligned with this pivot point will help the exercise feel smooth and natural.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched or rounded, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position with their natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright. The forward torso lean should come from your hips rather than your back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your toes and foot back up toward your shin by moving at your ankle. Maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankle in the same position in which it would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heel up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankle in this position can increase your leg curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
2. Without moving your thigh, drive your heel toward your buttock by bending your knee through the full range of motion you can perform.
    1. The degree to which you can bend your knee can be influenced by which specific machine you use, but you have reached your end range once you can no longer pull your heel closer toward your buttock.
    2. If you experience a notable cramping sensation in your hamstrings at the end range, you can optionally stop curling the weight up just before the point where your hamstrings may cramp.
    3. Throughout your set, continue holding the handles while keeping your forearms resting on the forearm pads.
    4. While your lower leg moves through knee motion, keep the rest of your body stationary.
    5. Particularly avoid lifting your thigh off the pad or excessively arching your lower back.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your heel in a controlled manner to straighten your knee and return to the starting position.
    1. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower leg down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and set the knee pad to the side you will not train during this set if your machine has an adjustable knee pad.
    1. Some machines have a pair of fixed knee pads rather than a single adjustable knee pad.
    2. Your kneeling leg will be the non-working leg during this exercise.
2. With what will be your non-working leg, kneel on the knee pad with your thigh snugly against the thigh pad.
    1. Since you will keep this non-working leg in a kneeling position throughout your set, all the subsequent directions will apply to your working leg.
3. Position your working leg’s thigh snugly against the other thigh pad with the back of your lower leg contacting the front of the roller pad.
4. Lean forward at your hips until you can firmly grasp the handles in a comfortable position with your forearms resting on the forearm pads.
    1. In this starting position, your knee should be aligned with the machine’s pivot point (i.e., the center about which the machine’s lever arm rotates), which should naturally occur when your thigh contacts the thigh pad. Keeping your knee aligned with this pivot point will help the exercise feel smooth and natural.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched or rounded, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position with their natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright. The forward torso lean should come from your hips rather than your back.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your toes and foot back up toward your shin by moving at your ankle. Maintain this ankle position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract while doing so.
    2. This places your ankle in the same position in which it would be at the bottom of a calf raise on a step. It is the opposite of the ankle motion you would perform when lifting your heel up off the ground during a calf raise.
    3. Holding your ankle in this position can increase your leg curl strength (as explained in detail under the “notes” section).
2. Without moving your thigh, drive your heel toward your buttock by bending your knee through the full range of motion you can perform.
    1. The degree to which you can bend your knee can be influenced by which specific machine you use, but you have reached your end range once you can no longer pull your heel closer toward your buttock.
    2. If you experience a notable cramping sensation in your hamstrings at the end range, you can optionally stop curling the weight up just before the point where your hamstrings may cramp.
    3. Throughout your set, continue holding the handles while keeping your forearms resting on the forearm pads.
    4. While your lower leg moves through knee motion, keep the rest of your body stationary.
    5. Particularly avoid lifting your thigh off the pad or excessively arching your lower back.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your heel in a controlled manner to straighten your knee and return to the starting position.
    1. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo for this movement, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time, rather than letting the weight abruptly swing your lower leg down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train all your hamstrings, which are assisted by your gracilis, sartorius, and gastrocnemius.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell, hold it by your side with your palm facing inward, and stand facing a sturdy object reasonably near knee-height, such as a flat exercise bench.
    1. If you are tall, the head of an incline bench set to a low incline may be better for you to brace your hand against during this exercise, compared to a lower flat bench.
    2. Viable alternatives to a bench can include a plyometric box, sturdy chair, dumbbell rack, racked Smith machine bar, or a barbell on a rack’s J-cups.
    3. The appropriate object should let you achieve a position that feels stable and comfortable with a flat back once you brace against it.
    4. While using an object reasonably near knee height can let you set up effectively and is most common, some lifters may prefer to use a higher object closer to hip height, which can let you keep the non-working arm higher during the exercise. Using a taller object may also be sensible for people with mobility limitations who cannot comfortably support themselves on a shorter object without rounding their backs.
    5. For brevity, the instructions will refer to the use of a bench from this point on.
2. While standing with the head of the bench in front of your free arm, situate your feet near shoulder-width apart.
    1. When aligning your free arm with one end of the bench, you should have space in front of your arm holding the dumbbell, so you will not have to worry about the bench getting in the way of the dumbbell after you have assumed the starting position for this row.
    2. A near shoulder-width stance is an appropriate starting point, but feel free to widen your stance a bit if that feels more stable to you.
3. Based on your preference, you can adopt a staggered stance by taking a step backward with the foot on the same side as the dumbbell, or you can keep your feet symmetrical.
    1. Either stance can be effectively used for this exercise, so you can try out a set or two with each option and continue with the position where you feel most stable.
4. Lean your torso forward by sitting your hips back while allowing your knees to bend mildly until you can brace your free hand against the bench with a straight elbow.
    1. Keep your lower back flat, and let your arm (that is holding the dumbbell) hang down toward the ground with your arm angled in toward your opposite side (resulting in neutral grip).
    2. When standing appropriately close to the bench, your non-working side’s arm should ideally be angled downward to a greater degree than forward, so that you are not reaching excessively far forward.
    3. Keep your hand braced against the bench throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Drive your elbow backward and upward in an arcing motion to pull the dumbbell toward your hip. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade backward, and keep your elbow tucked by your side rather than flaring it outward.
    1. When bending your elbow by the appropriate degree, your forearm should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    2. Row through your full range of motion, which should result in your elbow finishing slightly behind your torso or at least in alignment with your torso at the top of the rep.
    3. Strive to keep your lower body and torso in a fixed position with your chest facing a similar direction throughout this movement. A mild degree of torso rotation is not problematic, but avoid letting it become excessive and turning the exercise into a cheat row.
    4. While performing this arcing motion, the dumbbell should not move straight upward in a vertical path.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled arcing motion until your arm is directed down toward the ground with your elbow straightened.
    1. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blade forward, which will stretch your upper back muscles and result in your working side’s shoulder being mildly closer to the ground than your other side’s shoulder at the bottom of the row.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight rapidly pull your arm down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Place a kettlebell on the ground next to the side of a flat bench (or whichever stable object on which you will rest your upper back) with the handle parallel to the length of the bench.
    1. As an alternative to a flat bench or a specialized hip thrust bench, you can use a soft plyometric box or couch reasonably close to knee height.
2. Sit down on the ground with the kettlebell directly in front of your hips as your legs straddle the weight, and your upper back contacts the edge of the bench.
    1. Specifically placing the lower part of your shoulder blades against the edge of the bench often is ideal, but feel free to adjust your upper back positioning mildly from this point based on what feels most stable to you.
3. Use both hands to grasp the horns (side handles) of the kettlebell and lift it up onto the midline of your pelvis with the handle facing up.
    1. The base of the kettlebell should be resting on your pubic bones, which lie directly below your bladder and just above your genitals. If you situate the weight above or below your pubic bones, applying pressure to those areas can naturally be uncomfortable.
    2. Continue holding the horns of the kettlebell with your palms facing in toward each other throughout the exercise.
4. While keeping the kettlebell resting on your pelvis, place the soles of your feet flat on the floor, hip-width apart, with your toes pointing forward and your knees bent by a little greater than 90 degrees.
    1. The optimal degree of knee bend should allow your feet to feel stable while being firmly planted on the ground throughout the set. It is normally appropriate to position your foot far enough back that your knee will be bent by approximately 90 degrees at the top of your hip thrust.
5. Tuck your chin mildly toward your chest, and maintain this neck position throughout the exercise.
6. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift your other foot off the ground by straightening your knee.
    1. Keep one foot off the ground throughout the set you perform with your other side.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to help stabilize your lower back.
    2. From this starting position, your back should either be flat or exhibit its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
2. Lift your hips up by driving your heel into the ground and squeezing your glute while continuing to hold the kettlebell on your pubic bones.
    1. At the top, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glute.
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glute engage.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your hip mobility comfortably permits without rounding or excessively arching your lower back.
    1. Some people can descend all the way down until they lightly contact the ground with their glutes, but you can prioritize lowering through the range in which you can achieve through hip motion rather than lower back motion.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. When you are ready to set the weight down, rest both feet and your buttocks on the ground, and place the kettlebell onto the ground in front of your hips.
## Description
This exercise will predominantly develop your gluteus maximus while also training your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Place a kettlebell on the ground next to the side of a flat bench (or whichever stable object on which you will rest your upper back) with the handle parallel to the length of the bench.
    1. As an alternative to a flat bench or a specialized hip thrust bench, you can use a soft plyometric box or couch reasonably close to knee height.
2. Sit down on the ground with the kettlebell directly in front of your hips as your legs straddle the weight, and your upper back contacts the edge of the bench.
    1. Specifically placing the lower part of your shoulder blades against the edge of the bench often is ideal, but feel free to adjust your upper back positioning mildly from this point based on what feels most stable to you.
3. Use both hands to grasp the horns (side handles) of the kettlebell and lift it up onto the midline of your pelvis with the handle facing up.
    1. The base of the kettlebell should be resting on your pubic bones, which lie directly below your bladder and just above your genitals. If you situate the weight above or below your pubic bones, applying pressure to those areas can naturally be uncomfortable.
    2. Continue holding the horns of the kettlebell with your palms facing in toward each other throughout the exercise.
4. While keeping the kettlebell resting on your pelvis, place the soles of your feet flat on the floor near shoulder-width apart with your knees bent by greater than 90 degrees.
    1. Adopting a shoulder-width stance can be fully appropriate, but feel free to use a somewhat closer or wider stance if that feels more comfortable to you.
    2. Based on what feels natural to you, you can opt to point your toes forward or somewhat out to the sides.
    3. The optimal degree of knee bend should allow your feet to feel stable while being firmly planted on the ground throughout the set. It is normally appropriate to position your feet far enough back that your knees will be bent by approximately 90 degrees at the top of your hip thrust. From this placement, you can adjust specifically where you situate your feet based on your preference.
5. Tuck your chin mildly toward your chest, and maintain this neck position throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set to help stabilize your lower back.
    2. From this starting position, your back should either be flat or exhibit its natural curvature, like when you are normally standing upright.
2. Lift your hips up by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes while continuing to hold the kettlebell on your pubic bones.
    1. At the top, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glutes engage.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your hip mobility comfortably permits without rounding or excessively arching your lower back.
    1. Some people can descend all the way down until they lightly contact the ground with their glutes, but you can prioritize lowering through the range in which you can achieve through hip motion rather than lower back motion.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, fully lower your buttocks to the ground and set the kettlebell onto the ground in front of your hips.
