    2. When standing appropriately close to the bench, your non-working side’s arm should ideally be angled downward to a greater degree than forward, so that you are not reaching excessively far forward.
    3. Keep your hand braced against the bench throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Drive your elbow backward and upward in an arcing motion to pull the kettlebell toward your hip. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade backward, and keep your elbow tucked by your side rather than flaring it outward.
    1. When bending your elbow by the appropriate degree, your forearm should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    2. Row through your full range of motion, which should result in your elbow finishing slightly behind your torso or at least in alignment with your torso at the top of the rep.
    3. Strive to keep your lower body and torso in a fixed position with your chest facing a similar direction throughout this movement. A mild degree of torso rotation is not problematic, but avoid letting it become excessive and turning the exercise into a cheat row.
    4. While performing this arcing motion, the kettlebell should not move straight upward in a vertical path.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the kettlebell in a controlled arcing motion until your arm is directed down toward the ground with your elbow straightened.
    1. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blade forward, which will stretch your upper back muscles and result in your working side’s shoulder being mildly closer to the ground than your other side’s shoulder at the bottom of the row.
    2. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight rapidly pull your arm down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to near mid-chest height.
    1. The optimal starting height should let you position the bar on your upper back and unrack it without lifting your heels off the ground.
2. Position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are slightly below the lowest height at which the bar descends at the bottom of your squat.
    1. To find this point for your body, you can perform a couple of squat reps with the unloaded bar as described in the “exercise technique” section, but you can initially put the safety stops somewhat below the top of your hip bones when standing upright as an approximation.
3. Add your desired weight to the bar, and face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, position yourself to face the side where the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (so the bottom of the rails is farther in front of you than the top).
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
4. Grasp each side of the bar with a somewhat wider than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
    2. If you are not accustomed to exercises that have a bar on your back, you may have to try out different hand placements to find one that personally suits you well.
5. While holding onto the bar, shallowly bend at your hips and knees to then walk under the bar and situate the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have the bar placed too low on your back.
    5. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
    6. If you are not accustomed to having a bar on your back, you may not feel particularly comfortable doing so, especially when it is loaded with additional weight, but whichever position you use should not be painful.
6. Position the back of your heels slightly in front of the bar with your feet near shoulder width apart from each other as your toes are pointing somewhat out to the sides (rather than pointing straight ahead).
    1. In contrast to when you barbell back squat, placing your feet a bit in front of the bar when Smith machine back squatting can assist with comfortably achieving a deep squat.
    2. Your feet should not be so far in front of the bar that you feel that they may slide out in front of you when squatting.
    3. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    4. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    5. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull down on the bar to tighten your upper back muscles, and unrack it by starting to stand up as you straighten your knees.
2. After the bar rests fully on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars with a slight wrist flick before standing fully upright with the bar.
3. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright. Keep your neck in this position throughout the exercise.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward while leaving your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to help you maintain a rigid back position.
5. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. As you descend, it is natural for your torso to lean forward mildly at your hips, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down from upper back rounding.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet, especially the deeper your squat.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
6. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion that your mobility enables without rounding your back, lifting your heels up off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, heels lift off the ground, or knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
7. Once you reach the bottom of your squat, pause in that position, remaining completely stationary for a brief period (e.g., about two seconds) while staying tight.
    1. Avoid rushing your pause to the best of your ability.
8. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips until you have returned to a fully upright position.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees stacked over your feet as you squat up.
9. Alternate between steps 5-8 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
10. After finishing the set, rerack the bar by bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to near mid-chest height.
    1. The optimal starting height should let you position the bar on the front of your shoulders and unrack it without lifting your heels off the ground.
2. Position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are slightly below the lowest height at which the bar descends at the bottom of your front squat.
    1. To find this point for your body, you can perform a couple of front squat reps with the unloaded bar as described in the “exercise technique” section, but you can initially put the safety stops somewhat below the top of your hip bones when standing upright as an approximation.
3. Add your desired weight to the bar, and face the center of the bar with approximately a shoulder-width stance.
    1. If your Smith machine has angled sliding rails, position yourself to face the side where the bar will track slightly forward as it descends (so the bottoms of the rails are farther in front of you than the top).
    2. If your Smith machine has purely vertical sliding rails, you can perform the same movement regardless of which direction you face.
4. Based on your preference, set up with a full hand clean grip, fingertip clean grip, cross-arm grip.
    1. With either type of clean grip, initially grasp the bar with your palms facing down and your hands slightly outside shoulderwidth. Keep as many fingers wrapped around the bar and the bar as close to the bases of your fingers as is comfortable to you during the subsequent steps when using a clean grip. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your wrists with a clean grip, but if it is painful, you can alternatively set up with the cross-arm grip.
    2. With a cross-arm grip, you will cross your forearms into an “X-shape” as your hands are on top of the bar with your palms facing down during the following steps. Based on what feels more stable to you, you can position your hands very close together, such that your thumbs are touching each other, or you can have your hands slightly wider apart, such that you have fingers contacting your front delts.
    3. Additional details and demo videos specific to setting up with each grip are included in the full hand clean grip, fingertip clean grip, and cross-arm grip front squat pages, which display the barbell variations but with the same grip details that apply when using a Smith machine.
5. While simultaneously bending your elbows, shallowly bend at your knees and hips with an upright torso to walk under the bar and situate it on the front of your shoulders just in front of the base of your neck.
    1. The bar should be resting on your front delts (which are the muscles on the front of your shoulders that attach to the outer aspects of your collarbones) and the outer portions of your collarbones, which together form a shelf with the base of your neck to cradle the bar.
    2. While the bar should be directly in front of your neck, it should not be pressing into your neck to the extent that it feels like it is choking you.
    3. If you are not accustomed to front squats, it is normal to experience discomfort from the pressure the bar places on the front of your shoulders (which may cause some skin bruising), but it should not be painful with proper bar placement.
6. With the bar now on the front of your shoulders, lift your elbows up until your upper arms are parallel to the ground.
    1. You can perform steps 5 and 6 simultaneously with each other.
    2. The bony prominence on the back of each elbow should now be pointing straight forward.
    3. The bar should first and foremost be resting on the front of your shoulders without necessarily needing your hands to hold it there.
7. Position your heels approximately under the bar with your feet near shoulder-width apart, and your toes pointing mildly out to the sides.
    1. Compared to if you position the middle of your feet directly under the bar, setting up with the middle of your feet a bit in front of the bar can often assist with comfortably achieving a deeper squat when using a Smith machine. Individuals with mobility limitations and/or body proportions generally not well suited for deep squats may benefit from setting their heels slightly in front of the bar, while lifters with mobility and body proportions well suited for deep squats may feel comfortable setting up the bar directly over midfoot.
    2. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting through your full range of motion, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    4. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arms parallel to the ground, stand upright by straightening your knees and hips to unrack the bar.
2. With the bar fully resting on your shoulders rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars.
    1. Your neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright. Keep your neck in this position throughout the exercise.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward while leaving your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to help you maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by simultaneously breaking at your knees and hips. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest and elbows up.
    1. Throughout the set, avoid dropping your elbows or letting your chest cave down.
    2. As you descend, it is natural for your torso to lean forward mildly at your hips to maintain your balance, but maintain a mostly upright torso position with your lower back flat.
    3. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    5. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet, especially the deeper your squat.
5. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion that your mobility enables without rounding your lower back, lifting your heels up off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your lower back starts rounding, heels lift off the ground, or knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
6. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees stacked over your feet as you squat up.
7. Alternate between steps 4-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. After finishing the set, rerack the bar by bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner as you rotate the safety hooks onto the safety bars and return the bar to resting on the safety bars.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
You have the option of performing this exercise while standing on a step and keeping both knees straight or standing on the ground and mildly bending one knee to keep that side’s foot off the ground. You can use essentially the same technique to perform the exercise with either approach, but their setups differ. Correspondingly, each approach receives its own setup section, but they share the same exercise technique instructions.

### **Setting up on a step**

1. Position what you will use as you step (e.g., aerobic step, thick bumper plate, short plyometric box) or approach the bottom step of a staircase.
    1. If you are using a mobile step, you can position it next to something where you can place a hand for balance assistance while standing on the step if you wish to receive balance assistance. This may involve setting up next to a rack, wall, chair, or exercise bench positioned upright.
    2. A step height of a few inches (about 8-10cm) is sufficiently tall for most people performing this exercise, since it involves a low amplitude of motion. This means that an aerobic step without risers or a 45lb (20kg) bumper plate is normally sufficiently tall, but a taller step naturally can be used as well.
2. Stand directly to the side of the step, and place one foot fully onto the step while leaving your other foot on the ground. Situate your feet about hip-width apart, and point your toes straight ahead.
    1. Step up with the side of your body you wish to train during this set.
3. Optionally place one hand against a nearby surface for balance assistance. Otherwise, keep your hands in your preferred position throughout the exercise, such as resting on your hips or by your sides.
4. Drive through your elevated leg and straighten your knee to step up until your feet are level, keeping your one foot hanging off the step.
5. On the side hovering off the step, pull your toes and the ball of your foot up toward your shin through an ankle motion that brings your heel closer to the ground than the ball of your foot.
    1. This is the opposite of the ankle motion that occurs when lifting your heels up during a calf raise, and you should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract.
    2. The purpose of keeping your foot in this position is to make sure that your toes do not contact the ground during the exercise and restrict your range of motion. If your step is tall enough that your free foot would not touch the ground in any position during the exercise, you can ignore this step and let the ball of your foot hang down.

### **Setting up on the ground**

1. In a hip-width stance, stand next to something where you can place a hand for balance assistance if you wish to receive balance assistance.
2. Optionally place one hand against a nearby surface for balance assistance. Otherwise, keep your hands in your preferred position throughout the exercise, such as resting on your hips or by your sides.
3. Bend one knee enough to lift that side’s foot off the ground, and keep that knee bent with your foot off the ground throughout the exercise.
    1. You will be training your hip muscles on the side of your body on which you keep your foot on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your torso upright and your stance leg straight, lower your free foot toward the ground by letting your hip bone (on the side of your free foot) tilt down in a controlled manner.
    1. Descend until you feel that your hip bones are fully tilted, which may prompt you to feel a mild stretching sensation in the side of your hip (of the hip bone whose leg remains on the step).
    2. As a cue, you can imagine that you have a basin of water on top of your hip bones, and you are trying to pour as much of the water as you can out to one side.
    3. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should be using your hip muscles to control the descent actively, rather than just letting your hip bone abruptly drop.
2. Reverse the movement by hiking your hip up to lift your free foot up and tilt your hip bones in the opposite direction.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and your legs straight as you exclusively move at your hip.
    2. Strive to finish with your free leg’s hip bone higher up than your stance leg’s hip bone.
    3. When imagining that you have a basin of water on top of your hip bones, you can think about leveling the basin before trying to spill the water toward the side of your stance leg.
    4. With the appropriate technique, you should feel tension in the muscles on the side of your stance leg’s hip as they contract.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat this exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the musculature on the side of your hip, including your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia latae.## Setting up
1. Optionally set up a pair of aerobic steps, thick bumper plates, or deadlift blocks near hip-width apart if you wish to extend this exercise’s range of motion beyond what you could achieve when standing on the ground.
    1. When standing on the ground, individuals who are sufficiently flexible may find that the kettlebell will contact the ground at the bottom of a rep before they reach their full range of motion. Standing on top of elevated objects (as you would during a deficit kettlebell deadlift) can alternatively permit a greater range of motion.
    2. Initially performing this exercise while standing on the ground will let you determine whether an elevated surface is required for you to train through your full range of motion.
    3. If your range of motion is not restricted when standing on the ground during this exercise, there is no purpose in standing on these objects to elevate your feet.
2. Pick up one kettlebell and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width stance while holding the kettlebell just in front of your thighs with your elbows straight and your palms back toward you.
    1. At this point, you will naturally be standing on the elevated objects if you wish to use them, or you can stand on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move without rounding your back.
    1. Throughout the set, keep the soles of your feet fully planted and your arms in a vertical position, perpendicular to the ground.
    2. This step is essentially the same as the lowering phase of a Romanian deadlift without keeping the weight as close to your legs (since the arms angle slightly backward during a Romanian deadlift, rather than hanging straight down during this exercise).
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal. How close to horizontal you can incline your torso will be affected by your hamstring flexibility.
    5. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range.
2. In a controlled curling manner, allow your whole back to round slowly through your comfortable range of motion without moving your lower body.
    1. The degree to which you can round your back will be affected by your spinal mobility.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your back muscles in the bottom position, but you should not feel any discomfort at the end range. Only round your back as far as is comfortable.
    3. Rather than moving only one part of your back, strive to round your lower, middle, and upper back simultaneously. Due to the anatomy of the spine, most of the motion will occur at the lower half of your back, but moving the upper half of your back as well can help you train more of your back musculature.
    4. As a cue, you can think about bringing the bottom of your ribs toward the top of your hip bones.
    5. While keeping your lower body in the same position throughout each rep, you should continue feeling a stretching sensation in your hamstrings. During the descent, particularly avoid dropping your hips by bending your knees.
3. While maintaining the same hinged-hip position, uncoil your spine in a controlled manner as you transition from a rounded-back to a flat-back position.
    1. All the motion should come from your back muscles, extending (straightening) your spine without any movement at your hips or knees, which should prompt you to feel tension in the muscles running along your spine as you flare your ribs up and away from your hip bones.
    2. Based on your preference, you can finish this step once your back is straight or when your lower back is mildly arched, like it normally is when standing upright.
    3. Performing this lifting phase with a controlled tempo can help you isolate the intended spinal motion without inadvertently moving your hips or knees.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebell down as you lower your hips by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep intrinsic spinal extensor muscles that run along the length of your entire back.## Setting up
1. Prepare the barbell with your desired load by adding weight plates to it or leaving the barbell unloaded, which is recommended if performing this exercise for the first time.
    1. The setup for this exercise is simply performing the lifting phase of a conventional deadlift.
    2. As an alternative to a standard barbell, in which you can load plates onto its sleeves, you can similarly use a fixed-weight barbell for this exercise.
2. Optionally set up a thick bumper plate, deadlift block, or aerobic step centered under the bar if you wish to extend this exercise’s range of motion beyond what you could achieve when standing on the ground.
    1. When standing on the ground, individuals who are sufficiently flexible may find that the weight can contact the ground at the bottom of a rep before they reach their full range of motion. Standing on top of elevated objects (as you would during a deficit deadlift) can alternatively permit a greater range of motion. Compared to loading the bar with large diameter plates, exclusively loading it with small diameter plates can also serve a similar function as standing on an elevated object.
    2. If your range of motion is not restricted when standing on the ground during this exercise, there is no purpose in standing on an object to elevate your feet.
3. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the middle of your feet centered under the bar.
    1. At this point, you will naturally be standing on the elevated object if you wish to use it, or you can stand on the ground.
4. Sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can grasp the bar with a near shoulder-width grip as your palms face backward (i.e., overhand grip) and your elbows are straight.
5. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move without rounding your back.
    1. Throughout the set, keep the soles of your feet fully planted and your arms in a vertical position, perpendicular to the ground.
    2. This step is essentially the same as the lowering phase of a Romanian deadlift without keeping the weight as close to your legs (since the arms angle slightly backward during a Romanian deadlift, rather than hanging straight down during this exercise).
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal. How close to horizontal you can incline your torso will be affected by your hamstring flexibility.
    5. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range.
2. In a controlled curling manner, allow your whole back to round slowly through your comfortable range of motion without moving your lower body.
    1. The degree to which you can round your back will be affected by your spinal mobility.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your back muscles in the bottom position, but you should not feel any discomfort at the end range. Only round your back as far as is comfortable.
    3. Rather than moving only one part of your back, strive to round your lower, middle, and upper back simultaneously. Due to the anatomy of the spine, most of the motion will occur at the lower half of your back, but moving the upper half of your back as well can help you train more of your back musculature.
    4. As a cue, you can think about bringing the bottom of your ribs toward the top of your hip bones.
    5. While keeping your lower body in the same position throughout each rep, you should continue feeling a stretching sensation in your hamstrings. During the descent, particularly avoid dropping your hips by bending your knees.
3. While maintaining the same hinged-hip position, uncoil your spine in a controlled manner as you transition from a rounded-back to a flat-back position.
    1. All the motion should come from your back muscles, extending (straightening) your spine without any movement at your hips or knees, which should prompt you to feel tension in the muscles running along your spine as you flare your ribs up and away from your hip bones.
    2. Based on your preference, you can finish this step once your back is straight or when your lower back is mildly arched, like it normally is when standing upright.
    3. Performing this lifting phase with a controlled tempo can help you isolate the intended spinal motion without inadvertently moving your hips or knees.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the weight down as you lower your hips by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep intrinsic spinal extensor muscles that run along the length of your entire back.## Setting up
1. After placing the base of a kettlebell on the ground, sit down on the ground with the kettlebell directly in front of your hips with your legs straddling the weight.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can place an exercise mat on the ground where you will lie.
2. Use both hands to grasp the horns (side handles) of the kettlebell and lift it up onto the midline of your pelvis with the handle facing up.
    1. The base of the kettlebell should be resting on your pubic bones, which lie directly below your bladder and just above your genitals. If you situate the weight above or below your pubic bones, applying pressure to those areas can naturally be uncomfortable.
    2. Continue holding the horns of the kettlebell with your palms facing in toward each other throughout the exercise.
3. While keeping the kettlebell resting on your pelvis, lean your torso backward and bend your knees to at least 90 degrees to position the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can position your feet shoulder-width apart, but it is up to you whether you would like them closer together or farther apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground.
4. While leaving one foot on the ground, lift your other foot off the ground by straightening your knee.
    1. Keep one foot off the ground throughout the set you perform with your other side.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heel into the ground and squeezing your glute while continuing to hold the kettlebell on your pubic bones.
    1. At the top, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side (on the side of your body with the foot contacting the ground). Your thigh and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glute.
    2. Focus on moving your hip and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glute engage.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you can lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side by switching which foot you keep on the ground.
    1. Once you are ready to set the weight down, you can march both knees up toward you and rock forward into an upright torso position while placing the base of the kettlebell onto the ground between your thighs.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. After placing the base of a kettlebell on the ground, sit down on the ground with the kettlebell directly in front of your hips with your legs straddling the weight.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can place an exercise mat on the ground where you will lie.
2. Use both hands to grasp the horns (side handles) of the kettlebell and lift it up onto the midline of your pelvis with the handle facing up.
    1. The base of the kettlebell should be resting on your pubic bones, which lie directly below your bladder and just above your genitals. If you situate the weight above or below your pubic bones, applying pressure to those areas can naturally be uncomfortable.
    2. Continue holding the horns of the kettlebell with your palms facing in toward each other throughout the exercise.
3. While keeping the kettlebell resting on your pelvis, lean your torso backward and bend your knees to at least 90 degrees to position the soles of your feet flat on the ground.
    1. You can position your feet shoulder-width apart, but it is up to you whether you would like them closer together or farther apart.
    2. Based on what feels comfortable to you, you can point your toes forward or slightly out to the sides.
    3. Specifically how much you wish to bend your knees (and correspondingly bring your heels toward your buttocks) is also up to your preference and which position feels comfortable to you.
    4. In this position, you can rest the back of your head on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your hips up off the ground by driving your heels into the ground and squeezing your glutes while continuing to hold the kettlebell on your pubic bones.
    1. At the top, you should be able to draw a straight line from your knee through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the side. Your thighs and torso should naturally become aligned when you fully contract your glutes.
    2. Focus on moving your hips and avoid excessively arching your lower back. Rather than flaring up (which occurs from lower back arching), the bottom of your ribs should remain pointing down toward your hip bones.
    3. If you wish, you can hold the full glute squeeze at the top for a moment to help you feel your glutes engage.
    4. As a cue for engaging your glutes, you can think about trying to crush a walnut between them at the top.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your hips in a controlled manner until you can lightly contact the ground with your buttocks.
    1. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the descent with your glutes rather than letting your hips drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your last rep, you can march your knees up toward you and rock forward into an upright torso position while placing the base of the kettlebell onto the ground between your thighs.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your adductor magnus and piriformis to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells by your thighs with straight elbows.
    1. You can hold the dumbbells in the same way you would during a dumbbell Romanian deadlift, where your palms are next to the outer aspect of the front of your thighs, so that your palms angle diagonally backward and in toward each other.
    2. If you are sufficiently flexible and think that the weights will contact the ground at the bottom of your reps before you have reached your body’s full range of motion when standing on the ground, you can stand on top of a sturdy elevated surface, such as an aerobic step or bumper plate. Initially performing this exercise while standing on the ground will let you determine whether an elevated surface is required for you to train through your full range of motion.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move without rounding your back.
    1. Throughout the set, keep the soles of your feet fully planted on the ground and your arms in a vertical position, perpendicular to the ground.
    2. This step is essentially the same as the lowering phase of a Romanian deadlift without keeping the dumbbells as close to your legs (since the arms angle slightly backward during a Romanian deadlift, rather than hanging straight down during this exercise).
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal. How close to horizontal you can incline your torso will be affected by your hamstring flexibility.
    5. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range.
2. In a controlled curling manner, allow your whole back to round slowly through your comfortable range of motion without moving your lower body.
    1. The degree to which you can round your back will be affected by your spinal mobility.
    2. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your back muscles in the bottom position, but you should not feel any discomfort at the end range. Only round your back as far as is comfortable.
    3. Rather than moving only one part of your back, strive to round your lower, middle, and upper back simultaneously. Due to the anatomy of the spine, most of the motion will occur at the lower half of your back, but moving the upper half of your back as well can help you train more of your back musculature.
    4. As a cue, you can think about bringing the bottom of your ribs toward the top of your hip bones.
    5. While keeping your lower body in the same position throughout each rep, you should continue feeling a stretching sensation in your hamstrings. During the descent, particularly avoid dropping your hips by bending your knees.
3. While maintaining the same hinged-hip position, uncoil your spine in a controlled manner as you transition from a rounded-back to a flat-back position.
    1. All the motion should come from your back muscles, extending (straightening) your spine without any movement at your hips or knees, which should prompt you to feel tension in the muscles running along your spine as you flare your ribs up and away from your hip bones.
    2. Based on your preference, you can finish this step once your back is straight or when your lower back is mildly arched, like it normally is when standing upright.
    3. Performing this lifting phase with a controlled tempo can help you isolate the intended spinal motion without inadvertently moving your hips or knees.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the dumbbells down as you lower your hips by bending your knees.
## Description
This exercise can dynamically train the erector spinae and deep intrinsic spinal extensor muscles that run along the length of your entire back.## Setting up
1. Connect a lat pulldown bar to the cable, select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. While a standard lat pulldown bar attachment is most commonly used, you can perform this exercise with essentially any attachment that you can comfortably hold throughout the set, so it is up to you which specific attachment you use.
2. While facing the bar and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms face forward.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
3. While holding the bar, sit down on the seat with an upright torso and the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the thigh pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbows to straighten as your arms and shoulder blades are pulled upward in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. In this starting position, you should feel tension from the cable. If you notice the cable remains slack (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), widening out your grip may be needed to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straight and your torso upright, fully pull your shoulders down away from your ears
    1. You can think about packing your armpits down into your pockets as a technique cue.
    2. While the range of motion for this exercise may vary among different individuals, this exercise involves a low amplitude of total movement.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by allowing the cable’s resistance to pull your shoulders upward into a fully shrugged position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will train your lats, lower traps, and pec minor.## Setting up
1. Load an adjustable EZ bar with your desired weight, or approach a fixed-weight EZ bar.
    1. Either type of EZ bar can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down and firmly grasp it with your palms angled backward and in toward each other (resulting in a semi-overhand grip). Your hands should be about shoulder-width apart or mildly closer.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a shoulder-width or mildly closer semi-overhand grip is accessible on the bar.
    2. The proper hand placement should result in your wrists and elbows feeling comfortable during this exercise.
3. Stand upright with the bar, and carry it to a flat or seated bench that is situated directly behind you.
    1. If you use a seated bench, the backrest should be low enough that it will not be in the path of the bar and restrict your range of motion for this exercise.
    2. The tall backrest of an adjustable bench, positioned fully upright, may be more likely to limit your range than a fixed seated bench with a backrest that does not reach your head.
4. Move the bar up to your shoulders with your elbows fully bent underneath and slightly in front of the bar.
    1. If the load is light enough to do so, you can just curl the bar up.
    2. With a heavier load, you can perform a cheat curl, where you generate momentum with your hips to help you curl the bar up. Initially sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and your torso to incline slightly forward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward while bending your elbows and mildly swinging them forward.
    3. The bar should now be in essentially the same position it would be for an overhead press.
    4. The bar should be sitting on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists. You will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
    5. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar hard will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
5. Sit down on the bench with an upright torso position.
6. Press the bar up overhead in a shoulder press motion until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. The bar should be vertically stacked above your shoulders and hips.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
## Exercise technique
1. Bend your elbows to lower the bar behind your head in a controlled manner. Based on your preference and what feels most comfortable to your elbows, you can select one of two approaches with respect to your elbow position.
    1. Option 1: Keep your elbows completely up with your upper arms in a vertical position throughout the rep. This will result in the bar moving in an arcing manner exclusively from your forearms hinging at your elbows.
    2. Option 2: Mildly arc your elbows forward and down while bending your elbows to lower the bar. You can mildly tuck your chin toward your chest to create additional space for the bar. This option will result in the bar moving in a closer to vertical path from a combination of shoulder and elbow motion.
    3. Neither of the approaches is inherently superior to the other, so you can feel free to try out both ways and continue using the technique you prefer.
    4. With either option, predominantly keep your elbows tucked in. However, you can allow them to flare slightly out away from your sides if that feels natural and comfortable to you.
2. Continue lowering the bar until you have fully bent your elbows or can lightly tap the base of your neck or your upper back muscles with the bar.
    1. How far you can lower the bar may be limited by the flexibility of your long head of the triceps, particularly if you keep your elbows completely up. While some lifters have the flexibility to reach near their upper back with the bar, others will reach the limit to which they can comfortably bend their elbows before that point. Do not lower the bar beyond the range that your flexibility permits without discomfort.
    2. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire way.
3. Reverse the movement by straightening your elbows to lift the bar into the starting position overhead.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened as the bar is vertically above your shoulders.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set.
    1. After you completed the lifting phase of your last rep, lower the bar in front of your head toward your shoulders like you would during an overhead press.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Don a weight vest, and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance in a location that has sufficient space in front of you for lunging forward repeatedly.
    1. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc.
    2. Keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
    3. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. If you are new to walking lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
2. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
3. Progressively extend (straighten) your knees to lift your body up until you can repeat step 1 to step forward with your rear leg past your stationary leg in the same manner that you initially performed with your other side.
    1. Strive to begin stepping forward as you are lunging up in a fluid manner, which will advance your body forward.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat step 2 with your other side now in the forward position, as you lunge down just like you previously did.
5. Continue repeating steps 1-4 as you progressively lunge forward with alternating legs until you have completed your set or have to turn around and continue lunging in the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. If you are using a step, position what you will use as your step slightly in front of the pull-up bar. The step should be situated where you can readily reach the bar without the step getting in the way of your feet during the exercise.
    1. Objects that can be used as a step include a plyometric box, aerobic step, bumper plate, and flat exercise bench.
    2. Ideally the step will be tall enough that you can grasp the bar with your elbows fairly bent, which will minimize how high you have to hop.
2. While standing under the pull-up bar on the step or ground, reach up and firmly grasp it with your palms facing backward (i.e., an underhand grip), about shoulder-width apart.
    1. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    2. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Hop up while pulling down on the bar to bring your chest up toward the bar with your elbows fully bent by your sides.
    1. You should now be in the top position of a pull-up.
    2. The degree to which you have to use your legs to hop up will naturally be affected by how high the bar is above your chest at the end of the setup. With a sufficiently tall step, you may be able to just step forward off the step without having to hop up.
    3. Since you can use meaningful assistance from your legs or start with your chest already close to the bar, this step should not feel particularly strenuous to your back and arm muscles. The objective is to have getting your chest up to the bar feel fairly easy, so that your muscles will feel fresh and ready to control the descent.
2. While keeping your chest by the bar for a moment, bend your knees as much as is required to avoid your feet contacting the step or ground during the lowering phase. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. If you can do so without the step getting in your way, you can allow your thighs to hang straight down toward the ground.
3. In a controlled manner, lower your chest away from the bar by allowing your elbows to straighten while your torso moves away from them.
    1. Maintain a “proud chest” posture as you avoid letting your shoulders shrug up toward your ears from the force of your bodyweight.
    2. Finish with your elbows fully straightened as your arms are in an overhead position.
    3. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should use your back and arm muscles to control the entirety of the descent rather than letting your body quickly drop down.
    4. If you are not currently strong enough to perform a full pull-up, it can be beneficial to have the intent to try to pull your chest up to the bar by driving your arms down toward your side. Without the strength to lift yourself up, striving to do so can help you control the descent.
4. Return your feet to the step or ground to assume the same position you had when you finished setting up.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Don a weight vest, and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance in a location that has sufficient space behind you for lunging backward.
    1. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc.
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
    3. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    7. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step backward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step forward with your rear leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging backward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Don your weighted vest, and stand upright with your feet near shoulder-width apart, as your toes are pointing somewhat out to the sides rather than pointing straight ahead.
    1. While people may commonly find that setting up reasonably close to this stance allows them to squat effectively, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when squatting, which typically requires trying out different stances over time and assessing how you feel with them.
    3. Some people may feel most comfortable squatting with their feet close to hip width apart as their toes are pointing straight ahead, while others will feel best with their feet outside of shoulder width with their toes rotated out to the sides at 45-degree angles.
2. Raise your arms out in front of you until they are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. You can alternatively keep your arms in a different position if you wish to do so, but keeping your arms outstretched throughout the set provides a counterbalance that often allows people to reach a greater squat depth and not have to lean as far forward to maintain balance.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your knees aligned with your feet.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but keep your back flat and chest up, as you avoid rounding your lower back or letting your chest cave down through upper back rounding.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
3. Continue to squat down through the available range of motion that your mobility enables without rounding your back, lifting your heels up off the ground, or letting your knees cave inward.
    1. People can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground, but what constitutes a full range of motion for a squat will vary among different individuals.
    2. If your back starts rounding, heels lift off the ground, or knees cave inward at the bottom of your squat, you may be squatting excessively deep relative to your current level of mobility and body control.
4. Reverse the movement by progressively standing up as you drive through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips until you have returned to a fully upright position.
    1. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees stacked over your feet as you squat up.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Connect a straight bar attachment or a V-bar row handle (AKA double D-handle) to the machine.
    1. If you prefer to use an overhand grip (with your palms facing backward), select a straight bar attachment, whether that be a standard lat pulldown bar with a long straight body and angled ends, or a straight bar closer to shoulder-width in length.
    2. If you prefer to use a neutral grip (with your palms facing each other), select a V-bar row handle.
    3. Neither attachment type is inherently better than the other, so feel free to try out both to determine which one personally feels better to you when performing this exercise.
2. Without any weight plates added to the machine, stand on the platform with the bar between your feet.
    1. Prior to getting into the starting position for your set, you need to adjust the machine so that its safety horn (on which the weight trolley initially rests) no longer restricts downward motion of the weight trolley. This adjustment requires the use of one hand, so you cannot perform it when your working weight is loaded onto the machine, in contrast to when you are belt squatting, where you can hold the machine’s handles rather than the bar you connect.
3. Squat down to grasp the bar with one hand, and stand upright with it before using your other hand to pull the handles back toward you to disengage the safety horn.
    1. It is unusual to grasp the center of a straight bar attachment with one hand or only one part of a V-bar row handle, but you will not be lifting meaningful resistance without any plates added to the machine, so the awkward grip is not problematic.
    2. If you prefer, you have the option of disengaging the safety horn while wearing a belt connected to the machine prior to swapping a bar for the belt.
    3. With the safety horn no longer resting under the weight trolley, the machine is now set for you to be able to hinge through your full range of motion.
4. Squat down and set the bar onto the platform before adding weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
5. Step up onto the platform with the cable centered between the middle of your feet. Position your feet approximately hip-width apart.
6. While keeping your back flat, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the bar with straight elbows.
    1. Regardless of whether you use an overhand or neutral grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles for the most stable grip.
7. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
8. Without rounding your back, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    2. Without thinking about “bending over,” your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the bar when focusing on “sitting back.”
    3. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    4. Throughout the set, keep your feet fully planted on the platform and your neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees.
    1. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    2. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
3. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    2. When you have finished lifting the bar, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips properly stacked under your shoulders, preventing excessive arching.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the platform.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe between reps in the fully upright position, rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. After finishing the last rep, set the bar down in the same manner in which you initially picked it up, which includes keeping your lower back flat.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Don a weight vest, and stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance with the back of your legs against the edge of the sturdy elevated object you will use to elevate your foot.
    1. If available, a padded cylindrical object positioned near knee height will normally be most comfortable and is recommended.
    2. A Smith machine bar with a squat/hip thrust pad on it, a barbell on top of the J-cups of a power rack, the leg pad of a lying hamstring curl machine, and a split squat roller stand are all high-quality cylindrical options that you can position to knee height.
    3. Alternatively, you can use an object like a flat exercise bench, couch, or plyometric box with a somewhat compliant surface, but objects with a flat surface tend not to be as comfortable for this exercise.
2. While keeping your feet approximately hip-width apart, step forward with one foot until its heel is just in front of your other foot’s big toe before doing the same with your opposite foot.
    1. This results in your front foot now being about two “foot lengths” in front of where you started when your calves were touching the object you will use to elevate your rear foot.
    2. While this strategy can consistently situate most people in a viable starting position for the exercise, a range of foot placements can be effectively used, including having your front foot two and a half “foot lengths” in front of where you started (which may be even more comfortable for some people).
    3. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc.
    4. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your bodyweight into your front foot before placing the top surface (on the side of your shoe laces) of your rear foot on top of the elevated surface with that side’s knee bent.
    1. To help shift your bodyweight into your front foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you slightly bend that side’s knee.
    2. Once you have situated your rear foot into the elevated position, you can return to keeping your torso in a fairly upright position, which should involve you maintaining the natural curvature of your lower back, like when you are normally standing upright (rather than excessively arching beyond that position).
    3. Strive to move your rear foot backward in a straight line to keep your feet approximately hip-width apart, rather than bringing your rear foot closer toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep the entire sole of your front foot firmly planted on the ground and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    6. If you are using a cylindrical object to elevate your foot, you can contact the surface with the front of your ankle along with your foot, but only your foot will contact the elevated surface if it is flat, like a bench.
2. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep most of your weight shifted onto your front foot while you are primarily using your rear foot to assist with balance.
    2. Continue keeping your torso fairly upright and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    6. If you initially positioned your front foot excessively far in front of your elevated object, you may reach the end of your available hip range of motion before your rear knee reaches the ground.
    7. If you initially positioned your front foot too close to the elevated object, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    8. In either case of suboptimal foot placement, you do not need to force your rear knee to tap the ground, and you can adjust how far in front your front foot is situated after finishing your rep.
3. While keeping your front foot firmly planted on the ground and your rear foot in its elevated position, reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees to lift your body up until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Like during the descent, keep your torso fairly upright and knees aligned with your feet while having most of your weight shifted onto the front foot.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position and which leg is in the elevated position.
## Description
While statically training your gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, this exercise primarily trains your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to an open trap bar with the low handles up and sufficient space behind the open part of the trap bar for lunging backward.
    1. Load weight plates with diameters that will permit you to lunge until one knee lightly contacts the ground without the weight plates reaching the ground during your reps.
    2. Whether the diameter of the weight plates can affect this exercise’s range of motion for you will be affected by your body proportions, but full diameter plates (like 45lb (or 20kg) plates and bumper plates) can commonly restrict lunge depth.
    3. Loading the bar exclusively with plates that do not weigh more than 25lb (or 10kg) and avoiding bumper plates is generally appropriate to be able to lunge through your full range of motion when holding the low handles of a trap bar, but this may differ for you as an individual.
2. While facing the close part of the trap bar, stand within the middle of the bar in a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead.
3. Without rounding your back, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the center of the parallel handles with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Avoid grasping the handles too close to the front or back of the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
5. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. The initial submaximal pull allows you to check if you have grasped the handles correctly, which will be indicated by the handles not rotating forward or backward when applying the pre-tension.
    2. If you pick up a trap bar with your hands positioned slightly away from the middle of the handles, the trap bar can start rotating in a manner that makes it very difficult to hold and lift appropriately.
    3. If the bar starts rotating when you apply the pre-tension, re-center your grip, and apply the pre-tension again until the bar no longer rotates.
6. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. As you keep your back flat, let your torso lean slightly forward to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    5. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    6. Strive to perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    7. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step backward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by driving through your front foot to lift your body up as you step forward with your rear leg. Stand fully upright with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart to return to the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet and your hip bones level.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging backward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down in a deadlift motion by bending your hips and knees while keeping your back flat.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to an open trap bar with the low handles up and sufficient space behind the open part of the trap bar for stepping backward.
    1. Load weight plates with diameters that will permit you to descend until one knee lightly contacts the ground without the weight plates reaching the ground during your reps.
    2. Whether the diameter of the weight plates can affect this exercise’s range of motion for you will be affected by your body proportions, but full diameter plates (like 45lb (or 20kg) plates and bumper plates) can commonly restrict split squat depth.
    3. Loading the bar exclusively with plates that do not weigh more than 25lb (or 10kg) and avoiding bumper plates is generally appropriate to be able to train through your full range of motion when holding the low handles of a trap bar, but this may differ for you as an individual.
2. While facing the close part of the trap bar, stand within the middle of the bar in a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead.
3. Without rounding your back, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the center of the parallel handles with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Avoid grasping the handles too close to the front or back of the bar.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the handles with the handles pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
5. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to hear the weight plates clink against the bar.
    1. The initial submaximal pull allows you to check if you have grasped the handles correctly, which will be indicated by the handles not rotating forward or backward when applying the pre-tension.
    2. If you pick up a trap bar with your hands positioned slightly away from the middle of the handles, the trap bar can start rotating in a manner that makes it very difficult to hold and lift appropriately.
    3. If the bar starts rotating when you apply the pre-tension, re-center your grip, and apply the pre-tension again until the bar no longer rotates.
6. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, take a fairly long step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Slightly bend the knee on the side of your stationary foot while doing so.
    2. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to split squats.
    3. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Keep the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    5. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. From your split stance position, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Continue keeping the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    2. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. As you keep your back flat, let your torso lean slightly forward to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    5. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground with your knee, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and step somewhat farther backward after you ascend before performing your next rep.
3. While keeping both of your feet planted on the ground to maintain your split stance, reverse the movement by driving through your front foot to lift your body into an upright position.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet.
4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position.
    1. After finishing the set with your other leg forward, set the bar down in a deadlift motion by bending your hips and knees while keeping your back flat.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. If you are using a step, position what you will use as your step slightly in front of the pull-up bar. The step should be situated where you can readily reach the bar without the step getting in the way of your feet during the exercise.
    1. Objects that can be used as a step include a plyometric box, aerobic step, bumper plate, and flat exercise bench.
    2. Ideally the step will be tall enough that you can grasp the bar with your elbows fairly bent, which will minimize how high you have to hop.
2. While standing under the pull-up bar on the step or ground, reach up and firmly grasp the parallel handles with your palms facing in toward each other (i.e., a neutral grip)
    1. If your bar has multiple pairs of parallel handles, select the pair that positions your hands close to shoulder-width apart.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide up your hand while hanging on it, which pulls on your hand skin in an uncomfortable manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Hop up while pulling down on the bar to bring your chest up toward the bar with your elbows fully bent by your sides.
    1. You should now be in the top position of a pull-up.
    2. The degree to which you have to use your legs to hop up will naturally be affected by how high the bar is above your chest at the end of the setup. With a sufficiently tall step, you may be able to just step forward off the step without having to hop up.
    3. Since you can use meaningful assistance from your legs or start with your chest already close to the bar, this step should not feel particularly strenuous to your back and arm muscles. The objective is to have getting your chest up to the bar feel fairly easy, so that your muscles will feel fresh and ready to control the descent.
2. While keeping your chest by the bar for a moment, bend your knees as much as is required to avoid your feet contacting the step or ground during the lowering phase. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades down and mildly arch your middle and upper back to angle your chest slightly upward.
    1. If you can do so without the step getting in your way, you can allow your thighs to hang straight down toward the ground.
3. In a controlled manner, lower your chest away from the bar by allowing your elbows to straighten while your torso moves away from them.
    1. Maintain a “proud chest” posture as you avoid letting your shoulders shrug up toward your ears from the force of your bodyweight.
    2. Finish with your elbows fully straightened as your arms are in an overhead position.
    3. There is no need to lower yourself extremely slowly, but you should use your back and arm muscles to control the entirety of the descent rather than letting your body quickly drop down.
    4. If you are not currently strong enough to perform a full pull-up, it can be beneficial to have the intent to try to pull your chest up to the bar by driving your arms down toward your side. Without the strength to lift yourself up, striving to do so can help you control the descent.
4. Return your feet to the step or ground to assume the same position you had when you finished setting up.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
You have the option of wearing a weighted vest (which is the ideal option if available to you) or positioning a weight plate on the front of your torso to provide resistance for this exercise. The technique is the same when using either type of equipment, but some setup details differ based on which option you select, which is why usage of each equipment option receives its own setup section.

### **Setting up with a vest**

1. Position a barbell in the J-cups of a rack set approximately to the height of your hips while standing, or alternatively position a Smith machine’s bar to the same height.
    1. This exercise can be performed with the same technique whether you use a barbell and rack or a Smith machine.
    2. If you use a rack, situate the barbell snugly in contact with the back corner of the J-cups since the middle portion of certain J-cups can be wide enough to allow the barbell to roll forward or backward, which is undesirable for this exercise.
2. Don your weighted vest, and sit on the ground with your shoulders under and slightly behind the bar, as your legs are straight out in front of you.
    1. The back of your heels should be on the ground, while the soles of your feet are off the ground and facing forward.
3. Slightly lean your torso backward, and firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside of shoulder-width overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing forward.
4. While holding the bar, drive your heels into the ground and squeeze your glutes to bridge your hips up and recline your torso until you can draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.

### **Setting up with a weight plate**

1. Position a barbell in the J-cups of a rack set approximately to the height of your hips while standing, or alternatively position a Smith machine’s bar to the same height.
    1. This exercise can be performed with the same technique whether you use a barbell and rack or a Smith machine.
    2. If you use a rack, situate the barbell snugly in contact with the back corner of the J-cups since the middle portion of certain J-cups can be wide enough to allow the barbell to roll forward or backward, which is undesirable for this exercise.
2. Place your weight plate slightly in front of the bar and on one side of the bar, so that you can sit down under the bar and reach the plate.
3. Sit on the ground with your shoulders under and slightly behind the bar, as your legs are straight out in front of you.
    1. The back of your heels should be on the ground, while the soles of your feet are off the ground and facing forward.
4. Place the weight plate against the front of your torso, with the bottom of the plate situated close to the crease of your hips.
    1. If you have a training partner, you can alternatively have your partner position the weight plate on your torso after you complete step 6.
5. Slightly lean your torso backward, and firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside of shoulder-width overhand grip, resulting in your palms facing forward.
6. While holding the bar, drive your heels into the ground and squeeze your glutes to bridge your hips up and recline your torso until you can draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your chest toward the bar by driving your elbows simultaneously downward and backward while progressively bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion while aiming for your chest to reach the bar.
    3. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your torso and lower body should remain nearly stationary in the bridged position that is akin to holding a reverse plank. This should result in you being able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side throughout the set.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your chest move down away from the bar in a controlled manner as your elbows progressively straighten and your shoulder blades naturally spread apart.
    1. Fully return to the starting position with your arms outstretched up in front of you.
    2. Continue keeping your knees, hips, and shoulders aligned as you avoid letting your hips sag.
    3. While you do not have to lower yourself extremely slowly, you should actively resist the descent rather than abruptly dropping in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Determine what you will use as your weight that can securely rest on your middle and upper back while performing your plank.
    1. Wearing a weighted vest is an excellent option if you have one due to its stability and ease of setting up, but most people lack a weighted vest.
    2. If someone can assist you, a partner can also place a weight plate or stack of weight plates on your back once you have assumed the plank position. With this approach, a rubber-coated plate is best since it is less likely to slide than a purely iron plate. If you are in a gym without a designated training partner, many lifters would be open to lending a hand if you just politely ask them to do so. Communicate to the other person that you would like him/her to lift the plate off your back after finishing your last rep, in addition to placing it on your middle back to begin the set.
    3. As a substitute for a weighted vest, you can add weighted objects to a sufficiently sturdy backpack. If you do so, you would want to try to have the weight as evenly distributed as possible to reduce the likelihood of it sliding around or shifting to rest on your lower back, which may be difficult to avoid with weight plates in many backpacks. Chains may be more likely to fill up a backpack evenly, and textbooks can also be viable when using lighter loads. When present, securely fastening chest straps and hip straps on a backpack can increase its stability.
    4. If you decide to use a vest or a loaded backpack to carry your weight, put it on yourself now. If you decide to use a weight plate, make sure your partner is standing nearby with your desired weight and understands what to do before proceeding to the next step.
2. Position yourself with your hands, knees, and toes on the ground as your elbows are straight and your knees are bent.
3. Prop your forearms onto the ground with your upper arms directed straight down such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    1. Based on your preference, you can make a fist with each hand and direct your palms in toward each other, or you can place your palms flat on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Straighten one knee to move your foot behind you, and position the bottom of its toes onto the ground before doing the same with your other leg.
    1. In this position, your feet should be approximately hip-width apart, with your heels facing backward.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, as you would normally do when standing upright, which should direct your gaze to the ground slightly out in front of you.
2. Tighten your abdominal muscles and squeeze your glutes to maintain a flat back position while keeping your knees straight and elbows bent.
    1. Doing so keeps your hips up (but not excessively hiked) rather than letting them sag, which would result in an exaggerated arch in your lower back.
    2. In this position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your torso.
    3. Do not allow your chest to sag, which would result in your shoulder blades being pinched back together.
    4. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like you would normally do when standing upright.
3. If you are using a weight plate for your resistance, have your partner now position it symmetrically on your middle and upper back, but you will naturally ignore this step if you are using a vest or backpack.
4. Statically hold your plank position for your target duration until you have completed your set.
    1. Continue keeping your abs tensed, glutes squeezed, and lower back flat throughout the set.
    2. If you are using a weight plate, direct your partner to remove the weight after finishing your last rep.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your core muscles, including your rectus abdominis and your  obliques, as they function to maintain a rigid trunk position.## Setting up
1. Strap an ankle weight to each of your ankles, and lie down on your back with straight legs as your feet are approximately hip width apart.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can lie down on an exercise mat.
2. Place the fingertips of each hand lightly in contact with the sides of the back of your head as your upper arms and elbows are directed out away from your sides.
    1. Since you are not interlocking your fingers along the middle of the back of your head, you should not have to lift your head up off the ground to situate your hands in the appropriate position.
    2. Keeping your hands and arms in this position can help you avoid inadvertently pulling down on your head during the exercise, which would otherwise be easier to do if you interlocked your fingers and directed your upper arms up toward the ceiling.
    3. Maintain this arm position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Tighten your abs to flatten your lower back and press it lightly into the ground. Keep your lower back flattened against the ground throughout all subsequent steps.
    1. In contrast to if your abs were relaxed and your lower back exhibited its natural arch, you should not be able to slide a hand under your lower back.
2. March your left leg up and back toward your chest while bending its knee.
    1. How far you can march this leg back will be affected by your hip mobility.
    2. So long as you follow the same movement pattern described here, you can choose whether you begin with your left or right leg marched, but mentioning the sides as an example can help with technique clarity.
3. With a straight knee, lift your right leg slightly off the ground or higher up for a lower intensity.
    1. As you lift this leg higher up, the difficulty of the exercise will decrease, so you can determine specifically how high you lift it based on your current ability.
    2. If you notice that you cannot keep your lower back flat with your leg slightly off the ground, you can try lifting it up to a height that allows you to avoid aching your lower back.
4. Tuck your chin slightly toward your chest to lift your head just above the ground.
5. In a crunch-like motion, mildly curl your upper back to lift your shoulder blades slightly up off the ground.
    1. While they are presented as distinct steps for instructional clarity, you can perform the actions from steps 1-5 simultaneously with each other once you understand the proper positioning that each step achieves.
6. March your right leg up and back toward your chest while bending its knee as you lower your left leg while straightening its knee. Simultaneously rotate your chest to the right to bring your left elbow toward your right knee.
    1. Strive to move your legs reciprocally at the same speed in sync with your torso rotation. This should result in you finishing lowering your left leg at the same time you have finished marching your right leg and turning your chest to the right.
    2. Move through the range of motion permitted by your spine and hip mobility. You have reached your end range once you feel that you can no longer rotate your chest to the right while marching your right leg back. This may involve achieving contact between the knee and elbow for some people, but touching your elbow to your knee is not the focus of this exercise.
    3. Your elbows should only move because you are twisting your torso, and your arms are attached to your torso through your shoulders. Keep your arms mostly relaxed to avoid moving your arms by themselves through shoulder motion.
    4. Finish lowering your left leg once it is slightly above the ground or has reached the starting height you initially opted to use for your right leg.
    5. You can imagine that a laser beam is shooting out from the center of your chest as you rotate that laser beam out to the right. If your chest and the laser beam do not turn toward the right, you are not appropriately rotating your torso.
    6. Avoid letting your lower back arch up as you continue keeping it flattened against the ground by tightening your abs.
7. Fluidly reverse the movement by performing the opposite motion with each leg and rotating your chest the other way.
    1. This will involve lowering your right leg, marching your left leg up, and turning your chest to the left based on the directionality of the last step.
8. Alternate between steps 6 and 7 until you have completed your set and are ready to rest your feet on the ground.
## Description
This exercise can develop your obliques and rectus abdominis while additionally training your hip flexors.## Setting up
To be able to hinge through your full range of motion, the machine’s safety arm (on which its lever arm rests) will have to be disengaged prior to starting your set. Some machines are designed to have the safety arm automatically disengage after lifting the lever arm up, while others require you to pull the handle back with your hand to disengage it. Steps 2-4 of the setup describe how to adjust a machine that involves manually disengaging the safety arm. If your machine has the automatic disengagement feature, you can add weight plates to the machine during step 1 and skip steps 2-4 of the setup.

1. Connect a straight bar attachment or a V-bar row handle (AKA double D-handle) to the machine.
    1. If you prefer to use an overhand grip (with your palms facing backward), select a straight bar attachment, whether that be a standard lat pulldown bar with a long straight body and angled ends, or a straight bar closer to shoulder-width in length.
    2. If you prefer to use a neutral grip (with your palms facing each other), select a V-bar row handle.
    3. Neither attachment type is inherently better than the other, so feel free to try out both to determine which one personally feels better to you when performing this exercise.
    4. Different machine models vary in their design, since some machines have a lever arm with a single attachment point, while others have a lever arm with multiple attachment points.
    5. When using a lever arm with multiple attachment points, the machine will provide less resistance when using a point near the base of the lever arm compared to the free end of the lever arm. Consequently, the specific lever attachment point can affect the difficulty of each rep, in addition to how much weight you add to the machine via plates. Consistently connecting the bar to the attachment point furthest from the base of the machine will minimize how many plates you have to add to the machine to provide your intended level of resistance.
2. Without any weight plates added to the machine, stand on the platform with the bar between your feet.
3. Squat down to grasp the bar with one hand, and stand upright with it before using your other hand to pull the handles back toward you to disengage the safety horn.
    1. It is unusual to grasp the center of a straight bar attachment with one hand or only one part of a V-bar row handle, but you will not be lifting meaningful resistance without any plates added to the machine, so the awkward grip is not problematic.
    2. If you prefer, you have the option of disengaging the safety horn while wearing a belt connected to the machine prior to swapping a bar for the belt.
4. Squat down and set the bar onto the platform before adding weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
5. Step up onto the platform with the bar’s attachment point centered between the middle of your feet. Position your feet approximately hip-width apart.
6. While keeping your back flat, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the bar with straight elbows.
    1. Regardless of whether you use an overhand or neutral grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles for the most stable grip.
7. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
8. Without rounding your back, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    2. Without thinking about “bending over,” your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the bar when focusing on “sitting back.”
    3. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    4. Throughout the set, keep your feet fully planted on the platform and your neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees.
    1. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    2. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
3. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    2. When you have finished lifting the bar, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips properly stacked under your shoulders, preventing excessive arching.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the platform.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe between reps in the fully upright position, rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. After finishing the last rep, set the bar down in the same manner in which you initially picked it up, which includes keeping your lower back flat.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand centered between its handles in a hip-width stance.
    1. If your machine model lets you adjust the handle height, you can initially set the handles to the lowest setting. If you cannot grasp the handles during the next step without rounding your lower back, you can subsequently raise the handles to a height that lets you grasp them with a flat-back position.
    2. If your machine has a roller pad that you can move horizontally, position it fully back toward the base of the machine so that it will not be in your way when lunging.
2. Face away from the base of the machine unless you intentionally want to perform your lunge with a meaningfully leaned forward torso position.
    1. It is not necessarily incorrect to face toward the base of the machine, but facing the other direction often helps this exercise feel more natural and is recommended when initially performing it.
    2. Feel free to try the exercise facing both ways to determine if you prefer one over the other.
3. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, sit your hips back and down while bending your knees to squat down until you can firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles, it is generally recommended to grasp the lower handles to let you train through a longer range of motion if you can reach those handles without rounding your lower back.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
5. Drive through your legs to stand upright as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Throughout the exercise, you should chiefly be using your lower body muscles to lift the handles through knee and hip motion while keeping your back in a rigid position.
6. Walk your feet slightly forward so that your buttocks are between your hands with your arms angled slightly backward.
    1. When you lunge backward while holding the handles, your torso will move backward relative to your hands, so starting with your hands a little behind your sides (rather than being directly by your sides) can help avoid your arms angling awkwardly far forward at the bottom of your lunge.
    2. Feel free to adjust precisely where you situate your feet to help the lunge feel most comfortable to you.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the ground, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee without contacting the machine with your foot. This may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. As you keep your back flat, let your torso lean forward to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you.
    2. Continue keeping the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    5. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    6. Strive to perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    7. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step backward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by driving through your front foot to lift your body up as you step forward with your rear leg. Stand fully upright with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart to return to the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet, hip bones level, and back flat.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging backward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the handles down by bending your hips and knees while keeping your back flat.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
Specifically how you set up for this exercise can differ depending on whether your machine has adjustable roller pads or a fixed foot bar for elevating your rear foot. The same exercise technique can be used with either type of machine, but they have their own setup sections for clarity.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your bodyweight into your front foot before placing the top surface (on the side of your shoe laces) of your rear foot on top of the foot bar/roller pad with that side’s knee bent.
    1. To help shift your bodyweight into your front foot, you can lean your torso mildly forward by hinging at your hip as you slightly bend that side’s knee.
    2. Strive to move your rear foot backward in a straight line to keep your feet approximately hip-width apart, rather than bringing your rear foot closer toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
2. From your split stance position, bend at your lead leg’s knee and hip to lower your body through the full range in which you can comfortably descend without rounding your back.
    1. If you initially set your feet appropriately, your end range of motion should be determined by either your rear knee reaching the ground or your lead leg’s hip reaching the end range to which it can bend.
    2. If you have reached the end range of your lead leg’s hip mobility, you will likely feel a stretching sensation in that side’s glute as your upper thigh is close to your torso. Lowering your hands any further after reaching the limit of your hip mobility could require rounding your back.
3. Once you are in the bottom position, firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows.
    1. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles and you can reach the lower handles, grasp these lower handles instead of the higher handles to let you train through a longer range of motion.
    2. If your machine has long handles that allow you to grasp them at different parts, grasp them in a position that feels comfortable to you while keeping your elbows straight. Feel free to try grasping the handles at different parts to determine if the exercise feels better to you when holding the handles closer toward their bases or free ends.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
5. Drive through your front foot to lift your body up while extending (straightening) your lead leg’s knee and hip until you have returned to an upright position in your split stance.
    1. Throughout the exercise, keep the entirety of your front foot’s sole (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) firmly planted on the ground while you bear most of your weight through your lead leg and primarily use your elevated rear foot to assist your balance.
    2. During both the ascent and descent, keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
6. Reverse the movement by lowering your body in a controlled manner through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably descend. As you bend your lead leg’s knee and hip, let your torso naturally lean forward while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. If you can descend with good technique until your rear knee reaches the ground, you can lightly tap the ground with your knee at the bottom. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
7. Repeat steps 5 and 6 until you have finished your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which leg is in the forward position and which leg is in the elevated position.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand centered between its handles in a near hip-width stance facing toward the base of the machine. 
    1. If your machine model lets you adjust the handle height, you can initially set the handles to the lowest setting. If you cannot grasp the handles during the next step without rounding your lower back, you can subsequently raise the handles to a height that lets you grasp them with a flat-back position.
    2. Compared to facing away from the base of the machine, facing toward it can often reinforce sitting back to hinge at your hips. 
2. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles, it is generally recommended to grasp the lower handles to let you train through a longer range of motion if you can reach those handles without rounding your lower back.
    2. More motion should come from hinging at your hips than bending your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift somewhat forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    3. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have fully sat back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the handles.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the handles as you “push the floor away” before thrusting your hips forward to stand fully upright. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. You should chiefly be using your lower body muscles to lift the handles through hip and knee motion while keeping your back in a rigid position.
    2. Avoid letting your hips shoot up and/rounding your back to start the rep, which could occur if you do not sufficiently drive your feet through the ground.
    3. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
    4. Finish with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
3. Reverse the movement by sitting your hips back and bending your knees to lower the handles to the starting position.
    1. This should simply be a reversal of the lifting phase, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and feet fully on the ground.
    2. Avoid trying to squat straight down with an upright torso and excessive forward knee travel. Particularly to begin the descent, emphasize sitting back while letting your knees naturally bend, and your torso transitions closer to horizontal.
4. Allow the weight to come to a dead stop at the bottom for a moment, rather than trying to bounce the machine’s lever arms off the stoppers underneath them.
    1. While you do not need to pause in the bottom position for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is resting on the stoppers rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can train your gluteus maximus and adductor magnus, along with your quads and hamstrings to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Don a weight vest, and stand upright with approximately a hip-width stance in a location that has sufficient space in front of you for lunging forward.
    1. You can position your hands wherever you prefer, such as resting by your sides, on top of your hips, in front of your chest, etc.
    2. You will keep your arms in your preferred position throughout your set.
    3. Positioning yourself into a hip-width stance rather than a narrower stance can help you maintain your balance during this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping one foot on the ground, take a fairly long step straight forward with your other foot far enough ahead that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee when lunging down.
    1. Like when you step forward while walking, you can initially contact the ground with your front foot’s heel prior to lowering the entirety of your sole onto the ground in a fluid motion, which will likely occur naturally without having to think about it.
    2. When your front foot contacts the ground, you can allow your rear leg’s heel to start lifting up off the ground, which is also likely to occur naturally.
    3. Keep your torso in an upright position and the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
    5. Strive to step forward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    6. If you are new to forward lunges, you may have to try out different step lengths to determine which step length places you in the appropriate position.
2. After contacting the ground with your front foot, allow your knees to bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner until you are able to tap the ground gently with the kneecap of your rear leg.
    1. Keep your rear leg’s forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot’s sole in contact with the ground.
    2. Continue keeping your torso upright and your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. At the end of the lowering phase, your front leg’s thigh should be close to parallel to the ground.
    5. You should perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step forward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground.
    7. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step forward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step backward with your front leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Like during the descent, keep your torso upright and knees aligned with your feet while doing so.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 while lunging forward with your opposite side.
5. Alternate between steps 1-4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise will primarily train your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. To set up for this row, you can perform the lifting phase of a conventional deadlift with a rather wide grip, followed by the lowering phase of a Romanian deadlift.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the middle of your feet under the middle of the bar.
3. Sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the bar with a well outside shoulder-width overhand grip. Keep your lower back flat and chest up throughout the exercise.
    1. It is generally appropriate to use a grip width that is approximately the distance between your elbows when your arms are raised straight out to your sides, parallel to the ground. The right grip for you should make flaring your elbows out from your sides during the row feel natural and comfortable.
    2. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    3. Throughout the exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, as when you are looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced from this point on.
5. Stand upright with the bar by driving through your legs and thrusting your hips forward.
6. While allowing your knees to bend mildly and keeping your back flat, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as your flexibility permits.
    1. To avoid rounding your lower back, you should think about sitting back rather than bending over.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain mostly vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively pushing your knees forward depends on your flexibility.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar up toward your chest by driving your elbows upward and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Keep your torso fixed in a hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back flat.
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your lower body should remain essentially stationary.
    3. While you do not need to touch your chest with the bar, you should finish with the bar close to reaching it, in contrast to a typical row, where you pull the bar lower down toward your abdomen.
    4. When viewed from behind, your upper arms and torso form close to a “T-shape” at the top of the row with flared elbows, in contrast to a standard row with a narrower grip that finishes with the elbows tucked closer to the sides.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner. Let your elbows move downward and your shoulder blades move apart from each other until you have returned to the starting position with straight elbows.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the floor by bending your knees further.
## Description
This row effectively develops your rear delts, traps, and rhomboids while also training your biceps, brachialis, brachioradialis, and three rotator cuff muscles.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright in a near hip-width stance while holding the kettlebells at your sides with your palms facing in toward your thighs.
2. Slightly bend your elbows and slightly lean forward at your hips.
    1. While you do not need to have an exaggerated “proud chest” posture with your shoulders fully pulled back, avoid standing with your shoulders rolled forward into a slouched upper back position.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arms out and away from your sides until they are approximately parallel to the ground.
    1. While you raise your arms through shoulder motion and allow your shoulder blades to move naturally, keep the rest of your body stationary as you maintain a slight elbow bend and slight forward lean throughout your set.
    2. Let the handles rotate in your hands to keep the base of each kettlebell facing the ground.
    3. At the top of the raise, your palms should be directed down toward the ground with mostly straight wrists.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering your arms in toward your sides in a controlled arcing manner until the kettlebells are directly next to your legs.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weights abruptly yank your arms downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus while also training your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of kettlebells and stand upright in a near hip-width stance while holding the kettlebells at your sides.
2. While letting your knees naturally bend and keeping your back flat, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as your flexibility permits.
    1. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something. Focusing on “sitting back” rather than “bending over” can help you avoid rounding your lower back
    2. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain mostly vertical, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriate degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
3. As your arms hang down toward the ground with the kettlebells vertically under your shoulders, point your palms in toward each other if they are not already facing this direction.
## Exercise technique
1. While letting your elbows bend slightly, sweep your arms out and away from your sides in a wide, arcing motion until your elbows are near shoulder height.
    1. Statically maintain your hinged torso position throughout the set as you avoid moving your lower body or lower back.
    2. The appropriately slight degree of elbow bend can naturally occur if you do not actively try to keep them fully locked. If you feel like you need to bend your elbows to a greater degree to lift the kettlebells up in a row-like motion, you likely selected a weight that is excessively heavy for this variation.
    3. As you move your arms outward and upward, avoid pulling them back toward your hips.
    4. Let the handles rotate in your hands to keep the base of each kettlebell facing the ground.
    5. At the top of the raise, your palms should be directed down toward the ground with mostly straight wrists.
2. In a controlled arcing manner, reverse the motion by moving your arms downward and in toward each other until they are hanging down toward the ground.
    1. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weights abruptly swing your arms downward. The weights should not bang against each other in the bottom position.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebells down.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. While placing one hand on each of a kettlebell’s horns (i.e, the side handles) with your palms facing in toward each other, pick up the kettlebell and stand upright in a near hip-width stance. Hold the kettlebell in front of your thighs with straight elbows.
    1. It may be awkward to use this grip to pick up a kettlebell directly from the floor (especially if you are using a heavier weight), so you have the option of initially deadlifting the kettlebell with a double overhand grip from the top handle and setting it on an elevated surface like a bench before grasping its horns with your palms facing in.
    2. Due to the shape of the kettlebell, it is not feasible to have the top handle pointing straight upward (like it would when the bottom base of the kettlebell is resting on the ground) while your elbows are straight, so you will have to angle the kettlebell’s base toward you.
    3. To hold the kettlebell in front of your thighs with straight elbows, you will need to move your upper arms slightly in front of your torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout the set.
2. While keeping your elbows straight, sweep your arms forward and upward in an arcing manner to lift the kettlebell to approximately shoulder height.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    2. All the movement should occur from shoulder motion while you keep your legs and torso stationary throughout the exercise.
    3. At the top, your arms should be outstretched straight in front of you, parallel to the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the kettlebell in a controlled manner until it is directly in front of your legs.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your arms pointing down toward the ground.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weight downward.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. With your palms facing back toward you (i.e., an overhand grip), firmly grasp the handle of one kettlebell with both hands and stand upright in approximately a hip-width stance. Hold the kettlebell just in front of you with straight elbows.
    1. Pick up the kettlebell with a deadlift motion, where you lift it by extending (straightening) your hips and knees while keeping your back flat.
    2. For the most stable grip, firmly wrap your fingers around the handle with the handle pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    3. Your neck should be near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead. Maintain this neck position throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Bracing your core will help you maintain a rigid torso position as you subsequently move about your hips rather than your back.
2. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    2. Without thinking about “bending over,” your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the kettlebell when focusing on “sitting back.”
    3. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    4. Keep the kettlebell just in front of your legs and your feet fully planted on the ground throughout the set.
3. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees.
    1. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    2. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically hinge until their hands are at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    3. If you have the flexibility to hinge until the kettlebell contacts the ground, descend until you can tap the weight very lightly on the ground, but do not let the weight rest on the ground.
    4. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
4. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    2. When you have finished lifting the kettlebell, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips properly stacked under your shoulders, preventing excessive arching.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the kettlebell down.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. After finishing the last rep, set the kettlebell down in a deadlift motion by bending your hips and knees while keeping your back flat.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Select your desired load at each weight stack with the machine’s loading pins, and adjust the seat to a height that lets you rest your chest comfortably against the chest pad once you are seated.
    1. It is generally appropriate to situate the seat where your upper chest can be positioned slightly above the top of the chest pad, while your middle and lower chest contact the pad once you are seated.
    2. The proper seat height should let the exercise feel smooth and natural, so if you think your arm path feels somewhat awkward during the set, it is recommended to adjust the seat height. When using this machine for the first time, trying out different seat heights may be helpful to determine specifically which setting helps the row feel best to you based on your body proportions.
2. Adjust the thigh pad to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against it while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
3. While facing the chest pad and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handles with an underhand grip that points your palms facing backward.
4. Sit down on the seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad. Simultaneously let your elbows straighten as your arms raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    1. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), you may need to lower the height of the seat and thigh pad to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your shoulder blades down away from your ears. Simultaneously drive your elbows down and back in an arcing manner to pull the handles toward you.
    1. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows finishing slightly behind your torso.
    2. When focusing on driving your elbows down and back, they should naturally bend to the appropriate degree.
    3. Keep your torso upright against the chest pad throughout your set rather than leaning backward during the pull.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to lift upward and away from your torso as they progressively straighten to raise the handles in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blades move naturally as they elevate with your arms.
    2. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arms up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Face the middle of an EZ bar loaded with your desired weight, whether that be a fixed-weight EZ bar or an adjustable plate-loaded EZ bar
2. With a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, firmly grasp the bar at a point where the grips angle downward as they move out to the sides of the bar.
    1. This angled grip should result in your hands being mildly tilted toward the thumb-side of your forearms.
    2. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before this grip is accessible on the bar.
    3. A slightly outside shoulder-width grip works well for many lifters, but feel free to try out somewhat different grip widths to determine if any particular hand positions feel best to you as an individual.
3. Stand upright with the bar, and hold it just in front of your legs with straight elbows.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift the bar upward by driving your elbows upward and out to your sides through a range of motion that feels comfortable to your shoulders while bending your elbows.
    1. Your mobility will affect how high you are comfortably able to lift the bar, and there is no need to force your shoulders into a range that provokes any discomfort.
    2. While different individuals may be able to lift higher up and others not quite as high, people often can lift their upper arms until their elbows are near shoulder height for a comfortable and effective range of motion.
    3. The bar should remain close to your torso throughout the exercise as it moves vertically.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbows to descend in toward your sides as you lower the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position with straight elbows.
    1. There is no need to lower the bar with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control the descent rather than letting the weight abruptly pull your arms down.
    2. At the end of the lowering phase, the bar should be situated in front of your thighs.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt, upper trap, and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine and adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad.
    1. It is generally appropriate to situate the seat where your upper chest can be positioned slightly above the top of the chest pad, while your middle and lower chest contact the pad once you are seated.
    2. The proper seat height should let the exercise feel smooth and natural, so if you think your arm path feels somewhat awkward during the row, it is recommended to adjust the seat height. When using this machine for the first time, trying out different seat heights may be helpful to determine specifically which setting helps the row feel best to you based on your body proportions and the machine model you are using.
    3. To row through your full range of motion, ideally place the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with your elbows fully straight.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, set it to a position that lets you grasp the handles with straight elbows only when your shoulders are fully rounded forward.
    1. Setting the chest further forward (toward the base of the machine) than this point can limit the row’s range of motion.
3. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet resting on the foot bar or the floor.
4. Reach fully forward and firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows and your palms angled upward.
    1. It may be easiest to grasp the handles one at a time.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward the sides of your torso by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked fairly close to your sides with this machine rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides.
    2. Keep your chest in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner, progressively straightening until they are outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you train them more effectively.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Select your weight with the loading pin(s), and sit down facing the dual cable row machine with a D-handle connected to each cable.
    1. Some machines will have a single weight stack that provides resistance to each cable, while others will have one weight stack for each cable.
2. Position your feet in contact with the machine’s foot plates.
    1. Allow your knees to bend to an angle that allows you to feel stable and comfortable, which can be a mild to moderately mild knee bend for many people.
    2. With greater knee bend, you will be able to sit closer toward the cables, so you can bend your knees further if you initially cannot reach the handles when leaning forward by bending at your hips.
3. Lean forward at your hips until you are able to grasp the handles firmly with straight elbows.
4. Lean your torso backward at your hips until you are sitting with an upright torso as your arms are aligned with the cables and your palms are angled in toward each other.
    1. When seated upright with straight elbows, you should feel tension from the cables, so if the cables are still slack, you can scoot yourself backward by extending your knees until you feel the resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward the sides of your torso by driving your elbows backward while pulling your shoulder blades together into a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Keep your elbows tucked close to your sides, rather than flaring them out away from your sides.
    2. Keep your forearms aligned with the cables, which naturally promotes the appropriate degree of elbow bend. This will let you draw a straight line from your elbows through your wrists to the cables’ pulleys.
    3. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position throughout the set as you keep your torso upright.
    4. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows finishing slightly behind your torso.
    5. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten to move the handles forward.
    1. Finish with your arms fully outstretched in front of you.
    2. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, without rounding your lower back. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you to train them more effectively.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine to a setting that will align the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are standing on the foot plate and slightly leaning forward at your hips.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle on some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel the most comfortable and natural.
    3. You can similarly use a dedicated standing pec fly machine or a multi flight machine.
    4. When using a multi flight, you can adjust the height of the pivot points, but a dedicated standing pec fly may let you adjust its foot plate height.
    5. Certain machine models may lack any adjustable features. If you are using one and notice that your shoulders are clearly below its pivot points when standing on its foot plate, you can elevate yourself by placing a step (or thick bumper plate) on the foot plate.
2. If you are using a multi flight with adjustable handles, set the handles near shoulder height.
    1. Setting the handles near shoulder height is an appropriate starting point for many lifters, but you can set the handles somewhat higher if your shoulders feel comfortable when training through that range.
    2. Alternatively, some people with pre-existing shoulder issues may feel more comfortable setting the handles a bit lower than shoulder height.
    3. Note that some multi flights will not let you set their handles higher than the point at which their lever arms are parallel to the ground.
3. Select your desired load with the machine’s loading pin, and stand centered on the foot plate facing toward the machine with the handles slightly in front of your torso (rather than situating your torso directly between the handles).
4. With your elbows slightly bent, firmly grasp the handles with your palms facing down.
    1. Based on how high up the handles are situated relative to your shoulders, it may be easiest to grasp one handle at a time and angle your torso as needed to get into the starting position comfortably.
    2. For instance, if you are using a dedicated pec fly machine with handles starting too high to reach simultaneously, you can turn your torso toward one side, grasp that handle, pull the handle down closer to your side, and repeat the process before finishing in a symmetrical position with your arms raised to a height comfortable for your shoulders.
5. While keeping your chest up, optionally lean your torso slightly forward at your hips by sitting your buttocks slightly backward with a slight knee bend.
    1. Avoid letting your shoulders round forward into a slumped position or shrugging your shoulders excessively up toward your ears.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows slightly bent, sweep your arms downward and inward to drive the handles together until they are close to touching in front of you.
    1. While you should not hunch over with your shoulders slouched forward, aim to let your shoulder blades move naturally.
    2. Keep your torso and lower body stationary throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your arms spread apart as they arc upward in a controlled manner through the full range of motion comfortable for your shoulders.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows slightly bent as the movement comes from shoulder motion rather than elbow motion.
    2. At the end of this movement, it is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest, but you should not move into any position that causes shoulder discomfort.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the machine abruptly swing your arms apart.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with an emphasis on the middle and lower regions.## Setting up
1. Select your weight with the loading pin(s), and sit down facing the dual cable row machine with a D-handle connected to each cable.
    1. Some machines will have a single weight stack that provides resistance to each cable, while others will have one weight stack for each cable.
2. Position your feet in contact with the machine’s foot plates.
    1. Allow your knees to bend to an angle that allows you to feel stable and comfortable, which can be a mild to moderately mild knee bend for many people.
    2. With greater knee bend, you will be able to sit closer toward the cables, so you can bend your knees further if you initially cannot reach the handles when leaning forward by bending at your hips.
3. Lean forward at your hips until you are able to grasp the handles firmly with straight elbows.
4. Lean your torso backward at your hips until you are sitting with an upright torso as your arms are aligned with the cables and your palms are angled down.
    1. When seated upright with straight elbows, you should feel tension from the cables, so if the cables are still slack, you can scoot yourself backward by extending your knees until you feel the resistance.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward you by driving your elbows backward and out away from your sides while pulling your shoulder blades together into a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Aim to flare your elbows about 45 degrees away from your sides as you move them equally backward and out to the side. Zero degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows purely backward and keeping them completely tucked by your sides, while 90 degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbows straight out to the sides, which would result in your upper arms and torso forming a “T-shape” at the top.
    2. Keep your forearms aligned with the cables, which naturally promotes the appropriate degree of elbow bend. This will let you draw a straight line from your elbows through your wrists to the cables’ pulleys.
    3. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your knees, hips, and lower back should remain nearly stationary in a stable position throughout the set as you keep your torso upright.
    4. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows finishing slightly behind your torso.
    5. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten to move the handles forward.
    1. Finish with your arms fully outstretched in front of you.
    2. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, without rounding your lower back. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you to train them more effectively.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and step up onto the footplates with the outer aspects of your knees contacting the pads.
    1. Based on your preference, you can point your feet straight ahead or angle them slightly out to the sides.
2. Grasp the handles at a height that feels comfortable and stable for you to hold.
3. While keeping your shins vertical with your knees against the pads, sit your hips back and down while bending your knees in a half-squat motion.
    1. People commonly descend until their thighs and torso form reasonably close to a 90-degree angle when viewed from the side, but you can try out different depths to determine what personally feels best for you, since different positions can be viable for this exercise.
    2. Lifters often naturally lean their torso mildly forward at their hips when squatting down, but it is up to you how upright or leaned forward you keep your torso during this exercise.
    3. Rather than excessively arching or rounding your back, keep it near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your knees outward to spread your legs apart through your hips’ available range of motion.
    1. How far apart you can spread your legs will be affected by your hip mobility.
    2. When viewed from the side, you should maintain a similar degree of hip and knee bend throughout each rep.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your legs to move in toward each other in a controlled manner.
    1. After doing so, you should now be in the starting position with your legs as close together as the machine permits while keeping your knees snugly against the pads.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should be in clear control of the weight, rather than letting it abruptly swing your legs inward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, you can stand upright before stepping off the footplates.
## Description
While also training several smaller muscles on the side of your hip, this exercise can dynamically train your glutes, likely with an emphasis on the upper region of your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the machine’s thigh pad to a level that positions the tops of your hip bones somewhat above the top of the pad when you set up with your feet on the foot plate.
    1. This position will allow you to move freely about your hips in contrast to if the pad is contacting the top of your hip bones, which would restrict hip motion and prompt you to perform the exercise via spinal motion.
    2. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones (rather than only somewhat lower), some individuals may feel knee discomfort, so a sweet spot can exist for pad placement.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position after initially completing step 4.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. While placing your hands onto the thigh pad or handles for balance support, step onto the foot plate and position your lower calves snugly against the lower leg pads with your heels on the back of the foot plate.
    1. Position your feet symmetrically in a stance that feels comfortable to you, which can involve pointing your toes somewhat outward or pointing them fairly straight forward.
    2. Situating your feet approximately hip width to shoulder apart will result in a viable stance width, but how wide of a stance you can use will be influenced by the width of your machine’s foot plate.
    3. To set your lower calves against the pads, your heels may hang off the edge of the foot plate, but the rest of your soles should be firmly planted on the foot plate.
    4. Your knees should be straight after you have finished setting your feet on the foot plate.
4. With your knees straightened, snugly rest your thighs against the thigh pad, let your arms hang down, and confirm that the tops of your hip bones are now somewhat above the top of the pad.
    1. If the pad is not positioned appropriately, readjust before getting into this starting position again.
## Exercise technique
1. Hinge at your hips to lower your torso until you are low enough to grasp the bar firmly with your elbows straight, palms facing backward, and hands about shoulder-width apart.
    1. Keep your lower back flat or slightly arched, rather than rounding your lower back to reach the bar.
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Maintain a straight knee position throughout your set, but avoid intentionally locking your knees out by squeezing your quads.
    4. Depending on your limb lengths and the height of your machine, you may have to round your upper back to reach the bar.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
3. While keeping your back in a rigid position, lift your torso up through your hips until you can fully squeeze your glutes with your torso aligned with your legs.
    1. Throughout your set, aim to have all the movement occur from hip motion.
    2. At the top, you should essentially be able to draw a straight line from your knees through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side, like you could do when standing upright.
    3. Avoid arching your lower back excessively at the top, which can occur if you try to lift up as high as you physically can, rather than finishing once your glutes are fully squeezed and your torso is in alignment with your legs.
4. Reverse the movement to lower your torso by hinging at your hips through your full range of motion in a controlled manner without rounding your lower back.
    1. How deeply you are able to hinge without rounding your lower back will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch, you have finished descending through your available hip range of motion.
    3. With sufficient flexibility, you may be able to lower the bar until the weight you selected lightly taps the rest of the weight stack, indicating you have moved the full range of motion in which the machine can provide resistance.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar back down and dismount the machine.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, and step up onto the footplates with the outer aspects of your knees contacting the pads.
    1. Based on your preference, you can point your feet straight ahead or angle them slightly out to the sides.
2. Grasp the handles at a height that feels comfortable and stable for you to hold.
3. While keeping your shins vertical with your knees against the pads, sit your hips back and down while bending your knees in a half-squat motion.
    1. People commonly descend until their thighs and torso form reasonably close to a 90-degree angle when viewed from the side, but you can try out different depths to determine what personally feels best for you, since different positions can be viable for this exercise.
    2. Lifters often naturally lean their torso mildly forward at their hips when squatting down, but it is up to you how upright or leaned forward you keep your torso during this exercise.
    3. Rather than excessively arching or rounding your back, keep it near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your knees outward to spread your legs apart through your hips’ available range of motion.
    1. How far apart you can spread your legs will be affected by your hip mobility.
    2. When viewed from the side, you should maintain a similar degree of hip and knee bend throughout each rep.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your legs to move in toward each other in a controlled manner.
    1. After doing so, you should now be in the starting position with your legs as close together as the machine permits while keeping your knees snugly against the pads.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should be in clear control of the weight, rather than letting it abruptly swing your legs inward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, you can stand upright before stepping off the footplates.
## Description
While also training several smaller muscles on the side of your hip, this exercise can dynamically train your glutes, likely with an emphasis on the upper region of your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
You have the option of performing this exercise while standing on a step and keeping both knees straight or standing on the ground and mildly bending one knee to keep that side’s foot off the ground. You can use essentially the same technique to perform the exercise with either approach, but their setups differ. Correspondingly, each approach receives its own setup section, but they share the same exercise technique instructions.

### **Setting up on a step**

1. Position what you will use as you step (e.g., aerobic step, thick bumper plate, short plyometric box) or approach the bottom step of a staircase.
    1. If you are using a mobile step, you can position it next to something where you can place a hand for balance assistance while standing on the step if you wish to receive balance assistance. This may involve setting up next to a rack, wall, chair, or exercise bench positioned upright.
    2. A step height of a few inches (about 8-10cm) is sufficiently tall for most people performing this exercise, since it involves a low amplitude of motion. This means that an aerobic step without risers or a 45lb (20kg) bumper plate is normally sufficiently tall, but a taller step naturally can be used as well.
2. Pick up a dumbbell, kettlebell, or weight plate and hold it by the side of your body that you will not train during this set. Alternatively, strap an ankle weight around the ankle on the side of your body that you will not train during this set.
    1. When using a dumbbell, kettlebell, or weight plate, you will hold it with your arm straight by your side throughout the exercise.
3. Stand directly to the side of the step, and place your foot (that is opposite to the weight) fully onto the step while leaving your other foot on the ground. Situate your feet about hip-width apart, and point your toes straight ahead.
4. Optionally place your free hand against a nearby surface for balance assistance. Otherwise, keep your free hand in your preferred position throughout the exercise, such as resting on your hip or by your side.
5. Drive through your elevated leg and straighten your knee to step up until your feet are level, keeping your one foot hanging off the step.
6. On the side hovering off the step, pull your toes and the ball of your foot up toward your shin through an ankle motion that brings your heel closer to the ground than the ball of your foot.
    1. This is the opposite of the ankle motion that occurs when lifting your heels up during a calf raise, and you should feel the muscles on the front of your shin contract.
    2. The purpose of keeping your foot in this position is to make sure that your toes do not contact the ground during the exercise and restrict your range of motion. If your step is tall enough that your free foot would not touch the ground in any position during the exercise, you can ignore this step and let the ball of your foot hang down.

### **Setting up on the ground**

1. Pick up a dumbbell, kettlebell, or weight plate and hold it by the side of your body that you will not train during this set. Alternatively, strap an ankle weight around the ankle on the side of your body that you will not train during this set.
    1. When using a dumbbell, kettlebell, or weight plate, you will hold it with your arm straight by your side throughout the exercise.
2. In a hip-width stance, stand next to something where you can place a hand for balance assistance if you wish to receive balance assistance.
3. Optionally place your free hand against a nearby surface for balance assistance. Otherwise, keep your free hand in your preferred position throughout the exercise, such as resting on your hip or by your side.
4. Bend one knee enough to lift that side’s foot off the ground, and keep that knee bent with your foot off the ground throughout the exercise.
    1. You will be training your hip muscles on the side of your body on which you keep your foot on the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your torso upright and your stance leg straight, lower your free foot toward the ground by letting your hip bone (on the side of your free foot) tilt down in a controlled manner.
    1. Descend until you feel that your hip bones are fully tilted, which may prompt you to feel a mild stretching sensation in the side of your hip (of the hip bone whose leg remains on the step).
    2. As a cue, you can imagine that you have a basin of water on top of your hip bones, and you are trying to pour as much of the water as you can out to one side.
    3. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should be using your hip muscles to control the descent actively, rather than just letting your hip bone abruptly drop.
2. Reverse the movement by hiking your hip up to lift your free foot up and tilt your hip bones in the opposite direction.
    1. Continue keeping your torso upright and your legs straight as you exclusively move at your hip.
    2. Strive to finish with your free leg’s hip bone higher up than your stance leg’s hip bone.
    3. When imagining that you have a basin of water on top of your hip bones, you can think about leveling the basin before trying to spill the water toward the side of your stance leg.
    4. With the appropriate technique, you should feel tension in the muscles on the side of your stance leg’s hip as they contract.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat this exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the musculature on the side of your hip, including your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia latae.## Setting up
You have the option of performing this exercise with an ankle weight or a dumbbell. You can use the same exercise technique with either form of resistance, but the setup details can differ depending on which type of weight you select. Correspondingly, each approach receives its own setup section, but they share the same exercise technique instructions.

### **Setting up with an ankle weight**

1. Strap an ankle weight around the ankle on the side of your body that you will train during this set.
2. Position yourself on your palms, knees, and toes, with your elbows straight and your knees bent by about 90 degrees.
    1. In this “on all fours” position, your upper arms and thighs should be directed straight down such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    2. Your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position with the natural curvature you have when normally standing upright, rather than being rounded or excessively arched.
    3. For comfort, you optionally can set up on an exercise mat.

### **Setting up with a dumbbell**

1. Set a dumbbell on the ground, and kneel next to it with the side of your body that you will train during this set closer to the dumbbell.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can set up on an exercise mat.
2. Place the dumbbell on the back of your knee crease with its handle resting between your upper calves and lower hamstrings.
    1. Keep the dumbbell in this position throughout the exercise.
    2. When you set up appropriately and use the correct technique for this exercise, the dumbbell should remain snugly in place.
3. Position yourself on your palms, knees, and toes, with your elbows straight and your knees bent by about 90 degrees.
    1. In this “on all fours” position, your upper arms and thighs should be directed straight down such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    2. Your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position with the natural curvature you have when normally standing upright, rather than being rounded or excessively arched.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. While keeping one knee on the ground, drive your working side’s knee backward and upward in an arcing manner while keeping it bent by about 90 degrees. Move through your hip’s full range of motion until you can squeeze your glute at the top.
    1. Strive to maintain the natural curvature of your lower back as you avoid excessively arching or twisting it. Excessive lower back arching can occur if you try to kick your leg up as high as you possibly can, rather than finishing the movement once you have reached the end of your hip’s range of motion.
    2. Being able to squeeze your glute fully is a sign that you have reached an appropriate end range. You can optionally pause in this top position briefly and hold the full glute squeeze for a moment if you wish to do so.
    3. How high up you can lift your leg through hip motion will be affected by your individual hip mobility, but people can commonly reach the point at which the thigh is approximately parallel to the ground.
    4. While motion occurs at your hip, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the exercise.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your leg to the starting position, where your thighs are aligned with each other.
    1. There is no need to lower your leg with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control the descent, rather than letting your leg abruptly swing down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your piriformis, adductor magnus, and hamstrings to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Position a short looped resistance band around both of your lower thighs.
    1. It is recommended to first inspect the band to confirm it does not have any small tears, since a small tear can swiftly become a full tear when stretching a band.
2. Kneel onto the ground, and adjust the band so that you are directly kneeling on it with the leg you will not train during this set. Keep the band looped around your lower thigh (just above the knee) on the side you will be training.
    1. For comfort, you optionally can set up on an exercise mat.
    2. This exercise can alternatively be performed with the band looped around both of your lower thighs, but kneeling on it with your non-working leg can help anchor the band and prevent it from inadvertently rolling up your thighs during the exercise.
3. Position yourself on your palms, knees, and toes, with your elbows straight and your knees bent by about 90 degrees as they are situated hip-width apart.
    1. In this “on all fours” position, your upper arms and thighs should be directed straight down such that they are approximately perpendicular to the ground.
    2. Your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position with the natural curvature you have when normally standing upright, rather than being rounded or excessively arched.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. While keeping your knee that is resting on the band grounded, drive your other knee backward and upward in an arcing manner while keeping it bent by about 90 degrees. Move through your hip’s full range of motion until you can squeeze your glute at the top.
    1. Strive to maintain the natural curvature of your lower back as you avoid excessively arching or twisting it. Excessive lower back arching can occur if you try to kick your leg up as high as you possibly can, rather than finishing the movement once you have reached the end of your hip’s range of motion.
    2. Being able to squeeze your glute fully is a sign that you have reached an appropriate end range. You can optionally pause in this top position briefly and hold the full glute squeeze for a moment if you wish to do so.
    3. How high up you can lift your leg through hip motion will be affected by your individual hip mobility, but people can commonly reach the point at which the thigh is approximately parallel to the ground.
    4. Aim to keep your knees hip-width apart as you avoid letting the band pull your moving leg in toward your stationary leg.
    5. While motion occurs at your hip, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout the exercise.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by lowering your leg to the starting position, where your thighs are aligned with each other.
    1. There is no need to lower your leg with an extremely slow tempo, but you should actively control the descent, rather than letting your leg abruptly swing down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
