## Description
This exercise will chiefly train your gluteus maximus, which may be assisted by your piriformis, adductor magnus, and hamstrings to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Pick up one kettlebell, hold it by your side, and stand facing a sturdy object reasonably near knee-height, such as a flat exercise bench.
    1. If you are tall, the head of an incline bench set to a low incline may be better for you to brace your hand against during this exercise, compared to a lower flat bench.
    2. Viable alternatives to a bench can include a plyometric box, sturdy chair, kettlebell rack, racked Smith machine bar, or a barbell on a rack’s J-cups.
    3. The appropriate object should let you achieve a position that feels stable and comfortable with a flat back once you brace against it.
    4. While using an object reasonably near knee height can let you set up effectively and is most common, some lifters may prefer to use a higher object closer to hip height, which can let you keep the non-working arm higher during the exercise. Using a taller object may also be sensible for people with mobility limitations who cannot comfortably support themselves on a shorter object without rounding their backs.
    5. For brevity, the instructions will refer to the use of a bench from this point on.
2. While standing with the head of the bench in front of your free arm, situate your feet near shoulder-width apart.
    1. When aligning your free arm with one end of the bench, you should have space in front of your arm holding the kettlebell, so you will not have to worry about the bench getting in the way of the kettlebell after you have assumed the starting position for this row.
    2. A near shoulder-width stance is an appropriate starting point, but feel free to widen your stance a bit if that feels more stable to you.
3. Based on your preference, you can adopt a staggered stance by taking a step backward with the foot on the same side as the kettlebell, or you can keep your feet symmetrical.
    1. Either stance can be effectively used for this exercise, so you can try out a set or two with each option and continue with the position where you feel most stable.
4. Lean your torso forward by sitting your hips back while allowing your knees to bend mildly until you can brace your free hand against the bench with a straight elbow.
    1. Keep your lower back flat, and let your arm (that is holding the kettlebell) hang down toward the ground.
    2. When standing appropriately close to the bench, your non-working side’s arm should ideally be angled downward to a greater degree than forward, so that you are not reaching excessively far forward.
    3. Keep your hand braced against the bench throughout the exercise.
    4. All the subsequent directions apply to the arm holding the kettlebell.
5. Angle your palm diagonally backward and in toward the opposite side of your body.
    1. Rather than just rotating your palm as your elbow and upper arm remain still, rotate your whole arm through shoulder motion, resulting in the bony bump on the back of your elbow pointing diagonally outward and backward. This is the direction in which you will row.
    2. Throughout your set, maintain this grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip and a neutral grip.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Drive your elbow in an arcing motion backward, upward, and moderately out away from your side to row the kettlebell. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade backward.
    1. Aim to flare your elbow about 45 degrees out away from your side as your elbow moves equal parts backward and out to the side. Zero degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbow purely backward and keeping it completely tucked by your side, while 90 degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbow straight out to the side, which would result in your upper arm and torso forming half of a “T-shape” at the top.
    2. When bending your elbow by the appropriate degree, your forearm should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    3. Row through your full range of motion, which should result in your elbow finishing slightly behind your torso or at least in alignment with your torso at the top of the rep.
    4. Strive to keep your lower body and torso in a fixed position with your chest facing a similar direction throughout this movement. A mild degree of torso rotation is not problematic, but avoid letting it become excessive and turning the exercise into a cheat row.
    5. While performing this arcing motion, the kettlebell should not move straight upward in a vertical path, which could occur if you bend your elbow excessively.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the kettlebell in a controlled arcing motion until your arm is directed down toward the ground with your elbow straightened.
    1. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blade forward, which will stretch your upper back muscles and result in your working side’s shoulder being mildly closer to the ground than your other side’s shoulder at the bottom of the row.
    2. Move your elbow diagonally inward and forward at a near 45-degree angle while it descends to reverse the moderate elbow flare you just utilized during the lifting phase.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight rapidly pull your arm down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell, hold it by your side, and stand facing a sturdy object reasonably near knee-height, such as a flat exercise bench.
    1. If you are tall, the head of an incline bench set to a low incline may be better for you to brace your hand against during this exercise, compared to a lower flat bench.
    2. Viable alternatives to a bench can include a plyometric box, sturdy chair, dumbbell rack, racked Smith machine bar, or a barbell on a rack’s J-cups.
    3. The appropriate object should let you achieve a position that feels stable and comfortable with a flat back once you brace against it.
    4. While using an object reasonably near knee height can let you set up effectively and is most common, some lifters may prefer to use a higher object closer to hip height, which can let you keep the non-working arm higher during the exercise. Using a taller object may also be sensible for people with mobility limitations who cannot comfortably support themselves on a shorter object without rounding their backs.
    5. For brevity, the instructions will refer to the use of a bench from this point on.
2. While standing with the head of the bench in front of your free arm, situate your feet near shoulder-width apart.
    1. When aligning your free arm with one end of the bench, you should have space in front of your arm holding the dumbbell, so you will not have to worry about the bench getting in the way of the dumbbell after you have assumed the starting position for this row.
    2. A near shoulder-width stance is an appropriate starting point, but feel free to widen your stance a bit if that feels more stable to you.
3. Based on your preference, you can adopt a staggered stance by taking a step backward with the foot on the same side as the dumbbell, or you can keep your feet symmetrical.
    1. Either stance can be effectively used for this exercise, so you can try out a set or two with each option and continue with the position where you feel most stable.
4. Lean your torso forward by sitting your hips back while allowing your knees to bend mildly until you can brace your free hand against the bench with a straight elbow.
    1. Keep your lower back flat, and let your arm (that is holding the dumbbell) hang down toward the ground.
    2. When standing appropriately close to the bench, your non-working side’s arm should ideally be angled downward to a greater degree than forward, so that you are not reaching excessively far forward.
    3. Keep your hand braced against the bench throughout the exercise.
    4. All the subsequent directions apply to the arm holding the dumbbell.
5. Angle your palm diagonally backward and in toward the opposite side of your body.
    1. Rather than just rotating your palm as your elbow and upper arm remain still, rotate your whole arm through shoulder motion, resulting in the bony bump on the back of your elbow pointing diagonally outward and backward. This is the direction in which you will row.
    2. Throughout your set, maintain this grip, which is a hybrid between an overhand grip and a neutral grip.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. Drive your elbow in an arcing motion backward, upward, and moderately out away from your side to row the dumbbell. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade backward.
    1. Aim to flare your elbow about 45 degrees out away from your side as your elbow moves equal parts backward and out to the side. Zero degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbow purely backward and keeping it completely tucked by your side, while 90 degrees of flare would mean arcing your elbow straight out to the side, which would result in your upper arm and torso forming half of a “T-shape” at the top.
    2. When bending your elbow by the appropriate degree, your forearm should remain fairly close to being perpendicular to the ground throughout the row.
    3. Row through your full range of motion, which should result in your elbow finishing slightly behind your torso or at least in alignment with your torso at the top of the rep.
    4. Strive to keep your lower body and torso in a fixed position with your chest facing a similar direction throughout this movement. A mild degree of torso rotation is not problematic, but avoid letting it become excessive and turning the exercise into a cheat row.
    5. While performing this arcing motion, the dumbbell should not move straight upward in a vertical path, which could occur if you bend your elbow excessively.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled arcing motion until your arm is directed down toward the ground with your elbow straightened.
    1. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blade forward, which will stretch your upper back muscles and result in your working side’s shoulder being mildly closer to the ground than your other side’s shoulder at the bottom of the row.
    2. Move your elbow diagonally inward and forward at a near 45-degree angle while it descends to reverse the moderate elbow flare you just utilized during the lifting phase.
    3. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight rapidly pull your arm down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
Using a dedicated seal row bench is ideal for this exercise due to its convenience and its ability to help you train through a full range of motion, but you can alternatively take the time to elevate a standard flat bench without a seal row bench. Each approach is described in its own setup section, but the exercise technique can be essentially the same with either technique, so they share the technique section.

### **Setting up with a seal row bench**

1. Adjust the height of the pad or the bar holders to a setting that lets you grasp the bar with straight or slightly bent elbows once you are lying down on the pad.
    1. Which part of the bench can be adjusted varies based on the model you are using, and certain models may lack any adjustable features.
    2. Some trial and error may initially be needed to find the setting that makes it easy for you to unrack and rerack the bar.
2. Place a cambered bar onto the bar holders and load it with weight plates that will not touch the ground when your arms are hanging down with straight elbows when holding the bar in the starting position of your seal row.
    1. You can similarly use a cambered bench/row bar or a dedicated seal row bar for this exercise. With either type of bar, position it so the cambered part is situated lower than where you will grasp the bar to provide sufficient clearance between the bench and the bar during your row.
    2. The diameter of the weight plates added to the bar is often irrelevant with an appropriately adjusted bench.
    3. However, for some people (particularly those with longer arms using non-adjustable benches), adding full diameter plates (such as 45lb (20kg) or any bumper plates) may cause the plates to contact the ground before the elbows can fully straighten during a rep. If this is the case for you, you exclusively load the bar with smaller diameter plates that will not restrict your row’s range of motion.
3. Lie down with the front of your torso flat on the bench as your neck is situated vertically above the bar.
    1. Position your head in a comfortable position, such as tucking your chin and lifting your head slightly up to keep your face off the bench.
    2. Situating yourself in a spot where your neck is situated above the bar (instead of your shoulders) will help you avoid hitting the bar holders during your set.
    3. While some seal row benches have one continuous flat pad to support your torso and legs in alignment, others have a torso pad and a lower leg pad onto which you can kneel with your hips bent.
4. Reach down and firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms face backward.
5. Unrack the bar by lifting it up just above the bar holders before moving the bar slightly backward to position it directly under your shoulders. Let your arms hang with straight elbows as your shoulder blades are pulled apart from the bar’s weight.

### **Setting up without a seal row bench**

1. Symmetrically elevate each end of a flat bench on top of sturdy objects like aerobic steps with risers, bumper plates, plyometric boxes, or deadlift blocks. Elevate it to the level where you can just grasp a loaded barbell under you while lying face down on the bench
    1. A non-adjustable flat bench is generally preferable to an adjustable bench, since the former type of bench typically has more space under it for bar clearance.
    2. When elevating the bench, make sure that each base is level and that you stack the bench onto only objects that are able to handle the combined weight of you, your barbell, and the bench.
    3. To row through your longest range of motion, the bench should ideally be high enough that you can grasp the bar with straight elbows at the end of your setup. If you can grasp the bar with a bent elbow position, a greater degree of bench elevation would be preferable. Some trial and error may be needed to determine specifically how high a bench elevation is optimal for you.
2. Place a cambered bar centered under the bench near one of its ends, and load it with weight plates.
    1. You can similarly use a cambered bench/row bar or a dedicated seal row bar for this exercise. With either type of bar, position it so the cambered part is situated lower than where you will grasp the bar to provide sufficient clearance between the bench and the bar during your row.
    2. The barbell should be situated in a spot where you can reach it while the bench supports your torso and thighs, which will involve positioning the barbell close to one end of the bench.
    3. If you are using an adjustable bench, place the barbell under the part of the bench that has the most space available under it.
    4. The diameter of the largest plates you add to the bar will affect how high off the ground the barbell lies, so both the size of your plates and your bench’s height can affect your row’s range of motion and your ability to reach the bar initially.
3. Lie down with the front of your torso and thighs flat on the bench as your shoulders are positioned above the bar.
    1. Based on your preference, you can either keep your knees straight or bent.
    2. Position your head in a comfortable position, such as tucking your chin and lifting your head slightly up to keep your face off the bench.
4. Reach down and firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms face backward.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced from this point on.
2. Pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen/lower chest by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward while pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Rather than lifting your chest up through excessive lower back arching, keep your chest and the rest of your torso in contact with the bench throughout your set.
    2. Row through your full range of motion, which ideally results in your elbows finishing slightly above shoulder height, since the bar’s camber prevents the bench from limiting how high you can row.
    3. Rather than fully flaring your elbows straight out to the sides or keeping them fully tucked by your sides, let your elbows naturally flare out away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable to you.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. If your bench is tall enough to do so, lower the bar until your elbows have fully straightened.
    2. If the plates reach the ground before your elbows have fully straightened, you can finish lowering the bar once the plates lightly tap the ground.
    3. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades apart to stretch your upper back muscles.
    4. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight pull your arms down rapidly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. If you are using a seal row bench, you can rerack the bar following your last rep.
    2. If you are using a flat bench elevated on objects, you set the bar onto the ground once you have finished your set.
## Description
This exercise effectively trains much of the musculature of your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. If your machine lets you adjust the angle of its backrest, fully recline it.
    1. A variety of machines have a backrest that is permanently reclined.
    2. If your machine has its backrest permanently fixed into an upright or semi-upright position, you can perform the pin-loaded leg press from the exercise library, since this horizontal hack squat specifically uses a machine with a fully reclined backrest.
2. Adjust the sled to a distance away from the foot platform that allows you to squat through a full range of motion without rounding your lower back.
3. Position the sled to the setting closest to the foot platform that lets you keep your feet fully planted on the platform without rounding your lower back when you are set up in this exercise’s starting position.
    1. Finding the ideal setting for you as an individual typically takes some trial and error, since you will not know if the sled is too close or too far back until you assume the starting position with your feet on the platform.
    2. If the sled is excessively close to the platform relative to your mobility, your lower back may have to round to place your feet on the platform, or you may not be able to place your heels firmly in contact with the platform
4. Select your weight with the machine’s loading pin.
5. Lie down with your shoulders snugly against the shoulder pads and your back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Based on your preference and your machine’s design, you can decide to rest your head against the pad or keep it just off the pad. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
6. Place your feet fully on the platform, near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward. Simultaneously check whether you selected the appropriate sled setting for you and adjust it if initially you did not.
    1. It is generally appropriate to set your feet near the middle of the platform or on the lower half of the platform, but the specific foot placement that may feel best to you can vary based on your mobility and which machine model you are using.
    2. The ideal degree stance width and degree of toe flare will also vary among different individuals, so trying out somewhat different foot positions can often help you find out which one is personally best for you.
    3. Wherever you place your feet should let you plant your heels (along with the rest of your feet) firmly on the platform and allow the exercise to feel comfortable when training through the full range of motion your mobility permits.
## Exercise technique
1. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout the exercise.
3. Drive your feet into the platform to push yourself away from it as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees during this exercise, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, aim to push evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your back and the back of your hip bones snugly up against the backrest.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
4. Reverse the movement by bending at your knees and hips in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your feet fully on the platform, knees aligned over your feet, and the back of your hip bones against the back rest.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time. A sign of an appropriately controlled movement is having the weights you selected lightly contact the rest of the weight stack at the end range. Having the weights lightly touch down will also confirm that you have moved through your full range of motion.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your gluteus maximus and multiple hip adductor muscles, this exercise primarily targets your quads.## Setting up
1. Pick up one dumbbell and place it on the ground right next to the long side of a flat exercise bench (or similar object on which you can sit, such as a plyometric or a couch if training at home).
2. Sit down on the bench with your legs spread wide apart and the dumbbell in between your legs as your feet are on the floor.
3. Lean forward at your hips to grasp the dumbbell firmly with the side you are about to train.
    1. For additional stability, you can place your free hand on top of your thigh (on the same side as your free hand) and keep it there throughout your set.
    2. All the subsequent directions will apply to the side in which you are holding the dumbbell since that is the arm you will be training first.
4. Lean your torso slightly back toward an upright position until the dumbbell lifts off the ground with your elbow fully straightened.
5. Position the back of your elbow against the inside of your thigh while holding the dumbbell with a hammer grip that directs your palm back toward you.
    1. In this starting position, your entire arm should be directed straight down toward the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow pinned against your inner thigh, curl the dumbbell upward in an arcing motion by fully bending your elbow.
    1. While your one forearm moves through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
    2. Maintain your hammer grip as you avoid rotating your palm.
2. After your elbow has fully bent, reverse the motion by lowering the dumbbell in a controlled manner until your elbow has returned to a fully straightened position with your forearm directed down toward the ground.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow braced against your inner thigh.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weight rapidly swing your forearm down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other arm.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. While standing under a pull-up bar, reach up overhead and firmly grasp it with your palms facing forward (i.e., an overhand grip) and your hands situated near shoulder-width apart or somewhat wider.
    1. Ideally use a bar that is high enough to allow you to hang from it with straight legs without your feet touching the ground.
    2. If you cannot reach the bar while standing on the ground and lifting your heels up, you can initially step up on a sturdy box, aerobic step, or bumper plate to place the bar within your reach.
    3. While not required, elevating yourself to reach the bar may help you set up with a more stable hand position around the bar compared to if you jumped up from the ground to reach the bar.
    4. If you use an object as a step to reach the bar, position that object a little behind the bar so that your feet do not touch that object when you are hanging from the bar with straight legs.
    5. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    6. If you situate the bar closer toward the middle of your palms (rather than the bases of your fingers), the bar can slide upward during the dead hang, which pulls on your hand skin in a manner that increases the risk of tearing a callus.
2. With your elbows straight and your hands firmly grasping the bar, lift your feet off the ground to begin hanging on the bar. Keep your legs close together and let them hang down toward the ground with straight knees.
    1. While hanging, you should be able to draw a straight line vertically down from your torso through your legs.
    2. If your bar is not high enough to keep your feet off the ground when your legs are directed straight down toward the ground, bend your knees by the minimum needed to lift your feet just off the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbows straight, pull your shoulder blades down to move your shoulders down away from your ears and lift your chest slightly up.
    1. While you do not need to have your shoulder blades fully packed down, it is recommended to avoid letting your torso sag and leave you in a shrugged-shoulder position, which will occur if you keep your mid-back muscles relaxed.
2. Lift your legs up and toward your torso while curling your hips forward until you can touch the bar with your toes. Only let your knees bend to the minimal degree needed for your toes to reach the bar.
    1. Toward the top of the movement, it is normal to lean the torso slightly back, which will move your head slightly behind your arms rather than staying fully between your arms (when viewed from the side). However, this slight lean should not become excessive and turn into a swinging motion.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows straight, legs together, and shoulder blades pulled down throughout the exercise. With your legs together, you will touch close to the center of the bar between your hands.
    3. If you keep your knees locked in a fully straightened position throughout the movement, your hamstring flexibility can limit how high you can lift your legs, so a soft knee bend is typically required. For the strict version of this variation, avoid bending your knees more noticeably during the first half of the movement and then straightening them in a kicking motion to reach the bar at the end.
    4. Strive to keep your knees completely straight during the initial portion of the movement and allow them to bend only as needed while progressively lifting your legs, since the hamstrings progressively lengthen as the thighs lift higher up (through hip flexion).
    5. Curling your hips forward will round your lower back and result in your buttocks facing forward at the end of the lifting phase.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by allowing your legs to descend while uncurling your lower back until you have returned to the starting position with your legs directed straight down toward the ground.
    1. Actively lower your legs throughout the descent rather than letting gravity rapidly pull you back down, which could result in your legs swinging back behind the starting position as momentum takes over.
    2. As you descend, your knees can progressively transition from a mildly bent position to a fully straightened position.
    3. In the bottom position, your wrists, shoulders, hips, knees, and ankles should be vertically aligned when viewed from the side.
4. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to return your feet to the ground.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rectus abdominis and hip flexors, which are assisted by your obliques.## Setting up
1. Adjust the Smith machine’s bar to near mid-chest height.
    1. The optimal starting height should let you position the bar on your upper back and comfortably unrack it without lifting your heels up.
2. Position a calf raise block, aerobic step, or similarly sturdy elevated object on the ground with its rear edge centered under the bar.
    1. A deadlift block, thick bumper plate, or slant board may also be used.
    2. Whichever object you use as your step should fully be able to support the combined weight of you and the bar, not slide or tip when standing on its edge, and let your heels descend to the point where you feel a full stretch in your calves.
    3. If you are new to this exercise, it is initially recommended to perform a couple of reps with the unloaded bar to confirm that you positioned your step appropriately and feel sufficiently stable with your setup.
3. Add your desired weight to the bar and position the safety stops (that are attached to the sliding rails) so that they are just below the height of the bar.
4. While facing the center of the bar with your feet hip-width apart and toes pointing straight forward, grasp the bar with a somewhat wider than shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. Continue holding the bar from now on.
    2. The proper grip width will vary from person to person, and what feels best to you will be influenced by your shoulder mobility.
5. Shallowly bend at your knees and hips to walk under the bar, step onto the step with the balls of your feet close to its back edge, and situate the bar on your upper back in a comfortable position.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    2. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    3. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    4. While positioning the bar on your upper back, you may have to readjust where you are grasping the bar if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
## Exercise technique
1. Un-rack the bar by standing fully upright as you straighten your knees
2. After the bar is resting on your upper back rather than the safety bars, rotate the safety hooks out away from the safety bars through a wrist flick motion.
    1. Throughout the exercise, keep your chest upright and maintain the natural curvature of your back, like when you are normally standing upright.
3. Push through the balls of your feet to raise your heels fully upward while keeping your knees straight and your torso upright.
    1. Once you have lifted your heels as high upward as your ankle mobility permits, you have finished the lifting phase.
4. While keeping the balls of your feet planted on the step, let your heels descend toward the ground in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits.
    1. Strive to lower your heels as far as you can below the balls of your feet to stretch your calves and train them the most effectively.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the bottom, you likely are not descending through your full range of motion.
    3. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid quickly dropping down and trying to bounce out of the bottom into the next rep.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.
6. To rerack the bar, rotate the safety hooks toward the safety bars, and lower the bar until it is fully resting on the safety bars.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop both your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
To set up for this exercise, you will be pressing the dumbbells overhead through a push press or strict overhead press without leg drive, so you can skip to the exercise technique instructions if you already know how to perform either of those movements. The setup directions describe how to perform a push press, since it will efficiently allow you to assume the overhead position with heavy weights, but you can alternatively press the weight up without leg drive if you have the strength to do so.

1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with approximately a shoulder-width to hip-width stance while holding the dumbbells at your sides with straight elbows as your palms face in toward your thighs and your toes point straight ahead.
    1. If you are using dumbbells that you consider fairly heavy, reaching forward from a bent-over position to pick up both dumbbells simultaneously from a rack may be awkward and challenging. For an easier setup, you can pick up one dumbbell, carry it, place it on a flat bench (or sturdy box), and move the other dumbbell onto the bench before picking up both dumbbells from a mechanically advantaged position. Initially, pick up the dumbbell with a deadlift or squat motion where you lift it by extending your hips and knees while keeping your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright (rather than meaningfully rounding your lower back while doing so).
2. Lift the dumbbells up near the front of your shoulders with your elbows bent by curling the weights and using momentum from your lower body as needed.
    1. You can perform a cheat curl-like motion to move the dumbbells into position if they are too heavy to lift without momentum. To do so, first sit your buttocks back while allowing your knees to bend mildly and simultaneously rocking the dumbbells mildly backward. Then drive your hips forcefully forward to generate momentum from your lower body while bending your elbows to curl the weights upward while swinging your elbows forward. The hip drive motion, which is similar to the lockout of a deadlift, will bring your torso into a vertical position with your knees straightened.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your shoulders with your forearms in a vertical position and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. Assume a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, strive to maintain the natural curvature of your lower back along with your neck.
4. In the starting position, your palms should be facing in toward each other as your upper arms are close to being parallel to each other in front of your body (as opposed to being flared out away from your sides).
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
6. Without moving your arms, mildly bend your knees in a quarter squat-like manner to dip down while maintaining an upright torso position with your chest up.
    1. Keep the soles of your feet fully in contact with the ground during this dip phase.
    2. Your knees should track straight forward toward your toes rather than caving inward.
7. Directly after reaching the bottom of your dip, forcibly push through your feet as you rapidly extend (straighten) your knees and hips to generate upward momentum.
    1. The intent of this leg drive phase should be akin to a jump without lifting your feet fully off the ground.
    2. At the end of this drive, you can either keep the soles of your feet fully on the ground, or you can allow your heels to lift up slightly off the ground while keeping the front of your feet firmly in contact with the ground if a slight heel lift feels natural to you.
8. Immediately after you have finished extending your legs, explosively push the dumbbells upward by forcibly driving your elbows upward as you swiftly straighten your elbows.
    1. At the end of the pressing phase, your knees, hips, and elbows should be fully straightened, and your arms should be directed straight up toward the ceiling with your elbows and dumbbells situated vertically over your shoulders.
    2. To transfer your leg drive into pushing the dumbbells upward in an explosive manner, you should perform steps 3 and 4 as a continuous movement.
    3. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your forearms should be in a vertical position throughout the rep as your wrists and dumbbells remain stacked over your elbows.
    4. Throughout the exercise, keep your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright, as you avoid excessively arching your lower back.
    5. Regardless of whether you lifted your heels up off the ground after extending your legs during the leg drive phase, your heels should be flat on the ground with the rest of your feet at the end of the pressing phase.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders fully up toward your ears while keeping your arms overhead with straight elbows.
    1. Keep your torso upright as you avoid leaning back through excessive lower back arching, which would prompt the bottom of your ribs to flare up from your hip bones. Continuing to brace your core can help avoid an unintentional backward lean.
    2. Throughout the exercise, look straight ahead to keep your neck near a neutral position.
    3. While you move the dumbbells through shoulder girdle motion, keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your shoulders fully descend away from your ears in a controlled manner as you keep your arms overhead.
    1. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting your shoulders drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to lower the dumbbells.
    1. Following your last rep, lower your elbows while bending them enough to keep your forearms vertical. You can shallowly bend at your knees while keeping your torso upright to help control the weights on their way down.
    2. After the dumbbells are by your shoulders with your palms facing each other, lower the dumbbells to your sides as you straighten your elbows and swing your elbows back by your sides.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps, as other shoulder girdle muscles assist in maintaining the overhead position.## Setting up
This overhead shrug starts with the bar in a fully overhead position, so the setup describes how to press the bar overhead through a wide grip overhead press or a push press. If you already know how to perform a standing barbell overhead press, you can skip the exercise technique instructions for details specific to the overhead shrug.

1. Position the J-hooks of a power rack (or squat stand) near the height of your upper chest.
    1. As an alternative to starting from a rack, you can begin with the barbell on the floor and power clean it into position. However, this page will describe how to set up from a rack.
2. Place a barbell onto the J-hooks and load it with your desired weight.
3. Stand in front of the middle of the bar with your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
4. Firmly grasp the bar with your hands moderately outside shoulder width as your palms are facing away from you.
    1. Using a wider grip than your standard overhead press often works well, but you can use a range of grip widths for this exercise, so feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine specifically where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip).
5. Slightly bend your knees, and step forward to situate your collarbone or upper chest directly under the bar. Simultaneously, position your elbows under the bar and just slightly in front of it. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists stacked under the bar.
    1. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid holding the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    2. Rather than being excessively arched, your lower back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Relative to when you overhead press with a closer grip, which normally involves a greater degree of elbow tuck (pointing forward), your elbows should naturally be flared out (pointing out to the side) to a greater extent during this wide grip variation.
6. Fully stand upright while holding the bar.
    1. With your knees straightened, the bar should now be above the tops of the J-hooks.
7. Take a couple of steps backward and set your feet approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable to you, you can point your toes straight ahead or rotate them slightly out to the side.
8. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap. Simultaneously, squeeze your glutes and quads.
    1. Continue bracing your core while keeping your quads and glutes tightened throughout your set.
9. While naturally allowing your lower back to arch mildly, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Avoid leaning back into an excessively arched position.
10. Tuck or tilt your chin back to move your head behind the bar.
    1. The goal is to move your head out of the bar’s way so that you can press straight upward without hitting your face with the bar.
11. Press the bar upward with as close to a straight bar path as you can perform.
    1. Do not lean back any further or excessively arch your lower back. Your lower back should stay locked in the same position throughout the exercise.
12. Immediately after the bar clears the top of your head, mildly push your head forward until your ears are close to being under the bar.
    1. Rather than pushing your head excessively forward, you primarily should be reversing the backward chin tuck/tilt you previously performed.
13. Finish the press with your elbows straightened as the bar is fully overhead.
    1. You should be able to draw a straight line down from the bar that passes through your shoulders, hips, knees, and the middle of your feet.
## Exercise technique
1. Shrug your shoulders fully up toward your ears while keeping your arms overhead with straight elbows.
    1. Keep your torso upright as you avoid leaning back through excessive lower back arching, which would prompt the bottom of your ribs to flare up from your hip bones. Continuing to brace your core can help avoid an unintentional backward lean.
    2. Throughout the exercise, look straight ahead to keep your neck near a neutral position.
    3. While you move the bar through shoulder girdle motion, keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your shoulders fully descend away from your ears in a controlled manner as you keep your arms overhead.
    1. While you do not have to perform the lowering phase with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting your shoulders drop abruptly.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to lower and then rerack the bar.
    1. Following your last rep, tuck your chin back and lower the bar toward your collarbone.
    2. Then walk forward until the bar contacts the back of both J-hooks before bending your knees slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your upper traps, as other shoulder girdle muscles assist in maintaining the overhead position.## Setting up
1. Position an adjustable exercise bench at a moderate incline near 45 degrees.
    1. Alternatively, you can also use a fixed moderate incline bench for this exercise.
    2. Any bench angle that is greater than 30 degrees but less than 60 degrees can be considered a moderate incline.
2. Pick up a pair of dumbbells, and sit down on the incline bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your lower thighs near your abdomen with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. To move the dumbbells into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
3. While marching your thighs up one at a time in quick succession to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench.
    1. Once you are inclined, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
4. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
5. Bring the dumbbells together until they touch directly above your torso, with your palms facing each other and elbows tucked by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Forcefully squeeze the dumbbells together with the intent to crush them. Continue squeezing them together throughout the whole set.
    1. This squeeze should prompt you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
2. Press the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward until you have straightened them with your arms directed up toward the ceiling.
    1. Strive to maintain the same intensity of squeezing the dumbbells together throughout each rep.
    2. Keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench throughout the exercise.
3. While keeping your elbows tucked by your sides, reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until they are just above your torso.
    1. Let your elbows bend to the degree that keeps the ends of the dumbbells level with each other while maintaining mostly straight wrists.
    2. Based on your preference, you finish lowering the dumbbells once they lightly touch your torso or just before that point.
    3. There is no need to lower the dumbbells extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, stop squeezing the dumbbells together, and rotate your forearms out until they are in a vertical position with your wrists stacked over your elbows.
    2. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your triceps and front delts.## Setting up
1. Place a dumbbell (or an alternative weight) on the ground and lie down on your side with the weight in front of your abdomen.
    1. A dumbbell is most commonly used for this exercise, but you can alternatively select a weight plate, ankle weight, or kettlebell. Of the four options, a kettlebell can be the most awkward weight to hold on your thigh, so it is the least recommended weight.
    2. You also have the option of using a weighted vest for resistance, which you would don before lying down, and then ignore the instructions about holding a weight by your side.
    3. If you are on a hard surface, you can set up on an exercise mat for comfort. You chiefly want a comfortable surface under the forearm that you will place on the ground.
    4. The muscles on the side closest to the ground are predominantly trained during your side plank.
2. Straighten and align your legs with your torso so that you can draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from above.
    1. Your top leg should be resting on your bottom leg with the inner portions of your feet touching.
3. Position your bottom forearm on the ground with your elbow bent to about 90 degrees, as it is close to being stacked directly underneath your shoulder.
    1. With your bottom hand, you can make a fist and keep the little finger-side of your hand in contact with the ground. Alternatively, you can keep your palm flat on the ground if you find that to feel more comfortable and stable.
    2. Your upper arm closer to the ground should be in a vertical position.
    3. You can perform this step simultaneously with step 2.
4. Using your top arm, pick up the weight, and position your arm by your top side with your palm facing down. Based on your preference, you can rest the weight on the side of your thigh with a straight elbow, or on the side of your hip with a mildly bent elbow.
## Exercise technique
1. Lift your bottom hip off the ground by pushing through your bottom foot and forearm. You should now be able to draw a straight line from your head through your belly button to the center point between your feet when viewed from the front.
    1. You should also be able to draw a straight line from your heel through your hip to your shoulder when viewed from the top.
    2. Your legs, torso, and head should be in the same alignment as they would be when standing upright with your feet together.
    3. Continue using your top arm to keep the weight stable against the side of your thigh/hip throughout the set.
2. Statically hold this position in proper alignment until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your opposite side contacting the ground.
    1. Avoid letting your lower hip sag, which would prevent you from drawing a line through the midline of your body when viewed from the front. If you notice your hip starting to drop down, it can help to think about pushing more forcefully through your bottom foot. When learning this exercise, it can be useful to face a mirror to receive visual feedback if your lower hip begins sagging down.
    2. Avoid letting your buttocks drift backward by bending your hips. This would prevent you from drawing a straight line through your whole body when viewed from above. Squeezing your glutes can help prevent you from unintentionally bending your hips.
## Description
This exercise can statically train your gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, lateral deltoid, and a variety of core muscles, including your obliques and quadratus lumborum.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of hex dumbbells, and sit down on a flat exercise bench while lifting the dumbbells onto your upper thighs near your abdomen with your palms facing in toward each other.
    1. Flat-sided hex dumbbells are ideal to use for this variation, but you can alternatively use round dumbbells while recognizing that they will be less stable when squeezed together. If you use round dumbbells, you most likely will not be able to squeeze them as intensely together.
    2. Position yourself so that there is enough room on the bench behind you so that you can lie your head down onto the end of the bench.
    3. To move the dumbbells into the proper position, you can situate the dumbbells on the front of your thighs while standing up against the end of the bench, and you can rest them on your thighs while sitting down.
2. While marching your thighs up to help lift the dumbbells back toward your chest with your elbows bent, lean back to lie down with your back and the back of your head in contact with the bench as your head is right by the end of the bench.
    1. Once you are lying down, your feet should be flat on the ground, approximately shoulder width apart from each other, in a stable position stacked under your bent knees.
    2. You should now be holding the dumbbells right by your chest with your forearms in a vertical position, and your wrists stacked over your elbows as the handles of the dumbbells are parallel to your upper arms.
    3. You should be holding the dumbbells on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set.
3. Pull your shoulder blades back together and keep them in this position throughout your set.
    1. You should feel your upper back muscles contract while doing so, which places you into a “proud chest” posture that lifts your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
    3. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
4. Bring the dumbbells together until they touch directly above your torso, with your palms facing each other and elbows tucked by your sides.
## Exercise technique
1. Forcefully squeeze the dumbbells together with the intent to crush them. Continue squeezing them together throughout the whole set.
    1. This squeeze should prompt you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
2. Press the dumbbells upward by driving your elbows upward until you have straightened them with your arms directed up toward the ceiling.
    1. Strive to maintain the same intensity of squeezing the dumbbells together throughout each rep.
    2. Keep your feet flat on the floor and your buttocks, upper back, and head in contact with the bench throughout the exercise.
3. While keeping your elbows tucked by your sides, reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until they are just above your torso.
    1. Let your elbows bend to the degree that keeps the ends of the dumbbells level with each other while maintaining mostly straight wrists.
    2. Based on your preference, you finish lowering the dumbbells once they lightly touch your torso or just before that point.
    3. There is no need to lower the dumbbells extremely slowly, but you should actively control the entire descent.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, stop squeezing the dumbbells together, and rotate your forearms out until they are in a vertical position with your wrists stacked over your elbows.
    2. Then march your thighs up toward the dumbbells, and rock forward to sit up with the dumbbells on your upper thighs before standing up with them.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your pec major, which is assisted by your triceps and front delts.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground.
    1. The setup for this exercise is simply performing the lifting phase of a conventional deadlift.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the middle of your feet under the middle of the bar.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
4. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish.
    2. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
5. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. While allowing the other side’s foot to lift up off the floor, begin hinging at the hip on the side to which you shifted your weight by sitting your buttocks back as you let your knee mildly bend.
    1. Allow your free leg to swing backward as it moves with your torso in a see-saw-like manner, but focus on hinging at the hip of your working leg and sitting back instead of kicking your other leg back and bending over.
    2. Keep your lower back flat, chest up, elbows straightened, and working leg’s foot fully planted on the ground.
    3. To the best of your ability, keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down.
    4. Most of the motion should come from your hip, and your shin should remain in a fairly vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knee fully straightened.
    5. You do not need to focus on bending your knee, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks back while not intentionally keeping your knee locked.
3. Continuing hinging at your hip in a controlled manner to lower the bar until you have reached the end of your available range of motion.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. If you have high hamstring flexibility and can hinge until the plates contact the ground, descend until you can tap the plates very lightly on the ground, but do not let the weight rest on the ground.
4. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hip forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, hip bones level, and working leg’s sole fully in contact with the ground.
    2. After you are fully upright, you could place your free leg’s foot back onto the ground if you could use some additional balance assistance.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which foot remains in contact with the ground.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    3. After finishing the last rep with your other side, set the bar down with both feet on the ground by sitting your hips back and bending your knees.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
Some machine models let you determine the range of motion for the exercise based on how closely you position the seat relative to the foot plate, and you start each rep with your knees fully bent when using these machines, as you would with pin-loaded leg presses. Other machines function similarly to 45-degree leg presses where you initially set the seat to a point where your knees are just mildly bent when placing your feet on the foot plate before straightening your knees, disengaging the safety stoppers, and starting the rep from a straight-knee position. For clarity, different setup and technique passages are provided for each of these two machine designs.

### **Setting up a machine without movable safety stoppers to disengage**

1. Adjust the seat to a setting that lets you leg press through a full range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    1. Finding the ideal setting for you as an individual typically takes some trial and error. The only way to know if the seat setting is appropriate is to sit down on the seat and situate your feet on the footplate in the same position you will use for your set.
    2. The appropriate setting will allow you to assume the starting position as your lower back maintains close to its natural curvature (like when you are normally standing upright). It should also allow you to keep your feet fully on the footplate.
    3. If the seat is excessively close to the footplate relative to your hip mobility, your lower back may have to round to place your feet on the footplate while seated. Alternatively, you may not be able to place your heels firmly in contact with the footplate if it is too close.
    4. You should ideally place the seat as close to the footplate as you can without having to round your lower back or lift your heels off the footplate. Placing the seat meaningfully farther back beyond this point may decrease how much muscle growth the exercise is likely to stimulate.
    5. As a general starting point, you can find the seat setting that allows your knees to be bent by about 90 degrees when your feet are on the footplate. If you are able to avoid rounding your lower back when set up with this setting, you can incrementally bring the seat closer until you find your optimal starting position. Alternatively, if you have to round your lower back to set up with your knees bent by about 90 degrees, you can incrementally bring the seat back until you can assume the starting position without rounding your lower back.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the footplate, near shoulder width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to leg press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the footplate works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the footplate you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select, your heels should be firmly on the footplate along with the rest of your feet. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the footplate if doing so feels awkward or limits your range of motion.
    5. As an alternative to having one large footplate, some machine models have two footplates that can move independently from each other. Regardless of which design your machine has, you can use the same setup and technique.

### **Setting up a machine with movable safety stoppers to disengage**

1. Adjust the seat to a setting that lets you place your feet onto the foot plate with mildly bent knees once you are sitting on the seat.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot platform, near shoulder-width apart, with your toes pointing slightly outward.
    1. Many people find that setting up with a stance reasonably close to this allows them to leg press effectively. However, the specific stance width and degree of toe flare that is ideal will vary among different individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Some people will feel that they can move their hips more freely with a wider stance, while others may prefer a closer stance. Similarly, certain lifters may feel better with their toes pointing straight up, while others may feel more comfortable with their toes pointing moderately further outward.
    3. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out somewhat different stances over time can often help you find out which position is personally best for you.
    4. While recognizing that near the middle of the platform works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select, your heels should be firmly on the platform along with the rest of your feet. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward and/or limits your range of motion.
## Exercise technique
### **Technique with a machine that lacks movable safety stoppers (starting from the bottom)**

1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
3. Drive your feet into the footplate to push it away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the footplate. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    4. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
4. Reverse the movement by bending at your knees and hips in a controlled manner to bring the footplate back toward you until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your feet fully on the footplate, buttocks on the seat, back against the backrest, and knees aligned over your feet.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should not let the platform abruptly swing back toward you.
    3. With the appropriate seat position, you should be able to avoid rounding your lower back at the end range of this motion. If you notice your lower back starts rounding at the end, you could finish your rep and mildly move the seat backward before resuming your set.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set.

### **Technique with a machine that has movable safety stoppers (starting from the top)**

1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handle with your hand to move the safety stoppers out of the platform’s way.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handle) and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Simultaneously bend at your knees and hips to move the platform back toward you in a controlled manner.
    1. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform.
    2. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet. It can help to think about pushing your knees out if you notice them starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
6. Continue bending your knees and hips through the full range of motion you can while keeping your buttocks firmly on the seat, lower back flat against the backrest, and heels fully on the platform.
    1. People can commonly reach the point where their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees, but what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. If you reached the end range to which your hips can bend, continuing any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your buttocks starting to curl up off the seat.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knees to track over your toes, continuing past this point would cause your heels to start lifting off the platform.
7. Reverse the movement by driving your feet into the foot plate to push the platform away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knees fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knees when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knees into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heels or all through your toes, aim to push evenly throughout your feet.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the foot plate, buttocks on the seat, back against the backrest, and knees aligned over your feet.
8. Alternate between steps 5-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. After your knees are straightened following your last rep, lock the safety handle with your hand to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the sled before bending your knees to set the platform back onto the safety stoppers.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your quads, gluteus maximus, and multiple hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand centered between its handles in a near shoulder-width stance facing away from the base of the machine.
    1. If your machine model lets you adjust the handle height, you can initially set the handles to the lowest setting. If you cannot grasp the handles during the next step without rounding your lower back, you can subsequently raise the handles to a height that lets you grasp them with a flat-back position.
    2. Compared to facing toward the base of the machine, facing away from it can often promote a comparatively more squat-like movement with greater quad involvement.
2. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, sit your hips back and down while bending your knees to squat down until you can firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles, it is generally recommended to grasp the lower handles to let you train through a longer range of motion if you can reach those handles without rounding your lower back.
    2. Let your knees naturally move forward to a degree that helps keep your torso more upright than if you were performing a typical deadlift with a hip hinge focus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
2. While keeping your chest up and back flat, drive through your legs to stand upright as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. As a cue to begin the lift, you can think about pushing the floor away.
    2. Aim to push evenly throughout your feet, rather than being all on your heels or toes.
    3. You should chiefly be using your lower body muscles to lift the handles through knee and hip motion while keeping your back in a rigid position.
    4. Avoid letting your hips shoot up and/rounding your back to start the rep, which could occur if you do not sufficiently drive your feet through the ground.
    5. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side. Avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching at the top.
3. In a controlled manner, reverse the movement by bending your knees and hips to squat down and lower the handles to the starting position.
    1. Rather than trying to squat straight down with a fully upright torso, let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips as you move your buttocks back and down, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. With your soles fully planted on the ground, strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    3. There is no need to descend extremely slowly, but you should be in control of this lowering phase and not look to bounce the weights out of the bottom into the next rep.
    4. To confirm that you have fully squatted down, you can lightly tap the machine’s lever arms against the stoppers underneath them.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
    1. Provided that you did not rest in the bottom position (with the weight resting on the stoppers) between reps, you will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise primarily develops your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. Plates weighing 45lb (or 20kg) may be awkward to remove from this machine, especially if you are adding at least two of them, so using no greater than 35lb (or 15kg) plates may make it easier to remove the plates at the end of the set.
2. In a hip-width to shoulder-width stance, stand centered directly in front of the handles.
3. Lift your arms slightly forward, so that your palms are just in front of the handles with your palms facing backward and elbows straight.
4. Mildly lean forward at your hips until you can hook the tips of your four long fingers around the bottom handle and firmly grasp it with straight wrists.
    1. Point your thumbs mildly forward so that they will be out of the way of the top handle.
    2. The handle should be resting on the joints of your fingers formed by your fingertip bones and the bones directly next to them that are closer toward your palms. These joints are most apparent when you make a fist.
## Exercise technique
1. Stand upright, using your hips to lift the bottom handle toward the top handle while keeping your elbows straight.
    1. Depending on your height, you may have to maintain a slight forward lean throughout your set, or you may be able to stand fully upright.
2. Move your working hand’s thumb over the top handle on its side closer to you, and snugly contact the top handle with the base of your palm.
    1. The appropriate grip should result in the webspace between your thumb and index finger touching the bar.
3. Let go of the handle with your non-working side and situate that arm in your preferred position throughout the set (such as resting by your side).
    1. Initially using both hands to help set your working hand into the starting position can be easier than only setting up with your working hand.
    2. The subsequent directions will apply to your working side’s hand.
4. Curl your four long fingers to lift the bottom handle upward until it taps the top handle.
    1. Throughout this exercise, you will predominantly be moving your fingers through motion at your knuckle joints.
    2. This motion is similar to the “come here” hand gesture performed with your four long fingers.
    3. Continue keeping your elbow and wrist straight while your thumb remains stationary around the top handle throughout your set.
5. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by uncurling your fingers to lower the bottom handle.
    1. You should finish with the finger bones attached to your knuckles pointing straight down while continuing to keep your fingertips hooked around the bottom handle.
    2. If you feel as if the bottom handle is sliding out of your fingers, you should wrap your fingertips further around it to secure your grip.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weight rapidly uncurl your fingers in an uncontrolled fashion.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. Following your last rep, hook the fingers of your other hand around the bottom handle and perform steps 2 and 3 with your hands switching positions before performing completing the set with your other side.
    2. Once you have finished training both sides and are holding the bottom handle with both hands, drive your elbows up in an upright rowing motion to lift the bases of your palms off the top handle and point your thumbs mildly forward out of the top handle’s way. Then set the plates down by lowering your elbows and leaning forward at your hips before releasing your four long fingers from the bottom handle after the plates are fully resting on the bottom of the machine.
## Description
While also effectively training the majority of your intrinsic hand muscles, this exercise can develop your forearms’s flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundas.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit on the seat facing the handles.
    1. Place your feet on the floor under your bent knees.
2. Reach forward with both arms and firmly hook the tips of your four long fingers around the front handles with your thumbs pointing up, so that they are not in between the front and back handles.
    1. This type of machine typically has a pair of higher handles (where gripping angles your palms downward and inward) and a pair of lower handles (where gripping angles your palms upward and inward).
    2. People most often use the higher handles, but you can use whichever handles you prefer.
3. By driving your elbows back in a rowing motion, pull the handles back toward you.
4. Once the front and back handles are sufficiently close together, firmly hook your working side’s thumb around the back handle, as the back handle snugly contacts the base of your palm.
    1. The appropriate grip should result in the webspace between your thumb and index finger touching the back handle.
5. Let go of the front handle with your non-working side and situate that arm in your preferred position throughout the set (such as resting by your side).
    1. Initially using both hands to help set your working hand into the starting position can be easier than only setting up with your working hand.
    2. The subsequent directions will apply to your working side’s hand.
## Exercise technique
1. Squeeze the handles together by curling your four long fingers toward your palm.
    1. Throughout this exercise, you will predominantly be moving your fingers through motion at your knuckle joints.
    2. This motion is similar to the “come here” hand gesture performed with your four long fingers.
    3. Continue keeping your elbow and wrist still while your thumb remains stationary around the back handle throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by uncurling your fingers to let the handles spread apart from each other.
    1. You should finish with the finger bones attached to your knuckles pointing straight forward away from your wrist while continuing to keep your fingertips hooked around the front handle.
    2. If you feel as if the front handle is sliding out of your fingers, you should wrap your fingertips further around it to secure your grip.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting the weight rapidly pull the handles apart in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. Following your last rep, hook the fingers of your free hand around the front handle before performing steps 3-5 of the setup, with your hands switching positions and then completing the set with your other side.
4. Once you have finished training both sides and are holding the front handles with both hands, drive your elbows back in a rowing motion to lift the bases of your palms off the back handles and point your thumbs mildly forward out of the handle’s way.
5. Set the front handle down by letting your elbows straighten before releasing your four long fingers from the front handles once the machine is no longer providing any resistance.
## Description
While also effectively training the majority of your intrinsic hand muscles, this exercise can develop your forearms’s flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor digitorum profundas.## Setting up
1. If your machine is not currently next to a seat, place it near a seat on which you can comfortably sit while your foot is on the machine’s foot platform.
    1. Having the seat be close enough to the machine where your knee can be bent by a bit less than 90 degrees once your foot is on the foot is commonly a comfortable position for this exercise.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine using plates with diameters that do not touch the ground.
    1. Most machines have loading sleeves that are close enough to the ground that plates larger than typical 10lb (or 5kg) plates may touch the ground and restrict your range of motion for the exercise.
3. Sit down on the seat, and place the sole of your working leg’s foot onto the machine’s foot platform.
    1. Keep your other foot in a comfortable position on the ground throughout your set.
    2. All the subsequent directions apply to your working leg.
4. Slide your foot forward on the platform until the top of your foot is snugly pressed against the foot pad and your ankle is aligned with the machine’s pivot points.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center point about which the platform rotates. When your ankle is aligned with the pivot points (such that a straight line can pass from one pivot point through the center of your ankle and to the other pivot point), the exercise will feel the most comfortable and natural.
5. If you did not already do so, allow the machine’s resistance to fully point your toes down away from you to assume the exercise’s starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. Fully pull your toes and the balls of your foot upward and back toward your shin by moving at your ankle.
    1. As the balls of your foot and toes lift up, your heel will correspondingly arc downward.
    2. How high you will be able to lift up will be affected by your ankle mobility, but people with normal ankle mobility should be able to finish with the balls of their feet and toes at least slightly higher than the bottoms of their heels.
    3. Throughout the set, keep the sole of your foot against the foot platform while the top of your foot remains against the foot pad.
    4. While motion occurs at your ankle, the rest of your body should remain stationary during this exercise.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting the balls of your foot and toes arc down away from your shin to lower the weight.
    1. Descend through the full range of motion your ankle mobility comfortably permits, which often results in a stretching sensation in the muscles on the front of your shin when your foot is fully pointed down with your toes and the balls of your foot lower than your heel.
    2. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your foot down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to slide your foot back off the platform before repeating the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can train the four muscle ankle dorsiflexor muscles on the front of your lower leg, the largest of which is the tibialis anterior.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the Roman chair’s thigh pad to a level that positions the top of your hip bones somewhat above the top of the pads when you set up with your foot on the foot plate.
    1. This position will allow you to move freely about your hips in contrast to if the pad is contacting the top of your hip bones, which would restrict hip motion and prompt you to perform the exercise via spinal motion.
    2. If the top of the pad is positioned much lower than the top of the hip bones (rather than only somewhat lower), some individuals may feel knee discomfort, so a sweet spot can exist for pad placement.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal pad position after initially completing step 2.
2. While placing your hands onto the Roman chair for balance support, step onto the foot plate with the back of your heel (of the leg you will train) in contact with the back of the foot plate. While resting your thighs against the thigh pad, position your non-working side’s foot just behind the foot plate/lower leg roller.
    1. After you have finished setting up on the foot plate, your working side’s knee should be straight, and your sole should fully contact the foot plate. If your Roman chair has a lower leg pad, the back of your lower calf should be snugly contacting it.
    2. You can maintain a slight bend in your non-working side’s knee to keep that side foot and shin just above the foot plate/lower leg roller.
    3. Aim to align your working side’s foot with your hip when viewed from the back, rather than situating your foot further out to the side of the foot plate. If you positioned your other leg in the same manner, this would result in a hip-width stance.
3. Check to make sure that the pad is positioned as described by step 1, and readjust the pad height if it is not prior to resuming your setup.
4. Let go of the Roman chair, and place your arms in your preferred position, such as crossing your arms across your chest with bent elbows to situate each hand on the front of the opposite shoulder.
    1. How you position your arms will affect the difficulty of this exercise, so you can select an arm position that promotes the degree of challenge you wish to have.
    2. Compared to placing your hands on your shoulders, the intensity will be greater if you hold your hands behind your head with bent elbows, and the difficulty will further increase if you raise your arms fully overhead with straight elbows.
    3. Maintain your preferred arm position throughout your set.
    4. All the subsequent instructions will apply to your working leg’s side.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Squeeze your glute to keep your torso up such that you can draw approximately a straight line from your leg through your hip and to your torso as opposed to having your torso bent over.
    1. Throughout your set, keep your lower back and neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright on one foot and looking straight ahead.
    2. This is the starting position of the exercise, where your body should look like it does when normally standing upright except at an incline.
3. Hinge at your hip to lower your torso in a controlled manner until you reach the end range to which your hamstrings (on the back of your thigh) can comfortably stretch.
    1. Rather than just bending over by rounding your lower back, aim to have nearly all of the motion occur from you moving at your hip as you keep your back in a rigid position.
    2. Maintain a straight knee position without intentionally locking your knee out by squeezing your quads.
    3. Keep your shoulders level rather than letting one side drop lower than the other through trunk rotation.
    4. People commonly have the flexibility to descend until their hip is bent by reasonably near 90 degrees (when measuring the angle between the leg and torso from a side view), but how deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    5. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch, you have finished descending through your full range of motion. If you have abnormally high hamstring flexibility and do not feel a meaningful stretch at any point in the motion, you can stop descending once you feel that gravity is no longer pulling you down into a deeper hip hinge.
4. Reverse the movement by lifting your torso up through your hip until you have returned to the starting position, where your torso is aligned with your leg.
    1. To help cue hip motion instead of spinal motion, it can help to think about squeezing your glute, particularly at the top of the movement.
    2. Avoid arching your lower back, since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of the hip motion you just performed during the lowering phase.
    3. At the end of the rep, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulder through your hip to your knee when viewed from the side.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the stoppers (that are positioned under the arms) to a height that allows you to press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with vertical forearms, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
    3. Some machine models do not allow you to adjust the stoppers.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine after determining your appropriate stopper height.
3. Lie down with your back, buttocks, and head on the pad. Position yourself so that your lower-to-middle chest is aligned with the handles.
    1. Your elbows will naturally tuck closer to your sides as you position yourself further back on the pad (situating your shoulders horizontally further behind the handles).
    2. You can decide specifically where you lie down on the pad based on what feels comfortable to your shoulders throughout the exercise, while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If you experience shoulder discomfort when pressing with the handles near mid-chest, you can try positioning yourself with the handles by the lowest part of your chest to be able to press with a lower degree of elbow flare.
    4. Your feet should be flat on the ground in a stable position, stacked under your bent knees.
4. Firmly grasp the handle on the side of your body you will train during this set. Direct your palm forward, and stack your elbow directly under the handle to place your forearm in a vertical position.
    1. Hold the handle on top of the base of your palm (AKA the heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip throughout your set. Squeezing the handle firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handle and position your elbow directly under it, you may have to increase the height of the machine’s stoppers before resuming your setup.
    3. Aligning your elbow directly under (rather than being in front or behind when viewed from the side) the handle will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you positioned your body appropriately on the pad.
    4. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Grasping the handle where it would result in a moderately outside shoulder-width grip if you used both handles works well for many lifters, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
    5. You can position your non-working arm wherever feels most comfortable and stable to you. Some people like to grasp the non-working side’s handle without moving it during the set, while others like to rest the free hand somewhere on the body.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the handle upward until you have straightened your elbow.
    1. You can think about driving your elbow upward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulder and elbow, your elbow should remain stacked under your wrist and the handle when viewed from the side.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back and buttocks snugly against the pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handle in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your elbow stacked under your wrist and the handle as you let your elbow move outward and downward while bending.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arm abruptly down.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulder at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
    4. At the end range, you have the option of lightly tapping the machine’s arm against the stopper under it to confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand centered between its handles in a hip-width stance while facing toward the base of the machine.
    1. If your machine model lets you adjust the handle height, you can initially set the handles to the lowest setting. If you cannot grasp the handles during the next step without rounding your lower back, you can subsequently raise the handles to a height that lets you grasp them with a flat-back position.
    2. Compared to facing away from the base of the machine, facing toward it can often reinforce sitting back to perform a high-quality hip hinge during the exercise.
2. While keeping your lower back flat, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles, it is generally recommended to grasp the lower handles to let you hinge through your full range of motion during the exercise.
3. Drive through your legs and thrust your hips forward to stand upright as you continue keeping your back flat and elbows straight.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and take a small step backward with your other foot so that its toes are close to being just slightly behind your lead foot’s heel while keeping your feet hip-width apart.
    1. Naturally let your knees slightly bend.
    2. Avoid stepping inward to the extent that your rear foot is directly behind your lead foot. Keeping your feet hip-width apart can help keep you stable.
2. Place the balls and toes of your rear foot on the ground while keeping its midfoot and heel off the ground.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you maintain a rigid back position.
4. While continuing to bear almost all your weight through your lead foot, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as you let your knees mildly bend.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat, chest up, elbows straight, and lead foot fully planted on the ground.
    2. Your rear leg’s purpose is to assist with maintaining your balance so that the exercise can feel more stable than a true single leg Romanian deadlift.
    3. If scales were under both of your feet, aim for the scale under your lead foot to show that you are bearing at least 90% of your weight through your front leg.
    4. Strive to keep your lead leg’s shin vertical with your weight distributed throughout the entirety of your lead foot. If you concentrate all your weight through your heel, your knee may track backward into an excessively straightened position. If you bear too much weight through the front of your foot, your knee may track forward and bend excessively.
    5. While motion will occur at both hips, you should focus on sitting your lead leg’s hip back (as if you were trying to close a car door with your buttocks) while letting your rear leg’s hip naturally move with it.
5. In a controlled manner, continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically hinge until their hands are at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
6. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and lead foot fully on the platform.
    2. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Following your last rep, step forward with your rear foot to position your feet symmetrically before repeating the exercise with your other leg in the lead position.
    1. Once you are ready to set the handles down, step forward again to position your feet symmetrically before sitting your hips back and bending your knees to lower the handles.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with the lower half of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is too low (placing the handles near shoulder level or above), some lifters may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort from having to flare their elbows outward excessively.
    4. If the seat is excessively high (placing the handles below the lowest part of the chest), the exercise may be suboptimal for developing the chest.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust either aspect, position its arms or back pad to a setting that enables you to press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Some machines allow you to increase the range of motion by placing the arms further back toward the back pad, while others allow you to move the back pad forward, closer toward the arms. However, certain machine models may not permit you to adjust either part.
    2. If you have normal shoulder mobility, you can initially position the arms or the seat to a setting that results in the handles being near the front of your chest (when viewed from the side) once you are seated. This commonly involves setting the arms as far backward or the seat as far forward as the machine enables.
    3. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles with proper positioning during step 5, you can adjust the machine to increase the distance between the back pad and the handles until you can do so.
    4. The ideal setting should allow your elbows to start as far behind your torso as is comfortable for your shoulders while keeping your elbows level with the handles.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. Firmly grasp the handle on the side of your body you will train during this set with your palm facing down. Situate your hand moderately further out to your side than your shoulder with your elbow positioned directly behind the handle, resulting in your elbow being level with the handle when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handle on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip throughout your set. Squeezing the handle firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbow directly behind (rather than being above or below) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    3. Grasping the handle where it would result in a moderately outside shoulder-width grip if you used both handles works well for many lifters, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best while being able to achieve the aforementioned stacking of your elbow and wrist.
    4. You can position your non-working arm wherever feels most comfortable and stable to you. Some people like to grasp the non-working side’s handle without moving it during the set, while others like to rest the free hand somewhere on the body.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping your forearm aligned with the angle at which you are pressing, drive the handle forward until your elbow is straight.
    1. You can think about driving your elbow forward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet stationary.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handle to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your forearm aligned with the angle of your press.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arm abruptly back in an uncontrolled fashion.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulder at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
    4. At the end range, you have the option of lightly tapping the machine’s arm against the stopper under it to confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. If your machine has an adjustable seat, set it to the most reclined position that allows your back to be well-supported by the backrest when seated with your feet on the foot platform.
    1. Starting with the most reclined position (which tilts the seat down in a closer-to-horizontal position) generally works well for most people.
    2. Compared to using a closer-to-upright seat angle, using an appropriately reclined position will allow you to leg press through a longer range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    3. The most reclined setting on certain machine models may be excessively reclined to the extent that the backrest may no longer be able to stabilize your body when leg pressing. If this occurs, your buttocks may lift off the seat, and your torso may slide back along the backrest due to the backrest being angled too close to a horizontal position. However, most leg press machines will not have an excessively reclined seat setting available.
    4. To determine if the seat angle you initially selected is appropriate for you, you can perform a couple of reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section without adding any weight plates to the leg press. If you feel stable and well-supported by the backrest, the seat is not excessively reclined, and you have selected a viable seat angle.
    5. Note that not all machines have an adjustable seat.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot platform, hip width apart, with your toes pointing up in the same direction.
    1. While recognizing that near the middle of the platform works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select, your heels should be firmly on the platform along with the rest of your feet. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward and/or limits your range of motion.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out different foot placements over time can often help you find out which position personally feels best for you.
    3. In the starting position, your knees should be at least slightly bent to let you unlock the leg press and begin your set. If you have short legs, you potentially may have to situate your feet lower on the platform to reach the platform with slightly bent knees. If you are using a machine that does not permit a lower starting height of the sled, that particular machine may not be feasible for your body type.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handle with your hand to disengage the safety stopper.
    1. The specific location of the safety handle and how to unlock it can vary among different machine models. However, the safety handle is often near where one of your hands will be when resting by your side.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handle) and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Lift your non-working side’s foot off the platform and place it in a comfortable position that will keep that leg from contacting the platform throughout your set.
    1. Situating the resting leg’s foot on the ground with a bent knee is commonly a comfortable position for a variety of machines.
6. Lower the sled toward you in a controlled manner by simultaneously bending at your knee and hip.
    1. Keep the entire sole of your foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the platform.
    2. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    3. Strive to keep your knee aligned with your second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or third toe as you avoid letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot. It can help to think about pushing your knee out if you notice it starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should be actively in control the entire time.
7. Continue lowering the sled through the full range of motion you can while keeping your buttocks firmly on the seat, lower back flat against the backrest, and heel fully on the platform.
    1. People can commonly lower the sled until reaching at least 90 degrees of knee bend. However, what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. If you reached the end range to which your hip can bend, lowering the sled any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your buttocks starting to curl up off the seat.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knee to track over your toes, lowering the sled any further would cause your heel to start lifting off the platform.
8. Drive your foot into the platform to push the sled away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knee and hip.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knee fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knee when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knee into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heel or all through your toes, you should be pushing evenly throughout your foot.
    3. Continue keeping your foot fully on the platform, buttocks on the seat, back against the backrest, and knee aligned over your foot.
9. Alternate between steps 6-8 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. After your knee is straightened following the lifting phase of your last rep, lock the safety handles with your hands to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the sled before bending your knee to lower the sled onto the safety stoppers.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your quads, gluteus maximus, and multiple hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
A chest press machine with vertical handles is required to perform this neutral grip variation. If your machine only has horizontal handles, you can select the overhand grip variation from this database, which uses the name “seated single arm plate-loaded machine chest press.”

1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles near the lowest part of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable and the pressing motion to feel natural during the exercise.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust either aspect, position its arms or back pad to a setting that enables you to press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Some machines allow you to increase the range of motion by placing the arms further back toward the back pad, while others allow you to move the back pad forward, closer toward the arms. However, certain machine models may not permit you to adjust either part.
    2. If you have normal shoulder mobility, you can initially position the arms or the seat to a setting that results in the handles being near the front of your chest (when viewed from the side) once you are seated. This commonly involves setting the arms as far backward or the seat as far forward as the machine enables.
    3. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles with proper positioning during step 5, you can adjust the machine to increase the distance between the back pad and the handles until you can do so.
    4. The ideal setting should allow your elbows to start as far behind your torso as is comfortable for your shoulders while keeping your elbows level with the handles.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. Firmly grasp the vertical handle on the side of your body you will train during this set with your palm inward. Position your elbow directly behind the handle, resulting in your elbow being level with the handle when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handle on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip throughout your set. Squeezing the handle firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbow directly behind the handle will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    3. You can position your non-working arm wherever feels most comfortable and stable to you. Some people like to grasp the non-working side’s handle without moving it during the set, while others like to rest the free hand somewhere on the body.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping your forearm aligned with the angle at which you are pressing, drive the handle forward until you have straightened your elbow.
    1. Keep your elbow stacked behind your wrist and the handle while driving your elbow forward.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet stationary.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handle to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your forearm aligned with the angle of your press.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arm abruptly back in an uncontrolled fashion.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulder at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
    4. At the end range, you have the option of lightly tapping the machine’s arm against the stopper under it to confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise predominantly trains your pec major, front delts, and triceps with a technique that some people find feels more comfortable for their shoulders compared to other presses.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with your lower chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, but feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is too low (placing the handles near shoulder level or above), some lifters may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort from having to flare their elbows outward excessively.
    4. If the seat is excessively high (placing the handles near your abdomen), the exercise may feel awkward and be suboptimal for developing the chest.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. Depending on the specific machine model you are using and what feels most stable to you, you can place your feet flat on the floor or on a foot bar.
    2. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
4. Firmly grasp the handle on the side of your body you will train during this set with your palm facing down. Situate your hand moderately further out to your side than your shoulder with your elbow positioned directly behind the handle, resulting in your elbow being level with the handle when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handle on top of the base of your palm (AKA heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip throughout your set. Squeezing the handle firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbow directly behind (rather than being above or below) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    3. Grasping the handle where it would result in a moderately outside shoulder-width grip if you used both handles works well for many lifters, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best while being able to achieve the aforementioned stacking of your elbow and wrist.
    4. You can position your non-working arm wherever feels most comfortable and stable to you. Some people like to grasp the non-working side’s handle without moving it during the set, while others like to reach across the body and hold the torso with the free hand.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While keeping your forearm aligned with the angle at which you are pressing, ****drive the handle forward until you have straightened your elbow.
    1. You can think about driving your elbow forward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet stationary.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handle to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your forearm aligned with the angle of your press.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arm abruptly back in an uncontrolled fashion.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulder at the end range. Some machines will limit the range of motion before reaching this point, even when set up ideally.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your pec major with a focus on its middle and lower regions while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to the machine and adjust the seat to a height that allows your chest to rest comfortably against the chest pad.
    1. It is generally appropriate to situate the seat where your upper chest can be positioned slightly above the top of the chest pad, while your middle and lower chest contact the pad once you are seated.
    2. The proper seat height should let the exercise feel smooth and natural, so if you think your arm path feels somewhat awkward during the row, it is recommended to adjust the seat height. When using this machine for the first time, trying out different seat heights may be helpful to determine specifically which setting helps the row feel best to you based on your body proportions and the machine model you are using.
    3. To row through your full range of motion, ideally place the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handle with your elbow fully straight.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, set it to a position that lets you grasp the handle with a straight elbow only when your shoulder is fully rounded forward.
    1. Setting the chest further forward (toward the base of the machine) than this point can limit the row’s range of motion.
3. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the chest pad and your feet resting on the foot bar or the floor.
4. With the side you are going to train, reach forward and firmly grasp the handle with an underhand grip as your palm faces up.
    1. Hold the handle with a straight wrist and elbow.
    2. If your machine has a stationary center handle (that is not connected to either of the machine’s mobile lever arms), you can grasp it with your free hand and continue holding it throughout the set for additional stability. Otherwise, place your free hand wherever you prefer.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handle back toward your side by driving your elbow backward while bending it. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade fully back.
    1. Move your elbow through a path that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping it tucked fairly close to your side with this machine rather than notably flaring your elbow out away from your side.
    2. Keep your chest in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow finishing slightly behind your torso.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to move forward in a controlled manner, progressively straightening until it is outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blade will naturally move forward and away from the center of your back, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull it further forward than your resting position at the end, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arm forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired weight onto the machine, adjust the thigh pad or seat to a height that lets you position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. Most machines have an adjustable thigh pad, but some models have an adjustable seat, while others let you adjust both.
2. While standing above the seat and facing the torso pad, firmly grasp the handle on the side you will train with a grip that feels comfortable to you.
    1. Which grip (e.g., overhand, underhand, neutral) is most comfortable will be affected by the angle of your specific machine’s handles.
3. While holding the handle, sit down on the seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the thigh pad and your torso contacting the torso pad.
    1. When sitting down, allow your elbow to straighten in a controlled manner and your arm to raise overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    2. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), you may need to lower the seat height to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
    3. If your machine has a stationary center handle (that is not connected to either of the machine’s mobile lever arms), you can grasp it with your free hand and continue holding it throughout the set for additional stability. Otherwise, place your free hand wherever you prefer.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbow down and back toward your side while progressively bending it to pull the handle toward you.
    1. Simultaneosuly pull your shoulder blade down away and back.
    2. Keep your torso in the same position throughout your set rather than progressively leaning backward.
    3. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow finishing slightly behind your torso.
2. Reverse the motion by letting your elbow move forward and upward while straightening in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blade move naturally forward as it elevates with your arm.
    2. Finish with your elbow fully straightened and the handle fully returned to the starting position.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arm up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
Some machine models let you determine the range of motion for the exercise based on how closely you position the seat relative to the foot plate, and you start each rep with your knees fully bent when using these machines, as you would with pin-loaded leg presses. Other machines function similarly to 45-degree leg presses where you initially set the seat to a point where your knees are just mildly bent when placing your feet on the foot plate before straightening your knees, disengaging the safety stoppers, and starting the rep from a straight-knee position. For clarity, different setup and technique passages are provided for each of these two machine designs.

### **Setting up a machine without movable safety stoppers to disengage**

1. Adjust the seat to a setting that lets you leg press through a full range of motion without rounding your lower back.
    1. Finding the ideal setting for you as an individual typically takes some trial and error. The only way to know if the seat setting is appropriate is to sit down on the seat and situate your foot on the footplate in the same position you will use for your set.
    2. The appropriate setting will allow you to assume the starting position as your lower back maintains close to its natural curvature (like when you are normally standing upright). It should also allow you to keep your foot fully on the footplate.
    3. If the seat is excessively close to the footplate relative to your hip mobility, your lower back may have to round to place your feet on the footplate while seated. Alternatively, you may not be able to place your heel firmly in contact with the footplate if it is too close.
    4. You should ideally place the seat as close to the footplate as you can without having to round your lower back or lift your heels off the footplate. Placing the seat meaningfully farther back beyond this point may decrease how much muscle growth the exercise is likely to stimulate.
    5. As a general starting point, you can find the seat setting that allows your knee to be bent by about 90 degrees when your foot is on the footplate. If you are able to avoid rounding your lower back when set up with this setting, you can incrementally bring the seat closer until you find your optimal starting position. Alternatively, if you have to round your lower back to set up with your knee bent by about 90 degrees, you can incrementally bring the seat back until you can assume the starting position without rounding your lower back.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your working side’s foot onto the footplate where your foot is aligned with your hip and your toes are pointing up. Situate your non-working side’s foot in a comfortable position that will keep that leg from contacting the platform throughout your set.
    1. Aligning your foot with your hip means that you would have a hip-width stance if your other foot was positioned in the same manner. This placement can feel more stable compared to if you situated your foot further out to the side or closer to your body’s midline.
    2. Rather than placing your foot on the top or the bottom of the foot plate, setting it near the middle is often an appropriate starting point, but you can also choose how high up on the platform you position your foot based on your preference. Wherever you select, your heel should be firmly on the footplate along with the rest of your foot. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the footplate if doing so feels awkward or limits your range of motion.

### **Setting up a machine with movable safety stoppers to disengage**

1. Adjust the seat to a setting that lets you place your feet onto the foot plate with mildly bent knees once you are sitting on the seat.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit down with your buttocks fully on the seat and your whole back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the back of the seat or keep it slightly in front of the seat. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Place your feet fully on the middle of the foot platform, hip width apart, with your toes pointing up in the same direction.
    1. While recognizing that near the middle of the platform works well for many people, you can also adjust how high up on the platform you position your feet based on your preference. Wherever you select, your heels should be firmly on the platform along with the rest of your feet. Placing your feet higher up potentially may bias the gluteus maximus to a degree, while placing your feet lower down potentially may train the quads preferentially. Avoid positioning your feet toward either end of the platform if doing so feels awkward and/or limits your range of motion.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet in a manner that results in you feeling comfortable and stable when leg pressing through your full range of motion. Trying out different foot placements over time can often help you find out which position personally feels best for you.
## Exercise technique
### **Technique with a machine that lacks movable safety stoppers (starting from the bottom)**

1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. For additional stability, firmly grasp the handles and continue holding them throughout your set.
3. Drive your foot into the footplate to push the platform away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knee and hip.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knee fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knee when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knee into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Keep the entire sole of your foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the footplate. Rather than pushing all through your heel or all through your toes, aim to push evenly throughout your foot.
    3. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    4. Strive to keep your knee aligned with your second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or third toe as you avoid letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot. It can help to think about pushing your knee out if you notice it starting to drift inward.
4. Reverse the movement by bending at your knee and hip in a controlled manner to bring the footplate back toward you until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your foot fully on the footplate, buttocks on the seat, back against the backrest, and knee aligned over your foot.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should not let the platform abruptly swing back toward you.
    3. With the appropriate seat position, you should be able to avoid rounding your lower back at the end range of this motion. If you notice your lower back starts rounding at the end, you could finish your rep and mildly move the seat backward before resuming your set.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.

### **Technique with a machine that has movable safety stoppers (starting from the top)**

1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing throughout your set.
2. Drive your feet into the platform to push it away from you by straightening your knees.
    1. After doing so, the sled should no longer be resting on the machine’s safety stoppers.
    2. Your knees should now be straight or just slightly bent.
3. Unlock the safety handle with your hand to move the safety stoppers out of the platform’s way.
4. For additional stability, firmly grasp the fixed handles (not the movable safety handle) and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Lift your non-working side’s foot off the foot plate and place it in a comfortable position that will keep that leg from contacting the platform throughout your set.
6. Simultaneously bend at your knee and hip to move the platform back toward you in a controlled manner.
    1. Keep the entire sole of your foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the foot plate.
    2. Continue keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the seat and your back snugly up against the backrest.
    3. Strive to keep your knee aligned with your second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or third toe as you avoid letting your knee cave inward relative to your foot. It can help to think about pushing your knee out if you notice it starting to drift inward.
    4. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should not let the platform abruptly swing back toward you.
7. Continue bending your knee and hip through the full range of motion you can while keeping your buttocks firmly on the seat, lower back flat against the backrest, and heel fully on the foot plate.
    1. People can commonly reach the point where their knees are bent by at least 90 degrees, but what constitutes a full range of motion will vary from person to person and be influenced by your individual hip and ankle mobility, along with your foot placement.
    2. If you reached the end range to which your hip can bend, continuing any further would cause your lower back to start rounding. This would be demonstrated by your buttocks starting to curl up off the seat.
    3. If you reached the end range to which your ankle mobility allows your knee to track over your toe, continuing past this point would cause your heel to start lifting off the platform.
8. Reverse the movement by driving your foot into the foot plate to push the platform away from you as you simultaneously extend (straighten) your knee and hip.
    1. Based on your preference, you can finish with your knee fully straightened or ever so slightly bent. It is not inherently dangerous to fully straighten your knee when leg pressing, but avoid forcibly snapping your knee into a locked-out position in an uncontrolled manner.
    2. Rather than pushing all through your heel or all through your toes, aim to push evenly throughout your foot.
    3. Continue keeping your feet fully on the foot plate, buttocks on the seat, back against the backrest, and knee aligned over your foot.
9. Alternate between steps 6-8 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. After your knee is straightened following your last rep, lock the safety handle with your hand to return the safety stoppers to being fully under the platform before bending your knee to set the platform back onto the safety stoppers.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train your quads, gluteus maximus, and multiple hip adductor muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s foot plate to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are standing on the foot plate.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the foot plate height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    3. To confirm whether you selected an appropriate foot plate height, you will have to stand on it and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
2. Select your desired load with the machine’s loading pin, and stand on the foot plate.
3. While facing sideways relative to the weight stack and away from the handle you will not use, align the side of your shoulder (that you will train during this set) with the machine’s pivot point.
4. With your arm closer to the handle, firmly grasp the handle with your palm facing backward (i.e., overhand grip).
    1. Based on your preference, you can keep your elbow fully straight or maintain a slight bend in your elbow throughout the exercise.
    2. In the starting position, you should be standing upright with your arm directed down toward the ground.
    3. Situate your free hand in a comfortable position, such as resting by your side.
## Exercise technique
1. Throughout your set, brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
2. Sweep your arm forward and upward in an arcing manner to lift the handle near shoulder height.
    1. Let your shoulder blade move naturally rather than trying to hold it in any particular position.
    2. All the movement should occur from shoulder motion while you keep your legs and torso stationary throughout the exercise.
    3. While it is fully appropriate to finish this step once your arm is outstretched straight in front of you, approximately parallel to the ground, you optionally can continue lifting your arm higher up if you wish.
3. Reverse the motion by lowering your arm lower in toward the starting position in a controlled manner.
    1. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack, or you can stop just before this point.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arm downward.
4. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and secure the weights with collars.
    1. In contrast to most machines, the weight plates may slide if you do not connect collars on a reverse hyper with a center pendulum and two sleeves.
2. If your machine has adjustable handles, position them at a setting that allows you to hold with mostly straight elbows during step 4.
    1. This can require some trial and error to find the ideal handle setting.
    2. A variety of machine models lack adjustable handles, but some have multiple pairs of handles that allow you to select the ones that you can grasp with mostly straight elbows.
3. Stand directly in front of the pad and the weights. If you are using a leg strap rather than rollers, step into the strap with your working leg.
4. After climbing on the pad, lie down with your abdomen on the pad, and firmly grasp the handles. Position the top of your hip bones just on the edge of the pad and angle your free leg out to the side in a comfortable position that will not get in the way of the pendulum during the exercise.
    1. The appropriate hip position should let your working leg move freely throughout the exercise.
    2. If you position yourself excessively far forward, the pad will restrict your hip motion.
    3. If you position yourself excessively far backward, you will be less stable and may round your lower back more than intended.
    4. Some machine models will have a pair of steps to help you climb onto the pad. When using one of these machines, you can angle your free leg out to the side and rest its foot on one of these steps.
5. If you are using a leg strap, move your working leg backward until its lower leg is snugly contacting the strap slightly above your ankle. If you are using leg rollers, position your lower leg snugly between the rollers slightly above your ankle.
    1. In the starting position, the strap should be taut rather than slack (if you are using a strap).
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Drive your leg backward and upward in an arcing motion as you squeeze your glute.
    1. Rather than aiming to lift your leg to a particular height, you can focus on forcefully squeezing your glute, which can naturally promote the appropriate range.
    2. It is normal for the range of motion of the initial rep or two to be lower than subsequent reps, since the initial reps help build momentum.
    3. Throughout your set, keep your torso fully in contact with the pad and your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing your leg to arc downward and forward in a controlled manner.
    1. Keep the strap snugly against your lower leg for the entirety of each rep if you are using a strap.
    2. The standard technique for this exercise involves a controlled swinging motion that the machine’s pendulum facilitates, but you can optionally use a very strict technique where you emphasize slowing the descent as much as you can.
    3. While you can work with the pendulum’s momentum, avoid letting the momentum take over the movement in an uncontrolled manner. If you feel that the momentum is excessive despite your effort to control it, it may be appropriate to try a lighter load.
4. After your leg is close to pointing straight down, drive your leg backward and upward while squeezing your glute.
    1. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable to you, you can begin driving your leg back once it is vertical, a little before this point, or a little after this point.
    2. Letting your leg travel a bit further forward can naturally occur when using a controlled swinging technique, while stopping just before your leg reaches a fully vertical position can help promote a stricter technique with a slower descent tempo.
5. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. It is normal for some lower back motion to occur simultaneously with hip motion during this exercise. However, your lower back should not fully arch at the top or excessively round at the bottom when appropriately using your glute through the proper range of motion.
6. While keeping your leg snugly against the strap or roller, allow the pendulum’s momentum to subside until it stops swinging before switching legs or dismounting the machine.
    1. Guiding the pendulum through a few cycles of progressively shorter swings may be required for it to come to a dead stop.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus while also training your hamstrings, adductor magnus, and spinal erectors.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired weight onto the machine, adjust the seat or thigh pad height to a level that allows you to position your thighs snugly against the thigh pad while sitting with your feet flat on the floor.
    1. The ideal seat height should let you feel tension from the machine in the starting position when your arm is raised fully overhead with a straight elbow.
    2. Some machine models have an adjustable seat, others have an adjustable thigh pad, and some have both.
2. While facing the thigh pads and standing above the seat, firmly grasp the handle on the side you will train with an underhand grip (angling your palm backward).
    1. Grasping the handle at a point where your wrist is aligned with your shoulder (rather than being wider out or closer in) is often appropriate, but the specific handle placement can be influenced by your machine’s handle design.
3. Sit down on the machine’s seat with the lower portion of your thighs snugly pressed against the machine’s thigh pad. Let your elbow straighten in a controlled manner as your arm raises up overhead in line with the machine’s resistance.
    1. If you do not feel tension from the machine in this position (which may occur for certain individuals who are quite tall and have long arms), you may need to lower the seat height to experience resistance throughout the full range of motion for this exercise.
    2. Place your free hand in whichever position you prefer, such as holding the machine center handle near the thigh pad (if your machine has a center handle) or holding the torso pad.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbow down toward your side while progressively bending it to pull the handle downward. Simultaneosuly pull your shoulder blade down away from your ear.
    1. Keep your torso in the same position throughout your set rather than progressively leaning backward during the pull.
    2. During the initial portion of the pull, you will drive your elbow downward, but you will drive your elbow simultaneously backward and downward during the second half of the pull.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically leave your elbow level with or slightly behind your torso at the end of the pull.
2. Reverse the motion by allowing your elbow to lift upward and away from your torso as it progressively straightens to raise the handle in a controlled manner.
    1. Let your shoulder blade move naturally as it elevates with your arm.
    2. Finish with your arm overhead and your elbow fully straightened.
    3. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance yank your arm up in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lat, teres major, and rear delt while also meaningfully training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
2. Adjust the roller leg pad to a setting that will position it slightly above your heel when you are positioned on the torso pad after step 4.
    1. This can require some trial and error, since you will not know if it is in the appropriate position until you continue setting up.
    2. You can adjust the roller pad later on if you notice it is not situated correctly for your body.
3. After climbing up, lie down with your abdomen on the torso pad, and firmly grasp the handles. Position the top of your hip bones just on the edge of the pad.
    1. The appropriate positioning should allow your leg to move freely throughout the exercise.
    2. If you position yourself excessively far forward, the pad will restrict your hip motion.
    3. If you position yourself excessively far backward, you will be less stable and may round your lower back more than intended.
    4. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles, select the ones that allow you to hold them with mostly straight elbows.
    5. Some machine models will have a step to help you climb onto the pad.
4. Position the back of your working side’s lower leg snugly against the roller pad slightly above your heel. Keep your non-working side’s leg hanging down a little in front of the roller pad where it will not touch the roller pad during the exercise.
    1. If your working leg is the side closer to the weight, you can angle your free leg out to the side away from the roller pad, but this approach is not viable when training the side further away from the weight, since the free leg would get in the way of the lever arm.
    2. Letting your free hand down in front of the roller pad will work regardless of which side you are training.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Drive your leg backward and upward in an arcing motion as you squeeze your glute.
    1. Rather than aiming to lift your leg to a particular height, you can focus on forcefully squeezing your glute, which can naturally promote the appropriate range.
    2. It is normal for some lower back motion to occur simultaneously with hip motion during this exercise. However, your lower back should not excessively arch at the top when appropriately using your glute to lift the weight through the proper range of motion.
    3. Throughout your set, keep your torso fully in contact with the pad and your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing your leg to arc downward and forward in a controlled manner.
    1. At the bottom, you can allow the machine’s lever arm to tap the stopper lightly, which can confirm you have moved through the full range to the starting position in a controlled manner. Alternatively, you can stop descending just before that point.
    2. Avoid letting the weight swing you down in an uncontrolled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 3 and 4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus while also training your hamstrings, adductor magnus, and spinal erectors.## Setting up
1. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand centered between its handles in a hip-width stance while facing toward the base of the machine.
    1. If your machine model lets you adjust the handle height, you can initially set the handles to the lowest setting. If you cannot grasp the handles during the next step without rounding your lower back, you can subsequently raise the handles to a height that lets you grasp them with a flat-back position.
    2. Compared to facing away from the base of the machine, facing toward it can often reinforce sitting back to perform a high-quality hip hinge during the exercise.
2. While keeping your lower back flat, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles, it is generally recommended to grasp the lower handles to let you hinge through your full range of motion during the exercise.
3. Drive through your legs and thrust your hips forward to stand upright as you continue keeping your back flat and elbows straight.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you maintain a rigid back position.
2. While allowing the other side’s foot to lift up off the floor, begin hinging at the hip on the side to which you shifted your weight by sitting your buttocks back as you let your knee mildly bend.
    1. Allow your free leg to swing backward as it moves with your torso in a see-saw-like manner, but focus on hinging at the hip of your working leg and sitting back instead of kicking your other leg back and bending over.
    2. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat, chest up, elbows straightened, and working leg’s foot fully planted on the ground.
    3. To the best of your ability, keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your free leg drop down.
    4. Most of the motion should come from your hip, and your shin should remain in a fairly vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knee fully straightened.
    5. You do not need to focus on bending your knee, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks back while not intentionally keeping your knee locked.
3. Continuing hinging at your hip in a controlled manner through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically hinge until their hands are at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
4. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hip forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, hip bones level, and working leg’s sole fully in contact with the ground.
    2. After you are fully upright, you could place your free leg’s foot back onto the ground if you could use some additional balance assistance.
5. Alternate between steps 2-4 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise by switching which foot remains in contact with the ground.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
    3. After finishing the last rep with your other side, set the handles down with both feet on the ground by sitting your hips back and bending your knees.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine to a setting that will align the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are standing on the foot plate while leaning forward at your hips.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle on some machines. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel the most comfortable and natural.
    2. To train your rear delts most effectively, you ideally want to be able to adopt a torso position that is closer to horizontal than vertical, but the torso position you can assume (while keeping your shoulders aligned with the pivot points) will be affected by your body proportions and machine design.
    3. If you notice that the lowest machine setting does not let you lean your torso sufficiently far forward, you have the option of placing a step (or thick bumper plate) on the foot plate to facilitate a more horizontal torso.
    4. Some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal machine setting, but starting with the lowest setting and adjusting from there can often be appropriate.
2. Fully rotate the handles toward each other near their lowest setting.
3. Select your desired load with the machine’s loading pin, and stand centered on the foot plate facing toward the machine with the handles slightly in front of your torso (rather than situating your torso directly between the handles).
4. While letting your knees naturally bend and keeping your back flat, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as needed to grasp the handles with your elbows slightly bent and palms facing each other.
    1. If your machine has a head pad, you can optionally rest your forehead against it.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arms out and away from each other in a wide, arcing motion until your elbows are near shoulder height.
    1. Statically maintain your hinged torso position and slightly bent elbows throughout the set as you avoid moving your lower body or lower back.
    2. Let your shoulder blades move naturally throughout the exercise.
2. In a controlled arcing manner, reverse the movement by letting your arms move down and in toward each other until you have returned to the starting position with your hands close together.
    1. While you do not have to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the machine abruptly swing your arms down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps, rhomboids, and lateral delt, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Load an adjustable EZ bar with your desired weight, or approach a fixed-weight EZ bar.
    1. Either type of EZ bar can be used interchangeably for this exercise.
2. While facing the middle of the bar, squat down and firmly grasp it with your palms angled backward and in toward each other (resulting in a semi-overhand grip) as your hands are about shoulder width apart.
    1. With the zig-zag-like shape of the EZ bar, you may have to rotate it before a shoulder-width grip is accessible on the bar.
    2. While a semi-overhand grip is often used for this exercise, you can alternatively use a semi-underhand grip with your palms angled forward and inward.
3. While keeping your elbows straight, stand fully upright while gripping the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
2. While keeping your elbows straight, sweep your arms forward and upward in an arcing manner to lift the bar to approximately shoulder height.
    1. Allow your shoulder blades to move naturally rather than trying to keep them in any particular position.
    2. All the movement should occur from shoulder motion while you keep your legs and torso stationary throughout the exercise.
    3. At the top, your arms should be outstretched straight in front of you, parallel to the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until it is directly in front of your legs.
    1. You should finish in the starting position with your arms pointing down toward the ground.
    2. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, avoid letting gravity abruptly swing the weight downward.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your front delt while also training your upper pec major, lateral delt, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. Adjust each pulley/arm of a functional trainer or motorized dual cable machine (e.g., Tonal) to its lowest height or whichever height is needed for you to hip hinge through your full range of motion.
    1. The ideal setting should be low enough for you to experience a meaningful stretching sensation in your hamstrings at the bottom of your Romanian deadlift. Simultaneously, it should not be so low that you have to round your lower back when initially picking the bar up.
    2. When using a Tonal, the lowest height setting or the second-to-lowest height setting is typically appropriate. Lifters with flexibility limitations or short arms relative to their leg length may be more likely to be able to train through their full range of motion with the second-to-lowest arm height.
    3. When using a functional trainer, you should use the lowest pulley setting and will likely have to set up a step as well during step 3.
    4. Note that the point where you start feeling resistance after connecting the bar will be a bit higher than the pulleys/arms rather than being fully level with them.
2. If your machine has arms that can be swiveled inward or outward to modify the width between the arms, adjust them to where the distance between the arm ends matches the length of your bar attachment.
3. If the lowest height of your machine’s pulleys/arms is too high for you to be able to perform a full range of motion Romanian deadlift, position a sufficiently sturdy step centered between the pulleys/arms for you to stand on.
    1. If it is rated to withstand the combined weight of your body and the machine weight you will use, an aerobic step elevated on however many risers you need to train through your full range of motion can be a quality option.
    2. Stackable deadlift blocks can also work well, and a thick bumper plate may alternatively be used, but it is not ideal due to its surface not being fully flat and its height limitations. Depending on your hip width, you may position two bumper plates directly next to each other if the soles of your feet do not fit fully onto one plate in a hip-width stance, since you do not want part of your feet hanging off the edge of what you will use as a step.
    3. How tall the elevated surface should be will vary among different individuals, so trial and error will be needed to determine if you selected a sufficiently high elevation. If the machine stops providing resistance before you reach the end range to which your hamstring can comfortably stretch during the exercise, a taller object will be warranted.
4. Connect each end of a long bar attachment to the cables and select your desired resistance.
5. In an approximately hip-width stance, stand centered with the middle of your feet under the bar.
    1. Since the bar can move a bit without receiving any tension from the machine, you can initially pick up the bar slightly off the ground to position your feet under it. During this maneuver, you should keep the bar low enough that the machine is not providing any resistance when setting up.
    2. Setting up with your toes pointing straight ahead is a good starting position, but you can point your toes slightly out to the sides if that feels more comfortable to you.
6. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
7. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish.
    2. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    3. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    4. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than at the base of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hands while you hold it, which pulls on your hand skin uncomfortably and increases the risk of tearing a callus.
8. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
9. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
## Exercise technique
1. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the bar just in front of your legs. Avoid letting the bar drift forward away from you, which may be more likely to occur if you think about “bending over” rather than “sitting back.”
    2. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the bar.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    5. Throughout the set, keep your feet fully planted on the ground and your neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
2. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
3. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, feet fully on the ground, and the bar close to your legs.
    2. When you have finished lifting the bar, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
    3. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching that would pull your shoulders a little behind your hips. Squeezing your glutes at the end of the lifting phase can help you finish with your hips properly stacked under your shoulders, preventing excessive arching.
4. Alternate between steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down onto the ground.
    1. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe between reps in the fully upright position, rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
    2. After finishing the last rep, set the bar down in the same manner in which you initially picked it up (e.g., keeping your lower back flat rather than simply rounding over at your lower back).
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust each pulley/arm of a functional trainer or motorized dual cable machine (e.g., Tonal) to its lowest height.
    1. If you have mobility limitations and have to round your lower back in the starting position of your deadlift, you can increase the starting height to a level where you can maintain a flat lower back position.
    2. A height that lets you start with the bar near mid-shin or a little lower is typically appropriate.
    3. Note that the point where you start feeling resistance after connecting the bar will be a bit higher than the pulleys/arms rather than being fully level with them.
2. If your machine has arms that can be swiveled inward or outward to modify the width between the arms, adjust them to where the distance between the arm ends matches the length of your bar attachment.
3. If the lowest height of your machine’s pulleys/arms is too high for you to be able to perform a standard range of motion deadlift, position a sufficiently sturdy step centered between the pulleys/arms for you to stand on.
    1. A step will typically be needed for a functional trainer that lacks movable arms.
    2. If it is rated to withstand the combined weight of your body and the machine weight you will use, an aerobic step elevated on however many risers you need to train through your full range of motion can be a quality option.
    3. Stackable deadlift blocks can also work well, and a thick bumper plate may alternatively be used, but it is not ideal due to its surface not being fully flat and its height limitations. Depending on your hip width, you may position two bumper plates directly next to each other if the soles of your feet do not fit fully onto one plate in a hip-width stance, since you do not want part of your feet hanging off the edge of what you will use as a step.
    4. How tall the elevated surface should be will vary among different individuals, so trial and error will be needed to determine if you selected a sufficiently high elevation. If the machine stops providing resistance when the bar is above mid-shin height, a taller object will likely be warranted.
4. Connect each end of a long bar attachment to the cables and select your desired resistance.
5. In an approximately hip-width stance, stand centered with the middle of your feet under the bar.
    1. Since the bar can move a bit without receiving any tension from the machine, you can initially pick up the bar slightly off the ground to position your feet under it. During this maneuver, you should keep the bar low enough that the machine is not providing any resistance when setting up.
    2. Setting up with your toes pointing straight ahead is a good starting position, but you can point your toes slightly out to the sides if that feels more comfortable to you.
6. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
7. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
    1. As an alternative to the double overhand grip, you can use a mixed grip (with one palm facing forward and the other facing backward) or lifting straps with the double overhand grip if you wish.
    2. Your arms should be directed straight down such that they form approximately 90-degree angles with the bar.
    3. For the most stable grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles.
    4. If you position the bar closer to the middle of your palms (rather than at the base of your fingers), the bar can slide down your hands while you hold it, which pulls on your hand skin uncomfortably and increases the risk of tearing a callus.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
2. Before lifting the bar, apply “pre-tension” to the bar as you develop tension throughout your whole body and start pressing the middle of your feet into the ground with enough force to tense the cables.
    1. Doing so can help you wedge your hips into an advantageous starting position and lock your back into a stable position as the muscles running up the length of your back are fully tensed.
3. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar as you “push the floor away” while extending (straightening) your hips and knees. Prioritize keeping your chest up, lower back flat, the bar close to your legs, and your core braced throughout the entire exercise.
    1. As you move about your knees and hips, your torso should remain in a similar position when initiating the pull rather than inclining further toward horizontal, which could occur if your hips shoot up from not driving into the ground appropriately.
4. After the bar reaches near the middle of your shins, focus on thrusting your hips forward as you continue extending your hips and knees until you are standing fully upright with your chest up and shoulders back.
    1. Lock out the rep with your glutes squeezed as you avoid leaning your torso back through excessive lower back arching, which can occur if you do not focus on squeezing your glutes.
    2. Once upright, you should be able to draw a straight line from your legs through your hips to your shoulders when viewed from the side.
5. Reverse the movement by hinging at your hips while bending your knees to lower the bar until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. Just like when lifting the bar, keep your lower back flat and chest up as you descend while actively avoiding letting your lower back round or chest collapse.
    2. You can initially focus on sitting your buttocks back while keeping your shins vertical and allowing your knees to bend naturally until the bar has cleared your knees. Then you can allow your knees to track mildly forward as you further bend your knees and hips to finish lowering the bar.
6. Pause in the bottom position for a moment before continuing to alternate between steps 2-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. While you do not need to pause in the bottom position for more than a second between reps, there should not be any momentum transferred between reps.
    2. You will be able to brace most effectively when you breathe in between reps while the weight is on the ground rather than during a rep. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before the next rep.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this time-tested exercise can meaningfully train your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus, along with your quads to a lesser degree.## Setting up
1. Adjust each pulley/arm of a functional trainer or motorized dual cable machine (e.g., Tonal) to its lowest height or whichever height is needed for you to row through a full range of motion with your torso angled close to horizontal.
    1. The lowest height will be appropriate unless you have mobility limitations and have to round your lower back when initially picking the bar up from that setting.
    2. Note that the point where you start feeling resistance after connecting the bar will be a bit higher than the pulleys/arms rather than being fully level with them.
2. If your machine has arms that can be swiveled inward or outward to modify the width between the arms, adjust them to where the distance between the arm ends matches the length of your bar attachment.
3. If the lowest height of your machine’s pulleys/arms is too high for the bar to provide resistance when your torso is close to horizontal with straight elbows, position a sufficiently sturdy step centered between the pulleys/arms for you to stand on.
    1. A step will typically be needed for a functional trainer that lacks movable arms.
    2. If it is rated to withstand the combined weight of your body and the machine weight you will use, an aerobic step elevated on however many risers you need to train through your full range of motion can be a quality option.
    3. Stackable deadlift blocks can also work well, and a thick bumper plate may alternatively be used, but it is not ideal due to its surface not being fully flat and its height limitations. Depending on your hip width, you may position two bumper plates directly next to each other if the soles of your feet do not fit fully onto one plate in a hip-width stance, since you do not want part of your feet hanging off the edge of what you will use as a step.
    4. How tall the elevated surface should be will vary among different individuals, so trial and error will be needed to determine if you selected a sufficiently high elevation. If the machine stops providing resistance before you reach the end range to which you can hinge at your hips into the bent-over position, a taller object will be warranted.
4. Connect each end of a long bar attachment to the cables and select your desired resistance.
5. In an approximately hip-width stance, stand centered with the middle of your feet under the bar.
    1. Since the bar can move a bit without receiving any tension from the machine, you can initially pick up the bar slightly off the ground to position your feet under it. During this maneuver, you should keep the bar low enough that the machine is not providing any resistance when setting up.
    2. Setting up with your toes pointing straight ahead is a good starting position, but you can point your toes slightly out to the sides if that feels more comfortable to you.
6. With your arms pointing straight down toward the ground, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track far forward. However, you can let your knees shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
7. With straight elbows, firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip, such that your palms face backward.
8. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point on.
9. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
10. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as your hamstring flexibility permits with a flat back.
    1. To avoid rounding your lower back, you should think about sitting back rather than bending over.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical (or close to vertical) throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively pushing your knees forward depends on your hamstring flexibility.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your lower body should remain nearly stationary.
    3. You can aim to row the bar until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. While lifting the bar, allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, which may result in your upper arms forming near a 45-degree angle with your torso toward the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the bar down by bending your knees further.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to a barbell situated on the ground under the head of an inclined bench.
    1. Ideally set the bench to an incline angle that lets you just reach the bar with straight elbows and shoulders pulled forward once you are resting your torso on the bench.
    2. Which angle is best will be affected by the diameter of the weight plates you add to the bar and your arm length. Using a higher incline will be better for lifters with long arms who use full-diameter plates (like 45lb (20kg) plates or bumper plates) compared to lifters with short arms who load the bar exclusively with smaller diameter plates.
    3. While trying out different bench angles may be helpful, initially selecting an incline near 30-45 degrees is often appropriate.
2. Lie down with your chest contacting the head of the bench and your legs spread out as you position the balls of your feet on the ground.
3. Reach down and firmly grasp the bar with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
    1. A slightly outside shoulder-width is often appropriate, but feel free to adjust your grip width based on your preference.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue keeping your core braced from this point on.
2. Pull the bar up toward your upper abdomen/lower chest by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward while pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Rather than lifting your chest up through excessive lower back arching, keep your chest and the rest of your torso in contact with the bench throughout your set.
    2. Row through your full range of motion, which ideally results in your elbows finishing at shoulder height or close to this point. Your setup can affect your end range, since the bottom of your bench may physically determine how high you may lift the bar.
    3. Rather than fully flaring your elbows straight out to the sides or keeping them fully tucked by your sides, let your elbows naturally flare out away from your sides to a degree that feels comfortable to you.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the bar in a controlled manner until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. If your bench setup permits, lower the bar until your elbows have fully straightened.
    2. If the plates reach the ground before your elbows have fully straightened, you can finish lowering the bar once the plates lightly tap the ground.
    3. Allow the weight to pull your shoulder blades apart to stretch your upper back muscles.
    4. You do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, but you should actively control the entire descent rather than letting the weight pull your arms down rapidly.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to place the bar back down onto the ground.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train much of the musculature constituting your middle and upper back, including your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s foot plate to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are standing on the foot plate.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the foot plate height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    3. To confirm whether you selected an appropriate foot plate height, you will have to stand on it and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
2. Select your desired load with the machine’s loading pin, and stand on the foot plate.
3. While facing sideways relative to the weight stack and toward the handle you will not use, align the side of your shoulder (that you will train during this set) with the machine’s pivot point.
4. With your arm closer to the handle, firmly grasp the handle with your palm facing backward (i.e., overhand grip) while standing with a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Based on your preference, you can keep your elbow fully straight or maintain a slight bend in your elbow throughout the exercise.
    2. In the starting position, you should be standing upright with your arm directed down toward the ground.
    3. Situate your free hand in a comfortable position, such as resting by your side.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arm backward and upward in an arcing manner to drive the handle behind you through your shoulder’s available range of motion. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade backward as you avoid letting your shoulder tip forward.
    1. How far you will be able to pull your arm back will be affected by your shoulder mobility, and the exercise will become progressively more difficult as you move your arm further back.
    2. All the movement should occur from shoulder motion while you keep your legs and torso stationary throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the motion by letting your arm move forward toward the starting position in a controlled manner.
    1. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack, or you can stop just before this point.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arm forward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can train your lats, teres major, and rear delt at short muscle lengths that may be suboptimal for hypertrophy training.## Setting up
1. Adjust the seat height so that your upper arms can be parallel with the floor or a little lower while your forearms are supported by the pads and you are in a position where you could comfortably grasp the handles.
    1. Some people notice that their shoulders feel more comfortable when the seat is set to a height that allows their elbows to be slightly lower than their shoulders, compared to when their elbows are completely level with their shoulders. Consequently, if you find that your shoulders do not feel the best when performing this exercise with your elbows at shoulder height, you can try increasing the seat height to let you utilize a slightly lesser degree of elbow flare.
    2. If your machine lacks handles, you can naturally ignore the details about the handles and otherwise use the same setup and exercise technique.
2. Select your weight with the loading pin, and sit down on the seat with your back against the back pad and your feet flat on the floor.
3. With your elbows bent by about 90 degrees, spread your arms backward into a “T-position” through your full range of motion.
    1. Note how far backward your shoulder mobility permits you to spread your arms apart comfortably, since this will indicate where you should adjust the lever arms.
4. Adjust the machine’s lever arms to a position that allows you to situate your arms just behind the arm pads when your upper arms are fully spread apart in the “T-position.”
    1. The ideal starting position of the lever arms should allow you to perform this exercise through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    2. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your chest or the front of the shoulders after you assume the starting position, you likely can move the lever arms a bit further back. Note that some machines have lever arms that cannot be set to a starting position that meaningfully stretches your chest, and other machines do not let you adjust the starting position of their lever arms.
    3. Some trial and error may be required to find what is personally the best setting for you.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to adopt a “proud chest” posture.
6. With your elbow bent by about 90 degrees and pointing out to the side, rest your working side’s arm against the pad and grasp the handle.
    1. Your upper arm and torso should now form half of a “T-shape” when viewed from the front.
    2. Place your non-working arm into a comfortable position.
    3. While you can initially rotate your torso toward your working side to help set your arm in the starting position, your torso should not be twisted toward one side when beginning your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbow forward and inward in an arcing manner until it is directly in front of your shoulder.
    1. While you do not have to keep your shoulder blade fully pulled back, avoid letting your shoulder excessively round forward into a slouched posture.
    2. Maintain the same degree of elbow bend throughout the exercise, as all of the movement occurs from motion of your working shoulder.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting your elbow arc out to the side and backward through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Adjust the seat height so that your upper arms can be parallel with the floor or a little lower while your forearms are supported by the pads and you are in a position where you can comfortably grasp the handles.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed the set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also meaningfully training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and adjust the seat height so that the handles are near shoulder height when you are seated.
2. Adjust the pins above the handles to set the handles back as close together (on the side of the chest pad) as your machine enables.
3. Sit down sideways on the seat with what will be your working side further away from the chest pad.
    1. Rest your feet on the floor with your knees bent.
    2. Compared to sitting with your chest against the pad, sitting sideways can let you train through a longer range of motion while loading your rear delt in a more lengthened position. However, you can alternatively set up with your chest symmetrically against the pad if you wish.
4. With your arm further away from the chest pad, reach across your body to grasp the handle while simultaneously turning your chest toward the handle to help you reach it.
    1. This type of machine typically has vertical handles that you can grasp with your palm facing inward (for a neutral grip), but you also have the option of grasping the horizontal part connected to the vertical handle with your palm facing down (for an overhand grip). Which part you hold is up to your preference.
    2. To grasp the handle, you can initially stand up before sitting back down during the next step if that helps you reach it.
5. While holding the handle with a straight or slightly bent elbow, turn your chest until it is facing the same direction as your imaginary belt buckle.
    1. In this starting position, your working arm should be situated fully across your body, which should prompt you to feel a stretching sensation in the back of your shoulder.
    2. Position your free hand wherever you prefer throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Without bending your elbow, smoothly sweep your arm outward and across your body in a wide arcing motion.
    1. For the standard version of this exercise, you can finish once your arm is directed straight out to the side of your shoulder, resulting in your torso and arm forming half of a “T-shape.”
    2. While you move your arm through shoulder motion, keep the rest of your body stationary throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your arm to sweep fluidly in toward the starting position through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Just like when you finished setting up, you should feel a stretching sensation in the back of your shoulder as your arm is fully across your body.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should actively resist the weight rather than letting it abruptly yank your arm inward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps and rhomboids, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
Some machine models have a stationary foot platform and a mobile seat that moves back when pressing into the foot platform, while others have a mobile foot platform and a seat that does not move during the exercise. Either type of machine can be similarly used, but certain details may differ based on the machine type you use, so their setups are presented separately.

### Setting up a machine with a stationary foot platform

1. Select your desired weight with the loading pin and adjust the machine’s seat to a position that allows your knees to be mildly bent when your feet are on the foot platform.
    1. If the seat is positioned too far back, the machine may not be able to provide tension throughout your full ankle range of motion.
2. Sit down with your back in full contact with the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture from sitting your hips excessively forward, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
3. With a hip-width stance, place the balls of your feet onto the foot platform with your heels hanging off the platform.
4. Grasp the handles for additional stability and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. While letting your heels move slightly forward to stretch your calves, straighten your knees to press yourself away from the foot platform and move the seat backward.
    1. After doing so, your knees should either be straight or very slightly bent. If you have the mobility to hyperextend your knees (which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position), you can avoid hyperextending them.
    2. The purpose of this step is to place your calves into a stretched starting position to help you train through a longer range of ankle motion compared to if you started with your ankles in a neutral position (like when you are normally standing upright and your foot and shin form a 90-degree angle). Ideally, you should now feel a stretching sensation in your calves.
6. Lift your non-working side’s foot off the foot platform and position it in a comfortable position on the ground.

### Setting up a machine with a mobile foot platform

1. Select your desired weight with the loading pin and adjust the machine’s seat to a position that allows your knees to be mildly bent when your feet are on the foot platform.
    1. If the seat is positioned too far back, the machine may not be able to provide tension throughout your full ankle range of motion.
2. Sit down with your back in full contact with the backrest.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture from sitting your hips excessively forward, your back should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
3. Place the ball of your working side’s foot onto the foot platform with your heel hanging off the platform.
    1. Situate your non-working side’s foot in a comfortable position, such as resting on the ground.
4. Grasp the handles for additional stability and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. While letting your heel move slightly forward to stretch your calf, straighten your knee to press your foot into the platform.
    1. After doing so, your knee should either be straight or very slightly bent. If you have the mobility to hyperextend your knee (which is when the knee bends backward beyond a straight position), you can avoid hyperextending it.
    2. The purpose of this step is to place your calves into a stretched starting position to help you train through a longer range of ankle motion compared to if you started with your ankle in a neutral position (like when you are normally standing upright and your foot and shin form a 90-degree angle). Ideally, you should now feel a stretching sensation in your calf.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the ball of your foot to press the platform fully away from you and point your feet forward, in a manner similar to stepping on a car’s gas pedal.
    1. If your machine has a mobile seat and a fixed platform, you can have the same intent, but you will press yourself away from the platform instead of pressing the platform away from you. With this machine style, you will similarly perform the reverse of that motion during the next step.
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep the ball of your foot firmly planted on the platform as movement occurs exclusively at your ankle without any knee or hip motion.
2. Reverse the movement by letting the ball of your foot arc back toward your shins in a controlled manner through the full range of motion permitted by your calf flexibility and machine.
    1. Ideally, you should feel a deep stretching sensation in your calf at the end of this step.
    2. Avoid letting your knee bend.
    3. While you do not have to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, avoid rapidly moving and trying to bounce into the next rep.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. Following your last rep, bend your knee to lower the weight you selected onto the rest of the weight stack.
    2. If you feel your foot start sliding down on the platform during your set, make sure to shift it back up to keep the ball of your foot on the platform.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the gastrocnemius and soleus that constitute your calves.## Setting up
Note that only some machine models have handles that are comfortable to grasp with an underhand grip.

1. Select your weight with the loading pin, and adjust the seat to a height that lets your torso rest comfortably against the pad and the row to feel natural.
    1. Setting the seat to a height that positions the handles just below your lower chest or near this point once you are seated is often an appropriate starting point, but the ideal reference point for the seat height varies among different machine models.
    2. If your machine has one pair of horizontal handles that can be used for an overhand grip or an underhand grip, it will generally be preferable to set the seat higher when using an underhand grip compared to an overhand grip.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height for you with the particular machine model you are using. If the exercise feels awkward regardless of which seat height you try, the machine model you are using may simply be better suited for rowing with a different grip.
2. Adjust the torso pad to a depth that lets you just grasp the handles when your arms are fully outstretched in front of you with straight elbows.
    1. To row through your full range of motion, ideally place the chest pad far enough back that you have to push your shoulder blades forward and apart to grasp the handles with your elbows fully straight.
3. Sit down with your chest resting snugly against the torso pad and your feet on the floor before reaching fully forward with straight elbows to grasp the handles firmly with your palms angled upward.
    1. While some machines have horizontal handles that let you grasp them with a purely underhand grip, others will have handles that angle downward and inward, allowing you to adopt a semi-underhand grip. Either type of grip can be similarly used for this exercise.
    2. If you were able to grasp the handles with any amount of elbow bend, you could let go of the handles and adjust the chest pad before grasping the handles again.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward the sides of your torso by driving your elbows backward while simultaneously bending them and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Move your elbows through a path of motion that feels comfortable to you, which will typically involve keeping them tucked close to your sides with this grip rather than notably flaring your elbows away from your sides (which is typically more comfortable with a wider overhand grip).
    2. Keep your chest in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    3. Pull through your available range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being level with your torso or slightly behind your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner, progressively straightening until they are outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the bottom. This will stretch your upper back muscles and may help you train them more effectively.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This row primarily trains your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids, which are assisted by your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of either the machine’s foot plate or the machine’s pivot points to a level that aligns your shoulders with the pivot point once you are standing on the foot plate.
    1. Which part of the machine can be adjusted in height varies based on the machine model you have.
    2. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    3. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the foot plate height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    4. To confirm whether you set up the machine appropriately, you will have to stand on it and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
2. If you are using a multi flight machine that lets you adjust the starting position of its handles, rotate the handles as high up as your machine allows.
    1. Standing pec fly machines typically do not let you adjust the starting position of the handles, but they can be used for this variation similarly to multi flight machines.
3. Select your desired load with the machine’s loading pin, and stand on the foot plate.
4. While facing sideways relative to the weight stack and toward the handle you will use, align the side of your shoulder (that you will train during this set) with the machine’s pivot point.
5. With your arm closer to the handle, reach up and firmly grasp the handle with your palm facing backward (i.e., overhand grip) while standing with a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Based on your preference, you can keep your elbow fully straight or maintain a slight bend in your elbow throughout the exercise.
    2. In the starting position, you should be standing upright with your arm directed down toward the ground.
    3. Situate your free hand in a comfortable position, such as resting by your side.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arm down and back in an arcing manner to pull the handle toward you until it is right by your thigh.
    1. All the movement should occur from shoulder motion while you keep your legs and torso stationary throughout the exercise.
2. Reverse the motion by letting your arm move forward and upward toward the starting position in a controlled manner.
    1. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack, or you can stop just before this point.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arm upward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres major, and rear delt.## Setting up
1. Adjust the height of the seat so that the middle points of the handles will be approximately level with your mid-chest once you are seated.
    1. Some lifters may find that their shoulders feel more comfortable when they can grasp the handle near lower chest height, while others may prefer to set the seat where they can grasp the handle at shoulder height, so feel free to adjust the seat to a position that helps the exercise feel best to you.
    2. After initially adjusting the seat, you will naturally have to sit down to confirm whether the height is appropriate.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit with your back against the backrest, and spread your arms backward into a “T-position” through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. People with normal shoulder mobility are able to spread their arms back until their elbows are slightly behind their shoulders.
4. Adjust the pins above the handles to a position that lets you comfortably grasp the handles when your arms are fully spread apart in this “T-position.”
    1. After initially adjusting the handles, you can recheck to see if they are positioned appropriately for you.
    2. If the handles are excessively far back relative to your shoulder mobility, it may be difficult or uncomfortable to grasp them.
    3. If the handles are not sufficiently far back, the range of motion you can use for this exercise will be limited, and you will not be able to train your chest in a stretched position.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture.
6. Raise your working side’s arm until it is close to parallel to the ground, and reach backward until you can grasp the handle with a slight bend in your elbow as your palm faces forward.
    1. You can rotate your chest toward the handle to grasp it more easily before rotating your chest back to face the same direction as your imaginary belt buckle.
    2. Place your non-working arm into a comfortable position.
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your elbow mildly bent and your torso stationary, sweep your arm forward and inward in an arcing motion until your arm is outstretched in front of your chest.
    1. While you do not have to keep your shoulder blade fully pulled back, avoid letting your shoulder excessively round forward into a slouched posture.
    2. Maintain the same degree of elbow bend throughout the exercise, as all of the movement occurs from motion of your working shoulder.
2. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting your arm arc out to the side and backward through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. You should finish in a “proud chest” posture while feeling a stretching sensation in your chest and the front of your shoulder.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, avoid letting the machine’s resistance abruptly swing your arm back in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed the set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s foot plate to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are standing on the foot plate.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the foot plate height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    3. To confirm whether you selected an appropriate foot plate height, you will have to stand on it and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
    4. A foot plate height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points will commonly allow you to hold the handles with slightly bent elbows.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin, and stand on the foot plate with the handles situated just in front of your thighs.
3. With the side of your body you will train during this set, firmly grasp the handle in front of it with a slightly bent elbow as your palm faces in toward your other side.
    1. Situate your free hand into your preferred position, which may involve holding the other handle, grasping a center hand grip available with certain machines, or resting it by your side.
4. Optionally lean your torso slightly forward at your hips by sitting your buttocks slightly backward with a very slight knee bend. You can alternatively stand fully upright if you prefer to do so.
    1. Maintain your preferred torso position throughout your set, as you subsequently move your shoulders without moving your lower body or torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep the handle out to the side and upward in a smooth, arcing manner until your elbow is near shoulder height.
    1. Let your shoulder blade move naturally rather than trying to hold it in any particular position.
    2. At the top of the raise, your arm should form half of a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from the front or back.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by letting your arm lower in toward the starting position.
    1. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the weights you selected against the rest of the weight stack, or you can stop just before this point.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arm downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine to a setting that will align the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are standing on the foot plate while leaning forward at your hips.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle on some machines. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel the most comfortable and natural.
    2. To train your rear delts most effectively, you ideally want to be able to adopt a torso position that is closer to horizontal than vertical, but the torso position you can assume (while keeping your shoulders aligned with the pivot points) will be affected by your body proportions and machine design.
    3. If you notice that the lowest machine setting does not let you lean your torso sufficiently far forward, you have the option of placing a step (or thick bumper plate) on the foot plate to facilitate a more horizontal torso.
    4. Some trial and error may be needed to find your ideal machine setting, but starting with the lowest setting and adjusting from there can often be appropriate.
2. Fully rotate the handles toward each other near their lowest setting.
3. Select your desired load with the machine’s loading pin, and stand centered on the foot plate facing toward the machine with the handles slightly in front of your torso (rather than situating your torso directly between the handles).
4. While letting your knees naturally bend and keeping your back flat, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as needed for the handles to be within reach.
    1. If your machine has a head pad, you can optionally rest your forehead against it.
5. With the side you are about to train, firmly grasp the handle with your elbow slightly bent and your palm facing in.
    1. Keep your free arm in a comfortable position throughout your set, which could involve holding the other handle with your non-working arm stationary throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep your arm out to your side and upward in a wide, arcing motion until your elbow is near shoulder height.
    1. Statically maintain your hinged torso position and slightly bent elbow throughout the set as you avoid moving your lower body or lower back.
    2. Let your shoulder blade move naturally throughout the exercise.
2. In a controlled arcing manner, reverse the movement by lowering your arm in toward the starting position with the handle under your shoulder.
    1. While you do not have to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, you should avoid letting the machine abruptly swing your arm down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your rear delt, which is assisted by your traps, rhomboids, and lateral delt, along with your rotator cuff’s infraspinatus, teres minor, and supraspinatus.## Setting up
1. If your machine lets you adjust the angle of its backrest, fully recline it.
    1. A variety of machines have a backrest that is permanently reclined.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin and set the machine’s sled to a position that allows your knees to be mildly bent when your feet are on the foot plate.
    1. If the sled is positioned too far back, the machine may not be able to provide tension throughout your full ankle range of motion.
3. Lie down with your shoulders snugly against the shoulder pads and your back firmly supported by the backrest.
    1. Based on your preference and your machine’s design, you can decide to rest your head against the pad or keep it just off the pad. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. With a hip-width to shoulder-width stance, position your feet onto the bottom of the foot plate with your heels just above the foot plate’s edge.
5. While grasping the hand grips (if they are present), drive your feet into the platform to push yourself away from it until your knees are straight.
    1. Depending on their mobility, some people may be able to adopt a position of knee hyperextension, which is when the knees bend backward beyond a straight position. Being able to reach a mildly hyperextended knee position is normal for many individuals and is not inherently problematic. However, certain individuals may experience knee discomfort if they utilize a hyperextended knee position during this exercise, so keeping a straight or slightly bent knee position instead of a hyperextended knee position may be preferable.
6. Walk your feet down until the front halves of your feet are on the bottom of the foot plate with your heels hanging off its lower edge.
    1. Aim to bear your weight through the balls of your feet, and maintain this foot position throughout your set.
7. To assume the starting position, let the machine’s resistance push your heels forward under the foot plate until you feel a stretching sensation in your calves.
## Exercise technique
1. Push through the balls of your feet to press the sled away from the foot plate and angle your feet forward away from you.
    1. Throughout your set, motion should occur exclusively at your ankles, while knees and hips do not move.
    2. This is the same ankle motion that occurs when fully lifting your heels up while standing on the ground.
2. While keeping the balls of your feet planted on the foot plate, reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting your heels move forward under the foot plate through the full range of motion your calf flexibility permits.
    1. If you do not feel a stretching sensation in your calf at the end, you likely are not moving through your full range of motion.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, avoid letting the machine’s weight abruptly pull your heels forward, and refrain from bouncing into the next rep.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, you can walk feet up until your heels are resting on the bottom of the foot plate before bending your knees to return the sled to its original position. Alternatively, you can bend your knees to return the sled to its original position while your heels are still hanging off the edge of the foot plate.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your gastrocnemius and soleus while simultaneously improving calf flexibility through loaded stretching.## Setting up
To be able to hinge through your full range of motion, the machine’s safety arm (on which its lever arm rests) will have to be disengaged prior to starting your set. Some machines are designed to have the safety arm automatically disengage after lifting the lever arm up, while others require you to pull the handle back with your hand to disengage it. Steps 2-4 of the setup describe how to adjust a machine that involves manually disengaging the safety arm. If your machine has the automatic disengagement feature, you can add weight plates to the machine during step 1 and skip steps 2-4 of the setup.

1. Connect a straight bar attachment or a V-bar row handle (AKA double D-handle) to the machine.
    1. If you prefer to use an overhand grip (with your palms facing backward), select a straight bar attachment, whether that be a standard lat pulldown bar with a long straight body and angled ends, or a straight bar closer to shoulder-width in length.
    2. If you prefer to use a neutral grip (with your palms facing each other), select a V-bar row handle.
    3. Neither attachment type is inherently better than the other, so feel free to try out both to determine which one personally feels better to you when performing this exercise.
    4. Different machine models vary in their design, since some machines have a lever arm with a single attachment point, while others have a lever arm with multiple attachment points.
    5. When using a lever arm with multiple attachment points, the machine will provide less resistance when using a point near the base of the lever arm compared to the free end of the lever arm. Consequently, the specific lever attachment point can affect the difficulty of each rep, in addition to how much weight you add to the machine via plates. Consistently connecting the bar to the attachment point furthest from the base of the machine will minimize how many plates you have to add to the machine to provide your intended level of resistance.
2. Without any weight plates added to the machine, stand on the platform with the bar between your feet.
3. Squat down to grasp the bar with one hand, and stand upright with it before using your other hand to pull the handles back toward you to disengage the safety horn.
    1. It is unusual to grasp the center of a straight bar attachment with one hand or only one part of a V-bar row handle, but you will not be lifting meaningful resistance without any plates added to the machine, so the awkward grip is not problematic.
    2. If you prefer, you have the option of disengaging the safety horn while wearing a belt connected to the machine prior to swapping a bar for the belt.
4. Squat down and set the bar onto the platform before adding weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
5. Step up onto the platform with the bar’s attachment point centered between the middle of your feet in a hip-width stance.
6. While keeping your back flat, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the bar with straight elbows.
    1. Regardless of whether you use an overhand or neutral grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles for the most stable grip.
7. Without rounding your back, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and take a small step backward with your other foot so that its toes are close to being just slightly behind your lead foot’s heel while keeping your feet hip-width apart. Place the balls and toes of your rear foot on the ground while keeping its midfoot and heel off the ground.
    1. Naturally let your knees slightly bend.
    2. Avoid stepping inward to the extent that your rear foot is directly behind your lead foot. Keeping your feet hip-width apart can help keep you stable.
    3. The size of your machine’s platform can constrain where you position your rear foot, so you may not be able to step as far back with your rear leg when using certain machines.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you maintain a rigid back position.
3. While continuing to bear almost all your weight through your lead foot, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as you let your knees mildly bend.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat, chest up, and lead foot fully planted on the platform.
    2. Your rear leg’s purpose is to assist with maintaining your balance so that the exercise can feel more stable than a true single leg Romanian deadlift.
    3. If scales were under both of your feet, the scale under your lead foot should ideally show that you are bearing at least 90% of your weight through your front leg.
    4. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the bar just in front of you.
    5. Strive to keep your lead leg’s shin vertical with your weight distributed throughout the entirety of your lead foot. If you concentrate all your weight through your heel, your knee may track backward into an excessively straightened position. If you bear too much weight through the front of your foot, your knee may track forward and bend excessively.
    6. While motion will occur at both hips, you should focus on sitting your lead leg’s hip back (as if you were trying to close a car door with your buttocks) while letting your rear leg’s hip naturally move with it.
4. In a controlled manner, continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
5. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and lead foot fully on the platform.
    2. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
7. Following your last rep, step forward with your rear foot to position your feet symmetrically before repeating the exercise with your other leg in the lead position.
    1. Once you are ready to set the bar down, step forward again to position your feet symmetrically before sitting your hips back and bending your knees to lower the bar.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with your middle-to-upper chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is excessively low or high, the exercise may feel awkward and uncomfortable.
2. If your machine allows you to do so, adjust the stoppers (that are positioned under the arms) to a height that allows you to press through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Ideally, position the stoppers as low as you can while being able to grip the handles comfortably with elbows stacked under your wrists in the starting position.
    2. Finding the optimal height may require some trial and error, but you can initially position the stoppers to the bottom setting before performing a couple of warm-up reps without any additional load on the machine. If you experience shoulder discomfort getting into the starting position or are unable to grasp the handles with proper positioning, the stoppers are likely too low, and you should try raising them. Otherwise, the stopper height is appropriate, and you can proceed to add weight to the machine.
    3. Certain machine models do not allow you to adjust the stoppers.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Lie down on the machine’s back pad with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against it.
    1. Your buttocks should be resting on the seat, and your feet should be flat on the ground under your bent knees.
    2. If the back pad is sufficiently tall, rest your head against it. Otherwise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. On the side of your body you will train during this set, firmly grasp the handle while positioning your elbow directly under your wrist, such that your forearm is in a vertical position when viewed from the side.
    1. Hold the handle on top of the base of your palm (AKA the heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip throughout your set. Squeezing the handle firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Whether you can grasp the handle with your palm facing inward (i.e., neutral grip) or forward (i.e., overhand grip) depends on how the handles are designed for the specific machine you use.
    3. If your machine has sufficiently long handles, you may be able to adjust your grip width with different hand placements. Grasping the handle where it would result in a moderately outside shoulder-width grip if you used both handles works well for many lifters, but you can try out different grip widths to determine where you personally feel best.
    4. Aligning your elbow under your wrist and the handle will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    5. You can position your non-working arm wherever feels most comfortable and stable to you. Some people like to grasp the non-working side’s handle without moving it during the set, while others like to rest the free hand somewhere on the body.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. While keeping your forearm aligned with the angle at which you are pressing, press the handle upward until you have straightened your elbow.
    1. You can think about driving your elbow upward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, buttocks on the seat, back against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handle in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Let your elbow naturally flare out away from your side to the degree that keeps your elbows vertically stacked under your wrist and the handle.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arm abruptly down.
    3. At the end range, you can lightly tap the machine’s arm against the stopper under it or stop just before this point.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
1. If your machine has an adjustable seat height, set it to a level that aligns your shoulders or chest with the handles when lying down on the pad.
    1. If you grasp the handles when they are aligned with your shoulders when viewed from the side, your arms will form 90-degree angles with your torso when viewed from the front.
    2. Some people find that their shoulders feel more comfortable when grasping the handles if they are aligned with the middle-to-lower chest, which reduces the degree to which the elbows will be flared away from the sides.
    3. Some trial and error may be needed to find the ideal seat height for you.
    4. Certain machine models lack an adjustable seat height and, alternatively, have rather long handles that let you vary your shoulder position based on how high up you grasp them.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
    1. Certain machine models position their loading sleeves close enough together that you avoid loading them with full-diameter weight plates (e.g., 45lb (20kg) plates) to be able to train through a full range of motion.
3. Lie down on the machine’s back pad with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against it while your buttocks are resting on the seat.
    1. Place your feet flat on the ground under your bent knees.
    2. Based on your preference, you can decide to rest your head against the pad or keep it slightly off the pad. Regardless, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. With your working side’s arm, grasp the handle with your arm flared out about 90 degrees from your side or a little lower if that feels more comfortable to you.
    1. You can rotate your body as needed to grasp the handle without exceeding the comfortable limit of your shoulder mobility. If you use this approach, make sure to have your upper back positioned symmetrically on the pad before starting your set.
    2. Some lifters may prefer to grasp the handle initially sitting upright on the seat before lying down.
    3. You can position your non-working arm wherever feels most comfortable and stable to you. Some people like to grasp the non-working side’s handle without moving it during the set, while others like to reach across the body and hold the torso with the free hand.
5. After setting your grip, hold the handle with a mildly bent elbow and pull your shoulder blades back together with a “proud chest” posture.
    1. Maintain this “proud chest” posture with your upper back planted on the bench throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull your arms together to move the handles upward and in toward each other until they are just about to touch.
2. Sweep your arm forward and inward in a wide arcing motion, as if you were hugging a thick barrel with one arm.
    1. You can finish when your elbow is aligned with your shoulder (rather than being further out to the side) when viewed from the front, which would situate your working arm in the same end position it would be when performing a fly with both arms.
    2. All the motion should come from your shoulder, as you keep your elbow mildly bent throughout the set.
    3. As you lift your arm, it is natural for your shoulder blade to move slightly forward, but you should avoid letting your shoulder excessively round forward into a slouched posture.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering your arm out to the side in a controlled manner as it arcs backward through the full range of motion that is comfortable for your shoulder.
    1. Finish in a “proud chest” posture with your chest up and shoulder blades pulled back.
    2. Continue keeping your elbow mildly bent as you resist letting it bend any further, which would shift the exercise into more of a chest press rather than a fly.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulder in the bottom position, but you should not experience any shoulder pain when moving through your comfortable range of motion.
    4. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, avoid letting the machine abruptly swing your arm outward in an uncontrolled fashion.
4. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed the set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delt and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near lower chest height.
    1. Setting the J-hooks a bit lower for a Marrs bar than for a straight bar is appropriate for most lifters, but the optimal starting height should specifically place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back.
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Place the Marrs bar on top of the J-hooks with the handles angled down so they will be in front of your torso when facing the direction in which you will perform your good mornings.
    1. The padded center of the bar will rest on your upper back.
    2. Note that the bar can start rotating after you place it on the hooks. When this happens, you can rotate the bar back to the appropriate starting position while setting up.
3. Face the center of the bar in a shoulder-width stance.
4. Shallowly bend your hips and knees, walk under the bar, grasp its handles, and position your shoulders snugly under the shoulder pads with the base of the bar resting on your back.
    1. Your palms should be facing inward while holding the handles with bent elbows.
    2. The bar should be securely resting in a comfortable and stable spot where you would not even need to use your hands to keep the bar in position, but holding the handles can provide additional stability.
    3. The appropriate bar placement should not be applying pressure to the base of your neck.
5. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of good morning reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
6. Load the bar with your intended weight and position the safety pins to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of the good morning.
7. Repeat steps 3 and 4 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Stand upright with the bar by straightening your knees and hips.
2. Take a couple of steps backward and position your feet near shoulder width to hip width apart with the soles of your feet fully contacting the floor.
    1. You can alternatively adopt a wider stance if you prefer.
3. Brace your core by initially taking a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward.
    1. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. After the deep “belly breath,” you can continue thinking about tensing the front of your abdominals, sides of your abdominals, and lower back muscles simultaneously in a manner that allows you to form a “360-degree brace” instead of only tensing the front of your core.
    3. As a helpful cue for your “360-degree brace,” you can tense the front and sides of your abdominals outward like you would if you are bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    4. Bracing will help you maintain a rigid trunk position while you move about your hips throughout your set.
4. Hinge at your hips through your available range of motion in a controlled manner to move your buttocks backward while you allow your knees to bend mildly.
    1. Keep your lower back near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright, rather than rounding your lower back while doing so.
    2. Keep your neck near a neutral position, like you have when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain in a vertical position throughout your set, but you should not keep your knees fully straightened.
    4. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward, without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    5. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something, as you keep your chest up (rather than letting your chest collapse through back rounding).
    6. While you hinge at your hips, the bar will naturally descend, and your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal.
    7. How deeply you are able to hinge without meaningfully rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees will be dependent on your hamstring flexibility.
    8. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit your buttocks any farther back, you have finished descending through your available range of motion.
    9. If you positioned them appropriately, the safety pins should not restrict how far you can hip hinge, but if you are able to hinge until the bar contacts the safety pins, you can lightly tap the safety pins with the bar without resting the bar on the pins.
5. Reverse the movement by pulling your hips forward until you have returned to the starting position with an upright torso.
    1. Since the lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, all of the technique points from the lowering phase apply to how to perform the lifting phase, except for the details on how deeply to hip hinge.
6. Alternate between steps 4 and 5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
7. Rerack the bar by walking forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook, and you are ready to rest the bar on the J-hooks by once again bending at your hips and knees in a quarter squat-like manner.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near mid-chest height, place a cambered squat bar on top of them, and face the center of the bar with a near shoulder-width stance.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back.
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a bar on your back.
2. Based on your preference, grasp the horizontal bar with a somewhat outside shoulder-width grip that feels comfortable to you, or grasp the vertical posts.
    1. If you have the mobility to grasp the horizontal bar comfortably, you can set up just as you would with a straight bar good morning.
    2. If using a straight bar prompts you to feel pain or discomfort in your shoulders, elbows, and/or wrists due to mobility limitations and/or prior injuries, holding the vertical posts may be more comfortable for you.
    3. Which part of the vertical posts is ideal to hold can vary based on the design of your specific bar and your mobility. The appropriate height for your hand placement should feel comfortable and stable to you once you are under the bar, while allowing you to keep your upper back tight. Finding which hand position best accomplishes these objectives for you as an individual can require some trial and error.
3. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar and positioning it on your upper back in a spot that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should be securely resting on the muscles of your upper back when squeezing your shoulder blades together.
    2. If you feel the bar is putting pressure on your neck, it is most likely placed too high on your back. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
    3. If you feel like the bar may slide down your back after standing upright with it, you most likely have it placed too low on your back.
    4. While you can use a lower bar placement, this variation is often performed with the bar resting on the muscular shelf formed by your traps above your rear delts (on the back of your shoulders) and above the bony ridge running across your shoulder blades (known as the scapular spine).
    5. When positioning the bar on your upper back, you may need to adjust your grip if your shoulders, elbows, or wrists feel uncomfortable with your initial placement.
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of good morning reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of the good morning.
6. Repeat steps 2 and 3 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Unrack the bar by standing up in a quarter squat-like manner as you straighten your knees while pulling down on the bar to keep it positioned on your upper back.
2. Take a couple of steps backward and position your feet into approximately a hip-width to shoulder-width stance with the soles of your feet fully contacting the floor.
    1. Alternatively, you can adopt a wide stance outside shoulder width if you prefer.
    2. With a closer stance, pointing your toes straight ahead tends to feel comfortable, but pointing toes somewhat out to the sides typically feels better with a wider stance. Use whichever degree of toe flair feels best to you.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point onward to help you maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
4. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat and chest up. Only start the descent once the bar is motionless.
    1. Avoid starting your descent if the bar is still oscillating following your walkout. You may need to stand still for a moment longer compared to when you use a straight bar.
    2. To prevent the bar from swinging excessively, it may be helpful to descend a bit slower than you typically do when back squatting. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
    3. While you hinge at your hips, your torso will transition to an angle that is closer to horizontal as you lower the bar.
    4. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    5. As a cue, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    6. Throughout the set, keep your feet fully planted on the ground and your neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
5. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. How deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. If you positioned them appropriately, the safety pins should not restrict how far you can hip hinge, but if you are able to hinge until the bar contacts the safety pins, you can lightly tap the safety pins with the bar without resting the bar on the pins.
6. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and feet fully on the ground.
    2. When you have finished lifting the bar, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Rerack the bar by walking forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-cup, and you are ready to rest the bar on the J-hooks by once again bending your knees in a quarter squat-like manner.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise dynamically trains your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the rack’s J-hooks to near upper-chest height.
    1. The optimal starting height should place the bar slightly above the tops of the J-hooks, with your feet flat on the floor, after you stand upright with the bar on your back.
    2. If you later need to rise up onto the balls of your feet and lift your heels off the ground while walking out in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks, you initially positioned the J-hooks too high. Walking with your heels raised increases the risk of losing your balance and should be avoided when you have a barbell on your back.
    3. If you need to bend your knees and hips beyond a mild degree (e.g., more like a half squat than a quarter squat) initially to place the bar on your back, you positioned the J-hooks excessively low. This can make it more challenging to rerack the bar at the end of your set.
2. Place the safety squat bar on top of the J-hooks with the handles angled down and toward the direction in which you will face while squatting.
    1. When resting on J-hooks, most safety squat bar models are designed in a way that results in the handles being mildly angled forward or backward rather than being directed straight down.
    2. The direction in which the handles from those bars are directed will influence how far forward the weight is situated relative to the upper back pad, which influences the lever arm of the weight.
    3. If you set up with the handles angled back toward you, the weight will be pitched relatively farther forward and increase the difficulty of the exercise compared to positioning the bar in the standard way.
    4. If you wish to do so, you can intentionally position the bar in the opposite manner as its own variation, but be aware that lighter loads will be required with that approach.
    5. Certain safety squat bar models are designed with the front and back sides being identical, so their handles can be directed straight down, and how you place the bar on the J-hooks is irrelevant.
3. Position a sturdy box of your desired height centered within the rack, a couple of step lengths behind the J-hooks.
    1. To train your lower body muscles most effectively, you want to pick a box height that enables you to squat near the depth of what is your full range of motion back squat (i.e., the lowest point at which you can squat with quality technique while keeping your back flat and feet fully on the floor). This depth will vary from person to person, but people can often descend at least until their thighs are fairly close to being parallel to the ground.
    2. A squat box or plyometric box is an ideal choice for your box. You can position either type of box with one of its corners directed to the middle point between the J-hooks to give you the greatest freedom in how you situate your feet when box squatting.
    3. Plyometric boxes are often available in gyms at heights of 12in (30cm), 18in (46cm), and 24in (61cm). Box squatting with an 18in (46cm) box, which is near the typical height of a flat exercise bench, restricts the range of motion for most people who are not clearly taller than six feet (183cm). If you currently do not have the mobility to squat down to a 12in (30cm) box with proper form, you can increase the height of the box by placing a thick bumper plate on top of the box to elevate it.
    4. While others have fixed heights like plyometric boxes, the height of certain squat box models can be adjusted to accommodate how deeply you wish to box squat.
    5. If you do not have access to a squat box or plyometric box, you can use an aerobic step provided that the step is rated to withstand at least half the combined load of your bodyweight and the bar weight. You can adjust its height based on how many pairs of risers you stack underneath it.
    6. Alternatively, you may be able to use a flat exercise bench as your box. A bench is excessively tall for many lifters to squat through a full range of motion, but it can be suitable for rather tall individuals, people with limited mobility, and anyone who intentionally wishes to train through a partial range of motion. To fit either a bench or an aerobic step within a rack, you may have to position it with the shorter side facing the side of the rack where you will place the barbell.
    7. While not ideal due to its lower stability, you can also stack bumper plates on top of each other to form a “box” of your desired height. If you opt to do so, make sure that the plates are completely centered on top of each other.
4. Face the center of the bar in a near shoulder-width stance.
5. Shallowly bend your hips and knees before walking under the bar, grasping its handles, and situating the bar on your upper back in a position that feels comfortable and stable for you.
    1. Your palms should be facing in toward each other while grasping the handles with bent elbows.
    2. Where precisely the bar feels most comfortable and stable will vary among different individuals, but the bar should feel like it is securely resting on the muscles of your upper back.
    3. If you feel like the bar is placing pressure on your neck, you most likely have the bar placed too high on your back.
    4. In particular, you should avoid placing the bar excessively high to the extent that the weight is pressing on any of the bony prominences at the base of your neck.
6. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of box squat reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins prior to reracking the bar onto the J-hooks.
    1. These initial reps can also help you determine if you should adjust where you positioned your box.
7. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends when box squatting.
8. Repeat steps 4 and 5 of the setup before proceeding to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Stand upright with the bar on your upper back by straightening your knees and hips.
    1. Continue holding the handles while keeping the bar on your upper back throughout your set.
2. Take a couple of steps backward until the box is just behind you. Position your feet near shoulder width apart or wider, with your toes pointing somewhat out to the sides.
    1. The ideal stance width and degree of toe flare for box squatting will vary among individuals based on their hip structure and mobility.
    2. Ultimately, you want to position your feet where you feel comfortable and stable when box squatting, which typically requires trying different stances over time and assessing how you feel in them.
    3. Based on your preference, you can use the same stance that you would when squatting without a box, or you can use a somewhat wider stance, which can facilitate sitting back a bit more.
    4. Throughout your set, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position.
4. Initiate the descent by starting to sit your buttocks back. Progressively bend at your knees and hips to squat down in a controlled manner while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Let your torso naturally lean forward at your hips to maintain your balance, but avoid letting your lower back round or chest cave down.
    2. Keep the entire sole of each foot (including the heel, outer aspect of the midfoot, and inner aspect of the midfoot) firmly planted on the ground as your weight is evenly distributed throughout your feet (rather than being all on your heels or toes) during your set.
    3. Strive to keep each knee aligned with the second toe (the toe next to your big toe) or the third toe of that side’s foot as you avoid letting your knees cave inward relative to your feet.
    4. As you bend your knees, it is normal for them to track forward over your feet (especially the deeper your squat) to help you maintain your balance.
5. Continue to squat down until you can sit onto the box in a controlled manner, as you avoid plopping onto the box or rocking back onto the box.
    1. As you sit on the box, your torso should maintain the same degree of forward lean that you had right before reaching the box.
    2. If your torso transitions to a more upright position after sitting on the box compared to at the end of your descent, you have rocked back onto the box.
6. While keeping your core braced and back flat, and feet fully on the ground, allow about half of your weight to deload into the box as you pause on the box for approximately one second.
    1. As a cue, you can think about a scale being on top of the box. If you weigh 200lb (91kg) and have 100lb (45kg) on the bar, the scale should read within the vicinity of 150lb (68kg).
7. Reverse the movement by driving through the ground while extending (straightening) your knees and hips to stand upright.
    1. Avoid rocking your torso forward to generate momentum before you start squatting upward.
    2. Continue keeping your chest up, back flat, feet fully on the ground, and knees aligned with your feet as you squat up.
8. Alternate between steps 4-7 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, resume bracing your core before initiating the descent for the next rep.
9. Walk forward until the barbell contacts the back of each J-hook before mildly bending your knees and hips to rerack the bar onto the J-hooks.
## Description
While statically training your spinal extensors, this exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your torso against the chest pad and row comfortably with your elbows tucked fairly close to your sides.
    1. The appropriate seat height often naturally allows you to finish the lifting phase with your elbows bent by about 90 degrees.
    2. If the seat is too low, the upward angle in which the handles move during the lifting phase can cause the row to feel awkward as you have to shrug your shoulders up and/or meaningfully flare your elbows out away from your sides.
    3. If the seat is too high, you may notice that you have to pack your shoulder blades down to reach the handles with your arms directed further downward than forward. This could limit your range of motion and prompt the row to not feel as natural as it otherwise could.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, set it to a position that allows you to just grasp the handles when your arms are fully outstretched with straight elbows.
    1. Machines often lack an adjustable chest pad.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit down on the seat with your torso against the chest pad.
    1. Based on your preference, you can position your feet against the machine’s foot plates (if your machine model has foot plates), or you can place your feet flat on the floor.
    2. As an alternative to sitting on the seat, you optionally can adopt a staggered stance with one foot forward and the other backward as you lightly contact the seat with your buttocks while predominantly bearing weight through your feet.
    3. Set up in whichever position feels most stable and comfortable to you.
4. Reach forward and firmly grasp the horizontal handles with an underhand grip as your palms face up.
    1. Some machines have handles that can freely rotate, but most have two pairs of handles, which include horizontal handles that you can grasp with an underhand grip.
    2. Hold the handles with straight wrists and elbows.
    3. If the handles are situated far enough in front of you to make it difficult to grasp both of them simultaneously, it may be easier for you to grasp one handle at a time.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handles back toward you by driving your elbows backward while bending them. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blades back together.
    1. Rather than meaningfully flaring them out away from your sides, keep your elbows tucked reasonably close to your sides to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you. This should keep your forearms aligned with the direction in which you are rowing.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbows being slightly behind your torso or at least level with your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    3. Instead of moving straight backward in a fully horizontal direction, this machine type will normally have its handles move at an upward angle while you pull them backward.
    4. Keep your torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
    5. As a cue for training your upper back muscles, you can think about squeezing your shoulder blades together at the end of the pull as if you were trying to pinch a pencil with your shoulder blades.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbows to move forward in a controlled manner as they progressively straighten until your arms are fully outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull them further apart than your resting position at the end, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arms forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Determine whether you will set up to press within a power rack, which could be modified to perform a variety of barbell lifts and would require you to position a mobile flat bench within the rack, or within a specific bench press station in which the bench is fused to the rack.
    1. Most power racks allow you to position J-hooks (on which the barbell rests before being unracked) and safety pins at incrementally different heights, respectively, to help you situate the bar close to your ideal starting position and to receive the bar safely if you fail a rep.
    2. Bench press stations may also have spotter arms (that function just like safety pins by receiving the bar if you fail a rep) and J-hooks that can be adjusted in height, but a variety of models lack spotter arms and have multiple pairs of fixed height J-hooks. While most people can find a J-hook height that allows them to board press effectively, some people will notice that their ideal starting position of the bar cannot be achieved with the available J-hook heights, which can make it difficult to unrack the bar independently.
    3. If you have access to a power rack with safety pins and a bench press station without spotter arms, board pressing within the power rack is recommended for its additional safety mechanism. The con of using a power rack is that it typically takes longer to prepare compared to a specific bench press station.
2. With a power rack, position the J-hooks to a height that initially allows you to grasp the bar with slightly bent elbows once you are set up on the bench, which will allow you to unrack the bar efficiently. With a bench press station, find which J-hook height places you closest to this position.
    1. After fully straightening your elbows to lift the bar off the J-hooks, the bar should ideally be close to 2in (5cm) above the top of the J-hooks while keeping your shoulder blades tucked back together.
    2. The proper height easily enables you to avoid bumping the J-hooks when unracking the bar while simultaneously not requiring much effort to lift the bar initially.
    3. If the J-hooks are too high, you may have to spread your shoulder blades apart in order for the bar to clear the J-hooks when unracking, which can place you in a less stable position to start your set, since it can be challenging to reposition your shoulder blades after unracking the bar.
    4. If the J-hooks are too low, the unrack will involve a greater-than-ideal pressing range of motion, which will require greater effort and generate more fatigue before your set begins.
    5. If neither of the two closest J-hook heights is ideal, it is generally better to use the height that is a little too low, since being able to start the set with optimal positioning is typically more advantageous than the alternative.
    6. Some trial and error is often needed to find the best J-hook height for you.
3. After situating a bar on top of the appropriate J-hooks, determine what you will use as your half-board.
    1. If you are using a standard wooden half-board, a partner will hold the board on your chest after you unrack the weight. The partner should stand next to you out of the bar’s way.
    2. Alternatively, you may use a specialized board press attachment that you would connect to the middle of the bar to board press without a partner. This type of equipment commonly allows you to perform variable height board presses based on which side you connect to the bar. For this variation, the thickness of the attachment under the bar should be approximately 0.75in (1.9cm).
4. Use the unloaded bar to perform a couple of board press reps with the form described under the “exercise technique” section to determine the appropriate height of the safety pins/spotter arms prior to re-racking the bar onto the J-hooks.
5. Load the bar with your intended weight, and position the safety pins/spotter arms to be slightly under the lowest point at which the bar descends by the end of the lowering phase of your board press.
6. Lie down with your back on the bench. Position yourself so that your mouth is close to being directly underneath the barbell, and your knees are bent with your feet on the floor, approximately shoulder-width apart.
    1. Based on what feels most stable, you can position the soles of your feet flat on the ground with your shins close to being vertical, or you can bend your knees beyond this point to dig the balls of your feet and toes into the ground with your heels off the ground.
    2. Similarly, you can decide whether you point your toes moderately out to the sides or straight ahead based on your preference.
7. Firmly grasp the bar with your palms facing forward (resulting in an overhand grip) with your hands moderately outside shoulder width. Let the bar sit on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with reasonably straight wrists.
    1. A moderate grip width for the bench press is commonly considered to be around 1.5 times your shoulder width, with a bit of leeway both narrower and wider. Feel free to try out different hand widths over time to determine where you feel best.
    2. For stability, wrap your thumbs around the bar, resulting in the bar being fully enclosed by your hands. If you’re new to this exercise, avoid positioning your thumbs on the same side of the bar as your other fingers (which is known as a thumbless grip, or a “suicide grip”).
    3. While your wrists do not need to be completely straight, you should avoid grasping the bar with your wrists cocked back into an excessively extended position that may contribute to an increased risk of experiencing wrist discomfort. Simply squeezing the bar as hard as possible will usually keep your wrists from cocking back excessively.
    4. After grasping the bar in a manner that allows you to feel comfortable and stable, you will maintain this hand and wrist position throughout your set.
8. While allowing your lower back to arch naturally, assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling by tightly pulling your shoulder blades back together. Maintain this chest position throughout your set.
    1. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades. This should prompt you to feel your upper back muscles contracting like at the end range of a rowing exercise.
    2. Focusing on adopting a “proud chest” position can naturally place your lower back into a mildly arched position, which is sufficient for optimizing muscle growth and general strength development. However, if you prefer to perform your standard bench press with a greater arch, you can also use the same degree of arching for this variation.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your feet into the ground and squeeze your glutes. Simultaneosuly brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing in this manner throughout your set to create full-body tension.
    2. From this starting position, your feet should be firmly dug into the ground, while your buttocks, upper back, and head are firmly planted on the bench. Maintain these five points of contact throughout your whole set to maximize your stability and performance.
2. Press the bar off the J-hooks by straightening your elbows, and move the bar forward away from the J-hooks until it is approximately above your shoulders, where you will hold it for a moment before starting to lower the bar.
    1. Focus on keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together to avoid losing your upper back tightness.
3. Lower the bar toward your lower-to-middle chest in a controlled manner by progressively bending your elbows and allowing them to flare out moderately away from each other while descending.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, strive to keep your elbows stacked underneath your mostly straight wrists (when viewed from the side). This will keep your forearms close to vertical (when viewed from the side) throughout the set and promote the appropriate balance between elbow tuck and elbow flare.
    2. When lowering the bar toward your lower-to-middle chest, the bar will track slightly forward toward your abdomen rather than moving exclusively in a vertical line. Relative to when you perform a standard bench press, you can aim to lower the bar slightly higher up on your chest with this board press.
    3. To reach that point with the bar, you will have to keep your elbows tucked to a degree instead of being fully flared out. Near the bottom of the rep, the proper balance should result in your upper arms forming approximately 45-75-degree angles with your torso (when 0 degrees means your upper arms are completely tucked by your sides and 90 degrees means your upper arms form a “T-shape” with your torso).
4. Continue lowering the bar until you are able to tap the board with the bar (if you are using a wooden board) or able to tap the board attachment to your chest.
    1. You should be in full control of your light tap, since you will not be bouncing out of the bottom position.
5. Reverse the movement by pressing the bar up. It may help to focus on driving your elbows upward and inward.
    1. You should finish with your elbows fully straightened and the bar situated above your shoulders.
    2. The bar will arc slightly back toward your face as you press it, so that you finish the rep with the bar over your shoulders instead of your chest.
    3. Continue driving your feet into the ground while keeping your buttocks firmly planted on the bench, and your shoulder blades pulled back together.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set and are ready to rerack the bar.
7. After completing the lifting phase of your last rep, keep the bar motionless over your shoulders for about one second before moving the bar back until it contacts the back of both J-hooks.
8. Bend your elbows slightly to lower the bar onto the J-hooks to finish reracking the weight.
    1. Only begin lowering the bar after you have already contacted the back of the J-hooks.
## Description
While effectively training your pec major, front delts, and triceps, this exercise can transfer to improved bench press strength.## Setting up
To perform this variation, your machine must have handles that you can grasp with your palms facing each other. If your machine lacks this handle design, you can alternatively perform the reclined overhand grip pin-loaded machine incline press.

1. If you are using a multi-press machine that can be adjusted to a flat or incline press setting, position the back pad to the incline setting.
2. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles approximately level with your middle chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, but feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is excessively low or high, the exercise may feel awkward and uncomfortable.
3. If your machine lets you adjust the starting height of the handles, set the handles to a position that lets you press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Setting the handles to where they are approximately level with your chest when lying down on the back pad is an appropriate starting point for many lifters.
    2. From that starting point, you can specifically set the handles to the lowest height at which you can grasp them without any shoulder discomfort while keeping your elbows vertically stacked under your wrists.
    3. If your machine does not let you adjust the handle height and you cannot comfortably assume the starting position, a different incline press variation may be preferable for you.
4. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
5. Lie down on the machine’s back pad with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against it.
    1. Your buttocks should be resting on the seat, and your feet should be flat on the ground under your bent knees.
    2. If the back pad is sufficiently tall, rest your head against it. Otherwise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
6. With your palms facing in toward each other and elbows bent, firmly grasp the parallel handles. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being in a vertical position.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbows directly under your wrists and the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
7. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the handles upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, keep your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles to maintain vertical forearms when viewed from the side.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, buttocks on the seat, back against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Keep your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles as you let your elbows move downward while bending.
    2. Descend through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    3. If you can comfortably lower the handles fully to the starting position, you can lightly tap the weights you selected to the rest of the weight stack. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
With an emphasis on your upper pec, this exercise primarily trains your pec major and front delts, which are assisted by your triceps.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that allows you to rest your torso against the chest pad and enables you to row comfortably with your elbow tucked fairly close to your side.
    1. The appropriate seat height often naturally allows you to finish the lifting phase with your elbow bent by about 90 degrees.
    2. If the seat is too low, the upward angle in which the handle moves during the lifting phase can cause the row to feel awkward as you have to shrug your shoulder up and/or meaningfully flare your elbow out away from your side.
    3. If the seat is too high, you may notice that you have to pack your shoulder blade down to reach the handles with your arm directed further downward than forward. This could limit your range of motion and prompt the row to not feel as natural as it otherwise could.
2. If your machine has an adjustable chest pad, set it to a position that allows you to just grasp the handle when your arm is fully outstretched with a straight elbow.
    1. Machines often lack an adjustable chest pad.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit down on the seat with your torso against the chest pad.
    1. Based on your preference, you can position your feet against the machine’s foot plates (if your machine model has foot plates), or you can place your feet flat on the floor.
    2. As an alternative to sitting on the seat, you optionally can adopt a staggered stance with one foot forward and the other backward as you lightly contact the seat with your buttocks while predominantly bearing weight through your feet.
    3. Set up in whichever position feels most stable and comfortable to you.
4. With the side you are going to train, reach forward and firmly grasp the horizontal handle with an underhand grip as your palm faces up.
    1. Some machines have handles that can freely rotate, but most have two pairs of handles, which include horizontal handles that you can grasp with an underhand grip.
    2. Hold the handle with a straight wrist and elbow.
    3. If your machine has a stationary center handle (that is not connected to either of the machine’s mobile lever arms), you can grasp it with your free hand and continue holding it throughout the set for additional stability. Otherwise, place your free hand wherever you prefer.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the handle back toward you by driving your elbow backward while bending it. Simultaneously pull your shoulder blade fully back.
    1. Rather than meaningfully flaring it out away from your side, keep your elbow tucked reasonably close to your side to a degree that feels natural and comfortable to you. This should keep your forearm aligned with the direction in which you are rowing.
    2. Pull through your full range of motion, which will typically result in your elbow being slightly behind your torso or at least level with your torso at the end of the lifting phase.
    3. Instead of moving straight backward in a fully horizontal direction, this machine type will normally have its handle move at an upward angle while you pull it backward.
    4. Keep your torso in contact with the chest pad throughout the set.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to move forward in a controlled manner as it progressively straightens until your arm is fully outstretched in front of you.
    1. Your shoulder blade will naturally move forward and away from the center of your back, but you can exaggerate this motion by intentionally allowing the weight to pull it further forward than your resting position at the end, which will stretch your upper back muscles.
    2. While you do not have to perform this step extremely slowly, you should avoid letting the weight abruptly yank your arm forward in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
While also training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this row can effectively develop your lat, teres major, rear delt, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
To perform this variation, your machine must have handles that you can grasp with your palms facing each other. If your machine lacks this handle design, you can alternatively perform the lying overhand grip pin-loaded machine chest press.

1. If you are using a multi-press machine that can be adjusted to a flat or incline press setting, set the back pad to its horizontal position.
2. If your machine lets you adjust the starting height of the handles, set the handles to a position that lets you press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Setting the handles to where they are approximately level with your chest when lying down on the back pad is an appropriate starting point for many lifters.
    2. From that starting point, you can specifically set the handles to the lowest height at which you can grasp them without any shoulder discomfort while keeping your elbows vertically stacked under your wrists.
    3. If your machine does not let you adjust the handle height and you cannot comfortably assume the starting position, a different chest press variation may be preferable for you as an individual.
3. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin.
4. Lie down with your back, buttocks, and head on the pad. Position yourself so that your lower chest is aligned with the parallel handles.
    1. Your elbows will naturally tuck closer to your sides as you position yourself further back on the pad (situating your shoulders horizontally further behind the handles).
    2. You can decide specifically where you lie down on the pad based on what feels comfortable to your shoulders throughout the exercise, while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. Your feet should be flat on the ground in a stable position, stacked under your bent knees.
5. With your palms facing in toward each other and elbows bent, firmly grasp the parallel handles. Position your elbows directly under the handles, resulting in your forearms being in a vertical position.
    1. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    2. Aligning your elbows directly under the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you positioned your body appropriately on the pad.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Keep your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles as you let your elbows move downward while bending.
2. Press the handles upward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. While motion simultaneously occurs at your shoulders and elbows, your elbows should remain stacked under your wrists and the handles when viewed from the side.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back and buttocks snugly against the pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by lowering the handles in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Keep your elbows stacked under your wrists and the handles as you let your elbows move downward while bending.
    2. Descend through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    3. If you can comfortably lower the handles fully to the starting position, you can lightly tap the weights you selected to the rest of the weight stack. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise predominantly trains your pec major, front delts, and triceps with a technique that some people find feels more comfortable for their shoulders compared to other presses.## Setting up
1. Adjust a cable station’s pulley to be near head height and select your desired load while leaving the cable without any handle attachment.
2. Reach up with the arm you will train, and firmly grasp the ball-shaped stopper at the end of the cable with a bent elbow while facing away from the base of the cable station.
    1. You can use your non-working side’s arm to help set your grip on the cable and assume the starting position.
3. Take a couple of steps forward, and adopt a staggered stance with one foot further in front than the other.
4. For balance, slightly bend your front knee and slightly lean your torso forward by hinging at your front leg’s hip.
5. If you did not already do so, hold your upper arm approximately parallel to the ground with your elbow bent to assume the starting position.
    1. Situate your non-working arm into your preferred position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your forearm forward in an arcing manner by straightening your elbow. Keep your elbow up as your upper arm remains essentially stationary throughout the set.
    1. Finish with your elbow fully straightened.
    2. Strive to have all the movement occurring from your forearm hinging at your elbow without any shoulder motion.
    3. You can think of your elbow as the center of an analog clock, while your forearm is the hand that rotates about this stationary center elbow.
2. Reverse the movement by allowing your elbow to bend in a controlled manner as your forearm arcs back toward the pulley.
    1. Let your elbow bend through the full range of motion provided by the cable until your forearm is aligned with it.
    2. There is no need to perform this extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the cables abruptly yank your hands toward the pulley in an uncontrolled fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
    1. After your last rep, step backward and bring the cable end toward the pulley until the cable loses tension and you can release it.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop the triceps, including the long head, which is not trained particularly well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
This page describes setting up an axle hold from the floor and deadlifting it into position, but you can alternatively set up with the bar in an elevated position like you would with a rack pull or block pull, since all you need to do is wind up in the lockout position of a deadlift while holding the bar in front of you.

1. Add weight plates of your desired load to an axle bar (AKA thick bar) situated on the ground.
    1. If you lack an axle bar, you can replicate the grip demands of an axle deadlift by connecting fat grip attachments to a standard barbell shoulder-width apart.
2. While facing the center of the bar, stand in an approximately hip-width stance with the middle of your feet under the bar and your toes pointing straight ahead or slightly out to the sides.
3. With your arms pointing straight down toward the bar, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until your hands are level with the bar. Keep your lower back flat and chest up rather than rounding your back.
    1. More motion should come from hinging at your hips than bending your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift mildly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks as you sit back while your hands are carrying something. After you have fully sat back and feel a stretching sensation in your hamstrings, you can bend your knees further to reach the bar.
    3. Throughout this exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. With your elbows fully straightened, firmly grasp the bar with an approximately shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
5. Forcefully squeeze the bar, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed or simply sucking your belly button inward.
    2. Continue squeezing the bar and bracing your core from this point onward.
6. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Keep your elbows straight and the bar very close to your legs as you lift the bar, rather than letting the bar drift forward.
## Exercise technique
1. Statically grip the axle while staying upright and keeping your core braced until you have reached your target hold duration.
    1. Continue keeping your shoulders stacked above your hips as you avoid leaning back to rest the bar on your thighs.
2. Lower the bar by sitting your hips backward and allowing your knees to bend while keeping your lower back flat and chest up.
## Description
This exercise can improve your grip strength by statically training your forearm and hand muscles that function as finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are seated.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the seat height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    3. To confirm whether you selected an appropriate seat height, you will have to sit down and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
    4. A seat height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points will commonly allow you to contact the arm pads with the outer aspects of your arms just above your elbows when keeping your upper arms near your sides.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Sit on the seat with your upper arms by your sides and your elbows bent to near 90 degrees. Place the outer aspects of your arms against the arm pads, and grasp the handles with your palms facing in toward each other if your machine has handles.
4. Sit on the seat with your working side’s upper arm by your side, your elbow bent to near 90 degrees, and the outer aspect of your arm against the arm pad.
    1. Most machines have arm pads and a backrest for you to sit against while facing away from the base of the machine, but certain models have handles while lacking arm pads or a backrest. If your machine has handles rather than arm pads, you can naturally grasp the handle.
    2. Situate your free hand into your preferred position, which may involve resting it by your side.
5. Based on your preference, you can sit with a fully upright torso, or you can lean slightly forward at your hips.
    1. Either torso position can allow you to perform this exercise effectively, so you can utilize whichever one feels most comfortable for your shoulder.
    2. Feel free to try out each posture during a different set to determine your personal preference.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your elbow out and away from your side in a smooth, arcing manner until it is near shoulder height.
    1. Let your shoulder blade move naturally rather than trying to hold it any particular position.
    2. At the top of the raise, your upper arm should form half of a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from the front or back.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by letting your elbow lower in toward your side.
    1. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the machine’s lever arm (onto which you loaded the plates) to the stopper underneath it, or you can stop just before this point.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arm downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to an axle (AKA thick bar) situated on the ground.
    1. If you lack an axle bar, you can replicate the grip demands of an axle deadlift by connecting fat grip attachments to a standard barbell shoulder-width apart.
2. While facing the middle of the axle, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can reach the axle with straight elbows.
3. Firmly grasp the bar with approximately a shoulder-width grip as your palms face forward (i.e., underhand grip).
4. Stand upright with the axle while keeping your elbows straight.
## Exercise technique
1. While squeezing the axle, curl it upward and back toward you in an arcing motion by bending your elbows until they are fully bent.
    1. Keep your upper arms in the same position as you avoid swinging your elbows forward through shoulder motion.
    2. While your forearms move through elbow motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary throughout your set.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the axle in a controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Nothing else should move except for your forearms lowering in an arcing manner through elbow motion.
    2. While you do not have to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should avoid letting weight abruptly swing your forearms down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the axle down.
## Description
While dynamically training your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis, this exercise can statically train your forearm’s extrinsic finger flexors and your hand’s intrinsic finger flexors.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to an axle (AKA thick bar) situated on the ground.
    1. To set up for this row, you can perform the lifting phase of an axle deadlift, followed by the lowering phase of a Romanian deadlift.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the middle of your feet under the middle of the axle.
3. With your arms pointing down toward the axle, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can reach the axle. Keep your lower back flat and chest up throughout the exercise.
    1. More motion should come from your hips than your knees as you descend, and your knees should not track particularly far forward, but you can allow your knees to shift slightly forward rather than maintaining completely vertical shins.
    2. Throughout the exercise, keep your neck near a neutral position, as when you are looking straight ahead while standing upright.
4. Firmly grasp the axle with a slightly outside shoulder-width overhand grip such that your palms are facing backward.
5. Forcefully squeeze the axle, and brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue squeezing the axle and bracing your core from this point on.
6. Drive through your legs to begin lifting the axle before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the axle.
    1. While motion occurs at your hips and knees, your back should remain rigid.
7. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks back to hinge at your hips and incline your torso as close to horizontal as your hamstring flexibility permits with a flat back.
    1. To avoid rounding your lower back, you should think about sitting back rather than bending over.
    2. As a cue for hinging at your hips, you can think about closing a car door with your buttocks while your hands are carrying something.
    3. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical (or close to vertical) throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    4. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the axle at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively pushing your knees forward depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    5. If you have the mobility to hinge until the plates touch the ground, hinge at your hips until the plates are just above the ground.
## Exercise technique
1. Pull the axle up toward your upper abdomen by driving your elbows simultaneously backward and upward while progressively bending your elbows and pulling your shoulder blades together.
    1. Keep your torso in a fixed hinged position as you maintain the same angle of bend in your hips and knees throughout your set while keeping your core braced and your lower back near a neutral position.
    2. Movement should occur at your shoulders, shoulder blades, and elbows, but your lower body should remain nearly stationary.
    3. You can aim to row the axle until it touches near your upper abdomen or is close to doing so, which will result in your elbows being behind your torso (or at least aligned with your torso) at the end of the lifting phase.
    4. While lifting the axle, allow your elbows naturally to flare out away from your sides in a manner that feels comfortable to you, which may result in your upper arms forming near a 45-degree angle with your torso toward the end of the lifting phase.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the axle in a controlled manner by allowing your elbows to move downward and forward until your arms are directed down toward the ground with straight elbows.
    1. During the lowering phase, your shoulder blades will naturally move apart from one another.
    2. The plates should not touch the ground when lowering the axle.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to set the axle down onto the floor by bending your knees further.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae and forearm muscles, this exercise can effectively develop your lats, teres majores, rear delts, traps, and rhomboids.## Setting up
1. Connect a straight bar attachment or a V-bar row handle (AKA double D-handle) to the machine.
    1. If you prefer to use an overhand grip (with your palms facing backward), select a straight bar attachment, whether that be a standard lat pulldown bar with a long straight body and angled ends, or a straight bar closer to shoulder-width in length.
    2. If you prefer to use a neutral grip (with your palms facing each other), select a V-bar row handle.
    3. Neither attachment type is inherently better than the other, so feel free to try out both to determine which one personally feels better to you when performing this exercise.
2. Without any weight plates added to the machine, stand on the platform with the bar between your feet.
    1. Prior to getting into the starting position for your set, you need to adjust the machine so that its safety horn (on which the weight trolley initially rests) no longer restricts downward motion of the weight trolley. This adjustment requires the use of one hand, so you cannot perform it when your working weight is loaded onto the machine, in contrast to when you are belt squatting, where you can hold the machine’s handles rather than the bar you connect.
3. Squat down to grasp the bar with one hand, and stand upright with it before using your other hand to pull the handles back toward you to disengage the safety horn.
    1. It is unusual to grasp the center of a straight bar attachment with one hand or only one part of a V-bar row handle, but you will not be lifting meaningful resistance without any plates added to the machine, so the awkward grip is not problematic.
    2. If you prefer, you have the option of disengaging the safety horn while wearing a belt connected to the machine prior to swapping a bar for the belt.
    3. With the safety horn no longer resting under the weight trolley, the machine is now set for you to be able to hinge through your full range of motion.
4. Squat down and set the bar onto the platform before adding weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
5. Step up onto the platform with the bar’s attachment point centered between the middle of your feet in a hip-width stance.
6. While keeping your back flat, sit your hips backward while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the bar with straight elbows.
    1. Regardless of whether you use an overhand or neutral grip, wrap your fingers around the bar with the bar pressed into where the bases of your fingers meet the palm side of your knuckles for the most stable grip.
7. Without rounding your back, drive through your legs to begin lifting the bar before thrusting your hips forward and squeezing your glutes to stand upright with the bar.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows straight throughout the exercise.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight onto one foot and take a small step backward with your other foot so that its toes are close to being just slightly behind your lead foot’s heel while keeping your feet hip-width apart. Place the balls and toes of your rear foot on the ground while keeping its midfoot and heel off the ground.
    1. Naturally let your knees slightly bend.
    2. Avoid stepping inward to the extent that your rear foot is directly behind your lead foot. Keeping your feet hip-width apart can help keep you stable.
    3. The size of your machine’s platform can constrain where you position your rear foot, so you may not be able to step as far back with your rear leg when using certain machines.
2. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, as if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or a forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set to help you maintain a rigid back position.
3. While continuing to bear almost all your weight through your lead foot, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips as you let your knees mildly bend.
    1. Throughout the set, keep your lower back flat, chest up, and lead foot fully planted on the platform.
    2. Your rear leg’s purpose is to assist with maintaining your balance so that the exercise can feel more stable than a true single leg Romanian deadlift.
    3. If scales were under both of your feet, the scale under your lead foot should ideally show that you are bearing at least 90% of your weight through your front leg.
    4. Continue keeping your elbows straight with the bar just in front of you.
    5. Strive to keep your lead leg’s shin vertical with your weight distributed throughout the entirety of your lead foot. If you concentrate all your weight through your heel, your knee may track backward into an excessively straightened position. If you bear too much weight through the front of your foot, your knee may track forward and bend excessively.
    6. While motion will occur at both hips, you should focus on sitting your lead leg’s hip back (as if you were trying to close a car door with your buttocks) while letting your rear leg’s hip naturally move with it.
4. In a controlled manner, continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. Lifters with normal flexibility can typically lower the bar at least below their knees, but how deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
5. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and lead foot fully on the platform.
    2. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching.
6. Alternate between steps 3-5 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
7. Following your last rep, step forward with your rear foot to position your feet symmetrically before repeating the exercise with your other leg in the lead position.
    1. Once you are ready to set the bar down, step forward again to position your feet symmetrically before sitting your hips back and bending your knees to lower the bar.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and step up onto the footplates with the outer aspects of your knees snugly contacting the pads.
    1. Based on your preference, you can point your feet straight ahead or angle them slightly out to the sides.
2. Grasp the handles at a height that feels comfortable and stable for you to hold.
3. While keeping your shins vertical with your knees against the pads, sit your hips slightly back while letting your knees slightly bend to assume the starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your knees outward to spread your legs fully apart while simultaneously sitting your hips back and down in a squatting motion.
    1. People commonly squat down until their hips are a little above knee height during this variation, but you can try out different depths to determine what personally feels best for you.
    2. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable to you, you can keep your torso fairly upright or lean forward at your hips to a greater degree when descending.
    3. Rather than excessively arching or rounding your back, keep it near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    4. Keep your shins vertical rather than pushing your knees forward toward your toes.
    5. How far apart you can spread your legs will be affected by your hip mobility.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by allowing your legs to move in toward each other while squatting up until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish in a mostly upright position with your hips and knees slightly bent and your legs as close together as the machine permits while keeping your knees snugly against the pads.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should be in clear control of the weight, rather than letting it abruptly swing your legs inward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, you can stand fully upright before stepping off the footplates.
## Description
While also training several smaller muscles on the side of your hip, this exercise can dynamically train your glutes, likely with an emphasis on the upper region of your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
1. If your machine has one, set the range limiter (situated in one of the machine’s back corners) just under the lowest point at which the lever arm will reach during your rep.
    1. If you are new to this exercise, you will have to perform a rep to determine which setting corresponds to this position. Consequently, you can initially set the range limiter toward the bottom, continue setting up, and then adjust from there if warranted.
    2. The range limiter should be low enough that you are able to train through the full range of motion (or very close to it) comfortably permitted by your shoulder mobility.
    3. The range limiter should be high enough that it will be able to receive the weight if you fail a rep and relax your upper back in the bottom position.
    4. After finding the appropriate setting, you can make a note of it and quickly know which one to use in the future, but initially finding it can take some trial and error.
    5. Prior to performing a challenging set, it is recommended to perform a warm-up set to confirm that you set the range limiter correctly if you are new to this machine.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
3. Step on the footplate and stand upright in a hip-width to shoulder-width stance with your back fully against the back pad.
    1. If the pad is long enough for you to do so, rest your head and/or the back of your hip bones against the pad as well.
    2. Rather than being rounded or excessively arched, you should be standing with your lower back near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. Note where the foot pedal is located, since you will have to press it at the end of your set.
4. Firmly grasp the vertical handles near the height of your lower chest with your palms facing in toward each other. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    1. Machines often have long, fixed vertical handles that allow you to grasp them at a variety of heights to accommodate different body types. Other machines have a pair of handles that you can manually adjust to near lower chest height. Certain machine models will also let you adjust the width of the handles in addition to their height. With one of those machines, setting the handles close to the point where they are slightly outside shoulder width is often appropriate.
    2. The appropriate hand placement should allow the exercise to feel natural and comfortable to your shoulders as you keep your forearms aligned with the direction in which you are pressing.
    3. The machine’s lever arms arc mildly downward during the rep’s lowering phase, so trying out a couple different hand positions may help you find the specific position that feels best to you throughout the entirety of each rep.
    4. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    5. Aligning your elbows directly behind the handles (rather than closer to your sides or further away from them) will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise.
5. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core throughout your set.
2. Press the handles forward until you have straightened your elbows, which will disengage the safety arm (i.e., what the machine lever arms initially rest upon).
    1. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the footplate.
3. Let the handles move backward in a controlled manner through the full range of motion your shoulder mobility comfortably permits.
    1. Keep your elbows and wrists aligned behind the handles as your elbows progressively move backward while bending.
    2. People commonly have the mobility to reach at least the point at which the handles are horizontally near chest level, but the end range will vary among different individuals.
    3. It is normal to feel a stretching sensation in the chest and the front of the shoulders at the bottom, but you should not force yourself into an end range that causes any shoulder joint discomfort.
    4. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should actively control the weight the entire time, rather than letting it quickly swing your arms back.
4. Reverse the movement as you press the handles forward by driving your elbows forward while straightening them.
    1. Continue keeping your elbows stacked behind your wrists with your forearms aligned with the direction in which you are pressing.
    2. Finish with your elbows fully straightened.
5. Alternate between steps 3-4 until you have completed your set.
    1. Note that the initial press from step 2 does not constitute a full rep. Its function is to assume the starting position.
6. Once you have locked out your final rep, press the foot pedal with one foot to engage the safety arm.
7. Move the handles back until you can rest the weight on the safety arm before letting go of the handles.
## Description
This exercise predominantly trains your pec major, front delts, and triceps with a technique that some people find feels more comfortable for their shoulders compared to other presses.## Setting up
1. If your machine lets you adjust it, set the pads to a starting height that allows you to stand with your shoulders snugly under the pads while your knees are slightly bent.
    1. Assuming the starting position with at least a slight knee bend will let you unrack the machine.
    2. If you are using a machine that lacks an adjustable starting height and are not tall enough to unrack the machine, a different variation can be more suitable for your body type.
2. In a hip-width to shoulder-width stance, stand with your feet fully on the footplate facing toward the machine’s base. Place your shoulders snugly underneath the shoulder pads in a position that feels comfortable and stable to you.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. Depending on the size of your machine’s footplate, you may have some space to vary specifically how far forward you place your feet. Starting near the middle of the footplate is often appropriate, but you can feel free to try out different foot placements to determine which position personally feels best to you.
3. Without having any weight plates added to the machine, perform a couple of reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section before finishing your setup.
    1. These initial reps will help you determine what your full range of motion entails and inform you if any adjustments (such as to your foot placement or machine settings) are warranted.
4. If your machine has an adjustable safety stopper, set it where it will be just below the machine’s lever arm at the bottom of your good morning.
    1. The ideal safety stopper height should be low enough to let you train through your full range of motion while being high enough to receive the lever arm if you fail a rep.
5. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
6. Repeat step 2 and proceed to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Straighten your knees to stand upright and lift the machine’s lever arm off the stopper.
2. Use your hand to disengage the stopper, and grasp the machine’s stationary handles (not the stopper release) for additional stability if your machine has them.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
4. While allowing your knees to bend mildly, sit your buttocks backward to hinge at your hips in a controlled manner as you keep your lower back flat, chest up, and core braced.
    1. Rather than thinking about “bending over,” focusing on “sitting back” will naturally angle your torso closer to horizontal.
    2. Most of the motion should come from your hips, and your shins should remain vertical throughout your set, but do not keep your knees fully straight. You do not need to focus on bending your knees, since the appropriately mild degree of knee bend can naturally occur when you sit your buttocks backward without intentionally keeping your knees locked.
    3. Rather than trying to “bend over,” you can think about “sitting back” as if you were trying to close a car door with your buttocks to cue the proper hip hinge.
    4. Throughout the set, keep your feet fully planted on the footplate and your neck near a neutral position, as when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
5. Continue hinging at your hips through the full range of motion in which you can comfortably move.
    1. How deeply you can hinge without rounding your lower back or excessively bending your knees depends on your hamstring flexibility.
    2. Once you have achieved what feels like the end range to which your hamstrings can safely stretch and can no longer sit back any farther, you have reached your personal end range. Descending past this point would require you to begin rounding your back or bending your knees in a squat-like manner.
    3. While you do not need to perform an extremely slow lowering phase, you should clearly be in control of the entire descent.
6. Reverse the movement by thrusting your hips forward to lift your torso upright into the starting position.
    1. The lifting phase should simply be a reversal of what you just performed, so keep your lower back flat, chest up, core braced, and feet fully planted on the footplate.
    2. In the top position, you should be able to draw a straight line from your shoulders through your hips to the middle of your feet when viewed from the side. Avoid leaning back at the top through excessive lower back arching, which would position your hips in front of the line passing from your shoulders to the middle of your feet.
7. Alternate between steps 4-6 until you have completed your set.
    1. Whenever you feel like you could use a breath, you can breathe in between reps while in the fully upright position.
    2. After taking a breath, make sure to resume bracing your core prior to initiating the descent for the next rep.
8. Once you are upright following your last rep, use your hand to engage the stopper before bending your knees to rerack the weight.
## Description
While statically training your erector spinae, this exercise can effectively develop your gluteus maximus, hamstrings, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. If your machine lets you adjust it, set the pads to a starting height that allows you to stand with your shoulders snugly under the pads while your knees are slightly bent.
    1. Assuming the starting position with at least a slight knee bend will let you unrack the machine.
    2. If you are using a machine that lacks an adjustable starting height and are not tall enough to unrack the machine, a different variation can be more suitable for your body type.
2. In a hip-width stance with your toes pointing straight ahead, stand with your feet fully on the footplate facing toward the machine’s base. Place your shoulders snugly underneath the shoulder pads in a position that feels comfortable and stable to you.
    1. Rather than being rounded in a slouched posture, your back and neck should be near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright and looking straight ahead.
    2. Depending on the size of your machine’s footplate, you may have some space to vary specifically how far forward you place your feet. Starting near the middle of the footplate is often appropriate, but you can feel free to try out different foot placements to determine which position personally feels best to you.
3. Without having any weight plates added to the machine, perform a couple of reps with the technique described in the “exercise technique” section before finishing your setup.
    1. These initial reps will help you determine what your full range of motion entails and inform you if any adjustments (such as to your foot placement, machine settings, or step length) are warranted.
    2. Note that not all leverage squat machines are ideal for every person to use for lunges. Certain machines may have a part of the base frame that can get in the way of the rear foot when lunging.
4. If your machine has an adjustable safety stopper, set it where it will be just below the machine’s lever arm at the bottom of your lunge.
    1. The ideal safety stopper height should be low enough to let you train through your full range of motion while being high enough to receive the lever arm if you fail a rep.
5. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
6. Repeat step 2 and proceed to the “exercise technique” section.
## Exercise technique
1. Straighten your knees to stand upright and lift the machine’s lever arm off the stopper.
2. Use your hand to disengage the stopper, and grasp the machine’s stationary handles (not the stopper release) for additional stability if your machine has them.
3. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. For additional stability, you can initially take a deep “belly breath” (AKA diaphragmatic breath) that expands the front and sides of your abdominals outward. Avoid taking a “chest breath” that predominantly lifts your chest upward and leaves your abdominals relaxed, or simply sucking your belly button in.
    2. Continue bracing your core from this point on to maintain a rigid back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
4. While keeping the entire sole of one foot firmly planted on the footplate, step backward with the other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Step far enough backward that the footplate or machine base will not get in the way of your rear knee. If your machine allows, your step length should ideally be long enough that you can contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying different step lengths.
    2. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    3. Keep the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability, rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
5. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, let your knees bend as you lower your body in a controlled manner. Lunge down through the full range of motion that is comfortable to you.
    1. For some lifters, a full range of motion may involve gently tapping the ground with the rear knee, but others may reach the end range of motion before that point.
    2. Keep your chest up, lower back flat, and the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot planted on the footplate.
    3. Keep your knees aligned with your feet rather than letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    4. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    5. Perform steps 4 and 5 as a smooth and continuous movement.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, your front leg’s ankle mobility may limit your range of motion as your knee tracks forward over toes. Descending beyond this point may require lifting your lead heel off the footplate, which you want to avoid.
6. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step forward with your rear leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet, chest up, and lower back flat.
7. Repeat steps 4-6 while lunging backward with your opposite side.
8. Alternate between steps 4-7 until you have completed your set.
9. Once you are upright following your last rep, use your hand to engage the stopper before bending your knees to rerack the weight.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Pick up a pair of dumbbells and stand upright with your upper back against the front of the pad of a sufficiently tall preacher curl bench, glute ham developer, or similar object.
    1. While tall preacher curl benches and glute ham developers are most often used, you can use any object that has a pad against which you can rest your upper back while performing biceps curls.
    2. Short preacher curl benches will not be suitable for most people, and your height will naturally affect which object may be suitable for you.
2. Walk your feet slightly forward while resting your triceps (on the backside of your upper arms) against the pad and setting your feet about hip width to shoulder width apart.
    1. Once you set your feet, your knees should be straight, and your back should be near a neutral position (rather than being excessively arched or rounded), like when you are normally standing upright.
3. While holding the dumbbells by your sides with straight elbows, direct your palms forward to adopt an underhand grip.
    1. Maintain this underhand grip on the dumbbells throughout the set.
## Exercise technique
1. Curl the dumbbells upward by fully bending elbows while keeping your upper arms in contact with the pad behind you.
    1. While your forearms move in an arc through elbow joint motion, the rest of your body should remain stationary as you avoid swinging your elbows upward through shoulder motion.
2. Reverse the movement by lowering the dumbbells in a controlled manner until you have fully straightened your elbows.
    1. Continue keeping your upper arms still as all the motion occurs from your forearms hinging about your elbows like the hand of an analog clock rotating about its center.
    2. There is no need to lower the dumbbells with an extremely slow tempo, but you should not let the dumbbells abruptly swing down.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
Like other curl variations, this exercise can effectively train your biceps, brachialis, and brachioradialis.## Setting up
1. Select your desired weight with the machine’s loading pin, and step up onto the footplates with the outer aspects of your knees contacting the pads.
    1. Based on your preference, you can point your feet straight ahead or angle them slightly out to the sides.
2. Grasp the handles at a height that feels comfortable and stable for you to hold.
3. While keeping your shins vertical with your knees against the pads, sit your hips slightly back while letting your knees slightly bend to assume the starting position.
## Exercise technique
1. Drive your knees outward to spread your legs fully apart while simultaneously sitting your hips back and down in a squatting motion.
    1. People commonly squat down until their hips are a little above knee height during this variation, but you can try out different depths to determine what personally feels best for you.
    2. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable to you, you can keep your torso fairly upright or lean forward at your hips to a greater degree when descending.
    3. Rather than excessively arching or rounding your back, keep it near a neutral position, like when you are normally standing upright.
    4. Keep your shins vertical rather than pushing your knees forward toward your toes.
    5. How far apart you can spread your legs will be affected by your hip mobility.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by allowing your legs to move in toward each other while squatting up until you have returned to the starting position.
    1. You should finish in a mostly upright position with your hips and knees slightly bent and your legs as close together as the machine permits while keeping your knees snugly against the pads.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should be in clear control of the weight, rather than letting it abruptly swing your legs inward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
    1. Following your last rep, you can stand fully upright before stepping off the footplates.
## Description
While also training several smaller muscles on the side of your hip, this exercise can dynamically train your glutes, likely with an emphasis on the upper region of your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
Note that different lunge machine models vary in terms of which features can be adjusted. The setup sequence will differ depending on whether you have a machine with a calf pad, but the exercise technique can be the same regardless of which machine you are using. Correspondingly, a setup section is provided for each type of machine design, but they use the same exercise technique section

### **Setting up a machine without calf pads**

1. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand centered between its handles in a hip-width stance.
    1. If your machine model lets you adjust the handle height, you can set them low enough for you to be able to lunge through your full range of motion without the machine’s lever arms contacting the stoppers under them. This will typically involve setting the handle below mid-shin height when you are standing on the footplate.
    2. If your machine can link the lever arms together (to ensure that they move together), you can link them together for the greatest stability.
2. Set the calf pad to be level with your upper calf below your knee when you are standing on the platform.
3. While facing away from the base of the machine with your feet hip width apart, stand with one foot on the stationary platform and the other foot on the gliding footplate.
    1. The calf of your front leg (which is the side you will primarily train during this set) should be directly in front of the calf.
4. If your machine has an adjustable roller pad in front of your lead foot, you can optionally adjust it to a setting that positions it above your toes.
    1. Lifters who prefer to lunge with a vertical shin may want to set the roller far enough back that it snugly secures the top of the front foot, but lifters who prefer to lunge with a greater degree of forward knee travel may want to set the roller further away from them so that it does not limit ankle motion.
5. In a hip-width stance facing away from the base of the machine, stand centered on the platform as the handles are aligned with the middle of your feet.
6. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, sit your hips back and down while bending your knees until you can firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles, you can grasp the lowest handles that you can reach without rounding your lower back to lunge through a full range of motion.
7. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
8. Drive through your front leg to stand upright as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips while keeping your chest up and back flat.
    1. Finish with a soft bend in your knees rather than fully straightening them.

### **Setting up a machine without calf pads**

1. After loading your desired amount of weight onto the machine, stand centered between its handles in a hip-width stance.
    1. If your machine can link the lever arms together (to ensure that they move together), you can link them together for the greatest stability.
2. In a hip-width stance facing away from the base of the machine, stand centered on the platform as the handles are aligned with the middle of your feet.
    1. If your machine has a calf pad, set it to be level with your upper calf below your knee.
3. While keeping your lower back flat and chest up, sit your hips back and down while bending your knees to squat down until you can firmly grasp the handles with straight elbows as your palms face in toward each other.
    1. If your machine has multiple pairs of handles, it is generally recommended to grasp the lower handles to let you train through a longer range of motion if you can reach those handles without rounding your lower back.
4. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point onward.
5. Drive through your legs to stand upright as you extend (straighten) your knees and hips while keeping your chest up and back flat.
6. Shift your weight into the leg you will primarily train during this, and step backward with your other foot to place your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes) onto the sliding footplate.
    1. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the footplate, the rest of the foot’s sole will be above the footplate as it is directed backward.
## Exercise technique
1. While chiefly bearing your weight through your front leg, bend at your front knee and hip to lunge down in a controlled manner while letting your rear foot slide back.
    1. Keep your knees aligned with your feet as you avoid letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    2. Keep your chest up and lower back flat rather than meaningfully rounding your back.
    3. Keep the top of your hip bones level with each other to the best of your ability as you avoid letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down.
    4. Based on your preference and what feels comfortable, you can vary how much you bend your rear knee and how upright you keep your torso. You have the option of bending both knees to similar degrees with a mostly upright torso, or you can bend your rear knee mildly while more notably leaning your torso forward at your lead leg’s hip. Feel free to try out different techniques to determine which one may feel best to you.
2. Continue descending through the full range of motion that is comfortable to you while keeping your lead foot fully planted on the platform.
    1. Your lead leg’s hip mobility will commonly determine how deeply you can lunge, and it is typical to feel a stretching sensation in your lead leg’s glute in the bottom position.
    2. Particularly when maintaining a more upright torso position, the mobility or flexibility of the rear leg may also determine lunge depth for some people, as indicated by a deep stretching sensation in the front of the rear leg’s hip.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your lead knee and hip as you drive through the front leg to lift yourself up and slide your rear foot forward to the starting position.
    1. You can finish the ascent with a slight bend in your front knee rather than fully locking it out.
    2. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet and chest up with a flat lower back.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to perform a set with your rear leg now in the front.
    1. If your machine lacks a calf pad, you can step forward with your rear leg to position your feet symmetrically after the set before switching sides or setting the weight down.
    2. If your machine has a calf pad between your legs, it may be easiest to set the weight down by bending at your hips and knees while your rear leg is still on the sliding footplate rather than first stepping forward with your rear leg around the calf pad.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your quads, gluteus maximus, and adductor magnus.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with the lower half of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is too low (placing the handles near shoulder level or above), some lifters may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort from having to flare their elbows outward excessively.
    4. If the seat is excessively high (placing the handles below the lowest part of the chest), the exercise may feel awkward and be suboptimal for developing the chest.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust either aspect, position its arms or back pad to a setting that enables you to press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Some machines allow you to increase the range of motion by placing the arms further back toward the back pad, while others allow you to move the back pad forward, closer toward the arms. However, most machine models may not permit you to adjust either part.
    2. If you have normal shoulder mobility, you can initially position the arms or the seat to a setting that results in the handles being near the front of your chest (when viewed from the side) once you are seated. This commonly involves setting the arms as far backward or the seat as far forward as the machine enables.
    3. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles with proper positioning during step 5, you can adjust the machine to increase the distance between the back pad and the handles until you can do so.
    4. The ideal setting should allow your elbows to start as far behind your torso as is comfortable for your shoulders while keeping your elbows level with the handles.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. Firmly grasp the handles with a wide grip that situates your hands clearly outside shoulder width. Position your elbows directly behind the handles, resulting in your elbows being level with the handles when viewed from the side.
    1. What is personally considered a wide grip to you can be influenced by the specific grip width you use for your typical chest press, which you can somewhat widen to individualize your wide grip. For instance, for your wide grip, you can place your index fingers on the handles where you would otherwise place your little fingers when performing your standard chest press.
    2. The appropriate grip width should let you feel meaningful tension in your chest throughout the exercise while feeling comfortable to your shoulders and enabling you to stack your elbows behind your wrists. Feel free to try out different hand placements to determine what personally feels best for you.
    3. Hold the handles on top of the bases of your palms (AKA heels of your hands) with mostly straight wrists that allow for a comfortable and stable grip that you will maintain throughout your set. Squeezing the handles firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    4. Aligning your elbows directly behind (rather than being above or below) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you select a proper seat height.
    5. While certain machines may have multiple pairs of handles that enable different grip types, most models will have one pair of handles that let you grasp them with your palms angled downward and inward (for a grip that is a hybrid between an overhand grip and a neutral grip).
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. Press the handles forward until you have straightened your elbows.
    1. You can think about driving your elbows forward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. Continue keeping your forearms aligned with the angle of your press as your elbows remain stacked behind your wrists.
    3. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet planted on the ground.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handles to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. At the end range, you optionally can lightly tap the machine’s arms against the stoppers behind them. A gentle tap will confirm that you have moved through the full range of motion in a controlled manner.
    2. Continue keeping your elbows and wrists stacked in alignment.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulders at the end range.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that situates the handles level with the lower half of your chest once you are seated.
    1. This is an appropriate starting point for most people, and it can result in somewhat of a range of viable seat heights. Feel free to try out different settings to determine what personally feels best to your body.
    2. The seat height that is ideal for you as an individual should allow your shoulders to feel comfortable throughout the exercise while simultaneously allowing you to feel meaningful tension in your chest.
    3. If the seat is too low (placing the handles near shoulder level or above), some lifters may be more likely to feel shoulder discomfort from having to flare their elbows outward excessively.
    4. If the seat is excessively high (placing the handles below the lowest part of the chest), the exercise may feel awkward and be suboptimal for developing the chest.
2. If your machine allows you to adjust either aspect, position its arms or back pad to a setting that enables you to press through the full range of motion that your shoulder mobility permits.
    1. Some machines allow you to increase the range of motion by placing the arms further back toward the back pad, while others allow you to move the back pad forward, closer toward the arms. However, most machine models may not permit you to adjust either part.
    2. If you have normal shoulder mobility, you can initially position the arms or the seat to a setting that results in the handles being near the front of your chest (when viewed from the side) once you are seated. This commonly involves setting the arms as far backward or the seat as far forward as the machine enables.
    3. If you cannot comfortably grasp the handles with proper positioning during step 5, you can adjust the machine to increase the distance between the back pad and the handles until you can do so.
    4. The ideal setting should allow your elbows to start as far behind your torso as is comfortable for your shoulders while keeping your elbows level with the handles.
3. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
4. Sit down on the machine’s seat with your back and the back of your hip bones snugly against the back pad.
    1. You can optionally rest your head against the back pad if it is tall enough, or you can simply keep your neck near a neutral position, like when you are normally looking straight ahead.
5. Firmly grasp the handle on the side of your body you will train during this set. Situate your hand clearly further out to your side than your shoulder, with your elbow positioned directly behind your wrist, resulting in your elbow being level with the handle when viewed from the side.
    1. Grasp the handle further out to the side than you would with a standard grip chest press. As a starting point, you can place your index finger on the handle where you would otherwise place your little finger when performing a standard chest press.
    2. The appropriate grip width should let you feel meaningful tension in your chest throughout the exercise while feeling comfortable to your shoulder and enabling you to stack your elbow behind your wrist. Feel free to try out different hand placements to determine what personally feels best for you.
    3. Hold the handle on top of the base of your palm (AKA the heel of your hand) with a mostly straight wrist that allows for a comfortable and stable grip throughout your set. Squeezing the handle firmly can help promote this wrist position.
    4. Aligning your elbow directly behind (rather than being above or below) the handles will naturally promote the appropriate degree of elbow flare during this exercise, provided that you selected a proper seat height.
    5. While certain machines may have multiple pairs of handles that enable different grip types, most models will have one pair of handles that let you grasp them with your palms angled downward and inward (for a grip that is a hybrid between an overhand grip and a neutral grip).
    6. You can position your non-working arm wherever feels most comfortable and stable to you. Some people like to grasp the non-working side’s handle without moving it during the set, while others like to reach across the body and hold the torso with the free hand.
6. Pull your shoulder blades back together to assume a “proud chest” posture that puffs your chest slightly up toward the ceiling.
    1. Maintain this posture throughout your set.
    2. Rather than excessively arching your lower back, maintain the natural curvature of your lower back like when you are normally standing upright.
    3. As a cue for pulling your shoulder blades back, you can think about trying to squeeze a pencil between your shoulder blades.
## Exercise technique
1. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forceful slap.
    1. You will continue bracing your core throughout your set, which will help your body function as a stable platform from which to press.
2. While keeping your forearm aligned with the angle at which you are pressing, drive the handle forward until you have straightened your elbow.
    1. You can think about driving your elbow forward and inward in an arcing manner.
    2. Throughout your set, continue keeping your shoulder blades pulled back together, back snugly against the back pad, and feet stationary.
3. Reverse the movement by allowing the handle to move backward in a controlled manner toward the starting position.
    1. Continue keeping your forearm aligned with the angle of your press as you let you let your elbow flare out from your side.
    2. There is no need to perform this step extremely slowly, but you should avoid letting the machine’s weight swing your arm abruptly back in an uncontrolled fashion.
    3. If your machine allows you to train through the full range of motion that your mobility enables, it is normal to feel a painless stretching sensation in your chest and/or the front of your shoulder at the end range.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all regions of your pec major while also training your front delts, triceps, and coracobrachialis.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates to a barbell situated in a landmine attachment (or a viable substitute).
    1. As an alternative to using a landmine attachment, landmine exercises can be performed with one end of the barbell situated in a wall corner.
    2. While the scraping can be lessened by placing a towel in between the wall corner and the barbell, be aware that performing landmine exercises in a wall corner can easily damage the wall corner, so this approach is not appropriate in every environment.
    3. The diameter of the plates you select may influence the range of motion of this lunge for some lifters. If you notice that full-diameter weight plates (like 45lb (20kg) or bumper plates) contact the ground during your lunge and restrict your depth, exclusively loading the bar with smaller-diameter plates can be warranted.
2. In a near hip-width stance, stand with the end of the barbell directly next to the side of your body that will be your stepping leg for this set of lunges.
    1. The lunge will chiefly train the leg further away from the bar, so you can position your right side next to the bar when training your left leg in the lead position.
    2. Based on your preference, you can set up with the end of the bar just to the side of the outer aspect of your lower leg, or you can stand with the end of the bar just in front of your shin. The first approach can make it more challenging for your core and hip muscles to stabilize your trunk and hip compared to if you have the end of the bar closer to the midline of your body.
3. While keeping your chest up and lower back flat, sit your hips back and down while bending your knees until you can reach the bar with your closer hand.
4. Firmly grasp the end of the bar’s sleeve with a straight elbow and an overhand grip, resulting in your palm facing backward.
    1. When grasping the end of the bar rather than closer toward the weight plates, there should not be any space between the end of the bar and your thumb.
    2. Based on your preference, you can wrap your thumb around the outer flat end of the bar, or you can wrap it around the circular end of the bar opposite to your other four fingers, like you would when typically grasping a bar with an overhand grip.
    3. Keep free arm in a comfortable position throughout the set. Some lifters may opt to hold the free arm out to the side away from the bar to provide a counterbalance against the weight, while others may let the arm hang down in a more relaxed position.
5. Brace your core by tensing the front and sides of your abdominals outward, like you would if you were bracing for someone to strike your stomach with a punch or forcible slap.
    1. Continue bracing your core from this point to maintain a stable back position while using your lower body muscles to lift the weight.
6. Stand upright with the bar close to your leg by driving through your legs to extend (straighten) your knees and hips.
    1. Keep your shoulders level and chest up as you avoid letting the weight bend your torso to the side or round your back.
## Exercise technique
1. Shift your weight into your leg that is further away from the bar, and step backward with your other leg before contacting the ground with your forefoot (ball of your foot and bottom of your toes).
    1. Keep the entirety (heel, inside of forefoot, and outside of forefoot) of your front foot in contact with the ground throughout your set.
    2. Step far enough backward that you will be able to contact the ground with your rear knee, which may initially require trying out different step lengths if you are new to backward lunges.
    3. Strive to step backward in a straight line rather than stepping closer in toward the midline of your body, which would position your feet horizontally closer together and narrow your base of support.
    4. Aim to keep the tops of your hip bones level with each other rather than letting the hip bone on the side of your back leg drop down. As a cue for keeping your hip bones level, you can imagine that you have a basin of water filled to the brim on top of your hip bones, and you are trying not to spill any water by tipping it to either side.
2. After contacting the ground with your rear foot, bend your knees to lower your body in a controlled manner until you can gently tap the ground with your rear leg’s kneecap.
    1. Keep your knees aligned with your feet as you avoid letting either knee cave inward relative to that side’s foot (when viewed from the front or the back).
    2. Rather than letting your back round or torso bend to the side, keep your chest up and shoulders level as motion occurs at your hips and knees instead of your back.
    3. As a cue for the appropriately controlled descent, you can imagine that a weight scale is underneath your rear knee, and gently tapping it will not cause the scale to read more than about two pounds (one kilogram).
    4. While the forefoot of your rear leg will be in contact with the ground, the rest of the foot’s sole will be off the ground as it is directed backward.
    5. Perform steps 1 and 2 as a smooth and continuous movement while letting your arm hang down toward the ground.
    6. If you initially took an excessively short step backward, you may not be able to tap the ground with your rear knee unless you also lift the heel of your front foot up off the ground. If you find that you have to lift your front heel up to tap the ground, simply descend as much as you can without lifting up your front heel, and take a longer step backward during your next rep.
3. Reverse the movement by progressively extending (straightening) your knees as you step forward with your rear leg to lift your body up until you have returned to the starting position with your feet symmetrically hip-width apart and your torso fully upright.
    1. Continue keeping your knees aligned with your feet, shoulders level, and chest up.
4. Repeat steps 1-3 until you have completed your set and are ready to perform a set where you step back with your opposite leg.
    1. After finishing the last rep, sit your hips back and down while bending your knees to set the bar down before pivoting 180 degrees to perform a set facing the opposite direction.
## Description
This exercise primarily trains your gluteus maximus, adductor magnus, and three of your four quad muscles.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that lets you comfortably grasp the handles and position your upper arm by your sides with your elbows bent.
    1. If your machine’s handles are connected to pivot points (the axes about which the lever arms rotate) that are situated in front of the back pad, set the seat to a height that lets you align your elbows with the pivot points when your shoulders are relaxed (i.e., not shrugged up or packed down). Doing so will help the exercise feel the most comfortable.
    2. Note that some models have pivot points positioned behind the seat, and you do not have to align the elbows with these pivot points when setting up.
    3. A minority of machines will have a pair of hand pads instead of handles.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and sit down on the seat with your back against the back pad and your feet flat on the ground.
3. Firmly grasp the handles and position your upper arms by your sides with your elbows bent.
    1. If your machine has arm pads, your triceps should now be resting against them, and if your machine has pivot points in front of the back pad, your elbows should now be aligned with them.
    2. If you are using a machine with hand pads instead of handles, you have the option of holding the pads with your palms against them, or you can place the little finger side of your lower forearms against the pads while closing your hands into fists. Select whichever approach feels most comfortable to you.
    3. With the appropriate setup, you should now feel stable in the starting position
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arms stationary, press the handles down by fully straightening your elbows.
    1. As the rest of your body remains stationary, all the movement should occur from your forearms arcing forward through elbow motion, like the hands of an analog clock rotating about the center points formed by your elbows.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your elbows bend in a controlled manner to raise the handles into the starting position.
    1. Avoid letting your elbows move forward through shoulder motion.
    2. Allow your elbows to bend through the full range of motion in which the machine provides resistance.
    3. While you do not need to perform this phase with an extremely slow tempo, avoid letting the weight abruptly swing your forearms upward in an unresisted fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s foot plate to a height that aligns the machine’s pivot points with the height of your shoulders once you are standing on the foot plate.
    1. On each side of the machine, the pivot point is the center about which the lever arm rotates, and it is denoted by a small dot or circle in some machines.
    2. Aligning the center of your shoulders with the pivot points can help this exercise feel more comfortable and natural compared to if the foot plate height resulted in your shoulders being above or below the pivot points.
    3. To confirm whether you selected an appropriate foot plate height, you will have to stand on it and visually check where your shoulders are positioned relative to the pivot points, so you may have to try out different settings before finding one that best suits your body.
    4. A foot plate height that aligns your shoulders with the pivot points will commonly allow you to hold the handles with slightly bent elbows.
2. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, and stand on the foot plate with the handles situated just in front of your thighs.
3. With the side of your body you will train during this set, firmly grasp the handle in front of it with a slightly bent elbow as your palm faces in toward your other side.
    1. Situate your free hand into your preferred position, which may involve holding the other handle, grasping a center hand grip available with certain machines, or resting it by your side.
4. Optionally lean your torso slightly forward at your hips by sitting your buttocks slightly backward with a very slight knee bend. You can alternatively stand fully upright if you prefer to do so.
    1. Maintain your preferred torso position throughout your set, as you subsequently move your shoulders without moving your lower body or torso.
## Exercise technique
1. Sweep the handle out to the side and upward in a smooth, arcing manner until your elbow is near shoulder height.
    1. Let your shoulder blade move naturally rather than trying to hold it in any particular position.
    2. At the top of the raise, your arm should form half of a “T-shape” with your torso when viewed from the front or back.
2. Reverse the motion in a controlled manner by letting your arm lower in toward the starting position.
    1. To confirm you have moved through your full range of motion, you can lightly tap the machine’s lever arm (onto which you loaded the plates) to the stopper underneath it, or you can stop just before this point.
    2. While there is no need to use an extremely slow tempo, you should be actively controlling the weight the entire time. Do not let the weight abruptly swing your arm downward.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set and are ready to repeat the exercise with your other side.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your lateral delt and supraspinatus, which are assisted by your front delt and upper trap.## Setting up
1. Adjust the machine’s seat to a height that lets you comfortably grasp the handles and position your upper arm by your sides with your elbows bent.
    1. If your machine’s handles are connected to pivot points (the axes about which the lever arms rotate) that are situated in front of the back pad, set the seat to a height that lets you align your elbows with the pivot points when your shoulders are relaxed (i.e., not shrugged up or packed down). Doing so will help the exercise feel the most comfortable.
    2. Note that some models have pivot points positioned behind the seat, and you do not have to align the elbows with these pivot points when setting up.
    3. A minority of machines will have a pair of hand pads instead of handles.
2. Select your desired weight with the loading pin, and sit down on the seat with your back against the back pad and your feet flat on the ground.
3. Firmly grasp the handles and position your upper arms by your sides with your elbows bent.
    1. If your machine has arm pads, your triceps should now be resting against them, and if your machine has pivot points in front of the back pad, your elbows should now be aligned with them.
    2. If you are using a machine with hand pads instead of handles, you have the option of holding the pads with your palms against them, or you can place the little finger side of your lower forearms against the pads while closing your hands into fists. Select whichever approach feels most comfortable to you.
    3. With the appropriate setup, you should now feel stable in the starting position
## Exercise technique
1. While keeping your upper arms stationary, press the handles down by fully straightening your elbows.
    1. As the rest of your body remains stationary, all the movement should occur from your forearms arcing forward through elbow motion, like the hands of an analog clock rotating about the center points formed by your elbows.
2. Reverse the movement by letting your elbows bend in a controlled manner to raise the handles into the starting position.
    1. Avoid letting your elbows move forward through shoulder motion.
    2. Allow your elbows to bend through the full range of motion in which the machine provides resistance.
    3. While you do not need to perform this phase with an extremely slow tempo, avoid letting the weight abruptly swing your forearms upward in an unresisted fashion.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop all three heads of your triceps, including its large long head, which may not be trained as well by compound pressing exercises.## Setting up
1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, ****and ****if your machine allows you to adjust the backrest angle, position it fully upright for this variation.
2. Sit down on the machine's seat with your torso upright against the backrest and the back of your hip bones against the backrest.
    1. In this position, your back should exhibit its natural curvature rather than being excessively arched or rounded.
3. Place your feet on the foot bar with the outer aspect of your knees and/or lower thighs contacting the knee pads.
    1. If you have rather short legs, you may have to scoot slightly forward for your knees to reach the pads, which will require you to lean your torso somewhat backward to keep your back against the backrest.
    2. Rather than knee pads and foot bars, certain machines may have lower leg pads onto which you can rest your lower legs.
4. If you can set the foot bars and knee pads closer together, adjust them to where they are as close together as they can be to train through the longest range of motion.
    1. It may be easiest to sit onto the machine when the foot bars and knee pads are initially positioned further apart before adjusting them once you are seated.
5. If your machine has a seatbelt, snugly fasten it around your hips.
6. Grasp the handles for additional stability and continue holding them throughout your set.
## Exercise technique
1. Push out against the pads to spread your legs fully apart.
    1. Your legs will naturally descend closer to the ground when you focus on spreading your legs apart along the machine’s path of motion.
    2. How far apart you can spread your legs will be affected by your hip mobility.
    3. Maintain the natural curvature of your back throughout the set rather than excessively arching it or rounding during the exercise.
2. Reverse the motion by letting your legs move fully in toward each other in a controlled manner.
    1. After doing so, you should now be in the starting position with your legs as close together as the machine permits.
    2. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance abruptly swing your legs together.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
While also training several smaller muscles on the side of your hip, this exercise can dynamically train your glutes, with an emphasis on the upper region of your gluteus maximus.## Setting up
A machine that lets you adjust its backrest angle to a reclined position or move the backrest back (letting you lean back) is ideal for this variation, but not all machine models let you adjust the backrest. If your machine does not permit you to adjust the backrest, you may alternatively bridge your hips up to place your hips in a similar position. Specifically how you will set up will differ depending on which equipment you use, so the set-up for each approach is described separately, but a single exercise technique section is provided. If you find this variation to feel awkward when using a machine without an adjustable backrest, you can select “upright machine 3D hip abduction” as an alternative exercise from this database.

### **Setting up with an adjustable backrest**

1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine, ****and ****fully recline the backrest angle.
2. Sit down on the machine's seat with your torso upright.
    1. It can be easier to set up while initially sitting upright before leaning back.
3. Place your feet on the foot bar with the outer aspect of your knees and/or lower thighs contacting the knee pads.
    1. If you have rather short legs, you may have to scoot slightly forward for your knees to reach the pads.
    2. Rather than knee pads and foot bars, certain machines may have lower leg pads onto which you can rest your lower legs.
4. If you can set the foot bars and knee pads closer together, adjust them to where they are as close together as they can be to train through the longest range of motion.
    1. It may be easiest to sit onto the machine when the foot bars and knee pads are initially positioned further apart before adjusting them once you are seated.
5. If your machine has a seatbelt, snugly fasten it around your hips.
6. Lean back at your hips and place your back on the backrest.
    1. In this position, your lower back should exhibit its natural curvature rather than being excessively arched.
7. Grasp the handles for additional stability and continue holding them throughout your set.

### **Setting up with an upright backrest**

1. Add weight plates of your desired load to the machine.
2. Sit down on the machine's seat and place your feet on the foot bar with the outer aspect of your knees and/or lower thighs contacting the knee pads.
    1. If you have rather short legs, you may have to scoot slightly forward for your knees to reach the pads, which will require you to lean your torso somewhat backward to keep your back against the backrest.
3. If you can set the foot bars and knee pads closer together, adjust them to where they are as close together as they can be to train through the longest range of motion.
    1. It may be easiest to sit onto the machine when the foot bars and knee pads are initially positioned further apart before adjusting them once you are seated.
4. Grasp the handles for additional stability and continue holding them throughout your set.
5. Position your shoulder blades against the backrest, and bridge your hips up above the seat while keeping your back flat. Maintain this bridged hip position throughout the exercise.
    1. Avoid arching your lower back excessively when lifting your hips. Tightening your abdominal muscles to brace your core can help you keep your back flat.
    2. How far you can lift your hips up while maintaining a stable position can be influenced by your body proportions and the specific machine you are using. The appropriate degree of bridging should allow you to feel stable.
    3. You do not need to lift your hips fully up until your thighs are aligned with your torso (like you would do during a glute bridge) if you feel unstable doing so. Feel free to try out different bridge heights over time to determine what feels best to you.
## Exercise technique
1. Push out against the pads to spread your legs fully apart.
    1. Your legs will naturally descend closer to the ground when you focus on spreading your legs apart along the machine’s path of motion.
    2. How far apart you can spread your legs will be affected by your hip mobility.
    3. Maintain the natural curvature of your lower back throughout the set rather than excessively arching it.
2. Reverse the motion by letting your legs move fully in toward each other in a controlled manner.
    1. After doing so, you should now be in the starting position with your legs as close together as the machine permits.
    2. There is no need to perform this step with an extremely slow tempo, but you should avoid letting the machine’s resistance abruptly swing your legs together.
3. Alternate between steps 1 and 2 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively develop your upper gluteus maximus, gluteus medius, and gluteus minimus.## Setting up
You can perform this exercise with an asymmetrically weighted implement by holding it by its lighter end, or you can use a symmetrically weighted implement by holding it closer to one end to provide a similar effect. Asymmetrical implements include wrist bars (a type of equipment specifically designed for wrist exercises that looks like an adjustable dumbbell where plates can be added only onto one side), adjustable dumbbells with plates loaded only on one side, and kettlebells. Symmetrical implements that may be used include dumbbells and weight plates. Based on your preference, you can also perform this exercise with your forearms supported on your thighs while seated, or you can kneel on the ground and rest your forearms on a flat bench in front of you.

### **Setting up** with an asymmetrically weighted implement

1. Firmly grasp a pair of asymmetrically weighted implements by their lighter ends with their heavier ends in front of you. If you are using kettlebells, firmly grasp them by their center handles.
    1. If you are using wrist bars or adjustable dumbbells weighted on only one side, grasping them further away from their weighted ends will increase the resistance they provide during this exercise.
2. Sit down on a seat or bench, and position your elbows and forearms on top of your thighs with bent elbows, your wrists a little in front of your knees, and your palms facing in toward each other. Alternatively, you can rest your elbows and forearms on a flat bench while kneeling on the ground with your palms facing each other.
    1. The appropriate position should let your elbows and forearms feel well-supported regardless of whether they are resting on your thighs or a bench.
    2. Your index fingers’ knuckles should be vertically stacked above your little fingers’ knuckles.
    3. If you are using kettlebells, their bases should now be directed forward.
    4. If you are using wrist bars or adjustable dumbbells weighted on only one side, the weighted ends should now be above your hands.

### **Setting up** with an asymmetrically weighted implement

1. Firmly grasp a pair of dumbbells with your little fingers contacting one end of the weight, leaving some space where your index fingers are by the other end. Alternatively, grasp a pair of weight plates with your thumbs against one side of the plates and your other four fingers against the other side.
    1. Maintaining a pinch grip with weight plates can be more challenging for your hand muscles compared to if you use dumbbells.
2. Sit down on a seat or bench, and position your elbows and forearms on top of your thighs with bent elbows, your wrists a little in front of your knees, and your palms facing in toward each other. Alternatively, you can rest your elbows and forearms on a flat bench while kneeling on the ground with your palms facing each other.
    1. The appropriate position should let your elbows and forearms feel well-supported regardless of whether they are resting on your thighs or a bench.
    2. Your index fingers’ knuckles should be vertically stacked above your little fingers’ knuckles.
## Exercise technique
1. Let the weights tilt your hands down through a range of motion comfortable to your wrists to assume the starting position of the exercise.
2. Tilt your hands up toward the thumb-side of your forearms through the full range of motion in which your weight provides resistance.
    1. Some implements will stop resisting radial deviation (the wrist motion you are performing) once you have achieved a neutral wrist position (i.e., when your hand is not tilted to either side), while others can provide resistance through the full range of motion in which you move your wrists.
    2. For instance, an adjustable dumbbell with plates loaded on only one side will provide a force that pulls you into greater radial deviation once you are radially deviated beyond neutral, so radially deviating beyond a neutral position is no longer training the target muscles. In contrast, kettlebells can provide resistance to your radial deviator muscles throughout the full range of motion of your wrists, because the center of mass of kettlebells remains in front of your wrists the whole time.
    3. Individuals with normal wrist mobility can typically tilt their hands only mildly beyond a neutral wrist position, so the range of motion for this exercise may look fairly similar when stopping in a neutral position or a radially deviated position.
    4. While you move your hands through wrist motion, keep the rest of your body stationary throughout the exercise, which includes keeping your forearms and elbows resting on your thighs/bench.
3. Reverse the movement in a controlled manner by letting your hands tilt toward the little finger-side of your forearms through your wrist’s comfortable range of motion.
    1. This will angle your knuckles at least mildly downward.
    2. Only move through the range of motion that is comfortable to your wrist and thumb.
    3. There is no need to perform this extremely slowly, but you should actively control the movement rather than letting the weight swing your hand down.
4. Alternate between steps 2 and 3 until you have completed your set.
## Description
This exercise can effectively train five forearm muscles, namely the two extensor carpi radialis muscles, flexor carpi radialis, extensor pollicis brevis, and abductor pollicis longus.